__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"CHAPTERS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={w1:{title:"Commercial Door Hardware \u2014 Field Reference",sections:[{heading:"How a pro reads an opening",body:"Before you quote, order, or turn a screwdriver, you read the opening as a system: the door, the frame, the hardware, and the code that governs them. Name each part correctly and the rest of the job \u2014 pricing, ordering, installing \u2014 stops being guesswork.\n\nStart with the door and frame. Is the door hollow metal (steel), solid core wood, or aluminum stile-and-rail (glass storefront)? Is the frame hollow metal, aluminum, or wood? Material decides your fasteners and your prep: through-bolts and machine screws into reinforcements on hollow metal, wood screws into solid backing on wood, and narrow hardware on aluminum stiles.\n\nThen read the hardware family, the function, and the handing. Family is the type of lock (cylindrical, mortise, exit device, deadbolt). Function is how it locks and unlocks (storeroom, classroom, entry, exit-only). Handing is which way it's hung and swings. Those three, plus finish and size, are exactly what a supplier needs to hand you the right part the first time."},{heading:"Handing, bevel, and why it matters",body:"Handing is determined from the OUTSIDE of the door \u2014 the secure or key side \u2014 every time. Facing the outside: hinges on your left is Left Hand (LH); hinges on your right is Right Hand (RH). If the door swings toward you as you stand outside (it pulls open), it is a reverse bevel, and you add 'Reverse': LHR or RHR.\n\nMost commercial exterior doors swing outward for egress, so from the outside they pull toward you \u2014 which is why so much commercial hardware is specified LHR or RHR. Getting the hand wrong means levers point the wrong way, cylinders and cams face backward, and closers and exit devices don't fit.\n\nBevel is the slight angle machined on the lock edge of the door so it clears the frame as it swings; on standard doors it's about 1/8\" in 2\". You rarely spec bevel directly, but understanding it explains why a 'reverse bevel' door (swinging out) reads differently from a standard-bevel door swinging in."},{heading:"Standards & grades: ANSI/BHMA and UL",body:"Commercial hardware is rated by ANSI/BHMA A156 standards, each covering one product type, each with three grades (Grade 1 highest, then 2, then 3). Know these numbers \u2014 architects and suppliers speak in them:\n\n\u2022 A156.2 \u2014 Bored (cylindrical) locks and latches\n\u2022 A156.13 \u2014 Mortise locks and latches (Grade 1 = 1,000,000 cycles, 1,000 lbf)\n\u2022 A156.3 \u2014 Exit devices (Grade 1 = 500,000 cycles)\n\u2022 A156.4 \u2014 Door closers and pivots\n\u2022 A156.5 / A156.36 \u2014 Cylinders and auxiliary locks/deadbolts\n\u2022 A156.30 \u2014 High-security cylinders (Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, ASSA, etc.)\n\u2022 A156.31 \u2014 Electric strikes\n\nGrade 1 is the commercial default: heavy, high-cycle, and what most spec sheets call for. Grade 2 is lighter-duty commercial or high-end residential; Grade 3 is residential.\n\nUL listings are separate from BHMA grades and cover life-safety performance: UL 305 lists panic hardware (free egress), UL 10C lists fire doors and fire exit hardware (positive-pressure fire test), and UL 437 lists high-security cylinders against picking and drilling. A door can be both \u2014 e.g., a fire-rated exit device is UL 10C listed and A156.3 graded."},{heading:"Finishes and the US / 626 numbering",body:"Finish is part of every order, and it's called two ways: the old US number and the current BHMA number. They map one to one, and you should know the common commercial ones cold:\n\n\u2022 US26D = 626 \u2014 Satin chrome (the commercial default)\n\u2022 US32D = 630 \u2014 Satin stainless steel (exteriors, wet/coastal)\n\u2022 US10B = 613 \u2014 Oil-rubbed / dark oxidized bronze\n\u2022 US4 = 606 \u2014 Satin brass \xb7 US3 = 605 \u2014 Bright (polished) brass\n\u2022 US15 = 619 \u2014 Satin nickel\n\u2022 US28 = 628 \u2014 Clear anodized aluminum (storefront)\n\nWhen you say '99-EO in 626' or 'ND80 storeroom, 626', the supplier knows exactly the part and the look. On exterior and coastal doors, push for 630 satin stainless \u2014 it holds up where 626 chrome will eventually pit."},{heading:"Cylindrical lever locks (A156.2)",body:"The cylindrical (bored) lever lock is the commercial interior workhorse. Its chassis passes through a 2-1/8\" cross bore in the door face, with a 1\" edge bore for the latch \u2014 that round, drilled prep is how you recognize it. Backset is usually 2-3/4\" on commercial doors (2-3/8\" on residential).\n\nWhat you spec is the function and the grade. Common commercial functions: Storeroom (outside lever always locked, key retracts latch, inside always free \u2014 the default), Classroom (outside locked/unlocked by key, no inside locking), Entry/Office (push-button or thumbturn locking), Passage (never locks), and Privacy (bathroom, inside thumbturn with emergency release). Storeroom (e.g. Schlage ND80) is what you'll install most.\n\nGo-to Grade 1 models: Schlage ND-Series and Falcon T-Series; Grade 2: Schlage AL, Falcon B. Levers are now standard over knobs for ADA accessibility. The cylinder is a small key-in-knob (KIK) cylinder that pulls for rekeying, or an interchangeable core if the building is on an IC system."},{heading:"Mortise locks (A156.13)",body:"A mortise lock is a full lock case housed in a rectangular pocket (the mortise) cut into the door edge, behind a tall faceplate. It's the heavy-duty, high-cycle choice \u2014 A156.13 Grade 1 demands a million cycles \u2014 and it's far more versatile than a bored lock: one case can carry a latch, a deadbolt, and lever trim, with dozens of functions available from the same body.\n\nRecognize it by the deep edge pocket and tall faceplate versus the round bores of a cylindrical lock. It's keyed by a threaded mortise cylinder that turns a cam; cam style and length matter when you swap cylinders, so note them.\n\nGo-to commercial models: Schlage L9000, Sargent 8200, Corbin Russwin ML2000, and Yale 8800. All follow the ANSI mortise pocket standard, so prep is consistent across brands even when the trim and cam differ. Spec the function code (e.g. L9080 storeroom), lever design, finish, and hand."},{heading:"Exit devices: what actually breaks, and how you fix it",body:"Free egress is the whole promise \u2014 one push and you're out, no key, no thought \u2014 and the code never bends on it (A156.3 grades it, UL 305 lists it). That's the theory. Here's the job.\n\nRead the type on the walk-up, because it tells you what trouble you're in for. A rim device \u2014 surface latch into a strike on the frame, the Von Duprin 99 you'll meet more than any other \u2014 is the workhorse and almost never fails; when a rim device 'won't lock,' nine times out of ten it's the strike or the dogging, not the device. A mortise exit hides a lock case in the door edge. Surface and concealed vertical rod devices (SVR/CVR) run rods up and down to latch top and bottom \u2014 and I'll be straight with you, vertical rod is where most of your exit-device calls come from. Rods drift, top latches miss, bottom bolts drag the threshold, and a door only has to sag a little to throw the whole thing off. What the outside can do is set by the trim: EO is exit-only (dead outside), NL is a night latch (a key outside pulls the latch), and keyed lever trims lock and unlock the door \u2014 you call that out when you order.\n\nSo you catch the call: 'won't latch,' or 'won't lock at night.' Work it in order, and don't grab a screwdriver yet. Check the dogging first \u2014 half the time somebody dogged the pushpad down with the 1/8\" hex so the door would push-pull during the day, and it's simply still dogged; back it out, no charge, you're the hero. Not dogged? Watch the door close. A closer that isn't pulling it all the way in, a dropped hinge, or a fat weatherstrip means the latch never reaches the strike \u2014 fix the swing before you blame the lock. Still short? Check strike alignment: a rim latch landing high, low, or shallow just needs the strike shimmed or the door brought back to square.\n\nOnly then do you get into the rods on a VR device, and there's a spec, so use it instead of eyeballing. If the latch won't stay retracted, adjust the top rod at the rod control assembly. On the bottom, run the connector until the bolt shows 1/2\" extension, then lock the set screw. Before you button the door channel back up, confirm 1/2\" latch projection up top and that it lines up with the rod control. Hit those numbers and a CVR that's been rattling for months goes quiet.\n\nHere's the one that ends careers if you're careless: fire. A fire exit device wears a red or metal 'FIRE' label and an '-F' in the model, has NO mechanical dogging, and must relatch every single time to hold that rated door shut (UL 10C). When a customer wants their fire stairwell door push-pull during the day, the answer is not to dog it or file the latch \u2014 the only legal way is electric latch retraction wired to drop out on the fire alarm. Defeat a fire latch and you own that liability when the fire marshal walks it. Treat any labeled opening as untouchable until you know better.\n\nOn brand, so you can source parts standing there at 6pm: Von Duprin 98/99 is the heavy benchmark; 33/35 is the lighter, lower-profile cousin. Precision Apex, Falcon 25, Sargent 80, and Yale 7000 play the same game, and since Von Duprin and Falcon are both Allegion, a lot of trim and rod kits cross over. Detex is the back-of-house door with the loud alarm and the 'emergency exit only' sign.\n\nAnd the judgment that separates a pro from a parts-changer: read repair-or-replace on the walk-up. Fifteen years old, chewed up, missing its dogging spring \u2014 that's a replacement; quote the device and the labor, because a Band-Aid just brings you back. A good device that's only out of adjustment or wants a strike is a twenty-minute service call and a customer for life."},{heading:"Detex V40 & the cylinder that actually goes in it",body:"You'll service more Detex V40s than you'd expect. It's Detex's Value Series rim panic bar \u2014 Grade 1, economical \u2014 the device that lands on back doors, stockrooms, and secondary exits where nobody wanted to pay Von Duprin money. It's nonhanded and field-sizeable, so one box covers a range of doors, and it ships with a 99-style surface strike (the same pattern as a Von Duprin 99, handy when you're mixing hardware on a site). And true to Detex's roots, the V40 offers an embedded alarm option \u2014 the battery-powered version arms the bar so a push on a secured door screams; that's the unit you've heard going off on a propped exit.\n\nHere's the part that trips everyone, and it's the question every apprentice asks: what cylinder goes in it? It's a RIM device, so people reach for a rim cylinder \u2014 wrong. The V40's outside pull / night-latch trim (the 03A: key retracts the pull) uses a cylinder bracket with a spring-loaded tailpiece that self-adjusts to door thickness, and it takes a 1-1/8\" MORTISE cylinder with a standard Yale cam. Running a 7-pin interchangeable core? You'll need a collar. Grab a rim cylinder because the device is 'rim' and you're making a second trip.\n\nAdams Rite teaches the same lesson from the other side. The MS1850 deadlock and the 4710 deadlatch on aluminum storefront also take a mortise cylinder \u2014 1-1/8\", about 1\" long \u2014 but with an ADAMS RITE cam. Not a Yale cam, and not the cam off a Schlage or Sargent mortise lock; drop a standard mortise cylinder in without swapping to the AR cam and the bolt won't throw right. Rim cylinders don't belong in these at all.\n\nSo burn this in: the name on the device \u2014 rim, mortise \u2014 tells you how it LATCHES, not which cylinder it eats. The trim and the cam tell you the cylinder. Keep a few 1-1/8\" mortise cylinders with the common cams (Yale for the Detex, Adams Rite for storefront) and an IC collar on the truck, and you finish these on the first visit instead of chasing a part across town."},{heading:"Electrified & access-control options",body:"Most commercial exit devices and locks come in electrified variants for access control. Learn the option suffixes, because they appear in specs constantly: EL (Electric Latch retraction), QEL (Quiet EL \u2014 motor-driven, no loud solenoid), RX (Request-to-Exit switch \u2014 signals the panel when someone pushes the bar), LX (Latchbolt monitor), and SS/CX (signal/latch switches).\n\nElectric latch retraction (EL/QEL) pulls the latch in on a signal so the door becomes momentary push/pull or free on a schedule \u2014 and, on fire doors, it's the only compliant way to hold the latch retracted because it fails secure (relatches) on alarm. It usually needs a heavier, filtered/regulated power supply and often an electric power transfer (EPT) hinge or door loop to get power across the gap.\n\nOn the frame side, electric strikes (A156.31) and electromagnetic locks (maglocks) are the alternatives; maglocks on egress doors trigger strict code requirements (fail-safe, motion sensor plus a push-to-exit button, and fire-alarm release). Know that a maglock is fail-safe (unlocks on power loss) while most electric strikes and EL devices are fail-secure."},{heading:"Maglocks \u2014 the one you can't wing (start from zero)",body:"If you've never touched a maglock, start here, because this is the piece of access control that bites you legally when you get it wrong. Mechanically it's dead simple: a big electromagnet mounts on the frame (usually the top), a steel plate \u2014 the armature \u2014 bolts to the door, and when the magnet is powered it grabs the plate and holds. No latch, no bolt, no moving parts. Typical holding force is 600 lbs on interior doors and 1200 lbs on perimeter doors.\n\nFirst thing that separates a maglock from every mechanical lock you know: it is ALWAYS fail-safe. Cut power and it unlocks, every time \u2014 there is no such thing as a fail-secure maglock. That's not a weakness, it's the whole point. A magnet holding a door shut becomes a trap the instant the power dies or a fire hits, so the code forces it to let go.\n\nAnd that's exactly where techs get in trouble, so tattoo this on your brain: a maglock on a door people exit through is NOT 'run a wire and mount it.' Free egress still rules, and the building code (IBC / NFPA 101) gives you two legal ways to let people out. One \u2014 the sensor-release setup: a motion sensor over the door unlocks it as someone walks up, PLUS a clearly marked push-to-exit button at the door that cuts power to the mag for at least 30 seconds, PLUS it drops on the fire alarm, PLUS it drops on power loss. Two \u2014 the door-hardware-release setup: a lever or panic bar with a built-in request-to-exit switch, so the single motion of grabbing the handle kills the mag, one operation, the way the code wants.\n\nEither way the maglock MUST release on the fire alarm and on any power failure \u2014 that fire-alarm interface is not optional and it is not yours to skip. If a customer or a low-voltage installer wants a maglock with just a card reader and no egress sensor or button, that's a red tag waiting to happen and it's your license on the line. Do it right or walk away.\n\nInstall gotchas that make them fail: the armature has to 'float' \u2014 it mounts on a sex bolt with a rubber washer so it can pivot and seat dead flat to the magnet; bolt it solid and it never makes full contact and the holding force falls to nothing. Set the correct voltage (they're usually 12/24 VDC selectable) off a filtered, regulated supply with battery backup, put an MOV or diode across the coil to kill the inductive spike when it drops out, and on an exterior door use a weatherized model and seal it. Brands you'll meet: Securitron (the M-series), SDC, RCI, Dortronics.\n\nBeginner's bottom line: the magnet part is easy; the egress and fire-release part IS the job, and it's the regulated part. Never leave a maglock that won't let someone out in a single motion and won't drop on the alarm."},{heading:"Detex EAX-500 \u2014 the exit alarm you'll arm, disarm & get called about",body:"The EAX-500 is Detex's battery-powered exit alarm \u2014 the gray box on the 'Emergency Exit Only, Alarm Will Sound' door. It is NOT a lock; it's a roughly 100 dB alarm that sounds when a secured door opens, so a store can keep a fire exit legal (free egress) and still know the instant someone pushes through it. It runs on a single 9-volt battery with a low-battery chirp, mounts on the door or the wall, and it's tamper-resistant.\n\nHow it works on the job: it senses the door with a magnet and reed switch, and newer units auto-detect which side the magnet is on, so handing isn't a fight. You arm it with the control key \u2014 close the door, turn the key counter-clockwise to ON; the red LED blinks twice, the green LED runs the arming delay (default 15 seconds, so staff can get out after locking up), then it chirps and the red LED flashes to say it's live. Open the door while armed and it screams; it can ONLY be silenced with the proper control key, turned clockwise. That control-key-only reset is the whole point \u2014 no employee slaps it quiet.\n\nThe calls you'll get: 'alarm won't stop' (lost key, or a dying battery false-chirping \u2014 a 9-volt and the key fix most of it), 'won't arm' (door not fully closed, magnet out of alignment, or a dead battery), and 'goes off by itself' (magnet gap too wide from a sagging door or a loose mount \u2014 reset the gap). Carry spare 9-volts, know where the control key lives, and most EAX-500 calls are five-minute calls.\n\nOne code note so you sound like you know it: an exit alarm is allowed on an egress door precisely because it does NOT stop egress \u2014 the door still opens freely, it just announces it. That's a different animal from a delayed-egress lock or a maglock, which actually hold the door and carry the heavier egress-code requirements."},{heading:"Aluminum storefront hardware",body:"Glass storefront doors have a narrow aluminum stile, so their hardware is built narrow to fit it. The everyday lock is the Adams Rite deadlatch (4710/4300 family): a spring latchbolt with a small auxiliary deadlocking bolt beside it. When the door closes, the auxiliary bolt rides the strike and holds the latch so it can't be shimmed or 'loided' with a card.\n\nBackset on storefront is small and specific \u2014 31/32\" is the standard, with 1-1/8\" and 1-1/2\" also common \u2014 so always confirm it. For real holding power you step up to the Adams Rite MS (Maximum Security) deadbolt, the MS1850, which by default throws a straight deadbolt; a hookbolt variant swings a hook into the strike, and that hook resists a pair of doors being spread apart. The 4710 deadlatch and MS1850 share the same stile prep, so one swaps for the other.\n\nStorefront locks are operated by a mortise cylinder with an MS-style cam (not a standard cam) and often a thumbturn inside. When you order, give the model, the backset, the finish (usually 628 clear aluminum or 313 dark bronze), the hand, and the cylinder/cam type."},{heading:"Cylinders, cores & keying",body:"The cylinder is the keyed part, and its body must match the lock: rim (flat tailpiece, mounts from behind \u2014 feeds rim exit devices), mortise (threaded body, turns a cam), key-in-knob (KIK, small cylinder in a cylindrical lever), or an interchangeable core. Small Format Interchangeable Core (SFIC, the Best-style figure-8) and Large Format (LFIC) cores pull and swap with a control key for instant rekeying \u2014 ideal for buildings with turnover.\n\nKeying is its own craft. Keyed Alike (KA) means several locks share one key; Keyed Different (KD) each its own; Master Keying builds a hierarchy where a master opens many locks that each also take their own change key, and a Grand Master sits above sub-masters. The keyway (the shaped slot \u2014 Schlage C, Sargent LA, etc.) and the pinning determine what key enters and turns.\n\nFor security, high-security cylinders (A156.30, often UL 437 listed) like Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, ASSA, and Schlage Primus add pick/drill resistance and, importantly, patented restricted keyways so keys can't be copied at a hardware store. Recommend these anywhere key control matters \u2014 schools, clinics, multi-tenant."},{heading:"Egress & life-safety code (NFPA 101 / IBC)",body:"Life-safety code governs every door in a means of egress, and it overrides convenience and even security. The core rule: from the egress (inside) side, a person must be able to open the door with ONE releasing operation, using no key, no tool, no special knowledge or effort, and no tight grasping/pinching/twisting. That's why levers, paddles, and push bars replaced knobs, and why 'free egress' is non-negotiable.\n\nPanic hardware (exit devices) is required on egress doors serving Assembly, Educational, or High-Hazard occupancies above the occupant-load thresholds in the code (commonly 50 or 100 people). The maximum force to release the latch is 15 lbf. Doors can be locked against entry from outside all day \u2014 but never against exit from inside.\n\nSpecial cases you'll meet: fire-rated openings require labeled fire exit hardware that always relatches (no dogging); delayed-egress locks (permitted in some occupancies) hold for 15 seconds then release, with signage and alarm; and electrified/maglock setups must fail safe and release on fire alarm and power loss. When a customer or an access-control salesperson asks you to defeat free egress, the answer is no \u2014 that's the line the code draws, and your license and liability ride on it."}]},w2:{title:"The Locksmith's Craft \u2014 Tools & Technique",sections:[{heading:"How a pin tumbler actually works",body:"Everything in the rekey and picking trade comes back to one mechanism, so know it cold. A pin-tumbler cylinder has a plug (the part that turns) inside a shell (the fixed body). Drilled across both is a row of chambers \u2014 usually five or six. Each chamber holds, from the bottom up: a bottom pin (key pin) that rides on the key, a top pin (driver) above it, and a spring pushing down.\n\nAt rest the springs push the drivers down across the gap between plug and shell, pinning the plug so it can't turn. The correct key lifts each bottom pin exactly enough that the split between the bottom pin and the driver lines up with that gap \u2014 the shear line. When every stack is split at the shear line, nothing crosses it, and the plug is free to rotate.\n\nA wrong key lifts pins too little or too far, so a driver or a bottom pin still bridges the shear line and blocks rotation. Rekeying, picking, impressioning, and master keying are all just different ways of getting every stack split at the shear line."},{heading:"Rekeying: the procedure and the pin kit",body:"Rekeying changes which key turns a lock by swapping the bottom pins to match a new key's cuts. The core kit: a pinning tray of bottom and top pins in graded sizes, springs, a plug follower, fine tweezers, a key gauge, and a plug holder or pinning block.\n\nThe procedure: retract or remove the retainer, insert the plug-follower behind the plug, and push the plug out with the follower so the follower keeps the drivers and springs trapped in the shell (drop them and you're chasing springs across the shop). Insert the NEW key into the plug, read each cut with a key gauge, and drop in the bottom pin whose height brings that cut flush to the top of the plug. When all chambers read flush, slide the plug back in, pushing the follower out ahead of it so the drivers reseat. Test with the new key before reinstalling.\n\nPin kits come as universal (LAB .003 \u2014 pins in .003\" steps that split the difference across brands) or system-specific. Manufacturers pin to fixed depth increments: Schlage uses ten depths at .015\" per step; Kwikset, Sargent, and others use their own. Learn the system for whatever brand you service most, and keep the pinning chart for it in the tray."},{heading:"Depths, spacing, MACS & bitting",body:"A key is defined by its bitting \u2014 the sequence of cut depths, written as numbers (e.g. Schlage 1\u20132\u20133\u20134\u20135, where 0 is shallow and 9 is deep). Each brand has a depth-and-space specification: the depth increment (how much metal per number) and the spacing (distance between cut centers). Cut to those and a key machine originates a working key from the numbers alone.\n\nMACS \u2014 Maximum Adjacent Cut Specification \u2014 limits how much two neighboring cuts can differ. Exceed it and the cutter or the pins can't physically make the transition, so the key binds or the pin chatters. Schlage's MACS is 7; know the MACS for a system before you originate or master-key it.\n\nThis is why blank identification matters: the keyway (the milled profile \u2014 Kwikset KW1, Schlage SC1, Sargent LA, and so on) must match the lock, and the depth/space system tells the machine how to cut it. Grab the wrong keyway family and the finished key won't even enter."},{heading:"Master keying fundamentals",body:"Master keying lets one master key open many locks that each also take their own change key. It's done by adding a master pin (a thin wafer) into a chamber between the bottom pin and the driver. That extra split gives the chamber TWO shear points \u2014 the plug now frees at two different heights, so two different cuts work in that position.\n\nAcross all chambers, the combination of which pins are split where builds a hierarchy: change keys at the bottom, a Master Key (MK) above a group, a Grand Master (GMK) above sub-masters, and so on. The pinning is laid out on a chart so every real key works and no unintended key does.\n\nThe danger is the flip side of those extra shear points: incidental key interchange (a change key from one lock accidentally opening another) and cross-keying. The more masters and wafers, the more phantom keys mathematically exist and the easier the system is to pick. Plan a master system on paper, respect MACS, and don't over-master \u2014 security drops with every wafer you add."},{heading:"Lock picking: tension and binding order",body:"Picking exploits manufacturing tolerance. No plug and shell are perfect, so under a light turning force from a tension wrench the plug rotates a hair and the chambers don't all bind at once \u2014 one pin binds first (the binding order). Set that binding pin to the shear line and the plug rotates another hair, freeing it and letting the next pin bind. Work the binding order and the lock opens.\n\nTension is the whole game: too much and pins bind before they reach the shear line and won't set; too little and set pins drop back. You're feeling for the pin that's stiff (binding) versus the ones that spring back freely, lifting the binder until it clicks and the plug gives a tiny rotation.\n\nSingle Pin Picking (SPP) sets one pin at a time with a hook and is precise enough for good locks. Raking scrubs a profiled pick across all pins under light tension to bounce them toward the shear line at once \u2014 fast on loose, low-quality locks, unreliable on tight ones."},{heading:"Security pins & how to beat them",body:"Better cylinders fight picking with security driver pins: spool, mushroom, and serrated shapes. Instead of a clean set, they create a false set \u2014 under tension the plug rotates slightly and catches on the pin's groove, so the plug feels 'open' but won't turn, and other pins loosen as if you were done.\n\nThe tell is counter-rotation: when you hit a spool's false set, the plug will actually turn backward a touch if you ease tension, and pins that had set will feel springy again. You beat them by lightening tension, finding the trapped security pin, and lifting it through the false set to the true shear line \u2014 patience and feedback, not force.\n\nRecognize which locks carry them (commercial Schlage, Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, ASSA, Abloy) so you set expectations on a lockout: a high-security cylinder may be faster to drill or to key from code than to pick, and that's a professional call, not a failure."},{heading:"Bypass & lockout technique by lock type",body:"On a lockout, match the method to the lock \u2014 bypass beats picking when it's available. Commercial door with an interior panic bar or lever: an under-door tool reaches under and trips the inside hardware for instant, damage-free egress. Interior door with a plain spring latch and no deadlocking bolt: loid it with a shove knife or shim. If it's a deadlatch, the auxiliary bolt defeats loiding \u2014 that's the point of it, so move to the cylinder or the mechanism.\n\nAluminum storefront: purpose-bent tools reach the thumbturn or work the latch through the gap; air wedges and long-reach tools hold a gap open. Worn wafer and simple warded locks often give to jiggler/try-out keys. Cabinet and mailbox wafers frequently pick or shim in seconds.\n\nKnow when NOT to bypass: fire-rated assemblies you shouldn't damage, high-security cylinders where drilling or coding is cleaner, and any door where the customer can't prove authority to enter. Verify authorization before you open anything."},{heading:"Key machines, duplication & originating",body:"Two machines cover cutting. A duplicator clamps an original next to a blank; a tracer follows the original's cuts while a cutter wheel mirrors them onto the blank. It's fast for spares but only as accurate as the original \u2014 copy a worn key and you copy the wear, so 'copy of a copy' keys drift until they stop working.\n\nA code machine cuts to the bitting: feed it the depth-and-space numbers (from a code, a decode, or an impression) and it originates a fresh, in-spec key with no original needed. That's what you use after a total lockout or on new construction.\n\nEither machine lives and dies on calibration: jaw alignment, cutter sharpness, and the tracer/guide zeroed to the cutter. A machine that's off cuts keys that 'almost' work \u2014 the most frustrating kind. Check calibration with a known-good key regularly. Manual machines (HPC, Framon) ride the truck; electronic/automatic ones (some HPC, ITL, laser cutters for automotive) live in the shop."},{heading:"Impressioning: a key from nothing",body:"Impressioning makes a working key at the lock with just a blank and hand tools \u2014 no original, no code, no machine. Fit a snug blank into the keyway, apply firm turning tension, and rock the blank up and down. The pins that are binding press against the soft brass and leave tiny marks exactly where each cut must go.\n\nRead the marks with a loupe, file straight down at each mark with a round or pippin file to deepen that cut, reinsert, and repeat. As a cut reaches the right depth its pin stops binding and stops marking \u2014 so a chamber that no longer marks is done. When every position has stopped marking and the blank turns, you have a working key.\n\nIt's slow and it's a skill, but it's the technique that separates a locksmith from a key-copier: you can open and key a lock that offers you nothing to start with. The kit is minimal \u2014 a fine round/pippin file, a firm blank holder or vise, and a loupe \u2014 and the real tool is patience."},{heading:"Decoding & drilling as a last resort",body:"Decoding reads a lock or key directly and turns it into a bitting you can cut. Some decoders probe the plug and read pin heights; some read an existing key's depths; automotive and safe work lean heavily on decoding tools. Decoding beats destructive entry whenever you can reach the information.\n\nDrilling is the last resort, chosen when picking, bypass, and decoding all cost more than a new cylinder. On a standard pin tumbler you drill at the shear line \u2014 just above the plug \u2014 to sever the pin stacks so nothing crosses the gap, then turn the plug with a pick or key. Use a sharp bit (carbide for hardened parts), a drill guide or jig to hold the exact height and angle, and steady pressure.\n\nHigh-security cylinders are built to resist this: hardened anti-drill pins and plates, and mechanisms (Medeco's rotating pins, disc detainers like Abloy) that a shear-line drill won't defeat. Recognize them and reprice the job \u2014 sometimes the answer is drilling in a different spot, sometimes replacing the whole lock. Always plan the repair before you drill: know the replacement cylinder you'll install so the customer leaves secure."}]}}},762,[]);
