__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"SPECS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});const o={w1l1:{spec:["State handing as hand + bevel, read from OUTSIDE: 'LHR' = left-hand reverse (hinges on the left, door swings out toward you).","Give a supplier: door material + thickness (usually 1-3/4\"), frame type, and the hand."],explain:"I check your door's handing from outside first so the lock, the closer, and any exit device all match the way it swings. Order the wrong hand and nothing lines up."},w1l2:{spec:["Schlage ND-Series, Grade 1. Say: function + lever + finish. e.g. 'ND80PD storeroom, Rhodes lever, 626 satin chrome'.","Budget Grade 1 alternative: Falcon T-Series."],explain:"This is a commercial-grade lever, built for constant daily use. Storeroom function keeps the outside locked and opens with a key, but anyone inside can always walk straight out \u2014 that part is code."},w1l3:{spec:["Schlage L9000 or Sargent 8200. Say: function + lever design + finish + hand. e.g. 'L9080P storeroom, 06A lever, 626, RH'."],explain:"A mortise lock is the heavy-duty option. The whole mechanism sits inside the door edge, so it takes more abuse and can carry a latch and a deadbolt in one case \u2014 that's why it costs more."},w1l4:{spec:["Von Duprin 98/99. Say: series + function + door width + finish. e.g. '99-EO, 3 ft, US28 aluminum' or '99-NL-OP, 4 ft, 626'.","Always call the chassis type: rim, mortise, surface vertical rod (SVR), or concealed vertical rod (CVR)."],explain:"This is your panic bar \u2014 code says people have to get out in a single push, no key, no thinking. 'Exit only' has no outside key; a night-latch adds a key so your staff can get back in."},w1l5:{spec:["Adams Rite 4710 deadlatch. Say: backset (31/32\" is the storefront standard), finish, and hand.","Stepping up to a deadbolt: Adams Rite MS1850 \u2014 it drops into the same stile prep."],explain:"This is the standard lock for aluminum glass doors. That little second bolt makes it a deadlatch \u2014 it stops someone from carding the latch open, so it's worth having on any retail door."},w1l6:{spec:["Say type + keyway + finish: 'rim cylinder, Schlage C keyway, 626' or 'mortise cylinder, 1-1/8\", standard cam'.","For interchangeable cores: 'SFIC, 7-pin, uncombinated' (Best A-series)."],explain:"The cylinder is the part your key turns, and I match its type to your lock. Going with interchangeable cores now means I can rekey you in seconds later without ever pulling the lock apart."},w2l1:{spec:["Buy/ask for: a 'LAB .003 universal pinning kit', a set of steel plug followers, fine tweezers, and a key gauge.","That covers almost any residential/commercial rekey out of one tray."]},w2l2:{spec:["Buy: a quality pick set with several hooks + rakes and both top-of-keyway and bottom-of-keyway tension wrenches (Peterson, Sparrows, or SouthOrd)."]},w2l3:{spec:["Lockout kit to ask for: an under-door tool, a set of try-out / jiggler keys, a shove knife, and an air wedge with a long-reach tool."]},w2l4:{spec:["Buy: a duplicator (Ilco or HPC) for copies and a code machine (Framon or HPC) to originate keys, plus a stock of common blanks (Ilco / Silca)."]},w2l5:{spec:["Buy: a lock installation jig kit (with hole saws), a self-centering 'Vix' bit, wood chisels, and a set of files.","Best practice: on hollow metal doors, fasten with through-bolts (sex bolts / Chicago bolts), never self-tappers \u2014 self-tappers strip out of thin steel under a lever's pull or a closer's slam."]},w2l6:{spec:["Buy: a decoder for the systems you service, plus round/pippin files and a loupe for impressioning."]}}},769,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,r,n,i){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(n,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(n,"MODELS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:"von-duprin-98-99",name:"Von Duprin 98/99 Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The 98/99 is Von Duprin's heavy-duty, full-size push-pad panic bar \u2014 the Grade 1 device every other panic gets measured against. You see it on schools, hospitals, government buildings, and any commercial opening that takes real abuse. Mechanically the 98 and 99 are identical; the only difference is cosmetic \u2014 the 98 has a smooth mechanism case, the 99 has a grooved one. If you want a true narrow-stile bar for an aluminum storefront door, that's Von Duprin's 33A/35A family, not this one. Learn to service the 98/99 and you've got the whole family \u2014 rim, mortise, surface vertical rod, and concealed vertical rod all share the same center case and pushpad guts.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.3 (2014) Grade 1 certified \u2014 highest grade for exit devices","Listed to ANSI/UL 305 (panic hardware); fire-rated models (-F) listed to ANSI/UL 10C","Device types by number: rim (no suffix) / 27 = surface vertical rod (SVR) / 47 = concealed vertical rod (CVR), 5/16\" throw / 48 = CVR with 5/8\" top and 1-1/2\" bottom throw / 75 = mortise lock device (also 47WDC/50WDC wood-door, 49 concealed cable, 57 three-point)","Latchbolt: deadlocking, with the 299 roller strike standard on rim and mortise devices","Vertical rod: 47 CVR is 5/16\" throw; 48 CVR is 5/8\" top / 1-1/2\" bottom throw. Less-bottom-rod (LBR) and less-bottom-latch (LBL) options available on vertical-rod units","Dogging: 1/4-turn hex key dogging standard (panic only). Cylinder dogging = -CD; less dogging = -LD; dogging-indicator kit available","Fire devices (-F): NO mechanical dogging \u2014 constant positive latching required by code; hex/CD dogging is deleted on fire units","Pushbar mounting centerline 39-13/16\" (1011 mm) from finished floor; 39-11/16\" (1008 mm) with a mullion","Push pad force: 15 lbs standard, 5 lbs on AX (accessible) devices; pad projection 3-13/16\" neutral / 3-1/16\" depressed","Mounting screws #10-24 x 3/4\"; through-bolt/sex-nut hardware for hollow-metal and thicker doors, sized in the install screw chart","Cylinders NOT furnished standard \u2014 specify separately. NL/night-latch and TP thumbpiece functions take a rim cylinder; keyed L lever / K knob / TP thumbpiece and TL turn-lever trims take a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder","Standard strikes: 299 (299F fire) single-door rim; 499F fire strike; cylinder-prep strikes 1439, 1606, 1609, 264; mullion strikes 4954/KR4954, 1654/KR1654, 5654, 5754, 9954","Common trims: 990 (wide escutcheon, 3\" x 14-3/16\"), 996 lever/knob (2-3/4\" x 10-3/4\"), 696/697TP thumbpiece, 374/392-7 pulls; VR910/VR914 vertical-rod trim","Finishes: 626 satin chrome, 628 clear-anodized aluminum, 630 satin stainless, plus 605/606 brass, 612/643e bronze, 619 satin nickel, 622 matte black, 625 bright chrome, 693/710; 626AM and 630AM antimicrobial","Electrified options run on 24VDC: QEL quiet electric latch retraction (1.0 A inrush / 0.14 A hold), E/M electric trim, LX/RX/RX2 monitor switches, CX Chexit delayed egress","Field sighting (unconfirmed at capture): Top and bottom rod with frame strike plates, latch visible at frame edge. Hollow metal frame. \u2014 to confirm: series/model not readable \u2014 need faceplate photo"],proAdvice:"When you call the supplier, give them the pieces in the order they think in or you'll get the wrong box: device number, function, hand, finish, and fire-or-panic. The device number is the mounting style \u2014 27 is surface vertical rod, 47 and 48 are concealed vertical rod, 75 is mortise, and a plain rim device has no rod number at all. Function tells them the trim and how you'll get back in: EO (exit only), NL (night latch, keyed pull), L (lever), K (knob), TP (thumbpiece), DT (dummy). Then the cylinder rules, and this trips up more guys than anything else \u2014 anything that retracts the latch from the outside with a key takes a rim cylinder, so your NL and your TP pulls run a rim cyl with the right tailpiece. A keyed lever, knob, thumbpiece, or a mortise-cylinder dogging device takes a standard 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder with an inverted straight cam. The cylinder is almost always sold separate, so if you don't say it, it won't be in the box and you're making a second trip.\n\nFire versus panic is the line you never blur. A panic device is dogged open with the hex key so the pad stays depressed and the door pushes free \u2014 a fire-rated device (the -F) has no mechanical dogging, period, because a fire door has to positively latch every time it closes. If a customer with a fire label wants the door to stay open during business hours, the only device number you order is fire, and the only legal hold-open is one tied to the fire alarm \u2014 a mag holder or an electric latch retraction unit that drops on alarm. Don't ever pin the latch, wedge it, file the latch, or drill the dogging back into a fire device. That's not a callback, that's the thing that gets somebody killed and your name on the report.\n\nOn the install, the centerline of the crossbar sits right around 39-13/16\" off the finished floor \u2014 mark that first and it fixes your strike and rod geometry for free. The single biggest callback preventer is how you fasten to hollow metal. Those #10-24 machine screws are for a reinforced door with tapped holes; on a hollow metal door with no backing you through-bolt it or you run sex nuts, because screwed into thin skin the whole device works loose in a month and now the pad rattles and the latch mistimes. Adjust in order too \u2014 get the device latching solid to the strike first, then chase the rods. On vertical rod units you set the top latch to the strike, then the bottom, then re-check the top because moving the bottom pulls the whole rod system. Do it out of order and you'll be up and down the ladder all afternoon.\n\nUnderstand why rim is bulletproof and vertical rod is the trouble child, because it drives every repair-versus-replace call you make. A rim device throws one latch into one strike \u2014 the 299 strike ships standard with it \u2014 and there's almost nothing to fall out of adjustment. Vertical rod, surface or concealed, has two latches, two rods, and a top and bottom strike all fighting temperature and a door that sags over time, so it goes out of latch, drags, or won't relatch. When you walk up to a dead device, read it fast: if the center case feels mushy, the pad won't return, or it won't dog, that's a center-case job. If it latches at the case but won't grab top or bottom, that's rods and strikes, not the case. Nine times out of ten a rim or a healthy-cased VR unit is a rod kit or a center-case kit, not a new device \u2014 a new full-size device is a couple hundred in parts before labor, and the center-case kit or a rod set is a fraction of that and half the ladder time.\n\nSourcing is easy once you know the family shares parts. The 98 and 99 are the same device \u2014 98 is the smooth flat case, 99 is the grooved case, cosmetic only \u2014 and rim, mortise, SVR, and CVR all share the same center case, so one center-case kit covers a lot of the fleet. Von Duprin and Falcon are both Allegion, so a lot of parts and logic cross-reference; when the Von Duprin part is backordered, check the Falcon equivalent. Keep the fast movers where you can get them same-day from any real commercial supplier \u2014 the 299 strike, the common rod kits, and the center-case rebuild kits \u2014 and you turn most of these into a one-trip fix. If it's narrow-stile aluminum storefront, that's not this device at all; that's the 33A/35A, don't try to hang a full-size 98/99 on a 2\" stile.\n\nThe money read: don't reflex to a full replacement on a walk-up. Most dead 98/99s are a rebuild, and the customer respects the guy who fixes it right for less. Where you do lean in is the fire door somebody's been propping with a trash can \u2014 that's your QEL or electric latch retraction upsell. Sell them the code-legal version: the device stays open on power, drops and latches on alarm, and now their held-open door is legal instead of a violation waiting for the fire marshal. That's the conversation that pays and keeps them off the liability hook at the same time.",commonServicingIssues:["Door won't latch at the top on a vertical-rod (27/47/48) unit \u2014 usually rod length/adjustment or a worn top strike, not a bad device; re-thread and adjust the rods to spec and reset the top strike before condemning anything","Pushpad depresses but latch won't fully retract \u2014 gummed or fatigued center-case latch/spring; clean and re-lube or drop in a new center-case kit rather than replacing the whole bar","Key won't unlock the lever/knob/thumbpiece trim (or key retracts latch when it shouldn't) \u2014 the NL drive screw on the back of the center case is in the wrong state; REMOVE it for 996L/996K/696TP/697TP/990TP, LEAVE it in for NL/EO/DT and -2 double-cylinder devices, and check the trim lock tumbler is UP","Fire door (-F) will not stay dogged open / customer wants it held open \u2014 by design there's no dogging on a fire unit; correct fix is a fire-alarm-tied magnetic hold-open or a QEL device, never dogging a labeled opening","Bar dogs down but pops back / won't hold retracted \u2014 worn hex dogging mechanism or wrong dogging key turn; service the dogging assembly or convert to cylinder dogging with the -CD conversion kit","Latch retracts but door drags and won't relatch reliably \u2014 deadlocking latch not seating on the 299 roller strike; check strike alignment and clearance and shim the strike before touching the device","Won't accept the cylinder or trim turns but nothing happens \u2014 wrong cylinder type: rim cylinder is for NL and TP functions, keyed lever/knob/thumbpiece and turn-lever need a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder; verify the cam/cylinder matches the function","Loose or rattling device after abuse \u2014 #10-24 mounting screws stripped in a hollow-metal door; move to sex-nut through-bolts (the install screw chart calls out the right lengths) instead of re-tapping"],schematic:"vd-9899-exploded",sources:["https://www.vonduprin.com/en/products/exit-devices/98-99-series.html","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/Von_Duprin_98_99_Series_Cut_Sheet_113139.pdf","https://www.andersonlock.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/Von_Duprin_98.99_Series_Catalog_106590.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/installation-documents/Von_Duprin_98_99_Series_Rim_Exit_Device_Installation_Instructions_107600.pdf","https://www.americanlocksets.com/von-duprin-99-series-exit-device"],photo:"photo-von-duprin-98-99",verified:!0},{id:"von-duprin-33-35",name:"Von Duprin 33A/35A Series Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The 33A/35A is Von Duprin's narrow-stile, low-profile touchpad (push pad) panic series \u2014 the slim cousin of the wide-stile 98/99. You see it all over aluminum storefront and narrow-stile glass entrances: schools, hospitals, hospitality, offices, government lobbies. The rim version lands on a door stile as narrow as 1-3/4\" (44mm), which is exactly where the fatter 98/99 mechanism case won't fit. The 35A has a smooth mechanism case; the 33A has a grooved case \u2014 that's the only difference between them, purely cosmetic. Same UL panic/fire listings, same ANSI Grade 1 guts, same deadlocking latch and 24VDC electric options as the 98/99, just packaged for skinny stiles.",specs:["Stile fit (rim): mounts on stiles as narrow as 1-3/4\" (44mm) for the 33A/35A rim device and the 3327A/3527A surface vertical rod. Watch the fire versions: rim fire (33A-F/35A-F) and SVR fire (3327A-F/3527A-F) go to 2-3/16\" (56mm) min; concealed vertical rod fire (3347A-F/3547A-F) needs 3-1/2\" (90mm) min stile","Grade / listing: ANSI/BHMA A156.3 Grade 1; UL listed Panic Exit Hardware, and UL/cUL Fire Exit Hardware on the -F suffix versions. Verify the exact catalog number in the BHMA certified directory","Device types and full catalog numbers: rim 33A/35A; surface vertical rod 3327A/3527A (the '27A', also available LBR); concealed vertical rod 3347A/3547A (the '47A') and 3348A/3548A (the '48A'); concealed vertical cable 3349A/3549A (the '49A'); and 3350AWDC/3550AWDC concealed-cable wood-door device. No mortise-lock version exists in this series","Rim latchbolt: deadlocking Pullman-type, 3/4\" (19mm) throw. SVR (3327A) rim latch is also 3/4\" (19mm)","Concealed vertical rod throws: 3347A (47A) = 5/8\" (16mm) top and bottom; 3348A (48A) = deadlocking anti-friction 3/4\" (19mm) top, 1-1/2\" (38mm) bottom throw. (A cut sheet lists the 48A top as 5/8\"; the catalog device spec page says 3/4\" \u2014 go with the catalog when ordering strikes)","Device lengths: 3' size covers a 2'4\"-3' door (711-914mm); 4' size covers a 2'10\"-4' door (864-1219mm). Field-cut the rail to the opening","Push pad projection (rim/SVR): pad neutral 3-13/16\" (97mm), pad depressed 3-1/16\" (78mm) \u2014 genuinely low-profile","Operating force: the AX (accessible) option is UL certified to the 5 lb max operating force in CBC 11B-309.4 and exceeds ANSI/BHMA. Standard (non-AX) devices are not published at a specific poundage; the governing ceiling is the 15 lbf code cap to release the latch (NFPA 101 / IBC), not a Von Duprin spec number","Mounting height: device centerline 39-13/16\" (1011mm) from finished floor; 39-11/16\" (1008mm) with mullion (rim/SVR)","Mechanism (end) case: 2-1/4\" x 2-1/4\" (57mm x 57mm). Center case: 8-3/16\" x 1-9/16\" x 2-13/32\" (208mm x 40mm x 62mm)","Dogging: hex-key dogging standard (panic only). Options: CD cylinder dogging, CDSI cylinder dog w/ security indicator, HDSI hex dog w/ indicator, LD less-dogging, CDK field-conversion kit. Fire (-F) devices are never field-dogged","Cylinders: NL night latch uses a rim cylinder; L (lever) / TP (thumbpiece) / TL trims use a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder. CD cylinder dogging uses a 1-1/4\" (32mm) mortise cylinder with an inverted straight cam \u2014 factory reference is Schlage 20-001, XQ11-949, giving the L583-477 cam inverted","QEL quiet electric latch retraction: 24VDC, 1.0A inrush (0.5 sec) / 0.14A holding, UL Class II. HD-QEL adds hex dogging; QEL-L is latchbolt-only. Does not include the power transfer, power supply, or control","Switch/EM options 24VDC: LX latch-bolt monitor, RX request-to-exit (WP-RX waterproof), CX Chexit delayed egress, ALK alarm kit (horn rated 85 dB), SS signal switch, ESL emergency secure lockdown (requires a QEL device), E360 electric lever trim (24VDC, 0.50A; specify FS fail-safe or FSE fail-secure)","Finishes: 605, 606, 612, 619 (35A only), 625, 626, 626AM (antimicrobial), 628, 710, 711 per the device spec page; the finish gallery also shows 622, 630, 630AM, 643e and 693","Fasteners: screw pack ships for 1-3/4\" (44mm) and 2-1/4\" (57mm) wood and metal doors; #425 SNB sex bolts standard for the end case (#325 SNB for exit-only). SNB available","Warranty: 36 months from date placed in service, or 42 months from date of shipment, whichever comes first"],proAdvice:"Rule of thumb picking between the two families: measure the stile first, before you talk about anything else. Aluminum storefront or a narrow glass door with a stile in that 1-3/4\" to roughly 3\" range \u2014 you're in 33A/35A territory, because the 98/99 mechanism case is simply too wide to land its screws on that skinny stile. Hollow-metal or wood door with a fat stile (5\"+) \u2014 spec the 98/99 for the beefier case, more trim real estate, and the fact that it's the more common repair part on the shelf. Nine times out of ten a mis-spec is somebody ordering a 99 for a storefront and finding the mounting holes want to fall off the edge of the glass pocket. The 33A/35A exists to solve exactly that. And know your model numbering: the narrow-stile devices carry a '3' prefix on the vertical types \u2014 surface rod is 3327A, concealed rod is 3347A (5/8\" throw) and 3348A (3/4\" top, 1-1/2\" bottom), concealed cable is 3349A. If you order a bare '47A' the counter guy knows what you mean, but the box says 3347A.\n\nUnderstand what you actually get for the narrow package. Panic performance, the Grade 1 rating, the UL fire listing, the 3/4\" deadlocking rim latch, the 24VDC electric options \u2014 all the same as the 98/99. You're not giving up strength to go slim. What you give up is a mortise-lock version \u2014 there isn't one in this series, it's rim, surface rod, concealed rod, and concealed cable only \u2014 plus some of the heavier trim options. If a job calls for a mortise exit on a narrow stile, that's a different animal, spec accordingly. For 33A vs 35A itself: cosmetic only \u2014 35A smooth case, 33A grooved. Match whatever's already on the building and move on. Don't let a customer agonize over it.\n\nOn dogging and fire, drill this in and never slip: hex dogging is standard and it's panic-only. The second a device carries the -F fire suffix, dogging is gone \u2014 a fire exit device has to relatch and stay latched every cycle to hold the door's rating. If you find a fire-rated 33A that's been hex-dogged so the janitor can prop it, that's a liability you fix on the spot. Same with less-bottom-rod on vertical-rod fire devices \u2014 you must use the fire-pin version (LBR-AFL, or LBL-AFL on the less-bottom-latch) so the top bolt still does its job; a plain LBR on a fire door kills the label. And egress always wins: the pad dumps the latch in one motion, no key, no thumbturn, no second operation on the egress side. If a trim or add-on ever needs two actions to get out, it's wrong. One counter note on CD cylinder dogging \u2014 it wants a 1-1/4\" (32mm) mortise cylinder with an inverted straight cam (Schlage 20-001 / XQ11-949, the L583-477 cam flipped), so don't hand it a standard cam and wonder why it won't dog.\n\nSpec'ing the electrics: if the customer wants access control or remote unlock, steer them to QEL over the old solenoid EL retrofits. QEL is a motor, not a slam \u2014 quiet, 24VDC, and it's what people expect in an occupied building. The reason to care about the numbers is power-supply sizing: QEL pulls a 1.0A inrush for about half a second, then drops to 0.14A holding, and it's low enough inrush to run off a standard Schlage PS902/914 (the old solenoid EL by contrast is a 16A inrush monster \u2014 that's what QEL saved you from). Budget the supply and the wire gauge for inrush across every device on the home run, not just holding, and don't undersize a long run. If they just need to know the door was used, that's RX (request-to-exit); if they need to know the latch is actually secured, that's LX (latch-bolt monitor) \u2014 people confuse those two constantly. If they want electric outside-lever unlock, that's E360 trim, 0.50A at 24VDC \u2014 and you tell them fail-safe (FS) or fail-secure (FSE) up front, because that's a code and egress decision, not a preference. For genuine controlled/delayed egress you're into CX Chexit: a self-contained unit that alarms and holds the door 15 seconds after an exit attempt with immediate release on fire, built to NFPA 101 Special Locking Arrangement and IBC Special Egress-Control. It has its own compliance story \u2014 fire-alarm tie-in, the 'PUSH UNTIL ALARM SOUNDS' signage, and the AHJ's blessing \u2014 so don't slap it on blind.\n\nRepair vs replace pricing judgment: these devices last decades, and the whole point of the series is that it's field-configurable and upgradeable \u2014 you can very often rebuild instead of replace. A worn 33A rim device usually just needs a latch/Pullman rebuild or a new touchpad assembly, not a whole new unit. If the chassis and rails are straight and the case isn't cracked, rebuild it \u2014 you save the customer a couple hundred bucks and the door's down 30 minutes instead of half a day. Replace the whole device when the case is cracked, the door's been abused past the point the rails are bent, or you're converting mechanical to electric and it's cheaper to drop in a factory QEL device than to retrofit a kit. When you order, get the handing right on lever trims (the L lever is handed but field-reversible; confirm anyway), verify door width so you land the right 3' vs 4' rail, and put the stile measurement in writing so nobody's surprised at install.",commonServicingIssues:["Door won't latch / bounces open on a rim 33A: nine times out of ten the strike is out of alignment or the door's sagging, not the device \u2014 check the rim strike position and hinge wear first. Fix the strike or shim the hinges before you touch the latch","Push pad feels dead or won't dog: on hex-dog models the hex socket or dogging pawl is worn or the hex key was forced \u2014 inspect the dogging screw, replace the dogging kit. Remember fire (-F) devices have NO dogging by design, so 'it won't dog' on a fire unit is not a fault","Concealed vertical rod device (3347A/3348A) top bolt not catching: rods out of adjustment after door settling or a service call \u2014 re-time the rods and set top-bolt engagement, verify the 5/8\" (3347A) or 3/4\" (3348A top) throw against the strike. On fire LBR jobs confirm the fire-pin AFL version is present, not a plain less-bottom-rod","QEL motor buzzes / won't retract or trips the supply: undersized or dirty 24VDC, or the supply can't cover the 1.0A inrush across all devices \u2014 check voltage at the device under load, confirm the supply is rated for peak inrush not just the 0.14A hold, look for a stuck motor gearbox","Loud slam / no damping on the pad: the Quiet One fluid damper (the reason the 33A case is grooved) is spent, or the QM quiet-mechanical relatching is worn \u2014 replace the damper/center-case assembly to restore controlled relatching","Latch retracts but door still won't open from outside: night-latch or lever trim fault \u2014 the rim NL cylinder or the 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder in the L/TL/TP trim is worn, or the trim linkage is disconnected. Rebuild or re-link the trim, don't blame the chassis","Fire door tagged by inspector for being dogged: someone hex-dogged a -F device or propped it \u2014 undog it, and if it's habitually propped that's a use problem to raise with the building, not a hardware fix","E360 electric lever won't release or fails the wrong way on power loss: verify it was ordered FS (fail-safe) vs FSE (fail-secure) to match the opening's egress/code requirement, and confirm it's on 24VDC at 0.50A \u2014 a starved supply or a fail-mode mismatch, not a dead motor, is usually the real issue","Pad projection reads wrong / rail hits the frame: device was field-cut for the wrong size \u2014 verify you're on the right 3' vs 4' unit for the 2'4\"-3' or 2'10\"-4' opening; a mis-sized rail throws the whole geometry off"],sources:["https://www.vonduprin.com/en/products/exit-devices/33a-35a-series.html","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/Von_Duprin_33A.35A_Series_Catalog_106593.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/VON_33A_35A_Series_Cut_Sheet_113234.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/Von_Duprin_Guide_to_Selecting_a_Power_Supply_110491.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/installation-documents/Von_Duprin_33_35A_Series_Rim_Exit_Device_Installation_Instructions_107607.pdf"],photo:"photo-von-duprin-33-35",schematic:"schematic-von-duprin-33-35"},{id:"precision-apex",name:"Precision Apex 2000 Series Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The Apex 2000 is Precision Hardware Inc's (PHI) touchbar-style Grade 1 exit device line, now sold under the dormakaba/BEST umbrella - you'll still see Stanley Security Solutions and the blue \"phi\" logo on older catalogs, covers, and boxes. It's the family that goes head-to-head with Von Duprin's 98/99: rim, surface vertical rod, mortise, and concealed vertical rod, in wide and narrow stile, fire and non-fire. You mostly run into it on schools, hospitals, and mid-grade commercial where the spec writer wanted a Grade 1 device but not Von Duprin money. Its whole calling card is the universal active-case and end-cap mounting-hole pattern - it's built to drop onto the holes another maker's device already left in the door, which is why it shows up so often as a retrofit.",specs:["Certification: UL Listed panic and fire exit hardware, conforms to UL10C and UBC 7-2; devices BHMA certified to ANSI/BHMA A156.3 Grade 1 (trims Grade 1 as well)","Cycle life: PHI publishes 10 million operational cycles on the Apex chassis - far past the 500,000-cycle Grade 1 minimum","Chassis: investment cast steel, zinc dichromated; covers/touchbar/device channel/lock-hinge end cap in heavy wrought brass, bronze, or stainless steel (628 devices use aluminum-brass-bronze-stainless components)","Rim latchbolt: stainless steel, deadlocking, 3/4 in throw; surface-vertical-rod top latchbolt also stainless, deadlocking, 3/4 in throw","SVR bottom bolt: steel plated, independent action, 5/8 in throw, with adjustment range to suit up to 3/4 in door undercut","Mortise version (2300): 2-3/4 in backset, 8 in x 1-1/4 in lock front, deadlocking anti-friction stainless latchbolt (3/4 in throw) plus sliding stainless guardbolt; uses M303 (01/02/03), M308 (05/08/14/15), M310 (10) mortise locks by function; S982 handed curved-lip strike standard","Touchbar height: 39-15/16 in from floor standard; 1/4 in touchbar clearance built in to clear glass moldings that project up to 1/4 in past the door face","Door sizes: wide-stile rim/SVR/mortise for openings up to 4 ft 0 in wide by up to 10 ft 0 in high; furnished standard for 1 ft 3/4 in thick, 3 ft 0 in wide, 7 ft 0 in high","Minimum stile width: wide-stile single rim (S300/S301 strike) 4-1/4 in with 2-3/4 in backset; narrow single rim (S988 strike) down to 1-3/4 in with 1-1/16 in backset","Dogging: 1/4 in turn hex-key dogging standard on non-fire devices (DK220 key, 7/32 in hex); cylinder dogging (CD, via CDK/NCDK kit + 1-1/4 in mortise cylinder) and less-dogging (LD) optional; NO mechanical dogging on fire (FL) devices","Series map: 2100 rim (reversible) / 2200 surface vertical rod (reversible) / 2300 mortise (handed) / 2400 narrow-stile rim / 2600 narrow CVR (reversible) / 2700 wood-door CVR (reversible) / 2800 CVR; 21/22 Series = nonhanded/reversible access-control-ready variants; FL-prefix = fire-rated version","ANSI function suffixes: 01 exit-only (cover plate), 02 dummy trim, 03 key retracts latch, 05 key locks/unlocks thumbpiece, 08 key locks/unlocks lever/knob, 10 double-cylinder inside key/thumbturn (handed), 14 no-cylinder lever/knob always active, 15 no-cylinder thumbpiece always active","Fire ratings: FL single doors carry up to 3-hour UL fire door rating (also 1-1/2 hour); FL822 and FLKR822 mullions UL Listed for fire exit hardware; fire devices include the FB277 fire bolt assembly","Finishes: 605, 606, 612, 613, 622, 625, 626W, 628, 630, 630AM, 690 - 630AM is Satin Stainless Steel Antimicrobial, 626W is Satin Chrome Weatherized; Torx pin tamper-resistant Security Screws (SEC) use a T20 driver on covers, T25 on end caps","Rim cylinder: 1E72 rim cylinder or 1E74-C4 mortise cylinder (1-1/4 in) by function - BEST 7-pin standard, cylinders/cores not furnished with device or trim","ELR electric latch retraction: continuous-duty solenoid, 4.75 A current pulse (2 sec max) / 3.6 VDC at 0.8 A continuous; requires ELR150 Series power supply (115/230 V user-selectable, 115 V at 1 A AC input) with a CM150-08 control module per leaf; UL 294 listed"],proAdvice:"Start with the branding, because it trips people up on the phone. Precision Hardware Inc (PHI) is the maker; over the years the box has read PHI, then Stanley Security Solutions, and now dormakaba/BEST. It's all the same Apex 2000 hardware - a 2101 rim device from 2006 and a BEST-branded 2101 today share the same chassis and most parts. When you're ordering parts, search on the PHI/Apex 2000 numbers first; that's where the illustrated parts catalog lives.\n\nThe reason this device exists in the wild is retrofit. PHI cast the active-case and end-cap holes to a universal pattern so the Apex drops onto a door already drilled for somebody else's device - most commonly a Von Duprin 99. That is the number-one reason you'll pull one out of your truck. But do not oversell \"no drilling.\" The touchbar cases line up on the common holes, but the strike location and any trim cutout won't always be a perfect match, so bring a strike and be ready to elongate a hole. For competitor trim cutouts, PHI factory-sets the retrofit trim: the 1700-series pull trim and 4900-series lever trim are ordered with an \"R\" prefix (e.g. 2108 R4908A) so the fingers land on the cutouts another maker left. Spec that R prefix up front and consult the factory - a standard 4900 covers the Apex's own prep, but the R version is cut to sit right over a competitor's existing prep. And note the 21/22 Series (nonhanded/reversible) is the one built to accept a center-driven cam that takes an access-control tailpiece - PHI publishes a minimum of 50 degrees of tailpiece rotation to retract the latch, so if you're mating this to electric lever trim or an access-control cylinder, that rotation spec matters, call the factory.\n\nCross-compat with Von Duprin, honestly: think chassis-and-hole compatible, not parts-interchangeable. A 99 latch, a 99 strike, a 99 rail - none of that swaps into an Apex. What swaps is the footprint on the door. So when a customer says \"just make my Von Duprin work,\" the right move nine times out of ten is a whole new Apex rim device onto the existing holes with a matching strike, not a Frankenstein of mixed parts. If they insist on staying all-Von-Duprin, that's a 99 conversation, not an Apex one.\n\nOn spec'ing new: match stile to device. Wide-stile aluminum or hollow-metal takes the 2100/2200/2300. Narrow-stile storefront takes the 2400 narrow rim or the 2600 narrow CVR - and the narrow rim uses the S988 strike on aluminum single doors, S458 on mullion applications, with S300/S301 on the wider medium and standard stiles. Get the strike right or the latch won't deadlock. Fire doors: order the FL-prefix, and remember you lose hex-key dogging entirely - fire devices are never dogged, that's code, not preference. If a customer wants a fire pair to stay latched but \"stay open during the day,\" the answer is electric latch retraction (ELR) tied to the fire alarm, not dogging.\n\nELR is where the money and the callbacks live. The Apex ELR uses a continuous-duty solenoid and it will not run off a doorbell transformer - it needs the ELR150 Series power supply with a CM150-08 control module per device (one module drives one leaf). Size the supply by leaf count: ELR151 for one, ELR152 for two, up to ELR154 for four; ELR150 is the bare supply with no modules. The solenoid pulls a 4.75 A current pulse for up to 2 seconds then holds at 3.6 VDC / 0.8 A continuous, so plan your power transfer accordingly - PHI doesn't publish a wire-gage/distance table in the catalog, so size the run to the load and confirm with the factory rather than guessing. Add the BT150-07 battery backup card (order the supply with the BT suffix, e.g. ELR151BT) if the AHJ wants latch retraction to survive a power loss - note that card also includes a 24 VDC 0.8 A power tap for readers or a mag lock. One correction worth burning into memory: ELR CAN be combined with hex-key or cylinder dogging on this line - PHI states it plainly - it is NOT available on delayed-egress (DE) devices. Don't repeat the shop rumor that ELR and dogging can't coexist; that's backwards.\n\nRepair vs replace: the Apex is a \"few moving parts, single push-bar spring\" design, which is a real advantage - there's no maintenance kit, and a sloppy touchbar is usually just a worn spring or a bent bar, cheap to swap. If the latch won't deadlock, check the strike alignment and the deadlatch plunger before you condemn the device. Where I replace rather than repair: rusted-out SVR bottom rods and bolts on exterior doors, and any fire device with a cracked or missing cover on a life-safety opening - you don't shim a fire exit device, you make it right. Pricing-wise the Apex sits noticeably under a comparable Von Duprin 99, which is exactly why it wins on bid jobs; a rim 2101 runs a few hundred, an ELR mortise or CVR with the power supply climbs into four figures once you add the ELR150 and modules.\n\nLiability instincts: this is life-safety egress hardware. Single motion to exit, always - never add a thumbturn, deadbolt, or secondary latch that defeats the single-operation release, and never dog a fire device. On stairwell reentry and ADA jobs, PHI offers the Braille touchbar (BRL suffix) - Grade 2 Braille, ADA compliant, 5/8 in letters - spec it where the AHJ or ADA calls for the tactile legend rather than fielding a stick-on sign.",commonServicingIssues:["Latch won't deadlock / door rattles - strike misaligned or deadlatch plunger not being depressed by the strike; realign the correct strike (S300/S301 wide rim, S988 narrow aluminum, S458 mullion, S982 on the mortise) so the plunger stays in, don't file the latch","Touchbar feels dead or won't spring back - worn or unseated single push-bar spring, or a bent touchbar from abuse; swap the spring/bar, it's a cheap fix and there's no maintenance kit to buy","Pair of doors won't latch at the top on SVR - bottom rod bound or top rod out of adjustment; the SVR bottom bolt is independent-action with adjustment for up to 3/4 in undercut, reset rod length and check the S460 bottom strike","ELR device buzzes but latch won't retract, or works intermittently - undersized power or wrong supply; you cannot run the continuous-duty solenoid off a low-voltage doorbell transformer, it needs the ELR150 supply with a CM150-08 module per leaf sized to the 4.75 A pulse / 0.8 A hold load","Fire pair fails inspection for being dogged open - someone dogged what they thought was a standard device; fire (FL) devices have no mechanical dogging by design - if they need daytime free egress, convert to ELR tied to the fire alarm, not dogging","Retrofit onto old Von Duprin holes but trim finger misses the prep - standard 4900/1700 trim ordered instead of the R-prefix retrofit trim; reorder the R4900/R1700 trim factory-set to sit over the competitor's existing cutout, and consult the factory","Access-control lever trim won't pull the latch - tailpiece rotation short of the 50-degree minimum the 21/22-series center-driven cam needs; verify you have the nonhanded 21/22-series device and the correct tailpiece length, consult factory","Key cylinder loose or won't retract latch (03 function) - wrong cylinder or loose set; rim (03) needs a 1E72 rim cylinder, mortise functions (08) and cylinder dogging need the 1-1/4 in 1E74-C4 mortise cylinder, BEST 7-pin, and cores are never furnished with the device"],sources:["https://www.bestaccess.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/PH-0015_Apex-2000-Catalog.NEW_44-01162026.pdf","https://media.topnotchinc.com/docs/catalogs/Precision%20-%202000%20Series.pdf","https://www.bestaccess.com/products/precision-2000/","https://dhwsupport.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/219090707-Electric-Latch-Retraction-ELR-Wiring-Instructions-and-Specifications","https://dhwsupport.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/201320503-What-is-the-part-number-for-the-control-module-for-the-ELR-150-Power-Supply"],photo:"photo-precision-apex",schematic:"schematic-precision-apex"},{id:"falcon-25",name:"Falcon 25 Series Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The Falcon 25 is Allegion's wide-stile, heavy-duty touchbar exit device \u2014 the Grade 1 panic/fire hardware you hang on offices, churches, retail, schools and healthcare corridors when you need real durability without paying Von Duprin 99 money. One center-case family covers all four device types: 25-R rim, 25-V surface vertical rod, 25-C concealed vertical rod, and 25-M mortise. The 24 Series is its narrow-stile sibling for aluminum storefront; the 25 is the wide-stile version for hollow metal and wood doors. Add the F prefix (F-25-R, F-25-V, F-25-M, F-25-C) and you get the UL-listed fire exit hardware \u2014 same device, but with NO mechanical dogging, because you can't hold a fire door's latch open.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.3 Grade 1 certified, all four device types; 10-year mechanical warranty","Device types / ANSI type: 25-R rim = type 1; 25-V surface vertical rod = type 2; 25-M mortise = type 3; 25-C concealed vertical rod = type 8","Latchbolt: stainless steel, 3/4\" throw, deadlocking standard; on both vertical-rod devices (25-V and 25-C) the TOP bolt is 3/4\" and the BOTTOM bolt is 1/2\" throw, deadlocking/latch-retention in the top latch","Dogging: half-turn hex dogging standard (no threaded parts to wear out); cylinder dogging = 'CD' prefix, uses a 1-1/8\" mortise cylinder \u2014 Falcon 985 (#4 cam) conventional, or C987 IC (#5 cam, blocking ring plus collar must equal 1/4\" thick)","Fire (F- prefix, UL 10C / ANSI A156.3): F-25-R rim 'A' label 3 hr at 4'0\" x 8'0\" (8'0\" x 8'0\" with F-4023 mullion), 'B' label 1-1/2 hr at 8'0\" x 8'0\" with KRF-4023 mullion; F-25-M mortise and F-25-C concealed 'A' label 3 hr to 4'0\" x 10'0\" single / 8'0\" x 10'0\" pairs; F-25-M 'B' label 1-1/2 hr with F1280 open-back strike. Fire devices have NO dogging","Doors: 1-3/4\" wood or metal standard (specify if other); mounting height 40-1/4\" from CL to finished floor; vertical rods furnished to a 40-1/4\" CL and 7'0\" door standard","Projection: 2-3/4\" full extend, 2\" fully depressed (dogged)","Handing: 25-R rim non-handed; 25-V and 25-C field reversible; 25-M mortise is handed","Min stile: 25-R 3-1/4\" to 4\" strike-dependent (down to 3-1/4\" with 1439 strike); 25-V 3-1/2\"; 25-C 3-1/2\" metal / 6-1/2\" wood; 25-M 4-1/2\"","Rim strikes: 299 standard (also 264, 1439 for the narrowest stile, 1606); 1609 used where a 25-V meets a 25-R on a pair; fire uses 299F single / 499F for pairs with mullion","Vertical rod (25-V) strikes: 3788 surface-applied top, 2130 bottom mortised/grouted into floor; 4215 angle bracket for flush-transom installs so you keep the 3788 top and the latch-retention feature","Concealed (25-C) strikes: 4188 mortised top, 2130 bottom; patented center-slide for field rod adjustment","Mortise (25-M) strikes: 1279 closed standard (single doors + fire pairs w/ coordinator), F1280 open-back 'B' label, 1281 open-back optional","Mullions (25-R pairs): 2923 (1-1/2\" x 2-1/2\"); 4023 / F4023 fire / KR-4023 keyed-removable / KRF-4023 fire keyed-removable (all 2\" x 3\"); KR/KRF mullion requires a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder with standard 'A' cam (5/16\" x 3/4\", e.g. Schlage B520-731 or similar)","Trims (510L lever designs): Avalon, Broadway, Latitude, Quantum, Boardwalk, Longitude, Dane, Danish, Sutro; 511L vandal-resistant lever (shear pin breaks at 70 ft-lb); 512 pull, 513K knob, 717 Delta; easily replaceable shear pin protects the device","Functions (ANSI): EO (01), DT (02), NL (03), TP (05), L or K (08), L(NL)/K(NL) (03 \u2014 rigid lever, key-retracted latch)","Cylinders: 985 1-1/8\" mortise (#4 cam) for cylinder dogging; 986 1-1/4\" mortise (#4 cam) for mortise NL/OP and L/K/TP/NL lever trims; 951/953 rim cylinders for rim & surface-vertical NL/OP; C987 1-3/8\" IC mortise (#5 cam)","Finishes: US3 (605), US4 (606), US10 (612), US11/643e, US15 (619), US19 (622), US26 (625), US26D (626), US28 (628), US32 (629), US32D (630), 313AN (710)","Electric options: MEL motorized electric latch retraction (fail-SECURE only); FSA fail-safe / FSE fail-secure electric lever/knob/pull trim; delayed egress; battery exit alarm (EA, resets with a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder, standard 'A' cam, single 9V lasts 6+ months on standby); RX/latch monitoring, RX/KOR options"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight, because it trips up half the guys who spec these: Falcon and Von Duprin are BOTH Allegion brands, but the parts do not interchange. Do not order a 99 pad, a 99 rim cylinder, or a 990 trim thinking it'll drop onto a 25 \u2014 it won't. Different center case, different rail, different everything. What Allegion shares across the family is keying, not hardware. So when a customer with a Von Duprin building buys a Falcon 25 for a back door to save a couple hundred bucks, plan the parts and the master-key ring as two separate ecosystems.\n\nThe one real crossover point is the cylinder, and this is where I'll correct a common shop myth \u2014 people say \"the 25 just takes a standard cam.\" Not quite. Falcon's own exit catalog calls out specific cams: the 985 (1-1/8\") dogging cylinder and the 986 (1-1/4\") mortise/trim cylinder both take a #4 cam; the C987 (1-3/8\") IC mortise cylinder takes a #5 cam with the blocking ring plus collar making up 1/4\" of thickness; rim NL functions use the 951/953 rim cylinders. The ONE place a true \"standard A cam\" (5/16\" x 3/4\", Schlage B520-731 or similar) is called out is the keyed-removable mullion \u2014 KR-4023 and KRF-4023 want a 1-1/4\" mortise cylinder with that standard A cam. So yes, you can key the 25 into a Schlage, Corbin, Sargent or whatever system the building runs \u2014 but pull the right cam for the application (#4 for the 985/986, #5 for the C987, A for the KR mullion). Grab the wrong cam and the tailpiece won't drive the mechanism, and you'll be standing there thinking the device is bad when it's the cam.\n\nWhen you spec, nail down four things: device type, fire or not, function, and trim. Fire is the big fork in the road \u2014 the F-prefix devices have NO mechanical dogging, period. That's code, not a Falcon quirk. If a customer wants to prop that door during business hours, you either give them motorized electric latch retraction (MEL \u2014 and note it's fail-SECURE only on this platform) or you tell them no and explain why the AHJ will red-tag it. Watch your fire sizes too: the rim F-25-R is 'A' label to 4'0\" x 8'0\" (8' x 8' only with the F-4023 mullion), while the mortise and concealed F-25-M/F-25-C go to 4'0\" x 10'0\" single and 8'0\" x 10'0\" on pairs \u2014 so if you've got a tall corridor opening, the mortise or concealed carries the taller label, not the rim. On a non-rated door, hex dogging is standard and cylinder dogging (CD) is a cheap upgrade \u2014 spend the money on CD for any door where you don't want the janitor dogging it down with an Allen key and leaving the building open overnight.\n\nStile width will bite you on the mortise and concealed versions. The 25-M needs a 4-1/2\" minimum stile \u2014 same as any mortise exit, because you're cutting a full mortise pocket into the door edge. The 25-C concealed needs 3-1/2\" on metal but a full 6-1/2\" on wood doors because of how the rods route. Measure before you promise a concealed device on a skinny wood stile \u2014 I've seen guys eat the return because the door physically couldn't take it. On the surface 25-V, remember the 2130 bottom strike gets grouted/mortised into the floor; if it's a slab you're drilling and setting it, and in a flush-transom condition you use the 4215 angle bracket so you can keep the standard 3788 top strike and the latch-retention feature.\n\nRepair versus replace: the 25 is worth rebuilding. The center case is heavy wrought and sintered metal, the rail is extruded aluminum, and Allegion still stocks the guts \u2014 pads, springs, center cases, rod assemblies, trims. A worn 510L lever or a broken shear pin is a fifteen-minute trim swap, not a device replacement (the 511L vandal trim shears at 70 ft-lb by design, so a floppy lever is usually just a sacrificed pin). Where I draw the line is a device that's been through a fire, been pried, or has a bent or twisted rail \u2014 replace the whole unit, because a compromised panic device is a liability you don't want your name on. Rough pricing to set expectations: a bare 25-R-EO runs roughly $300-450 depending on finish and where you buy; fire, mortise and concealed versions climb from there, and lever trims add real money \u2014 a full keyed 25-R-L device can land in the $500-700 range installed. That's the whole point of the 25: most of Von Duprin's toughness at a real discount.\n\nCode and liability instincts, always: this is egress hardware, so it must always open with a single motion from the inside \u2014 no key, no special knowledge, no tight grasp. Don't let anyone add a thumbturn, hasp, or slide bolt above it \u2014 that's an ADA and life-safety violation and it's on you the moment you touch that opening. On pairs, mind the coordinator: the 1279 mortise strike and the fire ratings assume a coordinator so the inactive leaf closes first. And on the electric side, keep the two failure modes straight \u2014 the MEL latch retraction is fail-SECURE only (it retracts for entry on command, drops closed on power loss), but egress trim can be spec'd fail-safe (FSA) or fail-secure (FSE). Either way, the pad always dumps the latch MECHANICALLY for exit regardless of power \u2014 if a fire alarm or power loss can ever trap someone inside on the egress side, you've built it wrong.",commonServicingIssues:["Door won't latch / bottom bolt not catching on a 25-V or 25-C \u2014 nine times out of ten it's rod adjustment or a worn/settled floor strike, not the device. Check the 2130 bottom strike is clean and grouted flush, then adjust rod length (25-C uses the patented center-slide, so it's a quick field adjust). Building settling and slab wear are the usual culprits on older installs.","Panic bar 'stays down' / latch stays retracted on a fire door \u2014 a fire device has NO dogging, so if it's staying retracted it's the latch mechanism or center case failing, not somebody dogging it. On a non-fire 25 that keeps getting dogged down and left open, the hex dogging is worn or being abused \u2014 upgrade to cylinder dogging (CD) so a janitor can't casually dog it with an Allen key.","Key turns but latch won't retract on an NL/OP function \u2014 usually the wrong cam. The 985 dogging and 986 mortise cylinders want a #4 cam, the C987 IC wants a #5 cam, and rim NL uses the 951/953 rim cylinders; grab a Von Duprin cylinder or the wrong cam and the tailpiece won't drive the mechanism. Confirm the cam before you condemn the device.","Lever trim flops loose / spins free \u2014 broken shear pin in the 510L (or 511L) trim, which is DESIGNED to break under vandalism or over-torque (511L shears at 70 ft-lb). Replace the shear pin and lever; door prep stays the same, and 511L vandal-resistant trim retrofits into the same prep if the location keeps getting abused.","Sagging hollow-core or wood door tearing out the device screws \u2014 sex bolts (SNB) weren't used. Match them to the device: 25-R EO = 8-425, with trim = 2-425; 25-V EO = 12-425, with trim = 8-425; 25-M and 25-C EO = 6-425, with trim = 2-425 (all #10-24). Standard sheet-metal screws pull out of composite and light-gauge doors under panic traffic.","Electric latch retraction stops working / trim dead \u2014 confirm it's the MEL motorized retraction and wired fail-SECURE (the 25's latch retraction is fail-secure only); a common miswire is treating it like a fail-safe mag setup. Note MEL is not available together with FSE trim on the mortise. Verify the power supply, the transfer hinge/EPT is passing power, and the MEL motor isn't stalled.","Pairs won't close in sequence / astragal binding \u2014 coordinator missing or misadjusted on a mortise pair. The 1279 strike and the fire label assume a coordinator so the inactive leaf lands first; without it the active leaf tries to latch into an open inactive leaf and the door won't secure."],sources:["https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/falcon/information-documents/Falcon_Exit_Devices_Catalog_116520.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/falcon/information-documents/Falcon_Keys_Keying_Systems_and_Cylinders_Catalog_106190.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/en/products/brands/falcon/24-25-series-push-pad-exit-devices.html","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/falcon/installation-documents/Falcon_25-M_and_F-25-M_Installation_Instructions_-_English_French_Spanish_107412.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-documents-2/InstallInstructions/Falcon_25-C_F-25-C_F-25-WDC-90_Installation_Instructions_-_English_French_Spanish_107415.pdf"],photo:"photo-falcon-25",schematic:"schematic-falcon-25"},{id:"detex-v40",name:"Detex V40 Value Series Rim Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The V40 is Detex's economy-tier Grade 1 rim panic \u2014 the value answer to a Von Duprin 99, built to hit a price on a real-world budget. You see it on the back doors of strip-mall retail, restaurants, warehouses, schools, and churches \u2014 anywhere the spec calls for a UL-listed panic or fire exit but the money is tight. It rides a heavy ribbed aluminum rail with a stainless 3/4\" deadlocking Pullman latch, covers a standard 161 door prep, and its whole selling point is the patented mounting-plate and strike-locator system that replaces the paper template so a crew hangs it fast and gets the strike right the first time. The big field draw is the EB option \u2014 an onboard 9V battery exit alarm \u2014 so you get a local-alarm door without pulling a foot of wire.",specs:["Grade: ANSI/BHMA A156.3-2008 Grade 1, Type 1; ANSI/BHMA certified","Latch: stainless steel 3/4\" deadlocking Pullman-type latchbolt","Dogging: 1/8-turn hex key dogging standard (HD); CD cylinder dogging and LD less-dogging optional. F fire-rated devices have NO dogging (code)","Cylinder: takes a MORTISE cylinder \u2014 Detex spec sheet calls for a standard Yale cam; collar required for 7-pin IC cylinders. Note their own factory cylinder, the MC65 (1-1/8\", Schlage C keyway std, US26D), ships with a STRAIGHT cam \u2014 confirm cam style before you order","Strike: 99 surface strike standard (fits 3-1/2\" min single-door stile, 4\" min on pairs); 98 semi-mortise strike optional","Rail: heavy-duty ribbed aluminum extrusion, high-impact ABS end cap; 628 satin aluminum clear anodized (std) or 711 satin black base rail","Static pull force: withstands over 1400 lbs","Door coverage: covers a 161 door prep","Device length: 18-1/8\" rail / 32-7/8\" overall for 36\" door; 24-1/8\" / 44-7/8\" for 48\" door; add 2\" for NS narrow stile","Door thickness: fits standard 1-3/4\" to 2-1/4\"","Door types: wood, hollow metal, fiberglass, aluminum; non-handed, field sizeable","Alarm (EB): 100 dB alarm, standard 9-volt battery, field-selectable key rearm or 2-minute auto rearm, visual and audible arming indicators","Listings: UL Listed Panic Hardware; UL Listed Fire Exit Hardware for 3 hours (max 4'x10' single, pairs of 4'x8' with F90KR keyed removable mullion); UL10B/UL10C positive pressure (UBC 7-2); meets NFPA 80 & 101, ADA; H hurricane-rated option available","Options: mechanical CD/HD/LD, 98/99 strike, NS narrow stile / NSK field kit, PT5 power transfer, EH8 energy-transfer hinge, FC3/FC10 conduit kits; electrical EA, EB, ED, EE delayed egress, EI, ER-EX, ES, EX","Warranty: 10-year mechanical / 3-year electrical limited"],proAdvice:"Spec it like a 99 but respect the differences. The V40 is a value device \u2014 it competes with the Von Duprin 99 and Precision Apex, and for a low-traffic back door it will do fine and save the customer real money. But it is not a 98/99 in cycle life or field-serviceability. On a high-abuse door \u2014 a busy loading dock, a school corridor, anything getting kicked all day \u2014 I steer people to a 99 or a Falcon 25. Pay the premium once instead of warranty-swapping a value rail every eighteen months. That 10-year mechanical / 3-year electrical split tells you where Detex themselves draw the line on the electronics.\n\nThe cylinder is where guys get burned, so read this twice. The V40 uses a MORTISE cylinder, not a rim cylinder. If you grab a rim cylinder off the truck out of habit \u2014 because it is a rim device \u2014 it will not seat and you are making a second trip. Now the cam: Detex's own spec sheet says the V40 accommodates mortise cylinders with a standard Yale cam, and collar required for 7-pin IC. But here is the gotcha nobody tells the apprentice \u2014 their factory cylinder, the MC65 (1-1/8\", Schlage C keyway standard, US26D finish), actually ships with a STRAIGHT cam, and plenty of resellers list the V40 and V40 EB as taking a straight-cam mortise cylinder. Translation: do not blind-order. Confirm the cam style the device on the bench actually wants before you buy the cylinder, because Detex literature and the real factory part do not read the same, and a mis-matched or mis-clocked cam is the number-one reason CD dogging or the alarm arming will not engage. And do not overthink the cam-versus-keyway thing \u2014 the cam is the tail profile, it has nothing to do with the keyway. Run Schlage C, Kwikset, Sargent, whatever keyway the building is keyed on, as long as the cam matches what the device needs. On the 7-pin small-format IC, you must add a cylinder collar to take up the length or the cam will not reach the lever \u2014 Detex calls for the collar but does not publish a clean part number for it in the standard literature, so pull it from the parts breakdown or call Detex tech (830-629-2900) rather than guessing a number.\n\nUnderstand the three dogging worlds before you quote. HD hex dogging is standard \u2014 the little Allen key that holds the pushbar down for push/pull free passage; fine for a non-rated opening. The second you have an F fire-rated device you get NO mechanical dogging, period \u2014 fire doors must positively latch, so dogging is not allowed on them. Do not let a customer talk you into defeating that. CD cylinder dogging lets them dog with a key instead of the hex wrench \u2014 nicer for a manager who does not want to fish for an Allen key. On the EB alarmed version that same cylinder does double duty: one key turn both dogs the device AND arms/disarms the alarm. Elegant, but it trips people up \u2014 if the door is dogged, know what state the alarm is in.\n\nThe EB alarm is the bread-and-butter reason people buy this device, and it is a genuinely simple animal \u2014 9V battery, 100 dB horn, visual and audible arming indicators, a couple of rearm options. Field-select key rearm (horn stays sounding until someone keys it off \u2014 good for a door that should never open) or 2-minute auto rearm (horn times out and resets itself \u2014 good for an occasional-use fire exit where you do not want a manager running back every time). Set it to the customer's tolerance for nuisance noise. Just remember it is a LOCAL alarm \u2014 it makes noise at the door, it does not call anybody. If they want monitoring or a real access-control tie-in, that is EA (hardwired exit alarm), EI (electric integration), ED (electric dogging), ES (electric switching), EX (request to exit), or ER-EX (latch retraction with RTE) territory \u2014 and now you are pulling wire through an FC3/FC10 conduit kit off the end cap, a PT5 power transfer, or an EH8 energy-transfer hinge. Delayed egress is its own animal \u2014 that is the EE option, and it carries real code weight (NFPA 101 fifteen-second irreversible timer, signage, fire-alarm release), so do not confuse a plain EB horn with delayed egress. Sell the EB honestly: a deterrent horn that runs on a battery, not a security system.\n\nOn strikes and stile: the 99 surface strike is standard and it is what the field expects \u2014 3-1/2\" minimum single-door stile, 4\" on pairs. On a narrow 2\" aluminum storefront stile you do NOT force a standard V40 on it \u2014 you spec the V40xNS (adds 2\" to the device, lower-profile strike, good down to a 2\" stile) or add the NSK narrow kit in the field. And on a mullion pair going for the 3-hour fire label, the removable mullion has to be the F90KR keyed-removable to hold the listing \u2014 max 4'x10' single or pairs of 4'x8'. Get the mullion listing right on paper before the fire marshal is standing at the door.",commonServicingIssues:["Alarm won't stop or sounds on its own \u2014 nine times out of ten it is a dead or dying 9V battery, not the board. Swap the battery first before you touch anything else. (The remote wall-mount EAX alarm cousins run a different pack; the onboard V40 EB is a plain 9-volt.)","New cylinder won't turn, won't dog, or won't arm \u2014 you or the last guy put in a RIM cylinder, or the wrong cam. The V40 needs a MORTISE cylinder. Confirm the cam the device actually wants (Detex literature says Yale cam, but the factory MC65 ships with a straight cam \u2014 verify before ordering), install a 1-1/8\" mortise cyl with the correct cam, orient the cam per the sheet, and it comes right.","7-pin IC cylinder spins, feels loose, or cam won't reach the lever \u2014 missing the cylinder collar. Add the collar to take up the length so the cam engages the dogging/arming lever. Detex calls for it but does not publish a tidy part number in the standard lit \u2014 pull it from the parts breakdown or call Detex tech.","Door won't stay dogged (push-pull) \u2014 hex dogging backed out or worn, OR someone is fighting a fire-rated F device that has no dogging by design. Confirm the device is not an F-suffix before you chase a 'broken' dogging feature \u2014 on fire doors that is not a defect, that is code.","Latch won't hold, door rattles or springs back open \u2014 strike misaligned. This is exactly what the strike-locator system exists to prevent; if a prior install ignored it, re-shim or relocate the 99 strike so the Pullman fully deadlocks. Check the deadlatch plunger is riding the strike face, not dropping into the keeper hole.","Alarm rearms too fast or too slow for the customer \u2014 that is the field-selectable key-rearm vs 2-minute auto-rearm setting, not a fault. Reconfigure to their preference.","Latch chewing up over time on a high-traffic door \u2014 it is a value rail; on abusive openings expect faster wear than a 99. Repair the latch or pin if it is a one-off, but on a chronic high-cycle door the honest call is upgrade to a 99 or a 25-series, not keep patching."],schematic:"detex-v40-device",sources:["https://www.detex.com/products/life-safety-security-door-hardware/value-series/v40/","https://www.detex.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/V40.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Detex-ValueSeries-V40-Parts-List.pdf","https://nationallocksupply.com/detex-v40-eb-cd-99-mc65-ka-value-series-rim-exit-device-w-battery-operated-alarm-cylinder-dogging-surface-strike/","https://uslocksupply.com/products/detex-v40-eb-cylinder-dogging-alarmed-rim-exit-device"],photo:"photo-detex-v40"},{id:"jackson-1295",name:"CRL Jackson 1295 Push Pad Rim Panic Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The 1295 is the rim member of Jackson's 12 Series push-pad panic family, and it is the device you meet on aluminum storefront and entrance doors all over the west coast. Jackson is a C.R. Laurence brand \u2014 CRL was bought by Oldcastle BuildingEnvelope in 2015 \u2014 so you order it through the glass-and-glazing supply chain, not the usual lock distributors. That trips up locksmiths who go looking for it in a Von Duprin or Adams Rite catalog. Mechanically it is a straightforward surface rim panic: extruded aluminum base and push pad, thermoplastic housing and end caps, a 3/4\" deadlocking latch, hex-key dogging standard. Its two headline traits are that it is NON-HANDED (unlike the older 10 Series crossbar it replaced, and unlike Jackson's own newer 2095) and that it fits narrow, medium, and wide 1-3/4\" stile doors from the same device. It comes in 36\" and 48\" sizes and the base extrusion is field-trimmable within a size.",specs:["Series: 12 Series push pad. Family members are 1275 surface vertical rod, 1285 concealed vertical rod, 1295 rim. The '12' is the series, the last two digits are the function \u2014 same convention as the 10 Series (1085/1095) and the newer 20 Series (2085/2095)","Handing: NON-HANDED. The 10 Series crossbar devices it descends from are handed (RHRB/LHRB parts), and the newer 20 Series 2095 rim device is handed. The 1295 is not","Doors: 1-3/4\" (44.5 mm) thick narrow, medium, and wide stile aluminum, wood, or hollow metal. Single doors, or pairs with the 896 removable mullion. No other door thickness is published","Sizes: 36\" (3/0) and 48\" (4/0) as discrete SKUs; base extrusion is field-trimmable for narrower openings within a size","Projection: 2-7/8\" (73 mm) from door face; concealed mounting hardware","Latch: 3/4\" (19 mm) throw deadlocking latch bolt, sintered steel (latches and strikes both)","Materials: base and push pad extruded aluminum (ASTM B221); active housing and end caps high-impact thermoplastic; mechanical components stainless and hardened steel","Finishes: satin aluminum anodized (628 / US28) and dark bronze anodized (313 / DBZ). Those two only","Push pad face: ribbed/fluted (cover plate 302674 for 36\", 30267448 for 48\") or smooth (302674S)","Grade: 12 Series spec says devices 'meet or exceed Grade 1 cycle test requirements of ANSI/BHMA 156.3'. Note that is a cycle-test claim, weaker than the flat 'Complies with ANSI 156.3, Grade 1' language Jackson uses on the 20 Series","Listing: UL 305 panic hardware is the referenced standard. The 10 Series spec states 'All exit devices shall be UL 305 Listed' outright; the 12 Series spec lists UL 305 under reference standards but substitutes the Grade 1 cycle language in its quality-control paragraph. Confirm the listing on the actual device label if a rating is being specified","Fire rating: NO fire-rated 1295 variant found in any Jackson literature \u2014 no UL 10C reference, and hex dogging is standard, which is inconsistent with fire exit hardware. Treat as 'no fire version' but verify with CRL before you promise it on a rated opening","Door prep: stile Y-line at 1-1/8\" (28.6 mm) from the outside edge of the stile, for ALL stile widths. Jackson publishes no backset figure as such \u2014 the 1-1/8\" Y-line is the number you lay out from","Mounting height: device and strike centerline 40\" (1016 mm) from bottom of door","Fasteners: (2) 12-24 x 3/4\" FHSMS at the active stile; (1) 1/4\"-20 x 1/2\" PHSMS into a 1/4\"-20 tapped hole at the end-cap bracket on the inactive stile; strike takes (2) 10-24 x 5/8\" pan head MS into tapped frame holes","Prep tooling per install sheet 11M0249: 5/8\", 11/64\", 13/64\", and 1-13/32\" drill bits, a 1/4\"-20 tap, and a jigsaw with a metal blade","Strikes (surface mounted, sintered steel): 302436 'C' type \u2014 the STANDARD strike shipped with all Jackson rim panics; 302501 'S' type \u2014 for blade-stop / applied-stop frame conditions (that application note is distributor copy, not a CRL PDF). Both strikes are shared across the 10, 12, and 20 Series rim panics","Dogging (standard): hex key dogging. Dogging assembly 302670 (shared with the 1285); hex key Cat. No. 302796, sold 2 per pack","Dogging (cylinder option): add 'C' behind the series number in the catalog number. Takes a 1-1/8\" (28.6 mm) MORTISE cylinder, NOT supplied. Uses special cam 302284 which replaces the cam that shipped with the cylinder; cam must sit PARALLEL to the push bar. Cylinder threads into a pocket in the push pad and is retained by 4-40 set screw 301164BLK, driven with a .050 hex key","Keyed entry / night latch: takes a RIM cylinder \u2014 Jackson's optional DL911. No cylinder mounting pad required. Mounted with (2) 1-7/8\" (47.6 mm) breakaway screws and installed BEFORE the device goes on the door; tailpiece is trimmed to fit the drive bushing assembly","Exterior dogging via trim: 821J cylinder mounting pad plus a standard 1\" mortise cylinder (same on 10 and 12 Series)","Catalog number decode: 31 1295 [C] [C|S] [3|4] [628|313] \u2014 311295 = the device, an optional C right after = cylinder dogging, then C or S = strike type, then 3 = 36\" or 4 = 48\", then finish. So 311295C3628 is a 36\" satin-aluminum device with a C strike; 311295CS4313 is a 48\" bronze device with cylinder dogging and an S strike","Packaged parts (install sheet 11M0249): 302654 base end cap pkg, 302679 latch assembly pkg, 302664 control arm hardware pkg, 302670 dogging assembly, 302650 push pad end cap pkg, 302674 / 30267448 / 302674S cover plates, 302436 'C' strike, 302501 'S' strike, 302796 hex key (2), 302284 cylinder dogging cam, 301164BLK set screw, DL911 optional rim cylinder","Trim: 9500LV family, shared with the 2095 \u2014 9500LV02 flat handle lever, 9500LV01 round handle lever. ANSI function 08 (key locks/unlocks), non-handed / field reversible, takes a 1\" mortise cylinder. A separate 9500 night latch flat lever trim exists for the 1295/2095 but its part numbers could not be verified. Vertical-rod counterparts are 8500LV (CVR) and 7500LV (SVR)","Pairs: 896 removable mullion. The mullion keeper bracket is DEVICE-SPECIFIC \u2014 30-2497 for the 1295, 30-2530 for the 2095, 30-784 for the 1095. Also 30-702 top and bottom shoes, 30-737 single-door 2-piece stabilizer, 30-703 pair 4-piece stabilizer, 30-896B8 / 30-896B10 blank mullion in 8' and 10'","Status: still current. Listed on crlaurence.com and stocked by multiple distributors; the full packaged-parts breakdown is published and parts are individually available"],proAdvice:"First thing to understand is where this device lives in the supply chain, because that is what makes it feel exotic to a lock guy. Jackson is a CRL brand \u2014 C.R. Laurence, the glass and glazing house, bought by Oldcastle BuildingEnvelope in 2015. It is not Adams Rite, it is not ASSA ABLOY, and you will not find it in a Von Duprin cross-reference. If a storefront job comes with Jackson panics on it, you are ordering parts through the glazing side of the business (CRL, U.S. Aluminum, or a glass supply distributor) not your usual lock wholesaler. Budget an extra day for parts and know that CRL's own tech docs at crlaurence.com are the authoritative source \u2014 install sheet 11M0249 for the 1295 is the one you want on your phone.\n\nThe cylinder situation is where money gets wasted, so read it twice. The 1295 can take TWO DIFFERENT CYLINDERS doing two different jobs, and they are not the same part. Keyed entry / night latch function takes a RIM cylinder \u2014 Jackson's own is the DL911 \u2014 and it needs no mounting pad. Cylinder DOGGING takes a 1-1/8\" MORTISE cylinder, which is not supplied, and which will not work out of the box because you have to swap its cam for Jackson's special cam, part 302284. That cam has to sit parallel to the push bar or the dogging will not engage, and it is held by a 4-40 set screw (301164BLK) that wants a .050 hex key \u2014 a size that is not in most truck sets. Carry it. If a device has both keyed entry and cylinder dogging you are buying a rim cylinder AND a mortise cylinder for one panic bar. Quote it that way up front rather than explaining it after.\n\nThe standard dogging is plain hex key \u2014 assembly 302670, key part 302796 which ships two to a pack, so leave one with the customer. Jackson's own manual carries one caution worth repeating: do not depress the push bar while the hex key is still inserted. The sequence is insert, turn clockwise to the stop, depress and hold the bar, release the key, then pull the key. Undogging is insert, turn CW to stop, depress the bar, turn and remove. Guys who muscle it with the key in are the ones who bring you a dogging assembly to replace.\n\nGet the series right before you order anything, because Jackson's part numbering rewards precision and punishes guessing. The 10 Series is the old CROSSBAR device (1085 CVR, 1095 rim) and it is HANDED \u2014 the parts list is explicitly RHRB and LHRB per assembly. The 12 Series is push pad (1275 SVR, 1285 CVR, 1295 rim) and the rim model is NON-HANDED. The 20 Series is the newer push-pad generation (2085/2086 CVR, 2095 rim, 2095E electric latch retraction) and confusingly the 2095 went back to being handed. So 'it's a Jackson rim panic, 36 inch' is not an order \u2014 you need series, dogging type, strike type, size, and finish. The catalog number carries all of it: 311295CS4628 reads as 1295, cylinder dogging, S strike, 48\", satin aluminum.\n\nStrikes: the 302436 'C' type is the standard and it is what ships in the box on every Jackson rim panic. The 302501 'S' type is the one you reach for on a blade-stop or applied-stop frame \u2014 that application note comes from distributor copy rather than a CRL sheet, so eyeball the frame condition before you commit. Good news is both strikes are common across the 10, 12, and 20 Series, so a strike off the shelf will serve any Jackson rim panic in the building.\n\nOn pairs with the 896 removable mullion, the mullion keeper bracket is device-specific and this is a real trap: 30-2497 for a 1295, 30-2530 for a 2095, 30-784 for a 1095. Order the wrong one and the mullion is on site and useless. Confirm the device series off the label before the mullion goes on the PO.\n\nDo not sell it as a drop-in replacement for anything. The 1295 prep is Jackson-template-specific \u2014 1-1/8\" Y-line, 40\" centerline, 12-24 and 1/4\"-20 fasteners \u2014 so swapping to or from a Von Duprin 33A, an Adams Rite 8600, or a Detex is a re-prep, not a bolt-up, and there is no published cross-reference chart saying otherwise. The one documented aftermarket swap path is the Command Access PD15-M motorized storefront device, which is explicitly marketed as a Kawneer 1686/1786 and Jackson 1285/1295 retrofit. That is your route if a customer wants electric latch retraction on an existing Jackson opening without re-prepping the door \u2014 otherwise the answer is Jackson's own 2095E and a new prep.\n\nLast one on ratings, and be honest with the customer here. The 12 Series spec language is softer than the 10 and 20 Series \u2014 it claims Grade 1 CYCLE TEST performance rather than flat Grade 1 compliance, and it lists UL 305 under reference standards without the 'shall be UL 305 Listed' sentence the 10 Series spec carries. On top of that there is no evidence of a fire-rated 1295 anywhere in Jackson's literature, and hex dogging as standard says there is not one. If the opening needs a fire exit label, do not put a 1295 on it and do not assume \u2014 read the device label and call CRL. And any modification outside the manual voids both the UL rating and the warranty, which matters on a device that field-trims and field-preps as much as this one does.",commonServicingIssues:["Cylinder dogging won't engage after a new cylinder goes in \u2014 almost always the cam. The mortise cylinder's factory cam has to be replaced with Jackson's 302284 cam, and that cam has to be oriented PARALLEL to the push bar. Wrong cam or wrong clocking and the bar will not stay down.","Set screw won't budge or you have no driver for it \u2014 the cylinder dogging retainer is a 4-40 set screw (301164BLK) that takes a .050 hex key, which is smaller than most truck sets carry. Stock the key rather than chewing the screw.","Wrong cylinder ordered \u2014 keyed entry / night latch takes a RIM cylinder (DL911); cylinder dogging takes a 1-1/8\" MORTISE cylinder. They are different jobs on the same device. Confirm which function the customer actually wants before ordering.","Rim cylinder won't go in with the device mounted \u2014 the DL911 installs BEFORE the device goes on the door, with (2) 1-7/8\" breakaway screws, and the tailpiece gets trimmed to fit the drive bushing. If someone hung the device first, it comes back off.","Dogging assembly worn or damaged \u2014 check whether the last person was depressing the push bar with the hex key still inserted, which the manual explicitly warns against. Assembly is 302670 (shared with the 1285); hex key is 302796.","Latch won't hold or door springs back \u2014 strike alignment. Verify the strike is at the 40\" centerline and that you have the right strike TYPE for the frame: 302436 'C' is the standard shipped strike, 302501 'S' is for blade-stop / applied-stop conditions.","Ordered the wrong mullion keeper for a pair \u2014 30-2497 (1295), 30-2530 (2095), and 30-784 (1095) are not interchangeable on the 896 removable mullion. Read the device label, not the door.","Customer wants a fire-rated version \u2014 no fire-rated 1295 appears in Jackson literature, and standard hex dogging is inconsistent with fire exit hardware. Do not order one on a promise; verify with CRL or spec a listed device from another line.","Parts hunt stalls at the usual lock wholesalers \u2014 Jackson is a CRL (Oldcastle BuildingEnvelope) brand sold through the glass and glazing channel. Go to crlaurence.com tech docs and CRL/U.S. Aluminum distribution, not your lock supplier."],sources:["https://azure.crlaurence.com/techdocs/PDF/11M0249-1295.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-1295-Rim-Panic-Device-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-1295-Rim-Panic-Device-Cylinder-Dogging-Instructions.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-Section-08-7100-12-Series-Push-Pad-Exit-Device-Specifications.docx","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-SECTION-08-7100-10-Series-Cross-Bar-Exit-Device-Specifications.docx","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-896-Removable-Mullion-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://absupply.net/pdf/CRL_Jackson_20_Series_Exit_Devices.pdf","https://mrlock.com/brand-index/jackson/exit-devices-by-crl-jackson/1295-device/1295-parts/","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/jackson-documentation/"]},{id:"jackson-1095",name:"CRL Jackson 1095 / 1095P Crossbar Rim Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The 1095 is Jackson's 10 Series rim exit device and it is the old-school crossbar bar you find on aluminum storefront doors that were hung decades ago and never touched since. The line dates to the 1970s and it is still in production \u2014 CRL sells the 10, 12, and 20 Series side by side, so the 1095 was never superseded, it just occupies the crossbar niche while the 12 and 20 Series cover push pad. Two things separate it from the 1295 push-pad device most people reach for today. First, it is HANDED \u2014 you specify right or left hand reverse bevel at order time, and the body, arm, back plate, and paddle assemblies are all hand-specific parts. Second, the P in 1095P means PADDLE, not panic: the 1095P swaps the crossbar for a push paddle engraved PUSH, on the same rim latch body. That is a paddle on a crossbar-family device, which is a different animal from the 12 Series push pad, and conflating the two is how people order the wrong parts. If you service one thing on this device it will be the crossbar cut, because CRL's own tolerance is brutally tight.",specs:["Series: 10 Series crossbar. Family is 1085/1085P concealed vertical rod and 1095/1095P rim. Parallel to, not replaced by, the push-pad 12 Series (1275/1285/1295) and 20 Series (2085/2086/2095) \u2014 CRL sells all three lines concurrently","1095 vs 1095P: P = PADDLE. The 1095P substitutes a push paddle engraved 'PUSH' for the crossbar on the same rim latch body. Because there is no bar to size, 1095P catalog numbers carry NO size digit (31-1095P-R-C-628) where the crossbar 1095 does (31-1095-R-C-4-628)","Handing: HANDED, must be specified when ordering. RHRB / LHRB (right/left hand reverse bevel \u2014 vendors abbreviate RHR/LHR). All 10 Series exit devices are reverse bevel because storefront doors are outswinging","Catalog number: 31-1095-[L|R]-[C|S]-4-[628|313] \u2014 CRL prefix 31, handing, strike type C or S, size 4 (4/0), finish. Live SKUs include 311095LC4628, 311095RC4628, 311095LC4313, 311095RC4313, 311095LS4628, and paddle versions 311095PLC628 / 311095PRC628","Size: 4/0 ONLY \u2014 48\" door. There is no 3/0 1095. (Contrast the 12 Series 1285, offered in both 3/0 x 7/0 and 4/0 x 7/0.) Max door width 48\"","Crossbar: ships 48\" (1219 mm), field-cut. Part 30733PKG, '48\" CROSSBAR WITH SCREWS'. Mounting screw is 30744, a 1/4\"-20 x 1\" oval head (fastener C). NOTE: two vendors describe the bar as 45\" \u2014 CRL's install sheet, the 1085 parts list, and CRL's own SKUs all say 48\". The 45\" figure may be usable length after the arms. Unresolved; measure, do not assume","Less-crossbar option: 1095-X-C-LC / 1095-X-S-LC = 'Less Cross Bar', for retrofitting onto an existing bar","Doors: 1-3/4\" (44 mm) thick narrow, medium, and wide stile aluminum, wood, or hollow metal. Wood door mounting kit is 894","Latch: rim latch bolt, sintered steel, 3/4\" throw (per the 10 Series architectural spec: 'Rim type devices shall be furnished with 3/4\" throw latch bolt')","Prep \u2014 Y-line: 1-1/8\" (28.6 mm) from the outside edge of the stile, for ALL stile widths. X-line is per template. Template 10-484 REV. D ships in the box","Prep \u2014 mounting heights: two datums, 35\" (889 mm) and 40-3/8\" (1026 mm), each measured to BOTTOM OF DOOR. Identical for 1095 and 1095P","Prep \u2014 tooling: 5/8\", 11/64\", and 13/64\" drill bits; 1/4\"-20 tap. Drill and tap for 1/4\"-20 shoulder studs (2 places); drill and countersink for 10-32 FHMS. A TORX T-10 is required for the dogging pin lock screw \u2014 carry it","Fasteners: (A) 1/4\"-20 x 1/4\" shoulder stud bolts, 2; (B) 1/4\"-20 x 1/4\" allen set screws, 3 per assembly on both active and inactive; (C) 1/4\"-20 x 1\" oval head for the crossbar, 1; (D) 1/4\"-20 x 1-7/8\" pan head for the rim cylinder, 2","Dogging: slotted PIN, not the 12 Series assembly. 301115 standard dogging pin ships with the device; 301117 optional thumbturn dogging pin is a field swap. Both are shared with the 1085 CVR. Retained by a single #10 Torx lock screw under the housing. This is NOT interchangeable with the 12 Series 302670 dogging assembly \u2014 that one is a multi-piece unit on four 3/16\" pan heads integrated with the push pad","Cylinder: optional DL911 RIM cylinder, installed into the exterior active stile on the two (D) 1/4\"-20 x 1-7/8\" pan head screws. No mounting pad required. Finish-specific: DL911SCKDA aluminum, DL911SCKDDU dark bronze","Cylinder dogging: DISPUTED. Resellers claim dual dogging via an outside mortise cylinder, but CRL's own install sheet shows only the two pins, and the price list lists cylinder-dogging variants for the 12 Series (1285C, 1295C) while listing NONE for the 1095. The 821J mortise cylinder pad appears under the 1085 CVR block and vendor copy scopes it to 1085/1285/2085/2086 \u2014 not the 1095. Verify with CRL before promising it","Strikes: 2436 type 'C' and 2501 type 'S', both surface mounted, shared across the 10, 12, and 20 Series (they appear on the 1095, 1295, and 2095 price-list pages). Strike type is a catalog-number position. Vendors also list these with the 30- prefix as 302436 / 302501","Trim: THERE IS NO LEVER TRIM for the 1095. The price list gives 7500LV for the 1275, 8500LV for 1085/1285/2085/2086, and 9500LV for 1295/2095 \u2014 the 10 Series rim device has no trim SKU, no dummy trim, no electric trim. Treat it as exit-only, or key access via the DL911 rim cylinder","Mullion: 896 removable. Complete assembly for the 1095 is 30-896-1095 in 8' and 10' ('complete mullion w/ strike installed'); blank stock is 30-896B8 / 30-896B10. Keeper is 30-784, 'Type P \u2014 for use w/ 1095 \u2014 pair'. Compare 30-2497 (1295) and 30-2530 (2095): three devices, three non-interchangeable keepers. Shoes 30-702; stabilizers 30-737 single door 2-piece, 30-703 pair 4-piece. Astragal alternative 9613, field-sizeable, 7' and 8'","Materials: body and arms high-tensile aluminum; crossbar extruded tubular aluminum; mechanical components stainless and/or hardened steel; strikes and latches sintered steel","Finishes: 628 / US28 satin aluminum anodized and 313 / DBZ dark bronze anodized. Those two only","Listing: the 10 Series architectural spec states outright 'All exit devices shall be UL 305 Listed', and classifies the 1095 as Type 1 (rim exit device) / Type 4 (narrow stile rim), with the 896 as Type 22. Reference standards cited: ANSI/BHMA A156.3, A156.18, A117.1, ADA, ASTM, NFPA 101","ANSI/BHMA grade: NOT ESTABLISHED. One vendor says 'Grade 1 certified', another categorizes it 'Standard Duty', and CRL's own spec cites A156.3 as a reference standard without ever stating a grade. No BHMA certified-products listing was found. Do not quote a grade to a customer","Fire rating: no CRL literature claims one, and none denies one. A device shipping with a standard mechanical dogging pin cannot carry a fire label, but that is engineering inference, not a cited fact. Do not put this on a rated opening without written confirmation","Packaged parts (crossbar 1095): 30988 RHRB active body+arm, 30989 LHRB active body+arm, 30990 RHRB inactive body+arm, 30991 LHRB inactive body+arm, 301247 RHRB arm, 301248 LHRB arm, 301242/301243 inactive arms, 301115 standard dogging pin, 30851 body hardware pkg, 30846 RHRB back plate sub-assembly, 30847 LHRB back plate sub-assembly, 30852 back plate hardware pkg, 30733PKG 48\" crossbar with screws, 301117 optional thumbturn dogging pin","Packaged parts (1095P paddle): substitutes 30998 RHRB active body+paddle, 30999 LHRB active body+paddle, 301245 RHRB paddle arm, 301246 LHRB paddle arm. Shares 30851, 30846/30847, 30852, 301115, 301117","Parts caution: CRL's own literature is internally inconsistent on the hand assignment of inactive arms 301242 / 301243 \u2014 the 1095 sheet's table and its diagram callouts disagree, and the 1085 parts list and mrlock read differently again. Confirm by eye against the part in hand, do not order off the number alone","Status: current, not superseded. All six primary SKUs are live on crlaurence.com and CRL maintains a separate '10 Series Rim Panic Accessories' category. Parts availability is good \u2014 one distributor lists 29 distinct 1095 parts. Handed slow-movers do go out of stock"],proAdvice:"Start with the number one field fact on this device, because it is the one that turns a two-hour job into a callback: CRL's install sheet says the crossbar must be cut within 1/16\" of correct size to work properly. That is a real tolerance, printed by the manufacturer, and it is tighter than most people cut anything on a door. The measurement is not the door width and it is not the old bar \u2014 it is from the inside edge of the left body arm assembly to the inside edge of the right body arm assembly, with both assemblies mounted. Measure that dimension, cut, deburr, then loosen the inactive body/arm assembly, seat the bar in the arm cavities, and re-secure. Cut it long and the bar binds; cut it short and the device rattles and will not latch reliably. Measure twice is not a saying here, it is the spec.\n\nGet the handing right at order time or you own the part. The 1095 is handed \u2014 RHRB or LHRB, right or left hand reverse bevel, and everything reverse bevel because storefront doors swing out. The handed parts are the active body and arm, the inactive body and arm, both arm assemblies, the back plate sub-assembly, and on a P device the paddle and paddle arm. Unhanded: the crossbar, the dogging pins, and the two hardware packages. Here is the honest gap you need to know about \u2014 CRL does not publish a handing determination diagram anywhere in the public literature. The install sheet assumes handing was settled when the order was placed. So determine handing from the door itself using standard practice, confirm it against the device in hand, and do not go looking for a Jackson handing chart, because there is not one.\n\nP means PADDLE. Not panic, not a finish code. The 1095P is the same rim latch body with a push paddle engraved PUSH instead of a crossbar, and you can spot it in the catalog number instantly because the paddle version has no size digit \u2014 31-1095P-R-C-628 versus 31-1095-R-C-4-628 for the bar. Do not let the word paddle make you think 12 Series push pad; that is a completely different device family with different dogging, different trim, and different parts. Two people on the same job saying 'the push one' about a 1095P and a 1295 is a parts order that comes back wrong.\n\nDogging on the 10 Series is a pin, and it is a genuinely different scheme from the push-pad devices. Standard is dogging pin 301115; the thumbturn version 301117 is a field swap and it is what you sell the customer who is tired of hunting for a tool. The swap is: depress the handle, back out the #10 TORX lock screw, pull the standard pin, depress the handle again, insert the 301117 pin AND its spring, lock it with the Torx screw, release. Carry a T-10 \u2014 it is not in most truck sets and you cannot do this job without it. Both pins are shared with the 1085 CVR, which is a nice bit of 10 Series commonality. What they are NOT is compatible with the 12 Series 302670 dogging assembly, which is a multi-piece unit on four screws living in the push pad. Different world, do not cross-order.\n\nNow the thing to be careful about, because the internet will lie to you. Reseller listings for the 1095 widely claim 'dual dogging \u2014 by outside cylinder using a standard mortise cylinder, or by slotted pins.' CRL's own install sheet shows no such thing: pins and an optional DL911 rim cylinder for keyed access, full stop. The price list carries explicit cylinder-dogging variants for the 12 Series (1285C, 1295C) and none for the 1095, and the 821J mortise cylinder pad is scoped by vendors to the 1085, 1285, 2085, and 2086 \u2014 the rim 1095 is not on that list. My read is that the reseller copy is 10 Series family boilerplate pasted onto the rim model. Quote it as an open question and call CRL before you promise a customer key dogging on a 1095.\n\nOn cylinders generally, the 1095 is simple in a way the 1295 is not: the optional cylinder is a RIM cylinder, the DL911, it goes into the exterior active stile on the two long 1-7/8\" pan head screws, and it needs no mounting pad. Order it in the finish \u2014 DL911SCKDA for aluminum, DL911SCKDDU for dark bronze. One reseller describes a field-selectable standard versus night-latch function; that is single-source, so verify it on the bench rather than selling it.\n\nSet expectations on trim before the customer does it for you. There is no lever trim for the 1095. The price list has 7500LV, 8500LV, and 9500LV covering the 1275, the CVR devices, and the 1295/2095 \u2014 and nothing for the 10 Series rim device. No lever, no dummy, no electric. If they want outside lever access on this opening, you are either adding a separate lock or you are changing devices. Say that on the first visit.\n\nWhen you have one apart, respect the gear timing. The exploded views carry an instruction in capitals: index mark up, install gears with the blank tooth in the upper position, must be inserted as shown. That is the back plate sub-assembly, and the fact that CRL sells 30852 as a standalone back plate hardware package to repair the gear mechanism tells you mis-timing is an anticipated failure. Between that kit and the individually stocked arm assemblies, the parts catalog is quietly telling you where this device wears: the arms and the back plate gear train. There is no published troubleshooting guide, no service bulletin, and no manufacturer failure list for the 1095 \u2014 so treat that as a parts-availability read on the wear points, not as a documented defect.\n\nLast, on paper claims. The architectural spec says flatly that all 10 Series exit devices shall be UL 305 listed, and it types the 1095 as a Type 1 / Type 4 rim device. What it never says is an ANSI grade, and the vendors contradict each other outright \u2014 one says Grade 1 certified, another files it under standard duty. No BHMA listing turned up. So do not quote a grade. Same discipline on fire: nobody claims a rating and nobody denies one, and a standard mechanical dogging pin is inconsistent with a fire label, but that is my inference and not a citation. On a rated opening, spec something whose label you can read.",commonServicingIssues:["Device binds, rattles, or won't latch after a crossbar swap \u2014 the bar is out of tolerance. CRL's spec is within 1/16\" of correct size. Re-measure from the inside edge of one body arm assembly to the inside edge of the other, with both mounted, and recut. This is the most common self-inflicted fault on the device.","Ordered the wrong hand \u2014 the 1095 is handed RHRB/LHRB and the body, arm, back plate, and paddle assemblies are all hand-specific. Only the crossbar, dogging pins, and hardware packages are unhanded. CRL publishes no handing diagram, so determine it off the door and confirm against the device before ordering.","Can't get the dogging pin out \u2014 it is retained by a #10 TORX screw under the housing, not a hex or a Phillips. Without a T-10 you are not doing this job. Depress the handle to relieve the pin before backing the screw out.","Thumbturn dogging pin installed and it won't hold \u2014 the 301117 ships as pin AND spring. If the spring was left in the bag the pin will not retain. Depress the handle, insert both, then lock with the Torx screw.","Tried to fit a 12 Series dogging assembly \u2014 302670 is a four-screw multi-piece unit for the push-pad 1295/1285 and does not go in a 10 Series device. The 10 Series takes pin 301115 or 301117, shared with the 1085.","Device operates roughly or intermittently after being apart \u2014 gear timing in the back plate. The exploded view says index mark up, blank tooth in the upper position. Re-time it; CRL sells 30852 back plate hardware package specifically to repair the gear mechanism.","Customer wants an outside lever \u2014 there is no lever trim for the 1095. No lever, no dummy, no electric trim exists in the price list for the 10 Series rim device. Options are the DL911 rim cylinder for key access, a separate lock, or a different device family.","Promised key dogging and it isn't there \u2014 cylinder dogging on the 1095 is disputed. Reseller copy claims it; CRL's install sheet and price list do not support it, and the 821J pad is scoped to the 1085/1285/2085/2086. Confirm with CRL before quoting.","Ordered the wrong mullion keeper for a pair \u2014 the 1095 takes 30-784 ('Type P'). 30-2497 is the 1295 and 30-2530 is the 2095. Not interchangeable. The complete 896 assembly for this device is 30-896-1095 in 8' or 10'.","Inactive arm ordered by part number arrives wrong-handed \u2014 CRL's own documents disagree on whether 301242 or 301243 is the right hand. Verify visually against the part you are replacing rather than trusting the number."],sources:["https://azure.crlaurence.com/techdocs/PDF/11M0246-1095_JACKSON_RIM_EXIT_DEVICE.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-1095-1095P-Panic-Exit-Device-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-SECTION-08-7100-10-Series-Cross-Bar-Exit-Device-Specifications.docx","https://accesshardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/Jackson-2009-Pricelist.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-1085-1085P-CVR-Exit-Device-Parts-List.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-896-Removable-Mullion-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://www.crlaurence.com/products/brands/listing/D12BA_DW","https://mrlock.com/brand-index/jackson/exit-devices-by-crl-jackson/1095-device/1095-parts/","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/jackson-documentation/"]},{id:"jackson-1285",name:"CRL Jackson 1285 Concealed Vertical Rod Exit Device",category:"Exit devices",whatItIs:"The 1285 is the concealed vertical rod member of Jackson's 12 Series push-pad family \u2014 the same push bar as the 1295, but instead of a rim latch into a surface strike it drives two rods hidden inside the door, throwing a bolt up into the header and down into the threshold. It is the device on aluminum storefront pairs where nobody wanted a mullion in the middle of the opening. Two facts reframe how you treat it. First, unlike the 1295 rim device, the 1285 is HANDED \u2014 the actuating lift assembly is hand-specific and explicitly not field reversible, and confusingly the older 1085 CVR that shares its rods is non-handed. Second, this is the Jackson device with a real service reputation, and CRL knows it: they publish a dedicated CVR troubleshooting guide whose opening line is 'All the magic happens at the top.' Every CVR you ever adjust is won or lost at the top rod, and the hump strike plate is not optional hardware.",specs:["Series: 12 Series push pad \u2014 1275 surface vertical rod, 1285 concealed vertical rod, 1295 rim. The 1285 is the CVR member","Handing: HANDED. Actuating lift assembly is 302669 LHRB / 302675 RHRB and is NOT field reversible. Note the trap: the older 1085 CVR is non-handed and the 1295 rim is non-handed, but this device is not","Catalog number: 31 + 1285 + [C cylinder dogging | E electrified | none] + [L|R] + [37 = 36\" | 4 = 48\"] + finish. Live examples: 311285L37628, 311285R4628, 311285CR4628 (cyl dogging), 311285ER4628 (electrified), 311285L37690 (champagne). Some distributors hyphenate as 31-1285-R-3-7-628. What '37' literally abbreviates is unverified \u2014 treat it as the code CRL assigns to the 36\" device, not as a dimension","Sizes: 36\" and 48\" stocked; base is field-trimmable. Door thickness 1-3/4\" (44.5 mm); narrow, medium, and wide stile aluminum, wood, or hollow metal. Projection 2-7/8\" (73 mm)","Door height: rods ship to fit up to 84\" (2.13 m). Taller doors take top rod extensions \u2014 301347 adds 12\" (305 mm), 301348 adds 24\" (610 mm), giving nominal 8/0 and 9/0. The rod and case assembly itself is 302452J, 'Standard Rod and Case Assembly 7/0'","Finishes: 628 / US28 satin aluminum and 313 / DBZ dark bronze; 690 champagne and 335 / BLK appear on some part packages","Prep \u2014 Y-line: 1-1/8\" (28.6 mm) from the outside edge of the stile, for ALL stile widths. That is how the device absorbs stile variation; no stile-width range is published","Prep \u2014 mounting centerlines to bottom of door: 39-5/8\" (1007 mm) interior active pad, 39-7/8\" (1013 mm) exterior lock mounting, 38\" (965 mm) exterior lever trim","Templates shipped: 10-942 REV. H (device), 10-941-1 REV. L (device + cylinder), 10-1036 REV. B (lever trim)","Tooling: 5/8\", 11/64\", 13/64\", 1-13/32\" drill bits; 1/4\"-20 tap; TORX T-10; hex set; jigsaw with metal blade. Inactive stile gets drilled and tapped 1/4\"-20","Fasteners: A (4) 10-32 x 7/8\" FHMS end caps; B (1) 1/4\"-20 x 1/2\" PHMS end-cap bracket; C (4) #10 x 1/2\" FH self-tapping bolt guides; D (4) #8 x 3/8\" FH strikes; E (2) #8 x 3/4\" FH trip bracket; F 1/4\"-20 x 1\" adjusting screw; G (2) #8-32 x 2\" FHMS cylinder pad; H (2) #10 x 2-1/8\" FHMS lever trim; I (4) 8-32 x 3/8\" FHMS base end caps; J (2) 8-32 x 1/4\" SHCS rod and case","INSTALL ORDER: the cylinder pad and exterior trim go on BEFORE the exit device. Bolt guides are set to extend 1/2\" (12.7 mm) past the frame before fastening","Bolts: hex-adjustable top and bottom rod bolts, one part number covers both \u2014 30763P. Plated hardened steel; strikes and latches sintered steel","Bolt THROW is not published for the CVR. The spec doc's 3/4\" throw figure applies to RIM devices only. What the 1285 literature specifies is ENGAGEMENT: 3/8\" into the header and 3/8\" into the threshold strike, closed and relaxed. Do not quote a 1285 throw number","Less-bottom-rod (LBR): NOT offered as far as any literature shows \u2014 no mention in the install sheet, spec doc, parts breakdown, price list, or distributor pages. The mechanics argue against it since both rods move in tandem off one lift assembly. Treat as unavailable but unconfirmed","Strikes: 301084 'Strike Package \u2014 Top and Bottom' covers both ends, in 628 / 313 / 335. The TOP STRIKE IS A HUMP STRIKE PLATE AND IT IS MANDATORY \u2014 the install sheet carries a boxed warning, 'Hump Strike Plate must be used for proper operation.' The hump drives the rod down an extra 3/32\" as it passes, which raises the bottom rod the same amount to clear the threshold. Without it, accurate adjustment is very difficult. Bottom is a threshold strike plate on (2) #8 x 3/8\" FH","Trip bracket 30824, mounted with (2) #8 x 3/4\" FH plus a 1/4\"-20 x 1\" adjusting screw. The top guide carries a trip-release slide that must move freely","Header gap must be 1/8\" +1/32\". Shim if it is larger","Rod adjustment: retract both bolts flush or slightly below the stile ends, dog the device closed, hang the door. Then with the pad FULLY DEPRESSED, run the bottom bolt clockwise to sit 1/16\" above the bottom strike plate, and the top to a 1/16\" (max 1/8\") gap from the header. Finish with the flat of each rod bolt facing outward, parallel to the door face. Tool is the 30PBAW Bolt Adjustment Wrench, supplied with the bolt guides. Rod silencer is 301552 / 301552PKG","ADJUSTMENT DIRECTION CONFLICT \u2014 both documents are CRL's: the install sheet says turn the bottom bolt clockwise to extend, and the troubleshooting guide agrees for the bottom (CCW shorten / CW lengthen) but states the opposite for the TOP rod (CW shorten / CCW lengthen). Read it as the two ends turning opposite ways, and verify by eye on the bench","Dogging: hex key standard \u2014 1/8\" hex dogging key Cat. No. 302796, turn clockwise until it stops. Dogging assembly 302670, confirmed shared with the 1295","Cylinder dogging: factory-ordered option, denoted by C in the catalog number. CRL supplies ONLY THE PREP, not the cylinder \u2014 it takes a 1-1/8\" (28.6 mm) mortise cylinder that CRL does not stock and must be special ordered. Swap the cylinder's cam for 302284, cam PARALLEL to the push bar, secure with 4-40 set screw 301164BLK via a .050 hex key in the side hole, lining a cylinder groove up to the set screw. Verification per drawing 10-974 sheet 1. Caution: do not depress the push bar while the hex key is inserted","Cylinder for KEYED ENTRY is a MORTISE cylinder, not a rim cylinder \u2014 DL2170, single 1\" (25.4 mm), into mounting pad 30821J, retained by a lock ring, fastened with (2) #8-32 x 2\" FHMS. The spec doc separately routes exterior dogging through the same 821J pad with a standard 1\" mortise cylinder. The DL911 rim cylinder belongs to the 1295 rim device and does NOT appear in any 1285 document","HARD CYLINDER LIMIT: the troubleshooting guide states the lock cylinder must be no more than 1\" long. An over-long cylinder binds against the interior case and is an enumerated cause of the device not operating","Trim: 8500LV family \u2014 8500LV-01 round lever, 8500LV-02 flat lever, ANSI function 08, mounted with (2) #10 x 2-1/8\" FHMS. The spec doc assigns trim strictly by device type: 8500LV for Type 6 narrow-stile CVR, 9500LV for Type 1/4 rim, 7500LV for Type 5 narrow-stile SVR. Removable mullion for the family is the 896","Packaged parts: 302652 base end cap pkg, 302669 LHRB actuating lift, 302675 RHRB actuating lift, 302664 control arm hardware pkg, 302670 dogging assembly, 302650 push-pad end cap pkg, 302674 / 30267448 ribbed base cover plate and 302674S smooth, 30824 trip bracket pkg, 301084 strike pkg top and bottom, 30916 rod and case hardware pkg, 30934 top bolt guide assembly, 30320 bottom bolt guide assembly, 302452J rod and case assembly 7/0, 301347 / 301348 top rod extensions, 30763P standard top and bottom rod bolts, 301552PKG rod silencer (12), 30PBAW bolt adjustment wrench (2), 302796 dogging hex key pkg (2), 302513 base 1085 retrofit hardware package. Older superseded numbers 30-2661 and 30-2697 appear in the 2006 price list for the end-cap positions","Grade: the 12 Series spec says devices 'meet or exceed Grade 1 cycle test requirements of ANSI/BHMA 156.3' \u2014 a cycle-test claim, not a flat Grade 1 compliance statement","Listing: UL 305 panic hardware is a referenced standard and the install sheet warns that modifications could cause failure to meet UL safety ratings. NO UL file number specific to the 1285 was found","Fire rating: no evidence of any \u2014 no UL 10B/10C label and no 'fire exit hardware' designation anywhere in the retrieved literature. What is verified is the ABSENCE of a claim, not a positive statement from CRL that it is unrated","Electrified: 1285E is a catalog option (E in the number). The 20 Series electrified guidance, which CRL's shared troubleshooting guide covers, runs a 2616 power supply \u2014 110 VAC in, 24 VDC on terminals 5&6 and 11&12, with an 8-10 amp INRUSH to fire the solenoid. Wire gauge by run: 16 AWG to 40 ft, 14 AWG to 60 ft, 12 AWG to 100 ft","Status: current, not discontinued. Standard, cylinder-dogging, and electrified SKUs are all live and parts are actively stocked. The 1285 shares concealed rods with the 1085 (it is effectively the push-bar version of that crossbar device) and 302513 exists specifically to retrofit an 1085 base to a 1285. Rod and case is shared across 1085 / 1285 / 2086 on 7' doors","2085 / 2086 are a separate, heavier CVR line, NOT a replacement for the 1285 \u2014 no source states supersession, and CRL's troubleshooting guide addresses 'the 1085, 1285 or 2086' as one adjustment family. The 2085 is 36\"-only, handed, and uses its own rod and case (301287J)","Cross-reference: one distributor states 'the Kawneer 1686 is the same device as the 1285'. Single-source \u2014 plausible, not confirmed. Note the Command Access PD15-M motorized retrofit is marketed for Kawneer 1686/1786 AND Jackson 1285/1295, which is consistent with that claim"],proAdvice:"Learn one sentence and you have learned this device: all the magic happens at the top. That is CRL's own opening line in their CVR troubleshooting guide, and the fact that Jackson publishes a dedicated three-page guide for adjusting these rods tells you exactly what kind of animal you are dealing with. The top and bottom rods move in tandem off one lift assembly, so if you start by adjusting the bottom \u2014 which is where everybody starts, because that is the end that is visibly not latching \u2014 you will chase it all afternoon and never get there. Set the top first. Get it right. Only then touch the bottom.\n\nThe hump strike plate is not a nice-to-have and it is not a generic strike. CRL puts a boxed warning in the install sheet: the hump strike plate must be used for proper operation. Here is the why, because knowing it stops you from substituting something flat off the truck. The hump drives the top rod down an extra 3/32\" as the door passes it, and because the rods are linked, that same 3/32\" lifts the bottom rod clear of the threshold. Pull the hump and the geometry that makes the bottom bolt clear the floor is gone, and CRL's own words are that accurate adjustment becomes very difficult. If you inherit a 1285 that nobody can make work, look at the top strike before you look at anything else.\n\nThe adjustment sequence, in the order that actually works: retract both bolts flush or a touch below the ends of the stile, dog the device closed, and hang the door. Then, with the push pad FULLY DEPRESSED, set the bottom bolt to sit 1/16\" above the bottom strike plate and the top to a 1/16\" gap from the header, 1/8\" absolute max. Closed and relaxed, each bolt should engage 3/8\". Finish with the flat of each rod bolt facing outward, parallel to the door face \u2014 that is not cosmetic, it is how the bolt presents to the strike. Use the 30PBAW wrench that came with the bolt guides. And know about a real inconsistency in CRL's paperwork before it confuses you at the door: the install sheet and the troubleshooting guide agree that the bottom bolt lengthens clockwise, but the troubleshooting guide says the TOP rod is the reverse \u2014 clockwise to shorten. Read the two ends as turning opposite ways, and confirm by watching the bolt rather than counting turns.\n\nHanding will bite you if you carry 1295 habits over. The 1285 is HANDED \u2014 actuating lift 302669 is LHRB, 302675 is RHRB, and the vendor note says plainly that it is not field reversible. What makes this genuinely confusing is the family: the 1295 rim device beside it is non-handed, and the older 1085 CVR whose rods it shares is ALSO non-handed. So within one manufacturer's CVR lineage you have a non-handed predecessor and a handed successor. Specify hand on the order, and when you pull a lift assembly, read the part number off the old one.\n\nCylinders on the 1285 are not the cylinders on the 1295, and this is where a truck stock assumption costs you the job. Keyed outside entry on the 1285 takes a MORTISE cylinder \u2014 the DL2170, single, 1\" \u2014 into mounting pad 30821J with a lock ring and two #8-32 x 2\" screws. The DL911 rim cylinder that the 1295 uses does not appear in a single 1285 document; it belongs to the rim side of the family. Then, separately, if the device was ordered with cylinder dogging (the C in the catalog number) that is a THIRD cylinder situation: a 1-1/8\" mortise cylinder that CRL does not even stock and you special order, fitted with cam 302284 parallel to the push bar and locked by a 4-40 set screw with a .050 hex key. And carry this limit in your head: the troubleshooting guide says the cylinder must be no more than 1\" long. Longer than that and it binds against the interior case, which presents as a device that will not operate and sends people hunting through the rods for a fault that is sitting in the cylinder pocket.\n\nWhen a 1285 binds, CRL enumerates the causes and they are almost never the rods themselves. Door out of plumb or square. Screws protruding from weatherstripping, astragals, or exterior trim, fouling the case. The exterior cylinder binding against the interior case. Weatherstripping stopping the door from fully closing. The diagnostic is to pull the top rod guide and watch the rod move while you operate the device \u2014 that tells you instantly whether the problem is upstream in the case or downstream in the rod. The escalation path is loosen the outside cylinder, loosen anything pressing on the case, verify the cylinder is under 1\", lubricate the case with white lithium grease. If the top bolt still will not retract after all that, CRL's own conclusion is that it is probably a spring issue and the rod and case assembly may need replacing. Quote that job honestly \u2014 it is not a five-minute adjustment.\n\nOne more from the guide that catches people on electrified units: the electronic time delay must expire before you close the door. Close it early and the device will appear correctly set when it is not, and you will hand the job back to yourself next week. On the electrified side generally, respect the inrush \u2014 8 to 10 amps to fire the solenoid, which is why the wire gauge table matters (16 AWG to 40 feet, 14 to 60, 12 to 100). Size the conductor for the inrush, not the holding current.\n\nOn order and paperwork, two habits. Install the cylinder pad and exterior trim BEFORE the exit device, per the sheet \u2014 get that backwards and you are taking the device off again. And set bolt guides to extend 1/2\" past the frame before you fasten them. On ratings, be conservative: the 12 Series language is a Grade 1 CYCLE TEST claim rather than flat Grade 1 compliance, there is no UL file number specific to this device, and no fire rating appears anywhere. What is verified is that nobody claims one \u2014 which is not the same as CRL telling you it is unrated. On a rated opening, spec a device whose label you can read with your own eyes.",commonServicingIssues:["Bottom bolt won't reach the threshold and no amount of adjusting fixes it \u2014 you started at the wrong end. Top and bottom move in tandem; CRL's rule is all the magic happens at the top. Set the top rod first, verify it, then set the bottom.","Device was 'working' and now nothing latches at the top \u2014 check for a missing or substituted top strike. The HUMP strike plate is mandatory; its hump pushes the rod down 3/32\" as the door passes, which is what lifts the bottom rod clear of the threshold. A flat strike makes correct adjustment nearly impossible.","Top bolt won't stay captured by the top guide after release \u2014 the troubleshooting guide's fix is to rotate it clockwise in half-turn increments until it is captured. Do not jump to replacing parts.","Device binds or won't operate and the rods look fine \u2014 CRL's enumerated causes: door out of plumb or square; screws protruding from weatherstripping, astragals, or exterior trim; exterior cylinder binding against the interior case; weatherstripping preventing full close. Diagnostic is to pull the top rod guide and watch rod movement while operating.","Cylinder installed and the device stopped working \u2014 the cylinder must be no more than 1\" long or it binds the interior case. Verify length before hunting through the rod assembly.","Ordered a DL911 for a 1285 \u2014 wrong device. DL911 is the rim cylinder for the 1295. The 1285 takes a 1\" MORTISE cylinder (DL2170) into pad 30821J for keyed entry, and a special-order 1-1/8\" mortise cylinder if it has the cylinder-dogging option.","Ordered the wrong hand \u2014 the 1285 actuating lift is 302669 LHRB / 302675 RHRB and is not field reversible. Read the number off the old part. Do not assume from the 1295 or the 1085, both of which are non-handed.","Turning the adjuster the wrong way \u2014 CRL's own two documents disagree on the top rod's direction. Bottom lengthens clockwise; the troubleshooting guide has the top as clockwise to shorten. Watch the bolt, don't count turns.","Top bolt still won't retract after loosening the cylinder, clearing obstructions, and lubricating the case with white lithium grease \u2014 per CRL that is probably a spring issue and the rod and case assembly may need replacement. Price it as a parts job, not an adjustment.","Electrified unit tests fine on the bench and fails on the door \u2014 the electronic time delay must expire before the door is closed. Closing early makes the device appear correctly set when it is not.","Tall door and the rods are short \u2014 the standard rod and case (302452J) fits to 84\". Use top rod extension 301347 for 12\" or 301348 for 24\" to reach nominal 8/0 and 9/0."],sources:["https://azure.crlaurence.com/techdocs/PDF/11M0248-1285_Jackson_CVR_PANIC_DEVICE.pdf","https://azure.crlaurence.com/adv/jackson/docs/Trouble-Shooting-Guide-Panics.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-1285-CVR-Cylinder-Dogging-Instructions.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Jackson-Section-08-7100-12-Series-Push-Pad-Exit-Device-Specifications.docx","https://gsdcinc.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Jackson-1285-Parts-Breakdown.pdf","http://webcache.crlaurence.com/jackson/PUBLISHEDPARTSLIST.pdf","https://mrlock.com/brand-index/jackson/exit-devices-by-crl-jackson/1285-device/1285-parts/","https://www.crlaurence.com/products/brands/listing/D12AC_DW","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/jackson-documentation/"],photos:[{diagram:"jackson-1285-rod-adjust",caption:"Rod adjustment order: top rod against the hump strike first, then the bottom to 1/16\" above the threshold strike."}]},{id:"adams-rite-4710",name:"Adams Rite 4710 Deadlatch",category:"Storefront",whatItIs:"The 4710 is Adams Rite's heavy-duty armored-faceplate deadlatch for narrow-stile aluminum glass doors \u2014 the workhorse latch you find on half the storefront entrances in America. It drops a spring-loaded, self-deadlocking latchbolt into the strike every time the door closes, and it's operated by any standard mortise cylinder fitted with an Adams Rite MS cam. It shares the same case family and function as the OEM-grade 4510, but the 4710 is the heavy-duty version carrying a full-length 1\" x 6-7/8\" armored faceplate over the case \u2014 that's what you spec where you want extra abuse resistance and a clean look on a high-traffic commercial door.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA Type E8321 (per Adams Rite catalog MA-117). Note: this is a deadlatch type designation, NOT a Grade 1 deadbolt rating \u2014 Adams Rite states in its own literature the 4710 does not provide the maximum security of the MS deadbolt","Cylinder backsets: 7/8\" (22.2mm), 31/32\" (24.6mm), 1-1/8\" (28.6mm), or 1-1/2\" (38.1mm) ONLY \u2014 those four, nothing in between","Latchbolt: 1/2\" x 1\" x 1/2\" throw, all metal with hardened steel internal pins; handing easily reversible without special tools","Auxiliary (deadlocking) bolt: all metal \u2014 deadlocks the latchbolt to defeat loiding / case-knife (credit-card) attack when the door is closed against the strike","Cylinder: any standard 1\" length, 1-5/32\" diameter mortise cylinder fitted with the Adams Rite special MS cam (dimensioned per spec \u2014 not a standard cloverleaf or straight cam)","Case: steel, corrosion-resistant plating, 1\" x 5-13/16\" x depth. Case depth by backset: 7/8\"=1.51\", 31/32\"=1.60\", 1-1/8\"=1.76\", 1-1/2\"=2.14\"","Armored faceplate: 1\" x 6-7/8\" (25.4mm x 174.6mm). Face shapes by last catalog digit: 4710 Flat, 4711 Radius, 4711W Radius w/ weatherstrip, 4712(RH) Right Bevel, 4712(LH) Left Bevel","Standard strike furnished: 4502-01 flat strike (dash 2 = 2.63\" size) for flush or near-flush closing; longer 4501 strike (dash 1 = 4.63\") to cover the slot of an MS deadbolt; radius strike for the opposite stile on paired doors and long-lip strikes for centerhung doors optional","Trim/faceplate finishes: 628 clear anodized, 313 dark bronze anodized, 335 black anodized","Interchangeable without stile modification with any MS1850A or MS1850S deadlock of the same backset and faceplate shape","Function (bolt holdback): a reverse turn of the key while the bolt is held fully retracted retains it for free two-way traffic \u2014 free entrance at some hours, exit-only at others"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight \u2014 the 4710 is a latch, not a deadbolt, no matter what the customer calls it. It self-locks when the door pulls closed, but anybody inside just works the handle or paddle and walks out. That's your egress story and it's why this thing lives on the market. Nine times out of ten when somebody wants \"more security\" on a 4710 door, what they actually need is a companion MS1850S deadbolt in a second prep above it, or a paddle-plus-deadbolt setup \u2014 don't oversell the latch as something it isn't. Adams Rite says it flat out in their own catalog: the 4710 does NOT provide the maximum security of the MS lock. And while you're at it, get the classification right \u2014 the 4710 is cataloged as ANSI/BHMA Type E8321, which is a deadlatch type designation. Grade 1 is a deadbolt rating that belongs to the MS1850/MS1890 line, so don't quote \"Grade 1\" on this latch to a spec-writer who knows better.\n\nBackset is the number that will bite you. This lock comes in 7/8\", 31/32\", 1-1/8\", and 1-1/2\" ONLY \u2014 those are the four, nothing in between. Measure from the centerline of the stile nose to the cylinder centerline. The two you'll pull off the truck most are 31/32\" (the classic narrow-stile storefront) and 1-1/8\". Show up with a 31/32\" against a 1-1/8\" prep and the cylinder won't line up \u2014 you're driving back to the shop. When in doubt, pull the old lock and measure the existing hole, or just match the part you're replacing. The 4710 drops straight into an MS1850A/1850S prep of the same backset and faceplate shape \u2014 that interchangeability is the whole beauty of the Adams Rite line.\n\nThe cam is the other classic screw-up. This takes a standard 1-5/32\" diameter mortise cylinder, 1\" length, but it MUST wear the Adams Rite MS cam \u2014 not the Yale standard cam, not a cloverleaf, not a straight cam. Throw a hardware-store mortise cylinder in there with the wrong cam and the key turns but never picks up the latch tailpiece \u2014 nothing retracts. Buy the cylinder with the MS cam already on it, or keep a bag of MS cams and swap it. Adams Rite spells out the cam dimension in the catalog and says cylinders with cams can be supplied. Set your cylinder depth so the cam clears \u2014 mic it, don't eyeball it; the tolerance is there for a reason. And don't forget the trim ring / spacer collar so you're not leaving threads exposed or bottoming the cylinder into the cam.\n\nHanding is reversible \u2014 pull the latchbolt, flip it, reinstall for the opposite bevel \u2014 so if you grabbed the wrong hand you can usually save the trip right there in the field. Five-minute fix, not a re-order. When you order new, though, you still spec face shape, backset, hand, finish, and strike, and the catalog number reads shape-backset-strike-finish; Adams Rite's own example is 4711-36-201-628. Flat is 4710, radius is 4711 (4711W with weatherstrip), bevel is 4712 RH or LH.\n\nRepair vs. replace: the case is cheap enough and these get beat to death on high-traffic doors, so I rarely rebuild one in the field. If the latch is dragging, sticking, or the aux bolt won't deadlock, price a whole new case against your labor \u2014 usually it's a wash, and you're better off dropping in a fresh 4710 and reusing the customer's existing cylinder and MS cam. Keep 31/32\" and 1-1/8\" flat and radius cases stocked with 4502-01 strikes and you'll cover most storefront service calls off the truck. On paired doors, remember the inactive/opposite stile wants the radius strike, and if you're replacing an MS deadbolt you may need the longer 4501 strike to cover the old slot cutout so you're not staring at raw aluminum.\n\nOne code note for storefront work: the 4710 latch itself is free-egress by handle, but watch the trim and what the AHJ wants. On a required egress door you cannot add anything that needs a key or special knowledge to get OUT \u2014 no double-cylinder deadbolt above it on an occupied-space egress door unless the occupancy and local code specifically allow it. And panic-hardware territory is Von Duprin, not this \u2014 if the door needs a panic device by occupant load, the 4710 isn't your answer, the exit device is.",commonServicingIssues:["Key turns but latch won't retract \u2014 almost always the wrong cylinder cam. A standard mortise cylinder went in without the Adams Rite MS cam, so the tailpiece never engages the latch. Fix: swap to an MS cam or install a cylinder built with one, and verify cylinder depth so the cam picks up the latch.","Door won't stay latched / rattles open \u2014 the aux (deadlocking) bolt is dropping into the strike cutout instead of riding the strike face, so the latch never deadlocks and can be pushed back. Cause is a misaligned or wrong strike, or door sag. Fix: shim/realign the strike so the small auxiliary plunger stays depressed against the strike face while the main latchbolt drops in the pocket.","Latch can be 'loided' / carded open \u2014 the aux bolt isn't depressing (worn, gummed with dirt, or bent) so it isn't deadlocking the latchbolt. Fix: clean and free the aux plunger, or replace the case; that plunger is the whole anti-card feature.","Latch sticks retracted or drags \u2014 spring fatigue or grit in the case on a high-cycle door. Fix: lubricate with a dry PTFE/Teflon lube (not oil, which attracts grit); if the return spring is weak, replace the case \u2014 not worth rebuilding.","Cylinder spins / pulled \u2014 the set screw in the faceplate backed out or the cylinder wasn't screwed to depth. Fix: reset cylinder to correct depth for cam pickup, then re-torque the faceplate set screw into the cylinder groove.","Wrong hand installed on a service swap \u2014 bevel faces the wrong way and won't latch. Fix: it's reversible; pull the latchbolt, rotate it, reinstall for the opposite hand \u2014 no new part needed.","Doesn't fit the prep on a replacement \u2014 showed up with the wrong backset (31/32 vs 1-1/8) or wrong faceplate shape. Fix: measure backset at the stile-nose centerline and match faceplate shape; the 4710 drops into an MS1850A/1850S prep only if backset AND shape both match.","Won't hold in the free-swing/holdback position \u2014 user doesn't know the feature or the holdback is worn. A reverse turn of the key while the bolt is held fully retracted is what retains it for two-way traffic; if it won't hold, the case is worn \u2014 replace."],schematic:"ar-4710-deadlatch",sources:["https://www.allmar.com/products/data/uploads/Adams_Rite_4710_catalogue.pdf","https://cdn.thomasnet.com/ccp/00413393/112377.pdf","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches/4510-standard-duty-deadlatch","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/tech-info/installation-instructions-templates/deadlatches/"],relatedGuides:["adams-rite-sagging-doors"],photo:"photo-adams-rite-4710"},{id:"adams-rite-ms1850",name:"Adams Rite MS1850S Deadlock",category:"Storefront",whatItIs:"The MS1850S is Adams Rite's maximum-security MS deadlock for single-leaf narrow-stile aluminum storefront and entrance doors \u2014 the one with the huge laminated stainless bolt, nearly three inches long, driven by a simple counterbalanced pivot mechanism. You see it in glass storefronts, mall shops, and commercial aluminum entrances, usually keyed one side with a thumbturn on the other, or double-cylinder where code allows. It matters because it drops straight into the standard Adams Rite MS prep \u2014 a 6-7/8\" case cutout with a 1-1/4\" cylinder hole \u2014 the same family of prep the 4710/4900 deadlatches use, so it's the go-to hardened-bolt upgrade for aluminum openings across the industry.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 (aluminum door), BHMA classification E8211 as shown on the Adams Rite spec label \u2014 that E8211 is the BHMA A156.5-style type/grade code, NOT a separate UL forced-entry listing; the sheet cites no UL number","Bolt (MS1850S straight): 5/8\" x 1-3/8\" x 2-7/8\" laminated stainless, 1-3/8\" (34.9mm) nominal throw; the stile-prep drawing dimensions the regular bolt projection at 1.41\" (35.81mm)","Bolt construction: eight-ply laminated stainless steel with an alumina-ceramic center ply that defeats hacksaw attack, including rod-type 'super' hacksaws","Hookbolt variant (MS1850S-050 / X5X): 13/16\" (20.6mm) throw, hook shape \u2014 for jambs too narrow for the long bolt, sliding doors, or pairs","Short-throw variant (MS1850S-020 / X2X): 13/16\" (20.6mm) throw, no hook (7/8\" backset not offered on the short-bolt X2X)","Backsets: 7/8\", 31/32\", 1-1/8\", or 1-1/2\" only, measured at the centerline of the stile nose","Case: steel with corrosion-resistant plating, 1\" x 6\" x depth; depth 'A' varies by backset (7/8\"->1-9/16\", 31/32\"->1-5/8\"/1.63\", 1-1/8\"->1-25/32\"/1.78\", 1-1/2\"->2-1/4\")","Cylinder: any standard 1\" length, 1-5/32\" (29.37mm) diameter mortise cylinder with MS-dimensioned cam \u2014 cam 'Dim A' = .800\" +/- .005\" (20.32mm); e.g. Adams Rite 4036 mortise cylinder, 4066 thumbturn, or 4550 deadlock lever","Operation: 360-degree turn of key or thumbturn throws/retracts the counterbalanced bolt; key removable only in a positively locked or unlocked position","Faceplate: 1\" x 6-7/8\"; flat (0S/MS1850S), radius (1S/MS1851S), radius w/ weatherstrip (1SW/MS1851SW), bevel LH (2S/MS1852S-X15) or RH (MS1852S-X16), plus armored faceplate option","Finishes: 313 Dark Bronze anodized, 335 Black anodized, 628 Clear anodized","Mounting: two #10 (M5) flat-head machine screws plus two tube spacers cut to stile (L = C - .25\"); Adams Rite mounting-tab option P/N 91-0965 for retrofits","Door prep (MS prep): 6-7/8\" case cutout with 1-1/4\" cylinder hole, radius corners; strike cutout cut in the metal jamb","MS1850SN = ANSI-size sibling for hollow-metal or wood doors: 1-1/4\" x 8\" faceplate, 6\" case (MS1850SN-410 straight / -450 hookbolt)","Recommended strikes: 4000, 4001, MS4002 (sold separately); Adams Rite recommends a latch guard on 1850 models for higher security","Shipping weight approx. 1-1/2 lbs; individually boxed with mounting machine screws (cylinder/thumbturn separate)"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight \u2014 the family. Base MS1850S ships with the long straight bolt; that's your default for a single swinging aluminum door, and it's what made this lock the standard of the narrow-stile industry. The hookbolt isn't a separate model, it's the -050 (X5X) suffix, and you spec it when the jamb is too narrow to swallow the long bolt, or when the bolt has to grab and pull rather than just cross a gap \u2014 sliding doors, and pairs of doors without a fixed mullion. On a normal swinging door with a decent jamb, the straight bolt is the stronger, simpler answer \u2014 don't put a hookbolt where you don't need one. The short-throw -020 (X2X) is the straight bolt cut down to a 13/16\" throw for narrow jambs that can't take the full 1-3/8\". Nine times out of ten a \"which one\" question is really a backset and jamb-gap question, not a bolt-shape question.\n\nBackset is where jobs go sideways, so nail it before you order. The MS1850 comes in 7/8\", 31/32\", 1-1/8\", and 1-1/2\" \u2014 and it's measured to the centerline of the stile nose, not the face of the faceplate. On aluminum storefront 31/32\" is the old-school standard you'll find on a lot of pre-2000 doors, and 1-1/8\" is the common modern default \u2014 but don't assume, measure. If you order the wrong backset the cylinder simply won't line up with the existing prep hole and you're eating the part; there's no field-adjusting the case backset. When you're not sure, pull the old lock and measure to the existing cylinder-hole centerline, then match it. This lock lands in the standard Adams Rite MS prep \u2014 a 6-7/8\" case cutout with a 1-1/4\" cylinder hole \u2014 which is exactly why it's the go-to hardened-bolt upgrade on aluminum; you're usually swapping guts, not cutting new metal.\n\nCylinders are the other trap. This is an MS cam, not a standard mortise cam \u2014 it wants a 1\", 1-5/32\" (29.37mm) diameter mortise cylinder with the MS-dimensioned cam, Dim A right at .800\". Any decent cylinder house (Schlage, Corbin, Sargent, GMS, plus Adams Rite's own 4036) can supply an MS cam, so you can key it into the customer's existing system \u2014 but if you grab a regular mortise cylinder off the shelf the cam geometry is wrong and it won't throw the bolt. Then comes trim-ring length, which apprentices forget and then wonder why the cylinder sticks out or bottoms out. Adams Rite's own formula is TR = A + .125\" - B, and the install sheet gives you a clean table: a 1\" cylinder in a 1-3/4\" door wants a 1/4\" ring, a 1-1/8\" cylinder in that same door wants 3/8\", step it out from there for thicker doors. Measure the door thickness, count the pins, and order the ring with the cylinder \u2014 not after.\n\nRepair vs. replace: the MS1850 body is stout and the bolt is essentially un-defeatable by hand tools, so most of your service is around it, not in it \u2014 worn cam, wrong-length cylinder, a stripped or undersized strike, or a door that's dropped so the bolt no longer lands in the strike slot. Fix those. If the pivot mechanism itself is gummed or the case is bent from a pry attempt, just replace the whole lock; the body isn't worth tearing down at shop labor rates. Price-wise these run mid-tier for the lock itself, but remember the true job cost is lock + MS-cam cylinder + correct trim ring + strike, and on aluminum the strike slot is usually the weak point. Adams Rite explicitly recommends a latch guard on 1850 models for higher security, and their box/armored strikes are the 4000, 4001, and MS4002. Sell the strike \u2014 a maximum-security bolt into a flimsy jamb slot is theater.\n\nCode and liability \u2014 this is the one that gets people sued. A double-cylinder MS1850 (key both sides) on a required means-of-egress door is a code problem: on egress you generally need to get out without a key, tool, or special knowledge. Retail storefronts sometimes ride an occupancy/hardware exception during business hours, but the second that becomes the only exit for an occupied space you want a thumbturn (the 4066) on the inside or you're on the hook. If they insist on double-cylinder for glass-adjacent break-in resistance, get it in writing and point them at their local AHJ and the IBC / NFPA 101 egress language \u2014 the install sheet itself says to install per all applicable building and life-safety codes. And the Schoolhouse function (SCH1850S) \u2014 inside is \"unlock only,\" handed LH/RH, run with a 4066 turn or 4550 deadlock lever \u2014 exists precisely for classroom/egress situations; know it's out there when someone asks for \"locks that can't trap people inside.\"",commonServicingIssues:["Key won't come out / turns but won't release \u2014 the MS bolt only lets the key withdraw in a positively locked or unlocked position; the user stopped mid-throw. Complete the 360-degree turn until it seats, then the key pulls. If it still hangs, the cam is worn or it's a non-MS cam cylinder \u2014 swap to a proper MS-cam cylinder.","Bolt won't throw or feels gritty \u2014 nine times out of ten it's the wrong cylinder cam (standard mortise cam instead of an MS cam, Dim A off .800\"), not the lock body. Verify the cam before you condemn the lock. Second cause is a dropped/sagging door binding the bolt against the strike slot edge.","Cylinder sticks out too far or sits too deep / spins \u2014 wrong trim-ring length for the door thickness and cylinder length. Recalculate TR = A + .125\" - B (or use the install table) and fit the correct ring; a floppy cylinder can also mean a loose cylinder set screw.","Bolt throws but door still rattles / can be pried \u2014 undersized or worn strike, not the lock. Upgrade to a box/armored strike (4000, 4001, MS4002) and add the recommended latch guard; on aluminum the strike slot is usually the weak point.","Bolt no longer lands in the strike \u2014 door has settled or the pivots/closer wore, dropping the leaf. Re-shim or adjust the pivots and, if needed, re-cut or relocate the strike slot; don't file the bolt.","Wrong backset ordered \u2014 cylinder hole doesn't line up with the existing MS prep. Confirm backset (7/8, 31/32, 1-1/8, 1-1/2) by measuring to the existing cylinder-hole centerline before ordering; there's no field-adjusting the case backset.","Loose or spinning lock body in the stile \u2014 the two #10 (M5) machine screws backed out or the tube spacers are the wrong length. Reset with correct-length spacers (L = C - .25\") or use the 91-0965 mounting tabs on a retrofit.","Double-cylinder on an egress door flagged by an inspector \u2014 swap the interior to a 4066 thumbturn (or spec a Schoolhouse SCH1850S) to meet key-free egress; get owner sign-off if they refuse."],sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.mfsales.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/ms1850.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Adams-Rite-MS1850S_MS1850A_MS1950-Deadlocks-Installation-Instructions-1.pdf","https://www.mfsales.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/inst-ms1850.pdf","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/MS1850SN_series"],photo:"photo-adams-rite-ms1850",relatedGuides:["adams-rite-sagging-doors"],schematic:"schematic-adams-rite-ms1850"},{id:"schlage-nd",name:"Schlage ND-Series Cylindrical Lever",category:"Cylindrical locks",whatItIs:"Schlage's heavy-duty Grade 1 bored (cylindrical) lever lock \u2014 the workhorse you see on the vast majority of commercial doors: schools, hospitals, offices, government, retail back-of-house. It's Schlage's strongest, best-selling cylindrical line and the direct cross to Sargent 10-Line, Falcon T, Corbin Russwin CL3300, and Yale 5400LN. The chassis carries the lock; the trim (lever + rose) is what you touch, and the function number (ND10, ND53, ND80, etc.) defines who gets in and how. When someone says \"put a good storeroom lock on it,\" nine times out of ten they mean an ND80.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.2, Series 4000, Grade 1 certified \u2014 and it overshoots: 3,100 in-lb abusive lever torque (over 690 lbs on the end of a 4-1/2\" lever, 2.6x BHMA), 1,600 lbs offset lever pull (8x), 100 vertical impacts with no failure (20x), latch retraction under 200-lb preload (4x), and near-zero droop/wobble after 16M cycles (16x)","Backset: 2-3/4\" (70 mm) standard; optional 2-3/8\", 3-3/4\", and 5\" (60/92/127 mm). The 5\" runs off backset extension link 43-005 (2-3/4\" latch only, needs reinforcement)","Door thickness: standard 1-5/8\" to 2-1/8\" (41\u201354 mm); optional 1-3/8\" (35 mm) up to 6\" with EE/EO/EI/ED spacer configs. For 1-3/8\" you need the 40-152 adapter plus chassis spacer XN12-012 (2 required)","Latch: standard 1/2\" (12 mm) throw stainless spring latch or deadlatch; optional 3/4\" (19 mm) throw anti-friction deadlatch (required for the pair-of-doors fire ratings). Default latch part 13-247, 2-3/4\" backset, 1-1/8\" x 2-1/4\" square-corner faceplate","Strike: standard ANSI curved-lip 1-1/4\" x 4-7/8\" (32 x 124 mm) with 1-3/16\" (30 mm) lip, no box (part 10-025); optional T-strike, alternative lip lengths, dust box","Cylinder: default 6-pin solid brass conventional (P6), patented Everest 29 S123 keyway, keyed different, two nickel-silver keys. Master-keyed conventional/FSIC default to Everest 29 S145; SFIC master default R125. Order code 'P' ships a 6-pin cylinder keyed 5-pin","Cylinder formats: conventional 6-pin (P6) and Everest SL 7-pin (Z); FSIC (R 6-pin, T construction core, M Everest SL 7-pin); SFIC Best-style (G 7-pin, plus H/BDC construction, B = less SFIC housing). Less-cylinder options made to fit Best/Corbin Russwin/Medeco/Sargent/Yale \u2014 and Primus/UL 437 high-security options","Chassis: modular zinc + steel, plated for corrosion protection; cast-steel spindle to fight droop/wobble; non-handed","Nine ADA lever designs: Athens (ATH), Boardwalk (BRK), Broadway (BRW), Latitude (LAT), Longitude (LON), Omega (OME), Rhodes (RHO), Sparta (SPA), Tubular (TLR), pressure-cast zinc; tactile (abrasive) versions available (8AT/8BK/8BY/8LT/8LN/8RO/8SP/8TR). Rhodes, Sparta, Tubular, Omega, Longitude, and Boardwalk have a 1/2\" (13 mm) or less return, so they meet CA fire code","Finishes (9): 605 bright brass, 606 satin brass, 612 satin bronze, 613 oil-rubbed bronze, 619 satin nickel, 622 matte black, 625 bright chrome, 626 satin chrome, 643e aged bronze; 626AM antimicrobial option; HSLR ligature-resistant trim in 630 satin stainless only","Functions: 27 mechanical plus 8 Vandlgard locked-lever functions, in 9 lever designs; status-indicator trim on 8 functions, HSLR trim on 14","Electrified: three functions \u2014 ND12EL/EU (exit), ND80EL/EU (storeroom), ND96EL/EU (Vandlgard storeroom). Motor-driven (not solenoid), auto-detects 12 or 24 VDC, 0.24 A peak / 0.01 A holding; EL (fail safe) vs EU (fail secure) is set by a field-selectable switch on the chassis. RX (request-to-exit) on ND10/12/80/81/96, field-add kit N123-062 (RX harness N523-194, rated 2 A @ 30 VDC)","UL 10C / CAN-ULC-S104: all locks 3-hour A-label single door 4'0\" x 10'0\"; pair doors 3-hour 8'0\" x 8'0\" and 90-minute 8'0\" x 10'0\", both with the 3/4\" latch. 10-year mechanical / 3-year electro-mechanical warranty. BAA compliant; wired electrified meets A156.25 (indoor)","ND80PD (F86 storeroom) is the mortise-to-cylindrical conversion workhorse: outside lever always rigid, key retracts the latch, inside lever always free (locks behind you). Ships with the 2-3/4\" backset latch and ASA strike; conventional 6-pin C keyway (SC1), keyed different, 1-3/4\" door standard.","PD vs BD suffix = cylinder prep: PD is a permanent conventional cylinder; BD is prepped for a 7-pin SFIC and ships LESS CORE. Spec ND80PD for a site-keyed job; spec ND80BD for national-account doors where the client supplies their own interchangeable core."],proAdvice:"Learn the function chart cold, because that's where the money and the callbacks live. The big ones you'll spec and stock: ND10 passage (F75, no lock, both levers always free), ND25 exit (blank plate outside, lever inside \u2014 egress only), ND40 privacy (F76, bath/bedroom push-button), ND50 office/entrance (F82, key outside plus inside push-button that holds it locked), ND53 entrance (F109, key outside plus push/turn button \u2014 warn customers the push-TURN mode can lock them out if they egress without releasing it), ND70 classroom (F84, key outside locks/unlocks the outside lever, inside always free), and ND80 storeroom (F86 \u2014 outside lever ALWAYS fixed, key retracts latch only, inside always free for egress). The ND80 is the default heavy-security door: you never leave it unlocked, you badge or key in, and there's no way to accidentally prop it unlocked because the key only pulls the latch, it doesn't change the lever state. ND81 is the accessible-storeroom variant \u2014 same F86 storeroom behavior, outside lever always fixed, key retracts the latch, but it's built and rated as the ADA/accessible version so the user isn't fighting a stiff key-and-lever motion at once. Know the difference before you order, because ND80 and ND81 look identical in the box.\n\nOn egress and life safety: every mechanical ND function keeps the inside lever free for immediate egress \u2014 that's by design and it's why these pass code on the vast majority of occupancies without a separate exit device. The one that bites people is the double-cylinder stuff (ND82 institution, ND72 communicating, ND70x80). Schlage themselves print the caution: key-both-sides on any egress door in an occupied structure is a life-safety hazard and generally a code violation. Don't let a customer talk you into a double-cylinder on a means-of-egress door to stop break-outs through glass \u2014 that's how people die in a fire and how you end up on the wrong end of a liability claim. Use laminated glass or a listed exit device instead.\n\nOrdering is a period-delimited string and it will humble you if you're sloppy: SCH.ND80.P6.RHO.626.RHO... brand (only needed on a 'less' order), series+function, cylinder type, outside style + finish, then inside style only if it differs. The cylinder attribute matters most for keying \u2014 P6 is the standard 6-pin conventional Everest 29, but a huge share of institutional jobs run SFIC so the facility can rekey with a control key and never pull the lock. Here's the trap: for SFIC you spec 'G' (7-pin SFIC housing) \u2014 'B' means LESS SFIC (no core at all, you're dropping the customer's Best-style cores in). If you're matching a full-size IC Schlage system, that's FSIC ('R'). Get the format wrong and you've bought a $400+ lock that won't take their cores. Also note the code 'P' (not P6) ships a 6-pin cylinder keyed to 5 pins \u2014 catches people who assume it's just \"conventional.\"\n\nRepair vs. replace and pricing: an ND80 with Rhodes lever in 626 runs roughly $400\u2013650 list depending on cylinder format and finish; street/contractor is meaningfully less. Because it's modular, you rarely scrap the whole lock. A worn or vandalized lever is a trim swap \u2014 you don't touch the chassis. A bad cylinder is a cylinder swap (or just a rekey). A latch that won't deadlatch is a $20\u201340 latch. So when a facilities manager wants to \"replace the whole lock\" because a lever is loose, stop them \u2014 nine times out of ten it's a loose lever catch, an adjustment-plate issue, or a set screw, not a dead chassis. Keep the lever removal (trim) tool 24002693 and the pin wrench N523-108 on the truck; that's how you pull trim and cores without marring anything. Full chassis replacement is warranted when the cast-steel spindle or internal slide is actually damaged \u2014 rare, given the abuse numbers \u2014 or when you're changing function and the chassis won't convert. Brand gotcha: the ND80 you pull off an old job may be marked \"ND80PD\" \u2014 PD was the old pin-tumbler suffix; the current catalog calls the standard cylinder P6. Same lock family, just don't let the nomenclature spook you.\n\nOn a mortise-to-cylindrical conversion the ND80PD is almost always the lock you're hanging: Grade 1 storeroom function, latch and ASA strike in the box, and it drops onto a standard 2-1/8\" cross-bore over a 2-3/4\" backset. The one order-time fork that matters is PD versus BD. Spec the ND80PD (conventional C keyway, SC1) when you're keying it to a site key on the truck; spec the ND80BD (SFIC-prep, less core) when it's a national-account door and the client hands you their own 7-pin interchangeable core. Get that wrong and you either can't key it on site or you've bought a core the client didn't want.",commonServicingIssues:["Outside lever loose / wobbles on a storeroom door \u2014 usually a loose lever catch or the trim backed off the adjustment plate (N523-054), NOT a dead chassis. Pull the lever with tool 24002693, reseat on the adjustment plate, check the set screw. Only if the cast-steel spindle is actually damaged do you replace the chassis.","Key turns but latch won't retract (ND80) \u2014 the keycam/tailpiece isn't driving the retractor, or the cylinder is loose in the housing. The keycam assembly is function-specific (N123-008, -010, -011, -045, etc.; electrified N223-066) \u2014 verify the correct one is seated and the cylinder cap is tight before condemning the cylinder.","Door won't stay latched / latch won't deadlatch \u2014 the deadlatch plunger is falling into the strike hole because the strike is misaligned; the deadlatch must land on the strike lip, not in the cutout. Realign strike or add a shim; a floating deadlatch = the door is defeatable with a shim/card.","'Locked out' on an ND53 \u2014 customer egressed on the push/TURN button without releasing it, so the button stayed locked. Not a lock fault; teach them to return the button to start position. Consider downgrading to ND50 (push-only) if it keeps happening.","Electrified ND80EL/EU dead or wrong fail mode \u2014 check the chassis-mounted EL/EU selector switch first (field-selectable) and confirm the 12/24V supply; it auto-detects voltage but a marginal PSU or bad transition-hinge/door-loop wiring will drop it. Peak draw is only 0.24 A, holding 0.01 A, so a hot lever or dropout usually points at wiring, not the motor. Fail-safe (EL) unlocks on power loss for egress on stair/fire doors; fail-secure (EU) stays locked \u2014 verify the code requirement for that opening before you set the switch.","Lever droops over time on an old unit \u2014 genuine ND droop is rare (near-zero after 16M cycles on a cast-steel spindle), so a drooping lever is almost always a knock-off or a worn pre-ND A/D-series lock; confirm you're actually working on an ND before chasing internal parts.","Won't fit a 1-3/8\" door \u2014 standard ND wants 1-5/8\" minimum; you need the 40-152 door adapter plus chassis spacer XN12-012 (2 required), or the door prep is wrong. Indicator trims use dedicated spacer kits (48029970/71/72) sized by door thickness.","Cylinder won't take the customer's cores \u2014 you ordered the wrong format. Conventional (P6) won't accept IC cores; match SFIC ('G' 7-pin, or 'B' less-core to drop in their Best-style cores) or FSIC ('R') to their existing system, or retrofit the correct housing rather than the whole lock.","Converting a dead import/mortise door and unsure PD vs BD \u2014 read the work order for keying. Site key = ND80PD conventional cylinder; client-supplied IC core = ND80BD SFIC-prep less core. Ordering the wrong one is a second trip."],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_ND_Series_Data_Sheet_106486.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_ND_Series_Electrified_Data_Sheet_104999.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_ND_Series_Service_Manual_(locks_manufactured_after_1.31.17)_111524.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/mechanical-locks/nd-series-grade-1-cylindrical-locks.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/electrified-locks/nd-series-electrified-cylindrical-locks.html"],photo:"photo-schlage-nd",schematic:"schematic-schlage-nd",relatedGuides:["t-keyways-schlage-kwikset"]},{id:"falcon-t",name:"Falcon T-Series Cylindrical Lever",category:"Cylindrical locks",whatItIs:"The Falcon T-Series is a Grade 1, ANSI/BHMA A156.2 Series 4000 extra-heavy-duty cylindrical (bored) lever lock built by Falcon, an Allegion brand sold right alongside Schlage. It's the value-priced Grade 1 you spec when the job wants ND-level durability without the ND price tag, and Falcon deliberately built it to drop into competitors' systems \u2014 it takes most makers' conventional cylinders, most small-format IC cores, and Schlage full-size IC cores. You see it constantly in multi-family, retail, offices, schools, and government/military corridors where the budget is tight but the abuse level is high. What sets it apart mechanically is a stainless steel compression spring behind each rose (instead of the usual torsion spring that snaps at the bend) plus Pressure Release lever rotation standard, so an over-torqued lever slips instead of shearing the guts out of the lock.",specs:["Certification: ANSI/BHMA A156.2, Series 4000, Grade 1; UL/cUL listed for 3-hour A-label fire door","Backset: 2-3/4\" standard; 2-3/8\" and 5\" optional (per price book)","Door thickness: 1-3/4\" to 2-1/4\" standard, non-handed chassis; thin-door adapter kit A30775-000-00 (with spacers) covers 1-3/8\" to 1-3/4\"","Faceplate/latch: 1-1/8\" x 2-1/4\" square-corner faceplate, 1\" housing diameter, 1/2\" throw; deadlatch part 23981145, non-deadlocking springlatch part 23981137","Strike: 4-7/8\" x 1-1/4\" ANSI square-corner curved-lip strike to ANSI A115.2 frame prep; dust box optional","Cylinder: conventional drilled 6-pin, pinned to 5 standard (6th chamber loadable at no cost); housings and plugs machined from solid brass; Schlage C available (CP6), plus SFIC (6- or 7-pin) and Schlage FSIC","Keyway: 'G' keyway standard on conventional cylinders, 'A' keyway standard on SFIC; also Falcon, Schlage, and many Best/Arrow keyways","Keys: two nickel-silver keys furnished standard","Inside lever secured by a hex/Allen set screw (part 030831-004-30) for anti-vandal/anti-tamper; hex wrench included","Rose: heavy wrought brass or bronze installed over a pressure-cast (case zinc) rose insert, through-bolted through the door","Lever styles (7): Avalon (AVA), Boardwalk (BRK), Broadway (BRW), Dane (DAN), Latitude (LAT), Longitude (LON), Quantum (QUA); abrasive coating and lead lining available on all levers","Finishes (8): 605/US3 bright brass, 606/US4 satin brass, 613/US10B oil-rubbed bronze, 619/US15 satin nickel, 622/US19 matte black, 625/US26 bright chrome, 626/US26D satin chrome, 643e/US11 aged bronze","Functions: T101 passage/closet (F75), T291 privacy hospital, T301 bath/bedroom privacy (F76), T351 closet, T381 classroom security (F88), T411 asylum (F87), T501 entry (F82), T511 entry/office (F109, push/turn button), T521 office (F81), T561 classroom (F84), T571 dormitory/corridor (F90), T581 storeroom (F86), T851 electrified storeroom fail-safe, T881 electrified storeroom fail-secure, T12 single dummy trim","Electrified T851 (fail-safe) / T881 (fail-secure): 24VDC standard, 12VDC option; when the outer lever is locked, key in the outside cylinder retracts the latch, inside lever always free"],proAdvice:"Think of the T-Series as Falcon's answer to the Schlage ND, and spec it the same way you'd spec an ND \u2014 pick the function by the ANSI F-number, not the marketing name. The cheat sheet that keeps you out of trouble: T561 = classroom (F84, key locks/unlocks the outside lever, inside always free), T581 = storeroom (F86, outside lever always fixed/locked, key entry only, inside always free), T511 = entry/office (F109, push/turn button), T101 = passage. Nine times out of ten a facilities guy calls a corridor lock a \"classroom lock\" when what he actually wants is a storeroom function so the door is always secure and nobody can leave it unlocked. Ask him one question: does the door ever need to be left unlocked from the outside without a key? If no, sell him a T581, not a T561. And keep the code instinct front of mind \u2014 on every one of these the inside lever is always free for immediate egress, so a single-cylinder T-series is code-legal on an egress door. The T411 asylum function and any double-cylinder arrangement is a life-safety no-go on an egress door; Falcon prints that caution right in the catalog, and it's the same egress rule you live by on every project.\n\nThe single biggest reason to reach for a T over an ND is money and system compatibility. Street price on a keyed function like a T561 or T581 runs roughly $225-$290 depending on function, finish, and trim \u2014 noticeably under the equivalent ND \u2014 and Falcon built this lock to drop into an existing Schlage house. It accepts Schlage full-size IC cores and most SFIC, and you can order it cut to Schlage C keyway (that's the CP6 conventional cylinder) instead of the default Falcon G. So when a property is already keyed Schlage C and wants to save on new openings, you hang T-series and core it straight into the existing system with no rekey headache. That's the play that sells this lock. Just be clear-eyed that \"fits the system\" means the cores interchange, not the chassis parts. Do not walk onto a job assuming your ND latches and strikes drop in \u2014 they don't. The T-series deadlatch is Falcon part 23981145 and the plain springlatch is 23981137; the ND uses Schlage's own 13-series latch and a different strike. Stock T-series service parts for T-series openings.\n\nInstallation gotchas that bite people: the chassis is non-handed, but standard door range is 1-3/4\" to 2-1/4\", and for anything under 1-3/4\" you need the thin-door adapter kit (A30775-000-00) and its spacers \u2014 skip the spacers on a thin door and the trim mounts loose and the lever sags. For functions T351, T381, T561 and T571 you MUST run the timing procedure after assembly, standard cylinder or IC. Skip the timing and the key feels gritty or won't fully retract the latch. When you cut in a fresh door, drill the 2-1/8\" (54 mm) cross-bore at 2-3/4\" backset, the two 5/16\" (8 mm) through-bolt holes, and the 1\" edge bore for the latch \u2014 then file the two 4 mm x 4 mm x 3 mm-deep notches on the sides of the cross-bore that key the mounting-plate tabs. Forget the notches and the inside mounting plate won't seat. On the strike side, watch the deadlatch plunger: the anti-friction plunger has to ride on the strike lip and never drop into the strike opening, or you've defeated the deadlatch and the door can be loided.\n\nRepair-versus-replace math on these is friendly, which is part of the appeal \u2014 the whole thing is modular. Levers, spring cages, latches, and cylinders are all separately stocked service parts, so a sagging or dead lever is almost never a whole-lock replacement. The classic failure point on cheap cylindrical locks is the return spring; Falcon's answer is that stainless compression spring in a serviceable cage (the locking spring cage is a stocked part, Q330-270 on keyed functions), and the cage components want a little lube on rebuild. If a T-series lever goes floppy, you rebuild the cage for a fraction of a new lock. On abuse doors the Pressure Release feature is doing its job when an outside lever spins free under a kicked or wrenched handle \u2014 that's by design to save the internals, and it means a lever that \"spins and won't retract\" isn't always broken; sometimes it just tripped and re-seats. Brand note: don't confuse the T-series (Grade 1, the ND competitor) with Falcon's lighter B- or W-Series. If someone hands you a Falcon lever lock and it's got the compression-spring rose and the hex set screw under the inside lever, it's a T.",commonServicingIssues:["Lever sags or feels loose within months of install -> spring fatigue, or a spacer left out on a thin door -> confirm door thickness (add the A30775-000-00 kit spacers below 1-3/4\"), then rebuild/replace the stainless compression spring cage; the cage is a stocked service part (Q330-270 on keyed functions), no need to replace the lock","Outside lever spins freely and won't retract the latch -> Pressure Release rotation tripped from over-torque/abuse, or the spring cage failed -> if it re-seats and works, that's the safety feature doing its job; if it stays free, swap the outside spring cage and spindle","Key turns but latch won't fully retract, or key feels gritty (T351/T381/T561/T571) -> lock timing was never set after assembly, or the IC tailpiece is wrong -> run the Falcon timing procedure for that function; verify the core/tailpiece matches (6-pin vs 7-pin)","Inside lever won't come off to service -> people hunt for a catch hole and there isn't one on the non-keyed side -> back out the hex/Allen lever set screw (part 030831-004-30) with the included hex wrench and slide the lever off; keyed levers use a lever retainer reached through the figure-8 cylinder opening","Door won't stay latched / deadlatch feels dead -> anti-friction deadlatch plunger is dropping into the strike opening instead of riding the lip -> realign the strike so the plunger stays depressed on the lip; a mis-set strike defeats the deadlatch and lets the door be loided","Won't accept the existing building key -> ordered in default Falcon 'G' keyway on a Schlage C property -> re-order the conventional cylinder as CP6 (Schlage C), or drop in the property's Schlage FSIC or SFIC core (the T chassis accepts them)","Retrofit trim mounts crooked or loose -> the two mounting-plate tabs never engaged the door notches -> the 2-1/8\" cross-bore needs the two 4 mm x 4 mm x 3 mm-deep side notches; re-notch so the inside mounting plate seats before through-bolting"],sources:["https://us.allegion.com/en/products/brands/falcon/t-series-grade-1-cylindrical-lock.html","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/falcon/information-documents/Falcon_T_Series_Data_Sheet_106191.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Falcon-T-Series-Cut-Data-Sheet-113148.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/falcon/technical-documents/Falcon_T_Series_Service_Manual_108002.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-documents-2/InstallInstructions/Falcon_T-Series_Extra_Heavy_Duty_Lever_Lockset_Installation_Instructions_-_English_106829.pdf"],photo:"photo-falcon-t",schematic:"schematic-falcon-t"},{id:"schlage-l9000",name:"Schlage L9000 Mortise Lock",category:"Mortise locks",whatItIs:"The L9000 is Schlage's Grade 1 heavy-duty mortise lock \u2014 the workhorse you'll pull off doors in schools, hospitals, offices, dorms, and government buildings all day long. It's a big cold-rolled-steel case that mortises into the edge of the door and carries a keyed lever set, a 3/4\" latchbolt with anti-friction tongue, and, on the deadbolt functions, a 1\" stainless deadbolt in one body. What makes it the benchmark is the combination: 67 mechanical functions, field-reversible handing without cracking the case open, and a security level that holds up to abuse when a cylindrical lock would be shot. If somebody says \"mortise lock\" in commercial work, nine times out of ten they mean an L9000 or its Sargent 8200 / Corbin ML2000 equivalent.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.13-2017 Series 1000, Grade 1 Operational and Security. Drop the grade with IC cores: FSIC core = Grade 1 operational / Grade 2 security; SFIC core = Grade 1 operational / Grade 3 security. The small-case L400 deadbolt is certified to A156.36-2010 Grade 1","Case (chassis): cold-rolled steel with zinc dichromate plating. L9000 case 4-7/16\" x 6-1/16\" x 1\" (113 x 154 x 25 mm); L400 small-case deadbolt 4-7/16\" x 3-5/8\" x 1\" (113 x 92 x 25 mm)","Backset: 2-3/4\" (70 mm) only \u2014 there is no other backset on this lock","Latchbolt: 3/4\" (19 mm) throw stainless steel with anti-friction tongue (the sliding tongue that lets the latch retract under door pressure/closer)","Deadbolt (44/46/60/62/etc. functions): 1\" (25 mm) throw stainless steel","Spacing: lever/knob to cylinder 3-7/8\" (98 mm); lever/knob to thumbturn hub 2-11/16\" (68 mm)","Door thickness: standard 1-3/4\" (44 mm); optional 1-3/8\" (35 mm); over 1-3/4\" you must specify thickness and cylinder/thumbturn position (EE/EI/EO/ED)","Handing: L9000 series is field-reversible without disassembly. (L400 small-case deadbolts are non-handed)","Armor front: standard 1-1/4\" x 8\" x 7/32\" (32 x 203 x 6 mm); optional 1-1/16\" x 8\" x 7/32\" (27 x 203 x 6 mm)","Strike: standard ANSI curved-lip strike 1-1/4\" x 4-7/8\" x 1-3/16\" lip-to-center, with dust box; extended-lip options 7/8\", 1\", 1-1/2\", 1-3/4\", 2\"","Cylinder format: 6-pin conventional mortise cylinder standard (Schlage 30-001 with compression ring/spring on rose trim); also concealed mortise, FSIC, SFIC, and 7-pin Everest SL formats, plus less-cylinder. Two nickel-silver keys per lock","Cylinder cam: the standard Schlage L cam \u2014 this IS the 'clover' cam, and it rotates the plug a full 360 degrees to run the lock. The exception is the L9060 outside cylinder, which uses a straight cam that only partially rotates to retract the latch (it can't lock/unlock or throw a bolt). Match the cam to the lock and the function, not the cylinder brand \u2014 a Corbin/Sargent/Medeco cylinder you drop in to match a keyway needs the right Schlage-compatible cam (e.g. Sargent's 13-0938 cam for Schlage L) or it won't run the lock","Keyway: patented Everest 29 S123 standard; open, restricted, and Primus XP high-security levels available, with master keying and construction keying. UL 437 listed when built with a UL 437 Primus cylinder","Lever designs: 33 (standard 01/02/03/05/06/07/12/17/18, M-Collection M51-M57/M61-M63/M81-M85/ME1-ME3, plus Accent/Asti/Latitude/Longitude/Merano/Omega); 2 knob designs (41, 42). Forged brass and cast stainless levers; wrought brass or stainless knobs","Roses: A (2.125\"), B (2.562\"), C (2.625\"), plus AVA and MER (2.625\"). Escutcheons: L full-face and L concealed (7-15/16\" x 1-3/4\" x 7/16\"), N full-face (7-7/8\" x 2-1/2\" x 7/16\"). Roses cannot be combined with escutcheons on one lock","Finishes: 14, including 605, 606, 609, 612, 613, 619, 622, 625, 626, 626AM antimicrobial, 629, 630, 630AM antimicrobial, 643e (612/613 not offered on M-Collection or decorative levers)","Fire: UL 10C / CAN-ULC-S104 listed for 3-hour fire doors \u2014 EXCEPT the L9076 and L9077 holdback functions, which are NOT UL/fire listed","Egress/code: all levers with a return-to-door of 1/2\" or less comply with the California State Fire Marshal requirement (formerly Title 19). Complies with ICC A117.1 accessibility","Electrified (L909x/L949x): 12 or 24 VDC, auto-detected; fail-safe (EU) or fail-secure (EL) selectable via chassis switch \u2014 EXCEPT the motorized-latch functions L9510/L9580/L9582. Holding current 0.1 A; peak 1 A @ 12 V, 0.4 A @ 24 V (2.0 A @ 24 V on motorized latch retraction, which is 24 VDC only). Options: RX (3 A @ 125 VAC / 2 A @ 30 VDC), LX latchbolt monitor, DPS, DM deadbolt monitor. UL 294 listed; ULC-S319 Class III on L909x/L949x","Warranty: 10-year limited mechanical, 3-year limited electromechanical"],proAdvice:"Start with the part number, because the L9000 nomenclature tells you everything before you ever see the door. Read it left to right: L = standard, LV = Vandlgard, LM/LMV = multi-latch. Next digit: 9 means it has a lock case (0 means dummy trim only). Then the deadbolt/drive digit: 0 = no deadbolt, 4 = deadbolt, 5 = motorized latch retraction, 6 = MLR with lever control. Then the two-digit function. So L9080 is standard, has a case, no deadbolt, function 80 = storeroom. L9453 is standard, case, deadbolt, function 53 = entrance/office with deadbolt. On the electrified side you tack EL (electrically locked = fail secure) or EU (electrically unlocked = fail safe) onto the function. Get fluent in this and you can spec or cross-reference an L-series in your head.\n\nThe L9080 storeroom is the function you'll handle most, so know it cold: key cylinder outside, fixed (dead) outside lever, active inside lever, latchbolt and deadlocking auxiliary latch, NO deadbolt. Outside lever is ALWAYS locked \u2014 you key it to retract the latch and get in; there's no unlocked state, and that's what makes it storeroom. Inside lever is always free for egress, which is why it's code-legal on almost any door. The auxiliary latch (that little plunger next to the main latch) deadlocks the latchbolt when the door is closed so you can't loid it with a card. If the customer wants to leave it unlocked during the day, they don't want storeroom \u2014 they want L9050 entrance/office (key outside or inside thumbturn unlocks the outside lever) or classroom L9070 (key locks/unlocks the outside lever, no inside thumbturn). Don't let a GC talk you into an L9080 for a classroom where the teacher needs to lock down from inside \u2014 that's exactly backwards. They need L9071 classroom security (keyed both sides, lockable from either side) or a classroom-holdback, and post-Sandy Hook a lot of jurisdictions now require inside lockdown capability on classrooms.\n\nVandlgard (the LV prefix) is worth understanding because it changes how the lock feels and how you troubleshoot it. On a standard L, when the outside lever is locked it's rigid \u2014 you push on it and it doesn't move. On Vandlgard, the locked outside lever droops down freely and spins loose so a kid hanging on it or a vandal cranking on it can't transfer force into the hubs and snap the internal parts. The gotcha: a customer calls saying \"the lever is broken, it just flops down\" and it's not broken at all \u2014 that's Vandlgard working exactly right. Key it or unlock it and the lever firms right up. Vandlgard is not available with hospital-latch or ligature-resistant trim, so don't quote both.\n\nNow cylinders and cams \u2014 this is where guys lose an hour on a service call, and it's where the apprentice writeup had it backwards. The L9000 takes a Schlage 30-001 conventional mortise cylinder (comes in 1-1/8\", 1-1/4\", 1-3/8\", 1-1/2\" \u2014 1-1/8\" is the common length on standard rose trim, but confirm against trim and door thickness). The CAM matters more than the length. The standard Schlage L cam IS the clover cam \u2014 that clover-shaped cam is what spins the plug a full 360 degrees to actually run the lock, and it's correct on nearly every L function. The one that trips people up is the L9060: its OUTSIDE cylinder uses a straight cam that only partially rotates to retract the latch \u2014 it deliberately can't lock/unlock or throw a bolt. So the real cam question is L cam (clover) versus L9060 straight cam, not \"L9000 versus clover.\" If you're dropping in a Corbin, Sargent, or Medeco cylinder to match an existing keyway, you MUST get it with the correct Schlage-compatible cam or swap one on \u2014 Sargent even sells a 13-0938 cam made to run a Sargent cylinder in a Schlage L. Set cylinder depth so the cam just clears the mechanism with the face flush, then lock it with the setscrew through the armor front. Too deep and it won't turn; too proud and it fouls the trim. For interchangeable core, spec an FSIC (drops to Grade 2 security) or SFIC (drops to Grade 3) and set the customer's expectation accordingly.\n\nRepair versus replace: the L9000 case is rebuildable and Schlage sells the guts, so on a good lock you replace the latch, the auxiliary latch spring, or the hub, not the whole lock. A worn latch that won't deadlock (auxiliary plunger not being held in by the strike) is usually a strike alignment or a tired spring \u2014 check alignment first, it's free. But if the case is sprung from somebody forcing the door, or you've got a 30-year-old L with a cracked hub, just replace the case; a new L9080 case runs roughly the low-to-mid hundreds and you'll spend more in labor chasing a worn-out one. Reuse the trim (levers/roses/escutcheons) and the cylinder if they're clean \u2014 that's where the money is. And always confirm handing and door thickness before you order; the L9000 is field-reversible, but the trim and cylinder length won't fix a 2-1/4\" door you speced as 1-3/4\".",commonServicingIssues:["Outside lever flops loosely / 'lever is broken' \u2014 on an LV (Vandlgard) lock this is normal: the locked outside lever is designed to drop and spin free to shed vandal force. Key it or unlock it and it firms up. Only suspect real damage if a NON-Vandlgard lever is loose.","Key turns but door won't open (latch won't retract) \u2014 nine times out of ten it's the wrong or mis-seated cam, not the lock. Confirm the mortise cylinder has the correct Schlage L (clover) cam, not an L9060 straight cam, and that the cylinder is threaded to the right depth so the cam clears. Set flush and setscrew it. Second suspect: broken or disconnected cam.","Door doesn't stay latched or you can loid it with a card \u2014 the deadlocking auxiliary latch isn't being held in by the strike. Check strike alignment first (the aux plunger must land on the strike face, not fall into the cutout); realign or shim the strike, then check the aux latch spring.","Latch drags/binds on the strike lip and won't close under the closer \u2014 anti-friction tongue or strike alignment. Verify the ANSI curved-lip strike is aligned and the lip isn't catching; a mis-set strike or a sagged door hits the anti-friction tongue wrong.","Customer wants to leave a storeroom door unlocked during business hours and can't \u2014 that's L9080 doing its job (outside lever is ALWAYS locked, no unlocked state). Wrong function for the need; retrofit to L9050 entrance/office or L9070 classroom if they need a lockable/unlockable outside lever.","Cylinder spins freely and won't lock/unlock \u2014 cam has come loose from the plug or stripped, or someone fitted an L9060 straight cam where a clover cam belongs. Pull the cylinder, re-secure or replace the correct cam.","Inside lever won't retract latch for egress \u2014 hub or spindle issue inside the case. On a life-safety egress door this is a code and liability problem: take it out of service and rebuild or replace the case. Inside lever must always give free egress.","Electrified L909x/L949x won't lock or unlock on power \u2014 confirm the fail-safe vs fail-secure chassis switch matches intent (EL = fail secure, EU = fail safe), verify 12/24 VDC at the lock (24 VDC only on motorized latch retraction), and check the harness/RX/DPS wiring. On MLR functions the motor draws up to 2 A at 24 V on inrush, so an undersized supply causes intermittent no-pull.","New cylinder sits too proud and fouls the trim, or too deep and won't turn \u2014 set cylinder depth so the cam just clears the mechanism with the face flush, then lock it with the setscrew through the armor front."],sources:["https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_L_Series_Cut_Sheet_113141.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/mechanical-locks/l-series-grade-1-mortise-lock.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_L909x_Series_Data_Sheet_104998.pdf","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/user-question/mortise-cylinder-versions/","https://buydoorhardwarenow.com/schlage_30-001_1.125inch_mortise_cylinder_x_l9000series_cam"],photo:"photo-schlage-l9000",schematic:"schematic-schlage-l9000"},{id:"sargent-8200",name:"Sargent 8200 Mortise Lock",category:"Mortise locks",whatItIs:"The Sargent 8200 is a heavy-duty Grade 1 mortise lockset \u2014 ASSA ABLOY's answer to the Schlage L9000 and Yale 8800, and one of the most-specified institutional mortise bodies in North America. You see it everywhere the door has to take a beating and stay code-compliant: schools, hospitals, courthouses, universities, government buildings. It is a single multi-function lockbody where the function (storeroom, classroom, office, privacy, electrified, etc.) is set by internal parts and trim, all sharing the same 2-3/4\" backset case. It matters because when a spec calls \"Sargent 8200 x function x finish,\" you are dealing with a serviceable, rebuildable lock that will outlast the door if you maintain it.",specs:["Certified to ANSI/BHMA A156.13 Series 1000, Operational Grade 1 AND Security Grade 1 with all standard trims (note: LFIC removable-core and SFIC cylinders do NOT meet Security Grade 1)","Exceeds ANSI/BHMA A156.13 Grade 1 cycle-testing requirement by 14 times, per Sargent's catalog","Case: 12-gauge heavy-duty wrought steel, zinc dichromate plated, 7/64\" (3mm / .109\") thick, with a 1/8\" (.125\") cold-rolled steel inside front that holds the lock in the door","Backset: 2-3/4\" (70mm) only","Latchbolt: stainless steel, 3/4\" (19mm) projection, one-piece anti-friction reversible latch","Deadbolt (deadbolt functions): one-piece hardened stainless steel, 1\" (25mm) projection","Auxiliary deadlatch: stainless steel, non-handed","Only 30 degrees of lever rotation to retract the latchbolt","Door thickness range 1-3/8\" (35mm) to 6\" (152mm); standard prep is 1-3/4\" (44mm) \u2014 you must specify thickness for 2\" and 2-1/4\" doors and above","Minimum stile: 4-1/2\" (114mm) for 7800/8200/R8200 mechanical, 4-3/4\" (121mm) for electrified functions","Field-reversible handing \u2014 specify hand on the order; if no hand is specified it ships one way","Conventional mortise cylinder cams (SARGENT keyways): Standard Cam 13-0664 on most functions, but NOT on 16, 50 & 92 functions. The #105 short cam (13-0665) is used for the 16 & 92 inside cylinder; the #115 hotel cam (13-2045) is used on 7850/8250 hotel cylinders only. For 6300 LFIC cores, cams are factory-installed and NOT sold separately: -105 for the inside of 8216/8292, -101 for the 50 hotel function","Fire: UL and cUL listed for A-label 4x10 single and 8x10 double doors (3-hour fire door) and lesser classes; stamped letter 'F' and UL symbol on the armored front indicate listing. Meets ANSI/UL 10C positive-pressure","Electrified (70 Fail Safe / 71 Fail Secure, i.e. 8270/8271): EcoFlex actuator, operates 12-24VDC regulated with full-wave rectification inside the lockbody; you still specify 12VDC or 24VDC on the order. Reduces energy consumption up to 96% (GreenCircle certified). Actuator draw .015A continuous; max 2 locks per 1A supply","Electrified options: RX- request-to-exit / enter signaling switch, LX- latchbolt monitor; NAC (networked) variants ship with door-position and request-to-exit monitoring installed (deadbolt monitoring too if ordered with deadbolt)","Door prep: ANSI/BHMA A156.115 or A156.115W, modified per template","Warranty: 10-year limited mechanical, 2-year limited on electrified (limited to replacement of the lockbody)"],proAdvice:"Order it right and half the callbacks never happen. The 8200 part number is \"82 x function x finish\" for the lever/rose-or-escutcheon version, \"78 x function x finish\" for the knob version, and \"R82 x function x finish\" for the Simpli roseless trim. Here is the gotcha the catalog spells out: Simpli only runs on the R8200 lockbody, which is a purpose-built body \u2014 the R8200 and standard 8200 bodies are dimensionally the same EXCEPT for the through-bolt locations, so you cannot bolt Simpli trim onto a plain 8200 and call it done. Don't promise a customer a roseless look on an existing 8200 without swapping to the R8200 body. Always specify hand, specify door thickness for anything over 1-3/4\", and specify cylinder type. Backset is 2-3/4\" only, so if you're replacing a 2-3/8\" cylindrical prep you're mortising the door, not swapping.\n\nThe function code is where apprentices get burned, so learn the common institutional ones cold. The 04 storeroom (8204) is outside trim always locked, key retracts latch, no inside trim/cylinder needed on the base function, inside always free for egress \u2014 that's your server room, mechanical room, stairwell-side door. The 05 office/entry (8205) lets the key or the inside thumbturn lock and unlock the outside trim, inside always free \u2014 general office door. The 37 classroom (8237) is the true classroom: key locks and unlocks the outside trim, no inside button for a student to flip, inside lever always retracts the latch for egress. The 38 (8238) is Classroom Security Intruder Latchbolt \u2014 key from EITHER side locks/unlocks the outside trim, which is how you get lock-from-inside-the-room intruder behavior; on the rose body you add a cylinder to the 37 body to make a 38. The 66 (8266) is privacy for bath/bedroom. If a district asks for lockdown from inside the room, the plain 37 won't do it \u2014 that's a 38 (or a 39/40/41/42 Classroom Security Intruder family, some of which are deadbolt-based and handed and NOT field reversible), and you want to walk them through egress code before you spec it. Nine times out of ten a \"classroom that won't lock from outside\" call is a district that bought 05 offices when they wanted 37 classrooms.\n\nCams: match the cam to the function, not the other way around. On conventional SARGENT-keyway mortise cylinders the Standard Cam 13-0664 covers most functions, but it is specifically excluded on the 16, 50 and 92 functions. The 16 and 92 inside cylinders take the #105 short cam (13-0665); the 50 hotel function runs on hotel cylinders (7850/8250) that use the #115 hotel cam (13-2045). If it's a 6300-series large-format interchangeable core, the cams are factory-assembled to the housing and are NOT sold loose \u2014 you order the cam as a suffix (e.g. 63-44-105), no suffix for the standard 6300 cam, -105 for the inside of 8216/8292, -101 for the 50 hotel function. Rebuild one of these with the wrong cam and it won't drive the hub. And when a customer runs Sargent LFIC or SFIC cores, remind them those cores knock the opening down from Security Grade 1 even though the lock body is still Grade 1 \u2014 that matters for insurance and spec compliance on high-security openings.\n\nElectrified: the modern 8200 EL is EcoFlex, and it operates across 12-24VDC regulated with full-wave rectification built into the lockbody \u2014 you still specify 12 or 24VDC on the order, but it tolerates that whole band, so you're not frying a coil over a small mismatch. Field-configurable fail mode, but you specify the function: 70 is fail-safe (8270 \u2014 power ON locks the outside lever, power OFF unlocks the outside trim, for egress and access-controlled corridors) and 71 is fail-secure (8271 \u2014 power OFF the outside stays locked, key always overrides). Get the fail mode right against the life-safety plan \u2014 fail-safe on a fire-rated stair door is often required so people get out under power loss, fail-secure on an exterior or secured-storage door so it doesn't dump open in an outage. Add RX for request-to-exit and LX for latch monitoring if the panel needs status. Note actuator draw is tiny (.015A continuous) but hold to two locks per 1A supply for peak. Electrified locks want the 4-3/4\" stile minimum and carry only a 2-year warranty, so set the customer's expectations.\n\nRepair vs replace: the 8200 is built to be rebuilt. The case is 12-gauge wrought steel with heavy-duty wrought-steel hubs and a high-carbon steel spring cartridge, and you can get hubs, spring cartridge, latch/deadbolt, cams, and trim as parts. A worn hub or a lazy latch spring is a $40-100 parts fix plus labor, not a $250-400 new lockset. Replace the whole body when the case is cracked, when a fire-rated opening's armored front is damaged (that \"F\" stamp is your listing \u2014 you don't field-fabricate it, and any field mod to a fire opening can void the rating, so consult the AHJ), or when someone converted function with the wrong parts and buggered the works. For institutional accounts, stock the common wear parts (hubs, latch, spring cartridge) and the standard 13-0664 cam \u2014 you'll turn most 8200 calls into same-visit repairs and keep your callback rate near zero.",commonServicingIssues:["Lever droops / won't spring back to horizontal -> worn wrought-steel hub or fatigued high-carbon spring cartridge in the case -> rebuild with the Sargent hub and spring cartridge rather than replacing the body; on institutional traffic this is your #1 call","Latch retracts sluggishly or door won't latch on close -> anti-friction latch gummed with paint/grime or misaligned strike -> clean and lubricate the latch, verify the 3/4\" throw and strike alignment; adjust the strike lip before you condemn the lock","Key turns but outside trim stays locked / won't drive the retractor -> wrong cylinder cam for the function (standard 13-0664 where the 16/92 function needs the #105 short cam or the 50 hotel function needs the #115 hotel cam, or a 6300 core with the wrong factory cam suffix) -> match the cam to the function code","'Classroom won't lock from outside key' -> lock is actually an 05 office function (thumbturn/key locks outside), not a 37 classroom -> confirm the function; a real classroom is a 37, and lock-from-inside intruder behavior needs a 38 or the Classroom Security Intruder family, not an office lock","Electrified lock stopped working after a power-supply swap -> voltage or wiring fault; EcoFlex operates across 12-24VDC regulated, so a small mismatch shouldn't kill it -> confirm a 12-24VDC regulated feed within +/-10%, then suspect wiring, connector, or the RX/LX harness before the actuator; don't share the circuit with another electromagnetic device","Fail-safe vs fail-secure behaving 'backwards' on power loss -> wrong function suffix specified (70 fail-safe unlocks the outside on power loss, 71 fail-secure stays locked) -> verify against the life-safety/egress plan; may require a body/function change, not a field fix","Deadbolt won't project fully or binds -> door swelled/sagged and the 1\" deadbolt is hitting the strike box, or debris in the box -> shim/adjust the strike and clear the box; check hinge sag before blaming the lock","Loose or spinning lever/rose after abuse -> vandalism or backed-out fasteners -> retighten; on high-abuse openings upgrade to the Torx or spanner secure-fastener and anti-vandal pull trim option","SFIC/LFIC core opening flagged on a security spec -> interchangeable/removable-core cylinders don't meet A156.13 Security Grade 1 -> swap to a conventional or 6300 mortise cylinder arrangement if the spec demands Security Grade 1 (the lock body itself is still Grade 1)"],sources:["https://www.assaabloy.com/hk/en/product-assets/mechanical-lock/sargent/assets/documents/SARGENT_Mortise_Lock_78008200_Series_Catalog.pdf","https://objects.eanixter.com/PD512451.PDF","https://bigc-products.s3.amazonaws.com/SARGENT_Mortise_Lock_8200_Series_Parts_Manual.pdf","https://www.sargentlock.com/en/commercial-locks-products/locks/mortise-locks","https://www.idn-inc.com/sargent-standard-cam-for-mortise-cylinder-13-0664.html"],schematic:"sargent-8200-exploded",photo:"photo-sargent-8200"},{id:"corbin-ml2000",name:"Corbin Russwin ML2000 Series Mortise Lock",category:"Mortise locks",whatItIs:"The ML2000 is Corbin Russwin's heavy-duty commercial mortise lock and the ASSA ABLOY brand-sibling to the Sargent 8200 \u2014 functionally it's a direct competitor to the Schlage L9000 and the Yale 8800. You see it in hospitals, universities, K-12, courthouses, and Class A office towers where the spec calls for ANSI/BHMA A156.13 Series 1000, Operational and Security Grade 1, and a 3-hour fire rating. It's a full 5-7/8 x 4 in. heavy-gauge steel case with a 1 in. stainless deadbolt, a quick-reversible anti-friction latch, and a deep menu of functions and trim. If a door has a mortise pocket and a Corbin logo on the front, that's almost certainly what's in it on anything built or renovated in the last 20-plus years.",specs:["Grade/certification: ANSI/BHMA A156.13 Series 1000, certified Operational and Security Grade 1; also meets ICC A117.1 accessibility","Fire rating: UL/cUL listed, UL10C positive pressure; listed for A label and lesser doors \u2014 4 ft x 10 ft single, 8 ft x 10 ft pair (3-hour fire door). The letter 'F' and UL symbol on the armored front indicate the listing","Backset: 2-3/4 in. (70mm) fixed mortise backset (not adjustable)","Lock case: heavy-gauge steel, 5-7/8 in. (149mm) x 4 in. (102mm) x 15/16 in. (24mm) deep; optional non-ferrous case for corrosion resistance","Front (faceplate): heavy-gauge steel, 8 in. (203mm) x 1-1/4 in. (32mm) x -3/32 in. (2mm); accommodates flat doors and doors beveled 1/8 in. in 2 in. Furnished 1-1/16 in. (27mm) width standard; optional D138 wide front for 1-3/8 in. (35mm) doors","Deadbolt: one-piece stainless steel, 1 in. (25mm) full throw","Latchbolt: 2-piece mechanical with anti-friction insert, 3/4 in. (19mm) throw; quick-reversible (patented) without opening the case","Auxiliary latchbolt: 9/16 in. (14mm) effective throw, 3/8 in. (10mm) effective deadlocking","Cylinder: brass, 6-pin, L4 keyway, 0-bitted standard; options include 7-pin, LFIC and SFIC interchangeable core, master ring, security, Access 3, Pyramid, and flex-head cylinders. Ships with two nickel-silver keys","Cylinder cam: standard keyed functions turn the plug directly in the case and need no cam; deadbolt and hold-back functions are marked 'cylinder cam required' in the catalog \u2014 use the specified cam or the plug just spins","Hub/spindle: standard knob/lever hub is steel, 5/16 in. (8mm) spacing; center-to-center lever to cylinder 3-5/8 in. (92mm), lever to thumbturn 2-7/16 in. (62mm)","Strike: wrought brass, bronze, or stainless, ANSI straight lip standard, 4-7/8 in. (124mm) x 1-1/4 in. (32mm), 1-1/8 in. (29mm) lip-to-center; optional strikes, lip lengths and ANSI wrought strike box available","Door thickness: 1-3/4 in. (44mm) standard; optional thicknesses available via Quick Codes","Functions: 63 mechanical and electrified functions incl. 2 vandal-resistant (ML2000VR, security Torx armored front) and 4 behavioral-health; workhorses are ML2010 passage, ML2051 entrance/office, ML2055 classroom, ML2057 storeroom, ML2065 dormitory, ML2075 entrance/apartment, ML2049 storeroom-deadbolt","Multi-function lockbody: ML2054 entrance (ANSI F04) universal body reconfigures into 11 other functions without disassembling the case, using few added parts","Electrified: 12/24 VDC @ 15mA continuous, peak current draw 0.5A (max 2 locks per 1A power supply); operating voltage not to exceed +/-10%. Monitor switch rated 2A @ 24VDC; M91 latchbolt monitor and M92 request-to-exit are SPDT switches. Fail-safe and fail-secure available; also offered with MELR (motorized electric latch retraction) and as the ML20600 NAC networked-access series, shipped with door-position and request-to-exit monitoring. EcoFlex technology on electromechanical functions for up to ~96% energy savings","Finishes: 16 finishes; common are 605/606 (bright/satin brass), 611 (bright bronze), 612 (satin bronze), 613 (oxidized/oil-rubbed bronze), 619 (satin nickel), 625/626 (bright/satin chrome), 629/630 (bright/satin stainless). Some trims not available in 629/630","Warranty: ten-year limited (mechanical); five-year MELR; two-year limited on electronic components"],proAdvice:"Start with what the ML2000 actually is on the job. It's the Corbin Russwin twin to the Sargent 8200, both under ASSA ABLOY's Access and Egress Hardware Group. That relationship matters because a lot of the internal logic and function-numbering philosophy rhymes between the two brands \u2014 but the parts are NOT interchangeable with Sargent, and they're absolutely not interchangeable with Schlage L9000 or Yale 8800. Corbin runs its own keyways (L4 is the standard 6-pin), its own hub and cam, and its own trim mounting. Nine times out of ten when somebody hands you a \"universal mortise part\" off the truck and it doesn't drop in, it's because they grabbed a Sargent or a Schlage piece. Match the brand on the front plate before you order anything.\n\nSpecifying one: the two decisions that trip people up are function and cylinder prep. Nail the function first. The workhorses are ML2055 classroom (outside lever locked/unlocked by key, inside always free, egress-compliant), ML2057 storeroom (outside always rigid, key retracts latch, inside always free \u2014 your mechanical/electrical/utility-room lock), ML2051 entrance/office, and ML2075 entrance/apartment. If the customer is genuinely unsure, or you're on a big campus job where room use changes over time, spec the ML2054 entrance body (ANSI F04). That universal lockbody reconfigures into eleven other functions without opening the case and with very few extra parts \u2014 a real money-saver on a facility that keeps changing room use. On cylinder prep, know before you order whether the function needs a cylinder cam. Most keyed-lever functions turn the plug directly in the case and don't care about the cam, but the deadbolt and hold-back functions are stamped \"cylinder cam required\" in the catalog. Drop a standard IC or LFIC in one of those without the right cam and you get a cylinder that spins and does nothing. That's a callback waiting to happen.\n\nInstall and adjust gotchas: the ML2000 front is 8 in. x 1-1/4 in. and ships set up for a flat door or up to 1/8 in. bevel in 2 in. If the latch drags or the front sits proud on a beveled door, don't force it \u2014 the front takes the bevel. For 1-3/8 in. doors you need the D138 wide front, not the standard 1-1/16 in. furnished front; get that right at order time because it's not something you improvise in the field. The best feature on this lock for a service tech is the patented quick-reversible latch \u2014 you re-hand the latchbolt without pulling the case apart. So when you show up and the \"wrong hand\" lock is the only one on the van, you flip the latch and go, no teardown. Learn that move, it saves you a trip. The 1 in. deadbolt throw is a genuine security feature and it's forgiving of a wide door-to-frame gap, but it also means your strike prep and box depth have to be right or the bolt won't fully extend \u2014 and you lose both the security and, on a fire door, the listing.\n\nCode and liability, because this is the stuff that gets somebody hurt or gets you named in a lawsuit. On egress doors the inside lever must always retract the latch freely \u2014 that's exactly why classroom (ML2055) and storeroom (ML2057) both keep the inside grip free at all times. Never install a function that needs a key or special knowledge to get out from the inside on a required egress door, and never defeat that with an aftermarket thumbturn or add-on. On fire-rated openings that \"F\" and UL stamp on the armored front is your listing; Corbin explicitly states that any field modification to a fire-rated opening can impact the rating and makes no representations about the result \u2014 so drilling the door, aftermarket wraps, or the wrong strike can void the listing, and when in doubt you kick it to the AHJ. For behavioral-health and detention-adjacent work, Corbin has specific vandal-resistant trim (ML2000VR \u2014 tapered stainless escutcheons, Torx tamper-resistant screws) and behavioral-health anti-ligature trim (BHSS/BLSS). Don't substitute standard lever trim on a psych unit \u2014 that's a life-safety spec, not an aesthetic one.\n\nRepair vs. replace and pricing judgment: the ML2000 case is a serious piece of hardware and a full lockset with trim is not cheap \u2014 figure a few hundred dollars for a standard keyed mechanical and up into four figures once you're into MELR, NAC networked access, indicator trim, or premium finishes and designer levers. Because of that, repair almost always wins on this lock. If the latch is sluggish, a spring feels weak, or the deadbolt is sticky, you rebuild the case with the ML2000 parts kit before you ever quote a whole new lockset. Corbin publishes two parts manuals with a hard dividing line at January 2016 \u2014 internals changed \u2014 so identify which era case you have before ordering parts. The usual failures are trim and cylinder, not the case body, and those are bolt-on or drop-in fixes. Reserve full replacement for a cracked case, a fire-door listing you can't verify after a prior hack job, or a function change the multi-function body can't cover. And when you do replace, replace like-for-like Corbin \u2014 don't try to marry Sargent or Schlage guts into a Corbin case to save a few bucks. It won't seat and you'll eat the labor twice.",commonServicingIssues:["Key turns but nothing happens on a deadbolt or hold-back function: wrong or missing cylinder cam. Those functions are stamped 'cylinder cam required,' so when you drop in an IC or replacement cylinder you must use the correct cam or the plug just spins. Fix: install the specified ML2000 cam.","Deadbolt won't fully throw or feels sticky: strike box too shallow, strike misaligned, or a gunked-up bolt. On a fire door a bolt that won't reach full 1 in. throw also compromises the listing. Fix: correct/deepen the strike prep, align the strike, clean and lube the bolt; rebuild the deadbolt from the parts kit if the spring is shot.","Latch is the wrong hand or dragging: door got re-hung or a wrong-hand lock was installed. Fix: use the patented quick-reversible feature to flip the latchbolt without opening the case; if it's drag from a beveled door, adjust the bevel on the front rather than forcing it.","Lever is loose, sagging, or spins free: worn hub inside the case or loose trim through-bolts. Fix: tighten the through-bolted trim first; if the hub is worn, rebuild the case with the correct-era parts kit (pre- vs post-January 2016 differ).","Callback after a cylinder swap where inside egress is now impaired: someone installed the wrong function or added an aftermarket thumbturn. Fix: restore a code-compliant egress function (classroom/storeroom keep the inside grip always free); never leave a required egress door needing a key from inside.","Electrified NAC/MELR lock intermittently fails to unlock or chatters: voltage drop on a long run, too many locks on one supply, or supply out of tolerance. It's 12/24 VDC @ 15mA continuous, 0.5A peak, max 2 locks per 1A supply, and voltage must stay within +/-10%. Fix: measure supply voltage at the lock under load, upsize the wire or move the supply closer, don't overload the power supply, and confirm fail-safe vs fail-secure matches the opening's life-safety intent.","Can't find the right internal part or the part doesn't fit: two parts eras. Fix: determine whether the lock was made before or after January 2016 and order from the matching ML2000 parts manual.","Trim not available in the requested finish: several trims aren't offered in 629/630 (bright/satin stainless). Fix: confirm finish availability before quoting; on 630 jobs, 619 satin nickel is sometimes substituted to approximate the look."],sources:["https://www.corbinrusswin.com/en/commercial-locks-products/locks/mortise-locks/product-details.aehpdp-mechanical-locks-and-exit-devices-mortise-locks-ml2000-series-34064-aeh_corbin_russwin_34064","https://prod-trudoor-media.s3.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/01131914/Corbin_Russwin_ML2000_Series_Mortise_Lock_Catalog.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Corbin-Russwin-ML2000-Series-Mortise-Locks-Catalog_45300.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Corbin-Russwin-ML2000-Series-Mortise-Lock-Parts-Manual-For-Locks-Made-After-January-2016_45556.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Corbin-Russwin-ML2000-Series-Mortise-Locks-Template_T30600.pdf"],photo:"photo-corbin-ml2000",schematic:"schematic-corbin-ml2000"},{id:"lcn-4040",name:"LCN 4040XP / 4041 Door Closer",category:"Door closers",whatItIs:"The LCN 4040XP is the heavy-duty, cast-iron surface closer you'll see on institutional and high-abuse openings everywhere \u2014 schools, hospitals, government buildings, apartment corridors. It's the modern replacement for the legendary 4041 (the changeover happened August 2011), and it's a direct retrofit: same 2-1/4in x 5in hole pattern, so a new XP drops right onto an old 4041's holes. Non-handed, non-sized, spring-adjustable 1-6, it's arguably the single most common commercial closer in North America. When a customer says \"just put a good closer on it,\" this is the one. One thing to get straight up front \u2014 the delay-action version is still ordered and cataloged as the 4041 (a 4040XP body with the 4041-3071 delay cylinder); LCN does not sell delay on the standard 4040XP.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.4 Grade 1; UL and cUL listed for self-closing (non-hold-open) doors \u2014 fire-door rated in that configuration","Independently tested and certified to 10 million cycles \u2014 5x the 2-million-cycle ANSI Grade 1 minimum","Meets ADA reduced opening-force requirement (under 5 lbf on a 36in door) when the spring is backed down toward size 1","Non-handed, non-sized cast-iron cylinder \u2014 standard cylinder is 4040XP-3071; field-adjustable spring size 1 through 6 via the patented LCN Green Dial spring-force indicator","Interior doors to 5ft0in, exterior doors to 4ft0in; match spring power to leaf width per the chart on the body (exact breakpoints vary \u2014 verify against the label, roughly size 1 for narrow leaves up through size 6 for the widest/exterior)","Three independent adjustment valves: main/sweep speed, latch speed, and backcheck","Positive-stop regulation valves with color indicators \u2014 staked so the screw can't back all the way out and leak; more visible = faster speed","Mounting hole pattern: 2-1/4in x 5in","Cast-iron cylinder with full-complement bearings, 1-1/2in diameter piston, and 3/4in diameter double heat-treated steel pinion journal","Filled with all-weather Liquid X hydraulic fluid for stable operation across temperature swings","Mounting: hinge/regular (pull side), top jamb (push side), parallel arm (push side); max opening 120deg on hinge/top-jamb, up to 180deg on parallel arm (template and reveal permitting)","Max mounting/installation torque 46 ft-lb; use SRT (self-reaming/tapping) screws in wood","Standard closing time from 90deg open is roughly 5 to 7 seconds, split between main (sweep) and latch speed","Delayed action is NOT offered on the standard 4040XP \u2014 order the 4041 with the 4041-3071 delay cylinder; it delays closing from about 120deg to 70deg, adjustable up to roughly 1 minute","Covers: 4040XP-72 non-handed plastic (standard, powder-coat only) or the handed metal cover (required for plated and custom finishes)","Finishes: powder coat 689 aluminum, 690 statuary bronze, 691 light bronze, 693 gloss black, 695 dark bronze, 696 brass, 622 matte black, plus custom RAL colors; plated finishes on the metal cover/arm/fasteners; SRI rust-inhibiting option exceeds 100-hr salt spray","30-year limited warranty"],proAdvice:"Sizing first \u2014 this is where apprentices go wrong. The 4040XP is non-sized, meaning you set the power in the field with the spring-adjustment screw, and LCN puts a Green Dial indicator right on the body so you can see where you are. Out of the box it usually lands around a 3. Match spring power to door width using the chart on the body \u2014 narrow interior leaves take a low setting, wide and exterior leaves take a high one. Don't quote yourself exact width breakpoints from memory; read the label. Here's the code catch nine times out of ten people forget: ADA reduced opening force (under 5 lbf on an interior door) only holds when you back the spring way down toward size 1. So on an accessible interior door you're detuning it \u2014 and then people complain it won't latch against stack pressure or a strong gasket. That's not a bad closer, that's physics. If it won't close AND meet ADA, the fix is at the door (adjust the weatherstrip, check the latch, add a low-energy operator), not by cranking the spring.\n\nThe three valves, and the order you touch them. On the 4040XP the valves are labeled \u2014 main/sweep, latch, and backcheck \u2014 three independent adjustments, and there's a swing diagram right on the body. Use it. Backcheck cushions the door in the last part of the opening swing so it doesn't slam into a wall or blow the arm apart; snug it in (clockwise) if the door flies open, but don't crank it to a hard stop \u2014 backcheck is a cushion, not a doorstop. That's what wall bumpers and CUSH arms are for. Main/sweep controls the bulk of the closing swing; latch speed controls the last 10-15deg to pull it shut and latch. Set sweep so the door takes 5-7 seconds from 90deg, then bump latch speed up a touch so it positively latches without slamming. Golden rule LCN prints on every sheet: never back a regulation valve out more than a couple turns. These are positive-stop staked valves now, but over-open one and you'll get a fluid weep \u2014 and a leaking closer is a replace, not a rebuild.\n\nMounting \u2014 pick the config by which side of the door you're on and what's in the way. Regular (hinge/pull side) is the strongest, most efficient pull, but the arm projects into the room and the closer sits on the door face \u2014 fine for a mechanical or stair door, ugly on a lobby entrance, tops out around 120deg. Parallel arm (push side) tucks the arm up flat against the underside of the frame soffit \u2014 cleaner look, most popular on corridor and out-swinging exterior doors, and it's what you want when you can't have an arm sticking out; PA mounting can go up toward 180deg depending on template and reveal. Remember the parallel-arm shoe (4040XP-62PA) is required for PA mounting \u2014 the standard regular arm won't do parallel without it. Top jamb (push side) mounts the closer on the frame face above the door. Reveal is the thing that bites you on top jamb and PA: shallow reveal takes the standard rod, deep reveal needs the long or extra-long rod-and-shoe kit. Measure the reveal before you order the arm, not after.\n\nArm selection is really door protection. Standard 4040XP comes with the regular arm, but on abusive openings spec the EDA (Extra Duty Arm) \u2014 forged main and forearm, it's what survives a middle-school hallway. If the door needs a built-in stop, go CUSH (Cush-n-Stop, stop in the soffit shoe) instead of relying on backcheck; SCUSH is the spring version that cushions before it stops \u2014 kinder to the frame and the closer on windy exterior doors. Hold-open arms (H, HCUSH, etc.) are convenient but never on a fire door \u2014 a mechanical hold-open defeats the self-closing rating, that's a fail on inspection and a liability if it's in a rated corridor or stair. If you need hold-open on a rated opening, that's an electrified hold-open tied to the fire alarm, not a mechanical detent.\n\nRepair vs replace and the 4041 question. The 4040XP is a sealed cast-iron cylinder \u2014 you do not rebuild these. If it's weeping oil, blowing past the seals, or the sweep won't hold a setting, it's done; put a new one on. The good news is the retrofit story: the XP shares the 4041's 2-1/4in x 5in hole pattern, so replacing a dead 4041 is peel-the-template-off-your-old-holes easy, and the XP uses the 4041 arms and accessories. Two gotchas on the swap \u2014 (1) if the old unit had delayed action, a standard 4040XP will NOT give it back; delay only comes on the 4041 with the 4041-3071 delay cylinder, so order that, don't drop a standard XP on a delayed opening and tell the customer it's the same. (2) For plated or custom-color finishes you must order the handed metal cover; the standard plastic cover (4040XP-72) is non-handed and powder coat only. Ballpark pricing runs roughly $150-$250 for a standard 4040XP depending on arm and finish, more for the metal cover, special finishes, or the delay cylinder (that 4041 delay assembly alone runs north of $500 at distribution) \u2014 cheap insurance versus the callback on a bargain closer. This is the closer you standardize on so your truck stock and your customer's inventory both stay simple.",commonServicingIssues:["Door slams shut / won't slow down \u2014 sweep or latch valve backed out too far, or (more often) the cylinder has lost fluid and can't hold pressure. Try snugging the main speed valve clockwise first; if it won't hold a setting or you see oil, the cylinder's blown \u2014 replace the closer.","Oil weeping from the body or arm shaft \u2014 internal seal failure, usually from someone over-opening a regulation valve or from age/abuse. Not rebuildable on a sealed XP cylinder; replace it.","Door won't latch / stalls short of closed \u2014 latch speed set too slow, spring power too low for the door (common after someone detuned it toward size 1 for ADA), or door drag from weatherstrip/latch. Bump latch speed and re-check spring power against the width chart on the body before blaming the closer.","Door flies open and bangs the wall or bends the arm \u2014 no or weak backcheck. Snug the backcheck valve in, but if the opening truly needs a stop, retrofit a CUSH/SCUSH arm or add a wall/floor stop; don't use backcheck as a hard stop.","Arm loose or clunking / closer 'lets go' \u2014 arm-to-shaft screw or forearm-to-shoe fastener backed out, or the arm was pre-loaded wrong at install. Re-set the arm pre-load per the template and tighten; on parallel arm confirm the 4040XP-62PA shoe is present.","Won't meet ADA opening force \u2014 spring not backed down far enough toward size 1; reduce spring power, then verify the door still latches. If it won't do both, the fix is at the door (gasket, latch, or a low-energy operator), not the closer.","Customer wants delayed action but has a standard 4040XP \u2014 delay isn't available on the standard XP cylinder. You have to swap to the 4041 with the 4041-3071 delay cylinder; it delays roughly 120deg to 70deg, adjustable up to about a minute.","Hold-open closer red-tagged on a fire door \u2014 mechanical hold-open arm on a rated opening defeats self-closing; illegal. Swap to a standard/EDA arm, or if hold-open is truly needed, install an electrified fire-alarm-released hold-open.","Cover won't seat / rattles or wrong color on a plated finish \u2014 plastic cover (4040XP-72) is non-handed and powder coat only; plated and custom finishes require the handed metal cover. Order the right cover for the finish and handing."],sources:["https://www.lcnclosers.com/en/products/surface-mounted/4040xp-series-closers.html","https://www.lcnclosers.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/lcn/information-documents/LCN_4040XP_Cut_Sheet_113110.pdf","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-difference-between-the-old-4041-standard-cylinder-and-the-4040xp-standard-cylinder/","https://www.pennerdoors.com/catalog/product/lcn-4040xp-series","https://zensupply.com/lcn-4040xp-surface-mounted-heavy-duty-door-closer-powder-coated-finish/"],schematic:"lcn-closer-anatomy",photo:"photo-lcn-4040"},{id:"norton-7500",name:"Norton 7500 Series Door Closer",category:"Door closers",whatItIs:"The Norton 7500 is the workhorse institutional surface closer you'll find on schools, hospitals, government buildings, and any door that gets abused daily. It's a cast-aluminum-alloy, rack-and-pinion, multi-sized (adjustable spring size 1 through 6) closer that ships Tri-Style, meaning one box does regular arm, top jamb, or parallel arm mounting. It matters because it's the default spec for high-traffic interior and exterior openings that need to hold up past 25 million cycles and stay ADA-legal, and because it's non-handed you can pull one closer for a whole building and stock a single SKU.",specs:["Adjustable spring size 1-6 (spring power set by the power adjustment nut, not by swapping the closer); body is stamped double-digit \"1-6\"","ANSI/BHMA A156.4 Grade 1 certified","UL/cUL listed for fire-rated doors; UL10C listed for positive-pressure fire test","Rated to exceed 25 million cycles","Cast aluminum alloy body; 30-year limited warranty against defect, life-of-building warranty on the aluminum housing","2-1/8 in (54mm) projection; 1-1/2 in (38mm) diameter piston; 5/8 in (16mm) diameter pinion journals","Rack-and-pinion design, non-handed; pinion shaft extends vertically through the body in both directions","Standard separate/independent latch, sweep and backcheck intensity valves (staked, hex-key adjusted)","Backcheck cushioning begins ~75 deg door opening standard; the backcheck position valve is adjustable up to ~20 deg farther (approx 95 deg); optional Enhanced Backcheck (EBC suffix) begins ~15 deg","Optional Delayed Action (DA suffix) with pressure-relief safety valve, effective when the door is opened and released beyond ~70 deg (delay: ~25-30 sec from 90 deg, 2-3 min from 120 deg, 4-5 min from 180 deg); standard 7500 has 4 valves (S/D, L, P, B), 7500DA has 5 valves (S/D, S, L, P, B)","NorGlide multi-viscosity hydraulic fluid, stable performance down to -40 F","Molded high-impact UL-listed plastic cover standard; optional metal cover (M suffix, handed for top jamb), lead-lined metal cover (MLL suffix)","Tri-Style packing: Regular (S), Top Jamb (JS), Parallel (P) mount; heavy-duty arm options include Regular Rigid (R), Parallel Rigid (PR), CloserPlus (CLP), CloserPlus Spring (CPS), and Unitrol (UNI)","Power adjustment nut: 16-1/2 turns (360 deg each) maximum, shipped set at 8 turns (mid-power) from the factory; clockwise increases power, counterclockwise decreases; use an 11/16 in wrench, never a power drill","ADA / ANSI A117.1 compliant for interior doors when adjusted to reduced opening force (5 lbf max on interior doors)","Options: 7500SS corrosion-resistant (non-hold-open only; brass valves, 440 stainless pinion, all-aluminum body, 302/303 stainless components, 8-18 stainless fasteners), 7706EP Extra Power (as much as 22 lbs closing force, non-adjustable, non-ADA), Windstorm / ICC 500 & FEMA 361 rated for single and pair assemblies up to 8'0 x 8'0","Standard finishes include 689 aluminum, 690 statuary bronze (waterborne acrylic, polyester powder coat, and plated finishes available)"],proAdvice:"Spec it right and it disappears into the wall for twenty years. The 7500 is a size 1-6 adjustable closer, so stop thinking in fixed sizes when you order \u2014 you dial the spring power in the field with the power adjustment nut. It ships at 8 turns (mid-power) from the factory, and you get a maximum of 16-1/2 clockwise turns before you're at full power. Clockwise adds power, counterclockwise backs it off, and you use an 11/16 wrench on that nut. Never, ever run that nut with a power drill \u2014 you'll damage the assembly and void the warranty in one trigger pull. And when you mount the body, install it with the tube (spring/power adjustment) end away from the hinge, toward the lock edge \u2014 get it backwards and your labeling and adjustment logic are all flipped.\n\nSizing is where apprentices get burned. Norton's own power chart runs off door width: a 32-inch interior door wants about 5 turns, a 36-inch about 8-1/2 turns, a 42-inch about 11 turns \u2014 and those numbers climb on exterior openings because you've got wind and stack pressure fighting you. Interior office or classroom door, you're realistically living in the size 2-3 range to stay ADA-legal \u2014 5 lbf max to open an interior door. Exterior 36-inch entrance, you want more spring, size 4 and up. Here's the gotcha Norton actually prints on the closer: when you crank the spring down low enough to pass ADA, the closer may not have enough oomph to reliably close AND latch the door based on the opening or site conditions. Nine times out of ten a \"won't latch\" complaint on an interior fire door is somebody who backed the spring off to satisfy a push-force reading and never checked latching. That's a real liability problem on a rated opening \u2014 a fire door that won't positively latch is a failed door. Get your latch speed dialed in before you start giving away spring power, and if you truly can't win both, the opening needs a different solution (lighter door, power operator, or a conversation about the code priority), not a compromised closer.\n\nMounting: it's Tri-Style, one box does regular arm (pull side, hinge face), top jamb (push side, on the frame face), or parallel arm (push side, arm runs parallel to the door). Regular arm gives you the most power efficiency and the best backcheck, so use it when you can \u2014 Norton calls it the most power-efficient application. Top jamb is the next best for efficiency and needs only a 2-1/8 in top rail; it's your best bet for out-swing exterior doors. Parallel arm is what you spec when you can't have the arm sticking out into the opening \u2014 corridors, vandalism-prone areas, where you want a clean look. Just know parallel arm (and slide track) run about 25% less power-efficient than a regular arm per Norton, so size up when you go PA.\n\nOn valves \u2014 the 7500 gives you separate, independent latch, sweep, and backcheck valves, and they're staked so they won't blow out if you're gentle. Never force a valve counterclockwise out of the body or you'll start a fluid leak, and never bottom out or fully close the backcheck valve \u2014 it is not a positive stop. Adjustment order that works every time: set your spring power first, then sweep (full open down to about 10 degrees), then latch (last 10 degrees into the frame), then backcheck last. Target closing time is 3 to 7 seconds from 90 down to 0 \u2014 that's the number an AHJ will check with a stopwatch on an accessible opening. Backcheck cushioning kicks in around 75 degrees standard, and the position valve lets you push it a bit later; if you've got a wall or a radiator the door slams into, spec the EBC enhanced backcheck that grabs the door at about 15 degrees \u2014 that's your school-and-detention move to stop peripheral damage.\n\nRepair vs replace: the 7500 is a sealed hydraulic unit. When it's leaking oil, it's done \u2014 you don't rebuild the cylinder in the field, you replace the closer. The arm, the shoe, the cover, and the mounting brackets are all serviceable parts, though, and you can order the body less-all-parts as a 7500LAP (7500SSLAP for the corrosion body). A bent arm or a stripped cover screw is a cheap part, not a whole closer. And a real money-saver: Norton makes retrofit plates \u2014 RP75-4040 replaces an LCN 4040XP, RP75-4010 replaces a 4010/4011, RP75-M2020 replaces a Rixson M2020 \u2014 so you can drop a 7500 into an existing hole pattern. If you're pulling an old stamped \"1-4\" or \"2-6\" Norton, the newer no-stamp 1-6 body is the direct replacement. For corrosive environments (pools, coastal, wash-down) spec the 7500SS; for X-ray and detention, the lead-lined MLL cover exists. And on any fire-rated opening, remember hold-open is a hard no \u2014 Norton states hold-open arm closers are not permitted on fire door assemblies, so if they want hold-open on a rated opening you tie it to the alarm with a separate electric hold-open device or you're creating a violation the moment you install it. One more warranty trap Norton prints: mounting the closer on the exterior side of an exterior-wall door voids the warranty, so put it on the interior \u2014 top jamb or parallel \u2014 every time.",commonServicingIssues:["Door slams / closes too fast the last few inches \u2014 latch speed valve backed out too far. Fix: close the latch (\"L\") valve slightly with the 1/8 in hex key to slow the last 10 degrees; if the whole cycle is fast, close the sweep (\"S/D\") valve too. Target 3-7 sec from 90 to 0.","Door won't close all the way / won't latch \u2014 spring power dialed too low (common after somebody set it for ADA 5 lbf push force) or gasketing/binding hinges. Fix: add spring power at the nut (clockwise, up to 16-1/2 turns max), verify latch speed has enough snap, and confirm the door swings free on ball-bearing hinges before blaming the closer.","Door won't stay open or drifts \u2014 that's expected on a standard closer; if they want it to hold, they need a hold-open arm (85-110 deg detent) or a CloserPlus/Unitrol arm. Don't try to 'adjust' a standard closer to hold open \u2014 and remember hold-open is not permitted on a fire door.","Oil on the door / arm, greasy film \u2014 cylinder seal failed, closer is leaking hydraulic fluid. Fix: replace the closer body. It's not rebuildable in the field; oil spots mean end of life. Also confirm nobody forced a valve counterclockwise out of the body, which starts a leak.","Door bangs into the wall behind it \u2014 backcheck not set, set too weak, or too late for the opening. Fix: increase backcheck cushioning at the \"B\" valve (never fully close it); the \"P\" position valve moves where cushioning starts. If backcheck at ~75 deg is still too late, spec/replace with the EBC enhanced-backcheck version that grabs at ~15 deg.","Closer works fine warm, sluggish and slow to close when cold \u2014 hydraulic fluid viscosity shift in a cold vestibule. NorGlide is rated to -40 F, so first re-check sweep/latch speed at the cold temp; if it truly won't close cold, the closer is worn or was set marginal to begin with \u2014 bump spring power or replace.","Arm loose / door clunks at the arm \u2014 main arm screw backed off, arm off the pinion flat, or spring preload lost (the power adjustment won't even work right without preload). Fix: re-seat the arm so the \"S\" mark lines up with the flat on the square pinion shaft, re-preload the spring by rotating the arm, tighten the hex washerhead main arm screw; replace a bent or sprung arm as a service part.","Stripped or spinning power adjustment nut \u2014 somebody used a power drill on it. Fix: replace the closer; that warning is on the instructions for a reason. Always turn the nut by hand with an 11/16 wrench."],sources:["https://www.assaabloy.com/hk/en/product-assets/door-closer/norton-75007700-series/assets/documents/Norton_Rixson_7500_Series_Door_Closer_Catalog.pdf","https://hardwarehut.com/images/pdf/norton/nor-7500-install.pdf","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/wp-content/uploads/Norton-7500-Series-Door-Closer-Tri-Style-RegularParallelTop-Jamb-Hold-Open-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://www.rbadoor.com/media/wysiwyg/norton-installation-instructions/norton-7500-installation-instructions.pdf","https://www.nortonrixson.com/en/products/mechanical-door-closers/surface-closers/product-details.aehpdp-7500-series-aeh_norton_12227"],photo:"photo-norton-7500",schematic:"schematic-norton-7500"},{id:"best-sfic",name:"Best SFIC (Small Format Interchangeable Core)",category:"Cores & cylinders",whatItIs:"The Best figure-8 core is the original small format interchangeable core (SFIC), now made by dormakaba but copied by half the industry \u2014 Falcon, Arrow, KSP, Medeco, GMS, and a stack of aftermarket houses all build to the same figure-8 envelope. You drop the whole core into a housing (mortise cylinder, rim cylinder, knob, lever, padlock, cabinet cam lock, exit device) and pull it back out with a control key in seconds, no disassembly. That is the whole point: one core design, and a facilities crew can rekey a building by swapping cores instead of taking locks apart. When somebody says \"IC core\" or \"I-core\" on a commercial job, nine times out of ten they mean a Best-style SFIC \u2014 as opposed to the larger LFIC (Corbin-Russwin, Sargent, Yale, Schlage full-size).",specs:["Core shape: standardized figure-8 (two stacked lobes), interchangeable across all SFIC-compatible housings and brands (Best/dormakaba, Falcon, Arrow, KSP, GMS, etc.); the small format, distinct from the larger LFIC","Keyways: single-section A through R (commonly stocked A, C, F, G, M) plus DD and multi-milled; key blank numbered 1A1[keyway]1 (A = 1A1A1, 6-pin; 7-pin cores use 7-pin blanks). Control, master and change keys share a keyway and differ only in bitting; multiplex master blanks (e.g. 1A1ADEFG1) combine sections to master across keyways","Pin configurations: 6-pin and 7-pin. 7-pin adds a chamber for far more differs and deeper master keying. They are NOT cross-compatible \u2014 a 7-pin key jams in a 6-pin core, and a 6-pin key seats too shallow in a 7-pin","Two independent shear lines: the operating (plug) shear line turns the plug and throws the bolt; the control shear line is formed by the control sleeve and is reached ONLY by the control key","Control key operation: aligns pins at the control shear line \u2014 which sits ten increments above the operating shear line in an A2 system (in each chamber the two key cuts differ by 10) \u2014 then turns approximately 15 degrees clockwise, retracts the control lug, and lets the core slide out of the housing","A2 system: two-step progression, .0125\" depth increment, 10 depths (0-9), MACS 0-9 (no adjacent-cut restriction), pin stack in each chamber totals 23 increments; most common SFIC system, master keyed with Total Position Progression","A3 system: single-step .018\" increment \u2014 cut spacing too tight to hold up as pins/core wear; discontinued for new systems, legacy only","A4 system: single-step .021\" increment; less common, still supported (Best/Arrow) for additions to existing A4 jobs","Standard cam on Best 1E mortise cylinders: 1E-C4 (straight cam). Special cams: C181 for Adams Rite MS deadbolt, plus C140/C161(Corbin-Russwin)/C191(30H latch)/C228(cabinet)/C229/C230/C258(45H deadbolt) for cabinet, hotel, lost-motion and OEM applications","Standard 1E cylinder body: 1-5/32\" diameter. Mortise length 1E-64 (6-pin) 1-1/8\", 1E-74 (7-pin) 1-1/4\". 3E body is the 1-1/2\" diameter mortise (6-pin 1-1/8\", 7-pin 1-1/4\"; standard 3E-C3 cam, RP4 ring package)","Materials: cylinders machined from solid brass or bronze bar stock; set screw mounts diagonally in the cylinder wall to retain the cylinder in the housing","Finishes (typical 1E): 605, 606, 611, 612, 613, 619, 625, 626 (satin chrome), 690; high-security 1EJ/1EK ships 630 (nickel-plated satin stainless)","High-security cylinders: 1EJ7J4 (UL437 listed, 7-pin only, MUST be factory-combinated) and 1EK7K4 (non-UL, 7-pin only, CAN be ordered uncombinated); both accept the special 5C high-security core, use hardened stainless ring/face/keyway disc, and conform to ANSI/BHMA A156.5 Grade 1A. C258 cam supplied standard. CORMAX is dormakaba's current patented restricted keying upgrade (BHMA A156.5 Grade 1 cores), the drop-in upgrade path for existing Best Standard/Premium/MX8 IC housings","Cores order uncombinated (Step: 1-uncombinated) or combinated to a specific keyway (2-combinated), so a locksmith can pin them in-house; specify keymark/keys or designate 'L/C' (less core) for a housing with no core","Retrofit/conversion stock: keep a few uncombinated 7-pin 626 cores plus blanks on the truck. National-account mortise-to-cylindrical jobs often use SFIC-prep locks (the BD suffix on a Schlage ND, and equivalents) and hand you nothing until you're at the door \u2014 an uncombinated core lets you pin to their system on site instead of a second trip."],proAdvice:"Get the pin count right before anything else. Best runs 6-pin and 7-pin and they are not cross-compatible \u2014 a 7-pin key jams in a 6-pin core, and a 6-pin key sits too shallow in a 7-pin. Most legacy institutional jobs (schools and hospitals from the 80s and 90s) are 7-pin because the extra chamber gives you the master-key depth for a big campus. Newer small commercial is often 6-pin. When you inventory a building, pull one core with the control key and count chambers \u2014 do not assume.\n\nUnderstand the two shear lines, because that is what makes SFIC different from everything else. The operating shear line turns the plug and throws the bolt. The control shear line is a second, independent line formed by the control sleeve, and only the control key reaches it \u2014 in an A2 core the control cut sits ten increments above the operating cut in each chamber, which is why the same blank can hit either line depending on how it is cut. Turn the control key about 15 degrees clockwise, the control lug retracts, and the core lifts out. That control key is effectively a master key to every core in the building \u2014 treat it like the crown jewels. If a customer lost their control key you are decoding it from a known core or the operating bitting, impressioning, or worst case drilling and replacing the housing. Never hand a control key to a tenant, and on a new system keep the control keyway off the operating keyways when you can.\n\nOn systems: A2 is what you want to be quoting and stocking. Two-step progression, .0125\" increments, ten depths 0 through 9, pin stacks totaling 23 in each chamber, and because there are no adjacent-cut violations you can put a 0 next to a 9 \u2014 MACS is a clean 0-9. The full theoretical space is usable and Total Position Progression master keying is clean. A3 is dead \u2014 the .018\" single-step spacing is too tight to hold up as cores and pins wear, so it is discontinued for new work; if you find one, plan to migrate it, do not extend it. A4 (.021\", single-step) you will still see on old jobs and Best and Arrow still support it, so if the customer wants to add doors to an existing A4 system, match it rather than converting the whole building \u2014 but if they are rekeying anyway, that is your opening to move them to A2.\n\nBuy cores uncombinated and pin them yourself if you do any volume \u2014 it is cheaper than keyed-to-order and you control the system. You will want a capping press or capping block, the right Best-spec pin kit (.0125\" A2 increments), and a control key or a core to decode against. Aftermarket cores (KSP, GMS, generic) pin identically, but understand you are stepping off genuine Best key control \u2014 CORMAX blanks are patent-restricted and flow through dormakaba sales offices, which is exactly the point on a high-security account. Do not sell a customer restricted key control and then hand them open aftermarket blanks.\n\nRepair vs replace: the core itself almost never breaks \u2014 it is cheap, you swap it, you do not fix it. What fails is the housing set screw stripping, worn plugs after decades of use, or somebody forcing the wrong key. If a mortise cylinder is chewed up, replace the cylinder body (a 1E74-C4 with the right cam and ring) and reuse or rekey the core. Rekeying a whole building by pulling and repinning cores is the cheap, fast play \u2014 that is the entire value proposition of SFIC, and why institutions spec it. When you spec a mortise cylinder, nail the cam and the ring: standard is 1E-C4, Adams Rite MS deadbolts need C181, cabinet locks want C228, a 45H deadbolt wants C258 \u2014 get the cam wrong and the bolt will not throw. Order format is series-core-cam-rings-finish, e.g. 1E74-C4-RP3-626 (RP3 is the 7-pin mortise ring package, RP2 the 6-pin).\n\nLiability and code: SFIC shows up in exit devices and fire-rated openings constantly. The core does not defeat egress \u2014 panic hardware still retracts mechanically from the inside \u2014 but if you are cylinder-dogging a fire-rated exit device, that is a code violation; fire-rated devices must never be held dogged/latch-retracted. On the high-security side, if the threat model calls for a UL437 listing (schools, controlled substances, evidence rooms), spec the 1EJ7J4 \u2014 but remember it must be combinated at the factory, so you cannot just pin it on your bench; the non-UL 1EK7K4 you can order uncombinated when the listing is not required but they still want the 5C core. CORMAX is dormakaba's current patented Grade 1 restricted upgrade and it drops into existing Best IC housings, which makes it the clean migration path when a customer wants patent-protected key control without ripping out hardware.",commonServicingIssues:["Core will not come out with the control key: nine times out of ten it is the wrong control key or a control key from a different system \u2014 verify keyway and system first. If it is the right key, the control lug is binding; work the key while applying light outward pull, and check the housing set screw is not over-tightened into the core","Key turns but the door will not operate (or core spins freely): the core operates but the cam or throw member is worn, wrong, or disengaged. On mortise, confirm the correct cam (1E-C4 standard, C181 for Adams Rite MS, C258 for a 45H deadbolt) and that the cam is timed and reaching the bolt","Customer's key enters but will not turn: check pin count mismatch \u2014 a 6-pin key in a 7-pin core (or vice versa) sits at the wrong depth. Confirm 6 vs 7 by pulling the core and counting chambers before you cut new keys","Lost control key, cannot service the system: decode the control cut from a known core or the operating bitting (control cut sits ten increments above operating in A2), impression it, or worst case drill and replace the housing. Then reissue and lock down the new control key","Core pulls out but pins are pied / rekey went wrong: A2 pins are .0125\" increments \u2014 mixing in A4 (.021\") pins or the wrong kit pies the stack. Verify the system, keep each chamber totaling 23, and use a proper capping block so caps seat fully","Set screw stripped in the housing, core loose or rattling: replace or re-tap the diagonal set screw in the cylinder wall; if the housing threads are gone, replace the cylinder body and reuse the core","Extending an old A3 or A4 system: A3 is discontinued \u2014 do not try to match it, migrate to A2. A4 you can still get (Best/Arrow) to add doors, but if they are rekeying anyway, convert the building to A2 for a cleaner, larger master system","Aftermarket core will not take a genuine Best key smoothly or vice versa: aftermarket (KSP/GMS/generic) pins identically but keyway variants and tolerances differ \u2014 match core brand to key blank source, and do not put open aftermarket blanks on a customer paying for restricted Best/CORMAX key control"],sources:["https://www.bestaccess.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/BA-0030_E-Series-Catalog-09-24.pdf","https://www.bestaccess.com/products/cormax/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/12440229/interchangeable-core-pinning-guide","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/document/10706797/a2-a3-and-a4-interchangeable-core-pin-sizes-and-dimensions","http://www.sopl.us/uploads/1/3/0/1/1301029/keynotes_apr08.pdf"],photo:"photo-best-sfic",schematic:"schematic-best-sfic",relatedGuides:["t-sfic-pinning","t-drilling-out-cores","lishi-commercial","high-security-keyways","t-master-keying"]},{id:"schlage-lfic",name:"Schlage LFIC / Full-Size IC (Everest)",category:"Cores & cylinders",whatItIs:"Schlage's large-format interchangeable core - a figure-eight core that drops into a housing and pulls out with a control key, no lock disassembly, no set screw. Schlage's own name for it is FSIC (Full-Size Interchangeable Core), catalog number 23-030; \"LFIC\" is the generic trade name that also covers the Sargent and Corbin Russwin figure-eights. You see these on campuses, hospitals, and any account that wants one guy with a control key to rekey a whole building in an afternoon. It's the bridge product: Schlage builds the same figure-eight in Schlage, Sargent, and Corbin Russwin formats (Everest 29 S and T families), so a facility can migrate legacy Sargent and Corbin openings onto one Schlage Everest 29 master system over time without ripping out hardware.",specs:["Catalog number 23-030 (Schlage FSIC core), 1/2\" plug diameter, industry-standard figure-eight format, non-handed","6-pin keying length on the standard 23-030; a 7-pin SL FSIC cylinder is also offered, but only in the restricted Everest 29 R keyway","Keyways: Classic C (open, off-patent), Everest C and Everest 29 S families (open), Everest D and Everest 29 T families (restricted, letter-of-authorization required); Everest 29 S is the default keyway on all new Schlage Commercial products and master key systems","Everest C family protected by U.S. utility patents 5,715,717 and 5,809,816 - those EXPIRED July 20, 2014, so the C family is open and off-patent. Everest 29 (introduced Aug 2012) is protected by U.S. utility patent 7,665,337 through Feb 18, 2029; the key-bow design patent runs to Jun 20, 2026","Everest check pin: a side/check pin rides in a hole in the cylinder shell and is lifted by the undercut groove milled up the RIGHT side of the key blade; the plug won't turn without it even if every top/bottom pin is at shear. Check pin part 34-007. There's a dedicated check-pin spring but Schlage doesn't publish a separate part number for it in the service manuals","Control function (Schlage FSIC in a Schlage housing): the extended tip of the control key throws the control pin and retracts the control lug with 15 degrees of CLOCKWISE rotation, then the core pulls straight out. Sargent and Corbin Russwin LFIC housings can differ - confirm the platform before you crank","FSIC core parts (per Everest FSIC service manual): control pin C603-967, actuating pin C603-964, actuating ring C603-956, cylinder cap pin C603-827","Control-chamber pinning follows the Rule of 23 (Control + 10 - Plug Total = build-up portion; total pin stack = 23). Operating chambers use the standard Schlage-15 rule. Two shear lines per control chamber. Control chambers: Corbin Russwin = chambers 2 and 3; Sargent = chambers 3 and 4","Key bitting: MACS 7, two-step progression, .015\" per depth step, .343\" blade width, root depths .335\" (depth 0) down to .200\" (depth 9), depth tolerance +.002\"/-0, spacing +/-.001\"; same spec and code card (C45) as Schlage Classic and Primus XP","Bottom pins 34-300 series (34-300 = .165\" at size 0, .015\" per step up to 34-309 = .300\"); master pins 34-202 (.030\") through 34-209 (.135\"); top pins sized by plug total - 34-101 (.235\", plug total 0-3), 34-102 (.200\", 4-6), 34-103 (.165\", 7-9); pin tumbler spring C503-113","Modular cams introduced July 2010: L583-474 (housings 1-1/8\", 1-3/8\", 1-5/8\") and L583-475 (housings 1-1/4\", 1-1/2\", 1-3/4\"); upgradeable to Everest/Everest 29 Primus XP; construction (disposable) cores available"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight, because it's the number one confusion on the phone: Schlage's full-size IC (FSIC, the 23-030) is a LARGE-format core. When a customer says \"I've got Schlage IC cores,\" ask which - because Schlage also sells small-format (SFIC) cores in the Everest B and Everest 29 R families that drop into Best-style 7-pin housings, and those come in both 6- and 7-pin lengths. Large and small format are not interchangeable - different housing, different control key, different service manual (the SFIC book is MS-C75). The figure-eight you can grab and read the Schlage logo on the plug face is the large-format 23-030. If it's the little Best-shaped one, that's a different animal. Nine times out of ten when a \"Schlage core won't seat\" call comes in, it's a small-format core somebody bought for a large-format housing or the reverse.\n\nOn the Everest itself - Everest and Everest 29 are the check-pin keyways. There's a check pin (34-007) riding in a hole in the shell, and the key has to have the undercut groove milled up the RIGHT side of the blade to lift it, or the plug won't turn even if every top and bottom pin is dead on the shear line. That's a lifesaver and a trap. Lifesaver because it kills the aftermarket-blank problem - Home Depot doesn't stock Everest 29 S blanks, and the S family is patent-protected (7,665,337) clear to Feb 2029. The trap: if you pin a core perfectly and it still won't turn, before you tear it back down, check that you're actually holding an Everest blank and not a Classic C blank that happens to enter the keyway. Classic C will slide into an Everest keyway but has no undercut groove, so it'll never lift the check pin. That single mistake eats more bench time on this core than any pinning error. And remember the old Everest C-family patents (5,715,717 / 5,809,816) expired back in July 2014 - the C family is open and off-patent now, so don't sell a customer on \"patented key control\" unless it's actually Everest 29 S, D or T. When you're done, spray a little lube in and cycle the key - working a key through the core is the only way to reach that side pin, straight out of Schlage's manual.\n\nControl-key discipline is where you earn your keep and where you expose yourself to liability. The control key operates a second shear line in the control chambers to retract the lug and pull the core - it is NOT the master key, and it should never be handed to the customer casually. Whoever holds the control key can pull every core in the building in seconds and walk off with them. Treat control keys like a great-grandmaster. On a properly designed system you carry the control, the top-master and change keys, and you can service the whole job. Geometry: Schlage FSIC in a Schlage housing, the control key turns 15 degrees CLOCKWISE to retract the lug and the core pulls straight out; same 15 clockwise to seat a new one, then back to vertical to lock it in. Sargent and Corbin Russwin LFIC housings can behave differently - check the platform before you crank on it, and never force it. If the lug's fighting you, the core's either the wrong format or the control chambers were mispinned.\n\nPinning: control chambers run the Rule of 23, not the Schlage-15 rule. Every control-chamber stack has to total 23, and the build-up pin spans the gap between the operating shear and the control shear (Control + 10 - Plug Total gives you the build-up portion). Corbin cores control on chambers 2 and 3, Sargent on 3 and 4 - and these cores are top-loaded, you combinate strictly by the numbers because you can't see the pins at the shear line, so there's no trial-and-error. Get a control chamber wrong and either the change key works but the control won't pull the core, or worse, the core pulls with an operating key, which is a security failure you own. Do one chamber completely - bottom, master, build-up, cap and test - then move on when you're learning it. The bitting spec is the friendly part: MACS 7, .015\" per depth step, same code card (C45) as Classic and Primus XP, so your existing pin kit and code machine already handle it.\n\nRepair vs replace and pricing: these cores are cheap enough and the account value high enough that you almost never repair a damaged core - you rekey it or swap it. A 23-030 in a common finish (626 satin chrome, 606 satin brass) runs roughly the price of a decent padlock; fighting a galled plug isn't worth the labor. Where the money and the liability live is the SYSTEM. If a customer's lost a control key or fired a locksmith who had one, that's not a rekey - it's a re-core of every opening the control touched, and you quote it as a project, not a service call. Same on migration jobs: the whole reason a facilities manager buys into LFIC is to fold Sargent and Corbin openings into one Everest 29 master system without swapping hardware - that's a design engagement, you register the restricted keyway through Schlage, and on the restricted D and T families the blanks, cut keys, cylinders and plugs drop-ship directly to the registered end user, not to you, by design. Don't fight that - that's the key control the customer is paying for. And on any opening with egress or fire duty - fire-rated door, stairwell, panic hardware - the core swap can't change the free-egress behavior of the lock; you're only touching the cylinder, but verify the lockset function is still code-compliant before you leave, because \"the new locksmith rekeyed it and now the exit is locked\" is a lawsuit, not a callback.",commonServicingIssues:["Core won't turn even though it's pinned right - almost always the wrong blank: a Classic C key entered the Everest keyway but has no undercut groove to lift the check pin (34-007). Fix: use a genuine Everest or Everest 29 blank and verify the groove on the RIGHT side of the blade.","Control key won't retract the lug / core won't pull - wrong rotation (Schlage FSIC = 15 degrees clockwise; Sargent and Corbin housings can differ) or mispinned control chambers. Fix: confirm the platform, re-verify control chambers (Rule of 23; Corbin ch. 2-3, Sargent ch. 3-4) and rebuild if needed.","Core pulls out with an operating key (security failure) - control chambers combinated so an operating cut reaches the control shear. Fix: decombinate and re-pin the control chambers to the control key only.","'My Schlage core doesn't fit the housing' - a small-format (Best-style SFIC) core ordered for a large-format 23-030 housing or vice versa. Fix: match the format; FSIC/LFIC is the large figure-eight, SFIC is the small Best-shaped core (different control key, MS-C75 manual).","Sluggish or gritty turn after rekey - side/check pin not lubricated, or graphite over-packed so the springs can't compress. Fix: spray a little light lube in the keyway and run a key in and out several times - that's the only way to reach the check pin; go easy on graphite.","Lost or compromised control key on an account - not a simple rekey; whoever holds it can pull every core. Fix: re-core all affected openings, issue a new control, and treat the old control as burned.","Can't source cut keys locally for a restricted job - Everest D and Everest 29 T families are restricted and letter-of-authorization gated; blanks, cut keys, cylinders and plugs drop-ship to the registered end user. Fix: route the order through the authorized Schlage distributor; don't cut off unauthorized blanks.","Conventional Everest key won't work a Primus cylinder after an upgrade attempt - a portion of the undercut groove is milled away on all conventional Everest/Everest 29 keys specifically to block Primus operation. Fix: keep Primus XP cores and keys within their own system; consult the high-security service manual (SC-GN-1001)."],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_Everest_Full_Size_Cylinder_Service_Manual_108484.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_LFIC_Service_Manual_015806.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/fsic-full-size-interchangeable-cores.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/small-format-interchangeable-cores.html","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-are-the-keyway-types-and-patents/"],photo:"photo-schlage-lfic",schematic:"schematic-schlage-lfic"},{id:"kaba-simplex",name:"dormakaba / Kaba Simplex 1000 / L1000 Pushbutton Lock",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"A 100% mechanical pushbutton combination lock \u2014 no battery, no wires, no electronics \u2014 that gives you keyless entry on a Grade 1 cylindrical chassis. The 1000 is the knob version, the L1000 is the lever version with fixed ADA-compliant levers. You see these everywhere: back doors of small businesses, server closets, pharmacy and stock rooms, apartment common doors, gym and pool gates. It holds ONE single access code that everybody shares, and it's programmed right on the door with no power source, which is exactly why it has stayed in the catalog for decades.",specs:["Type: 100% mechanical pushbutton combination lock, heavy-duty cylindrical chassis, ANSI/BHMA Grade 1. 1000 = knob, L1000 = fixed ADA lever. No battery, no power, no wires.","Keypad: 5 solid metal buttons (numbered 1-5, arranged in a vertical column), vandal resistant. Combination can be 1 to 5 buttons; each button used only ONCE; two or more buttons can be pressed simultaneously as a single step. Real-world combination space is small \u2014 roughly 1,081 to 1,084 valid codes; using a simultaneous press (or the seldom-used half-press) roughly doubles it.","Codes: single access code only (one code for all users) \u2014 this is NOT a multi-user lock.","Backset: 2-3/4\" (70 mm) standard, 2-3/8\" (60 mm) available.","Door thickness: 1-3/8\" (35 mm) to 2-1/4\" (57 mm); ships pre-assembled for 1-5/8\" (41 mm) to 2\" (51 mm).","Latch: 1/2\" (13 mm) throw standard; 3/4\" (19 mm) throw optional. ASA 161 / standard cylindrical door prep with extra through-holes for the trim posts. Standard strike plate; ASA strike available.","Fire rating: 3-hour UL/ULC on 'A' labeled doors (latch version).","Handing: knob 1000 is factory-handed left hand but FIELD REVERSIBLE. The L1000 lever is factory-handed and NOT field reversible \u2014 specify hand when ordering. Minimum stile 5\" (127 mm). Weight ~8.25 lb (3.7 kg).","Key override (optional, sold separately) \u2014 the override cylinder sits in the knob/lever face: SFIC (small format, Best-type and equivalents, 6 or 7-pin) or LFIC/removable core (Medeco/ASSA/Yale/Abloy 5 or 6-pin, Corbin Russwin, Schlage, Sargent).","Functions by model (last two digits): ...11 entry only; ...21 entry + key override; ...31 entry + passage/hold-open; ...41 entry + key override + passage; ...76 entry + key override + PRIVACY (lockout). Same suffixes on the L1000 lever (L1011/L1021/L1031/L1041/L1076).","Finishes: standard Satin Chrome 626 (26D) and Antique Brass 609 (05); optional Bright Brass 605 (03) and Bright Chrome 625 (026).","Compliance: D.O.D. 5220.22M and highly weather resistant; ADA-compliant fixed levers on L1000; 3-year warranty.","Combination-change tooling: DF-59 control key (unscrews the combination change plug) plus a spanner wrench; both are supplied with the lock. Factory-default code: press 2 and 4 together, release, then 3."],proAdvice:"Start with how you spec it, because a wrong order costs you a return trip. Figure out first whether the customer wants a knob or a lever \u2014 the Simplex 1000 is the knob, the L1000 is the ADA lever. If anybody's touching that door under an accessibility code, it's the lever, no argument. Now here's the one that bites people: the L1000 lever is factory-handed and it is NOT field reversible. You can flip a knob, you cannot flip that lever, so stand at the door and get the hand right before you order \u2014 inside standing, which way does it swing, where are the hinges. Get the backset right too (2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\"), pick your finish, and make sure you've got at least a 5\" stile to land it on. Prep-wise it's a standard cylindrical / ASA 161 door, so most doors already have the cutout \u2014 it drops in where a normal knob or lever lived.\n\nThen have the honest security conversation, because this is where you earn trust instead of a callback complaint. This lock has ONE shared code. Everybody who's allowed in knows the same number, and the day you fire somebody or lose a tenant, that code is burned and you're standing at the door reprogramming it. The code space is tiny \u2014 you're looking at roughly a thousand real combinations, because each button only gets used once in a code \u2014 so it is not hard to brute force if somebody's patient. And it telegraphs: the buttons that get pressed every day wear shiny and clean while the dead ones grime up, so a sharp person reads the used buttons off the face and the guessing gets a lot faster. There's no audit trail, no per-user codes, no record of who came and went. So sell it for exactly what it's good for \u2014 a back door, a stock room, a pool gate, a server closet where you just want to keep honest people honest and casuals out. Do NOT let a customer walk away thinking this is access control.\n\nAnd when they DO need real access control \u2014 multiple users, the ability to kill one person's code without changing everyone's, a log of who opened the door \u2014 that's your upsell, and you should be ready with it. Step them up to an electronic keypad: a Trilogy or an E-Plex. Now each user has their own code, you delete one without touching the others, and on the audit-trail models you can pull a report. That's a bigger ticket and a happier customer, and you look like the pro who told them the truth instead of the guy who oversold a mechanical lock.\n\nOn programming, know the reality before you promise a customer a quick change. To change the code you need the control key and the door OPEN \u2014 you're working the guts from the inside edge, this is not a punch-in-a-new-code-from-the-keypad deal like the electronic ones. So plan the change for when the door can stand open a few minutes. Teach the customer one trick while you're there: they can press two buttons at the SAME time as a single step, which expands what's possible and makes the code harder to shoulder-surf and harder to guess \u2014 a good habit to build in. And the big one \u2014 change the factory default the moment you install it. Every one of these ships on the same factory combination and half the world knows it. If you leave it on default you've installed an unlocked door.\n\nLast, the mechanical side and the repair-versus-replace call. These are 100% mechanical \u2014 a whole gearbox of little parts, and mechanical parts wear. When it starts feeling sloppy, gritty, or the buttons don't spring back clean, resist the urge to fix it wet. Dry-film lube ONLY \u2014 never spray oil into that keypad. Oil grabs dust, gums up the plunger stack, and you'll be back in six months with a worse problem than you started with. When one's genuinely worn out, make the honest judgment: a rebuild or service kit can bring a good chassis back to life for a fraction of a new unit, but if the chassis is beat or corroded, quote the new one and don't throw good parts at a dead body. And when a customer asks why you're still selling a lock design that's been around forever \u2014 that's the whole point. No battery, no board, nothing electronic to die. It doesn't care if the power's out or the batteries are dead, and in twenty years the only thing that fails is metal wearing on metal, which you can service. That's why it's stayed in the catalog for decades, and that's the pitch.",commonServicingIssues:["Latch retracts on EVERY turn of the knob/lever without entering a code \u2014 'zero code'. Cause: the combination got wiped to zero (bad change, worn mechanism, or a fumbled reset). Fix: run the code-change procedure but SKIP the 'enter existing combination' step since there's no code to clear, then set a fresh code. Don't pull the lock off the door for this \u2014 it's a zero-code, not a dead lock.","Factory default still works / anyone can walk in. Cause: nobody changed the 2+4-then-3 default after install. Fix: change the code on site immediately with the DF-59 and spanner; teach the customer to include a simultaneous two-button press.","Code won't change / 'the reset won't take'. Cause: customer lost the DF-59 control key and/or the spanner wrench, or is skipping the turn-to-stop steps. Fix: bring your own tools; door must be OPEN \u2014 DF-59 out the change plug (turn CCW), enter existing code, engage the change sleeve with the spanner (turn CW to stop, then CCW to stop), set the NEW code, re-secure the plug (CW), don't over-tighten.","Buttons feel mushy, sticky, or don't 'click'. Cause: worn return springs, grit, or someone oiled the keypad and gummed it. Fix: dormakaba 1000/L1000 service/rebuild kit; clean out the oil, dry-film lube only. If badly worn and you're on a ladder anyway, quote a new chassis.","Latch won't fully throw / door won't stay latched. Cause: door thickness or backset out of adjustment, misaligned strike, or a dragging spindle from a bad install. Fix: verify backset (60 vs 70 mm), reset for actual door thickness, align the strike; on rebuilt units check clutch/latch parts from the kit.","Passage/hold-open won't release or won't hold. Cause: the passage feature stuck engaged \u2014 thumbturn or key not returned to vertical. Fix: return the thumbturn/key to vertical to disengage passage before any code change or troubleshooting; if it won't hold, inspect the passage cam/clutch parts.","Locked out, code forgotten, no override core. Cause: entry-only model (...11) with no key override and a lost combination. Fix: if there's an override core, key it open; if not, you're into a chassis pull or the manufacturer's lost-combination reset \u2014 good reason to spec key override (...21) on any door that matters.","Keypad loose or trim spinning on the door. Cause: through-bolts backed off, wrong door-thickness setup, or missing spacers. Fix: re-seat and tighten the trim posts per template; confirm you used the correct prep with the EXTRA through-holes, not a plain cylindrical prep.","Wrong function shipped \u2014 customer wanted lockout, got a ...41. Cause: the ...41 is key override + passage, NOT privacy. Fix: privacy/lockout is the ...76 (L1076); re-order the correct suffix. Verify function AND handing (lever is not field reversible) before you install."],sources:["https://www.dormakaba.com/us-en/offering/products/door-hardware/simplex-mechanical-pushbutton-locks/simplex-l1000--ka_235245","https://www.dormakaba.com/us-en/offering/products/door-hardware/simplex-mechanical-pushbutton-locks/simplex-1000--ka_235259","https://s2.img-b.com/handlesets.com/mediabase/specifications/kaba_ilco/583431/l1000_specs.pdf","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/43910243311003-Simplex-1000-L1000-Changing-the-Code","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/37631849229339-Simplex-L1000-Series-Templates-and-Instructions"],photo:"photo-kaba-simplex",schematic:"schematic-kaba-simplex"},{id:"alarm-lock-trilogy",name:"Alarm Lock Trilogy DL2700 keypad lock",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"The DL2700 is Alarm Lock's flagship standalone electronic keypad cylindrical lever lock \u2014 a battery-powered, keypad-programmable BHMA Grade 1 lockset that drops into a slightly modified ANSI 161 (cylindrical knob/lever) door prep. You see it everywhere: schools, back offices, stock rooms, pharmacies, IT closets, rental units, and anywhere a customer wants keyless PIN entry without pulling wire. It matters because it's the workhorse of the category \u2014 cheap to buy, about an hour to install, 100% keypad programmed with no software required, and a mechanical key override cylinder so you're never locked out. Get this straight up front: the base DL2700 has NO audit trail \u2014 if the customer needs event logging they're stepping up to the DL2800, DL3000/3200, or a Networx model.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 heavy-duty cylindrical lockset; UL listed, FCC certified, ADA-compliant leverset","Capacity: 100 user codes total = 1 Master + 10 Managers + 90 Basic Users, plus 3 one-time Service codes; codes are 3-6 digits (digit count locks in when you set the Master Code)","NO audit trail on the base DL2700 \u2014 logging requires DL2800 (1000 events), DL3000/DL3200, or Networx (up to 40,000 events/lock)","Power: 5 AA alkaline batteries (manual specifies 1.5V Duracell), rated 200,000 cycles / up to ~5 years typical; audible low-battery alert; unique Flex Drive motor","External power option: external 7.5 VDC source, min 100mA, UL-listed supply with battery backup to run without batteries (Black=Negative, Red=Positive)","Backset: 2-3/4\" standard, 2-3/8\" available; door thickness 1-5/8\" to 1-7/8\" (others on request)","Latchbolt: UL listed, fire rated 3 hours, 1/2\" throw, solid brass, deadlocking, reversible; adjustable front for flat or beveled doors (latch front 1-1/8\" x 2-1/4\", wrought brass or stainless)","Strikes included: 1-1/8\" x 2-3/4\" T-strike and 1-1/4\" x 4-7/8\" ASA strike, with wood and metal jamb screws","Standard override cylinder: solid brass 6-pin, pinned 5, SC1 keyway; tailpieces included HW580 (Schlage/Corbin-Russwin CL/Sargent 480 Series/ASSA65-611) and HW620 (Ilco/Lori)","IC core models (special order -Y/-M/-R/-C/-S) accept 6 & 7 pin Best, Arrow, KSP, Medeco Keymark, Kaba Peaks, plus Yale/Medeco/Sargent/Corbin-Russwin CL/Schlage; Alarm Lock tailpiece supplied, IC core NOT included","Non-handed (field-reversible latch); rugged clutch \u2014 outside lever free-spins when locked, inside lever always gives instant egress","12-button all-metal vandal-resistant keypad; 4 keypad tones, silenceable; entry time programmable 5-20 sec","Aux relay: dry contacts, 300mA @ 60VDC max (Blue=Common, Yellow=N/O, Green=N/C); 2-wire remote override input (2 white wires, momentary N/O)","30-second keypad anti-tamper lockout after 3 wrong code entries (remote switch input clears it immediately)","Finishes: US3 polished brass, US26D satin chrome, 10B Duronodic","WP weatherproof variant water-sealed for interior/exterior use, +151\xb0F to -31\xb0F (+66\xb0C to -35\xb0C)","Dimensions: 3-1/4\"W x 8\"H, projects 6-3/8\" at lever","Networx networked cylindrical PIN version talks 900MHz GFSK (50 channels, 10mW) to an AL-IM2/AL-IM3-80211 Ethernet/802.11 gateway (up to 63 locks per gateway), managed in free DL-Windows software \u2014 adds audit trail up to 40,000 events/lock, global lockdown, and scheduling"],proAdvice:"Before you quote this lock, nail down one question: does the customer need to know who went in and when? The DL2700 has no audit trail \u2014 none. It stores no record of any entry, ever. If they say \"I want to see who opened the stockroom last Tuesday,\" the DL2700 is the wrong lock and you'll be eating it on the return. That's when you step them up \u2014 DL2800 gives you 1,000 events of onboard log you pull with a keypad or printer, DL3000/DL3200 add scheduling, and if they want central control across a building you're talking Networx wireless. Get that answered up front, then spec the rest like any cylindrical: backset (2-3/4\" standard, 2-3/8\" available), finish (26D satin chrome is the bread-and-butter, US10B and others on order), and hand. The DL2700 is field-reversible for handing, but confirm door swing before you show up so you're not fighting it on the ladder.\n\nHere's why it earns its keep. You're into a modified ANSI A115 161 cylindrical prep \u2014 same hole pattern as a standard cylindrical lockset with a little extra machining \u2014 and you're done in about an hour on a door that's already bored. It's Grade 1, so it holds up on a commercial opening. Everything is programmed right on the keypad \u2014 no software, no cable, no laptop, no license. Five AA batteries in the housing run it roughly five years of normal traffic. And it's got a mechanical key override, so you are never locked out of that door \u2014 dead batteries, forgotten code, doesn't matter, you carry the key and you're in. That combination \u2014 no software, key backup, hour install \u2014 is exactly why it's the category default.\n\nProgram it right at install and you'll never think about it again. First thing, every time: run the factory clear procedure so you're starting from a known state, not fighting whatever code the last guy or the warehouse left in it. Then decide your code length before you touch anything else, because the number of digits in the Master Code locks the length for every code in the lock \u2014 3 to 6 digits. Set a 4-digit Master and every User code is 4 digits, period. You can't mix lengths later without clearing the whole thing. The DL2700 holds 1 Master, up to 10 Manager codes, and up to 90 User codes \u2014 100-user capacity total \u2014 which covers the vast majority of small commercial jobs. One more thing to tell the customer and to know yourself: this lock uses a clutch. When it's locked, the outside lever free-spins \u2014 turns loose and does nothing. That is not a broken lock. That's the clutch doing its job, and if you don't say it out loud somebody will call you back swearing the lever's stripped.\n\nOn the money side, this is usually a replace-and-move-on part, not a repair job. The electronics are potted and the guts aren't worth chasing for parts \u2014 if the board's dead, you swap the lock. Where you make your margin is the upsell and the service. When a customer's running a pile of Simplex mechanical pushbuttons and they're tired of the whole office knowing the one combination \u2014 or they want to kill a code the day somebody quits \u2014 that's your move to keypad electronics, where you delete one User code in thirty seconds instead of re-setting a mechanical for the whole building. When they outgrow standalone and want to manage doors from an office, that's the Networx wireless conversation. And don't undersell the key override on a dead-battery lockout call \u2014 that's a five-minute in-and-out because you can key in, and a battery-change service is easy recurring money if you put these customers on a two-to-three-year reminder.\n\nLast, the field wisdom that keeps the phone quiet. Batteries don't just quit \u2014 the lock warns you first, giving you weak-battery feedback (repeated beeps and flashes on a valid entry) well before it dies, so tell the customer that a chirping lock means order a battery change, not panic. And the big one that fools people: after three wrong code attempts, the DL2700 goes into an anti-tamper lockout for about 30 seconds and won't respond to the keypad at all. Somebody fat-fingers their code a few times, the pad goes dead, and they call you swearing the lock's fried. It's not \u2014 it's the penalty timer. Wait it out, enter the right code, and it's fine. Know that before you roll a truck, and you'll save yourself a trip diagnosing a lock that's working exactly as designed.",commonServicingIssues:["Symptom: keypad dead / erratic / random beeps out of the box or after a battery change. Cause: factory clear procedure skipped. Fix: batteries out, hold any numeric key 10 sec and release; reconnect and within 3 sec hold the semicolon key for 6 beeps, wait 5 sec, then reprogram.","Symptom: 'my new short codes won't take / it wants more digits.' Cause: Master Code was set to a longer digit count, which locks the length for all codes. Fix: only remedy is Erase All Programming, reload factory defaults, then set the Master to the desired length.","Symptom: outside lever spins freely, customer says handle is broken. Cause: that's the clutch \u2014 outside lever free-turns when locked by design. Fix: none needed; verify a valid code/key retracts the latch and educate the customer.","Symptom: keypad won't respond for about 30 seconds. Cause: anti-tamper lockout after 3 wrong code entries (even spaced apart, even by different users). Fix: wait it out, or trigger the remote switch input to clear it immediately; verify the correct code, no repair.","Symptom: intermittent operation, slow motor, low-battery chirp. Cause: weak/mixed batteries or non-alkaline cells. Fix: replace all 5 AA with fresh matched 1.5V alkaline (manual specs Duracell); never mix old and new.","Symptom: 'I need to see who entered and when' but there's no log. Cause: base DL2700 has no audit trail. Fix: not a defect and no reprogram adds it \u2014 step up to DL2800 (1000 events) or Networx (up to 40,000/lock).","Symptom: exterior-mounted unit fails/corrodes over a season. Cause: standard (non-WP) unit installed outdoors. Fix: replace with the WP weatherproof model rated +151\xb0F to -31\xb0F.","Symptom: remote release button or buzzer-in doesn't unlock. Cause: miswired remote input or relay leads. Fix: momentary N/O across the two white wires (depress ~1 sec); relay is Blue=Common, Yellow=N/O, Green=N/C, 300mA @ 60VDC max.","Symptom: lost/forgotten Master Code, locked out of programming. Cause: no code on file. Fix: use the mechanical key override to get in, then run the battery-disconnect factory clear (Erase All Programming) to reset and set a new Master."],sources:["https://alarmlock.com/media/pdfs/ALA129I-DL2700-LR.pdf","https://alarmlock.com/media/pdfs/81026_ALA532-T2-Sheet-8_2020-FINAL.pdf","https://rapidlockanddoor.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/DL2700.pdf","https://tech.napcosecurity.com/index.php/techlibrary/downloadfile/id/565","https://alarmlock.com/media/pdfs/ALA529-Networx-Trilogy-LR.pdf"],photo:"photo-alarm-lock-trilogy",schematic:"schematic-alarm-lock-trilogy"},{id:"dormakaba-eplex",name:"dormakaba E-Plex 5000 keypad lock",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"The E-Plex 5000 is dormakaba's (formerly Kaba) heavy-duty standalone electronic pushbutton lock \u2014 a self-contained battery keypad lock with no wiring back to a panel. It's the direct competitor to Alarm Lock's Trilogy DL2700/DL3000: one lock, one door, codes stored in the lock, with an audit trail you pull at the door. You see these on commercial back doors, IT/server rooms, stockrooms, clinics, and school interior doors where somebody wants keyless access plus a record of who got in without running a card system. Heads up going in: dormakaba discontinued the whole E-Plex 5000 line effective December 31, 2025, so as of now this is a service-the-installed-base product, not something you're speccing new \u2014 for a fresh keypad install you're steering the customer to a current line, not this box.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 certified (A156.25 electronic locks; also meets A156.2/A156.13 for the mechanical lock body)","100 access codes per door standard; upgradeable to 300 with the optional Excel-based E-Plex software (EP-STD implementation kit)","Audit trail: 3,000 lock events standard, upgradeable to 9,000 with the optional software \u2014 includes logging of the mechanical key-override; software is required to read the events off the lock","User code length adjustable 4 to 8 digits (over 100 million combinations)","Anti-tamper lockout adjustable 3 to 9 invalid attempts, with an adjustable penalty/lockout period of 0 to 90 seconds","Power: four (4) AA alkaline batteries; up to ~180,000 cycles, up to ~400,000 with the high-capacity battery pack. Codes and audit data are retained through a battery death","Three operating modes: normal PIN entry, lockout, and passage (toggle on/off at the keypad, or auto-relock after a programmed 1 to 24 hours)","Cylindrical: 2-3/4\" (70 mm) backset, 1/2\" (13 mm) throw latch with floating faceplate; also offered in 2-3/8\" (60 mm) backset, and a 3/4\" (19 mm) throw beveled-faceplate variant. Door thickness 1-3/8\" (35 mm) to 2-1/4\" (57 mm), pre-assembled for 1-5/8\" to 2\"","Mortise: 2-3/4\" (70 mm) backset; faceplate 1\" (25 mm) handed no-deadbolt/with-deadbolt or 1-1/4\" (32 mm) non-handed no-deadbolt/with-deadbolt; 1\" deadbolt optional","Exit-device trim function available (E.T.) for panic hardware \u2014 compatible with dormakaba 9300/F9300 and a long list of rim devices (Von Duprin 22/98/99, Sargent 8800, Detex 10/F10, etc.); not for mortise or vertical-rod exit devices","UL/ULC listed up to 3 hours for A-label fire doors","Non-handed, field-reversible; ships pre-assembled for left-hand doors","Key override: universal dormakaba 1599 6-pin cylinder with tailpiece (two nickel-silver keys) plus four extra tailpieces on key-in-lever models. Keyway/core options by suffix \u2014 key-in-lever Kaba 90 (XK) or Schlage C (XS); SFIC Best & equivalents 6/7-pin (B); LFIC Corbin Russwin (C), LFIC Sargent (R), LFIC Schlage (S), LFIC Medeco/ASSA/Yale/Abloy 5/6-pin (M). Cores are not supplied with the lock","Winston lever (WL) standard; Winston knob (WK) on cylindrical only. Optional remote-unlock pigtail (wire your own momentary switch). Finishes: 626 satin chrome (std), 605 bright brass, 606 satin brass, 625 bright chrome, 676 black with satin chrome accents (while supplies last), 744 dark bronze with brass accents (simulated 10B/613)"],proAdvice:"The E-Plex 5000 sits in the same market slot as the Alarm Lock Trilogy \u2014 spec it the same disciplined way: grade, function, then keyway. The base cylindrical is Grade 1, 2-3/4\" backset, four-AA battery, 100 codes, and a 3,000-event audit trail (9,000 only if the customer bought the software upgrade \u2014 don't quote 9,000 as standard, that's the number resellers love to repeat and it's wrong out of the box). That audit trail, including logging the mechanical key override, is the whole reason you sell this over a $150 keypad deadbolt.\n\nThe big thing to have in your head now: dormakaba pulled the plug on the E-Plex 5000 as of December 31, 2025, and the Excel-based E-Plex software is legacy too. That changes your whole conversation. For a new job, you're not speccing this \u2014 you're pointing the customer at a current dormakaba electronic line or a Trilogy. Where the 5000 still lives is the installed base: you're servicing, re-keying, pulling audits, and swapping dead units. Keep a known-good copy of the E-Plex software and the interface cable on the truck, because you will not be downloading a fresh installer from dormakaba on a legacy job.\n\nOn keyway: the standard override is the dormakaba 1599 6-pin, and the lock ships with four extra tailpieces so you can drop in a lot of the common conventional cylinders. If the building runs an IC system, order the right suffix and core the lock to the house key \u2014 B for SFIC Best, C/R/S/M for the LFIC brands. Cores don't come with the lock, so if you show up with an 'IC-prep' unit and no core, you're not finishing that door.\n\nRepair-vs-replace: on a discontinued line, a truly dead board or worn clutch on an old 5000 is usually a replace-the-unit call, not a parts hunt \u2014 and since it's EOL, that replacement conversation is really a 'here's the current model' conversation. What you CAN fix on the truck is 90 percent of the calls: batteries, harness reseat, lockout wait-out, door/strike alignment, re-core. If it's egress on a fire door, respect the A-label rating \u2014 this thing is UL/ULC listed to 3 hours, so any strike, closer, or hardware you pair it with has to keep that rating and keep free egress from the inside. Don't kill egress to solve a security complaint.",commonServicingIssues:["Lock dead / keypad won't light or beep -> batteries flat (four AA in the interior housing) -> replace all four with fresh alkaline, don't mix old and new; if still dead, check the battery contacts and the ribbon/harness to the outside keypad. Nine times out of ten a 'dead E-Plex' is just batteries.","Valid code entered but lever won't retract latch -> motor/clutch or keypad harness, or the code got deleted / lockout tripped -> confirm you're not sitting in anti-tamper lockout (wait out the penalty, up to 90 seconds), test the master code; if the motor buzzes but nothing engages, the clutch/drive is worn \u2014 pull the interior housing and inspect. Key-override to get the customer in meanwhile.","Locked out / forgot master code -> default was never changed on install, then lost -> use the mechanical key override to get in, then factory-reset per the model procedure and re-enroll codes. Always change the default master on install so this call is 'lost my code,' not 'never set one.'","Won't hold codes / resets itself / erratic -> low-battery brownout or a loose keypad ribbon -> fresh batteries first, then reseat the connector between the outside escutcheon and the inside board. Data is supposed to survive a battery death, so persistent code loss points at the harness or board, not the batteries.","Passage mode stuck on (door stays unlocked) -> passage got toggled on, or the auto-passage/relock schedule (1 to 24 hours) is active -> toggle passage off at the keypad or reprogram the auto-relock setting; check nobody left it in passage after hours.","Sluggish/binding lever, latch not throwing fully -> door and strike misalignment, or a dry mortise case -> fix the door and strike first, lube the case. A dragging latch chews through motor life and battery, so this is a real service item, not cosmetic.","Key override won't turn / wrong key -> cored to the wrong keyway or an unmatched IC core -> verify the suffix matches the facility keyway (1599 standard, or B/C/R/S/M for the IC brands) and re-core to the building system so the master actually works this door.","Audit pull fails / can't get events off the lock -> missing interface cable, or the now-legacy E-Plex software isn't installed -> you need the E-Plex software and the interface cable to read events; the lock won't display them on the keypad. Since the software and the whole 5000 line are EOL as of Dec 31, 2025, keep a known-good install and cable on the truck for these legacy jobs."],sources:["https://www.dormakaba.com/us-en/offering/products/electronic-access-data/electronic-door-locks/e-plex-5000-series--ka_235209","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/37711038547739-E-Plex-5000-Series-Templates-Instructions-Manuals-and-Technical-bulletins","https://cbdigitaletl-prod.s3.amazonaws.com/products-pdf/3261_11_e-plex-5000-series.pdf","dormakaba E-Plex 5000 fact sheet, doc KAA1054 (dormakaba.com) \u2014 original link retired.","https://zensupply.com/dormakaba-e-plex-5000-series-electronic-pushbutton-cylindrical-lever-lock/"],photo:"photo-dormakaba-eplex",schematic:"schematic-dormakaba-eplex"},{id:"detex-eax500",name:"Detex EAX-500 Exit Alarm",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"The EAX-500 is Detex's bread-and-butter battery-powered standalone door alarm \u2014 the little tapered gray or black box you see bolted to the secure side of a restricted or emergency exit, sounding roughly 100dB off a piezo horn the moment somebody cracks the door without keying it off first. It's fully self-contained: a single 9V battery, a reed switch reading a magnet on the frame or mullion, and a control cylinder you turn to arm and disarm. You'll find these on stockroom doors, back-of-house exits, pool gates, school perimeter doors, and anywhere the customer wants \"keep this door shut\" deterrence without running power or tying into an access system. The redesigned 500 shrank the older EAX-300 footprint and added a patented cam assembly that self-adjusts to different cylinder lengths without spacers, plus a plastic magnet-locator template \u2014 that combo is why it's the go-to for a fast retrofit on a narrow stile.",specs:["Sound output: approximately 100dB piezo horn","Power: single 9VDC battery (Detex P/N PP-5567); Low Battery Alert chirps siren + flashes red LED at 45-second intervals when the battery drops to ~7V (cut the LV Opt jumper to disable)","Dimensions: 2.10\" W x 7.70\" L x 2.375\" D; tapered cover clears narrow stile doors","Cylinder: sold separately. Parts drawing calls for a mortise cylinder with a STANDARD YALE CAM. Factory options: MC65 Detex mortise cylinder (Schlage 'C' keyway, 1-1/8\", 6-pin housing keyed 5-pin) or IC7 7-pin interchangeable-core housing (fits most standard 7-pin IC-format cores). OKC option uses a rim cylinder (Detex ECL-445K-7)","Patented cam assembly (kit P/N 102655) auto-adjusts for cylinder length without spacers; optional collar ECL-1595 required if the cylinder sits more than 5/16\" proud","Control-key operation: key inserted/removed only in the vertical home position. Turn CCW to ON = armed; turn CW to ~2 o'clock OFF = disarmed. Key required to reset after an activation","Default arming delay: 15 seconds (red LED blinks twice, green holds through the delay, then siren chirps + red flashes 3x = armed)","Field-selectable 2-minute auto-rearm via Switch/Shunt A ON (custom timing programmable by Detex)","Extended vs. non-extended (timed) bypass on Switch/Shunt A: OFF = extended, won't arm while door open; ON = timed, arms regardless of door position and alarms if door still open at end of delay","Field-selectable status indicator (Switch/Shunt B ON): red LED blinks ~every 3 sec when armed, green ~every 3 sec when disarmed (shortens battery life)","OKC (Outside Key Control): bypasses the alarm for entry and rearms 15 seconds after the door closes; will not fully arm/disarm like the inside key","Silent Arming: field-activated by holding the OKC lever closed while installing the battery with the key at ON (two chirps = on, one chirp = off)","Magnet/reed: intelligent auto-handing circuit picks left, right, or external reed switch and stores the choice in memory, so battery swaps don't require re-handing; uses the plastic magnet locator (P/N 102607) for alignment","Finishes: GY (grey), BK (black)","Listings: UL Classified Miscellaneous Fire Door Accessory; ANSI/BHMA A156.29 certified","Variants: EAX-500W weatherized (no double-door and no SK/wall-mount option); EAX-500SK factory-prepped for wall mount with surface contacts; KS Key Stop option (key removable only when armed); SK1/SK2 add white MS-1039S contacts, SK3/SK4 add brown MS-1059S, SK5/SK6 add grey MS-2049S","Warranty: Detex Limited Warranty, 3-year mechanical / 1-year electrical"],proAdvice:"Spec it right and this alarm installs in twenty minutes; spec it wrong and you'll make two trips. First thing \u2014 the cylinder is NOT included. Detex's own parts drawing calls for a mortise cylinder with a standard Yale cam, so don't reach for an off-cam Schlage-style cylinder out of the bin. Order it factory-installed if you want it turnkey: MC65 for a Detex mortise cylinder (Schlage 'C' keyway, 6-pin housing keyed to 5), or the IC7 7-pin core housing if the account is on an interchangeable-core system. The patented cam assembly self-adjusts for cylinder length so you don't stack spacers, but if the cylinder sits more than 5/16\" proud you need the ECL-1595 collar. Keep a couple of those and a 103779 cylinder-nut wrench on the truck \u2014 the nut lives in a tight pocket and you'll strip your knuckles without it.\n\nUnderstand the arm/disarm logic before you're standing at the door with the customer watching. Key goes in vertical only. CCW is ARM, CW is DISARM \u2014 burn that in. On arming you get a 15-second delay: red blinks twice, green holds, then three chirps and red x3 and the box is live. That delay is your friend on a nuisance install \u2014 it lets an authorized person key-and-go without tripping it. If the customer wants the alarm to auto-reset after an event, that's Switch/Shunt A ON for the 2-minute auto-rearm; leave it OFF and the alarm stays latched until somebody keys it off, which is what most security-minded accounts actually want because a latched alarm proves the door was breached. And know about Silent Arming \u2014 if a customer hates the arming chirps, you can toggle it by holding the OKC lever closed while you drop the battery in with the key at ON (two chirps means you turned it on).\n\nThe single most important field decision is extended vs. timed bypass, and it's the one guys get wrong. Switch/Shunt A OFF = extended bypass: the unit will NOT arm while the door is open, so a propped door just sits there disarmed \u2014 good for a door people legitimately hold open. Switch A ON = timed bypass: it arms regardless of door position and screams if the door's still open at the end of the delay \u2014 that's your \"this door should never be propped\" setting. Match it to how the door actually gets used or you'll be back for a \"false alarm\" call that isn't false at all.\n\nMagnet handing trips up first-timers. When you first hook up the battery the box flashes and chirps five times \u2014 that's it clearing memory and going into handing mode, NOT a fault. Key it ON with the door OPEN (red blinks twice), then close the door or bring the external magnet in, and it chirps and picks its side automatically \u2014 red LED flash means left or external, green means right. Once it's handed, the side is stored, so a battery change later does not require re-handing. If you ever move the box to the other side of a door, you re-hand by pulling it off, momentarily shorting the Mag Rst (Test) jumper to force those five chirps again, then repeating the handing. Use the plastic magnet locator template \u2014 eyeballing the gap is how you end up with a door that alarms every time the wind loads it.\n\nOn repair-vs-replace: at the price of this unit, the labor math is brutal on anything past the battery, the cover lock, or the cam assembly. Dead battery, bad magnet alignment, wrong shunt setting \u2014 fix those on the spot. But if the cylinder microswitch lever snapped (device won't arm even though the key's turning fully CCW and it's handed), Detex wants the PCB pulled and returned for the microswitch \u2014 that's a board pull and a mail-back, and for most accounts it's cheaper and faster to swap the whole EAX-500. Do not return product to the distributor \u2014 Detex explicitly wants those calls to their tech line (800-729-3839, option 2). And mind the warranty split: 3 years mechanical but only 1 year electrical, so a two-year-old board failure is on the customer's dime.\n\nCode and liability: this is a secondary security device, not life-safety egress hardware. It's a UL Classified Miscellaneous Fire Door Accessory and ANSI A156.29 certified, but it does NOT provide delayed egress \u2014 do not let a customer believe a plain EAX-500 legally holds someone inside. If they need to actually delay egress on an exit door, that's a different animal (a delayed-egress exit device tied to the fire alarm, 15/30-second release, signage per IBC/NFPA 101). Mounting an EAX-500 as a lock or physically blocking egress will fail inspection and hang the liability on you. The alarm annunciates the exit; the door must still free-egress. Keep that line clean when you're quoting.",commonServicingIssues:["Chirp + red-LED flash every 45 seconds -> low battery (~7V) -> replace with a fresh 9V (PP-5567). If a customer explicitly doesn't want the low-battery chirp you can cut the LV Opt jumper, but I'd rather they hear it than lose the door.","Alarm sounds after the door closes and rearms -> magnet out of alignment or gap too wide -> re-set the magnet with the locator template and shim as needed; nine times out of ten it's a lazy magnet gap, not a bad board.","Won't arm at all when you key it CCW -> check the obvious three first: dead battery, cylinder installed wrong / key not turned FULLY counter-clockwise to ON, or it never got handed. Re-hand it (pull it, short the Mag Rst jumper for the 5 chirps, key ON with door open, close). Only if all that's good is it the cylinder microswitch lever \u2014 and per Detex that means pulling the PCB and returning it for the microswitch, not a field fix.","Flashes and chirps 5 times when you plug in the battery -> that is NOT a fault \u2014 it's normal handing mode clearing memory. Just complete the handing: key ON with the door open, then close the door and let it pick its side.","Wired external magnet not working after a wall-mount (SK) conversion -> board wasn't modified for the external switch -> disconnect power, cut the JP1 jumper, then with battery connected short the Test pins for 5 chirps and re-hand. On MS-2049 use only the black and white leads to the green PCB connector \u2014 the red wire is not used. (SK/external wiring is not available on the weatherized 500W.)","No armed/disarmed light and the customer wants one -> status indicator is just switched off -> set Switch/Shunt B to ON (warn them it shortens battery life a bit).","Customer complains it won't auto-reset after an event -> Switch/Shunt A is OFF -> flip it ON for the 2-minute auto-rearm \u2014 or leave it off on purpose if they want the alarm to latch as proof of a breach.","Cover won't come off / can't get in to service -> it's the control-key cover lock (PP-5572 lock, nut and keys) -> turn the key CCW roughly 8 turns to unlock it. Lube the threads on reassembly so the next tech isn't cursing you, and don't over-tighten going back on.","'False alarms' on a propped door -> wrong bypass mode -> they're in timed bypass (Switch A ON) on a door people hold open; move to extended bypass (Switch A OFF) so it won't arm while the door's open."],sources:["https://www.detex.com/products/life-safety-security-door-hardware/alarms/eax-500/","https://www.detex.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/EAX-500.pdf","https://www.detex.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/101321.pdf","https://www.detex.com/products/life-safety-security-door-hardware/alarms/eax-500w/","https://nationallocksupply.com/detex-eax-500-mc65-battery-powered-exit-alarm-w-mortise-cylinder/"],photo:"photo-detex-eax500",schematic:"schematic-detex-eax500"},{id:"securitron-maglock",name:"Securitron M-Series Magnalock (Electromagnetic Lock)",category:"Electric locking",whatItIs:"Securitron's M-series (M32/M62/M82B) is the direct-hold electromagnetic lock you find holding perimeter and stairwell doors, aluminum-and-glass storefronts, Herculite glass entries, and interior access-controlled openings. A DC coil in the magnet body bonds to a steel armature (strike plate) on the door leaf; energize the coil and it holds, cut power and it drops. It is inherently fail-safe by physics \u2014 no power, no hold \u2014 which is exactly why it lives on egress doors and why it comes with a stack of code baggage. The M62 (1200 lbf) is the workhorse; the M32 (600 lbf) covers lighter interior doors and gates, and the M82B (1800 lbf) is for high-security or abused openings.",specs:["Holding force: M32 = 600 lbf [272 kg]; M62 = 1200 lbf [544 kg]; M82B = 1800 lbf [816 kg] (Securitron install manual, Section 2)","Dual voltage 12/24 VDC, AUTOMATIC/auto-sensing \u2014 the lock circuit selects the operating voltage itself, there is NO field jumper to set. Must be full-wave rectified DC; the manual explicitly calls half-wave rectifier or AC power 'unacceptable' and lists it as the #1 cause of reduced holding force","Current draw: M32 = 300 mA @12V, 150 mA @24V; M62 = 250 mA @12V, 150 mA @24V; M82B = 350 mA @12V, 200 mA @24V. Low-current, non-inductive design (internal circuitry suppresses inductive kick-back; MOV noise suppression included)","Magnet body dimensions: M32 = 8\" L x 1.88\" H x 1.6\" D [203x48x41mm]; M62 = 8\" L x 3\" H x 1.75\" D [203x76x44mm]; M82B = 12\" L x 3\" H x 1.75\" D [305x76x44mm]. M62 strike is 6\" L x 2.75\" H x 0.5\" D","Certifications (per M62 datasheet): UL 10C Listed (fire rated 3 hours); ANSI/UL 294 Listed; CAN4-S104 / CAN/ULC-S533 and CSA-C22.2 No.205 Listed; CSFM approved. ANSI/BHMA A156.23, GRADE 2 (E08501-1000) \u2014 this is a Grade 2 device, not Grade 1","Finish: satin/brushed stainless steel magnet body, fully sealed (potted) electronics, rust-resistant plated ferrous surfaces, weatherproof for interior/perimeter/exterior use. Dress covers available in four architectural finishes (Securitron does not publish a single US finish code for the lock body itself \u2014 spec the dress cover finish to match the opening)","Floating/pivoting armature: strike mounts on 2-3 rubber washers and pivots around a single center 5/16-18 sex-bolt screw to self-align flat to the magnet face. Manual is explicit: 'If the strike is mounted rigidly, the Magnalock will NOT hold.' Do NOT put the washers on the roll pins","BondSTAT (\"B\") magnetic bond sensor: SPDT dry contact, rated 30 VDC max / 1 A max \u2014 reports actual magnetic bond, not just current. Secure-zone sensing range is model-specific (M62B +/- 5/16\" X, +/- 1/8\" Y per Table A)","DPS (\"D\") door position sensor: isolated SPDT reed switch with internal resettable protection device, rated 30 VDC max / 125 mA max \u2014 reports door open/closed","Patented instant-release circuit with no residual magnetism (magnet lets go cleanly on power drop, no sticky de-mag lag). Ships with 10 ft of jacketed stranded conductor cable","MagnaCare lifetime replacement, no-fault, no-questions-asked warranty on Securitron Magnalocks","Mounting brackets by application: UHB (universal header, extends frame 1\"-1.5\"), SFP (stop filler plate, extends 1.25\"), ASB (aluminum shim for 1/2\" blade stops/glass aluminum frames), CWB (concrete/wood header), GDB and HEB-3G (glass, use AKG adhesive kit), FMK-SL/FMK-SW (FLEX-MOUNT gate), Z/ZA brackets for F-series face-mount on in-swing. AOS/OS offset strike and ASS split strike available; split-strike holding force per leaf: M32=230 lbf, M62=550 lbf, M82=850 lbf"],proAdvice:"Start with the code conversation, not the hardware, because a maglock on an egress door is a life-safety device first and a security device second. There are two legal ways to release one and you need to know which you are building. The first is sensor-release (IBC 2018/2021 Section 1010.1.9.9, Sensor Release of Electrically Locked Egress Doors \u2014 that was 1010.1.9.8 back in the 2015 IBC, so watch which code year your jurisdiction is on; NFPA 101 7.2.1.6.2): a motion sensor over the door unlocks it whenever someone approaches from the egress side, the lock drops on loss of power to the sensor OR the lock, it drops on fire alarm and sprinkler activation and stays unlocked until that system is reset, AND you must have a manual unlocking device mounted 40 to 48 inches AFF within 5 feet of the door, marked \"Push to Exit,\" that directly interrupts power to the lock independent of everything else and holds it unlocked for a minimum of 30 seconds. That 30-second latch is the whole reason for a timer like the EEB2 in the circuit \u2014 Securitron's own wire-gauge worksheet literally shows an M62 + DK-26 reader + XMS motion + EEB2 timer on one 12V run. The second legal path is door-hardware release (IBC 2018/2021 Section 1010.1.9.10, Door Hardware Release of Electrically Locked Egress Doors): a REX switch built into the panic bar or lever hardware directly interrupts lock power the instant someone operates the hardware \u2014 immediate release, one hand, obvious operation, no motion sensor and no 30-second button required, and this path does not by itself require fire-alarm release. Nine times out of ten when an inspector red-tags a maglock it is because somebody wired the button through the access panel relay instead of breaking power directly, or they left the fire alarm interface off entirely. Fail-safe is not optional \u2014 power drop, and on the sensor-release path fire alarm and sprinkler, all have to drop that magnet.\n\nOn spec'ing: match holding force to the door and the abuse, not to ego. An M62 at 1200 lbf covers the vast majority of commercial openings \u2014 interior offices, stairwells, storefronts. Go M32/600 for light interior doors and pedestrian gates, and M82B/1800 only where you have genuine pull-attack or a heavy/high-traffic exterior door. Overspec'ing an M82B onto a flimsy aluminum frame is pointless \u2014 the lock will out-hold the frame and the frame tears first; the manual tells you to verify the mounting area meets or exceeds the lock's holding force before you drill. Confirm your power: these are automatic dual-voltage 12/24 VDC \u2014 there is no field-select jumper, the lock senses the voltage itself \u2014 but they demand clean full-wave rectified DC. Feed a half-wave supply or an unfiltered wall-wart and holding force craters and the customer complains the door \"pops\" \u2014 that is not a bad magnet, that is dirty power, and Securitron names it as the number-one cause of reduced hold. Run the wire gauge; Securitron's Appendix B worksheet puts that M62-plus-reader-plus-motion-plus-timer load at 18 AWG for a 200-foot one-way run at 12V, and undersized wire is the silent killer of holding force on long runs.\n\nThe single most important install detail is the floating armature. The strike plate MUST pivot on its rubber washers around the center sex bolt \u2014 that float is what lets the flat armature face self-align dead flat to the magnet even when the door is slightly racked. Bolt that strike down rigid, put the washers on the roll pins instead of around the center screw, or crank the center screw tight and you have just killed your holding force; the manual says it flat out \u2014 mount it rigid and it will NOT hold. Never drill into the magnet or the strike for any reason (there's a WARNING label on the template), never use sheet-metal screws \u2014 blind nuts only into aluminum or hollow-metal frames \u2014 use the supplied LOCTITE on the mounting screws, and don't over-tighten the magnet screws or you'll strip the collapsed blind nuts in the frame. For blade-stop aluminum frames use the ASB shim, for concrete or wood headers the CWB, and for glass the GDB or HEB-3G with the AKG adhesive kit \u2014 trying to hard-mount without the right bracket is where you lose a day.\n\nRepair-vs-replace is easy on these: the electronics are fully potted, so there is nothing to fix inside a dead magnet \u2014 it's a replacement, and with MagnaCare the magnet itself is a no-fault, no-questions-asked warranty swap. What you actually service is the strike (rust, sticking, lost float), the wiring, and the release logic. Spec the sensor versions deliberately: BondSTAT (\"B\") tells you the magnet is actually BONDED (real magnetic field, real security), while DPS (\"D\") only tells you the door is closed \u2014 a door can be closed with a weak or failed bond, so if the customer needs true \"is it locked\" monitoring, sell them BondSTAT, and use \"BD\" when they want both. On finish, don't promise a US32D code the way you would on a mortise lock \u2014 Securitron builds the magnet in satin stainless and offers dress covers in four architectural finishes, so quote the cover finish to match the opening rather than a single spec number. It's UL 10C fire rated 3 hours, UL 294 listed, and A156.23 GRADE 2 (not Grade 1 \u2014 don't let that error onto a submittal, an inspector or spec writer will catch it). Last liability note, drill it into the apprentice: Securitron prints it in the manual in all caps \u2014 THE END USER AND INSTALLER ARE LIABLE FOR FIRE AND BUILDING CODE COMPLIANCE. If that door doesn't free-egress on power loss and on fire alarm, you own the outcome.\n\nKnow the rest of the field, because the Magnalock is the reference point and not the only magnet you will meet. SDC's EMLock family (the 1581 surface unit at 650 lbf, the concealed 1561 shear at 2000 lbf), RCI/dormakaba MultiMag 8310 at up to 1200 lbf per magnet, DynaLock 3000 at 1500 lbf, Seco-Larm's Enforcer E-941SA in 600 and 1200 lbf, and Schlage/Locknetics M490 single and M492 double, both rated to the BHMA 1500 lbf standard, all turn up on service calls. Securitron's own catalogue runs wider than the M-series too... DM62 puts two M62 magnets in one housing for a pair of doors with no mullion, and the M680E EcoMag is the current low-power 1200 lbf unit. The badge on the housing changes the part number and almost nothing else that matters: every one of these is fail-safe by physics, every one wires normally-closed at the relay so the coil is energised to hold, and every one needs the fire interface and the manual release breaking lock power in series ahead of it. Get that release chain right and the brand is a purchasing decision rather than an engineering one.",commonServicingIssues:["Door 'pops' open or won't hold under a firm pull -> almost always weak bond, not a dead magnet. Check for full-wave rectified DC first (the manual names half-wave/AC and undersized wire on a long run as the #1 cause of reduced force), verify rated voltage AND current at the magnet, then check the strike floats and pivots. Rigidly mounted strike, over-tight center screw, or washers on the roll pins = no full flat contact = no hold.","Magnet won't release / door stays locked after power cut -> confirm ZERO voltage and ZERO current actually reach the coil (a stuck relay or bad button wiring can backfeed; reverse polarity also fails). If power is truly off, the strike face is sticky/rusty \u2014 clean magnet and armature with rubbing alcohol (indoor) or a silicone-based cleaner/lube like Super Lube with PTFE (outdoor). Never petroleum products, steel wool, or sandpaper; a plastic dish scrub pad is fine.","Inspector red-tags the opening / fails egress -> release logic is wrong. Sensor-release (IBC 1010.1.9.9) needs a 'Push to Exit' button 40-48\" AFF within 5 ft that DIRECTLY breaks lock power for a minimum 30 seconds, plus fire-alarm and sprinkler drop that stays released until reset. Hardware-release (IBC 1010.1.9.10) needs the REX in the panic/lever to directly interrupt power. If the button runs through the access relay instead of breaking power directly, it fails.","BondSTAT never reports 'secure' though door is closed -> strike alignment/pivot off, so the magnetic bond is weak even though the leaf is shut, or the strike is outside the model's secure-sensor range (M62B is +/- 5/16\" X, +/- 1/8\" Y). Realign and re-center the strike so it pivots flat to the magnet face; clean any debris/rust off the contact surfaces; verify voltage/current is in spec.","DPS reports wrong door status -> strike/armature misaligned to the sensor range, door not fully closing, or the internal resettable protection device tripped. Re-square the strike within Table A tolerance, verify full closure, and check switch voltage/current is within the 30 VDC / 125 mA rating.","Reduced holding force over time on exterior doors -> rust and grime on the armature face. Securitron's schedule: clean yearly indoors, every 6 months where minor rust occurs, every 3 months in severe conditions; keep the rubber washers elastic and the strike float free, and keep the faces clean.","Access panel resets or noise on the bus when the magnet drops -> confirm no stray voltage from magnet body to door frame (should read zero \u2014 a floating ground here shows up as intermittent controller glitches). These locks are low-current and non-inductive with an included MOV, so noise usually traces to grounding or a suppression problem on the release relay/board, not the coil itself."],sources:["https://www.securitron.com/content/dam/assa-abloy/americas/ems/securitron/document/AADSS1058203.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/M62-InstallationManual.pdf","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m62","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m32","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m82","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/dm62","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m680e","https://sdcsecurity.com/docs/SDC-Catalog-1-MagLocks.pdf","https://www.dormakaba.com/us-en/offering/products/electronic-access-data/electromagnetic-locks-maglocks/rci-83108320-multimag--ka_500245","https://www.seco-larm.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/MI_E-941Sx-xx00_211025.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_M490_Electromagnetic_Locks_Data_Sheet_104204.pdf"],schematic:"maglock-mount",photo:"photo-securitron-maglock",relatedGuides:["maglocks","fail-safe-fail-secure","low-voltage-wiring","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"]},{id:"hes-1006",name:"HES 1006 Series Electric Strike",category:"Electric locking",whatItIs:"The HES 1006 is the heavy-duty, ANSI/BHMA A156.31 Grade 1 electric strike in the HES (Hanchett Entry Systems, an ASSA ABLOY brand) lineup \u2014 the one you reach for on a busy commercial opening where a lighter strike would get chewed up. It's a stainless steel, non-handed strike that drops into a standard ANSI 4-7/8\" frame prep and releases the latchbolt on an electrical signal; with the right faceplate and deadbolt-recapture kit it also retains, releases and recaptures a mortise deadbolt up to 1\". You see it on aluminum-and-glass storefronts, office suite doors, stairwell doors, and anywhere a cylindrical or mortise lock needs remote or access-controlled release without swapping the whole lockset. It matters because its patented dual-interlocking-plunger design lets it stand up to pull-force and abuse that would defeat a smaller strike.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.31 Grade 1. UL 10C fire rated (per distributor/HES listings: fail-secure only \u2014 3-hour single door, 1-1/2-hour double door); also ASTM E152 / NFPA-252 fire-door compliant and CAL FIRE listed. UL 294 access control listed and UL 1034 burglary-resistant listed (suitable for outdoor use)","Holding force: HES rates static strength at 2,500 lb (fail secure) and dynamic strength at 350 ft-lb, and states the unit is tested to EXCEED 3,000 lb static. Endurance factory-tested to exceed 1,000,000 cycles","Dual voltage 12 or 24 VDC continuous duty \u2014 set by which plug-in pigtail you connect (both a 12 VDC and a 24 VDC pigtail ship in the box). Non-handed","Solenoid electrical ratings: 12 VDC = 25 ohms / 0.48 A (480 mA); 24 VDC = 100 ohms / 0.24 A (240 mA). Solenoids rated at +/- 10% of indicated value \u2014 any input over 10% of the rating can cause severe damage and voids the warranty. Meter the supply under load before you connect","Fail secure is standard; fail safe is the -F suffix (e.g. 1006-F, 1006CS-F). Fire-rated openings must be fail secure","Fits ANSI 4-7/8\" strike prep, metal or wood jamb; strike body depth 1-11/16\" [43 mm]. Plug-in connector and keeper offer horizontal adjustment; trim enhancer and two keeper shims (with screws) ship in the box","Faceplate options (many): for cylindrical/latchbolt work J, K, KM, N, NM, H, Z, R, E; for deadbolt/recapture N, KD, ND, HD, HM, NM. KD is for a mortise lock with the deadlatch (auxiliary latch) positioned relative to the latchbolt one way, KM the other \u2014 confirm deadlatch position against the template before you cut. AS deadbolt-recapture prep is the one used for Schlage L9000 / Yale 8700 continuous-recapture jobs; standard LB prep is NOT used with L9000/8700","Only HES strike that will retain, release and recapture a mortise lock with up to a 1\" [25.4 mm] deadbolt (with the deadbolt-recapture/low-profile kit)","Monitoring: Latchbolt Monitor (LBM) and Latchbolt Strike Monitor (LBSM), dry Form-C contacts. LBM: White=Common, Orange=N.O., Green=N.C. LBSM: Brown=Common, Blue=N.O., Yellow=N.C. Note: LBM/LBSM are NOT available on the CS, CDB or CAS Complete Pacs","Minimum wire gauge, one-way run (per HES install sheet): 200 ft or less = 18 ga (both 12V and 24V); 200-300 ft = 16 ga at 12V / 18 ga at 24V; 300-400 ft = 14 ga at 12V / 16 ga at 24V","Finishes (body only): 630 satin stainless (standard), plus 605 bright brass, 606 satin brass, 612 satin bronze, 613E dark oxidized satin bronze. IMPORTANT: Complete Pacs are only available in 630","Complete Pacs (all ship in 630 with strike body, faceplate kit(s) and hardware): CS = Complete Smart Strike (includes Smart Pac III bridge-rectifier/voltage regulator and J, KD, KM faceplates, for cylindrical or mortise latchbolt); CLB = Complete for latchbolt locks; CDB = Complete for deadbolt locks (up to 1\" deadbolt); CAS = Complete for deadbolt recapture (L9000 / 8700). Some distributor listings also cite ANSI A250.13 windstorm \u2014 that is not called out on HES's own 1006 datasheet, so verify before you rely on it for a rated opening"],proAdvice:"Spec the 1006 when a lighter strike would get destroyed \u2014 high-traffic storefront and suite entrances, stairwell doors, anything on an access-control home run that gets hammered. It is non-handed and drops into a standard ANSI 4-7/8\" prep, so most of your grief on this unit is not the strike, it is voltage and faceplate selection. Get those two right up front and the 1006 is close to bulletproof.\n\nOn voltage: nine times out of ten a buzzing, hot, or early-failed coil is overvoltage or AC on the coil \u2014 the wrong pigtail plugged in, a 24V supply feeding the 12V pigtail, or an unregulated buzzer/transformer supply that actually reads well above nameplate under no load. The install sheet is blunt about it: any input over 10% of the solenoid rating can cause severe damage and voids the warranty. Meter the supply UNDER LOAD before you land it, plug in the pigtail that matches your supply (both 12 and 24 ship in the box), and if the supply is at all dirty, add the Smart Pac / bridge-rectifier-regulator so you are feeding clean regulated DC. The 1006CS Complete already bakes in the Smart Pac III \u2014 worth it on any AC-fed or long-run job. When a coil does cook, replace the solenoid, not the whole body.\n\nOn faceplates: this is where installs go sideways on mortise locks. KD versus KM is about where the deadlatch (auxiliary latch) sits relative to the latchbolt \u2014 pull the faceplate, look at the lock, and match the template before you cut, because a KD prep where you needed KM will bind or fail to relatch. For continuous deadbolt recapture on a Schlage L9000 or Yale 8700 you must use the AS deadbolt-recapture prep and kit \u2014 the standard LB latchbolt prep is explicitly not used with those locks, and if you try you will grind. HES ships the CS Complete with J, KD and KM plates, which covers most cylindrical and mortise-latchbolt work; CDB and CAS are the deadbolt and recapture Pacs.\n\nOn repair-vs-replace and money: the 1006 is a premium strike \u2014 body-only typically runs roughly $180-300 and Complete Pacs $250-450 depending on config and finish, so verify current dealer pricing. Keeper shims, trim enhancer, replacement strike plate and the Smart Pac are all separately available service parts, so a worn keeper or shim gap is a cheap field fix, not a teardown. One finish gotcha: Complete Pacs only come in 630 satin stainless \u2014 if the architect specced 612 or 613E, you are ordering a body plus faceplates separately, not a Pac.\n\nCode and liability: on any fire-labeled door the 1006 has to be FAIL SECURE \u2014 the UL 10C fire listing does not hold in fail-safe, and an AHJ will red-tag a fail-safe (-F) strike on a rated opening. Fail-safe belongs on fail-safe-by-design egress where the access-control and life-safety design calls for it, backed by the fire alarm \u2014 never drop an -F onto a fire door to make a stuck problem go away. Static holding force: HES's own datasheet rates it at 2,500 lb fail secure and says it is tested to exceed 3,000 lb, so quote 2,500 lb as the rated number and 3,000-lb-plus as the tested ceiling \u2014 do not oversell the number on a spec sheet a plans examiner will read.",commonServicingIssues:["Buzzing / hot to the touch / early coil failure -> almost always overvoltage or AC on the coil (wrong pigtail plugged in, a 24V supply on the 12V pigtail, or an unregulated buzzer/transformer supply reading high) -> meter the supply UNDER LOAD, confirm the correct 12V vs 24V pigtail, and add a Smart Pac / bridge-rectifier-regulator for clean regulated DC. Replace the cooked solenoid, not the whole body","Won't relatch or won't release cleanly on a mortise lock -> wrong faceplate for the deadlatch position (KD where KM was needed, or vice versa) -> pull the faceplate, check whether the deadlatch sits above/below/center relative to the latchbolt, match it to the template, and swap to the correct KD/KM plate","Strike releases but door pops/rattles or keeper has play -> keeper not shimmed to the gap or missing keeper shims -> use the two keeper shims (with screws) from the box to take up the gap and tighten the keeper to the latch throw; check frame-to-strike alignment","Deadbolt won't recapture / grinds on a Schlage L9000 or Yale 8700 -> installed on the standard LB latchbolt prep instead of the AS deadbolt-recapture prep -> that is the wrong cut; the standard LB prep is not used with L9000/8700. Install the deadbolt-recapture kit and re-cut to the AS recapture template","Access panel fires but strike doesn't release -> voltage drop on a long home run, undersized wire, or the relay not passing enough current -> check gauge against the chart (18 ga good only to ~200 ft; go 16 ga at 12V past 200 ft, 14 ga at 12V past 300 ft) and meter voltage AT THE STRIKE while energized, not just at the panel","Monitoring not reporting -> LBM/LBSM leads miswired, wrong contact picked, or the monitor was ordered on a Pac that can't take it -> land per the diagram (LBM: White=Common/Orange=N.O./Green=N.C.; LBSM: Brown=Common/Blue=N.O./Yellow=N.C.) and remember LBM/LBSM are not available on the CS, CDB or CAS Complete Pacs","Failed fire inspection / AHJ red-tag on a rated door -> a fail-safe (-F) strike on a fire-labeled opening -> the UL 10C fire rating holds in FAIL SECURE only; convert or replace to fail secure on any fire door"],sources:["https://www.hesinnovations.com/en/products/electric-strikes/1006-series","https://www.tmhardware.com/products/HES/HS1006/HS1006_Installation_Instructions.pdf","https://accesshardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/HES-1006-Series-is.pdf","https://www.hesinnovations.com/content/dam/assa-abloy/americas/ems/hes/document/AADSS1203082.pdf","https://www.maglocks.com/hes-1006-series-fire-rated-grade-1-strike-stainless.html"],photo:"photo-hes-1006",schematic:"schematic-hes-1006"},{id:"von-duprin-6211",name:"Von Duprin 6211 Electric Strike",category:"Electric locking",whatItIs:"The 6211 is Von Duprin's 6200 Series heavy-duty electric strike for a single door running a mortise or cylindrical lockset WITHOUT a deadbolt, in a hollow-metal frame. It drops in place of the fixed strike and lets you release the door remotely by unlocking the beveled keeper (lip) so the latchbolt swings out of the pocket \u2014 nobody has to retract anything at the lever. You will see it on access-controlled openings, stairwell doors, and buzz-in entries where the mechanical lock stays fully functional and you only electrify the strike side. It matters because it is a true Grade 1, UL-listed strike that carries a 3-hour fire label when specified fail-secure \u2014 that label is the line between a legal opening and a red tag.",specs:["Application: single door, mortise or cylindrical lock WITHOUT deadbolt, hollow-metal frame. Retrofits Von Duprin VD 3140 and Folger Adam FA 712 (per 6200 Series data sheet 105368). Deadbolt will NOT function with this strike.","Faceplate: 4-7/8\" x 1-1/4\" (radius/ANSI face). Backbox depth 1-11/16\" on the 6211 (data sheet 105368). Frame prep: #16 drill and #12-24 tap, 2 places; suggested cutout per install sheet 931227.","Latchbolt throw accepted: 3/4\" (19mm) throw latchbolt; cylindrical locks 1/2\"-3/4\" (13-19mm). Signaling may not function on a 3/8\" (10mm) throw bolt.","Fasteners / clearance: two #12-24 screws; set 1/32\" clearance between latchbolt and strike lip (install sheet Figure 3).","Voltage: 24VDC standard (fail-secure). 12VDC, 16VDC, and 28VDC also selectable at time of order. 24VAC/12VAC via SO24/SO12 rectifier kit (field-installable, plugs in-line to the DC solenoid).","Solenoid power (strike label / install sheet 931227): 12VDC = 0.57 A (yellow wires); 24VDC = 0.29 A (black wires). Catalog amps-seated and amps-inrush @ 70F: 12V 0.6 A / 24V 0.33 A. Coil resistance +/-10% @ 70F: 21 ohm (12V), 82 ohm (24V). DC input is NONPOLARIZED. Continuous duty.","Power failure mode: furnished fail-secure (FSE) standard; fail-safe (FS) when specified. Field-convertible by swapping the slider. On the BASE 6211: FSE uses slider #090798, FS uses slider #090591 (Allegion KB). Note the 090591/090798 assignments INVERT on 6211WF/6212WF/6221/6223 \u2014 verify against the KB for the exact model before ordering a slider.","Strength / endurance: static strength 1500 lb; dynamic strength 70 ft-lb; endurance 250,000 cycles (install sheet 931227 and 6200 data sheet 105368). Some older 6000-series catalog prose lists static 1300 lb and endurance 1,000,000 cycles \u2014 treat 1500 lb / 250,000 c as the supported figures for the 6211.","Ratings: UL1034 burglary-resistant; UL10C 3-hour fire (fail-secure ONLY); ANSI/BHMA A156.31 Grade 1 (per 6200 data sheet spec chart; older docs cite A156.5). Rated 1500 lb hold force.","Monitoring (6211DS): two SPDT switches - S1 monitors the tripper (latchbolt seated in pocket), S2 monitors the strike lip (locked/open). Standard DS rated 24VDC, .050-2.0 A. DS-LC low-current (gold) contacts for computer/board inputs, .050 A or less. Switch contact ratings per install sheet: standard 5 A @ 30VDC, gold 0.25 A @ 30VDC. DS/DS-LC not available on open-back strikes.","Finishes (six): US3/605, US4/606, US10/612, US10B/613, US32/629, US32D/630. US32D (630) satin stainless is the everyday choice. Stainless steel construction, non-handed.","Options: EB entry buzzer (fail-secure only), latchbolt extension for short-throw latches to reach the tripper (6211DS), CON Allegion Connect prewired connector.","Backbox is adjustable side-to-side for door/frame misalignment: loosen screws A, B, C and shift; move screw A to hole D for more range (install sheet Figure 4)."],proAdvice:"Spec it by what is behind it, not by the door. The 6211 is for a mortise or cylindrical lock with NO deadbolt on a hollow-metal frame. If the frame is aluminum you want the 6211AL (retrofits FA 722); wood frame is the 6211WF. Get that wrong and you are eating a return and a second trip. And confirm the lock throw: a 3/4\" latch is happy, a 1/2\" cylindrical latch will just barely reach, and a 3/8\" throw will not reliably actuate the DS tripper \u2014 if you are monitoring, order the latchbolt extension up front.\n\nOrder fail mode deliberately and know the code. Fail-secure (FSE) is the standard build and the ONLY one that keeps the UL10C fire label \u2014 Von Duprin says it plainly, and building codes prohibit fail-safe strikes on labeled fire doors. So on any fire-rated or stairwell opening, FSE, full stop; the mechanical lever always gives you free egress from the inside, so life-safety is covered on the lock side. Save fail-safe for non-rated openings where the AHJ actually wants the strike to release on power loss, and even then think hard about what the door is protecting. If someone hands you a fail-safe 6211 on a labeled frame, that is a red tag waiting to happen.\n\nVoltage and the AC trap. It runs 24VDC standard, 12VDC optional, and the DC input is nonpolarized so you cannot wire the solenoid backwards. What kills these in the field is feeding a DC solenoid raw AC \u2014 it chatters, runs hot, and cooks the coil. If the supply is 12 or 24 VAC you MUST use the SO12/SO24 rectifier kit in-line. Always land a TVS/MOV across the coil; Von Duprin literally prints \"TVS installation recommended\" on the sheet, and the number one dead-solenoid call I get is a coil that took an inductive spike with no suppression. Coil checks: about 21 ohm at 12V, 82 ohm at 24V \u2014 open coil means replace the solenoid.\n\nThe slider part-number gotcha. Field-converting FS/FSE is a real thing on these, but do not trust your memory on the number. On the base 6211, FSE is slider 090798 and FS is 090591 \u2014 and those two numbers SWAP on the WF and 6221/6223 variants. Pull up the Allegion KB, match your exact model, and order the right one. Nine times out of ten a \"lip flops loose after a conversion\" call is the wrong slider or a keeper spring that did not seat, not a bad strike.\n\nRepair vs replace. The strike body is stainless and rated 1500 lb static / 250,000 cycles, so the frame usually outlives the electronics. Coil, slider, and the two-piece plug connector are all serviceable \u2014 a coil or slider swap is a 20-minute fix and cheap, so repair it. Replace the whole strike when the keeper casting is worn, the pocket is chewed, or you are moving to a monitored (DS) build. Ballpark, a bare 6211 runs a couple hundred dollars in common finishes and the DS version more; a service coil is a fraction of that, so unless the body is damaged you fix, you do not throw it away.",commonServicingIssues:["Door will not release on buzz-in / strike hums but lip stays locked -> low voltage at the strike, usually voltage drop on a long run or an undersized supply -> measure DC at the strike UNDER LOAD; a 24V unit should pull ~0.29A and hold near 24V, if you are sagging toward 20V the solenoid stalls; upsize the wire or move to a properly sized regulated supply","Buzzes loud and runs warm but works -> almost always AC (12/24 VAC) feeding the DC solenoid with no rectifier, or a failed SO kit -> confirm the SO12/SO24 is in-line and intact; AC on a DC coil chatters, overheats, and eventually opens the coil","Solenoid dead, no click at all -> burnt coil (usually an inductive spike where no TVS was fitted) or a broken plug connector -> unplug the two-piece connector and read coil resistance (~21 ohm at 12V, ~82 ohm at 24V); open coil = replace the solenoid, and add the TVS this time","Door binds or latch will not seat after install -> the 1/32\" latchbolt-to-lip clearance is off, or the backbox drifted -> loosen screws A/B/C and re-set the gap to 1/32\"; if the frame is misaligned move screw A to hole D for extra travel","Panel reads door-open when it is closed (6211DS) -> tripper not being actuated because the latch throw is too short (e.g. a 1/2\" or 3/8\" cylindrical latch not reaching the tripper) or monitor leads are on the wrong common -> fit the latchbolt extension and verify S1 (tripper) and S2 (strike lip) on their correct commons","DS-LC monitor contacts erratic or welded -> too much current pushed through the low-current gold contacts (.050A or less, 0.25A max) -> move the signal to a dry input within rating, or use the standard DS switches (rated .050-2.0A, 5A/30VDC contacts); gold contacts are board-level only","Deadbolt will not throw / door acts locked out -> somebody put a mortise lock WITH a deadbolt behind a 6211, which does not accommodate a deadbolt -> confirm the lock has no deadbolt; retract the bolt and spec the correct opening","Lip flops loose / no positive lock after a field conversion -> wrong slider installed or a broken keeper spring -> confirm the correct slider for the EXACT model (base 6211: FSE 090798 / FS 090591; these invert on WF and 6221/6223) and that the keeper returns fully; replace the slider or internals via the plug connector"],sources:["https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/installation-documents/Von_Duprin_6211_6211DS_Electric_Strike_Single_Door_Mortise_or_Cylindrical_Application_Installation_Instructions_107682.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/Von_Duprin_6200_Series_Data_Sheet_105368.pdf","https://store.jlmwholesale.com/Documents/VD6000DATA.pdf","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/how-to-convert-the-von-duprin-6000-series-electrical-strike-from-fail-secure-to-fail-safe-or-fail-safe-to-fail-secure/","https://www.trudoor.com/products/von-duprin-6211-electric-strike-for-cylindrical-or-mortise-locks"],photo:"photo-von-duprin-6211",schematic:"schematic-von-duprin-6211"},{id:"schlage-encode",name:"Schlage Encode / Encode Plus Smart WiFi Lock",category:"Smart locks",whatItIs:"Schlage's built-in-WiFi retrofit smart lock line \u2014 the BE489 Encode deadbolt, BE499 Encode Plus deadbolt, and FE789 Encode lever. Unlike a bridge-based lock, the radio and 2.4GHz WiFi live inside the interior escutcheon, so it talks to the Schlage Home app and Alexa/Google straight off the router with no separate hub. You'll see these on Airbnb and short-term rentals, small offices, and higher-end homes where the owner wants remote codes and an audit trail. It matters because both deadbolt models actually carry an A156.36 Commercial Grade 1 listing on top of the residential rating \u2014 so on a light-commercial opening you can spec it where a Kwikset Halo (Grade 2) wouldn't pass. Just know the paper trail is the sell, not real commercial iron.",specs:["Built-in WiFi (2.4 GHz) \u2014 connects straight to the home router, no hub or bridge; Bluetooth for setup and local control","Line: BE489 Encode deadbolt \xb7 BE499 Encode Plus (adds Apple Home Key NFC tap-to-unlock + Bluetooth) \xb7 FE789 Encode lever","Mechanical key override retained on a Schlage C (SC1) keyway, so it re-keys and masters like any Schlage cylinder","Power: 4x AA alkaline, ~6 months typical, with low-battery alerts. No 9V jump terminals \u2014 that is the older Schlage Touch/BE375","Access: up to 100 user codes (BE489); managed in the Schlage Home app; works with Amazon Key, Ring, Alexa and Google","Illuminated capacitive touchscreen keypad; built-in alarm senses activity at the lock","Certified ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 (deadbolt)"],proAdvice:"Spec it by the letter after the model, not by the marketing name. BE489 is the plain Encode \u2014 WiFi only, no HomeKit. BE499 is Encode Plus, which adds Bluetooth, Apple HomeKit and, the real selling point, Apple Home Key so an iPhone taps the keypad like a transit card. If a customer with an Apple household is paying smart-lock money, sell them the Plus \u2014 the base BE489 has no HomeKit and you'll get a callback about it. One thing the apprentice draft got backwards: the Plus does NOT give up the commercial rating. Both deadbolts carry A156.36 Commercial Grade 1 \u2014 Schlage's own ratings sheet lists it on the BE499 too. The lever FE789 is the odd one out: it's A156.39 Grade AAA residential only, no commercial listing, so do not spec the lever where the deadbolts' Grade 1 paper is what got you in the door on a light-commercial job. And don't oversell the fire angle \u2014 the deadbolts carry a UL 20-minute (no hose) listing, which is a residential figure, not a rated commercial fire opening. On a real commercial door \u2014 anything genuinely fire-rated, high-cycle, or under an egress code \u2014 none of these belong. This is a residential platform with a commercial paper trail on the deadbolt, not a mortise or a Grade 1 cylindrical. Put a Schlage L-series or a proper access-control head end on that door instead.\n\nInstall gotchas: the number one failed job on these is the 2.4GHz thing. Modern mesh routers push everyone onto a single band-steered SSID that hands the lock a 5GHz radio it can't use, and you'll swear the lock is dead when it's just never gotten on the network. Before you drive out for a 'won't connect' call, have them split the 2.4GHz band or set up a 2.4-only guest SSID for the lock. Second, get the handing and the tailpiece orientation right on first assembly \u2014 the interior mounting plate has to seat flat and the posts have to clear the cross bore, or the motor binds and you get a lock that throws halfway and reverses. Nine times out of ten a 'motor is weak' complaint is actually a strike alignment or a dragging bolt, not electronics \u2014 throw the bolt by hand with the door open before you ever blame the gearbox. Deadbolt needs a full 1-inch throw into a properly aligned strike box; if the frame's racked, the motor stalls trying to force it and the batteries drain in weeks.\n\nRekeying: the cylinder is standard SC1 Schlage 5-pin (5-pin only \u2014 don't try to drop a 6-pin plug in it), so you rekey it on the bench exactly like a B60 \u2014 pull the cylinder, key it to the customer's existing Schlage key, done. Don't let anyone tell you a smart lock can't be keyed alike; the mechanical override is a normal keyway. Keep that key override maintained, because \u2014 and this is the correction to make on any writeup that says otherwise \u2014 the Encode has NO 9V jumpstart terminals. That's the Schlage Touch series. When the AAs die on an Encode with no app access, the mechanical key IS the egress and the liability, full stop. Don't send a tech out expecting a 9V trick that doesn't exist on this platform. Repair vs replace: these are largely non-serviceable electronically \u2014 Allegion doesn't sell you a replacement WiFi board or motor as a field part. Dead keypad under the 3-year electronics warranty, you RMA the whole interior/exterior assembly through Schlage, you don't fix the board. Out of warranty, a dead motor or fried keypad means a new lock \u2014 quote the replacement, don't chase parts. Where you make money is the diagnosis-and-swap and the network setup, not component-level repair. Typical customer spend runs a few hundred installed depending on model and finish; the Plus commands the premium for Home Key. Finishes are the usual Schlage set (satin nickel, matte black, aged bronze, bright chrome) and they're not interchangeable across the escutcheon halves, so order the whole lock in one finish.",commonServicingIssues:["Won't connect to WiFi / drops offline: nine times out of ten it's a 5GHz-only or band-steered SSID \u2014 the Encode radio is 2.4GHz only. Fix: split the 2.4GHz band onto its own SSID or make a 2.4-only guest network, place the router within reasonable range, then re-add the lock in the Schlage Home app.","Motor throws halfway then reverses / 'weak motor': almost always mechanical \u2014 racked frame, dragging bolt, or a strike box that's out of alignment. Fix: open the door, throw the bolt by hand to confirm free travel, then correct strike/mortise alignment. Don't condemn the gearbox until the bolt moves freely.","Fast battery drain (weeks not months): motor is fighting a misaligned strike on every cycle, or cheap/partially-used AAs. Fix: align the strike so the bolt throws with no resistance, install a fresh matched set of alkaline AAs, verify no leftover partial cells in the pack.","Lock dead / no keypad response, no power: batteries fully depleted. Fix: there is NO 9V jumpstart on the Encode (that's the Touch series) \u2014 use the mechanical key override to get in, then replace all four AAs. Set customer expectations that the key is the only cold backup.","Codes stop working after re-add or reset: factory reset clears user codes and unpairs from WiFi. Fix: re-establish default programming code, re-enroll user codes, re-pair to app \u2014 warn Airbnb hosts their recurring guest codes need re-loading after any reset.","'No HomeKit' complaint on a BE489: base Encode has no Apple HomeKit or Home Key \u2014 only Encode Plus (BE499) does. Fix: set expectations at the quote, or upsell/swap to Encode Plus if Apple integration is required.","'Where's Matter/Thread support' complaint: there isn't any \u2014 Schlage states the Encode Plus is NOT a Matter device, and no Encode has Z-Wave/Zigbee. Fix: if the customer needs an open smart-home protocol, this is the wrong lock \u2014 point them at Yale Assure Lock 2 with the Matter module.","Touchscreen misreads / ghost presses: dirt, water film, or freezing on the capacitive pad. Fix: wipe dry, and in freezing climates set expectations that a wet-then-frozen keypad can misbehave \u2014 the keypad is capacitive, not mechanical buttons.","Firmware/app pairing loop: lock joins then won't finish setup. Fix: confirm 2.4GHz, pull one battery to power-cycle the lock, delete the partial device in-app, and re-run setup close to the router before mounting it far away."],sources:["https://www.schlage.com/en/home/products/BE489WBCENFFF.html","https://www.schlage.com/en/home/products/BE499WBCENFFF.html","https://www.schlage.com/en/home/products/FE789CENFFFLAT.html","https://schlage-res.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/37362216690068-What-Are-the-Security-Ratings-for-Schlage-Encode-Locks","https://schlage-res.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/37468159195796-Can-Schlage-Encode-Locks-Be-Jumpstarted-with-a-Battery"],photo:"photo-schlage-encode",schematic:"schematic-schlage-encode"},{id:"kwikset-smartkey",name:"Kwikset SmartKey Deadbolt",category:"Residential",whatItIs:"Kwikset's rekeyable residential deadbolt line \u2014 the 980 single-cylinder and 985 double-cylinder bolts, plus the cheaper 660/665 series and the Key Control units \u2014 all built on the patented SmartKey Security cylinder. Instead of a brass pin-tumbler stack, the cylinder runs a sidebar against five spring-loaded wafers, which is what lets a homeowner rekey the lock to a new key in seconds with a small tool and no disassembly. You see these on tract housing, rentals, and big-box shelves everywhere in North America because they ship cheap, install DIY, and let a landlord turn a key between tenants without calling anybody. That same rekey convenience is also exactly what makes the cylinder controversial on the security side.",specs:["Grade is per-series, not per-brand: the 980/985 are ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 certified across all finishes (confirmed on Kwikset's own 980 spec sheet \u2014 it does NOT drop to Grade 2/3 by finish). The cheaper 660/665 SmartKey deadbolts are Grade 3. Quote the actual model number, do not assume Grade 1 across the whole SmartKey line","Backset: adjustable latch, 2-3/8 in or 2-3/4 in, set with the adjustment pin at install \u2014 fits standard prep","Bolt: 1 in full throw","Door thickness: 1-3/8 in to 1-3/4 in standard; thick-door kit takes it to 2-1/4 in","Cross bore: 1 in (standard 2-1/8 in face bore)","Keyway: KW1 by default. Kwikset also sells SmartKey in SC1 (Schlage keyway) \u2014 but note SC1 SmartKey only launched Oct 2020, so older installs are KW1 only. Confirm the SKU","Cylinder mechanism: sidebar riding on five spring-loaded aluminum wafers (not a brass pin stack); rekeyable in-place with the SmartKey learn/reset tool. SC1 cylinders add two steel balls in the key face for drill resistance","Rekey sequence (Kwikset's actual procedure): throw the bolt to locked with the door open; insert the CURRENT working key, turn 90 degrees clockwise; push the SmartKey tool fully and firmly into the reset hole until it clicks; pull the tool, then pull the old key; insert the NEW key fully seated, rotate 180 degrees counter-clockwise, then 180 degrees clockwise, then 90 degrees counter-clockwise back to start; remove the new key","Finishes (980): Satin Nickel, Venetian Bronze, Polished Chrome, Satin Chrome, Polished Brass, Antique Brass, Matte Black","Microban antimicrobial coating on the finish","Ships with 2 keys and the SmartKey tool in-box; Kwikset lifetime mechanical and finish warranty","Marketed protections: pick resistant and bump proof (BumpGuard), plus the in-place rekey"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight for an apprentice: SmartKey is not a pin-tumbler lock, so throw out your pinning instincts. No bottom pins to read, no top pins, no master wafers. It is a sidebar cylinder \u2014 a sidebar drops against a set of five spring-loaded aluminum wafers, and the correct key pushes each wafer to a position that lets the sidebar retract so the plug can turn. The rekey magic is that the wafers can be re-taught. With the working key at 90 degrees, you push the SmartKey tool into that little hole next to the keyway until it clicks, which unlocks the wafers from their current positions; the next key you cycle through teaches them the new cuts. Get the sequence exact \u2014 current key to 90 CW, tool in and out, old key out, new key fully seated, then 180 CCW, 180 CW, 90 CCW back to start. That is Kwikset's actual procedure, and if you shortcut it you leave the cylinder in a phantom state. Great feature. It is also the whole ballgame on why these fail and why they get criticized.\n\nOn the security question \u2014 be honest with customers, because a nosy homeowner will have watched a YouTube video. The design got publicly torn apart at DEF CON 21 in 2013 by Marc Tobias and Toby Bluzmanis ('A Failure of Imagination'), and the two real attacks were a torque/plug-rotation attack that forces the plug and pops the sidebar without picking, and a decoder that reads the wafer positions with basic tooling. Their headline: a screwdriver and a wrench opened the cylinder in about fifteen seconds at roughly 100-plus inch-pounds of torque, against a lock Kwikset rated to withstand 300. Kwikset hardened the cylinder across later generations, but understand the aftermarket caught up \u2014 commercial decoders (LockTech and others) advertise coverage of SmartKey Gen 1 through 4. And the tolerances are loose enough that a set of 243 tryout keys covers the whole practical keyspace. The current stuff is fine for typical residential use and it genuinely is bump-proof, because there is no pin stack to bump. But the wafers are aluminum, not brass, so a determined destructive attack overpowers this cylinder sooner than a solid brass pin cylinder. My honest line to a client: SmartKey is convenience-grade security. For a rental turnover, an interior door, a budget rekey \u2014 perfect. For a client genuinely worried about targeted forced entry, I steer them to a Grade 1 brass pin-tumbler deadbolt (Schlage B60/B660 tier) and I do not apologize for it.\n\nSpec it right so you do not eat a callback. Grade is per-series \u2014 the 980/985 are Grade 1 BHMA across every finish, but the 660/665 are Grade 3, so quote the actual model, not 'a Kwikset deadbolt.' Do not repeat the myth that the 980 changes grade by finish; it does not. Backset is adjustable 2-3/8 to 2-3/4, so it fits either prep, but physically set the pin and test before you walk. Keyway matters for keying-alike jobs: default is KW1, SC1 SmartKey exists but only since 2020, and you cannot key a KW1 SmartKey and an SC1 SmartKey to the same key \u2014 match keyways up front. And the big one for pros: SmartKey cannot go on a master-key system. The sidebar design has no master wafer, so if a customer wants one key to open multiple differently-keyed doors, SmartKey is the wrong product, full stop.\n\nRepair versus replace: this is almost always a replace call. No serviceable pins, the wafer pack is a sealed assembly, and Kwikset does not sell a cylinder rebuild kit. A SmartKey deadbolt runs cheap enough \u2014 often twenty-something to forty-something dollars retail \u2014 that when the cylinder seizes or gets thrown into a bad state, you swap the lock, you do not rebuild it. Keep a couple of KW1 SmartKey deadbolts on the truck in satin nickel and matte black, and an SC1 or two if you work a Schlage-heavy area, and you clear most of these on the spot. The one time I try to save it is when the customer just fumbled a rekey and locked it to a phantom key \u2014 sometimes you recover that with a known-good working key and a clean re-teach, which I cover in the servicing notes. Liability-wise, these are residential only \u2014 do not let a customer talk you into hanging a SmartKey on a code-required egress or fire door in a commercial setting. That is Grade 1 commercial mortise/cylindrical territory, not this.",commonServicingIssues:["Symptom: rekey won't take, or lock now opens with no key / a wrong key. Cause: the reset sequence was interrupted or done out of order, leaving the wafers in a phantom state. Fix: get a key that currently works (often the last new key), run the full Kwikset sequence clean \u2014 working key to 90 CW, tool firmly in until it clicks, tool out then old key out, new key fully seated, 180 CCW, 180 CW, 90 CCW back to start. If it will not re-teach to any known key, the cylinder is toast \u2014 replace the lock.","Symptom: key turns but bolt is sluggish or key binds. Cause: nine times out of ten this is door/strike misalignment or a dry cylinder, not the SmartKey mechanism. Fix: check bolt-to-strike alignment and door sag first, throw the bolt with the door open to isolate it, then a shot of dry-film/graphite lube in the keyway (never oil \u2014 it gums the wafers). Only condemn the cylinder after you have ruled out alignment.","Symptom: cylinder seized, key won't insert or turn at all. Cause: internal wafer/sidebar failure or grit fouling the wafers \u2014 SmartKey is more sensitive to contamination than a brass pin stack. Fix: no serviceable internals, this is a replace. Swap the deadbolt.","Symptom: customer reports the lock was 'broken into' but their key still works. Cause: classic SmartKey torque/force-over attack \u2014 the plug was forced, damage is internal, and the tell is that the cylinder will no longer rekey cleanly. Fix: confirm by attempting a rekey; if it won't re-teach, the cylinder was compromised \u2014 replace it, and if security is a real concern move them to a brass pin-tumbler Grade 1 deadbolt.","Symptom: two SmartKey deadbolts won't key alike. Cause: mismatched keyways (one KW1, one SC1) or one is not actually a SmartKey cylinder. Fix: verify both are SmartKey and the same keyway before quoting key-alike; you cannot cross KW1 and SC1.","Symptom: customer wants this on a master system. Cause: SmartKey's sidebar design has no master capability. Fix: it cannot be mastered \u2014 spec a conventional pin-tumbler cylinder for any master-key job."],sources:["https://www.kwikset.com/smartkey-security","https://www.kwikset.com/products/detail/980-deadbolt-keyed-one-side-featuring-smartkey","https://www.kwikset.com/products/detail/660-deadbolt-keyed-one-side-featuring-smartkey","https://www.kwikset.com/support/answers/how-to-re-key-your-smartkey-security-locks","https://images.kwikset.com/is/content/Kwikset/Redesign%202019/Literature/Documents/KW-REKEY-MANUAL.pdf"],photo:"photo-kwikset-smartkey",schematic:"schematic-kwikset-smartkey",relatedGuides:["t-keyways-schlage-kwikset"]},{id:"sg-6730",name:"Sargent & Greenleaf (S&G) Safe Locks \u2014 6730 Mechanical Dial, 6120 & Spartan Electronic",category:"Safes",whatItIs:"These are the guts of the combination lock on a commercial safe, gun safe, or vault door \u2014 the mechanism the dial or keypad drives, not the boltwork of the container itself. The 6730 is the classic three-wheel mechanical dial lock, the one working safe techs reach for by default; the 6120 is the motor-driven electronic keypad that retrofits onto that same footprint; and the Spartan is S&G's newer EMP-hardened electronic line. You see all three mounted on the inside of the safe door, driving a lock bolt into the container's boltwork. Getting these right matters because a safe lock is a UL-rated life-and-liability part \u2014 a bad install or a hacked repair can lock a customer out of their own valuables or blow an insurance requirement.",specs:["6730 mechanical: UL 768 Group 2 listed. Three all-metal, key-changeable wheels, ~1,000,000 combinations. Sibling 6731 is 4-wheel / ~100,000,000 combos. The Zamak-alloy 6741 variant has wider dialing tolerances for tougher environments.","6730 bolt & guts: internal spring relock trigger to defeat punch/drop attacks, and a patented torque adjuster to set wheel-pack tension. Four-way drive cam for universal up/down/left/right mounting. Familiar left-right-left, one-handed dialing.","6730 certifications: UL Group 2, VdS Class 1 (the 4-wheel 6731 carries VdS Class 2), CEN/EN 1300, China CCC, RoHS2. Standard kit ships with a D300 dial and ring; front-reading, Spy-Proof, small-knob, key-locking and non-key-locking rings available in finishes from black/white to satin chrome up to gold plate. Confirm exact dial/ring model with S&G for a given finish.","Footprint: the 6730, 6731, and 6120 share S&G's standard commercial case size and mounting/spindle pattern \u2014 the reason a 6120 drops straight onto a 6730 hole layout. Always verify the actual hole pattern on the door before you promise a bolt-on.","6120 electronic: UL Type 1 listed, motor-driven deadlatching bolt, EMP-resistant. Factory ships on master code 1-2-3-4-5-6-#. One master + up to eight user codes (nine total); all codes are exactly six digits (digits or letters on the pad). NO audit trail. Optional seven-digit Management Reset Code that must be programmed before the master is first changed.","6120 power/operation: two 9V alkaline batteries in the keypad (Duracell recommended; will limp on a single cell in a pinch). Reseller-tested to ~8,000 openings per set. Enter 6-digit code + #, bolt retracts for ~6 seconds. Time delay programmable 0-9 minutes (0 = off). Low battery = twenty consecutive beeps after the code + #.","6120 penalty lockout: four wrong codes in a row = shutdown for up to fifteen minutes; pressing ANY key during the lockout resets the timer to full penalty. Important: for UL-listed installations the maximum number of user codes is ONE.","Spartan electronic: UL Type 1, tested to the highest EMP-impact standard. EN 1300 Class B, ECB-S R01 Class B, VdS Class 2, A2P Class B/E, SBSC, RoHS2. Master + user code with Management Reset and penalty lockout. Bolt bodies: Auto-Relocking PivotBolt (motor blocking) or Direct Drive square bolt (motor blocking with manual keypad-ring rotation via no-twist cable) \u2014 both stainless, both rated to 225 lbs of bolt end pressure. 1- or 2-battery keypads; low-profile (~1\") and rotating keypad options (rotating keypad on Direct Drive only). STAR and BASIC programming modes."],proAdvice:"Start with what the 6730 actually is: the industry-benchmark mechanical safe lock, and the reason is its footprint. S&G's commercial case size and spindle/screw layout is what nearly every commercial safe, gun safe, and off-brand import gets drilled and tapped for. That's your leverage. When somebody hands you a dead lock, you're almost never touching boltwork \u2014 you're dropping a same-footprint lock onto existing holes and mounting a dial ring. Keep 6730 kits on the truck and you cover most of your calls. Just don't promise a bolt-on before you've eyeballed the actual hole pattern, because \"industry standard\" doesn't mean \"identical on every import.\"\n\nSpec it by matching four things: footprint (verify hole pattern, don't assume), spindle length (measure door thickness plus dial ring plus a hair; you cut the square spindle to length and there's no do-over if you cut short \u2014 leave it long and trim), hand/mounting (the four-way drive cam is universal so orientation is a non-issue, but boltwork throw direction is not \u2014 confirm the bolt extends the way the container needs), and dial ring type. That last one bites people. Front-reading, Spy-Proof, small-knob, key-locking, non-key-locking \u2014 a key-locking ring on a business that day-locks the safe is a real feature, not a nicety. The standard kit ships a D300 dial and ring; if the customer wants a specific finish, confirm the exact dial/ring model with S&G rather than guessing a part number.\n\nThe big judgment call is mechanical versus electronic, and the honest answer is: mechanical for reliability and longevity, electronic for convenience and time delay. The 6120 is a direct retrofit onto the 6730 footprint \u2014 pull the dial and ring, mount the keypad, seat the harness. Bread-and-butter upgrade. Sell it on the time delay (0-9 minutes, a real deterrent against a robbery/duress grab) and multiple user codes \u2014 but here's the code trap: for a UL-listed installation the 6120 is limited to ONE user code. If a business wants eight users AND wants to keep the UL listing intact for insurance, that's a conflict you flag up front, not after. Also set expectations that it's battery-powered (two 9Vs, roughly 8,000 openings, low-battery warning is twenty beeps), and it'll eventually need a service call the 6730 never would. The batteries live in the keypad on the outside of the door on purpose \u2014 a dead battery is a two-minute swap, never a lockout by itself. And know that the 6120 has no audit trail; if the customer needs to know who opened it and when, the 6120 is the wrong lock.\n\nSpartan is where you go when EMP resistance is the selling point \u2014 gun-safe crowd especially. Tested to the highest EMP-impact standard, and it comes in two flavors that change how you install and how you counsel. The Direct Drive square-bolt version has no bolt motor to burn out \u2014 after the code accepts, the customer physically rotates the keypad ring to draw the bolt through a no-twist cable, so battery life is stellar but they have to work the knob. The PivotBolt version is motor-driven with auto-relock. Pick on the container and the customer's hands, not price alone.\n\nRepair versus replace: on a mechanical 6730, the wheels, wheel pack, and change key are serviceable, and the torque adjuster is a genuine feature \u2014 a customer complaining of a gritty or \"skating\" feel is often a tension set, not a dead lock, so reach for the adjuster before you condemn it. But if the relock trigger fired from an attack or a drop, or the case is compromised, replace it \u2014 a Group 2 lock with a tripped relocker is not something you band-aid. On electronics you almost never open the lock body; you swap the keypad, the harness, or the whole module. Ballpark: a straight mechanical 6730 supply-and-install runs a few hundred dollars parts-and-labor on an open, working safe; a lockout drilling job is a different animal, priced by the hour plus a guaranteed lock replacement afterward.\n\nCode and liability instincts: know your UL groups. Group 2 (6730) is the everyday commercial standard. Group 1 and 1R add manipulation and radiological resistance \u2014 if a spec calls for Group 1, a 6730 does NOT meet it, don't fudge it. UL Type 1 (6120/Spartan) is the electronic listing. And the cardinal rule that keeps you employed and out of court: never leave a safe on the factory default \u2014 mechanical ships on S&G's single-number \"50\" shipping combo, and the 6120 ships on master 1-2-3-4-5-6-#. Set a real combination, and run the customer through a supervised open-and-close two or three times before you leave, on the new combination, with the door hanging wide open. If they can't reliably dial or key it open in front of you, they will lock themselves out the moment that door swings shut \u2014 and that call comes back to you.",commonServicingIssues:["6730 dials but won't open on the right combination -> nine times out of ten it's dialing technique or a slightly-off dial ring index, not a bad lock. Confirm left-right-left counts, re-index the ring, and try picking up the last number a hair early or late. If it still won't gate, suspect a shifted wheel from a sloppy combination change.","6730 feels gritty, notchy, or 'skates' past the gate -> wheel-pack tension drifted. Reach for the patented torque adjuster before condemning the lock; a proper tension set restores clean feel and resists vibration walk-off. Old dry grease also gums it \u2014 clean and re-lube light.","6730 spins freely / dial turns but nothing happens -> spindle or drive-cam problem: sheared spindle, stripped cam, or the spindle backed out. On a retrofit this often traces to a spindle cut too short or the wrong length ordered.","6730 completely dead-locked after a drop, move, or attempted break-in -> the internal relock trigger fired. That's a defeat feature working as designed. You're now into a controlled drill-and-repair on a Group 2 lock, and the lock gets replaced afterward \u2014 don't reset and reuse a tripped relocker.","6120 keypad totally dead, no beep, no light -> batteries first, always. Two 9V alkalines in the keypad (Duracell recommended); twenty beeps after code + # is the low-battery warning that usually precedes a full death. Swap both with fresh alkaline, and if still dead, re-seat the ribbon/harness connector between keypad and lock body \u2014 it only seats one way.","6120 beeps and lights but bolt won't retract -> weak batteries can't drive the motor even though the keypad still beeps. Fresh 9Vs fix most of these; it'll even limp on a single cell in an emergency. If the motor is silent, suspect the harness or a failed lock module \u2014 that's a body replacement.","6120 owner locked out after wrong codes -> penalty lockout: four consecutive wrong entries triggers a shutdown up to fifteen minutes, and pressing ANY key during it resets the timer to full penalty. Coach them to walk away and touch nothing, then enter the correct 6-digit code + #. Also check they aren't fighting a time delay (0-9 min) they forgot was set \u2014 the lock chirps every ten seconds through a delay.","6120/6730 bolt retracts but door won't open -> not the lock. That's boltwork binding or the container handle/relocker. The 6120 manual even calls this out: move the safe handle to the fully locked position to relieve side pressure on the lock bolt, then re-enter the code. Look at door alignment and a bound relock too.","Spartan Direct Drive 'won't open' -> remember it's manual-throw: after the code accepts, the customer must physically rotate the keypad ring to draw the bolt via the no-twist cable. Half these calls are user technique, not a fault.","Any electronic model resets to default or won't take a new code -> program-mode confusion (STAR vs BASIC on Spartan; the SG-star master-code sequence on the 6120). A dropped keystroke mid-sequence leaves it on the old or factory code (6120 default is 1-2-3-4-5-6-#), which is also a liability if left that way. Walk the exact steps and re-verify with the door open."],sources:["https://sargentandgreenleaf.com/product/6700-series/","https://sargentandgreenleaf.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/SG-LIT-SEL-6730.pdf","https://sargentandgreenleaf.com/product/model-6120/","https://sargentandgreenleaf.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/Operation_Instructions_6120.pdf","https://sargentandgreenleaf.com/product/spartan/"],photo:"photo-sg-6730",schematic:"schematic-sg-6730"},{id:"padlock-american",name:"American Lock / Commercial Padlocks",category:"Padlocks",whatItIs:"American Lock is Master Lock's commercial/industrial padlock division \u2014 the workhorse locks you see on utility gates, fleet trucks, storage yards, transformer enclosures, and vending routes. The line runs from solid-steel and solid-brass rekeyable bodies (A700, A5200, A5560) up through hidden-shackle \"hockey puck\" locks (the A2010, which consolidated the older A2000/A2001/A2500) and a full range of interchangeable-core bodies (SFIC for Best/Arrow/Falcon, plus LFIC for Schlage FSIC and Yale). What sets them apart on the job is the hardened boron-alloy shackle, dual ball-bearing shackle retention, and the fact that nearly every body is field-rekeyable rather than throwaway \u2014 which is exactly why locksmiths and facilities people standardize on them for master-key systems.",specs:["A700 solid steel: 2-1/2in (64mm) wide case-hardened chrome-plated steel body, 7/16in (11mm) dia hardened boron-alloy shackle, 1-1/16in (27mm) shackle clearance, 5-pin APTC12 rekeyable cylinder, dual ball-bearing retention","A5200 solid steel: 1-3/4in (44mm) wide chrome-plated steel body, 5/16in (8mm) dia boron shackle, 1-1/8in (29mm) clearance, 5-pin APTC12 rekeyable cylinder","A5560 solid brass: 1-3/4in (44mm) wide brass body for corrosive/marine environments, 5/16in (8mm) dia boron shackle, 1-1/8in tall, dual stainless-steel ball bearings, 5-pin APTC12 brass cylinder","A2010 hidden-shackle puck: 2-7/8in (73mm) wide case-hardened steel body, flat-back (tapped/threaded for A801/A802 hasps), triple-plated, 3/8in (10mm) dia shackle, 6-pin APT2001 rekeyable cylinder; consolidated the older A2000/A2001/A2500 (effective ~Jan 2017)","A3560 SFIC brass body: 1-3/4in (44mm) wide, 1-1/8in (29mm) tall 5/16in (8mm) boron shackle, accepts Small Format Best/Arrow/Falcon cores; sold with or without core, ~16 SFIC keyway options","Keyway breadth: American Lock offers over 60 padlock, door-hardware, and small-format IC keyways; SFIC bodies for Best/Arrow/Falcon plus LFIC bodies for Schlage FSIC and Yale large-format cores","Shackle material: hardened boron alloy across the commercial line \u2014 markedly higher cut resistance than standard hardened steel","Restricted/key-control options: R2 restricted keyway (extra keys must be factory-ordered) and the Edge Key Control system (Master Lock controls all blanks, ~$10/lock) to block unauthorized duplication","Hidden-shackle hasps: A801 (8-3/4in / 222mm) and A802 trailer hasp (11-7/16in / 291mm) hardened zinc-plated steel, accept shackles up to 9/16in (14mm), for A2010/A2001/A2500-series puck locks","Keying options across the line: Keyed Different (KD), Keyed Alike (KA), Master Keyed (MK), Keyed Alike Master Keyed (KAMK)"],proAdvice:"Here's the mental model to carry onto the truck: American Lock is the \"spec it and forget it\" commercial padlock, and its whole value is that you can rekey and master-key it in the field. Do not treat these like a big-box hardware padlock. When a customer says \"I've got forty gates and I want one master key but each site keyed alike to itself,\" this is the line you reach for \u2014 A700s or A5200s on an R2 restricted keyway, mastered off a system you control. The R2 restriction (extra keys must be factory-ordered) and the Edge key-control program exist specifically so the customer's cleaning crew or ex-employee can't walk into a hardware store and cut a copy. If you're building a system for a utility, fleet yard, or anybody with turnover, sell the restricted keyway up front. It's the difference between a real security job and a bag of loose padlocks.\n\nOn body choice, match the metal to the environment, not to the customer's budget instinct. Solid steel (A700, A5200) is your default for gates, truck boxes, and machinery \u2014 hard, cheap enough, chrome-plated so it shrugs off weather for years. But the second you're near saltwater, chemical plants, wastewater, or a coastal utility, go brass \u2014 the A5560. Steel will weep rust and seize in a marine or corrosive setting inside a season; brass just keeps working, and the A5560 keeps the boron shackle and stainless ball bearings so you don't give up cut resistance to get corrosion resistance. I've cut off more rusted-solid steel padlocks at coastal sites than I can count, and every one of them should have been brass from day one. The boron shackle is the real story on all of these \u2014 boron alloy takes a bolt cutter far better than plain hardened steel, so when a customer's been getting shackles clipped, moving them to a boron-shackle American is a genuine upgrade, not a sales line.\n\nFor anything that's actually a target \u2014 cargo trailers, shipping containers, roll-up doors, storage yards that get hit \u2014 the answer is the hidden-shackle puck lock (the A2010, and the A801/A802 hasps that go with it). Nine times out of ten a trailer gets popped because somebody put a standard open-shackle lock where a bolt cutter or a pry bar could reach the shackle. The puck design buries the shackle inside the body \u2014 only 3/8in of it is exposed at all \u2014 so there's nothing to clip and nothing to lever. Note the A2010 is the current part: Master Lock consolidated the old A2000, A2001, and A2500 into it around January 2017, so if you're matching an existing site the A2010 is the drop-in. But the puck lock is only as good as the hasp \u2014 a hidden-shackle lock hanging in a regular open hasp is security theater. Sell the A801 (swinging/sliding doors) or the A802 trailer hasp (overlapping right-over-left doors) as a package with the lock; the lock's flat back is tapped and threaded to bolt straight to those hasps. If the mounting screws are exposed and drivable, you've solved nothing.\n\nOn interchangeable core: this is where you make yourself valuable to institutional and facilities customers. American builds bodies for Small Format IC (Best/Arrow/Falcon keyways) and Large Format IC (Schlage FSIC full-size, Yale). If a hospital, campus, or municipality already runs a Best SFIC system on their doors, you can drop their existing core keyway right into an A3560-style padlock body and the padlock keys off the same system as every door \u2014 one key, one control key, instant add to their masterkey chart. Just remember LFIC cores are brand-specific: a Yale large-format core will not seat in a Schlage FSIC housing and vice versa, so match the body to the brand they already run. Order these \"less core\" (WO / without cylinder) and stock the customer's cores yourself. When somebody gets terminated, you pull and swap the core in seconds instead of rekeying \u2014 that's the whole selling point, and most facilities buyers don't know it's available until you tell them.\n\nRepair-vs-replace and pricing judgment: because these are rekeyable, a frozen or worn A700/A5200 is usually a service call, not a throwaway. If the cylinder's just gummed or a customer lost keys, you rekey it \u2014 pull the cylinder, repin on the APTC12 (5-pin) or APT2001 (6-pin on the puck locks), done, and you keep it on their system. Replace the whole lock when the body's cracked, the shackle's been cut, or corrosion has seized the mechanism past saving \u2014 most common on old steel bodies left outdoors. Don't over-service a $30 lock; but on a mastered system, preserving the keying is worth the rekey labor every time, because re-integrating a new random lock into their chart costs more than the fix.",commonServicingIssues:["Symptom: shackle won't spring open even with the right key on an outdoor steel lock. Cause: rust/grit seized in the ball-bearing retention or a dried-out cylinder \u2014 classic on A700/A5200 left in weather. Fix: flush with a light penetrant (not heavy grease, it collects grit), work the shackle, then dry-lube; if the body is corroded through, replace and spec brass (A5560) for that location.","Symptom: key turns but shackle stays locked, or turns hard/gritty. Cause: contamination or a worn/lightly-jammed cylinder. Fix: clean and re-lubricate the APTC12/APT2001 cylinder with a dry PTFE or graphite-based lube; if pins are worn, pull and rekey rather than scrapping the body.","Symptom: customer lost keys to a mastered American padlock and doesn't want to break the system. Cause: no need to cut it off. Fix: rekey the APTC12 (5-pin) or APT2001 (6-pin) cylinder back onto their existing master system \u2014 the whole point of the rekeyable body is you keep it on the chart.","Symptom: hidden-shackle puck lock spins in the hasp but won't release, or the hasp is loose. Cause: usually the hasp, not the lock \u2014 wrong hasp for the door, worn mounting, or the lock body seated crooked on the threaded flat back. Fix: verify the A801/A802 matches the door type and that the lock's flat back is seated square; retighten/replace mounting through the door, not just surface screws.","Symptom: bolt-cutter attack clipped the shackle on a targeted site. Cause: open-shackle lock in an exposed hasp \u2014 wrong tool for a high-risk location. Fix: move to a hidden-shackle A2010 with an A801/A802 hasp so there's no exposed shackle to cut; harden the mounting so screws aren't drivable.","Symptom: SFIC/LFIC padlock body won't accept the customer's core, or the core won't seat/turn. Cause: wrong keyway family or control key not pulling the core, and 'less core' (WO) bodies ship empty. Fix: confirm the core is the correct Small Format keyway (Best/Arrow/Falcon) or the correct LFIC brand (Schlage FSIC vs Yale \u2014 they are not interchangeable), use the control key to seat/remove, and stock the right cores for their system.","Symptom: ex-employee or vendor made unauthorized key copies. Cause: locks were keyed on an open, freely-duplicated keyway. Fix: convert the system to the R2 restricted keyway (extra keys factory-ordered only) or the Edge key-control program (Master Lock controls all blanks) so copies can't be cut off the street; rekey affected locks onto the restricted system."],sources:["https://americanlocks.com/products/a700","https://americanlocks.com/products/a2010","https://americanlocks.com/products/american-lock-a5200-solid-steel-rekeyable-padlock-1-3-4in-44mm-wide","https://americanlocks.com/products/a5560","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/american-3560-sfic-ic-core-padlock"],photo:"photo-padlock-american",schematic:"schematic-padlock-american"},{id:"cam-lock",name:"Cam locks (cabinet / mailbox / drawer)",category:"Cam & furniture locks",whatItIs:"A cam lock is a cylinder that spins a flat metal arm \u2014 the cam \u2014 behind the panel to latch a drawer, door, or box. You see them everywhere: desk and file cabinet drawers, tool boxes, mailboxes, vending machines, gym lockers, elevator service panels, and telco/alarm enclosures. It is the workhorse of low-cost security hardware, so a big share of your bread-and-butter calls are cam locks. The cylinder is almost always disc-tumbler (standard security) or pin-tumbler (higher security), with tubular/ACE variants on vending and coin gear.",specs:["Standard mounting hole: 3/4\" (19mm); smaller furniture locks use 5/8\" (16mm). Head/collar diameter ~7/8\".","Cylinder (body) length is chosen to match material thickness. Common CompX National disc-tumbler bodies: 1-3/16\" (C8053), 1-7/16\" (C8055, clears up to 1-1/8\" material), 1-3/4\" (C8060).","Pin-tumbler example: CompX C8102, 1-1/16\" cylinder, 90-deg cam turn via internal shifter \u2014 works CW and CCW, D4291 blank, ships with four cams (two straight, two double-formed). Key removable in both locked and unlocked positions.","Cam turn: typically 90 degrees; some cabinet locks offer 180-degree throw. Rotation ordered as CW, CCW, or both.","Disc-tumbler standard blank: CompX National D8785 (5 discs). Disc-tumbler code capacity ~200 keyed-different / masterkeyed changes (C8055 rating).","Pin-tumbler code capacity (C8102): 850 keyed-different + 650 masterkeyed-different codes.","Cam types: straight cam (lipped/overlay construction), formed/offset cam (flush construction). Cam overall length commonly runs ~1-1/4\" to 2\".","Finishes (BHMA/US codes): satin/dull chrome 26D (BHMA 626), bright chrome 26 (BHMA 625), bright nickel 14A, bright brass 3, satin brass 4, antique brass 4G. Note: 26D is satin only \u2014 bright chrome is 26, not 26D.","Mailbox: CompX C8735 (pin-tumbler, D4291 blank, USPS L-1172 / drawing 1003833 for NDCBU/CBU 'double-D' retrofits and older 4B/4B+; 1,000 changes KD, 5 cams, dust shutter \u2014 will NOT fit new STD-4C boxes). C9100/C9200 (8-pin tumbler, USPS-STD-4C, USPS-L-1172C, 2,000 changes, KD only, cam rated to withstand ~1,000 lbf). C9100 = CW / left hinge, C9200 = CCW / right hinge. Mailbox locks are key-removable-in-locked-position only.","Tubular/ACE cam locks: pins arranged in a circle (commonly 7-pin) read by a tubular key; common on vending, coin boxes, and gun/display gear."],proAdvice:"Start with the cylinder type, because it decides everything else. Nine times out of ten a cabinet or desk cam lock is disc-tumbler \u2014 CompX National's C8055 family runs on the D8785 blank, five discs, 90-degree clockwise turn, key pulls in both locked and unlocked positions. That is your standard-security workhorse. When you feel real pins and deeper cuts, or the customer says \"it is a good lock,\" you are likely on a pin-tumbler body like the C8102: D4291 blank, and thanks to an internal shifter the 360-degree key turn throws the cam 90 degrees either direction, so one lock covers a drawer or a right- or left-hand door. CompX rates it as higher-security \u2014 850 keyed-different plus 650 masterkeyed changes against the disc lock's roughly 200 \u2014 but understand that \"higher security\" here is CompX's own language, not a UL or ANSI/BHMA grade; there is no published grade on these, so don't oversell it to a customer who needs a rated cabinet. Rule of thumb: disc tumbler for convenience, pin tumbler when the contents matter (narcotics cabinets, coin/cash storage), tubular/ACE when you are standing in front of vending or coin equipment.\n\nNow the part that actually gets you paid on the first trip \u2014 the cam. The cylinder and the cam are separate decisions and often separate part numbers. Measure the material thickness first (drawer face, door, box wall), then pick a cylinder length that clears it. A C8055 at 1-7/16\" handles up to about 1-1/8\" of material; a stubby 1-3/16\" body like the C8053 is for thin sheet metal or thin panels. Order the body too long and the threads bottom out before the nut bites; too short and the cam sits behind the frame and never catches. Then match the cam geometry to the construction: a straight cam for lipped/overlay drawers, a formed or offset cam for flush construction so the arm reaches back over the frame edge. Cam length commonly runs 1-1/4\" to 2\". This is why the better locks ship with two or even four cams in the bag \u2014 CompX puts two straight and two double-formed cams in the C8102 box precisely so you can size it on site. Carry an assorted cam kit and a bag of trim washers and spur washers, because the wrong cam or a missing washer is the number-one reason a \"five-minute swap\" turns into a return trip.\n\nRekeying disc-tumbler cam locks is a bench skill worth owning. Do not trust the stamped code on the plug \u2014 a marking like \"C413A\" is a keyed-alike group, not the bitting. You cross-reference it in the code book to the real five-digit number, then set the discs. The math is the trick: for a non-master lock you double each digit of the change-key number, so change key 40624 gives discs 44-00-66-22-44; if it is masterkeyed you pair the change number against the master, so with change 40624 and master 20642 you cut discs 42-00-66-24-42. You pop the five discs out of the plug without spilling the tiny springs (keep tweezers handy), drop in the new discs, seat them, and cut a key to match. It takes longer than rekeying a house lockset even though the parts are minuscule \u2014 bill accordingly. For pin-tumbler cam locks you rekey like any small-pin cylinder, and tubular/ACE locks can be rekeyed too but need dedicated tubular tools, so most shops swap the whole plug or the whole lock instead.\n\nRepair versus replace: a disc-tumbler cabinet lock is a five-to-fifteen-dollar part, so unless it is masterkeyed into an existing system you are almost always better replacing than fussing over a worn plug \u2014 the labor dwarfs the hardware. The judgment call is keying continuity. If the customer has fifty file cabinets all on one key, you rekey to hold the system; if it is one orphan drawer, you swap in a keyed-different unit and hand them two keys. On mailboxes, mind the liability. For USPS gang boxes the tenant lock is the property owner's or resident's responsibility, but the arrow/master compartment belongs to USPS and you do not touch it. Match the lock to the box: C9100/C9200 (USPS-L-1172C, 8-pin) are the only locks USPS approves for new STD-4C boxes, and their cams are built to shrug off pull-out force \u2014 rated to take on the order of 1,000 lbf. The older C8735 (USPS L-1172, drawing 1003833) is for NDCBU/CBU \"double-D\" retrofits and legacy 4B/4B+ boxes \u2014 it will NOT fit a new 4C box, so don't grab one off the truck for a 4C job. All of these are key-removable-in-the-locked-position-only by spec. Don't substitute a plain cabinet lock into a mailbox and expect it to pass or hold.\n\nLast, the security reality so you can harden as well as open. Disc-tumbler cam locks are convenience hardware, not real security \u2014 they yield to a jiggler or a few tries with a try-out key, and the whole point is deterrence and neatness, not resisting a determined attacker. When a client actually cares about the contents (narcotics cabinet, cash box, records), move them up to a pin-tumbler body or a high-security cylinder, add a hasp-and-padlock or a proper cabinet deadlatch, and stop relying on the disc lock alone. On vending and coin gear, standard 7-pin tubular/ACE locks fall to a tubular pick in seconds \u2014 knowing that is exactly why you recommend the high-security tubular upgrades or a puck lock over the OEM ACE. Knowing the bypass is how you sell the fix. And as always on a lockout: verify the person owns or is authorized on the cabinet, mailbox, or machine before you ever turn a pick.",commonServicingIssues:["Key turns but nothing latches \u2014 wrong or missing cam, or cam installed at the wrong clock position. Fix: pull the lock, fit the correct straight/offset cam for the construction, and set the cam so it clears the frame edge at rest and grabs at 90 (or 180) degrees.","Lock spins freely in the hole and won't stay put \u2014 cracked or omitted spur/trim washer, or over-length body that never tightens. Fix: add the spur washer (bites wood/thin metal to stop rotation) plus trim washer, and confirm the body length matches material thickness.","Cam swap-out that turned into a return trip \u2014 didn't measure material thickness or bring cams. Fix: carry an assorted cam and washer kit; measure panel thickness on site and match body length and cam offset before pulling the old lock.","Fifty cabinets, one lost key, customer wants them all to still match \u2014 orphaned masterkeyed system. Fix: don't just drop in keyed-different locks; read the code, rekey disc plugs to the existing change/master bitting so the system stays intact.","Stamped code doesn't cut a working key \u2014 the stamp is a KA group, not the bitting. Fix: cross-reference the group code (e.g., C413A) in the code book to the real five-digit number, then double each digit (non-master) or pair with the master to set the discs.","Mailbox lock replacement on a gang box \u2014 tenant locked out of a 4C/CBU compartment. Fix: use USPS-approved C9100/C9200 (USPS-L-1172C) for STD-4C, or C8735 (L-1172) for NDCBU/CBU double-D retrofits \u2014 never a C8735 in a new 4C box, and never touch the USPS arrow/master lock. Confirm the resident's authority first.","Sticky, gritty disc-tumbler action on old furniture \u2014 dried lube and dust in the plug. Fix: flush with dry graphite or PTFE (not oil, which gums), work the key, and if a spring is weak or a disc is worn, rekey or replace the cheap plug rather than fight it.","Vending or coin box compromised repeatedly \u2014 OEM tubular/ACE lock picked in seconds. Fix: explain the bypass, then upsell a high-security tubular cylinder or an external puck/hasp lock; standard 7-pin ACE is deterrent-only."],sources:["https://compx.com/product/disc-tumbler-cam-lock-1-7-16-c8055/","https://compx.com/product/pin-tumbler-cam-lock-1-1-16-c8102/","https://compx.com/product/disc-tumbler-cam-lock-1-3-16-c8053/","https://compx.com/product/five-disc-tumbler-pass-key-d8785/","https://hardwarehut.com/blog/disc-tumbler-cylinder-cam-lock/"],photo:"photo-cam-lock",schematic:"schematic-cam-lock"},{id:"rim-jimmyproof-deadbolt",name:"Rim / Jimmy-Proof (Vertical) Deadbolt",category:"Deadbolts",whatItIs:"A surface-mounted auxiliary deadbolt that locks with a vertical interlocking bolt (a drop-bolt / dead-latch) instead of the horizontal throw you get on a mortise or bored deadbolt. The lock case bolts to the interior face of the door and its bolt carries two rings that drop down through matching loops on a strike screwed to the jamb, so the door and frame get physically laced together. You see these on millions of apartment entry doors \u2014 brownstones, pre-war walk-ups, NYC in particular \u2014 usually running a rim cylinder keyed from the hall and a thumb-turn inside. It matters because that interlock is what makes it \"jimmy-proof\": you cannot spread the jamb and pry the door the way you can defeat a shallow-throw horizontal bolt. Samuel Segal, an NYPD officer, patented the design back in 1912 for exactly that reason.",specs:["Type: surface-mounted rim deadlock, vertical interlocking bolt (drop-bolt); NOT mortise or bored/cylindrical","Segal family: 667 / 667F single-cylinder (Prime-Line 15326), 666 double-cylinder; also sold as Prime-Line SE 15361 and under the SE600 catalog number, plus Yale and generic 'Concept'/'Norden' patterns \u2014 mounting holes and cylinder cutout are largely cross-compatible","Case, bolt and strike: solid high-strength bronze alloy; tamper-resistant shutter guard protects the cylinder tailpiece connection","Fits doors 1-3/8 in to 2-3/8 in thick (marketing spec); the factory tailpiece/screw cut table actually runs from 1 in up to 2-3/8 in","Cylinder: standard rim cylinder, 5-pin, Segal SE1 keyway; flat tailpiece plus two connector screws, both trimmed to door thickness per the cut table. Rim cylinder hole is bored 1-3/8 in dia with a 1-3/8 in hole saw","Rim cylinder is retrofittable to high-security: Medeco rim cylinder for Segal/Concept/Yale/Norden (approx 1-1/16 in), Mul-T-Lock, ASSA, etc.","Strikes: angle strike (for doors that sit proud/rabbeted) and flat strike both supplied; strike screws to the jamb with the supplied 3/4 in screws, bolt rings drop through the strike loops","Steel back-plate positioned 1/8 in from the door edge; 4 case mounting holes drilled with a 1/8 in bit x 3/4 in deep; case attaches with four 2 in screws","Finishes: US10/bronze, US3/brushed brass, and brushed chrome; solid bronze so it wears for decades","Single-cylinder (thumb-turn inside) vs double-cylinder (key both sides) versions; slam-lock/auto-relock variant (Segal 467F, model 466-7) with a function-hold button on the case","Accessory: Segal SE1320 riser bracket to raise the case when stop molding is in the way; SESTRK replacement strike; SE6050 (die-cast) / SE7050 (solid brass) replacement rim cylinder"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight, because apprentices mix it up constantly: this is a rim lock, not a mortise lock and not a bored deadbolt. Nothing gets pocketed into the door edge. The whole case sits on the inside face, held by four 2-inch screws into 3/4-inch-deep pilots and the two cylinder connector screws, and the security comes from the vertical interlock \u2014 two rings on the bolt drop down inside two loops on the jamb strike. That interlock is the entire point. A horizontal deadbolt only resists spreading as far as its throw; jam a jack in the gap, spread the jamb an inch, and a 1-inch throw walks right out of the pocket. You cannot do that here because the bolt is captured top and bottom by the strike loops \u2014 spreading the frame just binds it tighter. That is why the trade and the marketing both call it jimmy-proof, and why New York landlords have been screwing these on doors since Sam Segal patented it in 1912.\n\nKnow the family. The genuine article is the Segal \u2014 model 667 (667F) single-cylinder, 666 is the double, sold today under the Segal name and also boxed by Prime-Line as the SE 15361 and under the SE600 catalog number. Yale made a near-identical pattern, and there's a generic \"Concept\" / \"Norden\" clone; the mounting-hole spacing and the 1-3/8-inch rim-cylinder cutout are close enough that you can usually swap a case onto an existing bore. That interchangeability is your friend on service calls \u2014 you rarely have to re-drill the door. Everything runs a standard rim cylinder, 5-pin in the Segal SE1 keyway, with a flat tailpiece and two connector screws, and that is your upgrade path: pull the builder-grade five-pin and drop in a Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, or ASSA rim cylinder (Medeco literally catalogs a roughly 1-1/16-inch rim cylinder cut for Segal/Concept/Yale/Norden). You keep the jimmy-proof body and add a pick/bump/key-control cylinder. If the customer wants a hardened cylinder guard on top, Medeco's Bodyguard drop-bolt line is built to retrofit the same Segal/Yale hole pattern.\n\nOn the double-cylinder question \u2014 this is where your code and liability instincts have to fire before your wallet does. A lot of these doors carry a key-both-sides double cylinder because a landlord or a nervous tenant wanted it, usually on a door with glass nearby. On a dwelling-unit entrance that is the egress door, that's a problem. NYC Administrative Code 27-2043 for Class A multiple dwellings calls for a heavy-duty deadbolt operable by a key from the outside and a thumb-turn from the inside, plus a chain door guard \u2014 the thumb-turn inside is the operative phrase. Model code piles on: both the IBC and NFPA 101 want a dwelling-unit egress door openable from the inside without a key, tool, or special knowledge, and the IRC flatly says a double-cylinder deadbolt does not meet the requirement for a lock on an egress door. So if you're installing on an apartment's primary exit, spec the single-cylinder with the inside thumb-turn. If the tenant insists on double because of adjacent glass, that's a conversation about laminated glass or a grille, not a key-locked exit \u2014 and you document that you advised against it. On a fire-rated door, double cylinder isn't happening unless the whole assembly is labeled for it under NFPA 80. Don't be the guy whose lock trapped somebody.\n\nRepair-vs-replace: the Segal body is solid bronze and basically immortal \u2014 I pull 40-year-old cases that still work. Nine times out of ten the \"broken lock\" is not the case, it's the strike alignment or a worn cylinder, and both are cheap parts, not a whole new lock. Keep replacement strikes (SESTRK), a rim-cylinder refill (SE7050 brass or SE6050 die-cast), and the SE1320 riser brackets on the truck. The riser is the sleeper part: half these old doors have thick stop molding or a settled frame, and the case sits too low for the bolt rings to clear the strike loops \u2014 a riser lifts the case so the geometry works without re-drilling. Pricing-wise the complete lockset runs roughly $55\u2013$70; the real money and the real value is in getting the strike dead-nuts aligned and, if they care about security, a real cylinder. Reserve full replacement for a cracked case or a hopelessly munged bore.\n\nOne thing to preach: the surface-mount is a double-edged sword. It's the easiest serious security a renter can install \u2014 no mortising, hand tools, an hour \u2014 but the whole thing is only as strong as what those four case screws and the strike screws bite into. On a hollow-core door or a soft, delaminating old jamb, the interlock is theater; a good kick tears the strike or the case right off the wood. So on the install, check the substrate first. Solid-core or metal door, sound jamb \u2014 great. Punky frame \u2014 you reinforce the jamb or you're selling false confidence.",commonServicingIssues:["Bolt won't drop / won't engage the strike \u2014 nine times out of ten it's strike misalignment, not the lock. The door has sagged, swelled, or the frame settled so the bolt rings no longer line up with the strike loops. Fix: reset the strike (the install sheet even tells you to shim or mortise the frame for alignment); if the case sits too low because of thick stop molding, add a Segal SE1320 riser bracket to lift the case rather than fighting the strike position.","Lock feels sticky or gritty when you turn the thumb-turn \u2014 dirt and dried-out lube in the bolt mechanism, common on decades-old cases. Fix: put the bolt in the unlocked position, spray white lithium grease into the opening exposed under the retracted bolt (this is exactly what Segal's own maintenance note calls for), cycle it several times; graphite the exterior cylinder once a year. Do NOT flood it with oil \u2014 it gums up and collects dust.","Key turns but the bolt does nothing / lock feels disconnected \u2014 the tailpiece was cut too short at install (or a prior hack), or a connector screw backed out, so the cylinder cam no longer drives the case. Fix: pull the cylinder, check tailpiece length against door thickness per the cut table, replace or re-shim the tailpiece; re-seat and tighten the two connector screws.","Whole case is loose / rattles / can be pushed off the door \u2014 the four 2-inch case screws stripped out of a soft or hollow-core door, or someone kicked it. Fix: relocate to solid material, use longer/larger screws into blocking, or reinforce the door; on a hollow-core door recommend a solid-core or metal-clad door because the interlock is only as good as its anchoring.","Customer wants better security but keep the lock \u2014 the factory 5-pin SE1 rim cylinder is easy to pick/bump. Fix: swap in a Medeco / Mul-T-Lock / ASSA rim cylinder cut for the Segal/Concept/Yale/Norden pattern; keeps the jimmy-proof body, adds key control and drill/pick resistance.","Double-cylinder on an apartment exit door \u2014 key required on the inside, an egress and code problem (NYC 27-2043 wants a thumb-turn inside; NFPA 101/IBC/IRC want key-free egress). Fix: convert to the single-cylinder thumb-turn version; if glass adjacency is the worry, address the glass (laminate/grille), and document that you advised against the double.","Shutter guard fell off or cylinder spins/pulls \u2014 the tamper shutter or cylinder retention failed, or a pull attack loosened it. Fix: reseat the shutter guard over the tailpiece on the back-plate, retighten the connector screws; if the cylinder was wrenched, replace it and consider a Medeco Bodyguard cylinder-guard drop-bolt that retrofits the same hole pattern.","Slam-lock version (467F) won't stay unlocked / auto-relocks when they don't want it \u2014 that's the normal auto-relock feature. Fix: retract the bolt and push in the function-hold button on the lock face to hold it unlocked; reverse the procedure to re-enable auto-locking."],sources:["https://www.lockandhinge.com/product_images/import/Manuals_Templates/manual_667_Segal%20667.pdf","https://www.primelineparts.com/segal-solid-bronze-single-cylinder-jimmy-proof-deadbolt/","https://www.primeline.net/se-15361-segal-deadbolt-4-12-bronze-brushed-brass-single-cyl","https://www.edlocks.com/segal-single-cylinder-jimmy-proof-deadbolt/","https://www.edlocks.com/segal-double-cylinder-jimmy-proof-deadbolt/"],photo:"photo-rim-jimmyproof-deadbolt",schematic:"schematic-rim-jimmyproof-deadbolt"},{id:"interconnected-lockset",name:"Interconnected Lockset",category:"Residential",whatItIs:"An interconnected lockset is a single unit that mechanically links a knob or lever latch to a deadbolt so that turning the inside lever retracts BOTH bolts in one motion \u2014 that's single-motion egress. You see them almost exclusively on apartment and condo entry doors, extended-care and multifamily housing, where the code wants a panic-free way out. The point is life safety: a panicked or impaired person turns one lever and the door opens, no matter how many bolts a nervous tenant threw on the way in. From the hallway side it still behaves like a keyed entry with a separate keyed deadbolt.",specs:["Governing standard: ANSI/BHMA A156.12, Interconnected Locks (current edition A156.12-2022, revising 2018/2013) \u2014 covers a chassis with a separate latch/deadlatch and a deadbolt in bored openings where a single inside operation retracts all bolts; graded 1/2/3, Grade 1 highest","Typical residential/light-commercial offering is Grade 2 \u2014 Schlage CS210 is A156.12 Grade 2; note Kwikset's metal interconnect deadbolt is certified to A156.5 (auxiliary locks/deadbolts) Grade 2, NOT A156.12","Deadbolt throw: 1 in. (25 mm) full throw; residential units use a hardened/anti-saw deadbolt (Kwikset 780 uses a titanium-alloy roller/saw pin)","Latch throw: 1/2 in. (13 mm) brass latch/deadlatch","Backset: adjustable 2-3/8 in. or 2-3/4 in.; Schlage CS210 also offers 5 in. optional","Door prep: interconnect (two 2-1/8 in. bores linked). Kwikset metal interconnect is 5-1/2 in. center-to-center ONLY; Schlage CS210's adjustable escutcheon field-preps to either 4 in. OR 5-1/2 in. hole spacing","Door thickness: Schlage CS210 = 1-3/4 in. to 2 in. standard, 1-3/8 in. available; Kwikset metal interconnect handles standard 1-3/4 in. and 2-1/4 in.","Function: entrance / single-locking \u2014 Schlage CS210 is ANSI F95; keyed both sides available but avoid on egress dwelling doors (see pro advice)","Fire: Schlage CS210 is UL listed to 3 hours (fire door) on rated openings \u2014 verify the specific SKU/config is fire-listed before you hang it on a corridor door","Accessibility: lever versions meet ICC/ANSI A117.1 and ADA operable-with-one-hand, no-tight-grasp, no-pinching requirements"],proAdvice:"Understand what makes an interconnect an interconnect, because customers and even some counter guys confuse it with \"a knob and a deadbolt in the same box.\" A true interconnected lockset has a mechanical link \u2014 a bar or pawl inside the chassis \u2014 so that rotating the inside lever pulls the deadbolt back at the same time it retracts the latch. That single-motion egress is the whole reason the thing exists. Two separate locks stacked on a door do NOT give you that; a tenant has to thumb-turn the deadbolt AND turn the knob, which is exactly the two-operation problem the fire code is written around. So when someone says \"just put a deadbolt above the knob,\" on a code-governed egress door that answer is often wrong.\n\nHere's where code wants it. Under the 2021 IBC, section 1010.2.1 says the unlatching of any door or leaf for egress \"shall require not more than one motion in a single linear or rotational direction to release all latching and all locking devices.\" That's the one-motion rule. There's a narrow residential carve-out: 1010.2.4 lets doors from individual Group R dwelling or sleeping units with an occupant load of 10 or less carry a night latch, dead bolt or security chain in addition to the latch, PROVIDED those devices open from the inside without a key or tool. So a plain apartment door can legally have a separate thumbturn deadbolt. But \u2014 and this is the money point \u2014 the moment that unit is an accessible or Type A/B dwelling unit under ICC A117.1, or your local amendment or the AHJ pushes it, the two-operation luxury gets squeezed and the interconnect becomes the clean, compliant answer. That's why property managers on newer or renovated multifamily buildings spec interconnects across the board: one SKU, no arguments with the inspector, and maintenance can't defeat egress by adding a chain. Always confirm with the AHJ, because NFPA 101 and state accessibility codes (Massachusetts is the classic tightener) go further than the base IBC.\n\nOn grade and brand: for residential/light-commercial multifamily, Grade 2 is the sweet spot and what both majors actually build. Schlage's commercial answer is the CS210 (it superseded the discontinued S210/S200 series) \u2014 solid chassis, A156.12 Grade 2, adjustable escutcheon that field-preps to either 4 in. or 5-1/2 in. hole spacing, ANSI F95 entrance function, and it's UL listed to 3 hours on a fire door if you order the right configuration. That fire listing is why I reach for Schlage on rated corridor doors. Kwikset's play is the metal interconnect (506 knob / 508 lever family, a 780 deadbolt married to a keyed lever or knob, usually SmartKey) \u2014 genuinely all-metal, 5-1/2 in. center-to-center only. One accuracy note so you don't get caught bluffing: Kwikset certifies that deadbolt to A156.5 (auxiliary locks) Grade 2, not to A156.12 \u2014 it is still a legitimate Grade 2 metal interconnect, just cite the right standard. SmartKey is a real asset for a property because you can rekey a whole building's worth of turnover units in seconds without pulling cylinders or stocking pinning kits \u2014 just don't oversell its pick resistance to a security-minded client. Steer away from the flimsy tubular \"interconnect\" combo packs at the big box; the plastic linkage bars strip and you'll be back.\n\nRepair versus replace and pricing: a residential Kwikset metal interconnect runs you roughly $60-$140 at the counter, and a Schlage CS210 lands more in the $180-$320 range depending on lever, finish and whether it's fire-listed (prices move, so quote current). Given those numbers, if the chassis linkage is worn or the deadbolt no longer kicks back with the lever, replace the unit \u2014 do not spend an hour chasing a $12 part inside a $90 lock. Where you DO repair: latches, strikes, and cylinders. Keep the interconnect chassis, swap a bad deadlatch or re-pin the cylinder. On the liability side, never \u2014 ever \u2014 defeat the interconnect to satisfy a tenant who wants a \"double-keyed\" setup so nobody can get out without a key. Keyed-both-sides on an egress dwelling door is a code violation and a body-count lawsuit waiting to happen; the one-motion rule exists because people died behind double-cylinder deadbolts. If a client insists, document that you declined and why. That instinct \u2014 knowing exactly why the bolts link and refusing to unlink them \u2014 is also how you harden a door: understand that the single-motion path is the intended egress bypass, keep the exterior deadbolt and strike beefy (3-in. screws into the framing, box strike, 1-in. throw fully seated), and you get security without ever compromising the way out.",commonServicingIssues:["Symptom: turning the inside lever no longer pulls the deadbolt back (latch retracts, deadbolt stays thrown). Cause: worn or disengaged interconnect linkage bar/pawl in the chassis, or the deadbolt was installed a hair off so the link can't grab the tailpiece. Fix: pull the lock, verify the linkage tab seats into the deadbolt tailpiece; if the plastic/soft-metal link is stripped, replace the chassis \u2014 this is a life-safety failure, not something to shim.","Symptom: deadbolt is stiff or won't fully throw/retract. Cause: door sag or frame swelling has the bolt binding in a misaligned strike (nine times out of ten it's alignment, not the lock). Fix: check the bolt against the strike with the door open \u2014 if it throws freely open but binds closed, it's the strike/door; file or reposition the strike, add a box strike, adjust hinges before you condemn the lock.","Symptom: whole unit loose, levers wobble, escutcheon rocking. Cause: interconnect through-bolts backed out, or wrong hole spacing forced (a 4 in. Schlage prep fighting a 5-1/2 in. hole pattern, or a 5-1/2-in.-only Kwikset on the wrong bore). Fix: retighten mounting screws to the correct 4 in. or 5-1/2 in. prep; if the door was bored wrong, verify center-to-center before re-drilling.","Symptom: key turns but doesn't operate the deadbolt from outside. Cause: cylinder tailpiece worn or mis-clocked, or on SmartKey units a failed/half-completed rekey left it in learn mode. Fix: re-seat the tailpiece; for SmartKey, run the reset with the learn tool and a known-good key, then re-verify both sides before leaving.","Symptom: lever handle droops or won't spring back. Cause: broken return spring in the lever module or a lever installed on the wrong hand. Fix: rehand or replace the lever/spring cage \u2014 most interconnect levers are field-reversible; confirm handing off the door swing.","Symptom: inspector red-tags an apartment door with an add-on chain or slide bolt above the interconnect. Cause: the extra device creates a second operation / a non-compliant latching device on an egress door. Fix: remove the aftermarket hardware; the interconnect alone satisfies single-motion egress \u2014 educate the tenant on why."],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/mechanical-locks/cs200-series-grade-2-interconnected-locks.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_CS_210_Series_Interconnected_Lock_Sell_Sheet_105085.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_CS210_Service_Manual_0139547.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/mechanical-locks/s200-series-interconnected-locks.html","https://uslocksupply.com/products/schlage-cs210-interconnected-lock"],photo:"photo-interconnected-lockset",schematic:"schematic-interconnected-lockset"},{id:"cbu-mailbox",name:"USPS Cluster Box Unit (CBU) & 4C Mailbox Locks",category:"Mailbox & specialty",whatItIs:"The cluster box unit (CBU) is the freestanding pedestal or wall-recessed bank of locked mail compartments you see at the entrance of subdivisions, apartment complexes, and condos \u2014 plus the 4C horizontal wall units in lobbies and mailrooms. Two locks live on every one: the tenant compartment locks (a small pin-tumbler cam lock the resident carries a key for) and the carrier \"Arrow\" lock on the master loading door, which only the letter carrier's Arrow key opens. It matters because this is a federally regulated niche \u2014 the whole unit is built to USPS-STD-4C, the mail inside is protected by federal law, and who is allowed to touch which lock is spelled out by the Postal Service, not by you.",specs:["Governing standard: USPS-STD-4C (\"Wall-Mounted Centralized Mail Receptacles\"), mandatory for building plans submitted for new construction and major renovation after October 6, 2006 \u2014 it superseded the old 4B/4B+ formats and effectively retired the pedestal NDCBU for new installs","Tenant lock spec: PSIN O910 cam lock built to USPS-L-1172 (current rev USPS-L-1172C) \u2014 cam rotates 90 degrees locked-to-unlocked, key removable only in the LOCKED position","Lock-hole strength: the mounting must withstand 100 ft-lb of rotational torque so the lock can't be spun in the door to force entry","Current production tenant lock: USPS-L-1172C, ordered as PSIN O910A (clockwise cam) and O910B (counter-clockwise cam) \u2014 \"USPS-L-1172C\" is impressed on the lock face; CompX sells the same lock as C9100 (CW) / C9200 (CCW)","The new 1172C body uses a NEW keyed/notched mounting hole pattern; the OLD O910 lock used a \"Double-D\" mounting hole. On older equipment the 1172C is fitted into the old Double-D hole using the mandatory adhesive-backed washer that ships in the kit (per USPS install sheet O910-1172C / MMO-086-05)","Retainer hardware ships WITH the lock: a spring clip, an adhesive-backed washer, and a 5/16-24 nut \u2014 WARNING per the install sheet, the new nut has a DIFFERENT thread than the old nut; use only the new nut supplied with the new lock","Aftermarket/equivalents: AF-Florence 1570 \"F\"-series tenant lock = USPS-L-1172C / C9100; CCL/Eberhard 82011-series; key blank National D4292 = Ilco 1069LB (National NA12 family); tenant keys typically stamped a code followed by \"PS\"","Cylinder: pin-tumbler, keyed different (KD) \u2014 CompX rates roughly 2,000 key changes; ships with 3 keys per lock","Arrow (carrier) lock: mounts in the master loading door driving a 3-point (top/middle/bottom) latch; USPS-controlled, opened only by the carrier's Arrow key","Mounting heights (STD-4C with ICC A117.1 / ADA reach): Arrow (master) lock 36\u201348 in AFF; highest tenant lock no higher than 67 in AFF; lowest parcel-locker bottom shelf at least 15 in AFF; lowest compartment no closer than 28 in to the finished floor; at least one tenant COMPARTMENT (not lock) no higher than 48 in","Parcel lockers: original 2004 STD-4C required min 1 parcel locker per 10 tenant compartments; current spec tightened this toward 1:5 \u2014 confirm against the edition your AHJ/USPS is enforcing; min tenant compartment interior 12 in W x 15 in D x 3 in H","Outgoing-mail deposit slot: 10.75 in W x 0.75 in H, with weather and security shields to stop fishing/pilfering","Tenant key/lock service on USPS-CONTROLLED units is requested at the counter on PS Form 1094 (key & lock service). Note: PS Form 1093 is the separate \"Application for Post Office Box or Caller Service\" \u2014 don't confuse the two"],proAdvice:"First thing you settle on any cluster-box call \u2014 before you touch a screwdriver \u2014 is who owns the unit, because that answer decides whether this is even your job. Two flavors out there and they look almost identical. A USPS-owned/USPS-maintained unit (older loading-dock CBUs and NDCBUs where the Postal Service holds the Arrow lock and maintains the whole box) is federal equipment; you do not service tenant locks on those, period \u2014 the resident brings photo ID and proof of address to their post office, fills out PS Form 1094 for key and lock service, and USPS swaps it for roughly a $20\u2013$50 fee. The far more common call today is a privately owned 4C or CBU on an apartment complex, HOA, or condo \u2014 the developer bought it, the property manager owns the tenant locks, and the ONLY thing USPS controls is the Arrow lock on the master loading door. On those, tenant-lock work is legitimately yours. The tell is the master door: the triangular Arrow cylinder means USPS installed and holds that door; every tenant door is the building's. Get the property manager or HOA to authorize in writing \u2014 you're opening someone else's mailbox, and mail theft is a federal beef, so paper protects you.\n\nNine times out of ten the \"my mailbox key won't work\" call is not a bad lock \u2014 it's the wrong key, a worn key, or a tenant who lost all three and never told management. Check the easy stuff first: does the code stamped on their key match the door, and does a known-good spare turn it? Only after that do you condemn the cylinder. When you do replace, match the cam rotation \u2014 clockwise is O910A (CompX C9100), counter-clockwise is O910B (C9200) \u2014 and know your equipment, because some units force your hand. The USPS install sheet calls it out: most boxes take either rotation, but the last few years of American Locker CBUs with the hooked cams REQUIRE the CCW O910B. Match the mount too, and this is where guys get burned: the current 1172C body has a new notched mounting hole, but a mountain of older Florence, American, and Salsbury boxes were cut for the old \"Double-D\" hole. That's exactly what the adhesive-backed washer in the kit is for \u2014 it adapts the stronger new lock to the old Double-D hole. The correct sequence off the actual USPS sheet: pull the old lock, save its cam and any backing plate for reuse, wipe the door face clean (the structural adhesive will not bond to a dirty surface), slide the washer over the lock body with the adhesive side facing the door, peel the liner, seat the lock aligned to the old Double-D pattern so the locked cam sits perpendicular to the sidewall, press a few seconds to bond, install the spring clip in the same direction it came out, then run the new 5/16-24 nut down. If the clip seats but the lock is still loose, or the clip is too tight to squeeze in, add spacer washers on the BACKSIDE of the door to take up the gap \u2014 that's the sheet's own fix, not a bodge.\n\nNow kill a myth an apprentice will swear by: that you must never install one of these with the key in or you'll wreck the cylinder. That is not what USPS says. The actual instruction (step 9 on the O910-1172C sheet) is that the key CAN be inserted while you torque the nut but it is not recommended \u2014 there is no \"internal damage occurs if installed with the key in\" caution anywhere on it. The real caution is different and it's the one that actually matters: while you tighten the nut, keep the CAM from rotating \u2014 hold it by hand or clamp a vise grip to the door just below it \u2014 so you don't drive excessive internal load through the lock. And use ONLY the new nut that came with the new lock; the sheet flags in bold that the new nut has a different thread than the old one, so a leftover old nut will cross-thread and strip. Tighten until the cam is fully seated \u2014 snug, not gorilla-tight, it's die-cast \u2014 then test that the key inserts and the plug turns freely before you close the door, and remove the key when you're done. Access to the backside is through the master door: on a private unit the property should hand you a management/building key for the tenant-door bank; if not, you pick or drill the single failed tenant door and rekey it. Do NOT touch the Arrow lock \u2014 you don't have the blank, you're not authorized, and picking or drilling a carrier lock on federal mail equipment is the kind of thing that ends careers.\n\nRepair-vs-replace is easy here: these are cheap commodity cylinders, so you almost never \"repair\" a tenant lock \u2014 you swap it. Buy them in packs, keep both cam rotations on the truck, and carry a strip of the key blanks (National D4292 / Ilco 1069LB). Pricing: a private tenant-lock swap runs about $75\u2013$150 all-in depending on market and whether it's after-hours emergency \u2014 the lock itself is only $8\u2013$20, the rest is your trip and labor. If a whole 4C panel of doors is failing, that's a manufacturer/PM conversation, not a locksmith patch \u2014 a replacement tenant door with lock is a real Florence/National part number, and a full CBU unit runs roughly $2,000 for a bare small bank up to $3,900-plus for a 16-door with parcel lockers and pedestal. Where you add real value is the keying scheme: on a new private install, help the PM get the tenant locks set up so management can turn a unit at move-out without calling you every time. And know your lane on the Arrow lock \u2014 when a new 4C goes in, remind the builder the carrier will not deliver until USPS comes out and installs the Arrow lock (they schedule it, no charge), and you cannot do that step for them no matter how hard they push.",commonServicingIssues:["Symptom: tenant key turns but the whole lock spins in the door and won't retract the cam. Cause: a new 1172C body dropped into an old Double-D hole without the adhesive-backed washer, so it walks loose. Fix: pull it, clean the door face, install the correct-rotation O910A/B with the kit's adhesive washer (adhesive side to the door), seat it aligned to the Double-D pattern, add backside spacer washers if the clip won't snug it, then torque the new 5/16-24 nut \u2014 verify it resists twisting by hand.","Symptom: new replacement lock cross-threads or won't tighten. Cause: someone reused the OLD nut on the new lock \u2014 the install sheet warns the new nut has a different thread. Fix: back it off, install the NEW nut supplied with the lock; if the threads are already chewed, the lock is scrap \u2014 replace it.","Symptom: replacement lock binds or the cam feels notchy after install. Cause: the cam was allowed to rotate while the nut was torqued, driving load through the internals. Fix: while tightening, hold the cam or clamp a vise grip just below it so it can't spin; if it's already damaged, swap the cylinder.","Symptom: resident says 'key doesn't work' but door and lock are fine. Cause: worn key, a prior tenant's key, or they lost all three and are trying a neighbor's. Fix: verify the stamped code matches the door, cut a fresh key on the correct blank (D4292/1069LB); only replace the cylinder if a known-good key also fails.","Symptom: property manager wants you to rekey or open the master/carrier door on a private complex. Cause: they assume the whole unit is theirs. Fix: don't \u2014 the Arrow lock is USPS-controlled federal equipment. Open only the individual tenant doors (management key, pick, or drill) and rekey those; the master door stays with USPS.","Symptom: new 4C install, carrier isn't delivering mail. Cause: Arrow lock not yet installed by USPS \u2014 no one else may install it. Fix: builder/PM contacts the local Postmaster to schedule Arrow-lock installation (no charge); nothing for the locksmith to do on that door.","Symptom: tenant lost all keys on a USPS-owned unit and wants a locksmith to make one. Cause: locksmiths aren't authorized to service USPS-controlled boxes. Fix: route them to the post office with photo ID and proof of address to file PS Form 1094; on a PRIVATELY owned unit, get PM authorization, then pick/drill and re-lock the tenant door yourself.","Symptom: old American Locker CBU, replacement lock installs but the cam won't catch the keeper. Cause: those hooked-cam units require the CCW O910B rotation and the original cam reused in its original orientation. Fix: use the CCW (O910B / C9200) lock, remount the original cam at the same orientation and length; carry assorted cams so a mismatched cam length doesn't leave it over-traveling or short."],sources:["https://about.usps.com/publications/engineering-standards-specifications/spusps-std-4c001.pdf","https://about.usps.com/publications/engineering-standards-specifications/spusps-std-4c001/welcome.html","https://compx.com/resources/usps-l-1172c-instructions.pdf","https://compx.com/resources/usps-l-1172c-mmo.pdf","https://about.usps.com/postal-bulletin/2007/html/pb22206/mailboxkit.4.19.html"],photo:"photo-cbu-mailbox",schematic:"schematic-cbu-mailbox"},{id:"lcn-senior-swing",name:"LCN Senior Swing Low-Energy ADA Operator",category:"Auto operators",whatItIs:"The LCN Senior Swing is an electromechanical low-energy swing-door operator \u2014 the workhorse ADA auto-operator you see on schools, hospitals, government buildings, and vestibule pairs across North America. It comes in a 2800 overhead-concealed series (hidden in the header) and a 9500 surface-applied series (the box you see mounted on the header face), both driven by a separate control box you program from an on-board keypad. When triggered by a push-button, wave plate, or access-control relay, the motor drives the door open, then cuts power and lets spring force bring it back \u2014 so on a power failure it works as a manual size-3 closer. It matters because it is a life-safety and code-compliance device: set it wrong and you own the liability for anyone it hits.",specs:["Series: 2800 overhead-concealed / 9500 surface-applied; single (2810/9530/9540), simultaneous pair (2850/9550), independent pair (2860/9560); IQ variants (9500IQ/2800IQ) add AdaptivIQ self-adjusting logic","AC input: 120 VAC, single-phase, 60 Hz, dedicated fused 15 A 3-wire circuit brought to the side jamb (do NOT share it with lighting); control box internally protected by a 120 VAC 2.5 A time-delay fuse (per Control Box Instruction Sheet 107510)","Manual/power-off mode: functions as a spring closer, factory rated size 3 (stated in the install manual)","Door width: single door 33\" minimum, 48\" maximum per leaf; a simultaneous/independent pair covers a combined opening of about 52\"\u201396\". Concealed runs center-pivot, offset-pivot, or butt-hung; surface adds balanced (push side only)","Door weight: varies by arm and configuration \u2014 LCN publishes minimum times by width/weight rather than one hard cap; the 9500IQ/2800IQ literature cites operation up to about 600 lb. Verify the specific model's rating for a given leaf; on power loss it reverts to a size-3 manual closer","Accessory power output: 24 VDC, 1 A (resettable thermal fuse); separate logic power 5 V, 50 mA (resettable thermal fuse)","Lock output: Form C dry relay contacts rated 8 A, 30 VDC maximum \u2014 used for electric strike or EL retraction (P10 locking interface)","Inputs: Activate = dry N.O. (closed = open command); Safety/DMSS = 24 VDC sensor loops; Breakaway = removable jumper (part 761492-00) that also drops the board into diagnostic mode when pulled; 3-position switch: OFF / AUTO / HOLD","Keypad-adjustable (Table 4-1): opening speed 01\u201303 (default 01), backcheck speed 01\u201302 (default 01), backcheck position 45\u201380\xb0 (default 70), hold-open delay 01\u201332 s (default 05), latch position 00\u201323\xb0 (default 13), auto-reverse closing, Push-N-Go, Power Boost, electric lock off/SA/SE. Close speed is set by dials on the board, not the keypad","A156.19 low-energy limits: open closed\u2192backcheck/80\xb0 in \u22653 s, backcheck not before 60\xb0; close 90\xb0\u219210\xb0 in \u22653 s and 10\xb0\u2192closed in \u22651.5 s; stay fully open \u22655 s (\u22653 s if push-pull activation); kinetic energy \u22641.25 lbf-ft (1.69 Nm); \u226415 lbf to stop the door (and manual force limits 15 lbf release latch / 30 lbf set in motion / 15 lbf fully open)","Header/mounting: header section runs about 5-3/4\" high x 4-1/2\" wide (deep); minimum header length 33\" with an applied stop by others, 36\" with an LCN fixed/breakaway stop; double-door headers specify 49\"\u201398\". Offset-pivot arm 3077OP (3-3/4\"), center-pivot arm 3077CP (2-3/4\" or 3-3/4\")","Tech support: LCN/Allegion 1-877-671-7011"],proAdvice:"Let me start with the thing that gets guys in trouble: the Senior Swing is a LOW-ENERGY operator, not a full-power automatic. That word \"low-energy\" is not marketing \u2014 it means ANSI/BHMA A156.19, and it means a person has to consciously trigger it (a push-button, a wave plate, a card reader). If you want it to open on approach with a motion sensor you must add safety sensors and treat it like a full-power door, which is a different animal and more liability. Nine times out of ten when I get called to a \"the door hits people\" complaint, somebody sped it up or shortened the hold-open time to make it feel snappier, and now it is out of A156.19. Memorize the numbers cold, straight out of section 4 of the standard: three seconds minimum from closed to backcheck or 80 degrees, backcheck cannot kick in before 60 degrees, three seconds to close from 90 down to 10, another second and a half from 10 to fully closed, and it has to stand open at least five seconds (three if you are on push-pull activation). Kinetic energy caps at 1.25 lbf-ft \u2014 that's 1.69 Nm \u2014 and it must take no more than 15 pounds measured an inch from the latch edge to stop the leaf anywhere in its travel. The control box literally prints a warning on the board that ALL speed, closing, and hold-open settings must be field-adjusted to meet the latest ANSI/BHMA \u2014 and the manual tells you in plain English that if you skip the safety and instruction decals you are LIABLE for any accident. Take that seriously: put the decals on, and set the timing with the door glazed, because the manual is explicit that operator speed cannot be set properly until after the glass is in. Document what you set.\n\nKnow which box you are looking at before you quote a job. The 2800 is overhead-concealed \u2014 motor and arm buried in the header, clean look, used a lot on storefronts and nicer institutional entrances \u2014 and it runs center-pivot, offset-pivot, or butt-hung doors. The 9500 is surface-applied \u2014 the visible box \u2014 and it will run balanced (push side only), center-pivot, offset-pivot, or butt-hung on either push or pull mode. The suffix tells you the door count: 2810/9530/9540 is a single, 2850/9550 is a simultaneous pair (both leaves driven together off one control box with a companion motor lead), 2860/9560 is an independent pair. The newer 9500IQ/2800IQ adds AdaptivIQ, which self-adjusts to wind, stack pressure, and carpet drag on the fly \u2014 worth the upcharge on a windy vestibule or a door that fights HVAC pressure, because it saves you callbacks for a door that opens fine in the morning and stalls in the afternoon. Two arm part numbers worth knowing: the offset-pivot/butt-hung arm is the 3077OP (3-3/4 inch), the center-pivot arm is the 3077CP (2-3/4 or 3-3/4 inch depending on your mounting point). Watch the pivot mounting point \u2014 the template calls for 2-3/4 inch or 3-3/4 inch and the pivot block install differs; get it wrong and the door will not sit square in its stops.\n\nOn integration \u2014 this is where you earn your money. The lock output is a Form C dry relay, 8 A at 30 VDC max, on the P10 locking interface, and the golden rule from the manual is: wire the locking device to the N.O. contact and then set the keypad EL parameter to match. Set it to SA for a fail-safe electric strike \u2014 strike is powered/locked when the door is closed, drops power to unlock as the door opens, and unlocks whenever the 3-position switch is OFF. Set it to SE for a fail-secure electric latch retraction device \u2014 the classic being a Von Duprin EL exit device run through a PS914 power supply; the Senior Swing relay just signals the PS914 to retract, and on that run the manual specifies 12 AWG stranded wire for lock outputs 01 and 02, because latch-retraction solenoids inrush hard and voltage drop will leave you with a device that buzzes and won't pull. The magic setting people forget is the electric-lock delay: turning EL on (SA or SE) inserts a one-second pause between the activate signal and the motor starting so the strike or latch actually clears before the operator shoves the door \u2014 leave that off and you will hammer the lock, chew the strike, and eventually stall the motor or snap a latch. Sequence of operations always: unlock/retract first, THEN drive. For fire alarm and hold-open: the Senior Swing itself is not a fire-door hold-open device \u2014 if this is a labeled fire/smoke door assembly under NFPA 80/101 you must arrange for the alarm to release it to the closed, latched, self-closing condition. The clean way is to drop the operator's power or its activate/lock path on alarm through the fire-alarm control panel so the spring closer takes over and any EL latch re-projects; verify the leaf actually latches under alarm, because a fire door that won't latch is a failed inspection and a real hazard. Don't freelance the alarm interface \u2014 coordinate the interposing relay and the fail-state with the fire-alarm tech and the AHJ.\n\nRepair versus replace and pricing instinct: the mechanical guts on these are stout and the arms and spring packs run for years, so most trouble is in the control box, the motor cable, or the accessories. A control board runs a few hundred dollars and swaps in maybe an hour once you know the P-connector layout, so a bad board is a repair, not a reason to rip out the operator. If the header casting is cracked, the pivot/spindle is sloppy, or you are staring at a 20-year-old unit that's been rebuilt twice, that's when replacement \u2014 figure a low-energy operator install landing roughly in the low-to-mid four figures installed depending on arm, actuators, and access control \u2014 starts to pencil out, and it's also your chance to move a legacy unit to a 9500IQ and kill the wind callbacks. Two field habits that save you: on a two-leaf setup mind the companion motor power lead into P5 and re-run the sizing cycle any time you add or reconnect a leaf, or you'll throw an ER14; and remember the diagnostic trick \u2014 pull the breakaway jumper (part 761492-00) to drop the board into diagnostic mode, and it will flash every active input one per second: SA80 key switch AUTO/absent, SA81 OFF, SA82 HOLD, SA83 activate on, SA84 DMSS approach, SA85 DMSS safety slowdown, SA86 carpet safety, SA87 bodyguard, SA88 power-boost-disable. That tells you instantly whether it's the key switch, an actuator stuck closed, a safety sensor, or the power-boost-disable input that's holding the door hostage. Reinstall that jumper when you're done or the controller won't open the door.",commonServicingIssues:["Door opens but slams shut hard when it closes \u2014 usually the operator isn't actually talking to the control box (motor power cable loose at its P-connector) so there's no controlled close, or the close-speed dials are cranked to FAST. Reseat the motor cable, back the close-speed dials toward medium, verify latch position near the 13\xb0 default. The manual warns the leaf slams if the operator isn't connected to the box.","Door hits people / feels aggressive \u2014 someone sped it up or shortened hold-open below code. Reset to A156.19: open to backcheck/80\xb0 in \u22653 s, hold open \u22655 s (raise the hold-open delay, 01\u201332 s), close 90\u219210\xb0 in \u22653 s and 10\xb0\u2192closed in \u22651.5 s. Confirm \u226415 lbf stops the leaf, and only set timing with the glass installed.","Door stalls or won't fully open in the afternoon but was fine in the morning \u2014 wind or HVAC stack pressure fighting the motor. Turn on Power Boost for extra closing/latching force, or upsell an IQ unit with AdaptivIQ; on a pair, verify both leaves sized correctly.","Electric strike/EL device buzzing, chattering, or door binding on open \u2014 no electric-lock delay set, so the operator drives before the lock clears. Set the EL keypad parameter to SA (fail-safe strike) or SE (fail-secure EL retraction) to get the built-in 1-second pre-open delay, wire the lock to the N.O. contact on P10, and on EL runs use 12 AWG stranded through the PS914 to beat voltage drop.","Push-button/actuator does nothing (or door opens on its own) \u2014 check the activate input; it's a dry N.O. contact, closed = open command. Pull the breakaway jumper to enter diagnostics and watch for SA83 (activate ON). A stuck-closed button or shorted actuator wire = a door that opens or hunts constantly.","Door won't move and shows ER06 \u2014 the controller tested hardware just before opening and its voltage monitor read low when it should be high. Check/reseat the master motor cable and the motor power cable, and confirm the red/black two-pin harness is seated on the motor. Cycle power to clear.","Door shows ER14 \u2014 a companion leaf was added or reconnected to a system that was already sized. Turn the 120 VAC off, then on, and re-run the sizing cycle with BOTH doors connected. For most other Er codes, power-cycle first; if it persists, suspect a bad control box.","Fire/smoke door won't latch on alarm \u2014 the operator or an EL device is holding it. Confirm the fire-alarm panel drops the operator's power/lock path on alarm so the spring closer takes over and the latch re-projects; a non-latching labeled door fails NFPA 80 inspection. Coordinate the interposing relay with the fire-alarm tech and AHJ.","Nuisance stops or false obstruction \u2014 a door-mounted safety sensor is seeing a wall or guardrail. Adjust or deactivate the offending safety-side sensor, or use the Safety Slow/Stop response; verify in diagnostics via SA85 (DMSS safety slowdown)."],sources:["https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-documents-2/InstallInstructions/LCN_Senior_Swing_Series_2800_and_9500_Series_Installation_Instructions_107514.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-documents-2/InstallInstructions/LCN_2800_Series_and_9500_Series_Senior_Swing_Control_Box_Instruction_Sheet_107510.pdf","https://www.lcnclosers.com/en/products/automatic-door-operators/9500-2800-senior-swing-series-operators.html","https://store.jlmwholesale.com/Documents/SRSWINGDATA.pdf","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-are-the-lcn-senior-swing-error-codes/"],photo:"photo-lcn-senior-swing",schematic:"schematic-lcn-senior-swing"},{id:"schlage-ad-series",name:"Schlage AD / NDE / LE Networked & Wireless Locks",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Schlage's family of integrated electronic locksets from Allegion that put the credential reader, electrified lock, door-position switch and request-to-exit sensor all in one door-mounted unit \u2014 no separate reader, strike or panel wiring at the opening. You'll see them wherever someone wanted card or mobile access on interior and secondary doors without pulling wire to every frame: offices, classrooms, dorm and resident units, IT closets, stairwells, common-area and suite doors. The AD Series (AD-300 wired, AD-400 900 MHz wireless) is the older patented-modular commercial platform that reports to a real access control panel; the NDE (cylindrical) and LE (mortise) are the newer ENGAGE/BLE/Wi-Fi generation aimed at extending access control deeper into a building cheaply. Knowing which one you're standing in front of \u2014 and how each fails \u2014 is most of the job.",specs:["AD-400 (networked wireless): 900 MHz spread spectrum, direct sequence, 10 channels, 902-928 MHz; AES-128 bit key encryption; RF data rate 40 kbps; range up to 200 ft with obstructions (normal building construction) / up to 1,000 ft clear line of sight; requires PIM400 panel interface module (supports up to 16 locks depending on ACP); Wake-Up-on-Radio centralized lockdown < 10 sec (field configurable 10-to-1 sec); configurable Dynamic Channel Switching for RF interference; power 4AA/8AA or 12/24 VDC (4-26 VDC range), up to 250 mA; up to 2 yrs on 4AA; ANSI/BHMA Grade 1, A156.25; UL 294; ULC S319; UL 10C 3-hour fire; FCC Part 15","AD-300 (networked wired): RS-485 direct to panel at 9.6 kbps OR Wiegand / Clock & Data via PIB300 (PIB300-2D supports up to two AD-300 locks); power 12 or 24 VDC, voltage range 4-26 VDC, up to 250 mA; power run 18AWG 2-cond (Belden 8760 or equiv) up to 1,000 ft; data run 24AWG shielded (Belden 9841/9842 or equiv), RS-485 to PIB300/ACP up to 4,000 ft (1,219 m); PIB300-to-ACP Wiegand/Clock&Data only up to 500 ft (152 m); ANSI/BHMA Grade 1, A156.25; UL 294; ULC S319; UL 10C 3-hour fire","AD Series modular formats: cylindrical, mortise, mortise deadbolt, and exit trim (rim exit device) \u2014 one electronics module reconfigures across chassis without pulling the lock off the door. Storeroom and office/classroom functions NOT available on the mortise-deadbolt option. Cylindrical is A156.2 Series 4000 Grade 1; mortise is A156.13 Series 1000 Grade 1; exit trim is A156.3 Grade 1 \u2014 all A156.25 locked-outdoor","NDE (ENGAGE cylindrical): built on ND Series Grade 1 cylindrical (A156.2 Series 4000); A156.25 locked outdoor; UL 294; ULC 60839; UL 10C 3-hour fire; ICC ANSI A117.1 / ADA compliant; Schlage 6-pin Everest 29 S123 keyway; door thickness 1-5/8\" to 2\"; up to 2 yrs on 4AA","LE (ENGAGE mortise): built on L Series Grade 1 mortise; SKU family LEB (mobile-enabled BLE/NFC reader), LEBMS (smart/prox+mobile), LEBMD (mortise deadbolt); 4AA; ANSI/BHMA Grade 1, A156.25; UL 294; UL 10C 3-hour fire","NDE/LE wireless & comms: 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi 802.11 b/g/n (WPA2 / WPA / WEP / 802.1x), Bluetooth Low Energy 4.2, TLS 1.2, AES-256; RF data rate 54 Mbps; ENGAGE Gateway comm range up to 30 ft typical (survey with the ENGAGE Test Kit, TKE); BLE credential read range up to 15 ft; Wake-Up-on-Radio < 5 sec via gateway (requires PACS system)","Credentials (all four): standard multi-tech reader \u2014 Schlage MIFARE Classic/Plus/DESFire (13.56 MHz smart, secure sector), 125 kHz proximity (Schlage, HID, AWID, GE/CASI ProxLite, LenelProx, ISONAS), and NFC/BLE mobile (Apple Wallet / Google Wallet, Schlage Mobile). HID iCLASS/iCLASS SE read CSN-only on the standard reader; SECURE-SECTOR HID iCLASS/SE/SEOS requires the factory 'Si' reader option (e.g. NDEBSi). The Si reader adds HID secure + HID NFC mobile but DROPS proximity and (on NDE) HID Seos BLE mobile \u2014 it is not field-convertible","Capacity (NDE/LE): up to 5,000 credentials, up to 2,000 audit events stored on-lock; smart/prox verify <= 1 sec (mobile is device-dependent)","LE battery life (4AA alkaline, Allegion-tested, by mode / daily activations light-5 / medium-30 / heavy-75): BLE mobile 3.0 / 2.5 / 1.8-1.9 yr; proximity 3.0 / 2.3-2.4 / 1.7-1.9 yr; smart 2.8-2.9 / 2.0 / 1.2-1.3 yr \u2014 real-time-via-gateway runs shorter than offline/Wi-Fi","Emergency power: NDE/LE and Schlage Control accept a 9V alkaline jump held to the two contacts below the bolt throw / thumbturn (polarity doesn't matter); wait for the 1-red-then-3-green + 3 beeps, present a valid credential, then it opens","Operating temp: exterior -31 to 151 F (-35 to 66 C) \u2014 NDEBSi exterior is -13 to 151 F; interior/battery 32 to 120 F (0 to 49 C); 0-100% non-condensing humidity"],proAdvice:"First thing to get straight is which of these you're actually standing in front of, because they look similar on the door but are two different worlds under the trim. The AD Series (AD-300 and AD-400) is the older modular commercial platform. Its whole selling point is the patented modular chassis \u2014 one electronics module that reconfigures between cylindrical, mortise, mortise-deadbolt and exit trim without coming off the door \u2014 and the fact that it reports to a real access control panel through a PIM (wireless, AD-400) or PIB/RS-485 (wired, AD-300). The NDE (cylindrical) and LE (mortise) are the newer ENGAGE generation: Bluetooth Low Energy plus built-in 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, managed either by Allegion's free ENGAGE app/cloud or, more commonly on real jobs, integrated into a third-party PACS through the Alliance program. Rule of thumb: if there's a PIM400 or PIB300 on the wall, you're in AD land; if the lock talks to a phone over Bluetooth or hits the building Wi-Fi, it's NDE/LE.\n\nWired vs wireless vs ENGAGE is a design decision, not a preference, and you should be able to explain it to a GC on the spot. Hardwired AD-300 is what you spec when the opening needs true real-time, always-on decisions and no batteries \u2014 stairwell re-entry, an IT room the client wants logged to the second, anything the software has to see live. You're pulling 18AWG two-conductor for power (up to 1,000 ft to the supply) and 24AWG shielded for RS-485 data (Belden 9841/9842, good out to 4,000 ft), landing on a PIB300-2D or straight into the panel over RS-485. Watch the interface: RS-485 is your long run, but if the panel only speaks Wiegand or Clock & Data you go through a PIB300, and that PIB-to-panel leg is only good to 500 ft \u2014 overrun that and you get flaky comms. One PIB300 handles just two AD-300 locks, so count them. AD-400 wireless is the answer when running wire is brutal or destructive \u2014 historic buildings, block walls, doors across a courtyard. It rides 900 MHz back to a PIM400, and 900 MHz is the point: the longer wavelength punches through normal construction better than 2.4 GHz, so you get up to 200 ft through walls, up to 1,000 ft line of sight, and one PIM handles up to 16 locks. NDE/LE is the budget-extension play \u2014 cheapest way to get card and phone access on a lot of interior doors \u2014 but understand you're trading real-time for scheduled: without an ENGAGE Gateway those locks pull changes on a No-Tour credential or a once-a-day Wi-Fi cycle, meaning a revoked card can still work until the next sync. If the client says the word 'lockdown' or 'immediate revoke,' you either add gateways (30 ft typical range, do a site survey with the TKE test kit) or you put them on AD-400 with Wake-Up-on-Radio, which drops centralized lockdown in under 10 seconds. Don't let someone sell NDE for a job that needs a panel.\n\nCredentials are where you look like you know what you're doing or you don't, and there's one nuance that trips up even guys who've installed these for years. All four carry a multi-technology reader out of the box \u2014 125 kHz proximity, 13.56 MHz Schlage MIFARE smart (Classic/Plus/DESFire, secure sector), plus NFC and, on NDE/LE, BLE mobile. Here's the catch: the standard reader WILL read HID iCLASS and iCLASS SE \u2014 but CSN only, meaning it reads the card's serial number, not the encrypted secure application area HID actually uses for access decisions. So a plain multi-tech NDE will 'see' an HID card and still deny it in a real HID deployment. To read the secure sector of HID iCLASS/SE/SEOS you need the factory 'Si' reader option (look for the Si in the SKU \u2014 NDEBSi, AD-400 with Si reader), and it is NOT field-convertible. Worse, the Si reader is a trade-off, not an add: enabling secure HID DROPS 125 kHz proximity entirely, and on the NDE it also drops HID Seos Bluetooth mobile. So before you quote a job in an HID building, confirm the reader variant and confirm the client isn't also running prox on those same doors, or you'll be RMA'ing locks. Prox itself is legacy and trivially cloneable \u2014 a 125 kHz card copies in seconds off a $30 tool, which is exactly why you migrate clients to DESFire or mobile \u2014 and the beauty of the standard multi-tech reader is you can run prox and smart together during that transition. Mobile credentials in Apple/Google Wallet depend on the PACS provider actually supporting them, so check the Credential Compatibility Guide; don't assume.\n\nOn install and repair-vs-replace: NDE fits standard ND cylindrical prep 'with minor modifications' and LE fits L Series mortise prep the same way, which in practice means the wiring channel for the exterior reader harness and the door-position sensor lead \u2014 real minor mods, not zero, and Allegion sells retrofit kits to bring a legacy NDE up to mobile-enabled. Because the electronics, reader, DPS and RQE are all integrated, when the reader board or motor dies you're generally swapping the whole exterior escutcheon or the electronics module, not soldering a component \u2014 Allegion sells modules and reader assemblies, and on an in-warranty lock you RMA rather than open it. The mechanical chassis underneath (the ND or L Series lock body) is bog-standard Schlage \u2014 Everest 29 S123 keyway, standard backsets and strikes \u2014 so mechanical failures like a dragging latch or a worn spring cage you fix the same as any ND/L lock. Ballpark: an NDE/LE runs roughly the mid-hundreds to around a thousand per opening hardware-only depending on finish, reader and function, and an AD-400 with its PIM, antennas and panel integration lands higher once you count the infrastructure \u2014 so for a single door a wireless standalone almost always wins, but past a dozen-plus openings the wired/panel math and the real-time capability start justifying AD-300.\n\nCode and liability instincts: these are Grade 1, UL 294 access control, and the fire-rated versions carry UL 10C 3-hour \u2014 but a fire door assembly is only rated if you don't defeat the closer or the positive latching, so never wedge or modify one to 'fix' a latching complaint (NFPA 80). On egress, the interior lever always retracts the latch mechanically for free egress \u2014 that's non-negotiable under IBC and NFPA 101, and if you're ever asked to wire one so people can't get out, that's a hard no. Storeroom function (outside always locked, credential to enter, free egress) is the workhorse; office/classroom and apartment functions add the interior push-button toggle \u2014 just remember storeroom and office/classroom aren't offered on the mortise-deadbolt configuration. And the honest ethics line for any of this: you only work on an opening you're authorized to, you verify who's asking before you re-key or dump an audit trail, and the reason you learn the bypasses is to close them \u2014 knowing a 125 kHz prox card clones in seconds is exactly why you push a client to smart or mobile credentials.",commonServicingIssues:["Lock is dead, no lights, nobody can get in \u2014 batteries flat. On NDE/LE (and Schlage Control) hold a fresh alkaline 9V against the two contacts below the bolt throw / thumbturn (polarity doesn't matter \u2014 there's no wrong way to touch them), keep holding it, watch for 1 red light then 3 green with 3 beeps, then present a valid credential; door opens, then swap the 4AA pack inside. Note the lock loses its clock after a full drain or a battery change \u2014 reconnect the ENGAGE app once and it re-syncs date/time.","Card that should still work gets denied (or a revoked card still opens) \u2014 this is the No-Tour / daily-Wi-Fi sync gap on NDE/LE without a gateway. The lock only pulls changes on its scheduled Wi-Fi cycle (within 24 hrs) or when you walk up with the ENGAGE app. Fix: connect and push the update, or if the client needs real-time revoke add an ENGAGE Gateway (survey placement with the TKE kit, ~30 ft typical) \u2014 the lock itself is fine.","HID card reads on the old reader but gets denied on the new Schlage lock \u2014 the standard multi-tech reader only reads HID iCLASS/SE by CSN (serial number), not the secure application area the access system actually decides on. Secure-sector HID needs the factory 'Si' reader (NDEBSi / AD Si), which is not field-addable \u2014 AND the Si reader drops 125 kHz proximity (and HID Seos BLE on NDE). Confirm the reader SKU and the building's credential mix before install; swap the lock for the correct variant if it's wrong.","AD-400 locks intermittently drop off the system / slow lockdown \u2014 900 MHz path back to the PIM400 is marginal or channel-congested. Check line-of-sight and distance (up to 200 ft through walls, 1,000 ft clear), enable Dynamic Channel Switching, and for reliable Wake-Up-on-Radio lockdown add a remote antenna to the PIM400. One PIM caps at 16 locks \u2014 count them.","AD-300 reader lights come on but the panel sees nothing / no access decisions \u2014 a wiring or interface fault, not the lock. Verify power (12/24 VDC, <=250 mA draw), then the data path: RS-485 straight to panel (good to 4,000 ft) vs Wiegand/Clock&Data through a PIB300. Remember the PIB300-to-panel Wiegand/C&D leg is only good to 500 ft \u2014 overrun that and you get intermittent comms \u2014 and one PIB300 only serves two locks.","Motor cycles / clicks but latch won't retract, or lever spins free \u2014 mechanical, in the underlying ND (NDE) or L Series (LE) chassis, not the electronics. Check the spindle/hub engagement and the latch for binding or a worn spring; fix it like any ND/L lock. If the motor drives but nothing moves, the clutch or drive module is shot \u2014 replace the electronics module.","Battery life way shorter than expected \u2014 usually mode plus environment. Per Allegion's own LE testing, heavy-traffic smart-card openings run down to ~1.2-1.3 yr vs ~3.0 yr for BLE/prox on light use, and real-time-gateway mode is shorter than offline/Wi-Fi. Cold exterior doors and cheap cells make it worse. Use quality AA alkaline (Duracell/Energizer), and set expectations by credential mode, not the marketing 'up to 2 years.'","Lock won't take the ENGAGE app or acts commissioned to someone else's account \u2014 do a Factory Default Reset (FDR) to return it out-of-the-box. Know the limit: an FDR does NOT release it from a previously commissioned ENGAGE account, so if a prior integrator owns it, they have to release it on their side before you can take it over."],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_NDE_Series_Wireless_Lock_Data_Sheet_110409.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_AD-400_Data_Sheet_104446.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_AD-300_Data_Sheet_104448.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_LE_Battery_Life_Data_Sheet_113736.pdf","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/information-documents/Schlage_ENGAGE_Wireless_BLE_and_Wi-Fi_Security_White_Paper.pdf"],photo:"photo-schlage-ad-series",schematic:"schematic-schlage-ad-series"},{id:"yale-assure-smart",name:"Yale Assure / Kwikset Halo residential smart locks",category:"Smart locks",whatItIs:"These are the two dominant mid-market residential smart deadbolts you'll see on real doors: the Yale Assure Lock 2 family (both owned by ASSA ABLOY) and the Kwikset Halo / Halo Touch family. Both are battery-powered, motorized deadbolts with a keypad or touchscreen and app control, sold either keyed (mechanical cylinder backup) or key-free (no cylinder at all). The big practical split is the keyway: keyed Yale Assure 2 units use a standard KW1 pin-tumbler cylinder you rekey the old-fashioned way, while every keyed Kwikset Halo uses SmartKey, which you rekey in seconds with a learn tool. They're consumer-grade Grade 2 hardware, not a commercial access-control system.",specs:["Both families: 4x AA alkaline batteries (name-brand alkaline only, not rechargeables/lithium unless Kwikset spec says otherwise); Kwikset rates ~6 months at ~a dozen cycles/day; low-battery warning icon/tone before lockout","ANSI/BHMA Grade 2 certified (residential heavy-duty). Yale Assure 2 is IPX5 weather-rated. Kwikset Halo carries a UL 20-minute fire rating on the deadbolt","Yale Assure Lock 2 models: YRD410 (keypad/pushbutton), YRD420 (touchscreen), YRD430 (key-free keypad), YRD450 (key-free touchscreen), YRD410F/420F/450F = Touch (fingerprint). Plus (YRD450-N) adds Apple Home Key. Both keyed and key-free variants exist across the line","Yale connectivity: Bluetooth + Apple Home built into EVERY unit; WiFi / Z-Wave Plus / Zigbee are interchangeable snap-in modules above the battery compartment (buy the radio you need). Matter/Thread on newer modules and Plus. WiFi models are 2.4GHz","Kwikset Halo connectivity: WiFi (2.4GHz only, no hub) built directly into the lock \u2014 no module system. Original Halo = WiFi + Bluetooth + Alexa/Google. Halo Select adds Matter over Thread; Halo Select Plus adds Apple Home Key (NFC tap-to-unlock)","Yale KEYED cylinder = standard KW1 (Kwikset keyway) 5-pin pin-tumbler \u2014 a normal cylinder you pin/rekey conventionally; Yale sells replacement KW1 cylinders (keyed alike/different). This is NOT SmartKey","Kwikset Halo / Halo Touch keyed cylinder = SmartKey Security, KW1 keyway, rekeyable in seconds with the SmartKey learn tool; also marketed for pick/bump resistance (but has known locksmith-community bypass vulnerabilities)","Yale key-free models (YRD430/450) have external 9V battery contact terminals to jump-power a dead lock long enough to enter a code; keyed models fall back to the physical key. Kwikset Halo key-free relies on charged batteries + app; keyed Halo falls back to the SmartKey cylinder","Door fit: Yale 1-3/8\" to 2-1/4\" door thickness, 1\" edge bore, 2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\" adjustable backset. Kwikset Halo ~1-3/8\" to 2\", adjustable backset. Standard cross-bore prep \u2014 installs with a screwdriver","Both are deadbolt-centric. Kwikset offers matching keyed levers/handlesets (SmartKey) sold with Halo combo packs; Yale's Assure line is primarily deadbolt with separate passage/lever hardware. Up to 250 user codes on both","Retail pricing (2025-2026): keypad/Bluetooth entry ~$150-180; WiFi touchscreen ~$180-210; fingerprint/Touch and Home Key/Matter Plus/Select tiers ~$220-280"],proAdvice:"Get the mental model straight first: these are motors, radios, and a battery pack bolted to a mechanical deadbolt. The mechanical half is a Grade 2 deadbolt like you've serviced for years; the electronic half is a sealed consumer module you generally don't repair, you replace. Ninety percent of your service calls are one of four things \u2014 dead/weak batteries, a misaligned door making the motor strain, a WiFi module that dropped off the network, or a worn/dead keypad. Diagnose in that order and you'll close most tickets fast.\n\nThe single most important field lesson: door alignment IS battery life. When the deadbolt drags against the strike, the motor pulls extra current every cycle and the customer burns through a set of AAs in weeks and complains the 'lock is bad.' Before you condemn anything electronic, throw the deadbolt by hand with the door open, then closed. If it's stiff closed and smooth open, it's the strike, not the lock. Adjust the strike, deepen the bolt pocket, check for door sag. That fix alone resolves a huge share of 'jamming' and 'my batteries keep dying' calls, and it's the value you bring that a big-box return counter doesn't.\n\nKnow your keyways cold because they diverge. Kwikset Halo and Halo Touch are SmartKey \u2014 you rekey them on the door in under a minute with the learn tool (functioning key in, learn tool in the side port, click, new key, rotate). Great for tenant turnover and rekey-to-existing-key jobs. But SmartKey has a real reputation in our trade for being pickable/bypassable with the right tools; if a security-conscious commercial-ish client asks, be honest that it's convenience-grade, not high-security. The keyed Yale Assure 2 is different \u2014 it's a plain KW1 pin-tumbler cylinder. You rekey it like any Kwikset-keyway cylinder (pinning kit) or swap Yale's replacement cylinder; your standard rekey skills apply directly. Key-free versions of either brand have no cylinder at all, so there's nothing to rekey and no mechanical bypass \u2014 that's a selling point and a liability depending on the customer.\n\nConnectivity is where Yale and Kwikset differ most and where you'll earn support money. Yale is modular: Bluetooth and Apple Home are always onboard, and WiFi/Z-Wave/Zigbee/Matter are cards that snap in above the battery. If a Yale customer's WiFi died, reseating or swapping the ~$50 module often fixes it without touching the lock \u2014 cheap, profitable, clean. Kwikset Halo bakes WiFi into the lock, so a failed radio usually means replacing the interior escutcheon or the whole unit. Both WiFi radios are 2.4GHz ONLY \u2014 the #1 'won't connect' cause is a customer whose router merged 2.4 and 5GHz under one SSID, or a lock sitting too far from the router at an exterior door. Carry that knowledge; half your 'defective lock' calls are network problems you can talk through.\n\nRepair-vs-replace judgment: service the mechanical, replace the electronic. Strike/alignment, cylinder rekey, bolt lube, mounting \u2014 all serviceable and worth your labor. A dead motor, cracked touchscreen, water-killed keypad, or fried WiFi are not economically repairable on a $180 lock; the parts and labor approach the price of a new unit, and neither brand really sells motor/keypad subassemblies to the public. If it's out of the ~1-year electronics / limited-lifetime mechanical-and-finish warranty, quote a replacement. Where you make money is the install + rekey + setup: mounting and aligning it right, rekeying the keyed ones to the customer's existing key, getting it onto the WiFi/app, programming codes, and educating them on batteries. That bundled 'supply, install, and set up your smart lock' service is a solid upsell over a bare hardware sale, and it's the part customers consistently botch on their own.",commonServicingIssues:["Lock cycles slowly, jams, or 'motor grinds' -> deadbolt binding on a misaligned strike/sagging door forces the motor to strain -> adjust/enlarge strike, realign door, lube bolt; verify smooth manual throw before blaming electronics","Batteries die in days/weeks -> almost always the same binding/friction above, or the customer used rechargeables/off-brand cells -> fix alignment, install fresh name-brand alkalines; on Kwikset, disable lock sounds/LED in-app to cut draw","Lock unresponsive / won't take codes, keypad dark -> fully depleted batteries or a battery pack that shifted -> replace all 4 AAs at once; on Yale key-free, jump with a 9V on the external terminals to enter and rescue; on keyed units use the physical key","WiFi shows offline in the app, remote unlock fails -> lock only supports 2.4GHz and customer is on a merged/5GHz SSID, weak signal at the door, or changed the WiFi password -> put router on separate 2.4GHz SSID or add an extender, run a network reset; on Yale, reseat/replace the WiFi module","Keypad/touchscreen dead spots or ghost presses -> worn membrane or moisture intrusion at an exposed door -> if in warranty, RMA; if not, replace the exterior assembly/unit \u2014 not field-repairable, add a storm door/overhang for exposed installs","Keypad locked out after wrong entries -> 3 failed codes triggers a ~60-second lockout by design (Kwikset) -> wait it out; reassure customer it's anti-tamper, not a fault, and re-verify the correct user code","Customer wants the keyed lock rekeyed to their existing key -> Kwikset Halo is SmartKey (learn-tool rekey in under a minute); keyed Yale Assure 2 is standard KW1 pin-tumbler (conventional rekey or swap Yale's replacement cylinder) -> pick the right procedure per brand; key-free models have no cylinder to rekey","Motor/gearbox won't drive the bolt though batteries are fine -> failed drive motor or stripped gearing inside the sealed interior module -> not economically repairable; replace the lock (or interior assembly if the brand offers one) rather than chasing parts","Matter/Home Key/Z-Wave not appearing to a hub -> wrong module or firmware/commissioning issue (Yale needs the specific Matter/Z-Wave card; Kwikset Matter runs over Thread on Select, needs a Thread border router) -> verify the customer has the correct radio and a compatible hub before assuming hardware failure"],sources:["https://shopyalehome.com/pages/yale-assure-lock-2","https://support.shopyalehome.com/categories/getting-started-rJ5wjlWy6","https://www.kwikset.com/products/detail/halo-touchscreen-wi-fi-enabled-smart-lock","https://www.kwikset.com/products/detail/halo-select-plus-touchscreen-wi-fi-and-matter-enabled-smart-lock-with-apple-home-keys","https://www.kwikset.com/support/answers/how-can-i-troubleshoot-the-wi-fi-connection-of-my-halo-smart-lock"],photo:"photo-yale-assure",schematic:"schematic-yale-assure-smart"},{id:"hid-reader",name:"HID prox / iCLASS card readers & credentials",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"HID Global card readers and credentials are the front end of an electronic access-control system: the reader sits by the door, reads a card/fob/phone, and passes the credential number back to a controller (panel) that makes the unlock decision. The reader does not lock anything by itself. The line spans three generations of tech: legacy 125 kHz Prox (unencrypted, easily cloned), 13.56 MHz iCLASS SE and Seos smart cards (encrypted, mutually authenticated), and mobile credentials over Bluetooth/NFC. Readers connect to the panel by either legacy Wiegand or, on modern units, OSDP over RS-485.",specs:["Two RF technologies you will meet: 125 kHz Prox (older, EM/HID Prox/Indala/AWID family) and 13.56 MHz smart cards (iCLASS Classic, iCLASS SE, Seos, MIFARE Classic/DESFire EV1/EV2). Prox is a fixed unencrypted number; SE/Seos are encrypted with diversified keys and mutual authentication.","Security ladder, low to high: 125 kHz Prox (clone in seconds with a Proxmark3 or a sub-$200 Flipper Zero) -> iCLASS Classic (broken years ago) -> iCLASS SE with the SIO data model -> Seos (strongest cloning resistance, EAL5+ secure element, cleanest path to mobile). Spec new work in Seos or mobile.","Credential formats: the classic is 26-bit Wiegand (H10301) = 8-bit facility code (1-255) + 16-bit card number (1-65,535) + 2 parity bits. It is public/non-proprietary, so numbers can and do collide across sites and it is freely duplicated. Higher-security sites use 35-bit HID Corporate 1000 or a custom format with a registered, controlled facility/format code.","Reader-to-panel wiring is the real security boundary. Wiegand: one-way, unencrypted, no supervision (pull a reader off the wall and you have the Data0/Data1 lines to replay). OSDP (SIA, RS-485, half-duplex, bidirectional) with Secure Channel adds AES-128 encryption and line supervision so the panel alarms on a cut/tampered/removed reader. OSDP v2.2 (Dec 2020, per IEC 60839-11-5) is the current baseline.","Current HID Signo line and its form factors (12 VDC nominal, spec sheet lists 12V DC; readers tolerate ~5-16 VDC): Signo 20 = mullion/narrow surface mount; Signo 40 = single-gang wall-switch box; Signo 20K = mullion with keypad (2x6); Signo 40K = single-gang with keypad (3x4). All multi-tech (125 kHz + 13.56 MHz + mobile), native Bluetooth/NFC, Apple Wallet ECP, native OSDP Secure Channel, IP65, UL 294.","Legacy iCLASS SE / multiCLASS SE reader form factors still in the field: R10 mini-mullion, R15 mullion, R40 single-gang wall-switch, RK40 single-gang keypad, RKL55 keypad with LCD. multiCLASS SE variants add 125 kHz for migration; iCLASS SE are 13.56 MHz only.","Signo typical read range (per CEM/Tyco Signo datasheet): 13.56 MHz cards (Seos/iCLASS SE/MIFARE) about 1.6-4 in (4-10 cm); 125 kHz Prox about 2.4-4 in (6-10 cm). Do not expect a card to read through a metal door frame or on top of another card in a wallet.","Signo current draw (datasheet): standby ~60-70 mA, peak ~250 mA. Size the reader power leg of your supply accordingly, especially on long runs where voltage drop matters.","Cable & distance (HID Signo install guidance): Wiegand run up to ~500 ft (152 m) on 18 AWG, less on thinner wire; use 5-10 conductor overall-shielded. OSDP/RS-485 up to ~4000 ft (1219 m) total bus on 24 AWG twisted pair with overall shield (Belden 3107A class). Twisted pair matters for RS-485 -- old flat Wiegand cable often will not qualify when you convert.","OSDP termination: for RS-485 runs over ~200 ft (61 m) or in high-EMI areas, fit a 120 ohm end-of-line resistor across A/B at the bus ends. On the plus side, existing Data0/Data1 conductors can usually be repurposed as the RS-485 pair on a retrofit.","Fail-safe vs fail-secure is a property of the LOCK, not the reader. The reader is powered and passive. Fail-safe (fail-unlocked, e.g. mag locks) drops open on power loss -- required on most egress/life-safety doors. Fail-secure (fail-locked, e.g. most electric strikes) stays locked on power loss. This is code and liability territory: egress and fire-rated door hardware must comply with NFPA 101 / IBC and the local AHJ.","Mounting note: mullion readers (Signo 20, R10/R15) fit narrow aluminum storefront frames; single-gang readers (Signo 40, R40) need a 1-gang box or mud ring. Keep the reader head off large metal where possible or use the metal-tolerant/spacer mounting to avoid detuning the antenna and killing read range.","Field sighting (unconfirmed at capture): Black wall-switch form factor, single red LED. ProxPoint/MiniProx family. Mounted onto SES panel face. \u2014 to confirm: exact model not legible"],proAdvice:"First thing on any service call: figure out where the decision is made. The reader by the door does not unlock anything -- it reads a number and ships it to a controller in a closet or above the ceiling. Ninety percent of \"the reader is broken\" calls are actually a wiring, power, controller, or credential problem. Beep pattern tells you a lot. Solid or slow-flashing red with a chirp on power-up usually means the reader is alive and talking; if it reads a card (chirp + LED blink) but the door does not release, your problem is downstream at the panel, the relay, or the lock -- not the reader.\n\nKnow which generation you are standing in front of, because it changes everything. If it is 125 kHz Prox, understand you are looking at 1990s security: that card is a fixed unencrypted number and anyone with a $30 cloner off the internet or a Flipper Zero can copy it by brushing a bag against an employee's pocket. I do not scaremonger customers, but I do document it in writing when I hand over a quote -- that is your liability cover. If the site cares about security at all, the migration path is 13.56 MHz iCLASS SE or, better, Seos, and increasingly mobile credentials in Apple Wallet / Google Wallet so there is no physical card to clone or hand off. multiCLASS SE readers read both 125 kHz and 13.56 MHz at once, which is how you migrate a big site one credential at a time without a flag day.\n\nThe wire between the reader and the panel is the part nobody thinks about and the part that actually gets you owned. Legacy Wiegand is one-way, unencrypted, and unsupervised -- yank the reader off the wall and the Data0/Data1 lines are right there to replay or inject a known card number. That is the \"hole in the boat\" the industry has been yelling about for years. OSDP over RS-485 with Secure Channel fixes it: AES-128 encryption on the line and supervision so the panel alarms the instant a reader is cut, pulled, or dies. New work: run OSDP, full stop. Retrofit: you can usually reuse the existing conductors, but check that you actually have a twisted pair -- flat multiconductor Wiegand cable frequently will not meet RS-485 spec on a long run, and you will chase intermittent comms forever. Terminate long or noisy RS-485 runs with a 120 ohm resistor at the bus ends.\n\nPull your reader power and comms back to the controller and its power supply -- never tap the door strike or mag lock supply for reader power. Sharing that rail is how you get a reader that browns out and locks up every time the lock fires, because the strike inrush drops your voltage below what the reader needs. Reader on its own fused, filtered leg; lock on its own; and mind voltage drop on long home runs -- an 18 AWG Wiegand run at a few hundred feet can sag enough to matter.\n\nDrill fail-safe versus fail-secure into your head as a lock decision, not a reader decision, because that is where the code and the lawsuit live. Fail-safe hardware (mag locks, fail-safe strikes) unlocks on power loss -- that is what you want on most egress and life-safety doors so people are never trapped. Fail-secure (most electric strikes) stays locked on power loss -- fine on a storeroom, wrong on a required exit. NFPA 101, IBC, and your local AHJ dictate which doors must free-egress and how; when in doubt you defer to the fire marshal, not the customer's preference. Never wire a mag lock so that a dead panel or a cut reader can trap someone behind a door that has no mechanical egress.\n\nRepair vs replace: a Signo or SE reader that is genuinely dead (no LED, no beep, confirmed good 12 VDC at the terminals) gets swapped -- these are sealed potted units, there is nothing to fix inside. But before you condemn one, prove power at the reader, prove continuity on the data lines, and swap in a known-good card. A huge share of \"dead reader\" tickets are a backed-out terminal screw, a nicked conductor, water intrusion on an outdoor unit (check the IP rating and the gasket), or a controller that lost its head-end config. If you are already opening the wall for a Prox reader failure, that is your moment to sell the OSDP + Seos/mobile upgrade instead of dropping another cloneable 125 kHz reader back in the hole.",commonServicingIssues:["Reader reads card (chirp/LED) but door never unlocks -> problem is downstream, not the reader: check controller relay, lock power, and that the credential is actually enrolled/valid in the head-end. Verify the panel sees the card read in its event log.","Reader completely dead, no LED, no beep -> lost power or a wiring fault. Meter for 12 VDC (readers tolerate ~5-16 VDC) at the reader terminals; check for a backed-out screw, nicked/shorted conductor, blown fuse, or a shared lock supply browning it out. Swap only after power is proven good.","Reader resets/locks up every time the door lock fires -> reader is powered off the same rail as the strike/mag lock and the lock's inrush is dropping the voltage. Put the reader on its own fused, filtered supply leg back at the panel.","Short or dead read range, card must touch the reader -> reader mounted on/near large metal detuning the antenna, or wrong credential tech. Use metal-tolerant mounting/spacer, keep off ferrous surfaces, and confirm the card frequency matches the reader (125 kHz vs 13.56 MHz).","Intermittent comms after a Wiegand-to-OSDP conversion -> old flat Wiegand cable is not a proper twisted pair for RS-485, or the run is unterminated. Verify twisted-pair/shielded cable and fit a 120 ohm EOL resistor across A/B on runs over ~200 ft or in high-EMI areas.","Two cardholders at different buildings open each other's doors -> 26-bit format facility-code/card-number collision (only 255 facility codes, format is public). Move the site to a registered format, HID Corporate 1000 (35-bit), or a custom controlled format.","Card gets cloned / unauthorized entry with a copied badge -> site is on 125 kHz Prox (fixed unencrypted number, trivially cloned by Proxmark3/Flipper Zero). Migrate to iCLASS SE or Seos smart credentials or mobile; use multiCLASS SE readers to transition without re-badging everyone at once.","Outdoor reader flaky or dead after rain -> water intrusion past a failed gasket or a reader under-rated for the location. Confirm the unit's IP rating (Signo is IP65), reseal/replace the gasket, and swap for a properly rated reader if it was the wrong part for an exterior door.","New reader installed but no cards read at all -> reader keyset/technology mismatch: the reader is not provisioned to read the site's iCLASS SE/Seos keys, or is set for the wrong format. Verify the reader was ordered/keyed for the site's credential program (Elite/Corporate 1000) and the correct 13.56 MHz tech.","Mag-locked egress door traps occupants on power loss or panel fault -> fail-secure logic or bad wiring on a life-safety door. Egress doors generally require fail-safe (fail-unlocked) hardware and code-compliant free egress per NFPA 101/IBC; correct with the AHJ, do not leave it."],sources:["https://www.hidglobal.com/products/20","https://www.hidglobal.com/products/40","https://www.hidglobal.com/products/r10","https://www.hidglobal.com/products/r15","https://www.hidglobal.com/products/rk40"],photo:"photo-hid-reader",schematic:"schematic-hid-reader",verified:!0},{id:"resideo-vista-20p",name:"Resideo/Honeywell VISTA-20P",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"The VISTA-20P is the hybrid (hardwired + wireless) alarm control panel you will service more than any other in residential and light-commercial work \u2014 it has been the North American workhorse for two decades, sold under Ademco, then Honeywell, now Resideo. It ships with 8 hardwired zones on the board and expands to 48 total zones, runs two independent partitions plus a shared common area, and it is a raw board-in-a-can panel: you program it from a keypad, not an app. Its little brother is the VISTA-15P (single partition, 6 onboard zones, 32 max); learn the 20P and the 15P is the same panel with fewer seats.",specs:["Zones: 8 hardwired onboard, expandable to 48 total (wired + wireless). Zone doubling on zones 2-8 adds zones 10-16, giving you 15 hardwired total (it's 15 zones, not 16 \u2014 don't let the zone number 16 fool you); wireless (zones 9-48) fills the rest, and RF buttons/key fobs live in zones 49-64 (up to 16 of them)","Hardwire zone response: each zone individually set NO, NC, EOL-supervised, zone-doubled, or double-balanced (2.2k EOL resistors standard)","Partitions: 2 independent + 1 common area (e.g. a shared lobby); assign each zone to a partition during zone programming","User codes: up to 48 (installer, master, partition masters, plus user slots). Defaults: installer 4112, master 1234 \u2014 change both","Keypads / addressable devices: up to 8 keypads on ECP bus (addresses 16-23), wired to terminals 4-7 (4 = negative, 5 = +12V, 6 = data in, 7 = data out). Common heads: 6160 alpha, 6150 fixed-English, 6280 Tuxedo touchscreen","RF: 5881/5883-series receiver + 5800-series transmitters; the 5881ENH is the high-capacity receiver that supports the panel's full wireless zone count","Alarm/bell output: 12VDC nominal (10.5-13.8VDC), 2.0A max, terminals 3(+)/4(-) \u2014 drives sirens/strobes","Aux power output: 12VDC, 600mA max for keypads, motions, receivers, on terminals 5(+)/4(-) \u2014 budget it, this is what you run out of first","Transformer: Ademco 1321 (16.5VAC, 25VA) standard; 1361/1361-GT (16.5VAC, 40VA) when you need more headroom","Backup battery: 12V sealed lead-acid, 4-7Ah (a 7Ah gives real standby time; check the AHJ's required hours)","Event log: 100 time-stamped events, viewable/scrollable at a 6160 alpha keypad","Communication: onboard dialer; IP/cellular via add-on comms (7847i, GSMV, or a Resideo LTE module such as the LTEM-P series)"],proAdvice:"Get into programming two ways: power up and within 50 seconds press [*] + [#] together, or at any time enter the installer code + 8 + 0 0 (4112 8 0 0 on a default panel). If someone before you locked out installer-code access (field *98), the power-up window is your only door in \u2014 and on some firmware even that gets disabled, at which point you are defaulting the panel or replacing it. So the first thing you do on a service call to an unknown panel: try to get in before you touch anything, because knowing whether you have access changes the whole job.\n\nLearn the star fields \u2014 this panel lives and dies by them. *20 is the installer code. *56 is the interactive zone programming menu (the one you will use 90% of the time \u2014 it walks you through zone number, type, partition, report code, and for wireless zones it learns the serial by tripping the device or by hand-entering the transmitter ID). *58 is expert/device mode \u2014 same result, fewer prompts, for when you already know the panel. *91 sets partitioning options, *93 is the output/device menu for relays and the 4204/X-10 stuff, and *99 exits and saves. Zone types 92 and 93 (configurable types, 20P only) can only be built with the Compass downloader, not from the keypad \u2014 don't hunt for them at the head.\n\nOn partitions: the classic use is a house with an in-law suite, or a shop up front with a warehouse behind. Put the shared entry (front lobby, common hallway) in the common zone so either partition can arm independently but the common area only arms when both sides are armed. The gotcha that eats a whole afternoon: a keypad only shows and controls the partition it's assigned to (set per keypad address in device programming). If a customer says 'the back keypad won't arm the front,' that's assignment, not a fault. Also remember Goto \u2014 a valid user can press [*] + partition number to jump a keypad to the other partition temporarily.\n\nWireless is where the money and the callbacks are. The 5800-series is one-way, unencrypted, supervised RF \u2014 fine for residential, but understand it's sniffable/jammable if someone cares. When you learn a transmitter in *56, physically trip it twice to confirm the serial rather than hand-keying it \u2014 one transposed digit and you've got a zone that reports the wrong device forever. Set the RF supervision (field affects loop response) so a dead-battery transmitter actually faults instead of going silent. And mind the receiver's antenna orientation and mounting height; a 5881 buried in the bottom of a metal can behind a breaker panel will give you intermittent 'CHECK' faults that look like bad transmitters but are really range.\n\nBudget your aux current before you load the panel up. 600mA of 12VDC aux (terminals 5 and 4) has to feed every keypad, motion, glassbreak, and the RF receiver. Four alpha keypads and a couple of powered motions and you're over \u2014 the fix is a supervised auxiliary power supply (a 12V AD12612 or similar) with its own transformer and battery, relay-triggered off the panel, not more load on the board. Overloaded aux shows up as keypads that reboot or lose comms when the siren fires, because the bell draw sags the whole rail.",commonServicingIssues:["'CHECK 100' / 'SYSTEM LO BAT' at the keypad = the panel's own backup battery is dead or missing. SLA batteries die in 3-5 years; replace with a fresh 12V 4-7Ah and clear the trouble \u2014 this is the single most common service ticket on this panel","No AC / power trouble: bad 1321 transformer, tripped GFCI on the outlet it's plugged into, or someone switched the outlet off. Check for 16.5VAC at terminals 1-2 before condemning the board","'CHECK' on a wireless zone = dead transmitter battery, out-of-range receiver, or a transmitter that walked off (magnet/contact drifted apart). Check RF battery first, then supervision","Locked out of programming: prior installer set *98 to disable installer-code entry. Recover via the power-up *+# window; if that's also disabled, you're defaulting or replacing","Zone faults that won't clear = missing or wrong-value EOL resistor (should be 2.2k across the loop), or a zone-doubled loop wired wrong. Meter the loop","Keypads rebooting when the siren sounds = aux power overloaded; offload devices to a supervised auxiliary supply","Communication failures / 'FC' (fail to communicate): dead POTS line (VoIP kills the dialer), or an aging IP/cellular comms module \u2014 many 20Ps in the field still dial out and the phone line got cut years ago without anyone noticing until a real alarm","False alarms on hardwired motions: EOL/wiring, but often a PIR aimed at a heat register or a pet over the weight rating \u2014 verify the detector spec against the environment","Time drifts / wrong log timestamps after a power cycle: reset the clock (master code + #63) \u2014 no RTC battery, so the panel loses time on total power loss","'OC' / open-circuit on a partition after adding a keypad: duplicate keypad address (two heads set to the same 16-23 slot). Re-address one"],sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-many-wireless-zones-can-i-program-on-a-vista-20p","https://site.aesecurity.com/Manuals/v15pand20pprogrammingguide.pdf","https://safehomecentral.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/Vista-20P-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-get-into-programming-mode-on-my-vista-20p","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/what-is-the-power-source-for-a-honeywell-vista-20p","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/honeywell-vista-series-15p-20p-and-21ip-basic-wiring","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-add-an-additional-keypad-to-a-vista-20p","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/VISTA-20P-Data-Sheet.PDF","https://shop.ssandsi.com/honeywell-intrusion-1321-1-transformer-16-5vac-25va-for-vista-control-panel/","https://support.konnected.io/honeywell/ademco-vista-series-setup-configuration"],photo:"photo-resideo-vista-20p",schematic:"schematic-resideo-vista-20p"},{id:"dsc-neo-hs2032",name:"DSC PowerSeries Neo HS2032",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"The HS2032 is DSC's mid-tier hybrid intrusion panel in the PowerSeries Neo line \u2014 a bare board that ships in a metal can, no keypad, no communicator. \"Hybrid\" is the whole point: it takes hardwired zones on the board's Corbus and marries them to PowerG encrypted wireless devices through a transceiver, so you can wire a new build or retrofit an occupied space without fishing walls. It sits between the little HS2016 and the bigger HS2064/HS2128, and it's the one you'll reach for on mid-size residential and light commercial jobs.",specs:["Zones: 8 hardwired on-board, expandable to 32 total (any mix of hardwired and PowerG wireless)","Partitions: 4","User codes: 72 (plus master + installer)","Event buffer: 500 events, time/date stamped","Keypads: up to 8 supervised (hardwired HS2LCD, HS2LCDRF9 with built-in PowerG, or HS2TCHP touchscreen)","PGM outputs: 2 on-board (PGM1 50mA switched; PGM2 300mA current-limited, doubles as the 2-wire smoke power output \u2014 up to ~18 supervised 2-wire smokes with a 2.2k EOL), expandable to 38 via HSM2204/HSM2208","Aux power: 700mA @ 12VDC (9.6-13.8V range), 270mVp-p ripple max, shared with the Corbus RED terminal + PGMs","Bell/siren output: 12V 700mA, supervised, current-limited at 2A","Transformer (NA): PTD1640U / PTC1640U plug-in, 16.5VAC 40VA; international 220-240VAC 50/60Hz","Backup battery: 12V sealed lead-acid \u2014 4Ah/7Ah typical, up to 14Ah/21Ah for extended UL backup; ~72hr recharge to 80% (240/480mA charge rate)","PowerG wireless: 915MHz (North America), 128-bit AES encryption, FHSS + TDMA, up to 2km/1.24mi line-of-sight","Transceiver: HSM2HOST9 standalone, or built into an HS2LCDRF9-series RF keypad \u2014 the HS2TCHP touchscreen has NO built-in receiver and needs a separate HSM2HOST for wireless","Operating temp: -10C to 55C (14F to 131F) per EN; UL rated 0C to 49C (32F to 120F)","Communicators (slot): TL280 IP, TL2803G/3G2080 cellular (3G \u2014 obsolete since the carrier 3G sunset), LE9080 LTE, dual-path; Alarm.com and legacy DSC Connect24","Board/cabinet models: HS2032NK (board + metal cabinet, no keypad), HS2032NKCP01 (same with CP-01 firmware), HS2032PCB / HS2032PCBCP01 (bare board, no cabinet)"],proAdvice:"Install first: the board wants a clean 16.5VAC 40VA plug-in \u2014 use the DSC PTD1640U, don't grab whatever wall wart is in the truck. Terminate your Corbus (RED/BLK/YEL/GRN) as a home run or daisy chain, never a star or a T, and keep total wire run under about 1000ft on 22AWG. When keypads or modules brown out at the far end of a long run, that's Corbus voltage drop \u2014 you either run heavier gauge, split the load, or drop an HSM2300 1A supply out at the far end and repower the bus locally. That 700mA aux is shared with everything on the bus plus PGMs; two touchscreen keypads and a handful of powered motions will eat it, and then you get intermittent \"loss of supervision\" faults that look like device problems but are really a starved supply. Do the current-draw math before you leave, not on the callback.\n\nProgramming is section-based, old-school DSC \u2014 you're in [*][8][installer code] then punching section numbers. Learn the map or keep the installation guide open: zones live in the 000 blocks, zone definitions/attributes are separate sections from zone assignment to a partition, and that trips people up. Enroll PowerG devices two ways: manually key the printed serial into the enrollment section, or put the panel in auto-enroll and tamper each device to make it announce itself \u2014 auto-enroll is faster on a big job but manual is bulletproof when you've got two identical door contacts and need to know which is which. PowerG is genuinely good: 128-bit AES, frequency-hopping, and it reports back link quality, so before you screw a sensor down, check the signal strength reading at the keypad and reposition if it's marginal. Two kilometers open-air is real, but drywall, foil-backed insulation, and metal studs kill it fast.\n\nGotchas that'll bite you. First, the HS2032 caps at 32 zones even though the HSM2HOST9 transceiver spec sheet brags about 128 wireless zones and 32 keys \u2014 that's the transceiver's ceiling, not the panel's; the HS2032 will only take 32 total, period. Don't over-sell the customer. Second, know your part numbers when you order: NK is the board in a metal can with no keypad \u2014 you're still supplying keypad, battery, transformer, and communicator separately (the PCB version is the bare board with no cabinet at all). For UL residential fire/burg you want the CP-01 firmware version (HS2032NKCP01/PCBCP01) \u2014 it has the false-alarm-reduction dialer logic (swinger shutdown, cross-zoning, entry-delay defaults) baked in, and some AHJs require it. Third, the on-board PGM2 is your 2-wire smoke output \u2014 wire smokes there with the correct 2.2k EOL, string no more than about 18 on the loop, and remember the panel needs a momentary power reset to clear a latched smoke, not just a disarm. Fourth, the communicator is a plug-in module in its own slot; a \"FTC\" (failure to communicate) trouble is almost always the comms module or the account/receiver setup, not the panel \u2014 check the module LEDs before you suspect the board. And if you inherit an old install still running a 3G2080 or TL2803G, that radio is dead now that the 3G networks are shut off \u2014 swap to the LE9080 LTE or a current dual-path unit.\n\nOne more: when you inherit somebody else's Neo and don't have the installer code, there's no magic bypass \u2014 a hardware default requires the installer's lockout to be off, and if they enabled installer lockout you're looking at an RMA or a board swap. Ask the customer for their paperwork up front.",commonServicingIssues:["Low-battery / 'battery trouble' \u2014 SLA batteries die at 3-5 years; a panel on a bad battery will hard-drop on any AC blip. Load-test or just replace on schedule.","'AC trouble' / AC loss \u2014 check the plug-in transformer, the outlet (not on a switched circuit or GFCI that trips), and the transformer terminals on the board before condemning anything.","Aux power overload \u2014 too many powered devices on the 700mA bus causes brownout faults and random supervision losses; cure is an HSM2300 to add 1A locally.","PowerG device dropouts / low signal \u2014 sensor moved, battery low, or new metal/foil obstruction; recheck link quality and reposition or add a PG9920 repeater.","Corbus/keypad faults on long runs \u2014 voltage drop at the far keypad; heavier gauge or repower the bus mid-run.","FTC (failure to communicate) \u2014 communicator module fault, cellular signal, or receiver/account misconfig; diagnose the comms module, not the panel. Legacy 3G radios (3G2080/TL2803G) are now dead post-3G-sunset \u2014 replace with LTE.","Latched 2-wire smoke won't clear \u2014 needs a sensor reset (momentary PGM2 power cycle), not just disarm.","Tamper faults after service \u2014 cabinet/cover tamper or device tamper left open; walk-test after any enclosure work.","Lost installer code with lockout enabled \u2014 no field default possible; board replacement territory.","Enrollment confusion on identical sensors \u2014 use manual serial entry to keep zone-to-device mapping straight."],sources:["https://www.dsc.com/alarm-security-products/HS2032%20-%20PowerSeries%20Neo%20Control%20Panels/2442","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS2032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2128-E-Alarm-Control-Installation-Guide-TIS/1.35/Specifications","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-hs2032nk","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-hs2tchp","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-wire-and-program-a-dsc-powerseries-neo-for-2-wire-smoke","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/products/dsc-powerseries-neo-hs2032pcbcp01-board-only","ADI Global distribution listing for the DSC HSM2HOST9 wireless transceiver \u2014 original link retired.","https://www.geoarm.com/hsm2host9-dsc-wireless-powerg-host-transceiver-module.html","https://cms.dsc.com/download2.php?t=1&id=23346","https://www.geoarm.com/ptd1640u-plug-in-ac-power-transformer-for-dsc-powerseries-neo-and-honeywell-vista-panels.html"],photo:"photo-dsc-neo-hs2032",schematic:"schematic-dsc-neo-hs2032"},{id:"dmp-xr150",name:"DMP XR150",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"The XR150 is DMP's mid-tier commercial command processor \u2014 a 12 VDC combined burglary, fire, and access-control panel with battery backup, built by Digital Monitoring Products in Springfield, MO. It ships in a metal can with eight onboard burg zones and two powered zones (for 2-wire smokes), and expands over LX-Bus and the keypad bus into a full commercial system. It's the smaller sibling of the XR550 \u2014 same board family, just capped lower (142 zones, 8 areas, and 8 keypads vs. the 550's 574 zones, 32 areas, and 16 keypads).",specs:["Onboard zones: 8 burglary zones (1k or 2.2k Ohm EOL, zones 1-8) plus 2 powered Class B zones with reset for 2-wire smoke detectors (3.3k Ohm EOL, zones 9-10)","Max zones: 142 total on the XR150 (the XR550 is the 574-zone version) \u2014 programmable as burg, fire, or access","Keypad bus (terminals 7-10): up to 16 supervised devices total, but supervised keypads cap at 8 on the XR150 (addresses 1-8) \u2014 16 keypads is the XR550's number; max 7 wireless keypads. Devices include alphanumeric keypads, graphic touchscreens, zone expanders, single-zone detectors, and access modules","Zone expansion: one onboard LX-Bus (100 addresses; LX500-LX900 headers, XR550 has the extra buses), plus keypad-bus expanders and 1100 Series wireless (zones 400-449); XR150 supports up to 100 wireless zones (XR550 does 500)","Users/areas: 10,000 user codes, 99 user profiles, up to 8 areas, 1 guest-house system","Outputs: 2 onboard SPDT relays (require Model 305 relays, 1A @ 30 VDC resistive, Model 431 harness) + 4 open-collector outputs (50 mA each, via Model 300 harness); up to 25 Model 716 output modules per LX-Bus for 500 more relays","Bell output: 12 VDC @ 1.5 A max (terminals 5 & 6)","Auxiliary power: 12 VDC @ 1.5 A max","Standby battery: 12 VDC sealed lead-acid, float-charged at 13.8 VDC, 1.0 A max charge current (DMP 364/365/366/368/369); replace every 3-5 years","Transformers: Model 327 plug-in 16.5 VAC 50 VA / Model 322-323 wire-in 16 VAC 56 VA / Model 324-324P wire-in 16 VAC 100 VA","Communication: built-in 10/100 network to DMP SCS-1R / SCS-VR receivers, built-in digital dialer (on dialer-equipped board variants); optional 263LTE cellular, 763 Wi-Fi (needs V112 firmware / Level F hardware), 893A dual phone-line module. 128/256-bit AES encryption is XR550E-only \u2014 the XR150 does not do an encrypted network path","Programming: dealer-only via Remote Link (PC), Dealer Admin (dealer.securecomwireless.com), Tech App, or at the keypad; not sold to end users","Enclosures: Models 350, 350A, 341, 349, 352X (cold-rolled steel, gray or red)","Listing: UL commercial burg & fire; FCC Class A computing device"],proAdvice:"Know what you're looking at before you touch it. The XR150 and XR550 use the same board silhouette and the same install guide (LT-1233) \u2014 the difference is the feature ceiling, not the wiring. If a job needs more than 142 zones, more than 8 areas, more than 8 keypads, or you need AES-encrypted network to the central station, that's an XR550/XR550E and you can't just \"upgrade\" the 150 into one. Read the label. And there is no such thing as a walk-in-and-program XR150: these are dealer panels, tied to a programming account and a central-station receiver. You program them through Remote Link or Dealer Admin, not off a manual you found online. If you're at Metro and the panel isn't in your account, you're not getting in without the remote key or a factory default at the panel \u2014 plan for that before you promise a customer a same-visit fix.\n\nWiring gotchas that bite apprentices. Do NOT use shielded or twisted-pair on the LX-Bus or keypad bus \u2014 DMP calls for plain 18 or 22 AWG unshielded wire, and twisted pair will give you flaky bus comm that looks like a bad device. Onboard burg zones are EOL-terminated, and you program whether they're 1k or 2.2k; mismatch the resistor value to the programming and you'll chase a \"short\" or \"open\" that's really just the wrong terminator. Zones 9 and 10 are the powered/reset zones \u2014 that's where your 2-wire smokes land, 3.3k EOL, and the panel drops power to reset them on a fire alarm. Don't hang 2-wire smokes on a regular burg zone. Ground the can before you power anything: the guide is blunt about ESD frying the board, and a properly earthed panel is also your lightning survivability. Watch your power budget too \u2014 every keypad, expander, and 2-wire smoke draws off that 1.5 A aux; add it up before you assume onboard power covers it, or you'll need a supplemental supply.\n\nComms is where the money and the callbacks are. The default and preferred path on these is network to a DMP SCS-1R or SCS-VR receiver, with cell as backup (or cell primary at a site with no usable LAN). The cellular module today is the 263LTE \u2014 the old 3G/CDMA modules are long dead, so any panel still running one is a swap, not a repair. The LTE module has to be activated (Remote Link, Dealer Admin, Tech App, or a call to DMP at 866-266-2826) against a SIM and a rate plan before it'll ever pass a signal \u2014 a freshly installed module that \"won't communicate\" is almost always just not activated. Before you blame the module, run the onboard diagnostic: reset the panel, enter 2313 (DIAG) CMD, pick CELL STATUS, and read actual signal strength from the keypad. If you're on the network path, confirm the panel's IP setup (DHCP vs. static) and that the firewall isn't eating the port to the receiver \u2014 a \"network trouble\" with good LEDs is usually the customer's IT, not your panel.",commonServicingIssues:["Cellular comm failures \u2014 dead/aging LTE module, module never activated against a SIM/rate plan, or poor signal; verify with the 2313 DIAG > CELL STATUS diagnostic and reseat/reposition the antenna before condemning the board","Low-battery / no-battery-backup trouble \u2014 sealed 12V battery is a 3-5 year wear item; a panel throwing low-battery on AC is almost always just an aged battery","EOL resistor mismatch \u2014 zone programmed for 1k but wired with 2.2k (or vice versa), reading as a phantom short/open","Network path down \u2014 DHCP/static IP misconfig, or a customer firewall blocking the port to the SCS-1R/SCS-VR receiver; LEDs can look fine while nothing reaches the central station","Locked out of programming \u2014 panel tied to a dealer account with no remote key; requires a factory default at the panel or DMP dealer support to regain access","Keypad/LX-bus faults from wrong wire \u2014 shielded or twisted-pair on the bus causes intermittent device dropouts that mimic failed keypads or expanders","Bus addressing conflicts \u2014 two devices set to the same keypad-bus or LX-bus address after an add-on, causing missing/supervised-fault devices","Overloaded aux power \u2014 too many keypads/expanders/2-wire smokes on the 1.5A aux rail, causing brownout resets; needs a supplemental supply","ESD/lightning board damage on ungrounded installs \u2014 panel behaves erratically or won't power after a poorly grounded install or a nearby strike"],sources:["https://www.dmp.com/products/control-panels/xr-series/xr150","https://assets.dmp.com/assets/LT-1233.pdf","https://assets.dmp.com/assets/LT-1232.pdf","https://www.dmp.com/products/control-panels/xr-series/xr550","https://www.newagesecuritysystems.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DMP-XR550_SpecSheet.pdf","https://assets.dmp.com/assets/LT-2184.pdf","https://www.dmp.com/products/cell-network-communicators/xt-xr-series-communicators/263lte","https://www.dmp.com/products/modules-expanders/wi-fi-modules/763-wifi","https://www.sourcesecurity.com/dmp-xr150dnpcb-intruder-alarm-system-control-panel-technical-details.html"],photo:"photo-dmp-xr150",schematic:"schematic-dmp-xr150"},{id:"qolsys-iq4",name:"Qolsys IQ Panel 4",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"All-in-one self-contained wireless alarm panel with a 7-inch color touchscreen \u2014 the brains, keypad, siren, cellular/Wi-Fi radio, and sensor receivers all live in one wall-mounted unit, no separate control box in a closet. It runs on Alarm.com for monitoring and app control, and its headline feature is dual-band radio: PowerG encrypted long-range sensors plus a legacy security band (319.5, 345, or 433 MHz depending on the model, with S-Line encryption on Qolsys-branded sensors). Aimed at residential and light-commercial installs where you want everything self-hosted at the panel.",specs:["Display: 7-inch capacitive multi-touch LCD, 1280x800, 300 cd/m2","Dimensions: 6.1 in H x 7.5 in W x 1 in D (155 x 191 x 26 mm)","Power supply: plug-in transformer, input 100-240VAC 50/60Hz, output 7.0VDC 1.0A (Qolsys SW-070100A)","Backup battery: single 18650 lithium-ion, ~3000mAh, rated 24+ hours standby","Cellular: built-in LTE Cat 4 (AT&T IQP4004, Verizon IQP4001, TELUS IQP4007 \u2014 carrier is baked into the part number, not swappable)","Wi-Fi: 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac dual-band 2.4/5 GHz, with built-in access point","PowerG: up to 128 devices, 915 MHz, AES-128 encrypted 2-way, long range (roughly 2,000 ft open-air off this panel's internal antenna \u2014 the '2 km' PowerG figure is only with DSC PowerSeries Neo panels, not this one)","Legacy security RF: up to 128 devices on one band per panel \u2014 319.5, 345, or 433 MHz (model-specific); S-Line encryption on compatible Qolsys sensors","Z-Wave: Z-Wave Plus (700-series) up to ~137 nodes for home automation","Bluetooth: BLE 4.2 for disarm-on-approach / phone presence","User codes: up to 242, role-based (Dealer, Installer, Master, User, Guest, Duress)","Siren: ~85 dB built-in; four front-facing 4W speakers","Platform: Qualcomm Snapdragon 8-core SoC, Android-based OS","Operating temp: 32-120.2\xb0F (0-49\xb0C), indoor use"],proAdvice:"Mounting and power first. This is a self-contained panel, so where you hang it is where the radio lives \u2014 don't bury it in a metal utility closet or behind the fridge or you'll fight signal and cell reception forever. Put it central, on an interior wall, near a reliable outlet. Use the plug-in transformer that ships with it and a screw-terminal or tamper-resistant outlet if the AHJ wants it; run the low-voltage lead cleanly and leave the wall tamper plate seated or you'll get an immediate tamper trouble. The battery ships disconnected on newer units \u2014 plug the 18650 pigtail in before you wall-mount, because there's a known out-of-box issue (Qolsys TSB 221026) where a panel that sat on a shelf too long arrives deep-discharged and won't boot until it's had AC on it for a while. If a fresh panel is dead out of the box, that's your first suspect: give it AC and walk away for an hour before you call it defective.\n\nProgramming reality: this panel is dealer-programmed through Alarm.com, and the Installer code (default 1111) and Dealer code (default 2222) gate everything. Change them. The account has to be created on the Alarm.com side and the panel enrolled to it \u2014 you don't just walk up and program it standalone like an old hardwire panel. Learn sensors under Installation > Devices > Security Sensors, and here's the gotcha that bites everyone: pick the RIGHT frequency variant when you order. A 319.5 panel will not hear 345 or 433 sensors \u2014 the band is fixed in hardware per model. PowerG is the same across all of them, so when in doubt spec PowerG sensors and you're future-proof and encrypted. Always auto-learn PowerG by triggering the device rather than typing the ID; it pulls the full DL code and confirms two-way.\n\nS-Line is worth understanding so you can sell it honestly: on the legacy security band, only Qolsys S-Line sensors roll their encryption. A generic-brand 319.5 sensor learned into the panel works but transmits in the clear and can be defeated by a code-grabber. If the customer cares about that, it's PowerG or Qolsys S-Line, not just any compatible sensor. Set the panel's Wi-Fi AND make sure the LTE has bars \u2014 dual-path is the whole point; a panel running on Wi-Fi alone drops off the second the router reboots. On firmware: keep it current (Settings > Advanced > Updates), because Qolsys patches radio and Z-Wave behavior in point releases, but read the release notes before pushing a major jump on a fleet \u2014 a couple of updates have changed defaults. And label your sensors sanely in Alarm.com; \"Zone 7\" means nothing to a homeowner on a false-alarm call at 2am.",commonServicingIssues:["Won't power up out of the box \u2014 deep-discharged 18650 from long shelf storage (Qolsys TSB 221026); apply AC and let it charge before condemning the panel","AC power failure trouble \u2014 nearly always the plug-in transformer worked loose at the outlet, a switched/GFCI outlet that tripped, or a chewed low-voltage lead; check the transformer before anything else","Battery trouble / short backup runtime \u2014 aging or swollen 18650; replace with the correct Qolsys battery, don't sub a random 18650, and never install a swollen cell","Cellular / 'radio not responding' / communication failure \u2014 bad LTE placement, carrier-side deactivation, or panel not properly registered to the Alarm.com account; verify signal bars and account status","Wi-Fi drops after router changes \u2014 panel keeps stale credentials; re-enter Wi-Fi and confirm LTE is still active as the backup path","Unresponsive or ghost-touch touchscreen \u2014 try a soft reboot first (Settings > Advanced > Reboot); persistent issues point to a mainboard/screen hardware fault needing RMA","Nuisance sensor tamper/trouble \u2014 panel wall tamper plate not seated, or a legacy-band sensor with a weak/dying battery or fringe RF signal","Sensor won't learn \u2014 wrong frequency variant for the panel (319.5 vs 345 vs 433 is fixed per model); confirm the sensor band matches or switch to PowerG","Beeping you can't clear \u2014 usually an open/low-battery zone or an active trouble; clear the underlying condition rather than just silencing","Avoid factory default to fix problems \u2014 it wipes all programming and Alarm.com enrollment; use reboot/power-cycle and only default if support directs"],sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/documents/qolsys-iq-panel-4-specification-sheet","https://iqsecurityproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/10/IQ-Panel-4-Specification-Sheets.pdf","https://iqsecurityproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/IQ-Panel-4-Installation-Manual-4.1.0-220223-FINAL.pdf","https://iqsecurityproducts.com/iq-panel-4/","https://www.geoarm.com/iqp4004-qolsys-iq-panel-4-wireless-touchscreen-control-for-att-lte.html","https://www.geoarm.com/iqp4007-qolsys-iq-panel-4-wireless-touchscreen-control-for-telus-lte.html","https://www.alarmgrid.com/blog/clarification-on-wireless-range-of-powerg-sensors","https://suretyhome.com/qolsys-iq-panel-explained/","https://dealers.qolsys.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/TSB-221026-IQ-Panel-4-Not-Powering-Up-Out-Of-Box.pdf","https://brinkshome.com/help-center/articles/237-what-to-do-if-the-iq-panel-4-is-not-responding","https://brinkshome.com/help-center/articles/5831913616909-Qolsys-IQ-Panel-4-Failure-to-Communicate","https://iqsecurityproducts.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/IQ-Panel-4-Battery-Quick-Guide.pdf"],photo:"photo-qolsys-iq4",schematic:"schematic-qolsys-iq4"},{id:"pir-motion-detector",name:"Honeywell IS335 PIR Motion Detector",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"The IS335 is Honeywell/Resideo's bread-and-butter hardwired passive infrared (PIR) motion detector \u2014 the interior \"motion\" you'll hang on ninety percent of residential burglar-alarm jobs running a Vista, Lyric, or any conventional zone panel. Passive infrared means it sees heat, not motion directly: it reads the infrared energy in the room through a segmented Fresnel lens and trips when a warm body crosses from one lens segment (zone) to the next. It's got selectable pet immunity up to 80 lb set by a jumper, a 40 ft by 56 ft coverage fan, and it's a dumb powered contact \u2014 no addressing, no comms, just an energized Form A relay on a four-wire run back to the panel. Note the base IS335 has NO tamper; the IS335T is the same head with a cover/wall tamper switch added.",specs:["Coverage: 40 ft x 56 ft (12 m x 17 m) wide, wall-to-wall look-down fan across the room (datasheet range; the detection-pattern diagram plots the zones out to roughly 40 ft deep)","Detection zones: 100 total across three tiers per the datasheet pattern \u2014 18 lower (close/look-down), 46 intermediate, 36 long-range","Power: 9.0-15 VDC; 7 mA typical, 12 mA max draw; tolerates 3 V peak-to-peak AC ripple at nominal 12 VDC","Alarm relay: energized Form A, rated 90 mA at 15 VDC, 22 ohms max contact resistance; alarm relay duration 3 seconds per trip","Wiring: 4-wire (2 power, 2 loop); powered loop, so run your end-of-line resistor out at the detector head, not jumpered at the panel","Mounting height: 7 ft - 9 ft (2.1 m - 2.7 m); optimal coverage geometry at 7 ft 6 in (2.3 m) per the install manual","Pet immunity: jumper-selectable, animals up to 80 lb (36 kg / <= 80 lbs)","White-light immunity: 2,000 lux typical (patented black bug-guard behind the lens)","RFI immunity: 20 V/m from 10-1000 MHz, 15 V/m from 1000-2700 MHz","Operating temperature: 14 F to 131 F (-10 C to 55 C); relative humidity 5-93% non-condensing","Temperature compensation: Advanced Dual Slope (adjusts sensitivity as room temp approaches body temp)","Warm-up / stabilization: LED slow-flashes on power-up and stops in under 45 seconds \u2014 it's not reliable until then","Dimensions: 3.39 in H x 2.24 in W x 1.71 in D (8.6 x 5.7 x 4.35 cm); 2.15 oz (61 g) net","Optics: sealed optical chamber (blocks drafts/insects), custom Fresnel lens, always-enabled walk-test LED","Tamper: NONE on the base IS335 \u2014 it's a bare 4-wire head. Only the IS335T variant carries a cover/wall tamper switch","Listings: ETL to UL 639, cETL to ULC S306, FCC Part 15 Class B verified, IC ICES-003 Class B verified","Ordering: IS335 (no tamper); IS335T is the same detector with a tamper switch added. Accessories: SMB10 swivel wall bracket, SMB10C swivel ceiling bracket; wire 1103 (solid 22 AWG 4-cond) or 1104 (stranded 22 AWG 4-cond)"],proAdvice:"Location beats everything with a PIR, and the single rule that saves you callbacks is: aim it so traffic crosses the beams, not walks straight at them. PIR sees the body move from one lens zone to the next \u2014 walk perpendicular across the fan and you trip it in a step or two; walk dead-on toward the sensor down a hallway and you can get ten feet in before it catches, because you're staying inside one zone the whole way. So you corner-mount it to look diagonally across the room a burglar has to enter through, or you cover the pinch point they can't avoid \u2014 the top of the stairs, the hallway everything funnels through. Corner mounting also gives you the widest slice of the 40x56 fan for free.\n\nHeight matters and the sweet spot is 7 ft 6 in. The spec allows 7 to 9 ft, but go too high and the look-down zones (that lower tier of 18 zones aimed at the floor near the wall) shoot out too far and you lose your close-in catch and, more important on a pet job, you blow the pet-immunity geometry. Mount it on a solid wall stud or firm surface \u2014 a PIR on a flexing hollow-wall panel or a rattling piece of trim will false every time a door slams, because vibration reads as motion. And nothing near the top of the lens: no cobwebs, no hanging plants, no drapes that'll billow into the field.\n\nNow the false-trip list, because this is what the customer actually calls you about. PIR trips on rapid change in infrared, so your enemies are: HVAC and heat registers blowing warm air across the field (never aim a motion at a supply vent, a radiator, or a wood stove), sunlight and headlights sweeping a window inside the coverage \u2014 the 2,000-lux bug guard helps but it isn't magic, so don't point it at a west-facing window at sunset \u2014 reflective surfaces and mirrors bouncing heat, and rising heat off a fireplace or a kitchen. Ceiling fans usually don't trip a good PIR since the blades aren't body-temp, but a fan right in the field can stir warm air enough to matter. The Advanced Dual Slope temp compensation in this one is exactly for the worst case: when the room warms up near 98 degrees, the temperature delta between a person and the background collapses and a cheap PIR goes blind or hair-trigger \u2014 the dual-slope circuit ramps sensitivity to hold catch performance in a hot room. Good feature, but it's why you still don't want it staring at a heat source.\n\nPet immunity is a jumper, on or off, rated to 80 lb \u2014 but understand what it's really doing before you promise the customer the dog won't false it. Pet immunity works by masking the lower look-down zones and weighting detection to the upper zones, betting the animal stays low to the floor. That bet breaks the second the dog gets up on the back of the couch, climbs the stairs into the beam, or the cat walks a bookshelf. So on a pet job: enable the jumper, but ALSO physically aim the detector so the animal's normal cruising altitude stays under the fan, and warn the customer to keep furniture out from under it. An 80-lb dog that's allowed on elevated furniture will false a pet-immune PIR all day, and that's not a defective unit, that's placement.\n\nWiring gotcha that trips new guys: this is a powered, four-wire loop, and you want its end-of-line resistor out at the detector, not jumpered at the panel \u2014 that's how you keep the run supervised. The relay is an energized Form A \u2014 powered and held closed in the normal secure state, drops open on alarm \u2014 which is the fail-safe behavior you want (cut the wire or kill the detector's power and it alarms). Land the two power leads on the panel's aux/switched power, the two loop leads on the zone, put the EOLR across the loop terminals at the head, and set the panel zone as a standard EOL/motion zone. And know your part number: the base IS335 has no tamper, so if the job spec or the AHJ wants tamper supervision you order the IS335T instead \u2014 don't promise a tamper on a plain IS335 you can't deliver. If you're on a hardwired Vista, you almost always want that motion zone programmed as an interior-follower so it's bypassed on STAY and only live on AWAY \u2014 a motion armed while the family's home walking around is a guaranteed false dispatch. Give it its 45-second warm-up after power before you walk-test, and use the always-on LED to walk the room and confirm your pattern actually covers the entry paths, then talk to the customer about whether they want that LED tell left blinking (it advertises the sensor's aim to anyone who studies it, but it's also your free diagnostic later).",commonServicingIssues:["Random false alarms, no obvious pattern: nine times out of ten it's environmental \u2014 check for a heat register, radiator, or sunlit window that came into the field seasonally, and check the mounting surface isn't flexing/vibrating. Re-aim or relocate before you condemn the head","Pet owner getting falses despite pet immunity on: dog or cat is getting up into the upper zones (furniture, stairs, countertops). Confirm the PI jumper is set, then re-aim so pets stay under the fan and keep elevated furniture out from under it \u2014 not a hardware fault","Won't detect / slow to catch in a hallway: geometry problem \u2014 subject is walking straight at the sensor inside one zone. Corner-mount it so traffic crosses the beams, or lower the aim to break the walk-toward path","Zone shows trouble/open on the panel: powered loop is missing its EOL resistor or the resistor's at the panel instead of at the detector, or a power lead is loose \u2014 verify 9-15 VDC at the head and the EOLR is across the loop terminals at the device","Detector goes blind or hyper-sensitive on hot days: expected near ~98 F room temp as the body-to-background delta collapses; the dual-slope comp handles most of it, but a unit aimed at a heat source or in an un-conditioned space will still misbehave \u2014 relocate away from the heat","Constant tamper trouble (IS335T only \u2014 the base IS335 has NO tamper switch): cover not fully seated on the tamper switch, or the base pulled loose from the wall \u2014 reseat the cover and re-anchor the base to solid backing. If it's a plain IS335, there's no tamper to troubleshoot","Falses right after power-up or a power blip: unit wasn't given its <45-second stabilization window (LED slow-flashing means it's still warming up), or the supply has too much AC ripple (spec tolerates 3 V p-p at 12 VDC) \u2014 let it warm up and check the power supply/filtering","Dead unit / no response on walk test: no aux power reaching it (blown panel PTC, loose lead) or a fried board from a wiring short \u2014 meter the power terminals first before swapping the head"],sources:["https://www.servisecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/06/IS335.pdf","https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/wired-sensors/motion-sensors/is335-pet-immune-pir-detector-40-ft-x-56-ft-is335/","https://buildings.honeywell.com/us/en/products/by-category/sensors/motion-detectors/is335-wired-pir-motion-detector","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1311876/Honeywell-Is335.html","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1311876/Honeywell-Is335.html?page=3","https://www.manua.ls/honeywell/is335t/manual","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-is335","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/products/honeywell-is335-motion-sensor"],photo:"photo-pir-motion-detector",schematic:"schematic-pir-motion-detector"},{id:"door-window-contact",name:"Door/Window Contact (Magnetic Reed Switch)",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"A two-piece magnetic sensor: a sealed glass reed switch in one housing and a magnet in the other. Mount the switch on the frame and the magnet on the moving door or window sash; when they separate past the rated gap the reed changes state, signaling the panel that the opening moved. It is the workhorse point-detection device in every burglar alarm and access-control system.",specs:["Two form factors: surface-mount (screw-on blocks, e.g. GRI 29A / Honeywell 7939) and recessed (drill-in cylinders, e.g. GRI 180/184, Honeywell 951, Seco-Larm SM-4103)","Recessed cylinder diameters: 1/4in super-mini, 3/8in (most common), 3/4in, and 1in","Standard operating gap: recessed roughly 1/2in-3/4in; surface-mount around 1in (GRI 29A rated up to 1in, ~5/8in on steel)","Wide-gap surface: 1-1/2in (GRI 29AWG); extra-wide 2in; recessed wide-gap ~1.25in (Honeywell 951WG)","Steel/overhead door units (Sentrol/Interlogix 2200 series): gap runs 3/4in up to 3in depending on model (fixed per model, not one adjustable unit), IP67 aluminum housing, stainless armored lead","Contact form: SPST closed-loop (loop N.C. with the door shut) is standard for alarm; SPST open-loop (N.O.); SPDT/DPDT available for dual use","Reed contact rating (typical): ~10W max switching, up to ~100-200VDC, 0.1A-0.5A max current (GRI 29A is 10W/160VDC/0.40A; Seco-Larm SM-4103 is 10W/100VDC/0.1A)","Rare-earth (neodymium) magnet versions give tighter, more repeatable gaps and resist defeat by an external magnet better than ferrite","Leads: solid 12in-36in flying leads (recessed) or screw terminals (surface); most are polarity-independent"],proAdvice:"Wire the loop closed, not open. In a supervised burglar circuit you want the loop Normally Closed with the door shut, so opening the door OR cutting the wire both break continuity and trip the zone. If the panel zone is supervised, land the end-of-line resistor at the LAST contact in the loop, not back in the can \u2014 a resistor jumpered at the panel supervises nothing and any decent inspector will flag it. For access control (REX, door-position on a Mercury/HID or Software House board) you often want the same NC contact but read as a DPS input; check whether the board expects N.O. or N.C. and set the input accordingly.\n\nMind the gap, and mind the door. The published gap is measured on a wood/non-ferrous door in free air. Hang that same contact on a hollow-metal frame or a steel door and the ferrous mass bleeds off the magnetic field \u2014 your real-world make/break distance can shrink 30-50%. That is exactly why the wide-gap (1-1/2in / 2in) and the Sentrol 2200-style steel-door contacts exist. If you are on a metal frame, spec a wide-gap unit or a rare-earth magnet from the start; do not fight nuisance faults later because you saved forty cents on a standard contact.\n\nSet your working gap tight \u2014 aim for the door to break the loop within the first 1/4in-1/2in of travel, not at the rated maximum. If you install right at the edge of the spec, seasonal frame swelling, a sagging hinge, or a slammed door bouncing the sash will chatter the reed and throw intermittent faults that are miserable to chase. Test with a piece of paper feeler and the panel in test mode; walk every point and watch the zone actually restore, don't assume.\n\nRecessed vs surface is a trade of aesthetics for serviceability. Recessed disappears into the door edge and jamb \u2014 drill a 3/8in or 3/4in hole, matching depth in switch and magnet, keep them aligned across the gap. Beautiful on a residential wood door, a nightmare on a steel commercial door and un-serviceable once the door's hung. Surface-mount is ugly but you can align it, shim it, and swap it in ninety seconds. On overhead/roll-up and man-doors in steel, use the armored floor-mount or track contacts (2200 series) with the stainless flex lead \u2014 a plain contact gets torn off the first week.\n\nPolarity and defeat: most reeds don't care about lead polarity, but bias/balanced magnetic contacts (BMS, high-security / UL 634 Level 2) DO \u2014 they use an internal bias magnet so a burglar can't hold an external magnet to the switch and walk the door open. If the job is a jewelry store, pharmacy, or gun room, spec a balanced/biased contact and follow the maker's orientation exactly; a standard reed is trivially defeated with a rare-earth magnet slapped on the frame.",commonServicingIssues:["Nuisance/intermittent faults from the gap set too wide \u2014 door sag, frame swell, or a bouncing sash momentarily breaks the reed; re-align or shim tighter","Standard contact used on a steel door/frame, so the effective gap collapsed and the point won't restore \u2014 swap to wide-gap or rare-earth","Reed physically cracked from a slammed door or over-torqued mounting screw; glass envelope fractures and the point reads permanently open","EOL resistor terminated at the panel instead of the far contact, defeating supervision \u2014 re-terminate at the last device","Magnet and switch mounted on the wrong halves (magnet on frame, switch on a vibrating door) causing lead-fatigue breaks; keep the switch on the fixed side","Corrosion/water intrusion on exterior or overhead-door contacts that aren't potted/IP-rated \u2014 replace with sealed IP67 armored units","Painters or drywall crews burying recessed contacts or knocking surface blocks out of alignment during a remodel"],sources:["https://www.grisk.com/products/magnetic-reed-switches/recessed-magnetic-switches/","https://www.grisk.com/products/magnetic-reed-switches/surface-mount/","https://www.adiglobaldistribution.us/product/gi-29aw","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-951wg","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-7939wg","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/180-12-DataSheet.pdf","https://www.seco-larm.com/product/sm-4103-tqw/","https://www.homecontrols.com/Seco-Larm-Rare-Earth-Recessed-Mount-Magnetic-Contact-Switch-SESM4103TQW","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/2202A-L-DataSheet.pdf","https://static.interlogix.com/library/2200 CAT PAGE.pdf"],photo:"photo-door-window-contact",schematic:"schematic-door-window-contact"},{id:"liftmaster-la400",name:"LiftMaster LA400 (LA400UL) Residential Swing Gate Operator",category:"Gate operators",whatItIs:"A 24VDC residential/light-commercial linear (ram-style) swing gate operator \u2014 a screw-driven actuator arm that pushes and pulls a single leaf, or a pair for a dual gate. Runs entirely on 24V DC off a control board, so it's happy on transformer power, battery backup, or solar, and it's UL 325 Listed when you run it with the monitored entrapment protection it ships with.",specs:["Type: 24VDC linear screw-drive actuator (ram arm), single or dual swing gate \u2014 worm/lead-screw driven, not a hydraulic ram","Gate capacity: up to 16 ft OR up to 850 lb per leaf \u2014 and those two trade off, they aren't both at once: about 850 lb at 10 ft, 750 lb at 12 ft, 650 lb at 14 ft, 550 lb at 16 ft. Don't read the headline as 850 lb on a 16-ft leaf","Duty cycle: rated ~100 cycles/day \u2014 a residential/light-commercial number, not a high-traffic HOA-entrance number","Speed: opens 90 degrees in roughly 15-17 seconds, adjustable at the board","Power in: 120/230 VAC single-phase through the control box to a 24VDC board \u2014 the whole operator logic runs on 24V DC","Solar-ready: runs off a panel-and-battery kit instead of line power in off-grid installs \u2014 the LiftMaster solar package is 20W (two 10W panels, e.g. LA400XL20W)","Battery backup: two 12V 7Ah sealed lead-acid batteries in series (24V), standard on the PKG; up to ~400 cycles or ~97 days of standby on a power loss (more with larger optional batteries)","Entrapment protection: monitored \u2014 ships (PKG) with the LMRRUL monitored retro-reflective photo eye; UL 325 compliance requires monitored external device(s), photo eye and/or monitored edge, wired to the monitored inputs","RF / smart: Security+ 2.0 encrypted, tri-band (310/315/390 MHz), HomeLink compatible; myQ with the optional expansion/internet gateway","Control board: main board K1D8052-1CC (LA400/LA412/LA500 family); older single/dual boards carried the K001A6039 part number","Model numbers: LA400UL = operator only; LA400PKGUL = operator + monitored photo eye + battery backup","Warranty: 2 years on the operator (residential)","Operating temp: roughly -20C to +60C (-4F to 140F) per the data sheet"],proAdvice:"Set your gate up right before you ever mount the operator. The LA400 is a linear ram, and rams live or die on geometry \u2014 the pivot-point (mounting) dimensions off the hinge determine how far it swings and how much torque you actually get. Pull the template out of the manual and measure the \"A\" and \"B\" dimensions off the gate hinge; get those wrong and the gate either won't hit 90 degrees or it slams and stalls at the ends. On a heavy or long leaf, gate frame and hinges matter more than the operator \u2014 a sagging leaf or a stiff hinge will trip the obstruction sensing every cycle and you'll be chasing a \"bad operator\" that's really a bad gate. And read the capacity chart honestly: that 850-lb number is at 10 ft, not 16 \u2014 a long leaf is a light leaf, so if you've got a 16-footer you're down around 550 lb and you'd better be under it. Level the leaf, grease the hinges, add a positive stop for both open and close, and confirm you can swing it by hand with one finger before power ever touches it.\n\nProgramming is limit-and-force, in that order. This is a DC operator with electronic limits \u2014 you drive the arm to full open and full close and teach the board those positions, then set the obstruction force. Do the limits with the leaf swinging freely, then set force to the minimum that reliably drives the gate through wind and its own weight without nuisance-reversing. Don't crank force to mask a mechanical problem \u2014 that's exactly the move UL 325 exists to stop, and a tech who dials out the safety to make a heavy gate close is one lawsuit from a bad day. Entrapment protection is monitored on this board: the photo eye isn't a dumb beam, the board watches it and will fault/refuse to run if a monitored device is missing or miswired. So don't jumper out the photo eye to \"test\" \u2014 pull the eye and the gate goes to a fail-safe, exactly as designed.\n\nTwo wiring gotchas that bite people. First, the electric gate lock / solenoid: land it on the NO and COM terminals, not NO and NC \u2014 the older diagrams tripped a lot of techs up here, so follow the current manual and check the lock logic against the gate before you leave. Second, the transformer's yellow/green wire: when you drive a proper earth ground rod at the operator (and on any outdoor gate op you should), that yellow/green needs to come off per the manual so you don't create a ground loop. On a solar or off-grid job, size the panel and battery for the real cycle count and the worst-case cloudy stretch \u2014 the \"400 cycles / 97 days\" backup number assumes you started full and the sun quits; a busy driveway on a north-facing panel in winter will out-run a stingy panel, and the stock 20W kit is a light-traffic number. And on dual-gate installs, run the two operators as master/slave (wireless dual-gate comms per the manual) and set the leaf delay so the astragal side closes last.",commonServicingIssues:["Nuisance reversing / \"obstruction\" faults every cycle \u2014 nine times out of ten it's the gate, not the board: sagging leaf, dry or binding hinge, wind load, or a missing positive stop; fix the mechanics and re-run limits before you touch force","Won't hold a charge / dies on a power blip \u2014 the two 7Ah SLA batteries are consumables, ~3-5 yr life; test/replace as a matched pair, and on solar confirm the panel is actually charging (dirty or shaded panel is the usual culprit)","Photo eye faulting the operator \u2014 monitored eye out of alignment, dirty lens, or the reflector shifted; the board will refuse to run rather than ignore a safety, so clean/re-aim the LMRRUL before condemning anything","Dead or intermittent control board \u2014 surge/lightning damage on outdoor installs; the board (K1D8052-1CC, older K001A6039) is field-replaceable, but land a real ground rod and consider surge protection or it'll come back","Electric lock / maglock behaving backwards \u2014 usually the solenoid lock landed on NO/NC instead of NO/COM; re-land per the current manual and confirm the lock releases before the gate moves","Arm binding, slow, or noisy travel \u2014 dry or worn lead screw / actuator; clean and re-grease the screw, check for water intrusion into the ram, replace the arm assembly if the nut is worn","Gate won't reach full open/close after a battery pull or board swap \u2014 limits lost; re-teach open and close limits, they don't survive a full power-down on some board revisions","Loses RF range / won't learn remotes \u2014 Security+ 2.0 pairing dropped or antenna wire tucked into the metal cabinet; re-learn and route the antenna out of the box"],sources:["https://www.liftmaster.com/24vdc-residential-linear-actuator/p/LA400UL","https://gatedepot.com/liftmaster-la400-linear-arm-gate-operator","https://www.devancocanada.com/files/manuals/LA400:Installation-manual.pdf","https://www.hooverfence.com/mas_assets/media/hooverfence/pdfs/wiring-diagram-LA400UL.pdf","https://www.affordableopeners.com/liftmaster-k1d8052-1cc-la500-la400-la412-main-control-board.html","https://elitegates.net/products/liftmaster-la400xl20w-dual-gate-openers-solar-package","https://allsecurityequipment.com/products/liftmaster-k1d8052-1cc"],photo:"photo-liftmaster-la400",schematic:"schematic-liftmaster-la400"},{id:"liftmaster-csl24u",name:"LiftMaster CSL24U / CSL24UL \u2014 24VDC Commercial Slide Gate Operator",category:"Gate operators",whatItIs:"The CSL24U (sold as the CSL24UL, and CSL24ULMC / CSL24ULWKMC with the battery-backup and wiring kits) is LiftMaster's high-traffic, continuous-duty 24VDC slide gate operator for commercial and industrial vehicular gates. It's a chain-drive, worm-gear machine listed to UL 325 / UL 991 for Class I through IV, so it covers everything from a residential drive to a warehouse yard or a gated apartment complex. This is the workhorse you'll bolt to a pad more than almost any other slider in the LiftMaster line.",specs:["Drive/motor: LiftMaster P3 24VDC continuous-duty motor (the high-cycle P3 family LiftMaster markets to ~1,000,000 cycles), chain-driven off a commercial oil-bath gearbox with 10:1 worm-gear reduction. Heads up: LiftMaster does NOT publish a horsepower number on this unit \u2014 DC sliders are rated by gate weight/length, not HP, so ignore any \"1 HP\" figure a reseller floats.","Gate capacity: up to 1,500 lb and 50 ft of gate leaf","Speed: 12 in/sec (about a foot a second), soft-start / soft-stop every cycle","UL listing: UL 325 / UL 991, Class I, II, III and IV vehicular gate operator (2016 UL 325 guidelines)","Input power: 120VAC or 230VAC single phase (field-selectable); 24VDC internal control/motor bus","Accessory power: 24VDC 500mA output, both switched and unswitched terminals","Entrapment protection: 3 monitored safety inputs on the main board, plus 3 more on the optional expansion board (6 total); ships with LMRRUL monitored retro-reflective photoeye + S505AL 5 ft resistive edge to cover the two independent monitored devices UL 325 requires per entrapment zone","Battery backup (MC/kit): 24VDC system. Standard (2) 7-Ah batteries give ~208 cycles / up to 24 days standby; upgrade to (2) 33-Ah and you get ~1,179 cycles / up to 105 days standby. Those are basic-setup numbers (remotes only) \u2014 added accessories eat into both.","Connectivity: MyQ (via internet gateway/hub; myQ Facility / myQ Community) plus built-in Security+ 2.0 rolling-code radio","Programming capacity: up to 50 Security+ 2.0 remotes / KPW250 keypads on the onboard receiver (effectively unlimited with a passport/expansion receiver)","Chain: ships with 30 ft of #41 black-oxide chain; idler pulleys and chain brackets at each end","Chassis: powder-coated steel enclosure with a 2-piece high-density UV-resistant polycarbonate cover","Temperature: -4\xb0F to 140\xb0F standard; -40\xb0F to 140\xb0F with the optional HTR heater","Manual operation: keyed manual disconnect (release lever) for hand-operation on power loss","Warranty: 7-year residential / 5-year commercial"],proAdvice:"Get the pad and the gate right before you ever touch the operator. This is a chain-drive slider \u2014 you're running #41 chain the length of the gate (it ships with 30 ft of #41 black-oxide) with a chain bracket at each end and idler pulleys. The pad has to be level, in the right plane relative to the gate track, and the chain has to run level and parallel to the path of travel. If the gate rides rough on its own V-track or the rollers bind, do not fix that with force settings \u2014 you'll just cook the motor or nuisance-reverse forever. A slider that won't roll smooth by hand is not ready for an operator. Set the gate stops and end-of-travel hardware first.\n\nWire it for the voltage you've actually got. The board is field-selectable 120/230 single phase \u2014 verify the jumper/tap matches before you power up. Bring the operator power on its own circuit, and get your safety devices in before you program limits: the LMRRUL photoeye and the S505AL edge that ship in the box are monitored, and the board will not run a normal cycle without its required entrapment devices present and healthy. That's UL 325 by design, not a defect \u2014 techs who \"temporarily\" jumper a safety to get a gate moving are the ones who get a call back or worse. Wire the photoeye across the opening protecting the entrapment zone, and the edge on the leading (and trailing, if the layout needs it) gate rail.\n\nProgramming is straightforward once safeties are happy. You set travel limits and force with the onboard buttons/menu \u2014 learn the open and close limits by running the gate to each stop, then let it auto-learn force over a cycle or two. Rule you tattoo on your brain: never crank force past the minimum needed to move the gate, and never use force to mask a binding gate. For remotes, tap the LEARN button on the primary operator (it beeps into learn mode), then hit the remote. For MyQ you need the gateway/hub and the customer's Wi-Fi; Security+ 2.0 handles the local rolling-code radio regardless. If it's a two-operator install (dual gate or primary/secondary), the boards talk over wireless dual-gate communication \u2014 one is primary and you program to it.\n\nSpec the battery-backup version (the MC / WKMC kits) on almost any real commercial job \u2014 a slider that dies locked or open during an outage is a liability. The standard (2) 7-Ah pack buys you 24 days of standby / ~208 cycles; the (2) 33-Ah upgrade jumps that to ~105 days / ~1,179 cycles, so on a busy gate that upgrade is days-not-minutes of real runtime. In cold country add the HTR heater; below -4\xb0F the plain unit is out of spec and you'll see sluggish starts and battery droop. Show the customer the manual disconnect and make them practice it \u2014 half your after-hours \"gate is stuck\" calls are a healthy operator with a dead accessory or a tripped safety, and a customer who can hand-release avoids a truck roll.",commonServicingIssues:["Nuisance reversing / gate stops mid-travel \u2014 almost always a photoeye out of alignment or a dirty/wet lens, a damaged resistive edge, or force set too low for a gate that's gotten harder to roll. Check the LED diagnostic flash code first, then the safeties, then the gate hardware.","Monitored-device faults \u2014 the board expects the LMRRUL and S505AL edge to report in; a failed edge, a chewed sensor wire, or the wrong (non-monitored) accessory swapped in throws a fault and blocks normal operation.","Gate won't roll smooth / binds \u2014 worn V-groove rollers, debris in the track, or a bent track. The operator gets blamed but the gate is the problem; fix mechanically, don't chase it with force settings.","Chain issues \u2014 #41 chain too loose (slaps/skips) or too tight (drags the reducer, overheats), broken chain bracket, or seized idler pulley.","Weak or dead backup batteries \u2014 7-Ah cells age out in a few years, more so in heat; symptoms are short backup runtime or slow/weak movement on battery. Load-test and replace in pairs.","Cold-weather sluggishness or no-start below -4\xb0F on units without the HTR heater; also condensation in the enclosure.","Limits drifted / gate no longer seats or won't fully close \u2014 re-learn travel limits; check end stops and chain tension.","Remote/keypad programming loss or range problems \u2014 re-learn on the primary operator; check the antenna and for local RF interference.","Power/board faults \u2014 tripped circuit, wrong input voltage tap, corroded terminals from water intrusion at the conduit; read the LED flash pattern against the manual's chart."],sources:["https://www.liftmaster.com/24vdc-high-traffic-commercial-slide-gate-operator/p/CSL24UL","https://www.edl-gm.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/CSL24UL_Product_Guide_EN.pdf","Liftmaster csl24ul mc (controlledproducts.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://allsecurityequipment.com/products/liftmaster-csl24ul","https://www.liftmaster.com/s505al-resistive-edge/p/S505AL","https://gatehousesupplies.com/csl24ul-commercial-slide-gate-operator-with-battery-backup-csl24ulmc/","https://manuals.plus/liftmaster/csl24ul-24vdc-commercial-slide-gate-operator-manual","https://www.manualowl.com/m/LiftMaster/CSL24UL/Manual/529669"],photo:"photo-liftmaster-csl24u",schematic:"schematic-liftmaster-csl24u"},{id:"doorking-9150",name:"DoorKing 9150 Slide Gate Operator",category:"Gate operators",whatItIs:"The DKS 9150 is DoorKing's heavy-duty commercial/industrial chain-drive slide gate operator \u2014 the one you'll see all over apartment complexes, storage yards, and fleet lots. It's a continuous-duty AC unit that pulls the gate open and closed with a #40 roller chain running the length of the gate, driven through a single cog-belt reduction and a slip clutch off the motor. It's the workhorse tier above their residential 9100 line and below the gearbox-driven 9200/9300, built to run all day and take abuse.",specs:["Motor: 1/2 HP or 1 HP, continuous-duty AC (industrial rated), single phase","1/2 HP model: up to 30 ft / 1,000 lb gate (800 lb with DC Convenience Open / battery-backup option)","1 HP model: up to 45 ft / 1,500 lb gate (1,300 lb with DC Convenience Open / battery-backup option)","Gate speed: approx. 10 in/sec (~1 ft/sec) \u2014 fixed, not variable","Drive: single cog-belt primary reduction to a mechanical slip clutch and drive sprocket; #40 roller chain (20 ft supplied) runs the length of the gate. No gearbox/oil bath here \u2014 that's the 9200/9300","Power: 115 VAC standard; 208/230/460/575 VAC available with accessory high-voltage transformer kit (P/N 2600-266)","Control board: 4602-010 (self-learning limits \u2014 the operator learns open/close positions against the physical gate stops; no mechanical limit switches or cams to set). Pre-2016 units ran the 4602-012 / -018 board","Front, center, or rear drive; pad-mount or post-mount","Cover: steel (9150-380 series) or polypropylene (9150-385 series, ships with the MicroPlus RF receiver kit for better clicker range through the plastic)","Enclosure ~15.25 in W x 24.5-24.75 in H x 16.75 in D; ~125-185 lb shipping depending on config","Listings: ETL listed to UL 325 (2016 edition) / UL 991","Entrapment protection: inherent reversing sensor (UL 325 Type A) plus a mechanical slip clutch (Type C) factory-set to slip and reverse at no more than 75 lbf on an obstruction"],proAdvice:"Get the pad and the chain right and this operator runs for a decade; get them wrong and you'll be back every month. Set your concrete pad so the operator height puts the drive sprocket in line with the chain mounted on the gate frame \u2014 the chain wants to run dead level and straight through both idler pulleys with no side pull. Mount the chain brackets to the gate, run the chain through the operator's pulleys, and tension it so it's snug but has a little give. Do NOT crank it drum-tight. Over-tightened chain is the number one thing techs do wrong here \u2014 it loads the bearings, chews the sprocket, and trips the reverse sensor on hard starts. There's an adjustment nut on each end; snug it and leave it.\n\nLimits are self-learning on this board (4602-010), which throws people used to old mechanical cam switches. Every time you power it up, the first open command runs a learn cycle \u2014 it drives the gate to find the physical open and close stops and memorizes those positions. That means you need solid, repeatable positive stops on the gate track (a post or bolt the gate leaf hits), or the operator never learns a consistent limit and you get short-cycling or crushing. Set your stops first, then power up and let it cycle. If you change a DIP switch, you have to cycle power off and back on for it to take \u2014 it only reads the switches at boot.\n\nThe clutch does double duty: it's your primary reduction AND your last-ditch entrapment device (UL 325 Type C), set at the factory to slip and reverse at 75 lb of force or less. You adjust it with the hex bolt on the big pulley against the magnetic spring assembly, and you do it with power OFF. Here's the rule that keeps you out of court: do NOT tighten the clutch to muscle a heavy, sagging, or binding gate through its travel. Over-tightening the clutch defeats the reversing force and turns the gate into something that can crush a kid. Fix the gate \u2014 rollers, track, plumb \u2014 don't tighten the clutch to hide it.\n\nOn safety, don't hand it over on the inherent reverse sensor and slip clutch alone \u2014 that will not pass a UL 325 inspection and it won't cover you if someone gets hurt. You must add external monitored entrapment devices: a Type B1 monitored photo eye and/or a Type B2 sensing edge, wired into the monitored inputs so the board watches for the device and faults if it's missing. The board is built for monitored (pulsed) devices \u2014 pair it with DKS or compatible monitored eyes, not cheap two-wire IR beams, or the self-test won't see them. Loops: get your exit loop, safety/reversing loop, and shadow loop planned before you pour, because retrofitting saw-cut loops into an existing apron is a bad afternoon.\n\nFor access control, the 9150 is just the muscle \u2014 it takes dry-contact triggers. Pair it with a DKS 1837/1838 telephone entry or keypad, a 1812 receiver for clickers, or your card system, all landing on the open/common terminals. If the customer wants the gate to auto-open on power loss for fire access, that's the DC Convenience Open / battery-backup option, and note it derates your max gate weight (800 lb on the 1/2 HP, 1,300 lb on the 1 HP). Spec the horsepower to the gate: a big steel picket gate on a windy lot is a 1 HP job even if the length says 1/2 HP would do it \u2014 wind load and roller drag matter more than the number on paper.",commonServicingIssues:["Gate reverses or stops mid-travel \u2014 usually over-tight chain, a binding V-groove roller/track, or debris in the track loading the motor past the reverse sensor threshold. Check chain tension and roll the gate by hand (release the clutch) first.","Won't learn limits / short-cycles \u2014 missing or loose physical gate stops, or the learn cycle got interrupted. Re-establish positive stops, cycle power, let it run the learn passes.","Chain wear and sprocket teeth hooking \u2014 dry chain and misaligned brackets. Keep the chain lubed and the run level; replace a hooked drive sprocket rather than fighting it.","Manual disconnect / clutch left disengaged \u2014 after a manual release the operator won't drive; the slip clutch (or the optional fail-secure release) has to be re-set before it takes commands.","Clutch over-tightened to compensate for a heavy or binding gate \u2014 kills the reversing force and creates an entrapment hazard, and it burns the clutch. Fix the gate; set the clutch back so it reverses at 75 lbf or less.","Monitored safety device faults \u2014 board faults when a photo eye or edge is added that isn't truly monitored, or an eye is misaligned/dirty. Use monitored-type devices and clean the lenses.","Won't run on trigger but master control works \u2014 bad or shorted loop, water in a loop lead, or an access control dry contact not making. Isolate by jumping the input at the board.","No power / dead board after storms \u2014 blown fuse or surge-killed 4602-010 control board on unprotected 115 VAC feeds; put it on a surge protector.","DIP switch change 'didn't take' \u2014 power wasn't cycled; the board only reads DIPs at boot."],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/gate-operators/9150-slide-gate-operator/","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/slide-gate-operator/9150-commercial-and-industrial/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/05/9150-065-M-5-18.pdf","https://www.doorkinggateopeners.com/pdf/9150-spec-sheet.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1089080/Doorking-9150-Series.html?page=24","https://elitegates.net/products/doorking-2600-266-high-voltage-kit","https://www.southwestautomated.com/doorking_9150-385","https://www.gateoperator.net/doorking-9150"],photo:"photo-doorking-9150",schematic:"schematic-doorking-9150"},{id:"hikvision-turret-ip",name:"Hikvision ColorVu IP Turret Camera (DS-2CD23x7G2 series)",category:"Video surveillance",whatItIs:"The ColorVu turret is Hikvision's bread-and-butter fixed-lens IP camera \u2014 the round \"eyeball\" you bolt to a soffit or wall that gives full-color video 24/7, even at night, instead of the old black-and-white IR look. One Cat5e/6 run carries both power and video back to the NVR or PoE switch. On this ColorVu turret line the part number frame stays DS-2CD23_7G2 and the digit that moves is megapixels: DS-2CD2327G2 = 2MP, DS-2CD2347G2 = 4MP, DS-2CD2387G2 = 8MP, with the -L/-LU/-LSU-SL suffix flagging the ColorVu and deterrence options.",specs:["Resolution by model, same body three sensors: DS-2CD2327G2 = 2MP (1920x1080), DS-2CD2347G2 = 4MP (2688x1520), DS-2CD2387G2 = 8MP/4K (3840x2160). Heads up: the DS-2CD2087G2 (the -0087- number) is the BULLET, not the turret \u2014 turrets live on the 23x7 line","Sensor: 1/1.8\" progressive-scan CMOS on the 2MP/4MP; the 8MP steps up to a larger 1/1.2\" sensor. That big sensor is what makes color-at-night work, not just the LED","Lens: fixed (non-motorized) \u2014 common options 2.8mm, 4mm, 6mm. Horizontal FoV shifts with sensor size: 4MP runs ~112 deg (2.8mm) / ~95 deg (4mm) / ~58 deg (6mm); 8MP is a bit tighter (~102 deg at 2.8mm). Pick 2.8 for a wide porch/lot, 4mm to reach a driveway/entry, 6mm for a long approach","Aperture: F1.0 'super aperture' on Pro ColorVu turrets \u2014 gathers max light so the sensor sees color down to 0.0005 lux (F1.0, AGC on), and 0 lux with the white light on","Supplemental light: warm-white LED (~2700-3000K), range up to ~30m/98ft; -LSU/SL variants add a strobe + audible alarm (~95 dB speaker) for active deterrence","PoE: IEEE 802.3af, Class 3, 36-57V DC, ~7.6W max on the -LU. The deterrence -LSU/SL is still 802.3af Class 3 (~8W) \u2014 everything in this line stays inside 802.3af, so any decent af switch runs them; watch total switch wattage, not per-port class","Also runs on 12V DC (+/-25%, ~6.6W, coax barrel plug) if you don't have PoE \u2014 dual-power on the -LU","Weatherproofing: IP67 body, operating -30C to 60C (-22F to 140F). Hikvision does NOT publish an IK vandal rating on these turrets \u2014 if the job needs anti-vandal, spec a vandal dome instead","WDR: 130 dB WDR on G2 for backlit doorways/glare","Onboard: microSD slot up to 256GB for edge recording, built-in mic on -U models, ONVIF Profile S/G/T compliant","AcuSense on G2: on-camera human/vehicle classification to cut false motion alerts and false light triggers","Default IP 192.0.0.64, HTTP 80 / RTSP 554; ships un-activated (no default password \u2014 you set one on first connect)"],proAdvice:"Buy the model by three things: megapixels, lens focal length, and deterrence suffix \u2014 and get all three right BEFORE you roll, because it's a fixed lens. There's no zoom knob on site. If you show up with a 2.8mm and the customer wanted to read plates at the end of a 100-ft driveway, you're driving back for a 6mm or a varifocal. Rule of thumb: 2.8mm for wide area coverage where you just need to see \"a person was here,\" 4mm for a normal entry/driveway at moderate distance, 6mm when you need to identify a face or plate down range. More megapixels is not free \u2014 8MP on a 2.8mm spreads your pixels thin and eats bandwidth and storage; 4MP is the sweet spot for most jobs. And mind the part number: the turret is the 23x7 line (2327/2347/2387), the 2087 is the bullet \u2014 order the wrong body and you're sending it back.\n\nCabling and power: one Cat5e/Cat6 back to a PoE switch or the NVR's built-in PoE ports. This whole line is 802.3af Class 3 \u2014 the -LU pulls about 7.6W, even the -LSU/SL deterrence model is only ~8W \u2014 so you do NOT need 802.3at here; any half-decent af switch runs them. What actually bites you is total power budget on a cheap 8-port switch: even if each port is fine, four cameras can walk up on the switch's combined watt rating, and it'll start browning ports out. Check the total budget number, not just per-port class. Max run is the standard 100m/328ft for Ethernet \u2014 past that you need a PoE extender or a fiber-to-copper media pair, same low-voltage rules as any structured cabling.\n\nThe RJ45 pigtail is the number-one callback waiting to happen. The camera head is IP67, but that flying lead with the connector is NOT \u2014 water wicks down the jacket and into the connector, and you get intermittent dropouts weeks later. Terminate and land that connection inside a weatherproof junction box or the mount base, use the supplied waterproof RJ45 gland/boot, and put a drip loop in the cable so water runs off before the entry point. If you're using the little screw-on waterproof cap Hikvision ships, seat the gasket right or don't bother.\n\nProgramming is fast once you know the flow. The camera ships un-activated with no default password \u2014 first thing you do is run Hikvision's SADP tool (or the NVR's device management page) on the same subnet, find it at 192.0.0.64, and set a strong activation password. If you're plugging into a Hikvision NVR's PoE ports it's basically plug-and-play \u2014 the NVR activates, IPs, and adds the camera automatically on its internal 10.x network. On a third-party NVR you'll add it over ONVIF (Profile S/T); set the ONVIF user separately from the admin login, that trips people up. For remote viewing, bind it to Hik-Connect, but understand the privacy/security tradeoff and turn off UPnP/port-forwarding you don't need.\n\nTwo big gotchas beyond the lens. First, ColorVu is not magic in true zero light \u2014 the color image comes from that warm-white LED kicking on, and customers WILL complain it's a \"spotlight in my window\" or the neighbor hates the glow. Set the smart/supplement-light schedule so the white light only fires on an AcuSense human/vehicle event, or dims after hours, otherwise it burns all night and generates complaints. If they want covert, ColorVu is the wrong tool \u2014 sell them an IR model. Second, and you need to say this out loud to commercial and any government/school/federal-adjacent customer: Hikvision (and Dahua) are on the FCC/NDAA covered list \u2014 banned from federal facilities and many government contracts, and increasingly written out of school and municipal specs. For a house or a private shop it's a great value camera. For anything touching public money, check the spec first or you'll be pulling them back out.",commonServicingIssues:["Intermittent dropouts / camera going offline days-to-weeks after install \u2014 almost always water in the RJ45 pigtail or a marginal PoE run, not a bad camera. Re-terminate the connector in a dry junction box, check the run length/switch watt budget.","Whole PoE switch or NVR resetting cameras \u2014 total PoE power budget exceeded (too many cams stacked on an undersized/cheap switch). These are only ~7.6-8W each, so it's the switch's combined wattage, not port class \u2014 move to a switch with more total PoE headroom.","Foggy or condensation-hazed eyeball at night \u2014 moisture got inside the dome (over-tightened or nicked gasket, or big temp swings). The desiccant is spent; reseat/replace the gasket, don't just wipe it.","Blown-out or washed-out night image / bright center hotspot \u2014 that's the warm-white LED reflecting off a nearby wall, eave, or spiderweb; the light glare also draws bugs that trigger motion. Re-aim off the wall, enable smart supplement light so it only fires on events.","'Color at night' looks grainy/noisy or drops to black-and-white \u2014 not enough ambient light and the supplement light is off or scheduled off, or it's genuinely a non-ColorVu/IR model. Check the light mode is set to White Light or Smart, not IR.","Forgot the activation password / locked out \u2014 no factory default; use the SADP tool export-file password reset (needs the device serial and date, submitted to Hikvision) or the physical reset button under the eyeball, hold ~10-30s while powering up.","Won't show on a third-party NVR \u2014 ONVIF not enabled or the ONVIF user/password wasn't created separately from admin. Enable ONVIF and add a dedicated ONVIF account.","Firmware/Hik-Connect connectivity flapping \u2014 outdated firmware or a stale P2P registration; update firmware from the same G2 branch and re-add to Hik-Connect.","Spiderwebs/insects on the lens ring generating nightly false motion alerts \u2014 physical, not electronic; AcuSense human/vehicle filtering plus periodic cleaning solves it."],sources:["https://www.hikvision.com/content/dam/hikvision/usa/data-sheet/colorvu-ip/Datasheet-for-DS-2CD2347G2-LU.pdf","https://www.hikvision.com/content/dam/hikvision/usa/data-sheet/colorvu-ip/Datasheet-for-DS-2CD2347G2-LSU_SL.pdf","https://www.hikvision.com/content/dam/hikvision/usa/data-sheet/colorvu-ip/DS-2CD2387G2-LUC_Datasheet_V5.5.115_20230418.pdf","https://assets.hikvision.com/prd/public/all/doc/sm000058328/DS-2CD2327G2-LU-C_Datasheet_V5.5.113_20230418.pdf","https://www.hikvision.com/content/dam/hikvision/usa/data-sheet/colorvu-ip/DS-2CD2087G2-LUC_Datasheet_V5.5.115_20230418.pdf","https://www.hikvision.com/en/core-technologies/see-clearer-technology/colorvu/","https://specsheets.dvs.co.uk/DS-2CD2347G2-LU.pdf","https://www.ipsecuritydepot.com/hikvision-ds-2cd2347g2-lsu-sl-4mp-colorvu-ai-wdr-poe-turret-ip-camera/ds-2cd2347g2-lsu-sl/"],photo:"photo-hikvision-turret-ip",schematic:"schematic-hikvision-turret-ip"},{id:"poe-nvr",name:"PoE NVR (Network Video Recorder with built-in PoE)",category:"Video surveillance",whatItIs:"A network video recorder with an integrated PoE switch built into the back panel \u2014 one RJ45 per camera channel that carries both the video data and the power, so each IP camera runs on a single Cat5e/6 home run with no separate switch or power supply. It records, stores, and plays back the streams to local SATA drives, and serves them out to a monitor (HDMI/VGA) and to phone/desktop apps over the LAN or internet. The built-in ports run their own isolated camera subnet, which is what makes these \"plug-and-play.\"",specs:["Channel count: sold by port count \u2014 4ch/4-PoE, 8ch/8-PoE, 16ch/16-PoE are the bread and butter; 32ch and 64ch exist but usually with fewer PoE ports than channels (e.g. 32ch body with 16 PoE)","Total PoE budget: ~50W on a 4-port, ~75-80W on most 8-port, ~150-200W on 16-port. This is the number that bites you \u2014 a Hikvision DS-7604NI-K1/4P is ~50W, the DS-7608NI-K2/8P and NXI-K1/8P are both ~75-80W total, the DS-7616NI-K2/16P is ~200W (the Q2/16P ~150W). ePoE/802.3bt variants run higher","Per-port PoE: 802.3af (Class 0, ~15.4W at the port) and 802.3at (PoE+, up to ~30W) per port; budget is shared across all ports, not per-port guaranteed","Incoming/recording bandwidth: ~80 Mbps in on a small 8ch, 160-256 Mbps on 16ch AI models \u2014 this caps how many high-res streams you can actually record","Storage bays: 1 bay on entry 4/8ch (1U compact), 2 SATA bays typical on 8/16ch, 4 bays on 16/32ch, 8+ bays on rackmount enterprise units","Max HDD per bay: often 6-10TB on older/entry K-series datasheets, 16TB+ on current gen \u2014 always check the datasheet cap for that exact model/revision, not the drive size on the shelf","Camera resolution: up to 8MP (4K) per channel standard now, 12MP on higher tiers; 4K/30fps HDMI decode on the local monitor","ONVIF: Profile S (streaming) and Profile G/T on mainstream units \u2014 this is how you record third-party cameras","Third-party camera cap: many NVRs only let a fraction of channels be non-native ONVIF cams; read the fine print","Compression: H.265/H.265+ standard, roughly halves storage vs H.264","Power draw: the NVR body itself is ~10-15W (Hik K2/8P is rated 15W max without PoE load); the PoE budget is on top of that from the same brick or internal supply"],proAdvice:"The single mistake that turns a clean job into a callback is overrunning the PoE budget. An 8-port NVR rated ~80W total does NOT mean 8 cameras at 10W \u2014 you're already at the ceiling. Turret and bullet cams with IR sit around 5-8W idle but spike to 12-15W the second the IR LEDs and heater kick in at night \u2014 so a rig that looks fine at 2pm drops the far cameras at 2am. Pan-tilt-zoom (PTZ) and units with heaters/blowers can pull 30W+ and need a full 802.3at port; budget them individually and, honestly, power a PTZ from its own midspan injector rather than starving the NVR. Add up worst-case wattage, not nameplate idle, and leave 20% headroom.\n\nWire it as a home run: each camera gets its own Cat5e/6 straight back to a PoE port on the NVR \u2014 no daisy-chaining, no unmanaged switch hanging off one port unless you know exactly what you're doing. PoE over copper is a hard ~100m (328ft) limit including patch leads; past that the camera either won't negotiate power or boots in a loop. For long runs use solid-copper cable, not CCA (copper-clad aluminum) \u2014 CCA eats voltage on the power pairs and is the hidden cause of half the \"camera keeps rebooting\" tickets.\n\nUnderstand the two-network thing before you program it. The built-in PoE ports live on their own internal subnet (Hikvision defaults to 192.168.254.x, Dahua similar) that is separate from the NVR's LAN/uplink NIC. Cameras plugged into those ports get auto-discovered and IP'd by the NVR \u2014 that's the plug-and-play. But it means those cameras are NOT reachable from your regular LAN unless you go into the camera's web page THROUGH the NVR or enable virtual host. If a customer wants to hit a camera directly, or you're mixing in a cam powered from an external switch, you plug that one into the LAN side and add it by IP, not the PoE port.\n\nMixing brands: ONVIF Profile S gets you basic streaming from almost anything, and the newer profiles add better event handling. It works, but you lose the deep integration \u2014 smart events, two-way audio, on-camera analytics often go dark or get flaky over ONVIF. Match the camera brand to the NVR brand when the customer wants the AI features (line crossing, face, ANPR) to actually work. And watch the third-party channel cap \u2014 some NVRs only allow ONVIF on a subset of channels.\n\nStorage math up front so you don't undersell: rough rule at H.265, a 4MP cam continuous-recording eats ~1-2GB/hour, an 8MP ~2-4GB/hour, less on motion-only. Eight 4MP cams recording 24/7 will chew a 4TB drive in roughly a week to two weeks \u2014 spec the drive to the retention the customer actually needs (often 14-30 days) and use a surveillance-rated drive (WD Purple, Seagate SkyHawk), never a desktop Blue/Barracuda. Surveillance drives are tuned for constant write and multi-drive vibration; a desktop drive in a 24/7 recorder dies in months. Also mind the per-bay cap on the datasheet \u2014 an entry K-series may top out at 6-10TB per bay even though 20TB drives exist.",commonServicingIssues:["Cameras dropping at night / rebooting: PoE budget overrun once IR and heaters engage, or CCA cable eating voltage on a long run \u2014 recalc worst-case watts and check for copper-clad cable","'No link' or camera won't power on one port: dead/blown PoE port (surge or short), or a bent pin \u2014 move the cam to a known-good port to isolate NVR vs cable vs camera","HDD failure / recording gaps: #1 service call. Desktop drive used instead of surveillance-rated, or drive simply worn out. Check S.M.A.R.T. in the NVR menu; swap to WD Purple/SkyHawk","Camera not found after swap: replacement cam has a static IP or wrong subnet and won't take the NVR's auto-assignment \u2014 reset cam to DHCP/plug-and-play or manually set it onto the NVR's internal subnet","ONVIF third-party cam won't add or keeps going offline: wrong ONVIF port, admin credentials not matching, or the channel is over the non-native camera cap","Can't see cameras remotely / app won't connect: port forwarding, P2P/cloud (Hik-Connect, Dahua DMSS) not enabled, or DDNS/UID misconfigured \u2014 not a hardware fault 90% of the time","Fan noise or overheating shutdown: dust-clogged fan in a hot closet; NVRs throttle/shut down to protect the drives \u2014 clean the fan and give it airflow","Locked out / forgotten admin password: needs manufacturer reset code tied to device serial and date (Hikvision SADP export), or physical reset jumper on some boards","Firmware mismatch after a camera firmware update: cam and NVR fall out of sync on codec/features \u2014 keep both on compatible firmware, don't blindly update one side","Full-channel image loss after a power event: PoE controller or main supply took a surge hit; put the whole rig on a UPS with surge protection to prevent it"],sources:["https://www.hikvision.com/content/dam/hikvision/products/S000000001/S000000132/S000000007/S000000026/OFR000040/M000000568/Data_Sheet/Datasheet-of-DS-7608NI-K2_8P-NVR-D-or-E_4.71.410_20221026.pdf","https://www.hikvision.com/en/products/IP-Products/Network-Video-Recorders/Pro-Series/ds-7608nxi-k1-8p/","https://www.dipolnet.com/ip_nvr_ip_nvr_hikvision_acusense_ds-7608nxi-k1-8p_8_ch_80_mbps_1_x_sata_vga_hdmi_8_x_poe_h_265_K22071.htm","https://www.getic.com/product/hikvision-nvr-ds-7608ni-k2-8p","Hikvision DS-7604NI-K1/4P NVR product page \u2014 original link retired.","https://cctvplanner.io/nvr/hikvision-ds-7616ni-k2-16p","https://ipcamtalk.com/threads/nvr-with-poe-ports-do-you-have-to-use-them.74079/"],photo:"photo-poe-nvr",schematic:"schematic-poe-nvr"},{id:"ptz-camera",name:"PTZ Camera (Pan-Tilt-Zoom)",category:"Video surveillance",whatItIs:"A motorized surveillance camera that physically pans (side to side), tilts (up and down), and zooms with a real optical lens, all driven remotely from a keyboard, joystick, or VMS. One PTZ covers ground that would take four or five fixed cameras, but it can only look at one spot at a time \u2014 wherever it isn't pointed, you have nothing.",specs:["Optical zoom: commonly 20x-40x on outdoor domes (a few go to ~45x); e.g. 30x, or 40x (4.25-170mm) for fence-line reach \u2014 don't count digital zoom, it's just cropped pixels","Pan: continuous 360-degree endless rotation on most enterprise domes; cheaper units limited to ~340 degrees with a hard stop","Tilt: commonly a ~180-220 degree range; many heads tilt ~15-20 degrees above the horizon (a 'look-up'/flip feature, e.g. Hanwha's -20-degree look-up) so you can watch hillsides, rooftops, or overhead on pole mounts","Pan/tilt speed: fast enterprise domes spec up to ~450 deg/s (Axis Q60/Q6075 rate 0.05-450 deg/s) for slew and auto-tracking, down to a slow ~0.05 deg/s creep for fine framing","Resolution: 2MP/1080p up to 4K (3840x2160); note a lot of long-range zoom PTZs are still 2MP (e.g. Hanwha XNP-6400R) to keep low-light sensitivity up","Presets: ~256 saved PTZ positions (some vendors up to 300); guard tours/sequences (Axis up to 100), patterns, and scans with power-off memory","Preset return accuracy: roughly +/-0.1 degree on quality units, returns to position in ~1 second","IR / night range: ~150-200m (up to ~656 ft) with zoom-linked adaptive IR (Hanwha XNP-6400R rated 200m); some add a white-light floodlight for color at night","Power: PoE+ (802.3at Type 2, ~25.5W to the camera) minimum; heated/long-range-IR domes need Hi-PoE / 60W (802.3bt Type 3/4) or 24VAC \u2014 a plain 802.3af port will NOT run a PTZ","Housing: IP66/IP67, often IK10 vandal-rated dome; wind-load and heater ratings matter for tower/pole mounts","Control protocols: ONVIF Profile S, plus legacy Pelco-D/Pelco-P and VISCA over RS-485 for joystick keyboards","Ethernet run limit: 100m (328 ft) hard max over Cat5e/6; plan for 90m plus patch leads"],proAdvice:"Power is where most PTZ jobs go sideways before you ever touch the lens. A PTZ is not a bullet cam \u2014 the motors, the heater, and long-range IR pull real current. Plan on Hi-PoE (60W) or a proper 24VAC/12VDC supply for anything with a heater or 200m IR, and confirm the switch port and midspan actually deliver that class. Drop a PTZ on a plain 802.3af port and it'll boot, look fine on the bench, then brown out and reboot every time it swings and fires the IR at night. Measure your cable run too: 100m is the hard Ethernet limit, and you want to be at 90m or under with margin, or run fiber with a media converter and local power at the pole.\n\nMounting: get it high enough to see over traffic and heads but low enough that the zoom still resolves faces \u2014 a 30x on a 40-foot pole is great for watching a lot but useless for ID if everything's a bird's-eye dot. Kill vibration. PTZs live on the end of a long lens, so any pole sway, HVAC hum, or loose pendant arm gets multiplied by the zoom and turns telephoto shots into jelly. Use the manufacturer's pendant/wall arm, torque it down, and if it's a windy pole location, factor wind load \u2014 a lighter dome helps here and installs safer.\n\nProgramming is the part clients actually judge you on. Set your presets with a helper standing at each real subject position \u2014 a doorway, a gate, a register \u2014 not in an empty room, so you verify framing AND that autofocus locks instead of hunting. Name presets clearly (Front Gate, Loading Dock) not \"Preset 7.\" Build tours with realistic dwell times; 5-8 seconds per stop is usually right, too fast and the operator gets seasick and autofocus never settles. Run the full tour twice before you leave and watch for drift \u2014 a preset that's a few degrees off on the second lap means the position sensor or gear is worn or the mount flexed.\n\nKnow the fundamental limit and set expectations in writing: a PTZ sees one thing at a time. The classic lawsuit is the incident that happened while the camera was zoomed in on the far corner or parked on a tour leg looking the other way. If continuous coverage of a critical area matters, pair the PTZ with a fixed multisensor or a fixed cam on the key choke point, and let the PTZ be the \"go look closer\" tool. Auto-tracking is a nice demo but it chases blowing leaves, headlights, and the wrong person in a crowd \u2014 treat it as an aid, not a guard.\n\nOn control: if you're integrating into an existing joystick keyboard or old DVR, you're probably talking Pelco-D or Pelco-P over RS-485. Match the protocol, baud rate, and camera address, and mind polarity \u2014 swap DATA+ and DATA- and there's no error, the camera just sits there ignoring you. On a modern VMS it's all ONVIF/IP and you drive it from the mouse, which is where things are headed anyway.",commonServicingIssues:["Preset drift \u2014 camera stops a few degrees off after a while; worn gears/motor or a flexing mount, sometimes fixed by a firmware re-home/recalibration","Focus hunting \u2014 lens racks in and out and never locks, common in low light, on backlit scenes, or when the dome bubble is dirty/scratched","Brownout reboots \u2014 undersized PoE; camera resets when it moves and IR fires; needs Hi-PoE or local power","Dome bubble haze, scratches, and internal condensation \u2014 kills the picture; reseat gaskets, replace desiccant, swap the bubble","Water ingress \u2014 failed housing gasket or unsealed cable gland at the pendant; corrodes the board","Slip-ring / motor wear on continuous-rotation heads \u2014 noisy pan, stalls, or dead spots after years of touring","IR LED failure or uneven IR \u2014 dark night image or hotspot; often a bad IR board or failed cutoff filter","Sticky/stiff pan-tilt after cold weather \u2014 heater failure or hardened grease in the gimbal","Loss of control while video still works \u2014 RS-485 wiring/address/protocol mismatch, or ONVIF/IP PTZ credentials","Sun-scorched sensor \u2014 parked or toured across the sun and burned a permanent line/spot into the imager"],sources:["https://www.axis.com/products/axis-q60-series","https://www.axis.com/products/axis-q6075","https://www.axis.com/dam/public/78/04/2e/datasheet-axis-q6075-ptz-network-camera-en-US-336737.pdf","https://help.axis.com/en-us/axis-q6075","https://www.hanwhavision.com/en/products/camera/network/ptz/xnp-6400rw/","https://www.hanwhavision.com/en/products/camera/network/ptz/xnp-6400/","https://www.surveillance-video.com/camera-xnp-6400r.html","https://versatek.com/high-power-poe-ieee-802-3bt-poe/","https://www.pelco.com/fs/documents/pelco-standard-ptz-operations-manual-en.pdf","https://www.infinitioptics.com/glossary/pelco-d","https://www.onvif.org/specs/srv/ptz/ONVIF-PTZ-Service-Spec.pdf"],photo:"photo-ptz-camera",schematic:"schematic-ptz-camera"},{id:"glassbreak-detector",name:"Glass-break detector",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"An acoustic sensor that listens for the specific two-part signature of a window being smashed: the low-frequency \"thud\" of the impact followed by the high-frequency \"shatter\" of glass letting go. Because it hears the room instead of watching one pane, a single detector covers every window within its range and line of sight, unlike a shock/vibration sensor that has to be stuck on each individual pane. Dual-stage logic (thud AND shatter, in the right order) is what keeps it from screaming every time a dish drops.",specs:["Detection method: acoustic dual-stage \u2014 must hear low-frequency flex/thud plus high-frequency shatter to trip; single-stage 'flex-only' units are cheaper and false more","Range: typically ~25 ft (7.6 m). Honeywell/Resideo FG-1625 is 25 ft omnidirectional, range adjustable, no minimum","Mounting: opposite or adjacent wall, or ceiling \u2014 never the same wall as the glass; needs clear line of sight, roughly 4\u201320 ft from the protected glass","Glass compatibility (FG-1625): plate, tempered, wired, laminated, film-coated, and sealed insulating (dual-pane) glass","Power (FG-1625): 6\u201318 VDC, ~12 mA typical at 12 VDC, 22 mA latched; wired units draw from panel aux, wireless run on lithium cells","Sensitivity: set via internal DIP switches (FG-1625) to match room acoustics/size","Operating temp (FG-1625): 14\xb0 to 122\xb0 F (-10\xb0 to 50\xb0 C)","Form factors: hardwired, wireless (FG-1625RF / DSC PowerG PG9922), and combo units"],proAdvice:"Placement is the whole game. Put it on the wall opposite the window, on an adjacent wall, or on the ceiling \u2014 anywhere it has an unobstructed acoustic path to the glass. Do NOT mount it on the same wall as the window; the sound travels along the wall and the detector can miss it. Keep it out from behind heavy drapes, blinds, and shelving \u2014 soft furnishings absorb the shatter frequency and will dead-spot a window you thought was covered. One well-placed detector in a room can cover three or four windows; that's the reason to use acoustic over shock sensors, which need one puck per pane.\n\nDial in sensitivity to the room. A big room with hard surfaces echoes and wants lower sensitivity; a small carpeted room can run hotter. On the FG-1625 that's the internal DIP switches. After install, TEST it \u2014 don't trust the datasheet range in a real room full of furniture. Use a proper glass-break simulator (Honeywell FG-701 tester or equivalent); clapping and jingling keys won't reliably reproduce the two-stage signature the detector is actually looking for, and you'll either leave a gap or set the thing too hot.\n\nKnow the false-trip sources before the customer calls you at 2 a.m.: breaking dishes and dropped glassware, dogs barking, slamming doors and windows, thunder, and \u2014 the classic \u2014 a TV or speaker near the detector playing a scene with shattering glass. Jingling keys, metal wind chimes, and clinking pots hit the high-frequency band too. Keep the detector away from kitchens, TVs, and speakers, and lean on the dual-stage requirement instead of cranking sensitivity.\n\nWhat to buy: the Honeywell/Resideo FG-1625 is the workhorse hardwired unit and what you'll see on most panels \u2014 reliable, DIP-adjustable, handles every glass type. DSC's line (including the wireless PowerG PG9922 for their PowerSeries Neo/Pro panels) and Bosch acoustic detectors (DS1100-series) are the other names you'll encounter. Match the detector to the panel ecosystem: don't put a DSC PowerG sensor on a Honeywell panel. Where a customer has one specific vulnerable door-light or fixed pane, a shock sensor on the glass is a legitimate cheaper alternative \u2014 but for whole-room window coverage, acoustic wins.",commonServicingIssues:["False alarms from a TV/speaker or kitchen too close to the detector \u2014 relocate or drop sensitivity, don't just crank it","Dead spots from drapes, blinds, or furniture blocking line of sight to the glass \u2014 sound gets absorbed and the window isn't really covered","Sensitivity set too low for a large/echoey room, so a real break doesn't trip; verify with a proper glass-break tester, not a hand clap","Wireless units (FG-1625RF, DSC PowerG): low-battery and supervision faults \u2014 check lithium cells and RF signal at range","Mounted on the same wall as the glass \u2014 a wiring/placement mistake that kills reliability","Sealed insulating (dual-pane) or laminated/security glass can dampen the shatter signature \u2014 confirm the model is rated for that glass and test it"],sources:["https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/wired-sensors/glass-break-wired/0-001-027-01-fg-1625-glassbreak-detector-0-001-027-01/","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-fg1625","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/when-should-i-use-shock-sensors-vs-glass-break-detectors","https://www.security.org/home-security-systems/glass-break-sensor/","https://www.safewise.com/home-security-faq/how-glass-break-detectors-work/"],photo:"photo-glassbreak-detector",schematic:"schematic-glassbreak-detector"},{id:"door-armor-kit",name:"Door reinforcement kit (jamb/strike armor)",category:"Door reinforcement",whatItIs:"A bolt-on steel kit that fixes the real weak point on a residential door: not the lock, but the softwood jamb and the flimsy strike plate the deadbolt throws into. Kicking a door in doesn't break the lock \u2014 it splits the jamb where three-quarter-inch pine is held by half-inch screws, so these kits wrap the strike area, the door edge, and the hinges in gauge steel anchored by long screws that reach the wall stud. Installs over the existing frame in about 30 minutes with hand tools; no need to remove trim or replace the door.",specs:["Door Armor MAX / EZ Armor (Armor Concepts): 46\" jamb shield, two door-edge/lock shields, two hinge shields \u2014 16-gauge galvannealed steel, powder-coated","Door Armor screws: 17 \xd7 3.5\" hardened Torx-drive screws plus 4 \xd7 2.5\", with a hardened Torx drill bit included","StrikeMaster II Pro: 1/8\"-thick two-piece steel, adds ~48\" of reinforcement, fits 1-3/4\" doors, spaced for 5-1/2\"\u20136\" between deadbolt and knob, right/left swing","Long screws are the whole point: 3\"+ screws bite the framing stud behind the jamb instead of just the trim","Install: over existing frame, ~30 min, no trim removal; door must be shimmed/aligned first","Finishes: white powder coat (paintable), plus bronze/other on some kits","Coverage: strike/deadbolt area, door edge around the lock, and hinge side \u2014 a full kit does all three","Grades: DIY 16-gauge (Door Armor/EZ Armor) vs heavier 1/8\" plate (StrikeMaster II Pro) for higher security"],proAdvice:"The failure mode you're fixing: a standard strike plate is held by two 1/2\"\u20133/4\" screws into pine trim, and the deadbolt only penetrates the soft jamb. One good kick splits the jamb and the door swings open with the lock still locked. The fix is spreading force over a long steel plate and driving screws all the way into the framing stud. If you take nothing else from the kit, the single highest-value move is swapping the deadbolt's stock screws for 3\" screws into the stud \u2014 but the full jamb shield is what actually stops the split.\n\nInstall notes: hang and align the door first. If the door sags or the reveal is uneven, the steel just locks in a bad fit and the deadbolt won't throw clean. Pre-drill every hole with the supplied bit \u2014 hardened 3.5\" screws will cam out, snap, or wander in hardwood framing if you dry-drive them, and a snapped screw head flush in the jamb is a bad afternoon. Use the included Torx bit and go slow on the last turn so you seat, not strip. Check that the deadbolt and latch still line up through the new steel strike before you call it done.\n\nGotchas: these kits assume a wood-frame door in a wood jamb \u2014 they don't do much for steel or fiberglass doors in metal frames, or for double/French doors without the specific French-door variant. They armor the door; they do nothing for a window sidelight next to the lock or a hollow-core door itself. And a reinforced jamb around a $15 builder-grade deadbolt is half a job \u2014 pair it with a Grade 1 or solid Grade 2 deadbolt with a 1\" throw. On out-swing doors the hinge side and exposed pins matter more, so the hinge shields and set-screw/security-pin hinges earn their keep.\n\nWhat to buy: for most residential kick-in jobs, Armor Concepts Door Armor MAX (same product family sold as EZ Armor at big-box) is the go-to \u2014 16-gauge, complete jamb/lock/hinge coverage, easy install. Where the customer wants heavier and the budget's there, StrikeMaster II Pro's 1/8\" plate is a step up in gauge. Both are DIY-grade home security, not UL-rated commercial door hardware \u2014 for a commercial opening you're specifying a proper security strike and a Grade 1 mortise/cylindrical setup, not a bolt-on kit.",commonServicingIssues:["Snapped or stripped 3.5\" screws from not pre-drilling into hard framing \u2014 pull, re-drill, and re-drive","Door not aligned before install, so the deadbolt binds or won't throw clean through the new steel strike","Kit installed but deadbolt is still cheap/short-throw \u2014 reinforced jamb around a weak bolt is only half the fix","Wrong application: mounted on a metal or fiberglass door/frame, or a double/French door without the French-door kit","Hinge side left stock on an out-swing door \u2014 exposed pins and short hinge screws remain the weak point","Screws hitting the trim/casing instead of the stud because the jamb sits proud of the framing \u2014 longer screws or shims needed"],sources:["Door Armor single-door security kit (MAX / Mini / Plus) product listing, doorarmor.com \u2014 original link retired.","https://www.amazon.com/Door-Armor-MAX-Complete-Reinforcement/dp/B00E9ZGWH2","https://asafehome.net/PAGES/Safe-Homes-International-StrikeMaster-II-Pro.html","https://suretyhome.com/product/strikemaster-ii-pro-door-frame-reinforcement/","https://mapleleaflocksmith.com/armor-concepts-door-armor-a-review/"],photo:"photo-door-armor-kit",schematic:"schematic-door-armor-kit"},{id:"donjo-conversion-plates",name:"Don-Jo Mortise-Conversion & Wrap Plates",category:"Door reinforcement",whatItIs:"Don-Jo's steel conversion and wrap-around plates are the retrofit locksmith's scar-cover kit \u2014 the parts that let you pull a butchered mortise or an oversized keypad lock off a door and hang a standard cylindrical lockset without the door looking like it lost a fight. Three pieces do most of the work: the CV-86 edge conversion plate turns an old '86' mortise edge cutout into a clean '161' cylindrical latch prep; the 14-CW wrap-around plates (two per door, one per face) cover the racetrack cutout and orphaned cylinder holes a Simplex or Trilogy leaves behind; and a flat filler / blank strike plate closes the old frame strike mortise when the new ASA strike doesn't reach it. All ship prime-coat and paintable so you rattle-can them to the door color on site. This is the hardware that makes a same-day mortise-to-cylindrical conversion look factory instead of field-butchered.",specs:["CV-86 edge conversion plate: 8\" x 1-1/4\", 1/4\" steel \u2014 converts a mortise '86' edge cutout to accept a standard '161' cylindrical latch. The standard CV-86 carries the latch prep 3/8\" OFF-CENTER of the plate; the CV-86-C is centered. Confirm which one you have and orient it before you bore.","14-CW wrap-around plate: ~5\" x 14\", 22-gauge stainless, precision-punched 2-1/8\" hole at 2-3/4\" backset \u2014 covers the oversized cutout and mounting-hole scars left by a Kaba Simplex 1000 or an Alarm Lock Trilogy when you drop back to a cylindrical lever. Two per door, one per face.","Wrap plates are door-thickness- AND backset-specific: the common 14-CW / 14-2-CW is cut for a 1-3/4\" door at 2-3/4\" backset; a 1-3/8\" door needs the matching thinner-door part. Confirm thickness and backset BEFORE ordering or the wrap won't cover the cutout.","Flat filler / blank strike plate: closes the old frame-side strike mortise (euro or oversized) when the new ASA strike lip doesn't reach it. Prime coat.","Finishes: prime coat (PC, paintable) standard on the conversion and filler plates; wraps also come in 630 satin stainless, 626, and duranodic. One-year limited warranty.","Not structural armor \u2014 these are cosmetic/adaptive cover plates. For active anti-pry / anti-kick resistance that's a latch-guard or a reinforcement kit (see the Door Armor entry), not a wrap plate."],proAdvice:"Think of these as the difference between a conversion that passes and one that gets you a callback for 'it looks terrible.' The mechanical fix \u2014 pulling the mortise case and hanging a cylindrical lever \u2014 is the easy part; the plates are what hide the evidence, and the whole job lives or dies on ORDERING them right before you ever touch a hole saw.\n\nThe CV-86 is the piece people bore wrong. The standard plate carries the latch prep 3/8\" off the centerline, not centered, so if you slap it on and drill to the middle of the plate you've put your cross-bore in the wrong place and the latch won't line up. Dry-fit it to the edge cutout, find the actual latch centerline the plate gives you, and mark THAT before the hole saw comes out. If the door geometry wants a centered prep, that's the CV-86-C \u2014 order it on purpose, don't fight the offset one.\n\nWraps are the other classic screw-up, and it's always the same one: wrong door thickness. The 14-CW family is punched for a specific door thickness and backset \u2014 the common part is a 1-3/4\" door at 2-3/4\" backset \u2014 and a plate cut for the wrong thickness either won't sit flat or won't cover the cutout you're trying to hide. Measure the door and confirm the backset on the work order before you order, and remember you need two per door, one per face, because the scar shows on both sides. Use the wrap itself as your drilling template so the 2-1/8\" bore lands where the plate fully covers the old racetrack hole \u2014 bore first and dry-fit second and you'll get a crescent of the old cutout peeking out past the plate edge, which is the one thing you can't fix without a bigger plate.\n\nDon't forget the frame. Half the guys convert the door beautifully and leave the old euro strike mortise gaping in the jamb because the new ASA strike doesn't reach it \u2014 that's what the flat filler / blank strike plate is for. Fill it, then verify the latch actually throws into the new strike with the door closed and there's no rub. Finish the whole set the same way: these come prime-coat for a reason, so mask and rattle-can them to the door color before you leave, sweep your shavings with a magnet, and the customer sees a finished door, not a science project.\n\nWhere these earn their keep is the import/OEM mortise or the ripped-out keypad job with no matching parts \u2014 a fiberglass pump shed with a dead euro-pattern case, or a door that had a Simplex 1000 or a Trilogy on it and the client wants to go back to keyed. Reach for the plates when you're COVERING scars and adapting a cutout; reach for a real latch-guard or reinforcement kit when the door has to resist a pry or a kick. They solve different problems and customers conflate them constantly.",commonServicingIssues:["Wrap ordered for the wrong door thickness \u2014 plate won't sit flat or won't cover the cutout. Fix: confirm door thickness (1-3/8\" vs 1-3/4\") AND backset before ordering; the 14-CW is thickness-specific.","Old cutout peeks past the plate edge \u2014 cross-bore was drilled before dry-fitting the wrap as a template. Fix: always template off the wrap, then bore; a peek-through can only be hidden by a larger plate.","CV-86 latch won't line up \u2014 bored to the center of the plate instead of the 3/8\" off-center prep. Fix: dry-fit and mark the latch centerline the plate actually gives you, or spec the centered CV-86-C when the door wants centered.","Frame strike cutout left gaping after conversion \u2014 the new ASA strike doesn't reach the old euro mortise. Fix: install the flat filler / blank strike plate on the jamb.","Plates look raw or mismatched on a finished door \u2014 left in prime coat. Fix: mask and paint to the door color on site; that's what the paintable prime coat is for.","Customer expected security reinforcement, got cosmetic covers \u2014 wrap and filler plates don't resist pry or kick. Fix: set expectations and add a latch-guard or reinforcement kit if the door needs real attack resistance."],sources:["https://www.don-jo.com/product/cv-86/","https://www.don-jo.com/product/cv-86-c/","https://www.don-jo.com/product-category/wrap-arounds/","https://www.don-jo.com/wp-content/uploads/WraparoundPlates.pdf","https://www.trudoor.com/products/don-jo-14-s-cw-wrap-around-plate-for-converting-from-simplex-1000-or-alarm-lock-trilogy-to-cylindrical-lock"],schematic:"donjo-conversion"},{id:"alarm-keypad",name:"Alarm Keypad / Touchscreen (Fixed LCD/LED & Touchscreen)",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"This is the arming station \u2014 the human interface to the alarm panel. The important thing to get in your head early: the keypad is a dumb terminal. The brains, the zones, the programming, the communicator all live in the panel can down in the closet. The keypad just displays what the panel tells it and sends your keystrokes back. That's why a bad keypad almost never means a bad system, and why swapping one is usually a five-minute job once you know the bus.\n\nThere are two families you'll touch. Fixed keypads \u2014 a two-line alphanumeric LCD (Honeywell/Resideo VISTA 6160, DSC PowerSeries PK5500) or an older fixed-LED annunciator with zone lights. These hang on the panel's proprietary 4-wire data bus, get an address or a slot, and draw their power right off the panel's aux terminals. Then touchscreens \u2014 DSC PowerSeries Neo HS2TCHP (7\", wired to the Neo Corbus, with a PowerG radio and prox reader built in) and the Qolsys IQ family, where the IQ Panel is itself the all-in-one keypad/panel and the IQ Remote (IQR-PG) is a wireless 7\" secondary keypad that talks back over WiFi or PowerG. That wired-versus-wireless split is the whole ballgame for how you install and troubleshoot one, so nail down which you're standing in front of before you pull a wire.\n\nOn a VISTA the bus is called ECP (also the older \"console\" bus); on DSC PowerSeries it's the Keybus; on Neo it's the Corbus. Same idea \u2014 power plus a data pair \u2014 but the panels are not cross-compatible. A 6160 will never talk to a DSC panel and a PK5500 will never talk to a VISTA. Match the keypad to the panel family, always.",specs:["Honeywell/Resideo 6160 (VISTA) \u2014 4-wire ECP bus: BLACK = ground, RED = +12VDC aux, GREEN = data-in to panel, YELLOW = data-out from panel. 32-character (2 x 16) backlit alpha LCD","6160 current draw \u2014 ~160 mA active (armed LED, backlight, sounder on) down to ~40 mA standby; operating temp 32-122F (0-50C); dims 5-5/16\" H x 7-3/8\" W x 1-3/16\" D","6160 addressing \u2014 ships defaulted to address 31; program to an enabled address (typically 16 for the first keypad, 17-23 for additional) and enable that address in panel fields *190-*196. Every keypad on the bus must have a UNIQUE address","DSC PK5500 (PowerSeries) \u2014 4-wire Keybus: RED = +, BLACK = -, YELLOW = clock, GREEN = data. 32-char LCD, 5 programmable function keys, 1 onboard zone/PGM, wall tamper","PK5500 electrical \u2014 12VDC nominal, ~50 mA standby / ~125 mA max; LEDs: Ready (grn), Armed (red), Trouble (yel), AC (grn)","PK5500 slot assignment \u2014 LCD keypads slot 1-8, set by the 2nd digit of the keypad assignment; PK5500/RFK5500 default to slot 8, and at least one LCD keypad MUST stay in slot 8. Enroll via [*][8][installer code]; broadcast labels to other LCDs with section [998]","DSC HS2TCHP (PowerSeries Neo) \u2014 7\" touchscreen, wired to the Neo Corbus (12VDC), with built-in PowerG transceiver and prox-tag reader, microSD slot. Supports systems up to 128 zones","HS2TCHP current draw \u2014 ~100 mA standby (screen off) / ~160 mA active (regular power) / ~230 mA active in extra-power mode; Corbus run limit ~332 ft (101 m) max, dropping to ~177 ft (54 m) in extra-power mode; temp 32-120F (0-49C), up to 93% RH non-condensing","Qolsys IQ Remote (IQR-PG) \u2014 7\" 1024x600 LCD touchscreen, Android-based, wireless secondary keypad for IQ Panel 4/5 and IQ Pro. Connects over WiFi (802.11 b/g/n/ac dual-band 2.4/5 GHz) OR PowerG (912-915 MHz) \u2014 NOT a bus device","IQR-PG power \u2014 12VDC 1A plug-in adapter, 2600 mAh Li-Poly backup battery, 85 dB speaker; dims 6.1\" x 7.7\" x 1\" (155 x 195 x 27 mm)"],proAdvice:"Get the wiring colors burned into memory per ecosystem, because they don't match and mixing them up is the classic rookie fry. On a VISTA/6160 it's a data-in and a data-out (green and yellow) plus red 12V and black ground \u2014 four wires, home-run or daisy-chained, doesn't much matter to ECP as long as every keypad has its own address. On DSC PowerSeries the Keybus is RED/BLACK for power and YELLOW/GREEN for clock/data \u2014 memorize it as \"red-black power, yellow-green data.\" Neo uses the same four-wire Corbus. Different panels, same physics: power pair plus a data pair. Get red and black backwards and you'll pop the keypad, so meter before you land wires on a live panel.\n\nAddressing versus slotting trips people up because the two big brands do it differently. VISTA uses an address \u2014 each keypad is told a number (16-23 range) AND that number has to be turned on in the panel's *190-*196 fields, or the keypad sits there dark or throwing a bus fault. DSC uses slots 1-8 for LCD keypads, set by the second digit of the keypad's assignment, and here's the one that bites everybody: on a PowerSeries you must leave one LCD keypad in slot 8. Move them all off 8 and the system loses its reference. New keypad out of the box defaults to slot 8, so if you're adding a second one, that's the one you re-slot, not the original. And on either brand, two devices sharing one address/slot gives you garbage \u2014 flicker, \"OC\" or bus-fault messages, unpredictable behavior. Unique every time.\n\nNow the mistake I see most on touchscreen upgrades: power budget. A 6160 sips 40-160 mA. A DSC HS2TCHP pulls up to ~230 mA in extra-power mode, and if you hang three or four touchscreens off a panel's aux output you can blow right past the aux rating and start browning out \u2014 random reboots, \"AC trouble\" that isn't the transformer, keypads dropping off the bus at night when the backlight's up. Do the current math against the panel's aux spec before you promise the customer four shiny screens. When you're over budget, that's what a supervised aux power supply (a supplementary 12V supply with battery) is for \u2014 power the screens off it, keep the data pair on the panel. Same story on long runs: the Corbus tops out around 332 ft in normal mode but only about 177 ft in extra-power mode, and voltage drop on a thin, long home-run will starve a touchscreen even when a fixed LCD on the same wire is happy. 18 AWG minimum, heavier and shorter for the hungry screens.\n\nThe Qolsys world flips your whole approach because the IQ Panel is the panel and the keypad in one slab \u2014 there's no separate arming station to wire. When they want a keypad at the back door too, you're not pulling a data bus, you're adding an IQ Remote and enrolling it wireless over WiFi or PowerG. That means your \"wiring\" job is really a network and RF-range job: check WiFi signal at the mount location, or lean on PowerG's range and encryption if the WiFi is junk. It also means the failure modes are network failures \u2014 an IQ Remote that \"won't connect\" is usually the AP or a channel problem, not a bad wire.\n\nLast thing, on service calls: before you condemn a keypad, prove it's the keypad. Swap it onto a known-good address/slot, or drop a known-good keypad onto the suspect wire. Nine times out of ten a \"dead keypad\" is a nicked data wire, a loose terminal, a staple through the cable in the wall, or a shared-address goof \u2014 not the board. Keypads are cheap to swap and the customer's happy, but if you throw a new one on a shorted bus you've fixed nothing and burned a part.",commonServicingIssues:["Blank or dark keypad / no display \u2014 almost always a bus/power problem, not a dead board: meter for ~12V on red-black at the keypad, check for a nicked or stapled data wire, a loose terminal, or voltage drop on a long thin home-run before condemning the keypad","\"OC,\" \"bus fault,\" flicker, or a keypad that keeps dropping off \u2014 two devices sharing the same address (VISTA) or slot (DSC). Re-address/re-slot so every keypad is unique; remember one DSC LCD must stay in slot 8","New keypad installed but panel won't see it \u2014 on VISTA the address isn't enabled in *190-*196; on DSC the keypad wasn't enrolled ([*][8]) or is on a slot the panel isn't looking at. Enroll/enable, don't just hang it on the bus","Random reboots or \"AC trouble\" with multiple touchscreens \u2014 aux power overloaded. Add up the screen current (up to ~230 mA each on HS2TCHP in extra-power mode) against the panel aux rating; offload the screens to a supervised auxiliary 12V supply","Touchscreen works during day, flakes at night \u2014 backlight/active draw pushes an already marginal aux or a too-long/too-thin Corbus over the edge; shorten the run, go heavier gauge, or add a power supply","Labels wrong or blank on some DSC LCD keypads after programming \u2014 labels weren't broadcast; enter section [998] on the programmed keypad to push labels to all other LCDs on the Keybus","IQ Remote won't connect / drops offline \u2014 this is a WiFi or PowerG range issue, not wiring. Verify AP signal at the mount, check for channel congestion, or switch the Remote to PowerG for range and encryption","Sluggish or dead touch response on an older screen \u2014 dirty/aged digitizer or a firmware bug; clean it, push the current firmware (microSD on the DSC, OTA on the Qolsys) before replacing","Keypad locked out / won't accept codes after too many wrong tries \u2014 panel-side lockout timer, not a fault; wait it out or clear from the panel, don't start swapping hardware"],sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-wire-a-honeywell-6160-keypad-to-my-system","https://alarmrelay.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/Honeywell-6160.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-6160","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-program-a-honeywell-6160-keypad","https://dscbg.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/pk5500-install-manual-en.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1039857/Dsc-Pk5500.html?page=2","https://www.trentsecurity.com/pdf/powerseries-keypad-manual-PK5500-RFK5500-v13.pdf","https://www.alarmax.com/customer/docs/skudocs/dsc-hs2tchp-spec-sheet.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1526220/Dsc-Powerseries-Neo-Hs2tchp.html?page=2","https://www.geoarm.com/hs2tchp-b-dsc-powerg-touchscreen-black-alarm-keypad-with-prox-support.html","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/qolsys-iqr-pg","https://suretyhome.com/product/iq-remote-powerg-secondary-touchscreen/"],photo:"photo-alarm-keypad",schematic:"schematic-alarm-keypad"},{id:"cellular-communicator",name:"Alarm cellular/IP communicator (LTE) \u2014 Alula BAT-Connect / Telguard TG-7 / DSC LE9080 / Napco StarLink",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"This is the little board \u2014 usually its own can, sometimes a plug-in module inside the panel \u2014 that gets the alarm signal from the premises out to the central station over the cell network and/or the customer's internet, instead of the old copper phone line. Every POTS line is dead or dying (VoIP won't reliably pass alarm handshake), so on nearly every service call and every new install you're either adding one of these or replacing an aging one. Two flavors you'll meet: panel-specific radios that snap into a matching board (a DSC LE9080 onto a PowerSeries Pro, a Honeywell/Resideo LTE module into a VISTA), and universal communicators (Alula BAT-Connect, Napco StarLink, Telguard) that bolt onto almost any panel by capturing its dialer, reading its keybus, or taking hardwired zone inputs. \"Dual-path\" means it reports two ways \u2014 cellular plus IP (Ethernet/Wi-Fi) \u2014 and if one dies the other carries the signal and the station gets a trouble. The radio is LTE-M (Cat-M1, aka LTE-M / CAT-M) \u2014 a low-bandwidth, long-reach flavor of LTE built for machines, chosen because it rides the carriers' long-life IoT bands and dodges the sunsets that killed every 2G and 3G alarm radio before it.",specs:["Radio: LTE-M (LTE Cat-M1). This is the whole point \u2014 2G (AT&T Jan 2017) and 3G (AT&T Feb 2022, Verizon Dec 2022) sunsets bricked millions of alarm radios; LTE-M/Cat-M1 rides the carriers' committed IoT bands, so these are the units built to outlive the next sunset. Many now market '5G-ready' but the air interface is still LTE-M","Carrier: locked per SKU, not a swap-any-SIM phone. AT&T units and Verizon units are different part numbers (DSC LE9080-US = AT&T, TL880LECVZ = Verizon; Alula BAT-CONNECT-A = AT&T, BAT-CONNECT-V = Verizon; Telguard TG-7FS-A = AT&T, TG-7FS-V = Verizon). Napco StarLink MAX 2 is the exception \u2014 dual-SIM, one AT&T + one Verizon on the same board, auto-picks the stronger network","SIM: embedded/soldered on most modern units (not a removable card you provision) \u2014 activation is done in the dealer/monitoring portal (Alarm.com, AlulaConnect, Telguard, Napco StarLink), by MEID/IMEI, not by handling a SIM. The account IS the SIM","Paths: single-path cell, or dual-path cell + Ethernet. Alula BAT-Connect is tri-path \u2014 cellular + Ethernet + Wi-Fi. StarLink MAX 2 dual-path = two cell networks, or one cell + Ethernet","Supervision (heartbeat): the check-in that proves the path is alive. Residential/burg typically 24 hr or 60 min; UL commercial burg tightens to ~200 sec; commercial fire (UL 864 / NFPA 72 sole path) requires the 5-minute test \u2014 Telguard TG-7FS offers selectable 60-min and 5-min link supervision to satisfy the AHJ. Miss the window and the station gets a path-fail trouble","Encryption: 128-bit AES over the cellular link (DSC LE-series, and standard across the class)","Panel interface (universal units): dialer/tip-ring capture (reads Contact ID, SIA, 4x2, Pulse, DMP, Radionics off the panel's phone terminals), plus keybus/data integration on supported panels for zone/status, plus onboard hardwired zone inputs. Napco StarLink MAX 2: 4 supervised programmable EOLR zone inputs + 2 Form-C relay outputs + two RJ telco jacks","Panel interface (panel-specific units): DSC LE9080 plugs onto the PowerSeries Pro PCB via its onboard connector \u2014 no dialer needed, it's native (the PowerSeries NEO equivalents are the LE2080 / TL880LE series)","Power: universal units run off the panel's 12V/24V aux (or included plug-in transformer); budget the aux \u2014 an LTE radio pulls a real transmit surge. StarLink MAX rated for 12V or 24V FACP aux","Antenna: internal on most; StarLink MAX uses patented Signal Boost dual-diversity twin antennas and can mount up to ~100 ft from the panel to chase signal. External/remote antenna extension kits (30/50/75/100 ft low-loss LMR cable) exist for metal cans and interior/basement locations","Compatibility (universal): Alula BAT-Connect covers Honeywell/Resideo VISTA, DSC PowerSeries, Interlogix NX/Concord, Napco GEM and most hardwired/hybrid panels; Telguard/Napco are largely panel-agnostic via dialer capture (Contact ID / 4-2)","Interactive services: cellular units are the on-ramp to app control \u2014 Alarm.com (DSC, many), AlulaConnect (Alula), Telguard/Napco portals \u2014 remote arm/disarm, Z-Wave, notifications ride the same radio","Listings: look for UL 864 (commercial fire) and UL 1610/UL 985 as applicable; commercial fire units (Telguard TG-7FS, StarLink MAX 2 Fire) are red-can, the burg units are not fire-rated \u2014 don't cross them"],proAdvice:"First rule, and it's the one that saves the callback: signal is everything, and you cannot judge it by your phone's bars. Before you screw the can to the wall, power the unit where it's going to live and read the actual signal on the LEDs or in the app \u2014 RSSI/dBm, not \"it connected.\" These panels love to end up in the worst RF spot in the building: a basement mechanical room, a metal utility closet, inside a steel can behind a breaker panel. A unit that shows two solid bars on the bench and one flickering bar in the closet will report fine on a quiet day and drop the one signal that matters when the siren's blaring and the customer's on hold. If the number's marginal, you have three moves \u2014 relocate the whole can, mount a remote/external antenna and route the coax up out of the metal (Napco's StarLink will sit up to ~100 ft from the panel for exactly this reason), or switch carriers to the one that's strong at that address. Do this at install, not on the trouble ticket six weeks later.\n\nGet the carrier right for the site, not for your habit. AT&T and Verizon coverage flips street by street, and the unit is locked to its SKU \u2014 an AT&T LE9080 will not talk on Verizon, period, you'd swap the whole radio. So check coverage for that specific address before you order. When a site is genuinely marginal on both, or it's a high-value/commercial account where a path failure is a real liability, that's your case for the dual-SIM StarLink MAX 2 \u2014 two networks on one board, it rides whichever is stronger, and you've bought yourself a hedge. Cheaper accounts, pick the winning single carrier and move on.\n\nUnderstand what \"dual-path\" actually buys and how it fails. Cell + IP isn't for speed, it's so the station learns instantly when a path dies \u2014 cut the internet, the cell heartbeat still checks in and the station gets an IP-fail trouble, and vice versa. But the supervision interval is a setting, and it's where techs get burned. Residential defaults to a 24-hour or 60-minute check-in; that's fine for burg but it is NOT fire. Commercial fire under UL 864 / NFPA 72 as a sole communication path demands the 5-minute test \u2014 Telguard's TG-7FS and the StarLink/DSC fire units let you select it, but somebody has to actually set it and the AHJ may want to see it fail-and-report on the walk-test. Set it wrong on a fire job and you fail inspection, or worse, you've got a code violation on a life-safety system with your name on the ticket.\n\nActivation is portal work, not SIM-swapping \u2014 the SIM is soldered down on modern units. You register the radio by its MEID/IMEI in the monitoring platform (Alarm.com, AlulaConnect, Telguard, Napco StarLink), the central station links it to the account, and only then does it report. Two things bite here: the account has to be created and the radio linked before it'll pass a real signal, so coordinate with the monitoring company before you leave \u2014 do a live signal test to the station and get verbal confirmation they received burg, fire, and a trouble, don't assume. And on a takeover, the old communicator's account has to be released by the losing dealer or the radio's stuck; sort that out before the truck roll, not on the ladder.\n\nLast, power and terminations. An LTE radio draws a real surge when it transmits, and universal units run off the panel's aux \u2014 the same rail already feeding keypads, motions, and the RF receiver. Overload it and you get a radio that reboots or drops the network exactly when the bell fires and sags the rail. Budget the aux current or drop in a supervised auxiliary supply for the comm. On dialer-capture units, land tip/ring on the panel's phone terminals AND set the panel to actually dial (Contact ID/SIA) \u2014 a shocking number of \"it's not reporting\" calls are a panel that was never told to communicate, or a communicator wired to a dead dialer output. And keep the antenna lead away from the transformer and the bell wiring; you'll chase phantom signal complaints that are really coupled noise.",commonServicingIssues:["'No signal' / 'Not reporting' but the unit's powered = poor RF at the mounting location. Read actual RSSI/dBm at the unit, not phone bars; relocate the can, add a remote antenna, or switch to the stronger carrier. Marginal signal is the #1 cause of intermittent comm trouble","Communicator installed but account never provisioned or radio never linked in the portal \u2014 it'll power up and look alive but the station gets nothing. Confirm activation (MEID/IMEI) and do a live signal test before leaving","Takeover stalled: old dealer never released the radio/account, so it won't activate on the new platform. Release has to happen upstream \u2014 handle before the truck roll","Path-fail troubles at the station = wrong supervision interval or a genuinely dropped path. Verify the heartbeat setting matches the application (24hr/60min burg vs 5-min fire), then check which path is failing","Panel not actually dialing on a dialer-capture unit \u2014 panel comm was left disabled, or reporting format/account number is blank/wrong. The communicator can only forward what the panel dials; program the panel to communicate (Contact ID/SIA)","Aux power overload: LTE transmit surge on an already-loaded 12V aux rail makes the radio reboot or drop network when the siren fires. Move it to a supervised auxiliary supply","Wrong carrier SKU for the site (ordered AT&T, address is a Verizon hole, or vice versa) \u2014 you can't reprovision it to the other network; swap the radio for the correct-carrier part number","Crossed a burg unit onto a fire job \u2014 burg communicators aren't UL 864 listed and won't pass fire inspection. Use the fire-rated red-can variant (TG-7FS, StarLink MAX 2 Fire)","Ethernet path down on a dual-path unit after a router/ISP change (new gateway, DHCP, or the customer unplugged it) \u2014 cell still reports so it's easy to miss until the IP-fail trouble; re-seat/reconfigure the LAN side","Firmware/network drift on older LTE-M units \u2014 remote OTA update via the dealer portal (AlulaConnect, Alarm.com) usually fixes odd check-in behavior before you roll a truck","Antenna coax pinched, kinked, or run alongside the transformer/bell wiring on a remote-antenna install \u2014 shows as unexplained signal drop; re-route away from power and re-seat the connector"],sources:["https://www.dsc.com/index.php?n=products&o=view&id=2659","https://shop.ssandsi.com/powerseries-pro-lte-alarm-communicator-at-t-sim-le9080-us/","https://shop.ssandsi.com/powerseries-pro-neo-lte-internet-dual-path-communicator/","https://alula.com/communicators/bat-connect/","https://www.geoarm.com/bat-connect-v-alula-universal-tri-path-verizon-alarm-communicator.html","https://napcosecurity.com/starlink/starlinkmax","https://napcosecurity.com/media/pdfs/StarLink-MAX2-Fire-AHJ-Resource-Guide.pdf","https://www.telguard.com/product/tg-7fs","https://prod-edam.honeywell.com/content/dam/honeywell-edam/hbt/en-us/documents/literature-and-specs/engineering-specifications/TG7FS-Series-Engineering-Specs.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/documents/dsc-tl880lt-tl880le-dual-path-communicator-install-guide"],photo:"photo-cellular-communicator",schematic:"schematic-cellular-communicator"},{id:"dual-tech-motion",name:"Dual-Tech Motion Detector (PIR + Microwave) \u2014 Bosch Blue Line Gen2 TriTech & Honeywell DUAL TEC",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"A dual-tech motion is two different sensors in one housing \u2014 a passive infrared (PIR) element that reads body heat crossing its lens zones, and an active microwave (Doppler radar) transceiver that bounces X-band RF off the room and watches for a frequency shift when something moves. The trick is the logic between them: the detector only declares an alarm when BOTH technologies trip inside the same short time window (AND logic). That's the whole reason the thing exists. A PIR alone falses on sunlight, HVAC blowing warm air, and heat off a register; a microwave alone falses on movement it sees straight through the drywall \u2014 people in the next room, a ceiling fan, cars past a window \u2014 because RF doesn't stop at a wall the way heat does. Those two failure modes almost never fire at the same instant, so requiring the two technologies to agree kills the nuisance trips while still catching a real intruder who's both warm AND moving. The two names you'll hang most: Bosch's Blue Line Gen2 TriTech (ISC-BDL2 family \u2014 \"Tri\" for PIR + microwave + the signal processing that ties them together) and Honeywell/Resideo's DUAL TEC line (DT8035/DT8035V residential, DT8016 series for commercial with anti-mask). You spec dual-tech for the tough rooms \u2014 sunrooms, warehouses, spaces with heat sources, high ceilings, big west-facing glass \u2014 where a plain PIR would false all day. You do NOT default to it everywhere: it costs more, draws more current (the microwave transmits continuously), and the AND requirement slightly lowers catch sensitivity, so placement discipline matters more, not less.",specs:["Detection logic: AND \u2014 the PIR channel AND the microwave channel must both detect motion within a common time window before the alarm relay trips. This is what cuts false alarms; it also means either channel going blind (masked PIR, dead microwave) suppresses the alarm, which is why anti-mask matters on high-security jobs","Microwave: X-band Doppler radar. Bosch Blue Line Gen2 TriTech = 10.525 GHz on US and China models; European 'GE' models run a 10.510-10.580 GHz band and the French 'HE' variant 10.570-10.610 GHz (there is no 10.588 GHz UK spec \u2014 that was wrong). Honeywell DUAL TEC = X-band (~10.5 GHz). Microwave is active RF and passes through drywall/glass \u2014 that's the placement trap","PIR: passive infrared, segmented Fresnel (Bosch) or mirror optics (Honeywell DT8016) with a dedicated look-down zone; reads heat only, penetrates nothing","Coverage \u2014 Bosch ISC-BDL2-W12G / WP12G: 12 m x 12 m (40 ft x 40 ft) wall-to-wall. Short-range ISC-BDL2-WP6G: 6 m x 6 m (20 ft x 20 ft)","Coverage \u2014 Honeywell DT8035/DT8035V: ~40 ft x 56 ft. DT8016 commercial: ~12 m (40 ft) at a 2.3 m (7'6\") mount; the 16 m AF5 cert sheets list microwave range out to 16 m x 22 m","Mounting height \u2014 Bosch: 2.2 m to 2.75 m (7.5 ft to 9 ft), no coverage adjustment needed at any height in that band. Honeywell DT8035: 6 ft 9 in to 8 ft 9 in, optimal 7 ft 6 in (2.3 m)","Pet immunity \u2014 Bosch -WP (pet-friendly) models: installer-selectable on/off, immune to one or two pets up to 45 kg (100 lb), or numerous small animals. Non-pet -W models have identical catch performance with the feature off","Pet immunity \u2014 Honeywell DT8035: up to 100 lb (45 kg), toggled by DIP switch, BUT the look-down zone must be disabled for pet immunity to work. The commercial DT8016 mirror-optics models (including the AF anti-mask variants) ARE also pet-rated \u2014 Honeywell rates them to distinguish human intruders from dogs up to 45 kg (100 lb)","Power: 9 VDC to 15 VDC. Bosch draws ~10 mA at 12 VDC (microwave transmitting). Budget more aux current than a plain PIR on your panel math","Alarm output \u2014 Bosch: solid-state, supervised, Form A normally-closed (NC) contacts, rated <=100 mA / 25 VDC / 2.5 W, <20 ohm. Honeywell DT8035/DT8016: Form A relay, 30 mA at 25 VDC, ~22 ohm max. Built-in/selectable EOL resistors on both lines","Tamper: cover tamper standard; wall (back) tamper on most. Honeywell 'A' variants (DT8016AF4/AF5) add active anti-mask \u2014 they detect an attempt to spray, block, or blind the sensor and signal a trouble","White-light immunity: Honeywell DT8016 ~10,000 lux typical; DT8035 ~6,500 lux; Bosch uses a sealed optical chamber + bug guard. Both run Dynamic/adaptive temperature compensation to hold catch performance as room temp approaches body temp","Operating temperature \u2014 Bosch US 'G' models: -20 C to +55 C (-4 F to +131 F); European 'GE' models -10 C to +55 C. UL/C-UL-listed operation is rated 0 C to +49 C (+32 F to +120 F) \u2014 the listing caps the top end, it isn't open-ended","Signal processing: Bosch = First Step Processing + Microwave Noise Adaptive Processing (rejects repetitive microwave sources) + Dynamic Temperature Compensation. Honeywell = DualCore signal processing","Listings: Bosch ISC-BDL2 to UL 639 (US) / cUL / FCC / CE / CCC. Honeywell DT8016 to EN50131-2-4 Grade 2/3, Environmental Class II, NF&A2P, PD6662; NA DUAL TEC models UL-listed","Mounts: Bosch B335-3 swivel low-profile wall bracket (+10 to -20 deg vertical, +/-25 deg horizontal), B328 gimbal, and a swivel ceiling bracket. Corner/flat wall mount native on both lines","Walk-test LED (disable-able after setup on Bosch); externally visible alarm LED can be turned off so it doesn't advertise the sensor's aim"],proAdvice:"Understand the AND logic before you sell it or troubleshoot it, because everything about placing a dual-tech follows from it. The alarm only fires when PIR and microwave agree within a short window. That's a false-alarm killer, but it flips the placement rules versus a plain PIR. On a PIR you aim so traffic CROSSES the lens beams. Microwave Doppler is the opposite \u2014 it's most sensitive to motion coming toward or away from the head (radial motion changes the reflected frequency; motion straight across the beam barely shifts it). A good dual-tech is engineered so the two patterns overlap and cover each other's weak axis, but you still want to mount it so an intruder's likely path has both some cross-motion for the PIR and some toward/away component for the microwave. Corner-mount looking diagonally across the room they must enter through, or cover the pinch point they can't avoid, and you satisfy both.\n\nNow the trap that catches everybody new to microwave: RF goes through walls, PIR does not. The microwave beam does not stop at the sheetrock \u2014 it'll see the customer in the next room, someone on the stairs, a furnace fan cycling in the closet behind the sensor, traffic through an exterior wall, even a metal-bladed ceiling fan in the field. On a plain microwave that's constant falsing; on a dual-tech those don't alarm because the PIR (which is blind past the wall) doesn't confirm them. Fine \u2014 until you defeat your own logic. Do NOT aim a dual-tech through a big window or a thin partition at a busy area, and do NOT mount it right in front of moving metal (fan, overhead door, HVAC ducting) or a fluorescent/HID fixture, because a strong steady microwave source can hold that channel 'active' \u2014 and then all it takes is one PIR trip from a sunbeam and you get the false you bought dual-tech to avoid. The Bosch Microwave Noise Adaptive Processing exists exactly for repetitive microwave noise, but don't lean on it to fix bad placement.\n\nHeight and the pet story. Bosch made this easy \u2014 7.5 to 9 ft with no pattern adjustment, so mount it level (there's a bubble level built in) and move on. Honeywell DT8035 wants 7 ft 6 in for its 40x56 pattern. On any pet job, two rules: enable pet immunity, AND on the Honeywell you must disable the look-down zone or the immunity won't hold \u2014 that look-down tier is what stares at the floor where the animal lives. Pet immunity on a dual-tech is doing the same bet as a PIR \u2014 animal stays low, weight the detection to the upper zones \u2014 so the microwave doesn't rescue you if the 90-lb dog gets on the couch back or climbs the stairs into the field. Enable the jumper/DIP, physically aim so the pet's normal cruising height stays under the fan, and tell the customer to keep furniture out from under it. Rated 45 kg / 100 lb (Bosch -WP, Honeywell DT8035, and the DT8016 mirror-optics units) assumes that discipline.\n\nWiring and programming, quick and clean. It's a powered four-wire device (two power, two loop) just like a PIR, but budget the extra current \u2014 the microwave transmits continuously, ~10 mA on the Bosch versus a couple mA on a plain PIR \u2014 so don't overload a small panel aux or a long thin run. The output is an energized Form A that opens on alarm (fail-safe: cut power or the wire and it alarms). Both lines have selectable/built-in EOL resistors \u2014 read the jumper block and put the supervision where the panel expects it. Set the sensitivity to the environment, not to max: a big echoey warehouse wants a different setting than a small office, and cranking sensitivity on a dual-tech partly defeats the AND benefit. If the head has anti-mask (Honeywell AF variants), wire the mask/trouble output to a zone or a trouble input if the AHJ or the customer's grade requires it \u2014 that's the feature that catches somebody spraying or taping the lens to blind a channel. On a Vista or any residential panel, program the zone as an interior-follower so it's bypassed on STAY and only live on AWAY \u2014 a motion armed while the family's home walking around is a guaranteed false dispatch. Give the microwave its warm-up after power, walk-test with the LED to confirm your pattern actually covers the entry paths and doesn't shoot through a wall into a busy room, then kill the external LED if the customer doesn't want the sensor advertising its aim.",commonServicingIssues:["Falsing in a room where a plain PIR was fine before: check what the microwave is seeing THROUGH the wall/window it faces \u2014 furnace or bath fan behind it, foot traffic in the next room, cars past an exterior wall, a metal ceiling fan in the field. Re-aim away from the moving/penetrable target or drop microwave sensitivity; do not just crank the PIR","Detector won't catch / slow to alarm: the AND logic isn't getting agreement \u2014 one channel isn't tripping on the intruder's path. Usually microwave starved of toward/away motion (subject walking straight across the beam only) or PIR aimed high past the body. Re-aim for a mix of cross and radial motion, verify mounting height","Pet owner still getting falses with immunity on: on Honeywell the look-down zone was left enabled (must be OFF for pet immunity), or the animal is getting into the upper zones on furniture/stairs. Confirm the DIP/jumper and re-aim so the pet stays under the fan \u2014 not a hardware fault","Zone reads trouble/open at the panel: EOL resistor set wrong on the built-in jumper block, a loose power lead, or aux current sagging on a long/thin run (microwave draws more than a PIR). Meter 9-15 VDC at the head and confirm the selectable EOL matches the panel","Constant tamper or anti-mask trouble (Honeywell AF units): cover not fully seated on the tamper switch, base pulled off the wall, or something too close to the lens reading as a mask attempt (a plant, a sign, cobwebs). Reseat cover, re-anchor to solid backing, clear the lens field","Microwave channel 'stuck active' from a nearby fixture or moving metal: an HID/fluorescent ballast or a rotating fan holds the Doppler channel on, so any stray PIR trip becomes an alarm. Relocate away from the noise source or use the noise-adaptive setting; verify by disabling one channel on the walk test","Blind or hyper on hot days: expected as room temp nears body temp and the PIR's heat delta collapses; dynamic temp compensation handles most of it, but a unit aimed at a register, radiator, or in an unconditioned space will still misbehave \u2014 relocate away from the heat source","Dead unit / no walk-test response: no aux power (blown panel PTC, loose lead) or a shorted run cooked the board \u2014 meter the power terminals before condemning the head. Remember the microwave needs its warm-up after power before it's reliable","Wrong part for the spec: the base -W Bosch (non-P) isn't the pet model \u2014 pet immunity on the Bosch line is -WP only \u2014 so don't promise pet immunity on a non-WP head. The Honeywell DT8016 mirror-optics/AF units DO carry a 45 kg pet rating, so you're covered there. Order the -WP (Bosch), or DT8035 / DT8016 (Honeywell), when there's an animal, and the AF anti-mask variant when the grade/AHJ wants mask supervision"],sources:["https://objects.eanixter.com/PD645534.PDF","https://www.expressalarm.cz/documents/isc-bdl2-wp12ge/cs/v/datasheet_isc_bdl2_wp12ge.pdf","https://www.jmac.com/Bosch_Security_ISC_BDL2_W12G_p/BOSCH-ISC-BDL2-W12G.htm","https://www.jmac.com/Bosch_Security_ISC_BDL2_WP12G_p/BOSCH-ISC-BDL2-WP12G.htm","https://www.a1securitycameras.com/content/product_documents/40677/Bosch-ISC-BDL2-WP12G-Datasheet-A1.pdf","https://www.a1securitycameras.com/content/product_documents/40677/Bosch-ISC-BDL2-WP12G-User-Manual-A1.pdf","https://www.adiglobaldistribution.us/Product/DS-BDL2","https://prod-edam.honeywell.com/content/dam/honeywell-edam/hbt/en-us/documents/literature-and-specs/datasheets/hon-ba-hsc-dt8016af4-en-ds.pdf","https://prod-edam.honeywell.com/content/dam/honeywell-edam/hbt/en-us/documents/literature-and-specs/datasheets/hon-ba-dt8016af5-mf5-data-sheet.pdf","https://www.ifsecglobal.com/intruder-alarms/honeywell-launches-pir-dual-tec-motion-detection-sensors/","https://www.alarmclub.com/dt8035-honeywell-dual-tec-motion-detector.html","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-dt8035v","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/products/honeywell-dt8035-dual-technology-pet-immune-motion-sensor","SBSC product certificate 19-48, Honeywell DT8016F4 dual-tech motion detector \u2014 original link retired."],photo:"photo-dual-tech-motion",schematic:"schematic-dual-tech-motion"},{id:"wireless-receiver",name:"Wireless Sensor Receiver / RF Module (5881EN, RF5132-433, HSM2HOST, IQ Panel SRF)",category:"Alarm systems",whatItIs:"This is the box that lets a hardwired panel \u2014 or an all-in-one panel \u2014 hear wireless sensors. A wireless door contact, PIR, or smoke doesn't talk to the panel directly; it fires a coded RF packet that the receiver picks up, decodes, and hands to the panel as a zone. Two flavors you'll meet: a bolt-on receiver module that mounts to an existing wired panel and rides the keypad data bus (Honeywell/Resideo 5881EN series, DSC RF5132-433, DSC HSM2HOST PowerG transceiver for PowerSeries Neo), and a receiver built right into a modern panel (Qolsys IQ Panel's dual SRF daughtercards, Alarm.com/2GIG boards). The single thing that decides everything is FREQUENCY, and it's brand-locked: Honeywell 5800 sensors run 345 MHz, Interlogix/GE and Qolsys legacy + S-Line run 319.5 MHz, DSC PowerSeries legacy runs 433 MHz, and DSC PowerG is 915 MHz frequency-hopping. Heads up \u2014 the tasking called Honeywell \"319.5,\" that's wrong in the field: 319.5 is Interlogix/GE/Qolsys, Honeywell is 345. A sensor only enrolls into a receiver tuned to its band. There is no universal receiver; if the customer has GE 319.5 sensors and you bolt on a Honeywell 345 receiver, nothing enrolls. That's the whole game.",specs:["Honeywell/Resideo 5881ENH: 345 MHz, receives all non-bidirectional 5800-series devices, effectively unlimited zones (limited by the panel, e.g. VISTA-20P = up to 40 wireless of its 48 zones), ~200 ft nominal RF range, mounts up to the keypad-bus wire limit from the panel on the 4-wire ECP/keypad bus (yellow/green data + red/black power)","Honeywell 5881 family variants: 5881ENL (~8 zone) / 5881ENM (~16 zone) / 5881ENH (unlimited, panel-dependent) / 5881ENHC (maximum-zone commercial listing, tamper) \u2014 same 345 MHz radio, different zone caps and listings","DSC RF5132-433: 433 MHz, 32 wireless zones + 16 wireless keys, dual-antenna diversity design, mounts up to 750 ft (230 m) from panel on 22 AWG Keybus, works with PC585/PC1616/PC1832/PC1864 PowerSeries","DSC HSM2HOST8/HSM2HOST9 (PowerSeries Neo): 915 MHz PowerG transceiver, up to 128 wireless zones + 32 wireless keys + 16 keypads + 16 sirens (panel-dependent), 2-way encrypted","PowerG (915 MHz, ~912\u2013919 band): frequency-hopping spread spectrum (FHSS), 128-bit AES encryption, 2-way/TDMA, up to 2 km (~1.2 mi) line-of-sight range \u2014 4 km with a PowerG repeater \u2014 far beyond 300-series one-way RF","Qolsys IQ Panel 4 built-in dual SRF: a 319.5 MHz card (Interlogix/GE, Qolsys S-Line encrypted, Resolution) plus a PowerG 915 MHz card \u2014 up to 128 wireless zones TOTAL shared across both radios, not 128 per card","Frequency is brand-locked and NOT cross-compatible: 345 = Honeywell 5800 / 319.5 = Interlogix-GE + Qolsys S-Line / 433 = DSC PowerSeries legacy / 915 = DSC PowerG. Bridge only with a frequency-converter module","Supervision: supervised sensors send a periodic check-in (roughly hourly on legacy one-way RF); panel declares a 'loss of supervision' trouble after a programmed window \u2014 typically selectable ~4 h (residential burg) to ~24 h, with fire/CO devices required to report faster. PowerG supervision interval is programmable down to minutes","Enrollment: auto-learn by tripping the sensor (open the cover / trip the reed) so the receiver captures the transmitter's serial/ESN, or manual serial entry; then assign loop number (1-4) and zone type. Each 5800/PowerG device has a unique factory ESN \u2014 no DIP-switch address like old crystal sensors","Power/environment (RF5132-433): 12 VDC off the Keybus, ~40 mA draw, 0\u201350\xb0C operating, ~146 x 105 x 25 mm housing with onboard tamper"],proAdvice:"First thing on any wireless job \u2014 before you quote anything \u2014 find out what frequency the existing sensors are, because that dictates the whole receiver choice. Pop one sensor cover and read the label: a Honeywell 5800-series part number and 345 MHz means you need a 5881EN receiver; a GE/Interlogix or Qolsys sticker means 319.5; a DSC WS4900-series or the old crystal-tuned stuff means 433; PowerG parts (PG9xxx) are 915. Match the receiver to the sensors, never the other way around. If a customer 'wants to add a few wireless doors' to a wired VISTA, you're bolting a 5881ENH on the ECP bus and staying in the 345 world for every sensor after that. Mixing bands means a second receiver or a converter, and that's a red flag on your labor estimate.\n\nMounting and range is where guys get lazy and it bites them. The receiver wants to be high, central, and OUT of the metal can. Do not leave a 5881 or an RF5132 buried inside the panel's steel enclosure sitting on top of the transformer \u2014 the steel shields RF and the transformer throws noise right in the band you're trying to hear. That's the whole point of the 4-wire remote mount: run it out on the keypad bus (Honeywell ECP, DSC Keybus) and put the receiver up on a wall in the middle of the building, antennas vertical and away from ductwork, conduit, and mirrors. RF5132 gives you 750 ft of bus wire to play with and a dual-antenna design \u2014 use it. Rated ranges (200 ft Honeywell, 'up to 2 km' PowerG line-of-sight) are open-air fantasy numbers; through two demising walls and a steel door you lose most of it. Real answer: walk-test every single sensor after enrollment, at the actual mounting spot with the cover on, and read the signal margin. Honeywell shows you good/marginal, PowerG gives you an actual signal-strength number \u2014 aim for margin, not just 'it beeped once.'\n\nEnrollment gotchas. Every modern transmitter carries a unique factory ESN/serial, so you enroll by auto-learn \u2014 put the receiver in learn mode and trip the sensor (open the reed, pull the cover tamper) and it grabs the ID. The one that trips up apprentices is the LOOP number. A single 5800 transmitter can carry multiple loops \u2014 a 5816 door contact has loop 1 for the internal reed and loop 2 for the external terminal, a 5800 combo might have four. Enroll the wrong loop and the zone reads normal forever while the door swings open. Always confirm which loop you actually want and test it. Same discipline on zone type: a wireless smoke or CO has to go in as a fire/supervised-24hr type, not a burg zone, or you've built a life-safety hole.\n\nSupervision is the feature people quietly disable and then regret. A supervised wireless sensor checks in on a schedule; drop the supervision window too long or turn it off and a dead battery or a sensor somebody ripped off the wall looks perfectly fine on the keypad. Leave supervision ON, set the loss window per code and per zone class (fire faster than burg), and educate the customer that wireless means batteries \u2014 every one of those sensors has a coin cell or AA pack that dies in 3\u20137 years and will throw a low-battery trouble first. PowerG is the grown-up option here: 128-bit AES so nobody's replaying or jamming your door contact with a $20 SDR, frequency-hopping so it rides over noise, two-way so the panel confirms delivery and can push settings back to the device, and genuinely long range. If the customer's paying for a serious commercial job and the panel's a Neo or an IQ Panel, spec PowerG and stop fighting one-way 300-series RF.",commonServicingIssues:["Sensor won't enroll at all \u2014 wrong-band receiver for the sensor (345 receiver, 319.5 sensor, etc.); verify the sensor's frequency off its label before blaming the receiver or the battery","Zone shows 'normal' while the door is open \u2014 enrolled the wrong LOOP number on a multi-loop transmitter (e.g. 5816 loop 1 reed vs loop 2 terminals); re-enroll the correct loop and walk-test","Intermittent 'RF loss of supervision' or 'check' troubles \u2014 receiver buried in the metal panel can or next to the transformer, marginal signal, or a distant sensor at the edge of range; remote-mount the receiver on the bus, raise it, reorient antennas, and re-walk-test the offending zone","Random false alarms / a whole system flaky on 433 or 345 \u2014 RF interference or a noisy neighbor device in a crowded one-way band; check the receiver's noise/jam indication, relocate the receiver, or move the customer to encrypted frequency-hopping PowerG","Low-battery troubles cascading a few years in \u2014 normal end-of-life on wireless sensor coin cells / AA packs (typ. 3\u20137 yr); batch-replace, don't chase one at a time, and reset the supervision","Receiver dead / panel reports the module missing \u2014 lost the 4-wire keypad-bus connection (data or power), a bus address conflict, or the receiver's DIP/address not set to what the panel expects; meter the bus and confirm the module address","Supervision turned off by a previous installer \u2014 dead or removed sensors read normal; re-enable supervision and set the loss window per zone class (fire/CO faster than burg)","Enrolled on the wrong zone type \u2014 a wireless smoke or CO learned in as a burglary zone won't behave as 24-hr fire; re-type the zone as supervised fire/life-safety"],sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-5881enh","https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/receivers/wireless-receivers/5881enh-unlimited-zone-light-commercial-fire-wireless-receiver-5881enh/","https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/receivers/wireless-receivers/5881enl-8-zone-light-commercial-fire-wireless-receiver-5881enl/","https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/receivers/wireless-receivers/5881enm-16-zone-light-commercial-fire-wireless-receiver-5881enm/","https://www.resideo.com/us/en/pro/products/security/receivers/wireless-receivers/5881enhc-maximum-zone-commercial-5800-series-receiver-5881enhc/","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-rf5132-433","https://www.geoarm.com/rf5132-433-dsc-wireless-receiver.html","https://www.dsc.com/alarm-security-products/RF5132-433%20-%20PowerSeries%20Wireless%20Receivers/56","https://www.geoarm.com/hsm2host9-dsc-wireless-powerg-host-transceiver-module.html","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-hsm2host9","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/blogs/diy/wireless-alarm-system-technology-showdown","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/will-interlogix-slash-ge-sensors-work-on-a-dsc-impassa","https://www.geoarm.com/iqp4004-qolsys-iq-panel-4-wireless-touchscreen-control-for-att-lte.html","https://nellyssecurity.com/products/qolsys-iq-panel-4-security-and-smart-home-platform-with-improved-dual-srf-powerg-legacy-319-5-mhz-for-qolsys-s-line-and-interlogix-sensors-with-built-in-at-t-lte-4g-iqp4004","https://cdn.thefirepanel.com/docs/dsc/Dsc%20-%20Power832%20Wireless%20Zones%20832%20Rev%20005.pdf"],photo:"photo-wireless-receiver",schematic:"schematic-wireless-receiver"},{id:"transponder-chip",name:"Automotive Transponder Chips (4C / 4D / ID46 / ID48 / Hitag)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"The transponder is the little RFID glass or carbon slug living in the head of the key (or soldered into a remote/smart key PCB) that talks to the immobilizer coil ringed around the ignition lock or the start button. Cut alone, a key turns the cylinder but the car cranks and dies \u2014 the ECU won't fuel it until the antenna reads a valid chip ID over 125 kHz. Every automotive chip falls into a handful of families, and knowing which one you're standing in front of decides whether you clone it in 30 seconds or spend an hour programming to the car. The order of the world, oldest to newest: Texas 4C (fixed-code, no crypto, dead simple), Texas 4D crypto (the DST40 workhorse \u2014 Toyota's \"dot\"/4D67, Ford 4D63, Nissan/Subaru 4D60/4D62, and the DST80 Toyota \"G\" chip that replaced it), Philips/NXP ID46 which is Hitag2 (the single most common chip on the road \u2014 GM, Chrysler/Fiat, Renault, Honda, Nissan, and early BMW CAS2/CAS3), Megamos ID48 (the VAG Crypto chip \u2014 VW/Audi/Seat/Skoda plus Porsche and Volvo), and the modern AES generation: Hitag-AES / ID4A / ID8A (Hyundai/Kia, Subaru) and Toyota's DST-AES \"H\", plus Megamos AES / ID88 (late VAG). Physically it's either a glass ampoule (the classic cylinder you drop in a TPX/clone head) or a carbon/ceramic wedge chip potted onto a circuit board in a flip or smart key. Same silicon, different package.",specs:["Operating frequency: 125 kHz low-frequency, inductively coupled \u2014 the key is passive (no battery for the chip), powered by the immobilizer antenna ring around the lock/start button; range is only a few cm","Texas 4C (ID4C / TP02): fixed-code, read-only ID, NO encryption \u2014 older Ford, Mazda, Toyota; fully cloneable in seconds","Texas 4D DST40: 40-bit crypto challenge-response \u2014 Toyota 4D67 'dot', Ford 4D63, Nissan/Subaru 4D60/4D62, plus 4D68/4D70/4D72 variants; cloneable to a 4D-rated blank","Texas DST80 (Toyota 'G', ~2010-2014): 80-bit crypto \u2014 will NOT copy to a plain 4D blank; needs a DST80-rated clone chip (CN5, T39/ILCO, VVDI/KD super chip) and a tool that runs the 80-bit calc","Texas DST-AES 128 (Toyota 'H', 2014+): 128-bit AES, IDs 4D74/ID74 through 4D75/ID75 \u2014 long treated as program-only; some tools (e.g. MiraClone) now clone select H keys, but most jobs still program to the car","Philips/NXP ID46 = Hitag2 crypto (~48-bit): the most common chip on the road \u2014 PCF7936 (transponder-only) and PCF7941/PCF7945/PCF7961 (transponder + remote in one); GM, Chrysler/Fiat/Jeep/Dodge, Renault, Honda, Nissan, early BMW CAS2/CAS3","Megamos ID48 = Megamos Crypto: the VAG chip \u2014 VW/Audi/Seat/Skoda, plus Porsche and Volvo; needs a dealer-precoded or online-calculated chip before it will learn","Megamos AES / ID88 (VAG MQB, ~2013+): AES crypto \u2014 usually requires online component-security/immo data, not a parking-lot job","Hitag-AES / ID4A / ID8A: AES generation on Hyundai/Kia and Subaru \u2014 program-to-car","Package: glass ampoule (loose cylinder, drops into a key channel / TPX / clone head) OR carbon/ceramic chip soldered to a flip-key or smart-key PCB \u2014 same chip family inside, different physical form","Common bench/clone tools: CN900 / CN900 Mini, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool / Key Tool Max, Tango, MVP Pro, AD900 Pro; universal 'super' clone blanks (Xhorse XT27, KD, VVDI super chip) cover 4C, most DST40, and ID46 on one re-writable blank"],proAdvice:"First thing on any transponder job: figure out which of three buckets the chip lives in, because that decides your whole approach and your price. Bucket one is CLONEABLE \u2014 you read the customer's working key with your machine (CN900, VVDI Key Tool, Xhorse, Tango, MVP Pro) and copy the ID onto a blank clone chip. The car never knows the difference; there's no learning, no OBD, no PIN, and you can do it in the parking lot in a minute. Fixed 4C and DST40 4D (the classic Ford/older-Toyota stuff) are the poster children \u2014 a CN5 or YS-01 or TPX eats those. Bucket two is PRECODE-AND-PROGRAM \u2014 the chip can't just be copied but you can write a specific ID to a blank (PCF7936/ID46, precoded ID48) and then LEARN it to the car through the OBD port. Bucket three is PROGRAM-ONLY \u2014 an AES chip that only exists as a virgin blank; you plug into the car, run an add-key or all-keys-lost routine, and the car marries the chip. The mistake that eats new guys is trying to clone something in bucket three, or reaching for the program tool on something you could've just cloned and skipped the whole immobilizer dance.\n\nCloning is your money-maker when the customer hands you a working key \u2014 spare-key jobs. No security PIN, no risk of bricking the immobilizer, no OBD gremlins. Keep a stock of universal clone/super chips (KD, XT27, VVDI super chip) because one blank in your pocket covers 4C, most DST40, and ID46, and you can re-write it if you mis-key it. Where cloning falls on its face: DST80 (Toyota 'G') needs a DST80-rated clone chip and a tool that actually does the 80-bit calc \u2014 it won't land on a plain 4D blank \u2014 and the AES chips are program-to-car for most of us. Don't promise a clone until you've read the chip and confirmed the family; sniff it first with your tester so you're not standing there explaining why the copy cranks and dies.\n\nToyota is where the letter matters most, and it's an exam favorite. The old 'dot' chip (4D67, DST40) clones fine. The 'G' chip (DST80, roughly 2010-2014) does not clone to a normal 4D \u2014 you need a DST80-rated clone and a capable tool, and plenty of guys just program the G to the car instead. The 'H' chip (DST-AES 128-bit, 2014+) was program-only for years; cloning tools like MiraClone now copy some H keys, but treat that as the exception \u2014 for most jobs it's still program-to-car, and Toyota all-keys-lost on an H car means the 16-minute (sometimes longer) immobilizer reset or a dealer-grade tool. Read the key's chip generation before you quote; a 'quick spare key' on an H-chip Corolla is not quick.\n\nKnow your two big crypto families cold because they map to whole brand groups. ID46 IS Hitag2 \u2014 that's GM, Chrysler/Fiat/Dodge/Jeep, Renault, Honda, Nissan, and early BMW CAS2/CAS3. It's the most common chip you'll touch, and it's generally precode-or-clone friendly with the right tool. ID48 is Megamos Crypto \u2014 that's the VAG world: VW, Audi, Seat, Skoda, plus Porsche and Volvo. ID48 is the one where you need a precoded chip (dealer token or an online calculator/OEM-tool subscription) before it'll learn, and MQB-platform VAG (Megamos AES / ID88, ~2013+) usually wants online component-security data \u2014 that's not a parking-lot job, that's a bring-the-right-subscription job. Don't quote a walk-up AKL on a newer Audi like it's a Chevy.\n\nGlass versus carbon is just packaging, not a different animal \u2014 same chip family inside. Glass is the loose cylinder you drop into a shell, a TPX head, or a clone head; carbon/ceramic is the flat chip soldered to a flip-key or smart-key board. When you order, match both the chip ID (the electronics) AND the package to how the key is built: a proprietary remote head key may need the carbon chip on a board, while a basic transponder key takes a glass slug in the channel. And keep the clone families straight on your shelf \u2014 a Texas clone chip won't emulate a Philips ID, and vice versa; that's why the universal 'super chips' earn their keep. Stock a small board of the fast movers \u2014 4C glass, 4D60/67 clone, ID46 clone, a couple precode PCF7936, and a handful of super chips \u2014 and you turn most spare-key calls into a one-visit fix.",commonServicingIssues:["Cloned key cranks then stalls / immobilizer light stays on: you copied the wrong family or a plain 4D clone onto a DST80 'G' or an AES chip \u2014 verify chip generation with a tester before writing, step up to a DST80-rated clone, or program to the car","'Anti-theft'/immo light flashing with a freshly cut key that has no chip or a dead/wrong chip: key was mechanically cut but never chipped or the transponder cracked \u2014 confirm a live chip is present and reads before blaming the car","ID48/VAG key won't learn \u2014 'programming failed': the ID48 blank isn't precoded; Megamos Crypto needs a dealer-precoded chip or an online-calculated precode before the car will accept it, and MQB (Megamos AES/ID88) needs online component security","Toyota/Lexus all-keys-lost dragging on forever: it's an 'H' (DST-AES) car \u2014 for most tools it's program-only via the 16-minute immobilizer reset; if you quoted it like a G/dot job you're underwater on time (a few clone tools now do some H keys, but don't count on it until you've confirmed support)","Chip reads fine on the bench but car won't recognize it: weak or unplugged immobilizer antenna ring around the ignition lock, or a damaged coil/harness \u2014 the chip and key are fine, the car's reader is the fault; test the coil before re-cutting","Cloned super chip works on one car but a spare won't take: the blank was already written/locked to another ID \u2014 re-initialize or use a fresh writable clone; some clone chips are one-time-write once locked","Glass chip rattles or cracked after being forced into the wrong shell: wrong package for the key channel \u2014 match glass vs carbon and shell size; a cracked glass ampoule is dead, replace it, don't glue it","Program-only AES job fails mid-write and now no key works: interrupted immobilizer session (voltage drop on OBD) can brick the sync \u2014 keep a stable 12V+ maintainer on the OBD during AKL programming, especially on VAG and Hyundai/Kia AES cars","Two chips near the coil (customer's spare taped to the ignition, or two transponder keys on one ring): the reader sees a collision and rejects both \u2014 read/program with only one chip in the field"],sources:["https://www.autolocksmithtraining.com/pages/blog?p=what-is-a-transponder-chip-auto-locksmith-training","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/toyota_transponder_catalog.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2026/05/26/id46-vs-pcf7936-vs-hitag2-transponder-chip/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_signature_transponder","https://tches.iacr.org/index.php/TCHES/article/download/8546/8111/5065","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/t39-dst80-cloneable-80-bit-encrypted-cloning-chip-ilco-22439","https://www.internationalkeysupply.com/products/miraclone-cloning-software-for-toyota-h-keys-dst-aes-128-package-13","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/11360683/programming-and-cloning-automotive-transponder-equipped-keys-remotes-and-fobs","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/cn5-cloneable-transponder-chip-clone-4d-toyota-g-chips-cn900-cn900mini-tango","https://keyless-city.com/blogs/locksmith-101/the-truth-about-transponder-super-chips-a-guide-for-automotive-locksmiths"],photo:"photo-transponder-chip"},{id:"smart-key-fob",name:"Smart Key / Proximity Fob (Push-to-Start)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"This is the passive-entry, push-to-start fob \u2014 the one the customer never takes out of their pocket. It does two radio jobs at once, and understanding the split is the whole game. The car constantly pushes a 125 kHz Low-Frequency (LF) field out of antennas in the door handles, bumper, and cabin. When you touch the handle or press the start button, that LF field wakes the fob and tells it to prove it's near. The fob answers on the high-frequency RF side \u2014 315 MHz on most North American cars, 433.92 MHz on European makes, newer Hondas, and a lot of GM \u2014 with an encrypted rolling-code packet. Car sees a valid answer, unlocks or lets you start. That LF handshake is also how the car confirms the fob is physically inside the cabin before it'll crank, and it's why a dead-battery fob still starts the car when you hold it against the start button or a marked spot on the column: the transponder coil in the fob is passively powered by the car's field, no fob battery needed for that immobilizer function. Every one of these also hides an emergency mechanical blade that slides out of the fob body to open the driver's door (and sometimes turn a hidden ignition slot) when the 12V is flat and the handle won't wake. The fob's coin battery only runs the RF side \u2014 remote lock/unlock range and the passive door-handle wake. Kill that battery and passive entry dies, but the immobilizer transponder and the mechanical blade still get you in and running.",specs:["RF transmit: 315 MHz on most North American Toyota/Honda/Ford/Nissan/GM; 433.92 MHz on European makes, 2018+ Honda/Acura, much of GM, later Hyundai/Kia \u2014 ASK or FSK modulation, encrypted rolling code (verify per VIN, frequency varies)","LF wake/immobilizer channel: 125 kHz Low-Frequency field from vehicle door-handle, bumper, and cabin antennas \u2014 this is the passive-entry and in-cabin-detection path, and it passively powers the fob's transponder coil (no fob battery required)","Transponder chips by make: Toyota/Lexus 8A (DST-AES, board 0020/2110); Honda/Acura ID47 (Hitag-3) then 4A; Ford/Nissan/Hyundai 4A (Hitag-AES); GM leaves the transponder in the vehicle-side module on many platforms","FCC ID is printed on the fob or the internal PCB \u2014 always match it. Common examples: Toyota HYQ14FBA/HYQ14FBC/HYQ14AAB; Honda KR5V2X/KR5T44/KR5TP-4/ACJ932HK1210A; Ford M3N-A2C931426/M3N-A2C931423; GM HYQ4EA/HYQ4ES; Nissan KR5TXN1/KR5TXN7","Battery: CR2032 3V coin is by far the most common; some use CR2025 (thinner) or CR2450 (Ford/GM higher-drain, 4-5 button). Match the number stamped in the holder \u2014 a CR2025 in a 2032 seat gives weak intermittent range","Buttons: 3B (lock/unlock/panic) up to 5-6B adding trunk/hatch, remote start, and power-slider on vans; button count and part number are tied together","Emergency mechanical blade: high-security laser/sidewinder or edge-cut, releases via a slide latch or button on the fob; cuts by code (e.g. Toyota TR47/TOY51, Honda HO01/HON66, Ford HU101) or by decoding the door lock","Programming: OBD-II via aftermarket tools (Autel IM508/IM608, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus, Smart Pro, AutoProPad) for add-a-key and many all-keys-lost jobs; some makes (post-2010 Toyota AKL, GM, FCA) require an online/dealer session through NASTF Secure Data (VSP login) or a factory subscription (Techstream, FDRS, GDS2)","Add-a-key needs one working key present; all-keys-lost pulls a PIN/immobilizer data or runs an EEPROM/ISK read on the module \u2014 Nissan/Infiniti 2013+ use a rolling 20/28-character PIN read from the BCM (newer cars need the hands-free module read via an adapter like the Autel APB131, often with dash removal), and some makes throw a security wait timer (typically 10-30 min)","Working range: roughly 15-50 ft for RF remote functions; LF passive-entry wake is short-range by design, inches to a couple feet at the handle"],proAdvice:"Before you quote a single fob, get the FCC ID and the frequency confirmed for that exact VIN \u2014 not the year, the VIN. The same model in the same year gets sold with both a 315 and a 433 MHz fob depending on trim and build date, and a 433 fob will program to a 315 car all day long and then just never trigger passive entry. The customer thinks you sold them a bad fob; you sold them the wrong radio. Pop a working fob open, read the number off the board, and match board number plus FCC ID, not just the shell shape. Shells are universal, guts are not.\n\nOn the battery: half the \"my remote stopped working\" no-starts I get called for are a dead CR2032, and the tell is that passive entry and remote lock quit but the car still cranks when they hold the fob to the button. That's diagnostic gold \u2014 it tells you the transponder and LF side are fine and it's purely the RF/battery side that's dead. Carry fresh 2032s, 2025s, and 2450s. And when you swap one, handle the new cell by the edges \u2014 skin oil on a coin cell bleeds it down over months and you'll get a comeback.\n\nKnow your programming path before you roll. Add-a-key with one working key is a five-minute OBD job on most makes. All-keys-lost is a different animal: on Toyota post-2010, GM, and a lot of FCA you're not getting it done on the driveway with a cheap tool \u2014 you need an online session through your NASTF VSP credentials or a factory subscription, and you need to budget for a security wait timer. Nissan and Infiniti are a PIN game: pre-2013 is a fixed 4-digit PIN decoded off the BCM number, but 2013-up rolls a 20-plus-character PIN every start \u2014 you pull it live from the BCM, and on newer Sentra/Rogue-era cars you're reading the hands-free module above the glovebox with an adapter like the APB131, which means pulling the dash. Tell the customer that up front so a \"lost key\" isn't a surprise teardown, and get your NASTF Vehicle Security Professional registration handled early if you're going full-time at a shop \u2014 it's the difference between doing AKL work and turning it away.\n\nRelay theft is the thing customers ask about now, so have the real answer. These passive systems are vulnerable to a relay/signal-amplification attack: two thieves, one with a repeater by the house wall picking up the car's 125 kHz LF probe and relaying the fob's RF answer from wherever the keys are sitting inside, one at the car. The car thinks the fob is at the handle. It is not a code hack \u2014 the rolling code is never broken \u2014 it's a range extension, so it only works while the fob is live and transmitting. The fixes that actually work: a Faraday pouch or a steel tin for the keys at night, keeping keys away from the front wall or hall table, and on newer fobs the motion-sensor sleep mode (Toyota, Ford, Mercedes fobs that go dormant when they sit still for a minute \u2014 the fob won't answer a relay if it thinks it's asleep). Check whether the fob supports sleep mode and show them how to confirm it's on; that's a value-add that costs you nothing.\n\nLast thing \u2014 respect the mechanical blade. Every one of these has one, most customers don't know it's there, and it's your bailout for a dead-12V lockout where the handles won't wake. Learn where the hidden door keyhole is on each make (usually under a pry-off cap on the driver's handle) and how the blade releases from the fob, because someday it's you at the door with a flat vehicle battery and no other way in.",commonServicingIssues:["Wrong frequency ordered (315 vs 433 MHz) \u2014 fob programs fine but passive entry and remote never trigger; always verify by FCC ID/board number against the VIN, not the year","Dead or wrong coin battery \u2014 CR2025 seated in a CR2032 holder gives weak, intermittent range; low battery kills passive entry and RF remote while the transponder/mechanical blade still work","All-keys-lost blocked by access requirements \u2014 Nissan/Infiniti 2013+ need a rolling PIN read from the BCM (newer cars via the hands-free module with an APB131-type adapter, often pulling the dash); Toyota/GM/FCA needing an online NASTF or factory subscription session; not a driveway job without the right access","Fob programmed but immobilizer not synced \u2014 car unlocks/beeps but won't crank because the transponder side wasn't registered; RF and transponder are separate steps on some tools","Cracked case / worn buttons letting moisture in \u2014 corrodes the PCB and the button carbon pads; reshell only if the original board and blade transfer cleanly","Water-damaged or washed fob \u2014 LF coil and crystal usually survive, but board corrosion causes dropped range and no-response; often cheaper to replace than chase","Emergency blade not cut or not transferred \u2014 aftermarket fob shipped with an uncut/wrong blade so the mechanical backup won't open the door; cut by code or by decoding the lock","Relay-theft exposure on live fobs \u2014 customer wants a fix; enable motion-sleep mode if the fob supports it and recommend a Faraday pouch, not a fob swap","Aftermarket/clone fob rejected or with short range \u2014 cheap boards use weaker RF output or unsupported chips; use OEM or known-good aftermarket (Autel/Xhorse universal) for reliability","Bricked fob from a bad write or wrong tool profile \u2014 interrupted programming or wrong make/model selection can lock the board; verify tool coverage before starting AKL"],sources:["https://keylessbest.com/how-to-identify-if-your-car-key-is-315mhz-or-433mhz-visual-guide/","https://keylessbest.com/hyq14fba-key-fob-guide-compatibility-programming-battery/","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2012-2019-toyota-smart-key-4b-fcc-hyq14fba","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/toyota_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/honda_transponder_catalog.html","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/id4a-transponder-hitag-aes-type-for-hyundai-nissan-jeep-renault-aes-pcf7939ma","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/09/18/read-nissan-28-digit-pin-with-autel-im608-apb131/","https://mcguirelocksmith.com/locksmith-services/automotive-locksmith/lost-keys-nissan-vehicles/","https://keylessbest.com/chevrolet-key-fob-fcc-id-hyq4es-compatibility-and-programming/","https://www.deepsentinel.com/blogs/signal-extenders-stealing-vehicles/","https://www.compustar.com/blog/how-to-prevent-key-hacks-and-relay-attacks/","https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/DeviceDoc/PKERefDesignMnl.pdf"],photo:"photo-smart-key-fob"},{id:"auto-ignition-lock",name:"Ignition Lock Cylinder (Automotive)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"The keyed mechanical cylinder that lives in the steering column or dash and that the key turns through its positions - LOCK/OFF, ACC(essory), RUN/ON, and the spring-return START. It's the mechanical half of vehicle security. Unlike a house lock, an automotive ignition cylinder runs wafer tumblers, not pins: flat spring-loaded wafers stacked in a rotating plug, cut on one or both sides of the key. Most late-model GM, Ford, and Chrysler cylinders add a sidebar - a spring-loaded bar that drops into a milled groove on the side of the wafers, so the wafers have to sit at the right height AND the sidebar has to clear before the plug will rotate. That's what makes them tougher to pick and impression than a residential pin-tumbler. The nose of the cylinder mates into the steering/column lock housing: turning the plug retracts the steering-column lock pawl and drives a secondary actuator (a rod, rack, or the ignition switch itself) that runs the electrical switch. Wrapped around the cylinder - or around the key collar - is the immobilizer antenna ring (also called the exciter coil or transponder ring), a ~125 kHz induction coil that powers the transponder chip in the key head and reads its ID back to the immobilizer module. Turning the cylinder is only half the job: even a perfectly cut key that spins to START will crank-and-stall, or not crank at all, if that antenna ring doesn't hand the ECU/BCM a valid transponder code. The lock is the mechanical gate; the immobilizer is the electronic gate, and a modern car won't run unless both pass.",specs:["Tumbler type: side-milled wafer tumblers (flat spring-loaded wafers), NOT pins - most GM/Ford/Chrysler add a spring-loaded sidebar for a second shear line","Cut/wafer count: typically 8 or 10 cut - GM 10-cut sidebar (Strattec, mid-'90s+), Ford 8-cut sidebar ('96+) and 10-cut, Chrysler 8-cut Y-series; older GM 6-cut","Key positions: 4 detents - LOCK/OFF, ACC, RUN/ON, START (START is spring-return/momentary)","Depths: GM 10-cut sidebar uses 4 depths (tumblers #1-#4); Ford 8-cut runs 5 depths; wafers sold in depth sets to key to an existing key ('key up')","Immobilizer antenna ring: ~125 kHz low-frequency exciter coil around the cylinder/collar; powers and reads the transponder - part of the immobilizer, not the lock itself","Retention: spring-loaded detent/retaining pin - cylinder must be rotated to a specific position (often RUN or ACC) and the pin depressed through a hole to release","Mounting: column-mount (in the steering column casting) or in-dash/remote-mount depending on platform","Early GM VATS/PassKey ('86-'05): a resistor pellet in the key blade (one of 15 values, #1 dropped after '89, 14 used on '95+ 10-cut) read by two contacts in the cylinder - a mechanical-electrical hybrid, separate from later transponder immobilizers","Cylinder does NOT contain the transponder or store codes - it's purely mechanical; the chip is in the key, the codes live in the immobilizer/BCM/ECU"],proAdvice:"First thing on any no-start or won't-turn: separate the mechanical fault from the electronic one, because they get blamed on each other constantly. Key won't turn, binds, sticks, or falls out - that's the cylinder: worn wafers. Key spins the cylinder fine and the starter cranks but the engine won't run (or dies in 2 seconds) - that's almost always the immobilizer/transponder side, not the lock. A security light flashing steady after crank is the immobilizer telling you it didn't see a valid key. Don't tear a good cylinder out chasing a no-start that's really a dead antenna ring or an unprogrammed transponder.\n\nWorn wafers are the number-one mechanical failure. Steel key against brass/pot-metal wafers, 20 years of turns, and the wafers round off. Symptoms escalate: sticky at cold, needs jiggling, then it'll turn with the key half-inserted, and the classic end stage - the key comes out in the RUN position while the engine's running. That's a worn cylinder, full stop. Same wear also lets the plug drift, so the sidebar rides loose and the thing feels sloppy. When you replace or rebuild, key it to the customer's EXISTING key by reading the wear pattern or decoding, not to the old worn cuts - cut a fresh key to code and rebuild the cylinder to match it, or you're just building the wear back in.\n\nStuck/seized cylinder: before you condemn it, work the steering wheel. A huge share of 'ignition won't turn' calls are just steering-lock bind - wheel torqued against the column lock pawl with the front tires cocked against a curb. Rock the wheel left-right with light key pressure and it frees. If it's truly the cylinder binding, dry lube only - graphite or a Teflon/PTFE dry lube. Never oil or WD-40 an ignition; oil turns graphite and brass dust into grinding paste and you'll be back in a month.\n\nRemoval is position-dependent and platform-specific, so don't force anything. The general drill: get the cylinder to the release position (commonly RUN or ACC), find the retaining-pin hole in the housing, depress the detent pin with a punch or pick, and the cylinder slides out the front. If you've got no working key and it's locked, you rotate the plug with a pick or by picking/impressioning first - you can't pull most of them in the LOCK position. Newer cars bury it: pull the column shrouds, and mind the immobilizer antenna ring and its harness sitting right around the collar - unplug it, don't yank it, a torn exciter coil turns a cylinder job into a no-start.\n\nLast thing, and it's the whole point: the cylinder is only half the security. You can pick it, punch it, or drop in a brand-new one and the car still won't run until the electronic side is satisfied - a transponder key programmed to that immobilizer (or on old GM, the right VATS pellet value / PassKey resistance). When you sell a customer an ignition cylinder replacement, quote the programming too, because a fresh cylinder with an unprogrammed key is a very expensive way to crank an engine that will never start.",commonServicingIssues:["Worn wafers - key sticks/won't turn, or turns with key partly inserted; end stage the key pulls out in RUN with engine running","Seized/binding cylinder often masked by steering-column lock bind (wheel torqued against curb) - rock the wheel before condemning the lock","Key stuck in ACC/won't return, or ignition won't spring back from START - worn detents/springs or a failing cylinder cap","Broken key extraction - snapped blade in the wafers, common on worn cylinders and cheap aftermarket blanks","Immobilizer antenna ring damaged or unplugged during R&R - 'key not detected'/security light, cranks but no-start","Confusing a mechanical no-turn with an electronic no-start - replacing a good cylinder when the fault is the transponder/immobilizer","New cylinder installed without keying to the existing key or without transponder programming - car cranks but won't run","Wrong dry lube - oil/WD-40 gumming graphite and wear dust into paste, accelerating failure","GM VATS/PassKey contacts worn or the in-key resistor pellet reading intermittently on older platforms"],sources:["https://www.clksupplies.com/products/gm-ignition-lock-uncoded-w-wafers-709271","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/gm-702767-pinning-wafer-kit-10-cut-service-by-strattec","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/strattec-703373-ford-8-cut-service-kit","https://transponderisland.com/shop/tik-str-323121-4-strattec-gm-10-cut-sidebar-ignition-tumbler-1-4-323121-323122-323123-323124-4123","https://remotesandkeys.com/blogs/blog/how-to-determine-which-vats-resistor-value","https://mcguirelocksmith.com/service-articles/automotive/vats-pellet-number/","https://www.cars.com/auto-repair/glossary/ignition-lock-cylinder/","https://motoristcare.com/how-to-replace-ignition-lock-cylinder/","https://www.carlock.co/blog/en/2023/11/28/anti-theft-immobilizer-system/"],photo:"photo-auto-ignition-lock",schematic:"schematic-auto-ignition-lock"},{id:"vats-pats",name:"GM VATS/PASS-Key & Ford PATS (SecuriLock) Anti-Theft Systems",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"Two different legacy anti-theft systems that people constantly confuse. GM VATS (Vehicle Anti-Theft System, later branded PASS-Key and PASS-Key II, GM's own name being Personal Automotive Security System) is NOT a transponder system, it's a resistor pellet molded into the key blade. A little black oval on the blade holds one of 15 fixed-value resistors; the ignition lock has two spring contacts that read the resistance, and if the ohms match what the decoder module expects, it enables fuel and start. No radio, no chip, no rolling code, pure electrical resistance. Started on the 1986 Corvette (it was the #1 stolen car), spread to Camaro/Firebird, Caprice, Cadillac and lots of other GM, and ran into the early 2000s. Ford PATS (Passive Anti-Theft System, marketed as SecuriLock) is the real transponder: a glass RFID chip in the plastic key head, starting on 1996 Taurus and Mustang. The ignition ring is an antenna/transceiver that energizes the chip, the chip answers with its coded ID, and the PCM (or a separate theft module on early cars) verifies it before allowing start. Bottom line: VATS you measure with a meter, PATS you program with a transponder tool. Don't cross the two up.",specs:["GM VATS/PASS-Key: 15 resistor codes, NOT a transponder. Pellet values in ohms - #1:402, #2:523, #3:681, #4:887, #5:1130, #6:1470, #7:1870, #8:2370, #9:3010, #10:3740, #11:4750, #12:6040, #13:7500, #14:9530, #15:11800","GM: original VATS used all 15 codes, but #1 (402 ohm) was dropped around 1988-89; the double-sided VATS keys introduced in 1995 use only codes 2-15, so don't waste time forcing a #1 on a later car","GM: measure the black pellet on the blade with a multimeter (20k ohm / 2k scale), then pick the CLOSEST chart value, your reading won't land exactly on it (usually within 3-5%)","GM PASS-Key II vs original VATS: functionally identical to a locksmith, PASS-Key II just moved the decoder (TDM) into the BCM but still only reads resistance","Ford PATS/SecuriLock: true 125 kHz RFID transponder in the key head, passive (no battery)","Ford transponder generations: 40-bit encryption on vehicles roughly 2010 model year and older, 80-bit from about 2011 up; 80-bit blanks are backward-compatible down to 40-bit vehicles, but a 40-bit blank will NOT work on an 80-bit car","PATS I (1996-1998): separate theft/immobilizer module that talks to the PCM over a dedicated wire (not CAN); PATS II arrived for the 1999 model year and tightened key requirements","Ford onboard no-tool programming (add a spare): requires TWO already-programmed keys in hand - cycle both, then the third takes; have fewer than two and you need a scan tool and a timed relearn (often two ~10-minute cycles). Support and exact steps vary by model/year","Ford security/theft indicator light: flashes/behaves differently by generation, a solid or wrong-flash light is a fast diagnostic tell"],proAdvice:"First thing on any GM up to early-2000s: figure out if you're even dealing with VATS before you cut anything. Look at the blade. See a black oval pellet in the middle of the blade? That's VATS/PASS-Key, resistor, no chip. Pull a known-good key and measure the pellet across the two metal faces with your meter on the 20k scale. It'll read something like 3.2k, you match it to the closest of the 15 values (that's a #9 at 3010). Cut the blank, drop in the matching pellet, done. On a later double-sided VATS key remember only codes 2-15 are in play, #1 was dropped. If you're making a key with no original, you can walk the values with a resistor box/VATS bypass tool at the ignition until the car cranks, that tells you the code. The gotcha: over years the lock contacts and pellet oxidize and the resistance reads high or intermittent, that's a big cause of \"car won't start sometimes\" no-crank complaints. Clean the contacts before you condemn a module.\n\nFord is a different animal, PATS is a transponder, so a meter tells you nothing. You program it, not measure it. If the customer already has two working keys, most Ford onboard procedures let you add a spare with no tool: cycle key one on/off, within the window cycle key two on/off, then insert the new key and it takes. That's why Ford always wanted a minimum of two keys on file. Fewer than two keys, or an all-keys-lost, and you're on a scan tool sitting through timed security cycles (commonly two ten-minute waits) while it relearns, budget the time on the invoice. Watch the theft light: a normal quick flash at key-on is healthy, a solid light or a rapid double-flash is the system telling you the key wasn't recognized or the module isn't talking.\n\nMoney/time tip for the truck: VATS jobs are cheap parts (a pellet and a blank) but the value is in decoding fast, carry a pellet assortment and a VATS interrogator. PATS jobs live and die on having the right transponder blank (40 vs 80-bit) and a programmer that covers early separate-module cars, the old ones aren't always in the newer all-in-one tools. Stock 80-bit Ford blanks, they cover the 40-bit cars too.",commonServicingIssues:["GM: intermittent no-start / stall right after start = dirty or worn ignition contacts reading the resistance wrong, or a cracked/oxidized pellet, clean before replacing the module","GM: guessing the wrong pellet value because the meter reading fell between two chart values, always pick the nearest standard value, don't split the difference","GM: a worn VATS decoder module in the column/BCM, expensive, so rule out contacts and pellet first","GM: aftermarket 'VATS bypass' resistor left dangling under the dash from a prior owner, will fight your legit key","Ford: trying to measure a PATS key with a meter, it's a transponder, wasted time","Ford: onboard 2-key programming fails because the customer only has one working key, that method REQUIRES two already-programmed keys","Ford: wrong transponder generation blank (40 vs 80-bit) won't program or won't crank","Ford: early separate-module (PATS I) cars not supported by newer all-in-one programmers, need older tool coverage","Ford: theft light stays solid / rapid-flash after programming = key not learned or module comms fault, not necessarily a bad key","Both: replacing modules before verifying the key itself, the cheapest part (pellet or blank) is the usual culprit"],sources:["https://remotesandkeys.com/blogs/blog/how-to-determine-which-vats-resistor-value","https://mcguirelocksmith.com/service-articles/automotive/vats-pellet-number/","https://www.transpondery.com/vehicle-security-systems/vats-pass-key-description.html","https://www.lockmasters.com/site/pdfs/TT4002%20Vats%20Overview%20Article.pdf","https://www.corvettesalvage.com/c4-to-c5-corvette-vats-key-decoding-guide-1986-to-2004/","https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/understanding-the-ford-pats-passive-anti-theft-system/","https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/ford-chip-key-basics-or-what-blank-key-should-i-buy/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SecuriLock","https://www.blueovaltech.com/tech/patskey.php","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/single-sided-vats-key-4"],photo:"photo-vats-pats",schematic:"schematic-vats-pats"},{id:"auto-door-lock",name:"Automotive Door Lock Cylinder",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"The keyed cylinder that lives in the outside door handle (or the door skin on older sheet-metal doors) and mechanically locks and unlocks the door. Like the ignition, it runs wafer tumblers, not pins - flat spring-loaded wafers that ride in slots in a rotating plug. On older single-sided GM stuff the cuts read on one edge; the high-security systems (GM 10-cut B106/B111, Ford double-sided) are double-bitted so the key reads on both edges. Turn the plug and the tailpiece on the back of the cylinder swings a link rod (the lock rod) that pushes the latch's lock lever to LOCK or UNLOCK. That's the whole job: it's a mechanical override for the door latch. On a lot of vehicles it's a straight backup now - the fob and the actuators do the daily work - but the cylinder is still there for a dead battery, a dead fob, or a lockout. The cylinder seats into the handle casting or the door and is held by a spring retaining clip - usually a C-clip, horseshoe, or E-clip - that snaps into a groove on the back of the housing; the face is a crimped-on cap. The one thing that trips people up: the door lock almost never carries every cut the key has. It holds a subset of the wafers, so you can read the door and get part of the bitting, but not a full ignition key. Later GM sidebar (VATS-era and up) door cylinders use split wafers and a sidebar just like the ignition, which is why picking or impressioning them is harder than a house lock.",specs:["Tumbler type: side-milled wafer tumblers (flat spring-loaded wafers); older GM is single-sided, high-security GM 10-cut and Ford are double-sided so cuts read on both key edges; later GM adds split wafers with a sidebar","Actuation: tailpiece/cam on the rear of the plug drives a link rod (lock rod) to the latch's lock lever - no electrical function in the cylinder itself","Retention in the handle/door: spring retaining clip - C-clip, horseshoe clip, or E-clip - snaps into a groove on the cylinder body; front is a crimped face cap","GM 10-cut: door cylinder holds only a subset of positions - most 97-up and 2001-up hold spaces 3-9, older cars varied (5-10, 6-10, etc.). Ignition needs spaces 1 and 2 the door lacks; the trunk/deck lock carries space 10","Ford 8-cut/10-cut: door locks commonly use cuts 1-6 or 2-7; trunk locks often 3-8 - door and trunk carry different cut ranges","Chrysler: 7-cut and 8-cut wafer systems; door typically a partial read","Depths: GM 10-cut is a 4-depth system; wafer depth steps are shallow, so worn keys drop out of spec fast","Read/decode tools: Original Lishi 2-in-1 (pick + decode in the door), Determinator, EEZ-Reader/Kobra/AccuReader caliper gauges, or scope the wafers with the plug in hand","Service parts: sold coded (to a code/key) or uncoded (rekey to customer key) - ASP and OEM Strattec door cylinders, service packages include cylinder, face cap, dust cover, springs, clips, and lube","Immobilizer: none - the door cylinder is purely mechanical; transponder/immobilizer lives at the ignition, not here"],proAdvice:"Work the cylinder off the vehicle whenever you can. Pull the outside handle or drop the inner door panel, pop the plastic clip off the end of the lock rod so the rod comes free, then get behind the cylinder and spread the retaining clip - the C-clip or horseshoe that holds it in the casting. The cylinder pushes out the front once that clip releases. Trying to decode or rekey it fighting the rod and the return spring in place is how you lose small parts into the bottom of the door.\n\nNow the part every new guy learns the hard way: the door does not hold the whole key. On a GM 10-cut, the door carries a chunk of the cuts - most later stuff is spaces 3-9, older cars varied - but the ignition needs spaces 1 and 2 that the door never had, and the tenth cut lives in the trunk/deck lock. So you can decode the door, cut a key that opens the door all day long, and it still won't turn the ignition. Ford's the same story - door runs cuts 1-6 or 2-7, the trunk runs 3-8. Read the door to get in, but if the customer wants a key that does everything, you still have to read the ignition (or the trunk) to fill in the missing spaces, then combine the cuts into one full-bitted key.\n\nOnce you know it's a partial, decode it like a partial. A Lishi in the door will pick it open and give you the cuts it holds in one shot - that's the fast play on a lockout. No Lishi, pull the cylinder, drop the wafers out, and read depths with a caliper gauge or off a code sheet. Cut what the door gave you, verify it turns, then progress or read the ignition for the rest. Don't guess the missing spaces on a 4-depth system - a wrong depth binds the sidebar and you're back in the door.\n\nSelling it to the customer or spec'ing it to your supplier: call it by vehicle year range and coded-vs-uncoded. Coded means it comes cut to a code or key; uncoded means you rekey it to their existing key so one key runs the whole car. ASP or Strattec, short vs medium body on the old GM stuff - give the supplier the ASP DL number or the Strattec part and the finish (chrome/black) and you'll get the right box the first time.",commonServicingIssues:["Key opens the door but won't turn the ignition - normal, not a fault: the door only carries a subset of the cuts (GM missing spaces 1-2 and the trunk holds 10, Ford door vs trunk ranges). Read the ignition or trunk to fill in the missing spaces before cutting a full key","Cylinder loose or falling into the handle - retaining C-clip/horseshoe clip has popped out of its groove or the face cap crimp let go; reseat or replace the clip and re-crimp/replace the cap","Key turns but the door won't lock/unlock - the tailpiece-to-lock-rod link is disconnected: the plastic clip on the end of the lock rod has popped off or the rod is bent; reconnect or replace the clip","Sluggish or seizing in cold/wet - dirt and old grease packed around shallow wafers; flush and re-lube with a dry PTFE or graphite-safe lube, not heavy oil that gums the wafers","Key sticks, won't fully seat, or is hard to turn - worn wafers or a worn key on a 4-depth system dropping out of spec; rekey the cylinder to a freshly cut key rather than chasing the symptom","Later GM sidebar door cylinder won't pick/impression like a house lock - it's split wafers plus a sidebar; use a keyway-specific Lishi or pull and read the cylinder instead","Cylinder spins freely and does nothing - internal tailpiece/cam sheared or the retainer failed; replace the cylinder (service package) rather than trying to rebuild it"],sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12042138/auto-lock-decoding","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1979-1989-gm-medium-uncoded-door-lock-cylinder-dl1582u-asp-chrome","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1967-1996-gm-short-uncoded-pin-tumbler-door-set-dl1580u-asp","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/gm-10-cut-door-locks-spaces-3-9-aerolock-to-90-b86-1263","http://www.lockmasters.com/site/pdfs/KDGM10INST.pdf","https://www.firgelliauto.com/blogs/mechanisms/wafer-tumbler-lock","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer_tumbler_lock","https://www.key4.com/locks-strattec"],photo:"photo-auto-door-lock"},{id:"auto-trunk-glove-lock",name:"Trunk, Deck, Hatch & Glovebox Locks (Secondary Auto Cylinders)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"These are the secondary keyed cylinders on a vehicle: the trunk/deck-lid lock, the rear hatch lock, and the glovebox lock. Mechanically they are the same animal as the door cylinders \u2014 a wafer-tumbler (disc-tumbler) plug in a housing, turned by the key, rotating a tailpiece that works a pawl, cam, or actuator rod. What sets them apart is duty cycle: a driver beats on the door and ignition every day for twenty years, but the trunk and glovebox get worked a fraction as often. That is exactly why they matter to a locksmith. When a customer rolls up with a chewed-out ignition, no key, and door cylinders half full of road grime and worn wafers, the trunk or glovebox lock is very often the least-worn cylinder on the car \u2014 and the one that still holds a clean, full key code. Pull it, decode it, and you have the bitting to cut a working key for the whole vehicle. On most domestics before automakers split the door/trunk code from the ignition code \u2014 GM, Ford 8-cut, Chrysler \u2014 and on plenty of imports, the glovebox and trunk were keyed to the same code as the doors, so they are the go-to fallback on a lost-key job. They are also a routine repair item in their own right: cylinders seize from corrosion, tailpieces and pawls snap, and the plastic actuator clips age out and crack.",specs:["Mechanism: wafer-tumbler (disc-tumbler) plug, same family as auto door cylinders; typically 5\u20138 wafer positions. Ford 8-cut uses 5 depths across 8 spaces, and most door/deck cylinders carry only 6 of the 8 tumblers","Keying relationship: pre-2000s domestics and many imports keyed trunk + glovebox to the DOOR code (one key does entry, trunk, and glove); later vehicles split ignition code from door/trunk code, and the mechanical secondaries are usually NON-transponder even when the ignition is chipped","Codes: each wafer is stamped 1\u20135 (depth). Cylinders come 'coded' (pinned up, cut keys included, ready to install) or 'uncoded' (loose wafers supplied to build any bitting, e.g. to match the customer's original)","Retention: most mount from behind the panel with a flat U-shaped spring clip (pull the clip down/off to release) OR a single retaining bolt/nut; the tailpiece or a pawl arm on the back drives the latch, often through a plastic actuator-rod clip","Common blanks/keyways: GM B-series (B10/B44/B48 etc.; B44 and B48 are the door/glove secondary keys), Ford H-series (H50/H51/H60/H72, and H75 for the 8-cut), Chrysler Y-series (Y157/Y159); imports use make-specific wafer keyways. Older independents (Nash, Hudson, Packard) ran Briggs & Stratton 5-tumbler wafer locks","Aftermarket examples: Dorman 'Lock - Trunk (Cylinder & Keys)' assemblies (e.g. 989-722 for Toyota Corolla/Matrix 2003\u201308 trunk, 77101 tailgate lock kit for GM S10 Blazer / Jimmy / Bravada), plus Classic Industries / United Pacific reproduction glovebox and trunk assemblies for classics","Decode-in-place tools: Ford push-key / push-button decoders read the deck or door lock directly; Lishi 2-in-1 pick-and-decode tools read many auto keyways without pulling the cylinder"],proAdvice:"Treat the trunk and glovebox as your code reservoir, not just parts to replace. On a no-key car, before you touch the ignition, check whether the vehicle is old enough that the doors, trunk, and glove ran one code. If it is, the glovebox lock is usually the cleanest, least-worn wafer stack on the whole vehicle \u2014 it has been turned a few hundred times in its life, not a hundred thousand. Pull it, decode the wafers, and cut to code. You get a factory-clean cut instead of a progressed key off a sloppy worn ignition. That single habit saves you an hour and gets the customer a key that actually feels right.\n\nKnow how it comes out before you start prying. Nine out of ten of these are held by one of two things: a flat U-shaped spring clip, or a single bolt/nut behind the panel. The clip is the classic \u2014 it slides into slots on the sides of the cylinder housing, narrow end down; you pull it down until the narrow gap clears the housing and the cylinder pushes out the front. Work through the trunk liner or the glovebox back, pop the plastic push-rivets on the liner, and get eyes on the back of the lock. Before the cylinder frees, note how the tailpiece and any actuator rod sit \u2014 those plastic rod clips are brittle and they are the part you will break if you yank blind. Photograph it. Reassembly in the dark behind a deck lid is where the callbacks come from.\n\nWhen you sell a replacement, set the keyed-alike expectation up front. A parts-store or repro glovebox cylinder is not going to match the customer's existing key out of the box \u2014 near impossible. Either you rekey the new cylinder to their code, or they carry a second key for the glove. If you want it to match, disassemble the old cylinder, read the wafer sequence side to side, and load the same numeric sequence into the new one; a lot of uncoded replacements ship with a wafer assortment for exactly this. That is a fifteen-minute bench job that turns a 'here's your extra key' complaint into a one-key car and a happy customer.\n\nCorrosion and duty are the two failure stories. Trunk and hatch cylinders live at the back of the car, low, catching every bit of weather and salt spray \u2014 they seize, the return spring rusts, the tailpiece corrodes off. Gloveboxes fail the opposite way: pried by owners who lost the key, so you see broken cam ears and cracked housings more than wear. Read which one you've got before you quote. A seized-from-rust deck lock might come back with penetrant and a fresh tailpiece; a pried glovebox usually needs the whole assembly.",commonServicingIssues:["Seized/stiff from corrosion \u2014 trunk and hatch cylinders sit low and catch weather/road salt; the wafers gum up and the return spring rusts. Flush, lube with a dry PTFE or graphite (not oil, which holds grit), and exercise; heavily rusted ones get replaced","Broken tailpiece or pawl \u2014 the arm/tailpiece on the back that drives the latch snaps or corrodes off, so the key turns but nothing actuates; often a cheaper fix than a full cylinder if the part is available","Cracked plastic actuator-rod clip \u2014 the brittle clip joining the cylinder to the latch rod ages and breaks (or gets broken during removal); key turns freely with no latch movement","Glovebox lock pried/forced \u2014 broken cam ears and cracked housing from owners jimmying it after losing the key; usually needs the complete assembly, not just a cylinder","Replacement not keyed alike \u2014 aftermarket/repro cylinders won't match the customer's key; rekey by transferring the original wafer sequence, or the customer carries a separate key","Lost-key decode gone wrong \u2014 decoding a worn cylinder gives a sloppy cut; when the car is single-code era, pull the least-used cylinder (glovebox first, then trunk) for a cleaner read","Removal damage \u2014 yanking the cylinder before releasing the spring clip/retaining bolt or before freeing the rod bends linkage and breaks clips; get eyes on the back first"],sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12042138/auto-lock-decoding","https://locksmithledger.com/article/10228044/a-look-back-at-vintage-auto-locks","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21293956/testing-the-push-key-decoder","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer_tumbler_lock","https://unitedlocksmith.net/blog/glove-compartment-lock-replacement-tips-for-fixing-a-broken-glove-box-lock","https://www.yourmechanic.com/article/how-to-replace-a-trunk-lock-cylinder-by-michael-france","https://www.partcatalog.com/blogs/body/how-to-replace-your-cars-trunk-lock-cylinder","https://www.dormanproducts.com/p-91096-989-722.aspx","https://www.dormanproducts.com/p-24739-77101.aspx","https://michaelhyde.com/products/ford-8-cut-tumbler-pack-a-for-some-ignitions-door-trunk-locks","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/top-10-automotive-key-blanks-in-the-usa-locksmithing-101","https://www.clksupplies.com/collections/automotive-locks-accessories"]},{id:"remote-head-key",name:"Remote Head Key (RHK)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"An all-in-one automotive key that combines three functions in a single molded plastic head: a cut mechanical blade to turn the ignition/doors, a glass or PCB transponder chip for the immobilizer, and a remote keyless entry (RKE) circuit board with lock/unlock/panic (and sometimes trunk/remote-start) buttons. It replaced the older setup of a separate transponder key plus a detached keyfob. Dominant OEM key style on 2000s-through-mid-2010s domestics (Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep, Ford) and Toyota before proximity/smart keys took over; on GM the integrated style was more often a flip key (e.g. OHT01060512) than a fixed-blade head. You cut the blade to the vehicle, then do a two-step program: onboard/EEPROM for the transponder side and remote-learn mode for the RKE buttons.",specs:["Blade: cut mechanical blade, edge-cut or high-security laser (sidewinder) depending on make \u2014 e.g. Ford H75 edge-cut or HU101 laser, GM B111/B119, Chrysler Y159/Y160/Y157","Transponder chip: varies by platform \u2014 Chrysler PCF7941A / Hitag2 (ID46), GM ID46 (Philips 46 Circle+), Ford 4D63 (80-bit) / PCF7953A, Toyota 4D67/G; fixed (glass) or PCB style","Remote frequency: 315 MHz on most North American domestics and Toyota; 433 MHz on some export/European platforms","Buttons: 3-button (Lock/Unlock/Panic) or 4-button (adds Trunk/Hatch); GM SUVs add Remote Start on 5/6-button versions","Battery: CR2032 3V coin cell (most common); some GM/Ford/Toyota use CR2025 or CR2016","Common FCC IDs: Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep OHT692427AA / OHT692713AA / KOBDT04A; Ford OUCD6000022 / CWTWB1U793; Toyota HYQ12BBY; GM keyless-side OUC60270 / OUC60221 (fob/flip-paired)","OEM P/N examples: Chrysler 68051387, Ford 164-R8046 / 164-R7043, GM 20869056/15913420/20952475","Aftermarket equivalents: Ilco RHK-series, Keyless2Go, Xhorse/KD universal remote heads"],proAdvice:"Identify by the FCC ID stamped on the back of the head first \u2014 it pins down frequency, button map, and usually the chip family faster than guessing off the vehicle. Peel the sticker or pop the battery cover; the ID is molded or printed inside. Cross that against the OEM part number so you order the right blade profile, because the same head shell ships with different keyways (a GM head can be B111 or B119; a Chrysler head can be Y159 or Y160). Cut the blade before you program \u2014 a lot of guys program the chip, then find the mechanical cut is wrong and have to redo the whole thing.\n\nProgramming is two independent jobs and people forget one. The transponder side (immobilizer) either onboards through the ignition PIN sequence or needs a tool touching the CAN/OBD \u2014 GM and Chrysler often onboard with two existing working keys, Ford 4D63 usually wants the 10-minute security wait or a tool for all-keys-lost. The RKE side (buttons) is a separate remote-learn sequence and does nothing for start. If the doors lock/unlock but the car won't crank, your chip didn't take; if it cranks but the buttons are dead, you skipped or fumbled the remote learn. Confirm both before you leave.\n\nOn aftermarket: Ilco RHK and Keyless2Go clones are fine daily drivers and save real money, but the blades are softer than OEM and the button membranes wear faster. For Ford 4D63 and GM ID46, aftermarket chips clone/generate reliably; where I go OEM is anything with a locked or rolling-code quirk. Keep a stock of CR2032s \u2014 half the \"my remote broke\" calls are just a dead coin cell, and a fresh battery plus a re-seat of the board is a five-minute upsell instead of a new key.",commonServicingIssues:["Dead CR2032 coin cell \u2014 most common 'remote stopped working' complaint; buttons die but the key still starts the car since the chip is passive","Cracked head shell / broken blade-to-head joint from being dropped or used as a pry \u2014 transponder and board still good, re-shell into a new remote head key case","Worn button membranes (rubber pads) \u2014 lock/unlock stop registering after years of thumb pressure; common on high-mileage Chrysler OHT and GM heads","Blade wear / mis-cut \u2014 soft aftermarket blades round off and stop turning the ignition; recut or replace blade","Only half-programmed \u2014 chip programmed but remote not learned (car starts, buttons dead) or vice versa; re-run the missed sequence","Water intrusion corroding the PCB and battery contacts \u2014 key left in a pocket through a wash or rain","Transponder de-sync / weak read \u2014 worn ignition antenna ring or a chip that needs re-cloning; intermittent no-start that clears on a second try","Wrong FCC ID / frequency ordered \u2014 315 vs 433 MHz mismatch means the remote never pairs even though the blade and chip are right"],sources:["https://lowratelocksmith.com/car-keys/ford-remote-head-key/","https://www.key4.com/remote-head-key-ford-oucd6000022-164r7043","https://www.key4.com/keyless-entry-remote-gm-ouc60270-20869056","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2004-2016-chrysler-dodge-jeep-4-button-remote-head-key-oht692427aa-rk-chy-oht-4","https://www.bestkeysolution.com/products/2-new-keyless-entry-remote-key-fob-replacement-fits-for-2007-2008-2009-2010-car-toyota-camry-key-fob-with-fccid-hyq12bby-4-button-4d67-chip","https://www.bestkeysolution.com/products/key2u-keyless-entry-remote-control-car-key-fob-replacement-for-oht692427aa-kobdt04a-pack-of-2-key","https://www.jbtools.com/ilco-rhk-chry-3b2-chrysler-3-button-remote-head-key/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/b111-transponder-key-gm","https://certified24hourlocksmith.com/car-key-locksmith-services/remote-head-keys/"],photo:"photo-remote-head-key"},{id:"flip-key",name:"Flip / Switchblade Transponder Key",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A remote-head key where the metal blade folds into the fob body and snaps out at the press of a side release button on a spring-loaded hinge. It combines three things in one shell: the mechanical cut blade, the RF remote board (lock/unlock/trunk), and a transponder chip for the immobilizer. Common OEM on VW/Audi (HU66 blade, Megamos ID48, later Megamos AES on MQB) and 2010s GM (HU100 blade, ID46 PCF7937E/NCF2951E Hitag2). Heavily cloned by aftermarket universal remotes from Xhorse and Keydiy, where you drop in your own blade and chip and program the RF to match the vehicle. Sits between the old separate-remote-plus-key era and modern proximity/smart fobs.",specs:["Form: 2-in-1 remote-head folding key, blade releases via side button on a spring hinge","Frequencies: 315 MHz (NA-market GM and NA VW/Audi) and 433/434 MHz (Euro VW/Audi); universal remotes generate roughly 300-500 MHz","Buttons: 2 to 5 (Lock, Unlock, Trunk/Hatch, Panic, Remote Start)","Blade profiles (separate, swappable): HU66 VW/Audi, HU100 GM, HU101 Ford, HU83/VA2 PSA","Transponder VW/Audi: Megamos ID48 (older 4th-immo), Megamos AES (ID88 / MQB48) on MQB","Transponder GM: ID46 PCF7937E, later NCF2951E (Hitag2)","Aftermarket chip options: Xhorse Super Chip (XT27), Keydiy KD chip, or glass 4C/4D/46/48; Xhorse MQB48 (XT1M) for AES","Battery: CR2032 lithium coin","OEM FCC / PN examples: OHT01060512 with PN 13504199/13504200 (GM), NBG010206T / 5K0837202 variants (VW)","Aftermarket examples: Xhorse XKB501EN / XKB505EN (VW B5 style, wired), Keydiy B01-3 (VW type)","Wired universal = generate on VVDI/KD tool before it transmits; wireless (Xhorse XN, Keydiy B-series) programs over the air and stores a profile","Immobilizer note: MQB VW/Audi (post ~2013) uses Megamos AES, not simply clone-able like 96-bit ID48; add key via tool with online CS calculation"],proAdvice:"Diagnose the shell and the electronics separately, because a flip key fails in two unrelated ways. The most common call is mechanical: the spring hinge weakened or the roll pin behind the blade wore out, so the blade won't snap open, flops loose, or the housing cracked at the hinge after a drop. In a lot of these the board and chip are perfectly good, so pull the electronics and reshell rather than reprogramming from scratch. Keep an assortment of empty flip shells (VW B5, GM 5-button, Xhorse/KD universal) on the truck for exactly this.\n\nBlade swap is the routine job on VW/Audi and aftermarket units. The blade is held by a small roll pin, punch it out, drop in the correct profile (HU66 for VW/Audi, HU100 for GM), then cut it, either by code or by decoding the lock. On uncut aftermarket blades cut to code first, then reassemble; it's miserable to re-pin a blade you cut wrong. Watch blade thickness and hinge fit; a blade that's slightly proud will bind in the fold slot.\n\nOn the electronics, match three things or the key is dead weight: frequency (315 vs 433), the transponder the immobilizer expects, and the remote programming method. For older VW ID48 and GM ID46 you can often clone or onboard-program. The gotcha is MQB: post-2013 VW/Audi run Megamos AES, and you can't just clone an AES key like a 96-bit ID48. You add a key through proper tooling (VVDI2, Key Tool Plus, Autel IM508/608) that reads and calculates the component security (CS) data online, which the tool does itself over its own server, so add-key and even all-keys-lost are aftermarket jobs now, not dealer-only. Where you still need the dealer/ODIS is component protection coding when a module gets swapped, not routine key programming. Don't promise a customer a cheap clone on an MQB car, but don't tell them it's dealer-only either.\n\nFor universals, Xhorse and Keydiy are the workhorses. Wired remotes (XKB501EN, B01-3) are cheapest but you must generate them on a VVDI or KD tool before they'll transmit; wireless versions program over the air and store a profile. Both need you to add your own chip, use an Xhorse Super Chip or KD chip so one blank stock covers dozens of vehicles, generate the chip type on the tool, then program to the car. Stock the VW B5 style heavily, it covers an enormous slice of the VW/Audi fleet and the shells are cheap.",commonServicingIssues:["Weak/broken spring hinge, blade won't snap out or flops loose","Worn blade roll pin causing blade wobble; re-pin or reshell","Cracked housing at the hinge from drops, electronics usually salvageable","Worn rubber button pad / dead buttons, remote board reshell","CR2032 dead or corroded battery contacts, water intrusion","Wrong or uncut blade profile, must cut to code before assembly","Frequency mismatch on universal remotes (315 vs 433)","MQB Megamos AES keys can't be simply cloned, add key via tool with online CS calculation","Wired universal remote not generated on VVDI/KD tool, transmits nothing until generated","Transponder not added or wrong chip type set on aftermarket blank"],sources:["https://gskeyae.com/product/xhorse-xkb501en-wire-remote-vw-b5-type/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/xhorse-vw-volkswagen-b5-type-xkb501en-universal-remote-key-for-vvdi-key-tool-wired-3-buttons-black","https://www.myremotekey.com/products/flip-remote-key-for-chevrolet-buick-id46-hitag2-pcf7937e-315mhz-genuine-circuit-board-oht01060512-gm-13504200","https://gskeyae.com/product/vw-golf-polo-jetta-flip-key-433mhz-5k0837202-oem/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2010-2020-gm-5-button-flip-key-oht01060512-rk-gm-fp5","https://remkeys.com/product/uds-flip-id48-433mhz-5k0837202-aj-keyless-oem-vgrk04-remkeys/","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/xhorse-mqb48-xt1m-megamos-aes-auto-transponder-chip-for-vw-audi-fiat","https://www.capitollocksmithdc.com/blog/vw-amp-audi-key-programming-guide-immo-4-bcm2-mqb-amp-mlb-systems-explained","https://abkeys.com/products/keydiy-kd-universal-remote-key-3buttons-vw-type-b01-3"],photo:"photo-flip-key"},{id:"tibbe-key-lock",name:"Tibbe Key & Lock (Ford/Jaguar Rotary Disc)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A rotary-disc sidebar lock, not a wafer or pin-tumbler setup. The blade is a fat, roughly square/cylindrical bar with six angled flats milled along it (six cuts), and it rotates six spring-loaded discs stacked in the plug. Turn all six discs to their correct angle and a slot lines up across all of them so the sidebar drops in and the plug turns. Miss one disc and the sidebar stays proud and nothing rotates. Blade profile is FO21 (Silca), also cut as JMA FO-6. Designed by the Tibbe company; Ford ran it on door, boot, glovebox and ignition locks roughly 1995-2005 before moving to the HU101 high-security sidewinder around 2005-06. The later Ford-owned Jaguars (S-Type, X-Type, XJ, ~1999-2009) share the same 6-cut FO21 Tibbe. Heads up: the older pre-~1998 Jaguars used a different 8-cut/8-position Tibbe variant with only three depth angles, so not every \"Jaguar Tibbe\" is a 6-cut FO21. Blade is close to symmetrical so on most locks it enters either way up.",specs:["Blade/keyway profile: Silca FO21 (JMA FO-6)","6 rotary discs / 6 cuts along the blade (Ford standard and later Ford-era Jaguar)","Older pre-~1998 Jaguar variant: 8-cut / 8-position with only 3 depth angles \u2014 different blank and pick, don't assume 6-cut on early cats","4 cut depths (angles): #1 = no-cut, doesn't touch the disc; #4 = deepest, nearly flush with the side rib","~4096 theoretical combinations (4^6); usable set is smaller","Every real key has at least one #4 cut (the pick's reference disc)","Cut spacing from bow to tip: 1=.6220in, 2=.5433in, 3=.4646in, 4=.3858in, 5=.3071in, 6=.2283in","Operates door, boot/tailgate, glovebox and ignition locks","Ford Europe (~1995-2005): Fiesta, Focus, Mondeo, Escort, Ka, Puma, Scorpio, Cougar, Courier, Transit, Transit Connect","US Ford Tibbe was mainly Fiesta and Transit Connect","Jaguar (~1999-2009, 6-cut FO21): S-Type, X-Type, XJ, XJR","Blanks/transponder: Silca FO21T3, FO21T7, FO21T17; Ilco FO21T3, FO21T7-SI, cloneable FO21-style; Jet S30FD-PH; Strattec 5920217"],proAdvice:"Don't read this thing like a normal key. There are no wafer depths here, just four angles, and #1 is a no-cut so the disc sits where it started. Your working number is the depth code 1-4 at each of the six positions. Code machines don't decode Tibbe electronically, so you decode it mechanically off the lock or off a good key, then cut to code. Depth 1 is flat (no metal removed), depth 4 is almost down to the side rib, so a worn or chewed original still cuts back to spec because you're cutting to the number, not tracing the wear.\n\nThe Tibbe/Lishi-style pick-decoder is the whole game. It's a barrel that slides over the discs with numbers printed along the top. The method: work through the discs and find the one that resists and won't move, that's always a #4. Load light turning pressure toward unlock on that #4 disc to bind the sidebar, then rotate each remaining disc until it sets. When the plug turns the lock is open and you read the six numbers off the top of the pick, bow to tip. That's your cut code, ready to make a key. Practice on a door lock first, the ignition is stiffer and less forgiving.\n\nWatch direction and entry orientation. Ford doors and ignitions can be handed, and because the blade is near-symmetric people decode a reading upside down and then wonder why the cut key won't turn. Confirm which way the disc numbers run before you commit metal. On ignitions especially, if the lock is worn the discs get sloppy and a fresh key to correct code will actually turn better than the customer's tired original.\n\nConfirm the variant before you pull a blank. Later Ford-era Jaguars (S-Type, X-Type, XJ) are the same 6-cut FO21 as the Fords, but an early pre-'98 Jaguar can be the 8-cut/8-position Tibbe, which is a different blade and a different pick \u2014 grab the wrong one and you'll fight a lock that was never going to read on a 6-cut tool.\n\nCutting: use a proper Tibbe machine or a Tibbe adapter/jaw (e.g. Silca clamp on a Futura/Triax, or an FO21 jaw on a SEC-E9). A make-up key kit with loose 1-4 discs lets you assemble a working key by hand from a decode if you don't have a machine on the van. For anything with a transponder (later Fiesta/Transit Connect, most Jaguars), the mechanical decode only gets the door and ignition to turn, you still have to program or clone the chip, so carry FO21T7/cloneable blanks and don't promise a runner off a decode alone.",commonServicingIssues:["Worn ignition discs go sloppy, the plug turns rough or the key sticks, a fresh key cut to code usually turns better than the customer's original","Decode read upside down because the blade is near-symmetric, cut key won't turn until you flip the code","Confusing depth #1 (no-cut) with a missing position, throwing off the code","Grabbing a 6-cut FO21 blank/pick for an early pre-'98 Jaguar that's actually the 8-cut/8-position variant","Ignition is stiffer than the doors to pick/decode, beginners burn time on the ignition instead of decoding off a door","Sidebar won't drop if even one disc is off, so a single misread number kills the whole key","Transponder cars: mechanical decode alone gets it to turn but it won't start until the chip is programmed or cloned","No electronic code machine reads Tibbe, techs waste time hunting a code lookup that doesn't exist, decode mechanically"],sources:["https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/a-guide-to-the-tibbe-key","https://www.ukbumpkeys.com/blogs/news/75745540-ford-tibbe-lock-pick-and-decoder-how-it-works","https://abkeys.com/products/original-lishi-ford-tibbe-pick-tool-fo21-lishi-pftpd-original-lishi-3519","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/31685889-ford-tibbe-key-blank-information","https://www.carlocksandkeys.com/blog/how-to-decode-your-jaguar-tibbe-key-and-find-your-tibbe-key-code/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12057410/servicing-the-ford-transit-connect"],photo:"photo-tibbe-key-lock"},{id:"sidewinder-laser-key",name:"Sidewinder / Laser (Internal-Cut) Key",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A high-security automotive key whose bitting is milled as a winding channel (track) down the flat face of a thick, square-ish blade instead of notches on the edges. The cuts are internal \u2014 a constant-width groove that snakes up the center at varying depths \u2014 so wafers ride the sidewall of the track, and on 2-track keys the lock reads both faces. Called \"sidewinder,\" \"laser cut,\" or \"internal cut.\" Requires a milling machine with an end-mill cutter (Ilco/Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus, Xhorse Dolphin XP-005/XP-007, Triton, Miracle A9, Silca Futura) \u2014 you cannot cut it on a standard edge-cut clipper/duplicator with a slotter wheel. Common keyways: HU100 (GM), HU101 (Ford/Volvo/JLR), HU66 (VW/Audi/Porsche), HU64 (Mercedes), TOY48 (Toyota/Lexus). It is the opposite of an edge-cut (mechanical, \"tibbe\" excepted) key, which has V- or square-notch cuts along one or both edges cut by a rotary wheel.",specs:["Cut style: internal/track cut \u2014 winding central groove milled into the blade face, not edge notches","Blade: thicker and squarer than edge-cut; typically ~2.5-3.0 mm thick, cut with a 1.5-2.5 mm carbide end-mill (not a slotter wheel)","Cutter/tooling: milling machine required \u2014 Ilco/Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus, Xhorse Dolphin XP-005/XP-007, Triton, Miracle A9/A7, Silca Futura Auto/Pro","HU100 (GM): 8-cut, 4 depths \u2014 GM sidewinder for 2008/2010+ Chevy/GMC/Buick/Cadillac (Malibu, Cruze, Camaro, Enclave, Acadia, SRX) and Saab 2008+; a separate 10-cut HU100 variant exists on some later GM trucks; U-blade emergency insert common on flip/PEPS fobs. NOTE: HU100R is the BMW 2-track variant \u2014 different depths, do not confuse with GM HU100","HU101 (Ford): 10-cut Ford high-security 2011+ (Focus, Fusion, Escape, Edge, Explorer, F-150 select) plus Jaguar 2007+, Land Rover 2005+, Volvo 2005+; common emergency-blade PN 164-R8022","HU66 (VW/Audi): 8-cut, 4 depths/heights, 2-track \u2014 VAG group (Golf, Jetta, Passat, A3, A4) plus Porsche/Bentley/Bugatti \u2014 one of the most common sidewinders; older single-track vs newer 2-track blanks (V.1 and V.3 refer to Lishi tool generations, not internal vs external cut)","HU64 (Mercedes): Mercedes-Benz 2-track laser key (NOT 4-track); older HU64 is a 2-track transponder blade (44-chip era, ~1998-2005); newer/updated HU64 profiles carry forward on later models","TOY48 (Toyota/Lexus): 8-cut laser, single-sided/non-reversible (40-tooth), Camry/Avalon/Lexus ES/GS/IS/RX/LS select years; related Toyota laser profiles (TOY49/G- and H-chip era) share the family","Origin/decode tools: Lishi 2-in-1 pick/decoders exist for all of these (HU100, HU101, HU66 V.3, HU64 V.3/V4, TOY48/TOY2) \u2014 read the lock, no key needed","Cut depths/spaces come from a code series or are decoded off the lock; most are 4-depth systems"],proAdvice:"Identify the profile before you touch a machine. Look at the blade tip and shoulder and count the cuts: GM HU100 is 8-cut, Ford HU101 is 10-cut, so the cut count alone separates the two keyways that beginners confuse most \u2014 but still confirm with a Lishi or a keyway gauge, because cutting a GM code onto an HU101 blank (or vice versa) is the classic rookie scrap. HU66 has a distinct arrow/keyway with the track offset to one side. TOY48 is non-reversible \u2014 it only goes in one way, so flipping the blank in the jaw gives you a mirror-image dead key.\n\nOrigination almost always beats duplication on these. The face track is hard to trace/duplicate cleanly on a machine, and a worn customer key duplicated blade-to-blade carries the wear forward. If the vehicle is on-site, put a Lishi 2-in-1 on the door or ignition, decode it, and cut to code from a fresh blank \u2014 you get a factory-spec key, not a copy of a worn one. Keep your Lishi picks clean and lightly lubed; a dirty HU66 or HU101 Lishi will misread a depth and you will cut a key that turns the door but not the ignition.\n\nMachine setup is where money gets lost. Use the correct jaw/clamp position and the manufacturer's blade reference in the software (Condor/Dolphin/Triton have the profile baked in). Check the end-mill: a dull or chipped cutter leaves a rough track that binds in the wafers and reads \"cut wrong\" when it is actually a tooling problem. Deburr every cut key with a brush or your fingernail before you test it \u2014 a burr in the track feels like a bad cut.\n\nMost of these are transponder/PEPS blades, so cutting is only half the job. HU100 GM flip keys and HU101 Ford remote-heads need the chip/fob programmed (add-a-key or all-keys-lost via the OBD tool and, on Ford/GM AKL, a security wait timer of 10-30 min). Quote the customer the cut AND the programming, and confirm the emergency blade in a proximity fob matches \u2014 a lot of PEPS cars ship a sidewinder emergency insert (U-blade) that also has to be cut to the same code for the mechanical door override.",commonServicingIssues:["Cross-cut on wrong blank \u2014 GM HU100 (8-cut) and Ford HU101 (10-cut) look alike to a beginner; count the cuts and verify profile with Lishi/gauge before cutting, wrong blank = instant scrap","Key turns door but not ignition (or vice versa) \u2014 a decode misread (dirty Lishi) or worn source key; re-decode off the ignition lock and cut to code","Rough/binding cut \u2014 dull or chipped end-mill leaves a burred track that drags in the wafers; replace cutter, deburr every key","TOY48 cut backwards \u2014 non-reversible blade flipped in the clamp yields a mirror-image dead key","Cut key won't start car \u2014 forgot the transponder/PEPS step; blade is only mechanical, chip still needs programming (AKL wait timers on Ford/GM)","Worn factory key mis-duplicates \u2014 duplicating blade-to-blade carries wear/play forward; originate from code instead","Emergency/valet insert mismatch \u2014 proximity fob's U-blade override not cut to the same code as the working key","HU66 blank confusion \u2014 older single-track vs newer 2-track VAG versions; grab the wrong blank and it won't seat (V.1/V.3 is the Lishi tool generation, not the blank type)","HU100 vs HU100R mix-up \u2014 GM HU100 and BMW HU100R are different keyways with different depths; don't grab the BMW blank for a GM job","Loose clamp/jaw = shallow or shifted track \u2014 the #1 machining fault; confirm blade seats to the shoulder stop before milling"],sources:["https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/aerolock-gm1-gm-hu100-8-cut-space-depth-keys","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/aerolock-gm-hu100-10-cut-space-depth-keys-gm2-6358","https://www.zippylocks.com/products/ford-high-security-uncut-emergency-blade-164-r8022-for-2013-2015-ford-focus-escape-c-max","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2013-2019-ford-emergency-key-blade-hu101-pn-164-r8022-ilco-hu101emer","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1998-2005-mercedes-hu64-2-track-transponder-key-44-chip-k-hu64","https://yourcarkeyguys.com/products/original-lishi-hu66-v3-generation-1-2-twin-lifter-2-in-1-8-cut-ign-dr-bt-anti-glare","https://www.locksmithmag.com/news/vag-laser-key/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/toy48-toyota-lexus-transponder-key-shell-short-blade-no-chip-st-toy48","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/article/12433009/sidewinder-key-machines-for-the-automotive-locksmith","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/xhorse-condor-dolphin-key-cutting-machine.html","https://key.me/article/how-professionals-cut-and-program-high-security-laser-keys"],photo:"photo-sidewinder-laser-key"},{id:"immobilizer-ecu",name:"Immobilizer / IMMO Box (BCM / ECU / CAS / FEM-BDC / EIS)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"The immobilizer is the module that challenges the transponder in the key and decides whether the engine is allowed to crank and run. It is not one universal box \u2014 its physical location depends on the platform. On older/simpler cars it lives inside the engine ECU/PCM or a small dedicated IMMO box behind the dash near the ignition ring (the \"ring antenna\" feeds it). On most modern cars the immobilizer function is folded into the Body Control Module (BCM) or a keyless/comfort module. On German makes it is a named unit: BMW CAS (Car Access System, E-series) or FEM/BDC (F/G-series front electronic/body domain controller), and Mercedes EIS/EZS (Electronic Ignition Switch, the master immo). The module stores the secret key data \u2014 the transponder IDs, the immobilizer secret/PIN/SK, the ISN (Individual Serial Number that syncs the immo to the engine ECU), and the VIN \u2014 in an EEPROM or in the MCU's flash. When a car cranks but stalls in 1-2 seconds, or dashes an immobilizer/key icon, this box is the brain that said \"no.\" For All Keys Lost, when the OBD port is locked out, the fix is to pull this module, read the EEPROM/flash on the bench, calculate the key data, write a working key, and reinstall.",specs:["Key data storage: a serial EEPROM or the MCU's internal flash is the target. Common EEPROM types: Microchip/ST 93C46/93C56/93C66/93C86 (3-wire Microwire), 24C02/24C04/24C16 (I2C), 95040/95080/95128/95160/95256/95320 (SPI, the '95xx' family)","BMW chip map (get this exact before you clip): CAS1/CAS2/CAS3/CAS3+ carry key data in a 24C16 EEPROM; CAS4/CAS4+ keep it inside the Freescale MC9S12 MCU (9S12XEP100/XDP512, mask 1N35H or 5M48H) read with R270/Xprog/bench probe, NOT a simple external EEPROM; FEM uses a 95128 SPI EEPROM, BDC uses a 95256","BMW CAS: CAS1/CAS2 (E60/E65 early), CAS3 (E9x, E6x, E70/71), CAS3+ (E-series post ~2010), CAS4/CAS4+ (F01/F10 and later 1-series) \u2014 CAS3+ and CAS4 are ISN/encryption locked and often need bench or special OBD unlock; a CAS3 procedure will not run on CAS3+","BMW FEM/BDC: F20/F30/F15/G-series. FEM (Front Electronic Module) ~2013-2016, BDC (Body Domain Controller) 2016+. Must be pre-processed/unlocked on the bench (boot-mode patch) before a key can be written; read the 95128 (FEM) or 95256 (BDC) via 8-pin clip, no desolder needed with most tools","Mercedes EIS/EZS: master immobilizer in the dash. W202/208/210 use infrared (IR) keys, later W204/207/212 use RF smart keys. AKL = read EIS + calculate the EZS password to make a key, or emulate the EIS. DAS (Drive Authorization System) generations DAS1-DAS4; DAS3 IR cars each hold one of 8192 EZS-password combinations","FCA Security Gateway (SGW / '12+8'): 2018+ (some 2018 models, all 2019+) Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep/RAM/Fiat/Maserati. Not the immo itself \u2014 a firewall on the CAN bus that blocks bi-directional/programming access. Bypassed with a 12+8 pass-through cable at the SGW connector (under dash, behind radio, or side kick panel). 2021+ many models integrate the gateway into the BCM (no standalone connector to tap) \u2014 those need AutoAuth/gateway authentication instead","ISN (Individual Serial Number): the secret that binds immobilizer to DME/DDE engine ECU. Needed for BMW module/ECU swaps and some AKL; read from DME in factory/bench mode. In an unencrypted CAS the CAS ISN and ECU ISN must match","Interfaces: OBD-II (when open) or direct EEPROM/BDM/boot-mode on bench via clip, probe, or solder. Bench tools: Autel IM608/IM508, Xhorse VVDI Prog / Key Tool Plus / BIMTool, CGDI BMW, OBDSTAR, Autohex II, Yanhua Mini ACDP, Orange5, Xprog, R270, Lonsdor K518","Transponder families the box authenticates: Megamos (ID48, crypto), Philips/NXP Hitag2 (ID46), Hitag-AES/Hitag-Pro (ID4A), Texas DST40 (4D) and DST80/DST-AES (Toyota 8A 'H', Audi 8E), older fixed-code 4C/4D"],proAdvice:"Rule one: before you cut into anything, know WHERE the immo lives on that specific platform, because it moves. Domestic and Asian econoboxes usually keep it in the PCM/ECU or a WCM/immo box near the column; modern platforms bury it in the BCM. German cars name it \u2014 BMW CAS or FEM/BDC, Mercedes EIS/EZS. Look the car up first. Guessing which module holds the secret is how you pull the wrong box and waste an hour.\n\nFor everyday jobs with a working key present, you almost never touch this module \u2014 you add a key over OBD and the immo enrolls it. The bench game starts with All Keys Lost and with security gateways. When the OBD port is dead-ended (FCA SGW, or a platform that simply won't let you add the first key over the bus), you go to the metal: remove the module, read the EEPROM or flash on the bench, let the tool calculate key data / dealer key / PIN, write a transponder, put it back. On BMW FEM/BDC there's an extra step \u2014 the module must be \"unlocked\" or pre-processed first, then you read the 95128 (FEM) or 95256 (BDC) and program. Always save the original read as a backup file before you write anything. That backup is your undo button; skip it and a bad write bricks a module you now own.\n\nOn BMW specifically, understand the ISN. The immobilizer (CAS/FEM) and the engine controller (DME/DDE) are married by the Individual Serial Number. For a straight key job you often don't need it, but the moment you swap a used module or a used ECU, or you're doing a full AKL on the locked generations (CAS3+, CAS4, some FEM/BDC), the ISN is the thing that makes the two talk. Read it from the DME in bench/factory mode. A \"programmed a key but it cranks-no-start\" on a swapped module is almost always an ISN mismatch. And know your chip target: CAS1 through CAS3+ is a 24C16 read, but CAS4/CAS4+ hides the data in the Freescale MCU \u2014 different tool, different approach, don't go looking for an external EEPROM that isn't there.\n\nThe FCA Security Gateway is not the immobilizer and don't let anyone tell you it is \u2014 it's a CAN firewall Chrysler added in 2018 to block bi-directional access. Your programmer sees the car but can't do relearns, clear codes, or add keys until you get past it. The clean way is the 12+8 bypass cable: unplug the SGW connector (under the dash, sometimes behind the radio or in the kick panel), insert the pass-through, and now you have full bus access. Heads up: 2021+ many FCA vehicles moved the gateway function into the BCM, so there's no separate connector to tap \u2014 those need a tool that authenticates through the gateway (AutoAuth registered) instead of a physical bypass.\n\nMercedes EIS/EZS is the master immo and it's its own discipline. The EIS in the dash authorizes everything; AKL means reading the EIS and calculating the EZS password to make a key, or emulating the EIS entirely on the tougher DAS generations. It's read-heavy, password-heavy work \u2014 know which DAS generation you're on and whether it's an IR key (W202/208/210) or an RF smart key (W204/212) before you quote it.\n\nTwo practical habits that save you: identify the exact EEPROM or MCU part number on the board before you clip or solder \u2014 a 95128, a 95256, and a 24C16 are not the same read, and clipping the wrong chip gets you garbage. And second, respect the difference between \"read over OBD in seconds\" and \"pull the module and go to the bench.\" Quote and schedule accordingly; a bench AKL on a locked German module is a shop-time job, not a driveway 15-minute add-a-key.",commonServicingIssues:["All Keys Lost with OBD locked out \u2014 must remove module and read EEPROM/flash on the bench to calculate key data or PIN; the single biggest reason you touch this box","Wrong module targeted \u2014 pulling the ECU when the secret actually lives in the BCM/CAS/EIS (or vice versa); always confirm where the immo function lives on that platform first","BMW FEM/BDC not pre-processed/unlocked before attempting to write a key \u2014 key programming fails until the module is unlocked and the 95128 (FEM) / 95256 (BDC) handled correctly","ISN mismatch after a used CAS/FEM or used DME/DDE swap \u2014 key programs but cranks-no-start because immo and engine ECU aren't married; ISN must be read and synced","FCA Security Gateway blocking all programming \u2014 no keys/relearns/code-clear until 12+8 bypass is installed, or (2021+ BCM-integrated) gateway auth is used instead","No backup taken before writing \u2014 a failed or interrupted EEPROM/flash write bricks the module with no original to restore","Clipping/soldering the wrong chip or misreading a 93Cxx vs 24Cxx vs 95xxx (or looking for an external EEPROM on CAS4 where the data is in the MCU) \u2014 produces bad data and wasted reads","CAS3+/CAS4 encryption \u2014 these later BMW generations are locked and need the correct bench or special OBD method; a CAS3 procedure won't work on CAS3+","Mercedes EIS/EZS password calculation on tougher DAS generations \u2014 may require EIS emulation rather than a simple read","Corrupted or water-damaged module giving false immobilizer faults \u2014 sometimes it's a failed BCM/immo board, not a key problem, so the fix is module repair/replacement plus re-coding, not another key","Virginizing / renewing a used module to reuse it on another car \u2014 must be reset to factory before it will accept new key/VIN data"],sources:["https://www.microtronik.com/technical-info/autohex/fem-and-bdc-unlocking","https://abkeys.com/blogs/news/how-to-program-bmw-1-series-fem-all-keys-lost-on-bench-with-the-autel-im608","https://www.cgdishop.com/wholesale/cgdi-prog-bmw-msv80-car-key-programmer.html","https://help.auto-explorer.com/help_eng/Features_of_the_program/Functions/Write%20keys%20%20CAS4%20(EWS4)%20by%20Dump/Write%20keys%20%20CAS4%20(EWS4)%20by%20Dump.htm","https://binunlock.com/resources/bmw-cas-eeprom-creator-recover-lost-cas-1-4-data.31/","https://jscan.net/fca-security-gateway-module-basic-info-and-location/","https://www.youcanic.com/fca-security-gateway-module-explained-obd2-sgm-sgw/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2022/02/24/complete-list-of-chrysler-jeep-dodge-security-gateway-locations/","https://www.bowlinggreenlocksmiths.com/mercedes-benz-module-repair-and-reprogramming/mercedes-benz-eis-ezs-immobilizer-ignition-repair-service/","https://abrites.com/blog/das-manager-deep-dive","https://www.vvdishop.com/service/vvdi-key-tool-plus-program-bmw-cas3-all-keys-lost.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2022/03/21/autel-im608-bmw-fem-bdc-module-replacement-guide/"],photo:"photo-immobilizer-ecu"},{id:"moto-ignition-lock",name:"Motorcycle Ignition & Fork Lock Set",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A matched set of mechanical locks that share one key on a motorcycle. The core is the ignition switch: a wafer-tumbler cylinder mounted in the top triple clamp or dash that usually also carries a push-down STEER LOCK detent to lock the fork hard over. Sold as a set, it typically also includes the steering/fork lock (either integral to the ignition or a separate barrel in the steering neck), the seat/saddlebag lock, the helmet lock, and the fuel/gas cap lock. On a factory bike these are all keyed alike, so one key runs the whole machine. Older and small-displacement bikes are purely mechanical wafer locks. From roughly the late 1990s on, mid- and large-displacement street bikes add an electronic immobilizer: a transponder chip in the key head read by an antenna ring around the ignition, so a correctly cut key still will not start the bike unless the chip is also programmed to the ECU. Brand immobilizer names to know: Honda HISS, and generic transponder immobilizers on Yamaha, Suzuki, and Kawasaki (Kawasaki uses a red ring around the switch and a separate red master/programming key).",specs:["Lock type: side-wafer tumbler cylinder; ignition typically 5-6 wafers, seat/steer/helmet locks typically 4-5 wafers; commonly 2-3 cut depths","Honda keyways: HON68 (H74/H75 family), 5 wafers / 3 depths, spacing 098-197-295-394-492; Yamaha: YM63/YM64 (X248); Suzuki: SUZ18/SUZ19 (X241); Kawasaki: KW14/KA15 (X259, shared with Suzuki/Triumph); Harley: HD72/HD74/HD75/HD103-108","Set contents (typical OEM): ignition switch, fork/steer lock, seat lock, helmet lock, fuel cap lock, 2 keys, mounting brackets/screws","Immobilizer systems: Honda HISS; Yamaha/Suzuki/Kawasaki transponder immobilizer. Up to 4 keys registerable to the ECU; 2 keys shipped from factory","Transponder chips (bike-dependent): Honda HISS uses a crypto glass transponder (ID46 / PCF7936 / Hitag2); other Japanese bikes may use ID13, ID48/Megamos, or Texas 4C/4D chips depending on model/year","Key code: stamped 3-4 digit code on Honda ignition cylinder collar/shaft; on a knock-out tag or cylinder body for others; also on the plastic owner's key-code card","Ignition switch positions: OFF, ON, LOCK (steering), and often P (park/parking lights); push-to-turn on many models","Immobilizer telltale: dash indicator (HISS light) that flashes/holds when the chip is not recognized; antenna ring visible around the key hole"],proAdvice:"First call on any bike lockset job: mechanical-only or immobilizer? Look at the ignition switch face. A plain switch means wafer-only, so you cut a key and you are done. A plastic collar or antenna ring around the key hole plus an immobilizer telltale on the dash (Honda HISS lettering, Kawasaki red ring) means there is a transponder chip in the key head and cutting alone will not start it. That one observation changes the ticket from \"cut a key\" to \"cut and program,\" and it changes the price and the tooling you bring.\n\nCutting by code is the money play and it keeps you off the bike's mechanicals. Japanese bikes stamp a key code: Honda commonly puts a 3-4 digit code on the silver collar the key enters on the ignition cylinder; Suzuki, Yamaha, and Kawasaki use a knock-out tag or a stamp on the cylinder body, and the code is also on the plastic owner's key-code card if the customer kept it. Feed the code to your bitting tables (or a code machine with the bike series loaded) and cut a fresh key to factory bitting, no picking, no disassembly. If there is no code, pull the least-buried lock in the set (usually the seat or helmet lock, not the ignition) and decode or impression that one, since they are keyed alike and far easier to get to than the ignition buried under the fork clamp.\n\nKeyed-alike is the whole point of the set and also the trap. If a customer has lost every key, you can read the easy lock, cut to it, and that key should run ignition, seat, and cap. But if someone has already replaced one barrel (common on stolen-recovery or crash bikes), the set is no longer alike and you will chase a key that opens three locks but not the fourth. Try your freshly cut key in every lock before you call the job done. When locks have been mixed, the clean fix is to re-pin/re-wafer the odd barrels to match the majority rather than hand the customer two keys.\n\nOn immobilizer bikes, mechanical and electronic are two separate jobs and both have to succeed. Cut the blade to code, then program the chip to the ECU. Honda HISS and most Japanese bikes let you add keys with an existing working key via the ignition-cycle procedure (with HISS, an unprogrammed ID46 chip and the battery-disconnect/HISS-light dance); all-keys-lost usually needs a dealer tool or an aftermarket programmer (Bike Decoders, ABKEYS-type units) and sometimes PIN/pre-code from the VIN. Kawasaki's red master key is what registers new user keys, so if that red key is gone the job gets much harder. Always tell the customer up front whether their bike is chipped, because the price gap between a wafer cut and a programmed transponder key is large and it is the number one surprise on the invoice.\n\nWatch the worn-lock cases. High-mileage bikes get sloppy ignition wafers and a factory-cut new key can feel loose or intermittent, especially in the STEER LOCK detent where side load is highest. If the customer's problem is \"key turns but no crank,\" do not assume the cylinder; on chipped bikes that is often the immobilizer not reading, and on any bike it can be the switch contacts, the kill switch, sidestand cutout, or starter relay, not the lock at all. Diagnose before you sell a barrel.",commonServicingIssues:["Worn ignition wafers: key sticks, rattles, or fails to turn in the STEER LOCK position where side load is highest; a fresh factory-cut key can feel loose in a worn barrel","Mixed/non-keyed-alike set after a prior barrel swap (crash or theft-recovery bikes): one key opens 3 of 4 locks; fix by re-wafering the odd barrels to match","Seized steering/fork lock from corrosion or a bent detent, especially on bikes stored outside; the fork will not lock or will not release","Immobilizer no-start: correctly cut key cranks but engine will not fire because the transponder is unprogrammed or the antenna ring/immobilizer is faulty (HISS light flashing)","All-keys-lost on a chipped bike: needs code/PIN and a programmer; Kawasaki cases stall if the red master programming key is missing","Broken key snapped in the ignition (small bike wafer locks are shallow and easy to snap); extract, then decode remaining plug to cut a replacement","Ignition switch electrical failure mistaken for a lock fault: no-crank from worn switch contacts, kill switch, sidestand cutout, or starter relay rather than the cylinder","No key code available: customer lost the owner's key-code card, forcing impression or decode instead of a quick cut-by-code"],sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10761589/fitting-keys-to-a-honda-motorcycle-is-a-relatively-easy-job-for-most-locksmiths","https://global.honda/en/tech/Tech_Views_Vol4_Honda_Ignition_security_system_HISS/","https://global.honda/en/tech/innovation/technology/motorcycle/tech-views/vol04_hiss/qa.html","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/key-blanks-for-motorcycles-the-complete-guide-for-locksmiths-in-2025","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/honda_transponder_catalog.html","https://carlocksandkeys.com/blog/how-to-find-your-motorcycle-key-code-lost-keys/","https://bikedecoders.com/suzuki-motorcycle-key-programming/","https://abkeys.com/collections/kawasaki-keys-kawasaki-transponder-key-programmer","https://www.harley-davidson.com/us/en/shop/fork-lock-and-ignition-switch/p/71400126A","https://4into1.com/square-plug-ignition-switch-seat-lock-set-cb200-350f-360-500k-550k-750k-cl200-360/","https://keymateinc.com/shop-online/key-blanks/automotive-keys/motorcycle/ilco-x259-kw14-key-blank-kawasaki-suzuki-triumph-motorcycles-detail.html"],photo:"photo-moto-ignition-lock"},{id:"hager-3700",name:"Hager 3700 Series Interconnected Lock",category:"Residential",whatItIs:"The Hager 3700 is a Grade 2 interconnected lockset: a top deadbolt and a lower lever chassis linked so one turn of the inside lever retracts BOTH in a single motion \u2014 code-legal single-motion egress. It's the retrofit-friendly answer to apartment and multifamily entry doors, because it drops onto the same two 2-1/8\" cross-bores an old knob-over-deadbolt setup already has instead of forcing a mortise pocket. From the hallway it reads like a normal keyed entry with a separate keyed deadbolt; from inside, one grab-and-turn gets a panicked or impaired tenant out no matter how many bolts got thrown. It's the branded, spec-out-of-a-box version of the generic interconnected lockset concept.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.12 (Series 5000 interconnected), ANSI Grade 2; ADA-compliant lever trim","Doors 1-3/8\" to 2\" (35\u201351 mm) thick","Two 2-1/8\" (54 mm) cross-bores \u2014 one for the upper deadbolt, one for the lower lever chassis \u2014 over a 4\" (102 mm) center-to-center interconnect spacing","Suggested install/template height ~38\"; the template registers off the high edge of the door bevel","Common functions: 3710 entry single-locking (F95) \u2014 key outside / thumbturn inside throws the deadbolt, inside lever retracts both bolts; 3753 entry double-locking (F97); 3796/-indicator versions add an occupancy 'Vacant/In Use' or deadbolt-thrown indicator. The packing-slip unit here is a 3770 interconnected lock with indicator","3710/3753 are UL/cUL listed up to 3 hours on A-label single doors; UL10C positive-pressure and UL10B neutral-pressure rated","Lever styles by code (WTN = Withnell on this unit); finishes include 26D/626 satin chrome, 10B, 3, etc.","Cylinder usually NOT installed at the factory \u2014 shipped with a separate Cylinder Kit (e.g. 40-100) so you key it on site; this slip lists S123/Schlage-C keyway, 626 finish, keyed different (KD) with change keys (CK)","Install/service reference: Hager instruction sheet I-LS00770"],proAdvice:"This is a retrofit lock, so read the door before you read the box. The whole reason to reach for a 3700 is that the opening is already bored for a knob and a deadbolt \u2014 two 2-1/8\" holes, 4\" apart. Confirm that spacing and the door thickness first; if the existing prep is off-center or the door is under 1-3/8\", you're into a bigger job than the customer thinks. Set the template off the high edge of the bevel like the sheet says, around 38\", or your interconnect link won't line up and the lever won't pull the deadbolt.\n\nThe cylinder is the part that trips people up. These ship with the cylinder as a separate kit, not pinned, so you're keying it on the door. Before you pin a single bottom pin, check that the S123 / Schlage-C keyway actually matches the building's existing system \u2014 if the property is keyed alike or on a master, you want this on their keyway and their bitting, not a random KD cut, or you've just created a one-off key the manager has to carry forever. Pin it to the system, hand over the change keys, done in one trip.\n\nThe safety point you never compromise: interconnected means single-motion egress. The inside lever must retract the deadbolt and the latch together, every time, with no thumbturn step. If a tenant has added a secondary deadbolt above it, that defeats the whole purpose \u2014 flag it. That's the reason this lock exists and the reason it's code on multifamily.",commonServicingIssues:["Inside lever doesn't fully retract the deadbolt \u2014 interconnect link out of adjustment or wrong template height; re-set to the I-LS00770 dimensions before condemning the chassis","Deadbolt binds or won't throw fully \u2014 strike misaligned or door/frame gap out of spec; align and deepen the strike pocket rather than forcing it","Won't fit the existing prep \u2014 old knob/deadbolt bores not on the 4\" interconnect spacing, or door under 1-3/8\"; verify prep before ordering","Key won't match the building \u2014 cylinder was pinned to a stock KD cut instead of the property's S123/Schlage-C system; re-pin to their bitting","Cylinder missing from the install \u2014 the 40-100 cylinder kit ships separate and gets left in the box; confirm it's on the truck before you leave the shop","Loose lever/rattling trip after use \u2014 through-bolts backed out; the concealed screws need re-torquing, not replacement"],sources:["https://www.hagerco.com/locks/3700-series-interconnected-grade-2","https://www.hagerco.com/Files/Images/Images/Instructions/I-LS00770.pdf","https://www.hagerco.com/locks/3700-series-interconnected-grade-2/3700-series-functions/3710-entry-single-locking-f95","https://www.hagerco.com/locks/3700-series-interconnected-grade-2/3700-series-functions/3753-entry-double-locking-f97","https://www.hagerco.com/Files/Images/Images/TemplateDetails/3700-Series.pdf"],schematic:"schematic-hager-3700",photo:"photo-hager-3700"},{id:"pls-l240",name:"PLS L240 Privacy Lever",category:"Cylindrical locks",whatItIs:"The PLS L240 is a Grade 2 cylindrical privacy leverset \u2014 the budget commercial bed/bath lock you find on restrooms, changing rooms, private offices, and clinic exam rooms. PLS is an imported (Taiwan-made) house brand sold through security-hardware distributors as a low-cost alternative to a Schlage ND40 or Falcon T301. It's a push-button/turn-button privacy function: locked from inside, no keyway outside, just an emergency slot for a flat tool or coin so staff can get in. Panic-proof by design \u2014 turning the inside lever always releases, so nobody gets trapped.",specs:["ANSI/BHMA A156.2 Series 4000, Grade 2; UL listed","Function: privacy (bed/bath) \u2014 inside push/turn button locks; no outside cylinder, emergency release slot outside","Backset 2-3/4\" standard (2-3/8\" typically available)","Strike: 4-7/8\" x 1-1/4\" ANSI/ASA lip strike; T-strike (full-lip) option \u2014 this unit shipped with both boxes checked","Doors 1-3/8\" to 2\" thick","Clutched/independent-spring lever so a hung-on lever doesn't sag or wear the return; ADA-compliant lever","Finish US26D / 626 satin chrome (26D-4 = 26D finish, 2-3/4\" backset)","Removable through-bolt feature for install/service","Keyway noted SCC (Schlage-C) on the box \u2014 but a privacy function is NOT keyed; see pro advice"],proAdvice:"Treat this for what it is \u2014 a disposable-tier import, not a lock you rebuild. When a PLS L240 fails, you swap it; there's no parts kit worth chasing and no locksmith margin in pulling one apart. Keep a couple of Grade 2 privacy leversets on the truck and you turn every one of these into a five-minute R&R.\n\nDon't get thrown by the 'SCC KD' marking on the box. This is a privacy function \u2014 bed/bath \u2014 and privacy functions have no outside cylinder to key. That keyway/KD note is a line-spec carryover from the shared L240 family (the same chassis in an entry or classroom function does take a Schlage-C cylinder); on the privacy unit there's nothing to pin, just the emergency turn slot. If a customer actually wants it keyable from outside, you ordered the wrong function \u2014 they want the entry or storeroom L240, not this one.\n\nInstall is standard 161 prep, 2-3/4\" backset, both strikes in the box so you match whatever the frame is cut for \u2014 ASA flat-lip for a standard hollow-metal frame, the T-strike where the jamb wants a full wraparound lip. The one real-world gotcha on cheap imports: check the lever handing and that the button mechanism actually catches before you leave, because QC on this tier is uneven and a sticky privacy button is the #1 callback.",commonServicingIssues:["Privacy button won't hold or won't release \u2014 worn/sloppy button mechanism on a low-cost chassis; replace the lockset rather than repair","Customer wanted a keyed lock \u2014 privacy function has no outside cylinder; the 'SCC/KD' box note doesn't make it keyable, they need the entry/storeroom L240 instead","Lever sags or feels loose out of the box \u2014 uneven import QC; verify the clutch/spring and handing before final install","Latch won't align to the frame \u2014 wrong strike installed; the unit ships both ASA and T strikes, fit the one that matches the jamb prep","Emergency release slot won't open the door in a lockout \u2014 cheap tolerance binding; a flat driver in the outside slot should always retract it, if not, swap the unit"],sources:["https://www.rhsecurityhardware.com/pls-l240-26d-4/","L240 26d 4 pls l240 26d grade 2 lever privacy asa 2 3 4 brushed chrome (keyinnovations.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published."],photo:"photo-pls-l240"},{id:"dw-blackjack-spectrum",name:"Digital Watchdog Blackjack Server & DW Spectrum VMS",category:"Video surveillance",whatItIs:"This is the recorder-and-software half of a Digital Watchdog system. Blackjack is the hardware \u2014 a line of purpose-built NVR servers running from small appliance boxes up to rack-mount servers \u2014 and DW Spectrum is the video management software (VMS) they run. Keep the two ideas separate: Blackjack is the box, DW Spectrum is the brains. Under the hood DW Spectrum is Network Optix's Nx Witness, OEM-rebranded, so if you have ever touched Nx you already know the client. The important thing on the truck is that it is vendor-neutral: DW Spectrum records any ONVIF or RTSP camera, not just DW's own, which is exactly why you see it paired with third-party cameras like the LTS Platinum line.",specs:["Software = DW Spectrum (Network Optix Nx Witness OEM). Three parts: the Media Server (runs on the Blackjack, discovers cameras, records, serves streams), the Desktop client (configure and view, not the recorder), and the Mobile/Web clients","DW Spectrum server default web/API port is 7001 (HTTPS); ONVIF discovery and RTSP pull happen on the camera subnet","Licensing is per recorded channel (perpetual Professional licenses), bound to the server hardware ID; Blackjack boxes typically bundle a number of channels \u2014 verify the included count per model before you spec camera count","Rack-mount Blackjack servers generally ship with NO built-in PoE \u2014 cameras are fed from a separate PoE switch into the server NIC; the smaller DW NVR appliances include built-in PoE ports on an isolated camera LAN","Storage: internal surveillance-grade SATA HDD bays (count varies by model), RAID on the larger servers; per-camera min/max archive retention with automatic purge of the oldest footage","Codecs: H.265 and H.264 record and decode; hardware-accelerated decode on supported GPUs to keep CPU down in multi-view","Merges multiple servers into one 'System' with a shared user set and layouts; roles for admin/operator/viewer; LDAP/Active Directory supported","Remote access via DW Cloud (Nx Cloud) with no port-forwarding, or manual port-forward + DDNS the old way","Blackjack appliances ship on a hardened Ubuntu-based OS; DW Spectrum server also runs on Windows and Linux for software-only installs"],proAdvice:"Build it the way it wants to be built and it runs for years untouched. Put the server AND the switch on a UPS \u2014 the recorder is a database writing to disk, and a dirty power-down corrupts the archive or the OS and turns a service call into a reimage. Feed cameras from a dedicated PoE switch rather than leaning on built-in ports, because a switch scales, isolates camera traffic, and swaps out without you ever touching the recorder.\n\nAt commissioning, set per-camera retention and then walk away from storage \u2014 DW auto-purges the oldest once the archive is full, so you size disks to the retention the customer is paying for, not to 'forever.' Turn on SMART monitoring and actually respond to the warning; a failing archive disk is a silent failure that nobody notices until they need the one clip that isn't there. Connect DW Cloud at install while you are already logged in \u2014 it is the difference between a five-minute phone-view setup and a callback fighting the customer's ISP later.\n\nTwo things that bite integrators: license binding and version skew. Licenses tie to the server hardware, so if you replace the box you deactivate and move them, you don't just re-buy. And keep the Desktop client and the server on matching versions \u2014 a client that is a major version behind will connect but misbehave, most visibly as black tiles on H.265 cameras. Export the System configuration after you finish so a rebuild is a restore, not a from-scratch afternoon.",commonServicingIssues:["A camera pings but will not add to the System \u2014 almost always the camera side: unactivated, ONVIF service disabled, ONVIF credentials different from the web login, or the camera is on a different subnet/VLAN than the NIC doing the scanning. Prove the camera is alive in a browser, then fix ONVIF","Multi-view is choppy and CPU is pegged \u2014 the client is pulling main streams into the layout instead of sub streams, or too many H.265 mainstreams are being software-decoded; enable hardware decode and confirm cameras are handing DW a proper low-res secondary stream","Black tiles with no image on cameras that are clearly connected \u2014 codec mismatch on an out-of-date Desktop client; update the client to the server version or drop that camera to H.264","Recording gaps in the archive \u2014 disk full or a failing/dropped archive drive, or clock drift between camera and server; check server storage health and confirm cameras are NTP-synced to the server or a common source","Cannot view remotely \u2014 DW Cloud not connected, ISP has put the site behind CGNAT so the old port-forward no longer works, or the remote client version does not match the server; use Cloud rather than fighting the port-forward","License shows invalid after a hardware change \u2014 Professional licenses are bound to the server hardware ID; deactivate before swapping the box and reactivate on the new one instead of buying again"],relatedGuides:["dw-spectrum-lts-cameras","video-surveillance","conduit-cctv"],sources:["https://www.digital-watchdog.com/","https://dwspectrum.com/","https://www.networkoptix.com/nx-witness/","https://www.onvif.org/profiles/"],schematic:"dw-blackjack-schematic"},{id:"lts-platinum-ip-camera",name:"LTS Platinum IP Cameras (Dahua OEM)",category:"Video surveillance",whatItIs:"LTS (LTS Security) sells IP cameras, and the Platinum line is their Dahua OEM series \u2014 domes, turrets, bullets, PTZ, and multisensor units that are Dahua hardware and firmware under an LTS label. That is the single most useful fact about them: they behave exactly like Dahua cameras, inherit Dahua's quirks, and take Dahua's tools and RTSP paths. They onboard to any ONVIF/RTSP video management system, which is how they end up recording to a Digital Watchdog DW Spectrum server. Learn the Dahua activation-and-ONVIF dance once and every camera in this line installs the same way.",specs:["Dahua OEM hardware and firmware. Default service ports: 80 (HTTP + ONVIF), 554 (RTSP), 37777 (Dahua SDK)","New cameras ship UNACTIVATED \u2014 first connection (ConfigTool, SmartPSS, or the camera web page) forces you to set a strong admin password before the camera will answer; there is no admin/admin out of the box on current firmware","ONVIF login can be separate from the web-admin login on some firmware; a VMS authenticates over ONVIF, so the ONVIF user must be enabled with known credentials","RTSP stream path (Dahua): rtsp://user:pass@camera-ip:554/cam/realmonitor?channel=1&subtype=0 for the main stream, subtype=1 for the sub stream","Dual/triple streaming: main stream (record/fullscreen) + sub stream (low-res multi-view); H.265, H.264, and Smart Codec (H.265+) to cut bitrate and storage","Power: 802.3af PoE for fixed domes/bullets/turrets (~15W); 802.3at PoE+ for PTZ and units with IR heaters/blowers (~30W); most fixed units also take 12VDC","Resolutions commonly 2MP\u20138MP (4K); fixed, varifocal, and motorized-zoom lens options; higher tiers add low-light 'Starlight' sensors and Smart IR to curb foreground wash-out","Analytics on higher tiers: Smart Motion Detection and line-crossing/intrusion (perimeter) events, exposed to the VMS over ONVIF","Enclosure ratings typically IP66/IP67 weatherproof; vandal domes IK10 impact-rated","NDAA: Dahua-manufactured, therefore on the federal Section 889 covered list \u2014 LTS Platinum is generally NOT NDAA-compliant. Disqualifying on government, federally-funded, and many institutional jobs"],proAdvice:"The whole job goes smoothly or badly on one step: activate the camera before you try to onboard it. Bench-activate with the LTS/Dahua ConfigTool, set the admin password, set the IP (DHCP or a static on the server's camera subnet), enable ONVIF, and confirm the ONVIF user and its password. Do that and the camera drops into DW Spectrum's auto-discovery on the first scan; skip it and you get a healthy camera that pings, logs in fine in a browser, and still refuses to add \u2014 because the VMS is talking ONVIF and the ONVIF side is off or has a different password. That one detail is the number-one reason a good camera won't onboard.\n\nSet the streams with intent. Record the main stream and, if storage matters, run it H.265 to roughly halve the archive \u2014 just confirm the recorder decodes it in the live layout (DW does on a current client; a black tile means update the client). Let the recorder pull the sub stream for multi-view so it isn't chewing CPU decoding a wall of 4K. Point the camera's NTP at the recorder or a shared source; clock drift is invisible until someone needs footage from an exact minute and the timestamps don't line up.\n\nPower is where the night-time mysteries come from. A camera that is perfect all day and drops at dusk is not a network problem \u2014 it is the IR illuminator kicking on and pushing draw past what a long cable run or an overloaded switch can deliver. Keep runs under 100 m, respect the PoE budget, and put anything with a heater or PTZ on a PoE+ port.\n\nAnd before you spec these on anything institutional, ask about funding. Because they are Dahua OEM they are Section 889 covered and generally not NDAA-compliant, so a government building, a school, or a hospital on federal money legally cannot run them \u2014 that is a pull-and-replace you find out about after you've hung the cameras. On those jobs, DW's own NDAA-compliant camera line is the answer.",commonServicingIssues:["Camera won't add to the VMS despite pinging \u2014 unactivated camera, ONVIF service disabled, or ONVIF credentials different from the web-admin login; activate it, enable ONVIF, and set/confirm the ONVIF user","Camera works by day, drops at night \u2014 PoE undervolt: the IR ring raises draw at dusk past what a long run or a loaded switch delivers; measure voltage at the camera, shorten the run, or move it to a PoE+ port or midspan injector","Black tile / no image after it's added \u2014 codec mismatch (H.265 on an out-of-date client) or a broken main/sub stream config; update the client or drop to H.264 and re-check the stream settings","Washed-out or hot-spotted night image \u2014 IR bouncing off a near wall, eave, or spider webs on the dome; re-aim, add a shroud, clean the bubble, or enable Smart IR to pull down foreground gain","Timestamp gaps or wrong times in the archive \u2014 camera clock drifting from the recorder; set the camera NTP to the server or a common time source","Job rejected on compliance \u2014 LTS Platinum is Dahua OEM and not NDAA-compliant; on any Section 889 site it has to come out and be replaced with an NDAA-compliant camera"],relatedGuides:["dw-spectrum-lts-cameras","video-surveillance","camera-defense"],sources:["https://www.ltsecurityinc.com/","https://dahuawiki.com/Remote_Access/RTSP_via_VLC","https://www.onvif.org/profiles/","https://www.fs.com/blog/understanding-poe-standards-and-wattage-21.html","https://www.pelco.com/blog/ndaa-compliant-cameras"],schematic:"lts-platinum-schematic"},{id:"dks-1834-80-series",name:"DoorKing 1834 80-Series Telephone Entry System",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The DoorKing 1834 is a telephone entry panel \u2014 the callbox on a gate or lobby door where a visitor dials a resident and the resident grants access from their phone. The 80-Series is the PC-programmable branch of the family with no built-in card reader: you manage it from a computer over its modem, not from the keypad. One board stores up to 3000 phone numbers (the 6705 Memory Module 3000) and controls two independent entry points. The -080 is the surface-mount box (there are flush-mount and reader-equipped siblings in the 1834 line). You see these on apartment and condo drives, commercial lots, and any multi-tenant gate that needs directory-dial access.",specs:["1834-080: surface-mount, PC-programmable, no card reader (the 1834 family also comes flush-mount and with a built-in reader)","Memory: 6705 Memory Module 3000 \u2014 stores up to 3000 phone numbers","Controls two independent entry points (two relays) from a single board","REQUIRES TWO transformers, both 16.5 VAC / 20 VA UL-listed, and both must be installed: one powers the system at main terminals 19 & 20 (16AC/16AC), the second powers the RS-232 side at the Aux terminal. AC, so polarity does not matter","Do NOT power any other device (electric strike, mag lock, etc.) from these transformers \u2014 dedicated to the entry system only","Power wiring: 18 AWG up to 100 ft, 16 AWG up to 200 ft, 600V insulated; keep runs short to avoid induced hum","Supplied ferrite filter clips over the 16 VAC leads at terminals 19/20 and at the Aux terminal to cut hum on the phone line","Comms: standard POTS phone line, or the 1830-010 AG cellular/modem PCB for DKS cellular voice/data where no landline exists (requires a provisioned DKS cellular account before it will dial out)","Display: 1891-010 (Rev D) LCD","Programming: DoorKing Remote Account Manager (RAM) software, v6.3.b or higher, over the modem/data link \u2014 PC-programmed, not keypad-programmed","Standby power (optional): 12 VDC 0.7 Ah SLA battery \u2014 positive to terminal 18 (BAT), negative to terminal 6 (COM); two batteries to back up both system and aux. Battery not supplied","Grounding: CGND with 12 AWG minimum to a ground point within 10 ft (panel, metallic cold-water pipe, or a 10-ft ground rod). A post set in concrete is NOT a ground","DKS recommends two 1878-010 low-voltage surge suppressors within 10 ft of the unit","Manual: P/N 1834-065 (Rev W, 11/24). Wiring diagram on p.21; terminal quick-guide inside the front cover","Relays are dry contacts, 30 V / 3 A max \u2014 they switch the strike's own supply, not panel power. Relay 1 is full form C (1C / 1NO / 1NC); Relay 2 is a single contact (2C / 2RY) set NO or NC by the Relay 2 jumper (section 4.6)","Strikes/mags require a SEPARATE UL-listed supply sized to inrush + holding. Fail-secure on Relay 1: PSU(+)\u21921C, 1NO\u2192strike(+), strike(\u2212)\u2192PSU(\u2212); fail-safe or mag uses 1NC instead of 1NO. DC strikes want a diode or MOV across the coil","Programming: phone directory via RAM (PC); at the unit, relay/strike times * 03 then 1* or 2*, talk time * 08. Tone-open defaults Relay 1 = 9876, Relay 2 = 5432 (* 05 to change). Postal switch fires Relay 1 (* 50 to reassign)","No default master code \u2014 set a unique four-digit code via the cabinet master-code switch (\xa73.2.1). Unrecoverable if lost; reprogram a new one (directory data survives). Commands: press the 2-digit code, enter the master code, beep; end with * + 0","Tenant turnover by directory code: delete phone * 01 (\xa73.5.6) + delete name * 65 (\xa73.5.7), then re-add phone * 01 (\xa73.5.2) + name * 66 (\xa73.5.5) on the same code. * 60 / * 95 erase the WHOLE directory; changing code length * 20 wipes all entries","8000-event transaction buffer; live transaction reporting requires the RS-232 connection"],proAdvice:"The number-one field mistake on a 1834 is power. It takes two transformers, both 16.5 VAC / 20 VA, and both have to be installed \u2014 one on terminals 19 and 20 for the system, one on the Aux terminal for the RS-232 side. It will not run right on a single transformer, and the day you borrow the entry transformer to feed an electric strike or a mag lock you brown it out and inherit a box that resets and faults at random. Power the lock from its own dedicated supply and keep these two transformers doing nothing but running the entry system.\n\nGround and surge come next, because this is a circuit board sitting outside on a metal box at the end of a long wire run, which is a lightning magnet. Run CGND with 12 AWG minimum to a real ground within 10 feet \u2014 the service panel, a metallic cold-water pipe, or a proper 10-foot rod. A gate post set in concrete is not a ground, no matter how solid it feels. Add the two 1878-010 low-voltage surge suppressors DKS calls for within 10 feet; a surge board is pocket change next to a cooked main board and the truck roll to replace it.\n\nSort the comm path before you roll. If the site still has a POTS line, easy. If it does not, the box needs the 1830-010 AG cellular PCB and a provisioned DKS cellular account \u2014 and the account has to be live before the unit will dial out, so start it ahead of time or you will install a system that cannot call anybody. And since the 80-Series is PC-programmed, bring Remote Account Manager (v6.3.b or higher) and know how you are connecting; you are not typing 3000 numbers into a keypad.\n\nLast, the small stuff that generates callbacks: clip on the supplied ferrite filter at the 16 VAC terminals, keep the low-voltage runs short and at least 18 inches from 115V in separate conduit, and never land the transformers on a switched receptacle. Hum on the voice line and a mysteriously dead gate trace back to those three every time. The terminal quick-guide inside the front cover and the wiring diagram on page 21 of the manual are the two pages worth keeping open on the tailgate.\n\nAnd the wiring detail that separates a clean install from a callback: the transformers power the panel only, and the two relays are dry contacts (30 V / 3 A) that switch the strike's own supply. Size a separate UL-listed supply to the strikes' inrush plus holding, land a fail-secure strike on Relay 1 as PSU positive to 1C, 1NO to the strike, strike back to PSU negative, and move to 1NC for a fail-safe strike or a mag. Relay 2 is a single contact you set NO or NC with the section-4.6 jumper. Snub any DC coil with a diode or MOV so the inductive kick does not pit the relay over time, and remember the tone-open codes default to 9876 on Relay 1 and 5432 on Relay 2 \u2014 map them to the right gate.",commonServicingIssues:["Random resets or intermittent faults \u2014 undersized or single transformer, or an accessory (strike/mag lock) stolen off the entry transformer; confirm two dedicated 16.5 VAC / 20 VA supplies and move accessory loads to their own power","Won't dial out / no voice path \u2014 dead POTS line, or a 1830-010 AG cellular board with no provisioned DKS account; verify the comm path and that the cellular account is live","Hum or buzz on the voice line \u2014 missing ferrite filter, or long/parallel power runs, or wiring run alongside 115V; clip the ferrite at terminals 19/20 and Aux, shorten and separate the runs","Board damaged after a storm \u2014 no ground, a fake ground (post in concrete), or missing surge suppressors; ground CGND to a real ground within 10 ft and add the two 1878-010 suppressors","Can't program at the keypad \u2014 it's a PC-programmed 80-Series; use Remote Account Manager v6.3.b or higher over the modem/data link","Loses time or programming on a power blip \u2014 no standby battery; add a 12 VDC 0.7 Ah SLA at terminal 18 (BAT) / terminal 6 (COM), two if backing up the aux side as well","Strike chatters, won't hold, or relay contacts pit over time \u2014 strike powered off the panel transformer (brown-out), or a DC strike with no snubber; give strikes a separate UL supply sized to inrush + hold and add a diode/MOV across a DC coil","Tone-open opens the wrong gate \u2014 Relay 1 (9876) and Relay 2 (5432) codes mapped to the wrong entry point; check the * 05 assignments and which relay wires which door","Locked out of programming \u2014 master code forgotten. No recovery: switch a new master code in at the cabinet (\xa73.2.1); the existing directory and settings survive, only the old code is lost","Unit will not dial out, or the resident never gets the call -> no dial tone at the line terminals, the wrong pair, or a dead VoIP/cellular path behind it -> put a test phone on the line terminals first; no dial tone means the fault is upstream of the panel, not in its programming."],relatedGuides:["dks-1834-telephone-entry","gate-operators","access-control-systems"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1834-pc-programmable-80-series/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1834-065-W-11-24_80_Series.pdf","https://www.doorking.com/","Product photos: own field photos (DKS DoorKing 1834 callbox + board), 2026"],schematic:"dks-1834-power-wiring",photo:"photo-dks-1834",photos:[{asset:"photo-dks-1834",caption:"The unit \u2014 DKS DoorKing telephone entry callbox"},{asset:"photo-dks-1834-board",caption:"Inside \u2014 1891-010 board and the labeled terminal strip"},{diagram:"dks-1834-power-wiring",caption:"Power, ground & standby wiring"},{asset:"photo-dks-entry-alloy-keypad",caption:"An 1834 in service on an exterior wall: LCD directory, A/Z/CALL buttons, speaker and keyswitch, fed by conduit from the enclosure alongside. The black keypad on its face is an aftermarket ALLOY ACCESS unit added later, sharing the same opening... a retrofit rather than a factory pairing."}]},{id:"adams-rite-7160-7170",name:"Adams Rite 7160 / 7170 Electric Strike",category:"Electric locking",whatItIs:"The Adams Rite 7160 and 7170 are Grade 1 electric strikes for mortise or cylindrical locksets with up to a 3/4\" latchbolt and no deadbolt. Get the series split right first: the 7160 is for aluminum narrow-stile applications, the 7170 for hollow-metal or wood door jambs \u2014 both on 4-7/8\" faceplates, the 7160 with radius corners, the 7170 with square corners. The wrong series still mounts and seems to work, then fails later on fit, alignment, and jamb depth, so confirm the jamb material before you order. The single most useful thing on the sheet is the model-number decoder, because the dash number tells you voltage, duty, and function at a glance \u2014 and duty mismatched to the power is exactly what kills these strikes young.",specs:["Series: 7160 = aluminum narrow-stile (radius faceplate corners); 7170 = hollow-metal or wood jamb (square corners). Both 4-7/8\" faceplate, up to 3/4\" latchbolt, no deadbolt","Model decoder digit 1 (VOLTAGE): 3 = 12V, 4 = 16V, 5 = 24V","Model decoder digit 2 (CURRENT/DUTY): 1 = DC continuous or intermittent; 4 = AC intermittent","Model decoder digit 3 (FEATURES): 0 = standard/fail-secure, 5 = fail-safe, 7 = monitor/fail-safe, 9 = monitor/fail-secure. Fail-secure/fail-safe is field-convertible either way \u2014 EXCEPT an AC intermittent solenoid must NOT be used continuously in either mode","Finish codes: 313 dark bronze anodized, 335 black anodized, 626 satin chrome, 628 clear anodized","Lip extension (Dim A, door centerline to jamb edge): 00 = 1-1/16\" or less, 50 = 1-3/8\", 01 = 1-1/2\", 02 = 1-5/8\", 03 = 1-3/4\", 05 = 2\", 07 = 2-1/4\", 09 = 2-1/2\", 12 = 2-7/8\"","Worked example 7170-510-628-00 = 24V / DC continuous-or-intermittent / standard fail-secure / clear anodized / lip for 1-1/16\" or less","Sound is the duty tell: DC continuous units are SILENT; AC intermittent units BUZZ on operation \u2014 the core power-diagnosis fact","Current draw \u2014 12V: 1.42 A AC-intermittent / 0.33 A DC-continuous; 24V: 0.74 A AC / 0.17 A DC. AC intermittent draws roughly 4x DC continuous at the same voltage","Physical (7170): case approx. 1\" x 3-3/8\" x 1-7/8\" deep; strike opening 5/8\" x 1-7/16\" x 5/8\" deep; fits jamb sections as shallow as 1-7/8\"; stainless bolt-retainer jaw; center on the latchbolt; 1/8\" minimum door gap","Retrofit: the 7170 fits existing prep for the discontinued 7560AN, 7570, 7860AN, 7870","Listings: Grade 1; UL Burglary (Electrified), file E09321, listed 323R; static strength 1,500 lbs, dynamic 70 ft-lbs; endurance 250,000 cycles (UL 1034) / 500,000 (ANSI/BHMA A156.31)","Monitor options (dash 7 / 9): two signal switches sense latchbolt-in-strike and jaw-blocked","Related parts, order separately: 4603 rectifier (AC to DC, in-line between transformer and strike, 2 A); 4605 transformer (40 VA, bracket fits a JB knockout); 4606 transformer (plug-in); PS-1 power supply; piezo horn","Compatible deadlatches: 4500 and 4900 series (4700 discontinued, replaced by 4900)","Ships with mounting screws, mounting clips, and adhesive shims for use where welded tabs are omitted","[VERIFY] 7160 case dimensions and minimum jamb depth; full 7160 dash decoder (assumed parallel to 7170, not confirmed); solenoid part numbers for field voltage/duty conversion (possible, not sourced)","Field sighting (unconfirmed at capture): Narrow-stile hollow metal frame application. Faceplate stamped ADAMS RITE with AR diamond logo. \u2014 to confirm: series number not legible \u2014 likely 7000 family, confirm"],proAdvice:"Series first, then decode the dash. The 7160 is aluminum narrow-stile with radius faceplate corners; the 7170 is hollow-metal or wood with square corners. Get it wrong and it mounts anyway, then fights you on fit and jamb depth. Then read the dash: digit one is voltage (3/4/5 = 12/16/24), digit two is duty (1 = DC, 4 = AC intermittent), digit three is function. That two-digit \u2014 the duty \u2014 is the one that matters, because duty mismatched to the building's power is what cooks a Grade 1 strike inside a year.\n\nThe trap is the AC buzz-in building. It is AC by design and it has always buzzed, so a DC strike wrongly dropped onto that AC transformer buzzes too and looks normal while it destroys itself. Diagnose by the label and the meter, not the ear \u2014 see the electric strike power diagnosis field manual. Size the supply as real math: AC intermittent pulls about four times DC continuous, two 24V AC strikes run near 35 VA, and the 4605 is 40 VA for a reason. If you convert power with a 4603 rectifier, remember it changes the type, not the amount \u2014 filtered output climbs toward 1.4x the RMS, so measure the bus under load. And whenever you change a solenoid, the case label becomes a lie: tag the actual voltage and duty on the case and the work order, or the next tech re-creates the mismatch you just fixed.",commonServicingIssues:["Dead in under a year \u2014 not wear; a power-type or overvoltage mismatch (often a DC coil on an AC buzz-in transformer cooking itself). Read the date code and the duty checkbox","Buzzes but won't release \u2014 wrong power type, or the keeper binding under door preload","Works, then fails when held open \u2014 an AC intermittent unit used continuously; it is buzz-in only, no hold-open","Second strike added and both go weak \u2014 transformer sagging under the combined load; recalculate VA (the 4605 is 40 VA for reference) and measure under load","Far strike weak, near strike fine \u2014 voltage drop on the run; check gauge and length, measure at the leads under load","Won't fire on the bench \u2014 undersized test source (a 9V battery will not pull a 24V coil); test at rated voltage and type","Wrong series installed \u2014 7160 (aluminum / radius corners) vs 7170 (hollow-metal / square corners) mounts anyway but fails on fit and jamb depth later"],relatedGuides:["electric-strike-power-diagnosis","fail-safe-fail-secure","maglocks"],sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/","https://www.assaabloyopeningsolutions.us/en/brands/adams-rite"],schematic:"adams-rite-decoder",verified:!0,photo:"photo-adams-rite-7160-7170"},{id:"dks-1802-epd",name:"DoorKing 1802 / 1802EPD Telephone Entry System",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The DoorKing 1802 is the EPD-family telephone entry panel \u2014 a callbox where a visitor dials a resident who grants access from their phone. It comes two ways: the plain 1802 with a printed directory holding 600 numbers (1000 in a legacy 7-digit mode), and the 1802EPD with an electronic 16-character LCD directory (A/Z scroll and a CALL button) holding 100. It is keypad-programmed (or remotely by touch-tone), and \u2014 this is the trap that started a whole comparison \u2014 its programming codes are the EPD set (* 17 strike, * 23 tone-open), NOT the newer 1834's codes; do not cross them. Circuit board 1862-010 Rev P or higher.",specs:["Two versions: 1802 (printed directory, 600 numbers / 1000 in legacy 7-digit mode) and 1802EPD (electronic 16-char LCD directory, A/Z scroll + CALL, 100 numbers). Surface or flush mount; board 1862-010 Rev P+","Power: 16.5 VAC, 20 VA minimum on terminals 13/14 (16AC), ferrite clipped on those leads. Panel only \u2014 never powers strikes/mags/lights; never on a switched receptacle; polarity does not matter","Relays: two, dry contacts, 30 V / 3 A max. Electric strike to NO, magnetic lock to NC, gate operator to NO. Lock power is a SEPARATE UL-listed supply (CL2-type wire)","Terminal map: 1/2 PHONE, 3 CGND (earth only, not a common), 4 PSW (switch input 1 / postal), 5 MICG, 6 MIC, 7 SPKR, 8 COM (switch-1 + speaker + battery negative), 9 BAT (12 VDC 0.7 Ah SLA positive), 10-12 Relay 1 (NC/NO/C), 13/14 16AC. Switch Input 2 and Relay 2 are separate terminals in the upper-left corner (remove the LCD to reach SW2 on the EPD)","Ground (required): 12 AWG to a ground point within 10 ft \u2014 panel, buried metallic cold-water pipe, or a 10-ft rod. A post set in concrete is NOT a ground. Bad ground = hum, dropped audio, flaky dialing","Wire: power 18 AWG <=100 ft / 16 AWG <=200 ft; phone (twisted, isolated from ground) 24 AWG <=800 ft / 22 AWG <=1600 ft; keep entry wiring 18\" off high voltage, separate conduit","Connections: POTS (voice + remote programming); DKS cellular voice-only (1801-080, AT&T 4G LTE); DKS VoIP (1815-568, Cat-5 <=325 ft); third-party ATA. Cellular/VoIP may not pass clean DTMF \u2014 program from the keypad","Programming (EPD family, NOT 1834 codes): master * 04 (switch ON, no default, unrecoverable), strike time * 17, talk * 05, tone-open sound * 08, tone-open numbers * 23 (R1 9876 / R2 5432), phone add/delete * 01, names * 66 / del * 65 / all * 67 (EPD, keypad only), directory length * 20. Read settings with * 06 / * 25","WIPE-ALL codes, unrecoverable: * 20 (directory length), * 44 (16-digit mode), * 45 (7-digit mode); also * 22 deletes all phones (9999), * 00 / * 11 delete all entry codes. Never press to \"check\" a setting","Board triage (lid open): MASTER CODE switch OFF, TONE jumper on TONE OFF, RING jumper installed, HF/HS one position only, CLCK SENSE full CCW unless rotary phones","Tone-open number logic: 4 digits per relay = momentary / hold / release / hold-1-hour; residents get momentary only; do not duplicate digits within a relay's code; use # for an unwanted function","Standby: 12 VDC 0.7 Ah SLA (DKS 1801-008), must run the system >=30 min for Canadian certification. Surge: 1877-010 (phone) / 1878-010 (low voltage) within 10 ft","UL 294: the gravity-NC tamper switch under the mic board MUST be connected to an alarm or UL 294 certification is void. Phone: RJ11C, REN 0.0, FCC Reg DUF6VT-12874-OT-T","[VERIFY] Relay 1 terminal order (NC/NO/C at 10/11/12) renders inconsistently in the source \u2014 confirm on the board silkscreen before landing wires; terminal numbering is derived, not read from a clean table; the section-3.1.12 stay-online code is illegible in the retrieved manual (page 25)","Field sighting (unconfirmed at capture): A / Z / CALL buttons, 12-key pad, LCD, key cylinder. Surface box. \u2014 to confirm: model not confirmed \u2014 could be 1802 or 1834; verify on faceplate","Powered from a plug-in 16 VAC / 20 VA (about 250 mA) transformer; the door or gate device wires to the dry Form C relay, never to the transformer"],proAdvice:"Triage the board before the phone line, because half the \"dead\" 1802s are a jumper or a switch. Master-code switch left ON long-tones every 30 seconds; the TONE jumper left on TONE ON after a feedback adjust squeals in the speaker (the number-one callback); a missing RING jumper means it never answers or programs remotely; CLCK SENSE set too high trips the relay on loud noises. Lid open, thirty seconds, and you have caught the common ones for free.\n\nThen respect two hard lines. The codes are the EPD set \u2014 * 17 strike, * 05 talk, * 23 tone-open, * 08 tone-open sound \u2014 and are NOT interchangeable with the 1834; program the wrong family and nothing happens or you hit a wipe code. And * 20, * 44, and * 45 each erase the entire directory with no undo, so on a 600-resident building you never press them to peek \u2014 read settings back with * 06 or * 25. The relays are dry contacts only: strike on NO, mag on NC, gate on NO, all on a separate UL-listed lock supply, and on a vehicular gate the box mounts at least 6 feet away or it does not get wired.\n\nAudio setup is three potentiometers on the board \u2014 SPK VOL, MIC VOL and FEEDBACK \u2014 and the manual is blunt that all three interact with each other. Set them in order, on a live call, with a real resident on the far end. Speaker volume first: place a call, and while they talk rotate the speaker pot clockwise for louder, counter-clockwise for quieter, until the visitor side is comfortable. Then microphone volume: talk into the panel in a normal voice and have the resident tell you when they can hear you properly. Adjust for the person standing at the door, not for yourself with your face against it.\n\nFeedback gets its own procedure and it is the one people skip. Call a resident and ask them to stay silent. Move the jumper from the TONE OFF terminals to TONE ON \u2014 a tone comes out of the speaker. Rotate the feedback pot clockwise, then counter-clockwise, and leave it where the tone is at its MINIMUM. That is the correct setting. Then move the jumper back to TONE OFF; leaving it on TONE ON is a callback waiting to happen.\n\nIf you cannot null the squeal, it is a levels problem, not a bad board. High speaker and high microphone volumes cause feedback together \u2014 the manual's own guidance is to back the speaker down if the mic is set high, and back the mic down if the speaker is set high. Chase it by lowering one, never by cranking the feedback pot further.\n\nBefore you touch any pot, though, look at the holes. A large share of 'bad audio' calls on these panels is mechanical obstruction, and no amount of pot-turning fixes it: paint over the speaker grille from a building repaint, RTV or sealant smeared across the microphone hole by whoever weathersealed the panel, foam or insulation packed behind the faceplate, a wasp or mud-dauber nest inside the grille, a rain hood or aftermarket bezel whose cutouts do not line up, or a gasket rotated so it covers the openings. Pull the faceplate and look at the speaker and mic holes from behind before you decide the audio circuit is at fault. That last part is field experience, not manual guidance \u2014 the manual assumes the holes are clear.",commonServicingIssues:["Long tone every ~30 seconds \u2014 master-code switch left ON","Constant tone in the speaker \u2014 TONE jumper left on TONE ON after a feedback adjustment (the number-one callback)","Won't answer inbound / no remote programming \u2014 RING jumper missing","Relay trips on loud noise \u2014 CLCK SENSE set too high; full CCW unless the site has rotary phones","Hum, dropped audio, flaky dialing \u2014 bad ground; 12 AWG to a real ground within 10 ft, never a post in concrete","Won't dial at all \u2014 line side; put a butt set on the PHONE terminals for dial tone before touching any * code","Remote programming fails on cellular or VoIP \u2014 DTMF not passing cleanly; program from the keypad","Board dead with no clear cause \u2014 vibration mount (slamming door/gate) or static discharge during service; discharge on a real ground before handling","Audio too quiet, too loud, or one-way \u2014 set the three board pots in order on a live call: SPK VOL, then MIC VOL, then FEEDBACK. They interact, so a change to one usually means revisiting the others","Squeal or howl on every call \u2014 high speaker and high mic volumes feed each other back. Lower one rather than cranking the feedback pot: back the speaker off if the mic is high, or the mic off if the speaker is high","Feedback never nulls out \u2014 run the actual procedure: resident silent on the line, jumper moved from TONE OFF to TONE ON, rotate the feedback pot to where the tone is MINIMUM, then move the jumper back to TONE OFF. A jumper left on TONE ON is its own callback","Muffled or dead audio that no pot adjustment fixes \u2014 check for mechanical obstruction before the circuit. Paint over the speaker grille, RTV smeared across the mic hole during weathersealing, foam or insulation behind the faceplate, an insect nest in the grille, or a rain hood / custom bezel that does not line up with the openings. Pull the faceplate and inspect the holes from behind","Panel responds to loud noises and opens on its own \u2014 click sensitivity is set too high. It is only needed for rotary-dial phones; if none are in use, run it fully counter-clockwise to disable it. Otherwise back it off 1/8 turn at a time and retest","Cannot program over the phone line -> carriers no longer reliably pass analog modem tones, so POTS/modem programming is not dependable -> program over IP or on site via RS-232 instead."],relatedGuides:["dks-1802-telephone-entry","dks-1834-vs-epd","dks-1834-telephone-entry","intercom-triage","cable-noise-hum-rfi"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/","https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1802-entry-system/"],schematic:"dks-1802-terminals",verified:!0,photo:"photo-dks-1802-epd"},{id:"medeco-high-security",name:"Medeco High-Security Cylinders (Original / Biaxial / M3 / M4)",category:"Cores & cylinders",whatItIs:"Medeco is the benchmark high-security pin-tumbler platform, and its whole security argument is one idea: a Medeco bitting carries three coordinates per position, not one. Depth sets how far the pin lifts (the shear line). Angle sets how far the pin rotates (the sidebar). Fore/aft is the cut's offset from nominal spacing (also the sidebar). Both the shear line AND the sidebar must be satisfied to turn the plug \u2014 that single fact is the pinning problem, the picking problem, and the masterkeying-capacity story at once. At the bench, angle comes from the key and fore/aft is a pin-selection decision: pull an aft pin for a fore cut and the tip sits wrong, the sidebar never drops, and you chase a phantom \"key won't turn.\" NOTE: the depth-and-space, angles, and pin lengths below are now sourced from a certified-locksmith bitting reference (cross-checked, see the first spec) \u2014 bench-usable, but confirm on a pin-kit lid before a critical cut; keyway-section codes and SKUs remain dealer-only.",specs:["CONFIDENCE: depth-and-space below is compiled from a certified-locksmith bitting-spec reference (Key Bitting Specifications (Tyler J. Thomas, CJIL/CMKA/CRL \u2014 LSA Michigan, Ed.1 2025)), covering Biaxial / Original / X4 / M3 / M4, and cross-checked against the published D&S card \u2014 HIGH confidence, but a professional compilation, not literally Medeco's factory manual. Confirm on a pin-kit lid before a critical cut","MEASUREMENT OFFSETS (read before trusting a caliper): Medeco bottom pins measure ~.012\" SHORTER than the listed reference length, and cut depths measure ~.008\" SHALLOWER with standard calipers. So a chart's \"reference depth\" and its \"root depth\" differ by ~.008\" \u2014 know which convention a number is in","Common pin data (Biaxial / M3 / M4 share these): pins .135\" dia, 85\xb0 tip; bottom-pin REFERENCE lengths #1-6 = .239/.264/.289/.314/.339/.364\" (.025\" steps; measured ~.012\" less); master pins .025/.050/.075/.100/.125\"; top pins .270/.240/.210/.210/.180/.150\". Stack is NOT constant \u2014 must fall between .499\" and .524\"; top pin makes up the plug total","BIAXIAL depth-and-space: 6 depths, .025\" increment, single-step, included angle 86\xb0. Root depths #1-6 = .264/.239/.214/.189/.164/.139\". Spacing (fore / aft) P1 .213/.275, P2 .383/.445, P3 .553/.615, P4 .728/.785, P5 .893/.955, P6 1.063/1.125\". MACS varies \u2014 see the reference's MACS-violation table","M4 depth-and-space: 6 depths, .025\" increment, single-step, 86\xb0 included angle. Root depths #1-6 = .272/.247/.222/.197/.172/.147\". Spacing same as Biaxial (P1 .213/.275 \u2026 P6 1.063/1.125). Sidecut (side bitting) positions .329/.499/.669/.839\" at depths .084/.064/.044\" (position 4 N/A). MACS varies (see table)","M3 depth-and-space: shares the Biaxial pin stack and .025\" increment; spacing same as Biaxial. Angle codes Fore K/B/Q \xb7 Aft M/D/S. Side-bitting locations #1-6 = .6085/.5585/.5085/.4585/.4085/.3585\" (change = master). Original (75-series) is a SEPARATE chart: .030\" full-step / .015\" half-step, MACS 4/8, root cut .015\"; X4 is .0125\" two-step, 90\xb0 included angle, root cut .054\"","Generations: Original (1968, angled cuts, 3 angles left/center/right); Biaxial (1985, adds fore/aft offset for 6 orientations per position); M3 (2004, keyway slider driven by a lug on the blade); M4 (2019, side pins replace the slider, movable shuttle pin). Field ID: an \"M4\" logo left of the keyway","Biaxial / M3 / M4 SHARE bottom pins, master pins, top pins, spacing, and depths \u2014 the differences are entirely in the sidebar mechanism and secondary cuts. This shared-but-different pattern is the #1 source of false generalization: right about the pin stack, wrong about the sidebar","M4 side pins (4): 3 finger pins read factory secondary cuts on the blade, 1 lift pin is driven by the key's shuttle pin; a flipper rotates to align the sidebar engagement lug. M4 uses 5-6 tumblers; the sidebar is redesigned with cutouts for the side pins","Key anatomy: primary/angled cuts are SHOP-cut (lift and rotate the tumblers); secondary side-cuts are FACTORY-cut (read by the finger pins); the shuttle pin is a factory-installed 303 stainless movable element permanently retained in the blade (drives the lift pin). Anti-3D-print: you can print the geometry, not a moving steel part \u2014 no shuttle pin, no sidebar drop","\u26a0\ufe0f An M4 \"blank\" is NOT a blank: secondary cuts + the shuttle pin arrive from the factory assigned to a specific keyway. Your machine cuts primary cuts only, so any M4 blank is not any M4 job \u2014 it must be the right sidecut for that system. The bin is accountable inventory, not consumable stock (the same was true of M3 factory-cut sliders)","Parts: housing with hardened steel anti-drill inserts, plug, sidebar (the whole security argument), cam, top pins (standard/mushroom/barrel), bottom pins (depth x angle x fore/aft), master pins/wafers, springs and retainers. Solid brass; M4 is UL 437 rated, ANSI/BHMA tested","Machine: origination, not duplication \u2014 it indexes each position independently (set angle, set depth, cut, advance). A Biaxial/M3 machine cuts M4 with the right jaw or jaw accessory. LAB kits cover the shared pin stack; kit lids carry FORE and AFT columns per depth","Key control is contractual, not just mechanical: restricted/patented keyways, a signed authorization card on file, factory- or dealer-only blanks. The counter sequence is \"are you on the card\" THEN \"can we cut it\" \u2014 never the reverse","RESOLVED via the reference: cut angles are Left \u221220\xb0 / Center 0\xb0 / Right +20\xb0 (included angle 86\xb0 Biaxial/M3/M4, 90\xb0 X4); root depths, spacing, pin/master/top lengths and stack height as above. STILL open (OEM/dealer only): exact keyway-section designations, part numbers/SKUs, the factory side-bitting/level codes on Primus-style secondary cuts, and full MACS-violation tables (this entry cites \"varies \u2014 see table,\" not the grid)"],proAdvice:"Hold the three-coordinate model in your head and most Medeco confusion clears: every position is depth, angle, and fore/aft, and the plug turns only when both the shear line and the sidebar are satisfied. Angle is cut into the key; fore/aft is which bottom pin you pick. The most common bench failure is a fore/aft pin picked wrong for the cut \u2014 the key lifts fine and still will not turn because the sidebar never drops.\n\nTwo traps beyond the mechanics. First, \"shared but different\": Biaxial, M3, and M4 share the pin stack, spacing, and depths, so a tech who learned Biaxial assumes it all transfers and is right about the pins and wrong about the sidebar and secondary cuts. Second, the M4 blank is not a blank \u2014 factory secondary cuts and a permanently-retained shuttle pin make it a pre-committed, keyway-assigned credential, so \"cut me one more\" is a purchase order against the system and accountable inventory, not a two-minute duplicate. And at the counter, key control runs first: are you on the authorization card, THEN can we cut it. One discipline for this entry specifically \u2014 it is concepts only, with no confirmed numbers, so do not read depths, spacings, or angles off it; pull the Medeco depth-and-space chart and the pin-kit lid before you touch a cylinder.",commonServicingIssues:["Key lifts fine but won't turn \u2014 wrong fore/aft pin selection: an aft pin under a fore cut leaves the tip wrong and the sidebar never drops. Verify pin fore/aft against the key, not just depth","Biaxial pinning \"knowledge\" fails on M4 \u2014 the pin stack transfers, the sidebar mechanism and secondary cuts do NOT; M4 adds side / finger / lift pins and the shuttle pin","Customer wants \"one more key\" from an M4 blank \u2014 a blank is a pre-committed, keyway-assigned credential (factory secondary cuts + shuttle pin); it is a purchase order against the system, not a duplicate","A scanned or 3D-printed M4 key won't work \u2014 no shuttle pin (a moving steel part), so the lift pin never sets and the sidebar never drops","Sidebar won't drop after a re-pin \u2014 a gate not aligned; every position's depth AND angle AND fore/aft must satisfy both the shear line and the sidebar","Numbers: depth-and-space for Biaxial / Original / X4 / M3 / M4 is now sourced (certified-locksmith reference, cross-checked) \u2014 usable at the bench, but mind the .012\"/.008\" caliper offsets and confirm against a pin-kit lid before a critical cut. Keyway-section designations, SKUs, and full MACS grids remain dealer-only"],relatedGuides:["medeco-picking","medeco-lishi-decode","high-security-keyways","t-master-keying","keyway-blank-id"],sources:["https://www.medeco.com/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/","https://resources.locksandsafes.com/depthsandspaces/medeco-biaxial/","https://lsamichigan.org/Tech/KeyBittingSpecifications-TylerJThomas.pdf"],schematic:"medeco-three-coordinate",photos:[{asset:"medeco-m4-exploded",caption:"M4 exploded \u2014 housing with steel inserts, plug, sidebar, top/bottom pins, and the M4 side/shuttle pins that read the factory secondary cuts"},{asset:"medeco-parts-exploded",caption:"Full-size cylinder \u2014 shell, plug, double-locking sidebar, rotating bottom pins, finger/lift pins, and the multiple drill-resistant inserts"},{diagram:"medeco-three-coordinate",caption:"The three-coordinate bitting \u2014 depth sets the shear line, angle and fore/aft set the sidebar"}]},{id:"schlage-primus",name:"Schlage Primus / Primus XP High-Security Cylinder",category:"Cores & cylinders",whatItIs:"The Schlage Primus is a two-system high-security cylinder: a conventional Schlage pin-tumbler stack you rekey normally, PLUS an independent sidebar that a row of finger pins releases only when they read the correct angled side bitting off the key blade. Both systems must be satisfied to turn the plug. Primus (Classic) sits on the Schlage Classic keyway; Primus XP sits on the Everest 29 keyway and also carries the Everest check pin. The side bitting is the key-control level \u2014 restricted, patented, and authorized-only \u2014 so the counter reality is closer to Medeco than to a hardware-store Schlage.",specs:["Two independent locking systems: a conventional Schlage pin-tumbler (standard bitting, field-rekeyable) plus a sidebar released by finger pins that read the key's side bitting (angled side cuts). Both must be satisfied to turn","Primus (Classic) = Schlage Classic keyway; Primus XP = Everest 29 keyway (also carries the Everest check pin / undercut)","Side bitting = the key-control LEVEL (dealer / geographic / single-end-user exclusivity); the cylinder's finger pins are set to that level. Restricted and patented \u2014 blanks and side-cutting are authorized-only, on a Primus-capable machine","Listing: UL 437. Available across most Schlage cylinder formats (rim, mortise, key-in-knob/lever, FSIC/LFIC 6- and 7-pin)","Field rekey changes the STANDARD pin stack only (normal Schlage pinning with a standard kit); the sidebar, finger pins, and side bitting are the level and are NOT a bench rekey","[VERIFY / restricted] standard-bitting depth-and-space (Schlage Classic on Primus / Everest on XP \u2014 confirm exact increments and root depths against a Schlage source); finger-pin heights, side-cut angles, and level codes are restricted and NOT sourced here"],proAdvice:"Split every Primus problem into \"top\" and \"side\" and it stops being mysterious. The top is a normal Schlage \u2014 rekey the bottom pins to the new standard bitting with a standard kit and you are done there, and you never touch the sidebar or finger pins doing it. The side is the level: the finger pins are set to a controlled side bitting, so your new keys must carry the same level, and changing the level is a Primus-authorized job, not a bench rekey. When a key with the right top cuts will not turn, you are on the side \u2014 wrong level, worn side cuts, or a bound sidebar \u2014 not the pins.\n\nTwo practical notes. A Primus key wears out its side bitting on a ring before its top cuts look worn, so an intermittently-failing old key is often just tired side cuts; cut a fresh one on the level. And Primus XP adds the Everest check pin on top of the sidebar, so an XP key has to lift the undercut AND satisfy the sidebar AND reach the shear line \u2014 three things, and a wrong or worn Everest blank fails it before the sidebar ever matters. Do not reproduce or guess the side-bitting or level numbers; source them from Schlage.",commonServicingIssues:["Correct standard cuts but won't turn \u2014 the side: wrong-level key, worn side bitting, a bound/gummy sidebar, or a stuck finger pin. Clean and lube the sidebar channel; confirm the key's level matches the cylinder","Intermittent failure on an old key \u2014 side cuts worn round from years on a ring (they wear before the top cuts); cut a fresh key on the correct level","Rekey done but new key won't work \u2014 new key cut to the right standard bitting but wrong (or missing) side bitting / level; Primus keys must carry the system's level","Primus XP key won't enter or turn \u2014 the Everest side: wrong-generation blank or a stuck check pin, failing before the sidebar; verify the Everest undercut/keyway first","Plug won't reassemble after a rekey \u2014 sidebar or finger-pin components disturbed or misseated; the standard rekey should not touch them, so reset the sidebar assembly"],relatedGuides:["schlage-primus","schlage-everest","high-security-keyways"],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/primus-security-upgrade.html","https://us.allegion.com/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Schlage_Primus","US Patent 4,756,177 (Bo Wid\xe9n, 1988), public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"],photo:"photo-schlage-primus"},{id:"schlage-everest",name:"Schlage Everest Keyway Cylinder (B / 29)",category:"Cores & cylinders",whatItIs:"Everest is Schlage's patented, restricted keyway family. Its defining feature is a check pin in the bottom of the keyway that the plug cannot turn past until an undercut (a ramped groove along the bottom of the key blade) lifts it \u2014 so a non-Everest blank, even correctly bitted, will not work. The check pin is a key-control and patent feature, not a combination element, which is why rekeying an Everest is normal Schlage pinning while the blank itself is controlled. Everest B is the original generation, Everest 29 the current one; Everest also underpins Primus XP.",specs:["Check pin: a spring-loaded pin in the keyway that blocks plug rotation until the key's undercut (a bottom-of-blade ramp) lifts it. A non-Everest blank cannot raise it \u2014 key control by patented keyway, NOT a combination element","Generations: Everest B (original patent family) and Everest 29 (current generation, revised keyway/undercut and fresh patent term). Everest 29 also underpins Schlage Primus XP (Primus sidebar over the Everest check pin)","Restricted / patented blanks \u2014 controlled distribution; not sold at big-box retail. Use the correct generation blank (B vs 29) and keyway section","Field rekey = standard Schlage pinning (change the bottom pins with a standard kit); the check pin is left alone. New keys MUST be Everest blanks with the correct undercut or they will not lift the check pin","[VERIFY / restricted] Everest 29 depth-and-space is SHIFTED from Schlage Classic (do not cut an Everest key on a Classic chart); exact depths, the undercut dimension, and keyway-section designations are restricted and NOT sourced here"],proAdvice:"The mental model that prevents wasted keys: the check pin is a gate on the KEYWAY, not on the combination. Rekey an Everest exactly like any Schlage \u2014 bottom pins to the new bitting, check pin untouched \u2014 but the moment you cut a key, the blank has to be a real Everest of the right generation, because a perfectly bitted non-Everest (or wrong-generation) key cannot lift the check pin and will not turn. Confirm B versus 29 before you pull a blank.\n\nWhen a cut-right key fails, work the keyway: wrong-generation blank, a worn undercut ramp, or a check pin stuck or corroded. Clean and lube the keyway and check the pin springs freely, and remember a tired old key that lost its undercut ramp will fail intermittently \u2014 cut a fresh one on the correct blank before condemning the cylinder. And do not cut Everest bitting off a Classic depth-and-space; the Everest chart is shifted, so source it from Schlage rather than assuming.",commonServicingIssues:["Correctly bitted key won't enter or turn \u2014 non-Everest or wrong-generation blank (B vs 29); it cannot lift the check pin. Verify the blank before re-cutting","Intermittent failure on an old key \u2014 worn undercut ramp no longer lifting the check pin reliably; cut a fresh key on the right Everest blank","Plug binds / won't turn with a known-good key \u2014 check pin gummed or corroded, stuck up or down; clean and lightly lube the keyway, confirm the pin springs free","New key cut on a Classic chart won't work \u2014 Everest 29 depth-and-space is shifted from Classic; use the Everest chart","Primus XP won't work but standard cuts are right \u2014 the Everest check pin failing ahead of the Primus sidebar; diagnose the undercut/keyway first"],relatedGuides:["schlage-everest","schlage-primus","high-security-keyways","keyway-blank-id"],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/everest-29-keyways.html","https://us.allegion.com/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Schlage_Everest"],schematic:"schlage-everest-checkpin"},{id:"ses-tec1-series",name:"SES TEC1 Series Telephone Entry (T1HF50)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Select Engineered Systems' basic telephone-entry callbox \u2014 a visitor scrolls the LCD directory, keys a dialing code, the panel dials the resident, and the resident releases the door with a touch-tone from their own phone. It is sold by display size and capacity: TEC1 is the two-line LCD, TEC1M the four-line, and both come in 50, 125, 250, 500 and 995-name capacities. T1HF50 is the hands-free (no handset) 50-name unit. The 'CA' suffix \u2014 TEC1CA, TEC1MCA \u2014 adds card-access capability plus a modem, clock/calendar and SES's basic software; a plain TEC1 is programmed entirely at the keypad. Two dry-contact entrance relays are standard on every model and every board ships with surge suppression at no extra cost. Stainless front door, aluminum back box. The user guide covers firmware 1.XX across TEC101, TEC1, TEC1C and TEC1CA.",specs:["Models: TEC1 (2-line LCD), TEC1M (4-line LCD); CA variants (TEC1CA / TEC1MCA) add card access, modem and clock/calendar. Capacities 50 / 125 / 250 / 500 / 995 names \u2014 T1HF50 = hands-free, 50 names","Power: 16.5 VAC 20 VA transformer, supplied. Heads up \u2014 SES's own documents disagree: the product sheet says 16.5 VAC, the installer spec page says 18 VAC, and the connection table says 16 VAC. Meter what is actually landed on terminals 3/4 rather than trusting the page in front of you","Relays: two entrance relays, Form A or B dry contact, rated 24 V AC or DC at 3 A. N/O or N/C is a jumper selection on the main board \u2014 the relays are dry contacts only, they switch the lock's own supply and never power it","Terminals 1-12: 1/2 phone line, 3/4 SES transformer, 5 COMMON (also the optional 12 V battery negative), 6 battery positive, 7 REX door 1 (N/O), 8 REX door 2 (N/O), 9/10 door 1 relay contacts, 11/12 door 2 relay contacts","Transformer run: 16 Ga twisted, 50 feet maximum. Ground is REQUIRED \u2014 earth ground or cold-water pipe at the ground stud, 16 Ga or larger","Programming: fully keypad-programmable, or by modem on CA models. Code length programmable 1-6 digits, strike-out count 0-9 tries, talk time 1-99 minutes, dials out up to 14 digits tone or rotary","Enclosure: stainless steel front door, aluminum back box, 8 1/2\" W x 11 3/4\" H x 3 1/2\" D, roughly 11-14 lb shipping. Surface, semi-flush ring or pedestal mount","Environment: 0 to +158 \xb0F, 5% to 95% RH non-condensing. Memory is non-volatile \u2014 the standby battery is NOT what holds your programming","Tone detection: crystal controlled, will detect a 50 ms burst. Postal lock capability included, REX input on both relays, programmable Hide-a-Name to conceal resident identities","Options: OPTKMUI Multi-Unit Interface (up to four TEC1s share one phone line), pinhole color camera, nite-lite weather hood, semi-flush ring, standby battery, Alliance voice processor (no-phone-bill), blue backlit keypad, NIC card","Non-POTS paths: a VoIP bridge, or the SES SCMI cellular interface, when the site has no clean copper left","Successor: the TEC1A series raises capacity to 1000 users with 10 facility codes; the TEC1A PREMIUM adds an Ethernet NIC and two 26-bit Wiegand reader inputs"],proAdvice:"Getting into programming is the part nobody remembers on the truck: press * and 0 AT THE SAME TIME \u2014 not in sequence \u2014 and the display prompts PASSWORD. Key the password then *. The factory default is 777777. The TEC1 guide gives '777' on page 7 under Add a New Name, but that is a documented manual error \u2014 every other page in the book says 777777, and 777777 is the one that works. Star and 0 together also exits from anywhere, and 0 with # is backspace during entry. Two timers to know: you get 6 seconds to start entering the password after the prompt appears, and 60 seconds of no keypress during programming drops you back to normal operation. From there the main menu is numbered 1-9 and every procedure starts from that same two-step entry.\n\nThe password is one credential in three tiers, and this is the trap. Changing the program password changes all three at once: the full 6 digits is the program tier and gets everything, the FIRST THREE digits are the manager tier and get Codes/Names, and the FIRST TWO are the control tier and get remote touch-tone relay control. Whoever you give the first three digits to can edit the directory. Change it at 4 (Comm Setup), 3, new password, star star. One more: the front keypad takes digits only, but over a terminal the password can be alphanumeric \u2014 if a previous installer set one with letters in it, keypad programming is locked out entirely and recovery is a terminal or another factory reset.\n\nThe factory tone-open digit is 6. A resident presses 6 during the call and entrance 1 releases. That single default is why so many of these buildings are effectively open \u2014 anybody who has ever been buzzed into the place has learned to press 6, and it keeps working after they move out. Changing the tone-open digit and the program password off 777/777777 is the minimum you do before you leave a takeover, and it is the first thing to check when a manager says people are getting in who should not be.\n\nNon-volatile memory is worth knowing because it kills a common misdiagnosis: a dead standby battery does NOT wipe the directory. If a panel came back blank, the battery is not your answer \u2014 look at the board. And the two entrance relays are dry contacts rated 24 V at 3 A, which means the lock runs on its own separate supply. A strike pulled off the entry transformer is the classic cause of a panel that resets or browns out every time somebody opens the door.\n\nTwo things to know before you quote work on one. Programming is keypad-based on the base unit and by modem on CA models, with SELCOM as the PC-side software... which matters because modem programming over a carrier line is increasingly unreliable, the same way it is on a DoorKing. And when a customer on an aging TEC1 asks for card readers or a network connection, the base platform is telephone-entry-first and will not grow into it: that request is a move to the TEC1A PREMIUM, which carries the Ethernet NIC and two Wiegand reader inputs, or to a different platform entirely. Price the migration, not an upgrade.",commonServicingIssues:["Fob system appears to have vanished \u2014 no CARD field when editing a resident and fobs do nothing, while the directory is still intact. Somebody ran Clear Data > Set Parameters (8 then 1), which zeroes the site code to 000, and 000 both disables card reading and hides the CARD field. Card numbers on the resident records usually survive, so restoring the site code brings it back with no re-enrollment. See the SES TEC1 field triage guide","Cannot get into programming \u2014 * and 0 must be pressed together, not one after the other, then password followed by *. The default is 777777; the '777' printed on page 7 of the TEC1 guide is a known manual error. You also only get 6 seconds to start typing after the prompt","Keypad programming refuses every password \u2014 a previous installer set an alphanumeric password over a terminal. The front keypad is digits only, so recovery is a terminal session or another factory reset","Anyone can open the door / residents share a magic number \u2014 the factory tone-open digit 6 was never changed. Change it and change the program password","Panel resets or browns out when the door is used \u2014 a strike or mag is being fed from the entry transformer. The relays are dry contacts; the lock gets its own supply","Surge or storm damage despite the board-level suppression \u2014 the unit was never earth grounded. SES requires a ground at the stud to earth or cold-water pipe, 16 Ga or larger","Flaky or dead panel on a long transformer run \u2014 SES specifies 16 Ga twisted and 50 feet maximum. Undersized or overlong power fakes every other symptom","Relay is closing but the lock does not move, or vice versa \u2014 N/O vs N/C is a jumper on the main board, and fail-safe vs fail-secure has to match the lock. Meter across the contacts before condemning either end","Will not dial out after a phone-service change \u2014 these are POTS-era dialers, and a fiber/VoIP conversion frequently breaks dial-out or the voice path. Confirm with a test set at the demarc before touching the panel","Locked out of programming \u2014 the password was changed by a previous installer. There is no published backdoor; recovery goes back to SES or the prior integrator","Site went VoIP or lost its copper and the panel stopped dialing -> base TEC1 expects a real line -> fit the VoIP bridge or the SCMI cellular interface, and confirm audio quality after, since two-way breaks up before dialing fails."],sources:["https://www.selectses.com/clientuploads/TEC%201%20PDF/TEC1_Series_Product_Sheet.pdf","https://www.selectses.com/clientuploads/TEC%201%20PDF/TEC1_Manual_8_28_23_REV.pdf"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Specs and programming taken from the SES TEC1 product sheet and the TEC1 user programming guide (firmware 1.XX). The field photo on this entry is a confirmed SES panel \u2014 the Select Engineered Systems logo and a 'Sound Communication Systems' installer plate are both visible \u2014 but no model number is legible on the faceplate, so it is not proven to be a TEC1. Confirm at the board silkscreen inside the enclosure before ordering parts.",photo:"photo-ses-telephone-entry-panel",relatedGuides:["ses-tec1-triage","intercom-triage"]},{id:"ses-tec2-series",name:"SES TEC Series Telephone Access \u2014 TEC2 (T2HF50)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The step up from the TEC1, and the platform SES documents together with the TEC4 and TEC10 in a single book (600TECMANUAL, TEC Series v3.2X). Same core job \u2014 directory, dial the resident, touch-tone release \u2014 but with the programming and reporting depth the TEC1 does not have: separate Program and Control passwords, a Manager password, latching entrance relays, an RS-232 port for a printer or CRT or computer, a transaction buffer that logs events, plus time zones, holidays and access levels. T2HF50 is the hands-free 50-name unit. If you are standing in front of one of these, the TEC2 manual is the thick one and the answer is almost always in it.",specs:["Documentation: 600TECMANUAL (Mar 2015), TEC Series v3.2X \u2014 one manual covering TEC2, TEC4 and TEC10. T2HF50 = hands-free, 50 names","Power: 16.5 VAC 20 VA UL-listed transformer, provided, 16 Ga to 50 ft. The manual is explicit: NEVER use the AC transformer and a DC supply at the same time","Relays: two entrance relays, dry contact. Entrance 1 flips N.O./N.C. at jumper 201-1&2, entrance 2 at jumper 202-1&2. Relays can be latched, which requires the Remote Control Password","Terminal barrier strip, in order: shield terminal, phone line (two), one unused, transformer (two), COMMON to battery and REX inputs (three), backup battery positive, N.O. REX #1, N.O. REX #2, entrance 1 N.O./N.C., entrance 2 N.O./N.C.","Ground: the manual states the TEC unit MUST be grounded, 16 Ga or larger","Passwords: Program and Control passwords are separate, and the Manager password is the FIRST THREE DIGITS of the Program password. All TEC systems ship from SES with a factory password of 777777","Comms: RS-232 port, current loop, or NIC interface to a printer, terminal or computer. Baud rates 300, 600, 1200, 2400, 4800, 9600, 19200","Standby battery (OPTB12V) is optional and is NOT required for memory storage. Transaction buffer size scales inversely with unit capacity \u2014 the smaller the capacity, the larger the buffer"],proAdvice:"Two passwords is what trips people on a TEC2 that a TEC1 tech is servicing. Program password gets you into configuration; Control password is what unlocks remote relay control and latching. They are not the same field and knowing one does not give you the other.\n\nOn the serial/CRT side there is a timer that catches everyone: after you press Esc the PASSWORD prompt appears and you have TEN SECONDS to start entering it. Wait longer and the unit reverts to normal operation and you press Esc and start again. Characters echo as asterisks so you get no feedback on a typo. Factory is 777777. At the keypad it behaves like the TEC1 \u2014 * and 0 together.\n\nThe Manager password being the first three digits of the Program password is a genuine trap: change the Program password and you have silently changed the Manager password too, and the on-site manager who used to get into the directory suddenly cannot. Tell them the new three digits before you drive away. Factory tone-open is 6 here as well, same as the TEC1, and it deserves the same treatment.",commonServicingIssues:["Password accepted at the keypad but rejected on the serial port, or vice versa \u2014 Program and Control passwords are separate credentials. Confirm which one the procedure actually wants","Serial login keeps failing \u2014 the PASSWORD prompt times out ten seconds after Esc. Have the password ready before you press it; characters echo as asterisks so typos are invisible","The building manager lost directory access right after a password change \u2014 the Manager password is the first three digits of the Program password and moved with it","Anyone can open the door \u2014 factory tone-open digit 6 was never changed, same default as the TEC1","Relay wired but the lock behaves backwards \u2014 entrance 1 N.O./N.C. is jumper 201-1&2 and entrance 2 is 202-1&2. Confirm the jumper before rewiring the lock","Board damage after adding a supply \u2014 the manual forbids running the AC transformer and a DC supply simultaneously. Pick one","Event log is shorter than expected \u2014 the transaction buffer shrinks as unit capacity grows. A high-capacity TEC holds fewer transactions, which is by design"],sources:["https://www.selectses.com/clientuploads/TEC%202%20PDF/man_t2_32x.pdf"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Specs, terminal strip, passwords and jumper numbers taken from the SES 600TECMANUAL (TEC Series v3.2X, Mar 2015), which documents TEC2/TEC4/TEC10 together. Confirm capacity-specific details against the unit's own label.",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"strike-rectifier",name:"Full-Wave Bridge Rectifier (AC to DC for strikes)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Four diodes in a bridge that turn the AC off a transformer into DC for an electric strike or lock. The reason you care is noise: an AC-driven strike coil buzzes at line frequency by design \u2014 that loud rasp people call a 'buzz-in' \u2014 while the same strike on DC is silent and holds harder. Rectifiers reach you three ways: built into the strike or its faceplate, as a small standalone module you land between the transformer and the lock, or as four discrete diodes already on the power supply board. When a customer says their strike is 'too loud' and the strike is otherwise working correctly, this is the part that fixes it.",specs:["Function: full-wave bridge \u2014 AC in on two legs, DC out on + and \u2212. Output polarity matters; DC strikes and mags are polarity-sensitive and many will simply not actuate backwards","The voltage trap: an UNFILTERED bridge outputs an average of about 0.9 x the AC RMS, so 24 VAC gives roughly 21.6 VDC. Add a smoothing capacitor and the output rises to the PEAK \u2014 about 1.414 x RMS minus two diode drops \u2014 so the same 24 VAC gives roughly 32 VDC. A 24 VDC coil fed from a filtered bridge on a 24 VAC transformer sees ~32 V and cooks","Sizing: the bridge has to carry the strike's inrush, not just its holding current. Rate it well above the coil draw and heatsink it if it runs warm in the can","Duty: rectifying does NOT convert an intermittent-duty strike into a continuous-duty one. An intermittent strike held energized burns out on DC exactly as it does on AC","Suppression: fit a flyback diode across a DC coil, or an MOV or RC snubber across an AC coil, to clamp the back-EMF that pits relay and REX contacts over time"],proAdvice:"Before you buy anything, check whether the strike is already dual-voltage or has the rectifier built in \u2014 a lot of modern strikes are field-selectable AC/DC on a jumper or a slide, and people order a rectifier for a lock that has one inside it. Meter the coil terminals while it fires: AC and buzzing means you have something to fix, DC and silent means the noise is mechanical and you are chasing the wrong fault.\n\nThe capacitor question is the one that burns hardware. Unfiltered, a bridge on 24 VAC lands near 21.6 VDC and a 24 V coil is happy. Filtered, it lands near 32 VDC and that same coil runs hot and dies early. If you are building this yourself rather than buying a module, know which one you have made and meter the output under load before you connect the lock.\n\nAnd remember that silence is not the only reason to rectify. DC coils hold more consistently and draw less than the equivalent AC coil, so a marginal strike on a long run sometimes firms up on DC. But if the strike is dropping out because of voltage drop down a long wire, the rectifier is not your fix \u2014 bigger wire or a closer supply is.",commonServicingIssues:["Strike buzzes loudly and the customer wants it stopped \u2014 it is running on AC. Rectify to DC, or reconfigure the strike if it is already dual-voltage","New coil burned out within weeks \u2014 a filtered bridge on a 24 VAC transformer is delivering ~32 VDC to a 24 VDC coil. Meter the DC output under load","Strike does not actuate at all after rectifying \u2014 DC devices are polarity-sensitive and the output leads are reversed","Rectifier runs hot or fails repeatedly \u2014 sized to holding current instead of inrush, or mounted with no airflow in a sealed can","Relay or REX contacts pitting and welding over time \u2014 no flyback diode or snubber across the coil, so the back-EMF is arcing across the contacts every release","Rectified the strike and it still burns out \u2014 the strike is intermittent duty and something is holding it energized. Rectifying never fixes a duty-cycle problem"],sources:[],verified:!1,verifyNote:"Class-level part rather than a single manufacturer SKU \u2014 the electrical behaviour here is general, but confirm voltage, current, duty rating and built-in rectification against the specific strike's own data sheet before ordering."},{id:"akuvox-video-door-station",name:"Akuvox video door station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"An Android-based IP video door station \u2014 dual camera, colour touchscreen with Call / PIN / Directory softkeys, backlit metal keypad, speaker grille, dropped into a custom stainless bezel. This is a different animal from the SES, DKS and Linear panels elsewhere in this catalog: there is no phone line and no dialer. It is a network device that registers to SIP and rides Ethernet, so the failure modes are network failure modes and the service tool is a browser, not a keypad. The unit photographed is confirmed Akuvox but its model is not legible; the X915 and R29 series are the two families matching this layout, and the notes below hold across both.",specs:["Manufacturer: Akuvox. Android-based SIP video door phone. Candidate families for this unit: X915 series and R29 series \u2014 confirm before following either admin guide","Power: PoE (R29 takes PoE on the Ethernet connector; X915 is specified PoE+). Both accept an external supply instead \u2014 the R29 has a 12V/GND terminal for when PoE is not available","Network: DHCP is the factory default on both families; static IP is a configuration change. Web administration is over the device's IP in a browser","Web login: the initial account is 'admin'. The R29 guide gives the initial web credentials as admin / admin","Relays: the R29 series provides three relays (RelayA/B/C) with N.O./N.C. control terminals. Relay hold and trigger delay are both configurable in the web UI under Intercom > Relay","The relays are dry contacts \u2014 the manual is explicit that a relay 'operates a switch and does not deliver power'. The lock runs on its own supply","All relays are enabled for unlock by default on the R29; you disable the ones you do not want openable rather than enabling the ones you do","An HTTP API can trip the door remotely, of the form http://<ip>/fcgi/do?action=OpenDoor&UserName=admin&Password=<pw> \u2014 which is exactly why the default credentials matter"],proAdvice:"Treat this as a network appliance that happens to open a door. When it 'stops working', the chain is power (PoE), link, IP, SIP registration, then the relay \u2014 walk it in that order and you will find the break faster than poking at the touchscreen.\n\nThe credential situation is the thing to fix first on any takeover. The initial web account is admin, and the R29 guide documents admin/admin as the shipped web login. Combine that with the HTTP door-open API \u2014 a plain GET carrying the username and password in the query string \u2014 and an unchanged default on a reachable network is a door anybody can open from a browser. Change the web password, and get this device off the flat tenant VLAN. That API is a feature, not a vulnerability, but it is only as strong as the password you left on it.\n\nDHCP being the default is the other operational trap. The panel comes up on a lease, and when the lease moves or the network is rebuilt, whatever you bookmarked or hard-coded against its old address stops working. Give it a reservation or a static address on install. Finding a unit whose address you have lost means going to the screen and reading the network status rather than scanning blind.\n\nOn the relay side, remember the R29 carries three of them and ships with all three enabled for unlock. If a site only intends one door to be openable, the other two are live until somebody turns them off. And relay hold time is a web setting, not a strike problem \u2014 a door that relocks too fast is almost always a hold-delay value.",commonServicingIssues:["Panel dead with no lights \u2014 PoE budget or the wrong PoE class. The X915 is specified PoE+; a switch port or injector that only delivers standard PoE can leave it browning out or refusing to boot. Confirm the port's class, or feed it externally","Cannot reach the web UI after a network change \u2014 DHCP is the factory default, so the address moved. Read the current address off the device's network status screen, then give it a reservation so it stops happening","Anyone on the network can open the door \u2014 the web account ships as admin/admin and the HTTP open-door API passes credentials in a URL query string. Change the password and segment the device off the general tenant network","Relay clicks but the lock does nothing \u2014 the relays are dry contacts that switch, not supply, power. Meter the lock's own supply before condemning the panel","Doors that should not be openable are openable \u2014 the R29 has three relays and all are enabled for unlock by default. You disable the unwanted ones explicitly","Door relocks before the visitor reaches it \u2014 relay hold delay is a web setting under Intercom > Relay, not a fault in the strike","Calls ring nowhere or drop \u2014 this is SIP, so the fault is registration or the network path, not a phone line. Check the device's SIP registration status before anything else","Following the wrong manual \u2014 X915 and R29 admin procedures and menu paths differ. Read the model off the rear label or the device web UI before working from a guide"],sources:["https://knowledge.akuvox.com/","https://enssecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Akuvox-X915-Series-Door-Phone-Administrator-Guide_V1.0-1.pdf","https://shop.cie-group.com/files/Akuvox%20R29%20Series%20Door%20Phone%20Admin%20Guide%20V4.pdf","Model not yet identified \u2014 the two admin guides above are the X915 and R29 series, the two Akuvox families that match this layout. Confirm before relying on either."],verified:!1,verifyNote:"Akuvox branding is legible on the module but no model number is visible in the photo. The unit is Android-based with dual cameras, a colour touchscreen and a separate backlit metal keypad. Read the model off the rear label or the device web UI (Status page) before ordering parts or following a manual \u2014 the X915 and R29 admin procedures differ.",photo:"photo-akuvox-video-door-station"},{id:"linear-access-telephone-entry-panel",name:"Linear Access telephone entry panel",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Flush-mount stainless telephone entry from Linear (now Nortek) \u2014 LCD directory, keypad, postal slot, operating instructions engraved right into the face. Visitor scrolls to a name, keys the directory code, the panel dials the resident, and the resident releases the door from their phone. The whole AE family is POTS-era: it dials a real phone line, and a single Form C access relay drives the strike or mag. The panel photographed here is an AE-family unit whose model is not legible; the figures below are the AE-100, which is the 125-resident member of the family, and they should be treated as AE-100 figures until the board is read.",specs:["Manufacturer: Linear (Nortek). Family: AE telephone entry \u2014 AE-100 and AE-500 both match this faceplate layout","AE-100 capacity: up to 125 residents (name, phone number, entry code). Up to 125 stand-alone entry codes for service and maintenance staff \u2014 but every stand-alone code used reduces the available resident directory entries by one","AE-100 codes: entry codes are 4 digits; directory code length is programmable and defaults to 3","AE-100 power: 16 VAC plug-in transformer. Supports a 12 V backup battery \u2014 the cabinet holds a 1.2 Ah sealed lead-acid gel cell, and the on-board charger will carry an external battery up to 7 Ah","AE-100 relay: one heavy-duty Form C (N.O. and N.C.) access relay rated 10 A AC / 7 A DC at 30 V. It is a dry contact \u2014 it switches the lock's own supply and never powers it","AE-100 memory is non-volatile: directory, codes and system programming survive a total loss of power","AE-100 networking: up to four units on one dedicated phone line as master and sub-units, with programming copied master-to-sub (function 30 for access data only, 34 for all data)","Terminal blocks are removable for servicing; phone line lands on an on-board RJ-11 jack or the terminal block. Postal key turn fires the access relay"],proAdvice:"Start with the screen, because on this family it tells you something. The factory default welcome text on an AE-100 is literally 'LINEAR ACCESS TELEPHONE ENTRY' (programming position 57). A panel sitting on that banner has never had its welcome text changed, which is a fair hint that nothing else was customised either \u2014 start by trying the rest of the factory defaults.\n\nThe master password default is 123456. Programming has two entry styles and this is the part that confuses techs moving between brands: Linear ships BOTH a set of Standard Position Numbers and a set of Alternate Function Codes for the same operations, so the same job has two different numbers depending on which reference sheet you are holding. Change master password is position 01 or function 00. Exit program mode is 99 or 91. Delete all resident data is 92 or 04. TOTAL MEMORY RESET is 200 or 50 \u2014 know which of those two you are typing before you commit, because one of them is the nuclear option.\n\nThe defaults worth checking on any takeover: keypad lockout at 3 tries, access relay activation 4 seconds, maximum visitor talk time 60 seconds, automatic telephone answer ON, resident control of the access relay ON. That last one matters \u2014 resident control ON means residents can trip the relay from their phones, which is what you want on a normal install and exactly what you disable when a manager is trying to stop a specific unit from buzzing people in.\n\nAnd remember the stand-alone entry code arithmetic. Those 125 codes are not in addition to the 125 residents, they come out of the same pool. Programming twenty service codes leaves you room for 105 residents, and on a full building that is how you run out of directory space without understanding why.",commonServicingIssues:["Cannot get into programming \u2014 the master password default is 123456, and Linear publishes two competing numbering schemes (Standard Position Numbers and Alternate Function Codes). Confirm which reference the previous installer used before deciding the password is wrong","Ran out of directory space well before 125 residents \u2014 every stand-alone entry code consumes one resident slot. Audit and delete unused service codes (function 33, or 93 to clear all stand-alone codes)","Wiped the system trying to clear one resident \u2014 92/04 deletes all resident data and 200/50 is a total memory reset. Neither is undoable and the memory is non-volatile, so a power cycle will not bring it back","Panel is alive but the strike does nothing \u2014 the access relay is a dry Form C contact rated 10 A AC / 7 A DC at 30 V. It switches the lock's supply, it does not provide it. Meter across the contacts, then meter the lock's own supply","Door releases for four seconds and relocks too fast for the walk \u2014 access relay activation time defaults to 4 seconds (position 52 / function 71). Extend it rather than blaming the strike","Residents complain the call cuts off \u2014 maximum visitor talk time defaults to 60 seconds","Someone unauthorised is buzzing people in from a resident phone \u2014 resident control of the access relay defaults to ON. Turn it off for the unit in question (position 77)","Panel loses time or behaves oddly on a power blip \u2014 the memory is non-volatile so programming is safe, but the 12 V backup battery is not included from the factory and is frequently just absent. The cabinet takes a 1.2 Ah gel cell","Multi-unit site where one panel has stale residents \u2014 up to four AE-100s share a line as master and sub-units, and the sub-units only update when data is copied (30 for access data, 34 for all data). The copy is a manual step somebody skipped","Will not dial out after a phone-service change \u2014 this is a POTS-era dialer, and a fiber or VoIP cutover routinely breaks dial-out or the voice path. Prove dial tone with a test set at the demarc before condemning the panel"],sources:["https://linear-solutions.com/wp-content/uploads/AE-100.pdf","https://www.aegates.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/linear-telephone-entry-system-ae500-facility-managers-guide-gate-manual.pdf","https://lineargateopeners.com/store/resources/resources-hub/manuals.html","Model not yet identified \u2014 AE-100 and AE-500 manuals are both linked above because the photo does not distinguish them."],verified:!1,verifyNote:"No model number is visible on the faceplate, so this is not confirmed as an AE-100. Two pieces of evidence point that way: the idle LCD reads 'Linear Access Telephone Entry', which is word for word the AE-100 factory default welcome text (programming position 57), and the flush stainless / 2-line LCD / recessed keypad / postal slot layout matches. That is evidence of an unmodified AE-100, not proof of model \u2014 the banner is a programmable string and the AE-500 shares the faceplate style. Read the board before following the programming reference, since the AE-100 and AE-500 differ. Service plate on the unit reads Metro Locksmiths, Inc., San Francisco, which is the fastest route to a changed master password.",photo:"photo-linear-access-telephone-entry-panel"},{id:"doorking-1800-series",name:"DKS 1800 access control board",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"White NEMA enclosure, 16.5V 1.6A input per label. ETL listed, ANSI/UL-294.",specs:["Manufacturer: DoorKing","Model: 1800 series","Input: 16.5 V 1.6 A (per label)","Enclosure: white NEMA","Listed: ETL, ANSI/UL-294"],proAdvice:"Audio on an 1800-series board is three interacting potentiometers \u2014 SPK VOL, MIC VOL and FEEDBACK \u2014 plus a CLCK SENSE pot you will almost always want disabled. The manual states outright that speaker volume, microphone volume and feedback all interact with each other, so you set them in sequence on a live call and expect to revisit earlier ones.\n\nSpeaker volume first. Place a call from the panel to a resident and, while they talk, rotate the speaker pot clockwise to raise and counter-clockwise to lower until the visitor side sounds right. Then microphone volume: speak into the panel in a normal voice and have the resident tell you when they can hear you clearly. Set it for somebody standing a normal distance from the panel, not for you leaning into it.\n\nFeedback has a specific procedure that most techs skip and then wonder why it squeals. Call a resident and ask them to stay silent on the line. Move the jumper from the TONE OFF terminals to TONE ON, and a tone comes out of the speaker. Rotate the feedback pot clockwise and then counter-clockwise, and leave it at the point where that tone is at MINIMUM. That is the correct adjustment. Move the jumper back to TONE OFF before you close up \u2014 a panel left on TONE ON is a guaranteed return trip.\n\nWhen you cannot null the squeal at all, stop turning the feedback pot: it is a levels problem. High speaker and high microphone settings drive each other. The manual's own instruction is to reduce the speaker if the mic is set high, and reduce the mic if the speaker is set high. One of the two comes down.\n\nAnd before any of that, look at the holes. A large fraction of 'bad audio' service calls on street-facing panels is plain mechanical obstruction that no pot will fix: the speaker grille painted over during a building repaint, RTV or silicone smeared across the microphone hole by whoever weathersealed the box, foam or insulation stuffed behind the faceplate, a wasp or mud-dauber nest in the grille, or a rain hood or custom bezel whose cutouts do not line up with the speaker and mic openings. Pull the faceplate and look at both openings from behind before you touch the board. That inspection step is field experience, not manual guidance \u2014 the manual assumes the holes are clear.\n\nTwo more pots worth knowing. Click sensitivity exists only for rotary-dial phones: if there are none on the property, run it fully counter-clockwise to disable it, because set too high the system starts responding to loud street noise and opening on its own. LCD contrast on the 1803 and 1810 is factory-set and should not normally need touching; if it does, let the system run at least ten minutes first, turn the MASTER CODE switch ON so the display lights and reads MST CODE, adjust, then turn that switch back OFF. The master code switch must be OFF for normal operation. Expect a long tone about thirty seconds after switching it on, and expect the display to keep reading MST CODE for about thirty seconds after you switch it off \u2014 both are normal and neither means a fault.",commonServicingIssues:["Audio too quiet, too loud, or good one way only \u2014 set the three pots in order on a live call: SPK VOL, then MIC VOL, then FEEDBACK. They interact, so changing one means rechecking the others","Squeal or howl on every call \u2014 speaker and microphone volumes are feeding each other. Lower one of them rather than cranking the feedback pot: back off the speaker if the mic is high, or the mic if the speaker is high","Feedback will not null out \u2014 run the real procedure. Resident silent on the line, jumper moved from TONE OFF to TONE ON, rotate the feedback pot to where the tone is MINIMUM, then return the jumper to TONE OFF","Left the jumper on TONE ON \u2014 the panel is audibly wrong on every call afterward. It is the most common self-inflicted callback from an audio adjustment","Muffled or dead audio that no adjustment fixes \u2014 inspect for obstruction before the circuit. Painted-over speaker grille, sealant across the mic hole, foam behind the faceplate, insect nest in the grille, or a rain hood / aftermarket bezel not lining up with the openings. Check both openings from behind the faceplate","Gate or door opens on its own, or responds to loud street noise \u2014 click sensitivity is set too high. It is only for rotary-dial phones; disable it fully counter-clockwise if none are in use, otherwise back it off 1/8 turn at a time and retest","Display unreadable on a 1803 or 1810 \u2014 contrast is factory-set, so let the system run ten minutes first, then adjust with the MASTER CODE switch ON and return that switch to OFF when done","Panel behaving oddly with a long tone after programming \u2014 the MASTER CODE switch was left ON. It must be OFF for normal operation; a long tone about thirty seconds after switching it on is normal, as is MST CODE persisting on the display about thirty seconds after switching it off","Wiring the audio pair by colour \u2014 speaker output is the purple wire, microphone input green, microphone ground white, landing on the SPKR, MIC and MICG terminals alongside CGND, PSW, COM and BAT"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1800-060-Issued-7-19_C.pdf","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/","'1800 series' is a family, not one board \u2014 the index above carries the per-model manual (1802, 1810, 1812, 1815, 1816, 1820, 1830, 1833-1838). Match the number on the board."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Enclosure and label are confirmed DoorKing 1800 series, but the specific 18xx model is not readable in the photo and the programming codes differ across the family \u2014 the 1802/EPD set is not the 1834 set. Read the board number before programming.",photo:"photo-doorking-1800-series",photos:[{asset:"photo-doorking-1800-series",caption:"The board in its white NEMA enclosure \u2014 16.5 V 1.6 A input per the label, ETL listed to ANSI/UL 294"},{asset:"photo-dks-door-entry-installed",caption:"Installed flush-mount pedestrian entry: LCD strip, 12-key pad, call button and engraved instructions. Note the accretion \u2014 a PDK prox reader retrofitted to the left and an ADA push-to-open actuator to the right, neither original to the panel"},{asset:"photo-dks-gate-entry-installed",caption:"Surface-mount unit with weather hood on a vehicle gate at the same property, marked Vehicles Only. Same family, different mount and a separate gate \u2014 one site commonly runs two"}]},{id:"doorking-volte-gateway",name:"DKS VoLTE cellular gateway",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Black modem in DKS enclosure. Carries network name, password, and 192.168.0.1 admin label. SIM ID on enclosure door.",specs:["Manufacturer: DoorKing","Model: VoLTE gateway","Admin page: 192.168.0.1","SIM ID: on enclosure door"],proAdvice:"Provisioning is the first thing to check and the thing most often missed. A DKS cellular gateway that has never been activated, or whose account has lapsed, looks completely healthy \u2014 lights on, admin page reachable \u2014 and simply never passes traffic. Before you diagnose anything else, confirm the account is live with DKS. An unprovisioned gateway is not a hardware fault and no amount of rewiring fixes it.\n\nGet the variant right, because they wire differently. A voice-and-data gateway lands on BOTH the entry system's phone line and its RS-232 port; a data-only gateway lands on RS-232 alone. Wiring a voice+data unit as though it were data-only gives you a panel that reports fine to the software and never passes a call.\n\nSignal is a survey item, not a hope. Test the actual mounting location with a phone on the same carrier before you commit \u2014 a gateway in a metal cabinet in a gate vault is the classic marginal install, and marginal signal presents as intermittent faults that look like everything except what they are. The module also wants a long first charge on its internal battery before you rely on it, and its lights should come up within about a minute of power.\n\nThe 192.168.0.1 admin page and the network name and password on the label are how you get in locally. Record them on the work order \u2014 that label is inside the enclosure, and the next tech will not have it.",commonServicingIssues:["Gateway looks alive but never passes a call or a poll \u2014 the account is unprovisioned or lapsed. Confirm with DKS before touching wiring","Data reports fine but voice never works \u2014 a voice+data gateway wired as data-only. Voice+data needs the phone line AND the RS-232 connection","Intermittent faults with no pattern \u2014 marginal cell signal at the mounting point, often a metal cabinet or a below-grade vault. Survey with a phone on the same carrier","Dead after a power interruption \u2014 the internal battery was never given its initial charge, so it has no reserve to ride through","Cannot reach the admin page \u2014 it is at 192.168.0.1 with the network name and password printed on the enclosure label. Note them on the work order, because that label stays in the box","No lights within a minute of power-up \u2014 check the module's own ON/OFF control before assuming the unit is dead"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/accessories/cellular-control-box/","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"DoorKing's cellular gateways come in voice+data and data-only variants that wire differently \u2014 voice+data lands on both the phone line and the RS-232 port, data-only on RS-232 alone. Confirm which one is in the can before wiring. Service requires an active DKS cellular account; an unprovisioned gateway looks alive and simply never passes traffic.",photo:"photo-doorking-volte-gateway"},{id:"ditec-entrematic-ha9-939d",name:"Ditec HA9 swing door operator",category:"Auto operators",whatItIs:"120 VAC, 60 Hz, 75 W. UL325 and UL991 listed, ICC-ES ESR-2740, No. 1019865.",specs:["Manufacturer: Ditec Entrematic","Model: HA9 / 939D","Power: 120 VAC, 60 Hz, 75 W","Listed: UL325, UL991","ICC-ES ESR-2740, No. 1019865"],proAdvice:"The brand is the first practical problem. Ditec Entrematic's swing operators are now sold and documented under the record name, so searching for current HA9 documentation under 'Ditec' turns up increasingly stale material. Go to record's document library for the install manual \u2014 the product is the same operator.\n\nUnderstand which listing you are working to. This is a LOW-ENERGY operator: UL 325 covers the operator, UL 991 covers the safety-related controls, and the operating behaviour is governed by ANSI/BHMA A156.19. Low energy means the door is required to open and close slowly enough that it does not need presence sensors guarding the swing, and that timing is a compliance requirement, not a comfort setting. Speeding one up because a customer finds it slow can take the installation out of compliance, and on an accessible entrance that matters legally as well as practically.\n\nPower is 120 VAC, 60 Hz, about 75 W \u2014 a dedicated circuit, not a shared convenience receptacle, because a nuisance trip on this circuit takes the accessible entrance out of service.\n\nWhen a low-energy door starts misbehaving, separate the operator from the door before you adjust anything. A binding hinge, a dragging sweep or a closer fighting the operator will present as an operator fault, and turning up force to overcome mechanical drag is how these units get destroyed.",commonServicingIssues:["Door opens or closes faster than low-energy timing allows \u2014 that is a compliance problem under ANSI/BHMA A156.19, not a preference. Do not speed it up to satisfy a complaint","Operator seems weak or strains \u2014 check the door first. Binding hinges, a dragging sweep or a closer fighting the operator all present as operator faults, and adding force to overcome drag wrecks the unit","Searching for documentation and finding nothing current \u2014 the line is now documented under the record brand rather than Ditec Entrematic","Nuisance trips taking the accessible entrance out of service \u2014 it wants its own 120 VAC circuit, not a shared receptacle","Activation works intermittently \u2014 separate the actuator and its radio path from the operator itself; a wireless push plate with a flat battery looks exactly like an operator fault","Hold-open time complaints \u2014 that is an adjustment on the control board, but confirm the resulting cycle still meets the low-energy timing requirement"],sources:["https://www.recorddoors.com/us/en/market-product-assets/products/swing-doors/ha9/downloads/installation-manual/ha9-install-manual-v3.pdf","Nameplate data read from the field photo: 120 VAC 60 Hz 75 W, UL 325 and UL 991 listed, ICC-ES ESR-2740 No. 1019865."],verified:!0,photo:"photo-ditec-entrematic-ha9-939d",verifyNote:"Nameplate data is read directly from the photo and is reliable. The linked install manual is record's current HA9 manual \u2014 the line moved from Ditec Entrematic to record branding \u2014 so confirm it matches the board revision in front of you before following adjustment procedures."},{id:"ditec-operator-control-board",name:"Ditec operator control board",category:"Auto operators",whatItIs:"Trim pots labeled SPTE, OPSP, CLSP, POAS, CLTQ, HOT. 6-position red DIP bank. Second DIP bank labeled PAG, MAT, SOS, ARM1, ARM2. Pluggable green terminal blocks. Companion module terminals: LOUT, OPD, XIMP, IIMP, GND, ARM1, ARM2, Sync ON/OFF-HO.",specs:["Manufacturer: Ditec Entrematic","Trim pots: SPTE, OPSP, CLSP, POAS, CLTQ, HOT","DIP bank: 6-position (red)","Second DIP bank: PAG, MAT, SOS, ARM1, ARM2","Companion terminals: LOUT, OPD, XIMP, IIMP, GND, ARM1, ARM2, Sync ON/OFF-HO","Terminal blocks: pluggable green"],proAdvice:"Work this board by reading it, not by remembering it. The trim pots are silkscreened SPTE, OPSP, CLSP, POAS, CLTQ and HOT, and there are two DIP banks \u2014 a six-position red one, and a second labelled PAG, MAT, SOS, ARM1 and ARM2. The companion module terminals are LOUT, OPD, XIMP, IIMP, GND, ARM1, ARM2 and a Sync ON/OFF-HO selector, on pluggable green blocks.\n\nOf those, CLTQ is the one confirmed in the manufacturer literature: closing force and spring tension. The rest read as obvious abbreviations \u2014 opening speed, closing speed, hold-open time \u2014 but obvious is not confirmed, and on a low-energy operator the speed and timing pots are compliance-relevant. Get the manual for the specific board revision before you turn any of them. Guessing at a pot on an accessible entrance is not a defensible way to work.\n\nTwo practical habits. First, photograph the pot positions and both DIP banks before you touch anything \u2014 they are analogue settings with no readout, and there is no undo. Second, the terminal blocks are pluggable, which makes it tempting to unplug a block to isolate a circuit; label them first, because they are physically interchangeable and a swapped block is a genuinely miserable fault to chase.",commonServicingIssues:["Adjusting a pot from its abbreviation without the manual \u2014 only CLTQ (closing force / spring tension) is confirmed in the literature. On a low-energy operator the speed and timing pots affect compliance","No record of the original settings \u2014 these are analogue trimmers with no readout and no undo. Photograph every pot and both DIP banks before the first adjustment","Terminal blocks swapped during troubleshooting \u2014 the green blocks are pluggable and physically interchangeable. Label them before unplugging any","Chasing an operator fault that is actually mechanical \u2014 prove the door swings freely by hand before adjusting force or speed","Board revision does not match the manual \u2014 the line spans Ditec Entrematic and record branding, and boards differ across revisions. Match the manual to the board in front of you"],sources:["https://www.recorddoors.com/us/en/market-product-assets/products/swing-doors/ha9/downloads/installation-manual/ha9-install-manual-v3.pdf","Pot, DIP and terminal legends read directly from the field photo."],verified:!1,photo:"photo-ditec-operator-control-board",verifyNote:"The legends here are transcribed from the photo and are accurate as labels, but the board's own part number is not visible and its revision is not established. Only CLTQ (closing force / spring tension) is confirmed against manufacturer literature \u2014 the remaining pot abbreviations are unexpanded on purpose rather than guessed. Identify the board revision and get its manual before adjusting anything."},{id:"ada-push-to-open-bollard-actuator",name:"ADA push-to-open bollard actuator",category:"Auto operators",whatItIs:"Aluminum floor-mount post, black cap, wheelchair symbol. Internals: momentary switch, 9V battery, inline transmitter module.",specs:["Manufacturer: unknown","Mount: aluminum floor post","Internals: momentary switch, 9V battery, inline TX module"],proAdvice:"The thing to understand about this style of actuator is that it is wireless and battery-powered, which is why it exists: a floor-mounted bollard in the middle of a walkway is expensive to trench power to, so instead the post holds a momentary switch, a 9 V battery and a small inline transmitter module that talks to a receiver at the operator. No conduit to the post at all.\n\nThat design decision sets every failure mode. The battery is the number one call, and it fails gradually \u2014 the post works from a hard press, then only sometimes, then not at all, which reads to a building manager as 'the door is getting temperamental' rather than 'the battery is flat'. Carry 9 V batteries.\n\nSecond most common is the radio link. The transmitter has to be learned into the receiver at the operator, so a replaced module, a reset receiver or a receiver whose memory was cleared all present as a dead post. Prove the operator responds to another input before you dismantle the bollard.\n\nThe module inside is frequently a generic potted transmitter with flying leads and a battery snap rather than anything proprietary \u2014 this catalog's Transmitter Solutions SETMC1C9V entry is exactly that pattern. That is good news for service, because a failed module is often replaceable with an equivalent, provided you can learn it into the receiver.\n\nAnd because it is a floor-mounted post outdoors, water gets in. Check the base and the switch boot before assuming electronics.",commonServicingIssues:["Works intermittently, then not at all \u2014 flat 9 V battery. It degrades gradually, so it presents as the door being temperamental rather than as a dead actuator. Carry spares","Post completely dead but the operator works from other inputs \u2014 the radio link. The transmitter has to be learned into the receiver; a replaced module or a cleared receiver memory looks identical to a dead post","Whole door blamed for a bollard fault \u2014 prove the operator responds to another activation before dismantling the post","Water ingress at the base or through the switch boot \u2014 it is a floor-mounted outdoor post; check for corrosion before condemning the electronics","Switch worn out from footfall and repeated hard presses \u2014 the momentary switch is a wear item on a high-traffic accessible entrance","Replacement module will not operate the door \u2014 an equivalent potted transmitter usually works, but only after it is learned into the receiver"],sources:[],verified:!1,photo:"photo-ada-push-to-open-bollard-actuator",verifyNote:"Manufacturer is NOT established \u2014 nothing on the post identifies it, and this style of bollard actuator is sold by several vendors. What is confirmed is the internal arrangement, read from the photo: a momentary switch, a 9 V battery and an inline transmitter module. Identify the receiver at the operator before ordering a replacement, since the radio pairing matters more than the post does."},{id:"transmitter-solutions-sel2641r433-nn",name:"1-relay nano receiver, 433 MHz",category:"Gate operators",whatItIs:"433.92 MHz, AM/ASK. Supply 12/24 V. Clear DIN-clip housing, 4-screw terminal block, whip antenna.",specs:["Manufacturer: Transmitter Solutions","Model: SEL2641R433-NN / RECTSNANO433","Frequency: 433.92 MHz, AM/ASK","Supply: 12/24 V","1 relay","4-screw terminal, whip antenna, DIN-clip"],proAdvice:"A nano receiver like this is what you add when an operator has no radio of its own, or when the built-in one has died and you do not want to replace a control board. It is a DIN-clip box with a whip antenna, a four-screw terminal block and one relay, and it does exactly one job: hear a transmitter, close a dry contact.\n\nProgramming is self-learning from a single button \u2014 hold the programming button until the LED lights, then press the transmitter button you want stored, and the LED blinks and the relay cycles to confirm. There is no computer and no dip-switch address to match. Memory holds up to 85 transmitters, and that ceiling is a real service issue on an established property: once it is full, new fobs simply will not learn, and the symptom is 'the new remote does not work' rather than any error.\n\nIt uses rolling-code encoding, which is the point on a gate. You cannot clone a working remote onto a blank the way you could with a fixed-code system \u2014 each new transmitter has to be learned into the receiver. If a customer expects you to copy their remote at the counter, that expectation is wrong and worth correcting before you take the job.\n\nAntenna placement decides whether you get the rated range. The whip wants to be out of the metal cabinet and away from the operator's own wiring; a receiver buried in a steel box with the antenna coiled inside will give a fraction of its specification, and that reads as a weak transmitter.",commonServicingIssues:["New transmitter will not learn \u2014 the memory is full at 85. There is no error, the fob just does nothing. Clear unused entries or fit a larger receiver","Customer wants a remote cloned \u2014 this is rolling code, so cloning is not possible. Every transmitter has to be learned into the receiver individually","Poor range \u2014 the whip antenna is coiled inside a metal cabinet or run alongside the operator's wiring. Get it outside the box and clear of the harness before blaming the transmitters","Relay wired expecting a changeover \u2014 this unit provides one relay. Confirm the contact arrangement suits the operator input before wiring","Receiver dead \u2014 check the supply, which is 12/24 V. It is not powered from the radio side and it is not fussy about which, but it does need one of them","Works at the gate and not from a car \u2014 mounting height and the vehicle's own metal both cut range. Test from the position drivers will actually use"],sources:["https://www.cdviwireless.com/en/our-products/sel2641r433-nn/","Label data read from the field photo: SEL2641R433-NN / RECTSNANO433, 433.92 MHz AM/ASK, 12/24 V supply."],verified:!0,photo:"photo-transmitter-solutions-sel2641r433-nn",verifyNote:"Model and frequency are read directly from the unit. The manufacturer page is the CDVI Wireless listing (Erone / Transmitter Solutions branding varies by market) and it refuses automated fetches, so it will show as blocked rather than dead in a link check. Confirm the transmitter memory limit and learn procedure against the sheet shipped with the specific unit."},{id:"transmitter-solutions-setmc1c9v",name:"Transmitter module, 433 MHz",category:"Gate operators",whatItIs:"433.92 MHz. Supply 9 Vdc. FCC ID PWJTMC1C9V. Potted black module, flying leads, battery snap.",specs:["Manufacturer: Transmitter Solutions","Model: SETMC1C9V / TRAHANBUT433","Frequency: 433.92 MHz","Supply: 9 Vdc","FCC ID: PWJTMC1C9V"],proAdvice:"This is not a handheld remote \u2014 it is a potted transmitter module with flying leads and a 9 V battery snap, meant to be built into something else. That is the whole point of it: a push plate, a bollard actuator, a key switch or any dry-contact device becomes a wireless activation by wiring its contact across this module's leads and learning it into a receiver.\n\nWhich makes it the natural companion to the wireless bollard actuator elsewhere in this catalog \u2014 a momentary switch, a 9 V battery and an inline transmitter module is exactly this arrangement. If you are servicing a battery-powered accessible push post and find a black potted lump on flying leads inside, this is the class of part you are looking at.\n\nService reality: the battery snap tells you the power arrangement and the failure mode in one glance. A 9 V primary cell in an outdoor post is a consumable, and it is the first thing to change and the last thing anyone thinks of.\n\nThe FCC ID stamped on the module, PWJTMC1C9V, is worth knowing because it is the reliable way to identify an unmarked module. FCC's own database will confirm the grantee and the frequency when the vendor branding has worn off or was never there \u2014 and on this class of part, vendor branding frequently was never there.",commonServicingIssues:["Device it drives has gone intermittent then dead \u2014 the 9 V cell. On an outdoor installation this is a scheduled consumable, not a fault","Module replaced and nothing happens \u2014 it has to be learned into the receiver. The module is only half the pairing","Unmarked potted module and no idea what it is \u2014 look for the FCC ID. PWJTMC1C9V identifies this one, and the FCC database resolves grantee and frequency for others","Expecting to program it like a handheld remote \u2014 it has no buttons. Activation comes from whatever dry contact is wired to its leads","Wired to a maintained contact instead of a momentary one \u2014 this is meant to transmit on a momentary closure; a held contact behaves unpredictably and flattens the battery"],sources:["https://fccid.io/PWJTMC1C9V","Label data read from the field photo: SETMC1C9V / TRAHANBUT433, 433.92 MHz, 9 Vdc, FCC ID PWJTMC1C9V."],verified:!0,photo:"photo-transmitter-solutions-setmc1c9v",verifyNote:"Model, frequency and FCC ID are read directly from the module. The pairing with the wireless bollard actuator in this catalog is an observed pattern, not a documented compatibility claim \u2014 confirm the actual module in any given post before ordering."},{id:"doorking-1835",name:"DoorKing 1835 Telephone Entry",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A classic surface-mount DoorKing telephone entry \u2014 stainless faceplate, two key cylinders, a green LCD directory strip, 12-key pad, A/Z/CALL scroll buttons for walking the directory, a DKS prox target and a speaker grille. The 1835 sits in DKS's 80-Series alongside the 1833, 1834, 1837 and 1838, which matters more than the model number suggests: the 80-Series is PC-programmed through DKS Remote Account Manager, not keyed in at the panel like the older 1802/EPD family. Turn up expecting to program this from the keypad and you will be standing there a while.",specs:["Family: DKS 80-Series (1833 / 1834 / 1835 / 1837 / 1838) \u2014 PC-programmed via DKS Remote Account Manager, not keypad-programmed","Visible on the unit: stainless faceplate, two key cylinders, green LCD directory strip, 12-key pad, A/Z and CALL scroll buttons, DKS prox target, speaker grille","Mount: surface, in a DKS enclosure","Connection options across the 1800 range: POTS line, DKS cellular, or VoIP \u2014 which one this unit uses is a site question, not a model question","Relays are dry contacts on every 1800-series board: the lock runs on its own separate supply, never off the entry transformer","Directory name entry is multi-tap off the 12-key pad, standard phone lettering: 1 SP, 2 ABC, 3 DEF, 4 GHI, 5 JKL, 6 MNO, 7 PQRS, 8 TUV, 9 WXYZ. One press for the first letter, two for the second, three for the third","The fourth press is the DIGIT on a three-letter key, but the fourth LETTER on 7 (S) and 9 (Z)... on those two the digit only arrives on the fifth press","Press * to accept each character, then * once more to commit the finished name. # clears the display and restarts the name","Names are 15 characters maximum and a space counts as one. Upper case, numbers and spaces only from the panel keypad"],proAdvice:"The first question on any 80-Series call is how it gets programmed, because the answer is not the keypad. The 1833/1834/1835/1837/1838 are PC-programmed through DKS Remote Account Manager, and the sibling 1834 entry in this catalog already documents the version floor \u2014 v6.3.b or higher. If a property manager tells you the last tech 'changed it at the box', they are describing a different DKS family.\n\nDo not carry the 1802/EPD programming codes over to this unit. The EPD set (* 17 strike, * 23 tone-open) belongs to the older family and the 80-Series does not share it. Cross the two and at best nothing happens; at worst you hit a code that wipes a directory with no undo.\n\nThe A/Z and CALL buttons are the visitor's directory scroll, and they are also your fastest field test: if the LCD scrolls and CALL dials, the board, display and keypad are all alive and your fault is downstream in the phone path or the relay. Audio adjustment on the 1800-series boards is three interacting pots plus a TONE ON/OFF jumper \u2014 see the DKS 1802 entry, which carries the full procedure.\n\nProgramming names from the dial is a phone keypad and nothing cleverer, which is the good news... 2 is ABC, 7 is PQRS, and the space lives on 1. Two details cost people an afternoon. The first is that **the star key is a character separator, not just a finish key**: you press * after EVERY letter, and if you skip it the next press keeps cycling the character you are already on rather than starting the next one, which is why a name comes out as \"JN\" when you meant \"JON\". The second is the fourth press. On a three-letter key it gives you the number, but 7 and 9 carry four letters each, so there the fourth press is S and Z and the digit only turns up on the fifth. DoorKing's own worked example is press 7 four times for the S in JONES.\n\nFifteen characters is the ceiling and a space spends one of them, so \"JONES APT 1\" is most of your budget. Upper case, digits and spaces are all the panel keypad can produce... anything fancier is a job for the PC software rather than a trick at the gate.",commonServicingIssues:["Cannot program at the keypad \u2014 this is an 80-Series unit and it is PC-programmed through DKS Remote Account Manager. That is by design, not a fault","Programming codes do nothing, or something destructive happens \u2014 the 1802/EPD code set was used on an 80-Series panel. They are different families and the codes are not interchangeable","Panel resets or browns out when the gate fires \u2014 a strike, mag or operator is being fed from the entry transformer. The relays are dry contacts and the lock needs its own supply","Directory scrolls but nothing dials \u2014 board and display are proven alive by the scroll, so work the phone path: dial tone at the demarc, or an unprovisioned DKS cellular account, before suspecting the panel","Recurring surge damage \u2014 1800-series boards need a real earth ground. A post set in concrete is not a ground","Audio squeals or is inaudible \u2014 the three board pots interact and there is a TONE ON/OFF jumper that must be returned to OFF. Full procedure is on the DKS 1802 entry"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/","Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'Doorking 1835'."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed \u2014 the showroom placard reads Doorking 1835 \u2014 but capacity, board revision and the connection type (POTS / cellular / VoIP) are not readable from the photo and vary by unit. Pull the 1835 manual from the DKS document index for the specific figures, and read the board before programming.",photo:"photo-doorking-1835",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"],photos:[{asset:"photo-doorking-1835",caption:"The 1835 as installed"},{diagram:"dks-keypad-letters",caption:"The letter map, how many presses gives what, and the star key that separates characters."}]},{id:"dks-telephone-entry-board",name:"DoorKing 1862-010 Telephone Entry Main Board",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The main board out of a DoorKing telephone entry \u2014 the part you actually diagnose when the chassis is fine and the electronics are not. This one is silkscreened 1862-010 Rev T, carries a Tekmos TK68HC1100CFNE3 microcontroller and an 1810-068 EPROM, and lands its field wiring on a fourteen-position barrier strip. Everything you adjust by hand lives on this board: the audio trimmers, the tone jumper, and the ring and handset options.",specs:["Board: 1862-010, Rev T on this example. EPROM 1810-068. MCU Tekmos TK68HC1100CFNE3. Omron relays, UL marked","Terminal legend, in order: PHONE, PHONE, CGND, PSW, MICG, MIC, SPKR, COM, BAT, C, NC, NO, 16AC, 16AC","That is the 1802/EPD-family terminal map: 1/2 phone, 3 CGND (earth only, not a common), 4 PSW switch input, 5 MICG, 6 MIC, 7 SPKR, 8 COM, 9 BAT, 10-12 relay C/NC/NO, 13/14 16 VAC","Adjustments silkscreened on the board: MIC VOL, SPK VOL, FEEDBACK and CLCK SENSE trimmers","Jumpers: TONE ON / TONE OFF, HF, RING, HS","Wire colours at the audio terminals: speaker output purple, microphone input green, microphone ground white"],proAdvice:"The terminal legend is how you identify which family a loose board came out of, and it is more reliable than the model number somebody wrote on the enclosure. PHONE PHONE CGND PSW MICG MIC SPKR COM BAT C NC NO 16AC 16AC is the 1802/EPD map \u2014 fourteen positions, one relay's worth of C/NC/NO, and a single 16 VAC pair. The 1834/80-Series board does not look like this. If you are holding a board and reaching for a manual, read the strip first.\n\nThe four trimmers are the whole audio service in one place: MIC VOL, SPK VOL, FEEDBACK and CLCK SENSE. All three audio pots interact, so they get set in order on a live call, and FEEDBACK has its own routine using the TONE ON/OFF jumper. The full procedure is on the DKS 1802 entry in this catalog. CLCK SENSE is click sensitivity, for rotary-dial phones only \u2014 run it fully counter-clockwise if the property has none, because set too high the panel starts tripping on loud street noise.\n\nAnd the jumper that causes callbacks: TONE must go back to OFF when you finish the feedback adjustment. A board left on TONE ON is audibly wrong on every call afterward.",commonServicingIssues:["Trying to identify a loose board \u2014 read the terminal legend, not the enclosure label. PHONE PHONE CGND PSW MICG MIC SPKR COM BAT C NC NO 16AC 16AC is the 1802/EPD family","Audio wrong in every direction after a board swap \u2014 the three audio pots interact and none of them carry over from the old board. Set SPK VOL, then MIC VOL, then FEEDBACK, on a live call","Panel audibly wrong on every call after a service visit \u2014 the TONE jumper was left on ON instead of returned to OFF","Panel opens on loud noises \u2014 CLCK SENSE is set too high. It is only needed for rotary-dial phones; disable it fully counter-clockwise otherwise","CGND wired as a common \u2014 it is an earth-ground terminal only. Using it as a circuit common causes hum and flaky dialing","Board damaged after a storm \u2014 no real earth ground. The on-board protection needs somewhere to dump"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1800-060-Issued-7-19_C.pdf","Board silkscreen read directly from the field photo: 1862-010 sn 71771, Rev T, EPROM 1810-068."],verified:!1,verifyNote:"The board is positively identified \u2014 1862-010 Rev T is legible on the silkscreen, and its fourteen-position terminal legend matches the 1802/EPD map in this catalog exactly, so it is an 1802/1808/1810-family board and NOT an 1834/80-Series one. What is not established is which chassis this particular board came out of, because the host unit is not in frame. Confirm the enclosure before ordering a replacement by model.",photo:"photo-dks-telephone-entry-board",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"liftmaster-capxl-v2",name:"LiftMaster CAPXL V2 (myQ Community)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"LiftMaster's large-format smart video intercom, built on myQ Community. A big portrait touchscreen offers a visitor two routes in \u2014 Resident Search or an Entry Code (PIN) \u2014 with an integrated camera above, a prox target below the screen, and a keyed access panel. This is a cloud-managed device rather than a dialer: residents and codes live in a myQ Community property account, not in the panel's own memory, which changes who you call when something needs adding.",specs:["Platform: myQ Community. Property, residents and credentials are managed in the cloud account, not stored locally as a panel directory","Visitor flow on the idle screen: Resident Search, or Entry Code (PIN). The screen also carries a 'You may be monitored and recorded for security purposes' notice","Integrated camera above the display, prox/credential target below it, and a keyed access panel for service","Large portrait touchscreen \u2014 the wide-format sibling to the narrower CAPXM, which runs the same interface","Network-connected device: it needs a working data path to the myQ service to authenticate visitors and residents"],proAdvice:"Treat this as a cloud endpoint that happens to open a door. When it 'stops working', the chain is power, network, myQ service reachability, then the relay \u2014 and most of the calls stop at the network. There is no local directory to fall back on, so a panel that cannot reach the service cannot look a resident up, which is the operational difference from every DKS and SES unit in this catalog.\n\nThat also changes who owns the fix. Adding a resident, changing a PIN or fixing a wrong name is a property-account task in myQ Community, not something you key in at the panel. Turning up with a screwdriver for a directory problem is a wasted trip. Get the property manager's myQ access sorted before you drive out.\n\nThe CAPXL and CAPXM run the same interface in different form factors, so what you learn on one transfers to the other. The choice between them is a mounting and sightline decision \u2014 column width, reach range, how much screen the entry wants \u2014 not a capability one.",commonServicingIssues:["Visitors cannot find a resident, or the panel is 'blank' \u2014 this is a cloud-managed device with no local directory. Check the myQ Community property account and the panel's data path before touching hardware","Resident added but not appearing \u2014 the change was made in the wrong place, or the panel has not synced. It is a myQ account task, not a panel task","Panel up but nothing authenticates \u2014 network or service reachability. Prove the data path first","Door does not release although the screen says granted \u2014 the fault is downstream: the relay wiring, the lock's own supply, and fail-safe vs fail-secure matching the lock","PIN works for some visitors and not others \u2014 entry codes are managed per-resident in the account; audit them there rather than assuming a keypad fault"],sources:["Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'LiftMaster CAPXLV2'.","Screen content read directly from the field photo: Resident Search, Entry Code (PIN), myQ branding, monitoring notice."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard and the on-screen myQ interface. Electrical specs \u2014 supply voltage, current, relay ratings, PoE support and network requirements \u2014 are not readable from the photo and have not been confirmed against LiftMaster documentation. Get the CAPXL V2 install manual before wiring one.",photo:"photo-liftmaster-capxl-v2",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"liftmaster-capxm",name:"LiftMaster CAPXM (myQ Community)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The narrow-column member of LiftMaster's myQ Community intercom line \u2014 same software and same visitor flow as the CAPXL, in a slim vertical format with camera, speaker, prox target and touchscreen stacked in a single column. Worth knowing next to the CAPXL precisely because the capability is the same and only the form factor differs: the decision between them is about the opening, not the feature list.",specs:["Platform: myQ Community, same as the CAPXL V2 \u2014 cloud-managed residents and credentials, no local directory","Visitor flow: Resident Search or Entry Code (PIN), with the same monitoring notice on screen","Narrow single-column format: camera and speaker above, touchscreen centre, prox target below","Same interface as the CAPXL, so training and troubleshooting transfer directly between the two","Network-connected: needs a working data path to myQ to authenticate anybody"],proAdvice:"Pick between the CAPXM and the CAPXL on the opening, not the spec sheet. They run the same myQ Community software and offer the visitor the same two routes in, so the real questions are column width at the jamb or pedestal, mounting height and reach range, and how much screen the entrance wants to present. A narrow gate post takes the CAPXM; a lobby wall with room to spare can carry the CAPXL.\n\nEverything in the CAPXL entry's troubleshooting applies here unchanged, including the important one: there is no local directory. A panel that cannot reach the service cannot look anybody up, and resident and PIN changes are property-account work rather than anything you do at the door.",commonServicingIssues:["Same cloud-first failure chain as the CAPXL: power, network, myQ reachability, then relay. Most calls stop at the network","Directory or PIN changes not appearing \u2014 these are myQ Community account tasks, not panel tasks","Chosen for an opening it does not fit \u2014 the CAPXM is the narrow-column option; confirm the mounting width and reach range before ordering rather than after","Screen grants access but the door stays locked \u2014 downstream wiring, the lock's own supply, or fail-safe vs fail-secure mismatched to the hardware"],sources:["Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'LiftMaster CAPXM'.","Screen content read directly from the field photo: Resident Search, Entry Code (PIN), myQ branding."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard. Dimensions, supply requirements and relay ratings are not readable from the photo and have not been confirmed against LiftMaster documentation \u2014 get the CAPXM install manual before specifying or wiring one.",photo:"photo-liftmaster-capxm",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"akuvox-x916",name:"Akuvox X916 Video Door Station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The widest of the Akuvox stations in this catalog \u2014 a landscape stainless panel with a dual-camera pair for face recognition, a six-tile touchscreen interface, card reader and dual speakers. The tile set is the tell: Delivery, Temp Key, PIN, Dial, Contacts and Reception, which is a far broader visitor model than dial-a-resident. Like every Akuvox here it is an Android-based SIP device, so it is a network appliance and the service tool is a browser.",specs:["Widescreen stainless faceplate with a dual camera pair (the second camera is the face-recognition half)","Six-tile interface: Delivery, Temp Key, PIN, Dial, Contacts, Reception","Card reader below the display, dual speakers","Android-based SIP video door phone \u2014 no phone line, no dialer. Registration and network health are the failure modes","Web administration over the device's IP; DHCP is the factory default across the Akuvox range"],proAdvice:"The tile set is the reason to spec this over the smaller Akuvox panels. Delivery and Temp Key exist because parcels and short-lived contractor access are the two things multi-tenant properties actually struggle with, and Reception routes a visitor to a staffed desk rather than a resident. If a property does not need those flows, the X915 or E16 gives you the same platform in less glass for less money.\n\nEverything in the Akuvox platform notes applies: change the default web credentials before it goes live, give it a reservation or a static address so it does not wander off DHCP, and remember the relays are dry contacts that switch a lock's own supply rather than powering it. Face recognition is a convenience feature layered on top \u2014 the door still opens through a relay, and that relay still needs its own supply and correct fail-safe or fail-secure wiring.",commonServicingIssues:["Cannot reach the web UI after a network change \u2014 DHCP is the factory default, so the address moved. Read it off the device and set a reservation","Default credentials never changed \u2014 an Akuvox on default web login and a reachable network is a door anybody can open from a browser. Change it and segment the device","Face recognition unreliable \u2014 it is a convenience layer, not the access path. Confirm the relay, lock supply and PIN route work before chasing the camera","Relay clicks, lock does nothing \u2014 the relays are dry contacts. Meter the lock's own supply","Following the wrong manual \u2014 X916, X915, R29 and E16 differ in menu structure. Confirm the model before working from a guide"],sources:["https://knowledge.akuvox.com/","Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'Akuvox X916'.","Tile interface read directly from the field photo."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard and the tile interface is read from the photo. Electrical specs \u2014 PoE class, relay count and ratings, supply options \u2014 are not confirmed for the X916 specifically; the X915 and R29 admin guides do not cover it. Pull the X916 documentation from the Akuvox knowledge base before wiring.",photo:"photo-akuvox-x916",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"akuvox-x915",name:"Akuvox X915 Video Door Station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A tall glass-front Akuvox station with a dual camera pair at the top and a card reader below, running a deliberately reduced interface \u2014 Inside and PIN, rather than the six tiles of the X916. Android-based SIP, PoE-powered, administered through a browser. The pared-back tile set is a design choice worth understanding: fewer routes in means less for a visitor to get wrong, at the cost of the delivery and reception flows the X916 offers.",specs:["Tall glass front, dual camera pair (one is the face-recognition half), card reader below the display","Reduced tile interface: Inside and PIN","Power: specified PoE+ \u2014 a standard PoE port or injector can leave it browning out or refusing to boot","Network: DHCP is the factory default; static IP is a configuration change","Web administration in a browser; the initial account is 'admin'","Android-based SIP door phone \u2014 failure modes are network and registration, not phone line"],proAdvice:"The PoE class is the specific trap on this model. The X915 is specified PoE+, so a switch port or injector that only delivers standard PoE will give you a panel that boots erratically, browns out under camera load, or simply does not come up. Confirm the port's class before you diagnose anything else \u2014 it presents as a dead or flaky device and sends people hunting for faults that are not there.\n\nAfter that it is the standard Akuvox platform discipline. DHCP is the factory default, so give it a reservation or a static address on install or you will lose it on the next network rebuild. The initial web account is admin \u2014 change the password before it goes live, because Akuvox exposes an HTTP door-open call and default credentials on a reachable network mean anyone with a browser can open the door.\n\nRelays are dry contacts here as everywhere: they switch the lock's supply, they do not provide it.",commonServicingIssues:["Boots erratically, browns out, or will not come up \u2014 PoE class. The X915 wants PoE+; a standard PoE port is not enough. Confirm the port or feed it externally","Cannot reach the web UI after a network change \u2014 DHCP default, so the address moved. Read it from the device and set a reservation","Default web password still in place \u2014 combined with the HTTP open-door call, that is a door anybody on the network can open. Change it and segment the device","Relay clicks but the lock does not move \u2014 dry contact. Meter the lock's own supply, and check fail-safe vs fail-secure matches the hardware","Calls ring nowhere \u2014 this is SIP. Check registration status before anything else"],sources:["https://enssecurity.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Akuvox-X915-Series-Door-Phone-Administrator-Guide_V1.0-1.pdf","https://knowledge.akuvox.com/","Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'Akuvox X915'."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard, and PoE+, the DHCP default and the admin account come from the X915 series administrator guide. Relay count and terminal detail for the X915 specifically were not confirmed \u2014 the R29 guide documents three relays, but do not assume that carries across. The linked admin guide is a third-party mirror; Akuvox's own knowledge base is the durable source.",photo:"photo-akuvox-x915",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"akuvox-r29",name:"Akuvox R29 Video Door Station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The stainless Akuvox station with the camera above the screen, a six-tile interface and \u2014 the detail worth noticing \u2014 a multi-language selector across the top offering English, Espanol, Francais and Chinese. On a mixed-tenancy building that selector does more for actual usability than any other feature on the panel. Android-based SIP, PoE or external 12 V, three relays, administered through a browser.",specs:["Stainless faceplate, camera above the display, prox target, bottom speaker","Multi-language selector on the idle screen: English / Espanol / Francais / Chinese","Six-tile interface: Delivery, Temp Key, PIN, Dial, Contacts, Reception","Power: PoE on the Ethernet connector, or a 12V/GND terminal when PoE is not available","Relays: three (RelayA/B/C) with N.O./N.C. control terminals. Trigger delay and hold delay are configurable under Intercom > Relay","The manual is explicit that a relay 'operates a switch and does not deliver power' \u2014 the lock runs on its own supply","All relays are enabled for unlock by default; you disable the ones you do not want openable","Network: DHCP by default. Initial web login documented as admin / admin","An HTTP call can trip the door, of the form /fcgi/do?action=OpenDoor with the username and password in the query string"],proAdvice:"Two things on the R29 catch people out, and both are defaults rather than faults.\n\nThe first is the relays. There are three of them and all three ship enabled for unlock \u2014 you disable the ones a site does not want openable rather than enabling the one it does. On a property that only intends one door to open, the other two are live until somebody turns them off. Relay hold time is also a web setting under Intercom > Relay, so a door that relocks before the visitor reaches it is a configuration value, not a failing strike.\n\nThe second is credentials. The guide documents admin / admin as the shipped web login, and Akuvox exposes an HTTP open-door call that carries the username and password in the URL query string. That combination on a flat tenant network is a door anybody can open from a browser. Change the password and get the device off the general VLAN \u2014 the API is a feature, but it is only as strong as the password left on it.\n\nThe language selector is the thing to actually sell on a mixed-tenancy building. Being able to hand a visitor the panel in their own language solves more real complaints than face recognition does.",commonServicingIssues:["Doors that should not be openable are openable \u2014 all three relays are unlock-enabled by default. Disable the unwanted ones explicitly","Door relocks before the visitor reaches it \u2014 hold delay is a web setting under Intercom > Relay, not a strike fault","Anyone on the network can open the door \u2014 shipped web login is admin / admin and the HTTP open-door call passes credentials in the URL. Change it and segment the device","Panel dead with no lights \u2014 PoE not present or not delivering. The R29 also takes 12V/GND if PoE is unavailable","Cannot reach the web UI \u2014 DHCP is the default and the lease moved. Read the address off the device, then set a reservation","Relay clicks, lock does nothing \u2014 the manual states the relay switches but does not deliver power. Meter the lock's own supply","Calls ring nowhere \u2014 SIP registration or network path, not a phone line"],sources:["https://shop.cie-group.com/files/Akuvox%20R29%20Series%20Door%20Phone%20Admin%20Guide%20V4.pdf","https://knowledge.akuvox.com/","Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'Akuvox R29'."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard, and the relay behaviour, power options, DHCP default and admin/admin login come from the R29 series admin guide. The linked guide is a third-party mirror; Akuvox's knowledge base is the durable source. Confirm the firmware generation on site, since menu paths move between versions.",photo:"photo-akuvox-r29",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"akuvox-e16",name:"Akuvox E16 Video Door Station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The sparest panel in the Akuvox set \u2014 a slim all-glass front with a camera pair at the top and an RFID target in the centre, and very little else. No tile grid, no directory scroll, no keypad. It is the credential-first end of the range: badge in, or be let in. Useful as the counterpoint to the X916 when you are showing somebody how much interface a door actually needs.",specs:["Slim all-glass panel, camera pair top, RFID target centre","Sparest interface of the Akuvox range \u2014 credential-first rather than directory-first","Android-based SIP door station on the same platform as the X915, X916 and R29","Network: DHCP is the factory default across the range; administered through a browser","Relays are dry contacts, as across the Akuvox line: they switch the lock's supply and do not provide it"],proAdvice:"Spec this where the credential is the access method and the intercom is the exception, not the rule \u2014 a staff entrance, a secondary door, a garage lobby where residents badge in and visitors go to the main entrance. The moment a property needs visitors to find a resident by name, this is the wrong panel and you want the R29 or an X-series.\n\nBecause there is no keypad and no directory, the failure modes narrow usefully: it is the credential path, the network, or the relay. A badge that does not open the door is a reader, credential-format or relay question, and there is no PIN fallback to muddy the diagnosis. That simplicity is the point of the panel.\n\nThe platform discipline is unchanged: change the default web credentials, pin the address rather than leaving it on DHCP, and run the lock on its own supply.",commonServicingIssues:["Badge does not open the door \u2014 with no keypad or directory there are only three places to look: the credential and its format, the network and SIP path, or the relay and lock supply","Specified for an opening that needs a visitor directory \u2014 the E16 has no directory or keypad. That is a specification error, not a fault","Cannot reach the web UI \u2014 DHCP default, address moved. Read it off the device and set a reservation","Default web credentials still in place \u2014 same exposure as the rest of the Akuvox range. Change them and segment the device","Relay clicks but the lock stays put \u2014 dry contact; meter the lock's own supply"],sources:["https://knowledge.akuvox.com/","Model identity from a showroom placard reading 'Akuvox E16'."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is confirmed from the showroom placard, and the physical description is read from the photo. No E16-specific manual was consulted \u2014 PoE class, relay count, supported card technologies and terminal detail are unconfirmed and should be pulled from the Akuvox knowledge base before wiring. The platform-level notes here are inferred from the X915 and R29 guides and may not hold exactly.",photo:"photo-akuvox-e16",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"linear-accesspro-ap5",name:"Linear AccessPro AP-5 Keypad",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"A standalone Linear AccessPro access keypad \u2014 three-digit seven-segment display, a bank of status LEDs, and four function keys beside the number pad. It is a self-contained access controller rather than an intercom: no directory, no phone line, no camera. Somebody keys a code and a relay fires. The status LEDs are the reason it is pleasant to service, because the panel tells you what it thinks happened.",specs:["Three-digit seven-segment display for programming feedback and diagnostics","Status LEDs: POWER, ACCESS GRANTED, SUSPENDED, PROGRAM MODE, RADIO","Function keys: DISPLAY, PROGRAM, CANCEL, OPEN, alongside a 12-key pad","Standalone access control \u2014 codes are held in the unit, no directory and no phone line","RADIO LED indicates the unit's radio receiver option, for transmitters as well as keyed codes","Pairs with an AccessPro controller board carrying the strike relay and its own diagnostic LEDs"],proAdvice:"Read the LEDs before you touch anything, because this unit distinguishes faults most keypads collapse together. SUSPENDED is not the same as a wrong code \u2014 it means the code exists and has been disabled, which is an administrative state and sends you to the programming rather than the wiring. PROGRAM MODE lit when nobody is programming means somebody walked away mid-session, and the panel will behave oddly until it drops out. RADIO covers the transmitter path, so a resident whose remote stopped working but whose keyed code still works is a radio-side problem.\n\nWork the keypad and the controller board as two separate things. The keypad decides whether a code is good; the controller board fires the strike relay. Its own LEDs \u2014 CODE SENT, CODE GOOD, CODE BAD \u2014 let you watch a code cross between the two, and that tells you which half is at fault in about ten seconds. CODE SENT with no CODE GOOD means the keypad is talking and the controller is rejecting; nothing at all means the link or power is out.\n\nAnd as always the relay is a dry contact. It switches the strike's own supply and never powers it.",commonServicingIssues:["Valid code refused with SUSPENDED lit \u2014 the code is disabled, not wrong. That is an administrative change in programming, not a wiring fault","Panel behaving unpredictably with PROGRAM MODE lit \u2014 a programming session was left open. Cancel out of it","Remote stopped working but keyed codes still open \u2014 the RADIO side, not the keypad. Check the receiver and the transmitter","Code accepted but the strike does not fire \u2014 watch the controller board LEDs. CODE SENT plus CODE GOOD means the fault is downstream at the relay, wiring or the strike's own supply","CODE SENT with no response \u2014 the controller is rejecting or the link between keypad and board is down. Prove power and the connection before replacing either","No LEDs at all \u2014 power. Meter it at the board, not at the supply"],sources:["https://lineargateopeners.com/store/resources/resources-hub/manuals.html","Panel identity, LED legend and key legend read directly from the field photo: Linear ACCESS AccessPro MODEL AP-5."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity is certain \u2014 'Linear ACCESS AccessPro MODEL AP-5' is legible on the faceplate, along with the full LED and function-key legend. Programming procedure, default codes, code capacity and relay ratings are NOT established here and have not been confirmed against a Linear manual. Pull the AP-5 manual from the Linear resources hub before programming one.",photo:"photo-linear-accesspro-ap5",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"linear-accesspro-controller-board",name:"Linear AccessPro Controller Board",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"The controller board that sits behind a Linear AccessPro keypad and does the actual switching. The keypad decides whether a code is good; this board fires the strike relay. It carries an Axicom 12 VDC relay at the strike output and a row of diagnostic LEDs \u2014 CODE SENT, CODE GOOD, CODE BAD \u2014 that let you watch a code cross from the keypad and see which side rejected it.",specs:["Silkscreened STRIKE RELAY at the relay output section","Axicom 12 VDC relay carrying the strike output","Diagnostic LEDs: CODE SENT, CODE GOOD, CODE BAD, alongside RF DATA and status indicators","Pairs with the AccessPro keypad \u2014 the keypad is the credential reader, this board is the switch","Relay output is a dry contact: it switches the strike's own supply rather than providing it"],proAdvice:"This board is the reason an AccessPro is quick to diagnose, and the three code LEDs are the whole trick. Have somebody key a code and watch: CODE SENT tells you the keypad is talking to the board at all; CODE GOOD means the board accepted it and the fault is downstream at the relay, the wiring or the strike's own supply; CODE BAD means the credential is genuinely rejected and you go back to programming. Nothing at all means the link or the board's power is out and the keypad's decision never arrived.\n\nThat sequence separates the two halves in seconds and stops the common mistake of swapping a working keypad because a door will not open. Meter across the relay contacts to confirm the switch is actually closing, then meter the strike supply separately \u2014 the relay is a dry contact and a board that is switching correctly into a dead supply looks exactly like a dead board.",commonServicingIssues:["Door will not open and the keypad gets blamed \u2014 watch CODE SENT, CODE GOOD and CODE BAD to see which half rejected the code before replacing anything","CODE GOOD lit but no strike \u2014 the fault is downstream. Meter across the relay contacts, then meter the strike's own supply separately","CODE BAD on a code that should work \u2014 a programming problem, not a wiring one. Check whether the code is suspended at the keypad","No LEDs at all when a code is keyed \u2014 the link between keypad and board, or board power. Prove both before condemning either part","Relay contacts pitting over time \u2014 no suppression across an inductive strike coil. Fit a diode or snubber; see the strike rectifier entry"],sources:["https://lineargateopeners.com/store/resources/resources-hub/manuals.html","Board legend read from the field photo behind the AP-5 keypad: STRIKE RELAY, Axicom 12VDC relay, CODE SENT / CODE GOOD / CODE BAD."],verified:!1,verifyNote:"This board appears behind the AP-5 keypad in the same photo rather than in a dedicated shot, so the legend is read at an angle and the board's own part number is not visible. Its exact model and the relay ratings are unconfirmed. A dedicated photo of the board and its part number is the next thing to capture; until then treat the pairing with the AP-5 as observed rather than documented.",photo:"photo-linear-accesspro-ap5",relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"]},{id:"siedle-multi-tenant-door-station",name:"SSS Siedle Multi-Tenant Door Station",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A surface-mount aluminium Siedle door station: six call buttons with backlit nameplate windows in two columns of three, a louvered speaker and microphone panel, and a dedicated stair-light button marked with a bulb icon. It is the only non-electronic-directory unit in this catalog's telephone-entry set \u2014 fixed button-per-unit with paper inserts, no scrolling, no PIN, no camera. Siedle is German, and the modular door-station system does not follow US conventions for parts or programming.",specs:["Surface-mount aluminium, modular Siedle door-station construction","Six call buttons with backlit nameplate windows, arranged two columns by three","Louvered speaker and microphone panel","Dedicated stair-light button, marked with a bulb icon \u2014 a European convention, rarely seen on US equipment","Fixed button-per-unit addressing with paper name inserts: no electronic directory, no scrolling, no dialing code, no keypad","European system \u2014 parts, module numbering and programming do not follow US telephone-entry conventions"],proAdvice:"This unit is the anchor of a progression worth teaching deliberately. Tenant addressing evolved through three stages and this catalog now has all of them: Siedle's fixed button per unit with a paper insert, then DoorKing and SES with an LCD directory the visitor scrolls to reach a dialing code, then Akuvox and myQ with resident search, PINs, temporary keys and face recognition. Each step trades physical simplicity for administrative flexibility. A Siedle needs a screwdriver and a printed slip when a tenant moves; a myQ panel needs a cloud login. Neither is strictly better and the trade is the lesson.\n\nThe stair-light button is the detail American techs miss. It is a European convention \u2014 a momentary button on the door station that fires the common stair or hallway lighting circuit, entirely separate from the door release. If a customer reports 'a button that does nothing', that is usually it, and the fault is in the lighting timer rather than the intercom.\n\nBe careful ordering. Siedle is a modular system, and the series matters \u2014 Vario and Classic are not interchangeable, and the series reads off the rear casting or the module part numbers, not the face. US supply houses often cannot source these at all.",commonServicingIssues:["A tenant moved and the name is wrong \u2014 this is a paper insert behind the nameplate window, not a programming task. The trade-off of fixed addressing","A button 'does nothing' \u2014 check whether it is the stair-light button. That fires the lighting circuit, not the door, and the fault is usually the lighting timer","Ordering the wrong module \u2014 Siedle series are not interchangeable and the series is not readable off the face. Read the rear casting or the module part numbers","Parts unobtainable locally \u2014 this is European equipment, and US supply houses frequently cannot source it. Plan lead time before promising a repair","Applying US telephone-entry assumptions \u2014 there is no directory, no dialing code, no PIN and no keypad to program. Diagnosis is button, wiring, speaker and lock, and nothing else","Nameplate backlighting out \u2014 the windows are lit; a dark window on an otherwise working panel is a lamp or supply fault, not a call-button fault"],sources:["Physical description read directly from the field photo; the faceplate is marked SSS SIEDLE.","No Siedle manual was consulted \u2014 series and module part numbers are unconfirmed."],verified:!1,verifyNote:"Manufacturer is certain \u2014 the faceplate reads SSS SIEDLE \u2014 but the series is NOT established. Vario and Classic share this general face and the difference reads off the rear casting or the module part numbers, so do not order against this record. Photo note: six resident surnames were legible on the nameplates of a real occupied building and have been redacted; the #1-#6 unit numbers and the layout are deliberately preserved because the addressing format is the teaching content.",photo:"photo-siedle-door-station",relatedGuides:["siedle-in-home-bus-audio","intercom-triage"]},{id:"securakey-et-wxm",name:"Secura Key ET-WXM / ET-WXS e*Tag Reader",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The ET-WXM is Secura Key's mini-mullion 13.56 MHz e*Tag reader/writer \u2014 the narrow black brick you find screwed to an aluminum door mullion or, as on the captured unit, straight onto a metal entry panel next to a video intercom. The ET-WXS is the identical board in a switchplate housing with a bigger antenna and correspondingly longer read range. Both replace the older ET4 generation (older units carry FCC ID NNHETAGS4; this generation carries NNHETXXX). The reader makes no unlock decision: it reads the credential, ships the number out on Wiegand or RS-485, and a controller decides. It reads HF e*Tag / MiFare only \u2014 present a 125 kHz prox card to it and you get nothing at all, which is the single most common \"the new fob doesn't work\" call on these.",specs:["Frequency and standards: 13.56 MHz HF, ISO 14443 + ISO 15693, MiFare compatible, DES encryption. It will NOT read 125 kHz prox (HID Prox, Radio Key, EM, AWID) \u2014 different frequency, different reader family.","Read range, ET-WXM (mini-mullion): up to ~4 in (10.2 cm) on an ISO card, ~2.5 in (6.35 cm) on a key tag. ET-WXS (switchplate): up to ~6 in (15.2 cm) card, ~3.5 in key tag. Mounting on metal detunes the antenna and shortens both figures.","Power: 5-14 VDC at 50-90 mA. Red = positive, Black = ground. Reversed polarity can kill the board \u2014 meter before you land it.","Outputs: Wiegand 26-bit (H10301) or 32-bit, plus RS-485. Two operating modes: RO (reads card/UID out on Wiegand) and RW (host-managed over RS-485).","LED and buzzer are host-driven, ground-to-activate. The reader does not decide its own feedback \u2014 the panel sinks the Brown (red LED), Orange (green LED) and Yellow (buzzer) lines. A panel that does not wire them gives you a silent, dark reader even when access works perfectly.","10-conductor harness pinout: 1 Black = ground, 2 Red = +9-14 VDC, 3 Blue = hold line, 4 Yellow = buzzer, 5 Brown = red LED, 6 Orange = green LED, 7 Gray = RS-485 A (TXD on 232/TTL models), 8 Violet = RS-485 B (RXD), 9 White = Wiegand DATA 1, 10 Green = Wiegand DATA 0. Verify color-to-function on the actual pigtail \u2014 harness revisions happen.","Minimum wire for a standard Wiegand-to-panel install: Black, Red, White, Green. Everything else is optional feedback and comms.","Maximum runs: Wiegand 500 ft, RS-485 4000 ft, RS-232 100 ft, TTL 10 ft. Use shielded cable and bond the shield to earth ground at the PANEL end only \u2014 per Secura Key, DC signal ground is not to be tied to earth ground.","Wiegand output timing for scope/logic-analyzer work: ~50 us pulse width, ~1.0 ms pulse interval, idle high at +5 V. One missing data line reads at the panel as all-zeros or all-ones.","Housing: ET-WXM is ABS, 3.50 x 1.60 x 0.60 in. ET-WXS is polycarbonate, 4.50 x 3.20 x 0.84 in. Both black. Ambient -40 to 70 C, but the boards are unpotted and rated 0-90% RH non-condensing \u2014 moisture is what kills them.","Credentials (all HF e*Tag, 26 or 32-bit, DES): ETCI04 ISO card (dye-sub printable for photo ID), ETST03 compact key tag, ETKT03 bullnose key tag, ETAT03 adhesive tag. Each carries a unique ID number plus a facility/site code shared across the site.","Panels: native on Secura Key SK-ACP / SK-ACPe (2-door) and SK-MRCP (NOVA.16), managed in SK-NET software. Otherwise it lands on any controller with a standard SIA Wiegand input \u2014 Mercury/LenelS2, Software House, Avigilon, Kantech.","Field capture, this unit: model label ETM-WXM-14 (ET e*Tag, mini-mullion, WX board), firmware V1.04, ASSY 2015560, LOT 3231001, SER 2307069, FCC ID NNHETXXX. Mounted on a metal entry panel beside a ButterflyMX video intercom. Hairline crack noted at the bottom seam of the faceplate.","Wiegand output in virtually any SIA-standard format up to 40 bits; SecuraKey's own encoding is Format 201 (26-bit, for third-party panels) and Format 303 (32-bit, for SK-ACPe), with e*Tag facility codes prefixed 'E'","Dual-technology option (RKDT) reads Radio Key 26/32-bit alongside HID Prox 26-bit and most non-proprietary HID up to 37 bits, for migrating an existing HID site"],proAdvice:"Start every call on one of these by splitting the two systems apart, because on multi-tenant entries the reader is almost never alone. The captured door runs a ButterflyMX panel and this Secura Key reader as two completely independent paths to the same lock: the intercom triggers its own onboard relay from the cloud, the card reader goes out on Wiegand to an access panel whose relay drives the strike. They share no logic. So if card access is dead but visitors still get buzzed in, the lock and its power are fine and your problem is entirely on the Secura Key side. If BOTH fail, stop looking at either head end and go straight to what they share downstream \u2014 the strike or mag lock itself, the lock power supply, or a fire-alarm release or lockdown input that has latched.\n\nThe frequency mismatch is the call you will take over and over. This is a 13.56 MHz HF reader. Someone at the property orders \"key fobs\" online, they arrive as 125 kHz prox, and the reader does not so much as chirp \u2014 no beep, no red flicker, nothing, because the card never enters the field. Before you tear into wiring on a no-read complaint, put a known-good e*Tag credential (ETCI04 card or ETST03/ETKT03 tag) on it. Half these tickets close right there.\n\nUnderstand that the LED and buzzer belong to the panel, not the reader. Brown, Orange and Yellow are ground-to-activate lines the controller sinks. On plenty of third-party installs the integrator ran four wires and nothing else, so the reader is functionally mute \u2014 no green flash, no beep \u2014 while access works fine. That is not a fault, and you should not condemn a reader for it. When the LED never changes on a known-good card, the question you actually need answered is whether the read reached the panel, and the only place that answers it is the panel event log. Card in the log means the reader and the Wiegand pair are doing their job and the break is downstream at the relay, the lock, or the credential's enrollment. Card absent from the log means the data is not arriving and you go meter White and Green.\n\nMetering discipline on the pigtail: 5-14 VDC across Red and Black, and take that reading UNDER LOAD, with a card being presented, not idle. A supply that reads a healthy 12 V at rest and sags on the read is the classic cause of intermittent behavior, and it is usually a reader sharing a rail with the strike. Give the reader its own fused leg back at the panel. On the data pair, White (DATA 1) and Green (DATA 0) idle around +5 V and pulse low on a read \u2014 flatline on one line is a broken or shorted conductor or a blown open-collector output, and the panel will be seeing either all-zeros or all-ones.\n\nMind the mounting surface, because these get screwed to steel and aluminum constantly and HF antennas hate it. The captured unit is on a metal entry panel, which is exactly the install that generates \"I have to touch the card to it\" complaints. If range is chronically short and the credential is correct, a foam spacer or standoff buys you some back; if the door needs real range, spec the ET-WXS switchplate with its larger antenna instead of fighting the mini-mullion.\n\nLast thing, the boards are unpotted and only rated to 90% RH non-condensing. Inspect the housing seams on every PM \u2014 the captured unit already has a hairline crack at the bottom of the faceplate. On an exterior or semi-exposed door that crack is a moisture path and a scheduled failure, so flag it for replacement rather than waiting for the callback in the rain.\n\nOrder by format name, not by bit count alone. SecuraKey encodes to order and ships Format 201 for 26-bit third-party panels and Format 303 for 32-bit SK-ACPe systems, so 'a 26-bit card' is only half the order... the facility code and ID range are locked in at encoding, and a batch from a different facility code will read and then be denied. When you are migrating a site off HID rather than replacing every credential at once, the dual-tech RKDT head reads both families on one reader while the swap happens.",commonServicingIssues:["Completely dead \u2014 no LED, no beep, no read -> meter Red to Black at the pigtail for 5-14 VDC. No voltage: trace back to the supply, the panel's aux output, and any inline resettable fuse or PTC. Voltage present and still dead: suspect a failed board or a previously reversed polarity.","Reader beeps and flashes but the door never unlocks -> the read is happening, the break is downstream. Pull the panel event log. Granted in the log with no lock movement means relay, lock power supply, strike/mag, or a latched fire-alarm lockdown \u2014 meter the lock output at the panel relay.","Reader responds but the panel logs 'denied / invalid facility code / not in system' -> credential not enrolled, wrong facility code, or a format mismatch between the card batch and the panel config (26-bit vs 32-bit).","No response at all to a brand-new batch of fobs -> wrong frequency. This reader is 13.56 MHz e*Tag/MiFare only and gives zero feedback to a 125 kHz prox credential. Confirm with a known-good e*Tag card before touching anything else.","Reads are not reaching the panel \u2014 nothing in the event log -> meter White (DATA 1) and Green (DATA 0) to ground while badging. Both idle near +5 V and pulse low on a read. A flat line is a cut, shorted, or mis-landed Wiegand conductor, or a blown open-collector output.","Card must touch the reader / range collapsed -> HF antenna detuned by the metal mounting surface, which is the normal case on a metal entry panel. Add a foam spacer or standoff, or move the door to an ET-WXS switchplate for the larger antenna.","Access works but the reader never lights or beeps -> the controller is not driving the feedback lines. Brown (red LED), Orange (green LED) and Yellow (buzzer) are host-grounded; a four-wire install simply has no feedback. Not a reader fault.","Reader resets or misbehaves every time the lock fires -> it is sharing a power rail with the strike or mag lock and the inrush is dropping it below its 5 V floor. Move it to its own fused, filtered leg.","Intermittent reads and comms in RW / RS-485 mode -> Gray (A) and Violet (B) swapped, or the SK-NET loop is unterminated. Confirm polarity and termination at the bus ends.","Flaky after weather, or visible crack at the housing seam -> the board is unpotted and rated 0-90% RH non-condensing. Moisture ingress is terminal; replace rather than dry it out, and check the shield is grounded at the panel end only so you are not carrying noise in on the cable."],relatedGuides:["keypad-reader-servicing","access-control-systems","low-voltage-wiring","electric-strike-power-diagnosis"],sources:["https://www.securakey.com/","Secura Key e*Tag ET-WXM / ET-WXS reader-writer data sheet (specs, read ranges, credential part numbers).","Secura Key ET4-WXM / ET4-WXS installation guide (10-conductor harness pinout, cable distances, shield grounding).","Field capture: unit label ETM-WXM-14, FW V1.04, ASSY 2015560, LOT 3231001, SER 2307069, FCC ID NNHETXXX."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Model identity, firmware, assembly/lot/serial and FCC ID are read directly off the captured unit's label, and the specs come from the ET-WXM/WXS data sheet and ET4 installation guide. Two things to confirm on site before you cut anything: the harness color-to-function map (meter the actual pigtail \u2014 revisions happen), and which controller this reader lands on, since LED and buzzer behavior is entirely the panel's firmware and the SK-ACP defaults listed here may not be what is installed.",photo:"photo-securakey-et-wxm-label",photos:[{asset:"photo-securakey-et-wxm-label",caption:"The label on the back of the reader: ETM-WXM-14 with its assembly, lot and FCC identifiers. This is what you read before ordering anything, because the technology is decided here, not on the wall."},{asset:"photo-securakey-tag",caption:"A Secura Key proximity tag. The credential technology has to match the reader family... an MS-format tag will not read on a 13.56 MHz head."}]},{id:"butterflymx-video-intercom",name:"ButterflyMX Video Intercom Panel",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"ButterflyMX is a cloud-managed video intercom for multi-tenant entries \u2014 a touchscreen panel at the door, a resident directory in the cloud, and a smartphone app that takes the call and releases the door. It replaces the old wire-per-unit telephone entry model entirely: there is no house wiring to tenant stations, no directory board to relabel, and no local programming to speak of. The panel needs power (usually PoE) and a working internet path, and everything else lives in the property's ButterflyMX portal. On a shared entry it typically sits beside a card reader \u2014 the captured door pairs one with a Secura Key ET-WXM \u2014 and the two run as independent paths to the same lock.",specs:["Architecture: cloud-managed. The panel is a thin client \u2014 directory, permissions, call routing, delivery passes and door-release logic all live in ButterflyMX's cloud and the resident/property apps, not in the panel.","Door release: its own onboard relay and REX path, triggered from the app over the internet. It does not need, and does not use, the card reader's access panel.","Connectivity: requires a network drop and internet. Offline panel means no calls and no app release, regardless of how healthy the door hardware is.","Power: typically Power over Ethernet on the standard panels \u2014 confirm the class and the exact model's power method against ButterflyMX's install documentation before you size the switch or injector.","Independence from card access: a ButterflyMX failure does not take down credential access, and a reader or access-panel failure does not take down visitor entry. They only meet at the lock, its power supply, and any fire-alarm release.","Account model: the property manager (or whoever holds the ButterflyMX account) owns the directory, the resident list and the credentials. A locksmith or integrator without portal access cannot make those changes.","Field capture: panel mounted on a metal entry panel alongside a Secura Key ET-WXM reader, idle screen live and showing the 'Tap to start' prompt.","Panels: 7in, 11.6in and 21.5in capacitive touchscreens, surface or recessed, with camera (reads QR codes), speaker/mic, RJ45 and 4 dry-contact relays","Lock wiring by fail mode: a mag lock lands on N.C./COM so the relay momentarily cuts power (fail-safe), a fail-secure strike or an externally controlled door on N.O./COM","An isolation relay (or diode/resistor) between panel and lock is required; the panel relays are not rated to carry large lock current","Backup battery keeps the panel operating through a power or internet outage; without one, an outage means no entry","Commercial model: cloud software subscription, typically on a 12-month contract, separate from the hardware"],proAdvice:"The most valuable thing you can do on a ButterflyMX door is know where your scope ends, and say so early. This is a cloud product on someone else's account. If the complaint is \"my name is not in the directory,\" \"calls go to the wrong unit,\" \"the new tenant cannot get in,\" or \"delivery drivers cannot get a pass,\" none of that is fixable at the door with tools. It is a property-manager task in the ButterflyMX admin portal, or a ButterflyMX support ticket. Diagnose it, document it, hand it back. Unless your company holds the account, you cannot and should not be making directory or credential changes on that panel.\n\nWhat IS yours is everything physical and everything downstream. Black screen or dead panel: check its PoE \u2014 switch port, injector, the drop itself \u2014 before you assume the panel failed. A live screen sitting on an error or spinner usually means power is fine and the network path is not, and that is a conversation with whoever runs the building's IT or ISP.\n\nWhen the complaint is \"the door will not open,\" split the two systems immediately, because on a shared entry that single sentence covers two unrelated faults. Try the card reader. If credentials work and only the app release fails, the fault is on the ButterflyMX side \u2014 its relay wiring or its cloud connection \u2014 and the card path proves the strike, its supply and the door hardware are all healthy. If credentials also fail, stop chasing either head end: you are looking at the shared downstream, meaning the strike or mag lock, the lock power supply, or a fire-alarm release or lockdown that has latched. That single test saves you an hour on nearly every one of these calls.\n\nOn install and retrofit work, the honest pitch is that ButterflyMX removes the wiring problem that made old telephone-entry systems miserable \u2014 no house pairs to chase, no dead station to trace through a riser. What it adds is a hard dependency on the building's network and internet. A property with flaky Wi-Fi backhaul or a contested IT department will produce intermittent \"nobody can get in\" calls that no amount of door hardware work will fix, so get the drop and the uplink sorted before the panel goes on the wall.\n\nOn the wiring side the isolation relay is the entry that keeps the panel alive, and skipping it is the most common install mistake on this product. The panel's dry relays are small and the real exposure is the intercom itself taking the coil's kickback when the lock drops... put an isolation relay, or a diode and resistor, between the panel and any lock, and keep the lock's power circuit isolated from the panel's. Land the lock by failure mode while you are there: a mag lock on N.C./COM so the relay momentarily cuts power, a fail-secure strike or an externally controlled door on N.O./COM, and check the strike voltage against the relay rating before energizing anything.\n\nTwo commercial points worth raising before the customer signs. Surge is the leading hardware-failure cause on these panels, so put it on a surge-protected circuit and expect a storm to be the reason one dies. And the cloud subscription is an ongoing contract rather than a one-time purchase... the customer should hear that from you rather than discover it at renewal. Where the product genuinely shines is retrofit: the relays can take over an existing multi-tenant panel's call buttons, which modernizes an old building to app-based video without rewiring a single unit.",commonServicingIssues:["Black or dead screen -> PoE or network drop. Check the switch port and its power budget, the injector, and the cable run before condemning the panel.","Screen live but the panel will not place calls -> lost internet. The panel is useless without a cloud path; escalate to building IT or the ISP, not to door hardware.","App will not release the door, but card access works -> ButterflyMX side only. Its relay wiring or its cloud connection. The working card path already proved the strike, lock supply and door hardware are fine.","Neither the app nor a card opens the door -> shared downstream. Go to the strike or mag lock, the lock power supply, and any fire-alarm release or lockdown input that may have latched.","Resident missing from the directory, calls routing to the wrong unit, or delivery passes not working -> account-side configuration in the property's ButterflyMX portal. Not serviceable at the door; hand it to the property manager or ButterflyMX support.","New tenant cannot get in while existing tenants can -> enrollment, not hardware. Directory and credential provisioning is portal work owned by whoever holds the account.","Intermittent 'nobody can get in' complaints at a property with weak network -> the panel's dependency on the building uplink. Document the network as the root cause; no door-hardware fix exists for it.","Panel dead or flaky right after a lock was wired to it -> no isolation relay, so the lock's kickback reached the panel -> fit the isolation relay, isolate the lock's power circuit, then assess the panel.","Door does the opposite of what is wanted on release -> lock landed on the wrong relay terminal for its failure mode -> mag on N.C./COM, fail-secure or externally controlled on N.O./COM.","Panel failed after a storm -> no surge protection on the circuit -> move it to a surge-protected and ideally isolated circuit.","No entry at all during a power or internet outage -> no backup battery fitted -> add the backup battery if the customer needs entry through outages."],relatedGuides:["intercom-triage","access-control-systems","dks-1834-telephone-entry"],sources:["https://butterflymx.com/","ButterflyMX product and installation documentation (cloud architecture, PoE-powered panels, portal-managed directory).","Field capture: panel in service on a multi-tenant entry alongside a Secura Key ET-WXM reader, idle screen showing 'Tap to start'."],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Identity is confirmed from the panel in the field photo and the triage/scope guidance is drawn from how the product is architected. The exact panel model was not captured, so PoE class, relay count and terminal detail here are generic to the line \u2014 pull the specific model's install sheet from ButterflyMX before wiring or sizing a switch port.",photo:"photo-butterflymx-panel",photos:[{asset:"photo-butterflymx-panel",caption:"The panel on its idle screen. Everything a visitor uses lives in the cloud behind this glass, which is why the network drop matters more than the hardware."}]},{id:"ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din",name:"ICT Protege GX PRT-CTRL-DIN Integrated System Controller",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The DIN-rail brain of an ICT Protege GX system. It is the CPU for access control, intrusion, and building automation... it talks to every module on the network, stores all config and event history, and reports to the head-end software or a monitoring station. One controller handles 2 doors (up to 4 readers), with onboard inputs, relays, a bell output, and 10/100 Ethernet. The units labeled Upper/Lower Garage and Front Door Reader in the field cabinet are readers landed on this controller's two reader ports.",specs:["2 reader ports, each independently selectable for Wiegand or RS-485 (OSDP configurable) operation... up to 4 readers, 2 doors","8 high-security monitored inputs (zones, shown 1-8 on the front panel)","2 Form C relay outputs, 7A at 30VAC/DC resistive/inductive","1 monitored bell output (electronically fused)","4 low-current outputs","10/100 Ethernet onboard; 32-bit processor; AES (128/192/256) with factory HTTPS cert","Powered from 12 VDC on the N+ / N- terminals... no internal regulation or isolation, so feed it from a PRT-PSU-DIN","Onboard reader is disabled by default... it must be addressed/enabled in the Protege UI to come alive"],proAdvice:"Wire the lock output by failure mode, not by habit. The relay outputs are Form C with both NO and NC. A fail-secure strike lands on NO (energize to pass power and unlock). A fail-safe mag lock lands on NC (energize to hold power and lock, de-energize to release). The controller ships a 1N4007 diode for the lock output... it mounts at the lock terminals, not at the panel. Do not exceed 7A on a relay or you damage the output.\n\nThe lock output is shared with the bell/siren function by default. If you need the bell and a lock, reassign the lock to Relay 1 (CP001:03) or Relay 2 (CP001:04) in software. Out of the box the desired lock output is not configured on the reader port.\n\nThe onboard reader behaves like a separate reader-expander on its own address. A real one, the [[model:ict-prt-rdm2-din|PRT-RDM2-DIN-485]], behaves the same way and is how you add doors three and four. It is off by default... set its address in the Protege interface to use it. The R1 and R2 front indicators show reader data activity, so if a reader is dead, glance at R1/R2 before you pull the reader apart... no blink usually means no data (unaddressed reader, or a Wiegand-versus-RS-485 mode mismatch). The numbered 1-8 indicators mirror input status once each input is typed and assigned to an area, which lets you verify a contact or REX at the panel without opening the software.\n\nFor new work, prefer RS-485/OSDP readers over Wiegand: easier and cheaper to wire, longer runs, firmware updates over the wire, and real encryption. Wiegand is there for compatibility with legacy readers.\n\nUse a PRT-PSU-DIN as the supply. It supports battery backup and reports a monitored supply back over the module network, which matters for a UL-style install. In a small job one supply can feed the controller and the module network as long as you stay under its load.",commonServicingIssues:["Reader dead, R1 or R2 not blinking on the front panel -> onboard/expander reader not addressed or mode mismatch (Wiegand vs RS-485) -> address and enable the reader and set the correct port mode in Protege.","Door will not release on a valid card, or the bell will not sound -> lock output still defaulted to the shared bell/lock function -> assign Relay 1 or Relay 2 as the dedicated lock output.","A zone sits in constant alarm or tamper -> wrong end-of-line resistor value, or the input was never typed and assigned -> set the input type and area and verify the EOL against the programmed config.","Controller shows offline in software -> Ethernet link, module RS-485 wiring/termination, or power -> check the Ethernet indicator, confirm N+/N- voltage from the PSU, and check module-network termination.","Relay contacts welded or a lock output that stopped switching -> switching an inductive DC lock with no suppression, or exceeding 7A -> install the supplied 1N4007 across the DC lock and confirm the load is under 7A.","Unlock schedule does not free the door during business hours, but valid cards still work -> a card grant proves power, reader, relay and comms are all fine, so the fault is the schedule itself -> most often First Person In / unlock-on-first-credential is enabled and no authorised card has arrived yet to release it; failing that, check the time zone, the holiday list and the door mode assigned to that schedule."],sources:["https://www.ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/protege-din-rail/protege-gx-din-rail-controller/","https://www.ict.co/media/jwahkfsl/prt-ctrl-din_protege_gx_din_rail_system_controller_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.en.adiglobaldistribution.ca/Product/IN-PRTCTRLDI"],relatedGuides:["request-to-exit-rex-wiring","fail-safe-fail-secure","maglocks","sequence-of-operations"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Specs cross-checked against the ICT product page and the PRT-CTRL-DIN installation manual (7A Form C relays, 8 inputs, 2 reader ports Wiegand/RS-485, 12 VDC N+/N-, NO=fail-secure / NC=fail-safe, 1N4007 supplied). Matches the field units in the cabinet photos.",photo:"photo-ict-protege-cabinet",photos:[{asset:"photo-ict-protege-cabinet",caption:"The cabinet as installed: PRT-CTRL-DIN on the right, a PRT-RDM2-DIN-485 reader expander on the left, PRT-PSU-DIN and its standby battery below. Blue-tape labels mark the upper and lower garage readers and the run to the elevator room."},{asset:"photo-ict-prt-ctrl-din",caption:"Controller close-up. Zone LEDs 1-8 across the top right, Ethernet, Power, Status and Fault down the left, Reader, Bell and Relay 1/2 across the middle."},{asset:"photo-ict-keypad-reader",caption:"An ICT keypad reader retrofitted onto a timber entry door, its plate covering the previous device's cutout."}]},{id:"access-control-plugin-power-supplies",name:"IEI/Linear PIP plug-in supplies + Codex AC transformer",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The plug-in power packs that feed low-voltage access gear. The IEI/Linear PIP series are regulated switching DC supplies that plug straight into a wall outlet... PIP12VDCRU is 12 VDC at 2 A (24 W), PIP24VDCRU is 24 VDC at 1 A (24 W). The Codex SEP-1620U is a plug-in AC transformer, 16.5 VAC 20 VA, the classic feed for a telephone-entry board or intercom. All three showed up on one outlet strip in the field cabinet, feeding readers, a controller, and the DKS panel.",specs:["IEI/Linear PIP12VDCRU (0-291312RU): input 100-240 VAC 50/60 Hz, output 12 VDC at 2 A (24 W), regulated, auto-resetting poly fuse, surge protected, red power LED, ground prong, EISA-2007, UL listed","IEI/Linear PIP24VDCRU (0-291324RU): input 100-240 VAC 50/60 Hz, output 24 VDC at 1 A (24 W), same protection and features","Codex SEP-1620U-OE6: input 120 VAC 60 Hz 0.23 A, output 16.5 VAC 20 VA, UL Class 2 transformer","The two PIP bricks are regulated DC; the Codex is AC (step-down transformer, not rectified)"],proAdvice:"Know which is which before you land a wire. The two black IEI bricks are regulated DC (12 V or 24 V). The white Codex is AC (16.5 VAC)... it is a transformer, not a DC supply. Feeding a DC device from the AC transformer, or the reverse, fries things. Read the label every time, because they hang side by side on the same strip.\n\nThe 16.5 VAC transformer is the telephone-entry and intercom feed. A DKS board wants that AC input (its spec calls for a 16 VAC 20 VA transformer). Do not substitute a 24 VDC PIP for it.\n\nMatch voltage to the device and leave current headroom. A 12 VDC reader or controller wants the 12 V PIP; a 24 V strike or mag wants the 24 V PIP. The PIP12 gives 2 A, the PIP24 gives 1 A... add up the device draw and stay under the rating, or the poly fuse trips. It auto-resets, so a device that browns out and recovers on a cycle is a sign you are over the current budget.\n\nThe auto-resetting poly fuse is a feature: after a short clears, it resets itself, no fuse to replace. But a supply that keeps cycling is telling you there is still a short or overload downstream.\n\nPlug-in supplies are the fast install (no cabinet, no board), but they hang on an outlet. On a backed-up system that outlet needs to be on the UPS or battery side, or the whole door drops on a power blip... in the field cabinet these share a strip, so the strip's source matters.\n\nVoltage drop still applies past the supply. The PIP regulates at the plug, but a long thin run to the lock still sags... measure at the device under load, not at the brick.",commonServicingIssues:["Device dead, supply LED off -> dead outlet, failed supply, or strip switched off -> confirm AC at the outlet, check the PIP's red LED, swap the supply.","Device browns out or cycles under load -> draw exceeds the supply rating and the poly fuse is tripping and resetting -> total the load, move to a higher-current supply or split the load.","Reader or controller behaves erratically -> an AC transformer feeding a DC device, or wrong voltage (12 vs 24) -> verify the label matches the device input and correct the supply.","Telephone-entry board dead or resetting -> wrong or failed AC transformer (needs about 16.5 VAC), or a DC supply mistakenly landed on it -> restore the 16.5 VAC Class 2 transformer.","Everything on the strip drops on a power blip -> plug-in supplies sitting on a non-backed-up outlet -> move the strip to the UPS or battery-backed source."],sources:["https://www.jmac.com/IEI_PIP12VD_CRU_p/IEI-PIP12VDCRU.htm","https://buydoorhardwarenow.com/iei_24vdc_power_supply","https://www.keylessaccesslocks.com/iei-linear-corp-iei-pip24vdcru-plug-in-24-vdc-power-supply-0-291324ru/","Field observation, cabinet outlet strip - IEI PIP12VDCRU, PIP24VDCRU, and Codex SEP-1620U-OE6 nameplates (16.5 VAC 20 VA)."],relatedGuides:["low-voltage-wiring","fail-safe-fail-secure","electric-strike-power-diagnosis"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"PIP12VDCRU (12 VDC 2 A) and PIP24VDCRU (24 VDC 1 A), 100-240 VAC input, auto-resetting poly fuse, EISA/UL cross-checked against IEI/Linear distributor sheets. Codex SEP-1620U-OE6 (120 VAC in, 16.5 VAC 20 VA out, UL Class 2) read from the field nameplate.",photo:"photo-access-control-psu-bricks",photos:[{asset:"photo-access-control-psu-bricks",caption:"The trap this entry leads with, on one outlet strip: two black IEI/Linear DC bricks (PIP12VDCRU 12V 2A, PIP24VDCRU 24V 1A) beside a white Codex SEP-1620U-OE6 putting out 16.5 VAC. DC and AC, side by side, same shape."}]},{id:"standalone-wiegand-keypad-reader",name:"Standalone / Wiegand keypad-reader (weatherproof)",category:"Keypad & standalone",whatItIs:"The common outdoor metal keypad-reader you see at gates and doors... a zinc-alloy vandal case with a backlit keypad and a built-in prox reader. It works two ways: standalone, storing its own users and driving a lock relay with no panel, or as a plain Wiegand reader passing card/PIN data to a controller like ICT or DKS. Most read 125 kHz EM (often 13.56 MHz Mifare too), hold roughly 1000-2000 users standalone, and output Wiegand 26-bit, in an IP65 to IP68 potted housing.",specs:["Modes: standalone controller OR Wiegand output reader","Credentials: 125 kHz EM (often plus 13.56 MHz Mifare), PIN, or card+PIN","Standalone capacity: roughly 1000-2000 users, model-dependent","Output: Wiegand 26-bit (some units 26-37 bit)","Relay: one lock output, adjustable door/relay time, short-circuit protection","Inputs: exit button (REX) and often a doorbell","Housing: zinc-alloy anti-vandal, IP65-IP68, backlit, LDR tamper, buzzer","Power: 12 VDC typical, low draw; a 1N400x diode (usually included) goes across a shared-supply DC lock"],proAdvice:"Decide standalone vs Wiegand first, because it changes everything. Standalone means the keypad IS the controller... it stores users and switches the lock on its own relay. The weakness is that the credentials and the relay live at the door, so a smashed keypad exposes the lock wiring. Wiegand means the keypad is dumb... it just sends card/PIN to a panel inside, and the panel holds the lock. That is more secure, because tampering the keypad does not release the door.\n\nOn a standalone unit, protect the relay. If the keypad's own relay drives a mag lock or strike directly, that lock output at the door is the attack surface. Where security matters, run it as a Wiegand reader into a controller behind the wall, or keep the real lock relay inside on a separate supply and use the keypad only as the trigger.\n\nInstall the diode. When the keypad shares a supply with a DC lock, the coil kicks back and can damage the keypad... land the supplied 1N400x across the lock, band toward positive. Skipping it is a slow keypad-killer.\n\nWiegand wiring is the usual four... D0, D1, V+, ground, plus the exit-button and lock lines on a standalone. Swapped D0/D1 reads as nothing or garbage. Confirm V+ and ground at the head before you suspect the keypad.\n\nProgram housekeeping on standalone units: there is no software and no backup. Keep a written record of user IDs, codes, and the master/programming code... a lost master means a factory reset and re-enrolling everyone.",commonServicingIssues:["Keypad backlights but no card or PIN opens the door -> in Wiegand mode the panel is not seeing data (swapped D0/D1 or format mismatch); in standalone mode the relay or lock circuit is open -> confirm the mode, then check the data pair or the relay/lock wiring.","Works on PIN but not card, or the reverse -> wrong card frequency (125 kHz EM vs 13.56 MHz Mifare) or that entry mode is disabled -> confirm credential type and the enabled entry modes.","Keypad died right after a lock was added -> no suppression diode across the DC lock, inductive kickback damaged it -> install the 1N400x across the lock, replace the keypad.","Lost programming or master code -> no software backup on standalone units -> factory reset and re-enroll; keep a written code record going forward.","Intermittent operation outdoors -> water past a cracked pot or a non-rated unit, or voltage drop on a long 12 VDC run -> confirm the IP rating and mounting seal, measure voltage at the keypad under load."],sources:["https://fpc-security.com/products/visionis-vis-3022-outdoor-weatherproof-keypad-reader-standalone-wiegand","https://www.seco-larm.com/product/sk-2612-spq/","https://www.getkisi.com/guides/wiegand","Field observation, keypad at a DKS telephone-entry door - exact manufacturer and model not yet confirmed."],relatedGuides:["keypad-reader-servicing","fail-safe-fail-secure","low-voltage-wiring"],verified:!1,verifyNote:"Written to the standalone/Wiegand keypad-reader device class from multiple manufacturer datasheets (Visionis, SECO-LARM, Kisi) because no datasheet for the specific field unit could be located. The field photo now confirms the branding reads ALLOY ACCESS, but no manufacturer datasheet under that name has been found, so capacity, frequency and IP rating remain class-typical rather than unit-exact and this stays verified:false until a label or datasheet is captured.",photos:[{asset:"photo-dks-entry-alloy-keypad",caption:"The field unit this entry was written from: an ALLOY ACCESS keypad surface-mounted onto the face of a DoorKing 1834 telephone-entry panel, sharing the same opening. A retrofit, not a factory pairing."},{diagram:"standalone-vs-panel-keypad",caption:"The decision this entry leads with, drawn: in standalone mode the credentials AND the lock power sit outside the door; as a Wiegand reader only data crosses, and the lock stays behind the wall."}]},{id:"dolphin-uhf-long-range-reader",name:"Transmitter Solutions Dolphin UHF Long-Range Reader (DOLCWIUHF-910)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A long-range UHF vehicle-access reader. It reads a passive windshield tag on an approaching car from up to about 30 ft and hands the ID to the access panel as 26-bit Wiegand, so a resident rolls up and the gate opens hands-free. Operates in the 902-928 MHz UHF band with frequency hopping, IP66 for outdoor gates. It pairs only with Dolphin UHF windshield stickers... not 125 kHz prox or 13.56 MHz smart cards. The DOLPHIN-labeled runs in the garage cabinet feed this reader.",specs:["Brand/model: Transmitter Solutions Dolphin, DOLCWIUHF-910 (reader) or DOLCWIUHFP-910 (mini, integrated antenna)","Frequency: 902-928 MHz UHF, frequency-hopping (North America)","Read range: up to about 30 ft, adjustable (typical 8-25 ft)","Output: 26-bit Wiegand to the access panel; the R1 also provides optocoupler inputs and two relay outputs","Antenna (R1): 9 dBi circular-polarized, 50 ohm, N/SMA connector; the mini has an integrated antenna","Read modes: timing and trigger","Housing: IP66 outdoor-rated","Power: 12/24 VDC","Tags: Dolphin UHF passive windshield stickers, EPC Class 1 Gen2, pre-programmed 8-digit (3-digit facility + 5-digit ID), tamper-destruct one-time-use","Accessory: DOLWCV Wiegand filter, limits repeat re-transmission from stopped or parked tagged vehicles"],proAdvice:"It only reads its own tags. This is the number-one gotcha. The Dolphin UHF reader is a 900 MHz system... it will not read a 125 kHz prox fob or a 13.56 MHz smart card. If a customer wants one credential for the pedestrian door AND the vehicle gate, they need a dual-tech credential (one that carries both an LF/HF chip and a UHF inscription) or two separate credentials. Do not promise cross-compatibility.\n\nWiegand out, so it lands like any reader. It sends 26-bit Wiegand to the panel (ICT, DKS, etc.), so from the panel's side it is just another reader on a gate. The tag's 8-digit number (3-digit facility + 5-digit ID) gets enrolled into the panel like any credential, so match the panel's facility-code handling.\n\nAim and range are the whole install. The reader has to see the approaching car's tag with enough lead time for the gate to open, but not so much range that it reads a car in the next lane or one idling in front. Set the range so only one vehicle and one tag sit in the read zone. The manual's rule of thumb: place it so the tagged car can roll through without stopping, while an untagged car cannot fit between the read point and the gate.\n\nThe Wiegand filter (DOLWCV) exists for a reason. A tagged car idling in the read zone, waiting for the gate or parked nearby, re-transmits its tag over and over and can hammer the panel or hold the gate open. The DOLWCV limits how often a given tag ID re-fires. If a site has stop-and-go traffic at the gate, plan for it up front.\n\nMetalized windshields kill range. Modern metallic or heat-reflective windshield coatings and defroster lines block UHF. The tag has to go on a non-coated spot (many windshields have an uncoated cutout near the mirror), or the customer needs a headlight-mount tag. Test the read range on the actual vehicle before you commit the mount... a tag that reads on the bench may not read through the glass.\n\nTamper-destruct tags are one-time. The stickers self-destruct if peeled, by design, so they cannot be moved car to car. That non-transferability is a feature, but a resident who changes vehicles needs a new tag, not a moved one. Budget for that.\n\nBe honest about the security posture. UHF vehicle tags are convenient but low-security... the ID is a static number, and these tags can be read and cloned with the right UHF equipment. For a gated community that is usually an acceptable convenience tradeoff. For a high-security site, pair it with a second factor at a manned point or an encrypted credential, and set the panel to flag duplicate or rapid reads.",commonServicingIssues:["Gate does not open as a tagged car approaches -> read range too short, tag on a metalized part of the windshield, or reader mis-aimed -> raise the range, move the tag to a non-coated spot or a headlight mount, re-aim the antenna down the approach lane.","Gate opens for cars in the wrong lane or too early -> read range set too wide -> reduce the range so only one lane and one vehicle sit in the zone.","Gate holds open or the panel floods with reads -> a tagged car idling or parked in the read zone re-transmitting -> install or adjust the DOLWCV Wiegand filter, or reposition so idling cars fall outside the zone.","New tag will not grant access -> tag number not enrolled, or wrong facility code in the panel -> add the 8-digit tag number (3-digit facility + 5-digit ID) to the panel and confirm the facility handling.","Reader dead, no reads at all -> no 12/24 VDC power, or D0/D1 not landed at the panel -> meter power at the reader, confirm D0/D1 and ground to the panel.","Range fell off over time or goes intermittent in weather -> loose antenna connection, water ingress past the IP66 seal, or a failing tag -> check the N/SMA connector, inspect the housing seal, and retest with a known-good tag."],sources:["https://www.maglocks.com/dolphin-dolcwiuhfp-910-mini-uhf-long-range-reader.html","https://www.maglocks.com/dolphin-uhf-windshield-sticker.html","https://manuals.plus/dolphin/dolcwiuhf-910-uhf-long-range-reader-manual","https://elitegates.net/products/transmitter-solution-dolcwiuhfp-long-range-card-reader"],relatedGuides:["gate-operators","access-control-systems","keypad-reader-servicing"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Transmitter Solutions Dolphin DOLCWIUHF-910/DOLCWIUHFP-910 cross-checked against maglocks product pages, the DOLCWIUHF-910 and UHF-R1 instruction manuals, and Elite Gates (902-928 MHz UHF, ~30 ft range, IP66, 26-bit Wiegand, EPC Gen2 windshield stickers with 3-digit facility + 5-digit ID and tamper-destruct; DOLWCV Wiegand filter accessory). Matches the DOLPHIN-labeled garage reader runs in the field photos.",photos:[{diagram:"uhf-read-zone",caption:"Setting the read zone, which is the whole install. Wide enough that a tagged car never stops, tight enough that an untagged car cannot fit between the zone and the gate."}]},{id:"keri-systems-pxl-nxt",name:"Keri Systems PXL-500 / NXT Controllers and Credentials",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"Keri Systems is a US access control manufacturer whose PXL-series controllers you inherit on a lot of older commercial sites. The PXL-500 Tiger (and Tiger II) is the multi-door workhorse... one door onboard, two with the SB-593 expansion, two reader ports, up to 256 doors per site, managed by Doors.NET (or legacy Doors32) software. Its signature feature and its headache is the proprietary Keri MS credential format. The newer NXT line runs 13.56 MHz.",specs:["PXL-500 Tiger / Tiger II: 1 door onboard (2nd via SB-593 expansion board), 2 reader ports, 1 alarm output, up to 256 doors per site, up to 65K cardholders per controller","Reader-port variants: PXL-500P for Keri MS readers, PXL-500W for Wiegand readers... set by which model you have","PXL-510: the same controller with one extra serial port, used to arm and disarm a NetworX NX-8E alarm panel from the access system. Doors, readers, relays and network are identical to the PXL-500... it is NOT a TCP/IP-native board, and Ethernet on either one still comes from a LAN-500/520 adapter","Lock relay: 1.0 A, 24 VDC max, dry SPDT (Form C) for a lock, gate, or door","Reader cable: 6-conductor, 24 AWG or heavier, shielded, no coax","Networking: RS-485 between panels, RS-232 to a PC, LAN-500/LAN-520 adapter for TCP/IP, or modem; auto-configures RS-485 grounding","Software: Doors.NET (current) or Doors32 (legacy)","Family: PXL-250 single-door predecessor; NXT controllers with NXT-RM3 reader interface module","Credentials: Keri MS / K-format (proprietary 125 kHz PSK, the '10X' series... PKT-10X fob, KC-10X/MT-10X cards, AP-10X patch); NXT format (13.56 MHz MIFARE Classic 1K, NXT-K fob); 26-bit Wiegand ('26X' series, PSK-3 fob)","MS readers: MS-3000 mullion, MS-5000 single-gang, MS-7000 extended range, MS-4000 vandal... all 125 kHz, PXL-500 only; also read Indala ASP","Alarm output: a second 1.0 A Form C dry closure, programmable for door-forced and door-held-open","Onboard inputs: door status (normally closed), two configurable request-to-exit, and a global release input that drops every lock relay on the network","Event buffer: 3,600 transactions, first-in-first-out once full","RS-485 network: up to 128 controllers, 256 doors, with automatic open/short detection on the loop","Only the master controller (address 1) talks to the PC; every other controller reaches it over RS-485","Surge protection: MOVs across every relay output, transorbs across inputs, plus reverse-polarity protection on the power input"],proAdvice:"The credential reorder is the whole pain, and it is why this entry exists. Keri MS / K-format is proprietary 125 kHz PSK encoding, not standard 26-bit Wiegand... generic prox stock is useless on a Keri MS reader. To reorder you need genuine Keri MS credentials, or compatible blanks on T5577/EM4305 encoded with the K-format PSK structure and matched to the site's facility code and card-number range. Confirm which reader is installed (MS vs Wiegand vs NXT) before you quote a customer replacement fobs, or you ship the wrong thing and eat it.\n\nThree credential worlds, do not mix them. A PXL-500P reads Keri MS (125 kHz proprietary). A PXL-500W reads Wiegand. NXT controllers and readers read 13.56 MHz NXT. An MS fob will not read on a Wiegand or NXT reader. Identify the reader model, then match credential technology first and format second.\n\nThe onboard relay is small... 1.0 A at 24 VDC. A mag lock or a big strike can exceed it, so switch high-current locks through a separate power-supply relay rather than the Keri onboard relay, which is the trigger pattern rather than the pass-the-current pattern.\n\nSoftware generation matters. Doors32 is legacy; Doors.NET is what current installs run. A site still on Doors32 may need a migration to add features or get support... check what they are running before promising software changes.\n\nNetworking is old-school serial at heart. Panels chain on RS-485; TCP/IP is bolted on with a LAN-500/520 adapter. If a Keri site 'fell off the network,' suspect the LAN adapter or the RS-485 chain before the controller... it auto-senses the comms method.\n\nIt is a mature, obsolescent platform. Keri still supports PXL, but parts and MS credentials are stocked by few suppliers. For a customer facing expansion or credential-supply pain, this is the migration conversation... NXT, or another manufacturer on OSDP and encrypted readers.",commonServicingIssues:["New fobs will not read at all -> wrong credential technology for the reader (MS vs Wiegand vs NXT), or generic 26-bit ordered for an MS reader -> identify the reader model and order the matching Keri format encoded to the site's facility code and range.","Card reads but denies -> facility code or card range not enrolled, or wrong format loaded in Doors.NET -> verify the site facility code and the format setting in software.","Controller offline in software -> LAN-500/520 adapter, RS-485 chain, or comms method -> check the LAN adapter and RS-485 wiring and grounding before suspecting the board.","Lock chatters or the relay fails on a mag -> lock current exceeds the 1.0 A onboard relay -> switch the lock via a separate power-supply relay, not the Keri relay.","Cannot add features or get support -> site still on legacy Doors32 -> migrate to Doors.NET."],sources:["https://kerisys.com/products/pxl-500/","https://www.keri-kb.com/help/Content/Hardware%20Overview.htm","https://securityidsystems.com/blog/keri-systems-cards-explained/","https://accesshardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/Keri-PXL-500-Tiger-II-Control-ss.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500-Installation.pdf"],relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","credential-technology-and-card-formats","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","keri-pxl-reader-fault-checklist","keri-pxl-takeover","keypad-reader-servicing"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Specs cross-checked against the Keri PXL-500/510 architectural bid specification (Keri Technical Publications) as well as the product and KB pages: 1.0 A / 24 VDC max SPDT lock relay and a second 1.0 A Form C alarm output, 65,000 cardholders, 3,600-transaction FIFO buffer, two reader inputs in either MS proximity or Wiegand, door-status / dual-REX / global-release inputs, RS-485 to 128 controllers and 256 doors, master at address 1, 12 VDC operation with reverse-polarity protection. Note a common retail listing states a 3 A lock relay; the manufacturer's own bid spec says 1.0 A, and that is the figure used here. No field photo captured; the entry carries a drawn board layout instead.",photos:[{diagram:"keri-pxl500-board-layout",caption:"What is on the PXL-500 controller, and the two things that cost the most time: the reader technology is fixed by which model you have, and the 1.0 A lock relay is a strike, not a mag lock."}]},{id:"kantech-kt-400",name:"Kantech KT-400 Ethernet Four-Door Controller",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A four-door, Ethernet-ready access controller from Kantech (Johnson Controls), managed by EntraPass. It carries an onboard 10/100Base-T port and talks to the gateway over 128-bit AES, holds 100,000 cards and 20,000 events standalone, and drives four readers, four supervised lock outputs and four Form C relays off removable, colour-coded terminal blocks. Expansion modules take it to 256 inputs and 256 outputs, which is what makes it the usual choice for elevator interfacing on a Kantech site. It also back-integrates with older KT-100, KT-200 and KT-300 controllers.",specs:["Four doors / four reader interfaces; supports Wiegand, proximity, ABA clock-and-data, bar code, magnetic, integrated keypad and smart card readers","Capacity: 100,000 cards per controller, 20,000 events stored in stand-alone mode","Encryption: 128-bit AES to the EntraPass gateway; UL-294 and UL-1076 listed","Inputs: 16 monitored points onboard, single or double EOL, expandable to 256 (max 128 using double-EOL); AWG #22 to 600 m (2,000 ft)","Reader outputs: 16 open-collector outputs, 25 mA max each (4 per reader interface) for LED and buzzer feedback","Controlled output relays: four onboard Form C, 30 VDC 3 A max each","Reader power output: max 1.0 A at 5 VDC or 12 VDC, typical 250 mA per reader, protected and supervised","Door strike power: 12 VDC, 2.0 A max, typical 500 mA per lock, supervised; external lock power 12 or 24 VDC (to 28 VDC), up to 750 mA per lock, 3 A total","Input power: 16.5 VAC or 24 VAC, 75 VA Class 2 transformer; battery backup 12 VDC 7 or 12 Ah supervised, up to 12 hours","Communication: Ethernet 10/100Base-T (RJ-45), RS-485 (COM1), RS-232 (COM3, RJ-12); up to 115,200 baud with automatic detection","Expansion: SPI 6-pin port supplying 12 VDC / 500 mA shared with the auxiliary port; KT-MOD-INP16, KT-MOD-REL8, KT-MOD-OUT16 modules","Memory: 256 MB flash, 128 MB RAM; configuration and events survive at least 10 years without power","Operating temperature 2 to 49 C (35 to 120 F), indoor only; max 85% RH non-condensing","Cabinet 37.6 x 30.5 x 12.6 cm (14.8 x 12.0 x 4.95 in), 4.0 kg; PCB 23.1 x 14.0 x 3.4 cm"],proAdvice:"The removable terminal blocks are the reason this board is pleasant to service, and using them properly is the whole technique. Every reader, input and output lands on a plug-in block that lifts off the header, so adding a reader means pulling one plug, landing the head on it in your hand, and re-seating it... the other three doors stay terminated and running. Kantech even sells the spare block kit (KT-400-CON) because the blocks are meant to be worked off the board. The step-by-step is in the reader wiring guide.\n\nReader voltage is selectable and unforgiving. The reader power output does 5 VDC or 12 VDC at up to 1.0 A total, typically 250 mA a head. Put a 5 V reader's red lead on a 12 V terminal and you damage the reader, so confirm the head's rating before you land anything.\n\nYou cannot mix reader technologies across the four ports. The controller supports Wiegand, ABA clock-and-data, keypad and smart card heads, but one KT-400 runs one technology... establish what the site already has before you order a replacement. And whatever you fit, the door's reader type and card format have to be set in EntraPass to match, because a perfectly wired head configured as the wrong type reads nothing or returns garbage.\n\nMind the two current budgets separately. Reader power is 1.0 A across all four heads. Onboard lock power is 12 VDC at 2.0 A total, about 500 mA a lock. A mag lock or a latch-retraction device with real inrush belongs on external lock power (12 or 24 VDC, 750 mA per lock, 3 A total) or its own supply, not hung off the board. The Form C relays are 30 VDC at 3 A, which is generous for a strike and still worth checking against a big lock.\n\nIt is the elevator controller on Kantech sites. Floor control needs relay-per-floor capacity, and that is what the KT-MOD-REL8 and KT-MOD-OUT16 modules on the SPI port are for, up to 256 outputs. Budget the SPI port's 500 mA, which is shared with the auxiliary port.\n\nIt back-integrates, which is why you meet it on retrofits. A KT-400 will sit on an RS-485 loop with KT-100, KT-300 and (on a separate loop) KT-200 hardware, so it is the usual way to put an Ethernet head-end on an older Kantech site without replacing every panel at once.",commonServicingIssues:["Reader beeps and flashes but no read reaches the controller -> D0/D1 swapped at the reader block, or the door's reader type/format not set in EntraPass -> confirm green to Data 0 and white to Data 1, then check the reader type and card format in software.","New reader dead at power-up -> no V+ or no ground at the head, or a 5 V reader landed on the 12 V terminal -> meter voltage and ground at the reader under load and confirm the head's rated voltage before re-powering.","Card reads and is denied -> facility code or card format does not match what is configured, or the card was never enrolled -> verify the format and facility code against a known-good card.","Lock chatters, or the board resets when a lock fires -> lock draw exceeds the 2.0 A onboard budget, or inrush from a mag or latch-retraction device -> move the lock to external lock power or its own supply.","Controller offline in EntraPass -> Ethernet link, the RS-485 loop, or power -> check the network LEDs, confirm the 16.5/24 VAC supply under load, and check the RS-485 chain before suspecting the board.","Readers on the same controller behave inconsistently after a head swap -> a reader of a different technology fitted to a controller running another -> match the technology across all four ports.","Zone stuck in trouble or alarm -> EOL resistor value does not match the configured supervision (single vs double EOL) -> confirm the resistor against the programmed input type."],sources:["https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/kantech/v/u/Kantech/en-US/KT-400-Ethernet-Four-Door-Controller-Datasheet","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/KT-400-DataSheet.pdf","https://www.turnstiles.us/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/KT-400-Installation-Manual-www-TURNSTILES-us-w.pdf","https://www.anixter.com/content/dam/Suppliers/Kantech/Literature/KT-400%20Four%20Door%20Controller%20-%20LITERATURE.pdf"],relatedGuides:["wiring-and-testing-a-reader-kt400","keypad-reader-servicing","credential-technology-and-card-formats","elevator-access-control","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Every figure here is read directly from the Kantech KT-400 datasheet PDF (100,000 cards / 20,000 stand-alone events, 128-bit AES, 16 monitored points expandable to 256, 16 reader outputs at 25 mA, four Form C relays at 30 VDC 3 A, reader power 1.0 A at 5 or 12 VDC, door strike 12 VDC 2.0 A, external lock power 750 mA per lock / 3 A total, 16.5 or 24 VAC 75 VA input, 12 V 7/12 Ah battery to 12 hours, SPI 12 VDC 500 mA shared with aux, 256 MB flash / 128 MB RAM, 2-49 C, cabinet 37.6 x 30.5 x 12.6 cm, KT-MOD-INP16/REL8/OUT16, UL-294 and UL-1076). No photo captured; the field unit was not documented.",photos:[{diagram:"kt400-board-layout",caption:"What is on the board. The two current budgets are separate and both small... 1.0 A across all four readers, 2.0 A across all four locks."},{diagram:"kt400-reader-wiring",caption:"The Wiegand colour map at a reader port, landed on the removable mod plug rather than on a live board."}]},{id:"ict-tsec-reader",category:"Access control",name:"ICT tSec Multi-Technology Card Reader (Standard / Extra / Mini)",whatItIs:"The reader half of an ICT Protege system, and the head you land on the PRT-CTRL-DIN's reader ports or on a PRT-RDM2-DIN-485 expander. One reader reads both 13.56 MHz smart cards (MIFARE and DESFire) and 125 kHz proximity, so a site can move to smart cards a badge at a time instead of in one weekend. It comes in three sizes on the same electronics... Standard is the mullion strip for an aluminium door stile, Extra is the wall-plate size that covers the cutout the last reader left behind, and Mini is the short one for tight jambs and turnstile posts. Optional Bluetooth and NFC add phone credentials, and it speaks Wiegand, ICT's own encrypted RS-485, or OSDP, which is what makes it the reader you can drop onto somebody else's panel as well as a Protege one.",specs:["Three sizes, one platform: Standard 117 x 46 x 18mm (mullion), Extra 117 x 75 x 18mm (full wall-plate / lightswitch footprint), Mini 85 x 46 x 17mm","Card technologies: 13.56 MHz ISO/IEC 14443 Type A (MIFARE, DESFire EV1/EV2/EV3) and 125 kHz pulse-width-modulated proximity, on multi-technology models. Single-technology editions exist... check the order code before you promise a customer both","Read range: MIFARE card 60mm, DESFire EV2/EV3 ISO 36mm, DESFire EV1 ISO 15mm, 125 kHz clamshell 40mm. Fobs read roughly half of that","Three output modes: standard Wiegand (26 or 34 bit, card-defined), ICT encrypted RS-485, or OSDP 2.2 with Secure Channel (AES-128)","Cable distance is the whole argument for RS-485: Wiegand maxes at 150m (492ft) on 22 or 18 AWG; ICT RS-485 and OSDP run to 900m (3000ft) on minimum 24 AWG 120-ohm shielded twisted pair","Wiegand cable is NOT suitable for RS-485 or OSDP... ICT says so explicitly, because crosstalk between untwisted data wires corrupts reads and drops readers offline. Re-pull the cable when you convert a site","Reader pigtail: red V+, black V-, green D0/NA, white D1/NB, orange L1, brown L2, blue BZ, yellow and violet the RS-485 pair. The D0 and D1 outputs switch 50mA max... exceed that and you damage the output","EOL termination: one 120-ohm resistor across the yellow and violet wires at the cable splice terminates the far end of an RS-485 bus","Power: 12 VDC (9.5-14 VDC), 10.5 VDC startup. Peak reading current 254mA Standard, 298mA Extra, 203mA Mini","Configurable LED strip: 2-colour control over the external LED wires, or 16 selectable colours mapped to Protege function codes... that 16-colour mode needs an RS-485 connection or the Protege Config App","Optional Bluetooth 4.0 (proximity unlock to 0.5m, shake-to-unlock to 5m, AES-128) and NFC (to 60mm, AES-256 DESFire credential, Android 4.4+ with ISO7816-4)","IP65 fully encapsulated, -35 to 66 C (UL/cUL), keep-alive transmission every 30 seconds for tamper management","Order codes run PRX-TSEC-STD / -EXTRA / -MINI with suffixes for keypad (-KP), Bluetooth (-BT), 125kHz-only (-125), DESFire (-DF) and colour (-B/-W). Vandal-resistant covers PRX-SVRC / PRX-XVRC / PRX-MVRC"],proAdvice:"Ship-state is the first thing to know, because it explains most of the confusion on a first install: tSec readers leave the factory in Wiegand mode, and they switch themselves to ICT RS-485 when they are wired in the RS-485 configuration. That auto-switch is why a head can behave differently on the bench than on the door, and why the fix for a silent reader is often the wiring configuration rather than a config card. TSL readers are the opposite, RS-485 by default, so do not carry a habit across the two ranges.\n\nThe wire colours are where the money is. Wiegand rides green (D0) and white (D1); RS-485 and OSDP ride yellow and violet. They land on the same D0/NA and D1/NB terminals at the module, so a reader wired with the wrong pair looks perfectly terminated and does absolutely nothing. That is the number-one silent-reader call on this range. Address is set by wiring too, not software... green and orange left apart gives address 0, the entry reader, and green tied to orange hardwires address 1, the exit reader, permanently. On an entry/exit pair the second head is a wire link, not a programming step.\n\nFor new work take OSDP with Secure Channel and be done with it. ICT's own recommended settings say so: OSDP output, site-specific custom encryption keys ordered from ICT rather than the shipped default keys, and every card technology you are not actually using disabled, because leaving all of them enabled slows the read. Default keys are for compatibility, not for security, and a site running default keys with the factory ICT encryption is a finding you should raise in writing. Baud has to match the reader port... default is 38400, and a baud mismatch is an offline reader with perfectly good wiring.\n\nShield discipline is stricter here than on a generic reader run. Join the pigtail shield and the cable shield at the splice, frame-ground the cable shield to the metal enclosure at ONE end, and do not land it on AUX-, 0V or V-. The reader's own shield wire is not terminated inside the reader, so there is nothing to chase at that end. Get this wrong and you get the classic intermittent... a reader that is fine all morning and misreads when the plant equipment starts.\n\nFirmware is the reason to own a [[tool:rs485-to-usb-converter|485-to-USB converter]]... ICT's configuration guide requires one plus LoadIT 1.5 or later, and the boot-mode strapping and load procedure are written out on that tool's page. The reader-side detail worth keeping here is that you do not need to touch the head to find out where you stand: on a live system the controller's web interface lists connected readers and their current firmware under Application Software.",commonServicingIssues:["Reader is powered and lit but the controller sees nothing -> wired on the wrong data pair. Wiegand uses green/white, RS-485 and OSDP use yellow/violet, and both land on the same D0/NA and D1/NB terminals -> re-land the correct pair for the mode the port is set to.","Reader offline in OSDP mode with correct wiring -> baud mismatch between reader and reader port (reader default 38400) -> set both ends the same with the Protege Config App or an encoded MIFARE config card.","Both readers on a door answer as the same device, or the exit reader never appears -> address is set by wiring, not software. Green and orange apart = address 0 (entry); green tied to orange = hardwired address 1 (exit) -> link or unlink the pair at the splice.","Site converted from Wiegand to RS-485 and readers now drop offline or corrupt reads -> old Wiegand cable reused. ICT states Wiegand cable is unsuitable for RS-485/OSDP -> pull 24 AWG 120-ohm shielded twisted pair, and terminate the far end with a 120-ohm resistor across yellow and violet.","Card reads fine on one reader and not another after a technology swap -> single-technology edition installed, or the technology was disabled during config -> check the order code for the -125 / -DF suffix and re-enable the technology in the reader config.","LEDs will not follow Protege function-code colours -> the 16-colour mode only works over RS-485 or via the Config App; a Wiegand-wired reader gets 2-colour control off the external LED lines only -> move it to RS-485 or accept two colours.","Reader intermittently misreads when nearby plant runs -> shield landed at both ends, or landed on AUX-/0V/V- -> frame-ground the cable shield to the enclosure at one end only, joined to the pigtail shield at the splice.","Firmware update fails or never starts -> the reader gives about 30 seconds from power-up in boot mode -> set up LoadIT 1.5+ and select the file first, then power the reader with blue, orange and brown strapped to black."],sources:["https://www.ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/proximity-card-rfid-readers/tsec-standard-rfid-card-reader/","https://www.ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/proximity-card-rfid-readers/tsec-extra-rfid-card-reader/","https://www.ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/proximity-card-rfid-readers/tsec-mini-rfid-card-reader/","https://www.ict.co/media/hwgn4jxj/prx-tsec_tsec_card_reader_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.ict.co/media/2podvv4n/ict_card_reader_configuration_guide.pdf","https://ict.co/media/iz1p2x1y/an-254_configuring_osdp_readers_in_protege.pdf"],relatedGuides:["keypad-reader-servicing","credential-technology-and-card-formats","wiring-and-testing-a-reader-kt400","low-voltage-wiring","access-control-systems"],photo:"photo-ict-tsec-extra-reader",photos:[{asset:"photo-ict-tsec-extra-reader",caption:"A tSec Extra with the capacitive keypad, out of the box. The ICT mark sits above the keys and the pigtail is already stripped... black, red, green, blue and yellow visible, which is the colour map the wiring slide decodes."},{asset:"photo-ict-tsec-extra-box",caption:"The carton the identity comes from: PRX-TSEC-EXTRA-DF-KP-B beside a PRT-PSU-DIN-2A. Serials and barcodes are redacted. Note the description reads 13.56MHz only... this is a DESFire part, not multi-technology."},{diagram:"ict-tsec-reader-wiring",caption:"Which pair carries the data, and why the wrong one looks perfectly terminated"},{diagram:"ict-tsec-order-code",caption:"Decoding the order code before you quote a customer"}],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Field capture. The order code PRX-TSEC-EXTRA-DF-KP-B is legible on the carton in the photo, which fixes the size (Extra, wall-plate), the technology (DF, DESFire and MIFARE at 13.56 MHz with NO 125 kHz), the keypad and the colour. Specs are from the ICT tSec installation manual and card reader configuration guide. The reader shown is that exact part... a multi-technology or Standard-size unit differs in the ways the order-code slide sets out."},{id:"ict-prt-px8-din",category:"Access control",name:"ICT Protege PRT-PX8-DIN DIN Rail 8 Output Expander",whatItIs:"The relay board you add to a Protege system when the controller's two onboard Form C outputs run out. It clips onto the same DIN rail as the PRT-CTRL-DIN and the PRT-PSU-DIN, joins the same RS-485 module network, and gives you eight more high-current Form C relays to drive locks, sirens, lighting circuits, HVAC and whatever else the sequence of operations says has to switch. On a job where the controller's Relay 1 and Relay 2 are already spoken for by a lock and a bell, this is the module that lets you say yes to the extra door strike or the gate trigger without adding a second controller.",specs:["8 Form C relays, 7A N.O./N.C. at 30 VAC/DC resistive or inductive... the same rating as the controller's onboard relays","DC input 11-14 VDC; operating current 80mA typical; total combined current 3.25A max","DC pass-through output 10.83-14.0 VDC at 0.7A typical, with electronic shutdown at 1.1A","Low-voltage cutout 8.7 VDC, restore 10.5 VDC... it sheds the load before a flat standby battery does damage","Single RS-485 module network port, encrypted module comms, firmware upgradable over the network","Up to 250 modules on the Protege module network in any combination, over 900m (3000ft); further with a network repeater","Addressed in software using the module's serial number off the identification sticker... there is no DIP switch","157 x 90 x 60mm, 340g, standard DIN rail mount, indoor use only (0-93% RH non-condensing)","UL 294 / UL 1610 / CAN-ULC listed; UL and cUL installs require a listed enclosure inside the protected area","Do not confuse it with the PRT-HPX8-DIN. The H is Half DIN, a narrower 78mm two-tier module, and per ICT's datasheet its relays switch resistive loads up to 5A rather than this one's 7A... check which is on the rail before you quote a replacement"],proAdvice:"Add the module for outputs, not for current. Eight relays is eight dry contacts, and the module's own combined budget is 3.25A across everything including the pass-through... so it triggers locks, it does not feed them. Any maglock or bank of strikes still runs off a PRT-PSU-DIN or a separate supply, switched through these contacts, exactly as on the controller.\n\nSuppression is not optional on inductive loads. These are the same 7A Form C contacts as the controller's, and the same failure mode applies: switch a DC lock coil with nothing across it and the collapsing field welds the contacts over a few thousand cycles. Fit the diode at the lock, not at the panel, so the spike is clamped before it travels the run.\n\nAddressing catches people because there is nothing to flip. You set the address in the Protege interface using the serial number printed on the module, and the two errors you will actually hit are Address In Use, meaning another module already holds it, and an address above the controller's configured range. Run a view network status before you assign, and if the controller has secure registration turned on it will refuse the module entirely until you clear that setting... which reads exactly like a dead module if you do not know to look.\n\nOn the network side it is one more node on the same RS-485 module bus as everything else, so the bus rules apply unchanged: home the run properly, terminate the two physical ends, and remember the 900m budget is the whole bus rather than per module. A module that registers and then drops is nearly always termination or a power sag at the far end, not the board.",commonServicingIssues:["Module never comes online -> address not set, or set above the controller's configured module range -> assign an unused address using the serial number from the sticker, then power-cycle the module.","Module reports Address In Use -> another module already holds that address -> run view network status to list registered devices, pick a free address, and refresh with a network update.","Module refuses to register at all with correct wiring and address -> the controller has secure registration enabled -> set the network to not secured long enough to register the module.","Relay contacts welded or an output that stopped switching -> an inductive DC load switched with no suppression, or a load over 7A -> fit a diode across the lock at the lock, and confirm the load rating.","Outputs behave erratically or the module drops offline intermittently -> RS-485 bus not terminated at both physical ends, or supply voltage sagging toward the 8.7 VDC cutout at the far end of the run -> terminate the bus and meter the module's DC input under load.","Devices downstream of the pass-through cut out -> the pass-through electronically shuts down at 1.1A -> feed those loads from the PSU directly rather than through the module."],sources:["https://ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/protege-din-rail/protege-din-rail-8-output-expander/","https://ict.co/media/pbkd2kxf/prt-px8-din_protege_din_rail_8_output_expander_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.en.adiglobaldistribution.ca/Catalog/shopproducts/access-control/keypads-readers/reader-extenders/IN-PRTPX8DIN","https://ict.co/media/3zdkgevv/prt-hpx8-din.pdf"],relatedGuides:["dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","fail-safe-fail-secure","sequence-of-operations","low-voltage-wiring"],photos:[{diagram:"ict-prt-px8-din-outputs",caption:"Eight Form C relays, the current budget, and the half-DIN part it gets confused with"}]},{id:"sentex-infinity-telephone-entry",category:"Access control",name:"Chamberlain / Sentex Infinity Telephone Entry (S, M, L, B, DI)",whatItIs:"Inherited legacy. Sentex built telephone entry for gated communities and apartment complexes, Chamberlain bought the company in 1997, and the whole line was retired in 2013-14... which means every Infinity you meet is a service call on somebody else's install, never a new one. It is a close cousin to the DoorKing and SES panels already in this catalog and it triages the same way: a POTS dialer, a directory the visitor scrolls, four relays, and a resident who presses a digit on their phone to open the gate. There are five family members. B is card and code entry only with no telephone dial-out at all. DI carries a printed directory insert instead of a screen. L, S and M have the electronic directory. The earlier Spectrum, Ovation and Crown Jewel products are the same era and the same conversation... Crown Jewel was the high end of residential gate control in its day, Spectrum and Ovation were the budget printed-directory units.",specs:["Retired by Chamberlain in 2013-14 per the Chamberlain/myQ product history. Service, repair and takeover only... major components are no longer manufactured, so plan every job around parts you can actually source. Reseller pages describe the phase-out loosely as 'almost two decades ago', which is the older Vista and Horizon era rather than the Infinity line... no source disputes that the whole family is dead, so do not let the date argument stall the replacement conversation","Five variants: B (card/code only, no phone dialer), DI (printed directory insert), and L / S / M (electronic directory)","Multi-Link firmware is standard: up to 16 units chained on one system with shared programming and event reporting","Four relays. Relays 1 and 2 are always door/gate control; relays 3 and 4 are configurable as CONTROL, SHUNT, ALARM or CCTV","Resident opens from the phone by dialing a digit... relay 1, relay 2, relays 3 and 4, hang up without entry, or reset the call timer. Rotary pulse can trigger the first action only; everything else needs tone","Programming access code is six characters, factory 000000, at programming number 00","Relay activation (door open) time is 01-99 seconds per relay, factory 10 seconds. On a gate operator taking a momentary contact, set this down to about 2 seconds or the gate cycles until the relay drops","Entry codes are 4 or 5 digits; directory codes default to 3 digits (programming number 08)","Strikes-and-out keypad lockout: factory 2 incorrect entries, then that function is disabled for 3 minutes while the rest of the system keeps running","Call length limit factory 60 seconds; alarm call factory disabled; door-held threshold 60 seconds","Two card readers per unit; 26-bit format is enabled at programming number 82, facility codes at 24","Access restrictions: time zones, true and timed anti-passback, date-limited and use-limited codes (those two need full-control codes and SPS software 1.02+), up to 16 holidays","Door status monitoring responds to forced and held-open with a modem call to a preprogrammed number, a relay to a siren or light, or a relay to a monitoring station","Three ways to program: the 12-button keypad on the faceplate of the first unit in the chain, a modem, or Sentex SPS software"],proAdvice:"Frame it for the customer before you touch it. This is discontinued equipment on a dead product line, so the honest options are keep it limping on scavenged parts or budget a replacement panel, and saying that on the first visit is worth more than a clever repair. Where it earns its keep is that the surrounding infrastructure... the conduit, the pedestal, the gate operator, the strike... is usually fine, so a swap to a current DKS or an Akuvox unit is mostly a board-and-faceplate job rather than a dig.\n\nThe locksmith detail nobody expects: Chamberlain says outright that the locks on most Sentex cabinets are commonly keyed. Every Sentex enclosure in the county opens with the same key. If a customer is relying on that cabinet lock to protect the programming, tell them, and rekey or replace the cylinder... that is a five-minute upsell on a panel you are already standing in front of.\n\nTriage exactly as you would a DKS or SES: power, then comm path, then audio, then relay, then programming, in that order. The failure that has aged worst is the phone line, because these are POTS-era dialers and a fiber or VoIP cutover routinely kills dial-out while leaving the panel looking perfectly alive. Meter tip to ring before you blame the board... about 48 to 52 VDC on-hook says the line is live and the fault is inside, near zero says the fault is upstream and is not yours.\n\nOn a takeover, the programming access code is the gate. Factory is 000000 and a surprising number of installs never changed it. If it has been changed and nobody has it, you are into a factory reset conversation, and a reset costs you the whole directory... so photograph or print the directory first if the unit will still print (programming number 23 dumps memory, 22 verifies entries). Watch the relay-3 and relay-4 configuration on any panel that already works: if relay 3 is set as SHUNT it is bypassing an alarm sensor every time the door opens, and repurposing it as a second gate trigger silently un-shunts that sensor.\n\nThe two settings that generate the most complaints are both defaults. Ten seconds of door time is short for anyone carrying groceries, and on a momentary-contact gate operator it is far too long and makes the gate cycle repeatedly... it wants about two. Sixty seconds of talk time cuts residents off mid-sentence. Both are one programming number each and both are worth adjusting before you leave.",commonServicingIssues:["Panel is alive but will not dial out after a phone-service change -> POTS-era dialer on a VoIP or fiber line -> meter tip to ring for ~48-52 VDC on-hook and prove dial tone with a butt set; if the line is dead the fix is with the carrier, not the panel.","Cannot get into programming on a takeover -> access code changed and undocumented -> try the factory 000000 first; if it fails, print or photograph the directory before any reset, because a reset takes the directory with it.","Gate opens then keeps cycling -> relay activation time left at the 10-second factory default while the operator takes a momentary contact -> set that relay to about 2 seconds.","Residents complain the call cuts them off -> call length limit at the 60-second factory default -> raise it at programming number 07.","Keypad stops accepting codes for a few minutes -> strikes-and-out lockout after 2 incorrect entries disables that function for 3 minutes by design -> confirm before chasing a hardware fault; raise the threshold if the site warrants it.","Alarm sensor starts falsing after a relay was repurposed -> relay 3 or 4 was configured as SHUNT and was bypassing that sensor on every valid entry -> restore the shunt configuration or shunt the sensor another way.","Programming a card or code on one unit does not work at another entrance -> Multi-Link chain out of sync, or the unit was addressed to a single location -> address the whole chain when programming shared credentials.","Customer expects parts on a next-day basis -> line retired around 2013-14 and no longer manufactured -> quote replacement rather than promising a repair you cannot source.","Cabinet found unlocked or programming tampered with -> Sentex cabinets are commonly keyed from the factory -> rekey or replace the cabinet cylinder."],sources:["Chamberlain Group / myQ Resources, \"The Evolution of Sentex Telephone Entry Systems\" (resources.myq.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published. Manufacturer-side attribution for the 1997 Chamberlain acquisition and the 2013-14 retirement.","https://www.affordableopeners.com/manuals/telephone_entry/sentex/man.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1786508/Chamberlain-Sentex-Infinity-Series.html","https://www.gatesnfences.com/Access-Control-TelePhone-Entry-Intercom-Systems-Keypads/Sentex-Infinity-L-Telephone-Entry-Systems.html","https://www.gateoperator.net/Sentex/"],relatedGuides:["intercom-triage","dks-takeover-reset","dks-1834-telephone-entry","gate-operators","fail-safe-fail-secure"],photos:[{diagram:"sentex-infinity-relays",caption:"The four relays, the digits that fire them, and the defaults that generate the callbacks"}]},{id:"ict-prt-rdm2-din",name:"ICT Protege PRT-RDM2-DIN-485 DIN Rail 2-Door Reader Expander",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The module that adds doors to a Protege system once the controller's own two reader ports are spoken for. It clips onto the same DIN rail beside the PRT-CTRL-DIN, joins the same encrypted RS-485 module network, and brings two more doors with it... two reader ports that each take an entry and an exit head, two Form C lock relays, eight monitored inputs and six open-collector outputs. In the field cabinet photographed for this catalog it is the module on the left, addressed 5, feeding the upper and lower garage readers while the controller beside it handles the front door. The feature that separates it from a dumb expander is that it keeps granting access when the controller drops off the network... it holds its own user data and makes its own decisions offline.",specs:["2 reader ports, each independently configurable for Wiegand or RS-485... up to 4 readers, entry and exit on two doors","A port is Wiegand OR RS-485, never both at once. Mixing reader technologies means putting them on separate ports","RS-485 reader ports support OSDP; Wiegand supports up to 1024 configurable bits","2 Form C lock relays, 7A N.O./N.C. at 30 VAC/DC resistive/inductive... a 1N4007 diode is supplied and mounts at the strike terminals, not at the panel","6 open-collector outputs, 50mA max (reader LED, beeper and control lines)","8 high-security monitored inputs, input speed programmable from 10ms to 1 hour, plus 16 trouble inputs","Offline access modes: All Users, First 10 Users plus a 150-card cache, or No Users... set this deliberately, it is the module's behaviour when the controller is gone","DC input 11-14 VDC; operating current 80mA typical; total combined current 1.56A max","Reader 1 and 2 pass-through 10.45-13.85 VDC sharing 0.7A typical, electronic shutdown at 1.1A","Low-voltage cutout 8.7 VDC, restore 10.5 VDC","Module network: encrypted RS-485, up to 250 modules on a Protege system over 900m (3000ft), further with a repeater","Reader terminal legend, left to right: C, BZ, L2, L1, D1/NB, D0/NA, V-, V+. RS-485 A lands on D0/NA and RS-485 B on D1/NB","Factory address is 254, deliberately above the controller's supported range... a new module never comes online until you address it","157 x 90 x 60mm, 300g, standard DIN rail, indoor use only. UL 294 / UL 681, ASIAL Class 5 as part of a compliant Protege GX/WX system"],proAdvice:"Read the front panel before you open the software, because this module tells you more than most. The Reader 1 and Reader 2 indicators do not just say \"data\"... a short red flash under 250 milliseconds means data arrived but the format was wrong, and a long red flash over a second means it read cleanly and the format was right. That single distinction splits your entire troubleshooting tree in two. Long flash and no grant is a software problem, the card not enrolled or the format not configured. Short flash is a format or technology mismatch at the port. No flash at all is wiring, power, or a port set to the wrong mode. Most techs pull the reader apart before checking a light that already answered the question.\n\nThe reader itself talks too. Two beeps is access granted, one long beep is denied, and four beeps means access granted while the module is running offline from the controller. That last one is the tell nobody teaches: if a user mentions the reader started beeping differently, the module has lost the controller and has been making its own decisions, possibly for days. Chase the module network before the customer notices the event log has a hole in it.\n\nAddress configuration is the number-one dead-module call and it is not a fault. Protege modules ship at address 254, which sits above the range any controller supports, so a brand-new expander out of the box does nothing at all until you assign it a valid unused address using the serial number off its sticker. There is no DIP switch to look for. The errors you will actually meet are Address In Use, meaning another module already holds it, an address above the controller's configured range, and a controller with secure registration enabled refusing every registration... all three read exactly like dead hardware.\n\nWhile you are in the cabinet, write the address on the module's face. The field unit in the photo has 1, 4 and 5 stuck on the controller, the supply and this expander, and that beats tracing a serial number with a torch every time somebody adds a door. Label the reader runs at the same time... the blue-tape \"UPPER GARAGE\" and \"LOWER GARAGE\" tags on that cabinet are worth more on the next service call than any drawing that ended up in a filing cabinet.\n\nDecide the offline mode on purpose rather than accepting a default. All Users means the doors keep working normally through a controller outage, which is what a garage or a lobby usually wants. First 10 Users plus a 150-card cache is the compromise for a site where the whole population should not get through on a dead network. No Users means the doors go secure the moment comms drop, which is right for a server room and wrong for a fire-egress path where somebody has to get back in. Whatever you choose, write it into the sequence of operations, because it is the behaviour nobody sees until the day it matters.\n\nOn current, treat it exactly like the controller: it switches locks, it does not feed them. Total combined draw is 1.56A and the reader pass-through shares 0.7A across both ports with electronic shutdown at 1.1A, so a pair of readers is fine and a maglock is not. Run locks off the PRT-PSU-DIN through these relays, fit the supplied 1N4007 across the lock at the lock, and stay under 7A per relay.",commonServicingIssues:["Brand-new module never comes online -> it ships at factory address 254, above any controller's supported range, by design -> assign a valid unused address in the Protege interface using the serial number from the module's sticker.","Module refuses to register with a correct address -> the address is already in use by another module, or the controller has secure registration enabled -> run view network status to list registered devices, pick a free address, and clear the secure setting long enough to register.","Reader indicator gives a SHORT red flash and no grant -> data is arriving but in the wrong format... a Wiegand bit-length or card-technology mismatch, or a port set to the wrong mode -> match the port mode and card format to the head actually installed.","Reader indicator gives a LONG red flash and access is still denied -> the read and format are both correct, so the fault is in software -> check the card is enrolled, the facility code matches, and the user has access to that door.","Reader indicator does not flash at all -> no data reaching the port... V+/V- missing at the head, data pair reversed or landed on the wrong terminals, or the port set to Wiegand for an RS-485 reader -> meter the head under load, then confirm RS-485 A on D0/NA and B on D1/NB.","Users report the reader beeping four times on a grant -> the module is operating offline from the controller and granting from its own user data -> chase the RS-485 module network, termination and the controller before the event log gaps become a problem.","Doors all go secure, or all stay open, during a network outage -> the offline access mode is set to No Users or All Users and nobody chose it deliberately -> set the mode to match the opening and record it in the sequence of operations.","Readers brown out or reset when both are busy -> the two reader ports share 0.7A of pass-through with electronic shutdown at 1.1A -> feed higher-draw heads from the supply directly rather than through the module.","Lock relay contacts welded or a lock output that stopped switching -> an inductive DC lock switched with no suppression, or a load over 7A -> install the supplied 1N4007 at the strike terminals, not at the panel, and confirm the load rating."],sources:["https://www.ict.co/media/uvtddsut/prt-rdm2-din_protege_din_rail_2_reader_expander_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/products/Ict-Prt-Rdm2-Din-485-12497500.html","https://ict.co/products-solutions/our-hardware/protege-din-rail/protege-din-rail-2-reader-expander/"],relatedGuides:["wiring-and-testing-a-reader-kt400","keypad-reader-servicing","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","sequence-of-operations","fail-safe-fail-secure","elevator-access-control"],verified:!0,verifyNote:"Identified from the field cabinet photo. Enlarged, the module face reads PRT-RDM2-DIN-485, and its terminal legend reads C BZ L2 L1 D1/NB D0/NA V- V+, which matches the PRT-RDM2-DIN-485 installation manual exactly, as do the Reader 1/Relay 1 and Reader 2/Relay 2 front-panel groupings and the 8-zone indicator block. Specs are from that manual. The single digit after RDM is the softest character at photo resolution, so read the identification sticker before ordering a replacement... ICT builds this expander only as the 2-door RDM2, and the half-DIN PRT-HRDM-DIN is the other body style to rule out.",photo:"photo-ict-protege-cabinet",photos:[{asset:"photo-ict-protege-cabinet",caption:"The expander as installed, on the left of the rail with a hand-written 5 for its module address. Reader 1 and Reader 2 feed the upper and lower garage heads on the blue-tape labels; the PRT-CTRL-DIN sits to its right at address 1, the PRT-PSU-DIN and battery below at 4."},{diagram:"ict-prt-rdm2-indicators",caption:"Reading the panel: what a short flash, a long flash and four beeps each mean"}]},{id:"keri-ms-series-readers",name:"Keri MS-Series Proximity Readers (MS-3000 / MS-4000 / MS-5000 / MS-7000)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"The reader half of a legacy Keri PXL-500P site, and the part the PXL entry only mentions in passing. All four are 125 kHz reading Keri's own proprietary K-format... not Wiegand, not generic prox, and that one fact decides every credential conversation you will have on the site. Four bodies on one platform: the MS-3000 MicroStar mullion strip for a door stile, the MS-5000 MiniStar on a single-gang box, the MS-7000 SuperStar medium-range head sold for parking entrances, and the MS-4000 vandal-resistant wall unit. Every one of them lands on the controller's TB5 (the A reader) or TB6 (the B reader), and the LED and beeper you see at the door are driven by the CONTROLLER rather than the reader... which is what lets you split a fault in half at the door with no laptop and no meter.",specs:["Four models on one platform: MS-3000X MicroStar (mullion), MS-5000BX MiniStar (wall / single-gang), MS-7000BX SuperStar (medium range), MS-4000X (wall, vandal resistant)","Read range: MS-3000 up to 4 in (10 cm), MS-5000 up to 6 in (15.25 cm), MS-7000 up to 14 in (35.5 cm)... every one of those figures is quoted with a KC-10X CARD held parallel to the reader face in a clean RF environment. A PKT-10X fob, or a credential presented at an angle, reads materially less","Input power: MS-3000 5-14 VDC @ 50 mA typical, MS-5000 5-14 VDC @ 100 mA, MS-7000 11-14 VDC @ 200 mA. The MS-7000's window is the narrow one... it needs 11 V minimum where the others tolerate 5 V, and it draws four times the MS-3000","Operating frequency: 125 kHz excitation, 62.5 kHz data return","LED is a standard tri-colour (red, amber, green, off) plus a standard beeper... driven over the reader cable by the controller. The MS-4000 has NEITHER, which is why it takes a thinner cable","Cable: 6-conductor, 24 AWG, stranded, shielded (Belden 9536 or heavier). The MS-4000 only needs 4-conductor (Belden 9534) because there is no LED or beeper to feed","Maximum run: MS-3000 / MS-4000 / MS-5000 reach 500 ft (150 m) on 24 AWG. The MS-7000 reaches only 250 ft (75 m) on 24 AWG, and needs 20 AWG to make 500 ft","Credentials: Keri KC-10X and MT-10X cards and the PKT-10X key tag, plus any Indala ASP card or tag when the reader is on a PXL-500 controller","Controller side: the A reader lands on TB5, the B reader on TB6, both 7-pin. Keri pinout is 1 blue antenna, 2 green beeper, 3 red power, 4 black ground, 5 brown green-LED, 6 white red-LED, 7 silver shield","Dimensions: MS-3000 3.4 x 1.4 x 0.38 in (2.1 oz), MS-5000 4.3 x 3.0 x 0.38 in (3.2 oz), MS-7000 8.5 x 6 x 0.83 in (1.3 lb)","IP67, -40 to 149 F (-40 to 65 C), 0-90% humidity non-condensing","Certifications CE, FCC, CSA C22.2, UL294; lifetime warranty against failure under normal use, physical abuse excluded","Keri specifies a LINEAR power supply for these readers. A switcher is what most panels actually have, and it is a noise source on a 125 kHz receiver","Also usable on NXT-2D/-4D and MSC-2D/4D controllers through an interface module, which is how a site part-migrates without replacing every head"],proAdvice:"Identify the reader before you promise anybody anything, and do it at the pigtail rather than off the case, because the cases were re-used across ranges. A BLUE wire on pin 1 is the reader antenna, and blue on pin 1 means Keri MS on a PXL-500P. No blue, with green on pin 1 and white on pin 7, means a Wiegand head on a PXL-500W. That single wire decides which credentials the site can buy.\n\nWatch the colour collision while you are in there, because it has caught better techs than the pigtail deserves. Green and white appear in BOTH pinouts and mean completely different things: on a Keri MS reader green is the beeper (pin 2) and white is the red LED (pin 6), while on a Wiegand reader green is Data0 (pin 1) and white is Data1 (pin 7). Same colours, different jobs, different pins. Match the wire's PURPOSE to the pin, never the colour to the pin... and note that Keri themselves warn the Wiegand colours are only an industry convention that plenty of manufacturers ignore.\n\nThe MS-7000 is the one that bites, and it bites in a way that looks like anything but a power problem. It is the head that gets fitted to parking entrances and vehicle gates because of the 14-inch range, which means it is also the head at the end of the longest cable run on the property. It needs 11 VDC minimum at the reader and draws 200 mA getting there, and 24 AWG only carries it 250 feet. Undersize the cable or lengthen the run and the voltage at the head sags... at which point the reader does not die, it just gets DEAF. Range collapses from fourteen inches to two or three, and the symptom that reaches you is 'it works for some fobs and not others', because you have just quietly raised the bar to the strongest credentials in the building. Meter at the reader, under load, with the fob presented... not at the power supply.\n\nRemember what the published range figure actually is. It is a card, held flat and parallel, in clean RF. Residents present a fob on a keyring, at an angle, next to a car door. Treat the datasheet number as a ceiling nobody in the building will ever hit, and design for roughly half of it.\n\nThe LED and beeper are your free diagnostic, and it is worth knowing they belong to the controller. Steady amber means idle and waiting. A green LED and one long beep is access granted. A flashing red LED and one short beep is access DENIED... which means the read succeeded and the controller made a decision. That distinction is the whole game: no response at all is a reader, credential or range problem, while red-and-a-beep proves the reader, the cable, the power and the network are all fine and sends you to the database instead. On an MS-4000 you do not get this, because it has no LED and no beeper... on that one, go straight to the event log.\n\nCredentials are proprietary and that is a supply risk you should raise before it is an emergency. K-format is Keri's own 125 kHz encoding, so generic prox stock will not read no matter how good it is. Order genuine KC-10X / MT-10X cards or PKT-10X tags, matched to the site's facility code and card range, and keep a small stock on a large MDU rather than discovering the lead time on a Friday.",commonServicingIssues:["Some fobs work at a door and others do not -> almost never a broken reader; it is read range that has quietly shrunk, or a credential batch the controller does not hold -> use the LED/beep split at the door first, then meter the reader under load.","Range gradually got worse over years on an MS-7000 -> supply voltage at the head sagging below 11 VDC on a long or undersized run, or a failing battery-backed supply -> measure at the reader, not the panel; re-pull to 20 AWG if the run is over 250 ft.","Reader mounted on metal or right beside a second reader reads badly -> 125 kHz heads detune against metal and interfere with each other -> space them, or use the spacer the model expects; the MS-3000 is the one designed for a metal mullion.","Short read range across a whole controller -> the earth ground at TB2 pin 3 is missing or poor, or the cable shield was never terminated -> Keri wants the shield landed at the controller end only, on a corner screw, the green ground lug J6, TB10 pin 3, or the enclosure lug.","Reader beeps continuously at power-up -> the door contact input on TB4 pins 1-2 is open, not a reader fault -> jumper those pins if no door switch is fitted, or fix the contact.","Every card at one controller stops working after a power event -> controller RAM corrupted, or the 3 V lithium coin cell (CR2325 / BR2325) is flat -> measure the cell at about 3 V, replace, RAM reset via JP10 with S1 held, then a Total Update from Doors.","New residents' fobs fail at one door but work everywhere else -> that controller never received the cardholder download; it holds its own database and grants locally -> check it is online, then run a network update, and an Auto-Configuration if the panel was reset or re-addressed.","A Wiegand head on a PXL-500W does nothing at all -> JP5 supply voltage jumper is on the wrong pins; it defaults to 5 VDC on pins 2-3 and needs pins 1-2 for a 12 V reader -> set it with the power off; the warning LED lights when 12 V is being supplied.","Reader works on the bench, misbehaves on the door -> EMI. Keri asks for at least 4 ft of separation from the power supply, no reader cable run alongside power cable, and a linear supply rather than a switcher."],sources:["https://cdn.adiglobaldistribution.us/pim/Original/10808/KF-MS5000BX_Product-Data-Sheet.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500_Installation_Guide.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500-Installation.pdf","https://kerisys.com/products/ms-7000x-microstar-proximity-reader/","https://www.keri-kb.com/Content/DoorsNET/Troubleshooting/Troubleshooting_PXL.htm","https://help.kerisys.com/portal/en/kb/articles/pxl-500-installation-guide"],relatedGuides:["keri-pxl-takeover","keri-pxl-reader-fault-checklist","keypad-reader-servicing","credential-technology-and-card-formats","low-voltage-wiring"],photos:[{diagram:"keri-reader-pinout",caption:"TB5/TB6 pin by pin, Keri MS against Wiegand, and the two colours that mean different things in each."},{diagram:"keri-fob-fault-tree",caption:"The LED-and-beep split: what the door tells you before you open a laptop."}]}]},770,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,n,i,s){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(i,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(i,"TOOLS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return r}});const r=[{id:"plug-follower",name:"Plug Follower",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A plug follower is a short, precision-ground rod the exact diameter of a lock plug that you push in behind the plug as you slide it out the front of the shell. Its whole job is to keep the top pins (driver pins) and their springs trapped up in the shell so they don't rain out on your bench. No follower means you're chasing tiny springs across the shop floor, so it earns a permanent spot in the rekey kit right next to your pinning tweezers and plug spinner.",useCases:["Rekeying a residential deadbolt or knob (Kwikset, Schlage, Weiser) \u2014 pop the retainer, push the plug out on the follower, dump the bottom pins, re-pin to the new key","Master keying and re-pinning where you're in and out of the same cylinder several times and can't afford to lose top pins or springs","Servicing rim, mortise, and padlock cylinders \u2014 anything where the plug rides out the front of the shell","Cleaning or lubing a gummed-up cylinder, then reassembling without disturbing the top chambers","Working commercial small-format IC and standard cylinders where the top pin stack has to stay put"],howToUse:"The move is simple but the feel matters. With the plug already unlocked (correct key in, retainer clip or cam off), you hold the follower flush and square against the rear face of the plug and push the plug forward out the front while the follower slides in to take its place. The follower and plug faces have to stay kissing the whole time \u2014 the instant a gap opens at the shear line, the first driver pin drops into it and jams everything, or springs launch. Keep steady axial pressure, don't cock it sideways, and let the plug walk the follower in like one continuous rod.\n\nDiameter is everything. The follower has to match the plug bore snugly \u2014 most run about .395 to .550 inches depending on the lock. A follower a hair too small lets a top pin sneak past the seam; too big won't enter the shell. For the bread-and-butter residential stuff a .495 universal covers Kwikset, Schlage, and Weiser \u2014 they all share that .495 plug diameter even though the keyways differ. Then you step up for the fat ones \u2014 roughly .500 for Weslock, .550 for Corbin \u2014 and drop down to around .398 for small peanut and cabinet cylinders. That's why followers come in sets: LAB's anodized aluminum LFTSA set is .398, .495, .500, and .550.\n\nWhen you're done, reverse it: seat the re-pinned plug against the follower and push the follower back out the rear as the plug seats home, again keeping the faces tight. Rotate the plug to the correct-key position first or you'll shear-line jam on the way in. Gotcha to burn in: never let go mid-swap, and never use a follower shorter than the shell is deep \u2014 you want it to fully bridge before the plug clears.",proTips:["Buy a set, not a single. A .495 universal handles most house calls, but the day you hit a Corbin or Weslock you'll want the .550 and .500 already in the tray.","Steel/aluminum over plastic if you're doing volume. Delrin (white plastic) followers are cheap and fine for occasional home rekeys, but they wear, can mushroom on the ends, and flex; anodized aluminum or brass hold their diameter and last for years.","Color-coded, laser-engraved sets (like LAB's anodized aluminum LFTSA) save you squinting for the right size mid-job \u2014 grab by color, read the engraved diameter to confirm.","Keep the ends clean and slightly chamfered. A burr or dinged end will hang a driver pin and cause the exact jam you bought the tool to avoid.","In a pinch a wood dowel or the plug of another lock can follow, but the diameter's never right and you'll lose pins \u2014 not worth it. A single universal runs about ten bucks and a full LAB set around thirty-five; just carry the real thing.","Match to the cylinder, not the keyway. It's the plug's outside diameter that matters, so a Schlage and a Kwikset can share the same .495 follower even though the keyways differ."],sources:["https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Plug_follower","https://foleybelsawlocksmithing.com/products/lab-anodized-aluminum-plug-follower-set-lftsa","https://www.labpins.com/locksmiths/p/anodized-plug-followers/","https://www.clksupplies.com/collections/plug-followers","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/plug-follower-for-kwikset-schlage-weiser-etc","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/lab-lftsa-anodized-plug-followers-aluminum","Lab 5 piece plug follower set (learnlockpicking.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.amazon.com/HPC-Follower-Rekeying-Cylinders-Letterbox/dp/B01N7WQ1X7"],photo:"photo-tool-plug-follower",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-plug-follower",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"plug-follower",caption:"How it works \u2014 the follower traps the top pins"}]},{id:"pinning-kit",name:"Pinning kit (LAB universal)",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A tray of every pin size you'd ever drop into a pin-tumbler cylinder, sorted by depth so you can rekey a lock to a new key on the spot. The LAB universal kit is the shop standard \u2014 one tray of .003-increment bottom pins, top pins (drivers), master pins, and springs that covers Kwikset, Schlage, Weiser, Yale, Arrow, Sargent, Weslock, Corbin, Russwin and most everything else with pin tumblers. \"Universal\" means one set of standardized LAB pin sizes fits all those brands, versus a system kit that carries only one manufacturer's exact factory sizes.",useCases:["Rekeying a house or business \u2014 customer changes tenants, loses a key, moves in \u2014 you repin every cylinder to one new key or a master system","Setting up master keying \u2014 dropping master pins so a change key and a master key both run the same lock","Compensating for a worn cylinder \u2014 .003 steps let you fudge a pin up or down a size when the plug's sloppy and a factory depth won't read clean","Pinning off-brand or import locks that don't hold true to major-brand depth specs","Combinating fresh cylinders and knobs from parts, or fixing a lock somebody butchered with the wrong pins","Rebuilding a lock after you've picked or drilled it and need it working on a customer key"],howToUse:"You work off the tray, not off a chart in your head. The LAB Color Passport tray prints each pin's size in the same color as the pin itself, under a clear dual-view lid so you can read the number from above or from the side with the tray tipped up. On the full LPK003 kit the bottom pins run .156 to .360; top pins (the drivers that sit under the springs) run .010 to .200; springs live in their own pocket. You decode the new key with a caliper or a code \u2014 each cut depth tells you which bottom pin goes in that chamber \u2014 then pull pins with tweezers and drop them in, tallest cut gets the shortest bottom pin and so on. Top the stack with a driver, then a spring, and cap or reload the follower.\n\nThe feel is in the follower and the plug. You clear the old pins by pushing the plug out with a follower the exact diameter of the plug \u2014 too thin and a top pin drops behind it and jams you, too fat and it won't enter. Keep constant forward pressure so no spring ever escapes into the housing. Load bottoms into the plug first, spin your key in to check every pin sits flush at the shear line \u2014 run a fingernail or a flat blade across the plug, you should feel no pin standing proud and none sunk. Then reintroduce the plug under the follower so the tops and springs seat as you push the follower out the back. Roll it slow.\n\nGotchas: .003 steps put a lot of close sizes side by side, so it's easy to grab the size one line over \u2014 that's why the color coding matters, verify the number, don't just eyeball the length. Master pins go between the bottom pin and the driver, and the total stack per chamber has to equal the same overall height or the plug binds \u2014 add a master, you shorten the driver or the bottom to compensate. Don't mix a .005 and a .003 kit's pins in one lock, the depths won't agree. And a pin on the floor is a pin gone \u2014 work over a tray or a towel, magnetize your tweezers.",proTips:["Buy the .003 increment kit, not the .005 \u2014 the tighter steps let you dial in worn cylinders and pin oddball import locks that .005 can't touch. .005 is fine only if you're strictly doing clean major-brand residential and want fewer sizes to keep straight.","The LAB LPK003 Classic Pro is the full metal-tray bench kit \u2014 124 pin sizes, ~72 pins per pocket, ~115 springs \u2014 and streets around $240 to $280. If you're pinning out of a service truck, the Mini (LMK003) is the same universal .003 pins in a smaller polyethylene case that rides in a bag.","Check what your kit ships with \u2014 the pin trays are pins, springs, and masters only. Order a good follower set and fine tweezers separately so you're not caught at the first job without them.","Universal covers most jobs, but keep a brand system kit \u2014 LAB's LSK2N1 2-in-1 Schlage/Kwikset is the common one \u2014 for factory-exact work and master systems where you want the manufacturer's real depths. Universal pins are close, factory pins are dead-on.","Restock from LAB refill vials by size the moment a pocket runs low \u2014 running out of one common middle size mid-job will stop you cold, and those middles go fastest.","When you set masters, sketch the pinning chart on paper before you touch a pin. Figuring master combinations in your head over an open plug is how springs end up on the floor.","Keep the Seal-Tight lid latched when the tray travels \u2014 a tipped kit that cross-mixes pockets turns a $250 kit into an afternoon of re-sorting."],sources:["https://zensupply.com/lab-lpk003-professional-cylinder-pinning-kit-metal-color-passport-003-depths/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/lab-lpk003-003-classic-pro-universal-rekeying-pin-kit","https://www.lockpicks.com/lab-classic-pro-universal-pin-kit-003.html","https://www.lockpickersmall.com/lpk003-universal-pin-kit/","https://www.labpins.com/locksmiths/p/mini-universal-rekeying-kits/","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/lab-super-compact-mini-universal-rekeying-kit-003-lmk003-2448","https://www.labpins.com/locksmiths/p/kwikset-schlage-rekeying-kit/","https://easykeys.com/products/1743_LAB_Schlage_Kwikset_Pin_Kit_LSK2N1"],photo:"photo-tool-pinning-kit",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-pinning-kit",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"pin-tumbler",caption:"How it works \u2014 the pin stack you build to the shear line"}]},{id:"plug-spinner",name:"Plug Spinner",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A plug spinner is a recovery tool for when you've picked a cylinder but rotated the plug the wrong way. It stores torque \u2014 almost always in a coiled spring \u2014 and dumps it all at once to snap the plug around fast enough that the driver pins can't drop back across the shear line before it's parked in the open position. It earns its spot because it saves you from re-picking a lock you already beat, and on a service call that's minutes you don't have to burn.",useCases:["You picked a plug open but it turned the wrong direction (common on locks that want to be picked to the back) and you need it around to the unlock position without re-picking","Fast field entry work where you're beating a bunch of cylinders and don't want to fight direction on every one \u2014 pick it however it picks, then spin it right","Disc-tumbler and wafer cabinet/desk locks that flip past their wafers cleanly","Auto and padlock cylinders where re-picking is a pain and you'd rather flip once","As a training/diagnostic tool \u2014 proves your pick got a clean shear line even if you went the wrong way"],howToUse:"Two ways they get wound and released, but almost all of them work the same underneath: a coil spring snaps the plug around. The wind-and-release flipper style \u2014 A1, Peterson, HPC Flip-It \u2014 is what most guys carry. You pick the plug open first. The moment it's picked, you back off almost all your tension \u2014 leave the barest whisper so the plug doesn't reset, but if you're still cranking torque against it the spinner fights you and either won't move the plug or bends a blade. That's the number one rookie mistake: trying to spin a plug you're still leaning on with the tension wrench. Slide the spinner blade into the keyway snug, set it for the direction you need (these are bi-directional \u2014 you pick which way it dumps), preload the spring by twisting the knurled knob the opposite way you want the plug to travel, and release. It snaps the plug through the pins in a blink. On the HPC Flip-It (FIT-2) you preload with the knurled knobs and trigger it with a thumb push; it's got a sure-stop so you can't over-wind it in either direction and a 2-pin quick lock/release. The Peterson runs ambidextrous \u2014 the trigger keeps your fingers off the spinning tip, and its EZ-change tips swap between a 0.060\" automotive tip and a smaller residential/commercial one.\n\nThe push-button style \u2014 Rytan RY57 and the compact RY5798 \u2014 is the same spring-driven flip, it just drops the wind-and-lock-and-release dance. You seat the blade, pick your direction, and press the button; it flips the plug for you. No collar to preload, so it's simpler and quick to learn. The bulkier German Wendt units are still spring-tension spinners too \u2014 heavier and pricier, but fast and tough. None of these \"apply steady torque\" like a wrench; they all snap the plug with stored spring force. The difference is how you charge them, not what they do to the plug.\n\nGotcha to keep in mind: a spinner beats standard spring-biased drivers on speed alone. It does NOT reliably beat heavy-spring cylinders, high-security stuff, or a lock loaded with serrated/spool pins that grab \u2014 those can catch the pins mid-flip and you'll be back to picking. And if the plug's still under real tension, the spinner just can't overcome it. Back off, then spin.",proTips:["Carry at least two. Springs break, bend, or just have a bad day mid-call \u2014 every trainer worth listening to says buy a spare. A snapped spring with no backup means you're re-picking everything.","Peterson is the easy call for a working kit \u2014 slim, ambidextrous trigger, and it ships with both tips (auto and residential/commercial) plus a spare spring. Buy that combo and your backup spring is already in the box. Replacement springs sell separately too.","The A1 is the veteran's gold standard \u2014 slim, quick, tough enough that guys report 15-plus years out of one \u2014 but the original's out of production. What you'll actually buy now is a reissue/replica (BAY-CO and 'A1-style' units). HPC's Flip-It (FIT-2) is the other proven workhorse, and its sure-stop keeps you from over-winding.","Almost no tension is the whole game. If it won't spin, you're gripping the plug too hard \u2014 ease off before you blame the tool.","Match the blade to the keyway. A sloppy fit wastes torque and is how you bend a cheap blade. Blades bend on any spinner if you over-crank or fight the wrong direction \u2014 that's why the Rytan units sell replaceable tips (the compact RY5798's tip kit is the RY5704).","It's a recovery tool, not a magic key \u2014 don't reach for it on high-security or spool-loaded cylinders expecting a clean flip. Learn to pick both directions so you're not spinner-dependent.","If you're new, a push-button Rytan (RY57 or the pocket-size RY5798) skips the winding step and the loose-collar fiddling \u2014 worth it if you hate messing with preload."],sources:["https://www.thinkpeterson.com/plug-spinner-with-both-tips-and-spare-spring/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/hpc-fit-2-flip-it-plug-spinner","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/ry57-plug-spinner-original-construction","https://foleybelsawlocksmithing.com/products/rytan-compact-plug-spinner-ry5798","https://foleybelsawlocksmithing.com/products/rytan-compact-plug-spinner-ry5798-replacement-tip-kit","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/a1-style-plug-spinner-its-back-baby","https://www.mrlocksmithtraining.com/essential-mr-locksmith-tools-plug-spinners-flippers-open-locks-picked-wrong-way/","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/how-to-use-plug-spinners-tips-and-tricks-for-locksmith-success","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Plug_spinner","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/best-plug-spinners-for-locksmiths-in-2025"],photo:"photo-tool-plug-spinner",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-plug-spinner",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"plug-spinner",caption:"How it works \u2014 snapping the plug open before the pins drop"}]},{id:"pinning-tweezers",name:"Pinning Tweezers & Tray",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A fine-tipped tweezer for picking up and setting the little bottom and top pins into a plug, paired with a tray or work mat that keeps your loose pins, springs, and small parts from rolling off the bench and into the void. Nothing exotic \u2014 but rekey a lock without them and you'll spend more time chasing dropped .003 pins across the floor than actually cutting keys. The tweezer is the hand tool that does the placing; the tray is the staging area that keeps the job organized.",useCases:["Rekeying pin-tumbler cylinders \u2014 Kwikset, Schlage, and the like \u2014 dropping bottom pins into the plug chambers by cut","Loading top (driver) pins and springs back into the shell after a rekey","Servicing SFIC and small-format cores where the chambers are tight and you can't get fingers in","Master-keying jobs where you're staging a dozen pin stacks at once and cannot afford to mix them up","Working padlock cores \u2014 American, Master, Abus \u2014 where a specialized tip grips those odd little pins","Recovering a pin you dropped into the plug or shell without dumping the whole thing","Keeping your bench sane on any job with a lot of small springs and pins in play"],howToUse:"Two tweezer styles you'll meet. A standard tweezer you squeeze to close \u2014 fine for quick pin drops but your fingers fatigue on a long master-key job. A reverse-action (cross-lock) tweezer is closed at rest and you squeeze to OPEN \u2014 grab the pin, let go, and it holds on its own while you line up the chamber. That self-holding trick is worth a lot when you're placing top pins and springs and want a free hand. For the tips, look for a formed or curved end \u2014 LAB's formed-end tweezers (part LZT001) have curved ends that cradle the pin instead of pinching it flat, so it doesn't ping out of your grip.\n\nWorking bottom pins: I set the plug on the mat, key inserted and turned so I'm reading the cuts, and drop each bottom pin into its chamber by feel. Then I sight straight down the length of the plug \u2014 every bottom pin should sit flush with the curved surface with the correct key in, nothing high, nothing low. That flush read is your check that you pulled the right pin size. For top pins and springs going back into the shell, the reverse-action tweezer earns its keep \u2014 grip the spring, seat it, then the driver on top, working chamber by chamber with a follower holding the whole stack down.\n\nThe tray is where the discipline lives. Get one with separate wells \u2014 a decoding row (7 spaces covers standard and SFIC), a staging area, and finger-friendly bumps you can pluck a pin off of without tweezers. A raised lip so nothing rolls off. Some guys run a magnetic tray, and it does keep steel pins put \u2014 but be aware magnetized pins cling to each other and to the tweezer tip, which is its own small annoyance. A PVC or rubber pinning mat with molded wells is my default; a magnetic tray is a nice second surface for parts I don't want to lose.\n\nGotchas: cheap tweezers with tips that don't meet cleanly will launch a pin every time \u2014 that's the number-one frustration. Work over the mat, never over open floor or a drain. And keep your bottom pins in the tray in size order \u2014 the second they mix, you're gauging every one by eye.",proTips:["Buy the reverse-action (cross-lock) tweezer for the pin work you do most \u2014 it holds the pin closed on its own, saves your hand on long jobs, and frees a finger for the spring","Get formed/curved tips, not flat points \u2014 LAB's formed-end tweezers (part LZT001, roughly $15\u201325 depending on supplier) cradle a pin so it doesn't ping loose; a bargain-bin flat-tip pair will fight you","Own two: a fine general tweezer for bottom pins and a self-locking one for top pins/springs. They're cheap enough that there's no reason to compromise","The HPC (Hudson Lock) TPT-5 pin tumbler tweezers add a pin-pushing attachment for seating top pins, and the loading wire pulls out so it handles bottom pins too \u2014 one tool, two jobs, if you can find it in stock","Spend on the tray, not just the tweezer \u2014 a good work mat with a decoding row and a raised lip pays for itself the first time it saves you from re-sorting a mixed pin kit","Modular mats with removable mini-trays let you flatten the surface when you need it and section it off when you're master-keying \u2014 the LAB/Lockboss-style mats are the ones to look at","Skip the deep-nub cheap mats \u2014 you'll need tweezers just to pry your own pins back out; finger-friendly bumps are worth it","Magnetic trays keep steel pins put but magnetize them together and to your tip \u2014 fine as a parts catch, less fun for active pinning; a PVC mat is the better main surface","Work over the mat, always. One pin bounced down a floor drain and you've learned this lesson the expensive way"],sources:["https://www.labpins.com/locksmiths/p/formed-end-tweezers/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/lab-formed-end-tweezers","https://www.lockpicks.com/lzt001-tweezers.html","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/pin-tumbler-tweezers-with-pin-pushing-attachment","https://www.hudsonlock.com/product/pin-tumbler-tweezers-with-pin-pushing-attachment/","https://covertinstruments.com/products/pinning-tweezers","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/the-ultimate-locksmith-pinning-and-work-mat","https://www.amazon.com/Lock-Pinning-Cross-Locking-Tweezers/dp/B0BHZXB5HN"],photo:"photo-tool-pinning-tweezers",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-pinning-tweezers",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"pinning-tweezers",caption:"How it works \u2014 load each chamber during re-pinning"}]},{id:"key-gauge",name:"Key Gauge / Decoder",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A flat stainless-steel card with stepped slots cut into each edge, one row per lock brand, that reads the depth of each cut on a key and tells you the bitting number for that cut. It's the fast bridge between a key in your hand and the right bottom pin in the chamber \u2014 slide, read, pin. A LAB 6-in-1 runs you about seven bucks and lives in the tray of every rekey kit, so there's no reason not to have one.",useCases:["Decoding a customer's key to a bitting code so you can pin a new lock to match it without cutting a fresh key first","Rekeying a cylinder to an existing key \u2014 read each cut, drop the matching bottom pin, done","Reading a worn or off-brand key well enough to figure the closest factory depth before you originate a new one","Field decoding when you don't want to haul out calipers and a depth-and-space chart","Sanity-checking a pinning you just did \u2014 run the key back through the gauge to confirm each cut reads the depth you pinned to","Quick brand ID and depth range check on an unfamiliar key (Kwikset 1-6, Schlage 0-9, etc.)"],howToUse:"The gauge is nothing but a go/no-go depth reader with numbers stamped next to each step. Pick the row marked for your brand \u2014 Kwikset, Schlage, Weiser, Weslock, Master, whatever the card carries \u2014 and don't cross rows, because every brand has its own depth system and the numbers don't transfer.\n\nWork it one cut at a time. Seat the shoulder of the key against the reference edge, slide the first cut into the widest slot on that brand's row, and push it down the steps until the cut bottoms out and stops. The number sitting next to where it stops is your bitting for that position. Write it down, index over to the next cut, repeat across all five or six spaces. On a Kwikset you'll read a string like 3-5-2-4-1; that string is both your cut code and your pinning order. Each of those numbers points straight at a bottom pin depth \u2014 a Kwikset 1 is the shallowest cut and takes the longest bottom pin, a 6 is the deepest cut and the shortest pin. Deeper cut, shorter pin. That trips up every apprentice once, so burn it in now.\n\nThe feel is the gotcha. A worn key rides a hair shallow and can read a depth low; a key with a burr or a little brass curl on the cut reads deep because the junk stops the slide early. Rock the key gently and go by where it seats clean, not where it first catches. If a cut reads dead between two steps, you've either got wear, a two-step MACS problem, or you're on the wrong brand row \u2014 check the keyway and blank first. Grip the key square so it doesn't cant in the slot; a tilted key fouls the reading. And these are decoders, not cutters and not micrometers \u2014 they'll get you to the right pin every time, but for originating a key to code you still verify on a real depth-and-space chart or calipers.\n\nThe C-clip cutout \u2014 that little half-moon notched into the card \u2014 is there so you can pop follower and cap clips without hunting for pliers. Handy, but it's a bonus, not the job.",proTips:["Buy the LAB LKG002 6-in-1 (Schlage, Kwikset, Medeco, Yale, Master, Sargent) \u2014 US-made stainless steel, about $7, tucks in the pin-kit tray. The LKG001 5-in-1 covers Kwikset/Schlage/Weiser/Weslock/Master if that's your residential mix.","Match the depth system to the brand every time. Kwikset KW1 runs 6 depths at a .023 step; Schlage Classic runs 0-9 at a .015 step. Read a Schlage key on the Kwikset row and you'll pin it wrong and never know why the plug binds.","Deeper cut number = shorter bottom pin. Say it out loud until it's automatic \u2014 it's the single most common pinning mistake.","Clean the key first. A wire-brush pass to knock off brass burrs and grime changes a reading by a whole depth. Decode dirty and you'll chase a phantom.","Worn keys read shallow. If a customer's daily key decodes but the new pinning feels notchy, bump the questionable cut one depth and test \u2014 real-world wear lies to the gauge.","Decode the key twice and confirm it matches before you commit pins. Ten seconds of re-reading beats dumping a pinned plug back on the mat.","It's a decoder, not a code cutter. For originating to a code or anything security-rated, back it up with calipers and a Framon/HPC depth-and-space chart \u2014 the gauge gets you the pin, the chart gets you the exact cut."],sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/lab-lkg002-6-in-1-key-gauge-decoder-schlage-kwikset-medeco-yale-master-sargent","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/lab-lkg001-5-in-1-key-gauge-decoder-weslock-master-weiser-schlage-kwikset","https://www.amazon.com/LAB-Key-Gauge-Keychain-Included/dp/B0CK9NX9Q2","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/kwikset-kw1-space-depth-keys","https://lockreference.com/how-key-bitting-specifications-work/","https://www.lsamichigan.org/Tech/Kwikset_KeySpecs.pdf","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Kwikset_Classic","https://www.framon.com/pdfs/manuals/Framon-Depth-and-Space-Manual.pdf"],photo:"photo-tool-key-gauge",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-key-gauge",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"key-gauge",caption:"How it works \u2014 read each cut\u2019s depth to the code"}]},{id:"pick-set",name:"Lock pick set",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A lock pick set is a spread of thin steel picks and tension wrenches you use to open a pin-tumbler lock without its key \u2014 manipulating the pins to the shear line one at a time or all at once. It's the core of non-destructive entry, and every set is really just two families of tool: lifters (hooks, diamonds, balls) that work a single pin, and rakes (city, bogota, snake) that scrub the whole pin stack at once. It earns its spot because it opens the lock and leaves it working \u2014 no drilling, no replacement, customer keeps their hardware.",useCases:["Residential lockout \u2014 a standard KW1 or SC1 house lock where the customer's locked out and there's no damage justification for drilling","Padlocks \u2014 Master, American, and the like, where raking gets you in fast","Quick entry when you don't want to drill a cylinder you'll then have to replace and bill for","Wafer locks \u2014 cabinets, mailboxes, older desk furniture, and some auto glove boxes where a ball or diamond shines","Testing and diagnosing a lock you suspect is worn, sticky, or has security pins before you decide to pick, impression, or drill","Locksport practice and skill-keeping so your single-pin picking is sharp when a job actually needs it"],howToUse:"Tension first, always. A pick set is worthless without the wrench \u2014 pop a tension wrench (bottom of the keyway is your default, top-of-keyway for tight or paracentric ways) into the plug and give it light, steady rotational pressure in the direction the key turns. Too much tension and pins bind solid and won't set; too little and set pins drop back. Most beginners crush the wrench \u2014 think of the weight of a nickel resting on it. You're feeling for the plug to rotate a hair each time a pin sets.\n\nFor single-pin picking, reach a short hook to the back pin, push up gently, feel for the click and the slight give in the plug, then work forward pin by pin. The hook gives you the most feedback of anything in the set \u2014 it's how you actually learn what a lock is doing. Deep or offset hooks are for high pins, paracentric keyways, or when a standard hook can't clear the wards. Diamonds and balls you push and rock; half-diamond is a jack-of-all-trades on pins, full and half balls are your wafer tools.\n\nFor raking, drop in a city, bogota, or snake and scrub or rock it in and out under light tension \u2014 you're bouncing pins hoping they set at random. Rake first on a cheap lock to get it open fast; if it fights you, switch to SPP. Gotchas: security pins (spools, serrated) give false sets \u2014 the plug rotates a little then stalls, and you'll feel a springy counter-rotation. Back off tension slightly and feel each pin; the bound spool needs to be re-pushed. And keep your picks clean of burrs \u2014 a nicked tip lies to your fingers.",proTips:["Buy your tension wrenches to outlast your picks \u2014 most sets skimp on them and the wrench is where the whole thing lives. Peterson's tension is worth buying separately; get a few widths and both twist-flat and Z/prybar styles.","Gauge matters. .025\" steel is stiffer and more forgiving of a heavy hand \u2014 good starting out (the SouthOrd MPXS-14 metal-handle set is a solid, well-priced first buy with a broken-key extractor and four tension tools in the case). Go to .018\" or .015\" 'slender' (Peterson Talon 2, government steel) once you're picking tight paracentric and euro keyways where thick picks won't fit.","Government steel (Peterson) and Sparrows' 301 Cold Max stainless are the two you want for the pro kit \u2014 they flex and snap back instead of taking a set. Cheap chrome picks bend and lie to you.","Start with three tools and get good: a short hook, a bogota/triple-peak rake, and one good tension wrench. That opens most of what you'll meet. The 20-piece cases look impressive but you'll reach for the same three.","Handled picks (rubber or molded) save your fingers on a long job and give better torque control than bare metal \u2014 a Peterson GEM rubber-handle hook in .025\" government steel is the classic daily-carry.","Learn SPP even though raking is faster \u2014 security pins beat rakes, and the day a customer's high-security lock is the only thing between them and their house, scrubbing won't cut it.","Carry a broken-key extractor and a follower alongside the picks \u2014 half your 'pick this' calls turn into a snapped-key extraction or a rekey once you're in."],sources:["https://www.southord.com/products/lock-pick-set-14-piece-metal-handles-mpxs-14","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/peterson-talon-2-slender-0-015-government-steel-pick-set","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/peterson-gem-rubber-handle-gov-steel-pro-lock-pick","https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/","https://www.pickpals.com.au/blog/advanced/when-to-use-slim-picks-standard-lock-picks/","https://lockpickpros.com/blogs/news/best-professional-lock-pick-set-in-2026-a-master-picker-s-guide-to-precision-tools","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_picking"],photo:"photo-tool-pick-set",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-pick-set",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"single-pin-picking",caption:"How it works \u2014 lifting each pin to the shear line under tension"}]},{id:"tension-wrench",name:"Tension / Turning Wrenches",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A tension wrench \u2014 call it a turning tool, torsion wrench, whatever \u2014 is the little L-shaped or flat piece of spring steel you drop into the keyway to put rotational load on the plug. It's the half of picking nobody talks about but it's the half that actually opens the lock. The pick sets the pins; the wrench is what holds them set at the shear line and turns the plug home. Get the tension wrong and the best pick hand in the world opens nothing.",useCases:["Every single pin-tumbler pick job \u2014 SPP, raking, jiggling. No wrench, no open.","Tight residential and automotive keyways where there's no room for a bottom-of-keyway (BOK) wrench and you have to go top-of-keyway (TOK) to make space for the pick.","Higher-security locks with security pins (spools, serrated) where you're reading feedback through the wrench and feathering tension at each false set.","Reading a lock \u2014 figuring out which way it turns, whether it's binding, how much rotational give the plug has before you commit a pick.","Holding a set while you reset your pick hand, or backing off to drop an oversprung pin without losing the whole stack."],howToUse:"First thing: pick your position. Bottom of keyway (BOK) is where everybody starts \u2014 wrench sits low in the warding, pick works above it. It's roomy and forgiving but it tilts the plug, so the pins don't all bind evenly front to back. Top of keyway (TOK) sits the wrench up high, just barely into the mouth of the keyway \u2014 a good TOK bar like a Peterson Pry Bar is notched so it stops itself before it fouls the first pin. TOK sits closer to the true axis of the plug, so it binds the pins more evenly and it frees up the whole bottom of the keyway for your pick. Narrow keyway, deep pins, security pins \u2014 go TOK.\n\nNow the load. This is the whole game and it's all feel. Rule of thumb I give apprentices: use way less than you think. Rest a finger on the wrench, not a fist. Light tension lets pins set crisp and lets you feel the counter-rotation click when one drops. Too heavy and you'll overset pins, jam spools solid, and turn the plug before the stack is done \u2014 then you're fighting the lock. On a stubborn spool you work the give-and-take: press to bind, and as the security pin false-sets and the plug gives a tiny counter-rotation, you feather off just enough to let the driver walk over the false gate without dropping everything you've already set. A slightly springy wrench does that flex for you \u2014 a dead-stiff bar makes it clumsy.\n\nFeeler tension is the other move. Instead of a fixed press, you're actively riding the wrench, feeling the plug breathe \u2014 micro-counterrotations telling you a pin just set, a spring pushing back telling you you're overset. You're not just holding torque, you're listening through the metal.\n\nGotchas: don't bottom the wrench out against the pins or the warding \u2014 that eats your feedback and can push a pin. Make sure the tip actually seats in the plug and not just the shell, or you'll turn nothing. Watch your turn direction \u2014 commit the wrong way and you'll set to a false shear line and chase your tail. And keep the wrench out of your pick's road; if it's crowding the keyway, you're on the wrong bar or the wrong position.",proTips:["Buy a set, not one wrench. Sparrows' 18-piece Ultimate Tension set (six styles \u2014 double-ended, twisted-shaft, thinned-tip and the rest \u2014 in three widths each) or a Peterson Pry Bar in both 0.040\" and 0.050\" covers you. Wrench choice quietly limits more people than pick choice does.","Thinner is not automatically better. A thin bar flexes more and feels alive, but a wider/thicker bar transmits load with less twist and less deflection. Standard locks: heavier bar. Tight keyway or high-feedback work: thinner.","TOK is worth learning early, not late. Most people white-knuckle BOK for a year. Once TOK clicks you'll open tighter locks and read spools better because you're on the plug's true axis.","Feather don't crank. If you're muscling the wrench you've already lost \u2014 back off till the plug is barely loaded and let the pins do the talking.","The Peterson Pry Bar (0.050\" standard, 0.040\" Lite) is the benchmark TOK bar \u2014 government stainless with a serrated edge that bites and holds its position in the keyway, and it doubles as a light pry/bypass tool. Peterson also makes a no-teeth version if you want to save wear on customer keyways from repetitive picking.","Make your own in a pinch \u2014 a street sweeper bristle or a filed-down Allen key opens doors, but for daily work buy proper spring steel; it holds temper and gives consistent flex.","Keep a twisted-shaft (Z/twist) bar in the kit for locks where a flat bar won't clear \u2014 the twist puts your finger tab out of the pick's way while keeping the tip flat in the keyway."],sources:["https://www.thinkpeterson.com/peterson-pry-bar-0-050-thick/","https://www.thinkpeterson.com/peterson-pry-bar-lite-0-040-thick/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/peterson-pry-bar-lite-tok-top-of-keyway-tension-wrench","https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/products/tension-set","https://www.ukbumpkeys.com/products/ultimate-wrench-set-18pc","https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/collections/tension-wrenches","https://www.lockpickworld.com/blogs/news/applying-tension-in-the-top-of-the-keyway-tok","https://toool.uk/lockpicking/lockpicking-beyond-the-basics-tension-tricks/","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-tension-wrench-why-is-it-important-in-lockpicking-complete-guide"],photo:"photo-tool-tension-wrench",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-tension-wrench",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"single-pin-picking",caption:"How it works \u2014 tension holds the plug while you pick"}]},{id:"under-door-tool",name:"Under-Door Tool (UDT)",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A long, thin strip of flex-steel or spring-steel \u2014 most full-size UDTs run somewhere around 40 to 43 inches \u2014 with a cable or cord loop rigged at the business end and a pull ring at your end. You feed it flat under a door, hook the inside lever or push bar, and pull the ring to work the handle and pop the door. It earns its spot because most commercial and institutional doors are code-required to allow free egress from the inside \u2014 the outside can be locked all day, but the inside handle or panic bar always releases the latch, and this tool reaches around to work that inside hardware. It's a bypass, not a pick: you never touch the cylinder.",useCases:["Commercial and institutional lockouts \u2014 offices, schools, medical buildings, anywhere code forces free-egress hardware on the inside","Getting into a room where the key's lost or the cylinder's seized but the inside handle still works","Doors that free-egress by design \u2014 panic/crash bar and touch-bar exits that always release the latch from the inside","Fast non-destructive entry when the alternative is drilling a good mortise lock or wrecking a frame","Pentest and red-team physical entry work where you're paid to find the gap under the door","Double-door gaps \u2014 feeding through the meeting stile to work a push bar when there's no mullion"],howToUse:"First thing you do is read the door before you touch it. Is there a gap at the bottom? Sweep or threshold too tight and you're done before you start \u2014 no gap, no UDT. Then find the handle: which side is the hinge, how high is the lever, and does the door pull toward you or push away. That tells you which way you're pulling the ring. Code puts egress release hardware between 34 and 48 inches off the floor, so that's the band you're fishing in \u2014 start your search there.\n\nFeed the tool flat under the center of the door with the loop end going in and the ring in your hand. Push it in until you're past the door, then let the top of the rod fall to the latch side \u2014 the trick with the good ones (the Sparrows Stretcher works exactly like this) is the bottom stays centered on the door and only the top angles over toward the lever. You're fishing blind, so you work by feel: you'll feel the cable loop catch the underside of the lever, then you rock and lift until the loop is seated over the handle. Take your time here \u2014 this is 80% of the job.\n\nOnce you feel it's hooked, pull the ring steady and downward. On a lever the loop drags the handle down and the latch retracts; you push or pull the door open in the same motion before the handle springs back. On a panic bar you're pulling the loop down onto the touch bar to depress it \u2014 same idea, bigger target, usually easier. Keep tension on the ring the whole time; slack lets the loop slip off and you start over.\n\nGotchas: metal doors with a sharp bottom edge will shear a cheap cord loop \u2014 that's the number one failure. Levers that return-to-lock or have a heavy spring fight you. And a door with a decent bottom seal or a raised ADA threshold can shut you out entirely. If you can't get the loop to seat after a few tries, back off and reassess the handle height rather than sawing away at it.",proTips:["Buy one with a real cable/Kevlar loop, not just plain cord \u2014 the Sparrows Stretcher (flex steel, flat black, around $44) and the Covert Instruments Reach Around are the two to look at; the Covert runs a Kevlar line with a Dyneema SK99 backup roll specifically because metal door bottoms shear ordinary line","Carry a spare loop/cord. Sharp galvanized door bottoms will cut you off mid-job and you don't want to be done for the day over a few feet of line \u2014 the Reach Around ships with the SK99 backup for exactly this","String management is the hidden headache \u2014 get a tool with a coiled retractor or medallion so the line doesn't birds-nest in your bag. The Covert's quick-detach coiled-spring storage medallion solves this","The Sparrows Stretcher arrives under tension \u2014 handle it carefully unpacking, it wants to spring open","Practice on your own shop door until seating the loop is muscle memory. This is a feel tool; you cannot see the hook, so reps are everything","Panic bars are the easy money \u2014 big flat target, always free-egress. Levers with a lock-return spring are the fight","Know your limits: no bottom gap, tight sweep, or a raised threshold and the UDT is dead \u2014 reach for another bypass instead of forcing it","Only touch panic hardware and egress doors on jobs you're authorized for \u2014 tampering with exit devices is a life-safety and liability issue, so document the work order"],sources:["https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/products/the-stretcher-under-door-tool","https://covertinstruments.com/products/improved-under-door-tool","https://www.lockpickings.com/lock-picks/sparrows-under-door-tool/","https://us.allegion.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/allegion/information-documents/Understanding_Code_Requirements_for_Panic_Hardware_110963.pdf","https://idighardware.com/panic-hardware/"],photo:"photo-tool-under-door-tool",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-under-door-tool",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"under-door-tool",caption:"How it works \u2014 loop the inside lever and pull it down"}]},{id:"shove-knife",name:"Shove Knife / Loid",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A thin, flat spring steel blade \u2014 usually around 10 inches long and a couple centimeters wide \u2014 with a curved, notched nose ground into one end. You slide it into the gap between the door and the jamb and use the hooked nose to shove a spring latch back into the door so it can swing open. That's the whole trick. It doesn't touch the cylinder, it doesn't pick anything \u2014 it just slips the latch. Old-timers call the move \"loiding,\" from celluloid, because guys used to do the same thing with a strip of film or a credit card. It earns its slot because on a plain latch it's a five-second, no-damage open, and you'll meet those latches constantly on interior doors, cheap exterior knobs, and jobs where somebody locked themselves out and there's no deadbolt thrown.",useCases:["Lockout on a knobset or leverset with only a spring latch and no deadbolt thrown","Interior doors \u2014 bedrooms, offices, bathrooms \u2014 where the latch is exposed and there's no deadlatch at all","Cheap exterior hardware with a sloppy strike or worn-out deadlatch plunger that isn't seating","Quick non-destructive open before you commit to picking the cylinder or drilling","Reset/verification work \u2014 confirming to a customer their 'secure' latch is actually loidable because the deadlatch isn't engaging"],howToUse:"First thing you do is read the door. Which way does it swing, and where's the latch relative to the gap? On an outswing door the beveled face of the latch points at you, which is friendly \u2014 you can get straight at it. On an inswing door the bevel faces away and the gap is tighter, so a stiff plastic shim or a J-tool often works better than a metal shove knife. Don't force the metal one where a shim wants to be.\n\nFind the latch by feel. Slide the blade in at the latch line and rock it up and down till the nose catches the sloped face of the bolt \u2014 you'll feel it bite. Then it's pressure plus door movement: work the nose against the bevel and at the same time push or pull the door to take the load off the latch. That's the part people miss. If you just shove the knife hard the latch is jammed against the strike lip and won't move. Rock the door, unload the bolt, and it slips back easy. On an outswing you push the door into the frame; on an inswing you pull it toward you. Either way you're trying to walk that latch back inside its own faceplate.\n\nNow the wall you'll hit \u2014 the deadlatch. Any decent exterior latch has a little secondary plunger, the deadlocking pin, riding alongside the main bolt. When the door's shut and the strike is set right, that plunger gets held depressed against the strike plate and it mechanically locks the main latch so it can't be pushed back. That's the whole point of it \u2014 to defeat exactly this tool. If you feel the latch and it just will not budge no matter how you unload the door, stop and look: you've got an engaged deadlatch and you're wasting your time trying to muscle it. The move there is to get in ahead of the pin \u2014 pry or wedge the door and frame apart enough that the plunger drops off the strike plate into the gap or the strike hole, which frees the main latch, then loid it. If you can't beat the pin, you're done with this tool \u2014 go to picking, or a different bypass. Gotcha to keep in mind: a lot of installs have the deadlatch plunger falling into the strike hole instead of resting on the plate \u2014 that's a bad install, and it means the \"deadlatch\" isn't doing its job and the door loids wide open. You'll see it all the time.",proTips:["Buy a real one \u2014 the SouthOrd Shove-It is model SJ-50 (about $14 direct from SouthOrd, closer to $20 at resellers), roughly 10 in / 26 cm long, 2 cm wide, thin flat spring steel that flexes without taking a set. Zak Tool makes a 6-in stubby (ZT64) that lives better in a duty pouch if you're a first responder","Carry a stiff plastic shim alongside it. Metal shove knife = outswing/push; plastic shim = inswing/pull. Two doors, two tools \u2014 trying to do both with one is how you scratch paint and still don't get in","The deadlatch is the whole ballgame. Before you sweat the latch, check whether that plunger is seated on the strike plate. If it's dropping into the strike hole, the door's loidable; if it's held depressed on the plate, don't fight it \u2014 pry the gap to release the pin or switch tools","Unload the bolt with door movement \u2014 push or pull to take the strike lip pressure off before you try to slip it. Shoving harder just wedges it tighter","Watch the paint and the weatherstrip. A thin blade worked with feel opens clean; jamming it leaves a gouge on the jamb and a callback on your reputation","Round or dress any burrs on the nose so it slides on the latch bevel instead of digging in"],sources:["https://www.southord.com/products/shovit-tool-sj-50","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/shove-it-lock-bypass-tool-for-quick-entry","https://www.zaktool.com/product/new-zt64-zak-shove-knife/","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Loiding","https://www.go-rbcs.com/articles/the-deadlocking-plunger-weakness","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/55141853/whats-in-a-deadlatch","https://www.lockpicks.com/shovit-tool.html"],photo:"photo-tool-shove-knife",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-shove-knife",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"shove-knife",caption:"How it works \u2014 camming a beveled spring latch"}]},{id:"air-wedge",name:"Air Wedge & Long-Reach Tool",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A two-part non-destructive entry pair: an inflatable air wedge \u2014 a ballistic-nylon bladder with a hand pump bulb \u2014 that pries a controlled gap in a door, and a long steel rod you feed through that gap to physically work the lock. On a car that rod pokes an unlock button, an inside handle, or a lock stem; on a house or storefront it reaches a thumbturn. It earns its spot because it opens the huge class of lockouts where the mechanism is right there on the inside and you just need to touch it \u2014 no picking, no drilling, no callback for a broken cylinder.",useCases:["Auto lockouts on cars with an electric unlock button or an interior lock post you can reach \u2014 feed the rod, hit the button, done in a couple minutes","Reaching an interior lever or door handle on trucks and SUVs where the button won't cooperate","Residential lockouts where there's a thumbturn deadbolt visible through a gap \u2014 under the door, over the top, or through a mail slot with a J-hook rod","Double doors and storefront glass doors where the two leaves leave a gap right at the thumbturn \u2014 classic J-tool territory","Reach-through on security grilles and grates to flip an inside latch","Backing up a pick job \u2014 when the customer just wants in and the lock's a pain, the button's faster","First-responder / property-manager non-destructive entry where you cannot damage the door or frame"],howToUse:"On a car, start by reading the door. You want the top corner near the mirror or the rear upper corner \u2014 wherever the frame flexes and the weatherstrip is forgiving. Slip the deflated air wedge into the seam between door and frame, just past the rubber, then pump it slow. Two, three squeezes. You're making a gap the width of a finger, not prying the door off its hinges \u2014 over-inflate and you'll bend sheet metal, pop the trim, or crack a window on a frameless door. Once you've got a slot, feed the long-reach rod in past the wedge and down inside the door. A good BigEasy-style rod runs about 55 inches, so you've got the reach to hit any lock \u2014 now it's all by feel and sight: work the bent tip toward the unlock button or the lock post, and get your eyes on it \u2014 a helper or your own head at the glass line watching the tip land. Press the button or lift the post, listen for the thunk, then deflate the wedge (there's a release valve on the bulb) and pull everything out clean.\n\nThe big gotcha on cars is what's inside that door skin. The one to genuinely respect is a door-mounted side airbag \u2014 those live in the door itself, and jamming a rod blind past one can disturb the module or its wiring so it won't fire when it's supposed to. The curtain-airbag-along-the-headliner scare gets repeated a lot, and PRO-LOK and NHTSA will tell you the trigger-it-with-a-tool stories are mostly myth \u2014 but you've still got the window regulator, linkages, and wiring harness down in there to snag. So the rule stands: go slow, know roughly what's behind the panel on that model, and don't drive the rod blind. A GLO or lighted tip helps you see it at night, which is when half these calls happen.\n\nOn a residential or storefront door you're usually not inflating much \u2014 you're working a J-shaped rod through an existing gap between double doors, under the door, or through a mail slot, hooking the thumbturn and rotating it. The trick is orientation: the thumbturn only turns one way to retract, and you're working it upside-down and backward from your side of the door. Get the hook seated on the turn, take up the slack, and rotate smooth \u2014 jerk it and you'll skip off. On a tight single door with a real weatherstrip gap, the air wedge buys you the room to get the rod in at the frame edge near the deadbolt.\n\nEverything here is by feel and patience. The wedge sets the stage; the rod does the work; your eyes confirm the tip is where you think it is before you push.",proTips:["Buy a real ballistic-nylon inflatable air wedge, not a flimsy pry-in rubber pad \u2014 the good ones (Access Tools Air Wedge, the New DOT-style wedges, around $25-30) hold their shape and lift straight without folding. Don't confuse the air wedge with a plastic wedge \u2014 see below.","Know the difference: the air wedge is the inflatable bladder that makes the gap; the Steck/Milton Easy Wedge is a smooth plastic wedge that holds the gap and rides the rod through it without scuffing the door. They're two different tools that work together \u2014 the draft that calls the Easy Wedge an 'air wedge' has it wrong.","For the rod, the Steck BigEasy is the workhorse in most kits \u2014 get the GLO (glow/lighted tip) version so you can see the tip against the button at night, and against tinted glass. It's about 55 inches, reaches any door, and the BigEasy-with-Easy-Wedge kits run roughly $75-110 depending on case.","Never air-wedge the door on a frameless-window car (coupes, a lot of luxury sedans) \u2014 that gap flexes the glass, not the metal, and you'll crack it. Wedge lower and gentler, or go a different route.","The airbag you actually respect is a door-mounted one \u2014 don't feed a rod blind into a door that has a side airbag in the panel. The curtain-airbag-in-the-roof-rail trigger fear is mostly myth per PRO-LOK and NHTSA, but the wiring, regulator, and linkages down in the door are real and snaggable.","Keep a set of soft non-marring plastic wedges to hold your gap open so you're not fighting the door with one hand while you drive the rod with the other. The inflatable makes the gap; the plastic wedge holds it.","For residential, a dedicated J-tool (Dangerfield / Covert Instruments, around $26-29) beats improvising \u2014 it's shaped exactly for reaching over or between doors to a thumbturn, and it works grilles and grates too.","Protect the paint: a paint-protector sleeve or a slice of leather over the rod where it rides the door edge, and wipe the rod down. A scratch on a customer's door turns a clean open into a damage claim.","If the button won't take, stop forcing and go for the interior handle or lock post instead \u2014 different geometry, and sometimes the door just wants a different target.","These are trade tools \u2014 most reputable suppliers (Access Tools especially) only sell to verified locksmiths, tow, repo, and dealers. Keep your credentials handy when ordering."],sources:["https://www.autobodytoolmart.com/product/steck-bigeasy-glo-deluxe-lockout-kit/car-lockout-tools-kits","https://miltonindustries.com/products/classic-bigeasy-kit-with-easy-wedge-kit","https://steckmfg.com/collections/big-easy-lockout-tools","https://www.accesstoolsusa.com/Air-Wedge-p/aw.htm","https://www.accesstoolsusa.com/Long-Reach-Kits-s/1477.htm","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/collections/new-dot-air-wedges","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/accesstools-large-super-air-wedge-long-reach-car-opening-stick-tool-bundle","https://www.pro-lok.com/side-impact-airbag-myths-and-misconceptions/","https://auto.howstuffworks.com/car-driving-safety/accidents-hazardous-conditions/slim-jims-side-impact-airbags.htm","https://covertinstruments.com/products/j-tool","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/dangerfield-j-tool-reach-through-door-thumbturn-opening-tool","https://parts.ectts.com/car-door-unlock-lockout-kit-w-air-wedge-2-pc-long-reach-tool/"],autoAlso:!0,photos:[{diagram:"air-wedge-tool",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"air-wedge",caption:"How it works \u2014 pump to spread the door from the frame"}]},{id:"jiggler-keys",name:"Jiggler / Try-Out Keys",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"Thin steel blanks stamped in generic key profiles that vaguely mimic real cuts \u2014 you slip one in a wafer keyway, hold light turning pressure, and rock it until the wafers stumble into line and the plug turns. They earn a spot because on worn wafer and warded locks they're the fastest thing you own: no picking, no decoding, no reach tool. On the right lock a jiggler is open before you'd have your pick and tension wrench seated.",useCases:["Popping worn auto door locks on older GM, Ford, Chrysler and import wafer cylinders \u2014 the classic car-entry job before you reach for a Lishi or long-reach tool","Desk drawers, file cabinets and cabinet locks \u2014 most are cheap single or double-bitted wafer cylinders that jigglers open fast","Glove boxes, center consoles and toolbox locks where the key's lost and it's not worth cutting one by code","Luggage, lockers and vending-style cam locks with loose tolerances","A quick first attempt on any tired wafer lock \u2014 opens in 30 seconds or you move on to picks having lost nothing"],howToUse:"Pick a profile that roughly matches the keyway shape and insert it most of the way \u2014 not bottomed out. Now the whole game is light tension. Put the barest twist on it in the direction the key would turn, lighter than you think, then rock the key up and down like you're nudging a steering wheel while you vibrate it in and out a hair with your wrist. You're not picking wafers one at a time \u2014 you're jostling all of them at once and letting the ones that happen to sit at the shear line hang there while the rest keep bouncing. When enough of them stack, the plug rolls over on you.\n\nThe single biggest mistake is too much tension. Lean on it and you bind the wafers solid and nothing moves \u2014 the lock feels dead. Wafer locks set with a lot less tension than a pin-tumbler cylinder, so back the pressure almost to nothing and let the key do the work. Vary your depth too: pull out an eighth, push back in, because the profile only catches certain wafers at certain depths. Auto jigglers usually come double-sided, so if one edge does nothing, flip the key over and work the other bitting without pulling it fully out.\n\nWork one profile maybe 10 to 20 seconds of honest jiggling. No hint of the plug wanting to move? Switch keys. Don't marry a profile \u2014 the whole set is a numbers game and you're cycling through shapes until one matches the lock's average cuts closely enough. On a car door, remember the door lock and ignition are often keyed alike but the door is the looser, more worn of the two, so that's where you start.",proTips:["Wear is your friend \u2014 jigglers shine on beat-up locks with sloppy tolerances. A tight, near-new wafer lock or anything with security wafers will fight you all day; don't waste time, go to a Lishi or pick","They do NOT touch high-security, modern transponder-era sidebar autos, dimple, or laser/sidewinder cuts \u2014 wrong tool, stop trying","Light tension, light tension, light tension. If you learn one thing it's that heavy turning pressure is what kills a jiggler job every time \u2014 wafers set on a whisper of pressure","Buy steel, not the pot-metal junk. SouthOrd's SJG-10 auto jiggler set (full-hard spring stainless) and the standard 10-piece steel try-out sets from Lockpicks.com / LockPickWorld hold their profile; the cheap cast ones bend and round off in a keyway","For volume auto work, brand-specific try-out key sets from Aero Lock (the TO-series \u2014 cut to a maker's actual space-and-depth series like GM, Ford, Toyota, Nissan) beat generic jigglers hands down. They're real cuts, not guesses, and many read the first cut or two off the lock so you're only trying 20 to 60 keys instead of the whole book \u2014 worth the money if you open a lot of one make","Keep the set on the ring it came on and fan them out \u2014 you'll cycle profiles faster than digging loose keys out of a pouch","Feel beats sight. Close your eyes if it helps \u2014 you're reading the plug through your fingertips, that faint give when the wafers start stacking","Know the law where you work \u2014 a jiggler set is bypass gear and looks like exactly what it is. Keep it with your locksmith kit and your credentials, not loose in a glovebox"],sources:["https://www.southord.com/products/auto-jigglers-car-opening-tool-sjg-10","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/southord-auto-jigglers","https://www.lockpickworld.com/collections/lock-jigglers","https://grimworkshop.com/blogs/news/how-to-use-jiggler-key-picks-for-lock-picking","https://www.lockpicks.com/automotive/try-out-key-sets.html","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/automotive-jiggler-keys-how-do-they-help-unlock-car-doors-quickly","https://www.aerolock.com/general-motors.html","https://www.americankeysupply.com/category/aerolock-try-out-keys-90","https://www.ukbumpkeys.com/blogs/news/77566468-jigglers-the-lock-picking-tool-you-already-know-how-to-use","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wafer_tumbler_lock"],photo:"photo-tool-jiggler-keys",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-jiggler-keys",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"jiggler-keys",caption:"How it works \u2014 rake and jiggle the wafers to the shear line"}]},{id:"lishi-tool",name:"Original Lishi 2-in-1 Pick/Decoder",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A keyway-specific pick and decoder built into one flat tool \u2014 a shaped body that clips into one exact keyway (KW1, SC1, HU100, HON66, TOY43, whatever), a tension arm, and a single lifting arm that runs a graduated scale printed right on the tool. You pick the lock and read the cut depths at the same time, so you walk away with the bitting to cut a working key. Designed by Mr. Li; the genuine ones say \"Original Lishi\" and carry a scratch-off QR verification label on the back \u2014 worth every penny over the clones.",useCases:["Car lockout where you want a working key, not just entry \u2014 pick the door or ignition and decode the cuts in one go so you can cut to code","Cutting a first key for a car with no key present, reading the lock instead of pulling and disassembling it","Residential lockouts on Kwikset (KW1) and Schlage (SC1) \u2014 open the door and read the pins so you can hand the customer a fresh key","Rekey and duplicate jobs where the original key is long gone but the lock is intact","Padlocks and commercial cylinders that have a dedicated Lishi (AM5 for American, M1/M2S for Master) when you need both open and decode","Any job where speed and a clean non-destructive open matter \u2014 apartments, property managers, repos"],howToUse:"Pick the right tool for the exact keyway first \u2014 that's the whole game. A KW1 Lishi only fits a 5-pin Kwikset; an SC1 only fits a 5-pin Schlage; step up to a 6-pin and you want KW5 or SC4. HU100 is GM, HU101 is Ford, HON66 Honda, TOY43 and TOY48 Toyota, and so on. Wrong tool, wrong keyway, you get nothing. Insert it gently and seat it \u2014 the body is cut to clip into that profile so it self-aligns. If you feel ANY resistance going in, stop, pull it, and re-seat. Forcing it bends the delicate lifter tip and then the tool's junk.\n\nApply light turning tension with the tension arm and vary it \u2014 you're persuading pins up into the bible, not muscling them. Run the lifter arm to chamber one and start setting pins. When a pin sets you get a little bounce in the arm, one or two depths of movement, and a faint click. Locks rarely pick in order \u2014 expect something like 1-4-3-5-2. On spool pins (Kwikset loves spools) you'll get a false set where the plug rotates a few degrees and stops solid; back off tension, let it settle, and keep working. The tell: if a wafer or pin feels springy, it's set \u2014 leave it. If it feels solid, it still needs picking. Over-pick and trap one at the shear line and it goes solid again \u2014 dump all tension to free it and re-pick.\n\nOnce it turns, don't yank it out. Open the tension arm, go back to cut one, and gently slide the lifter until it stops on the pin \u2014 read the number off the printed scale. Do all five (or six, or however many). Those numbers are your bitting, per the tool's scale, and you cut straight to code. Keep your hands light while reading \u2014 lean on it and the scale lies to you.",proTips:["Buy Original Lishi (Mr. Li) only. The clones have sloppy tolerances, the scale drifts off, and the tips bend \u2014 on a decode job an off scale means you cut a key that won't turn. Since 2021 the genuine tools carry a scratch-off QR verification label on the back: scratch the coating, scan the code, and confirm it's real before you trust it on a decode.","Get the AG (anti-glare) version if it's offered. The polished tools throw glare off the scale in sunlight or under a flashlight and you'll misread cuts. The AG finish is matte and you read it clean in the field \u2014 same tool otherwise, no durability difference.","Clean the lock before you go in \u2014 spray it out. Debris and grit inside will chew the lifter tip and give you garbage feedback and false readings.","Master-keyed and master-pinned locks will lie to you. The Lishi can read a master cut instead of the change cut, so verify \u2014 if the key won't work, the lock's likely master-pinned and you may have to pull the plug.","Buy by the jobs you actually run. KW1, SC1, and KW5/SC4 cover the bulk of residential \u2014 5-pin and 6-pin of the two big makes. For auto, stock the makes on your routes: HU100 for GM, HU101 for Ford, HON66 Honda, TOY43/TOY48 Toyota. Don't blow the budget on 100 tools you'll never touch.","Right-hand vs left-hand keyways matter \u2014 there's a KW1-L for left-hand Kwikset and an SC1-L for left-hand Schlage. Grab the wrong hand and it won't seat.","Read the cut, then double-check by feel before you cut metal. On a fresh-key job a mis-decode by one depth is a wasted blank and a second trip."],sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.originallishi.com/lishi-tools-full-list/","https://www.classiclishi.com/how-to-choose-the-right-lishi-pick","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/original-lishi-schlage-sc1-5-pin-2-in-1-tool","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/original-lishi-kwikset-kw5-6-pin-2-in-1-tool","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/schlage-sc4-6-pin-2-in-1-tool-by-original-lishi","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-kw1-5-pin-kwikset-keyway-tool-2-in-1-pick","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-kw1-l-5-pin-kwikset-keyway-tool-2-in-1-pick-left-hand","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/original-lishi-am5-2-in-1-lock-pick-decoder","https://www.redteamtools.com/Lishi-2-in-1-AM3-AM5-AM7-American-Padlock-Keyway-Pick-and-Decoder"],photo:"photo-tool-lishi-tool",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-lishi-tool",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"lishi-tool",caption:"How it works \u2014 pick and read each cut at once"}]},{id:"pick-gun",name:"Pick Gun / Snap Gun",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A spring-loaded (or motorized) tool that drives a steel needle up against the bottom of every key pin at once. The needle slaps the pin stacks, kinetic energy jumps up the column, and for a split second the driver pins fly above the shear line \u2014 while you're feeding light tension with a wrench, so the plug catches and turns. Manual \"snap guns\" cock a spring and fire on the trigger pull; electric \"pick guns\" (the HPC Electropick and other electric strikers) buzz the needle up and down many times a second. It earns its spot because on a cheap, loosely-toleranced pin stack it beats single-pin picking on speed by a mile, and any warm body can be trained on one in a weekend \u2014 which is exactly why cops and fire crews carry them.",useCases:["Cheap residential and commercial pin-tumbler locks with sloppy tolerances \u2014 Kwikset, Defiant, no-name deadbolts \u2014 where the pins bounce easy","Speed entry when you don't care about being covert and just need it open now \u2014 the gun can pop a loose lock in a couple seconds","Emergency-service / first-responder style entries where the person on the tool isn't a full picker but needs a repeatable method","A fast first attempt before you commit to single-pin picking \u2014 if it's going to snap open, you'll know in a few pulls","Padlocks and cam locks with standard pin stacks and no security pins"],howToUse:"Pick your needle first. Most guns come with a few \u2014 a straight/flat needle for standard keyways, a bent needle for low or tight work, and a long one for reach. You want the needle riding under the bottom pins without dragging on the plug walls. If it's smacking the brass on the way up you'll leave marks and rob energy from the pins.\n\nInsert the needle along the bottom of the keyway, then get your tension wrench in the bottom of the plug with the lightest touch you can manage \u2014 feather-light. This is where people fail: they crank tension like they're single-pin picking, and the pins bind before they can jump. The gun needs the pins free to fly, so you give it a whisper of rotational pressure and let the snap do the work.\n\nOn a manual, set your needle strength with the adjusting nut (the Lockaid's got a brass knurled nut for this \u2014 start soft, dial up if pins aren't clearing). Pull the trigger to snap, and pulse the tension in rhythm with each snap \u2014 snap, ease, snap, ease. Some guns like the Sparrows Double Tap fire twice per pull, so time your tension to that. On an electric, you hold the trigger and it hammers continuously; you just modulate tension and listen. When enough drivers clear at once, the plug rolls. It either opens in the first handful of snaps or it's fighting you \u2014 don't just keep hammering blind.\n\nGotchas: the number-one mistake is too much tension. Number two is a needle set too high, hitting the plug instead of the pins. Number three is expecting it to work on good locks \u2014 security pins (spools, serrated), tight tolerances, and anti-bump designs shrug it off, and you'll waste ten minutes when a pick and tension wrench would've walked it. Know that it's not truly covert either \u2014 the needle leaves impact marks on the bottom of the pins that a forensic examiner can read (fired enough times they fan out like the spokes of a bicycle, and an examiner can count them), and hammering away puts extra wear on the lock.",proTips:["Buy the Majestic Lockaid (around $60) if you want the one that's been the standard since 1931 \u2014 cold-rolled steel, brass knurled adjusting nut, blackened rust-resistant finish, serial-numbered, lifetime warranty, and it just works. The Sparrows Double Tap ($75) is a solid modern manual with a tool-less quick-change tip and a 'double click' per trigger pull; ships with three tension wrenches, a spread of needles, and a case","For volume work go electric \u2014 HPC's Electropick (EPG-1) is the original cordless electric, runs on 4 AA batteries with a thumbscrew to set the stroke, and comes with 4 pick blades and 2 tension tools; saves your hand from trigger fatigue when you're firing all day","Tension is everything. If you take one thing away: use less than you think, roughly half what you'd use single-pin picking. Feather it","Keep the needle tuned low so it only ever contacts pins, never the plug \u2014 protects the lock and keeps you cleaner forensically","Carry spare needles \u2014 they're cheap and they bend and fatigue with use; a tweaked needle throws your hits off","It's a screening tool, not a master key. Give it 15\u201320 snaps; if it hasn't budged, it's got security pins or tight tolerances \u2014 switch to your picks and stop chewing up the lock","Don't reach for it on a customer's high-end lock where a service-call reputation matters \u2014 the impact marks on the pins are real, and a good lock won't give anyway"],sources:["https://www.lockpicks.com/products/lockaid-pick-gun","https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/collections/snap-gun","https://www.hudsonlock.com/product/electropick/","https://mbausa.com/majestic-lockaid-tool-pick-gun/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Pick_gun","https://www.lockpickingforensics.com/pick_guns.php","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snap_gun","https://www.offgridweb.com/gear/review-double-tap-snap-gun-from-sparrows-lock-picks/","https://unitedlocksmith.net/blog/lock-pick-guns-what-are-they-and-how-do-you-use-them"],photo:"photo-tool-pick-gun",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-pick-gun",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"pick-gun",caption:"How it works \u2014 the needle snaps the key pins so the drivers overshoot the shear line"}]},{id:"key-duplicator",name:"Key Duplicator",category:"Key machines",whatItIs:"A duplicator copies an existing key by tracing it \u2014 the original rides against a tracer stylus while a blank rides against the cutter wheel, both clamped in the same carriage, so the cutter mirrors every cut the stylus feels. Manual and semi-automatic bench machines like the Ilco Speed 040 and 045 are the workhorse that lives on every counter; electronic machines like the Ilco/Silca Futura Auto swap the mechanical tracer for a decoder and a tablet that reads the cuts and drives the cutter itself. If you cut keys for a living, a duplicator is the first machine you buy \u2014 it earns its bench space every single day.",useCases:["House and office keys \u2014 Kwikset, Schlage, and the commercial SC1/KW1 stuff that walks in all day","Standard edge-cut automotive keys off a good original (before the cars went all sidewinder and transponder)","Padlock and cabinet keys, mailbox keys, anything with a straight edge cut and a defined shoulder","Cranking out a stack of copies from one master fast \u2014 retail counter volume work","Copying a worn key well enough to work, using the paper-shim trick when you don't want to decode by hand","Electronic machines: decoding a key you can't read and originating a fresh cut when you've got no clean original to trace"],howToUse:"Everything lives or dies on the shoulder stop. Flip up the gauge, seat both the original and the blank hard against it so the shoulders line up, clamp them down, then drop the gauge out of the way before you cut. Get that reference wrong and every cut is off by the same amount \u2014 the key either won't enter or won't turn. On the four-sided nickel-plated jaws of the 040/045 you pick the jaw slot that matches the blank's keyway and blade thickness so the key sits flat and doesn't rock; a rocking blank gives you shallow, wandering cuts.\n\nHow you cut depends on the machine. The 045 is a straight manual duplicator \u2014 you feed the original into the tracer stylus and let the blank track the cutter at the same time, moving smooth and steady from shoulder to tip. The 040 is dual-function: run it manual the same way when you want the control, or hit automatic, where the spring-loaded carriage engages at the press of a button and applies its own consistent cutting pressure across the blade. Either way, don't force it \u2014 ride the deepest cuts a hair slower so the cutter doesn't deflect and leave the cut shy, then run a second pass to clean them up. When it's cut, hit both sides on the deburring brush \u2014 knock the burr off but don't grind the key, or you'll change the bitting. Test-feel it against the original: lay them cut-edge to cut-edge and eyeball that the peaks and valleys line up.\n\nWorn originals are the classic trap \u2014 a duplicate of a worn key is a worse key, and copy-of-a-copy just compounds the error. The counter trick: fold a sticky note, seat it in the jaw behind the worn original, tighten, tear off the excess \u2014 the shim lifts the key so the tracer reads a touch deeper and gives you back some of what wore off. One layer usually, two if it's really flat. When in doubt, decode the original with a key gauge and cut to code instead of tracing garbage.\n\nElectronic machines (Futura Auto) work different \u2014 you don't trace. You clamp the original, it decodes the cuts, or you pull the bitting off the tablet's make/model/year database, it shows you a cut preview, then the machine indexes and cuts the blank itself. Big win is originating a key with no good original, and the partial-cut function that lets you decode what's left of a broken key and build a cutting tree to get back to a working key from the piece you've got.",proTips:["Calibrate before you blame the key. Two adjustments matter: depth (tracer-to-cutter contact) and spacing/axis (the tracer stop vs the cutter). Back the depth off until the cutter just grazes the blank while the tracer touches the original, then lock it \u2014 the 040's depth tracer micro-adjusts in .00098 in (0.025 mm) steps, so it's worth getting right. Check it every time you change or dress the cutter wheel.","Cut a test key and physically compare it to the original edge-to-edge before you hand a customer the copy \u2014 thirty seconds that saves a callback.","Never duplicate off a duplicate. Always go back to the best original you can find. Errors stack and by the third generation the key's junk.","Match the blank right \u2014 correct keyway AND correct blade thickness. Wrong-thickness blank rocks in the jaw and cuts shallow.","Keep the cutter sharp and clean. A dull or brass-gummed wheel leaves ragged, shy cuts and burns the blank. Blow the chips out of the jaws too \u2014 one chip behind the shoulder throws your whole reference.","What to buy: Ilco Speed 045 is the basic manual counter standard; the Speed 040 gives you automatic OR manual in one machine and is the reliable step up. Know the difference on the HPC Blitz \u2014 the base 1200CMB is a CODE machine (cuts by depth/space dials and code cards, not a tracer); if you want code-cut plus duplicating in one box, that's the Switch Blitz. For modern automotive, a Futura Auto or Pro is the electronic machine to grow into \u2014 but keep a manual duplicator on the bench for volume house-key work, it's faster for that.","Nickel-plated jaws and a TiN-coated cutter (both standard on the 040/045) hold up better than the bargain kits \u2014 you're spinning that cutter thousands of times a week."],sources:["https://www.ilco.us/products/speed-040-automatic","https://www.ilco.us/products/speed-045-manual","https://mbausa.com/content/downloadfiles/speed-040-044-045-046-manual-english.pdf","https://www.keylessaccesslocks.com/kaba-ilco-speed-045-manual-key-duplicator-basic-duplicator-for-any-key-cutting-outlet-four-sided-nickel-plated-jaws-for-clamping-tin-titanium-nitride-coated-cutter-nylon-deburring-brush/","https://www.hudsonlock.com/product/the-original-blitz/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hpc-1200-switch-blitz-code-machine","https://hudsonlock.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/blitz-small.pdf","https://www.ilco.us/products/futura-auto","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/how-to-cut-a-key-on-the-ilco-speed-040-key-duplicator-video","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/locksmith-tip-duplicate-a-worn-key-trick","Product photo: Visualeagle (Wikimedia Commons, Key_Duplication_Machine_Code_Cutter.jpg), CC BY-SA 4.0"],autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-key-duplicator",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"key-duplicator",caption:"How it works \u2014 trace the original, copy onto the blank"}],photo:"photo-tool-key-duplicator"},{id:"code-machine",name:"Code Cutting Machine (Framon #2 / HPC 1200 Blitz)",category:"Key machines",whatItIs:"A code machine cuts a working key from a number \u2014 a bitting or blind code \u2014 with no original in hand. Instead of tracing an existing key like a duplicator, it positions the blank by depth and space off the manufacturer's published specs, so you can originate a key from a code stamped on a lock, a padlock, a filing cabinet, or pulled off a code series. The two you'll see on every serious bench are the Framon #2 (manual, micrometer-driven) and the HPC 1200 Blitz (card-driven, crank-fed).",useCases:["Originating a key when there's no original \u2014 customer lost every key but you've got the code off the lock or the paperwork","Lock-outs where you can read or decode the code (padlocks, desks, filing cabinets, tool boxes, mailboxes) and cut a key on the spot","Rekeying/master-key work \u2014 cutting a clean set of change keys and the master to a pinning chart you generated","Automotive originating from a code (progressioned or factory code) when you can't get a key blank read","Fixing a worn or badly-cut key by cutting a fresh one to spec instead of duplicating the wear","Cutting bump keys, try-out sets, and depth keys to exact factory depths"],howToUse:"The whole game is depth and space. Every cut on a key lives at a horizontal position (the space) and gets filed to a vertical depth (the depth), and both come out of a chart for that lock \u2014 Kwikset, Schlage, GM, whatever. You look up the manufacturer, get the depth-and-space numbers, and the machine does the positioning. On the Framon #2 you've got two cranks: depth on the front, spacing on the right side. One full revolution of either crank moves the carriage .050\" \u2014 that's the number to burn into your head, and the dials are graduated in .001\" increments so you set each depth and space to the thousandth. The machine itself is built and hand-fit to originate keys accurate to about half a thousandth (.0005\"), which is finer than the lock will ever care about. You set the space, set the depth on the dial, then feed the blank straight into the cutter. That straight-in feed is the Framon's whole trick \u2014 the key doesn't pivot, you dial the depth away from the wheel first so you physically can't over-cut and ruin the blank, and it gives you a clean perpendicular cut at the root. Cut, back it off, crank to the next space, reset depth, cut again. On a GM ten-cut you plan your order so you're only touching the depth crank two or three times instead of resetting every single space \u2014 group your same-depth cuts together and it flies.\n\nThe HPC 1200 Blitz works the same principle but hides the math behind a code card. You pull the card for that lock, drop it in the machine, and the card carries the depth and space marks plus the cutter, jaw, blank, and code series you need. You set the space needle to the number 1 mark on the card, crank the depth needle to the depth number for cut one, feed and cut, then walk the space needle to the next mark. New key to factory spec in under a minute, no dial reading. The card is faster and more forgiving for a newer hand; the Framon's book is deeper \u2014 900-plus cylinder charts plus 18 letterbox and 32 safe-deposit charts \u2014 and you can pencil in your own numbers on the blank pages for anything the card set doesn't cover.\n\nGotchas: backlash will bite you. Both machines have gear lash, so always crank into your final position from the same direction every time \u2014 if you overshoot a space or depth, don't just back up a hair, run past it and come back in the working direction so the slack is always taken up the same way. Cut your numbers, then verify the first few keys against a set of depth keys or a good micrometer before you trust the machine blind. Watch your cutter \u2014 a dull or wrong-angle wheel throws your depths off even when the dial is perfect. Match the cutter to the keyway: the Framon runs its FC series, and on the HPC the CW-1011 is the 90-degree small-cylinder/auto wheel you'll grab constantly (it ships alongside the CW-14MC 100-degree cylinder cutter). Keep the vise jaws clean and seat the blank against the shoulder or tip stop consistently, because a blank sitting .010\" proud shifts every space down the key.",proTips:["Learn the depth-and-space system, not just the machine \u2014 once you understand that a key is nothing but numbers on a grid, you can cut on any code machine, decode a lock by reading cut depths, and generate your own pinning charts","Framon #2 vs HPC 1200 Blitz: the Framon is the tank \u2014 mechanical, dials in thousandths, hand-fit to originate keys accurate to about .0005\", and its D&S book covers 900-plus cylinders plus letterboxes and safe-deposit; the Blitz is faster to learn because the code cards do the thinking. If you want one machine to originate anything for the next 20 years, most old hands point at the Framon","Budget it right \u2014 a Framon #2 complete package runs right around $2,000 (list is about $1,999 with the Genericode software and D&S manual), and that's a buy-once tool. Don't cheap out on a knockoff code machine; sloppy tolerances give you keys that work today and fail after the lock wears","Buy the depth keys for the lines you cut most, or at least keep a micrometer at the bench \u2014 verify your first cut of a new setup before you make ten bad keys","Always approach final depth and space from the same crank direction to kill backlash \u2014 this one habit separates keys that work from keys that 'almost' work","Keep spare cutters and know which wheel goes with which work \u2014 the HPC CW-1011 for small cylinder and auto (carbide CW-1011CC if you cut a lot), and don't run a chipped wheel; it'll read as shallow cuts","Write in your book \u2014 the Framon gives you blank chart pages on purpose. Every oddball lock you decode, log the depths and spaces so you never have to figure it out twice","A code machine plus a decent code-lookup (Framon's Genericode software or the printed manual) turns 'I need the original' into 'give me the code' \u2014 that's the whole reason it earns bench space"],sources:["https://www.framon.com/pdfs/manuals/Framon-Depth-and-Space-Manual.pdf","https://www.framon.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/12/framon-2.pdf","https://www.framon.com/index.php/project/framon-2/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/key-machine-key-cutters/article/21151915/still-the-one-framon-2-key-cutter","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/framon-2-code-machine","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/framon-2-code-machine","https://www.hudsonlock.com/product/the-original-blitz/","https://hudsonlock.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/blitz-small.pdf","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hpc-1200-blitz-code-machine","https://www.zoro.com/hpc-acquisitions-hpc-1200cmb-blitz-key-machine-cw-14mc-cw-1011-cutters-150-code-cards-1200cmb-hpc/i/G112322141/","https://foleybelsawlocksmithing.com/products/cw-1011-small-cylinder-auto-cutter-for-hpc-blitz-1200-machines"],photo:"photo-tool-code-machine",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-code-machine",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"code-machine",caption:"How it works \u2014 cut to coded depths, no original needed"}]},{id:"broken-key-extractor",name:"Broken Key Extractor",category:"Extraction",whatItIs:"A thin steel probe you slide into the keyway alongside a snapped-off key blade to grab it and drag it back out \u2014 without pulling the cylinder or drilling. Two families do the work: hook/saw-tooth extractors (flat blades with barbs or a serrated edge that catch behind a cut in the key) and spiral extractors (a twisted, threaded blade, like a tiny corkscrew, that you press against the blade and rotate so the threads bite the soft key metal). Most locksmiths carry a small interchangeable set \u2014 the HPC EZ-6 is the classic \u2014 which stows four blades in a hollow handle: one saw blade plus three spirals (two at .040\" / 1mm and one at .050\" / 1.27mm). Plenty of guys also carry the single spiral EZ-5 (.040\") loose as their grab-and-go.",useCases:["Customer snapped their key off in a door lock, deadbolt, or padlock and the plug still turns fine once the stub is out","Key broke off in an ignition or cam lock \u2014 anywhere pulling the cylinder is a pain and you'd rather fish the piece out in place","A worn or bent key finally fatigued and let go flush with the face of the plug","Clearing a foreign object \u2014 a jammed pick tip, a snapped blade, a paper clip, debris \u2014 out of a keyway","Any job where a non-destructive removal saves you rekeying or replacing the whole lock"],howToUse:"First thing, before you touch an extractor \u2014 get the plug back to 12 o'clock. The keyway has to be at its neutral, straight-up rest position or that stub is never coming out; the pins have to drop back down into the plug and clear the shear line. If the key broke mid-turn, gently walk the plug back to vertical with a flat tension tool first. Skip this and you'll fight it forever.\n\nNow read the keyway and figure out which side the cuts are facing. On most keys the bitting (the cut edge) is what you want to catch, so you're working the extractor down the flat of the blade on the cut side, riding a warding groove that gives you the cleanest line to a valley in the bitting. Slide it in past the broken end.\n\nWith a hook or saw-tooth blade: get the tip past the break, then draw it back until the barb drops into one of the cuts. You'll feel it \u2014 a solid catch, not a grate, not a click. Learn those three feels: a grating drag means you're skating on the warding, a soft click usually means you snagged a pin (back off, you're too high), and a firm seat with the tool sitting still under gentle pull is your bite. Saw-tooth wants the key lying flat against the cylinder wall so the teeth can hook behind a notch \u2014 it's fussier to place than a spiral. Once it's hooked, pull the extractor and the key stub out together in one smooth motion. Steady outward pressure \u2014 you're doing micro-surgery, not setting a hook on a bass.\n\nWith a spiral: run it in alongside the blade down one of the grooves, then the whole trick is pressure plus rotation at the same time. Press the spiral firmly against the key and twist \u2014 the threads eat into the soft metal and screw in against whatever they're up against, and as you keep tension the stub walks out with it. No precise hooking required, which is why the spiral is the one a lot of locksmiths reach for first. If it loads up and stalls, give it a yank or two to break it loose, then start over rather than grinding on it.\n\nGotchas: a hair of lube \u2014 silicone or graphite, not oil that'll gum a pin stack \u2014 cuts friction and makes the stub slide. Never turn tension while you extract; binding pins clamp the blade and you're done. Several light attempts beat one hard yank every time, and forcing it just snaps your extractor tip off in there too, which turns one problem into two.",proTips:["Two tools, one down each side of the blade, is the move on a stubborn stub \u2014 pinch the fragment between them and it comes out straight instead of cocking sideways and jamming","Match the spiral diameter to the keyway. The fat .050\" won't fit a tight paracentric keyway; the EZ-6 gives you both the .040\" and .050\" so you've got a size that actually reaches the metal","Spiral is your do-everything blade \u2014 it bites soft key metal without needing to hook a cut. Reach for the saw-tooth when the key's lying flat and you can drop a barb cleanly behind a notch for a positive catch","Buy the interchangeable set, not a bag of loose wires \u2014 the HPC EZ-6 stores all four blades in the hollow handle so nothing's floating around your kit, and you swap blades without carrying separate tools. A loose EZ-5 spiral (.040\") in a pocket is a fine backup","Keep spares. Extractor tips are thin and they snap; a broken tip left in the keyway is the one thing worse than the key you came to remove","If the stub's proud of the face at all, try locking pliers or tweezers first \u2014 cheaper on your extractors, and sometimes it just walks right out","Confirm 12 o'clock every time before you fish. Half the 'this extractor won't work' calls are just a plug sitting a few degrees off vertical with the pins still binding"],sources:["https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hpc-interchangeable-key-extractor-set-ez-6","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hpc-spiral-broken-key-extractor-ez-5","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/broken-key-removal-how-to-extract-a-broken-key-with-the-hpc-ez-5-key-extractor","https://easykeys.com/83_HPC_Key_Extractors.aspx","https://www.southord.com/blogs/news/key-extractor-tools-what-they-are-and-when-to-use-them","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-broken-key-extractor-and-how-does-it-work","Hpc ez 5 spiral key extractor (lockpickings.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published."],photo:"photo-tool-broken-key-extractor",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-broken-key-extractor",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"broken-key-extractor",caption:"How it works \u2014 hooking a cut to drag the blade out"}]},{id:"install-jig",name:"Lock Installation Jig & Hole Saw",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A clamp-on metal template that grips the edge of a door and gives you two hardened, aligned bushings \u2014 one steering a 2-1/8\" hole saw through the face for the cross bore, one steering a 1\" bit through the edge for the latch bore, both dead-on the backset you picked. The homeowner version is a stamped plate with a spade bit and a bi-metal saw; the pro version (Major DrillMaster 3, PRO-LOK Killer Jig) is machined aluminum with swappable bushing plates that outlives you. It exists because those two holes have to meet at a right angle and land on a 2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\" backset every single time, and freehand you cannot do that.",useCases:["Boring a brand-new undrilled door \u2014 slab replacements, new construction, a shop-built door with no prep","Adding a deadbolt above an existing knob where the two bores have to stack clean and share a backset","Re-boring or upsizing a door that was drilled wrong or drilled for a mortise and now takes a cylindrical lock","Batch work \u2014 a house full of new interior doors or a small commercial job where you're prepping ten-plus openings and freehand drift will kill you","Metal-clad and hollow-metal commercial doors where a walking hole saw ruins the skin and there's no filling it"],howToUse:"First thing \u2014 pick your backset before you clamp anything. Residential is almost always 2-3/8\" (interior) or 2-3/4\" (exterior), and the jig has two positions or two bushing holes for exactly those. On the pro jigs you swap the backset with a couple of knobs; guess wrong and your latch bore misses the cross bore, so measure or match the old prep. Set your height off the manufacturer's mark or a factory template \u2014 36\" to 40\" off the finished floor to the centerline is the normal window, but confirm against the existing knob if you're stacking a deadbolt above it.\n\nClamp the jig on the door edge and snug it \u2014 but check that the face plate sits flat and the door's actually plumb-ish in the frame or up on a jack. If the jig rocks, your bores tilt. On the pro units you swap in the 2-1/8\" bushing plate for the cross bore; the cheap kits just have the one plate. Run the 2-1/8\" hole saw through the face at full drill speed, moderate feed, and here's the gotcha \u2014 do NOT blow straight through. The saw's pilot pokes out the back, then you stop, walk to the other side, and finish the cut from the back face. Punch through in one pass and the far side splinters and blows out the veneer every time. On a solid slab you'll also want to back the pilot out and clear the plug so the saw doesn't bind and stall.\n\nThen the edge bore \u2014 1\" through the door edge into that cross bore. The jig's edge bushing keeps it square; without it the bit wanders and you break into the cross bore off-center. Go slow as you feel it break into the open cavity so you don't catch and split the edge. Mortise the latch face, mark and drill the strike, and you're done.\n\nBy feel: let the saw do the cutting, don't lean on it. If it's smoking or squealing you're feeding too hard or the teeth are dull \u2014 bi-metal dulls fast on metal doors. Keep the saw plumb; if you drift the bore goes oval and the rose won't cover it. And clear the plug from the saw cup between doors or it packs solid and you're prying it out with a screwdriver.",proTips:["Buy the backset and door-thickness range you'll actually see. The pro jigs handle 2-3/8\" and 2-3/4\" backsets \u2014 but watch the thickness spec: the Major DrillMaster 3 fits 1-3/8\" up to 2-1/2\" doors, while the PRO-LOK Killer Jig kit tops out around 2\". A homeowner kit built only for 1-3/8\" to 1-3/4\" chokes on a thick commercial slab.","If you touch metal doors, spend on a real jig \u2014 Major DrillMaster 3 (HIT-49, ~$300) or PRO-LOK Killer Jig kit (INJIG-KJ, ~$577). Machined aluminum with hardened bushings, and the DrillMaster comes with break-out plates for the 1-1/2\" and 2-1/8\" saws so the hole saw doesn't blow the back skin on hollow metal.","The ~$15-25 Kwikset 130WD homeowner kit is fine for the occasional WOOD door \u2014 positioning template, plastic drill guide, 2-1/8\" bi-metal saw, 1\" spade bit for the edge. But note it's wood-only; the plastic guide flexes and the saw's disposable. For metal-and-wood in a cheap kit, look at the Irwin 3111002 instead.","For the edge bore, a spur or auger bit tracks straighter than a spade \u2014 that's why the Killer Jig kit ships a dedicated edge-bore bit and cross-bore spur bits. A plain spade wants to wander when it breaks into the cross-bore cavity.","Always finish the cross bore from the back side after the pilot pokes through. Number-one rookie mistake is punching straight through and blowing out the far veneer on a customer's new door.","Carry spare bi-metal saws. They dull, and a dull 2-1/8\" on a fire-rated or metal-clad door will smoke, harden the cut, and cost you more time than the blade costs money. Carbide-grit saws hold up better on metal and fiberglass.","Keep the jig's bushings clean and lightly oiled \u2014 grit in the bushing scores your bit and adds slop, and slop is drift."],sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/major-mfg-drillmaster-3-hit-49","https://www.dkhardware.com/major-mfg-hit-49-drillmaster-3-cylindrical-lock-drill-guide-jig-for-use-with-hole-saws-fits-door-widths-1-3-8-to-2-1-product-7239547.html","https://www.pro-lok.com/shop/pro-lok/pro-lok-installation/cylindrical/standard-installation-jig/killer-jig-installation-kit/","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/new-killer-jig-kit","https://www.amazon.com/Kwikset-130WD-INSTL-WOOD-Installation/dp/B0002YUQ62","https://www.irwintools.com/product/3111002/metal-wood-door-lock-installation-kit","https://www.homedepot.com/p/Milwaukee-Door-Lock-Installation-Bi-Metal-Hole-Saw-Set-49-22-4073/204402141","https://www.majormfg.com/c-16-door-lock-installation-tools-hit-44-50-55-series.aspx"],photo:"photo-tool-install-jig",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-install-jig",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"install-jig",caption:"How it works \u2014 the jig sets the backset and center"}]},{id:"self-centering-bit",name:"Self-Centering (Vix) Bit",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A Vix bit is a spring-loaded self-centering drill bit \u2014 a twist drill living inside a tapered, spring-loaded nose sleeve. You seat the tapered nose into the countersunk screw hole of a hinge, strike plate, or latch face, and the taper drops the drill dead-center in that hole every time. It earns its spot because a pilot hole even a hair off-center walks your screw and pulls the hardware crooked, and by hand you cannot hit center on a countersink reliably \u2014 the Vix does it for you, mounted or handheld. Worth knowing: \"Vix\" started as a trade name (S.E. Vick), but it's used loosely now for the whole self-centering style, and the good ones today come from Whiteside, Snappy, W.L. Fuller and the like.",useCases:["Piloting hinge screw holes when hanging or re-hanging a door so the leaf sits flush and the screws don't cock the hinge","Drilling pilots for strike plates and box strikes in the jamb so the strike lands square and the screws bite true","Piloting mounting screws for deadbolt and knob/lever latch faces and faceplates on the door edge","Setting screws for door closers, panic hardware, flush bolts, and floor strikes where the mounting plate has countersunk holes","Any hardware swap where you're reusing existing holes or the hardware has factory countersinks and you need the pilot exactly centered","Working overhead or in tight jamb corners where you can't sight the drill straight \u2014 the taper centers it even when you're tilted"],howToUse:"The whole trick is trust the taper. Chuck the Vix in your drill (most are 1/4-inch hex quick-change, so they'll snap into an impact-ready driver, but run them in a regular drill/driver, not a hammer drill). Set the hinge or strike where you want it, hold it firm, and press the tapered nose straight into the countersink of the screw hole. The nose seats in the cone, the spring loads up, and as you push forward the sleeve retracts and the drill feeds through the center of that cone. Let the spring do the aiming \u2014 don't fight it, don't try to steer. Even if the drill body is tilted off square, the nose finds center because it's referencing the countersink, not your hands.\n\nBy feel: press in until the nose is fully seated before you squeeze the trigger, run it at a medium speed, drill, then release and let the spring push the bit back out on its own. Don't ride the trigger while pulling out or you'll flute the hole bigger. On a full mortise hinge, do two diagonal screws first, drive them, check the leaf is flush, then pilot the rest \u2014 that way if something's off you catch it before you've committed every hole.\n\nGotchas. Size to the screw, not to the hole in the hardware. A #8 wood screw \u2014 what most 3.5-inch residential hinges take \u2014 wants roughly a 7/64-inch pilot; a #6 wants about 5/64; a #10 (common on 4-inch and exterior hinges) wants about 1/8; a #12 runs 11/64. Those are the shank-referenced sizes the bits themselves are stamped for \u2014 go a hair smaller in softwood for grip, a hair bigger in dense hardwood so you don't split it. Just know the mapping isn't dead uniform between makers: Whiteside, for instance, runs one bit across #8/#9/#10, so read the box for the actual drill diameter rather than trusting the screw number alone. Too small and the screw splits the jamb or snaps; too big and it strips and won't hold. In hardwood jambs (oak, maple) drill the full length or the screw's still going to fight you. The nose only self-centers if the hardware actually has a countersink \u2014 flat-punched sheet metal strikes with no cone won't reference, so on those you eyeball it. And keep the nose clean: pack it with dust or paint and it won't seat flush, which throws off the whole point. The spring is the wear item \u2014 they get weak or snap with hard use, so back off the pressure and buy a bit you can rebuild.",proTips:["Buy replaceable-bit bodies, not throwaways. Good Vix bits hold the drill with an Allen set screw so when the tip dulls or snaps you swap a $5-7 drill instead of the whole tool. Whiteside and Snappy are the ones to own \u2014 both make the bit replaceable and made-in-USA; the no-name cabinet-supply sets are fine for a spare but the springs die fast.","The number stamped on the tool is the screw size it's meant for, not the drill diameter, and makers map them slightly differently (some put one bit across #8/#9/#10). Don't assume \u2014 confirm the actual drill diameter for the screw you're driving. For hinge work you'll live in the 7/64 (#8) size most of the time; carry a 5/64 (#6) and a 1/8 (#10) to cover the rest.","Run it in a plain drill/driver, never a hammer drill or an impact set to hammer \u2014 the impact wrecks the spring mechanism and the self-centering nose.","Keep the pilot depth right: for wood-screw hinges you often only need to pilot the threaded reach, but in dense hardwood jambs go the full screw length or you'll cam out drivers and snap heads.","On a flat metal strike with no countersunk cone, the Vix has nothing to reference \u2014 mark center with an awl or center punch and drill that instead of forcing the nose.","Blow or brush the nose clean between doors. A nose full of oak dust or wet paint won't seat, and a nose that won't seat drills off-center \u2014 which defeats the entire reason you're holding the tool."],sources:["https://www.whitesiderouterbits.com/collections/vix-bits-self-centering","https://www.whitesiderouterbits.com/products/8110008","https://www.snappytools.com/product-category/self-centering-hinge-bits/","https://boltdepot.com/Fastener-Information/Wood-Screws/Wood-Screw-Pilot-Hole-Size","https://l-36.com/woodscrewpilot.php","https://www.mcfeelys.com/eazypower-7-64-in-hinge-centering-drill-bit-for-8-screws.html","https://windowhardwaredirect.com/blogs/news/what-size-screw-for-door-hinge-a-comprehensive-guide-to-choosing-the-right-fasteners","https://toolguyd.com/self-centering-drill-bit-inflated-pricing/"],photo:"photo-tool-self-centering-bit",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-self-centering-bit",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"self-centering-bit",caption:"How it works \u2014 the cone centers the pilot in the hole"}]},{id:"impressioning-files",name:"Impressioning Files & Loupe",category:"Decoding & impressioning",whatItIs:"A 6-inch Swiss-cut #4 round or pippin file paired with a 10x jeweler's loupe \u2014 the two tools that let you cut a working key straight out of the lock with no original and no code. The pippin's teardrop cross-section (rounded belly, two flats meeting at a knife edge) shapes the cut and its flanks; the #4 cut leaves a fine, slightly dull, corrugated surface so a binding pin marks the blank under almost no pressure. The loupe is how you read those marks. Earns its spot because it's the one skill that gets you in when everybody else is stumped.",useCases:["Cutting a key for a lock where you have no original, no code, and no way to pull the cylinder","Making a key to a padlock or cabinet lock on the spot when a code series or decode isn't practical","Automotive and desk locks where impressioning beats disassembly","Proving competency \u2014 impressioning is a benchmark skill and a good way to sharpen your feel for how a lock actually binds","Situations where a customer needs a working key fast and you'd rather not destroy the cylinder to read it"],howToUse:"Start with a soft brass blank \u2014 brass takes marks clean and files easy. Steel is too hard to mark well, and aluminum develops fatigue cracks on you. First thing, file the top of the blade flat to a slight knife edge along the bitting line so a single pin marks a single clean spot instead of a smear. One caution while you're thinning: don't thin the blade right at the shoulder where it enters the keyway \u2014 that's where your turning tension lands and where a blank likes to crack. Then blacken the blade so marks read \u2014 smoke it over a lighter or candle until it's sooted, or lay down a coat of Sharpie or Dykem. Old hands lean toward soot: it survives shoving the blank in and out better, so your marks stay put.\n\nGrip matters more than people expect. Don't hold the bow with your fingers \u2014 that damps the vibration you need. Clamp a small pair of vice-grips (5-inch, no bigger) onto the bow aligned with the long axis of the blade so it's an extension of the key, not a right-angle wrench. Insert the blank fully, apply firm turning tension one direction, and while it's binding, add a little pulling pressure and rock and tap \u2014 wiggle up and down at the tip, then at the shoulder, tap the bow with something. You're trying to get the binding pin to scrub a mark on the soot. Firmer than picking pressure, but not so hard you snap the blank. And don't flip-flop your tension direction any more than you have to \u2014 turning one way then the other fatigues the blank faster.\n\nPull the blank, hit it with the loupe under good light. Look for small round marks or shiny scuffs sitting in the center of the bitting positions. That's a pin telling you it's still too high. Start each cut with the round file to get depth going, then switch to the pippin to true up the shape and cut the flat flanks clean \u2014 or run a round file with a small flat or triangular file to shape the flanks, whichever you prefer. File only the positions that marked \u2014 a couple of light swipes, no more \u2014 re-soot, and go again. As you get near depth the marks turn heavy and obvious. When a pin quits marking it's set; another pin becomes the new binding pin and you chase that one. Keep cycling \u2014 mark, read, cut, re-soot \u2014 until the plug turns and the lock opens. Standard pin-tumbler residential is 5 or 6 cuts, so that's five or six positions you're juggling.\n\nGotcha to watch: if a blank starts to crack \u2014 usually on the blade right at the shoulder where it enters the lock \u2014 stop immediately. You do not want a snapped blank stuck in the cylinder. Cracking more than occasionally means you're cranking too hard on the tension. Ease off.",proTips:["Buy the Grobet Swiss #4 \u2014 6-inch pippin (item 31.269) and a 6-inch #4 round. Cheap hardware-store files are the wrong cut and leave a surface too smooth to mark well; the whole trick lives in that #4 corrugation. Note these are sold as '6-inch' but run closer to 8 inches with the tang.","Soot beats Sharpie once you have a little experience \u2014 it survives insertion and removal better, so you're reading real marks instead of smeared ink.","Knife-edge the top of the blank first. A flat-topped blank smears every pin together and you'll never separate the positions.","Torque through the bow with vice-grips or a commercial impressioning handle rather than gripping the bow with your hand \u2014 your grip kills the movement that makes the marks.","Reset your tension every reading. This is the number one thing beginners skip and it's why their marks lie to them.","Get a real 10x jeweler's loupe you can hold with skin tension around the eye \u2014 hands-free \u2014 and work under bright, raking light. If you want to spend money, the Dysonian impression box is a shielded 10x magnifier with blended red/blue LEDs that pops the marks; a good loupe and a bright flashlight get you most of the way there.","Light strokes only \u2014 two or three swipes per pass. You can always take more; you can't put brass back. Over-file one position and you've scrapped the blank.","Keep a stack of brass blanks. You'll ruin a few learning, and cracked or over-cut blanks are just part of the practice. A four-pin Master padlock is the classic first lock to learn on."],sources:["https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Impressioning","https://www.libertyreferences.com/key-impressioning.html","https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Locksmithing/Key_Impressioning","https://patcosta.com/impressioning-manual-for-amateur-locksmiths/","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/31696705-impressioning-tools-and-files","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/dysonian-key-impression-light-and-magnification-box","https://grobetusa.com/product/swiss-precision-6-pippin-file-cut-4-item-31-269/","https://lockmasters.com/productdetail/war72064/grobet-swiss-file-pippin-4-cut-6in"],photo:"photo-tool-impressioning-files",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-impressioning-files",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"impressioning",caption:"How it works \u2014 file the binding marks the pins leave"}]},{id:"borescope",name:"Borescope / Safe Scope",category:"Safe & specialized",whatItIs:"A borescope is an optical probe \u2014 a slim rigid tube or flexible cable with a lens and light at the tip \u2014 that you feed through a small hole you drilled so you can actually see inside a safe body, a lock case, or the bolt works. Nine times out of ten it's how you finish a drilled safe: the hole gets you in, the scope tells you what you're looking at so you can dial it open, read a change key, spot a bolt, or clear a fired relocker. Range runs from a $30 USB endoscope on your phone all the way to a $1,900-plus rigid Hawkeye kit, and the price mostly buys you a smaller-diameter probe and a sharper image that fits a smaller hole.",useCases:["Finishing a drilled safe \u2014 you drill a scope hole to the lock, then look in to dial the wheels open or read the gates instead of punching or peeling the door","Reading a Group 2 combination lock's wheel pack, fly positions, and drop-in point so you can manipulate or verify a lost combo","Spotting and clearing a relocker \u2014 see whether a spring-loaded or fired relock is holding the bolt work before you drill again in the wrong spot","Checking bolt-work position and linkage on a jammed safe, gun safe, or file cabinet so you drill to the drive cam or bolt, not blindly","Inspecting behind a hardplate or into a lock case on a stubborn high-security deadbolt or padlock to see what's binding","Verifying you're clear of a glass relocker before committing a bit \u2014 scoping is the whole reason you can open a manipulation-proof lock without tripping the glass"],howToUse:"You drill the hole first, off a good drawing or a real drill point for that safe model, and you keep the hole just big enough \u2014 a standard scope hole is about 1/4 inch, and that's not an accident. A rigid Hawkeye scope is .165 inch (4.2mm), and the mirror tube slips it up to .188 inch (4.8mm), so a 1/4-inch hole passes it with a hair to spare and lets you sweep the tip around inside. Drill it too big and you've made more repair for the customer; drill it too small and your probe won't seat or rotate. Angle the hole so the scope's line of sight actually lands on what you need \u2014 the wheel pack, the drop-in, the drive cam \u2014 because you can't bend a rigid scope once it's in the hole. That aiming geometry is the whole game, and it's why you plan the hole around where the scope has to look, not just where the lock is.\n\nLight is your real problem, not resolution. Only a sliver of the light you throw down the probe comes back as an image, so more light is almost always better. A rigid scope pushes its light down a fiber-optic bundle from a separate source \u2014 the classic kits ran a Mini-Maglite, the newer Hawkeye kits ship a bright rechargeable LED (SuperNOVA), and the shop-based fiber illuminators run on 110VAC when you want to flood serious lumens through that quarter-inch hole. Get the light in, then work the focus on the eyepiece until the wheel edges snap sharp. Most kits give you angled mirror tips or a mirror tube \u2014 commonly a 90-degree tube \u2014 so you can look sideways or back toward the door instead of just straight ahead; swap tips to get the angle you need. On a combination lock you park the scope on the wheel pack, dial slowly, and watch the gates line up so you can read the combination or bring the fence into the gates by feel.\n\nGotchas: glass relockers. High-security safes hang a sheet of tempered glass between the door and the lock, and if your bit cracks it, the relock fires and throws extra bolts \u2014 now the correct combination won't open it and you've got a much bigger job. Scoping exists precisely so you can look before you leap and stay clear of the glass. Watch your probe on metal edges too \u2014 the fiber and lens are fragile, a rigid scope especially, so a cheap Scope Saver sleeve is worth the money: you drop the sleeve in the hole first and slide the scope through it, so the jagged drilled edge never touches the probe. And a rigid scope beats a flexible one on image and light every time; only reach for a flexible fiberscope or video probe when the path bends and a straight probe simply can't reach.",proTips:["Buy the light first, the resolution second \u2014 guys chase megapixels but lighting is what actually kills a scope job. A dim image on a $30 phone endoscope is a lighting problem, not a camera problem.","A $30 USB/phone endoscope opens plenty of safes and looks professional to the customer \u2014 but those cheap heads run 8mm to 10mm and need a bigger hole. The whole reason a real Hawkeye scope costs $1,900-plus is the .165-inch (4.2mm) probe that fits a standard 1/4-inch scope hole. Smaller head = more money.","For a serious safe kit, the Hawkeye Grand Opening kit (7\" and 17\" rigid scopes, ~$1,920) covers most work \u2014 the 7\" for close lock work, the 17\" to reach across a big body. It ships with the 90-degree mirror tubes and light source; you'll use the mirror constantly.","Consider an electronic video scope like the Lockmasters LKM6100 \u2014 a 5.5mm probe with a built-in 3.5\" LCD \u2014 so you're not squinting into an eyepiece at a weird angle in a customer's basement. Easier on your neck and easier to show the customer.","Keep the drill hole minimal and clean and always plan to plug it \u2014 a scope hole you can drive-pin and cap is a repair, a blown-out hole is a new door. Aim the hole for the scope's sightline, not just the shortest path to the lock.","Never drill a high-security safe without knowing where the glass relocker sits \u2014 scope your progress and stop before the glass. Tripping it turns a two-hour open into a full teardown.","Buy the Scope Saver sleeve and actually use it. Rigid fiber-optic scopes are fragile against a drilled steel edge, and replacing one costs a whole job's pay."],sources:["https://mbausa.com/grand-opening-kit-w-adjustable-focus/","https://www.hawkeyeborescopes.com/product/hawkeye-pro-slim-borescopes/","https://mbausa.com/scope-saver/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10229326/factors-to-consider-when-choosing-a-scope","https://www.safeandvaultstore.com/blogs/expert-advice-on-safes-and-vault-doors/glass-plate-relockers","https://www.lockmasters.com/v4-video-borescope-replacement-36-flexible-probe-lkm6100probe","https://www.oasisscientific.com/collections/locksmith-borescopes"],photo:"photo-tool-borescope",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-borescope",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"borescope",caption:"How it works \u2014 see the internals without opening it"}]},{id:"cordless-drill",name:"Cordless Drill & Carbide Bits",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"The cordless drill is the workhorse of the truck \u2014 you'll grab it more than any pick. It runs your hole saws for lock installs, your self-tappers, and when a lock won't cooperate any other way, it's your destructive entry tool. Pair it with the right bits \u2014 cobalt for hardened pins, carbide when you hit an anti-drill plate \u2014 and a drill guide to keep your bores clean and on center.",useCases:["Boring 2-1/8\" cross bores and 1\" edge bores for fresh deadbolt and knob/lever installs","Running self-tapping screws for strikes, closers, exit devices and metal-door hardware","Destructive entry \u2014 drilling a cylinder at the shear line when you're locked out of a re-key and there's no other way in","Drilling out broken or seized set screws, sheared keys, and stripped mounting screws","Popping SFIC/removable cores with a drill jig when you don't have the control key","Enlarging or cleaning up existing prep on a retrofit"],howToUse:"Get one drill dialed in as your truck drill and know it cold. An 18V/20V brushless hammer drill is the sweet spot \u2014 you want a 1/2\" chuck and 1,000+ in-lbs of torque so a 2-1/8\" hole saw in a steel door doesn't stall and twist your wrist. (Note the marketing: DeWalt's \"20V MAX\" and Milwaukee's \"M18\" are the same nominal 18V pack \u2014 20V is just the peak no-load reading, not more power, so don't pick on the number.) Carry two batteries minimum; a fresh full-install day will eat one. Keep it clutched down when you're driving screws so you don't strip strike holes, and switch off hammer mode for anything but masonry \u2014 hammer on a lock cylinder just chatters the bit and dulls it.\n\nFor drilling a lock open, your target is the shear line \u2014 the seam just above the keyhole where the plug meets the housing. Center-punch it first so the bit doesn't walk, start a 1/8\" pilot, then step up to 1/4\" to shear the pin stacks. Go slow with firm, steady pressure and keep it wet \u2014 a drop of cutting oil every few seconds keeps the bit cutting instead of burning. Let the bit do the work; leaning on it and running high RPM just work-hardens the steel and cooks your edge. Once the top pins are sheared you can usually turn the plug with a flathead or a broken key blank.\n\nBit selection is where guys go wrong. For hardened lock pins and security pins, run cobalt \u2014 M35 (about 5% cobalt) is the everyday choice because it's tough enough to survive a handheld drill; M42 (about 8% cobalt) cuts harder steel and holds an edge longer but it's more brittle and wants a rigid setup, so it snaps if you're wobbling. Get 135-degree split-point tips \u2014 they bite immediately and won't skate. Save the tungsten carbide for when you actually hit an anti-drill pin or hardened insert in a high-security cylinder (Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, Abloy and the like all embed hardened steel to kill ordinary bits). Carbide will grind through that hardened insert, but it's glass-brittle \u2014 any side load or catch and it shatters, so it only works when the bit is held dead-square, which is exactly why you use a guide.\n\nThe drill guide is the difference between clean work and a chewed-up door. For installs, clamp a cylindrical-lock jig like the Major Mfg DrillMaster 3 (HIT-49) on the edge \u2014 it takes 1-1/2\" and 2-1/8\" hole saws, registers the cross bore on the right backset (2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\"), and keeps it square and concentric so the lock sits flush; you still drop the 1\" edge/latch bore separately off that centerline. For destructive entry on tough cylinders, a cylinder drill jig (or an SFIC/I-core jig from Peterson) clamps to the plug face and gives you the exact drill point and a bushing that holds the bit on line so it can't wander off a hardened pin.",proTips:["Buy into ONE battery platform for the whole truck \u2014 drill, impact, recip, work light all on the same packs. DeWalt 20V MAX XR and Milwaukee M18 FUEL are both proven; whichever you already own wins.","A hammer drill/driver covers 95% of the work. You don't need a rotary hammer unless you're anchoring strikes and closers into concrete/block regularly \u2014 then keep a cheap SDS as a second tool.","Cobalt for pins, carbide only for anti-drill inserts. Don't waste money drilling ordinary brass/steel locks with carbide \u2014 cobalt is cheaper, tougher, and won't shatter if the bit catches.","Keep cutting oil in the truck and USE it. Dry-drilling hardened steel burns the tip in seconds. Wet + slow speed + firm pressure = the bit lasts; run it fast and dry and you'll toss it after one lock.","Center-punch every time \u2014 on the door for installs and on the plug for drill-outs. Thirty seconds with a punch saves a walked bit and a scarred door.","A drill guide isn't optional for high-security drill-outs. Carbide only survives if it's held perfectly square; hand-holding it into a Medeco is how you snap a $15 bit and gouge the cylinder.","Stub/screw-machine length cobalt bits are stiffer and wander less than long jobber bits \u2014 use the shortest bit that reaches.","Carry a step bit and a good self-feed/hole-saw arbor too \u2014 a sharp bimetal hole saw with a fresh pilot beats a dull one that smokes and grabs in a steel door."],sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/major-mfg-drillmaster-3-hit-49","https://www.dkhardware.com/major-mfg-hit-49-drillmaster-3-cylindrical-lock-drill-guide-jig-for-use-with-hole-saws-fits-door-widths-1-3-8-to-2-1-product-7239547.html","https://www.majormfg.com/c-21-drill-guides.aspx","https://www.ttp-hard-drills.net/cobalt-drill-bit-faq/","https://www.hk-tools.com/news/m35-and-m42-cobalt-drill-bits-differences-and-usage/","https://ashdowntooling.co.uk/blogs/tool-guides/what-is-the-difference-between-m35-m42-and-m48-cobalt","https://toolguyd.com/dewalt-20v-max-18v/","https://www.protoolreviews.com/20v-max-vs-18v-battery-power/","https://www.medeco.com/en/products/deadbolts/product-details.aehpdp-medeco4-maxum-aeh_medeco_227524","https://surelockkey.com/blog/how-to-drill-out-a-cylinder-lock-safe-pro-methods/","https://www.thinkpeterson.com/i-core-drill-jig-for-sfic-cores/","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/cylinder-jig-and-drill-bit","https://www.allaboutdoors.com/Article-Guide-to-Door-Lock-Measurements"],photo:"photo-tool-cordless-drill",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-cordless-drill",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"cordless-drill",caption:"How it works \u2014 drilling the shear line (last resort)"},{asset:"photo-tool-milwaukee-cordless-drill-driver",caption:"A Milwaukee cordless drill/driver \u2014 the brushless 18 V class most techs settle on for lock work"}]},{id:"multimeter",name:"Multimeter (Access Control)",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A handheld digital multimeter (DMM) is your voltage, resistance, and continuity meter \u2014 the single most-used diagnostic tool on any low-voltage access control call. When a maglock won't hold, a strike won't fire, or a reader's gone dead, this is what tells you whether the problem is power, wire, or the device itself. Get an auto-ranging meter with a continuity beeper \u2014 and a low-impedance (LoZ) mode to kill ghost voltage \u2014 and you'll reach for it on nine out of ten EAC troubleshoots.",useCases:["Confirming a power supply is actually putting out its rated 12VDC or 24VDC \u2014 at the terminals, under load, not just at rest","Chasing voltage drop down a long wire run to a maglock or strike that's chattering or dropping out","Checking DC voltage at a reader's power terminals when a reader's gone dark (12V or 5V depending on model)","Verifying a strike or maglock actually gets voltage when you trigger the REX, a keypad, or the controller relay","Continuity-testing a run of wire, a door contact, a REX loop, or a magnetic bond sensor to find an open or a short","Ringing out fuses and PTC resettable fuses on the power supply board before you swap a 'dead' device","Telling AC from DC power at a transformer or strike so you don't fry a DC-only device on AC or vice versa","Confirming a relay on the controller is actually closing (voltage present on the switched leg when it fires)","Reading an end-of-line resistor in ohms to confirm it matches what the panel is programmed to supervise"],howToUse:"Black lead in COM, red in the V\u03a9 jack \u2014 that's home base and it stays there for voltage, resistance, and continuity. For voltage, set it to DC volts (or let an auto-ranging meter with AutoVolt sort AC/DC for you), put the red probe on the positive terminal and black on negative or ground, and read it. Always meter at the device, not just at the panel \u2014 that's the whole game. A power supply reading a clean 12.0V at its own terminals means nothing if the maglock 200 feet away is only seeing 10.8 because of an undersized wire run. Measure at the lock, under load, with it energized. More than about a half-volt of drop from panel to device and you've got a wire-gauge or connection problem, not a device problem.\n\nContinuity is where you live for dead-circuit work: turn the dial to the continuity setting (the little sound-wave symbol), touch the probes together first to hear it beep and confirm the meter's alive, then ring out your wire, contact, or REX loop. Beep means closed and good; silence means open or broken. Power down the circuit before you continuity-test \u2014 the beeper mode pushes its own tiny current and a live circuit gives you garbage or damages the meter. Same for resistance (ohms): it only reads true on a de-energized circuit.\n\nWatch for ghost voltage \u2014 a floating wire near a live one can show you 30, 40 phantom volts on a high-impedance meter and send you chasing nothing. A meter with a low-impedance (LoZ) mode kills that read instantly so you know it's a real supply or just induced noise. And know your rest-versus-load difference: a battery-backed supply might show 12.4V sitting idle but sag under the pull of an energized maglock \u2014 that sag is the diagnosis. Most battery-backed supplies have a deep-discharge cutoff that drops the load around 10.5V on a 12V system, so a lock that works cold and fails after a few minutes on backup is often a tired battery or marginal supply, and the meter under load is how you catch it.\n\nDial settings, quick reference. DC volts is the everyday setting \u2014 12/24V lock power supplies, strikes, maglocks. AC volts (V~) reads a transformer's output before the rectifier (16/24 VAC). Resistance (\u03a9) checks coils and long wire runs, and flags a request-to-exit or a bad splice reading high or open. Ohms is also how you verify an end-of-line resistor: read it across the loop with the zone de-energized and confirm the value matches the panel's supervision setting, because a zone stuck in trouble or alarm is very often the wrong EOL value rather than a bad contact. Continuity (the beep) confirms a closed circuit \u2014 door contacts, a cut wire, a switch actually making. DC current, with the meter in series, shows a lock's draw against the PSU's rating. Diode test checks the flyback diode or MOV across a relay or lock coil.",proTips:["Buy the Fluke 117 if you can swing it \u2014 true-RMS, auto-ranging, AutoVolt AC/DC, a continuity beeper, non-contact VoltAlert in the nose, and the LoZ mode that eats ghost voltage. It's CAT III 600V, runs off a single 9V, and it's the meter most access guys settle on for good.","On a tight budget the Klein MM600 handles DC volts and continuity fine \u2014 just know it's average-responding, not true-RMS. If you want true-RMS plus a LoZ mode to kill ghost voltage, step up to the MM700 (or MM720). For DC access-control work true-RMS doesn't matter anyway \u2014 it only counts on messy AC \u2014 so don't overpay for it if you're mostly metering locks and readers.","The non-contact voltage tester built into the meter is a fast first pass \u2014 wave it before you probe to know if a wire's hot at all \u2014 but never trust it as your only read. It only sees AC, not DC, and it'll false on induced voltage. Confirm with the probes.","Always meter under load. Rest voltage lies. A supply that reads perfect idle can collapse the second the maglock pulls its full amp \u2014 that collapse is the whole answer on intermittent drop-outs.","Touch your probes together to hear the continuity beep before every continuity test. Two-second habit that tells you the meter, the leads, and the battery are all good before you go blaming the door.","De-energize before continuity or ohms. The beeper injects its own current \u2014 testing a live circuit gives false readings and can hurt the meter.","Keep a spare 9V and spare meter fuses in the kit. A blown internal fuse from probing current on the wrong jack leaves you meter-less mid-call, and it always happens on the worst job.","Alligator-clip or magnetic probe leads are worth it \u2014 clip onto the terminals and free both hands to trigger the REX and watch the reading swing. Probing a maglock overhead one-handed on a ladder is how you drop a lead. Klein's stock leads are more flexible than Fluke's, so plenty of guys buy the meter for the guts and upgrade the leads either way.","Learn your rated targets cold: 12VDC and 24VDC are the two you'll meter all day, and readers commonly want 12V or 5V. Anything sagging more than about 5% under load is a wire or supply problem.","Reversed leads on DC just flip the sign \u2014 a minus in front of the reading means your probes are backwards, not that the supply is. Use it deliberately to identify polarity on an unmarked pair."],sources:["https://www.fluke.com/en-us/product/electrical-testing/digital-multimeters/fluke-117","https://www.instrumart.com/assets/fluke117_datasheet.pdf","https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/multimeters/digital-multimeter-auto-ranging-1000v","https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/multimeters/digital-multimeter-trmslow-impedance-1000v","https://www.onesdr.com/klein-mm600-vs-mm700-multimeters-whats-the-difference/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/electronics-access-control/control-panels-accessories/article/21151325/powering-up-access-control","https://www.getkisi.com/guides/backup-batteries-for-access-control","https://www.fluke.com/en-us/learn/blog/digital-multimeters/how-to-test-for-continuity"],photo:"photo-tool-multimeter",autoAlso:!0,photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-multimeter",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"multimeter-settings",caption:"Dial settings \u2014 what each does & when"}]},{id:"auto-key-programmer",name:"Automotive Key Programmer (Diagnostic Tablet)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"The brain of the whole key job. It's a handheld tablet that plugs into the car's OBD-II port and talks to the immobilizer (IMMO) system so you can add keys, erase lost ones, and get a car that lost all its keys running again. The tablet does the talking to the car; a separate key/transponder programmer box (Autel XP400 Pro, Xhorse mini programmers) reads and writes the actual chips. Main players a shop will run: Autel MaxiIM IM508/IM608 Pro II (diagnostic-plus-key tablet, Android based, XP400 Pro + MaxiFlash JVCI in the kit), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus and Multi-Prog (locksmith-focused, strong on VAG and Mercedes), and Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro (token/subscription driven, big in dealer and fleet work). These are not the same as a cheap OBD code reader \u2014 they read PIN/CS codes, back up and restore IMMO data, and do EEPROM/MCU reads when the OBD path won't cut it.",useCases:["Add a spare key or remote to a car that already has a working key (add key / key learning)","All Keys Lost (AKL) \u2014 pull the PIN/CS or IMMO data and generate a fresh key when the customer has nothing","Erase lost or stolen keys from the immobilizer so old keys no longer start the car","Read and write transponder chips (Toyota H/G, Hitag, ID46/48, etc.) and clone or generate keys","Program proximity/smart keys and push-to-start remotes, including remote-only relearn","Backup and restore IMMO data before an ECU or immobilizer swap, plus IMMO reset/adaptation","Read PIN codes off the car for makes that require a code before programming","Bench work via EEPROM/MCU read-write when OBD programming is locked or the module is pulled","Clear the software error codes and DTCs that pop up after key programming (Autel's diagnostic side shines here)"],howToUse:"Start by pinning down year/make/model and the immobilizer type \u2014 that decides whether it's a simple OBD add-key or a full bench/AKL job. Plug the VCI/tablet into the OBD-II port under the dash, power the car to ignition-on, and let the tablet ID the vehicle. From the IMMO menu pick the function: Add Key, All Keys Lost, or Erase Keys. For an add-key with a working key present, most jobs are straight OBD \u2014 the tablet reads the immobilizer, you drop the new key/transponder in the programmer box or in the slot/coil, and it writes and learns it.\n\nAll Keys Lost is where the tablet earns its money. Depending on the car you'll read the PIN/CS code, pull IMMO data off the ECU, or go to bench \u2014 sometimes with a helper adapter like the Autel APB112 or G-BOX for encrypted CAN gateways and 12-second timers. Generate the key on the programmer, then learn it to the car through the OBD session. Watch the guided prompts closely: cycle ignition, wait out anti-scan timers, and don't yank the cable mid-write.\n\nKeep the tablet on a good 12V supply the whole time \u2014 a battery maintainer on the car is not optional on long AKL sessions, because a voltage sag mid-write can brick a module. After programming, run the diagnostic scan, clear the IMMO/DTC codes the process throws, and verify every key starts the car and the remote functions all work before you hand them over.\n\nBudget for the money side too: Autel runs on annual update subscriptions (kits ship with 1-2 years, then you renew for new coverage), Xhorse Key Tool Plus does free lifetime updates on existing functions but charges for premium unlocks like MQB and BMW, and Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro runs on tokens or an Unlimited Token Plan (UTP) subscription, plus per-car-line software modules.",proTips:["Two tools beat one: pros run an Autel IM508/IM608 alongside a Xhorse Key Tool Plus \u2014 Autel for diagnostics and clearing post-programming faults, Xhorse for VAG and Mercedes coverage. They cover each other's gaps.","North America gotcha: Autel pulled AKL / immo-reset in the NA region \u2014 and not just Toyota. At the OEMs' request it dropped all-keys-lost on Toyota plus GM, Ford, Mazda, Nissan and Chrysler. Xhorse, Lonsdor and OBDSTAR aren't affected, so keep one of those on the truck for those AKL jobs.","Always throw a battery maintainer on the car before an AKL job. A voltage drop mid-write can corrupt the immobilizer module \u2014 that's a warranty-buster.","The tablet talks to the car; the programmer box (XP400 Pro, Xhorse mini) reads/writes the chip. Know which one the job actually needs before you quote.","Check what your subscription/tokens actually cover before you commit to a job \u2014 coverage lists change with every update, and a lapsed sub means no new car lines. Xhorse's core is free-for-life, but MQB and BMW G/U-series work needs the paid license active.","Keep firmware and vehicle databases updated before you leave the shop. New models fail to ID on stale software, and you don't want to find out in the driveway.","For encrypted-gateway and short-timer cars, have the helper adapters on hand (Autel APB112 / G-BOX) \u2014 without them some AKL jobs are a dead end."],sources:["https://store.autel.com/products/maxiim-im608-pro-ii","https://www.autel.com/immotool1/3250.jhtml","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/09/20/what-is-a-better-alternative-after-autel-removes-toyota-akl-function/","https://www.xhorsevvdi.com/wholesale/xhorse-vvdi-key-tool-plus.html","https://www.advanced-diagnostics.com/ad-products/smart-pro","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/blogs/news/autel-vs-xhorse-key-programming-devices"],photo:"photo-tool-auto-key-programmer",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-auto-key-programmer",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"auto-key-programmer",caption:"How it works \u2014 tablet \u2192 OBD \u2192 immobilizer learns the key"}]},{id:"auto-transponder-cloner",name:"Transponder Cloner",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A bench tool that reads the transponder chip out of a working key and writes an identical copy onto a blank cloneable chip, so the new key starts the car without ever touching the immobilizer or the OBD port. The immobilizer sees the clone as the original \u2014 same chip ID, same crypto response \u2014 so no programming, no PIN, no relearn. Common units are the Xhorse VVDI Key Tool / Key Tool Max / Mini Key Tool, the Keydiy KD-X2, and the Scorpio-LK Tango. They pair with special \"super\" or cloneable blanks: the Xhorse Super Chip (XT27) and 4D/4C/G copy chips, Keydiy KD 4D/4C chips, and Tango-writeable Megamos/Texas glass blanks. The key word is fixed-code \u2014 cloning shines on 4C and older fixed-code 4D transponders that don't roll their response. Encrypted chips like Toyota G, ID48 and Megamos crypto can still be cloned, but only after the tool runs a calculation (usually a paid online token), not a straight memory copy.",useCases:["Cutting a spare when the customer already has one working key and you want a fast bench duplicate with no car present","Fixed-code 4C copies (Texas TX) \u2014 Toyota/Lexus, older Honda, Ford, Chrysler","Fixed-code 4D clones (4D-60, 4D-63, 4D-DST40 non-crypto) \u2014 Ford, Mazda, older Nissan/Subaru","Toyota/Lexus G-chip (4D-72G, DST40 crypto) \u2014 clones onto a G copy chip via the tool's onboard/token calculation, not a plain copy","ID48 / Megamos 96-bit cloning (VW/Audi/older Euro) using online 48-clone tokens on the KD-X2 or VVDI","T5 fixed-code chips (ID 11/12/13/33) onto a carbon/glass T5 blank for cheap older-car spares","Making a backup of a rare or single-key vehicle before you attempt any AKL or add-key work"],howToUse:"Drop the customer's working key into the tool's transponder coil slot and hit Identify/Read. The unit tells you the chip family \u2014 4C, 4D, 46, 48, T5, DST \u2014 and whether it's cloneable. Fixed-code (4C, non-crypto 4D) reads and copies straight across in seconds. If it comes back as ID48/Megamos 96-bit or a Toyota G/H crypto chip, you're into a challenge-response calculation that most units run through the maker's server, so you'll burn an online token and need internet at the bench.\n\nPut a matching cloneable blank in the coil \u2014 Xhorse Super Chip or 4D/4C/G copy chip for VVDI, KD 4D/4C for the KD-X2, or the correct writeable glass blank for a Tango. Confirm the blank type matches what you read, then hit Write/Generate. The tool writes the ID and crypto data onto the blank so it answers exactly like the original.\n\nCut the mechanical blade to the customer's code or by decoding the original, drop the fresh chip in the head (or it's already in the shell if you're using a chip-in-key blank), and test it in the ignition. A good clone cranks and runs with zero immobilizer light \u2014 no OBD, no relearn, no lost minute of security wait. If the dash flashes the immo light, you either wrote the wrong blank type or you're dealing with a rolling-code chip that can't be cloned \u2014 those need real onboard programming instead.\n\nThe whole point is the car never enters the picture: one working key in, one working spare out, all on the bench. That's why cloning is the go-to for add-a-key jobs and the fallback when the vehicle won't let you into programming mode.",proTips:["Cloning only works when you have a working key to read \u2014 no working key means it's an all-keys-lost job and you're back to OBD/EEPROM programming, not the cloner","Know fixed-code vs rolling-code before you promise a clone: 4C and non-crypto 4D copy fine; ID46 Hitag2, ID49 Hitag-Pro, Toyota H (DST-AES) and most proximity chips do NOT clone \u2014 they must be programmed to the car","Toyota G, ID48/Megamos 96-bit are 'clone but pay' \u2014 the crypto is calculated on the maker's server, so keep online tokens/credits loaded and internet up or the job stalls at the bench","A clone is a duplicate ID, not a new key in the immobilizer's memory \u2014 the car can't tell them apart and can't disable one without disabling the other; tell the customer if that matters","Match the blank to the chip family exactly \u2014 writing a 4D copy chip when the read was 4C (or vice versa) throws a write error; the Xhorse 4D-to-4C special function exists to bridge certain Toyota 4D reads onto a 4C-style clone","Cheaper than a dedicated programmer for spares, but it will not do add-key or AKL on rolling-code cars \u2014 carry both a cloner and a real programmer, they're different jobs","Buy your cloneable blanks from the same brand as your tool \u2014 Xhorse Super Chip for VVDI, KD chips for Keydiy \u2014 mixing brands often won't write"],sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/keydiy-kd-x2-kd-x2-remote-maker-cloning-tool-w-96bit-48-transponder-copy-function","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/mini-key-tool.html","https://abkeys.com/products/xhorse-clonable-transponder-chip-for-4c-4d-toyota-g-chips-4206","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/the-american-key-supply-guide-to-cloning","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/cn5-cloneable-transponder-chip-clone-4d-toyota-g-chips-cn900-cn900mini-tango","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/tango-professional-transponder-programmer-device-for-reading-and-writing-chips-1-year-software"],photo:"photo-tool-auto-transponder-cloner",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-auto-transponder-cloner",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"auto-transponder-cloner",caption:"How it works \u2014 read the original, write the clone"}]},{id:"auto-key-cutter",name:"Automotive Key Cutting Machine (Laser/Sidewinder)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A CNC key machine that mills automotive key blades from a code or from a decoded original. Unlike a duplicator (which traces an existing key with a follower), a cutting machine positions the blank in a clamp, spins a cutter, and drives the blade against it on computer-controlled axes to hit exact spacing and depths. The mainstays: Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus / XC-Mini Plus II and the budget Dolphin XP-005 / Dolphin II XP-005L, the Triton Plus (Automotive/Ultimate editions, built by LockLabs), and the Miracle A9 (REDT). All cut internal-cut/laser (sidewinder) keys, dimple keys, and standard edge-cut; tibbe and track keys need the right clamp/cutter. Most can decode a customer key by probing it, then cut a fresh one, or cut straight from a lock/key code you punch in.",useCases:["Cutting laser/sidewinder keys for late-model cars (VW, BMW, Mercedes, Nissan, and up) from a code or a decoded original","All-keys-lost jobs: milling a fresh blade off the lock/key code when there's no working key to copy","Replacing high-security and dimple keys a follower-style duplicator can't trace accurately","Cutting edge-cut, dimple, and tibbe keys off one machine by swapping the clamp and cutter","Decoding a worn or aftermarket customer key to recover the bitting before cutting a clean copy"],howToUse:"Pick the machine to the work. The Condor XC-Mini Plus II is the shop default: aluminum body, 7-inch Android touchscreen, onboard database and decode function, and it swaps clamps for edge, laser, dimple, and tibbe (ships with M3 and M5 clamps). The Dolphin is the mobile/backup line running the same cutting engine: the original XP-005 has no onboard screen and runs off the Xhorse app or Key Tool Max, while the newer Dolphin II XP-005L adds a built-in adjustable 5-inch touchscreen and Wi-Fi updates. Triton Plus is the American-made (LockLabs, Las Vegas) all-in-one with a strong onboard code database and a 7-inch touchscreen; you swap cutters on its spindle to move between edge and laser. Miracle A9 (REDT) is a solid mid-price all-rounder with a touchscreen and built-in decoder.\n\nBefore you cut, get the bitting. Two paths: decode or code. To decode, clamp the customer key, run the machine's decode routine, and it reads the depths off the blade, always eyeball the result against the key because a worn or aftermarket blade decodes soft. To cut by code, look up the lock/key code (VIN-to-key through the dealer or a code service, or read it off the lock), enter it, and the machine converts code to bitting from its database.\n\nClamp selection is where keys get scrapped. Each blade family seats in a specific jaw and side (M1/M2/M3/M5 on Xhorse, for example), get the blank fully seated against the stop and clamped square or every cut walks. Confirm you loaded the matching cutter (edge cutters and laser/dimple end-mills are not interchangeable) and that the machine profile matches the actual blank you're holding, not just the car. Cut, deburr, and test in the lock. For transponder/proximity keys remember the cut is only half the job: you still program the chip/remote separately.",proTips:["Decode is a suggestion, not gospel, always sanity-check the decoded depths against the original before you commit a blank; a worn key decodes shallow and you'll cut a key that won't turn.","Buy blanks that actually match the profile the machine expects. Cheap aftermarket blades with wrong blade thickness or a mis-milled neck throw off clamping and give you cuts that gauge fine but bind in the lock.","Keep the clamp jaws and cutter clean, aluminum swarf packs into the jaw and lifts the blank a few thousandths, which is enough to miss on high-security.","Calibrate on a schedule and after any hard crash or clamp swap. These are CNC, the axes and cutter wear; a machine that cut perfect last month can drift.","Match cutter to key: use the correct end-mill for laser/dimple and the slotting cutter for edge. Running the wrong cutter chews the blade and wrecks tolerances.","The original Dolphin XP-005 needs the Xhorse app or Key Tool Max to run at all; on any of them keep the account and firmware current or newer profiles won't be there.","Cutting the blade doesn't start the car, sidewinder keys are almost always transponder-equipped; plan to program the chip and remote after cutting."],sources:["https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/xhorse-condor-xc-mini-plus-ii.html","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/condor-xc-mini-plus-ii-automotive-key-cutting-machine-xhorse-14861","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/blogs/news/xhorse-dolphin-xp-005-vs-dolphin-ii-xp-005l-is-the-upgrade-worth-it-in-2026","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/xhorse-dolphin-xp005l.html","Triton key machine product range (tritonkeymachine.com) \u2014 original link retired.","https://www.aeswave.com/Triton-Plus-Key-Cutting-Machine-Automotive-Edition-TPAE-p10328.html","https://www.redtinc.com/products/key-cutting-machine/miracle-a9-edge","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/triton-plus-automatic-key-cutting-machine-one-machine-does-it-all-automotive-editon-in-stock-now"],photo:"photo-tool-auto-key-cutter",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-auto-key-cutter",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"auto-key-cutter",caption:"How it works \u2014 cut the sidewinder track to code"}]},{id:"auto-lockout-kit",name:"Car lockout kit (long-reach + wedge)",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A non-destructive vehicle entry set built around two working pieces: an inflatable air wedge (pump wedge / air bladder) that gaps the door away from the pillar, and a long-reach tool \u2014 a coated steel rod 40 to 60 inches long, tipped or hooked \u2014 that goes through that gap to hit the interior unlock button, the door handle, or a lock cable. Better kits add plastic/nylon corner wedges and pry levers to start the gap, an under-window \"coat hanger\" style reach tool, and a tipped end you can swap. Common pro names: the Access Tools Long Reach Kit (their rods paired with Air Jack wedges), the Steck/Milton BigEasy (a 55-inch rod-and-wedge kit \u2014 BigEasy is a Steck Manufacturing product, now Steck by Milton, not an Access Tools tool), and generic 22-piece air-wedge-plus-rod kits. This is the everyday non-damaging opener \u2014 no slim jim down the glass channel, no destructive work.",useCases:["Keys locked inside on a vehicle with an interior unlock button, rocker switch, or manual door lock post you can reach","Fast roadside lockout where you can't afford paint damage \u2014 tow operators, roadside assistance, fleet","Vehicles where lock cylinders are absent, hidden, or not worth picking and the interior control is the fastest path","Backup when the door is a decode/pick nightmare but the inside handle or unlock button is exposed","Any make where the customer wants zero cosmetic risk versus a slim jim in the door cavity"],howToUse:"Start by making a gap, not by forcing one. Slide a thin plastic/nylon wedge or a pry lever into the top corner of the door where it meets the pillar \u2014 the top corner flexes most and is farthest from linkages. Work it just enough to slip the deflated air wedge in behind it. Never drive a metal tool into the seam; you tear weatherstrip and dent the door frame.\n\nOnce the air bladder is seated an inch or two inside the gap, pump it slowly. Watch the gap open \u2014 you want maybe a half inch to an inch, only as much as the rod needs. Stop the moment you can pass the tool. Over-inflating bends the door skin and can crack window glass or pop the door alignment. On frameless-window doors, crack the door open a hair rather than prying the glass.\n\nFeed the long-reach rod through the gap. Protect the paint on the way in \u2014 a rubber grommet or a wrap of tape on the tool where it rides the sheet metal saves the finish. Get a light on the interior: a flashlight, or a windshield-mounted light if the kit has one, so you can see the button or handle. Guide the tip to the interior unlock button, the lock post, or the inside handle and actuate it. Pull the handle or push the button; listen for the latch. Some jobs want the tipped end to press a button, others want a hook to pull a handle or cable \u2014 pick the tip for the target.\n\nThen reverse cleanly: unlock or open the door, pull the rod straight out, deflate the wedge fully, and remove the corner wedge last. Deflate before you yank anything so you're not dragging tools past a pinched, stressed gap.\n\nAIRBAG CAUTION \u2014 this is the one that hurts people. Many vehicles run side-curtain and seat-side airbags with inflators and wiring in the A-pillar, roof rail, and along the door frame \u2014 exactly where your wedge and rod travel. Keep the wedge to the door corner, keep the rod path away from pillar trim and the headliner edge, and never wedge or pry directly over a pillar known to house a curtain airbag inflator. Know the vehicle before you go in.",proTips:["Gap with plastic first, air wedge second \u2014 metal in the seam is how you dent doors and tear weatherstrip. The air bladder does the real spreading.","Stop inflating the second the rod fits. A half-inch gap opens most cars; more just risks the glass and door alignment.","Wrap tape or slide a grommet where the rod rides the sheet metal \u2014 cheap insurance against scratched paint and a callback.","Light is half the job. A windshield or dash light beats fumbling blind; you unlock in seconds once you can actually see the button.","Map the airbags before you wedge \u2014 curtain/side inflators live in the A-pillar and roof rail right where your tools go. When in doubt, work the far corner from any pillar.","Deflate fully before pulling tools out; dragging a rod through a pressurized, pinched gap chews trim and bends the tool.","Frameless windows: crack the door itself, don't lever the glass \u2014 the channel is unsupported at the top and cracks easy."],sources:["https://www.accesstoolsusa.com/Long-Reach-Kits-s/1477.htm","https://steckmfg.com/products/classic-bigeasy-kit-with-easy-wedge-kit","https://miltonindustries.com/products/classic-bigeasy-kit-with-easy-wedge-kit","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/collections/new-dot-air-wedges","https://www.jbtools.com/access-tools-aw-air-wedge/","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/best-lockout-tools-for-automotive-technicians","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/what-are-best-locksmith-tools-for-automotive"],photo:"photo-tool-auto-lockout-kit",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-auto-lockout-kit",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"auto-lockout-kit",caption:"How it works \u2014 wedge the gap, reach the unlock button"}]},{id:"auto-eeprom-programmer",name:"EEPROM / Bench Programmer",category:"Automotive",whatItIs:"A bench tool that reads and writes the memory chips inside a vehicle's electronic modules directly, instead of talking to the car through the OBD port. You pull the BCM, immobilizer box, ECU, or instrument cluster out of the car, open it up, and connect the programmer straight to the EEPROM (the small serial memory chip, usually a Motorola/ST 93Cxx, 24Cxx, or 95xxx) or to the MCU. From that raw dump you extract the immobilizer PIN, the secret/security key, or the CS/component-security bytes needed to program a key when nothing else will read it. Common units are the Xhorse VVDI Prog, XPROG-M (X-Prog box), Iprog+ Pro, and Autel's XP400/XP400 Pro that pairs with the IM508/IM608 tablets. They ship with clip clamps, socket adapters, probe pins, and solder-on adapter shells, plus software with a database of high-res board diagrams telling you exactly which pads to hit.",useCases:["All-keys-lost on a car with no OBD path \u2014 read the immo box EEPROM on the bench to pull the PIN/secret key so you can make a key","Cloning or replacing a locked/dead BCM or immobilizer module by copying the EEPROM dump chip-to-chip","Reading BMW ISN (individual serial number) off DME/DDE engine ECUs (N20, N55, B-series) for CAS/FEM key work and ECU swaps","Extracting VIN-coded immo data from a faulty BCM to marry a replacement module","Pulling a PIN from PCF79xx / Megamos / EEPROM on VAG, Renault, Hyundai/Kia, Ford modules that lock out or need pre-code","Odometer, airbag crash-data reset, and dash EEPROM work on the same bench (Iprog+/XPROG cover these)"],howToUse:"Start by knowing which module holds the secret. For a given car you look up whether the PIN/key lives in the BCM, a standalone immo box, the cluster, or the engine ECU. Remove that module from the vehicle, open the housing, and identify the memory chip on the board \u2014 the programmer software's diagram library (VVDI Prog and Autel both have this) shows the exact chip and connection points for that part number, which saves you guessing.\n\nPick your connection method by how invasive you want to be. Cleanest is a clip/clamp (like Autel's APB103 clamp) that sits on the chip in-circuit \u2014 no desoldering, fast, but you sometimes fight the board pulling the lines up or down and get a bad read. If in-circuit won't read clean, you desolder the chip and drop it into a socket adapter (APA002-style) for a rock-solid read/write, or solder it onto an adapter shell (APB129, supports 6- and 8-pin). For MCU work and locked processors you're often soldering fine probe wires to specific legs per the diagram. Always confirm the chip is powered correctly and oriented pin-1 right before you read.\n\nRead the dump, then verify it. Read twice and compare \u2014 the software has a file-compare/verify function so you can confirm two reads match before you trust the data. A single read with one flipped bit gives you a wrong PIN and a wasted job. Save the original dump untouched as a backup before you write anything.\n\nFeed the dump to your calculator. The raw EEPROM goes into the immo/PIN calculator (built into VVDI2/Autel/Xhorse Key Tool or an online service) which decodes the PIN or secret key, or you edit the specific bytes for a virginize/clone. Then either program the key with that PIN through the normal tool, or write the modified/cloned dump back to the chip and reinstall the module.\n\nReassemble and test in the car. Solder the chip back if you removed it, reflow cleanly, reinstall the module, and verify the key works and no immo/warning lights are set before you close up.",proTips:["Read twice and diff the two dumps before you trust a single bit \u2014 in-circuit clip reads are the ones that lie; a one-bit error gives a wrong PIN","Save the virgin original dump to a named file before writing anything; that backup is your undo if the module bricks","In-circuit clamp first, desolder only if it won't read clean \u2014 you save time and avoid heat-damaging a good board","Match the exact chip part number to the software's board diagram; the same module year-to-year can move the chip or change the memory","Use a temperature-controlled iron and flux for 8-pin SOIC removal \u2014 lifted pads on a customer's BCM turn a read job into a replacement","XP400/XP400 Pro only works tethered to the Autel IM508/IM608 tablet; VVDI Prog, XPROG, and Iprog+ run standalone off a laptop","Don't overpay for a clone XPROG/Iprog \u2014 cheap ones read fine until a firmware mismatch corrupts a write; buy the unit whose software you actually get updates for"],sources:["https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/xhorse-vvdi-prog-eeprom-programmer","https://keyless-city.com/products/xhorse-vvdi-prog-eeprom-programmer","https://blog.obdii365.com/2024/09/09/3-methods-to-read-eeprom-chip-with-autel-im608-xp400/","https://www.autel.com/immotool1/3588.jhtml","https://www.obd2gate.com/product-technical/how-to-read-eeprom-data-using-autel-im608-ii-and-xp400-pro.html","https://ecutools.eu/additional-equipment/iprog-pro/","https://www.abkeys.com/abk-3732-xprog-m-box-x-prog-x-prog-original-set-with-adapters-ic-programmer-reader--mcu-ic-immobilizer-airbag-ecu"],photo:"photo-tool-auto-eeprom-programmer",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-auto-eeprom-programmer",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"auto-eeprom-programmer",caption:"How it works \u2014 clip the EEPROM, read/write the data"}]},{id:"bump-keys",name:"Bump Keys & Bump Hammer",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A bump key is a key blank for a given keyway cut to all-deep bitting... every cut driven to the maximum depth the code allows, which is why you'll hear them called \"999 keys\" (all-9s in a 9-depth system). Paired with a bump hammer... a stubby, deliberately flexible polymer mallet... it's the crudest fast-entry tool in the pin-tumbler world and the one every homeowner has seen on the news. The physics is pure Newton's cradle: you seat the deep-cut key one click shy of home, apply a whisper of turning tension, and rap the bow. The peaks of the key slam the bottom pins upward, the bottom pins transfer that momentum into the drivers the way the end ball of a Newton's cradle does, the drivers fly up off the shear line while the bottom pins... having nothing driving them... stay put. For a few milliseconds the whole pin stack is split at the shear line, and your standing tension lets the plug snap around before the springs slam everything home. It earns a slot in the box because it's the single most persuasive vulnerability demo you can put in a customer's hands... but as a working entry tool it's a distant third behind Lishi and picks, and honestly you'll reach for it more in the showroom than on the truck.",useCases:["Vulnerability assessments and upsell demos... open a customer's builder-grade Kwikset/Schlage in three raps to justify the sale of an anti-bump cylinder or a Grade 1 lock","Legit fast entry on a cheap, keyed-alike pin-tumbler you're about to replace anyway... faster than picking a mushy 5-pin when the cylinder is landfill-bound","Training and demonstrating shear-line mechanics to apprentices... the split is easy to feel and see, so it teaches how a pin stack actually works","Bench work on padlocks and desk/cam locks where a Lishi won't seat and the lock has no security pins","Explaining and selling countermeasures... showing why security pins, sidebars, and BumpStop cylinders defeat the attack"],howToUse:"Pick the right blank for the keyway first... bumping is keyway-specific, a KW1 999 does nothing to an SC1 plug. Cut it to max depth on every position (a Brockhage/HPC pre-cut set does this for you) and knock the shoulder back a touch, or file a small relief, so the key can sit \"one click out\" and still snap forward under the strike. Insert fully, pull back exactly one pin-spacing, lay on the lightest rotational tension you can manage with a fingertip on the bow... this is the whole game, too much tension and the drivers can't clear, too little and the plug won't catch the gap. Strike the bow with the flexible hammer while keeping that tension constant, and let the key walk forward into the pins on each rap. The flex in the hammer matters: a rigid hammer over-drives the pins and chatters, the polymer flex delivers a clean impulse and spares the cylinder... run the least-flexible head on stiff commercial springs, the most-flexible on light residential ones.\n\nThe failure mode, named honestly: timing. You are trying to catch a gap that exists for a few milliseconds, and the window closes the instant the springs reseat. Beginners fail on tension far more than on the strike... they death-grip the bow and wonder why nothing turns. Fix it by backing tension off to almost nothing and adding it back a hair at a time between raps. Deep-cut chambers are the other honest killer: if the real key has a couple of max-depth cuts, that bottom pin is already sitting across the shear line and bumping shoves it the wrong way... some combinations simply will not bump no matter how clean your technique.\n\nWhen it fails, stop hitting it. Repeated bumping mushrooms nothing but marks up the plug face and can chip a driver, and you'll have taught the pins to bind. Switch to a Lishi 2-in-1 (which reads and decodes so you leave with a code, not just an open door) or single-pin picking. And the moment you meet security pins, a sidebar, or a Bumpstop/BumpStop cylinder, put the hammer away entirely... that's not a technique problem, that's the lock doing its job, and it's your cue to write the quote for the upgrade.",proTips:["For a starter set, Brockhage's Basic 6 Bump Key Set covers the common residential keyways for about $20 at LockPicks.com; step up to the Brockhage 30-key Professional set (~$129) only once you're doing this on commercial calls and need the odd keyways","Match hammer flex to the lock: Peterson sells the bump hammer in three durometers (least-flexible blue, medium black, most-flexible grey) around $11 each... blue for stiff commercial springs, grey for light residential, so keep at least two on the truck","The Peterson Bump Hammer Weight Kit (~$9.50) lets you tune impulse mass instead of swinging harder... more consistent strikes and far less chatter than muscling a heavier hit","Sell against the attack, not just with it: after the demo, quote a Master Lock BumpStop cylinder (reverse-rake tapered driver + high-performance spring, tested past Grade 6 bump criteria) or a sidebar lock like a BiLock/Medeco... a sidebar or side-pin adds a second action a 999 key can't satisfy","Wrap a small O-ring or a couple of rubber bands around the key's shoulder as a bumper... it springs the key back out after each strike so you're not manually re-seating between raps, which steadies your tension hand","Reach for a Lishi 2-in-1 before the hammer on a real job... it decodes as it opens so you leave with a cut key, it's non-destructive on the plug face, and it works on plenty of locks that shrug off bumping"],sources:["https://www.lockpicks.com/collections/bumping-keys-tools-1","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230150/key-bumping-makes-the-news","https://www.masterlock.com/cms/keying/security-enhancements","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_bumping","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/what-is-key-bumping/","https://securitysnobs.com/LA-BiLock-Bump-Proof-High-Security-Deadbolt_Z4.html"],photo:"photo-tool-bump-keys",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-bump-keys",caption:"The tool"},{asset:"diagram-bump-keys",caption:"How it works"}]},{id:"padlock-shims",name:"Padlock Shims",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A padlock shim is a thin U-shaped or L-bent sliver of spring steel, usually cut from the same 0.1 mm stock you'd find in a feeler gauge or a soda can, sized so the crook rides down the shackle and the thin blade slips into the hairline gap between the shackle leg and the lock body. It earns its slot because it isn't a picking tool at all... it's a bypass. You're not manipulating the cylinder, reading pins, or defeating the keyway. You're going around the whole security core and attacking the mechanical latch that actually holds the shackle down. On the right lock it opens in five seconds with zero skill, no key, no marks, and the lock relocks perfectly afterward like nothing happened. That last part is exactly why it matters to you as a locksmith: a shimmed lock leaves no evidence, so when a customer swears their storage unit was \"locked the whole time,\" a shimmable padlock is your first suspect. A set is nothing fancy... twenty pre-formed shims in four or five widths so you can match the shackle diameter, because a shim that's too wide binds and one too narrow folds.",useCases:["Non-destructive entry on a cheap single-locking padlock when the customer lost the key and the lock isn't worth drilling","Opening a Master combination padlock (the 1500-style dial lock) fast when someone forgot the code","Demonstrating to a commercial account, on their own gate or locker locks, why their bargain hardware is worthless... the best upsell close you have","Clearing seized/import padlocks off a job site where nobody has keys and you don't want metal shavings everywhere","Field-testing a customer's lock to confirm whether it's shim-vulnerable before you quote a security upgrade"],howToUse:"The mechanism you're beating is a spring-loaded latch or pawl. On a basic single-locking padlock, only the long (heel) leg of the shackle is actually held... a spring pushes a latch into a notch on that leg, and the short (toe) leg just floats free in its bore. When you turn the key, the cam retracts that latch and the shackle pops. A shim does the cam's job. You seat the U of the shim over the shackle so the blade points down the gap on the latching side, then push it down while rocking the shackle slightly inward (press the shackle down into the body to unload the latch, take pressure off it). The blade wedges between the latch face and the shackle notch, riding the ramp until the latch is held clear. Rotate the shim a few degrees toward the body and pull the shackle... it lifts. If you can't tell which side latches, do the heel side first; that's where the spring latch almost always lives.\n\nDouble-locking is where it gets honest. A double-locking padlock latches BOTH legs, so a single shim frees one leg and the shackle still won't rise... it just cants over on the leg you didn't shim. That's the two-shim need: you seat one shim per leg, hold the first in place while you thread the second, and only then does the shackle come free. It's fiddly because you're managing two thin pieces of steel in tight tolerances with no third hand, and the first shim loves to pop out while you fight the second. A binder clip or a rubber band to hold shim one under light tension saves your sanity.\n\nNow the failure mode, named straight: shims do not beat a real ball-bearing (deadlocking) mechanism, and you will waste a customer's afternoon pretending otherwise. In a ball-bearing lock the shackle notches are round detents and a hardened ball sits in each one, backed by an actuator that only moves when the cylinder turns. The ball is deadlocked... there's no ramp for your blade to climb and nothing a wedge can push back into the body, because the actuator physically blocks it. No amount of shim, spin, or force gets there. Same story when the tolerance is simply too tight: high-security bodies leave no gap at the shackle, so the blade can't even enter. When the shim won't seat or won't climb, stop... you've either hit ball bearings or zero clearance, and the answer is picking, a bypass driver, or the drill, not a thicker shim. Identify the lock before you commit: if the maker advertises \"dual ball bearing\" or \"anti-shim,\" put the shim back in the roll.",proTips:["Buy a formed set, don't cut cans on the job. Sparrows Padlock Shims run about $19.95 for twenty shims in four widths (approximate), and SouthOrd's 20-piece set is around $26.99 (approximate)... both are pre-crooked spring steel so you're matching shackle diameter instead of hoping a Coke-can strip holds its bend.","Match the shim width to the shackle, not the lock size. Too wide binds in the gap and won't seat; too narrow folds the instant it hits the latch ramp. Carry the multi-width set for exactly this reason.","On double-locking bodies, hold the first shim under tension with a small binder clip or rubber band while you thread the second... freeing both hands is the whole trick, since the first shim popping out is the number-one reason people call double-locks 'unshimmable' when they aren't.","Push the shackle DOWN into the body as you seat the blade. Unloading the spring latch takes the friction off the ramp so the shim climbs; fighting a spring-loaded latch is why beginners bend tools.","Know your upsell targets by name. Cheap laminated single-locking padlocks and the Master 1500 combo dial shim wide open; steer the customer to a dual-ball-bearing lock like an ABUS Granit or a Master Lock Magnum/ProSeries with the boron-carbide shackle and dual ball bearings... that's the honest close, 'your current lock opens with a strip of steel, this one doesn't.'","Keep two or three shims of each size in rotation... they take a set (permanent bend) after enough climbs and a tired shim folds instead of wedging. They're consumables, not heirlooms."],sources:["https://www.southord.com/products/padlock-shim-set-sps-20","https://www.sparrowslockpicks.com/products/padlock-shims","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/southord-padlock-shims","https://www.lockpickworld.com/pages/padlocks-explained","https://covertinstruments.com/products/padlock-shims","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/assa-kik-padlock-double-ball-bearing-locking-mechanism-1-shackle","Master Lock PK15KADLF padlock multi-pack listing, store.masterlock.com \u2014 original link retired.","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10229664/new-padlock-choices-from-pacific-lock"],photo:"photo-tool-padlock-shims",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-padlock-shims",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"padlock-shims",caption:"How it works \u2014 the shim cams the latch off the shackle"}]},{id:"drill-doctor",name:"Drill Doctor Bit Sharpener",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"The Drill Doctor is a bench-top machine that resharpens dull twist bits \u2014 you clamp the bit in a chuck, index it against a diamond wheel, and it grinds a fresh point and relief angle in about a minute. For a locksmith it pays for itself fast because destructive entry eats bits: punching pilot holes through hardened pins and drilling out cylinders dulls or burns the tip on every job. Instead of throwing a $6 cobalt bit after two drill-outs, you touch it up and keep going. The workhorse model is the Drill Doctor 750X, which handles 3/32\" up to 3/4\" bits and split-points, and does standard 118\xb0 and split 135\xb0 tips.",useCases:["Resharpening cobalt and HSS bits after drilling out cylinders \u2014 the tips glaze and go blunt fast on hardened lock pins","Restoring a split point on a 1/8\" pilot bit so it bites metal without walking across the plug face","Bringing a truck full of odd loose bits back into service instead of buying new sets","Dressing a bit's angle for a specific job \u2014 118\xb0 for general steel, 135\xb0 split for hard, stainless-heavy commercial hardware","Any shop that drills daily: strike-plate pilots, install prep, and destructive entry all consume bits"],howToUse:"The trick is the alignment fixture, not the grinding. You loosen the chuck, drop the bit in, and set it in the port-side alignment jaws that rotate the bit until both cutting lips are indexed the same \u2014 this is what makes both flutes come off the wheel identical. Snug the chuck on the indexed bit, move it to the sharpening port, and rotate the chuck back and forth against the diamond wheel through its detents (usually a half-dozen wipes per side, flip 180\xb0, repeat). Pull it, eyeball the point \u2014 the two lips should be even and the tip centered. A lopsided grind means one lip does all the cutting and the hole drills oversized and off-center.\n\nFor a split point (recommended on anything 1/8\" and up you use for pilots), you move to the third splitting port after sharpening and lightly touch each flute to thin the web at the tip. That's what kills the walk \u2014 a split-point bit self-centers and starts cutting the instant it touches, which matters when you're drilling a shear line freehand. Don't overheat: light pressure, let the diamond do it, and dunk the bit if it goes blue or you've drawn the temper right out of it.",proTips:["The 750X is the model to own \u2014 it does the full 3/32\"\u20133/4\" range and split points. The cheaper 350/500 models cap out smaller and skip features you'll want.","The diamond wheel is a wear part. Keep a spare on the shelf; a glazed wheel just polishes the bit instead of cutting a new edge and you'll swear the machine is broken.","It sharpens standard twist bits only \u2014 not masonry, spade, step, or carbide-tipped bits. Don't force those in.","A freshly split-pointed pilot bit is the single biggest upgrade to freehand drill-outs. Sharpen your 1/8\" and 5/32\" pilots split and they stop wandering."],sources:["https://www.drilldoctor.com/","Drill Doctor 750X bit sharpener retail listing (homedepot.com) \u2014 original link retired.","Drill Doctor DD750X bit sharpener retail listing (amazon.com) \u2014 original link retired."],photos:[{diagram:"drill-doctor-portrait",caption:"The tool (illustration)"},{diagram:"drill-doctor",caption:"How it works \u2014 resharpen the cutting lips to the point angle"}]},{id:"drill-bits",name:"Drill Bit Assortment (Cobalt, Carbide, Titanium)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"There's no single bit for lock work \u2014 you carry a small assortment matched to what you're cutting. Titanium-coated HSS is the cheap general-purpose bit for wood and soft metal. Cobalt (M35/M42) is the real destructive-entry bit: the cobalt is alloyed through the whole bit so it keeps an edge in hardened lock pins and stainless where a coated bit just burns. Carbide-tipped or solid carbide is the escalation for anti-drill defenses \u2014 hardened pins, ball bearings, and drill plates in high-security cylinders \u2014 because carbide is harder than the hardened steel it's fighting. Knowing which to reach for is the difference between a two-minute drill-out and a smoking, ruined bit.",useCases:["Drilling out a standard pin-tumbler cylinder at the shear line \u2014 1/8\" cobalt pilot, then open up to break the pin stacks","Defeating anti-drill pins and hardened inserts in high-security locks \u2014 carbide-tipped where cobalt stalls","Pilot holes for strike plates, closers, and hinges in wood and light metal \u2014 titanium HSS is plenty","Step bits for cleanly enlarging or deburring holes in thin metal doors and mounting brackets","Masonry bits for the odd anchor into a block or brick jamb when you don't need the full rotary hammer"],howToUse:"Match the bit to the target and the target dictates speed and pressure. Hardened steel (lock pins, drill plates) wants a slow drill speed and firm, steady pressure \u2014 cobalt and carbide cut by shearing, and high RPM just generates heat that cooks the edge. A drop of cutting oil on metal roughly doubles bit life and keeps the tip from bluing. Soft wood and drywall want the opposite: higher speed, let the flutes clear the chips.\n\nFor a drill-out you start with a small split-point pilot (1/8\"\u20135/32\") placed just above the keyway on the pin line \u2014 that's where the drivers and springs live \u2014 and drill through the pin stacks to destroy the shear line. Then you either step up in diameter to free the plug or move to a pick after the pins are gone. Let the bit do the work: leaning on a dull or wrong bit just work-hardens the hole and makes the next bit's job worse. When you hit something that won't cut and the bit's sharp, that's a hardened anti-drill element \u2014 stop, switch to carbide, back off the speed.",proTips:["Cobalt grades matter: M35 is the affordable daily bit, M42 has more cobalt and survives the hard stuff better. For pins and stainless, spend on M42.","Buy pilot bits in multiples. You'll snap 1/8\" bits \u2014 they're thin and the destructive-entry point is exactly where they bind. A dozen on the truck is cheap insurance.","Solid carbide is brittle. It out-cuts everything on hardness but shatters if you cock it sideways or let it grab \u2014 keep it square and steady.","Titanium and 'black oxide' bits are coatings, not through-alloy. Once the coating wears at the tip they're just plain HSS, so don't trust them on hardened targets."],sources:["https://www.ttp-hard-drills.net/cobalt-drill-bit-faq/","https://www.hk-tools.com/news/m35-and-m42-cobalt-drill-bits-differences-and-usage/","https://surelockkey.com/blog/how-to-drill-out-a-cylinder-lock-safe-pro-methods/","https://www.irwintools.com/","Product photo: Glenn McKechnie (Wikimedia Commons, DrillsMorseTaperShank1234.jpg), CC BY-SA 3.0"],photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-drill-bits",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"drill-bits",caption:"How it works \u2014 match the bit to the material"}],photo:"photo-tool-drill-bits"},{id:"rotary-hammer",name:"Rotary Hammer (SDS)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A rotary hammer is a dedicated masonry drill that hammers as it spins, driving an SDS-plus bit through concrete, block, and brick that a cordless hammer-drill just polishes. It's not something you use every day, but the day you're mounting a commercial strike, a door closer, an electric strike, or a gate operator into a concrete or block jamb, nothing else does it cleanly. The SDS chuck also takes chisel bits, so it doubles as a light demolition tool for busting out old anchors and mortar. The Bosch Bulldog is the shop standard; any corded SDS-plus in that class works.",useCases:["Anchoring commercial strike plates, panic hardware, and door closers into concrete or block jambs","Setting anchors for gate operators, bollards, and access-control mounts in masonry","Drilling clean pilot holes for concrete screws (Tapcons) and sleeve/wedge anchors","Light chiseling with an SDS chisel bit \u2014 knocking out old anchors, mortar, or a stuck lag shield","Any install where a cordless hammer-drill overheats and glazes without making progress in concrete"],howToUse:"Pick the anchor first, because the anchor's spec dictates the bit diameter and hole depth \u2014 a 1/4\" Tapcon wants a 3/16\" bit, wedge anchors match their bolt size. Drop the SDS bit in the chuck (it clicks and self-locks \u2014 no chuck key, and it's meant to slide back and forth, that's the hammer action), set the mode to rotary-hammer, and let the tool's weight do the pushing. You don't lean on a rotary hammer; the impact mechanism does the work and shoving just wears you and the bit out. Pull the bit part way out a couple times to clear dust so it keeps biting.\n\nDrill about 1/4\" to 1/2\" deeper than the anchor's embedment so debris at the bottom doesn't stop the anchor short, then blow or vacuum the hole clean \u2014 a dust-packed hole halves an anchor's holding value. For overhead or repeated work, corded is the honest choice; battery SDS exists but a corded Bulldog won't die mid-hole. Wear eye protection and a mask \u2014 masonry dust is silica.",proTips:["You do not need this for wood or metal doors \u2014 a hammer-drill/driver covers 95% of install work. Buy the SDS only when concrete and block are a regular part of your jobs.","SDS-plus is the right class for locksmith work; SDS-max is heavy demolition overkill. Stay with plus.","Keep a few common Tapcon/anchor bit sizes (3/16\", 1/4\") dedicated to this tool \u2014 masonry bits dull and you don't want to hunt for the right one on a job.","Blow the hole out before you set the anchor. It's the single most-skipped step and the reason strikes work loose."],sources:["Rotary hammers 11255vsr 134586 p (boschtools.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","Best rotary hammer drill (protoolreviews.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","Rotary hammer drill category listing (homedepot.com) \u2014 original link retired.","Product photo: Dmitry G (Wikimedia Commons, Milwaukee_PLH28XE.JPG), public domain"],photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-rotary-hammer",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"rotary-hammer",caption:"How it works \u2014 the SDS shank lets it hammer as it spins"}],photo:"photo-tool-rotary-hammer"},{id:"spade-bits",name:"Spade & Auger Bits",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"Spade bits (flat paddle bits) and auger bits are the wood-boring tools for door and frame prep. Where a hole saw cuts the big 2-1/8\" cross-bore for the lock body, you still need a bit to run the 1\" edge-bore for the latch, to drill through-bolt and harness holes, and to route wiring for electric strikes and access control. Spade bits are cheap, fast, and rough \u2014 great for holes you won't see. Auger bits have a screw tip that pulls the bit through and clears chips up the flutes, so they drill deeper, straighter, and cleaner, which matters when you're boring a long lead hole or need the exit side not to blow out.",useCases:["Boring the 1\" edge-bore for a latch or deadbolt bolt where the cross-bore meets the door edge","Running wiring channels through a door or frame for electric strikes, mag locks, and access control","Through-holes for carriage/through-bolts on exit devices and heavy commercial trim","Fast rough holes in a wood jamb or blocking where finish doesn't matter (spade)","Deep, straight lead holes and clean exit holes where blow-out would show (auger)"],howToUse:"For an edge-bore, mark center on the door edge and keep the bit dead square to the edge \u2014 a spade bit that wanders here means the latch won't line up with the cross-bore and the lock binds. Spade bits cut aggressively and want speed: run the drill fast and feed steadily, but ease off as the tip breaks through the far side or it'll splinter the exit. The classic trick to avoid blow-out is to drill until just the spade's center spur pokes through, then finish the hole from the other side.\n\nAugers are the opposite feel \u2014 the screw tip self-feeds, so you run a lower speed and let it pull itself in; forcing it just clogs the flutes. Back it out periodically on deep holes to clear chips. For running wire, an extra-long auger or a bell-hanger bit lets you go through a stud bay or the full door height in one pass, and some have a hole in the tip to hook and pull the wire back through. Match the bit to the finish you need: spade where it's hidden, auger where it shows or has to be straight.",proTips:["A 1\" bit is the standard latch edge-bore \u2014 carry that size for certain. Most residential lock instructions call for exactly 1\".","Spade bits with spurs on the outer edges (not just a flat paddle) score the rim first and leave a much cleaner hole \u2014 worth the extra dollar.","For electric-strike and access-control wire runs, a 12\"\u201318\" bell-hanger/installer auger is the tool that turns a two-hour fish into a five-minute pull.","Keep spade bits sharp with a few file strokes on the cutting edges \u2014 they're flat and take an edge back in seconds, unlike an auger."],sources:["https://www.irwintools.com/","https://www.allaboutdoors.com/Article-Guide-to-Door-Lock-Measurements","https://www.homedepot.com/b/Tools-Power-Tool-Accessories-Drill-Bits-Spade-Bits/N-5yc1vZc264","Product photo: Luigi Zanasi (Wikimedia Commons, Spade_bits.JPG), CC BY-SA 2.0 Canada"],photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-spade-bits",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"spade-bits",caption:"How it works \u2014 spade for fast holes, auger for clean deep ones"}],photo:"photo-tool-spade-bits"},{id:"rotary-tool",name:"Rotary Tool & Cutting Bits (Dremel)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A rotary tool \u2014 the Dremel is the household name \u2014 is a high-RPM handpiece that takes interchangeable cutting, grinding, and sanding bits for the small, precise cut a full-size tool is too clumsy for. For locksmiths it's the do-anything finisher: a cut-off wheel to slice a seized shackle or a stubborn bolt, a carbide cutter to notch a strike pocket or open up a mortise, grinding stones to dress a burr off a drilled hole or reshape a pick, and RotoZip-style cut-out bits for opening a hole in a door skin or drywall. Corded models hold RPM under load; cordless (Dremel 8250-class) trades a little grunt for getting into a doorway without a cord.",useCases:["Cutting a seized padlock shackle or a rusted bolt with a reinforced cut-off wheel when you can't pick or bump it","Notching and enlarging a strike pocket or mortise so new hardware seats \u2014 carbide cutter, freehand","Deburring and cleaning up a drilled cylinder or a rough edge-bore so the plug or latch runs smooth","Grinding and reshaping picks, tension wrenches, and key blanks \u2014 thinning a tension tool or relieving a blank","Cutting an opening in a hollow-core door or drywall for an electric strike or wiring \u2014 RotoZip-style bit"],howToUse:"The whole tool is about speed and light touch, not force. Cut-off wheels do the work at high RPM with the wheel just kissing the metal \u2014 you guide it and let the abrasive cut, and the instant you lean in and the wheel flexes, it shatters. Always use the reinforced (fiber-mesh) cut-off wheels for metal; the plain gray discs are fine for a light score but explode under side load. Cut on the waste side of your line and keep the wheel square to the cut.\n\nFor grinding and shaping \u2014 a pick, a burr, a strike pocket \u2014 pick the right bit (carbide cutters for metal removal, aluminum-oxide or diamond points for hardened steel and finishing) and use the edge or side with steady, sweeping passes so you don't dig a gouge or overheat one spot. Heat is the enemy on tempered tools: grind in short passes and cool the part, or you'll draw the temper out of a pick and it'll take a set. Eye protection is non-negotiable \u2014 these throw sparks and the occasional wheel fragment. Match RPM to the bit's rating; running a big sanding drum at full speed just flings it apart.",proTips:["Stock reinforced cut-off wheels by the box \u2014 you go through them, and reaching for the wrong plain disc is how you catch a fragment.","Corded holds RPM under real load; the cordless is worth it purely for reaching a door without dragging a cord and extension across the threshold.","A diamond or aluminum-oxide point cuts hardened steel that eats carbide burrs \u2014 keep both, and use the harder one on drill-plate and hardened-pin debris.","Great for modifying your own tools: thinning a tension wrench, relieving a pick, trimming a key blank. Go slow and cool so you don't ruin the temper."],sources:["https://www.dremel.com/en_US/products/-/show-product/tools/8250-cordless-rotary-tool","https://www.dremel.com/en_US/rotary-accessory-guide","Best dremel rotary tool (protoolreviews.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","Product photo: VGPaleontologist (Wikimedia Commons, Dremel_MultiPro_Model_285.jpg), CC BY-SA 4.0"],photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-rotary-tool",caption:"The tool"},{diagram:"rotary-tool",caption:"How it works \u2014 a cut-off wheel spun at high RPM"}],photo:"photo-tool-rotary-tool"},{id:"hinge-doctor",name:"Hinge Doctor (Sagging-Door Hinge Reformer)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A shaped steel lever that repairs a sagging, dropped, or binding door by re-forming its worn hinges WITHOUT taking the door off or replacing the hinges. Over years a light pressed-steel butt hinge splays open at the knuckle, the door drops on its latch side, and now it catches on the frame, won't latch, or drags the floor. The Hinge Doctor clamps over the hinge knuckle and you use the door itself as the lever to bend the hinge back to square, lifting the latch edge back up to meet the strike. It is a residential/light-duty fix \u2014 pressed-steel butt hinges, the kind on interior house doors (very common in the UK, where the tool comes from). It is NOT for heavy cast or solid-brass hinges, ball-bearing hinges, or commercial door weights; those get shimmed, swapped, or the frame reset.",useCases:["Interior door that dropped and now catches the frame or won't latch \u2014 reform the hinge instead of rehanging the door","A door binding at the top corner (latch side high) or dragging the carpet (latch side low) from splayed hinges","Quick callback fix where the customer doesn't want new hinges cut in or the door taken down","Tuning a door back to an even reveal before you fit or adjust a lockset and strike"],howToUse:"First confirm the sag is actually the hinge, not loose screws or a dropped frame. Open the door and lift the latch edge: if there's play and the hinge knuckles have splayed open, that's the Hinge Doctor's job. If the screws are just loose in stripped wood, fix that first (longer screws or plugged holes) \u2014 the tool won't cure a hinge that isn't fastened.\n\nPick the size that matches the hinge, open the door, and clamp the tool over the knuckle of the hinge you're correcting. Which hinge you work sets which way the door moves: reforming the top hinge lifts or drops the latch side one way, the bottom hinge the other, so read the reveal first and correct the hinge that moves the door back toward square. Then close the door slowly and firmly \u2014 the door is the lever arm and the tool concentrates that force on the knuckle, bending the hinge back. Go in small increments: close, check the reveal and the latch against the strike, and repeat rather than over-bending in one go. Pressed steel reforms cleanly; push too far and you can crease a thin hinge, so sneak up on it.\n\nWhen the reveal is even and the latch lines up with the strike, you're done. If the door still won't come to square after a couple of passes, the hinge is too far gone or too heavy for reforming \u2014 replace it.",proTips:["Diagnose before you bend: loose screws and a racked frame mimic a sagging hinge. Tighten and check the frame square first, or you'll 'fix' the wrong thing.","It's for thin PRESSED-STEEL butt hinges. On solid brass, cast, or ball-bearing hinges it won't reform them and you risk cracking the tool or the hinge \u2014 those doors get shimmed or new hinges.","Work in small steps and recheck the latch-to-strike each pass. Over-bending creases the hinge and you've traded a sag for a crease.","Mostly a residential tool. Commercial openings run heavy hinges and heavier doors \u2014 reach for hinge shims, a hinge-side or strike adjustment, or replacement instead."],sources:["https://www.hingedoctor.com/","How to fix a sagging door (thefamilyhandyman.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published."],photos:[{diagram:"hinge-doctor",caption:"How it works \u2014 clamp the hinge, close the door to reform it, latch lifts back to the strike"}]},{id:"linemans-butt-set",name:"Lineman's butt set",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A lineman's handset \u2014 the red one with the piercing clips \u2014 is a telephone in a fist-sized package, and on access-control work it is how you prove whether a fault is the panel or the line. Clip onto a pair and you can listen for dial tone, watch the polarity LED, place a call, or sit silently on the pair and monitor. On every POTS-era telephone entry in this catalog \u2014 DoorKing, SES, Linear \u2014 the single most useful diagnostic question is 'is there dial tone at the demarc?', and this is the tool that answers it in thirty seconds instead of an hour.",useCases:["Proving dial tone at the demarc before you condemn a telephone entry panel","Confirming a panel actually seizes the line and dials when a visitor presses call","Monitoring a pair silently while somebody else operates the panel, without loading the line","Checking line polarity, which some entry equipment cares about","Listening for noise, hum or crosstalk on a pair that is causing bad intercom audio","Establishing whether a 'dead' entry system is a fibre or VoIP cutover rather than a hardware failure","Verifying dial tone at a jack before and after relocating a modem or other line-powered equipment","Mapping which jack lands on which pair when you are working an unlabelled block"],howToUse:"Clip on with the piercing clips or the alligators \u2014 the piercing clips get through insulation without stripping, which is what you want on a working pair. Know what you are landing on: a POTS pair is tip and ring, and the convention is red to ring, black or green to tip. Watch the polarity LED as you land: reversed polarity is a real finding on some equipment and a non-issue on others, but you want to know which you have.\n\nThe switch that matters is monitor versus talk. MONITOR is high impedance: it lets you listen without loading the pair or announcing your presence, and it is what you use on a live line you do not own. TALK is low impedance and puts the set on-hook/off-hook like a telephone \u2014 it seizes the line. Use talk when you need to draw dial tone or place a test call, and be deliberate about it, because seizing a line mid-call drops whoever was on it.\n\nOn a telephone entry fault the sequence is: monitor first to hear what is happening, then talk to draw dial tone yourself. Dial tone present at the demarc with the panel dead means the panel; no dial tone means the line, and you are done arguing with the customer about whose problem it is.\n\nMoving a line is the same tool, different order. Verify dial tone at the source jack first so you know what you started with, trace the pair to where it is going \u2014 tone it with [[tool:greenlee-tone-and-probe-kit|the tone generator and probe]] if the run is unlabelled \u2014 land it on the new jack, then draw dial tone again before you plug the equipment back in. That last check is the one people skip, and it is the one that tells you whether the fault you are about to chase is yours. The same sequence answers 'which jack is this pair?' when you are mapping an unlabelled block.\n\nYou do not strictly need a butt set for the dial-tone half of this. Any analog handset on the truck will draw dial tone off an RJ11 and dial a test number, which is enough to prove a line is live in both directions. What the handset cannot do is monitor without seizing, so it is a stopgap for verification, not a replacement for the set.",proTips:["Never put a butt set in TALK across a pair carrying data \u2014 DSL, alarm or RS-485. Monitor is safe, talk can disrupt the circuit","Piercing clips beat stripping on a working pair, but they leave a hole in the insulation; use them on the demarc side, not on a run you are leaving in service","Dial tone at the demarc but not at the panel narrows the fault to the run between them, which is usually the cheapest possible answer to give a customer","A panel that has 'stopped dialling' after a service change is very often a fibre or VoIP cutover. Prove dial tone before you open the enclosure","Monitor mode on a noisy pair will tell you about hum and crosstalk that the panel's own speaker masks","No dial tone right after a move is usually a swapped tip and ring or the wrong pair punched down \u2014 re-check the pair before you suspect the equipment"],sources:["Field practice, A&D access control crew 2026 \u2014 line verification and relocation sequence from a telecom-engineer lead; no single public doc."],verified:!1,photo:"photo-tool-linemans-butt-set",photos:[{asset:"photo-test-phone-clarity",caption:"The stopgap version of this tool: an amplified analog handset on the truck. It draws dial tone off an RJ11 and dials a test number, which proves the line both ways... but it cannot monitor without seizing, so it verifies, it does not replace the set."}]},{id:"caig-deoxit-d5",name:"DeoxIT D5 contact cleaner",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"DeoxIT D5 is a contact cleaner and conditioner in a 5 oz aerosol \u2014 the D-series formulation, which removes oxidation from metal contacts and leaves a thin protective film behind. On access-control work it earns its place on connections that have gone intermittent rather than dead: reader terminals, screw blocks in an outdoor enclosure, edge connectors, relay contacts and ribbon headers that have sat in a damp gate vault for a decade. It is safe on most plastics, which matters when you are spraying near a moulded connector body, and it is flammable, which matters more.",useCases:["Intermittent reader or keypad connections where the terminal screws show green or white corrosion","Screw terminal blocks in outdoor gate and entry enclosures that have been through years of condensation","Ribbon cable headers and edge connectors that work when you wiggle them","Relay contacts that have started chattering or passing intermittently","Cleaning a connector before reassembly rather than after a callback"],howToUse:"Kill power first. This is an aerosol solvent with a flammable propellant and you are spraying it at electrical contacts \u2014 energised terminals are how people start fires and destroy boards.\n\nSpray sparingly onto the contact, not into the enclosure generally. The point is to wet the mating surfaces, then work the connection several times \u2014 unplug and replug a connector, or back the screw off and retighten it \u2014 so the cleaner actually abrades the oxide layer mechanically as well as chemically. Wipe the excess. A connector left swimming in cleaner attracts dust and can wick into places you did not intend.\n\nIt leaves a conditioning film deliberately; that is a feature, not residue to be removed. Let it flash off before restoring power.",proTips:["Power off before spraying. Flammable propellant plus live terminals is the one way this tool bites you","Work the connection after spraying \u2014 unplug and replug, or loosen and retighten. The mechanical action does as much as the chemistry","Use it on intermittent faults, not dead ones. A connection that is properly open is a broken wire or a failed component, and cleaner will not bridge it","Spray the contact, not the cabinet. Excess wicks and collects dust","It is not a lubricant for lock cylinders. Contact cleaner in a keyway attracts grit \u2014 use the correct dry lubricant there"],sources:[],verified:!0,photo:"photo-tool-caig-deoxit-d5"},{id:"assorted-screwdrivers",name:"Assorted screwdrivers",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"The unglamorous set that opens everything. Locksmith and access-control work is disproportionately Phillips #2 and a handful of flat sizes, but the ones that actually decide whether a job goes well are the odd ones: a long thin flat for reaching a terminal block at the back of an enclosure, a stubby for a closer bracket with no clearance, and a genuinely correct-fitting tip for the soft screws that hold faceplates and strike plates.",useCases:["Faceplate, strike and latch screws on every lockset install","Terminal block screws in access-control enclosures, where a long thin flat reaches what a normal driver cannot","Closer and operator brackets in tight clearance, where a stubby is the only thing that fits","Backing out captive screws in an enclosure door without dropping them into the works","Adjusting board trimmers, where a small flat that fits the slot exactly avoids destroying the pot"],howToUse:"Fit the tip to the screw properly and the rest looks after itself. Most stripped screws on lock hardware are cam-out from a driver one size too small in a soft brass or plated screw \u2014 a Phillips #1 in a #2 head feels like it engages and then chews the recess out. If a driver rocks in the head, it is the wrong one.\n\nFor terminal blocks use a flat that matches the slot width, not the biggest one that fits. Terminal screws are soft and shallow, and a wide blade riding on the edges rounds them out; once a terminal screw is rounded, you are drilling it or replacing the block.\n\nInsulated drivers are worth having for anything near a live supply, and worth the discipline of actually reaching for them.",proTips:["Most stripped hardware screws are cam-out from an undersized Phillips. If the driver rocks in the head, stop and get the right size","A long thin flat is the single most useful driver for access-control enclosures \u2014 terminal blocks are always at the back","Match the blade to the terminal slot width. Terminal screws are soft, shallow and unforgiving","A magnetised tip stops you fishing faceplate screws out of a door cavity, but demagnetise before working near a reader's board","Keep a stubby for closer and operator brackets. It is dead weight until the day nothing else fits"],sources:[],verified:!1},{id:"conduit-bender",name:"Conduit Hand Bender (and the hickey)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"The shoe-and-handle tool you stand on to put 90s, offsets, saddles and kicks into EMT. It is sized to the pipe \u2014 a 1/2\" bender bends 1/2\" EMT and nothing else \u2014 and the numbers you need are stamped right into the head along with the size. Once you are running your own conduit for card readers, cameras and door cordage instead of subbing it out, this is the tool that separates a job that looks installed from one that looks improvised.\n\nA word on the name, because it causes real confusion at the counter: tradespeople call any bender a 'hickey', but strictly a hickey is a different tool \u2014 a compact head that bends RIGID conduit in small increments, walking the pipe through a series of short pulls to build a curve. The one-shot shoe bender you use on EMT is a hand bender. Ask for a hickey at a supply house and you may well get handed the rigid segment bender.",useCases:["Stubbing a 90 up a wall to a card reader, keypad or camera box","Offsetting off a wall to meet a surface-mounted enclosure or a strike raceway","Saddling over an obstruction \u2014 a pipe, a beam, another run \u2014 without breaking the line","Kicking a run out of a box at an angle that is not 90 degrees","Back-to-back bends for a run that has to cross a space and come back down","Rolling offsets where the obstruction is off-axis and the bend has to twist as well as rise"],howToUse:"The head carries three marks and knowing which one to use is most of the skill. The ARROW is for a stub-up: subtract the take-up from your desired stub height, mark the pipe, put the arrow on the mark and pull to 90. The take-up figures are 5\" for 1/2\" EMT, 6\" for 3/4\", 8\" for 1\" and 11\" for 1-1/4\" \u2014 the distance the bend eats between your mark and where the pipe actually stands up. The NOTCH (the little dimple) is the centre mark, used for the centre of an offset or a saddle. The STAR is for back-to-back bends.\n\nFoot on the pedal, steady pressure straight down, and let the pipe come to the degree marks or the level cast into most heads. Do not jerk it \u2014 a fast pull kinks the pipe and a kinked EMT will not pull wire. Keep the pipe flat on the floor through the bend or you will roll the bend out of plane, and check that each bend is square to the last before you cut. The Field Manual guide 'Bending conduit: 90s, offsets, saddles, back-to-back & the math' carries the full geometry and the shrink figures.",proTips:["Nine botched 90s out of ten are not a bad bend, they are a tech who forgot to deduct the take-up. Mark, subtract, then bend","Buy the bender for the pipe you actually run. A 1/2\" EMT bender is physically bigger than people expect and will not help you on 3/4\"","A hickey is the rigid segment bender, not the EMT hand bender. Say 'EMT hand bender, half inch' at the counter and you will get the right tool","Bend a scrap piece first when you are working an unfamiliar size \u2014 the take-up is stamped on the head but confirming it on scrap costs one offcut and saves a stick","Pull slow and keep the pipe flat. Speed kinks the wall and a kink means you pull that stick out and start again"],photos:[{diagram:"conduit-90",caption:"90\xb0 stub-up \u2014 mark = stub height minus take-up, arrow on the mark"},{diagram:"conduit-offset",caption:"Offset \u2014 two equal bends to step the run off a surface"},{diagram:"conduit-saddle",caption:"Three-bend saddle over an obstruction"},{diagram:"conduit-4bend-saddle",caption:"Four-bend saddle for a wider obstruction"},{diagram:"conduit-kick",caption:"Kick \u2014 a single bend out of a box at less than 90\xb0"},{diagram:"conduit-rolling-offset",caption:"Rolling offset \u2014 the obstruction is off-axis, so the bend twists as it rises"},{diagram:"conduit-back-to-back",caption:"Back-to-back bends \u2014 the star mark sets the second bend"}],sources:[],verified:!1},{id:"glow-rods",name:"Glow Rods (Fish Sticks) & Fish Tape",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"The two tools that get wire from where it is to where it needs to be. Glow rods \u2014 fish sticks \u2014 are fibreglass segments that thread end to end into a long semi-rigid push rod with an interchangeable tip: hook, bullnose or flex leader. They glow in the dark so you can see the tip through a ceiling void or behind a wall. Fish tape is the flat steel or fibreglass ribbon on a reel, and it is the tool for pulling through conduit rather than open space.\n\nThe distinction matters when you buy. Rods push \u2014 you steer them through insulation, over top plates and past fire blocks. Tape pulls \u2014 it goes in one end of a raceway and comes out the other. Doing low-voltage drops for readers, contacts and cameras you will reach for the rods far more often than the tape.",useCases:["Dropping a reader or keypad cable down a stud bay from an attic or a ceiling void","Running a horizontal ceiling pull for camera or door cordage where there is no raceway","Getting past a fire block or a top plate that a straight push will not clear","Marrying two rods from opposite ends of a long run in the middle of the space","Pulling a string or a cable through conduit that is already installed (fish tape's job, not the rods')","Retrieving a dropped cable inside a wall cavity with a hook tip"],howToUse:"Thread enough rod segments to reach plus a margin, and check every joint is tight \u2014 a segment that unscrews inside a wall is a very bad afternoon. Pick the tip for the job: bullnose to push through insulation, hook to catch and retrieve, flex leader to negotiate a bend.\n\nPush from the opening rather than dragging, and when you hit a fire block do not force it \u2014 back off, rotate the rod, and walk the tip past the obstruction. Forcing a rod is how you snap a segment or punch through drywall you then have to patch. On a long horizontal run, push a rod in from each end and marry the tips in the middle; that halves the distance each rod has to travel and it is far easier than fighting one rod the whole way.\n\nOnce the tip is through, tie your pull string to it \u2014 not the cable itself on a long or tight run \u2014 draw the string back, then use the string to pull the cable. Leave a service loop at both ends. The Field Manual guide 'Dropping & fishing low-voltage wire' walks the whole sequence.",proTips:["Tape the joints on a long rod assembly. A segment backing off inside a ceiling void turns a ten-minute pull into opening the drywall","Rods push, tape pulls. In open framing reach for rods; in installed conduit reach for tape","Pull a string first, then use the string to pull the cable. Tying the cable straight to the rod tip is how connectors get stripped off in a wall","Glow rods need charging \u2014 leave them in the light for a few minutes before you go into a dark attic or they will not glow when you need them","Rotate, do not force. A fire block is negotiated by backing off and walking the tip past it, never by leaning harder"],photos:[{diagram:"fish-step-1",caption:"Rig the rod: segments threaded end to end with a hook, bullnose or flex leader"},{diagram:"fish-step-2",caption:"Push from the opening; at a fire block back off, rotate, and walk the tip past"},{diagram:"fish-step-3",caption:"Long horizontal run: push a rod in from each end and marry the tips in the middle"},{diagram:"fish-step-4",caption:"Tie the pull string to the tip, draw it up, and leave a service loop"}],sources:[],verified:!1},{id:"greenlee-tone-and-probe-kit",name:"Greenlee/Tempo 77HP-G Tone Generator + 200EP-G Probe",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A two-piece wire tracer. The 77HP-G high-power generator clips onto a conductor and pushes an audio warble tone down it; the 200EP-G probe picks that tone up inductively through the insulation so you can find the same wire at the far end of a bundle, tray, or cross-connect. It also does continuity and polarity checks. It is the fast way to answer 'which of these forty wires is the one I just pulled?'",useCases:["Identifying one run inside a fat bundle at the panel or head-end","Finding the far end of a cable you pulled but did not label","Ringing out continuity and checking polarity on a de-energized pair","Sorting reader, lock, and contact runs at a messy access control cabinet"],howToUse:"Clip the generator's leads to the target: red and black across a pair, or red to a single conductor and black to earth/ground. Set the switch to TONE and pick the warble. At the far end, run the probe tip along each conductor or jacket... the tone gets loudest on your wire. If several wires sing because the tone bleeds to neighbors, back the probe's volume down until only one is left, and separate that wire from the group to confirm. Trust the loudest, not the first one that beeps.\n\nThe generator's switch has more than tone. The OFF (VOLTS) position checks a live phone pair for polarity with the red/green LED. The SHORT position, on the 77HP-G, sounds a buzzer for a resistive short under 200 ohm, which is how you confirm a shorted pair fast. There is an audible continuity buzzer and a dual-line polarity indication built in.\n\nThe hard rule: tone and continuity work on de-energized or low-voltage wiring only. Do not clip the generator to a live AC or DC circuit... it is not a meter and you can damage it or yourself. Kill the run first, verify it is dead, then tone it.",proTips:["Back off the probe gain to isolate... at full volume half the bundle sings.","Single-conductor tracing: red to the wire, black to a solid ground, and the tone rides that one conductor.","The tone couples to adjacent pairs, so pull your candidate away from the group before you call it.","Keep a fresh 9V in each unit... a weak battery is a faint tone you will chase for ten minutes."],sources:["https://www.tempocom.com/products/77hp-g-tone-generator/","https://www.farnell.com/datasheets/1309654.pdf","https://assets.tequipment.net/assets/1/26/Greenlee_200EP_Instruction_Manual.pdf"],photo:"photo-tool-tone-and-probe",photos:[{asset:"photo-tool-tone-and-probe",caption:"The 200EP-G probe and the 77HP-G generator with its clip leads. The generator's switch legend reads TONE / SHORT / OFF (VOLTS) with L1 and L2 for the polarity check."}]},{id:"flipper-zero",name:"Flipper Zero",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A pocket multi-tool on an ultra-low-power STM32 MCU for exploring access control and radio protocols. For access work it reads, saves, and emulates both 125 kHz LF and 13.56 MHz HF RFID, plus sub-GHz remotes, IR, iButton, and USB. In a locksmith kit it is a credential-identification and authorized-duplication tool, and a blunt way to show a customer why a weak credential needs upgrading.",useCases:["Identify an unknown credential's technology (LF prox vs HF smart card) before ordering replacements or a matching reader","Duplicate a customer's own 125 kHz fob or tag for an authorized spare","Verify during commissioning that a reader actually reads the format you think it does","Show a customer why a legacy 125 kHz credential is low-security, to justify an upgrade","Capture and replay a sub-GHz gate remote for authorized testing of a system you maintain"],howToUse:"Pick the app for the credential. For a 125 kHz fob, open the 125 kHz RFID app, hold the fob to the back of the Flipper, and Read... it identifies the protocol (EM4100, HID Prox, Indala, and so on) and stores the ID. For an authorized duplicate, Write it to a compatible blank (a T5577). For a 13.56 MHz card, use the NFC app the same way... Read, and it reports the card type (MIFARE Classic, DESFire, iCLASS, etc.).\n\nWhat reads is not what clones, and that distinction is the whole lesson. A 125 kHz EM4100 or basic HID Prox is a static number with no authentication... it reads and copies to a blank in seconds, which is exactly why it is considered obsolete for security. A 13.56 MHz card with real encryption (DESFire EV2/EV3, SEOS, iCLASS SE) reads as present but will NOT clone... the keys cannot be pulled, and that resistance is the point of those formats. MIFARE Classic sits in between, since its legacy crypto is broken. UHF vehicle tags like the [[model:dolphin-uhf-long-range-reader|Dolphin]] are outside the Flipper's LF/HF range entirely... it does not do 900 MHz.\n\nThe highest-value field use is diagnostic, not duplication. Tap an unknown fob, learn it is HID Prox 26-bit, and now you know which reader and which blanks to order instead of guessing.\n\nKeep the authorized-use line bright. Duplicate credentials you are contracted and authorized to duplicate, on systems you maintain, and get the duplication or audit in writing on the work order before you start. Many jurisdictions and most property-management contracts treat credential duplication as a controlled service, and the paperwork is what makes it one. That documentation is the difference between a locksmith service and a problem.",proTips:["If a card reads but will not clone, that is the credential doing its job... it is an encrypted format, and the customer's fix is not a workaround, it is that their system is actually secure.","Stock T5577 blanks (LF) and UID-writable blanks (HF) for authorized duplication... the Flipper reads native credentials but writes to these.","The 'it reads, so it is insecure' demo is your best upgrade tool... show a property manager their 2005-era prox fob copying in five seconds and the DESFire upgrade sells itself.","Get it on the work order every time. Written authorization naming the site and the credential is what keeps an audit or a spare-fob job on the right side of the line.","It will not touch Dolphin UHF gate tags (900 MHz) or a properly encrypted smart card... know the boundary so you do not promise a copy you cannot make."],sources:["https://flipper.net/","https://lab401.com/products/flipper-zero","https://www.getkisi.com/blog/how-to-copy-access-cards-and-keyfobs"],photos:[{diagram:"credential-read-vs-clone",caption:"The boundary this tool actually teaches: what it identifies versus what it can duplicate, and why a card that refuses to copy is the customer's system working rather than a problem to solve."}]},{id:"angle-grinder",name:"Angle Grinder & Cut-Off Wheels",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A handheld high-speed grinder, usually 4-1/2 or 5 inch, spinning an abrasive cut-off or grinding wheel. On locksmith work it is the last resort and the fastest one: a seized padlock shackle, a rusted hasp, a cut-down bolt, a hardened staple nobody is picking. It is also the loudest, hottest, most destructive thing in the van, which is exactly why it goes on the truck and not in the hand until the non-destructive options are spent.",useCases:["Cutting a padlock shackle or hasp when the customer owns it, has proved it, and picking or drilling has failed or is not worth the time","Removing seized, painted-over or vandalised hardware that will not come off with a driver","Trimming a bolt, threaded rod or strike lip that is fouling an installation","Cutting off a sheared or spun fastener on a door reinforcement or gate frame","Deburring a cut edge before it goes back on a customer's door"],howToUse:"Match the wheel to the job and to the tool first. A thin cut-off wheel is for cutting only, never for side-loading or grinding on its face... a grinding wheel is the thick one. Both are rated by maximum RPM, and that rating has to be equal to or higher than the grinder's no-load speed. Fitting an underrated wheel is how wheels come apart.\n\nGuard stays on, and it goes between the wheel and you. Set it so sparks and any fragment throw away from your body and away from whatever is behind the work, then check that the workpiece is captive... a padlock swinging on a hasp will grab the wheel and kick.\n\nCut, do not lean. Let the wheel do the work at its own rate; forcing it flexes a thin wheel and that is when they shatter. On a shackle, cut ONE side and then the other rather than trying to open the loop in a single pass, and expect the metal to move as it releases tension.\n\nHeat is the part people underestimate. A cut shackle is hot enough to burn through a glove for a while after, and on a door the same heat travels into paint, glazing seals and anything combustible behind the cut. Sweep the area first, know what is on the other side, and give the piece time or water before you hand it to anybody.\n\nFull face shield, not just glasses. Hearing protection, because these run well past the level where damage starts. Gloves and long sleeves for spark and fragment. And when the guard is in the way of the cut you want, that is the cut telling you to use a different tool, not the guard telling you to remove it.",proTips:["The wheel's RPM rating must meet or beat the grinder's no-load speed, and a thin cut-off wheel is for cutting only... side pressure on one is the classic way to break it.","Cut both sides of a shackle rather than forcing one long cut. It is quicker in practice and the metal is far less likely to pinch the wheel.","Ask what is behind the cut before you start. Sparks travel a surprising distance and a storefront has glazing seals, sealant and stock right behind the frame.","Everything you cut stays hot far longer than it looks. Do not hand a fresh-cut padlock to a customer.","It is the loudest thing you will do all day and it draws attention... on a lockout, have your authorisation in hand BEFORE the wheel spins, not after somebody calls it in."],sources:["https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.243","https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.215","https://www.milwaukeetool.com/Products/Power-Tools/Metalworking/Grinders"]},{id:"portable-band-saw",name:"Portable Band Saw (Portaband)",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A handheld deep-cut band saw running a continuous blade, the cordless descendant of the shop bandsaw. On lock work it does the same jobs an angle grinder does, but slowly, quietly and cold: padlock shackles, bolts, threaded rod, conduit, hasps. No sparks, no shower of hot fragment, and a cut face clean enough to leave on a customer's door.",useCases:["Cutting a padlock shackle where sparks are unacceptable... near glazing, fuel, stock, dust, or inside an occupied space","Cutting bolts, threaded rod and all-thread to length on an installation","Cutting EMT and conduit square, without the burr and heat a cut-off wheel leaves","Removing a hasp, staple or bracket when the surrounding finish has to survive","Any cut where noise matters, because it is dramatically quieter than a grinder"],howToUse:"Pick the blade by tooth pitch against material thickness, and the rule is the one that decides everything: at least three teeth must be in contact with the work at all times. Too coarse a blade on thin wall and the teeth straddle it, snag, and strip or break. For hardened shackles and general lock work a bi-metal blade in a fine pitch, commonly 14 or 18 TPI, covers most of it.\n\nLet the saw's own weight feed the cut. These are low-speed, high-torque tools and they want steady contact, not pressure... pushing bends the blade, wanders the cut and shortens blade life sharply. Start the cut with the shoe resting against the work so the blade enters square rather than skating.\n\nSupport the workpiece so it cannot rotate. A padlock free to swing will bind the blade, and a bound blade on a portaband either stalls the saw or throws the loop off its wheels.\n\nBlade tracking and tension are maintenance you actually have to do. A blade that has come off its guides, or one run slack, cuts crooked and fails early. Check both when you change a blade, and carry spares... they are consumable, and the one you need always goes on the job where you have no signal to order another.",proTips:["Three teeth in the cut, minimum. It is the whole blade-selection rule and it is why a coarse blade strips its teeth on thin conduit.","Let it feed itself. Leaning on a portaband is the single fastest way to ruin a blade and to wander off your line.","Where a grinder would work but sparks are a problem, this is the tool... it cuts cold, which means no scorched paint, no ignition risk and nothing too hot to hand over.","It is far quieter than a grinder, which matters more than it sounds on an occupied building at night.","Deep-cut models take a noticeably bigger shackle than the compact ones. Know which you are carrying before you promise a customer a cut."],sources:["https://www.milwaukeetool.com/Products/Power-Tools/Metalworking/Band-Saws","https://www.lenoxtools.com/Pages/Band-Saw-Blades.aspx","https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.243"]},{id:"bolt-cutter",name:"Bolt Cutters",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"Compound-lever cutters, commonly 14 to 42 inches, that shear a padlock shackle, chain link or hasp staple in one squeeze. The oldest destructive-entry tool on the truck and still often the right one: no power, no sparks, no heat, no noise beyond the crack of the cut, and it works in the rain. What decides whether it works at all is the jaw's rated capacity against the shackle's hardness, and that is a smaller window than most people assume.",useCases:["Cutting a padlock shackle or chain on a customer's own property, with authorisation, when picking and shimming have failed or are not worth the time","Removing a hasp staple or a seized gate chain during a lockout","Cutting soft wire, threaded rod and light chain on an installation","Working where sparks are prohibited outright... fuel, dust, stock, oxygen, or an occupied space","Any cut where the noise and attention of a grinder are the wrong trade"],howToUse:"Length is leverage, and it is the whole tool. A 14 inch pair handles soft wire and light chain; a 24 inch takes on a modest shackle; a 36 or 42 inch is what a hardened 3/8 inch shackle actually needs. Reaching for one too short and leaning harder is how you round the jaws and how you get hurt when they slip.\n\nSeat the work deep in the throat, right back against the pivot, never out at the tips. The jaws are strongest where the leverage is greatest, and tip-cutting is what chips edges and springs the heads out of alignment. Cut square to the shackle, not at an angle.\n\nOn a padlock, cut ONE side of the shackle and lift the body off, rather than trying to take both sides. The shackle is under spring tension and it will move when it releases, so keep your face out of the plane of the cut and expect the offcut to go somewhere.\n\nWhere they simply will not work is worth knowing before you promise a customer a cut. A hardened boron alloy shackle, and the shrouded padlocks built specifically to deny jaw access, are designed to defeat this tool. If the jaws skate off the shackle or the handles start to spread without the metal yielding, stop... that is the lock telling you it is the wrong tool, and forcing it damages the cutters and can throw a fragment.\n\nKeep the jaws adjusted. Most quality cutters have an adjustment for jaw gap and alignment, and a pair that has been over-loaded or tip-cut will no longer meet cleanly, at which point it crushes rather than shears and nothing gets cut.",proTips:["Buy the length the job needs. A 36 inch pair cuts what a 24 cannot, and the fix for a cut that will not go is longer handles, never more weight on short ones.","Deep in the throat, against the pivot. Tip-cutting chips the jaws and it is the fastest way to ruin an expensive pair.","Cut one side of the shackle and lift the body off... you rarely need the second cut, and the shackle is sprung so it moves when it lets go.","A shrouded or hardened-boron padlock is built to beat this tool. Recognise it early and change tools rather than destroying the cutters proving a point.","It is the quiet option, and on an occupied building at night that matters as much as the speed. Authorisation in hand first, all the same."],sources:["https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/bolt-cutters","https://www.ridgid.com/us/en/bolt-cutters","https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.242","https://www.paclock.com/"]},{id:"hydraulic-spreader",name:"Hydraulic Spreader / Porta-Power",category:"Picking & bypass",whatItIs:"A hand-pumped hydraulic ram, either a rescue-style spreader with jaws or a porta-power cylinder driven through a hose, that pushes a pair of doors or a frame apart under enormous force. On aluminium storefront it is the heavy end of the same idea as an air wedge: open the gap between the two leaves far enough that the deadlatch bolt clears its keeper. It opens doors that nothing else will, and it can bend a frame permanently, which is why it is a last resort and a documented conversation.",useCases:["Opening a pair of aluminium storefront doors on an authorised lockout when the cylinder is gone, the latch is inaccessible and cutting is not acceptable","Spreading a distorted frame enough to free a bound door or a jammed exit device","Any authorised forced entry where the alternative is cutting the bolt or breaking glass, and the customer prefers the damage be bendable rather than replaceable","Recovering a door that has already been pried, to get it closed and secured before you leave"],howToUse:"Understand what you are exploiting before you pump anything. A pair of storefront doors meet at the astragal with the deadlatch bolt engaging a keeper in the opposite stile. Spread the two leaves apart far enough and the bolt loses engagement... that is the whole mechanism, and it is also exactly the attack the guides describe, which is why the same knowledge sells the fix.\n\nSpread against the stiles, low and near the lock line, with the widest pad you can get behind the ram. Force concentrated on a narrow point dents and folds aluminium rather than moving it, and a folded stile is a replacement door instead of a straightened one. Work in small increments and check the latch between each, because the moment the bolt clears you are done and every further pump is damage you are choosing.\n\nExpect the frame to take some of the load. On a tired opening the header or the jamb will move before the leaves do, and that is how a lockout becomes a glazing job. If the doors are not moving but the frame is, stop.\n\nThe defensive half is the reason this belongs in a locksmith's head at all. A pair fitted with a proper astragal, an anti-spread bar, or a bolt with deep engagement does not open this way, and once you have seen a door spread you can sell that hardware honestly. Storefront prying and spreading covers both sides of it.\n\nAuthorisation is not a formality here. This tool leaves visible damage on someone's building, so the work order names the site, the opening and the method BEFORE you rig it, and the customer knows a bent stile is a possible outcome rather than a surprise.",proTips:["Widest possible pad against the stile. A narrow contact point folds aluminium instead of spreading the pair, and a fold is not straightenable.","Small increments, check the latch every time. The job finishes the instant the bolt clears its keeper, and everything after that is damage you chose.","If the frame is moving and the doors are not, stop. You are about to turn a lockout into a glazing and framing job.","This is the attack the storefront guides describe, so the same visit that opens the door is the one where you quote the astragal and the anti-spread hardware that stops it.","Get the method on the work order before you rig it. Visible damage on a customer's storefront is not something to explain afterwards."],sources:["https://www.enerpac.com/en-us","https://www.hurstjaws.com/","https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-1936-standard-development/1936","Field practice, authorised storefront lockouts - spreading technique and the damage threshold, no single public doc."]},{id:"smartkey-rekey-tool",name:"SmartKey Learn Tool",category:"Rekey & pinning",whatItIs:"A small L-shaped steel wire, supplied with every Kwikset SmartKey lock and sold as a cheap spare, that puts a SmartKey cylinder into its learn state. It is the entire toolkit for rekeying that platform... no pinning tray, no follower, no plug removal, no cylinder off the door. It is worth carrying several, because the one that ships with the lock is the one the customer lost, and it is the difference between a two-minute rekey and a trip back to the van.",useCases:["Rekeying a Kwikset SmartKey or Weiser Powerbolt deadbolt or knob to a new key without removing the lock","Rekeying a run of SmartKey units on a rental turnover so the whole property runs one key","Rekeying a customer's existing SmartKey to a key you already carry, or to their other locks, at the door","Resetting a SmartKey after a tenant change when the customer has the working key but lost the learn tool"],howToUse:"The sequence matters more than the tool, and the full step-by-step lives in the SmartKey rekey guide. In outline: the lock must be operable by a working key before you start, the working key goes in and turns a quarter turn, the learn tool goes into the small hole beside the keyway and comes back out, then the old key comes out and the new key goes in and turns back. The cylinder learns the new bitting mechanically.\n\nWhat the tool actually does is release the internal wafer rack so it can re-register to a different blade profile. There are no pins to load and nothing to lose on the floor, which is why the platform exists at all.\n\nThe preconditions are where jobs fail. The lock has to be working and you have to have a key that operates it... SmartKey cannot be rekeyed from a lock you cannot already open, so a lockout is not a rekey job. Both keys must also be the same keyway family, and both must be cut cleanly; a worn or badly cut new key teaches the cylinder a bad bitting and the lock becomes unreliable from that moment.\n\nIf the cycle fails, stop rather than repeating it. A SmartKey that has been half-taught, or forced with the wrong keyway, can jam its wafer rack, and at that point it is a replacement rather than a service. Confirm the new key works several times, from both the locked and unlocked position, before you leave.\n\nAnd know what the platform is before you recommend more of it: SmartKey trades pin-tumbler serviceability for speed, and it has a documented bypass history of its own, which the model entry and the high-security residential guide both cover. It is a convenience rekey system, not a security upgrade.",proTips:["Carry several. They cost almost nothing, they are the one thing customers lose, and without one the whole platform's advantage evaporates.","You need a key that already works. A SmartKey lock you cannot open is not rekeyable... that is a replacement or a destructive entry, not a service call.","Both keys must be the same keyway family and both must be cleanly cut. Teaching the cylinder a worn key gives you a lock that fails intermittently later.","If a learn cycle fails, do not just try again. A jammed wafer rack is a replacement, and repeating a failed cycle is how you get there.","Test the new key several times from both positions before you pack up. A rekey that works once is not a rekey that works."],sources:["https://www.kwikset.com/smartkey","Field practice, residential rekey work - learn-tool handling and the failure modes, no single public doc."]},{id:"conduit-blower",name:"Conduit Blower / Fisher",category:"Drilling & install",whatItIs:"A blower or vacuum unit that pushes or pulls a lightweight carrier... a foam mouse, a dart, or a small bag on a mule tape or pull string... through a length of conduit, so the pull line arrives at the far end without anybody rodding it by hand. On a long or bendy run it turns a two-person, half-hour fight with fibreglass rods into a job one person finishes in a couple of minutes.",useCases:["Getting a pull line through a long conduit run where rods would bind or take two people","Runs with several bends, where a rod stops moving well before the far end","Underground or between-floor runs you cannot reach the middle of","Clearing a run and proving it is continuous before you commit to pulling cable through it","Any occupied space where feeding rods through a ceiling means more disruption than the cable pull itself"],howToUse:"Pick the carrier to the conduit, not to the tool. The foam mouse or dart wants to be a close fit... slightly undersized and it slips past the airflow and stalls, oversized and it jams somewhere you cannot reach. Tie the pull line on properly and tie it well; a carrier that arrives at the far end without its string has simply wasted the trip.\n\nSeal the launching end around the carrier so the air goes down the pipe rather than back past you, send it, and watch for it arriving. Feed the string in freely from a bucket or a reel so it pays out without snagging, because a line that binds at your end stops the carrier mid-run.\n\nMost units both blow and vacuum, and knowing when to swap is the real skill. Blowing is the default. Vacuum from the far end is what you use when the run is partly blocked, wet, or already has cable in it, because pulling the carrier toward you gives you a much better feel for where it stops than pushing it away does. If a carrier stalls at the same distance twice, that is the run telling you it has a blockage or a crushed section, and more air pressure will not fix it.\n\nWhat the tool does not do is make a bad run good. Water, silt, a collapsed section or an unswept bend will stop a carrier the way it will stop a rod, and you have simply found out sooner and with less sweat. Treat a repeated stall as a locate-and-dig, not as a reason to reach for a bigger blower.",proTips:["Fit the carrier to the conduit size. Undersized slips past the air and stops; oversized jams somewhere you cannot get at.","Tie the line on like it matters and let it pay out freely. A carrier that lands with no string attached, or one snagged on your own coil, is the whole trip wasted.","Vacuum from the far end for anything blocked, wet, or already occupied... pulling toward you tells you far more about where it is stuck than blowing away from you does.","A stall at the same distance twice is a blockage, not a pressure problem. Stop and find it.","Blow a light string first, then use it to pull a proper mule tape. Trying to blow the heavy tape straight through is how carriers stall."],sources:["https://www.greenlee.com/","https://www.jonard.com/","Field practice, low-voltage conduit runs - carrier sizing and the blow-versus-vacuum call, no single public doc."]},{id:"rs485-to-usb-converter",name:"RS-485 to USB Converter",category:"Access control",whatItIs:"A thumb-sized adapter with a USB plug on one end and a two- or four-screw terminal block on the other, which turns a laptop into a node on an RS-485 bus. It shows up as a COM port in Windows Device Manager and from there any serial tool, or the manufacturer's own utility, can talk to a reader or a DIN-rail module directly. The point is that it takes the panel out of the equation: instead of wiring a reader to a controller and inferring what it did from an event log, you power the head on the bench, watch its raw output, and know within a minute whether the reader is the problem. ICT's own documentation requires one to push tSec firmware, and the same adapter serves any 485 device you meet.",useCases:["Bench-proving a card reader before it goes on the wall... power it, badge a card, and see whether it actually outputs anything","Deciding whether a dead door is the reader or the controller, without pulling either one out of service","Reading raw card data off a reader to identify a format or facility code you were never told","Pushing firmware to an ICT tSec reader, which ICT's configuration guide specifies is done with a 485-USB converter and LoadIT 1.5 or later","Configuring or interrogating a Protege DIN-rail module on the bench before it goes in the cabinet","Proving an RS-485 run end to end: clip on at the far end and see whether the controller's traffic is arriving at all","Confirming a baud-rate mismatch, which looks identical to a dead module from the software side","Working out which of two conductors is A and which is B on an unlabelled run, by swapping and retrying"],howToUse:"Land the bus on the terminal block: A to A and B to B, or if the device labels them - and +, match those instead. There is no universal answer to which of A/B carries the + label... vendors genuinely disagree, so wire to the legend printed on the device in front of you. If nothing talks, swap the two conductors and try again. A reversed differential pair is silent, not damaged, and swapping costs you ten seconds against an hour of suspecting the board. If the adapter has a GND terminal, tie it to the device's 0V, and remember the adapter does not power the device... a reader on the bench still needs its own 12 VDC.\n\nPlug into the laptop, open Device Manager, and note which COM port appeared under Ports (COM & LPT). Every tool you use afterwards asks for that port number, and picking the wrong one is the most common reason a first attempt does nothing. Then set the speed to match the device. 38400 is the ICT default and 9600 is the other common one; a baud mismatch reads exactly like a dead device, so when a known-good reader is silent, walk the baud rates before you walk the wiring.\n\nFor a straight read, open any serial terminal on that COM port and badge a card at the reader. On a device speaking a documented protocol you get frames you can decode; on anything else you at least get traffic, and traffic versus silence is the answer you came for. Silence with correct wiring, correct power and correct baud means the head is the fault.\n\nFor firmware and configuration, use the manufacturer's utility rather than a terminal. On a tSec that means putting the reader in boot mode by strapping the blue, orange and brown wires to black, landing yellow and violet on the converter, and running LoadIT... and you get roughly 30 seconds from power-up to begin the load, so have the file selected and the port set before you energise anything.",proTips:["Buy an isolated converter if you can. A non-isolated adapter ties your laptop's ground to the panel's, and on a site with a ground-potential difference that is how a USB port dies.","Label the adapter's A and B leads the moment you unbox it. Cheap adapters silkscreen the terminals in 4-point type inside the shell where you cannot read them on a ladder.","Swap-and-retry is the diagnostic, not a mistake. Reversed A/B is dead comms with no damage, so trying both ways is faster than reasoning about which vendor convention applies.","Match the baud rate before you touch the wiring. A known-good device at the wrong baud is indistinguishable from a dead one.","The adapter does not supply power. Bring a bench supply or a spare 12 VDC plug-pack, because a reader with data landed and no V+ looks exactly like a reader that will not talk.","Terminate long benches the same as long runs. If you are tapping the far end of a real 900m bus, the 120-ohm end-of-line resistor still matters.","Keep a known-good reader in the van. Once you own a converter, a spare head plus the adapter settles reader-versus-panel arguments on the spot rather than on the next visit.","Write down the COM port the adapter enumerates as. It changes between USB sockets on the same laptop, and half of all 'it stopped working' moments are the port number."],sources:["https://www.ict.co/media/2podvv4n/ict_card_reader_configuration_guide.pdf","https://www.ict.co/media/hwgn4jxj/prx-tsec_tsec_card_reader_installation_manual.pdf","https://ict.co/media/iz1p2x1y/an-254_configuring_osdp_readers_in_protege.pdf","https://www.ti.com/lit/an/slla070d/slla070d.pdf"],photos:[{diagram:"rs485-usb-bench-test",caption:"The bench rig, and the three things that make a good reader look dead"}]},{id:"punchdown-tool",name:"Punchdown Tool (110 / 66)",category:"Cable & termination",whatItIs:"An impact tool that seats a solid conductor into an insulation-displacement contact and trims the tail in one motion. The IDC does the work: forcing the wire down between two sharp blades slices through the insulation and makes a gas-tight connection against the copper, which is why a properly punched conductor needs no stripping and no screw. You will meet two blade profiles. The 110 is the modern one, used on patch panels, keystone jacks and 110 wiring blocks; the 66 is the older split-block you still find on every phone-entry panel and a lot of legacy alarm gear. A decent tool takes both on a reversible or swappable blade, so one handle covers the site.",useCases:["Terminating cable onto a patch panel, keystone jack or 110 block on any IP camera, intercom or access-control job","Working the 66 block in an older telephone-entry or alarm cabinet, where the whole house pair count lands","Adding a cross-connect or tap onto an existing block without cutting the run you are tapping","Re-terminating a suspect pair that tone-and-probe or a wiremap tester has already proved is the problem","Cleaning up an inherited cabinet where the last tech used a screwdriver and left half-seated conductors"],howToUse:"Set the blade the right way round first, because this is the mistake everyone makes once. Each blade has a cut side and a punch-only side, usually marked or colour-coded, and the cut side must face the tail you want trimmed... that is the free end running off the block, not the run coming in. Get it backwards and you cut the cable you just pulled. Seat the conductor in the correct slot by hand, hold the tool square and perpendicular to the block, and drive it straight down until the mechanism releases with a distinct snap. Let the impact do it... leaning on the tool at an angle bends the contact and gives you a joint that reads fine today and opens up in a year. Most tools have a high and low impact setting: low for terminating single conductors into jacks, panels and faceplates, high for seating a 110 connecting block into a wiring block. Pick the tail off, tug the conductor gently to confirm it is captive, and move on.",proTips:["Buy the tool with interchangeable blades rather than two fixed tools. A 110/66 combination blade lives in the handle and covers everything from a Cat6 patch panel to a 1970s split block, which matters because low-voltage work drags you across four decades of hardware in one week.","Maintain the pair twist right up to the contact. Untwisting an inch of pair to make it easier to dress is exactly how you turn a certified Cat6 run into something that fails a wiremap at 100 MHz... 110 blocks carry 100 MHz where a 66 block only ever managed about 16, and you throw that advantage away at the termination. Strip the jacket, keep the twist, punch, done.","A screwdriver is not a punchdown tool. It will make a connection that appears to work, because a partly seated conductor still touches copper. What it will not do is make a gas-tight one, so it corrodes, and you get the intermittent fault that costs three visits to find. Use the tool or do not terminate."],sources:["https://crimpshop.com/blog/how-to-install-110-vs-66-block/","https://community.fs.com/blog/punch-down-tools-for-network-cabling.html","https://icc.com/product/110-66-single-blade-punch-down-tool/"]},{id:"rj45-crimper",name:"RJ45 Crimper & Connectors",category:"Cable & termination",whatItIs:"The plier-style tool that presses an 8P8C modular plug onto a cable end, driving all eight gold contacts down through the conductor insulation at once while a separate jaw clamps the strain relief onto the jacket. It is the field end of every Ethernet run you will ever make for a camera, an IP intercom or a network-attached controller. Two connector styles matter: the traditional plug, where you trim all eight conductors dead flush and hope they stayed in order inside the housing, and the pass-through plug, where the conductors run out the front of the plug so you can SEE the order before you commit, and the crimper's blade shears them off as it closes.",useCases:["Making a patch lead on site because the one you brought is a foot short","Terminating a camera or door-station drop straight to a plug where there is no jack or patch panel","Re-heading a run whose plug has a snapped latch, which is most of the field failures you will meet","Building a known-good short test lead to prove a switch port before you blame the cable","Converting an inherited run that was terminated badly and never worked reliably"],howToUse:"Strip roughly an inch and a half of jacket without nicking the conductors... a ring cutter or the crimper's own stripping notch set light does this better than a knife. Fan the four pairs, untwist only as far as you must, and dress them flat into the pin order you are working to. That is T568B in most of North America and T568A in a lot of residential and government work: the two differ only in swapping the orange and green pairs, and the rule that actually matters is that both ends of a given cable use the SAME scheme. Trim all eight square and flush at about half an inch, slide them fully home so every conductor touches the front of the plug, confirm the jacket is inside the strain-relief zone, then crimp firmly until the tool bottoms out. Test it with a wiremap tester before it goes anywhere you cannot reach.",proTips:["Use pass-through plugs and a matching crimper. Being able to see all eight conductors emerge from the nose in the right order, in daylight, on a ladder, removes the single most common cause of a failed termination... and the tool trims them flush as it crimps, so it costs you nothing.","A crossover cable is a museum piece. Every switch and nearly every device made this century does auto-MDI-X, so straight-through at both ends is the answer unless you have specific documentation saying otherwise. Do not let an old habit send you hunting a fault you created.","Solid versus stranded matters and the plugs are not interchangeable. Solid conductor is for permanent in-wall runs, stranded is for flexible patch leads, and each takes a plug with a different contact geometry. A stranded plug on solid cable makes an intermittent connection that will pass a continuity test and fail under vibration."],sources:["https://www.cablewholesale.com/support/technical_articles/network_cable_connectivity.php","https://www.trend-networks.com/power-over-ethernet-class-vs-type-vs-voltage/"]},{id:"lv-wire-strippers",name:"Low-Voltage Wire Strippers & Flush Cutters",category:"Cable & termination",whatItIs:"The pair of tools you touch more than anything else on a low-voltage job and the pair people cheap out on hardest. Strippers with real gauge-matched notches, typically running 22 to 16 AWG for security wire and up to 12 for lock power, remove insulation without scoring the copper underneath. Flush cutters trim a conductor square with no pinch or burr. Both matter more here than in line-voltage work because security cable is thin, stranded and easy to wreck: a conductor nicked at the strip line will carry current happily on your bench and then fatigue and open months later inside a door loop that flexes every time the door swings.",useCases:["Landing reader, lock, REX and door-contact conductors on a controller's terminal block","Stripping 22/4 and 18/2 for strikes, mag locks, keypads and telephone-entry panels","Trimming conductors square before they go into an IDC, a ferrule or a screw terminal","Working inside a dense inherited cabinet where a slip takes out the pair next door","Preparing shielded cable, where the drain wire has to survive and the foil has to be dressed back cleanly"],howToUse:"Match the notch to the gauge printed on the jacket rather than eyeballing it, close only until the insulation parts, and pull straight off the end. If you feel the tool bite metal, you have gone a size small... move up a notch and re-strip, because a scored conductor is a future callback you have personally installed. Strip only as much as the terminal needs, usually a quarter inch for a screw terminal and whatever the block specifies for an IDC. On shielded cable, ring the outer jacket, fold the foil and braid back over it, and keep the drain wire full length so you can land it properly at the controller end. Cut conductors with the flush cutter rather than the stripper's shear, and cut them square: a burr or an angled tail is what stops a conductor seating fully under a clamp plate.",proTips:["Self-adjusting strippers are excellent on solid conductor and a liability on fine stranded security cable, where they crush and shear strands. Keep a gauge-notched pair for anything 22 AWG and thinner and save the automatic ones for lock power runs.","Never tin a stranded conductor that is going under a screw terminal. The solder cold-flows under clamping pressure, the joint loosens over months, and the resulting high-resistance connection heats and drops voltage right where you least want it... on a lock feed. Use a ferrule if you want the strands controlled.","Strip length is a discipline, not a guess. Copper visible outside the terminal is a short waiting for a screwdriver to slip, and insulation trapped under the clamp is a connection that is not made at all. Aim for the conductor to bottom out with the insulation just kissing the terminal mouth."],sources:["https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/wire-strippers-cutters","https://www.cablinginstall.com/cable/article/14035166/tia-606-c-standard-requirements-for-cable-plant-administration"]},{id:"cable-tester",name:"Wiremap Cable Tester",category:"Cable & termination",whatItIs:"A two-piece tester... a main unit and a remote... that proves a terminated run end to end, pin by pin. It is the tool that answers a different question from the tone-and-probe already in your bag: the toner FINDS a pair in a bundle, the wiremap tester VERIFIES the one you found is correctly and completely terminated. It catches the four faults you cannot see, which are opens, shorts, crossed pairs where two conductors are swapped, and split pairs where the map looks perfect but the twist has been broken across two pairs. Better units add a length measurement by time-domain reflectometry, which tells you how far along the run the open actually is.",useCases:["Proving every drop before you hand a job over, so a fault is yours to fix now rather than a callback later","Confirming an inherited run is good before you spend an afternoon blaming a camera or a controller","Finding which of eight identical drops in a cabinet actually goes to the door you care about","Catching a split pair on a run that passes a simple continuity check and still will not carry gigabit","Measuring cable length to decide whether a run has quietly exceeded the 100 m Ethernet limit or a reader's cable spec"],howToUse:"Put the remote on the far end and the main unit on the near end, and read the map. A good result walks 1 through 8 in order on both ends. An open shows a pin missing at one end, a short shows two pins lighting together, and a crossed pair shows the numbers arriving out of order... which on a freshly made lead almost always means one plug got T568A and the other got T568B. A split pair is the sneaky one: every pin maps correctly straight through, so a cheap tester passes it, but the conductors have been paired against the wrong twists and the run will fail at speed or pick up noise. If your tester calls out split pairs, believe it. When a fault shows, re-terminate the near end first, because it is the end you are standing at and it is statistically the one you just made.",proTips:["A continuity tester and a wiremap tester are not the same instrument, and the cheap ones only do continuity. If it cannot report a split pair it is telling you a comfortable half-truth on every gigabit run you certify with it.","Test before you dress and clip the cable, not after. Finding an open in a run you have already stapled, labelled and closed the ceiling on is a self-inflicted wound.","This is not a certifier and you should not sell it as one. A wiremap tester proves the run is wired correctly; a certifier proves it meets a performance category to a standard, costs an order of magnitude more, and is what a customer means if they ask for certified results. Be straight about which one you are handing over."],sources:["https://www.trend-networks.com/us/product/poe-pro/","https://www.cablewholesale.com/support/technical_articles/network_cable_connectivity.php"]},{id:"dc-clamp-meter",name:"DC Clamp Meter (with Inrush)",category:"Test & measurement",whatItIs:"A meter that reads current by clamping around a single conductor instead of breaking the circuit to get in series with it. The word that matters is DC: plenty of cheap clamp meters are AC-only, and every lock, reader, controller and camera you care about runs on DC, so an AC-only clamp is useless on this work. The second thing to insist on is an inrush function, which triggers on the rising edge and samples hard over the first fraction of a second to catch the peak a normal averaging meter smooths away. That peak is the number that sizes your power supply and explains why a system that measures fine at rest browns out every time a lock fires.",useCases:["Measuring a mag lock's or strike's actual holding current against the figure on the datasheet","Catching the inrush spike when a lock or gate operator energises, which is what really sizes the supply","Adding up the true load on a shared power supply before you hang one more device on it","Finding the device that is dragging a rail down when a controller reports supply voltage out of range","Confirming a reader is drawing what it should, which separates a dead head from a starved one"],howToUse:"Clamp around ONE conductor only. Put the jaw around both the positive and the negative of a pair and the fields cancel, and the meter reads a confident zero that means nothing... this catches everybody once. Select DC amps, zero the jaw before you take a reading, because DC clamps drift and an unzeroed meter carries an offset straight into your result. For a holding current, clamp the lock's positive feed and read it with the lock energised and settled. For inrush, arm the inrush function first and then energise the device, so the meter is already watching when the edge arrives. Compare what you get against the manufacturer's figure and against the supply's rating, and remember to add the standby load of everything else already on that supply before you decide there is headroom.",proTips:["Inrush can run several times the steady-state figure, which is exactly why a supply sized off the datasheet's holding current alone will misbehave under load without ever looking overloaded on a meter. Size for the peak, not the average.","A flexible current probe reaches into places a rigid jaw cannot, and a dense access-control cabinet with a hundred conductors in a two-inch gutter is precisely that place. If you are buying once, the flexible-probe models earn it back the first time you meter a lock feed in a full enclosure.","Use the clamp to settle voltage-drop arguments too. Measured current plus the run's length and gauge gives you the drop, and that is how you prove a long reader or lock run is the reason the far end is sagging under 11 V rather than guessing at it."],sources:["https://www.fluke.com/en-us/learn/blog/clamps/how-and-why-to-measure-inrush-current","https://www.fluke.com/en/product/electrical-testing/clamp-meters/fluke-376","https://www.electronicspecifier.com/industries/industrial/how-to-use-a-clamp-meter-to-measure-inrush-current/"]},{id:"sla-battery-load-tester",name:"SLA Standby Battery Load Tester",category:"Test & measurement",whatItIs:"A tester that puts a real load on a sealed lead-acid standby battery and watches what the terminal voltage does under it. This exists because a resting voltage measurement is close to worthless: a battery with almost no capacity left will happily sit at 12.8 V on an open circuit and collapse the moment anything asks it for current. Every access control panel, alarm panel, camera supply and telephone-entry cabinet you work on has one or more 12 V sealed cells in the bottom, usually 7 Ah, and they are the component most likely to fail silently and only reveal it during the outage they exist to cover.",useCases:["Proving the standby batteries in an access or alarm panel before a handover or a service report","Deciding which of several undated batteries in an inherited cabinet actually need replacing","Investigating a panel that resets, loses its clock or corrupts memory after every power cut","Annual service on a system where standby runtime is a documented requirement","Confirming a new battery is genuinely good rather than old stock that has sat discharged on a shelf"],howToUse:"Charge and rest first or your result is meaningless: a battery straight off the charger carries a surface charge that reads high. Let it sit disconnected for a while, note the resting voltage, then apply the load and watch. What you are looking for is the voltage holding up rather than sagging away under the draw... a healthy 12 V cell stays well up, a tired one dives immediately and recovers as soon as you release the load, which is the classic signature of lost capacity. Check the float voltage the panel is applying while you are there, because a charger sitting outside roughly 13.5 to 13.8 V at room temperature is either undercharging the battery or cooking it, and both shorten its life dramatically. Write the date on every battery you fit, in marker, on the case.",proTips:["Age is the honest test most of the time. Sealed lead-acid standby cells are generally done at three to five years regardless of how they measure, and a battery with no date on it should be treated as suspect and replaced on any system that matters. The tester tells you which of the undated ones to do first.","Eighty percent of rated capacity is the usual retirement line, and fire alarm work under NFPA 72 requires an annual load test outright. Even where you are not bound by that code, borrowing the discipline gives you a defensible service record.","Replace batteries in a set and match them. Mixing a new cell with an old one in a series or parallel standby string drags the new one down to the old one's behaviour, so you have spent money and bought very little runtime."],sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/ultratech-im-1272f2","https://www.geoarm.com/im-1272f1-ultratech-12v-7ah-rechargeable-sealed-lead-acid-battery.html"]},{id:"bench-power-supply",name:"Adjustable Bench / Portable Test Supply",category:"Test & measurement",whatItIs:"A regulated supply with an adjustable output and a current limit, used to power a device on its own so you can find out whether the device is the problem. Set the voltage, set the current limit, connect the lock or reader or panel, and you have removed the site's wiring, its shared supply, its battery and its controller from the question entirely. The current limit is the half people ignore and the half that saves hardware: wound down before you connect, it means a short in the thing you are testing trips the limit instead of cooking anything.",useCases:["Bench-testing a strike, mag lock or reader that a site says is dead, before you order a replacement","Proving a reader works at its rated voltage when you suspect the run is starving it on the door","Powering a controller on the bench to configure or firmware-update it before it ever goes to site","Deliberately sagging the supply to reproduce a fault that only appears when the site's voltage drops","Confirming a device's real current draw at a known voltage, alongside the clamp meter"],howToUse:"Set the voltage with nothing connected, then wind the current limit down low before you attach anything. Connect, then bring the limit up slowly until the device runs. If the supply goes into current limit immediately, you have found a short and cost yourself nothing. Watch what the display does as the device operates: a strike or mag lock will show you its inrush as a momentary excursion and then settle to its holding figure, and a reader should sit at a steady, unremarkable few hundred milliamps. The most useful trick is deliberate under-voltage. Feed a reader its rated 12 V and confirm it behaves, then wind down toward its stated minimum and watch where it stops working... that number tells you whether the voltage you measured at the door is genuinely marginal or comfortably fine.",proTips:["This is how you settle the argument the site cannot settle. A device that works perfectly on a bench supply and misbehaves on the door has a wiring, voltage or noise problem, not a hardware one, and you have proved it in five minutes rather than replacing parts hopefully.","A modest portable unit beats a big bench one for this trade, because most of the value is on site rather than in the shop. You want something you can carry to a cabinet, not something that lives on a workbench you visit twice a month.","Respect the polarity and the limit on anything with electronics in it. Access control boards commonly have reverse-polarity protection and plenty of readers do not, and a current limit set generously will happily deliver enough to destroy a head while you are working out why it is warm."],sources:["https://www.fluke.com/en-us/products/electrical-testing/clamp-meters","https://www.ict.co/media/2podvv4n/ict_card_reader_configuration_guide.pdf"]},{id:"onvif-service-monitor",name:"IP Camera Service Monitor (ONVIF Tester)",category:"Video & PoE",whatItIs:"A handheld screen with an Ethernet port, a battery and enough network tooling to stand under a camera and see what it sees. It discovers cameras over ONVIF, pulls a live stream, drives PTZ, and critically it can POWER the camera it is testing... PoE out on the port, plus switchable 12 V and sometimes 24 or 48 V outputs for the analogue and non-PoE heads. That combination is the whole point: it turns aiming and commissioning a camera into a one-person job on the ladder rather than a two-person job with a phone call to somebody at the recorder.",useCases:["Aiming and focusing a camera at the mount, watching the actual image instead of guessing and climbing twice","Proving a drop delivers both link and PoE before you hang a camera on it","Finding a camera's IP address when nobody documented it and the recorder cannot see it","Deciding whether a dead camera is the camera, the cable, the switch port or the recorder","Checking the view a customer is disputing without needing recorder access or a login"],howToUse:"Work outward from the tester. Plug into the drop, let it power the camera from its own PoE, and see whether you get link and a device. If the camera appears and streams on the tester's power but not on the site's, the fault is the switch port, the injector or the run's power delivery, and you have split that in one step. Use ONVIF discovery to find the head rather than hunting subnets by hand, then bring up the stream to aim: set the field of view against real landmarks, check the corners for the mounting bracket intruding, and confirm focus at the distance that actually matters rather than at the wall. Use the PTZ control to walk presets on a pan-tilt head before you leave. Where the tester has cable tools built in, run the wiremap and length test on the same drop while you are standing there.",proTips:["Set the view at the resolution and codec the site will actually record, not the tester's best-case stream. A scene that looks sharp on a handheld at full bitrate can be useless at the recorder's real settings, and the customer judges the recording.","The PoE-out port is the feature to pay for. Testers without it can look at a camera the site is already powering, which is the easy half of the job. Testers with it can prove a drop and commission a head with nothing else energised, which is the half that saves you a return visit.","Mind the identification discipline. These testers will happily enumerate and stream from cameras on any network you plug into, so use them on systems you are engaged to work on, get the site's authority in writing on a takeover, and record what you touched."],sources:["https://videos.cctvcamerapros.com/pdf/test-monitors/MON-IP7-IP-camera-test-monitor-manual.pdf","https://www.triplett.com/collections/security-camera-testers","https://www.adiglobaldistribution.us/Catalog/shopproducts/data-communications-networking/test-equipment/surveillance-camera-testers"]},{id:"poe-tester",name:"PoE Tester & Injector",category:"Video & PoE",whatItIs:"An inline tool that tells you what power is actually present on an Ethernet drop... which standard is being offered, at what voltage, how many watts are available and which pairs are carrying it... plus an injector, which is the other half of the pair: a device that puts PoE onto a run that has none, so a camera or door station can be fed from a switch that cannot power it. Together they answer the question that stalls more camera and IP-intercom jobs than anything else, which is whether the far end is being offered enough power to do its job.",useCases:["Proving a switch port is actually delivering PoE, and at which class, before blaming a camera","Diagnosing a camera that boots, runs briefly and reboots, which is the classic under-powered signature","Feeding a PoE device from a non-PoE switch on a takeover, without replacing the customer's switch","Confirming a long run has not eaten so much voltage that the far end drops below the PD's window","Checking a PTZ with a heater actually has the wattage headroom it needs in winter, not just in the showroom"],howToUse:"Put the tester inline at the device end, because that is where the power has to be adequate and that is where the losses have already happened. Read the standard it reports and compare it with what the device demands: 802.3af tops out around 15.4 W at the source with roughly 13 W usable at the device, 802.3at raises that to about 30 W, and 802.3bt goes higher again for the heated and motorised heads. Then read the voltage. Healthy sits in the mid-forties to mid-fifties; a reading that has sagged well under the low forties means the cable, the terminations or the run length is eating your budget, and the fix is the cable rather than the camera. Note which pairs carry the power, because a run with a damaged pair can still pass data and fail to power. If you are injecting, put the injector at the switch end and respect its standard... an af injector will not run an at device.",proTips:["Budget for the worst case, not the idle case. A PTZ with a heater and a blower can ask for several times what it draws sitting still on a mild afternoon, and a system commissioned in September fails in January because nobody sized for the heater.","A switch's total PoE budget is not the sum of its ports. Filling every port on a switch whose overall budget is lower than that arithmetic means the switch starts shedding devices under load, usually the ones furthest down its priority list, and it looks exactly like a random camera fault.","Passive injectors and standards-based PoE are different things and mixing them destroys hardware. A standards-compliant injector negotiates before it energises; a passive one puts voltage on the pairs regardless of what is plugged in. Know which is in your bag."],sources:["https://www.trend-networks.com/power-over-ethernet-class-vs-type-vs-voltage/","https://crimpshop.com/blog/poe-tester-guide/","https://www.trend-networks.com/us/product/poe-pro/"]},{id:"knockout-punch",name:"Knockout Punch (Slug-Buster)",category:"Enclosure & labeling",whatItIs:"A punch, a die and a draw stud that pull toward each other through sheet metal to shear a clean round hole, driven by a wrench, a ratchet or a hydraulic ram. It is how you add a conduit entry or a gland to a controller enclosure, a power supply can or a camera back-box without wrecking it. The alternative... a hole saw or a step bit on thin steel... walks, chatters, leaves a ragged edge that cuts insulation, and throws swarf into the live equipment underneath. The Slug-Buster profile is the common one because it splits the slug as it cuts, so the offcut falls out instead of jamming in the die.",useCases:["Adding a conduit entry to an access-control or power-supply enclosure that arrived without one where you need it","Opening a clean gland hole in a camera or door-station back-box","Enlarging an existing knockout that is the wrong size for the fitting you have","Making tidy entries in a takeover cabinet where the previous tech used a drill and a screwdriver","Punching plastic and fibreglass enclosures, which the same tool handles"],howToUse:"Drill a pilot hole for the draw stud, assemble die on the outside face and punch on the inside, or as the tool's instructions specify, then tighten steadily and let the tool do the work. Do not fight it: if it stalls, back off and check you are within the tool's material rating rather than leaning harder. Mild steel up to about 10 gauge is the usual limit for a manual punch, and it will also do aluminium, fibreglass and plastic. Take the slug out and check the edge. Deburr anything that will touch a conductor and fit a bush or a proper connector in the hole... a punched edge is far better than a sawn one, but it is still steel against insulation on a run that may be there for twenty years.",proTips:["The sizing convention catches everybody once, so learn it now: a punch is named for the CONDUIT size it serves, not the hole it cuts. A half-inch conduit punch makes a hole about seven-eighths of an inch across, because that is the outside diameter the fitting needs. Order by conduit size and stop trying to reconcile it with a tape measure.","Punch before you populate wherever you possibly can. Making holes in a cabinet that already holds boards, batteries and terminated conductors means metal filings landing on live electronics, and filings across a board are a fault that appears weeks later and makes no sense at all.","A ratchet driver is the upgrade that matters more than hydraulics for this trade. Access-control enclosures are thin and the holes are small, so the speed you actually gain is in not having to swap wrenches in a cramped cabinet."],sources:["https://www.greenlee.com/us/en/1-2-conduit-size-slug-buster-knockout-punch-k2p-1-2","https://store.tremtech.com/greenlee-slug-buster-manual-knockout-punch-set/"]},{id:"torpedo-level",name:"Magnetic Torpedo Level",category:"Enclosure & labeling",whatItIs:"A short spirit level, usually six to ten inches, with a magnetic edge so it stays put on a steel frame or an enclosure while both your hands are busy. It is a trivial tool that does a disproportionate amount for how your work is judged, because a reader, keypad or door station mounted a couple of degrees out is the single most visible thing on the job. Nobody inspects your cable dressing. Everybody sees a crooked reader beside a door they walk through twice a day.",useCases:["Mounting readers, keypads and door stations plumb on a mullion, jamb or wall","Hanging a controller or power-supply enclosure square in a cupboard or riser","Levelling a camera bracket so the horizon is not tilted before you even start aiming","Setting a gate operator's mounting pad or post fittings","Checking a door or frame is actually plumb when you are diagnosing a latching or alignment complaint"],howToUse:"Mark, level, then drill... in that order, and resist the temptation to reverse the first two. Hold the level against the device or its template rather than against the wall, because the wall is quite often not plumb and it is the device the customer looks at. On a metal mullion the magnet does the holding for you, which is the whole reason to buy the magnetic version. For a reader with a back-plate, level the plate and mark all the holes before you drill any of them. On a camera, level the bracket and not the camera body, then let the camera's own adjustment take care of the fine aim.",proTips:["Level to what the eye compares it against. A reader beside a door frame should look parallel to the FRAME, and if the frame is visibly out of plumb then a technically level reader will look wrong to everybody who uses the door. Judge it against the reference the customer's eye uses, and say so in your notes if the frame is the problem.","Buy one with a magnet strong enough to hold on a vertical surface with a bit of grit on it, and check it against a known reference occasionally. A dropped level that reads half a degree out will quietly make everything you mount that month crooked in the same direction.","Tape a small level to a mullion reader while the adhesive or the first screw sets, and you buy yourself both hands back at the exact moment you need them."],sources:["https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/levels","https://www.greenlee.com/us/en"]},{id:"label-printer",name:"Handheld Label Printer",category:"Enclosure & labeling",whatItIs:"A portable thermal-transfer printer that produces durable, adhesive labels sized for cable, terminal blocks and equipment faces. The cable-specific consumables are what separate it from an office labeller: self-laminating wraps whose printed area is covered by a clear tail as it goes around the conductor, and flag labels for cable too thin to wrap legibly. It matters because documentation is a maintainability control, and on a system with dozens of near-identical pairs it is the only thing standing between the next technician and an afternoon of toning out runs somebody could have labelled in a minute.",useCases:["Labelling every cable at both ends as you terminate, which is when it costs nothing","Identifying doors, readers and zones on terminal blocks inside a controller enclosure","Marking power supply outputs and their voltages so nobody guesses on the next visit","Dating standby batteries and equipment so the replacement clock is visible to whoever opens the cabinet","Producing legend cards and panel identification for a handover pack"],howToUse:"Label as you terminate, at both ends, before the cable disappears into the wall or the bundle. TIA-606 is the standard worth borrowing even on a small job: every cable carries a unique identifier at each end, applied close to the termination... within about 300 mm... and the scheme identifies physical location, so building, room, cabinet, rack and port rather than a bare sequence number that means nothing to a stranger. Make the scheme match the drawings and the as-built exactly, because a label that disagrees with the documentation is worse than no label at all. Use self-laminating wraps on cable so the print survives handling, and put the label where it can be READ once the cable is dressed, not where it will end up face-down against a wall.",proTips:["Consistency beats cleverness. A dull scheme applied identically at every door on the site is worth more than an elegant one applied to half of them, because the value is entirely in being able to predict what a label will say before you find it.","Buy the printer that takes cable consumables and heat-shrink, and accept the cartridge cost. Office label tape on a cable in a warm riser curls and drops off within a couple of years, which means you paid for the labour and got none of the benefit.","Label the things people forget: supply outputs with their voltage, the battery with its install date, the controller with its address, and the enclosure with whose system it is and who to call. On an inherited site those four labels are the difference between a service call and an investigation."],sources:["https://www.bradyid.com/resources/tia-606-c-cable-labeling-standards","https://www.cablinginstall.com/cable/article/14035166/tia-606-c-standard-requirements-for-cable-plant-administration","https://silverfoxlabeling.com/blogs/article/tia-606-cable-labeling-compliance-guide"]},{id:"flex-installer-bit",name:"Flexible Installer Bit & Retrieving Grip",category:"Wire running",whatItIs:"A long spring-steel auger bit, commonly 54 or 72 inches, that flexes along its length so you can drill through a stud, a plate or a fire-block from inside a wall cavity and then pull the cable back through the hole you just made. The bit carries an eye near the tip; a retrieving grip clamps into it and holds the cable while you reverse the bit out. It is the tool that makes retrofit low-voltage work possible in a finished building, which is most of the access control, camera and intercom work you will ever do. Names vary... flexible installer bit, bell hanger bit, D'VersiBIT... same idea.",useCases:["Getting a reader or keypad cable from a door opening up into an accessible ceiling","Drilling a top or bottom plate from inside the cavity, where a rigid bit simply cannot be presented","Running a camera or door-station drop through a finished wall without opening it up","Fishing between floors or through a fire-block in an existing partition","Pulling the cable back in the same operation as the hole, rather than making the hole and then losing it"],howToUse:"Get the bit into the cavity, bow the shaft in the direction you want to travel, and let the self-feeding tip pull itself through... you are steering, not pushing. A placement tool clipped to the shaft lets you aim and control the bit inside the wall where you cannot see or reach it, and it is worth having. Once the tip breaks through and is visible or reachable at the far end, stop, fit the retrieving grip into the eye, clamp your cable into it, and run the drill in REVERSE to walk the bit back out, bringing the cable with it. Feed the cable in as it goes rather than letting it drag. Take the pull steadily: the grip holds well, but it holds a lot better when nothing is snatching at it.",proTips:["Know what is in the wall before the bit is. This tool drills through whatever it meets with real authority, and that includes line-voltage cable, plumbing and a fire-rated assembly you have just breached. Kill nearby circuits where there is any doubt, and if you penetrate a rated wall or floor, fire-stop it properly... that is a code obligation, not a courtesy.","The grip is not optional and it is the part people lose. Without it you have drilled a hole you cannot find again from the other side, and the whole advantage of the tool evaporates. Keep spares.","Let the tip do the feeding. Leaning into a flexible bit bows it further and further off your intended line until it exits somewhere you did not plan, which in a finished corridor is a patch-and-paint conversation with the customer."],sources:["https://www.greenlee.com/us/en/dversibit-kit-type-b-auger-dbitkit","https://www.licensedelectrician.com/Store/ET/Bell-Hanger-Installer-Bits.htm","https://www.drillbitwarehouse.com/product-category/long-drill-bits-aircraft-long-boys/bellhanger-drill-bits-long-reach-bits-up-to-72-inch/flexible-drill-bits/"]},{id:"wire-spool-caddy",name:"Wire Spool Caddy & Cable Reel Stand",category:"Wire running",whatItIs:"A frame that holds cable spools on axles so they pay out under control instead of being dragged off a box on the floor. On a job pulling several runs at once... which is every multi-door or multi-camera install... it is the difference between a tidy morning and a bird's nest. The failure it prevents is specific and expensive: cable that comes off a stationary spool in loops rather than off a turning one puts a twist into every foot you pull, and twisted cable kinks, and a kinked run has damaged geometry that a certifier will fail and a wiremap tester may not even catch.",useCases:["Pulling multiple reader, lock and camera runs from one point in a riser or ceiling","Feeding a long conduit pull where the cable has to move freely as somebody pulls at the far end","Running several different cable types at once and keeping them separated and identified","Working in a plant room or cupboard where boxes on the floor get walked on and dirty","Keeping part-used spools organised and re-usable rather than tangled into scrap"],howToUse:"Set the caddy where the cable will run straight to the first bend rather than immediately across an edge, and load the spools so they pay out from the top in the direction of pull. Check each spool spins freely before you start... a stiff axle is a twist generator. For a long pull, station somebody at the caddy to feed and watch, because the person on the far end cannot feel a spool starting to bind until it has already snatched. Keep the cable off sharp edges and away from anywhere it can rub as it moves, and never let a run drag across a metal stud edge under tension. Cut and label each run at the caddy end as you finish it rather than after they are all pulled and identical.",proTips:["Reel-payoff versus box-payoff is a real distinction. Cable designed to come out of a box does so without twist if you leave the box alone; cable on a spool must ROTATE. Mixing the two habits is how you get twist without noticing.","Respect the pull tension and the bend radius. Low-voltage cable is easy to over-pull because it feels light, and a run stretched around a tight corner is damaged in a way that produces intermittent faults nobody will ever trace back to the day it was installed.","A caddy also pays for itself in stock control. Part-used spools that go back on the van labelled and intact get used on the next job; the same cable dragged off a battered box ends up as offcuts nobody trusts."],sources:["https://www.greenlee.com/us/en","https://www.cablinginstall.com/cable/article/14035166/tia-606-c-standard-requirements-for-cable-plant-administration"]},{id:"non-contact-voltage-tester",name:"Non-Contact Voltage Tester",category:"Wire running",whatItIs:"A pen that lights and beeps near energised line-voltage conductors without touching them, sensing the AC field capacitively through insulation. Low-voltage work is not line-voltage work, but it takes place in exactly the same ceilings, walls, risers and cabinets, and you drill through them with a flexible bit that does not care what it meets. This is the cheapest tool in the bag and the one with the largest downside if you skip it.",useCases:["Checking a wall cavity or ceiling void for live conductors before you drill or fish through it","Confirming a circuit is actually dead after you have thrown what you believe is the right breaker","Checking an inherited enclosure for line voltage before you put your hands in it","Finding out whether an unlabelled cable in a bundle is line voltage before you cut it","Verifying a power supply's input side is isolated before you work on the transformer end"],howToUse:"Test the tester on a known live source first, every time, then test your target, then test the known live source again. That three-step habit is the whole discipline: a non-contact tester that has failed, or whose battery is flat, indicates exactly the same as a dead circuit, which is the one failure mode that can hurt you. Sweep slowly along the conductor rather than pointing at it once. Understand the limits before you trust it: it senses AC fields, so it will not see DC at all and it can be fooled by shielded or armoured cable, by conductors buried deep in a wall, and by induced voltage on a nearby dead conductor giving you a false positive. Treat a negative result as one input, not as proof.",proTips:["Never use it as the sole proof that something is safe to touch. Confirming dead means testing with a meter at the conductor where the work is being done. The pen tells you where to be careful; the meter tells you it is safe.","Induced voltage produces phantom indications on long parallel runs, and beginners chase these for hours. If the pen lights on a conductor you are confident is dead, meter it before you conclude anything.","If the work genuinely involves line voltage rather than avoiding it, know where your ticket ends. In most places the line side of a power supply is an electrician's work, and the fastest way to turn a low-voltage job into a serious problem is to treat the mains connection as an extension of your scope."],sources:["https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/non-contact-voltage-testers","https://www.fluke.com/en-us/products/electrical-testing/basic-testers"]}]},771,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,n,r,i){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(r,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(r,"GUIDES",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return s}});const s=[{id:"t-master-keying",title:"How to pin a master key system",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Let's get one thing straight before you ever touch a pinning kit: master keying is not about making a lock accept two keys. It is about making a lock accept two keys and refusing every other key that the math will hand you if you get sloppy. Nine times out of ten when a master system goes wrong, it is not the master key that fails \u2014 it is a change key three doors down that suddenly opens a room it has no business opening. That is called key interchange, and it is the ghost that haunts every system you build. Everything I am about to teach you exists to keep that ghost in the box. So slow down, draw the chart first, and cut brass second.\n\n[[diagram:master-key-shear-points|One chamber, two shear points... and why every extra level multiplies the keys that open the door]]\n\nStart with the pin stack, because that is where the whole thing lives or dies. In a standard pin-tumbler lock you have a bottom pin sitting on the key, a top pin (driver) pushed down by the spring, and the shear line between plug and shell where those two meet. One key, one break per chamber. To master a chamber you drop in a third piece \u2014 a master pin (in wafer locks a master wafer) that sits between the bottom pin and the driver, splitting the stack into two sections. Now that chamber has two places where the stack breaks at the shear line: one height for the change key, a second, different height for the master. The rule that keeps you honest all day: in every chamber, bottom pin plus master pin plus top pin must total the same overall stack height, because the shell and spring don't change \u2014 only where the breaks fall. If a master pin gets left behind in a chamber from a previous job, that stack is now too tall, and you will crush a driver spring or bind the plug \u2014 Locksmith Ledger flags exactly that, a leftover master pin adding to stack height and damaging driver springs. When you re-pin, dump the chamber completely and rebuild it. Don't reuse what you didn't count.\n\nHere is the arithmetic you actually do at the bench. Say Schlage, six chambers, .015\" per increment, depths 0 through 9. Your top master key \u2014 the TMK \u2014 is cut, say, 3 5 2 6 4 1. A change key under it might be cut 5 3 2 6 4 3. Go chamber by chamber and subtract to find the master pin. Chamber one: change is 5, master is 3, difference of 2 \u2014 so the bottom pin is a #5 (the deeper cut lifts less), you drop a #2 master pin on top of it, and the driver makes up the rest. Chamber two: change 3, master 5, difference 2 again \u2014 bottom pin #3, master pin #2. Chamber three: both keys are cut 2 \u2014 no difference, so no master pin. That chamber is a constant. Chamber four both cut 6, constant. Chamber five both cut 4, constant. Chamber six: change 3, master 1, difference 2 \u2014 bottom #1, master #2. So that lock takes a two-piece master segment in three of six chambers and runs solid brass in the other three. The change key lifts every stack to its own break; the master lifts each mastered chamber to the second break; both turn. That is the whole trick, repeated across every door in the building.\n\nNow the hierarchy, so you speak the language and don't hand a customer the wrong key. Bottom level is the change key (CK) \u2014 it opens one door or one keyed-alike group and nothing else. Above it, a master key (MK) opens a defined group of those doors. That is a two-level system, the simplest master setup there is. Tie two or more masters together under one higher key and you have a grand master key (GMK) \u2014 a three-level system. Tie several grands together and the key over all of them is the great grand master (GGM), a four-level system. Stop there. ASSA ABLOY's design guide says it flat out: levels higher than four are not recommended for security reasons \u2014 with more levels, additional master pins are required in each chamber of the cylinder, and with those additional master pins the possibility of unauthorized key interchange goes up. That is the same rule from the pin stack, just at building scale: more master pins means more shear-line breaks, which means more chances for a key that shouldn't turn to find a combination that does. More cuts that work is the exact opposite of what you want in a lock.\n\nLay the chart out like an org chart, top down, before you cut anything. Standard key symbols keep it readable: masters get two letters and usually start at the front of the alphabet (AA is your first master), and in any system above two levels the change keys hang under their master with the number coming last (AA1, AA2, AA3). In a grand system the grand master gets a single letter (A), each master under it starts with that grand's letter (AA, AB, AC), and change numbers come last. Skip the letters I, O, and Q entirely \u2014 the guide calls them out because they read as 1 and 0 on a smudged tag and get keys cut wrong at 4:55 on a Friday. If you outrun 23 masters under one grand, you insert a rotation number between the letters \u2014 AA through AZ for the first 23, then A2A through A2Z for the 24th through 46th, and so on. Key similar core areas alike: one symbol for all electrical closets, one for all pipe chases, one for all mechanical rooms, so you are not handing maintenance a fistful of masters. And keep the Grand Master \"pie\" in your head \u2014 the system is one pie, and every big slice you cut (a top-heavy pile of masters, a huge cross-keyed group) is pie you can never get back for future expansion. Fewer, cleaner masters always beat many small ones, because they leave you more room to grow.\n\nThe mechanical ceiling on all of this is MACS \u2014 Maximum Adjacent Cut Specification \u2014 the biggest jump you are allowed between two neighboring cuts on a key. Blow past it and the key physically won't seat or won't pull back out, because a deep cut next to a shallow one lets the fat part of one pin ride up the ramp of the next. For Schlage conventional and full-size keyways, MACS is 7 \u2014 measured in those .015\" increments \u2014 and the illegal adjacent pairs are 0-8, 0-9, and 1-9 (and their reverses). Schlage small-format IC has no MACS restriction at all; every adjacent pair is fair game. Falcon conventional runs a tighter MACS of 5, so more pairs are off-limits \u2014 0-6, 0-7, 0-8, 0-9, 1-7, 1-8, 1-9, 2-8, 2-9, and 3-9 (Falcon small format, like Schlage's, has none). Know your keyway's number cold before you progress, because here is the sting: MACS applies to the change key AND to the master. You can generate a change bitting that is perfectly legal on its own, but once you drop the master pins in, the master key's own cut sequence must also pass MACS \u2014 and every incidental key the stacking creates must either work cleanly or fail cleanly. This is why theoretical numbers always get reduced in the real world: ASSA ABLOY spells out the order \u2014 the raw count comes down first by MACS, then further by cross-keying and the use of multiple masters. Always quote the customer real expansion numbers per level, never percentages, and don't \"go wild\" guessing \u2014 that language is straight out of the guide.\n\nProgression is how you generate the change keys under a master without stepping on each other, and here you must run two-step, not one-step, on Schlage-type systems. With .015\" increments, a chamber cut one step off can still turn a worn lock with a little jiggle \u2014 the tolerance is just too tight. So you progress in twos: for a given progressed chamber, the change-key depths available are two increments apart (say 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 in one family, or 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 in the other), never one apart. That is also the reason you rarely see a #1 master pin in a properly built Schlage system \u2014 a single .015\" segment is thin enough to tip and jam sideways between plug and shell, so you build in twos and keep your smallest master pin at #2. Lay it out as a Key Bitting Array: rows are the progression steps, columns are the chambers you are progressing. Run four change bittings progressing under the master across a six-pin system and that is 4 to the 6th \u2014 4,096 theoretical change keys, which is exactly the number Locksmith Ledger works out for a standard six-pin master key system \u2014 before MACS and cross-keying knock it down. You do NOT progress every chamber. The chambers that stay constant across the whole master group carry the master; the chambers you progress carry the differences. Pick which chambers are constants and which progress at the very start, write it down on the KBA, and never guess at the bench.\n\nNow the two failures that get locksmiths fired, both a species of key interchange. The first is the incidental master, also called a ghost key or one-pin master. If one change key happens to share one extra constant chamber with the master beyond what its siblings share, it becomes accidentally more powerful \u2014 that key with one extra constant, the \"one-pin master,\" typically operates four different locks instead of one. That is not a feature; it is a hole. It comes straight out of shoebox master keying \u2014 grabbing pinned-up cylinders at random and hoping the constants don't line up. Ralph Forrest-Ball, the CML who wrote the book on this, says it plainly and he's right: shoebox master keying relies on luck to avoid key interchange, and luck runs out sooner than you think. The cure is a disciplined progression from a documented KBA and a bitting list you keep current \u2014 a stale bitting list is itself a top cause of interchange, because you reissue a bitting that's already live somewhere else in the building, and as Forrest-Ball warns, it might have happened already and you just don't know it yet.\n\nThe second failure is cross-keying, and you need to understand the two flavors because one of them is a slow-motion disaster. Cross-keying means a cylinder is deliberately set to accept keys from outside its own set \u2014 you denote it with an X prefix and list every key that operates it. Controlled cross-keying keeps all those extra keys under the same higher-level master \u2014 annoying but contained. Uncontrolled cross-keying mixes keys from different masters or different grands into one cylinder (AA1 and AB1 both operating one door), and that is where you lose your shirt: the guide is blunt that it normally results in unplanned key interchange and reduces overall system capability, because it forces extra master pins into that cylinder's chambers, manufacturing shear-line breaks nobody planned. Lose one master in that setup and you lose all its changes; lose a grand and you can lose thousands of combinations across the system. Two clean cases to keep straight, both right out of ASSA ABLOY: if a cross-keyed door still needs its own dedicated change key \u2014 like a shared reception desk between two doctors' offices \u2014 the cylinder gets its own change symbol with the X prefix, written \"XAA3 operated by AA1, AA2, AA and A.\" If it does NOT need its own key \u2014 like a dorm hall door that only needs the students' bedroom keys to also work it \u2014 you bracket the number with X's on both sides, written \"X1X operated by AA1 through AA6, AA and A,\" and cut no separate key for it. Either way you write it down and summarize it at the beginning of the order, because the next locksmith has to know it exists.\n\nGHOST KEYS \u2014 THE ONES YOU NEVER CUT. Here is the trap living inside every master pin, and it answers to a dozen names: ghost keys, phantom keys, incidental keys, cross keys. When you master-pin a chamber you give it two working positions \u2014 the change cut and the master cut \u2014 and the plug turns the instant EVERY chamber finds a shear line, at ANY mix of those positions, not just the two you cut. Take two master-pinned chambers where the change key is 3-4 and the master is 5-6: that lock ALSO opens on 3-6 and on 5-4, two keys nobody ever filed, born free from the pinning. The math is brutal. With n master-pinned chambers there are 2^n working combinations; two of them are your real keys and the other 2^n minus 2 are ghosts, and every level of masterkeying you stack (grand master, great-grand master) multiplies the count into the thousands. That is the real reason not to over-masterkey: every extra master pin is more phantom keys that open that lock, more ways a decoded pair of keys becomes a working master, more accidental interchange between doors that were never meant to share. You cannot kill ghosts in a master system \u2014 two shear lines per chamber is the deal you signed the moment you master-keyed it \u2014 but you can shrink them: keep pin counts sane, choose the bitting deliberately, hold MACS so the ghost cuts aren't even cuttable, and never masterkey deeper than the building actually needs. (For how an attacker turns a couple of ordinary keys INTO those ghosts and climbs to the top master key, see the master-key system attacks guide.)\n\nWhich brings me to the judgment call, the thing the pin kit can't teach you. Every convenience the customer asks for \u2014 one huge complicated system, extensive cross-keying, a stock keyway anybody can duplicate, a pile of selective masters, no key control \u2014 buys them convenience by spending security and expansion. Every security move \u2014 small unrelated systems, patented or restricted keyways, security or high-security cylinders, real key control with signed key receipts \u2014 costs a little daily convenience. The guide puts it in one line worth memorizing: the simplest keying systems are often the most secure and will last longer than complicated ones. So when the property manager wants everybody's key to open everything \"just in case,\" don't cross-key the building into swiss cheese \u2014 supply extra keys instead, which is exactly what ASSA ABLOY recommends over jeopardizing the integrity of the system. Extra brass is cheap; a compromised master system that has to be re-pinned top to bottom is not. And keep the liability instinct alive on the life-safety side: master keying is about who gets IN, but never let a keying scheme leave a fire door or an egress path depending on a key to get OUT \u2014 under NFPA 101 that stays free-egress no matter whose master opens it from the corridor. Draw the chart, pin to the chart, document the chart, and the ghost stays in the box.",sources:["https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-maximum-adjacent-cut-specification-or-macs/","https://lsamichigan.org/Tech/Master_Key_System_Design_Guide_ASSA-ABLOY.pdf","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10229457/masterkeying-basics-lock-cylinders","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10290193/the-concepts-and-mechanics-of-master-keying-developing-master-keys-using-generic-types","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/article/21291219/five-common-mistakes-in-master-keying-how-to-fix-them"],relatedGuides:["aloa-ladder","institutional-ila","master-key-attacks"],tags:["Cross-keying","Change key","MACS","Progression","Shell","Ghost key","Shear line","Bitting"],aliases:["master key conflict","keyed alike","operating key","user key","day key","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","MK system","pin chart","housing","lock body","cylinder body","phantom key","unintended key","cross key","MK collision","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","lockout","locked out","get in","emergency opening"]},{id:"t-sfic-pinning",title:"Pinning an SFIC / Best control system",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Here is the thing that trips up every locksmith who came up on conventional cylinders and then meets a Best figure-8 core for the first time: an SFIC is not a plug in a shell. It is a plug in a sleeve in a shell, and that means it has two shear lines, not one, stacked one on top of the other in the same pin chambers. Get that picture in your head before you touch a pin. The lower break \u2014 between the plug and the sleeve \u2014 is the operating shear line. That is the one the change key, master key, and grand master lift the stack to, and it is what actually turns the lug or throw member to run the lock. The upper break \u2014 between the sleeve and the shell (the housing) \u2014 is the control shear line. That is the one the control key lifts to, and lifting to it retracts the control lug so the whole core slides out of the housing. Those are the exact names the manuals use \u2014 operating shear line for the plug-to-sleeve break, control shear line for the sleeve-to-shell break. You will also hear \"build\" or \"build-up\" line used loosely for the control break, because a build-up pin is what makes that second line exist. Same chamber, two jobs. Burn that in.\n\nBecause there are two shear lines to satisfy in one chamber, a fully keyed SFIC chamber runs four pins, not three. From the bottom up: bottom pin, master pin, build-up pin (the manuals also call it the control pin), top pin. The bottom pin and master pin do the same job they do in any master-keyed cylinder \u2014 they set the change cut and the master cut at the operating line. The build-up pin is the SFIC's special animal. It is the segment that spans the gap between the operating shear line and the control shear line, and its whole reason for existing is to put a break at the control line so the control key can find it. The top pin sits above that and, with its spring, holds everything down. If a chamber is not master keyed you may only run three pins (bottom, build-up, top), but the build-up pin is never optional in a keyed SFIC chamber \u2014 kill the build-up pin and you have killed the control function.\n\nNow the number that governs the whole system: the total pin stack in a Best/A2 core must add up to a coded stack of 23. That is not a suggestion, that is the law of the A2 system, straight out of Best's design and echoed by Schlage, Sargent, and every other A2 maker. Every chamber \u2014 bottom + master + build-up + top \u2014 sums to 23 in coded units. That is why the classic service-manual mnemonic you should memorize runs like this: Control cut + 10 \u2212 Plug Total = Build-up, then Top Pin = whatever is left to make the stack total 23. Let me walk it. \"Plug Total\" is the bottom pin plus the master pin \u2014 that is everything sitting below the operating shear line, i.e. the depth of your deepest operating (change or master) key in that chamber. Take the control key's cut in that chamber, add 10, subtract the plug total, and you get the build-up pin. Then whatever is left to reach 23 is the top pin. The \"+10\" is the A2 constant \u2014 in an A2 system the control cut operates 10 increments above the operating cut, which is exactly the physical distance from the operating shear line up to the control shear line expressed in pin steps. That +10 is the heartbeat of A2 control keying. (Every A-system has its own constant paired to its stack total: A2 is +10 to 23, A3 is +7 to 16, A4 is +6 to 14. Match the constant to the stack total or your control line lands in the wrong place.)\n\nThe A2 numbers you are working in: ten depths, 0 through 9, on a .0125-inch increment between adjacent depths, .108-inch pin diameter, two-step progression, cut tip-to-bow. Coded stack 23. That is the Best A2 world and it covers the overwhelming majority of what you will ever open \u2014 Best, Falcon, Arrow, KSP, Medeco SFIC, Sargent, and Schlage/Everest small format cores all live here. And here is a bonus that surprises people: A2 has no maximum-adjacent-cut restriction \u2014 the MACS is a full 0 through 9, so you can drop a 0 cut right next to a 9 with no adverse effect. That is why a 6-pin A2 master system generates the full 4,096 change keys with every combination usable; the two-step A2 design is what buys you that huge, clean change count.\n\nA3 and A4 are the other two systems and you need to know why they exist and why you mostly will not use them. A3 was Best's own attempt at a single-step master system on a .018-inch increment with a coded stack of 16 (constant +7); it proved mechanically impractical and got discontinued. Leave A3 alone unless you are servicing a legacy system already built in it. A4 is the survivor for legacy big systems: a single-step master system on a .021-inch increment with a coded stack of 14 (constant +6). The bigger increment and the single-step progression trade away the enormous change count that two-step A2 gives you, in exchange for coarser, more forgiving separation \u2014 Arrow and Best still support A4 for additions to old A4 systems, but nobody specs it new. Nine times out of ten, if somebody hands you an unmarked core and asks what system it is, it is A2 \u2014 A2 is the default the factories ship when nothing is specified, and it is what your standard A2 pin kit is for.\n\nHow do you tell them apart on the bench when you do not know? Decode the core, cut a key to the operating cuts, and read the increment behavior \u2014 but the fast tell is stack math. If your legitimate stacks are summing to 23 you are in A2; 16 means A3; 14 means A4. Do not mix pin kits between systems \u2014 an A2 pin dropped into an A4 stack, or the reverse, will not shear where it should, and you will chase a \"phantom binding\" ghost for an hour. Keep your A2 kit and your A4 kit physically separated and labeled. This is a real, common source of grief.\n\nLet me talk about the control key \u2014 the \"capping\" or core key \u2014 as a physical and a liability object, because this is where apprentices get sloppy and where you can actually get somebody hurt. The control key exists to remove and install cores. That is its only job. It rotates the plug just enough to lift to the control shear line and pull the lug in \u2014 on most SFIC designs you turn it clockwise, and the lug retracts so the core drops out into your palm or your core-removal tool. It is cut to the control bitting, which is why it will not run the lockset: the control cuts land at the control line, not the operating line, so the plug retracts the lug instead of throwing the bolt. Rule you never break: the control key must never operate the lock, and the operating keys must never pull a core. If a \"master key\" is pulling cores, somebody built the system wrong and you have a security hole the size of a truck \u2014 the whole point of interchangeable core is that facilities can hand out operating keys freely while the control key stays locked in the manager's safe, because whoever holds control can pull every core in the building and re-key the place in an afternoon. Treat the control key like the crown jewels. On a fire or egress opening that matters double \u2014 a mis-pinned core that binds and will not throw the latch is not just a callback, it is a life-safety failure, and \"the control cuts were in the operating stack\" is not a defense you want to give an inspector.\n\nCapping is the last step and the one that separates the pros from the guys with pins on the floor. Once you have your stacks figured, you load the core into a capping block \u2014 a fixture that holds the core square and presents each chamber. Springs and pins go in, then you set your caps. Chamber by chamber, you set the capping tool over the chamber and strike it with a plastic or rawhide mallet \u2014 never a steel hammer. A steel hammer peens your capping pin and eventually wrecks the tool; more immediately it will drive a cap too deep, and a cap set too deep binds the top pin so the key drags or won't pull. Best practice is to seat and drive one cap fully before moving to the next, because the jolt of the mallet loves to make an adjacent loose cap jump right back out of its chamber \u2014 set them all loose and hit one, and you will be picking caps off the bench. Watch your cap style, too: machined caps are flat top and bottom, while stamped caps have a slight taper on one side that helps them start into the chamber under the hammer. Old Best cores use pressed caps that you seal once; a lot of aftermarket and CX5-type cores use reusable screw caps, and a shop can also run a capping press with a cap strip for speed and consistency. Reusable caps are a gift for a shop that re-keys the same system a lot.\n\nNow the mistakes, in the order they will bite you. Number one, forgetting the build-up pin entirely or figuring it wrong \u2014 you build a beautiful operating stack, cap it, and the control key won't pull the core because there is no break at the control line. Number two, not summing to the system total \u2014 23 in A2, 16 in A3, 14 in A4. If an A2 stack sums to 22 or 21 you have effectively shortened the column; the cap sits proud or the top pin has slop, and you get a mushy operating line or a core that picks itself. There is very little leeway here, so a stack that is one or two short is not \"close enough,\" it is a callback. Number three, losing track of which bitting is the control cut when you write the stack \u2014 the fix the manual itself teaches is to write the control key bitting across the top of your pinning chart and underline it so you never confuse a control cut for an operating cut mid-calculation. Number four, cross-contaminating pin systems as I said above \u2014 an A2 pin in an A4 stack shears in the wrong place. Number five \u2014 and this one is pure field discipline \u2014 before you shove a core back into a housing, make dead sure the control lug is retracted (control key in, turned) and there is nothing loose in the chamber. Ram a core home with the lug extended or a spring cocked sideways and you will jam it, and now you are drilling out your own work. Turn any key ninety degrees, pull it, confirm the passage down the chambers is clear, then seat the core. Do it in that order every single time and SFIC becomes the easiest, most profitable re-key work you own \u2014 pull the core, swap or re-pin on the bench, drop it back, hand the customer new operating keys, and the control stays in the safe.\n\nKEYWAYS & KEY BLANKS\n\nA BEST core is cut to a keyway \u2014 the warding that decides which blank will enter.\n\n[[diagram:best-keyways|BEST / SFIC keyways & keys]]\n\nThe single-section keyways run A through R (the commonly stocked ones are A, C, F, G and M), plus DD and multi-milled sections. Each blank is numbered 1A1[keyway]1 \u2014 a plain A-keyway six-pin blank is 1A1A1, a C is 1A1C1. Seven-pin cores take seven-pin blanks; a six-pin key seats too shallow in a seven-pin core and a seven-pin key jams in a six-pin. Within one keyway the system is built by bitting: the control key slides the core out at the control shear line, a master opens many cores, a change key opens one. To master across keyways you step up to a multiplex blank milled for several sections at once \u2014 e.g. 1A1ADEFG1 passes A, D, E, F and G cores on one key.\n\n[[diagram:cylinder-bodies-and-cores|The three separate choices behind any cylinder order: keying platform, core format, body style]]",sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/cylinders-high-security/article/10228229/mastering-ic-pin-stack-mathematics","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/12440229/interchangeable-core-pinning-guide","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/document/10706797/a2-a3-and-a4-interchangeable-core-pin-sizes-and-dimensions","http://www.sopl.us/uploads/1/3/0/1/1301029/keynotes_apr08.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_Small_Format_Cores_Service_Manual_108230.pdf"],relatedModels:["best-sfic"],tags:["SFIC","Control key","Shear line","Shell","Change key","Progression","Bitting","Plug"],aliases:["small format interchangeable core","IC core","core removal key","pull key","IC control","picks open","won't set","line of separation","lock body","cylinder body","operating key","user key","day key","master key system","MK system","pin chart","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","maximum adjacent cut","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"t-lfic-everest",title:"Schlage LFIC & Everest keyways",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Let's get the vocabulary straight first, because half the confusion on this stuff is people using the wrong word. Schlage's full-size interchangeable core goes by two names that mean the same thing: FSIC (Full Size Interchangeable Core) and LFIC (Large Format Interchangeable Core). Schlage uses both terms in its own literature \u2014 the product line is titled FSIC, but the same pages talk about \"LFIC formats\" for the Sargent and Corbin Russwin versions, and the part number you'll live with on the standard Schlage-format core is the 23-030. It is NOT the same animal as the little Best-style figure-8 core you drop into a mortise cylinder with a control key \u2014 that's SFIC, small format, and Schlage sells that too. The tell is the shape and the pin count. A small-format core is the true figure-8 \"snowman,\" it runs 7 pins on the A2 keying standard, and because SFIC is an industry-standard format everybody builds to, small-format cores are broadly cross-compatible between brands. The Schlage large-format core is two stacked cylinders \u2014 a lower plug body that's slightly fatter than the upper \u2014 and in standard trim it's a 6-pin core (there's a 7-pin SL variant in the restricted R keyway, but 6 is what you'll see day to day). Here's the part that trips people: don't say \"LFIC never crosses brands.\" A standard Schlage-format 23-030 won't drop into a Sargent or Corbin Russwin housing, true \u2014 but Schlage MAKES large-format cores in Sargent format and Corbin Russwin format specifically to retrofit those locks and migrate a building over to a Schlage key system. That's exactly why the combinating manual carries separate Corbin Russwin and Sargent pinning examples. So when a spec calls out \"IC,\" your first job on the truck is to eyeball whether it's the small figure-8 or the big two-lobe Schlage core, and then which housing format you're feeding, because the control keys, the housings, and the pinning math all change with it.\n\n[[diagram:ic-core-control-key|Two shear lines in one plug: the operating line turns the lock, the control line takes the core out]]\n\nNow the LFIC control key, because this is where apprentices strip cores and panic. You do NOT pull a Schlage large-format core straight out. Insert the control key, rotate 15 degrees until it stops, and now the key will pull the core out of the housing. But get the DIRECTION right, because it's format-dependent and the draft version of this rule that says \"always counter-clockwise, memorize it\" will burn you. On a standard Schlage-format core the LFIC service manual has you rotate 15 degrees counter-clockwise to remove and 15 clockwise to reinstall \u2014 but that same manual footnotes it plainly: for Corbin Russwin and Sargent cylinders you rotate 15 degrees CLOCKWISE to remove. And the Everest full-size FSIC manual describes the control key doing its work with a 15-degree clockwise turn. So the honest rule is: it's 15 degrees, it's small \u2014 you are not turning it a quarter like a cam \u2014 and the direction rides on which housing format you're in, so try one way, and if it stops dead with no release, try the other before you assume the core is jammed. The control key on a large-format Schlage differs from a small-format control key in HOW it does the work: the extended tip of the LFIC control key operates a control pin and retracts a control lug inside the housing so the whole core releases. If somebody hands you a \"control key\" and it's a standard-length blade with no tip feature, it's an SFIC control \u2014 wrong tool for a big core. Nine times out of ten when a core won't come out with the control key, it's one of three things: wrong keyway family so the key won't seat fully, wrong rotation direction for that housing format, or the previous guy jammed a change key in and bent something. Check keyway and direction before you go grabbing pliers.\n\nThe pinning math on a large-format core is its own discipline and it's genuinely different from a standard cylinder, so slow down here. There are two shear lines in each of the control chambers \u2014 one for operating keys (change, master, grand master) and one for the control key \u2014 and a build-up pin bridges the gap between them. On a Schlage-format LFIC the control chambers vary by the housing design: for Corbin Russwin cores the control chambers are positions 2 and 3, and for Sargent cores they're positions 3 and 4. The rule for those control chambers is the Rule of 23: every control chamber pin stack must total 23. The working formula Schlage prints in the manual is Control + 10 \u2212 Plug Total + Build-Up, and the whole stack height comes to 23. For every OTHER chamber \u2014 the standard ones \u2014 you fall back to the Schlage-15 rule, the same total-of-15 logic you use on a conventional Schlage cylinder. Practical habit straight from the manual: combinate one chamber completely before moving to the next, treat each as a little math problem, and never try to load all the bottoms, then all the masters, then all the build-ups \u2014 Schlage flat-out warns that method breeds mistakes and forces you to write everything down instead of building speed. And a hard warning, verbatim from the book: never remove the plug from an LFIC core unless it's a Primus. These cores are designed to be top-loaded through the cap, it's blind addition-only work with no trial-and-error at the shear line, and popping the plug on a non-Primus just puts pins on the floor. With the plug turned you can watch the tops of the stacks line up evenly, and that's how you catch a pinning error before you cap.\n\nMove over to the Everest side, because \"Everest\" is a keyway/security story layered on top of whatever cylinder format you're in \u2014 it shows up in conventional cylinders, in the 23-030 FSIC, and in SFIC. What makes an Everest cylinder an Everest is two things: the keyway itself and the check pin. Except for that keyway and the special pin that checks for the lip on the key, an Everest cylinder is virtually identical to a Schlage Classic C-keyway cylinder \u2014 same platform that's been around since 1935. The check pin is the piece that trips people up during service. It's a spring-loaded pin sitting in a special chamber on the BOTTOM of the plug, and there's a corresponding hole in the cylinder shell for it. The Everest key has a lip milled on the side of the blade; when the correct key is inserted, that lip raises the check pin and holds it up in the plug so the plug can rotate. Wrong key, no lip, check pin stays down engaging the shell \u2014 plug won't turn. The critical service gotcha: when you push the plug out to rekey, the KEY is what holds the check pin and its spring captive. Keep the key in the plug while you swap bottom pins. The instant you pull the key with the plug out of the shell, hold that check pin in place with your finger or it and the spring launch \u2014 and that pin isn't in your standard Schlage pin kit; it's a separate service part (check pin 34-007, check pin spring C603-951), so carry a few. That's also why, after capping, Schlage tells you to spray lube into the keyway and cycle a key several times: it's the only way to lube that side check pin.\n\nThe keyway families are the part you have to get right for ordering and for key-control liability, so commit these to memory. The original Everest ran B, C, and D families. B came first \u2014 the small-format SFIC family, introduced November 1998 to retrofit 7-pin Best-style IC \u2014 and the full-size C and D families followed shortly after. In August 2012 Schlage rolled out Everest 29 with a revised undercut, and the \"29\" refers to the utility patent expiry: the new undercut groove is protected by U.S. utility patent 7,665,337 through February 18, 2029. (Don't hang \"2029\" on the design patent \u2014 D689,759 covers the key bow shape and runs out in 2026. It's the utility patent that gives you the 2029 key control, and the original Everest C family leaned on utility patents 5,715,717 and 5,809,816.) Everest 29 comes as R, S, and T families, and here's the clean mapping: Everest 29 R, S, and T are backward compatible with legacy Everest B, C, and D respectively. Now the restricted-versus-open distinction, which is the whole point commercially. S is the open family (full size, general use) \u2014 like the old C, an S-keyway blank can be stocked and duplicated by any locksmith or contract hardware dealer with no paperwork, and S is the default keyway on all new Schlage commercial product and master key systems; its job is just to keep box-store blanks out and guarantee genuine Schlage replacements. R (restricted, small format) and D/T (restricted, full size) are tightly controlled: blanks, cut keys, cylinders and cores ship only through authorized Schlage distributors, and only when the end user attaches a letter of authorization to the PO. Restricted product is drop-shipped straight to the end user or a location they authorize, so the dealer never has loose access to keys and cores, and as a further deterrent Schlage stamps a facility code or locksmith ID on the keys and blanks so a duplicate's origin is traceable. When a customer says \"I want keys nobody can copy at the hardware store,\" you're steering them to R or T and signing a key-control contract, not selling them S.\n\nOne more thing that will bite you if you don't know it \u2014 the undercut and Primus XP. The undercut groove is milled into the RIGHT side of the Everest/Everest 29 key blade, and it takes a secondary milling operation to cut, which is exactly what makes the blank patent-protected and a federal patent violation for anyone but Schlage to manufacture. But note this: on all CONVENTIONAL Everest and Everest 29 keys, a portion of that undercut groove is deliberately milled away. That's intentional \u2014 it prevents a conventional Everest key from being altered to run an Everest Primus XP or Everest 29 Primus XP cylinder. Primus is the high-security tier with the sidebar and finger pins; the conventional undercut is cut short so nobody grinds a plain Everest key into a Primus operator. Open S keyways (and legacy B/C/D) can be upgraded to patented, restricted control by moving to Primus XP cylinders and keys. Practically: if you're servicing a cylinder with sidebar chambers and finger pins, stop and grab the Primus service manual \u2014 different pin kit, different rules, and you never pop that plug the way you would a plain cylinder.\n\nNow the numbers you'll actually cut to, and these are the same for Schlage Classic, Everest, and Primus XP, which is the beauty of the platform \u2014 one code system. Ten depths, 0 through 9. Two-step progression, .015 inch per step (you'll see \".15\" printed on the old C45 code card \u2014 that's a long-standing typo on the card; the real increment between depths is .015). Root depths run from the shallowest, a 0 cut at .335 inch, to the deepest, a 9 cut at .200 inch \u2014 so .335, .320, .305, .290, .275, .260, .245, .230, .215, .200. MACS \u2014 maximum adjacent cut specification \u2014 is 7, meaning no two neighboring cuts can differ by more than 7 depth increments or the key won't clear the chambers smoothly. Blade width is .343 inch, depth tolerance is plus .002 to minus 0, and spacing tolerance is plus or minus .001 inch. That tight minus-zero depth tolerance matters: you have room on the deep side and none on the shallow, so if your machine drifts, drift it deep-safe not shallow. And on the restricted families, watch your punch: the S/C open sections and Classic obverse run on the standard blue punch (Schlage 40-075), but the D-family and Everest 29 T-family restricted keys have to be cut on the 40-099 punch because the top of that key blade isn't straight and a standard punch can't hold it \u2014 the small-format B/R sections use yet another punch (40-071). Don't fight a D or T blank on a jaw meant for straight-topped C and S sections.\n\nRepair-versus-replace and pricing judgment on this gear. LFIC's whole reason for existing is fast rekey without pulling hardware \u2014 that's what you sell to a property manager with tenant turnover or a campus: one control key, pull the core, drop a fresh one, done in seconds at the door, and you recombinate at the bench on your schedule. So on a building already on LFIC, you almost never \"replace the lock\" \u2014 you rekey or swap the core. The money is in the core inventory and the key-control system, not tearing out locksets. If a customer is on open C or S and getting keys copied at the hardware store against their wishes, the upsell is a migration to a restricted D/T (full size) or R (small format) system, or a jump to Primus XP if they need drill/pick resistance and sidebar-grade control. Be honest about the friction: restricted means they either sign a key-control contract themselves or route every duplicate through you with a letter of authorization \u2014 some customers love the control, some hate the paperwork, and you want them choosing that with eyes open. And carry the check pin and spring in your Everest service parts, plus FinLub TF or a comparable lube, because the number-one Everest callback is a plug that binds or a lost check pin from somebody who rekeyed it like a plain Classic cylinder and didn't know the pin was down there.",sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_LFIC_Service_Manual_015806.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_Everest_Full_Size_Cylinder_Service_Manual_108484.pdf","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/fsic-full-size-interchangeable-cores.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/small-format-interchangeable-cores.html","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/everest-29-keyways.html"],tags:["LFIC","Control key","Keyway","SFIC","Shell","Plug","Change key","Facility code"],aliases:["Schlage Everest","removable core","core removal key","pull key","IC control","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","small format interchangeable core","Falcon","A4","IC core","lock body","cylinder body","barrel","key plug","key spins","operating key","user key","day key","site code","FC","card range","wrong facility code","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","master key system","MK system","pin chart","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation"]},{id:"t-smart-locks",title:"Smart locks: what a locksmith must know",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Let me get one thing straight before we touch a screwdriver: a smart lock is still a lock. All the app talk, the push notifications, the \"unlock with your Apple Watch\" \u2014 that's the front end. Behind it is a bolt, a cylinder, a spring, and a motor, and every one of those things fails the same boring mechanical way a dumb lock does. The customer bought the radio. You're getting called because of the bolt binding on a misaligned strike, or a dead battery, or a cylinder that turns but doesn't retract. Fix the mechanical door first, always. Nine times out of ten the \"smart lock is broken\" call is a door that's dragging, a strike that's a sixteenth off, or four batteries at 20 percent \u2014 not the firmware. Rack the door, check the strike alignment, check the bolt throws freely with the door open before you ever open the app.\n\n[[diagram:smart-lock-radios-and-fork|The three radios, retrofit against native, and the cylinder still under all of it]]\n\nStart with the three radios, because they drive everything about install, support, and what breaks. Wi-Fi is the one homeowners want \u2014 it talks straight to your router and out to the cloud, so you get remote control and notifications with no hub. Cost of that: it's a battery hog. A Wi-Fi deadbolt like the Schlage Encode or Encode Plus runs four AA cells and Schlage rates it around six months typical use \u2014 and be honest with the customer, real-world it's often three to four months on a busy door; a comparable Z-Wave lock like the Kwikset SmartCode 916 sips power and Kwikset puts it around a year or better on the same four AAs. That difference is the radio staying awake to hold a Wi-Fi association versus a mesh node that wakes, beacons, and sleeps. Z-Wave and Zigbee are low-power mesh \u2014 they need a hub or panel (SmartThings, a Ring/Alarm.com panel, Hubitat, Home Assistant with a stick), but they're rock-solid for whole-house automation and they don't sweat your router firmware. US Z-Wave runs sub-GHz \u2014 the classic North American channel sits around 908.4 MHz \u2014 which punches through walls far better than 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, and the newer 700/800-series silicon and Z-Wave Long Range stretch range and battery further still. Zigbee sits up at 2.4 GHz and fights your Wi-Fi and microwave for airtime (note the 916 comes in both a Z-Wave and a Zigbee variant \u2014 check which one the customer's hub speaks). Then there's BLE \u2014 Bluetooth Low Energy \u2014 which is in basically every one of these locks as the setup and local-fallback radio. BLE is short range, call it 20 to 30 feet line of sight and a lot less through a solid slab door, which is exactly why \"auto-unlock\" reads your phone as you walk up but won't let you check the lock from work. If a lock is BLE-only (older August, Level Bolt, entry-level units), the customer needs a plug-in Wi-Fi bridge for anything remote. And know where the market's going: Matter is the new common language \u2014 Thread is a low-power 802.15.4 mesh, Zigbee's cousin, and Matter is the app-layer standard riding on top so a lock pairs to Apple Home, Google, Alexa, and SmartThings without picking sides. Level Lock+ and Aqara's U200 run Matter over Thread today; Yale's Assure 2 gets Matter through its swappable module. When a customer asks \"will it work with my system,\" the honest answer is: match the radio to their hub, and if they've got no hub and just want their phone, put Wi-Fi or Matter-over-Thread on it.\n\nNow the fork that decides half your installs: retrofit versus native. A retrofit lock \u2014 the classic August Wi-Fi Smart Lock, the Level Bolt \u2014 mounts on the inside only and drives the customer's existing deadbolt. You keep their exterior trim, their cylinder, their keys. August replaces just the interior thumbturn and motors the existing bolt; Level Bolt is even sneakier, it drops the whole motor assembly inside the door bore between the outside cylinder and the inside turn so from the street it looks like a plain deadbolt, running off a single 3-volt CR2 cell tucked in the bolt itself, roughly a year of average use. Retrofit is the move when the customer likes their hardware, wants to keep a matched keyway across the house, or lives in an HOA/historic setting that won't allow visible electronics. But \u2014 and hammer this before you commit \u2014 retrofit only works on a standard single-cylinder deadbolt with a clean thumbturn. It will not drive a mortise lock, a double-cylinder (key both sides), or any bolt that needs more than a full turn; August spells that out in their own compatibility notes and Level wants a standard bore, 2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\" backset, 1-3/4\" door. And retrofit inherits every sin of the existing installation: if that deadbolt was stiff by hand, the little motor is going to strain, drain batteries, and eventually miss throws. You must free up the mechanical bolt first \u2014 plane the strike, adjust the alignment \u2014 or you're just automating a bad door. A native lock (Schlage Encode/Encode Plus, Yale Assure 2, Kwikset Halo/916) replaces the entire deadbolt, inside and out, and you control the whole tolerance stack \u2014 bolt, cam, strike, motor \u2014 which is why natives are generally more reliable long-term. The tradeoff is you're swapping the keyway too, which matters more than people think.\n\nHere's where a locksmith earns the ticket and the amateur faceplants: the cylinder and keyway still matter, and the \"keyless\" pitch is a half-truth. Almost every keyed smart deadbolt has a mechanical override cylinder \u2014 a real pin-tumbler you can pick, bump, drill, and, more to the point, one that has to fit the customer's key system. Kwikset ships SmartKey: five wafers riding a steel sidebar instead of a normal pin stack, and the party trick is DIY rekey \u2014 insert the working key, turn the plug 90 degrees, poke the SmartKey tool in the little hole beside the keyway, pull the old key, insert the new one, turn back, done, no disassembly. Great for a landlord doing turnovers. But know its history: early SmartKey generations had a documented sidebar bypass \u2014 a flat tool for torque in the keyway plus a wire in the rekey hole would defeat it without a scratch, and the platform's been reworked through a couple of generations since to harden it. It's still a proprietary system, so you're not pinning it to a standard key bitting \u2014 a customer already on Kwikset KW1 you can rekey to match, but a customer on Schlage SC1 can't just match it without swapping the whole cylinder. That's why Yale did something smart on the Assure 2: the keyed version takes a replaceable cylinder and you can buy it in a KW1 (Kwikset) five-pin or a Schlage-keyway variant, keyed-alike or keyed-different, so you can bring the smart lock into the customer's existing key system instead of handing them a fifth key on their ring. That is the question you ask on every keyed job: \"How many keys do you carry, and do you want this to match?\" If yes, you're either rekeying to their bitting or ordering the right keyway cylinder. Push customers toward keyed-alike across the property \u2014 one key for everything is the whole point, and a smart lock that adds a key to the ring defeats itself.\n\nFailure modes \u2014 this is the bread and butter, learn to diagnose in order. Power first: dead batteries are the number one service call, period, and every good keypad lock gives you a low-battery warning for weeks that customers ignore. Now, a myth to kill right here: the Schlage Encode and Encode Plus do NOT jumpstart. Schlage's own support line is explicit \u2014 the Encode series has no 9V jump terminals, and if it's dead your only way in is the mechanical key. The 9V jumpstart trick belongs to the Schlage Touch line \u2014 the BE375 Touch deadbolt and FE695 Touch lever have two exposed contacts under the keypad; hold a fresh 9V alkaline to them and the keypad wakes long enough to punch the code. Their keypad cousins the BE365 and FE595 don't have jump contacts either, so don't promise a customer a jumpstart on those. Know which lock is on the door before you tell the homeowner how to get in, and either way teach them where their key lives, because a locked-out homeowner in the rain is your emergency rate. This is the whole reason you never sell a keyless-only deadbolt to someone with no other way in: no key cylinder, dead battery, no jump path, and now the \"solution\" is drilling a lock the customer paid two hundred bucks for. Second, after power: motor and bolt. If it beeps and clicks but won't throw, it's almost always mechanical binding \u2014 misaligned strike, swollen door, deadbolt catching the strike lip. Open the door, throw the bolt by hand; if it's stiff open, it'll be worse closed and the motor's fighting it. Fix the strike, don't blame the lock. Third: connectivity ghosts. \"It won't connect\" is usually the hub or router, not the lock \u2014 Wi-Fi locks choke on the 2.4 GHz band being congested or on a router that split its bands under one SSID; Z-Wave/Zigbee locks that dropped off need re-pairing to the hub or a repeater because they sat at the edge of the mesh. That's a network problem wearing a lock costume, and you should scope it as one before you touch hardware.\n\nBrands, straight talk. Allegion's Schlage is the one I trust most on the mechanical side \u2014 the Encode and Encode Plus deadbolts are built on the BE489/BE499 platform and carry ANSI/BHMA Grade 1 with a full one-inch bolt throw, which is genuinely commercial-grade metal under a residential radio, and the Encode Plus adds Apple Home Key, NFC tap-to-unlock with your iPhone or Watch. Yale makes the Assure 2, and its ace is that modular radio bay \u2014 you buy the lock once and slot in a cartridge for Wi-Fi, Z-Wave, Zigbee, or Matter, upgrading connectivity without replacing the lock, plus the swappable keyway I mentioned. Kwikset owns the value and rekey space \u2014 SmartCode 916 and Halo \u2014 solid enough, SmartKey for easy turnovers, but lighter-duty castings; fine for interior and low-abuse residential, I'd think twice on a heavy exterior door that sees weather and slamming. August and Level own retrofit and stealth. Now the piece amateurs miss entirely: none of these residential units belong on a commercial opening. The second you're on a business, a multifamily common door, or anything with egress and fire code in play, you're in different hardware. Alarm Lock's Trilogy T2 DL2700 is the workhorse standalone \u2014 cylindrical, ANSI Grade 1, up to 100 user codes (1 master, 10 managers, 90 users, plus service codes) managed right at the keypad with no wiring or software, runs on five AA cells rated to 200,000 cycles, and has a real SC1 six-pin key override built in; the weatherproof DL2700WP is rated roughly minus 31 to 151 F for exterior gates and the latch is UL listed, fire rated to 3 hours. For access control tied to panic hardware and mag locks you're into Von Duprin, HES strikes, Securitron, Adams Rite for aluminum storefront \u2014 and there the rule that overrides everything is life safety: egress must never require special knowledge or a key to get OUT, fail-safe versus fail-secure has to match the code and the fire plan, and you do not put a battery-only residential deadbolt on a required egress or fire-rated door. Get that wrong and it's not a callback, it's liability.\n\nLast thing \u2014 the install and support reality nobody prints on the box. These locks are 80 percent carpentry and 20 percent electronics. The single biggest determinant of whether a smart lock is reliable is strike alignment and backset, not the app. Confirm the backset \u2014 2-3/8\" or 2-3/4\", most kits are adjustable but set it right \u2014 verify the bolt throws fully and freely with the door open and again closed, and if it drags even slightly, file the strike or move it before you power up, because a motor fighting a tight bolt is a battery-eating warranty problem waiting to happen. Set expectations on the front porch: batteries are on the customer, and on AA-heavy Wi-Fi units that means teaching them to swap on the low-battery warning, not the dead beep. Remote features die if their internet or hub dies \u2014 the lock still works locally by key and code, and they need to hear that so a router outage isn't a panic call. Register the electronics warranty (Schlage runs a limited lifetime on the mechanical and finish and a shorter term on the electronics), tell them where the physical key lives, and \u2014 this is the pro move \u2014 hand them a one-card cheat sheet: which backup their lock actually has (jump contacts only if it's a Touch, otherwise the key), where the override key is, and your number. You'll cut your own nuisance callbacks in half, and the customer remembers who set them up right.",sources:["https://www.schlage.com/en/home/products/BE499WBCENFFF.html","https://www.schlage.com/en/home/products/BE489WBCAMFFF.html","https://schlage-res.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/37468159195796-Can-Schlage-Encode-Locks-Be-Jumpstarted-with-a-Battery","https://schlage-res.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/35147753364884-Schlage-Keypad-and-Touch-Locks-Change-the-Battery","https://www.schlage.com/content/dam/sch-us/documents/pdf/installation-manuals/23780042.pdf"],tags:["Backset","Strike","Fire-rated door","Life safety","Keyway","Cylinder","Mortise lock","Handing"],aliases:["distance to center","wrong backset","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","NFPA 101","IBC","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","lockout","locked out","emergency opening"]},{id:"t-fasteners",title:"Fasteners: self-tappers, through-bolts, sex bolts & carriage bolts",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here is the thing nobody teaches you in a class but every seasoned tech knows in his hands: the fastener is the job. You can hang a perfect Grade 1 lever or a beautiful Von Duprin rim device, but if you drove the wrong screw into the wrong substrate, that hardware is coming loose, and it is coming loose right when the building is full of people. Nine callbacks out of ten on \"loose closer\" or \"exit device rattling\" trace straight back to the wrong fastener, not the wrong hardware. So before you touch a screw, ask yourself the only question that matters: what am I threading into \u2014 hollow metal, solid wood, or aluminum extrusion? Everything else flows from that answer.\n\n[[diagram:fastener-substrate-matrix|The fastener comes from what is behind the hole... and most \"loose hardware\" calls are fastener calls in disguise]]\n\nStart with hollow metal, because it fools people. A commercial steel door and frame look solid, but they are a thin skin \u2014 face sheets run from about 20 gauge on a light-duty Level 1 door (0.032 in) up through 18 gauge Level 2 (0.042 in), 16 gauge Level 3 (0.053 in), to 14 gauge on a maximum-duty Level 4 (0.067 in), and frames are typically a gauge or two heavier than the leaf, exterior frames heavier still. That skin alone will not hold a machine screw worth a damn. What holds is the reinforcement plate spot-welded behind the prep. Per ANSI/SDI A250.6, the factory taps that reinforcement for machine screws \u2014 lock, strike, closer, and exit-device reinforcement is heavy stock, a minimum around 0.067 in (14 gauge), tapped so you get real thread engagement, and hinge reinforcement is thicker yet because it carries the whole weight of the door \u2014 figure roughly 0.093 in (12 gauge) on a 1-3/8 in door and up to about 0.123 in (10 gauge) on a 1-3/4 in door. The industry-standard thread you will meet over and over on hinge, lock, strike, and closer reinforcement is 12-24 UNC \u2014 that is the tap A250.14 shows on those preps again and again (you will also hit 8-32 on a lock-case center tab, so read the prep, do not assume). That is why a proper hollow metal install uses machine screws, not wood screws, not sheet-metal screws \u2014 the hole is already tapped for a 12-24 machine screw and your job is to chase clean threads into steel that was cut at the factory. Cross-thread that first turn and you have ruined a tapped hole you cannot re-tap easily in the field. Start every machine screw by backing it off a half turn until you feel it drop into the existing thread, then drive forward. Never power it in blind.\n\nNow here is where techs get into trouble on steel: they hit an untapped hole, or a field-drilled hole, or a knockdown frame that was never reinforced properly, and they reach for a self-tapper. There are two very different animals people lump together. A self-tapping screw cuts its own thread but needs a pilot hole drilled first \u2014 it taps, it does not drill. A self-drilling screw, the TEK screw, has a little drill-bit point that bores through the steel and forms thread in one shot, no pilot. The Buildex Teks #3 point is the workhorse for door work: that #3 drill point will drill and fasten a combined steel thickness up to roughly 0.14 inch \u2014 comfortably through your thin sheet and up through a 16-gauge (0.053 in) or even a 14-gauge (0.067 in) skin, and through two layers stacked. Match the point to the steel: a #2 point is for thin sheet, and if you try to drive a short-point TEK through doubled-up reinforcement past its rated capacity it will spin, burnish, and heat up before it ever cuts \u2014 that is the screw sitting there smoking and going nowhere. That is a point-capacity mismatch, not a bad screw. If you are into heavier stacked steel, step up to a #4 or #5 point, which drill far thicker.\n\nSo why do self-tappers strip? Almost always one of three reasons, and you check them in this order. One, wrong pilot for the substrate \u2014 a plain self-tapper (not a self-driller) needs the correct pilot diameter in steel, roughly the screw's root diameter; drill it too big and there is no meat left for thread to form, drill it too small and you snap the screw or the thread shears out as you seat it. Two, you re-drove into an already-formed thread and cross-threaded it \u2014 the second install is where self-tappers die, because you rarely find the original helix. Three, and this is the big one, you overdrove it. A self-tapper into thin steel or a soft substrate has a tiny window between \"snug\" and \"stripped.\" Once that formed thread shears, it is gone \u2014 the screw just spins. Clutch your driver down, seat by feel, and stop the instant the head kisses the surface. And understand the real lesson: a self-tapper in a load-bearing spot on hollow metal is a repair, a stopgap, not the right long-term answer. The right answer on steel that takes real load is a tapped hole and a machine screw, or you go all the way through with a through-bolt.\n\nWhich brings us to the two fasteners that actually save your reputation on high-abuse hardware: sex bolts and through-bolts. A sex bolt \u2014 the trade also calls it a binding post or a barrel-and-screw \u2014 is a two-piece fastener. A female barrel goes in one face of the door, a machine screw threads into it from the other face, and the two clamp the door leaf like a sandwich. That is the whole magic: the holding power no longer depends on thread bite in a thin skin or in wood that will crush over time \u2014 it depends on clamping the entire thickness of the door. This is non-negotiable on closers and exit devices, which see enormous cyclic and impact load. LCN ships through-bolt kits for the 4040XP and 4000-series closers threaded 1/4-20, sized standard for 1-3/4 inch doors, with barrel options for other leaf thicknesses \u2014 you order to the door thickness, you do not force a wrong-length barrel. Von Duprin sells sex bolt sets for its exit devices \u2014 the 425 sex bolt runs a 10-24 machine screw into the barrel \u2014 and on the mounting side of a rim or mortise device those through-bolts pull the device case tight against a reinforced door rather than hanging it off face-sheet screws that will loosen and let the device sag until the latch mis-catches the strike.\n\nAnd here is the code-and-liability instinct you must burn in: on a wood or wood-composite fire-rated door, the exit device MUST be through-bolted with sex bolts \u2014 this is a listing requirement, not a preference. Von Duprin says it flat out in their own literature: 425 sex bolts are required for the 22/88/98/99 series fire-rated devices when used on wood door applications, and sex bolts must be used on wood or composite fire doors, period. (They will even add the sex bolts to the device order automatically unless you specify their SLM blocking package, and on -F rim devices with certain outside trims they add 825 sex bolts on top.) The manufacturer's UL listing for that assembly is predicated on through-bolt mounting, because in a fire the wood chars and self-tappers or wood screws will pull right out, dropping your egress hardware off the door when people need it most. If you self-tap a Von Duprin onto a wood fire door, you have voided the label and you own whatever happens next. Same logic, less drama, on any high-traffic wood door: through-bolt the closer or you will be back in six months. The one honest tradeoff \u2014 through-bolting means the mounting screw heads and barrels show on the opposite face; on a closer that is fine, and manufacturers make finished sex bolt heads for exactly that.\n\nSolid wood without a fire rating gives you more freedom but its own failure mode. Wood screws hold by biting fiber, and fiber crushes and works loose under cyclic load \u2014 that is your \"sagging door, screws spinning in the hinge\" call. Three fixes, escalating: pilot properly (a real pilot sized to the screw's root diameter, not the outside of the threads, so you do not split the stile and so the thread has fiber to grab); go longer \u2014 the classic hinge fix is swapping the short factory screw for a 3-inch screw on the top hinge that reaches past the jamb into the framing behind it, which stops the sag by transferring load to structure instead of the thin jamb; and when a hole is chewed out, do not just jam in a fatter screw \u2014 plug it. Glue a hardwood dowel or golf-tee-and-glue the hole, let it set, re-pilot, and you have virgin material again. A fatter screw in a blown-out hole is a two-week fix. And on anything that takes closer or exit-device load, stop pretending wood screws will do \u2014 through-bolt it.\n\nAluminum storefront and narrow-stile doors are their own world, and it is the one where guys who came up on steel and wood get humbled. Aluminum extrusion is soft and the walls are thin, so thread bite is weak and aluminum galls \u2014 it cold-welds to a steel screw and either seizes or strips as you drive. On Adams Rite and similar narrow-stile hardware you are threading into the extrusion or into tapped holes, and you keep it snug, not gorilla-tight, because you will strip soft aluminum in a heartbeat. For anything carrying load \u2014 a surface closer, a rim exit device on a storefront door \u2014 through-bolt it; do not trust self-tappers in a thin aluminum wall. LCN and the closer makers spec aluminum-finish through-bolt kits precisely because the correct install on an aluminum door is a clamp through the leaf, not a bite into the extrusion. And mind your galvanic pairing and finish match \u2014 a raw steel screw in aluminum in an exterior opening will corrode and seize, so use the finished, plated hardware the kit ships.\n\nA word on carriage bolts, because people confuse them with sex bolts and they are not the same tool. A carriage bolt has a smooth domed head and a square shoulder under it that bites into the material and keeps the bolt from spinning while you tighten the nut on the far side. In our trade you see them on gates, on the security side of some panic hardware and strike installs, and anywhere you want a tamper-resistant, non-turnable head on the exposed side with a nut you can only reach from the secure interior. The tamper resistance is real \u2014 there is no drive on the outside face, so nobody backs it out with a screwdriver from the attack side \u2014 but a carriage bolt needs a nut and access to the back, so it lives where you have a frame you can get behind or a gate frame you can drill through. Where you have a closed door leaf and no back access, that is exactly the job for a sex bolt instead, because the sex bolt is a self-contained clamp with no exposed nut.\n\nReinforcements tie the whole subject together, so think about them before you drill, not after you have made a mess. On hollow metal, if the opening was not prepped for the hardware you are hanging, you are not going to make thin face sheet hold a device \u2014 you add a reinforcement: a mounting plate, a wrap-around plate, or a sex-bolt/through-bolt kit that turns the whole leaf into your anchor. Wrap-around plates are the standard rescue for a hollow metal door where the lock area is torn up or under-reinforced \u2014 they spread load across a wide footprint and give a solid seat. On wood doors, a mortised or surface reinforcement plate and long through-fasteners take load off crushed fiber. The judgment call, repair-versus-replace: if the tapped reinforcement in a steel door is stripped or blown, you can sometimes go up a thread size or drill-and-tap fresh, but if the reinforcement itself is torn loose from the face sheet, that door is done for that prep \u2014 reinforce with a through-bolt kit and a wrap plate, or replace the door, because chasing a dead tapped hole with bigger and bigger self-tappers just delays the callback. Match the fastener to the substrate, clamp instead of bite wherever load is high, through-bolt every closer and exit device on wood and on anything fire-rated, and you will not see that piece of hardware again until somebody drives a forklift through it.",sources:["https://steeldoor.org/ansi-250-6/","https://steeldoor.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/A250.14.pdf","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-are-door-levels-and-models/","https://steeldoor.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/A250_8-1.pdf","https://www.vonduprin.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/von-duprin-/information-documents/Von_Duprin_Auxilary_Catalog_106587.pdf"],tags:["Exit device","Stile","Rim exit device","Drill point","Fire-rated door","Strike","Opening","Plug"],aliases:["panic bar","crash bar","push bar","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","rim panic","surface strike panic","Von Duprin 98/99","drill spot","drill template","drill chart","where to drill","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"t-safe-basics",title:"Safe basics: types, UL ratings & getting in",module:"Safes & Vaults",body:"Before you touch a drill, get the vocabulary straight, because ninety percent of the \"safes\" people call about are not safe against anything but a nosy houseguest. There are two completely different problems a safe box can solve: keeping burglars out, and keeping fire out. They are engineered against each other. Burglary resistance wants mass, thick plate, and a hard, drill-defeating composite. Fire resistance wants a lightweight insulating fill \u2014 gypsum board, or a poured amalgamate of concrete, vermiculite, and perlite \u2014 that holds chemically bound water and sheds heat by giving up that moisture as steam when the fire cooks it. A cheap \"fire safe\" is a thin sheet-metal shell wrapped around fireboard; you can peel one open with a screwdriver and a pry bar in two minutes. A true burglary safe with no fire lining will cook its contents in a house fire \u2014 the steel conducts heat straight in. When a customer says they want \"a good safe,\" your first job is to find out which threat they actually care about, because the box that does both well is heavy, expensive, and rare.\n\n[[diagram:safe-rating-vocabulary|Burglary and fire are opposite engineering problems, and most residential boxes solve neither]]\n\nStart at the bottom of the burglary ladder so you know what you are looking at when you walk up on a job. The lowest tier that is not a joke is the UL RSC \u2014 Residential Security Container, tested under UL 1037. That is your gun-safe rating. Minimum construction is basically 12-gauge steel body (or two walls summing to about .100\" of steel), and the door is two layers of 12-gauge or a single 7-gauge plate. The UL test on an RSC is one operator, five minutes of net working time, with hand tools \u2014 pry bars, hammers (head under 3 lbs), chisels, punches, screwdrivers, wrenches, nothing over 18 inches long. Five minutes. That is the whole test. An RSC keeps out a smash-and-grab kid and satisfies a firearms-storage requirement, and that is it. Do not oversell it. Next up is the old B-rate designation, which is not a UL burglary rating at all \u2014 it is an insurance-industry construction spec: quarter-inch (1/4\") body steel, half-inch (1/2\") door. B-rate tells you the metal is real, but nobody stood there with a stopwatch and tools trying to beat it, so treat it as a build standard, not a tested attack rating. Worth knowing: the door plate on a true B-rate box is roughly 2.8 times thicker than what an RSC requires \u2014 half an inch versus a pair of 12-gauge layers at about .18\" \u2014 so there is a big jump in real metal between those two labels even though people lump them together.\n\nNow the labels that actually mean something to a pro \u2014 the UL 687 net-working-time ratings: TL-15, TL-30, and TL-30x6. \"TL\" is tool. The number is net working time in minutes \u2014 and net is the word that matters. The clock only runs while a tool is in contact with the safe. Torch off the metal, saw lifted, clock stops. So a TL-30 does not mean thirty wall-clock minutes; a real attack team burns setup, repositioning, and blade changes on top of that. UL techs are experts who already know the product's weak points, and they go straight for the throat rather than working every conceivable attack. TL-15 is fifteen minutes of net time with common hand tools, drills (bit diameter limited), punches, hammers, and pressure-applying devices. TL-30 is that same attack plus abrasive cutting wheels and power saws, for thirty minutes. Critically, both TL-15 and TL-30 are door-and-front-face-only ratings for the full net time \u2014 UL only has to fail to make a six-square-inch opening through the front. The body is held to a separate, shorter probe: where it is not solid steel, it just has to resist a five-minute attempt with TL-30 tools to open that same six square inches. That is the gotcha: a plain TL-30 can be flipped on its back and attacked through the thinner top, bottom, or sides. That is exactly what TL-30x6 fixes \u2014 the \"x6\" means the full 30-minute tool attack was applied to all six sides: front, back, top, bottom, and both sides. When a jeweler or a cannabis operator needs real protection, x6 is the floor, not TL-30. And these are heavy: UL 687 requires a TL-rated box to weigh at least 750 lbs or be anchored to the premises, and plenty of TL-30 units run over 1,000 lbs, with the burglary resistance coming from a poured drill-resistant composite between the steel plates, not from the plate alone. Above these you get into TRTL (torch and tool) and TXTL (torch, tool, and explosives) \u2014 bank and vault territory, not house calls.\n\nUnderstand the lock rating separately from the box rating, because they are two different UL standards and people conflate them constantly. The box is UL 687; the combination lock is UL 768, and it grades locks by manipulation resistance. Group 2 is the workhorse \u2014 no specified manipulation-resistance time, it is the standard commercial dial you find on most safes, held to construction and dialing-tolerance minimums only. Group 2M adds two working hours of resistance to expert manipulation. Group 1 is the serious one: 20 man-hours of expert manipulation resistance \u2014 and note \"man-hours\" is flexible, so that is one person for 20 hours or two people for 10. Group 1R is Group 1 plus 20 hours of resistance to radiological (X-ray) attack. Here is a liability point you need burned in: DEA scheduled-drug storage under 21 CFR 1301.72 pairs a minimum TL-30 box with a UL-listed Group 1R dial \u2014 a mechanical combination lock \u2014 and digital keypads are not accepted for that use. If a pharmacy or dispensary customer asks you to swap their dial for an electronic keypad \"for convenience,\" that swap can put them out of compliance. Know the rule before you turn a screwdriver.\n\nThe real S&G workhorse you will meet again and again is the Sargent & Greenleaf 6700 series \u2014 the 6730 and the older 6741 \u2014 UL 768 Group 2 mechanical dial locks, three key-changeable wheels, up to a million combinations. The 6730 is the modern upgrade: it carries an internal relock trigger built to defeat punch attacks and a torque adjuster that keeps wheel-pack tension right after repeated abuse. If you find a tired 6741 on a good safe, the 6730 is a straight upgrade path, but verify the mounting and dial/ring compatibility with the safe maker first \u2014 the 6700-series body uses a four-way drive cam for universal mounting, but footprint and spindle length still bite you if you assume. When you order, remember the \"-100\" package (e.g. 6730-100) is the full kit with dial and ring, and the bare \"6730\" is the lock body only. Order the body when you meant the package and you are making a second trip. And do not trust every retailer listing \u2014 some show \"1,000 combinations\" by mistake; S&G's own spec on the three-wheel 6730 and 6741 is one million.\n\nNow the defenses you are fighting when you legitimately open one \u2014 relockers and hardplate \u2014 because these are the whole reason drilling a safe is skilled work and not vandalism. A relocker is a spring-loaded secondary bolt that permanently fires if someone attacks the lock, and once it trips, the safe will not open even with the correct combination \u2014 it has to be drilled out. There are three kinds. A punch/mechanical relocker sits behind the lock body and fires if the lock is knocked off its mount or the back cover is removed. A glass relocker is a sheet of tempered glass mounted on the door interior holding back spring-loaded bolts (often with cables running to bolts at multiple corners of the boltwork); drill or punch through it and the glass shatters, the tension releases, and bolts drive home everywhere at once. A thermal relocker uses a fusible link \u2014 a low-melt alloy insert \u2014 so a torch attack melts the link and fires the relock; S&G builds this into their locks. One field note worth its weight: glass relockers are so sensitive that a bad transport bump can trip one, and now you are drilling a safe with a \"correct\" combo that will not open \u2014 do not assume a customer is lying about their number; suspect a tripped relock. Hardplate is the drill defense: a plate of case-hardened steel, or a ball-bearing plate, or the top tier \u2014 a diamond-embedded armor plate with industrial diamond bonded into a tungsten-steel alloy \u2014 set to cover the lock body and the drill points. Cheap carbide bits skate and burn on it; the diamond-armored stuff is harder than a standard bit and will dull your drill before you get through if you go in blind.\n\nSo how do you actually get in, at a professional level, legit locksmith work \u2014 always after you have proven ownership, because opening a safe you cannot prove belongs to the customer is a fast way to lose your license and worse. First rule: manipulation before metal, and metal before torch. Manipulation on a Group 2 dial is the clean, no-damage method \u2014 you read the wheels by feeling contact points on the drive cam through the dial, charting where the lever contacts change as you park wheels, and narrowing the gates until the combination falls out. It is real skill and it takes time, but it leaves the safe fully functional and there is nothing to repair or bill for beyond your labor. A Group 2M costs you two more hours of that and a Group 1 is 20 man-hours \u2014 at that point manipulation stops being economic and you go to the drill. Drilling is not \"make a hole in the door\" \u2014 it is precise. You pull the manufacturer's drill-point diagram (Lockmasters and MBA publish these; a reputable shop keeps a library), you locate the point that lets you scope the lock, you drill a small hole and put a borescope in to read the wheel gates or to see the bolt, and then you dial to the gates or manipulate the bolt directly under the scope. You are drilling to see, not to destroy. Two hard rules keep you out of trouble: know where the relockers and glass are before the bit goes in, because drilling blind into a glass relock turns a one-hole job into a boltwork excavation; and stay off the hardplate \u2014 you drill around it, through a softer path the diagram gives you, not through it. When you are done, you plug and repair the hole, replace the lock if you disturbed it, and reset a fresh combination.\n\nLast, the repair-versus-replace and pricing instinct. On a decent TL-rated box or a solid old money-chest, you almost always repair \u2014 the box is worth far more than the lock, a new S&G 6730 package is a modest part, and a drilled-and-repaired hardplate hole done right is invisible and does not meaningfully compromise the rating. On a big-box RSC gun safe, do the math honestly: if the lockout is bad and the customer is looking at a serious drill job, the labor can approach the price of a new RSC, and you tell them that straight rather than run up a bill on a five-minute box. And whatever you do, do not let a customer talk you into downgrading a compliance-driven lock \u2014 a DEA Group 1R dial, a fire rating on a document safe, an egress-relevant install \u2014 for convenience or a few dollars. On safes the liability lives in the ratings, and the ratings are the whole reason the box exists.",sources:["https://www.safeandvaultstore.com/pages/burglary-ratings","https://guardiansafeandvault.com/blog/tl-30x6-rating-explained","https://americansecuritysafes.com/a-tl-rated-safe-guide/","https://americansecuritysafes.com/burglary-ratings-explained/","https://www.ul.com/news/residential-security-container-standard-revised"],tags:["Manipulation","Hardplate","Relocker","Drive cam","Boltwork","Burglary safe","Fire safe","Glass relocker"],aliases:["dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","drill resistant plate","carbide plate","hard plate","re-locker","glass plate","punch relocker","cam driver","retainer cam","bolt work","handle spins","bolts won't retract","1 hour","UL 72","fireproof safe","tempered glass relock","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","housing","cylinder body","locked out","no key","emergency opening","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"how-locks-work",title:"How locks actually work: pin-tumbler, wafer, disc & lever",module:"Foundations",body:"Before you ever pick up a pick, a plug follower, or a rekey kit, you need to understand one thing that every mechanical lock on Earth is built around \u2014 the shear line. Get that in your bones and everything else falls into place. Nearly every cylinder you'll touch is two nested pieces of metal: an outer housing called the shell (or hull), and an inner cylinder called the plug that carries the keyway and turns to throw the bolt. The plug rides inside the shell with a whisker of clearance, and the seam where those two surfaces meet \u2014 where the plug's outer diameter kisses the shell's inner bore \u2014 is the shear line. A lock is nothing more than a machine for jamming little obstructions across that seam so the plug can't rotate. Unlock it and you've done exactly one thing: you've cleared everything off the shear line so the plug spins free. Every mechanism in this guide \u2014 pins, wafers, discs, levers \u2014 is just a different way of parking obstructions on that line and a different trick for lifting them off. Learn to see the shear line and you stop memorizing locks and start understanding them.\n\n[[img:diagram-pt-key|The correct key lifts each pin stack so the split between the key pin (red) and driver pin (blue) meets the shear line \u2014 then the plug turns. Diagram: Wikimedia, CC BY-SA]]\n\nStart with the pin-tumbler, because it's the bulk of the residential and commercial deadbolts and knobs you'll ever service, and everything else is a variation on the theme. Drilled straight down through the top of the shell and into the plug are a row of chambers \u2014 usually five, sometimes six on better hardware. Each chamber holds a stack: at the bottom, a key pin (the short one the key actually touches, cut to different lengths to match the cuts on the blade); on top of it, a driver pin (often called the top pin); and above that, a coil spring pressing the whole stack down. With no key in, those springs shove each stack down so the driver pins straddle the shear line \u2014 the top pin sits half in the plug, half in the shell, pinning the two together like a dowel through a hinge. Plug can't turn. That's the locked state, and it's dead simple. Now slide the right key in. Each cut on the blade lifts its key pin by exactly the right amount so that the joint \u2014 the flat where key pin meets driver pin \u2014 comes to rest precisely at the shear line, in all five chambers at once. When every gap lines up on that seam, nothing crosses it anymore, and the plug turns. Cut too shallow and the key pin's still poking up across the line; cut too deep and you've overset it, driving the joint above the shear line and pushing the driver pin's bottom down across it instead. That's the whole trick, and it's why a key that's off by one depth in one position won't turn \u2014 you only need one obstruction on the line to stop everything.\n\nHere's the part that separates a tech who parrots diagrams from one who actually gets it: the reason you can pick a pin-tumbler at all, and the reason binding matters, is that no lock is machined perfectly. The pin holes in the plug are microscopically out of alignment with the holes in the shell, and the pins and holes each run a slightly different diameter \u2014 we're talking tolerances down around a ten-thousandth of an inch, .0001\", and that's not me rounding for effect, that's the figure Matt Blaze cites in his picking notes. So when you put light rotational tension on the plug, it tries to turn maybe a hair, and only the single most out-of-alignment pin stack actually catches at the shear line and stops it. That pin is now binding \u2014 its top pin is pinched between plug and shell. Every other pin is loose. Push that one binding pin up until its joint clears the shear line, and the plug rotates a tiny fraction more \u2014 and now the next-worst-aligned pin becomes the binding one. That sequential, one-at-a-time binding order is the entire basis of single-pin picking: you're not lifting all five at once, you're finding the binder, setting it, feeling the plug give a micro-turn, and hunting the next. Don't expect it to march neatly front-to-back either \u2014 binding order jumps around, back to front to middle, lock to lock, and a good picker just follows whichever pin is binding right now. It's also why raking and bumping work \u2014 they're just ways of momentarily bouncing pins to the shear line and catching the plug's rotation before they fall back. And it's why you feel a pin \"set\" as a little counter-rotation and a change from squishy resistance to a springy free pin. Understand binding order and you understand picking, impressioning, and why cheap loose-tolerance locks open in seconds while a tight one fights you.\n\nThe manufacturers frustrate all this on purpose with security pins, and you need to recognize them by feel because they change your whole approach. Spool pins and mushroom pins are driver pins with a waist machined into the middle \u2014 a thinner section. When you tension and push one up, the shell catches on that waist and lets the plug rotate slightly into a false set: it feels like you set the pin and the plug turned, but really the spool is cocked in the chamber and the plug is now sitting counter-rotated. Green pickers chase their tails here for an hour. The tell is that counter-rotation \u2014 a spool gives the plug back to you a little when it catches, and you have to ease tension and push through the false gate to the true shear line. Serrated pins are worse in their own way \u2014 they're cut with a stack of little ridges so they false-set and jam at every serration as long as you hold torque, turning one pin into what feels like six. When a lock false-sets over and over and nothing frees, you're probably in serrated pins, and the answer is a lighter, more feathered tension. None of this is academic for you as a locksmith: knowing exactly how the bypass works is precisely how you sell and spec hardware that resists it. You can't harden a door if you don't know how it falls.\n\nNow flip the whole thing on its side and you've got the wafer lock \u2014 the mechanism in most desks, filing cabinets, RVs, older GM ignitions, and a river of cheap cam locks. Instead of stacked pin pairs riding in round chambers, a wafer lock uses single flat stamped-metal wafers \u2014 thin plates with a rectangular window cut in the middle that the key passes through. Springs push each wafer so it protrudes out of the plug on one side (single-bitted) or alternating sides (double-bitted, like a car key), and those protruding edges ride in a slot in the shell, pinning the plug. The right key catches each wafer through its window and pulls it flush with the plug's surface \u2014 clearing the shear line \u2014 and the plug turns. Same shear-line logic, one moving part per position instead of two. The catch, and the reason wafer locks are the easiest common mechanism to open, is that a wafer has to clear the shear line on only one side to bind, tolerances are sloppy, and there are usually only a handful of depths. A wafer jiggler \u2014 basically a try-out key that bounces the wafers \u2014 or a quick rake opens most of them in seconds. Treat a wafer lock as convenience hardware, not security. When a customer asks you to \"secure\" a wafer-locked cabinet holding anything real, the honest answer is you're upgrading the cylinder or the whole enclosure, not defending the wafers.\n\nDisc-detainer is where it gets serious, and you need to keep two very different things straight that both get sloppily called \"disc\" locks. The cheap one \u2014 the flat disc-tumbler you'll find in some cabinets and old car doors \u2014 is basically wafer-grade security and picks about as easily. The one that matters is the Abloy-style disc detainer. The rotating-detainer-disc principle was invented by Emil Henriksson, a Helsinki precision mechanic, back in 1907 \u2014 the story is he was fixing a cash register and realized its rotating disks could run a lock \u2014 but it didn't come to market under the Abloy name until 1918, with the patent granted in 1919. So call it a 1907 invention, not a 1907 Abloy product; the brand came a decade later. Either way it's a genuinely different animal. There are no springs and no pins. Instead the plug holds a stack of rotating discs, each with a notch (a gate) cut in its perimeter. The key isn't cut with up-and-down bitting \u2014 it's a half-round blade with angled cuts milled along its length, and as you insert and turn it, each angled cut rotates its disc to a specific clock position. When every disc is rotated to exactly its right angle, all the true gates line up into one continuous channel down the side of the disc pack, and a sidebar drops into that channel \u2014 that's what frees the plug to turn. If even one disc is a few degrees off, its gate isn't in the channel, the sidebar can't seat, and you're stopped. Because there are no springs, disc detainers shrug off water, ice, and grit, which is why you see them on padlocks, utility gates, and railroad and telecom installations. The good ones also machine false gates around the disc edges to catch the sidebar and fool you into thinking a disc is set when it isn't \u2014 the disc-detainer equivalent of a spool pin. A quality Abloy Protec-class detainer with many discs, aggressive false gates, hardened steel, and tight tolerances is about the hardest common mechanism there is to open non-destructively, and it needs a purpose-built tool, not a pick.\n\nLever locks round out the classics \u2014 the mechanism in a lot of European mortise locks, safe deposit boxes, older cabinetry, and British door locks. Here the obstructions aren't pins or wafers, they're a stack of flat pivoting levers, each spring-loaded, sitting side by side on a common pivot post. Every lever has a shaped slot called a gate cut into it, and fixed to the sliding bolt is a small post called the stump (or bolt stump) that has to pass through the lever pack for the bolt to move. When you turn the key, the bit rides up the belly of each lever and lifts it; how far each lever rises is set by the height of that step on the key's bitting. Lift every lever to exactly the right height and all their gates line up into a single horizontal channel \u2014 and now the stump can slide straight through the pack as the bolt throws. One lever lifted too little or too much and its gate is off the line, the stump jams against solid lever metal, and the bolt won't move. It's the same shear-line idea expressed as a channel instead of a seam: line up all the gaps, clear the path, throw the bolt. Better lever locks add false notches to the gates to trap the stump during picking, exactly like false gates and spool pins do in their families \u2014 the anti-manipulation idea keeps reappearing because the attack keeps reappearing.\n\nOnce you see it laid out like this, the foundation stops being trivia and starts driving every real job you do. Rekeying a pin-tumbler is nothing more than swapping the key pins for a new set of lengths so a different bitting brings all the joints to the shear line \u2014 that's why a rekey kit is a tray of pins in graduated depths, and why you must know the system. On a Schlage Classic (SC1) you're working in ten depths, 0 through 9, in .015\" increments, spaced about .156\" apart down the blade, with a MACS \u2014 maximum adjacent cut specification \u2014 of 7, meaning no two neighboring cuts can differ by more than 7 depths or the cutter chews into the shallower cut. On Kwikset (KW1) you've got depths 1 through 7 \u2014 call it six working depths plus a seventh that mostly shows up as a master or construction step \u2014 in .023\" increments about .150\" apart, and Kwikset's tighter geometry drops the MACS all the way to 4. Those aren't numbers to memorize for a test \u2014 they're what tells you which key pin to grab, why a bitting that jumps 0 to 9 on adjacent cuts is physically impossible on a Schlage, and how many differs a master system can actually hold. Impressioning leans on the same geometry: you're reading the tiny marks each binding pin scratches into a blank under tension, filing exactly where the pin bites, working position by position to the correct depth. Picking, as we walked through, is riding binding order to the shear line one obstruction at a time. Master keying is adding a third pin \u2014 a master wafer \u2014 into a chamber so the stack breaks at two different heights and two different keys both find a shear line. Every one of those skills is the same insight wearing a different hat: put all the obstructions on the shear line and the plug turns, and everything you do to a lock is either putting them there deliberately or taking them off. And a word that never goes out of style \u2014 every one of these techniques is for locks you own or are authorized to open. Verify the authority, know your local law, and remember the whole point of learning the bypass is to build doors that don't fall to it.",sources:["https://www.mattblaze.org/papers/notes/picking/","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Pin_tumbler","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pin_tumbler_lock","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/pin-tumbler-lock/","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-maximum-adjacent-cut-specification-or-macs/"],tags:["Binding order","Shear line","Shell","MACS","Plug","False set","Bitting","Impressioning"],aliases:["set order","which pin binds","picks open","won't set","line of separation","lock body","cylinder body","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","hangs up","feels open but won't turn","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","city rake","bogota","snake pick","rekey tool","pins fell out","dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","contact points","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"door-closers-adjustment",title:"Door closers: sizing, mounting & adjustment",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Let me tell you the first thing to get straight, because it trips up every apprentice: a door closer is a hydraulic pump, not a spring gadget. There is a spring inside that stores the energy when you open the door, sure, but the whole art of the trade lives in the oil. When the door swings open you wind up the spring; when you let go, the spring pushes back and forces hydraulic fluid through a set of tiny metering ports. The screws on the front of the closer body \u2014 the valves \u2014 throttle those ports. Turn a valve in and you pinch the oil flow, the door slows. Back it out and you open the port, the door speeds up. That is it. Nine times out of ten when somebody hands you a \"broken closer,\" it is not broken at all \u2014 it is out of adjustment, mis-mounted, or fighting a bound door. Learn the valves and the geometry and you will fix more of these in ninety seconds than you will ever replace.\n\n[[diagram:closer-valves-and-forces|Which valve owns which part of the arc, and the faults no valve will fix]]\n\nStart with the valves because that is where you will spend most of your time. A quality commercial closer \u2014 LCN 4040XP, Norton 7500 \u2014 gives you separate, independent needle valves for three regions of the swing, and I want you to think of the door's travel as three zones. The SWEEP valve controls the main arc, from the full-open position down to about 10 to 15 degrees from the jamb. That is the big, slow, controlled glide \u2014 the part people see. The LATCH valve controls the last stretch, roughly the final 10 degrees, where the door needs a little snap to overcome the weatherstrip seal and throw the latch bolt into the strike. The BACKCHECK valve is the odd one out \u2014 it does not control closing at all. It controls opening. Somewhere in the upper part of the opening swing \u2014 figure the 70-plus degree range, adjustable at the valve \u2014 the backcheck cushions the door so a gust of wind or an angry customer can't fling it into the drywall or tear the closer off the frame. And here is the rule that holds across Norton, LCN, all of them: the backcheck valve should NEVER be bottomed fully closed. It is a cushion, not a positive stop \u2014 screw it all the way in and you can hydraulically lock the door or blow a seal. Write that on the inside of your eyelids: backcheck cushions, it does not stop.\n\nNow the muscle memory for turning them, because on almost every North American closer the convention is the same: clockwise (in) restricts flow and SLOWS the door; counterclockwise (out) opens flow and SPEEDS it up. These are needle valves with fine threads, so you work in small bites \u2014 an eighth of a turn at a time, then walk the door and watch it. I have seen guys crank a valve a full turn \"to see what happens\" and end up with a door that either slams or won't move \u2014 and worse, back a sweep valve too far out on some bodies and the needle can pop out with a squirt of oil, and now you really do have a replacement on your hands. So: small turns, test, repeat. On the LCN 4040XP the sweep and latch needles take a 3/32\" hex key \u2014 turn them counterclockwise to speed the door up \u2014 and there are four adjustments on the body: backcheck, sweep (main) speed, latch speed, and the spring-power adjustment. Keep a full set of hex keys and a stopwatch on your belt; the stopwatch is not optional if the door is on an accessible route, and I will get to why.\n\nDelayed action is a fourth adjustment, and it is one you will only see on doors that are set up for it \u2014 think a hospital corridor, a classroom, anywhere a person in a wheelchair, on crutches, or pushing a cart needs extra time to clear the opening. Delayed action holds the door open, or nearly so, in the first part of the sweep \u2014 the door reaches full open, and instead of starting back immediately it pauses, drifting closed slowly for a stretch before the normal sweep and latch take over for the final close. On the LCN, the point where delay releases depends on the template you mounted to and the delay valve setting \u2014 it is adjustable, not a single fixed angle, and the hold can be dialed out to roughly a minute. Understand this, though: delayed action is a separate feature that has to be there in the cylinder. On LCN that is the 4041 delayed-action variant \u2014 the 4040XP takes an optional 4041 delay cylinder with its own delay-speed adjustment on top of backcheck, main, and latch. On Norton it is likewise an option. Do not promise a customer delayed action on a closer that doesn't have the delay port \u2014 you cannot add it by turning a valve.\n\nSpring size. This is where sizing lives, and it is graded 1 through 6 per ANSI/BHMA A156.4 \u2014 size 1 is the weakest spring, size 6 the strongest, and the standard pins a minimum closing moment to each grade (roughly size 1 at the low end climbing to size 6 for the heaviest). The rough field map: size 1-2 for narrow interior doors, size 3 for a standard 3'0\" interior door, size 4 for a 3'0\"-ish exterior door, and 5-6 for wide, heavy, or high-wind exterior openings. NFPA 80's annex leans on the same logic for fire doors \u2014 a size-3 closer as a starting point for a 3-foot interior fire door, size-4 for exterior. But here is the modern reality, and it is a gift: the good closers are non-sized and adjustable. The LCN 4040XP carries adjustable spring sizes 1 through 6 in one body and covers interior doors to 5'0\" and exterior to 4'0\". Norton's 8501 is likewise non-sized \u2014 spring sizes 1 through 6 in the body, and you dial spring tension from a barrier-free ADA setting all the way up to a 48-inch exterior door. On the 4040XP you set spring power with a 5/32\" hex on the spring-power adjustment, and clockwise adds power. Out of the box it ships around a mid setting, enough to close and latch most standard doors, and you tune from there.\n\nMounting geometry is the other half of sizing, and this is exactly where amateurs get blindsided \u2014 the SAME closer body delivers different closing power depending on how the arm is mounted. Three standard mounts. REGULAR (a.k.a. pull-side, hinge-side): closer on the pull face of the door, arm reaching to the frame \u2014 this is the most efficient geometry, the strongest close for a given spring. TOP JAMB: closer up on the frame face above the door on the push side, arm down to the door \u2014 nearly as efficient as regular. PARALLEL ARM: closer on the push face of the door with a bracket (\"shoe\") up under the soffit of the frame, arm folding back parallel to the door when closed \u2014 the cleanest look, best for vandal resistance and for keeping the arm out of the traffic path, and the one architects love. But parallel-arm geometry is the LEAST efficient \u2014 the hardware world commonly cites roughly 20 to 25 percent less closing power than a regular-arm mount, which is why the standing rule of thumb is that a parallel arm drops you about one size. So the working rule: when you go parallel arm, size UP. If a regular-arm install wants a size 3, plan on dialing that adjustable body toward a 4 for the same door on parallel arm, and expect to fight harder to get a positive latch. The 4040XP handles all three \u2014 hinge/pull side, top jamb push side, and parallel arm push side \u2014 from the same non-handed body, which is why it's the workhorse.\n\nNow let me walk you through an actual adjustment the way I do it on a call, because a checklist beats guesswork every time. Step one, and skip it and you'll chase your tail all day: check the DOOR before you touch a valve. Open it slowly by hand and feel for drag, binding, a hinge-bound leaf, a warped slab, or crushed weatherstrip. If the door won't close cleanly under its own residual spring with the arm disconnected, no valve on earth fixes that \u2014 you shim a hinge or address the bind first. Step two, baseline the valves: gently seat sweep and latch clockwise until they just stop (do not gorilla them \u2014 you'll damage the seat), then back each out about 1.5 turns. Now the door will at least move through its full arc. Step three, set the sweep: aim for a smooth, controlled glide, roughly 5 to 7 seconds from 90 degrees down to a few inches off the frame, tuning in eighth-turn steps. Step four, set the latch a touch FASTER than the sweep \u2014 you want a firm, clean click into the strike, no bounce, no hesitation, enough snap to crush the gasket and throw the bolt. Step five, set backcheck: with the door swung hard, you want it to meet cushioned resistance up in the 70-plus-degree range before it can reach the wall \u2014 and remember, never bottom that valve.\n\nHere is the part that separates a pro from a parts-changer: the code numbers, because on any accessible route these aren't suggestions. Opening force: interior hinged doors on an accessible route max out at 5 pounds of force (5 lbf) \u2014 that's ICC A117.1 \xa7404.2.8 and the 2010 ADA Standards \xa7404.2.9. That 5-pound cap is the interior spring you're allowed, which is why over-sized interior closers fail inspection even when they work fine mechanically. Note carefully: that 5-pound limit does NOT federally apply to exterior doors or to fire-rated doors \u2014 fire doors are exempt because a fire door MUST close and positively latch, full stop, and that often takes more spring than 5 pounds. For exterior and fire doors you fall back to the IBC ceiling: up to 30 lbf to set the door in motion and 15 lbf to swing it to full open. Closing speed on an accessible door: from 90 degrees open, it must take a MINIMUM of 5 seconds to reach 12 degrees from the latch (ICC A117.1 \xa7404.2.7 / 2010 ADA \xa7404.2.8). Minimum \u2014 so slower is compliant, faster is a violation. That's what the stopwatch is for, and that's the setting that gets buildings written up. (Spring hinges are their own animal: minimum 1.5 seconds from 70 degrees to closed.)\n\nTroubleshooting the two calls you'll get most \u2014 SLAMMING and WON'T LATCH \u2014 and here's the diagnostic order. Door SLAMS: first suspect the sweep or latch valve backed too far out, or an over-cranked spring \u2014 turn the appropriate valve clockwise in eighth-turns and re-test. If the door slams even with the sweep valve seated all the way in, that is your tell: the closer has blown a seal and lost hydraulic fluid, and no adjustment brings it back \u2014 replace the unit. Look for oil weeping from the body or arm to confirm; a closer that's leaking oil can't rebuild its own seals, so it's a replacement, not a repair. On the flip side, WON'T LATCH: resist the reflex to just crank the latch speed. Nine times out of ten it's not the closer, it's the opening. Check strike alignment first \u2014 the latch bolt has to line up with the strike hole; buildings settle and frames shift. Watch where the bolt hits the strike lip: catches high, shim the bottom hinge; catches low, shim the top hinge (a shim behind the top-hinge leaf drops the latch side, behind the bottom raises it). Check for a dragging weatherstrip or gasket that's eating the last of the closing energy. Check the arm geometry \u2014 on parallel arm especially, an under-sized spring simply can't overpower the seal in the last few degrees, so bump the spring size up. Only after all that do you nudge the latch valve open for a little more snap. Get in the habit of that order and you'll stop replacing good closers to solve bad alignment \u2014 which is the single most expensive mistake I see young guys make on this hardware.",sources:["https://idighardware.com/2012/05/decoded-opening-force-and-closing-speed/","https://idighardware.com/2024/06/decoded-door-and-gate-closing-speed/","https://idighardware.com/2015/04/door-closer-arm-options/","https://www.corada.com/documents/2010ADAStandards/404-2-9","https://up.codes/s/icc-a117-1-section-404-2-8"],tags:["Strike","Opening","Cylinder"],aliases:["strike plate","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"closing-fire-code",title:"Closing speed & the fire code (the 5-second rule)",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here's the thing that trips up ninety percent of the guys who buy a closer and start turning valves: they think the goal is to make the door close nice and slow so it doesn't slam. Wrong frame. The code isn't asking you to be gentle for the door's sake \u2014 it's protecting the slow-moving human trying to get through the opening. A person in a wheelchair, somebody on a walker, an old lady with an armful of groceries. That's who the five-second rule is written for, and once you carry that picture in your head, every adjustment you make on a closer suddenly makes sense.\n\n[[diagram:closing-time-code-limits|90\xb0 to 12\xb0 in at least 5 seconds, measured... and the two other numbers on the same door]]\n\nSo let's nail the number down cold, because you will get asked and you cannot bluff it. ADA 2010 Standards section 404.2.8, mirrored in ICC A117.1: from an open position of 90 degrees, the time required to move the door to a position of 12 degrees from the latch is 5 seconds minimum. Read that word \"minimum\" three times. It is a floor, not a ceiling. Six seconds is fine. Eight is fine. Faster than five is the violation. So when you're on your knees with a stopwatch and the door snaps shut in three and a half, you are non-compliant and you slow it down. You never get dinged for a door that closes too slowly on the accessibility side \u2014 you get dinged for one that closes too fast and clips somebody.\n\nNow, that 90-to-12 window is deliberate, and it maps directly onto the two adjustment valves on your closer. The big sweep of the door \u2014 90 degrees down to about 12 or 15 degrees off the frame \u2014 that's your sweep speed valve, and that's the portion the five-second clock actually measures. The last little bit, from roughly 12 degrees into the frame, is your latch speed valve, and that one you WANT a touch quicker so the door drives home and the latch bolt actually throws. Code doesn't time the latch zone. So the pro move is: sweep slow enough to eat up your five-plus seconds, then a firm little pop of latch speed at the end so it positively latches instead of dying an inch short. A door that closes slow and beautiful but stalls out and never latches has failed \u2014 especially if it's a fire door, and we'll get to why that's a big deal.\n\nSpring hinges are a different animal with a different number, so don't cross the wires. If the opening uses spring hinges instead of a closer \u2014 404.2.8 again \u2014 you adjust so that from an open position of 70 degrees the door moves to the closed position in 1.5 seconds minimum. Different start angle, different clock, still a minimum. I mention it because people memorize \"five seconds\" and then try to apply it to a spring-hinged screen door and get confused. Wrong tool, wrong spec.\n\nWhile your knees are already on the tile, check opening force too, because 404.2.9 lives right next door and inspectors test both in the same visit. Interior hinged doors, non-fire-rated: 5 pounds of force maximum to push the door open. Sliding and folding interior doors, same 5 pounds. You measure it with a push-pull gauge on the latch edge, door already cracked off the stop \u2014 the spec is about the continuous force to swing it, not the initial nudge to break inertia, and it explicitly does not count the force to retract the latch bolt or disengage a device holding it shut. Here's the gotcha that snags apprentices: that 5-pound limit does NOT apply to exterior doors and does NOT apply to fire doors. There's no ADA number for exterior door force at all \u2014 the Access Board says \"no maximum specified\" \u2014 because wind load, stack pressure, gasketing, and HVAC all fight you. And a fire door has to overcome its own closer AND positively latch, so ADA sets it at the minimum opening force allowed by the code, not 5 pounds. So if some property manager tells you their exterior storefront closer has to hit 5 pounds, you tell them straight: that's not what the code says, and if I detune it that far it won't shut and latch in a fire.\n\nWhere the real force numbers live is the IBC, section 1010.1.3, and this is the one to cite when someone pushes back. Interior swinging egress doors other than fire doors: 5 pounds max, same as ADA. But for other swinging, sliding, and folding doors the code gives you a three-part test applied to the latch side \u2014 the latch releases at 15 pounds, the door sets in motion at 30 pounds, and it swings to full open at 15 pounds. Those are the ceilings you have room to work with on an exterior or fire door before you're out of bounds. And note \u2014 same as ADA \u2014 those numbers don't count the force to retract the latch bolt or disengage whatever's holding the door shut. Know those three numbers and you'll win the argument every time, because most people arguing with you have never read past \"five pounds.\"\n\nNow the fire door, because this is where liability stops being about comfort and starts being about people burning. NFPA 80 governs fire door assemblies, and the two non-negotiables are branded into the standard: a swinging fire door must be self-closing AND self-latching. Self-closing means it returns to fully closed from any open position with zero human help. Self-latching \u2014 what the trade calls positive latching \u2014 means the latch bolt actually throws into the strike and holds every cycle, because in a real fire the pressure differential across that door will try to blow it open, and a door that closed but didn't latch is a door that pops open and feeds the fire oxygen and a path. So on a fire door your latch-speed adjustment isn't cosmetic, it's life safety. This is exactly why you never, ever put passage-function trim, dummy trim, or push/pull hardware on a fire door \u2014 no latch bolt means no self-latching means instant failure. If you find a fire-rated opening with a passage set on it, that's a red-tag find and you say so.\n\nAnd props \u2014 I cannot say this loud enough. No wedges, no kick-downs, no chair jammed under it, no coat hook screwed into the jamb to hold it open. NFPA 80 prohibits blocking or wedging a fire door in the open position \u2014 doorstops, wedges, and other non-listed hold-open devices are barred under all circumstances. The only legal way to hold a fire door open is a listed hold-open device tied into the fire alarm system \u2014 one that releases and lets the door close on alarm or power loss. That propped door is the number-one thing that kills people, because the door that was supposed to compartmentalize the fire was standing wide when the smoke came. When you see a rock holding a stairwell fire door open, you don't shrug \u2014 you remove it and you tell the owner why. If they genuinely need it to stay open, you sell them a listed electromagnetic hold-open connected to the alarm. That's the right, legal fix, and it's good money besides.\n\nThen there's the annual inspection, NFPA 80 Chapter 5 (section 5.2) \u2014 this became real teeth when the IBC and NFPA 101 started requiring documented annual fire door inspections, and it's a legit revenue line for a locksmith who gets certified. The functional criteria come out to roughly eleven or twelve points depending on how you group the pairs-only items, and you should be able to rattle them off: labels present and legible on door, frame, and hardware (a painted-over or removed label is an automatic fail \u2014 never let a painter roller over a fire label); no open holes or breaks in the door or frame that breach the assembly; no missing or broken parts; clearances within tolerance; self-closing verified \u2014 the door completely closes from the full open position; self-latching verified \u2014 the latch secures the door every cycle; door and frame secured, aligned, and undamaged, not warped or pulling off the wall; required gasketing and edge seals intact where required; no field modifications that void the label; on pairs, the coordinator sequences the inactive leaf shut first and the astragal is intact; and nothing wedging or blocking the door. Miss any one and the assembly fails. Log the date, your name, and the disposition of every opening \u2014 the AHJ can pull those records.\n\nClearances are where guys guess and get it wrong, so memorize these from NFPA 80. The gap between the door and the frame at the top and along both vertical edges, and at the meeting edges of a pair, is 1/8 inch plus or minus 1/16 inch, maximum \u2014 that's the number for hollow metal doors and for the lower-rated wood doors. Above a 1/3-hour rating on the wood stuff the tolerance tightens to a hard 1/8 inch max with no plus-or-minus, but \"eighth of an inch at the top and sides\" is the number you carry. The bottom is the loose one: clearance under the door is 3/4 inch maximum, measured from the bottom of the door to the top of the finished floor or threshold. That 3/4-inch undercut is what confuses people into thinking they can trim any fire door \u2014 you can undercut up to 3/4 inch, but not past what the door's own label service allows, and if the bottom of the door sits more than 38 inches above the floor (think transom-height or Dutch-door leaves), the max tightens to 3/8 inch. When in doubt, the label wins over the general number.\n\nPut it all together and here's your check-in-this-order on any door with a closer. One, stopwatch the sweep: 90 to 12 degrees, is it five seconds or more? Too fast, back the sweep valve out a hair. Two, does it drive fully closed and \u2014 if it latches \u2014 throw the bolt every time? If it dies short, add latch speed, don't add more sweep. Three, push-pull gauge on the latch edge: interior non-fire under 5 pounds, exterior and fire within the IBC 15/30/15 window. Four, if it's rated, walk the NFPA 80 checklist \u2014 label, latch, self-close, clearances, no props, no wrong trim. Nine times out of ten a \"won't latch\" fire door is over-slowed latch speed or a dead battery in a hold-open, not a bad closer. And nine times out of ten a \"closes too fast\" complaint is somebody who cranked the valves the wrong way trying to stop a slam. Set the sweep for the person, set the latch for the fire, and document it \u2014 because on a fire door, your adjustment is the last thing standing between a compartment and a body count, and that's a signature you want to be proud of.",sources:["https://www.access-board.gov/ada/guides/chapter-4-entrances-doors-and-gates/","https://www.corada.com/documents/2010ADAStandards/404-2-8","https://www.corada.com/documents/2010ADAStandards/404-2-9","https://idighardware.com/2012/05/decoded-opening-force-and-closing-speed/","https://idighardware.com/2024/06/decoded-door-and-gate-closing-speed/"],tags:["Life safety","Astragal","Opening","NFPA 101","AHJ","Strike"],aliases:["fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","strike plate","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"sagging-doors",title:"Why doors sag, and how to fix them",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"A sagging door is gravity winning a slow argument with a few short screws. Here's the physics you need in your bones: the door is a lever, and the top hinge is the fulcrum carrying almost all the tension. Picture the door slab hanging off the jamb \u2014 the whole weight wants to rotate the door down and away, pivoting around the bottom hinge and hanging by its teeth at the top. That means the top hinge is being pulled straight out of the jamb, and the two or three little 3/4-inch screws holding that top leaf are the only things fighting it. A hollow-core interior door runs 25 to 30 pounds, a solid-core is 50 to 100, and an exterior slab can push past 150. All of that load lands hardest on those top screws. So when you walk up to a sagging door, do not start by planing the slab or chasing the latch \u2014 nine times out of ten the door itself is fine and the top hinge has simply lost its grip on the framing.\n\n[[diagram:sagging-door-diagnosis|Where the gap opens tells you which part gave up, and each one has a different fix]]\n\nRead the reveal before you touch a tool, because the gap tells you exactly what's wrong. You want a consistent 1/8-inch reveal all the way around \u2014 top, sides, and the vertical hinge-side and latch-side gaps. Now look at the pattern. A classic sag shows a gap that opens up at the top-latch corner and pinches tight at the bottom-latch corner, and the door drags on the sill or the strike as it swings. That diagonal, \"the door is tipping toward the latch and down,\" is the signature of the top hinge pulling away from the jamb. If instead the whole slab has shifted uniformly toward the latch side, you're looking at the jamb itself racking or the frame settling. And if the gaps are weird and inconsistent \u2014 tight in the middle, wide at the ends, or the door bows \u2014 now you might actually have a warped slab, which is the rare case, not the first guess.\n\nConfirm it with your hands, because feeling the failure beats guessing at it. Grab the door by the latch edge and lift up \u2014 if it moves and clunks, you've got play in the hinges. Then run a screwdriver over every hinge screw and turn. This is the money moment: a screw that spins forever with no resistance is stripped \u2014 the threads have chewed the jamb wood to powder \u2014 and that's your culprit. Do the top hinge first, it's almost always the one that's let go. While you're there, look at the knuckles. Work the door and watch the barrel of the hinge: if you see the leaves shifting vertically against each other, or you find fine metal filings or graphite dust collecting at the knuckle, the hinge pin and barrel are worn out. Cheap plated-steel hinges grind themselves loose over years of a heavy door, and no screw on earth fixes a worn-out knuckle \u2014 that hinge gets replaced.\n\nNow the fix everyone comes for \u2014 the 3-inch screw into the stud. Here's why it works and why it's the first move. Your standard residential hinge screw is a #8 or #9 running 3/4 to 1 inch, and it only bites the jamb board itself \u2014 a thin piece of pine that's floating in the rough opening on a few shims. It was never anchored to anything structural. The jamb sits inside the rough framing with a shim gap on each side \u2014 commonly 1/4 to 3/8 inch on a factory prehung, more on a site-built jamb where they leave extra room to adjust. So you swap one of those stubby screws for a #9 or #10 wood or construction screw at 2-1/2 to 3 inches long \u2014 a GRK R4 or a good cabinet/construction screw \u2014 and drive it through the hinge leaf, through the jamb, across that shim gap, and deep into the king or trimmer stud behind it. Suddenly the top hinge isn't hanging on soft pine anymore; it's tied straight into the framing of the house. That single screw both re-grips the hinge and pulls the whole top of the frame back toward plumb, which lifts the sagging latch corner back up. Reach for this before you touch anything else.\n\nDo it right, because the technique separates a pro fix from a callback. Pull the center screw from the top-hinge leaf \u2014 the center, not the top or bottom hole, so the pull stays balanced. Drill a clearance hole through the hinge and jamb so the new screw's shank slides free and actually clamps the jamb tight instead of spinning in place, and I'll usually run a pilot into the stud on hardwood framing so I'm not snapping a screwhead. Drive it with the door closed or held to your target reveal so you're pulling the frame into alignment, not locking in the sag. Drive it snug with the drill, then finish the last quarter-turn by hand with a screwdriver, watching the reveal as you go. And here's the gotcha: one long screw per hinge. Crank in three of them and you'll suck the jamb hard against the studs, blow your reveal out on the other side, and open ugly gaps at the casing miters. You're steering the frame, not winching it. Snug until the reveal reads 1/8 inch and stop.\n\nIf the screws just spin because the holes are blown out, you have to give the wood something to bite \u2014 a long screw in a stripped hole is still a stripped hole. The fastest field repair: jam the hole full of wooden toothpicks or golf tees dipped in wood glue, snap them flush, let it set, and drive into fresh meat. Match sticks, glue-soaked, work the same way. Wood filler and putty do not hold a load-bearing hinge screw \u2014 do not fall for that shortcut. The long-into-the-stud screw is the better answer anyway when you can reach framing, because it bypasses the ruined jamb wood entirely and goes for the real anchor behind it.\n\nShimming behind the leaf is your fine-tuning move once the screws are solid. If the hinge-side reveal is too tight and the door binds against the hinge jamb, you fold a shim \u2014 a slip of cardboard cut to the hinge outline, or a purpose-made hinge shim in the 3.5- to 4.5-inch sizes \u2014 and set it in the mortise behind the leaf. That kicks the leaf proud and pushes the door away from the hinge side, opening that gap and swinging the latch edge toward the strike. If it's the opposite \u2014 too much gap on the hinge side \u2014 you deepen the mortise a hair with a sharp chisel so the leaf sits further in and the door pulls toward the hinges. This is how you dial a 1/16-inch correction without moving a single screw, and it's often what finishes the job after the long screw has done the heavy lifting. Set your shims behind the vacant holes and work to that consistent 1/8-inch reveal top to bottom.\n\nReplace the hinge outright when the metal is the problem, not the mounting. Worn knuckles, a sloppy pin, a bent leaf, or a rusted exterior butt \u2014 none of that gets screwed back to health. Match what you pull: interior residential butts are typically 3.5-inch with 5/8-inch radius corners, and heavier solid-core or exterior doors run 4-inch, so measure before you buy. Match the corner radius to the mortise \u2014 most residential hinges are 5/8-inch or 1/4-inch radius \u2014 or you'll be chiseling. And step up the grade while you're in there \u2014 ANSI/BHMA A156.1 sorts hinges Grade 1 heavy-duty, Grade 2 and Grade 3 standard weight, down through Grade 4 lightweight, and if a heavy door keeps eating hinges, you were under-hinged from the start. Put a ball-bearing hinge on anything solid-core or exterior; plain-bearing plated steel is what wears out and starts the sag. Change hinges one at a time with the door still hung on the others and you keep your alignment through the whole swap.\n\nKnow when the door was simply never given enough support \u2014 that's a reinforce call, not a repair. The rule of thumb from the hinge world: one hinge per 30 inches of door height. Doors up to 60 inches take two, 60 to 90 inches take three, and 90 to 120 inches take four. Somebody hangs a heavy solid-core slab on two hinges and it sags no matter how many long screws you drive, because two anchor points can't carry that lever arm. The cure is adding the third (middle) hinge, mortised dead in line, to split the load the top hinge was carrying alone. Same logic on wide or unusually heavy doors \u2014 add a hinge, go heavier-duty, and tie the top one to the stud. Reinforce when the geometry is wrong; repair when the geometry is right and the hardware just let go.\n\nLast, know the ceiling on this and the code line you don't cross. If you've tied the top hinge to the stud, replaced worn hinges, shimmed the leaf, and the door still won't sit square, the problem is upstream \u2014 the frame is racked, the rough opening moved, or the house is settling. The tell is more than one door in the building sticking at once, plus drywall cracks fanning off the door corners; that's structural, not a hinge, and it's a framing or foundation conversation, not a screw. And one hard rule for anyone working commercial or multifamily: do not freelance field fixes on a labeled fire door. Under NFPA 80, a fire-rated assembly is a listed, tested unit \u2014 the hinges, fasteners, and hole preps all have to fall inside the door and hardware manufacturers' listings. That standard spells out the fasteners (steel machine screws into reinforced doors, or No. 12 x 1-1/4-inch steel wood screws into wood and composite doors, with 5/32-inch pilot holes) and it does allow unused holes to be filled \u2014 but only by steel fasteners that completely fill the hole, the same material as the door or frame, or a listed patching material installed per the maker's procedure. So you don't just plug a hole with putty and re-drill wherever you like, and you don't drop in random non-listed hinges or oversize a mortise on a whim \u2014 you verify the listing allows the prep, use listed components, and follow the label, full stop. On a plain interior door in a house, the long screw and a shim are the whole game.",sources:["https://www.mrhandyman.com/blog/how-to-fix-a-stubborn-sagging-door/","https://www.hingeoutlet.com/blogs/news/do-i-need-2-or-3-hinges-1","https://monroeengineering.com/blog/do-doors-need-2-hinges-or-3-hinges/","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A1561-2025-Butts-and-Hinges","https://www.danddhardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-A156-1-Butt-Hinge-id3399779.html"],tags:["Opening","Strike","Plug"],aliases:["lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"hinges",title:"Hinge hardware",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here is the thing nobody tells you when you start: nine sags out of ten, the problem is not the door, it is the hinges \u2014 and more specifically the top hinge and the screws in it. A door that drags the threshold on the latch side, a gap that yawns open at the top, a door that will not stay put or swings on its own \u2014 before you touch the closer or start shimming the frame, put your hand on the top hinge and pull the door toward you. If the leaves separate and you can see daylight in the knuckle, you have found it. Gravity works on the top hinge in tension and the bottom in compression, so the top is always where the failure shows first. Fix the top hinge \u2014 usually by driving one or two 3-inch screws through it into the stud behind the jamb \u2014 and half your \"sagging door\" calls are done before you open the toolbox.\n\n[[diagram:hinge-selection|Size, bearing, pin, material... and the pin is the one that lets a door leave the building]]\n\nLet me walk you through the family, because \"a hinge is a hinge\" is exactly the kind of thinking that gets a 250-pound storefront door hung on residential butts that grenade in six months. The workhorse of commercial work is the five-knuckle full-mortise ball-bearing butt hinge. \"Full mortise\" means both leaves get routed flush \u2014 one into the butt edge of the door, one into the rabbet of the frame \u2014 so nothing stands proud when the door is closed. That is your steel-frame, steel-door standard, and it is what you spec unless something about the opening forces you off it. The reference part everybody in the trade knows is the Ives 5BB1: five knuckle, ball bearing, standard weight. Its heavier brother is the 5BB1HW. Learn those two numbers and you can talk to any supply house in the country.\n\nThe ball-bearing versus plain-bearing question is really a duty-cycle question, and it is not close. A plain-bearing (also called plain-friction) hinge is metal knuckle riding on metal knuckle \u2014 fine for a bedroom door that opens twice a day, a disaster on anything that moves. As the friction surfaces wear, they gall, then squeal, then the door starts to bind and drop. Ball-bearing hinges put hardened races between the knuckles so the door swings on bearings, not on galling steel. The rule I hold: any door with a closer on it gets ball-bearing, no exceptions. A closer slams that door into the same arc four hundred, six hundred, a thousand times a day, and a plain hinge simply will not survive it. That is also why fire code went this direction \u2014 NFPA 80 requires all hinges and pivots on a fire door assembly to be the ball-bearing type, with the one carve-out being spring hinges, and with other antifriction bearing surfaces allowed only if they meet ANSI/BHMA A156.1. So on a rated opening you are not even allowed to think about plain bearings.\n\nWeight and frequency decide standard versus heavy. Per the Ives spec sheets, the standard-weight 5BB1 runs two ball-bearing assemblies and is rated for medium-weight doors under roughly 150 lb and medium-frequency use \u2014 under about 400 cycles a day. The heavy-weight 5BB1HW carries four ball-bearing assemblies and steps you up to doors over 150 lb and high-frequency use \u2014 think a school entrance, a hospital corridor, a busy retail door. When in doubt, go heavy. The upcharge on a heavy-weight hinge is a few dollars a pair; the callback to rehang a door that ate its bearings is a whole afternoon plus your reputation. On a real fire door, mind the material minimums too \u2014 NFPA 80's sizing table calls for, on a typical 3-foot by 7-foot rated door, a steel hinge (mortise or surface-mounted) at least 4.5 inches high and 0.134 inches thick. That 0.134 is a real number, not decoration; a thin residential hinge does not carry the listing.\n\nNow sizes, because this is where amateurs guess and pros calculate. The height of the hinge (the 4.5-inch or 5-inch dimension) tracks door width and weight; the width of the hinge (open, leaf-to-leaf) has to clear the trim and casing when the door swings back. The height rule, straight off the industry sizing charts: a 1-3/8-inch door up to 32 inches wide takes a 3.5-inch hinge; over 32 up to 36 inches, step to 4 inch. A 1-3/4-inch door \u2014 your standard commercial door \u2014 up to 36 inches wide takes a 4.5-inch hinge, and over 36 up to 48 inches wide you go to a 5-inch. So the honest short version most of us carry in our heads: 1-3/4 door at 3-0 or narrower, 4.5 inch; anything wider than 36 inches or a heavy door, 5 inch. There is also a weight overlay \u2014 a door pushing past 100 lb or wider than 36 inches wants you stepping up a size category regardless of what width alone says.\n\nThe open width of the hinge is a separate calculation and it is the one that bites people on doors with deep trim or thick walls. The formula is: (door thickness minus hinge backset) times 2, plus the clearance you need to swing the door clear of the trim, equals minimum open hinge width. Standard backset for doors from 1-3/8 up through 2-1/4 inches thick is 1/4 inch; at 2-1/2 inches it opens up to 3/8. Work a 1-3/8 door with a 1/4 backset needing 1 inch of clearance: (1.375 minus 0.25) times 2 plus 1 equals 3.25 \u2014 round up to the next real size, a 3.5-inch hinge. When the wall is thick or the casing is fat and that number blows past a standard leaf, you are into wide-throw hinges \u2014 the ones built wider than they are tall to move the pivot point outward so the door clears a deep jamb or fat casing when it swings back. Do not confuse that with a swing-clear hinge, which is a different animal for a different problem: a swing-clear pulls the door leaf completely out of the opening at 90 degrees to recover clear width for an ADA opening (both the ADA Standards and ICC A117.1 want a 32-inch minimum clear width measured with the door open 90 degrees). One clears trim, the other clears the opening \u2014 do not spec the wrong one. Either way, do not fake it: measure the trim projection and run the math, or you hang a door that bangs its own casing.\n\nHinge count is a code item, so know the real rule and not just the shop shorthand. NFPA 80, and the door manufacturers' listings behind it, say a door up to 60 inches tall gets two hinges, and you add one hinge for every additional 30 inches of height or fraction thereof. So a standard 6-foot-8 (80-inch) door lands three hinges (60 plus a 20-inch fraction). A 7-foot (84-inch) door is still three. An 8-foot (96-inch) door needs four (60 plus a full 30 plus a 6-inch fraction). A 10-foot (120-inch) door needs four as well \u2014 120 minus 60 is exactly 60 inches, which is two full 30-inch increments on top of the base two hinges, so four total, not five; watch that one, because it is a common miscount. You will hear the trade rule of thumb stated as \"three hinges up to 7 feet, then one more per 30 inches over that\" \u2014 it lands on the same answers in the range you actually work in, but the letter of the code is the 60-inch-plus-one-per-30 version, so cite that one on an inspection and do the arithmetic. Spacing is the other half: the top hinge sits 5 inches down from the head (measured to the top of the hinge), the bottom hinge 10 inches up from the finished floor (to the bottom of the hinge), and the middle hinge centered between them \u2014 or evenly divided when you run four. Those 5-and-10 numbers are the template standard; a fourth hinge goes between the top hinge and the middle.\n\nSecurity is where the trade earns its keep, and it starts with the outswing door. Any door that swings out \u2014 most exterior commercial doors do, for egress \u2014 presents its hinge barrels and pins to the outside. An intruder with a punch and hammer taps the pins out, pops the hinges, and lifts your locked door right out of the frame with the deadbolt still thrown. The answer is a non-removable pin, NRP. On an NRP hinge a small set screw is threaded through the barrel that, when the door is closed, seats against a groove in the pin so the pin cannot be driven or pulled. The screw is only accessible when the door is open, so once it is shut and locked, that pin is captive. Here is the gotcha every apprentice needs beaten into them: NRP does nothing on an inswing door, because inswing doors already hide their pins on the secure side \u2014 spec'ing NRP there is just paying for a screw you will never use. NRP is for outswing. Every time.\n\nNRP is only half the anti-lift story, and this is the part cheap installs skip. Even with the pin captive, if someone shears or cuts the barrels, the door can still be pried off the hinge side. That is what the security stud is for \u2014 an interlocking stud (also called a security tab), a raised pin on one leaf that projects into a matching hole in the mating leaf when the door is closed. Now the two leaves are physically keyed together; the door cannot be lifted or forced off the hinge edge even if the pin or knuckles are compromised. On a serious outswing exterior opening you want both: NRP to defeat the pin attack, security stud to defeat the lift attack. Ives lists both as options on the 5BB1 and 5BB1HW families, along with security (one-way, spin-out-resistant) fasteners so nobody backs your hinge screws out with a cordless driver. And remember what all this hardening teaches you defensively: the reason you know to pull pins and lift outswing doors is exactly why, on any lockout or survey, the outswing door with plain removable-pin hinges is your fastest legitimate non-destructive way in \u2014 verify your authority to be there, then that opening is often softer than the lock.\n\nHospital tips are the next specialty you will meet, and the name is doing double duty so keep the two ideas straight. Classically, a \"hospital tip\" hinge has the ends of the barrel cut and rounded off flush and sloped rather than left as square-cornered button or steeple tips \u2014 so there is no ledge to catch dirt, mop water, or a gurney, and nothing that collects contamination. That is the sanitation origin. In modern behavioral-health, correctional, and psych-ward work, the same geometry is marketed as anti-ligature: the barrel tips are closed off and sloped so there is no gap, no protrusion, no anchor point where a patient could tie off a cord or belt. When you spec a door for a hospital, a jail, or a memory-care unit, \"hospital tip\" or \"anti-ligature\" is the term, and on continuous hinges you will see retrofit anti-ligature caps like the NGP (National Guard Products) TIPIT that close off the top of the gear channel on continuous hinges you have already got installed. Do not hang a standard steeple-tip hinge in a behavioral-health room \u2014 that is a liability finding waiting to happen at the next inspection.\n\nSpring hinges are self-closing hinges with a coil spring in the barrel, and their honest use is narrow. They are legitimate where a door needs to close on its own but a full closer is overkill or unwanted \u2014 some fire-rated residential and light-commercial openings, garage-to-house doors \u2014 and NFPA 80 gives them their specific exemption from the ball-bearing requirement precisely because they self-close. Where they earn their reputation for trouble is people using them as a poor man's closer on a real commercial door. They do not control the swing, they just slam it; they weaken over time and stop latching, which on a fire door means it fails to positively latch and fails inspection. If a rated door needs to close and latch reliably under traffic, put a real door closer on it. Save spring hinges for the light self-closing jobs they were built for, and when you do use them on a fire opening, mind that the listing usually calls for a specific number (often two or three) and they get tensioned per the manufacturer's wrench-and-pin procedure until the door closes and latches from any position.\n\nContinuous hinges \u2014 the full-height ones \u2014 are the answer when butt hinges keep failing on an opening and you are tired of going back. There are two flavors. Geared continuous hinges (Pemko, the Roton line, Select, Markar) are aluminum extrusions where the door leaf and frame leaf mesh through a geared channel running the entire height of the door, with a cap covering the gear. Pin-and-barrel continuous hinges run a continuous pin down interlocking knuckles instead. Either way, the win is the same and it is real: the load is spread across the whole height of the door instead of being concentrated at three points, so you eliminate the hinge-edge stress that tears out the top hinge and cracks the jamb on high-abuse doors. That is why continuous hinges get specified in lieu of pivots or butt hinges on high-frequency, high-abuse openings \u2014 school entrances, hospital doors, anything that gets kicked, propped, and slammed all day. They also mortise full-height so there is no pinch-point gap along the hinge edge, which is a genuine kid-safety and finger-safety feature in schools and daycares. The tradeoffs: they cost more, they demand a straight and true frame (a continuous hinge telegraphs a bowed jamb mercilessly), and geared aluminum ones come in specific listed lengths and duty ratings \u2014 an 83-inch is the common cut for a 7-foot door, in standard and heavy-duty extrusions, with full-surface units like the Pemko CFS83 rated up to a couple hundred pounds and change. If a door has eaten two sets of butt hinges, stop replacing butts and put a continuous hinge on it.\n\nOn repair-versus-replace and template discipline, the golden rule is: a factory-machined door and frame are cut to the ANSI/BHMA A156.7 template, and you honor that template or you fight the door forever. Full-mortise prep is 5 inches down from the head to the top hinge, 10 inches up from the finished floor to the bottom hinge, standard hinges spaced evenly between \u2014 with hinge-height, hinge-width, corner radius (typically 1/4-inch or 5/8-inch corners, or square), and screw-hole pattern all matching the existing prep. This is why you never mix hinge templates in one opening and why \"template\" hinges exist as a category: the screw holes land exactly where the machine cut them so you drop in and drive. When you replace, match the existing prep \u2014 same size, same corner radius, same template \u2014 do them one at a time with the door hung so it never drops, and shim behind a leaf with hinge shims rather than backing screws out to chase a gap. If the frame is racked or the mortise is blown out and mangled, that is when you stop fussing with butts and reach for the continuous hinge to bridge the damage, or repair the mortise with a weld-and-remachine on a steel frame. And always \u2014 before you condemn a hinge or a door \u2014 put your hand on that top hinge first. Nine times out of ten, that is your answer.",sources:["https://idighardware.com/2016/07/decoded-nfpa-80-requirements-for-hinges/","https://idighardware.com/2019/02/qq-hinges-for-fire-door-assemblies/","https://www.iveshinges.com/Ives-5BB1-Five-Knuckle-Ball-Bearing-Standard-Weight-Full-Mortise-Butt-Hinge-items.aspx","https://www.iveshinges.com/Ives-5BB1HW-Five-Knuckle-Ball-Bearing-Heavy-Weight-Full-Mortise-Butt-Hinge-items.aspx","https://doorsforpros.com/ives-commercial-5bb1-hinges-pack-of-3-hinges-choose-finish-choose-hinge-type.html"],tags:["Backset","Opening"],aliases:["2-3/8","2-3/4","distance to center","wrong backset","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"door-hardening",title:"Beefing up door security",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Before you touch a single screw, get one thing straight \u2014 a residential door fails at the jamb, not at the lock. Nine times out of ten the kick-in you get called to fix didn't beat the deadbolt at all. The bolt held fine; the pine jamb split around it, or the little strike plate that came in the blister pack tore right out of the wood because it was hung on two 3/4\" screws that never reached anything but soft trim. So when a customer says \"I want a stronger lock,\" what they usually need is a stronger opening. The lock is maybe a quarter of the fight. Frame, strike, hinges, and door edge are the other three quarters, and that's where your money and your hour go if you want the door to actually stop a boot.\n\n[[diagram:residential-hardening-stack|Spend in the order things actually fail, with the cylinder last]]\n\nStart with the box strike, because it's the single highest-value part you can install and most people have never seen one. A standard strike is a flat stamped plate with a hole and a little dust box behind it, or no box at all \u2014 just an open mortise. A box strike is a fully enclosed steel pocket that wraps the bolt on all four sides. Why does that matter? Loiding and spreading. With an open strike, a guy can get a pry bar in and spread the jamb away from the door just enough for the bolt tip to clear \u2014 an inch of gap and the door's open, no wood broken. The enclosed box captures the end of the bolt so it can't walk out sideways when the frame flexes. On a deadbolt, always spec the box strike. Bruteforce-wise, the box also spreads the impact load into the plate instead of letting the bolt lever against a thin lip of steel. Cheap, ugly, hidden behind the door when it's closed \u2014 nobody ever sees it, and it's one of the best twenty-dollar upgrades in the trade.\n\nNow the screws \u2014 this is the whole ballgame and it costs you three dollars. The strike plate has to be tied to the framing behind the jamb, not to the jamb itself. The jamb is usually 3/4\" pine or fingerjoint nailed to a rough stud with a 1/2\" to 3/4\" air gap and a shim or two between them. Short screws bite only the jamb, so a kick loads a floppy board with nothing behind it. You want screws long enough to pass through the jamb, cross the shim gap, and sink a solid inch into the king stud or trimmer. Here's the thing to know about where that \"3-inch screw\" rule actually comes from: the base model IRC and IBC don't mandate long strike screws on an ordinary house \u2014 that language lives in local security ordinances and building-code amendments that plenty of jurisdictions have adopted, the kind that read \"the deadbolt strike plate shall be a minimum of 18 gauge metal with four offset screw holes\u2026 attached to the door jamb with four screws not less than three inches in length and penetrating at least one inch into the nearest stud.\" That's the wording to quote when a customer balks \u2014 just don't tell them the national code forces it everywhere, because it doesn't; check your local amendment. Either way it's the right build. I run 3\" case-hardened screws top and bottom on every strike, latch and deadbolt both, and if the ordinance wants four screws in a longer plate, I give it four. Regular drywall screws snap \u2014 they're brittle and the head pops. Buy the hardened deck-style or the ones that ship with real security strikes. One more field note: if you drive a 3\" screw and it spins free, you missed the stud or you're into the gap only \u2014 back it out, angle it slightly toward the stud, or shim behind the jamb so the screw has something to clamp. A screw that doesn't hit wood on the far end is decoration.\n\nIf you want to go past the little box strike, step up to a high-security strike or a full-length security strike. These run 12 to 18 inches or more, some past 18\", with multiple screw holes \u2014 as many as eight or nine \u2014 drilled to accept #10 screws 3 inches or longer, and they're built to grab both the jamb and the studs over a long span. The whole point is load distribution \u2014 instead of concentrating a kick into the two square inches around the bolt, you spread it up and down the frame so no single point takes enough shear to split the wood. On a door that's been kicked before, or a rental where you know it's coming, a long security strike over both the latch and deadbolt is the move. Belt and suspenders: a box strike behind the bolt and a long face plate spanning the studs. That combination is what makes a hollow-core-in-a-pine-jamb door behave like something a lot more expensive.\n\nLatch guards and astragals cover the gap \u2014 the visible slot between the door edge and the jamb where every jimmy, shim, and pry bar goes in. A latch guard is a formed metal plate, usually cold-rolled or stainless, that bridges that gap and extends a few inches above and below the latch. On an outswing door \u2014 and this is the common one on commercial back doors \u2014 the latch faces the attacker and the guard is mandatory, because without it anyone can get a screwdriver on the exposed bolt. Here's the gotcha: on an outswing door you cannot mount a latch guard with ordinary screws from the attack side, because the attacker just backs them out. That's what sex bolts are for. A sex bolt (a barrel-and-screw through-bolt) passes clean through the door, so the guard is clamped by a fastener that has no exposed head to attack and can't be unscrewed from outside \u2014 the same reason you through-bolt door closers on aluminum storefront instead of trusting sheet-metal screws that rip out. Good latch guards for metal jambs come with a welded pin that seats into a drilled hole in the frame; when the door's shut that pin ties door and jamb together and defeats the spreading attack directly. Anti-loid lips on the guard block the credit-card-and-shim trick on a plain spring latch. Rule of thumb: outswing door, sex-bolt the guard, and if the frame is hollow metal, use the pinned version.\n\nAstragals are the vertical cousin \u2014 a full-height strip that closes the gap top to bottom, and the go-to on pairs of doors where two leaves meet with nothing but air between them. A flat astragal is a single strip; you want it heavy enough to actually resist a bar rather than fold, and it needs to be through-bolted, not screwed on \u2014 carriage bolts a few inches apart, because if you hang it with wood screws a pry bar peels it off in one motion, which is the number-one install failure I see. (Those thickness and spacing numbers are field practice, not a code spec \u2014 buy a heavy-gauge astragal and through-bolt it and you're fine.) The real answer on a high-risk pair is the interlocking security astragal: two heavy-gauge stainless pieces, one on the door and one on the frame (or one on each leaf), that physically hook together when the door closes. That interlock resists spreading and prying because you can't separate the leaves without shearing steel. It's the best forced-entry protection in this category and it's priced like it \u2014 figure a flat astragal on the low end and an interlocking one several times that \u2014 but on a jewelry back door or a pharmacy, that's cheap insurance.\n\nFor the door itself \u2014 the slab \u2014 a reinforcer wrap is how you keep the bore area from splitting. Any door with a knob bore and a deadbolt bore has two big holes drilled a few inches apart through the lock stile, and that's the weakest cross-section in the whole slab; a hard hit cracks the wood right between the two bores or blows the edge out. A door reinforcer, sometimes called a wrap plate, is a U-shaped steel sleeve that slides over the edge of the door and sandwiches both faces plus the edge, tying that whole weakened zone together and clamping it so it can't split. They're sized to the door thickness \u2014 1-3/4\" for a standard exterior slab, 1-3/8\" for interior \u2014 and to the backset, typically 2-3/8\", with the two bores set 5-1/2\" on center. Prime-Line and MAG make the common ones \u2014 the MAG U 10261 Install-a-Lock style and the Prime-Line U 10539 stainless wrap are the parts you'll reach for, and they double as a repair when someone's already misdrilled or cracked a door: the wrap hides the damage and restores the strength. Match the door thickness, the backset, and the bore spacing or the deadbolt won't line up. If the whole jamb is chewed up, there are full-length jamb repair kits \u2014 Prime-Line's U 11026 runs roughly 59\" of steel down the strike side \u2014 that reinforce the entire strike side in one shot.\n\nThe hinge side is the half of the door everybody forgets, and on an outswing door it's the whole game. If the door swings out toward the attacker, the hinge barrels and pins are on the outside \u2014 and a standard loose-pin butt hinge means a guy with a screwdriver taps the pins up, lifts the leaf off, and walks past every lock you installed because the door just comes off its knuckles. Two fixes, and you want both. First, the security stud, also called a set stud or hospital tip \u2014 a stud pressed into one hinge leaf that seats into a matching hole in the other when the door's closed, so even if every pin is pulled, the interlocked leaves hold the door in the frame and it can't be lifted or pried off the hinge edge. Second, NRP \u2014 non-removable pin \u2014 a set screw threaded through the barrel that lands in a groove on the pin and locks it in; the set screw only clears when the door is open, so from outside there's nothing to back out. Ives 5BB1 NRP and equivalent five-knuckle ball-bearing hinges are the standard commercial spec. On any outswing exterior door, the answer is NRP hinges with security studs, full stop \u2014 and while you're there, throw a couple 3\" screws into the hinge jamb leaf too, into the stud, because the hinge side gets kicked as often as the strike side.\n\nSex bolts deserve their own paragraph because apprentices under-use them and every seasoned guy leans on them. Any time hardware mounts to an aluminum storefront door, a hollow metal door, or anything where a screw is going into thin skin with air behind it, a wood screw or sheet-metal screw has almost no pullout strength \u2014 it strips, it rips, the closer or the guard or the pull tears off. A sex bolt is a through-bolt: a threaded barrel from one side, a machine screw from the other, clamping the hardware through the full thickness of the door. That's how you mount closers, latch guards, and pulls so they can't be popped, and on the security side it means there's no accessible screw head for an attacker to attack. They come sized to door thickness \u2014 1-3/4\" is the storefront standard. When in doubt on metal or glass-aluminum doors, through-bolt it.\n\nNow the lock, because it does matter once the opening is solid \u2014 spec a Grade 1 deadbolt with a 1-inch throw and you've matched the lock to the frame you just built. Auxiliary deadbolts are graded under ANSI/BHMA A156.36 (and residential deadbolts under A156.40) by real destructive testing, and the commonly cited benchmarks are worth knowing so you can talk a customer off a builder-grade blister-pack unit: Grade 1 survives on the order of 250,000 operating cycles and the full impact-strike series; Grade 2 runs around 150,000 cycles; Grade 3, the builder junk, about 100,000 cycles and the lightest strike test. The difference is a lock built to shrug off the impact battery versus one rated to fail early. The 1-inch throw is the other half, and here's the key fact: the 1-inch minimum bolt throw is a requirement for deadbolts across all three grades, not a Grade 1 exclusive \u2014 a proper deadbolt projects a full inch so that when the frame flexes or someone spreads the jamb, there's still enough bolt buried in the strike to hold. What you're really buying up the grade ladder is the impact and cycle survival, not more throw. Where you get burned is a short-throw auxiliary bolt or a spring latch masquerading as security \u2014 those clear the strike the instant the jamb moves, and that's your no-forced-entry burglary where nothing looks broken. Match it up: Grade 1 bolt, 1\" throw, box strike, 3\" screws to the stud. Any one of those alone is half a job \u2014 together they're a door that makes the burglar decide the window's easier.\n\nLast, the judgment calls, because part of being the pro is knowing when to stop. Repair versus replace: if the slab is solid-core or metal and only the bore area or the jamb is damaged, a wrap plate and a jamb kit save the customer a full door and hang \u2014 do that. But if it's a hollow-core exterior door, no amount of strike hardware fixes a slab you can punch through \u2014 the honest call is a solid-core or steel replacement, and don't let good strike hardware lull anybody into thinking a cardboard door is secure. On liability and code: this is where you have to be precise, because it's a life-safety line. The governing principle in the model codes (IBC Section 1010, and the IRC for dwellings) is that an egress door has to be openable from the inside without a key, tool, or special knowledge or effort. A double-cylinder deadbolt \u2014 keyed both sides, no thumbturn \u2014 violates that on a required egress door because someone has to find a key to get out in a fire. Key-operated locks are permitted only in narrow situations the code spells out (certain exterior or single-tenant doors, occupancy-limited, with a permanent \"THIS DOOR TO REMAIN UNLOCKED WHEN THIS SPACE IS OCCUPIED\" sign) \u2014 that's the exception, not the rule, and your local AHJ may not even allow it. So you don't put a keyed-both-sides bolt on a bedroom or a primary exit. When a customer wants one to stop a burglar reaching through adjacent glass, the right answer is usually a solid slab, laminated or security glazing, or a thumbturn with a captive-key setup \u2014 not trapping people inside. And on any of this work \u2014 a locksmith verifies authority before hardening or opening a door. Same knowledge that tells you how a jamb-spread or a pin-pull defeats a door is exactly what tells you how to shut those attacks down; that's the whole point of knowing the bypass. You harden what you understand.",sources:["https://up.codes/s/strike-plates","https://www.acmelocksmith.com/articles/ansi-lock-grades-explained/","https://blog.ansi.org/ansi/ansi-grade-levels-bhma-locks-hardware-tests/","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A15636-2020-Auxiliary-Hardware","https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/bhma/ansibhmaa156402020"],relatedGuides:["commercial-security-survey","video-surveillance","alarm-systems-service","storefront-anatomy-threats"],tags:["Astragal","Strike","Backset","Loiding","Stile","AHJ","Opening","Bypass"],aliases:["won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","2-3/4","distance to center","wrong backset","credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","no-pick entry","bypass tool","get past the lock","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"door-prep-jigs",title:"Door prep tools & jigs (using and making them)",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here is the first thing to get straight, because it governs every tool you'll buy or build: door prep in North America runs on a small handful of standardized numbers, and once you know them cold, the whole trade stops being guesswork. The workhorse is the \"161\" prep \u2014 the cylindrical (bored) lock cutout. You'll see it credited to ANSI A115.2, which is the historical door-prep designation; that A115 door-preparation series is now maintained under ANSI/BHMA A156.115, so both citations point at the same standard. The 161 is a 2-1/8\" crossbore straight through the face of the door, a 1\" edge bore drilled in from the strike edge to meet it, a backset of either 2-3/8\" (residential/light commercial) or 2-3/4\" (commercial standard), and a latch-face mortise of 1-1/8\" x 2-1/4\". Deadbolts run the same 1\" edge bore and the same backset options but a smaller 1-1/2\" crossbore. Burn those into your head. Nine times out of ten, when somebody's new lock \"won't line up,\" it isn't the lock \u2014 it's that they drilled a 2-3/8\" backset door for a 2-3/4\" lock, or vice versa, and the spindle is fighting the bore. So before any tool touches the door, you measure backset: distance from the door edge to the center of the crossbore. Everything downstream depends on that one number being right.\n\n[[diagram:door-prep-161|The dimensions every jig is built around, and the one prep you cannot undo]]\n\nTemplates first, because a template is the cheapest jig you own and the one that saves the most doors. Every quality lockset and deadbolt ships with a paper or card template \u2014 a folded L that wraps the edge of the door and prints two things: the crossbore center on the face and the edge-bore center on the edge. The trick amateurs miss is that the fold IS the reference. You crease it hard on the door edge, tape it flat, and center-punch through the printed marks with an awl or a spring-loaded automatic center punch before you drill a thing. That punch mark is what keeps your hole saw's pilot bit from walking. On a hollow metal door especially, an unpunched pilot skates across the paint and you've got a scar 1/4\" off center that no escutcheon will cover. The template also tells you which height line to use. Don't memorize a magic number \u2014 the industry-standard mounting figure the Steel Door Institute and the hardware schedules point to puts the lock centerline right around 38\" to the knob/lever and roughly 40-5/16\" to the strike/latch centerline off the finished floor, but that's a recommended location, not a code mandate. What IS enforceable is the operable-hardware envelope: ADA and ICC A117.1 require latches, locks, and operating devices between 34\" and 48\" above the finished floor, mounted so they work without tight grasping or twisting of the wrist. Always trust the template and the existing hinge/strike layout over a remembered number.\n\nNow the boring itself. For wood and for hollow metal alike, the two sizes that live in your bag are a 2-1/8\" hole saw and a 1\" hole saw \u2014 that pair does virtually every cylindrical lock and the crossbore-plus-edge-bore on a deadbolt too (the deadbolt crossbore drops to 1-1/2\", so add that saw if you set deadbolts). Buy bi-metal, not the cheap carbon-steel hole saws \u2014 a bi-metal saw with 8 or 10 TPI will cut wood AND the thin steel skin of a hollow metal door, where a carbon saw dulls on the first steel door and then burns. Here's the technique that separates pros from hacks on the crossbore: drill from BOTH faces, not straight through. You bore until the pilot bit just pokes through the far side, then stop, walk around, and finish from the other face using that pilot hole. Do that and the exit side stays clean \u2014 no blown-out splinters on a wood door, no torn burr ring on steel. Drill straight through and the backside of a veneer door tears out every time, and now you're explaining to the customer why their new door has a ragged hole. Keep the hole saw dead perpendicular; a jig helps, but even freehand, sight it against a square.\n\nMetal doors change the rules, and this is where a lot of guys get themselves in trouble. On a hollow metal (HM) door, the face is thin steel \u2014 a Level 2 heavy-duty door runs an 18-gauge (nominal 0.042\"\u20130.048\") face, lighter doors thinner, heavy Level 3/Level 4 doors 16 or 14 \u2014 but the important thing is what's BEHIND the skin. A commercial HM door built to a 161 prep already has a lock reinforcement spot-welded inside exactly where the crossbore goes. Get the gauges right, because this is where the draft floating around gets it wrong: under ANSI/SDI A250.6, the minimum reinforcement for BOTH the bored (cylindrical) lock and the mortise lock is MSG No. 14 (0.067\") \u2014 not 16-gauge for one and 14 for the other. Hinge reinforcements are heavier still: A250.6 calls for MSG No. 10 (0.123\") on a standard 1-3/4\" door and No. 12 (0.093\") on a 1-3/8\" door. (You may run into legacy 7-gauge hinge reinforcing on older or high-security doors, but the current standard's minimum is 10-gauge, so quote the standard, not a remembered number.) On a factory-prepped door you're often just enlarging or cleaning an existing hole \u2014 but on a BLANK slab, or when you're relocating hardware, you're cutting through two skins plus that reinforcement, and a bi-metal hole saw wants low RPM, firm steady pressure, and cutting oil. Run it fast and dry and you'll blue the teeth and glaze the steel in one hole. Slow, wet, and patient. For the 1\" edge bore on steel, the edge is a folded seam \u2014 go slow there too, it likes to grab.\n\nThe step bit (unibit) is your friend on thin metal for the SMALL stuff \u2014 pilot holes, screw clearance, enlarging existing holes in a strike or a frame \u2014 but understand its limit: common step bits top out around 3/4\" (a #3) to 1-3/8\" (a #5 or #11), so they don't do a 2-1/8\" crossbore. Where they shine is running up a hole in one pass through thin sheet steel without a hole saw's grab, and self-deburring as the next step shoulders the edge; each step is sized for material up to about 1/8\" thick. For anything bigger on sheet metal \u2014 and this is the classic deadbolt-in-a-metal-door trick \u2014 you reach for a knockout punch. Now, the gotcha that will bite you: knockout punches are named by CONDUIT trade size, not by the finished hole. A \"1-1/2 inch conduit\" Greenlee Slug-Buster (K2P-1-1/2) actually cuts a hole about 1.95\" in diameter \u2014 that's the NEC conduit clearance size, NOT a 1-1/2\" hole. A deadbolt crossbore is a true 1-1/2\". So if you punch a 1-1/2\"-conduit KO expecting a deadbolt bore, you've made the hole almost a half-inch too big and ruined the door. Match the knockout's ACTUAL cut diameter to your hardware, and use it only where a hole saw would chatter \u2014 like enlarging a bore in a thin gutless door skin, or frame work. You pre-drill a pilot for the draw stud, thread the stud, seat the die on the back and the punch cutter on the front, and wrench the two together until the slug pops. Clean, round, burr-free \u2014 far better than a hole saw skating on thin steel.\n\nMortise-lock prep is a different animal and the reason mortise jigs exist. A mortise lock isn't a bore \u2014 it's a deep rectangular pocket routed into the thickness of the door edge, typically for a 4-7/8\" or 8\" faceplate depending on the lock, with the pocket sized to the specific lock body (Sargent, Schlage L, Best, Corbin all differ). You can rough it by drilling a row of overlapping holes down the edge with a self-centering doweling setup and cleaning the walls with chisels \u2014 and honestly, on a one-off in the field, a sharp 1\" chisel and a brace of overlapping bores is still how a lot of us do it. But for a clean pocket you want either a dedicated mortising jig or a router setup. A router in a mortise jig gives you crisp, square-walled pockets to a controlled depth; you drill out the bulk first to save the router bit, then rout to the layout lines, then square the corners with a chisel because a router bit leaves radiused corners. Chisel work still matters everywhere: paring the faceplate mortise so the plate sits flush (proud of the edge and the door binds; too deep and it looks like garbage), squaring router-radiused corners, and cutting hinge gains on a wood door. Keep two chisels sharp \u2014 a 1\" and a narrower 1/2\" \u2014 sharp enough to pare end grain without a mallet, and you'll do faceplate and strike mortises clean every time.\n\nStrikes \u2014 ASA/ANSI strike prep \u2014 trip up more installs than people admit, because there are two families and they aren't interchangeable. The commercial standard is the ASA strike (also called the 4-7/8\" strike or ANSI strike): a full-lip plate 4-7/8\" tall x 1-1/4\" wide, mortised into the frame with a box/cup behind it for the latch to seat into. That's what a 161-prepped commercial opening expects, and it's what you cut into a hollow metal frame. The residential/light side often uses the T-strike \u2014 the little \"T\"-shaped plate, commonly around 2-1/4\" to 2-3/4\" tall \u2014 though you'll also see the 4-7/8\" ASA plate used residentially with cylindrical and mortise locks from Schlage, Yale, and Baldwin. The rule of thumb: a 161 commercial door and frame want the 4-7/8\" ASA strike; a bored residential door often takes the smaller T-strike. Prep is a shallow mortise for the plate face plus a deeper pocket for the latch. On a wood frame, layout off the latch \u2014 close the door, mark where the latch bolt contacts the stop, and the strike centers on that, NOT on some remembered dimension, because door swing and shim gaps drift. Get the strike a hair too far toward the stop and the door won't latch; too far away and it rattles. This is where a strike-locating tool earns its keep \u2014 several jigs and even a smear of lipstick/marker on the latch bolt will transfer the exact contact point to the frame.\n\nSelf-centering bits deserve their own moment because they quietly fix the sloppiest part of the job: screws. A Vix-style self-centering bit has a spring-loaded tapered nose that drops into the countersink of a hinge or strike screw hole and centers the pilot bit dead in the middle before it cuts. Use them and every hinge screw pulls the hinge tight and square; skip them and your pilot holes wander, screws drive off-center, and the hinge shifts as you tighten \u2014 now the door sags or binds. Match the bit to the screw: a 5/64\" Vix for #2\u2013#4 screws, 7/64\" for #5\u2013#6, and 9/64\" for #8\u2013#10. Hinge screws on a standard residential door are usually #8 or #9, so the 9/64\" (or 7/64\" for the finer ones) covers most hinge and strike work. On hollow metal you generally don't pilot \u2014 the reinforcements are drilled and TAPPED at the factory for machine screws \u2014 but on wood and on wood frames, a self-centering bit is the difference between a hinge that hangs true and one you fight all afternoon.\n\nCommercial jigs are where you graduate from \"installing a lock\" to \"prepping doors for a living.\" The two names you'll hear are Major Manufacturing and Templaco, and both build in solid aluminum, not plastic. Major Mfg's HIT series (the DrillMaster line \u2014 HIT-44 DrillMaster, HIT-50 DrillMaster 2, HIT-49 DrillMaster 3, and the HIT-55) clamps to the door edge and indexes drill guides so your 2-1/8\" and 1\" bores land at the exact backset and centerline every time \u2014 no template, no punch, just clamp and drill, and the kits carry the 7/8\", 1\", 1-1/2\", and 2-1/8\" bits to cover cylindrical, deadbolt, and edge work. Their self-centering clamp systems reference off both faces so the bore is centered in the door thickness, and they include mortise-lock locators for laying out pocket work. Templaco's Bore Master is the other pro standard \u2014 the full kit gives you the jig, the bits, and router templates for the cylindrical bore, the latch face, and the strike cutout, all in aluminum. What you're buying with either is repeatability and speed: on a job hanging thirty doors, a jig pays for itself before lunch because every door is identical and you're not center-punching thirty templates by hand. The judgment call is volume \u2014 for a locksmith doing occasional installs, templates plus a good hole-saw pair and a self-centering bit is plenty; for a shop doing commercial door packages or a new-construction crew, a Major or Templaco kit is not optional, it's the tool that makes the work profitable.\n\nWhich brings us to shop-made jigs \u2014 the mark of a locksmith who's been doing this long enough to get tired of the same repeated cut. When you're facing a run of identical work \u2014 say, prepping a batch of doors for the same latch, or drilling the same odd electric-strike frame cutout over and over \u2014 you build a jig. The simplest is a plywood drilling template: take a piece of 1/2\" or 3/4\" Baltic birch, lay out and bore your 2-1/8\" and 1\" holes with your good hole saws using the exact backset you need, then screw on a fence that hooks the door edge to register the backset automatically. Clamp it to each door, drill through the plywood-guided holes, done \u2014 the plywood keeps the hole saw from walking and enforces the backset without measuring. Level up to aluminum (1/8\" or 3/16\" plate or angle) when the jig will see heavy repeat use or you want it to guide a router; drill-bushing inserts (hardened steel press-fit bushings) let a plywood or aluminum jig guide a bit precisely without the wood wallowing out after fifty holes. A shop-made aluminum-angle fence with a couple of bushings for the strike-and-latch layout will let you mark or drift-punch frame preps in seconds. The whole philosophy: the first door you lay out carefully by template and punch; by the fifth identical door you should be reaching for a jig, and if you're not, you're leaving money and accuracy on the floor.\n\nOne last word on the authorized-work and liability side, because door prep isn't just carpentry \u2014 a fire-rated opening is a life-safety assembly and the standards have teeth. If a door or frame carries a fire label (you'll see it on the hinge edge), you cannot freely field-modify it: cutting a new lock prep, enlarging a bore, or adding hardware beyond what the listing allows can void the label. Under NFPA 80 (the standard for fire doors and other opening protectives), field modifications to labeled fire-rated doors are tightly restricted \u2014 job-site prep is limited to specific, small allowances (like function holes and surface-applied hardware within stated limits), and anything beyond that generally must be done by the manufacturer or a listed shop under the door's listing. Undocumented cutting on a labeled door is exactly the kind of thing that surfaces after a fire and lands on the person who did it. So before you put a hole saw to a labeled commercial door, verify: is this door fire-rated, and is the modification permitted under its listing and within the NFPA 80 field-prep allowances? On the entry/bypass side, the same discipline applies \u2014 knowing that a thin hollow-metal door will take a knockout punch, or that a poorly-piloted strike prep leaves a door that can be shimmed or pried, is precisely the knowledge that lets you HARDEN an opening: spec the ASA strike with a deep box and long screws that reach past the jamb into the framing, use the correct 14-gauge lock reinforcement, and prep it clean. But you only cut doors you're authorized to cut. Verify the customer owns or controls the opening, confirm the door's rating, and keep your work inside the standards \u2014 that's what keeps you a professional and not a liability.",sources:["https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-161-door-prep/","https://webstore.ansi.org/preview-pages/BHMA/preview_ANSI+BHMA+A156.115-2016.pdf","https://learn.reeb.com/knowledge-base/architectural-wood-bore-options/","https://learn.reeb.com/knowledge-base/hollow-metal-bore-options/","https://steeldoor.org/ansi-250-6/"],relatedGuides:["t-drill-bits-sharpening"],tags:["Hole saw","Self-centering bit","Backset","Mortise lock","Strike","Opening","Bypass"],aliases:["door prep bit","2-1/8 hole","bore the door","centering punch","hinge bit","self centering drill","distance to center","wrong backset","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","strike plate","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"job-prep",title:"Job prep: readiness before you roll",module:"The Business of Locksmithing",body:"Nine times out of ten, the second trip on a job is not caused by a hard lock or a stubborn door \u2014 it is caused by something you could have known in the driveway before you ever turned the key in your own van. Wrong handing, wrong backset, wrong cam, a finish that does not match the other four doors in the building, a cylinder length that is an eighth of an inch short. All of that is prep. A green tech thinks the job starts when he touches the lock. A seasoned hand knows the job starts when he reads the work order, and half the outcome is decided before the tires ever move. So slow down at the desk, because slowing down there is what makes you fast in the field.\n\n[[diagram:job-prep-callback-costs|Everything on this page is knowable before you load the van]]\n\nStart with the work order itself, and read it like you are getting paid to find the gap \u2014 because you are. A WO written by a dispatcher, a property manager, or a homeowner over the phone is a translation, and translations lose things. \"Front door won't lock\" could be a bad latch, a sagging door, a stripped mortise, a dead cylinder, or a strike that walked out of alignment when the building settled. \"Rekey the office\" could be four doors or fourteen, cylindrical or mortise, one keyway or a mess of three. Before you accept the WO as truth, extract the five things that actually determine what rides in your hand to the door: the lock model or family, the handing, the cylinder and cam, the finish, and the backset. If any one of those is blank on the paper, that is not a detail to sort out on site \u2014 that is a phone call to make now.\n\nHanding kills more first-trip jobs than any other single miss, because people guess it and they guess it backwards. Here is the discipline: you stand on the outside of the door \u2014 the secure side, the side the key goes in, the corridor or the street \u2014 and you look at the hinges. Hinges on your left, it is a left-hand door; hinges on your right, right-hand. Then you watch the swing. If the door swings away from you it is a straight hand \u2014 LH or RH. If it swings toward you it is a reverse \u2014 LHR or RHR, sometimes written LHRB and RHRB, the B being the reverse bevel. That reverse bevel matters because levers, closers, and especially exit devices and mortise locks with a handed function are not symmetric \u2014 put a handed lock in backwards and the key ends up on the inside, the deadlatch anti-friction plunger sits on the wrong side, or a panic bar dumps egress the wrong direction and now you have written yourself a code violation on top of a return trip. Get the customer to tell you handing over the phone and you still verify it yourself, because \"left\" from a homeowner standing inside the house is your \"right\" from the corridor. Do not trust their word for handing \u2014 trust their photo, or trust the model number that only comes in one hand.\n\nBackset is the next thing that sends you home empty. That is the measurement from the door edge to the centerline of the bore. Two numbers cover most of what you touch: 2-3/8\" is the residential standard, and 2-3/4\" is the standard commercial cylindrical (and shows up on plenty of residential exterior doors too). Mortise is its own animal \u2014 the modern ANSI/BHMA A156.13 commercial mortise lock is built to a 2-3/4\" backset, so your Schlage L9000, Sargent 8200, Corbin ML2000 all land there; the 2-1/2\" backset you will hear people quote is real but it lives on older and specialty mortise cases, not on the current standard body, so do not assume it \u2014 confirm it off the lock. A latch built for a 2-3/4\" backset dropped into a 2-3/8\" prep overshoots by three-eighths \u2014 the bolt will not reach the strike, or the whole lock rides off-center and looks wrong even if it limps. On a bored/cylindrical job, know your prep: the common commercial \"161\" door prep (ANSI/BHMA A156.115, formerly A115.2) is a 2-1/8\" cross bore for the lock body, a 1\" edge bore for the latch, with a 1-1/8\" x 2-1/4\" edge prep and a 2-3/4\" backset, mating to a 4-7/8\" ANSI/ASA strike. When someone says \"standard prep,\" that is usually what they mean \u2014 but confirm it, because a 2-3/8\" backset with a 2-3/8\" faceplate is a different animal and you want the right latch on the truck, not two of the wrong one.\n\nNow the cylinder and the cam, which is where mortise work eats amateurs alive. On a mortise lock the cylinder threads in, but the cam \u2014 the little tail that rotates and throws the lockcase \u2014 has to match the lockcase, and there is no universal. Get the cam wrong and the key turns but nothing moves, or it moves the wrong way. The big three you will meet: a standard/straight cam (Yale-pattern), an Adams Rite cam, and a Schlage L-series cam. The Adams Rite cam is what feeds the Adams Rite MS 4500 and 4700 deadlocks and Corbin Russwin DL3000-family deadlocks \u2014 Schlage even builds a dedicated part for it, the 20-013 with the AR cam, stocked in 1-1/8\" and 1-1/4\" lengths with a 3/8\" blocking ring. Schlage's own L-series mortise locks want the L9000 cam, not the standard one; drop a standard-cam cylinder into an L9000 and you will be standing there wondering why a brand-new cylinder does not run the lock (the fix, on a factory conventional cylinder, is swapping in the correct straight/L cam, not forcing it). And modular versus conventional matters too \u2014 a modular cylinder needs its modular cam, and mixing a conventional cam onto a modular body gives you a lock that fails to operate or runs backwards. So the question is never just \"mortise cylinder\" \u2014 it is length AND cam. Length runs in eighths: 1\", 1-1/8\", 1-1/4\" and up, sized to the door thickness and the ring/collar. Too long and the cylinder stands proud and gets wrenched; too short and it sits recessed where the cam cannot reach or the key will not seat. Measure or ask the door thickness, then carry the right length, not a fistful of \"close enough.\"\n\nFinish is the one that does not stop the door from working but absolutely gets you a callback and a chargeback, because the customer is paying for it to match. Learn the BHMA codes and their old US-code twins cold, because they are what is written on the WO and stamped on the box. The workhorses: 626 is satin chrome plated on brass or bronze (the old US26D), 630 is satin stainless (US32D), 605 is bright brass (US3), and 613 is oil-rubbed bronze (US10B), which is a living finish that ages and darkens on purpose \u2014 set that expectation with the customer or they will call it defective. Remember the BHMA number names the base material too, not just the look, so a satin-chrome finish on steel carries a different code (652) than the same look on brass (626) \u2014 match the number, not just your eye. 626 and 630 look nearly identical from six feet, but on a job where every other lever is 630 stainless, a 626 you slapped on because it was in the bin will read as the odd one out under the lobby lights. Match the building, not the bin. When the WO does not say, ask them to read you the numbers off an existing lock, or send a photo of the box or the stamped faceplate.\n\nOnce you know what the job actually is, you make the call ahead \u2014 and this is not a courtesy, it is diagnosis and it is money. The call ahead confirms four things at once: that someone will be there and the door is accessible (not blocked by a tenant, a delivery, a parked car), that you have authorization to do the work, that the customer's description survives contact with a second question, and that you get the details the WO left blank. Ask them to walk to the door and read you what is stamped on the faceplate or the lock face \u2014 \"Schlage L9080,\" \"Best 9K,\" \"Kwikset,\" \"Yale\" \u2014 because a model number tells you handing options, cam, backset, and prep in one shot. Ask them to feel the key: five-pin, six-pin, does it have a sidebar wiggle (a high-security keyway), is there a stamp on the bow. Ask the door thickness if it is anything but a standard 1-3/4\" commercial or 1-3/8\" interior residential. Every one of these questions on the phone is a part you do not forget to load.\n\nAuthorization is not paperwork you do to cover the shop \u2014 it is the line between locksmithing and burglary, and you own that line personally. Verify the caller has the right to open that door before you defeat it: they are the homeowner, the leaseholder, the property manager of record, or an agent who can prove it. Photo ID matched to the address. On a rental, a lease or a management authorization \u2014 a tenant can rekey their own unit in many places but a landlord's request to lock a tenant out is a different legal animal with eviction law attached, and you do not want to be the instrument of an illegal lockout. On a repo, an estate, a divorce, a commercial eviction \u2014 get it in writing before the drill comes out. If the story feels off, if the \"owner\" does not know which key works or cannot describe the inside of the place, that is your gut telling you to slow down and verify harder. The reason we learn every bypass \u2014 the loiding of a spring latch, the shimming, the under-the-door tools \u2014 is precisely so we know how a door gets beaten and can harden it for the people who hired us; that knowledge only stays legitimate when the authority to use it is nailed down first. No authorization, no entry \u2014 full stop.\n\nNow you stage. Staging means you physically pull the parts for THIS job and set them where you can grab them, instead of trusting that the van has \"one somewhere.\" Right latch at the right backset, right cylinder at the right length with the right cam, right finish, right strike, screws and shims and a spare faceplate. Pull it, lay it on the bench or a tray, and eyeball it against the WO one more time. If it is a rekey, count the pins and springs you will need and add margin \u2014 you will drop some in the grass and you know it. If it is an install, dry-fit in your head: does this lock need a new prep, a wrap plate for an old worn 161 hole, a filler plate for a mortise that used to be something else. Stage the tools to match the specific job, not just your everyday kit \u2014 a mortise job wants your cylinder wrench and cam kit and a proper mortise jig if you are cutting; an aluminum storefront wants your Adams Rite tools and the right MS cam; a safe or a high-security cylinder wants gear most of your calls never see. The five minutes staging costs you at the shop is the hour it saves you not driving back.\n\nThat is where truck stock earns its keep \u2014 the van is your insurance against the detail you could not confirm. You will never predict every job, so you carry the common denominators deep and the oddballs shallow. Carry latches in both 2-3/8\" and 2-3/4\" backset. Carry mortise cylinders and rim/KIK cylinders in 626 and in whatever second finish your market runs, in 1\" through 1-1/4\", and carry the cams loose \u2014 standard, Adams Rite, and Schlage L \u2014 so a wrong-cam surprise is a thirty-second swap and not a trip home. Carry a spread of strikes including the 4-7/8\" ANSI and the shorter T-strike. Carry pinning kit, springs, top pins, followers, plug holders, and blanks in your bread-and-butter keyways. Carry wrap plates and reinforcers for chewed-up prep, weatherstrip-friendly screws, and a handful of common closers or arms if you do that work. And here is the discipline that separates a stocked van from a junk drawer on wheels: restock the instant you deplete, not at end of week. The part you used on the last job and did not replace is the exact part the next job needs \u2014 that is not superstition, that is inventory math. Keep a running low list, mark the bin when it hits the reorder line, and reconcile it weekly so the van tells the truth about what is in it.\n\nLast, plan the route, because the most expensive thing you own is not the van or the tools \u2014 it is your billable hour, and windshield time does not bill. Sequence the day by geography and by constraint, not by the order the calls came in. Cluster the stops that are near each other. Front-load anything time-boxed \u2014 a business that opens at nine and locks at five, a school you can only be in before classes, a tenant who is only home at lunch. Weight the lockouts and true emergencies to the front where you can, because those are the calls where speed is the whole product. Leave the flexible rekeys and installs to fill the gaps between the hard-time stops. Check your access before you commit to a slot \u2014 gate codes, Access-gated buildings, a property manager who has to badge you in \u2014 and confirm that on the same call-ahead where you nailed down the parts. A tight route plus a staged van plus a WO you actually read is what turns a day of five scattered maybes into a day of five clean first-trip closes. That is the whole game: the trip you do not have to make twice is the most profitable trip you will ever run.",sources:["https://masterlocksmith.com/2-38-2-34-backset-what/","https://www.directdoorhardware.com/all-about-backsets.htm","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-161-door-prep/","https://learn.reeb.com/knowledge-base/architectural-wood-bore-options/","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A15613-2022-Mortise-Locks"],tags:["Backset","Cam","Handing","Mortise lock","Pinning kit","Loiding","Strike","Cylinder"],aliases:["distance to center","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","Best 45H","Corbin Russwin ML2000","mortise body","pocket lock","pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","threaded cylinder","mortise cyl","latch guard","anti-shim","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","shim","no-pick entry","bypass tool","get past the lock","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"pick-types-technique",title:"Lock picking: picks, tension & technique",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Before your pick ever touches a keyway, get your authority straight. You pick your own locks, the customer's lock after you've verified they own the place or the vehicle \u2014 ID against the registration, a lease, a title \u2014 and you pick under a work order. That's the whole game, ethically and legally. Everything below is lockout and hardening knowledge: the reason you learn every bypass is so you know which locks actually resist it and which ones you're selling a false sense of security. A tech who can't pick a spool-pinned cylinder doesn't know why it's worth the money. So learn the attack cold \u2014 then you can defend against it and price it honestly.\n\nStart with the picture of what's happening inside, because picking is just exploiting sloppy machining. A pin-tumbler plug has a row of chambers, each holding a spring-loaded driver pin sitting on top of a key pin. The right key lifts every key pin so the gap between key pin and driver lands exactly at the shear line \u2014 the seam between the plug and the shell \u2014 and the plug turns. Here's the thing that makes picking possible: those chambers are not drilled in a perfectly straight line. Manufacturing tolerance leaves slop, and on residential hardware \u2014 Kwikset, Schlage, most everything running the industry-standard .115\" diameter pins \u2014 the holes are off by thousandths. When you put rotational torque on the plug, one chamber's pin binds against the shear line before all the others. That's your binding pin. You set it, the plug rotates a hair, the next-tightest pin binds, and you walk down the stack one pin at a time. If chambers were drilled perfectly, single-pin picking wouldn't work at all \u2014 the whole craft lives in that slop.\n\n[[img:diagram-pt-unlocked|Under tension, each binding pin is set to the shear line one at a time until the plug is free to rotate. Diagram: Wikimedia, CC BY-SA]]\n\n[[img:diagram-pt-locked|At rest the driver pins (blue) bridge the shear line and jam the plug. You pick by setting each binding pin to that line, one at a time. Diagram: Wikimedia, CC BY-SA]]\n\nTension is 80% of picking and picks are the other 20 \u2014 get that ratio in your head early, because every apprentice fails on tension, not on pick selection. The tension wrench (turning tool, torsion tool, whatever your shop calls it) goes in the keyway and holds a light rotational load while your pick does the lifting. Two families: bottom-of-keyway (BOK) and top-of-keyway (TOK). A BOK wrench is the classic L or Z shape that sits in the lower, off-center part of the keyway. Because it's off the plug's true central axis, it doesn't just rotate the plug \u2014 it levers it, pulling one end down and popping the other end up a hair, which sets one end of the pin stacks binding differently than the other. That's not a bug, that's a tool. A TOK wrench rides in the top of the keyway, nearer the plug's true center axis, so it applies rotation more evenly along that axis and binds the stacks with more uniform force. The practical payoff: a lock has no single fixed binding order \u2014 the sequence changes with which wrench you use and how you load it. When you're stuck, don't just wiggle harder. Pull the wrench, flip to the other type, and the whole binding sequence rearranges under your pick. That alone opens locks that felt impossible.\n\nTorque itself is a dial, not a switch, and this is where twist-flex earns its name. You want the lightest tension that still binds one pin \u2014 think the weight of holding a pencil against paper, not cranking a bolt. Rest a finger against the wrench and modulate pressure in tiny increments as you probe each chamber. Too much torque and a set pin jams so hard it won't move, or a security pin never gets the chance to do its telltale trick. Too little and the plug counter-rotates fully and dumps every pin you set. Good wrenches have a little spring flex built in for exactly this \u2014 you load the flex, and as a pin sets and the plug creeps, that stored flex feeds the plug forward smoothly instead of in jerks. Skinny wrenches flex more (good feedback, easy to over-rotate), fat rigid wrenches give you a hard stop (precise, but punishing if you're heavy-handed). Carry both.\n\nNow the picks. The hook is your scalpel \u2014 the single-pin-picking tool. A short hook has a low, shallow tip and is what you'll reach for most of the time; it slides in over set pins without disturbing them and lifts the binding pin cleanly. A deep hook (or medium hook) has a taller tip to reach short key pins that sit down in the plug behind taller neighbors \u2014 high-low bittings where a short hook can't get under the low pin without scraping the tall one next to it. An offset hook (Gonzo/DeForest-style, where the tip is stepped forward on a raised neck) buys you reach and lets you clear a set pin to get to the one behind it in a tight or paracentric keyway. The half-diamond is the do-everything blade: it single-pins in a pinch, it rakes, it zips, it counts pin stacks, it works dimple lifters \u2014 small half-diamonds are the ones you want, big ones over-lift. The ball pick (full ball or half ball) is really a wafer-lock and some dimple tool \u2014 the rounded profile lifts two-sided wafer stacks; you'll use it more on desk locks, cam locks, and some automotive wafers than on residential pin tumblers.\n\nRakes are the shotgun \u2014 you're not aiming at individual pins, you're spraying the whole stack with motion and letting probability set them. A city rake (the multi-peak \"skyline\" profile) is aggressive and fast on standard cuts; you scrub it in and out under light tension and pins randomly catch at the shear line. A snake rake (double or single wave, the classic W profile) is gentler and better for scrubbing. The Bogota \u2014 designed by a locksport maker who went by Raimundo and named it after his time in Bogot\xe1, Colombia, its triple-peak profile meant to echo the three Andes cordilleras splitting through the country \u2014 is a nested pair of triple-wave and single-wave spring-steel rakes, and it shines in wide keyways like KW1 and SC1. Fun bit of trade lore: the originals were cut from street-sweeper bristle steel. Rakes buy speed on cheap locks and get you a fast false set on better ones; they will not defeat serious security pinning, and they chew up your feedback. Learn to single-pin first, then rake for speed \u2014 do it the other way and you'll never develop the touch.\n\nRaking, rocking, and zipping are the three motions and they're not the same thing. Raking is that in-and-out scrubbing pass with a wave rake under feathery tension \u2014 you're vibrating the stack and skimming pins to the shear line. Rocking is a see-saw pivot, rotating the pick so the peaks sweep the pins as you drag out. Zipping is a snap: you jam the pick (often a half-diamond or a snake) deep, then rip it out fast in one stroke so it flicks every pin up on the way past, like plucking guitar strings \u2014 the pins bounce and, with the right torque, some catch. All three are probability plays. The move that separates a picker from a scrubber: rake to a false set to find out what the lock's doing, then switch to a hook and single-pin the last couple of stubborn pins by hand.\n\nWhich brings us to security pins \u2014 the thing that makes a well-built cylinder worth several times a builder-grade one. A spool pin has an hourglass waist machined into the driver. As you lift it under tension, the plug rotates into the waist and the fat top shoulder of the spool catches on the shear line, and the plug over-rotates \u2014 because the narrow waist gives the plug more room to travel, it turns further than a real set ever would. That's a false set: the plug's rotated, some pins are trapped, but the lock's not open. The tell is counter-rotation. When you keep lifting a caught spool and briefly ease your tension \u2014 just barely crack it \u2014 the spool's shoulder slips past the ledge and the plug rotates backward toward you, a physical click-back you feel in the wrench. That backward kick is the lock telling you which pin was the spool and that you just set it. Serrated pins do the same con on a smaller scale: the driver (and sometimes the key pin) has a stack of tiny ratchet grooves cut in its side, and each groove catches the shear line for a mini false-set click. A serrated pin gives you a whole ladder of tiny false sets instead of one big spool ledge, and heavy tension will lock you out on every rung. Mushroom, gin, and barrel pins are cousins of the spool with a beveled or tapered shoulder \u2014 same counter-rotation game, sometimes subtler feedback.\n\nThe counter-intuitive rule for security pins is the one every beginner fights: lighten your tension into the resistance, don't add to it. Your instinct when the plug stops turning is to bear down. That's exactly wrong \u2014 heavy torque wedges the spool shoulder against the shear line and pins it there forever. Instead, find the pin that made the plug counter-rotate (probe with a hook, feel for the springy false-set pin), then feather your tension down just enough to let the spool's waist clear the ledge, push it up to a true set, and re-apply light tension before the plug dumps. It's a dance: lift, ease, catch, re-tension. Do it wrong and every gentle release resets the whole lock; do it right and you feel a series of little counter-rotations march the plug open. This is why a well-pinned Schlage with a couple of spools and serrateds takes a trained hand minutes and stops a casual attacker cold \u2014 and it's the honest sales pitch for upgrading a customer from builder-grade.\n\nSnap guns (pick guns) skip finesse and use physics. A snap gun has a needle you slide under all the key pins; you pull the trigger and it snaps the needle up hard against the whole row of key pins at once. Momentum transfers straight up through the key pins into the drivers \u2014 Newton's-cradle style \u2014 and the drivers jump off the key pins for a fraction of a second, opening a gap at the shear line across every chamber simultaneously. You hold a light separate tension wrench, and if you're rotating during that split-second gap, the plug turns before the springs slam the drivers back down. Snap a few times in rhythm and it opens. Electric pick guns automate the snap at a set rate. The catch: snap guns are lousy against security pins (spools don't care about a momentary gap the same way, and the geometry fights you) and they can peen up your pins and springs if you crank the impact \u2014 not something you do to a lock you intend to leave in service without inspecting.\n\nComb picks are a different animal \u2014 they don't manipulate, they over-lift. A comb has a stepped, comb-shaped tip that slides in and shoves every pin stack all the way up past the driver pins, so far that the driver pins clear the shear line entirely and the plug spins free. That only works on a lock with a manufacturing flaw: too much empty space above the shear line, so there's room to over-lift the whole stack into it \u2014 common on cheap padlocks with over-deep pin chambers. On a properly built cylinder there's nowhere for the pins to go and the comb does nothing. It's fast and dead-simple where it works, which is exactly why over-lift protection (master wafers, tight chambers, some designs use a second shear line as a trap) is a mark of a decent padlock. If a padlock combs open in two seconds, that's a padlock you tell the customer to throw away.\n\nBump keys are the kinetic attack every homeowner has heard of, and you should be able to explain and defend against it cold. A bump key \u2014 a \"999 key\" in the trade, cut to the maximum depth at every position \u2014 goes into a matching keyway. You pull it back one click, apply light turning pressure, and rap the bow with a bump hammer (a hard nylon-tipped, flex-handled mallet). The impact drives the key pins up, they smack the drivers, and the drivers fly off the key pins for a millisecond \u2014 same Newton's-cradle separation as a snap gun, just delivered through a key \u2014 opening a transient gap at the shear line in every chamber at once. Turn during that flash and the plug rotates before the springs reset. It's the same physics as a pick gun, just cheaper and quieter. Defenses are the real lesson here: security-pin geometry (spool and serrated drivers muddy the bump and make it far less reliable, though not impossible), tighter tolerances, and non-pin-tumbler designs. Kwikset's SmartKey, for instance, isn't a pin-tumbler lock at all \u2014 it runs wafers and a side-locking bar, so there are no spring-loaded drivers to bump and bumping is a non-starter (it has its own well-documented weaknesses, but not this one). And the standards you point a security-conscious customer to: UL 437 and ANSI/BHMA A156.30. UL 437 is a time-based listing \u2014 a cylinder has to resist picking, drilling, and other forced and surreptitious attacks for set durations to earn it. A156.30 is the high-security cylinder standard, and here's the detail most techs get wrong: it doesn't grade in Grade 1/2/3 \u2014 it uses attack-resistance levels A, B, and C, with A the highest and C the minimum, covering both surreptitious entry (picking, decoding) and destructive attacks like drilling and plug pulls. (You'll also see high-security cylinders carry A156.5 Grade 1, the general cylinder standard, which is where the \"Grade 1\" language properly comes from \u2014 don't confuse the two.) A cylinder carrying UL 437 and a high A156.30 level \u2014 Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, Abloy, and the like, most with active sidebars and angled or rotating elements on top of the pin stack (a Medeco 4, for example, runs an active sidebar with finger pins and a lift pin plus rotating angled key cuts) \u2014 is what you sell when the customer actually needs to keep skilled hands out. Below that grade, you're selling convenience, and it's your job to say so.\n\nOrder of operations on a real lockout, so this all hangs together: verify authority first, always. Then read the lock \u2014 brand, grade, keyway \u2014 because that tells you what you're up against before you insert anything. Try the easy stuff (is it even locked, is there a bypass, will it comb). Then light TOK or BOK tension, a gentle rake or zip to feel for a false set, and if it's clean cuts and cheap, it may just fall open. If it false-sets and fights you, switch to a hook and single-pin the security pins with the ease-tension-and-catch dance, flipping your wrench type if the binding order won't cooperate. Reach for the snap gun or bump key when you need speed on a standard cylinder and you're keeping it or replacing it. And when a lock beats all of it, that's not a failure \u2014 that's the lock doing its job, and the answer is a drill-and-replace with the customer's blessing, or a recommendation to upgrade. Knowing exactly where each attack stops is what makes you worth calling.",sources:["https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/security-pins/","http://www.theamazingking.com/lock-spp.php","https://www.lockpickworld.com/blogs/news/applying-tension-in-the-top-of-the-keyway-tok","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/what-is-binding-order/","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-tension-wrench-why-is-it-important-in-lockpicking-complete-guide"],tags:["Rake","False set","Shear line","Binding order","Tension wrench","Raking","Plug","Second shear line"],aliases:["snake pick","hangs up","feels open but won't turn","picks open","won't set","line of separation","set order","which pin binds","tension tool","pick tension","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","master pin ghost","MK shear line","false shear","anti-pick pin","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","housing","lock body","cylinder body","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"bypass-lockout",title:"Bypass & lockout technique by lock type",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Before you touch a tool, get one thing straight in your head: every bypass in this guide works because the fire code hands it to you. NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) and IBC section 1010.2 demand free egress \u2014 a door in the means of egress has to be openable from the egress side without a key, special knowledge, or special effort, and as of the current code language, the unlatching has to happen with not more than one releasing motion in a single linear or rotational direction that releases all latching and all locking devices at once. Read that again. Code forbids the manufacturer from putting anything on the inside face that would stop you from getting out in a fire \u2014 no tight grasping, no tight pinching, no twisting of the wrist. So the whole game of non-destructive entry is this: reach the egress hardware from the outside and make it do the thing it is legally required to do when a person on the inside touches it. You are not defeating the lock. You are impersonating the person standing inside. That framing tells you which technique to reach for before you ever look at the cylinder \u2014 if there's a lever, a panic pad, or a thumbturn on the inside, you go for that, not the keyway. Nine times out of ten the fast open is the egress side, not the lock side.\n\n[[diagram:bypass-egress-source|Free egress creates the release; the bypass just reaches it... which is also how you defend against one]]\n\nAuthority first, and I mean before the truck is in park. You verify who is asking and whether they can lawfully authorize entry to that specific opening. On a house it's the person who lives there or holds the lease, and you check ID against something tying them to the address \u2014 a utility bill, a lease, a driver's license with that address. On commercial you want the business owner, a manager with written authority, or a property manager, and you note it on the ticket. Landlord wanting into an occupied tenant unit is a hard stop \u2014 that's the tenant's lock as far as you're concerned, and you don't touch it without the tenant present or a court order. Car with a kid or pet locked inside and it's hot? That's the one time you go now and call 911 while you work, because a life beats a signature. Every other job, no signature, no signed authorization line, no open. The tools below will pop a code-compliant commercial door in under two minutes \u2014 which is exactly why you write down whose word put you on that door. When it goes to court, and occasionally it does, your ticket is the difference between \"licensed locksmith performed authorized lockout\" and \"someone broke in.\"\n\nStart with the under-the-door tool, because on interior commercial levers it is the king. It's a flat semi-rigid steel band \u2014 think a long thin spatula on a cord \u2014 that you slide flat through the gap at the bottom of the door, rotate up on the far side, hook the loop over the interior lever, and pull down. The lever retracts the latch and the door's open. That's it. It exploits lever handles specifically, and it needs a bottom gap and interior clearance to swing up. Where it shines: office suites, back-of-house doors, conference rooms, any commercial interior opening with a lever on the egress side and ADA-compliant hardware \u2014 the ADA Standards and ICC A117.1 both require operable hardware that doesn't need tight grasping, pinching, or twisting of the wrist (lever or push-type), which is precisely the shape the loop grabs. Where it dies: knobs (nothing for the loop to catch and pull), doors with a tight sweep or a threshold that kills the bottom gap, and doors where somebody hung a towel or screwed a guard plate beside the lever to block the loop's path behind the handle \u2014 that's the standard defensive fix and it works. Watch lever handing too: the loop has to pull the lever down, and on some the free end swings the wrong way for your approach angle. And know the door swing \u2014 if it opens toward you the geometry is friendlier; if it opens away you're fighting the lever's return spring at a bad angle.\n\nThe rod-and-string, or the loop-and-string, is the same idea applied to hardware you can't grab with a stiff band \u2014 panic bars and paddle-style exit devices. You feed a stiff rod or a bent wire through a gap (bottom, or the gap between a pair of doors), drop a loop of cordage or a pre-tied slip loop over the push pad or the touchpad's actuating cross bar, and pull. Depress the pad, the exit device releases, door opens. On double doors with vertical rod devices \u2014 the ones with no mullion, where the rods latch into the head and sill because there's nothing in the middle to latch to \u2014 this is where the firefighter's J-tool earns its keep: reach the J through the gap between the leaves, hook one push bar, yank, and the top and bottom rods retract as the bar depresses. Minimal damage, no cylinder touched. The reason all of this works, again, is code: a listed panic device per ANSI/BHMA A156.3 has to release with a single push anywhere across the actuating portion of the bar, with an operating force of no more than 15 lbf, no latching left engaged. You are supplying that push from outside. The catch is line of sight and gap \u2014 you often can't see the pad, so you're working the loop by feel, and a door with a gasketed astragal or a tight-fitting mullion between the leaves may leave you no path for the rod. Slow hands. Rushing pops the loop off the bar and you start over.\n\nNow the loid \u2014 the shove knife \u2014 and this is where amateurs waste an hour on a door that was never going to give. A loid works on a plain spring latch: the beveled latchbolt with nothing backing it up. You slip the thin springy blade (a proper shove knife, not a credit card, which snaps and shreds) between the door edge and the stop, catch the bevel of the latch, and cam it back against its spring. Interior residential passage and privacy sets, cheap closet and bathroom latches, some old commercial spring latches \u2014 those loid. What does NOT loid, and this is the whole point of the hardware, is a deadlatch. A deadlatch carries a second little spring-loaded plunger \u2014 the auxiliary bolt, sometimes called the deadlocking plunger or deadlatch pin \u2014 riding right beside the main latchbolt. When the door is properly closed and aligned, the strike holds that plunger depressed, and a depressed plunger mechanically deadlocks the main latchbolt so it cannot be pushed back. You shove your knife in, you feel the latch bevel, you push, and it just doesn't move \u2014 that dead solid stop is the aux bolt doing its job. So here's your diagnosis, and burn it in: if the shove knife catches the bevel but the bolt won't cam back, stop shoving \u2014 it's a deadlatch, go find the egress hardware or the cylinder. Don't force it. And a field tell worth money: a deadlatch only deadlocks when the plunger is fully riding the strike face. If the strike's misaligned or the plunger drops into the strike cutout because someone hung the door lazy, the deadlocking fails and the thing loids like a plain latch \u2014 that's not a bypass trick, that's a defect you flag to the customer, because if you can loid their \"deadlatch\" so can a burglar, and the fix is a strike adjustment so the plunger rides the strike face instead of dropping into the hole.\n\nStorefront aluminum doors are their own animal, and the Adams Rite thumbturn reach tool is the specialist's move. Narrow-stile glass doors typically run an Adams Rite MS1850S-style MS deadlock: on a single-leaf swinging door that's the straight laminated stainless steel bolt (the plain MS1850S), and a 360-degree turn of key or thumbturn throws or retracts that bolt. Don't confuse it with the hookbolt version \u2014 the MS1850S-X5X hookbolt is built for sliding doors, where the hook resists the parting motion of leaf and jamb; you'll meet it on slider storefronts, not swingers. Either way, inside there's commonly a 4066 thumbturn \u2014 because, egress again, you have to be able to get out without a key. That interior thumbturn is your target. The reach tool is a long, slim, angled rod you thread through a gap \u2014 the perimeter weatherstrip gap, the sweep, or a compromised corner \u2014 to catch and rotate that thumbturn a full turn. It is finesse work: you're spinning a small knob at the end of two feet of rod, by feel, through a gap the width of a nickel. When it's a keyed lock only with no interior thumbturn (a double-cylinder setup, which itself is a red flag on an egress door), the reach tool is off the table and you're picking or impressioning the MS cylinder, or drilling as a last resort. Know before you go: peek through the glass and see whether there's a thumbturn on the inside. If there is, reach it. If there isn't, it's a cylinder job.\n\nThe air wedge is the enabler for every reach and rod technique on a door with no useful gap. It's an inflatable bladder \u2014 the pump-up wedge, the pro version of the \"pump wedge\" \u2014 that you slide into the top or hinge-side corner of the door and inflate to spread the door off the frame a quarter inch, half inch, enough to pass a long-reach tool or a loop through. On aluminum storefront and hollow-metal commercial you pair the air wedge with the long-reach rod: wedge the top corner, feed the rod, work the thumbturn or the panic pad. Two hard rules. One: inflate slow and watch the door face \u2014 you are flexing metal and, on storefront, you're loading a big pane of tempered glass in an aluminum frame. Over-inflate and you'll bow a stile or, worse, blow the glass, and now your two-minute lockout is a several-hundred-dollar glazing bill and an incident report. Two: never wedge directly against the glass or into a glazed corner \u2014 you spread the frame, never the glass. On a wood residential jamb the air wedge will crush and dent soft pine trim, so protect the jamb or use it sparingly. It's a spreader, not a pry bar; the second you feel real resistance, back off the pressure.\n\nJigglers \u2014 try-out keys \u2014 belong on wafer locks and loose pin-tumbler junk, and knowing where they work saves you from looking foolish on a door that needs a pick. A wafer lock uses flat, single-piece wafers that a key pushes flush to the shear line; the tolerances are sloppy and the depth increments are few, so a set of jigglers cut to the common patterns will, with a slow up-and-down jiggle and light turning tension, set enough wafers to turn the plug. That's desk drawers, filing cabinets, cam locks, older mailbox and cabinet hardware, RV and some older automotive wafer locks, and gate/utility wafer locks. Gentle beats rough here \u2014 you're letting the varied cuts find the wafers, not forcing anything. Where jigglers fall on their face: any decent pin-tumbler entry lock (tighter tolerances, more depths, more pins \u2014 they just won't set), and modern automotive high-security sidebar and laser-track systems where the whole geometry defeats a tryout key. So the call is simple \u2014 wafer or cheap cabinet lock, reach for the jigglers first; a real deadbolt or a modern car, put them away and pick, or on the car go through the door with a wedge-and-reach to the interior lock knob or the electronic unlock, never the ignition.\n\nLast, and most important, when NOT to bypass \u2014 because the mark of a senior isn't the size of the pick kit, it's the jobs you walk away from. Don't bypass when authority isn't clean: no verified authorization, an occupied unit against the occupant's interest, a dispute where two parties both claim the door \u2014 you leave and let them sort it with a court, not your tools. Don't bypass a listed fire door assembly in a way that leaves it non-compliant: NFPA 80 governs fire door and opening protective assemblies, and if the door is a rated opening you don't defeat or leave defeated any part of the fire or egress hardware \u2014 you get in, and you leave it working exactly as listed and labeled. Don't bypass when it costs more than it saves: a high-security cylinder, a well-fitted deadbolt with a good strike, or a storefront with no interior thumbturn can eat an hour of picking when a fresh set of pins and a new key, or a clean drill-and-replace, gets the customer back in faster and cheaper \u2014 repair-vs-bypass is a real decision and the customer's time is part of the price. And don't bypass when the smarter answer is to fix the vulnerability: if you just loided their front door because the deadlatch plunger drops into a misaligned strike, or you popped their office in ninety seconds with an under-door tool because there's a bare lever and a half-inch bottom gap, the value you sell isn't the entry \u2014 it's telling them how you did it and hardening it. Adjust the strike so the aux bolt deadlocks. Add a latch guard or a lever block. Close the gap. Knowing the bypass is exactly how you know the fix, and the fix is where the repeat business lives.\n\nWhen the storefront lock is keyed-only with no interior thumbturn \u2014 a double-cylinder MS deadlock, itself a red flag on an egress door \u2014 the reach tool is off the table and you're working the cylinder or the cam. Two moves live here. First, the Adams Rite mortise cylinder picks, but it picks its own way: the MS cylinder drives a cam, not a tailpiece, so you're turning the plug maybe a sixth of a turn to rotate the cam that throws or pulls the bolt, and the narrow-stile mortise-cylinder profile is tight and shallow \u2014 a slim Adams Rite mortise pick and a light-feel wrench at the top of the keyway, because there's no room for a bottom bar. Second, and faster when the cylinder is loose, is the cam tool. Adams Rite MS cylinders retain on a single set screw in the stile, and a cylinder that's spun loose \u2014 or one you back out \u2014 lets you pull it and reach a driver straight into the cylinder pocket to catch and turn the cam directly. That's the tool that goes through the keyhole and acts like a cam: it isn't defeating pins, it's impersonating the cam the cylinder would have turned, which is exactly why the loose-set-screw failure in the storefront cylinder-attack article is such a gift. If the set screw is tight and it's a real high-security plug, weigh the pick honestly against a fresh cylinder and a key \u2014 on a good MS cylinder the pick can eat an hour that a clean pull-and-replace beats.\n\nOne more on the panic side, because the rod-and-loop isn't your only reach. When a single door has a bottom gap and a lever or a low panic pad inside, the under-door tool is the faster, blinder move: a long flat tool with a swiveling tip that you slide flat under the door, rotate up on the inside, and either hook an interior lever and pull it down or come up under the panic pad and push. It works the same code the loop does \u2014 free egress means the inside hardware releases with one motion and you're supplying that motion from outside \u2014 but it reaches over the threshold instead of through the gap between leaves, so it's the move on a single door with no between-leaf gap. Match the method to the opening: between a pair of no-mullion doors, the rod-and-loop or the J-tool through the astragal gap; a single door with a half-inch bottom gap and a bare lever or a low pad, the under-door tool. And the same senior's note as everywhere in this guide \u2014 if you just popped their exit in ninety seconds because there's a bare lever and a wide bottom gap, the sale isn't the entry, it's the latch guard, the astragal, or the sweep that closes the gap you just used.",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches/4510-standard-duty-deadlatch","https://idighardware.com/2011/02/single-operation-egress/","https://buildershardware.com/Portals/0/Files/codes-in-context/2023/Clarifying%20Not%20More%20Than%20One%20Releasing%20Motion%20for%20Doors%20in%20the%20Means%20of%20Egress.pdf","https://up.codes/s/door-operations","https://www.adamsrite.com/","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Bypass"],relatedGuides:["padlock-shimming-bypass","storefront-cylinder-attacks"],tags:["Bypass","Air wedge","Deadlatch","Free egress","Cam","Stile","Astragal","Hookbolt"],aliases:["shim","no-pick entry","bypass tool","get past the lock","wedge bag","car lockout tool","gap the door","anti-shim","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","stem","tail","spindle","deadbolt tail","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","panic bar","crash bar","Von Duprin","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","locked out","no key","emergency opening","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","binding","picks open","line of separation"]},{id:"lishi",title:"Lishi 2-in-1 pick & decode tools",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Here is the thing an apprentice has to understand before he ever touches one of these: a Lishi is not a pick set, it is a measuring instrument that happens to open the lock on the way to giving you the number. That is the whole trick, and it is why the pros carry them. A tension wrench and a hook will get you into a Kwikset, sure, but when you climb out you still do not have a key \u2014 you have an open door and a customer standing there while you go back to your van to impression or disassemble. A Lishi opens the lock AND hands you the bitting in the same thirty seconds. You walk to the machine, cut to code, and the customer never sees you sweat. That combination \u2014 pick and read the cuts at once, then cut to code \u2014 is what changed lockout and rekey work, and it is what turned automotive from a two-guy tow-and-program job into something one tech does at the curb.\n\n[[diagram:lishi-as-instrument|Picks give you an open door; this gives you the number, which is what the customer is paying for]]\n\nMr. Li is a real person \u2014 a locksmith \u2014 and the name Lishi is just his family name, \"Li,\" plus \"shi\" for family. Understand that up front because the marketplace is a swamp of clones and it will cost you money if you buy wrong. There are three things sold under some version of the name. There is Original Lishi (the genuine article, Mr. Li's tools), there is \"Genuine Lishi\" (a separate outfit reportedly run by his former business partner \u2014 NOT the same tool), and there is a pile of unbranded Chinese knockoffs. The one you want has Mr. Li's face laser-engraved on the front, a model designation and a serial number, and \u2014 this is the current tell \u2014 since 2021 every Original 2-in-1 and reader ships with a green verification label you scratch off to reveal a QR code you scan against the official site. The genuine tools are proper stainless with a crisp, responsive lifter arm. The fakes feel light, thin, and vague in the hand, and a vague feeler on a decoding tool is worthless because the whole game is reading a few thousandths of an inch by feel. Do not cheap out here. A clone that misreads a cut by one depth just sent you back to the truck to recut, and you have burned the time the Lishi was supposed to save.\n\nLet me walk you through what is actually happening inside the tool, using the KW1 because that is the one you will reach for most on residential lockouts. The tool is keyway-specific \u2014 it is milled to slide into the Kwikset KW1 keyway and nothing else \u2014 so it seats in the plug exactly like a key would, referenced off the same shoulder. Built into the body is a tension arm (you bias the plug with it, light \u2014 always light) and a lifter arm, a delicate feeler that rides along the pin stack positions. You pick the way you would with any single-pin-picking approach: bias tension on the plug, then walk the lifter to each chamber and gently persuade each bottom pin up until it sets at the shear line. On a genuine tool, when a pin sets you feel the lifter bounce \u2014 just one or two depths of travel \u2014 that little rebound is the pin catching the shear. You vary the tension pin to pin; that is not optional, it is the skill. Force the lifter and you bend the tip, and a bent lifter reads garbage and voids the warranty besides.\n\nNow the part that earns the tool its keep. Once the plug turns and the lock is open, you are not done \u2014 you decode. You run the lifter back to cut one and gently push it in until it stops against the seated pin, and you read the number off the engraved scale on the tool body. Each position, one through five on a KW1, gives you a depth number. Write them down bow-to-tip and you have the bitting \u2014 say 1-3-5-2-4 \u2014 a real, cuttable code pulled straight out of the cylinder without ever removing it from the door. That is the decode. Read all five, walk to the machine, cut a KW1 blank to that code, and you have made a working key for a lock you had never seen thirty seconds earlier.\n\nFor that code to mean anything at the cutter you have to know the system behind it, so here are the real Kwikset numbers, and memorize them because a senior will spot a bluff instantly. KW1 runs on a .023\" depth increment, single-step progression, MACS of 4 \u2014 meaning adjacent cuts can never differ by more than four depths or the milling cutter fouls the neighboring cut. Cuts are read and written bow to tip. The first cut sits .247\" from the stop, and the spacing between cuts is .150\". There are seven depths in the modern system. Here is the part apprentices get backwards, so slow down: the depth numbers run opposite to how deep the cut actually is. Depth 1 is the shallowest cut and leaves the most blade \u2014 its root sits at .329\" \u2014 and depth 7 is the deepest cut, rooting all the way down at .191\". So the bigger the depth number, the more metal comes off and the lower that root measurement goes; .329\" down to .191\" is 1 through 7, not 7 down to 1. The Lishi's scale is already calibrated to spit out those depth numbers directly, so you are not doing arithmetic in the driveway \u2014 you read a \"3,\" you cut a \"3.\" One gotcha worth carrying: Kwikset also had an older depth system built on a .031\" increment (depths ran 1 = .328\", 3 = .297\", 5 = .266\", 7 = .235\"). Do not repeat the shop myth that the .031\" system is the \"Corbin/Russwin\" one \u2014 it is the reverse. The modern KW1 class is what Corbin and Russwin used in their residential locks from 1966 to 1992; that old .031\" system was never used by Corbin Russwin at all. Ninety-nine times out of a hundred on a house you are on the modern .023\" system, but if a key you cut to a decoded code feels sloppy or binds on an oddball old lock, that increment mismatch is your suspect.\n\nThe SC1 tool is the same idea in a Schlage keyway, and the specs differ in ways that matter. Schlage Classic runs a .015\" depth increment \u2014 finer than Kwikset \u2014 with ten depths numbered 0 through 9, roots stepping from 0 = .335\" down to 9 = .200\", and a MACS of 7. That finer increment is exactly why a sloppy clone tool bites you harder on Schlage: fifteen thousandths between depths leaves you very little margin, so a mushy lifter that reads a 4 as a 5 is off by only .015\" but that is a whole depth, and a whole depth is a key that does not turn. The standard SC1 Lishi handles the common 5-pin Schlage residential and light commercial cylinders. When you meet a 6-pin Schlage \u2014 and you will, on commercial doors \u2014 that is a different tool, so do not try to force a 5-chamber reader across a 6-pin stack and trust the number. Schlage also loves security pins in the commercial line, spools and serrated, which brings us to false sets.\n\nHere is how a spool feels through the Lishi, because it is the number-one thing that makes an apprentice think the lock is picked when it is not. You put light tension on, you are lifting pins, and suddenly the plug rotates a few degrees \u2014 maybe five \u2014 and stops dead with a counter-rotation push-back that fights your tension. That is a false set: a spool pin has caught its groove at the shear line and the plug is hung up on it, not open. Green picker forces more tension and jams the whole thing solid. What you actually do is ease OFF the tension, feel for the pin that is sitting proud or springy, and gently rock it through the false set until it drops into true shear. The KW1 tool is specifically built to pick KW1 cylinders even with spool pins in them \u2014 that is a selling point \u2014 but the tool does not do it for you; it just gives you a feeler fine enough to feel the difference between a false set and a real one. If you cannot feel that difference, you are either on a clone or you are muscling the tool.\n\nPrep and lube separate the guys who get clean reads from the guys who fight the lock. Before you decode anything on a stiff or gummy cylinder, exercise it \u2014 run a blank in and out roughly twenty-five times to free the pins and springs \u2014 and lube it right. Silicone or a proper electronics-safe cleaner, never oil, never graphite over old oil. Oil grabs grit and turns the plug into sandpaper, and grit under a spring gives you a mushy, lying read where the pin feels seated but is dragging. A dry, clean, silicone-lubed cylinder reads crisp and repeatable. And repeatability is the test: decode it, back off, decode it again, get the same five numbers twice. If the two reads disagree, you have not learned the lock yet \u2014 you do not cut a key off a number you only saw once.\n\nNow the automotive side, because this is where the Lishi genuinely rewrote the trade. Cars stopped using flat, easily-impressed keys \u2014 they went to high-security laser-cut and internal-track keyways where you cannot rake, cannot impression easily, and often cannot even see the wafers. The old play was pull the lock, decode it on the bench, reassemble \u2014 an hour of labor and a real chance of breaking a fragile door lock or steering column. The automotive Lishi profiles \u2014 HU100 for GM (Buick, Cadillac, Chevrolet, Opel/Vauxhall, in 8- and 10-cut versions), HU101 for Ford, Volvo and Range Rover, HU66 for VW/Audi (and the rest of the VAG family \u2014 Seat, Skoda, Porsche), TOY2 for Toyota, HON66 for Honda, and roughly a hundred others \u2014 let you pick and decode the door cylinder in place, in about three minutes, without a single part coming off the car. Many of these are twin-lifter tools because the keyways are double-sided: two wafer tracks, so two lifter arms, and on the HU100/HU101 the reading arrow decodes in the same direction you pick. The technique is the same religion \u2014 feather-light fingertip pressure, feel the first contact with the wafer, then gently push past the spring until it stops, and read. Force it and you bend a lifter and misread a two-sided key, which on a car means an expensive recut and possibly a scheduled transponder session wasted.\n\nThat last point is the real economic story of these tools on modern cars, so sit with it. On a transponder or proximity vehicle, the mechanical cut is only half the key \u2014 the other half is programming the chip to the immobilizer, and that is a booked, timed, sometimes single-shot procedure. If you show up and have to figure out the mechanical bitting by trial and error, you burn your programming window and maybe throw the immobilizer into a lockout timer. Decode the lock cleanly with the Lishi first, cut the correct blade before you ever plug in the programmer, and the mechanical side is a solved problem \u2014 you spend your programming time programming, not guessing. That is the difference between a curbside all-key-lost job that pays and one that goes sideways.\n\nSo where does the Lishi sit in the repair-vs-replace, tool-vs-tool judgment? For a residential lockout where the customer wants back in and wants keys, the Lishi is almost always the right first move over drilling \u2014 non-destructive, you keep the hardware, and you leave with a code to make as many keys as they need, which is a rekey upsell sitting right there. Drilling is the last resort, for a cylinder that is seized, over-secured, or genuinely defeating you on the clock. For rekey, decoding the existing cylinder tells you the current bitting instantly so you can master-key intelligently or match an existing key. And the honest limit: a Lishi is only as good as the tech's touch and the cylinder's condition \u2014 a rusted, packed-with-graphite, or physically damaged lock may not give a trustworthy read, and a lock that has been apart and repinned sloppily can read fine and still fight a cut key. When the numbers will not repeat, stop trusting the tool and start trusting the disassembly.\n\nTwo non-negotiables to close on, because the ability to open a car or a house in three minutes cuts both ways. First, authority: you verify you have the right to be in that vehicle or that door before the tool comes out \u2014 registration, ID matching the address, a work order \u2014 same as any lockout. The reason we teach the bypass in this trade is defensive: knowing exactly how fast a stock KW1 falls to a decoder is precisely why you upsell the customer to a high-security cylinder with real spool-and-serrated pinning or a restricted keyway, and knowing how a Schlage spool false-sets is how you spec a lock that actually slows an attacker down. Second, respect the instrument: keep the lifter tips clean and straight, never store a Lishi loose in a bag where the feeler gets tweaked, and re-verify your read on any lock you are going to cut a code from. A bent lifter or a lying read does not just waste a blank \u2014 on a car it can waste your one shot at the immobilizer. Treat it like the precision measuring tool it is, not like a pick, and it will earn its cost back on the first hard lockout.",sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.originallishi.com/what-is-tool-verification/","https://www.classiclishi.com/classic-lishi-vs-original-lishi","https://gosolockpicks.com/which-lishi-is-truly-original","https://www.lsamichigan.org/Tech/Kwikset_KeySpecs.pdf"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","lishi-commercial","lishi-automotive"],tags:["Lishi","False set","MACS","Tension wrench","Bitting","Rake","Shear line","Immobilizer"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","hangs up","feels open but won't turn","maximum adjacent cut","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","turning tool","tension tool","TOK","BOK","pick tension","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","raking","city rake","bogota","snake pick","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","reading tool","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"access-control-bypass",title:"Access-control bypass: service and defend",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Let me set the frame before you touch a single sensor. Everything in this guide is for authorized work only \u2014 you're the tech the building owner called, you're doing a penetration test with a signed scope, or you're servicing a system you installed. Verify your authority to be on that door before you defeat it. The reason a good locksmith learns the bypass is the same reason a good doctor learns the disease: you cannot harden what you don't understand. Every one of these attacks has a defense, and the defense is usually cheaper than the callback. Nine times out of ten, when a building gets popped through an electrified door, it wasn't the reader that failed and it wasn't the credential \u2014 it was the egress side, the side nobody thinks about because it's supposed to let people out.\n\n[[diagram:mag-opening-attack-surface|Every line on the left is a line on the service checklist... and the one you must never \"fix\"]]\n\nStart with the classic, because it's the one that pays your bills and the one that embarrasses integrators: the REX bypass on a PIR motion sensor. Here's the setup you'll find everywhere. An electric strike or a maglock holds the door. On the secure side you badge in. On the egress side, mounted on the ceiling or the wall above the door, there's a passive-infrared request-to-exit sensor \u2014 a Bosch DS150i, a Securitron XMS, a Camden CM-RQE70, whatever the integrator had on the truck. Its whole job is to see a warm body approaching from inside and drop the lock so people can leave without touching anything. That's a life-safety function and it's mandated by code, which I'll get to. The problem is a PIR sees exactly two things: a change in infrared energy (heat) and motion across its zones. It has no idea which side of the door that heat came from. And most of these doors have a gap under them.\n\nSo here's the attack, and I want you to actually understand the physics because that's what tells you how to kill it. A PIR is a differential sensor \u2014 it's split into zones and it fires when a warm mass crosses from one zone to another, or when the thermal scene changes fast enough. From outside, you slide something warm and moving under the door. Simplest version: a stiff wire or a coat hanger with a chemical hand-warmer taped to the tip, pushed under the gap and waved. The warmer reads as body heat, the waving reads as motion, the PIR fires, the lock drops. Even lower-tech \u2014 a sheet of paper flicked under the door will sometimes do it on a twitchy sensor, because the moving paper occludes and reveals the warm floor. The one every red-teamer carries, though, is a can of compressed-air duster held upside down. Invert the can and it discharges liquid propellant \u2014 typically 1,1-difluoroethane (HFC-152a) \u2014 that flashes to gas, and that plume comes out around minus 60 degrees Fahrenheit. That's a violent thermal transient across the PIR's field of view, and it moves as it billows. Cold, not hot, but the sensor doesn't care about the sign of the change \u2014 it cares about the magnitude and the motion. Straw under the door, one long spray angled up toward the sensor, and a lot of doors just open. Takes about four seconds.\n\nNow, how you defend that, in the order I'd check it. First \u2014 and this is the single highest-value fix \u2014 kill the line of sight and kill the gap. The attack only works because the sensor can \"see\" heat coming through the undercut of the door. Relocate the PIR so it's aimed down the egress path and back into the room, not straight down at the threshold. A lot of installers point these things right at the door because it's convenient; that's the mistake. You want the pattern reaching out into the space where a real person walks up, not framing the door gap. Second, close the gap physically: an adjustable door shoe or an automatic door bottom, a security astragal on the meeting stile of pairs, and weatherstripping. If cold air can't creep through and a wire can't get under, half your attack surface is gone. Third \u2014 and this is the pro move that separates a service call from a real hardening job \u2014 get off single-technology PIR. Spec a dual-technology REX that requires both PIR and microwave/radar to agree before it fires, or better, switch the whole egress method. A can of cold air doesn't produce microwave Doppler motion, so dual-tech ignores it. Better still, where code allows, use a touch-sense bar or a mechanical request-to-exit switch built into the panic hardware or the mullion, so there's no field-of-view to spoof at all. Fourth, defense in depth: wire a floor pressure mat or a second sensor in series so the lock only releases when something is actually standing there, and put the door-forced/door-held alarm on a monitored input so even a successful spoof throws an event. You'd be amazed how many jobs have a beautiful access system and the door-position output landing on nothing.\n\nSecond attack surface: the electric strike, and specifically the difference between \"the system thinks this door is secure\" and \"this door is actually secure.\" Two separate failures live here. The first is loiding \u2014 the old shim-the-latch trick. A rim or cylindrical latch with a plain beveled latchbolt and no deadlatching plunger, or one where the plunger isn't riding the strike lip correctly, can be shimmed with a mica shim, a loid, or in a pinch a credit card. An electric strike doesn't make that better \u2014 if anything a sloppy strike prep with too much horizontal clearance makes it worse, because the latch isn't seating deep and the anti-friction/deadlatch geometry is off. First thing I check on a strike that keeps getting bypassed: is the deadlatch plunger landing on the strike face and not falling into the pocket? If that little secondary plunger drops into the keeper opening, the latch is no longer deadlocked and the door loids open. The fix is dead simple and it's mechanical \u2014 reposition the strike or add a shim behind the keeper so the plunger stays depressed against the faceplate, and if the lockset has no deadlatch at all, that's the wrong lockset for an access-controlled opening. Spec a lock with a proper deadlatch or an interconnected/mortise lockset, and hold your strike to the ANSI/BHMA A156.31 electric-strike standard so you're buying tested gear, not mystery metal.\n\nThe second strike failure is the monitoring lie, and this is where techs get fooled. Understand what your strike actually reports. A latchbolt monitor (sometimes called LBM or latch monitor) is an auxiliary switch buried in the strike body that tells you the latchbolt entered the pocket. That is not the same signal as a door position switch, which tells you the door leaf is closed. People treat them as interchangeable and they are not. Two ugly consequences. One: a door can drift closed enough for the latch to just catch the strike lip and report \"latched/secure\" while sitting open a quarter inch \u2014 false secure. Two, and this is the classic sabotage: someone jams a wad of paper or a folded shim into the strike pocket, holding the monitor switch depressed. Now the panel reads \"door secured\" permanently while the door swings free. The strike is telling the truth about its own switch \u2014 it just isn't telling you anything about the door. The defense is layered sensing: run a real door position switch \u2014 a recessed magnetic contact or a mechanical ball plunger DPS like the SDC DPS series \u2014 AND the latch monitor, and land both on the panel so the logic can flag the mismatch. When the DPS says \"closed\" but the latch monitor says \"not latched,\" or vice versa, that's your tell that something's wrong, and a good head-end will alarm on it. On a high-security opening I want both, plus a door-forced and door-held-open alarm, plus the request-to-exit shunt timed tight so a propped door actually reports.\n\nThird surface: the maglock and its bond sensor gap. A maglock is honest about one thing \u2014 when it's powered and mated to its armature, it holds. The common Securitron Magnalock family runs 600 pounds on the small M32 up to 1200 on the M62 and M680E, and the industrial M82 is rated 1800 pounds. The trap is that \"powered\" and \"holding at full force\" are not the same. The armature plate is deliberately mounted on shoulder bolts with a rubber washer so it can flex and seat flat \u2014 that little bit of float is what lets it grab full surface contact. But it also means the plate can be pulled or wedged slightly off the magnet face and lose most of its holding force while still drawing current. A cheap or misadjusted bond sensor can sit there reporting \"locked\" while the actual grip is a fraction of rated. And the low-tech maglock defeats are real: a shim or business card slid between plate and magnet to break the magnetic contact, prying the armature with a gap tool, or the reach-through \u2014 a maglock top-mounted on a door with a big undercut or a glass-slat gap lets an attacker reach the interior REX or the exit button and simply request-to-exit their way in. Which loops right back to attack one: the maglock didn't fail, the egress sensor did. This is exactly why maglocks pull the most scrutiny in the field and why some AHJs hate them.\n\nDefense on maglocks is about honest monitoring and correct mounting. Spec a real magnetic bond sensor, not just a current-draw indicator \u2014 Securitron's BondStat (the \"B\" suffix, e.g. M62B) actually monitors the magnetic field across the whole armature face and signals the panel when the plate moves beyond tolerance and holding force drops. Pair it with a DPS (the \"D\" suffix, so you're ordering an M62BD or an M680EBD) so you know door-open-or-closed independently of bond status \u2014 bond sensor tells you grip, DPS tells you position, and you want both because either alone lies to you in a different way. Add the \"X\" suffix if you want the integral REX. Mount the armature correctly: keep the shoulder-bolt float, use the sex-bolt/spacer where the door flexes, and don't overtighten it flat or you defeat the very compliance that gives full force. Close the reach-through gaps and get the interior REX and exit button out of arm's reach of any gap or glass. And here's the code point people forget: a maglock has to fail unlocked. On a means-of-egress door it must drop on loss of power, on fire alarm, and on sprinkler activation, and it needs a manual release. So paradoxically the \"unbreakable\" 1200-pound magnet is required to let go the instant power blinks \u2014 which is a safety feature and also, if a red-teamer can induce a power interruption on that circuit, an attack surface. Put the lock power supply and its release logic somewhere it can't be reached or browned out from the public side.\n\nLet me put the code stakes in front of you, because this is where you protect the client and protect your own license \u2014 and where you have to hold the line against the customer who \"just wants it more secure.\" Electrified egress doors live under the IBC and NFPA 101, and the exact section numbers depend on the adopted edition, so check what your AHJ actually enforces. In the 2015 and 2018 IBC, sensor release of electrically locked egress doors is Section 1010.1.9.9 and door-hardware release is 1010.1.9.10. The 2021 IBC renumbered these \u2014 sensor release became 1010.2.12 and hardware release 1010.2.11 \u2014 and the 2024 IBC shuffled the Chapter 10 numbering again, so cite the edition, don't cite from memory. The substance, which has held steady across editions, is what you must build to. For a sensor-released mag-locked egress door: a sensor on the egress side detects an approaching occupant and unlocks the door; loss of power to the sensor OR to the lock unlocks the door; there's a manual unlocking device on the egress side mounted 40 to 48 inches above the floor, within 5 feet of the door, marked \"PUSH TO EXIT,\" and pressing it directly interrupts power to the lock independent of the other electronics and holds it unlocked for not less than 30 seconds; and the lock releases on activation of the building fire alarm or sprinkler system and stays released until the system is manually reset. Note that the 40-to-48-inch mounting height is the ADA/ICC A117.1 reach-range logic \u2014 the release has to be usable by everyone, including someone in a wheelchair or someone who can't read the sign in a smoke-filled corridor.\n\nThat last point is the whole ethical spine of this trade, so I'll say it plainly. Every hardening measure you add on the security side has to respect free egress on the life-safety side. You do not get to defeat the REX, disable the manual release, or add a second lock that needs a key or code to get out on an occupied egress door \u2014 that's how people die in fires, and it's how you end up personally named in a lawsuit. The right answer to \"make this door impossible to bypass from outside\" is almost never \"make it harder to get out.\" It's: move and shield the egress sensor, go dual-technology or mechanical so there's nothing to spoof, close the door gaps, monitor bond AND position AND latch honestly so a spoof throws an alarm even if it works, and put the whole thing on a head-end that screams when the sensors disagree. Harden the sensing and the geometry, never the egress. Do it that way and the same knowledge that lets a red-teamer open the door in four seconds with a dollar can of air is exactly what lets you build the one door on the block that doesn't open for them at all.",sources:["https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IBC2021P1/chapter-10-means-of-egress/IBC2021P1-Ch10-Sec1010.2.12","https://idighardware.com/2022/08/2021-ibc-commentary-on-egress-doors-with-access-control/","https://idighardware.com/2020/08/decoded-electrified-locking-systems/","https://up.codes/s/sensor-release-of-electrically-locked-egress-doors","https://idighardware.com/2017/07/decoded-access-controlled-egress-doors-august-2017/"],tags:["Bypass","Bond sensor","DPS","Maglock","PIR","Door position switch","Sensor release","Holding force"],aliases:["no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","mag lock","magnetic lock","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","door holds shut","motion detector","passive infrared","false alarms","door contact","reed switch","request to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","latch guard","anti-shim","latch bolt","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","carding the latch","slip the latch","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail"]},{id:"keyway-blank-id",title:"Keyway & key blank identification",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Here's the truth about this trade that nobody tells you on day one: cutting a key is the easy part. Picking the right blank is where the apprentices burn their morning. You'll stand at the duplicator with a customer's key in one hand, staring at a wall of two hundred hooks, and the whole job lives or dies on whether you grab the right one. Grab wrong and you've cut a beautiful, perfect, useless key that won't slide past the first ward. So before you ever touch the wheel, you learn to read the blade. That's the daily skill \u2014 reading a keyway cold, in bad light, over a counter, without a chart.\n\n[[diagram:keyway-identification|Read the section, not the silhouette... and treat the stamping as a hint, never as the answer]]\n\nStart with the two you'll touch a thousand times before you touch anything else: KW1 and SC1. KW1 is Kwikset. SC1 is Schlage. Between them they cover the overwhelming majority of residential doors in this country, and telling them apart is the first reflex you build. The fastest tell is the shoulder \u2014 where the blade meets the bow. KW1 has a rounded, open shoulder and a comparatively fat, simple blade; the Kwikset bow tends to run with straighter sides and a peaked, house-shaped top. SC1 has a distinct milled shoulder, a more diamond-shaped bow, and a more aggressive warding pattern running down the blade. Flip both so you're looking at the tip end-on, at the cross-section, and the profiles are clearly different animals \u2014 the grooves are milled in different places at different depths. Here's a fast one that never lies: lay both flat, teeth up, and the Kwikset blade shows two grooves down the flat where the Schlage shows one. Nine times out of ten a homeowner's brass house key is one of these two, and after a few weeks your hand goes to the right hook before your brain finishes the thought. Ilco calls the KW1 blank the 1176; the SC1 is Ilco 1145. Silca cross-references KW1 as KS1 and SC1 as SH3. Learn those numbers \u2014 they're the same in Nashville as they are in Sacramento, and when you're phoning in an order or reading a catalog, the Ilco and Silca numbers are the real language, not the marketing letters. And know the stamped shorthand too: a \"66\" on the bow is Kwikset KW1, a \"68\" is Schlage SC1 \u2014 free money when the letters are worn off.\n\nNow the trap that catches everybody, and I mean everybody, at least once: SC1 versus SC4. Same family, same Schlage C keyway, and here's the cruel part \u2014 the warding pattern, the grooves and side milling, is identical between them. Put an SC1 and an SC4 side by side and the profiles match. So why won't your SC1 work? Length. SC1 is a five-pin blank; SC4 is a six-pin, and the SC4 blade is simply longer to reach that sixth chamber. Ilco numbers them 1145 for the SC1 and 1145A for the SC4; Silca runs SH3 for the five-pin and SH5 for the six-pin. Cut an SC1 for a six-pin Schlage and it slides right in, feels right, turns nothing \u2014 because the blade physically stops short of the last pin stack and that chamber never gets set. This is the single most common miscut in residential work. The rule that saves you: count the pins, or count the cuts on the customer's key. Five cuts, SC1. Six cuts, SC4. Don't eyeball the length alone \u2014 verify by counting. Same logic runs through the Everest-era SC8 and SC9 blanks too (five-pin and six-pin on the Schlage E section, not the C section), which is why Ilco's own reference splits them out \u2014 SC1 is 5-pin C, SC4 is 6-pin C, SC8 is 5-pin E, SC9 is 6-pin E. And note this so a customer never catches you flat: those SC letters are Ilco's designation for Schlage-compatible blanks. Ilco isn't Schlage, and Schlage never stamped a key \"SC1.\" When you're dealing with genuine Schlage commercial cylinders, especially patented Everest keyways, the factory blank is a different conversation entirely.\n\nWatch the Kwikset side of that same trap, because it hides differently. KW1 is the five-pin Kwikset; KW10 is the six-pin. But KW1 and KW10 don't split apart the way SC1 and SC4 do \u2014 line them up on the top shoulder and they read the same length, because the KW10's extra reach comes from its bottom shoulder sitting further back. That's why a KW10 blank will happily enter a KW1 lock and can even be cut and used like a KW1 if you index off the top shoulder. So on Kwikset you don't lean on the shoulder to sort five-pin from six \u2014 you count the cuts, same as always. Counting the bitting is the one move that settles pin count across every one of these look-alike pairs when profile alone won't.\n\nLet me explain why the wrong blank refuses to enter at all, because once you understand the mechanism you stop guessing. The keyway is the exact internal shape of the hole in the plug \u2014 a machined, broached profile with wards (raised ribs) and channels running the length of it. The blade of the correct blank is milled with matching grooves and side milling so it slides past those wards like a hand into a fitted glove. Here's the distinction that separates a pro from a parts-changer: bitting is not keyway. Bitting is the up-and-down cut pattern that sets the pins. Keyway is the side-to-side profile that lets the blade physically enter in the first place. Two keys can have the exact same bitting \u2014 the same cut depths \u2014 and if the keyway profiles don't match, one of them simply will not go in the hole. The wards block it. Even a small difference in where a groove sits or how deep the milling runs makes insertion physically impossible; that's not a tolerance issue, that's the wards doing their job. That's the whole point of a keyway \u2014 it's a mechanical gatekeeper that decides which blanks are even allowed to try before the pins ever come into play. So when you force a blank and it stops an eighth-inch in, don't lean on it. Pull it, look at the cross-section, and admit you've got the wrong section.\n\nOnce you're past the residential pairs, you step into the commercial keyways, and here the game changes \u2014 it's less about two brands and more about a dozen families, many of them restricted. The names you'll meet: Schlage Everest and Primus, Corbin Russwin, Sargent (LA, LB and the rest of that keyway series), Yale, Falcon, and the Best small-format interchangeable core world with its A2 pinning and its own lettered keyways \u2014 the letter's stamped right at the bottom of the bow (A, B, J, L and the rest), so read it before you guess. Some of these are open \u2014 you can buy the blank off any jobber. Many are restricted or patented. Everest 29 splits exactly this way: the S-family keyways are \"open\" in the sense that any locksmith or contract hardware dealer can stock and sell them (they're kept out of the home centers and the corner key kiosk), but they're still covered by Schlage utility patents against unauthorized manufacture \u2014 so \"open\" here means available to the trade, not unpatented. The T-family keyways are the restricted ones: six-pin, and you literally cannot order blanks without authorization, because the whole point is documented key control. When a customer hands you a key stamped \"DO NOT DUPLICATE,\" understand what that stamp actually is and isn't. By itself, that engraving carries no legal force \u2014 it's a policy request, a deterrent, not a law, and nothing in the code stops a shop from cutting it. The real wall is the blank: a patented restricted section that you can't legally source or duplicate without the manufacturer's authorization. So don't hide behind the stamp as if it were the law, and don't wave it away as meaningless either. You verify authority to cut, you check whether it's a patented section you're even licensed to source, and when in doubt you route it back through the end user's key control records. Knowing how a bypass or an unrestricted-blank workaround exists is exactly how you advise a client to harden their system \u2014 that's the defensive value of understanding it. But you verify authority and you close the gap; you don't exploit it.\n\nSo how do you actually read a keyway at the counter, in order, every time? First, look at the bow \u2014 the head \u2014 for stamped markings. Half the time the answer is printed right there: \"KW1,\" \"SC1,\" \"Schlage,\" a factory logo, an Ilco or a code number, that 66 or 68. Free information, take it. Second, if the bow's blank or worn, turn to the cross-section \u2014 sight down the blade from the tip and read the warding profile, the count and placement of grooves, whether it's a simple open channel or a tight paracentric section that runs the wards past the vertical centerline to fight picking. Third, count the cuts, because that's your five-pin-versus-six-pin tell and it settles the SC1/SC4 and KW1/KW10 kind of ambiguity that profile alone won't. Fourth, check blade length and thickness against a candidate blank before you commit \u2014 lay the customer's key on top of the blank and confirm shoulder-to-tip length and that the tip stops in the same place. Only then do you cut. A key gauge and a decent cross-reference chart \u2014 the Ilco directory or Silca's equivalent lives in every real shop \u2014 turns a guess into a lookup, and I'd tell any apprentice to keep the current Ilco reference within arm's reach until the top hundred blanks live in your head.\n\nRepair-versus-replace judgment shows up here too, because identification feeds pricing. If a customer wants three copies of a common KW1 or SC1, that's a sixty-second job on cheap brass and you charge accordingly. If they hand you a worn-down key and you can't read the keyway or the bitting because the blade's rounded off from years in a pocket, don't duplicate a bad copy of a bad copy \u2014 that's how you send someone home with a key that gets worse every generation. Identify the original section, pull the cylinder or read the code if there is one, and cut to code on a fresh factory-correct blank. And when a customer insists their \"Schlage key\" won't work in the new blank you cut, walk it back through this same order before you eat the cost: right family, right pin count, right length, right section. Nine times out of ten it's SC1 cut for a six-pin lock \u2014 not a bad blank, not a bad machine, just the oldest miss in the book, and now you'll catch it before it leaves the counter.",sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1176-kw1-kwikset-key-blank-ilco","https://keymateinc.com/shop-online/key-blanks/north-american/ilco-1176-kw1-key-blank-kwikset---1063-detail.html","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/sc1-1145-schlage-key-blank","https://ekeyblanks.com/Schlage-SC4-1145A-Key-Blanks/","https://www.quickturnca.com/quickfix-manual/schlage-sc1-vs-sc4-keyway-comparison"],relatedGuides:["impressioning"],tags:["Keyway","Key blank","Bitting","Key gauge","Duplicator","Bypass","Plug","Cylinder"],aliases:["KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","JMA","blank won't fit","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","key decoder gauge","depth gauge","read the key","HPC","key copier","key cutting machine","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"broken-key-extraction",title:"Broken key extraction",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"A snapped key looks like a disaster to the customer and a five-minute job to you \u2014 but only if you read it right before you touch it. Here's the first rule, and it's the one apprentices blow: do not slide anything into that keyway until you know where the break is and whether the plug is turned. Nine times out of ten the fragment is sitting loose in the keyway, unloaded, and it wants to come out. But if you jab a hook in blind and shove, you'll drive it past the pins and turn a five-minute job into pulling the cylinder. Look first. Get a light on it. Where did the metal fail?\n\n[[diagram:broken-key-extraction-methods|Three ways out, and the one question to ask afterwards]]\n\nUnderstand why keys break, because the failure point tells you the strategy. A key snaps at the shoulder \u2014 that thick step where the blade meets the bow \u2014 when it's been over-torqued: a sticky deadbolt, a frozen cam, a customer cranking on it. That break leaves the whole blade inside the plug, flush or nearly flush with the face, and it's usually the easy pull because the fragment is fully seated, straight, and not jammed deep. The nastier break is mid-blade, out at the tip cuts, and that one almost always means the key was worn thin or the blade fatigued from years of jiggling \u2014 those keys snap deep in the plug, sometimes with the fragment cocked at an angle. Cheap aftermarket blanks and skinny brass Kwikset-profile keys fatigue and snap far more than a fat Schlage blade or a nickel-silver key. And a key that broke off while being forced in \u2014 versus one that just fatigued while turning \u2014 is the one that fights you, because it's wedged, not seated. Know which one you've got before you pick a tool.\n\nYour two workhorses are the hook extractor and the spiral, and they work on completely different principles \u2014 don't confuse them. A hook (HPC EZ-2, or the EZ-3 if you want the foam handle \u2014 the EZ-2 is just plain spring steel, about .022\" thick, and runs a couple bucks) is a thin flat blade with a barb filed into the tip. You run it into the keyway alongside the broken blade \u2014 critically, along the same side as the cuts, riding in the bitting valleys \u2014 push it in until the barb is past a cut, then lift the handle to seat the barb into the down-slope of a cut and draw the whole thing straight out. The hook grabs a cut and pulls; it does not turn. The spiral (HPC EZ-5, or the EZ-6 interchangeable set that carries a couple spiral blades and a saw blade in a hollow handle) is the opposite \u2014 it's a stiff wire with a corkscrew twist, .040\" on the standard blade, and it works by biting into the metal of the key when you twist it. You feed it in above or beside the blade, bend it slightly so the spiral bears against the key, then twist so the threads dig in and walk the fragment out with each rotation. Reach for the spiral when the fragment is buried deep and you can't get a barb behind a cut; reach for the hook when there's a cut you can catch. Most jobs I do with a hook first \u2014 it's more controllable and less likely to spin the fragment sideways. One more thing: these tools are thin and they snap. Carry two or three of whatever you rely on, because the day you need the extractor is the worst day to be down to your last one.\n\nNow the technique that separates a locksmith from someone with a tool kit: never push the fragment deeper, and you prevent that with two habits. First, gravity is your friend \u2014 orient the lock so the keyway faces down or the cylinder is on its side, so the fragment can only fall toward you, never away. On a mortise cylinder in your hand that's trivial; on a mounted knob or deadbolt, work the tool from the top of the keyway so any downward pressure loads against the top of the plug, not into the pin stack. Second \u2014 and this is the big one \u2014 pull, don't push. The hook goes in with the barb pointing down and clear of the cuts, then you lift and draw outward; the only force toward the pins is the gentle feed to get past a cut, and the moment you feel resistance you stop feeding. With the spiral, that \"bend the tool against the key and twist\" motion converts your twist into an outward walking pull \u2014 the fragment retracts a hair with every turn. If it stalls, don't lean into it. Back off, re-index the barb one cut further out, try again. I'll often work two hooks \u2014 one on each side of the blade \u2014 and draw them together so the fragment comes straight and doesn't rotate and jam.\n\nLubrication before you extract, always \u2014 it's free and it changes everything. Shoot the keyway with a dry lubricant: a Teflon/PTFE dry spray like Tri-Flow, or graphite if that's what you carry \u2014 not oil. Dry lube slicks the blade against the plug walls and the pins so the fragment slides instead of galling; a PTFE dry lube goes on wet to carry in, then flashes off to a slick film that doesn't hold grit and come back to bite you in six months. Go easy on the graphite \u2014 it works, but pack too much in and it soaks up any liquid and cakes the cylinder solid over time. Stay off the WD-40. It's a water-displacer and a solvent, not a lock lubricant \u2014 it flushes out whatever real lube was in there, attracts dust, and gums the pins within days. If all you've got is WD-40 in the truck, you've got the wrong truck stock. A light dry-lube shot, work the extractor, and a stubborn fragment that wouldn't budge dry will often walk right out.\n\nKnow your other tricks, and know their price. If a sliver of the fragment protrudes past the face, needle-nose or a good pair of tweezers and a gentle wiggle in several directions beats any extractor \u2014 don't overthink it. The superglue trick \u2014 dab of glue on the tip of a matchstick or a spare key stub, touch it to the fragment, wait, pull straight \u2014 works but it's a gamble: get glue on a pin or the plug wall and you've bonded the fragment in permanently, and now you're drilling. I keep it as a last resort on a lock I'm already prepared to replace. The jigsaw-blade improvisation \u2014 a fine metal blade with the teeth pointing back toward you, run in beside the key and jerked out \u2014 is a genuine field fix when you're out of proper extractors, but a purpose-made hook does the same job with far less risk of scoring the plug. And if the fragment is truly wedged from being forced \u2014 the \"jammed, not seated\" case \u2014 quit fishing after two honest attempts. Pull the cylinder, push the plug out the back with a follower, and tap the fragment free from behind. That's cleaner and faster than mangling the keyway blind, and it sets you up for the next step anyway.\n\nOnce the fragment's out, you're not done \u2014 you clean up and you have a repair-or-rekey conversation. Blow the keyway out, dry-lube it, and test-cycle a good key or your original if it's intact. If the key snapped clean at the shoulder and the blade's undamaged, sometimes the customer just needs a duplicate cut and they're back in business \u2014 but tell them straight that a key that broke once from a hard-turning lock will break its twin unless you fix the mechanism (bad strike alignment, a dragging bolt, a worn plug). Don't hand them a fresh key into the same failure. If the fragment scored the plug or if the customer's uneasy about who else has a copy, rekeying is the honest upsell and it's quick.\n\nRekeying after a key break is bread-and-butter pin-tumbler work, so let me walk the mechanics so you can teach it. You pop the cylinder, insert a plug follower \u2014 a bar the exact diameter of the plug \u2014 into the back of the shell, and use it to push the plug out the front, the follower staying behind to trap the top (driver) pins and springs up in the shell so they don't spill everywhere. That's the whole trick: the follower holds the upper chambers while you work the plug. Keep steady contact and push the follower out with the plug \u2014 never pull, or the drivers and springs drop out of their chambers. Dump the old bottom pins (the key pins \u2014 the ones of varying length that read the cuts), fit the new key you're keying to, and drop in bottom pins that bring each stack flush with the plug surface at each cut. On a Schlage that's a clean system to learn on: bottom pins run size 0 through 9 in .015\" increments, from a #0 at 0.165\" up to a #9 at 0.300\", and adjacent cuts can't differ by more than 7 increments \u2014 that's the MACS, the Maximum Adjacent Cut Specification, which on Schlage's .015\" step works out to .105\" \u2014 or the key won't work the pins. Slide the plug back in behind the follower, pushing the follower out so the drivers and springs seat down onto your new pins, re-clip or re-cap it, and cycle it a dozen times with dry lube before it goes back in the door. Test the new key, test that the old key is dead. That last check is the whole point of the job \u2014 if the customer's paying to lock somebody out, prove it locks them out before you leave.\n\nA word on authority and liability, because this work puts you inside somebody's security. Verify you're supposed to be opening this lock \u2014 a broken-key lockout still deserves a \"can you show me this is your place\" if anything feels off, same as any lockout call. Knowing every bypass and extraction trick is exactly how you advise a customer on hardening: a key that fatigues and snaps is a lock that was fighting the door, and a keyway you can fish a fragment out of in thirty seconds is one worth upgrading if they care about security. And when you rekey, the liability is real \u2014 you are certifying old keys no longer work. Don't certify what you didn't test.",sources:["https://www.southord.com/blogs/news/key-extractor-tools-what-they-are-and-when-to-use-them","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-broken-key-extractor-and-how-does-it-work","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/broken-key-in-lock/","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/broken-key-removal-how-to-extract-a-broken-key-with-the-hpc-ez-5-key-extractor","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hpc-interchangeable-key-extractor-set-ez-6"],tags:["Plug","Shell","Plug follower","Keyway","MACS","Mortise cylinder","Cylinder","Cam"],aliases:["barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","housing","lock body","cylinder body","rekey tool","pins fell out","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"cutting-by-code",title:"Cutting keys by code",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Here is the thing that separates a real locksmith from a guy with a duplicator at a hardware store: you do not need the key. You never needed the key. If you can get a number \u2014 off the lock, off the paperwork, off the original invoice, off a car's glovebox tag \u2014 you can originate a working key from raw brass and never touch the customer's original. That is cutting by code, and once it clicks you will wonder why you ever fought a worn-out original on a tracer. A duplicate copies wear, burrs, and somebody else's bad copy. A code key is cut to the factory's own numbers \u2014 it is more accurate than the key that came in the box. When a customer hands you a mangled key that \"sort of works,\" the pro move nine times out of ten is not to duplicate it \u2014 it is to read it, or find its code, and originate a fresh one to spec.\n\n[[diagram:cutting-by-code|Code to bitting to depth-and-space to a cut key... with MACS sitting over the whole thing]]\n\nStart with the vocabulary, because people mix these up constantly and it will get you in trouble. A bitting is the actual cut pattern \u2014 the depths, position by position. On a Schlage 5-pin that is five numbers, something like 1-2-3-4-5 where each digit is a depth. A direct code IS the bitting \u2014 the number tells you exactly how to cut. If somebody hands you a Schlage lock stamped or recorded as \"12345\" and you cut a 1-2-3-4-5, that key works. Simple. A blind code (also called an indirect or concealed code) is a code that means nothing on its face \u2014 something like \"0-B\" on an old Kwikset, or a Master padlock's \"W512,\" or a car's \"X247.\" That code is just a pointer into a manufacturer's private code series. By itself it is worthless. You have to translate it through a code table or code software to get the real bitting. The whole point of a blind code is security: the factory can stamp it right on the lock or hand it to the customer, and it gives away nothing to anybody without the book.\n\nThat \"book\" used to be a literal book \u2014 a shelf of them. Code books are the published tables that convert blind codes into bittings for a given series, and they also give you the depth-and-space specs for the keyway. The old bibles you will still hear referenced are the HPC \"Blue\" code books and the A-1 Security \"Gold\" series. Today most of us live in software \u2014 InstaCode from WH Software and Framon's Genericode are the two big ones, plus Framon's older GEnesis and the code data baked into computerized machines. You type in the manufacturer, the series, and the blind code, and it spits out the bitting, tells you which blank to grab, and often which card and jaw to set up. InstaCode's real party trick is progression: enter what cuts you DO know from decoding a lock, leave the unknowns as dashes, and it sifts the entire code series to hand you the short list of valid keys that fit the pattern. On an automotive lockout where you read four wafers out of a door and the ignition is buried, that feature is the difference between cutting two test keys and cutting forty. That is not a luxury \u2014 for anybody working cars it is a must-have.\n\nBefore you can cut anything you have to understand depth and space, because that is the coordinate system the whole trade runs on. Every cut on a key lives at a space \u2014 a fixed distance from a reference point, either the shoulder (stop) or the tip \u2014 and every cut is made to a depth, a controlled height off the blade. The manufacturer publishes both as a spec sheet, and you memorize the ones you cut daily. Take Kwikset KW1, the most common residential key in North America: five cuts, depths numbered 1 through 7, first space at .247\" from the stop, then .150\" center-to-center out to .847\" at the fifth position. The depth increment is .023\" per step, single-step progression, blade width .335\". Watch the root-depth numbers on the spec sheet, because they run the opposite direction from the cut depth: a 1 measures .329\" and a 7 measures .191\". That measurement is taken up from the bottom of the blade, so the BIGGER number is the SHALLOWEST cut \u2014 a 1 (.329\") barely nicks the blade, and a 7 (.191\") is the deepest cut that removes the most metal. Depth tolerance is a tight \xb1.003\", spacing \xb1.004\". So on Kwikset the LOW number is the SHALLOW cut and the HIGH number is the DEEP cut, which trips up a lot of new hands who assume \"1\" means a big deep notch. It does not \u2014 and this is the same direction Schlage runs, so once you learn one you have both.\n\nNow Schlage SC1, the other key you will cut ten times a day: six cuts, depths 0 through 9, first space at .231\", then .1562\" spacing out to 1.012\", blade width .343\". Root depths run from a 0 at .335\" (shallowest) down to a 9 at .200\" (deepest) \u2014 so on Schlage, same as Kwikset, the HIGH number is the DEEP cut. The increment here is .015\" per depth step, but pay attention: Schlage is a TWO-step progression, which is a real gotcha for a new hand \u2014 adjacent chambers in a masterkeyed system step by twos, not ones, and the depth tolerance is asymmetric, +.002\"/-0. Schlage runs tight on the shallow side on purpose. The Primus and Classic keys share these same standard-cut specs \u2014 the sidebar bitting on a Primus is milled at the factory and comes pre-cut on the blank, so you only originate the six bottom cuts. And remember Schlage reads bow-to-tip, six standard cuts, five side-bit cuts on Primus \u2014 read from the bow.\n\nHere is the concept that ties depth-and-space together and that will save you from cutting garbage: MACS \u2014 Maximum Adjacent Cut Specification. MACS is the biggest allowable difference between two cuts sitting next to each other. It exists because a cutter has a physical angle and width \u2014 if you try to drop from a very shallow cut straight into a very deep one right beside it, the cutter physically cannot make that wall, the two cuts run into each other, and you get a ramp instead of two clean roots. Kwikset KW1 has a MACS of 4, meaning a 2 can sit next to a 6 (difference 4, legal) but a 1 next to a 6 (difference 5) is impossible \u2014 that key cannot exist and cannot be cut. Schlage conventional and full-size keyways run a MACS of 7; the prohibited adjacent pairs are 0-8, 0-9, and 1-9. Falcon conventional is tighter at MACS 5. Schlage's small-format Everest B and 29R keyways have no MACS restriction at all. Why does a code cutter care? Because when you run progression on an unknown lock and the software or your own list throws out a candidate bitting that violates MACS, you throw it out too \u2014 a MACS violation is a bitting that was never manufacturable, so it is a dead lead. It narrows your search and it catches your own typos.\n\nNow the actual origination \u2014 cutting a key from a code with no original in front of you. Say a customer gives you a Schlage deadbolt stamped with a bitting of 634527, or hands you a blind code you ran through InstaCode to get 634527. On a manual code machine \u2014 the HPC 1200 Blitz is the industry-standard workhorse \u2014 the flow is this. Grab the right blank (SC1) and clamp it in the vise. Pop in the correct code card for the manufacturer and series \u2014 the card is a template that positions your spaces and depths for that specific key; on the Blitz, most jobs literally only require swapping the card. Set the space needle to the \"1\" mark on the card for your first cut position. Now read your first depth digit \u2014 a 6 \u2014 turn the depth crank until the depth needle lands on 6, and that advances the blank into the cutter to exactly that depth. Back the carriage off, move the space needle to position 2, dial the depth to 3, cut. Space to 3, depth 4. And so on across all six positions. Deburr, and you have originated a factory-spec key that has never seen the customer's original. On a computerized machine \u2014 the CodeMax 1200MAX, or the 1200CMBX Extreme Blitz \u2014 you skip the cards entirely: type the code or the bitting in, and the machine indexes spaces and depths itself and cuts the whole key in one pass.\n\nThe judgment that makes this pay: calibration is everything, because a code machine cuts exactly what you tell it \u2014 if the machine is off, every key is off, and a duplicator would have hidden that by copying an original that already worked. Cut a key to a known code, gauge it against depth-and-space keys (a set of precision keys cut to each single depth \u2014 a \"1\" key, a \"7\" key, and so on \u2014 is cheap insurance and every code cutter should own a set for their common keyways). If your cuts read shallow or deep across the board, adjust the machine, not the code. HPC sells a calibration kit (CMB-CK) with a smooth disc, card, and reference key exactly for this, and it fits the whole Blitz 1200CMB series. And if you have no original and no depth keys, you can use any popular double-cut tip-stop auto key with known cuts as a calibration reference. Check your machine at the start of any code job you are unsure of \u2014 a customer who watches you cut a key with no original and have it work first try on their lock will call you for the rest of their life.\n\nTwo more field realities. First, sourcing the code. Residential Kwikset and Schlage locks are frequently sold keyed to a stamped code, and re-key kits and the lock body itself often carry it \u2014 but plenty of installed hardware has the code buffed off or was rekeyed in the field, in which case the stamped number is a lie and you decode or impression instead. Cars stamp key codes on the lock cylinders and sometimes on a tag in the glovebox or the dealer's records; those are almost always blind codes needing software. Second \u2014 and this is not optional \u2014 cutting by code is exactly the kind of capability that gets a shop in trouble if you are sloppy. Code books and code software are, and should be, restricted to verified locksmiths, and you cut a key to a code only for someone with authority over that lock. Log the job. Verify the customer owns the property or has the right to enter \u2014 same standard as any lockout. The reason the trade guards these codes is the same reason you should: a blind code plus a code machine originates a working key to a lock you have never seen, and that power cuts both ways. Knowing how trivially a stamped or recorded code becomes a working key is also exactly why you tell your commercial accounts to control their key codes like cash and never leave them buffable on an exposed cylinder.",sources:["https://www.lsamichigan.org/Tech/Kwikset_KeySpecs.pdf","https://lsamichigan.org/Tech/SCHLAGE_KeySpecs.pdf","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Kwikset_Classic","https://kc.allegion.com/kb/article/what-is-the-maximum-adjacent-cut-specification-or-macs/","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/31675265-max-adjacent-cut-specification-macs"],tags:["MACS","Progression","Code machine","Depth and space","Bitting","Duplicator","Keyway","Cylinder"],aliases:["cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","space and depth","DSD","depth chart","spacing chart","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Ilco","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"high-security-keyways",title:"Restricted & high-security keyways / key control",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Let me set the frame before we touch a single cylinder, because this is where apprentices get it backwards. There are two completely separate things going on inside a \"high-security\" lock, and a customer will pay for both without understanding either. One is physical attack resistance \u2014 can somebody drill it, bump it, pick it, pull the plug. The other is key control \u2014 can somebody walk into a hardware store and get your key cut on a Saturday. Those are different problems solved by different features, and a lock can be strong on one and weak on the other. A UL 437 listing speaks mostly to the physical side. A restricted keyway with a signature card speaks to the key-control side. Nine times out of ten the customer thinks they're buying \"a tough lock,\" but what they actually needed \u2014 the thing that stops the fired employee, the ex-tenant, the disgruntled aide \u2014 was key control. Sell them the right one.\n\n[[diagram:high-security-cylinder-layers|Three separate claims sold under one label, and only one of them is usually what the customer needs]]\n\nStart with the standard, because everybody name-drops it and half of them are bluffing. UL 437 is an Underwriters Laboratories standard for keyed locks and cylinders. It tests resistance to picking, impressioning, drilling, prying, pulling, and forcing, and it specifies the tools and the operators \u2014 common hand tools (chisels, screwdrivers not more than 15 inches long, hammers with a head up to three pounds, wrenches, pliers), hand or portable electric drills, saw blades, pullers, pry bars, picks \u2014 and the picking and impressioning tests are run by an experienced locksmith. The classic time benchmarks people quote are ten minutes against picking and impressioning and five minutes against forcing, though always read the current listing rather than trusting the folklore. It requires corrosion-resistant construction. Here's the nuance a pro knows: UL 437 by itself does not fully define \"high-security lock\" in the United States \u2014 it's an attack-resistance listing, and the dedicated high-security cylinder standard is ANSI/BHMA A156.30. A156.30 grades a cylinder in three separate categories \u2014 key control, destructive test, and surreptitious-entry resistance \u2014 each rated A, B, or C, with A the toughest and C the minimum to be listed at all. So when a spec sheet says \"UL 437 listed,\" that's real and it means something. When a salesman says \"it's high-security rated,\" ask which standard and which grade \u2014 an A156.30 listing with a Destructive Category A is the answer you want on a courthouse or a pharmacy, and if he can't tell you the category, he's selling you a keyway, not a fortress. (The current edition is A156.30-2025, which superseded the 2020 revision \u2014 cite the live one.)\n\nNow the mechanisms, because you should be able to look at a key and tell the customer what's protecting them. Medeco is the classic American answer and the one you'll meet most. The trick is rotating pins: the bottom pins have to be lifted to the shear line AND rotated to a precise angle by the angled chisel cuts on the key, so a sidebar can drop its legs into gates on the pins and free the plug. That's the Biaxial and M3 family \u2014 lift plus rotate plus sidebar. On the current Medeco 4 and on hardened M3 installs you'll find ARX pins, which are a defeat-resistance upgrade: the sidebar gate is not milled all the way down the pin, so decoding tools like the Medecoder can't grab and set the rotation. Here's the history that makes the point \u2014 the Medecoder, developed by Jon King, exploited the fact that on older pins the sidebar grooves were exposed at the bottom of the pin and the groove spacing was constant, so the tool could decode and set each pin. That's the defensive lesson \u2014 the manipulation tools exist and are documented, and the manufacturer's counter (ARX) is literally shaped around denying them. Knowing how the bypass works is exactly how you spec the version that defeats it.\n\nMul-T-Lock comes at it from a totally different direction: dimple keys and telescoping pins, pin-in-pin. There's an outer pin and an inner pin nested inside it, and both the inner and the outer shear lines have to line up at once. The Interactive line adds a spring-loaded element in the plug the key physically engages, creating what they call a virtual combination that only exists with the correct key inserted \u2014 you can't pick a combination that isn't mechanically present until the key completes it. MT5 (fifth generation, introduced 2007) added the Alpha spring at the tip of the key, which engages a pin at the rear to create an additional shear line; MT5+ stacks a slider-based sidebar (read off a laser track on the key) on top of the telescoping pins. Steel anti-drill inserts throughout. If a customer has a lot of dust, paint, or field abuse, the dimple key is forgiving and Mul-T-Lock's platform master-keys deep.\n\nASSA and Abloy are the ASSA ABLOY high-security brands and both worth knowing. ASSA Twin runs two independent locking systems off one key \u2014 a conventional pin stack (nine depths, six spaces) read off the top of the blade plus a coded sidebar read off five side cuts on the lower left of the blade, giving you two separate shear lines and serious pick resistance, with case-hardened drill inserts and a patented sidebar. ASSA introduced the Twin 6000 into the US in 1984 as a UL 437 retrofit cylinder \u2014 that's the pedigree. Abloy is the odd one and, in my hands, the strongest environmental pick: it's a disc-detainer lock \u2014 no pins, no springs. You insert the key and rotate it 90 degrees; angled cuts rotate a stack of discs (Protec2 runs eleven), and when every disc is at its correct angle the sidebar drops into alignment and the core turns. No springs means you cannot bump it \u2014 there's nothing to bounce \u2014 and it laughs at cold, wet, filthy environments where pin locks gum up. Protec2 adds false gates and its Disc Blocking System, which locks the discs together as one package so you can't set them individually \u2014 picking is effectively a non-starter, and it's UL 437 listed for pick and drill resistance. If it's a gate, a tower, a marina, a cold-storage door, an outdoor gate that eats padlocks \u2014 Abloy, every time.\n\nSchlage Primus and Everest are the ones you'll retrofit into existing Schlage-standard buildings, and that's their whole selling point. Primus adds a sidebar driven by five finger pins that a secondary set of bittings on the side of the blade must both raise and rotate \u2014 so a Primus key carries the normal six top cuts plus five side cuts. The beautiful part for a big facility: a Primus key will still operate a standard conventional Schlage cylinder (the sidebar just isn't checked there), even though a conventional key can't touch a Primus cylinder \u2014 so you can phase in high-security cylinders on the sensitive doors without rekeying the whole building on day one, and Schlage explicitly supports mixing Primus and conventional cylinders in one layered master-key system. Everest is the patented restricted platform with an undercut key and a cylinder check-pin that blocks a non-Everest blade from even entering; Everest 29 SL is the current generation, and Schlage offers UL 437-listed cylinders with hardened anti-drill pins. For an institution already standardized on Schlage, Primus/Everest is the low-friction upgrade \u2014 same hardware footprint, same lever bodies, just harder cylinders and controlled keys.\n\nHere's the part that pays your mortgage, so pay attention: key control and patents are two different clocks, and customers confuse them constantly. The patent is what makes a keyway legally \"restricted\" \u2014 while the patent is live, nobody but the manufacturer's authorized dealers can legally produce blanks or keys for that profile, and that's what stops the corner store from copying your key. But patents expire, and roughly twenty years is the life. The dates matter: Medeco Original lapsed around 1985, Biaxial around 2005, M3 in 2021, and Medeco 4 runs out around 2040. Mul-T-Lock Interactive expired 2013, with Interactive+ and MT5 (the Alpha spring) running out around 2025. ASSA Twin V-10 around 2014, Twin Pro around 2015. Abloy Protec expired 2019, Protec2 around 2030. Schlage Primus expired 2007 (Primus XP released 11/08/2007 and patented until around 2024), Everest 2014. So if somebody's running an old Biaxial or first-gen Primus system and thinks their keys are \"uncopyable,\" they're wrong \u2014 the patent lapsed and aftermarket blanks are legally in the wild. That is a real, honest reason to recommend a migration to a current-patent platform, and it's not a scare tactic \u2014 it's a documentable date.\n\nBut \u2014 and this is the senior move \u2014 patent expiration doesn't automatically kill your key control, because key control also lives in the paperwork. Even after a patent lapses, a good manufacturer will keep honoring the original key control agreement and require ordering authorization for cores, cylinders, cut keys, and blanks for the life of the system. That's the signature card. When a system is installed under a restricted program (Medeco's LSC and DBK dealer programs are the textbook examples), the authorized names go on a card the locksmith keeps on file, updated with key numbers, dates, and authorizations as each key is issued. To get a key cut, the requester's ID and signature have to match the card. That is the thing that actually stops the fired facilities manager from getting a master cut \u2014 not the metal, the paperwork and the authority verification. When you set up one of these systems, you are the gatekeeper. Verify authority before you cut. Log it. That log is both your liability shield and your recurring relationship.\n\nWhich brings us to the upsell, and the best one going is schools, clinics, and multi-tenant buildings \u2014 for a reason that's structural, not sales-y. These are exactly the environments with high key turnover and low tolerance for a loose key: staff churn, contractors, aides, temps, tenants moving out, students, per-diem nurses. Every one of those is a person who might walk off with a key, and every lost master in a school or a clinic is potentially a rekey of dozens of doors \u2014 or a life-safety and records-access exposure. A restricted system converts that from \"we have to rekey the wing\" into \"that key is dead on the signature card and can't be reproduced anyway.\" That's the pitch: not toughness, controllability. And it's genuinely recurring revenue done honestly \u2014 you become the single authorized source for every future key, every core swap, every new hire's credential, for the life of the platform. Price the system install fairly, then live on the controlled key duplication and the change-key/master maintenance. That's a relationship, not a transaction.\n\nTwo things I'll leave you with so you don't get burned. First, on schools especially: whatever fancy cylinder you spec, the door still has to obey life-safety code. The general rule in NFPA 101 is that a door in a means of egress needs no more than one releasing motion to unlatch and the classroom side must always let people out during occupancy \u2014 you can lock the corridor side to keep a threat out, but the inside lever must always release. The 2021 edition carved out a narrow allowance for up to two non-simultaneous releasing operations on existing classroom doors in existing educational (K-12) occupancies, with strings attached (releasing hardware mounted 34 to 48 inches above the floor, operable without tight grasping/pinching/twisting, and the two motions can't be required simultaneously) \u2014 but that's an existing-condition exception, not a design target, so don't lean on it for new work. Do not let a security-minded principal talk you into a surface deadbolt or a barricade device that adds a second motion or blocks egress \u2014 that's a code problem and a liability nightmare with your name on the invoice. The high-security cylinder goes into code-compliant hardware, period. Second: the repair-vs-replace call on these is usually replace-the-cylinder, keep-the-lockset \u2014 that's the whole architectural point of Primus and the retrofit cylinders. If a customer has a lapsed-patent high-security system and a real threat model, you don't scrap the door hardware, you re-cylinder to a current-patent restricted platform and re-issue on a fresh signature card. Same door, same lever, new key control clock. That's the clean, honest, profitable answer.",sources:["https://patcosta.com/ul-437-requirements/","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/UL_437","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/21288490/a-high-security-primer","https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/bhma/ansibhmaa156302025","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A15630-2020-High-Security-Cylinders"],tags:["Keyway","Impressioning","Shear line","Manipulation","Cylinder","NFPA 101","Plug","Bypass"],aliases:["KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","contact points","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"access-control-systems",title:"Access control systems: how it ties together",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Let me start you where every access control job actually starts \u2014 not at the panel, not at the software, but at the door. A reader on the wall, a lock on the frame, and a box in the closet that decides whether the two get to talk. Everything else is plumbing between those three things. Once you can see that skeleton, the fanciest enterprise system and the cheapest standalone keypad are the same animal wearing different clothes. Your job is to know which clothes, and \u2014 this is the part that gets amateurs in trouble \u2014 which parts are life-safety code and which parts are just preference. Get the code parts wrong and you don't get a callback, you get a lawyer.\n\n[[diagram:access-control-chain|The five links every job runs through, and the one decision on the wire you cannot cheaply undo]]\n\nStart with credentials, because that's where the money and the confusion both live. The old workhorse is 125 kHz proximity \u2014 HID Prox, Indala, AWID and their clones. Low frequency, dirt cheap, bombproof, been in walls since the 90s. Here's the thing you need to say out loud to a customer, though: 125 kHz prox is not encrypted, full stop. The card broadcasts its number in the clear, and a cheap gadget off the internet \u2014 a Proxmark or even a bottom-dollar Chinese cloner \u2014 reads and copies it in about a second. If somebody hands you a keychain fob that says \"iCLASS\" but it's the thin blue HID prox card underneath, that building's \"access control\" is a speed bump. Nine times out of ten when a client asks why their fired employee still got in, it's because they were running unencrypted 125 kHz and nobody ever changed a thing. So when you spec new, you do not spec bare prox anymore. You spec 13.56 MHz.\n\nAt 13.56 MHz you've got two families that matter: iCLASS and Seos, both HID. iCLASS came first, and its original \"legacy\" flavor got its master keys reverse-engineered years back \u2014 the loclass attack and leaked key sets mean legacy iCLASS is clonable, so if you're doing it right you're on iCLASS SE or, better, Seos. Seos is HID's current top tier \u2014 it's card-agnostic, uses real modern crypto (AES-grade with mutual authentication), and it's the same credential that lives in the mobile wallet. Be honest with yourself and the customer, though: no card is a magic force field. Researchers have shown \"legacy downgrade\" attacks against HID readers configured to still accept older credentials \u2014 SE and even Seos readers included \u2014 so the security lives in how the reader is configured, not just the sticker on the card. Lock the reader to your modern credential and turn the old formats off. That said, the migration story you sell is Seos: HID makes a combo card, the 5106, that's iCLASS Seos 8K plus 125 kHz prox on one piece of plastic, so you can hang new dual-technology readers, hand out combo cards, and cut the old prox readers over on your own schedule instead of in one panic weekend. That combo card is your friend on any retrofit where the customer has thousands of existing prox cards they don't want to reissue on day one. Mobile credentials \u2014 phone-in-wallet, Bluetooth or NFC \u2014 ride on Seos too. They're convenient and generally harder to clone than a bare card, but remember they live and die by the reader supporting them and by the tenant's phone having battery. I don't rip out card readers for mobile; I add mobile alongside cards.\n\nNow the wire between reader and panel, and this is the single biggest decision on a modern install: Wiegand or OSDP. Wiegand is the legacy protocol and you'll meet it on nearly every existing door. It's three wires that matter \u2014 DATA0 (usually called D0, often the green wire), DATA1 (D1, white), and a common ground \u2014 plus power. Both data lines idle high, pulled up to about +5 VDC. To send a zero the reader yanks D0 low for a moment; to send a one it yanks D1 low; the panel counts the pulses. The classic format is 26-bit Wiegand: a leading even-parity bit, 8 bits of facility code (0\u2013255), 16 bits of card number (0\u201365,535), and a trailing odd-parity bit. That's it. Notice what's missing \u2014 any encryption at all. Wiegand is plaintext going down the wall, one-way only, reader to panel, and most manufacturers rate it to about a 500-foot (150 m) cable run. It cannot tell you if somebody pulled the reader off the wall and clipped onto the data lines to harvest or replay card numbers. That vulnerability is real and it's why you should understand it: the way you harden a door is knowing exactly how it gets beaten.\n\nOSDP is the answer, and it's where the industry is going \u2014 hard. OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol) is a SIA standard \u2014 the spec was jointly started by HID, Mercury, and Lenel back in 2008 and handed to the Security Industry Association in 2012 \u2014 and it runs over RS-485 instead of Wiegand's dedicated data lines. Practical differences you'll feel on the job: it's two-way, so the panel and reader talk both directions \u2014 the panel drives the reader's LED and beeper over the same pair instead of you running extra wires for them. It's supervised, meaning the panel constantly polls the reader and screams if the reader goes silent or gets tampered. And with Secure Channel turned on it encrypts the link with AES-128, so no more harvesting card numbers off the wall. RS-485 also lets you run up to about 4,000 feet (1,200 m), and you can multidrop several readers on one bus. The catches: OSDP wants a proper twisted pair, it typically defaults to 9600 baud, and you want 120-ohm termination resistors at both ends of a long RS-485 run or you'll chase reflection gremlins all day. And here's the gotcha that bites people \u2014 installing a reader in OSDP mode is not the same as running Secure Channel. Out of the box, readers pair using a default base key (SCBK-D) in what's called install mode, and install mode is not secure \u2014 it exists only long enough for the panel to push a real key. Once the panel sets a new SCBK the reader drops out of install mode automatically, but if you never push that key you've got all the wiring hassle of OSDP with none of the encryption benefit. Look for the \"SIA OSDP Verified\" mark when you're buying \u2014 that's your assurance the reader actually implements the profile it claims, and there's a Secure Channel profile specifically.\n\nThe panel \u2014 the controller \u2014 is the brain. This is the box in the IT closet or above the ceiling that holds the credential database, makes the lock/unlock decision, drives the lock relay, and logs everything. The critical concept for an apprentice: a real intelligent controller makes its decisions locally and keeps working when the network dies. Take an HID Mercury LP1501, a common single-door edge controller \u2014 it runs on PoE or PoE+ (IEEE 802.3af or 802.3at) or 12 VDC, it drives one door and up to two OSDP multidropped readers, and once it's configured it runs independently of the host software. Antipassback, schedules, who's-allowed-when \u2014 all decided onboard. That's the difference between a controller and a dumb reader-with-a-relay. Your customer's internet goes down at 2 a.m., their badges still work, and the events queue up locally to sync when the link comes back. When you're comparing systems, that's the question you ask: does it make decisions at the edge, or does it phone home for every swipe? The phone-home ones fail closed and fail annoying.\n\nThat leads straight into standalone versus networked, which is really a scale-and-money conversation. A standalone lock \u2014 think a battery-powered keypad lever or a single-door controller with no head-end \u2014 is perfect for a back office, a storage room, a small tenant with three doors. Cheap, fast, no network, no server, no monthly fee. The tradeoff: no central audit trail, and to pull a lost credential you're walking to that door. Networked systems \u2014 whether on-premises server or cloud-managed \u2014 give you one pane of glass: revoke a badge once and it's dead at every door in seconds, real-time alarms, reports for the compliance auditor. Modern controllers like that Mercury will run on-prem, hybrid, or cloud through whatever platform sits on top. My rule of thumb: under about five doors with no compliance requirement, standalone is honest value and I'll tell the customer so rather than sell them a server they don't need. Once they've got turnover, multiple sites, or an auditor who wants access logs, you're networked \u2014 the labor of managing standalone locks across a real building eats the hardware savings alive.\n\nTwo little devices do the unglamorous work of making a door behave, and skipping them is how you fail an inspection. First, request-to-exit \u2014 REX. The whole point of access control is you badge to come IN; you should never need a credential to get OUT of a room in an emergency. REX is how the system knows a legitimate exit is happening. It's either a button by the door, a switch in the exit device, or most commonly a REX motion sensor (a PIR, often UL 294 listed like the venerable Detection Systems/Bosch DS160) mounted over the door on the inside. When it sees someone approaching to leave, it does two jobs: it releases the lock if needed, and \u2014 this is the part people forget \u2014 it shunts the door-position alarm so the system doesn't log a forced-entry when someone simply walks out. Second is the door position switch, the DPS \u2014 a magnetic contact, one half on the door and one on the frame, wired normally-closed so the loop is closed when the door's shut. That's what tells the panel door-open versus door-closed. Without a DPS your system is blind: it can't tell a held-open door from a forced door from a door that never opened, and \"door held open too long\" and \"door forced\" are exactly the alarms a security customer is paying for. On a proper install the REX shunts that DPS during a valid exit so you get clean events instead of a wall of false forced-open alarms.\n\nNow the piece that carries real liability: fail-safe versus fail-secure. This is about what the lock does when it loses power, and getting it backwards can kill people or get a building red-tagged. Fail-safe means power-off equals UNLOCKED. Fail-secure means power-off equals LOCKED. Say it back to yourself until it sticks, because the words are counterintuitive \u2014 \"secure\" means it stays locked in a fire, \"safe\" means it lets people out. A maglock is inherently fail-safe: it's an electromagnet, kill the power and it drops, the door opens. That sounds great for egress until you realize a maglock provides no free mechanical egress on its own \u2014 there's no lever that mechanically retracts it \u2014 so code makes you add release hardware. In the 2021 IBC there are two separate provisions, and you need to know which one you're building to. If the maglock is released by a switch built into door-mounted hardware \u2014 a lever or panic bar you push \u2014 that's Section 1010.2.11, \"Door Hardware Release of Electrically Locked Egress Doors.\" If instead the maglock is released by a motion sensor that detects someone approaching to leave, that's Section 1010.2.12, \"Sensor Release of Electrically Locked Egress Doors.\" The sensor-release version \u2014 the one most people mean when they say \"maglock with a motion\" \u2014 requires the lock to drop on loss of power, on fire alarm activation (and stay unlocked until the alarm resets), and on activation of the building sprinkler or fire-detection system where provided, plus that sensor on the egress side, plus a manual release button mounted 40 to 48 inches above the floor and within 5 feet (1524 mm) of the door that unlocks it directly. (Note the 2024 IBC renumbered these \u2014 sensor release moved to 1010.2.11 \u2014 so always confirm which code edition your AHJ has adopted before you cite a number.) That's a lot of interlocks, and every one of them is there because a maglock can trap people. Miss one on an egress door and you own that failure.\n\nElectric strikes flip the logic and that's exactly why I reach for them first on most interior doors. A strike lets the mechanical lockset keep working \u2014 the inside lever always retracts the latch, so you get free egress mechanically no matter what the electronics do. On a fire-rated door assembly you are required to use a fail-SECURE strike, per NFPA 80, and it must be a strike listed for fire door use \u2014 the door has to stay positively latched in a fire so it can do its job holding back smoke and flame. Put a fail-safe strike on a fire door and it unlocks when the fire alarm cuts power, the spring keeper is the only thing holding the door, the door won't stay positively latched, compartmentation is gone, and you've defeated the entire reason that door is fire-rated. So the instinct to build is: exterior perimeter and fire doors lean fail-secure with mechanical free egress; special applications like an elevator-lobby-to-tenant door that NFPA 101 specifically allows to be locked must be fail-safe \u2014 it has to unlock on loss of power and on fire alarm or sprinkler activation, with the hardware listed to UL 294 and a two-way communication system in the lobby \u2014 so nobody gets trapped in the lobby. When in doubt, you don't guess \u2014 you pull the occupancy type, check whether the opening is fire-rated, and read the section. This is one area where \"I think it's fine\" is not an answer a court accepts.\n\nLet me tie the whole thing into one basic single-door layout so you can see it breathe. Reader on the secure (outside) face of the door, wired back to the controller \u2014 OSDP twisted pair with Secure Channel if you're doing it right, Wiegand if you inherited it. Controller in the closet, on PoE/PoE+ or a dedicated power supply. Off the controller: the lock relay drives an electric strike (fail-secure on this interior office door, with the mechanical lever giving free egress); a REX motion sensor over the inside of the door feeding the controller's REX input; and a door-position magnetic contact, normally-closed, on the controller's DPS input. Somebody badges \u2014 reader reads the credential, sends it to the controller, controller checks it against the onboard database and schedule, and if it's good, energizes the strike relay for a few seconds while it logs a valid access. Somebody leaves \u2014 the REX fires, shunts the DPS so no forced-open alarm trips, and (if this were a maglock) drops the lock. Door opens and closes \u2014 the DPS reports each transition, so held-open and forced events are real. Power dies \u2014 the fail-secure strike stays locked but that inside lever still lets anyone out mechanically. That's the entire loop. Master that one door and a 200-door campus is just that drawing, copy-pasted, with more expensive plumbing between the boxes.\n\nLast thing, apprentice to apprentice: before you touch any of this, verify you're authorized to be on that door. Understanding how prox gets cloned, how Wiegand gets sniffed, how a maglock gets bypassed on the egress side \u2014 that knowledge is what makes you good at hardening the door, and it's exactly the knowledge that gets misused. On a legitimate lockout or a contracted install you've got a work order and a customer who owns the opening; that's your line. Stay on the right side of it, read the code section when life-safety is on the table, and you'll build a reputation that outlasts any credential technology on the market.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiegand_interface","https://getsafeandsound.com/blog/26-bit-wiegand-format/","https://www.hidglobal.com/products/202x","https://www.identisource.net/pd-hid-5106-iclass-seos-prox-composite-card.cfm","https://www.iscs.com.au/product/h-ic-se-seos-prox/"],relatedGuides:["wire-tracing-shorts"],tags:["SEOS","iCLASS","DPS","Maglock","OSDP","Secure Channel","Wiegand","Fail-secure"],aliases:["mag lock","magnetic lock","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","Securitron","door holds shut","OSDP SC","encrypted reader","Data 0","Data 1","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","access panel","head end","ACU","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","card reader","badge reader","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","DHO","held open alarm","propped door","door contact","reed switch"]},{id:"low-voltage-wiring",title:"Low-voltage wiring & power",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Here is the thing nobody tells you when you move from picking locks to wiring them: ninety percent of the \"the strike buzzes but won't release\" service calls you'll ever run are not the strike and not the panel \u2014 they're the wire. Wrong gauge, run too long, feeding off a dollar-store wall wart, or a battery that died two winters ago. Get the copper and the power right and the electronics practically install themselves. Get them wrong and you'll be back on that door three times, eating the trip charges, wondering why a brand-new $300 strike acts like it's tired. So before we touch a single terminal, understand this: on a powered opening, the wire IS the job.\n\n[[img:photo-ict-protege-cabinet|Where all of this lands: controller, expander, supply and the conductor discipline that keeps them working.]]\n\nStart with gauge, because it drives everything downstream. Two numbers dominate access control: 18 AWG and 22 AWG. Rule of thumb \u2014 22 AWG is your signal and data pair (reader Wiegand/OSDP data, request-to-exit contacts, door position switches, anything that carries information, not muscle), and 18 AWG (or heavier, 16 AWG) is your power conductor for anything that physically moves or holds \u2014 strikes, maglocks, electrified mortise locks. Remember AWG runs backwards: the *smaller* the number, the *fatter* the wire. 16 is thicker than 18 is thicker than 22. Fatter copper means less resistance, and resistance is the enemy on every long run. When a spec sheet or an installer tells you \"18 gauge minimum to the strike,\" that minimum is not a suggestion \u2014 that's the floor for a reason we're about to make painfully concrete.\n\nThat reason is voltage drop, and it's just Ohm's law being honest with you. Every foot of copper has resistance \u2014 22 AWG copper runs about 16 ohms per 1000 feet \u2014 push current through that resistance and you lose voltage as heat along the way, so the device at the far end sees less than what left the power supply. Here's a number worth tattooing on your forearm: run 22 AWG at 24VDC to a strike pulling half an amp, and you drop roughly 1.6 volts over just 100 feet of cable \u2014 and that already accounts for the fact that it's a *round trip*, out and back, so a \"100-foot run\" is 200 feet of copper the current actually travels (200 feet times 0.0161 ohms per foot is about 3.2 ohms, times 0.5 amp is that 1.6 volts). Now stretch that same 22-gauge run to 300, 400 feet and the strike is starving \u2014 it hums, the keeper won't fully retract, or it drops out the instant the compressor kicks on and sags the line. Nine times out of ten that's your \"intermittent\" strike. It's not intermittent. It's underfed, and the wire is the diet.\n\nYou fix voltage drop two ways, and you often use both: fatter wire and higher voltage. Go from 22 to 18 AWG and you cut the resistance per foot dramatically \u2014 18 AWG is only about 6.4 ohms per 1000 feet against 22's 16, so same run, far less drop. That's why 18 is the professional floor for strikes and 16 shows up on the really long or high-current pulls. The second lever is voltage itself, and this is the move amateurs miss: a 24VDC device tolerates voltage drop far better than a 12VDC one. Lose 2 volts off 24V and you're at 22 \u2014 the device barely notices. Lose that same 2 volts off 12V and you're at 10, which is below what a lot of hardware will reliably pull in on. So the pro instinct on any run past a couple hundred feet, or any high-inrush maglock, is: run it at 24V if the device supports dual voltage (most quality strikes and mags do \u2014 check the jumper). Higher voltage, same power, means less current, means less drop over the same copper. When in doubt, pull the manufacturer's own wire-gauge-versus-distance chart \u2014 SDC, HES, Von Duprin all publish them \u2014 and size to *their* numbers for *your* device's current draw. Don't eyeball it.\n\nNow the data side, because half your modern openings are network-fed, and this is where I watch guys torch a good install over a $40 spool. Cat5e versus Cat6 \u2014 the honest truth is that for standard gigabit (1000BASE-T) and for powering a reader or IP device over PoE, both run the full 100 meters, which is 328 feet, and that 100m is the total channel: your patch cords count against it, so plan the permanent run for about 90 meters and leave the rest for jumpers. The difference shows up only at 10 gigabit. Cat6 will do 10GBASE-T but only to about 55 meters (180 feet) under the IEEE spec \u2014 and down toward 37 meters in a noisy, tightly-bundled run per TIA's TSB-155 guidance \u2014 because the thing that kills it is *alien crosstalk*, noise leaking between cables packed together in a tray. Cat6a was built specifically to beat that; it runs 10 gig the full 100 meters. For access control you almost never need 10 gig at the door \u2014 a reader is a trickle \u2014 so Cat5e or Cat6 is plenty. The place you care about Cat6a is a camera or uplink backbone. Spend the money where the bandwidth lives, not on the door reader.\n\nWhile we're on data, straighten out three names that get used interchangeably and are not the same kind of thing at all, because this is the single most common vocabulary mix-up on a low-voltage job. RJ45 is a *connector* \u2014 the 8-pin modular plug crimped on the end of a Cat5e or Cat6 cable. It is not a protocol and it carries no meaning about what is riding the wire. RS-485 and RS-232 are *protocols* \u2014 they define how the data moves, and they say nothing about what plug is on the end. So \"run me an RJ45 to the reader\" and \"put the reader on 485\" are answering two different questions, and a spec that blends them is a spec you have to go back and clarify.\n\nRS-485 is the one you actually pull for readers and modules. It is a differential pair \u2014 the signal rides as the difference between two wires, which is why it shrugs off electrical noise that would wreck a single-ended run \u2014 and it is multi-drop, so devices chain along one bus with the two physical ends of that bus terminated. It runs thousands of feet in aggregate, and it is what OSDP readers and expander modules speak. Here is the mix-up that costs people an afternoon: A and B are the two lines of that ONE differential pair, not transmit and receive. A is the inverting line and B is the non-inverting one, which is why you also meet them silkscreened as - and +. Reading A as \"transmit\" and B as \"receive\" is RS-232 thinking carried across... half-duplex 485 talks and listens over the same two conductors, and there is no second pair to go looking for. Layered on top of that, vendors do not agree on which of A/B carries the + label: Kantech lands reader A on 485+ and B on 485-, the reverse of the convention above. So wire to the terminal legend printed in front of you rather than to the convention, and when a module sits silent on a bus you are sure is right, swap the two data conductors and try again before you suspect the board... a reversed pair is dead comms, not damage. RS-232 is the opposite animal: single-ended, point-to-point, short. You will meet it as a laptop into a panel's local config port and almost never as a run you pull. The practical takeaway for new work is to spec RS-485/OSDP over Wiegand where the hardware supports it \u2014 longer runs, simpler wiring, firmware pushed over the wire, and real encryption instead of a protocol that hands its card number to anyone with a clip.\n\nShielded cable earns its keep on the reader run, and then people ruin it at the termination. Reader cable is typically six-conductor shielded, home-run from the reader back to its controller \u2014 Wiegand readers do not daisy-chain, one reader one cable, with a shared elevator setup as the usual exception. RS-485/OSDP readers are the real exception to that rule \u2014 being a multi-drop bus is the whole point, so they legitimately chain along one run with the ends terminated, which is a large part of why they are cheaper to wire on a big job. The shield works by giving induced noise a path to ground instead of onto your data pair, and that only works if it goes to ground at *one* end. Land the drain at the panel, and cut it back and tape it off at the reader. Land it at both ends and you have built a ground loop \u2014 a circuit between two slightly different ground potentials \u2014 and now the shield is injecting noise instead of draining it. Symptom is a reader that works fine most of the day and throws random misreads or drops out when the HVAC kicks on, which is exactly the kind of fault that gets blamed on the reader and gets a good reader thrown away. While you are at the reader, check its voltage: most modern prox readers want 12 VDC, but there are still 5 VDC Wiegand readers out there and some readers jumper between them. Feeding 12 into a 5-volt reader kills it instantly and it is not a warranty claim. One habit worth stealing while you are naming pairs across a building: crews call them by their colours so the instruction is unambiguous from the top of a ladder. Red and green is \"Christmas,\" black and gold is \"Pittsburgh Steelers.\" It sounds silly right up until you are shouting \"ring out Christmas\" down a corridor and the other guy grabs the right pair first time.\n\nPower supply selection is where cheap kills you slowly. You want a *regulated and filtered* DC supply, and those two words matter. Regulated means it holds voltage steady as the load swings \u2014 a strike firing, a mag energizing \u2014 instead of sagging. Filtered means it smooths the ripple so you're feeding clean DC, not lumpy rectified AC that makes readers glitch and boards behave strangely on a Tuesday for no reason you can find. This is exactly why you don't power a strike off a random plug-pack: those things are often unregulated, unfiltered, and undersized. Reach for a proper access-control supply \u2014 Altronix eFlow/AL series and the like \u2014 listed to UL 294 (the access control standard). Those units give you individually protected (PTC or fused) 12/24VDC outputs, filtered and electronically regulated, and critically an onboard charger with automatic switchover to a sealed lead-acid standby battery the instant AC drops. That battery backup isn't a luxury \u2014 on a fail-secure opening it's the difference between \"power blip, nobody notices\" and \"the whole suite is locked out and calling you at 2 AM.\" Size the battery to the standby current draw of everything on that bus and the runtime the AHJ wants. And be careful about what \"the runtime the AHJ wants\" actually is, because access control has no universal standby figure the way fire alarm has its fixed 24 hours. UL 294 rates standby in performance levels rather than hours... the same Altronix AL125ULB is Level II on a 4Ah battery and Level IV on a 7Ah one... so the required runtime comes from the project spec, the owner, or the local AHJ. Ask, do not assume a default, and write the answer into the sequence of operations so it is a documented requirement rather than an installer's guess.\n\nGive the supply a dedicated AC circuit while you are at it. Sharing one invites nuisance trips from whatever else is on it and makes your load math meaningless the moment somebody plugs a heater in.\n\nNow the single cheapest habit that will save you the most callbacks: put transient suppression across every lock, at the lock. When you de-energize a maglock or a strike, that coil dumps its collapsing magnetic field back into your wiring as a voltage spike \u2014 hundreds of volts on a 12-volt circuit, every single time the door cycles. That spike travels back up the pair and hammers the controller's relay contacts and its logic. This is the number one cause of a controller that \"randomly\" locks up, resets, or drops offline after a few weeks, and it is invisible on a multimeter because it lasts microseconds. The fix is a transorb or a reverse-biased diode wired directly across the lock terminals \u2014 and it has to be *at the lock*, not at the panel, because a suppressor at the panel lets the spike ride the whole run before it gets clamped. Good manufacturers ship them in the box for exactly this reason; Keri includes them with their controllers. Guys throw them in the truck bin because they look like a spare part. Install them. It costs nothing and it is the difference between a system that runs for ten years and one that has you back on site every month chasing a ghost.\n\nGetting power *onto* a swinging door is its own craft, and it's where sloppy work shows fastest. You cannot just staple a wire across the hinge gap \u2014 flex it a few thousand cycles and the copper work-hardens and snaps, and now you've got an intermittent that only fails when the door's at a certain angle, which is a nightmare to chase. Two clean answers: a door loop (an armored flexible cable that bridges frame to door with a service loop that flexes gracefully) or, the better answer on a real electrified opening, an Electric Power Transfer \u2014 the Von Duprin EPT10 being the workhorse. The EPT10 mortises half into the frame and half into the door edge, hides completely when the door's closed, and carries ten conductors of 24 AWG through a flexible armored loop rated for the cycle life \u2014 good for up to about 1 amp at 24VDC (with a much higher momentary surge rating). Note that gauge: the EPT's internal conductors are 24 AWG because it's a short bridge carrying already-delivered power the last few inches, so it doesn't fight the voltage-drop battle your long home-run does \u2014 don't confuse the transfer's internal wire with what you pull from the power supply. If you need to move more current across the gap, that's what the two-conductor 18 AWG EPT2 is for. And it's UL listed for use on fire doors, which matters, because the next section is about fire.\n\nThe fire alarm interface is not optional and it is not the place to freelance \u2014 this is life-safety and liability, and an inspector will absolutely catch a maglock wired to ignore the fire panel. Here's the governing idea, and you have to get the arrangement straight because the code treats them differently. Model codes recognize two special-locking flavors for an electrified egress door. The *sensor-release* arrangement \u2014 a motion sensor unlocks the mag as you approach \u2014 is IBC Section 1010.1.9.8 in the 2015 code, renumbered to **1010.1.9.9 in the 2018 and later IBC/IFC**. The *door-hardware-release* arrangement \u2014 a switch built into the panic bar or lever releases the lock \u2014 was IBC 1010.1.9.9 in 2015 and renumbered to **1010.1.9.10 in 2018 and later**. (In 2018 the old 1010.1.9.8 slot became delayed egress, which is why guys quoting an old memorized number get it wrong \u2014 always confirm the edition your AHJ has adopted.) NFPA 101 has essentially parallel provisions.\n\nWhere those two arrangements really split is the fire alarm, and this is the correction most people get backwards: the *sensor-release* door (and a delayed-egress door) MUST automatically unlock on activation of the building fire alarm, sprinkler, or detection system, and stay released until the system is manually reset. The *door-hardware-release* door does **not** carry that fire-alarm-release requirement \u2014 because the panic bar or lever switch is always right there giving you free mechanical egress, the code doesn't force it to drop on alarm. What *both* arrangements require, and what makes maglocks code-workable on egress in the first place, is fail-safe operation: kill power and the lock releases, every time. So the sensor door needs the fire relay wired to interrupt lock power; good access supplies build this in with a fire-alarm-disconnect input that's individually selectable per output, installed per NEC Article 760 and NFPA 72.\n\nKnow the rest of the sensor-release checklist cold, because that's the one with the extra hardware. It requires a manual \"PUSH TO EXIT\" button mounted 40 to 48 inches above the floor and within 5 feet of the door, and pushing it must *directly* cut power to the lock \u2014 independent of all the other electronics \u2014 and hold it unlocked for at least 30 seconds. The door-hardware-release arrangement doesn't need that separate button because the hardware's own switch is the release, and it must release immediately on operation and on any loss of power. Wire either one wrong and you've built a door that traps people in a fire \u2014 so verify the AHJ's adopted code edition, verify the door releases on power loss (and, on a sensor door, on alarm), test the PUSH TO EXIT button, and document it before you leave. When you know how the release chain works, you also know exactly how it can be defeated \u2014 which is precisely why you test it, seal it, and never leave a bypass path an intruder could reach from the secure side.\n\nWhen a connection goes intermittent \u2014 the strike fires sometimes, the reader reads sometimes, the panel throws a random trouble \u2014 you'll be tempted to hit it with [[tool:caig-deoxit-d5|DeoxIT D5]] and move on. Know what the cleaner actually does first. DeoxIT dissolves and floats away the thin oxide film that builds on a mechanical contact that mates and unmates \u2014 a screw terminal that's been live for years, a plug-and-socket, a relay contact, a spade oxidized in a damp enclosure. On those, a shot, a work of the connection, and a wipe genuinely restores it and it stays fixed. Where it only masks the problem is a bad crimp or a cold solder joint. A crimp that was never gas-tight \u2014 oversized barrel, wrong die, strands that backed out \u2014 has resistance in the metal-to-metal joint itself, not in surface oxide, and no cleaner reaches inside a crimp. Flood that with D5 and it may read fine on your meter for the afternoon because you disturbed it, then drift right back. The tell is mechanical: if the wire moves in the connector, if the crimp slides on the conductor, if reseating it changes the reading, the fix is to cut back to clean copper and redo the termination, not to clean it. Rule of thumb \u2014 clean a CONTACT, redo a JOINT. And DeoxIT is a cleaner, not a cure for water: if the enclosure is letting moisture in you'll be back, so fix the enclosure first, then clean the contacts.",sources:["https://sdcsecurity.com/docs/SDC-Wire-Gauge-Size-Distance-chart.pdf","https://us.misumi-ec.com/blog/copper-wire-resistance-awg-chart/","https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/copper-wire-d_1429.html","https://www.altronix.com/app_notes","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category_6_cable","https://caig.com/deoxit-d-series/"],relatedGuides:["dropping-fishing-wire","fire-alarm-interface","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","wire-index","wire-tracing-shorts"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din"],tags:["RS-232","Standby","Maglock","RS-485","Current draw","UL 294","Wiegand","OSDP"],aliases:["UPS","how long on battery","mag lock","magnetic lock","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","Securitron","door holds shut","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","26-bit","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","wire gauge","sagging voltage","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","board in the closet","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"conduit-cctv",title:"Conduit bending & CCTV basics",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Here is the thing nobody tells the apprentice on day one \u2014 a locksmith who can run a clean stick of EMT and hang a working camera is worth twice the guy who only turns keys. Access control, door position switches, mag locks, request-to-exit sensors, and cameras all ride on low-voltage cable, and that cable wants protection. On a commercial job the inspector, the GC, and the customer all expect metal conduit in exposed areas, not a limp run of blue jacket stapled to a block wall. So learn to bend pipe. It is mechanical, it is repeatable, and once the numbers live in your head you will look like you have been doing it twenty years.\n\nStart with the [[tool:conduit-bender|bender]] itself. Your standard hand bender \u2014 Klein, Ideal, Greenlee, whatever is in the truck \u2014 is sized to the conduit, and the size is stamped right on the head along with the numbers you need. A half-inch bender bends half-inch EMT, and it is physically bigger than you would guess. The four figures burned into your brain are the take-up numbers, also called the deduct, for a 90-degree stub: 5 inches for 1/2-inch EMT, 6 inches for 3/4-inch, 8 inches for 1-inch, and 11 inches for 1-1/4-inch. That take-up is the distance the bend \"eats\" \u2014 the gap between where your mark is and where the finished pipe actually stands up. Nine times out of ten a botched 90 is not a bad bend, it is a guy who forgot to deduct.\n\nHere is how the 90 actually works. Say you want a stub that stands 20 inches tall in half-inch EMT. You do not mark at 20. You subtract the 5-inch take-up and mark at 15 inches from the end of the pipe. Line the arrow on the bender head up with that 15-inch mark, foot on the pedal, steady pressure straight down until the pipe reads 90 \u2014 most benders have a level or degree marks cast right into them, and the old-timer trick is to bring it just past 90 because EMT springs back a hair. Measure from the end of the stub to the back of the bend. If you nailed 20, you have got it. Do that fifty times and you will stop thinking about it.\n\n[[diagram:conduit-90|90\xb0 stub-up: mark = stub height \u2212 take-up deduct (5/6/8/11 in by size); put the arrow on the mark and pull to 90\xb0.]]\n\nOffsets are where the math earns its keep \u2014 an offset is two bends in the same plane that jog the pipe around an obstruction or step it off a wall to meet a box. The move is: pick a bend angle, use its multiplier, and mark two spots. The multiplier is just the cosecant of the angle, and you memorize the common ones: 10 degrees is 6.0, 15 is 3.9, 22.5 is 2.6, 30 is 2.0, 45 is 1.4, and 60 is 1.2. The workhorse is 30 degrees because the multiplier is a clean 2.0 \u2014 dead simple. Need a 4-inch offset at 30 degrees? Distance between your two marks is 4 times 2.0, which is 8 inches. Bend the first mark to 30, flip the pipe, bend the second mark to 30 with both bends facing the same way, and you have got a perfect 4-inch jog.\n\n[[diagram:conduit-offset|Offset: distance between the two bends = offset depth \xd7 the angle multiplier; add the shrink back to your length so the box lands.]]\n\nDo not forget shrink on an offset, because that is the other rookie killer. When you kick the pipe up and over, the overall length gets shorter \u2014 the far end pulls back toward you. The shrink per inch of offset rise: 10 degrees loses about 1/16 inch per inch, 15 degrees 1/8, 22.5 degrees 3/16, 30 degrees 1/4, 45 degrees 3/8, and 60 degrees a full 1/2 inch per inch of rise. So that 4-inch offset at 30 degrees eats 4 times 1/4, which is a full inch of length. If you had a box location dialed in, add that inch back before you cut or your termination lands short. Use a shallower angle \u2014 22.5 or even 15 \u2014 when you are running close to a wall or trying to keep shrink down on a long run.\n\nThe saddle is the fancy one, and it looks impressive to a customer watching you work. A three-point saddle jumps the pipe over a round obstruction \u2014 another conduit, a pipe, a sprinkler line. It is one 45-degree bend in the center flanked by two 22.5-degree bends on either side. Find the center of the obstruction, mark it on your pipe, then the center bend uses the notch (the little dimple) on the bender head instead of the arrow. The two outer marks sit 2.5 inches out from center for every inch of obstruction height, and the shrink is 3/16 inch per inch of saddle depth. Bend center to 45 on the notch, flip and bend each outer mark to 22.5 on the arrow, all three in the same plane. Practice this one on scrap before you do it in front of anybody.\n\n[[diagram:conduit-saddle|Three-point saddle: a 45\xb0 center bend on the notch with two 22.5\xb0 shoulders marked 2.5 \xd7 the obstruction height each side.]]\n\nNow the code you cannot ignore, because the inspector will and it will cost you a re-run. NEC 358.26 says no more than 360 degrees of total bend between pull points \u2014 that is four quarter-bends, or any combination that adds up. And offsets count double: a 30-degree offset is two 30-degree bends, so 60 degrees off your budget. Blow past 360 and pulling wire becomes a fight that shreds jackets and strips terminations. When you hit the limit you add a pull point \u2014 a box or a conduit body (an LB, a T, a C). On fill, EMT (Article 358) has no fill table of its own \u2014 it sends you to NEC Chapter 9, which caps you at 40 percent for three or more conductors, 31 percent for two, 53 percent for a single conductor or cable. Short nipples 24 inches or less between boxes get bumped to 60 percent. For low-voltage guys the practical takeaway is simple: do not stuff the pipe. If you are cramming Cat and it fights, go up a trade size \u2014 the extra buck of EMT is cheaper than pulling it all back out.\n\nWhich brings us to what actually rides inside that pipe on a camera job. Two worlds here \u2014 old analog and modern IP \u2014 and you will still run into both. Analog CCTV moves video over coax, classic RG59 with a solid copper center and a shield, and in the security trade it comes as Siamese cable: RG59 bonded alongside an 18/2 power pair so you pull video and power in one shot. Coax RG59 is good to roughly 700 to 1000 feet for video before you are fighting signal loss \u2014 the number floats with cable quality, resolution, and whether you throw an amp on it, so call it a comfortable 700 or so on a standard run \u2014 and the picture tends to be rock-steady. That is a legacy install or a cheap retrofit. If a customer's system is analog and working, do not talk them into ripping it out \u2014 sometimes the repair is just a corroded BNC connector, and a $2 crimp-on beats a $4000 upgrade.\n\n[[diagram:cctv-cabling|Two worlds: analog rides RG59 Siamese (coax + a power pair) to a DVR; IP rides one Cat cable with PoE to an NVR.]]\n\nIP is where the whole industry went, and it is where you want to be \u2014 because IP cameras ride on ordinary Ethernet, and Ethernet carries power on the same cable through PoE. That is the whole game: one Cat cable to the camera, no separate power run, no wall wart at the eave. Power over Ethernet comes in tiers you should know cold. IEEE 802.3af (Type 1, plain PoE) delivers 15.4 watts out of the switch port with about 12.95 watts landing at the device after line loss. 802.3at (Type 2, PoE+) pushes 30 watts at the port, roughly 25.5 at the device \u2014 that is your bread and butter for pan-tilt-zoom cameras and cameras with heaters or IR illuminators. 802.3bt (Type 3 and 4, PoE++) runs 60 up to 90 watts at the port for the power-hungry stuff. Plain PoE lives in the 44-to-57-volt DC window and PoE+/PoE++ run a touch higher, 50 to 57 volts, and it is all backward compatible down the chain, so a PoE+ switch happily feeds a plain-PoE camera.\n\n[[diagram:poe-tiers|PoE tiers: 802.3af / at / bt \u2014 watts at the port vs what actually lands at the device, and the voltage window. Pick the tier by the camera's load.]]\n\nThe distance rule for Ethernet is the one number that trips people: 100 meters, which is 328 feet, end to end \u2014 and that is the same whether it is Cat5e, Cat6, or Cat6a. That is a hard limit for the data, not a suggestion. But here is the field reality on power: voltage drop over thin conductors means a cheap Cat5e run pulling a full 15.4-watt load can start browning out well before 328 feet \u2014 figure a comfortable ceiling more like 50 to 60 meters if you are near the top of the power budget. Cat6 uses fatter 23-gauge copper versus Cat5e's 24-gauge \u2014 about 17 percent more copper in cross-section \u2014 so it holds voltage better, and I default to Cat6 on any camera run that matters. Blow past 100 meters and you need a PoE extender or a fiber media converter \u2014 do not just splice in another box of cable and hope.\n\n[[diagram:ethernet-distance|Ethernet: 100 m (328 ft) is the hard data limit, but power browns out sooner near a full PoE load \u2014 default to Cat6 on camera runs.]]\n\nThe NVR is the box everything lands in \u2014 network video recorder \u2014 and it is the analog DVR's smarter cousin. A lot of NVRs ship with PoE ports built right in, so the camera plugs straight into the recorder and the recorder both powers it and records it, no separate switch. Storage is just hard drives; size them to the camera count, resolution, frame rate, and how many days of retention the customer wants \u2014 a handful of 4K cameras recording continuously will chew through terabytes fast, so spec purpose-built surveillance drives, not desktop junk. And run cameras on their own subnet or VLAN when you can. A camera network hanging off the customer's main LAN is a liability \u2014 those cheap cameras are notorious for weak firmware, and you do not want to be the guy who opened a hole into their business.\n\nSo where does a locksmith actually fit in all this, and where do you stop? You fit squarely in the physical layer \u2014 the pipe, the pathway, the mounting, the pull, the terminations, and the tie-in to the access control you are already installing. The door hardware, the mag lock, the strike, the REX motion, the camera watching the entry \u2014 that is one integrated security package and the customer wants one contractor who owns it. Bending clean EMT and delivering solid PoE runs is what lets you bid the whole job instead of subbing out the ugly half. Know your lane on the code, though: line-voltage power to the NVR, panic hardware, fire-alarm tie-ins, and anything that touches egress or a fire door is its own world with real liability \u2014 NFPA 80 governs fire door assemblies and NFPA 101 governs means of egress, and in a lot of jurisdictions the low-voltage work itself wants a licensed electrician or a low-voltage license depending on the state. When in doubt, verify the local licensing before you pull the permit, and never let a camera bracket or a conduit run compromise a fire-rated door or block an exit. Do the low-voltage clean, keep the egress sacred, and you will own the job.",sources:["https://www.elliottelectric.com/StaticPages/ElectricalReferences/Guides/electrical-conduit-bending-chart-emt-bender-guide.aspx","https://expertce.com/learn-articles/how-to-make-90-degree-emt-bend/","https://utilitypipesupply.com/blogs/news/conduit-bending-basics-offsets-multipliers-and-field-techniques","https://elecalculator.com/charts/offset-bend-multiplier-chart/","https://elecalculator.com/calculator/conduit-bending/saddle-bend-calculator/"],tags:["Network video recorder","Power over Ethernet","Fire-rated door","VLAN","Voltage drop","REX","NFPA 101","Strike"],aliases:["wire gauge","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"ada-operators",title:"ADA Automatic Door Operators",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Let's get one thing straight before you touch a single arm or actuator, because it's the mistake that gets apprentices in trouble on their first operator call. There are two entirely different animals here, and the code treats them completely differently. A \"low energy\" operator runs under ANSI/BHMA A156.19 \u2014 the standard's actual title is \"Power Assist and Low Energy Power Operated Doors\" \u2014 and it opens only on a knowing act: somebody pushes a plate, waves a hand at a touchless actuator, swipes a card, or shoves the door itself. A \"full energy\" operator runs under A156.10, opens off a motion sensor when a body walks into the detection field, and because of that it needs safety sensors, guide rails, and a whole guarded threshold zone. Nine times out of ten the ADA retrofit you're being called for \u2014 the doctor's office, the church, the library, the retail entry that needs a button for wheelchair access \u2014 is a low-energy job under A156.19. That's the standard you live and breathe. If the customer wants the door to fly open when anyone walks up, that's A156.10 and a different, more expensive, more heavily regulated install. Know which one you're quoting before you open your mouth on price.\n\nHere's why A156.19 exists and why the numbers matter: a low-energy door is allowed to operate right in the path of people with no safety sensors because it moves slow and soft enough that it physically cannot hurt you. That's the whole bargain. So the standard pins you to hard field-adjustment numbers, and these are the ones an inspector will actually check with a stopwatch and a force gauge. Opening time to backcheck (or 80 degrees, whichever comes first) shall be 3 seconds or longer, and backcheck shall not occur before 60 degrees. Closing from 90 degrees down to 10 degrees shall take 3 seconds or longer, and from 10 degrees to fully closed not less than 1.5 seconds \u2014 that last slow inch is deliberate, it keeps the door from guillotining a slow-moving hand or a cane. The door must hold fully open not less than 5 seconds. And the kinetic energy of the door in motion shall not exceed 1.25 ft-lbf (1.69 Nm). In plain shop terms: on a light 36-inch door your cycle-to-open runs a few seconds, and a heavy, wide leaf runs a lot longer \u2014 heavier and wider means slower, because the standard's Table I derives minimum times off that 1.25 ft-lbf ceiling as a function of leaf width and weight. If you speed a door up to stop nuisance complaints and blow past those times, you've built an illegal door and you own whatever it does to somebody.\n\nGet your force gauge out, because the force numbers are just as enforceable and this is where you prove compliance. The force required to prevent a stopped door from opening or closing shall not exceed 15 lbf (67 N), measured 1 inch (25 mm) from the latch edge, at any point in the cycle. And critically \u2014 this is the part apprentices forget \u2014 if the power dies, that door still has to open by hand within human-accessible force: not more than 15 lbf to release the latch, 30 lbf to set the door in motion, and 15 lbf to swing it fully open, all measured 1 inch off the latch edge. That's why a low-energy operator is not just a motor \u2014 it's a motor bolted onto a real door closer with a real spring, and the spring is what closes the door and lets it be opened manually when the unit's dead. On the LCN Senior Swing and the Norton units the spring size is adjustable, and that adjustment is doing double duty: it's your manual-opening force AND your closing force. Set it too strong to \"make it close reliably\" and you've just failed the 15-pound stop test and the manual-opening numbers \u2014 and locked out the exact wheelchair user the door was installed for.\n\nNow the signage, because this trips up more final inspections than any adjustment. A156.19 requires signs visible from both sides, mounted 50 inches (plus or minus 12) from the floor to the centerline of the sign, letters 5/8 inch high minimum. On a knowing-act door with a separate wall switch you need the instructional placard reading \"ACTIVATE SWITCH TO OPERATE\" \u2014 white letters on blue, both sides. On a push-pull / push-and-go door it's \"PUSH TO OPERATE\" on the push side and \"PULL TO OPERATE\" on the pull side, same white-on-blue. And every low-energy door gets the round \"AUTOMATIC CAUTION DOOR\" decal, minimum 6 inches diameter, black letters on yellow, visible both sides. These come in the box with a legitimate operator \u2014 LCN, Norton, Stanley all ship them. If you leave them in the box because they look ugly on a nice storefront, the inspector red-tags you and you're driving back out to stick a decal on. Put them on. That yellow circle is code, not decoration.\n\nLet's talk the actual iron, brand by brand, because each one has its personality. LCN is the workhorse you'll see most in the institutional world \u2014 schools, hospitals, government. The current flagship is the electromechanical Senior Swing, sold as the 9500 series and the 2800 series. Here's the part apprentices get backwards: the difference between those two is NOT heavy-duty versus standard \u2014 they're the same medium-to-heavy-duty guts. The 9500 is the surface-applied header and the 2800 is the concealed/overhead-concealed version. Both carry LCN's AdaptivIQ self-adjusting control, which re-learns the door and cuts seasonal nuisance callbacks by quitting the fight against temperature swings on the spring. Older but still everywhere is the 4630/4640 Auto Equalizer, an electro-hydraulic unit \u2014 a motor drives the door open, mechanical spring closes it, and it acts as a plain door closer on power loss. The 4630 does up to 170 degrees manual, the 4640 up to 100. LCN also runs the 9100/9130 Benchmark line for lighter, budget interior jobs. All of it meets A156.19, ADA, and ICC A117.1 \u2014 verify the UL/cUL listing sticker is on the unit you're installing, because an unlisted operator on a code-required accessible door is a liability you don't want.\n\nNorton \u2014 now Norton Rixson under Allegion \u2014 gives you the 5700 and 6000 series, and here's the shop-smart part: the 5700 is basically Norton's heavy-duty 1600-family manual closer with a motor and control pack built onto it, so if you already know Norton closers, you already know 80 percent of this operator. The 5740 is the universal push-or-pull-side unit, non-handed, which is the one to stock because it covers most jobs off one shelf. The important spec for you as the integrator: the 5700 carries an onboard 24VDC accessory power supply rated about 1.1 amps, and the 6000 puts out 24VDC at 1.3 amps \u2014 that's your budget for powering an electric strike, a mag lock, or an RF receiver right off the operator without dragging in a separate power supply. Both also give you a dry relay for interfacing latch-retraction exit devices and a vestibule-sequencing input when you've got two operators to coordinate. Stanley Access Technologies runs the Magic-Force (M-Force) line \u2014 it's field-selectable between full-energy (A156.10) and low-energy (A156.19) at install, drives leaves up to 48 inches and 350-plus pounds, and comes surface, concealed, or in-ground. That field-selectable trick is handy but dangerous: set it to full energy and you've now created a door that legally requires safety sensors and guarding, so don't flip that switch casually to \"make it nicer.\" Horton Automatics rounds it out with the 4000LE and 7000 series \u2014 the 7000 is their barrier-free ADA retrofit unit compliant to A156.19 and A117.1, both feature Push-N-Go, an adjustable 2-to-30-second close delay, and the SoffTouch obstruction-reversing feature.\n\nThe actuator \u2014 the button \u2014 is where the low-energy world lives, since a knowing act is the whole legal basis for skipping safety sensors. Your bread and butter is the BEA or Camden push plate: 4.5-inch and 6-inch round or square, plus jamb-mount narrow plates for aluminum storefront stiles. They come hardwired or wireless \u2014 BEA's family runs 900 MHz wireless actuators paired to a receiver at the operator, which is a lifesaver when you can't fish a wire across a finished lobby to the far jamb. Since COVID the touchless actuator is the fast-growing line item \u2014 BEA's 10MS31 microwave touchless units in single-gang, double-gang, jamb, and 6-inch round wave-to-open, with adjustable detection range and a relay hold time you can set from about half a second to 30 seconds. Wire the plate's normally-open dry contact to the operator's actuation input, set your relay hold time, done. A wiring gotcha worth its own callback: most operators have two actuation inputs, an interior and an exterior with an independent delay, so put the outside plate on the delayed input if there's a lock to retract \u2014 you want the strike released before the door starts to pull, or the operator strains against a locked door, trips its obstruction sense, and stops. That's the classic \"it opens sometimes\" complaint, and it's almost always sequence, not the motor.\n\nThat sequence of operation is the real craft here, and it's where the operator ties into the lock, the exit device, and the fire alarm \u2014 which is exactly the work that separates a door-hanger from a systems integrator, and it's the growing money. The clean install: an actuator hit fires the operator, the operator throws a signal to retract the latch first, then drives the door. If it's an electric strike, it's got to release before travel. If it's a rim exit device on an egress door, you use an electric-latch-retraction device \u2014 a Von Duprin QEL (Quiet Electric Latch) or EL-type \u2014 and you MUST retract that latch before the door moves, so you build in a short delay: latch retracts, then a beat, then the door opens. Note the QEL, per Von Duprin, always provides mechanical egress and, on a fire-rated opening, must be paired with an automatic operator and under the control of the fire alarm system. On the egress side, remember the panic hardware still has to let anybody push out mechanically at all times regardless of what the operator or power is doing \u2014 free egress is non-negotiable, and a low-energy operator never gets in the way of the mechanical exit device. And here's the accessibility trap the standard flags in its own appendix: if the door swings toward you and you can't get out of its way in a wheelchair on the swing side, you need an activating switch positioned on the swing side too, or you've built a door that opens into the person trying to use it.\n\nFire alarm interface is the piece you cannot fudge, because it's life-safety and it's the part that gets locksmiths in real trouble. On a fire-rated opening, the operator must give up on alarm \u2014 NFPA 80 requires the door to close and positively latch in a fire, so the operator has to release and let its own spring close the leaf. You wire the fire alarm relay to kill the operator's hold-open and actuation on alarm; the spring closer takes over and latches the door. That means on a fire door you do not use stand-by battery power to keep the operator holding open through an alarm \u2014 it has to drop out and let the door close. Two more rules to burn in: on a fire-rated opening use a fail-secure electric strike, never fail-safe, because a fail-safe strike unlocks on power loss and the door won't latch \u2014 and a fire door that won't positively latch is a failed fire door. (Where a fail-secure strike is used on a fire door, best practice is to wire a fire-alarm interface to it so it can't be left standing unlatched by an access-control unlock when the alarm hits.) Fail-safe locking hardware belongs on non-fire electrified egress doors \u2014 stairwells, delayed-egress setups \u2014 where the code wants unlock-on-alarm for escape. Get those backwards and you've either built a fire door that flops open in a fire or an exit that traps people; both are the kind of mistake that ends up in a report with your name on it. When in doubt, the fire door closes and latches on alarm, and the egress path always opens by hand \u2014 hold those two truths and the wiring falls into place.\n\nLast, the business read, because this is a genuinely growing service line and you should price it like the skilled integration work it is. The install is the small part; the recurring money is inspection and adjustment. AAADM and the industry strongly recommend these doors be inspected at install and at minimum annually by a qualified (ideally AAADM-certified) inspector, on top of daily safety checks by on-site staff \u2014 and the whole reason is drift. Every temperature swing pulls the hydraulic and spring settings around, every worn arm bushing changes the geometry, every new lock somebody adds without telling you throws off the sequence, and after a life of cycles those open, hold, and close times wander out of spec. So sell the annual A156.19 safety check: stopwatch the open, hold, and close times against the standard's tables, put the force gauge 1 inch off the latch edge and confirm you're under 15 pounds to stop it and within the 15/30/15-pound manual numbers dead, verify the caution signage is present and legible, and confirm the fire alarm drop-out actually drops the operator and lets the door latch. Document it, sign it, hand the customer a report. That paper protects them on liability and it protects you \u2014 and it's a standing route of recurring revenue on hardware that, unlike a deadbolt, is legally expected to be maintained.\n\nWhen the operator on the door is a [[model:ditec-entrematic-ha9-939d|Ditec HA9 / 939D]], most of your service life is spent on one green control board, and it earns respect because everything is a two- or three-letter abbreviation silkscreened next to a trim pot or a DIP switch. Learn the map once. The trim pots set the motion \u2014 the speed pots run opening and closing speed (OPSP, CLSP) and the setup/travel (SPTE), POAS is the slow-down or approach zone, CLTQ is closing torque, HOT is the hold-open time. The two DIP banks set behavior, not motion: the six-position red bank sets the operating logic, and the second bank \u2014 PAG, MAT, SOS, ARM1, ARM2 \u2014 enables the mat/actuator, the safety sensors, and the two armed inputs. The pluggable green terminal blocks are where the field wiring lands, and the [[model:ditec-operator-control-board|companion module]] carries the logic terminals you'll actually troubleshoot: LOUT (lock output), OPD (door signal), XIMP and IIMP (external and internal impulse \u2014 your actuators and REX), GND, ARM1/ARM2, and the Sync ON/OFF-HO jumper that pairs a matched pair of doors.\n\n[[diagram:ditec-ha9-board|Ditec HA9 board map: the trim pots set motion, the two DIP banks set behavior, and the green blocks carry the field wiring and the LOUT/OPD/XIMP/IIMP logic terminals.]]\n\nSet open and close without chasing your own tail by working one motion at a time and in order: back the closing torque and the approach speeds down before you touch the mains, run the door and set the OPENING speed and its slow-down first, confirm it reaches the backcheck without slamming the stop, and only then set the CLOSING speed and its slow-down. Setting close before open, or riding both pots at once, is how you end up circling \u2014 every change to one interacts with the other's slow-down zone. And here's the line you don't cross: opening force, opening and closing speed, and hold-open time on a powered pedestrian door aren't whatever looks good \u2014 they're governed by ANSI/BHMA A156.19, and the only way to know you're compliant is to measure the leading-edge force and the time-to-close with a force gauge and a stopwatch on that specific door, not to copy pot positions from the last job. Anything touching egress or a fire-rated opening is out of scope here \u2014 that's NFPA 101 and NFPA 80 territory, covered in the fire-door inspection article, and you don't re-derive it at the operator. For the factory defaults and the full menu, the Ditec/Entrematic HA8-HA9 installation manual is the authority; this is the field map and the failure modes, not a transcription of it.",sources:["https://blog.ansi.org/ansi/ansi-bhma-a156-19-2019-power-assist-doors/","https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/bhma/ansibhmaa156192019","https://www.automateddoorsolutions.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/ANSI-A156-19-2007.pdf","https://www.autodoorandhardware.com/Understanding-ANSI-A156.19-Low-Energy-Door-Operator-Require-s/123397.htm","https://idighardware.com/2022/06/faqs-about-low-energy-automatic-operators/","https://www.ditecentrematic.us/","https://www.buildershardware.com/"],relatedGuides:["fire-door-inspection"],tags:["Exit device","Electric strike","Fail-safe","Fail-secure","Rim exit device","Relay","Opening","Strike"],aliases:["panic bar","crash bar","push bar","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","fail unlocked","fail open","mag lock behavior","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","rim panic","surface strike panic","Von Duprin 98/99","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","lockout","get in","no key","emergency opening","strike plate","door won't close","misaligned strike","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","volt free","form C","clean contact","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX"]},{id:"safe-anchoring-opening",title:"Safes: anchoring in concrete & getting in",module:"Safes & Vaults",body:"Let's split this job into the two halves you'll actually get called for: bolting the box down so nobody walks off with it, and getting into a box when the customer forgot the combo or the lock died. Different skills, opposite mindsets. Anchoring is muscle and geometry. Opening is patience and respect for what's behind the dial. Do both wrong and you'll either watch a thief roll a \"burglary-rated\" safe out on a hand truck, or you'll trip a relocker and turn a two-hour open into a two-day nightmare. I'll walk you through how I think about each.\n\n[[diagram:safe-anchor-and-open|Muscle and geometry on one side, patience and information on the other]]\n\nStart with the anchoring, because a safe that isn't bolted down is barely a safe. Nine times out of ten the customer's real threat isn't a guy with a torch \u2014 it's two guys with a dolly and ninety seconds. An RSC (residential security container, the rating on most gun safes) is listed under UL 1037 and, at the baseline RSC Level I, is rated to resist five minutes of attack by one operator with common hand tools \u2014 hammers up to three pounds, pry bars, chisels, screwdrivers, punches, nothing over eighteen inches. That rating means nothing if the box is sitting free on the slab, because the fastest \"attack\" is just tipping it onto a dolly and wheeling it out \u2014 no five-minute clock, no tools. So the anchor bolts aren't an afterthought; they're half the security. Most decent safes ship with pre-drilled bolt-down holes in the floor \u2014 usually two or four, quarter-inch or half-inch clearance holes stamped in the base plate \u2014 and a bolt-down kit with sleeve anchors or wedge anchors. If the kit's junk or missing, don't be shy about upgrading the hardware. The kit is the cheap part.\n\nYour tool for the concrete is a rotary hammer with an SDS-plus chuck, not your cordless \"hammer drill.\" Understand the difference because it decides whether you finish the job or cook a bit and your wrist. A conventional hammer drill makes its \"hammer\" with a pair of ridged cam discs ratcheting against each other \u2014 very high frequency, often over 20,000 blows per minute, but almost no real energy per blow. It'll get through brick or a soft slab and it'll struggle and glaze on cured structural concrete. An SDS-plus rotary hammer drives the bit pneumatically \u2014 a piston hammers the bit shank forward at a slower rate, roughly 3,000 to 5,000 blows per minute, but with dramatically more energy per strike \u2014 so you get real work done with far less bearing-down and less vibration in your hands. For anchor holes in the 3/16\" to about 1-1/8\" range \u2014 which covers every safe bolt-down you'll ever drill \u2014 SDS-plus is the right tool, and even a cheap cordless SDS will embarrass a premium conventional hammer drill on a half-inch hole. Buy quality SDS-plus bits, keep a couple of spares in the common sizes (3/8\" and 1/2\"), and pull the bit to clear dust every inch or so on deep holes so the flutes keep cutting instead of packing.\n\nNow the anchor choice, and this is where guys get it wrong. Three families you'll reach for: wedge, drop-in, and epoxy (adhesive). A wedge anchor is a stud with a flared clip at the bottom; you drop it in, torque the nut, and the clip wedges against the hole wall. It's the strongest mechanical anchor and my default for a good thick slab. The numbers you need to keep in your head are minimum embedment by diameter, because that's what makes the holding values real. In 2000 psi concrete the published minimums run: 1/4\" anchor needs 1-1/8\" embedment, 3/8\" needs 1-1/2\", 1/2\" needs 2-1/4\", and 5/8\" needs 2-3/4\". Your drill bit diameter matches the anchor diameter \u2014 a 1/2\" wedge anchor uses a 1/2\" bit, because the anchor is sized to cut its own clearance. And always drill the hole deeper than the anchor will sit \u2014 the rule of thumb is at least 1/2\" (or one anchor diameter) deeper than your embedment so the anchor doesn't bottom out before it's set and so the dust has somewhere to fall. A dead-set 1/2\" wedge in 2000 psi concrete pulls roughly 3,000 lbs ultimate in tension and shears around 5,500 lbs ultimate \u2014 but that's ultimate, the point of failure. Apply the standard 4:1 safety factor for a working load, so figure about a quarter of those numbers as what you can actually count on. That's still far more than any thief with a dolly is going to overcome.\n\nThe trouble with wedge anchors shows up on thin slabs and near edges, and that's where drop-ins and epoxy earn their keep. A wedge anchor works by radial expansion \u2014 it pushes outward hard against the concrete \u2014 and near a slab edge or in a thin pour, that expansion can crack the concrete and blow out the anchor. A residential garage or basement slab is often only about 4\" thick, sometimes less, and if an anchor wants more embedment than the slab is deep, you can't hit full spec \u2014 you're anchoring in whatever the pour actually gives you. A drop-in anchor is an internally-threaded shell you set flush with a setting tool; it lets you bolt down with a machine bolt and is handy when you want the anchor below the surface \u2014 but it's still an expansion anchor, so it fights you on thin or edge pours the same way. When the slab is thin, near an edge, cracked, or you just want maximum grip without splitting forces, go adhesive/epoxy \u2014 a Hilti HIT-HY 200 with HAS threaded rod, a Simpson SET, a Red Head, whatever quality system you trust. Epoxy develops its bond along the whole embedded length and exerts no expansion force, so it won't split a thin slab or an edge, and it generally out-holds an expansion anchor in weak or thin concrete. The catch: it lives and dies by hole cleanliness. Blow the hole out, wire-brush it, blow it again \u2014 dust in the hole can gut your bond strength, and a lazy epoxy install is worse than a good wedge anchor because it looks solid and isn't. Follow the cartridge's cure time before you torque anything.\n\nFloor safes are their own animal and honestly the strongest anti-theft play there is, because the concrete itself is the armor \u2014 a thief can't carry off a hole in your slab. Setting one is a wet job: you're either coring or forming a pocket in the slab, dropping the body so the door lip sits flush or just proud of the finished floor, and pouring concrete around the body so it's fully encased. Keep the door flange clean and level, keep concrete out of the bolt work and the dial spindle, and slope or seal around it because floor safes sit in the one spot in the building where water collects \u2014 a rusted-up floor safe lock is a call I've made too many times. For the retrofit \"drop it in an existing hole\" jobs, treat the anchor bolts and the surrounding grout as the whole ballgame and pack it solid; a floor safe that can be pried up because someone skimped on the pour is just an expensive planter.\n\nNow flip your head around to the other half \u2014 opening. And before the tools come out, verify authority every single time. You open your own property, or a customer's with proof it's theirs \u2014 a receipt, a bill of sale, matching ID to the address, a locksmith work order. No exceptions, no \"my buddy asked me to.\" The bypass knowledge in this section exists so you can serve legit lockouts and, just as important, so you understand how these locks fail \u2014 knowing exactly how a box gets drilled is precisely how you spec and sell a customer a box that resists it. That's the defensive value: you can't harden what you don't understand.\n\nNinety percent of \"my safe won't open\" calls are not a dead lock \u2014 they're operator error or a worn dial, and you fix those without touching a drill. Walk the combination yourself, slowly, and mind the dialing convention. Most Group 2 mechanical locks like the Sargent & Greenleaf 6730 \u2014 the industry-standard three-wheel Group 2, UL 768 listed, with a million theoretical combinations \u2014 dial left-right-left: turn left past the first number four times and stop on it, right past the second number three times and stop, left to the third number two times and stop, then right slowly to retract the bolt. Customers skip revolutions, dial the wrong direction, or \"park\" the dial and lose count. Also mind dial slop \u2014 Group 2 tolerance is about plus-or-minus 1-1/4 dial graduations per wheel, so a worn dial or a stretched combination can be off by a number or more; if the dialed combo doesn't quite catch, try each number plus or minus one. Check that the handle isn't being forced, that the boltwork isn't bound, and that the customer didn't recently change the combo with a bad change-key procedure. A lot of \"broken\" safes just need a patient re-dial and a customer who'll admit they might've fat-fingered it.\n\nTo troubleshoot smart, you've got to know what's happening behind the dial. Inside a 6730-type lock the dial turns a spindle that runs a drive cam; the drive cam has a drive pin that picks up the wheels in the wheel pack \u2014 three brass and aluminum wheels stacked on the spindle, each with a gate (a notch) cut in its edge. As you dial, the drive pin catches the first wheel, that wheel's fly catches the next, and so on, so you can set each wheel to a number. On the drive cam sits a lever with a nose \u2014 the fence \u2014 resting on the wheel edges. When all three gates line up under the fence, the lever nose drops into the gates and the drive cam's gate can pull the bolt. That's it. That's the whole trick: line up three notches, the fence drops, the bolt comes back. Understanding this tells you why a lock \"feels\" a certain way and where it's failing.\n\nManipulation is opening that lock with nothing but your fingers, your ears, and a graph \u2014 no drilling, no damage \u2014 and it's the skill that separates a real safe tech from a parts-changer. It works by exploiting tiny manufacturing tolerances. On every dialing there's a spot where the drive cam's gate reaches the lever nose; the nose drops slightly into the drive cam gate and the wheels' high spots contact the lever \u2014 those are your contact points, the left and right positions where you feel and hear the lever touch. You park wheels at test positions, then dial through and read where the two contact points fall, and you plot them on a graph against the dial number. When a wheel's gate rotates under the fence, it lets the lever nose settle a hair deeper, and the contact area on your graph gets narrower at that spot \u2014 the left and right contact points pinch together. You read that pinch, lock in that wheel's number, move to the next wheel, and repeat until all three gates are located; then you dial the numbers out. It's slow, it's tactile, and here's a spec worth getting right: a plain Group 2 lock like the 6730 carries no rated manipulation time at all \u2014 UL 768 only holds it to a dialing-tolerance and durability standard. The two-hour figure people throw around belongs to Group 2M (\"M\" for manipulation), which is rated for two hours of resistance to expert manipulation; Group 1 is rated for twenty hours. So a stock 6730 is a Group 2 \u2014 a good lock, but if a customer actually wants rated manipulation resistance, you're stepping them up to a 2M or a Group 1, not the 6730.\n\nGroup 1 locks are built with tighter tolerances (roughly plus-or-minus 1 graduation versus Group 2's 1-1/4) and often serrated or false-gated wheels specifically to defeat this feel \u2014 false gates give the lever a fake pinch that reads like a real one on your graph \u2014 which is why they resist manipulation far better and why you often skip straight to drilling on them. When manipulation won't pay \u2014 a Group 1, a lock that's mechanically failed with a broken drive pin or sheared bolt, or the clock's against you \u2014 you drill, and this is where respect keeps you out of trouble. The gold standard is the manufacturer's drill-point diagram: safe makers publish tightly-guarded drill guides to licensed locksmiths, giving you an exact point and angle, usually near the dial spindle axis, to reach a specific spot in the lock. You do not freelance holes on a good safe. The move most of the time is to scope it, not blast it: you drill a small hole to a point where you can slip a borescope in and watch the mechanism. The classic is scoping the change-key hole \u2014 drill so your scope sees the change-key hole on the back of the lock, then spin the dial and read the wheel gates directly against landmarks inside, and you've got the combination without wrecking the lock. Other times you scope the wheel pack cover, back the plate screws out, and set the wheels by hand. Scoping is surgical; you patch one small hole and the safe lives on.\n\nThe two things that will ruin your day if you drill blind are hardplate and relockers, and a professional plans around both. Quality safes put hardplate \u2014 specially hardened or alloyed steel, often composite with tungsten-carbide chips cast into a cobalt or vanadium matrix designed to shatter your bit's cutting tips \u2014 right in front of the lock and the drill points. You get through it with carbide or diamond bits, slow speed, steady pressure, and patience, but it's deliberately miserable, and burning through the wrong spot is how you find a relocker the hard way. The relocker (or auxiliary/secondary lock) is a spring-loaded bolt held back under tension, and its whole purpose is to fire and permanently block the boltwork the instant an attack disturbs it. The common trigger is a tempered glass relock plate \u2014 a sheet of glass (or a plastic/heat-sensitive link) mounted over the lock area, wired to spring-loaded relock pins. Crack that glass with a stray drill bit or a torch, and the pins release and jam the boltwork so it can't retract \u2014 now you're drilling a much harder, deliberately-hidden secondary lock too, and you can't clear it until every fired pin is found and drilled out. Punches, torches, and thermal lances trip these by design. So the whole reason you use the manufacturer's diagram, scope instead of blast, and go slow through hardplate is to reach the lever or the wheel pack without ever touching the glass. Know where the relocker and the hardplate live on the model in front of you before the bit spins \u2014 that knowledge is the difference between a clean open and a destroyed safe. And that same map, read the other direction, is exactly how you advise a customer which box to buy: hardplate over the lock, a glass relocker, real anchoring \u2014 the features you now know a burglar has to beat.",sources:["https://www.ul.com/news/residential-security-container-standard-revised","https://americansecuritysafes.com/knowledge-base/what-does-u-l-certified-rsc-mean/","https://www.confast.com/wedge-anchor-technical-specifications/","https://www.confast.com/articles-wedge-anchor-concrete/","https://www.concretefasteners.com/drop-in-anchor-faq/"],tags:["Anchoring","Drive cam","Wheel pack","Hardplate","Relocker","Manipulation","Boltwork","Cam"],aliases:["lag bolts","sleeve anchor","bolt it down","cam driver","retainer cam","combination wheels","dial wheels","wheel change","drill resistant plate","carbide plate","hard plate","re-locker","relock trigger","glass plate","punch relocker","dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","handle spins","bolts won't retract","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","housing","lock body","cylinder body","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"business-of-locksmithing",title:"The business: licensing, legal, pricing & the truck",module:"The Business of Locksmithing",body:"Here is the thing nobody tells you when you fall in love with picking a lock: the picking is maybe ten percent of the job. The other ninety is running a business that does not get you sued, fined, or de-licensed. You can be the best manipulator in three counties and still go broke \u2014 or worse, wear an ankle monitor \u2014 because you opened the wrong door for the wrong person on the wrong day. So let's talk about the parts of this trade that actually keep the lights on: what license you need, what letters go after your name, who you're legally allowed to let inside, what to charge, how to insure the whole mess, what rides in the van, and the recurring-revenue play that most solo guys leave sitting on the table.\n\n[[diagram:business-time-split|Where the day actually goes, what shuts you down, and what compounds]]\n\nStart with licensing, because it decides whether you can legally hang a shingle at all \u2014 and it is a patchwork, not a national scheme. As of early 2025 roughly thirteen states license locksmiths at the state level: Alabama, California, Connecticut, Illinois, Louisiana, Maryland, Nevada, New Jersey, North Carolina, Oklahoma, Oregon, Texas, and Virginia. Everywhere else, you're governed by city or county rules or nothing at all \u2014 but \"nothing at all\" still means you need a business license, a sales-tax permit, and whatever your municipality wants. And the map moves \u2014 the trend the last few years has been deregulation. Nebraska and Tennessee repealed their programs back in 2021. Florida wiped out all locksmith licensing effective July 1, 2025 \u2014 HB 735 preempted local occupational licensing, so the longstanding Miami-Dade and Hillsborough County programs are gone and a Miami locksmith now needs no locksmith-specific credential. Illinois has voted to let its program sunset \u2014 it's set to expire in January 2029. New York City's Department of Consumer and Worker Protection stops licensing individual locksmiths on May 31, 2027, moving to a business-only license (Local Law 183 of 2025), and dropping the fingerprint requirement. Nine times out of ten the guy who gets burned is the one who read a blog from four years ago and assumed the rule still held. Check your current state statute and your county clerk before you buy a single pick.\n\nKnow your own state's plumbing, because the agency tells you the flavor of the rules. In Texas it is the Department of Public Safety Private Security Program under Occupations Code Chapter 1702 \u2014 and here's the gotcha that trips up newcomers: it is a security-industry regime, not a trade board. You hold an individual locksmith license, but you must be affiliated with a DPS-licensed company (typically a Class B contractor with a Qualified Manager on staff), you get fingerprinted through IdentoGO, you clear a DPS/FBI criminal background check, and you enroll through the TOPS online system. California runs it through the Bureau of Security and Investigative Services (BSIS) with both a company license and individual employee registration, plus DOJ and FBI background checks. Virginia sits under the Department of Criminal Justice Services (DCJS), North Carolina has its own Locksmith Licensing Board with an apprentice-to-exam ladder. See the pattern \u2014 most of these states treat you like private security, which means a felony in your past can end the conversation before it starts. Sort that out before you invest.\n\nNow the letters. State licensing is legal permission; ALOA certification is proof of skill, and clients \u2014 especially commercial and institutional ones \u2014 read them like a menu. The ladder runs through ALOA's Proven Professional Registration Program (PRP). RL, Registered Locksmith, is the old entry designation \u2014 it was grandfathered in at the program's 1985 inception and is really a legacy designation now, not a graded cert. The real first rung is CRL, Certified Registered Locksmith: you sit a 100-question mandatory exam built on roughly ten categories of core knowledge, and you must clear at least two elective categories on top of it, everything scored at 70% or better to pass. From there, CPL \u2014 Certified Professional Locksmith \u2014 is a CRL who has passed an additional twelve electives at that same 70% bar. The top of the tree is CML, Certified Master Locksmith: a CPL who clears nine more electives, again 70% each, covering advanced ground including electronic locksmithing. The elective pool itself is deep \u2014 north of thirty individual exams to draw from. My advice to an apprentice: get CRL as fast as you honestly can. It is the credential that gets you past a property manager's insurance requirement, and it is the difference between \"some guy with a drill\" and \"a certified professional\" when you're bidding a building.\n\nHere is the part that ends careers, so read it twice \u2014 authorization to enter. The lock does not tell you who owns the door. Your job, before a single pin gets set, is to verify that the person hiring you has the legal right to be let in. For a homeowner: photo ID matching an item of mail, a utility bill, a title, or a lease at that address. For a car: registration and ID. For a business: someone on the authorized-signer list, not just whoever's standing there in a polo shirt. Document it \u2014 photograph the ID, note it on the work order. That paper trail is your defense the day someone claims you helped a stalker into an apartment. Nine times out of ten the \"emergency lockout\" is exactly what it looks like. It's the tenth one \u2014 the ex-boyfriend, the business partner in a dispute, the roommate who just got kicked out \u2014 that costs you everything.\n\nEvictions and foreclosures are where good locksmiths get dragged into court, so understand the mechanics cold. In every state, a residential tenant can only be physically removed by a sheriff or marshal executing a court-issued writ of possession. The landlord \u2014 and any locksmith the landlord brings along \u2014 may only act once that officer is present and executing. If a landlord calls you to \"just change the locks\" on an occupied unit before the sheriff has done the lockout, that is a self-help eviction, and most states impose statutory damages regardless of whether the tenant actually owed the money or had any right to stay. You, the locksmith who turned the key, are a named party. So the rule on the truck is simple: no writ, no sheriff on scene, no lock change on an occupied residence. Get a copy of the writ. Confirm the officer is present. Florida is explicit \u2014 a landlord cannot take possession or change locks until the sheriff enforces the writ. Foreclosures carry the same trap plus one more: former owners and their tenants may have federal Protecting Tenants at Foreclosure Act rights \u2014 that law was made permanent in 2018, and it gives a bona fide tenant a minimum 90-day notice to vacate \u2014 so a bank REO \"trash-out\" order is not automatic permission to lock someone out. When in doubt, you tell the client to come back with paper. That \"no\" has saved more locksmith businesses than any pick gun ever sold.\n\nLet's talk money, because bad pricing kills more shops than bad picking. As of 2025 the national all-in average locksmith job runs about $163, with most landing between roughly $107 and $242. Car lockouts run about $80-$150 in normal business hours and more after hours. House lockouts run $70-$200, climbing toward the top end at night. Rekeying a typical house runs $80-$150, with per-cylinder labor commonly in the $50-$100 range depending on the lock. Build your quote in two pieces: a trip charge that gets the truck to the curb \u2014 a modest fee layered onto a routine call, more like $150-$250 for genuine after-hours, nights, weekends, or holidays \u2014 plus the labor for the actual work. Quote it on the phone, in writing when you can, and honor it at the door. The scam that has poisoned this trade is the $19 lockout ad that becomes $350 on the doorstep with \"drilling fees\" invented on the spot. Do not be that guy. Transparent, fair, written pricing is not just ethics \u2014 it is how you get the repeat commercial accounts that actually pay your mortgage, because a property manager remembers the number you quoted.\n\nRepair-versus-replace judgment is where you earn the \"senior\" title. A sticky deadbolt on a decent Grade 2 lock is a five-minute clean-and-lube, not an $80 replacement \u2014 tell the customer that and you've bought a customer for ten years. A worn residential Kwikset SmartKey that keeps failing, though? Stop rebuilding it and sell the upgrade. On commercial, a mortise cylinder in a good mortise body is almost always a re-pin, not a rip-out. The instinct: fix what's serviceable, replace what's a liability, and always tell the customer which one you're doing and why. People can smell an upsell. They can also smell honesty, and honesty is the cheaper marketing.\n\nInsurance and bonding are non-negotiable, and they're two different animals people constantly confuse. General liability covers the damage you do \u2014 you drill the wrong cylinder, you scratch a $4,000 door, you break a customer's storefront glass. Carry at least $1 million per occurrence; most commercial accounts and property managers will require exactly that and ask for a certificate before they let you touch a building. A surety bond is different \u2014 it protects the customer against you, essentially guaranteeing you'll perform lawfully; several licensing regimes require one, and amounts commonly land in the $5,000-$25,000 range depending on jurisdiction. If you have employees, workers' comp is usually mandatory the moment you hire your first tech, and commercial auto on the van is its own policy \u2014 your personal auto insurer will deny a claim the second they learn the vehicle is a business tool. Get all four lined up before you take your first paid call, not after your first mistake.\n\nNow the truck, because a well-stocked van is a second locksmith you don't have to pay. Think in tiers. Entry/lockout tier: a full pick set, tension wrenches, a bump-key set, a pick gun, an under-the-door tool, a long-reach kit and air wedges for automotive, and a Pak-A-Punch or slotted try-out keys. Rekey tier: a pinning kit with a full range of bottom pins, a follower set, a plug spinner, and \u2014 this is the one rookies skimp on \u2014 deep bins of the two or three keyway blanks you actually cut all day (SC1, KW1, and your local commercial standard) plus a reliable duplicator and a code machine. Hardware tier: a rolling stock of Grade 2 deadbolts and levers in the finishes people ask for, a few Grade 1 commercial locksets, panic-bar parts, door closers, and strike plates. Automotive tier if you go that route: a quality key programmer, transponder and remote-head-key blanks, and \u2014 critically \u2014 access to NASTF's Secure Data Release System so you can pull key codes legitimately. Stock the fast movers deep and the slow movers thin, and track what actually sells off the truck each month so your inventory reflects your real jobs, not a catalog fantasy. Idle steel on a shelf is money not working.\n\nHere is the part most solo locksmiths sleep on, and it's the smartest move in this whole guide \u2014 fire door inspection as recurring revenue. NFPA 80 requires every fire door assembly in a commercial building to be inspected annually, plus after every install and after any maintenance that touches listed hardware. NFPA 101, the Life Safety Code, cross-references NFPA 80 as the inspection standard, which means hospitals, schools, hotels, and nursing homes are under an ongoing legal mandate to have this done \u2014 every single year, forever. That is not a one-off lockout; that is a subscription. And you're already the person on site who understands latches, closers, and hardware listings better than any general inspector.\n\nThe inspection itself is a defined checklist under NFPA 80 \xa75.2.4.2 \u2014 the standard spells out the minimum verification items, and the industry commonly packages it as an eleven-to-thirteen-point walk depending on how the edge-seal and signage lines get counted; the current published edition is 2025, though many AHJs still enforce the 2022. You inspect visually from both sides and verify, among other things: labels are present, clearly visible, and legible; no open holes or breaks in the door or frame; glazing, vision-light frames, and beads intact and securely fastened; door, frame, hinges, and hardware secured, aligned, and in working order with no damage; no missing or broken parts; the self-closing device fully closes the door from a full-open position; on a pair, the coordinator lets the inactive leaf close first; and the latching hardware actually catches and secures the door when it closes. Then the clearances \u2014 the one that flunks the most doors. Door edge to frame on the pull side must stay within the tolerances in NFPA 80 \xa74.8.4 and \xa76.3.1: the top and vertical edges run about 1/8 inch (\xb11/16 inch on many assemblies, with tighter limits called out for wood doors), and the bottom-to-floor gap must not exceed 3/4 inch. You also confirm no field modifications have voided the label (that after-market hole someone drilled for a wireless lock can fail the whole assembly), no auxiliary hardware interferes with operation, and edge seals or gasketing where required are present and intact. Document every door, every deficiency. NFPA 80 doesn't name a numeric retention period, but common practice \u2014 and what an AHJ expects to see \u2014 is keeping records on hand for the life of the assembly.\n\nTo get paid for this legitimately, get the credential. NFPA 80 doesn't mandate a specific certification \u2014 it requires a \"qualified person\" who by knowledge, training, and experience can evaluate fire door assemblies \u2014 but AHJs and facility risk managers overwhelmingly recognize two programs as proof: the Door and Hardware Institute's CFDAI (Certified Fire & Egress Door Assembly Inspector), which runs through the DAI600 course, and Intertek's own fire door inspector certification. Earn one, and you can walk into any hospital or campus, contract the annual inspection of a few hundred doors, hand them a compliant deficiency report, and then \u2014 because you're a locksmith \u2014 bid the repairs on every door you just flunked. Inspect, report, repair, come back next year and do it again. That's a route that pays whether or not anyone's locked out, and it's how the smart guy in this trade turns a truck and a pick set into an actual business.",sources:["https://www.aloa.org/certifications","https://www.howtobecomealocksmith.org/how-to-become-a-certified-registered-locksmith/","https://vortechpro.com/blog/locksmith-license-by-state-2026/","https://www.miamidade.gov/global/news-item.page?Mduid_news=news1751994849027515","https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/businesses/licenses-new-local-laws.page"],relatedGuides:["aloa-ladder","specialized-certs","institutional-ila"],tags:["DPS","Plug spinner","Pinning kit","Life safety","Credential","Duplicator","Mortise cylinder","Code machine"],aliases:["picked wrong way","picked backwards","pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","IBC","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","Ilco","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"covert-entry-overview",title:"Covert & emergency entry: the full technique map",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"Before you touch a single tool, get the authorization right \u2014 because everything below this line is the same skill set a burglar uses, and the only thing that separates you from one is paper. On a lockout, that means verifying the person hiring you actually has the right to enter: they own the property, they're on the lease, they're the registered vehicle owner. Ask for ID and proof of residency, write down what you saw, photograph the door before and after, and log it. Locksmith licensing in most states requires it, and it's your only defense if a domestic dispute turns into a \"he broke into my house\" complaint. On a sanctioned penetration test, authorization is a signed scope-of-work and a get-out-of-jail letter \u2014 a physical document, on the client's letterhead, naming the buildings and dates you're cleared for, listing what's in and out of bounds, and carrying a 24/7 phone number for the person who authorized it. You carry that letter on your body during the engagement. No letter, no entry \u2014 full stop. The rule that ties this whole guide together: never confuse capability with permission. Knowing how a lock fails does not entitle you to open it.\n\nThe mental model that makes you a professional rather than a vandal is the invasiveness ladder. You always work from the least invasive, most reversible technique toward the most destructive, and you stop the moment you're in. Ordered roughly: (1) verify it's actually locked and try the obvious \u2014 unlocked adjacent doors, latches you can bypass without a mark; (2) non-destructive manipulation \u2014 picking, bypass tools, under-the-door and latch-slip tools that leave zero trace; (3) decoding attacks that produce a working key \u2014 impressioning, master-key decoding, cloning a credential; (4) electronic and access-control exploitation \u2014 reader attacks, REX spoofing, controller wiring; (5) destructive entry \u2014 drilling the cylinder, forcing the door, cutting. Each rung up costs the client more, leaves more evidence, and narrows your options if you're wrong about the authorization. The discipline of the ladder is the whole job. A hack forces the door first; a pro exhausts the reversible options and reaches for the drill last.\n\nCategory one: lock picking and mechanical bypass. Picking exploits manufacturing tolerance. In a pin-tumbler cylinder, key pins and driver pins are stacked in the plug, and the plug only rotates when every pin gap sits exactly at the shear line \u2014 the seam between the rotating plug and the fixed housing. You apply light rotational tension with a tension wrench (bottom-of-keyway or top-of-keyway) and the plug tries to turn but binds on one pin at a time because no two pin chambers are perfectly aligned. Single-pin picking is setting each binding pin individually until it clicks to the shear line; raking is scrubbing all pins rapidly and letting a few fall into place by luck. Bypass is different and often faster \u2014 you skip the lock entirely. A latch slip or \"loid\" (the credit-card trick, industrialized) slides between door and frame to push back the angled spring latch on a door with no deadbolt thrown. A thumb-turn flip tool or J-tool reaches through a gap in a glass storefront door to physically rotate the interior thumb-turn. The Sparrows DDT (Double Door Tool) works pairs of commercial doors, slipping into the gap between them to reach the interior exit hardware. The defensive takeaway: picking is defeated by security pins (spools, serrated, T-pins) that produce false sets, by tight tolerances, and by high-security keyways like Medeco, Abloy, or Mul-T-Lock that add sidebars and rotating elements. Latch bypass is defeated by a thrown deadbolt, a latch guard plate over the gap, and anti-thrust/deadlatch plungers. If your door can be opened with a shim in three seconds, the lock was never the point of failure \u2014 the door prep was.\n\n[[img:diagram-pt-key|The correct key lifts each pin stack so the split between the key pin (red) and driver pin (blue) meets the shear line \u2014 then the plug turns. Diagram: Wikimedia, CC BY-SA]]\n\nCategory two: master-key and keyed-alike navigation. A master-keyed system puts a third component \u2014 a master wafer or master pin \u2014 into each pin chamber, creating two valid shear-line positions per pin: one for the change key, one for the master. That's convenience, and it's also the vulnerability Matt Blaze made famous in his paper \"Rights Amplification in Master-Keyed Mechanical Locks\" (IEEE Security & Privacy, March/April 2003; the earlier IACR preprint carried the title \"Cryptology and Physical Security\"). Given one lock and its legitimate change key, you can decode the master key one pin at a time by cutting progressively deeper test keys and probing which cut turns the plug \u2014 no special skill and no fancy gear beyond a few blank keys and a file, and the resulting master is undetectable in use. \"Keyed-alike\" is the simpler cousin: many low-security padlocks and cabinet locks ship on a handful of factory keyings, so one purchased key opens a whole class of hardware. On a lockout this matters because a property manager's master may legitimately open the door faster than picking. On a pentest it matters because if you lift or decode one janitor's key you may hold the whole building. Defense: use systems designed against decoding (Medeco's biaxial cuts and elevation, Mul-T-Lock's telescoping pins, or better, ditch mechanical master systems for electronic access control where \"privileges\" are revocable database entries rather than metal you can't recall). Enforce key control \u2014 patented, restricted keyways that a corner store can't duplicate \u2014 and audit who holds masters.\n\nCategory three: impressioning. This is the art of making a working key with nothing but a blank and a file \u2014 no picking, no disassembly. You dress the top of a soft brass blank flat with the file (a Swiss #4 cut leaves the fine, dull, corrugated surface that shows marks under very little pressure), insert it, apply firm turning tension, and rock the blank up and down. The binding pins press tiny witness marks into the brass at their positions. You file down at each mark, reinsert, and repeat; a pin that's cut to the right depth stops binding and stops marking. Pros use a 6-inch Swiss #4 cut pippin or round file and a good loupe, and they file only on the forward stroke with no pressure on the pull to keep the surface dull enough to read. It's slow and it consumes blanks, but it produces a real, permanent, cuttable key \u2014 which is exactly why it's a favorite on engagements where you need repeat access, and why it's a forensic concern: an impressioned key leaves a working key in an attacker's hand, not just an open door. Defense is the same family as anti-pick hardware \u2014 security pins increase false marks and frustrate the read, tight keyways and high-security cylinders make blanks hard to source, and paracentric keyways physically block the file stroke. If your locks defeat picking, they largely defeat impressioning too.\n\nCategory four: door and window bypass \u2014 attacking the opening rather than the lock. Most doors fail at the gaps and the hardware, not the cylinder. Under-the-door tools (a rigid rod with a cable loop) reach through the gap beneath a door to snag and pull down an interior lever handle \u2014 devastating against lever hardware with a big undercut. Latch shims defeat un-deadlatched spring latches. On outward-swinging doors, exposed hinge pins can be driven out. On glass storefronts, the mating gap between double doors or the thumb-turn is the target. Windows fail at the latch, the sash, or simply an unlocked one nobody thought about. The professional lesson: the perimeter is a system, and you're only as strong as the sloppiest opening. Defense: deadlatch plungers so the latch can't be loided, latch guards and astragals to close the gap, non-removable or set-screw hinge pins (or hinges on the secure side), door sweeps and thresholds that close the under-door gap against tools and REX-spoofing air, and lever handles with minimal undercut or a return-to-door design that a cable can't catch. Walk a client's perimeter and the bypasses announce themselves.\n\nCategory five: alarm identification and bypass \u2014 the sensor layer. You have to read the room before you cross it. Door and window contacts are magnetic reed switches; a \"door forced\" alarm fires when the magnet leaves the reed. Motion is usually PIR (passive infrared), which triggers on a moving heat differential across its zones \u2014 and the same PIR technology drives the Request-to-Exit (REX) sensor mounted inside over the door to unlock it for people leaving. That REX is the classic access-control weakness: because a PIR REX fires only when it sees both a heat change and motion, it can be spoofed from outside by supplying both. A puff of inverted canned air (which comes out very cold) or a hand-warmer taped to a coat-hanger and waved through the door gap \u2014 or even a sheet of paper slid under and flicked \u2014 can trip the interior REX and release the strike, no credential needed. Defense: this is why REX behavior should signal the panel to suppress a forced-door alarm rather than actively unlock the door; mount the REX back from the door and angle it up so under-door air and paper can't reach its cone; seal the under-door gap with a sweep or shoe; and prefer request-to-exit push bars or touch-sense bars over bare PIR where security matters. For contacts, use balanced/supervised magnetic sensors that detect an added external magnet, and put motion sensors in the interior volume rather than relying on a single defeatable line.\n\nCategory six: access-control exploitation \u2014 the wiring and logic behind the reader. The single biggest legacy weakness is the Wiegand protocol between reader and controller. Wiegand sends the credential in the clear over two data lines (Data0/Data1) \u2014 one pulse low signals a zero, the other a one \u2014 with no encryption and no authentication. A tap device like the ESPKey installs behind a reader in minutes, silently logs every badge's raw Wiegand string, and can later replay any captured credential straight into the controller \u2014 bypassing the reader entirely; it can even act as a stand-in door controller itself. Because the reader is on the unsecured outside of the door, the attack surface is literally hanging on the wall. Contrast with OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol) from the SIA, now also the international standard IEC 60839-11-5: run in Secure Channel mode it wraps reader-to-controller traffic in AES-128, authenticates the endpoints, and supervises the line so a tap or a cut is detected as a fault. That's the defense: migrate from Wiegand to OSDP and \u2014 critically \u2014 actually enable Secure Channel with real keys, because OSDP installed in clear mode is no better than Wiegand. Additionally, tamper-protect the reader housing, run reader wiring through conduit, and don't leave the door controller and its REX/strike wiring reachable from the insecure side or above an accessible drop ceiling.\n\nCategory seven: credential cloning and PACS reader attacks \u2014 the card in the pocket. Low-frequency 125 kHz credentials \u2014 EM4100, HID Prox, Indala, AWID \u2014 have essentially no cryptography; they broadcast a fixed facility-code-plus-card-number string (the ubiquitous 26-bit H10301 format is 8 bits of facility code, 16 bits of card number, and 2 parity bits) to anyone with a reader. A Flipper Zero or a long-range reader in a bag can capture a badge from a person standing near you, and a blank T5577 card acts as a universal rewritable blank that emulates any of those formats \u2014 read, write, done. High-frequency 13.56 MHz raised the bar: legacy iCLASS and MIFARE Classic are broken, but iCLASS SE, iCLASS Seos, and MIFARE DESFire EV2/EV3 use real crypto. The pro attacks there are subtler: downgrade attacks that read the PACS payload (the SIO, Secure Identity Object) off a higher-security card and re-encode it onto a legacy 125 kHz T5577 or Picopass clone the panel still accepts \u2014 which works precisely because most readers ship with iCLASS legacy/Prox fallback still enabled; weaponized long-range readers and tools like Doppelg\xe4nger; malicious readers with an HID SAM; and even a demonstrated relay attack against Seos (Loudmouth Security, 2023) that tunnels a live authentication over the internet with a pair of Proxmark3 RDV4s to open a door from half a continent away. Defense: kill 125 kHz Prox and legacy iCLASS/MIFARE Classic entirely; deploy Seos or DESFire EV3 with the site's own keys (not default/elite keys); disable legacy-format fallback at the reader and controller so a downgraded clone is rejected; add a PIN or phone-based factor at sensitive doors; and print no facility/card numbers on the badge face.\n\nCategory eight: destructive entry \u2014 the last rung, chosen deliberately. Sometimes the right professional answer is to destroy the lock: a customer standing in the cold at 2 a.m. with a lock you can't pick, a high-security cylinder that would take an hour, a safe-out lockout, or a pentest objective where speed beats stealth and the client accepts the damage. The textbook cylinder attack is drilling the shear line: center-punch a pilot just above the keyway so the bit can't walk, drill a small pilot hole with a hardened bit along the line where key pins meet driver pins, then step up to a larger bit to shred the pin stacks so the plug turns freely. You defeat a lock, not a door \u2014 replace the cylinder and the opening is whole. Other destructive routes: forcing the latch/deadbolt with a spreader, angle-grinding a padlock shackle or hasp, or cutting a hinge. The professional discipline is that destruction is a decision, made after the reversible ladder is exhausted and with the client's informed consent to the repair cost \u2014 never a default. Defense is layered hardening: hardened-steel anti-drill pins and drill plates at the shear line, ball-bearing inserts, a hardened cylinder guard and reinforced strike, and for high-value doors the whole assembly rated to a standard (UL 437, or safe/door ratings). You can't make destructive entry impossible \u2014 you make it loud, slow, and evidence-rich, which is often deterrent enough, and you back it with monitored alarms so \"slow and loud\" gets a response.\n\nTying the ladder to the two missions. On an authorized lockout your loyalty is to the fastest reversible open that leaves the customer with working hardware and no damage bill: try the obvious, pick or bypass, decode a key if the situation calls for it, and drill only when nothing else serves and the customer agrees. Alarm, access-control, and cloning skills rarely appear on a residential lockout \u2014 but reading a commercial alarm before you trip it absolutely does. On a sanctioned pentest the goal inverts: you're not just opening a door, you're documenting every rung an adversary could climb so the client can close them. You still work least-to-most invasive \u2014 because the quiet, reversible findings (a loidable latch, a Wiegand tap point, a clonable Prox badge, a spoofable REX) are the ones that prove the real risk and give you the most defensive value. In both jobs the through-line is the same: the authorization comes first, the least-invasive method comes next, and every attack you understand is really a map to the defense that stops it. That's the whole trade \u2014 you learn to open things so you can teach people how to keep them shut.",sources:["https://www.mattblaze.org/papers/mk.pdf","https://eprint.iacr.org/2002/160","https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1109/MSECP.2003.1193208","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Impressioning","https://unitedlocksmith.net/blog/a-beginners-guide-to-lock-impressioning"],tags:["iCLASS","MIFARE","REX","SEOS","Wiegand","PIR","DESFire","Secure Channel"],aliases:["request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","motion detector","motion sensor","false alarms","OSDP SC","encrypted reader","SCBK","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","picks open","won't set","line of separation","operating key","user key","day key","card reader","badge reader","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","anti-shim","plunger","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","prox card","key tag","card won't work","turning tool","tension tool","TOK","BOK","pick tension"]},{id:"alarm-systems-entry",title:"Alarm systems: identify and work around them (authorized)",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"AUTHORIZATION FIRST \u2014 read this before anything else. You do not touch an alarm system unless you have written authority to be on the premises and, on a monitored account, the account holder's credentials and passcode on file. A lockout customer who owns the home can authorize you at the door; a commercial site or a pen-test engagement needs a signed scope naming the address, the panel, and the hours. The single most important call you make before you fault a monitored zone is to the central station \u2014 you give them the account number and verbal passcode and put the account \"on test.\" That takes the system offline for dispatch so your work doesn't roll a police or guard response. Skip that call and you've committed, at best, a false-alarm violation and, at worst, something that gets you arrested even though you were legitimately hired. Everything below is taught so you can identify, work cleanly around, and \u2014 the real point \u2014 HARDEN these systems. Knowing exactly how a zone gets defeated is how you spec one that can't be.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-authorization-gate|Two kinds of authority, from two different people... and what to do the moment it trips]]\n\nStart by reading the building before you read the panel. Alarm architecture is layered: a perimeter layer (door and window contacts, glassbreak) and an interior/volumetric layer (motion, sometimes floor and ceiling coverage), all reporting to a control panel that decides, per zone, what a fault means and how fast. The whole game on authorized service is understanding that a \"zone\" is not a sensor \u2014 it's a programmed response type. The same magnetic contact on the front door can be an Entry/Exit zone (gives you a countdown to disarm), a Perimeter zone (instant trip, no delay \u2014 used on windows and back doors nobody should walk through), or an Interior Follower (only trips if an entry-delay zone was tripped first, so a legit user coming in the front doesn't set off the hallway motion). Read the door before you cross the threshold: which opening is the \"designed\" entry point? That's almost always the one wired for entry delay. Everything else is instant.\n\nThe workhorse perimeter sensor is the magnetic reed contact. Two ferromagnetic blades in a sealed glass capsule sit a few microns apart, held closed by a magnet mounted on the moving leaf (door or sash); the switch body sits on the fixed frame. Open the door, the magnetic field leaves, the blades spring apart, the loop opens, the panel sees a fault. Surface-mount contacts are the visible white blocks; recessed contacts are drilled into the top of the door and frame so they're invisible when closed \u2014 you'll only find those by looking for a small filled hole in the door edge or by knowing the install. On authorized work this matters because a recessed contact on a delayed door behaves exactly like a surface one; the difference is purely whether you can see it. DEFENSE takeaway: recessed contacts resist the old \"external magnet held to the frame\" bypass better because you can't easily locate them, and high-security installs pair the reed with a bias magnet or use a \"balanced/biased\" reed that trips if a foreign magnet is introduced \u2014 that kills the classic magnet-swap defeat.\n\nMotion sensors are where identification and defense get interesting. The standard interior detector is a Passive Infrared (PIR): it doesn't emit anything, it watches for a moving heat signature crossing between the wedge-shaped zones of its segmented Fresnel lens. That segmentation is why walking straight AT a PIR is far less detectable than walking ACROSS its field \u2014 you cross fewer boundaries. That's not a trick to teach an intruder; it's the reason a properly specified install aims detectors so the natural approach path cuts across the beams, and covers the entry door so anyone coming in is caught regardless of direction. Cheap single-tech PIRs are prone to defeat and to false alarms, which is why professional and commercial spec has moved to dual-technology detectors \u2014 a PIR AND a microwave Doppler radar in one housing, requiring BOTH to agree (an AND-gate) before the panel is triggered (DSC's LC-100-PI series and Optex's QX/WX lines both build these). Microwave sees motion and even penetrates thin materials; PIR sees heat. To fool a dual-tech you'd have to beat two different physics at once. Better units add anti-mask: an active near-field channel that detects if someone sprays, tapes, or blocks the lens, and reports a trouble/tamper \u2014 some, like the ProTech PIRAMID XL-HS, latch into a constant alarm condition roughly 15 seconds after the lens is masked and hold it until the obstruction is cleared. DEFENSE: for any space that matters, dual-tech with anti-mask and a tamper switch, aimed across likely approach, is the answer. Pet-immune lensing (ignoring mass under ~40\u201355 lbs, depending on model) cuts the false alarms that get systems left disarmed.\n\nGlassbreak detectors protect the window plane without wiring every pane. The good ones are dual-technology acoustic \u2014 they listen for the specific two-part signature of breaking glass: the low-frequency \"thud\"/flex of the pane bending and impacting (roughly 100\u2013200 Hz), immediately followed by the high-frequency shatter/tinkle (up in the several-kHz range), and BOTH must occur in the right sequence within a short window or it won't trip. Honeywell markets this as FlexGuard on units like the FG730. That two-part logic is what kills false alarms from a slammed door or a dropped pan. Shock/vibration sensors are the other family \u2014 mounted directly on the glass or frame, they react to the impact wave itself. On identification: acoustic glassbreaks are ceiling- or wall-mounted facing the protected glass, typically rated to about 20\u201325 ft and needing line-of-sight to the window; you'll see a small unit with a mic grille. DEFENSE: glassbreak plus perimeter contacts plus interior motion is the layered ideal \u2014 defeat the contact and the glassbreak still catches entry through the pane, and the motion catches you in the room. No single sensor is the line of defense; the layers cover each other's blind spots.\n\nEvery serious sensor and enclosure has a tamper switch, and every serious ZONE is supervised \u2014 this is the concept that separates a real system from a toy, and it's the heart of the defense. A supervised loop uses an End-Of-Line Resistor placed at the FAR end of the wire run, in series for a normally-closed loop. Values are brand-specific: Honeywell/Ademco Vista panels use 2.2K (2,000 ohm nominal), DSC PowerSeries uses 5.6K, and across the industry you'll also see 1K, 4.7K, and 6.8K depending on make. The panel doesn't just look for open vs. closed \u2014 it measures resistance. Roughly: the correct EOL value reads as \"normal/closed,\" a dead short (someone jumpering the wire to fake a closed door) reads near zero ohms and the panel calls it a FAULT, and an open (someone cutting the wire) reads infinite resistance and also faults. Double-EOL wiring adds a second resistor so one pair of wires can report multiple states \u2014 typically normal, alarm, and tamper/trouble \u2014 so the panel can tell a cut or shorted wire from an opened door from a pried-off sensor cover. The whole point: you cannot defeat a properly supervised zone by shorting or cutting it, because the panel is watching the resistance, not just the contact, and the tamper switch on the housing reports if you open the sensor to get at it. DEFENSE: if you're specifying or auditing, EOL resistors belong AT THE DEVICE, not tucked in the panel can where they supervise nothing \u2014 a shockingly common install error that reintroduces the short-the-wire defeat.\n\nNow, how entry actually interacts with a monitored system on authorized service, done right. You've verified authority and put the account on test with the central station. You identify the panel \u2014 not the keypad, the metal can, usually a roughly 16-inch enclosure in a closet, basement, or utility space. On Honeywell/Ademco you'll find a ~1-inch black PROM chip near the center of the board with a white label; the top line begins with \"WA\" and names the model and firmware \u2014 e.g. \"WA20P-9.12\" for a Vista-20P at rev 9.12 (the panel is also sold rebadged, e.g. ADT's Safewatch Pro 3000 = Vista-20P). On DSC, look for the sticker on the main board: it starts with \"PC\" for classic PowerSeries (PC1616/1832/1864) or \"HS\" for PowerSeries NEO (HS2016/2032/2064). Knowing the panel tells you the entry-delay behavior, default installer codes to AVOID assuming, and how zones are typed. When you fault the delayed entry door, you get the entry countdown (commonly 30 s default) and a keypad \"beep\" \u2014 you disarm with the user's code, not by racing it. If the space you must work in is covered by an interior-follower motion that only arms after an entry-zone trip, understand the sequence so you don't create an unexplained alarm. If you cannot disarm \u2014 no code available \u2014 you do NOT start defeating sensors; you call the monitoring station and the account holder. That's the professional line: on a monitored account, the correct \"work-around\" is credentials and coordination, not sensor defeat.\n\nPut it together as a defensive doctrine, because that's what a client is really buying from you. Layered detection so perimeter and volumetric cover each other. Correct zoning so the only delayed opening is the designed entry and everything else is instant perimeter. Interior followers so legit entry is easy but any OTHER path is instant. Dual-technology sensors with anti-mask on both motion and glass so no single physics trick works and blocking the sensor itself raises a flag. Full supervision \u2014 EOL resistors at the device, tamper switches on every housing and the panel can, and line supervision on the communicator (cellular/dual-path, so cutting the phone line doesn't blind the central station). And supervision of the humans: the account on test before service, verified passcodes, and a monitoring station that actually questions an off-hours open. Every \"attack\" in this guide has a clean defensive answer, and that's the job \u2014 you learn how a zone gets beaten precisely so you can hand the customer a system where it doesn't.",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/what-are-door-contacts","https://www.security.org/home-security-systems/door-sensors/","https://alarm.com/resources/what-is-contact-sensor/","https://www.dsc.com/?n=products&o=view&id=1015","https://www.homecontrols.com/Optex-120-Degree-Wide-Angle-Outdoor-PIR-Microwave-Dual-Tech-Motion-Detector-Anti-Masking-OPQXIxDT"],tags:["Tamper switch","PIR","Central station","Entry delay","Opening","Bypass"],aliases:["cover switch","tamper alarm","case open","motion detector","motion sensor","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"rfid-credential-cloning",title:"RFID Credential Cloning & PACS Reader Attacks",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"Before anything else, the rule that keeps you out of a cell: you do this on authorized work only. A lockout call where you can prove the property is the customer's, or a physical penetration test with a signed scope-of-work that names the buildings, the doors, the dates, and a get-out-of-jail letter you carry in your pocket. RFID cloning and reader tapping cross a line that lock picking mostly doesn't \u2014 you're touching a computer system and impersonating a person's identity credential. That's wire-fraud and computer-fraud territory if you freelance it. Get it in writing, keep the scope narrow, and log what you touch. Everything below is taught the way I teach an apprentice: understand the attack so you can find it on a site survey and kill it. The defensive takeaway is the point of each section, not a footnote.\n\nStart with the layer nearly every legacy building still runs on: 125 kHz low-frequency prox, the HID ProxCard II / H10301 family and its cousins (EM4100, Indala). Understand what these cards actually are and the whole thing stops being mysterious. A 125 kHz prox card is a coil and a tiny chip with no battery and no brain to speak of. Energize it in a reader's field and it does exactly one thing \u2014 it shouts a fixed number back, in the clear, forever, the same number every time. No encryption, no challenge, no nonce, no notion of \"who's asking.\" That's a 40-plus-year-old design meant to replace a magstripe, and it has zero defense against someone recording the number and repeating it. On the standard 26-bit H10301 format that number is a mere 8-bit facility code plus a 16-bit card number \u2014 that's up to 255 facility codes, and about 65,000 card numbers (65,536, one through 65,535) under each. It is, functionally, a barcode you can read through a wallet.\n\nThe tools make it trivial, and that's the security lesson, not a brag. A Proxmark3 (the RDV4 is the pro workhorse) reads a prox card with a command as simple as `lf hid read`, prints the facility code and card number, and writes an identical clone onto a blank T5577 chip with `lf hid clone`. The T5577 is the key detail: it's a chameleon 125 kHz chip that can be programmed to impersonate HID Prox, EM4100, Indala and most other LF formats, and a cheap bag of them plus a Proxmark turns any read into a working duplicate in seconds. A Flipper Zero does the same read-save-emulate cycle for common LF formats (EM4100, HID H10301, Indala) from your pocket, and can write to T5577 too \u2014 though emulation is flakier on some readers than a purpose-built clone. The honest boundary of these consumer tools is itself the defensive message: the Flipper's 125 kHz radio does nothing against 13.56 MHz iCLASS or Seos, and it cannot conjure a secret it never saw. Where you see a Flipper \"save but not emulate,\" the system is probably doing real crypto.\n\n[[diagram:credential-read-vs-clone|What a credential tool reads against what it can duplicate, and what each answer tells you about the system]]\n\nNow the crypto-card layer, where the story is \"old smartcards broke, new ones held.\" Moving to 13.56 MHz high-frequency didn't automatically buy security \u2014 MIFARE Classic and legacy iCLASS proved that. MIFARE Classic's CRYPTO1 cipher was reverse-engineered and cryptographically broken in 2008; the darkside, nested, and hardnested attacks let a Proxmark recover sector keys and dump a card in minutes to seconds. Legacy iCLASS was worse in one specific, catastrophic way: the master authentication key that every standard iCLASS reader on Earth uses to talk to standard-keyed cards was recovered by researchers (Garcia et al., \"Exposing iClass Key Diversification,\" WOOT '11, alongside the companion \"Dismantling iClass and iClass Elite\" work), and it has been public for well over a decade. Standard iCLASS derives a per-card key by encrypting the card serial under that global master and running a proprietary key-fortification function, hash0 \u2014 and the researchers showed hash0 is neither one-way nor collision-resistant, so once the master is known, the diversification is just obfuscation. \"Elite\" (a.k.a. High Security) keying gives an org its own custom master, which is better, but Elite systems have their own weaknesses and can leak the key with brief proximity to a legitimate reader. The unifying lesson: a broken cipher plus a globally shared secret plus a 15-year replacement cycle equals millions of cards that are effectively clonable today. If your site survey turns up MIFARE Classic or plain iCLASS on the doors, that's a finding, full stop.\n\nThe good news, and where you steer clients, is the modern HF stack: iCLASS SE and especially Seos. Seos is the current gold standard \u2014 credentials are secure-element based, use AES-128 with best-in-class mutual authentication (and ECC available in the underlying SIO processor for key establishment), carry a Secure Identity Object (SIO) that is cryptographically bound and firewalled, and they run a genuine challenge-response so a passive sniff yields nothing replayable. There is no consumer cheap-blank clone of a properly deployed Seos credential the way there is for prox. This is also why mobile credentials (Seos-backed NFC/BLE in a phone wallet) are a real security upgrade and not just convenience: the secret lives in the phone's secure element, the phone screen-locks and can be revoked instantly, and there's no plastic card to lose or hand across a counter to be read. The migration message to a client is blunt: SE/Seos or mobile is the destination; multi-tech cards (a Seos chip with a prox coil bundled in for legacy doors) are a transition crutch, not a resting place \u2014 that prox coil is still a plaintext barcode and undoes the crypto if you leave it live.\n\nHere's the trap that catches people who did upgrade their cards: the reader-to-controller wire. You can deploy flawless Seos cards and still be wide open, because the classic reader-to-panel link is Wiegand \u2014 a dumb, one-way, unencrypted, unauthenticated pulse train (two data lines, Data0 and Data1, held high and pulsed low, open-collector). The reader does all its fancy crypto with the card, then hands the panel the decoded card number as bare Wiegand pulses. That's where the ESPKey lives. The ESPKey is a postage-stamp logic tap you splice onto the Wiegand lines behind the reader \u2014 take the reader off the wall, land four wires (power +, ground -, D0, D1), and it passively logs every credential that badges through, stores up to 80,000 unique bitstreams in non-volatile memory, and serves them over its own Wi-Fi. Then it replays: it drives the same Wiegand pulses back down the line to the panel on command, which the panel cannot distinguish from the real reader because Wiegand has no supervision and no authentication. The whole class of attack \u2014 sniff-and-replay, or man-in-the-middle a chosen credential \u2014 exists because the protocol was designed in the 1980s to be simple, not secure. Cloning a fancy card is optional when you can just impersonate the wire.\n\nExtend that same \"capture the number\" idea outward and you get long-range harvesting, which is why 125 kHz survives as a target even when you can't touch a card. A standard prox reader has a few centimeters of range; a HID MaxiProx 5375 is a legitimate long-range parking-gate reader (HID rates it to roughly 6 feet, but that headline figure is with an active ProxPass tag \u2014 against a normal passive badge in a bag you get closer to 60 cm / two feet). Bishop Fox's original \"Tastic RFID Thief\" and successors like NetSPI's build and the Wiegotcha weaponize exactly that: a MaxiProx hidden in a bag or a car door panel, wired to a microcontroller that logs the Wiegand output the reader produces, with Wi-Fi and a real-time clock, and often a DIP switch flipped to silence the reader's beep. Walk past someone in a lobby, brush by in an elevator, park next to the smoking area \u2014 and you've captured a badge the target never handed over. It only works on the plaintext-shouting LF prox, which loops the lesson closed: the same cards that clone in one second on a Proxmark are the ones that capture at arm's length in a hallway.\n\nSo how do you actually defend, and what do you write in the report? Kill the two plaintext channels. On the wire, replace Wiegand with OSDP (SIA's Open Supervised Device Protocol) running Secure Channel. OSDP is RS-485, two-way, and its Secure Channel wraps the link in AES-128 so a spliced tap sees ciphertext instead of card numbers, and \u2014 this is the part people miss \u2014 it continuously polls the reader, so cutting the reader off to insert a device, or a reader going dark, is an immediate supervised alarm at the panel (the \"Supervised\" in the name). Wiegand's one-way open-collector design simply cannot do that; it can't tell a real reader from an ESPKey. But OSDP is not magic-by-default: Bishop Fox's \"Badge of Shame\" research (Dan Petro and David Vargas, DEF CON 31 / Black Hat) and their Mellon tool showed the failure modes you must check. The Secure Channel Base Key (SCBK) can be handed to a reader over the wire during \"install mode,\" and the ugly reality is that many controllers are left in install mode permanently \u2014 so a Mellon device splices in (they demonstrated it in about 60 seconds), requests the key, and gets it. Worse, some deployments allow a downgrade: trick the controller into dropping Secure Channel to an unencrypted or default-keyed mode. So the defensive checklist is specific: run OSDP with Secure Channel enabled and enforced (no clear-text fallback), take the controller out of install mode after commissioning and verify it's out, replace the default/guessable SCBK with a per-site random key, and confirm the panel actually alarms on reader tamper and loss-of-supervision \u2014 don't assume, pull a reader off the wall on the pen test and watch the console.\n\nOn the credential side, the same discipline: get off prox and legacy iCLASS/MIFARE Classic onto SE/Seos or mobile, and when you can't migrate overnight, at least practice facility-code and format hygiene \u2014 long, non-default formats (a custom 37-bit or larger format rather than the wide-open 26-bit H10301), a facility code that isn't a factory default, and active revocation of lost cards so a captured number goes dead. Physically, treat the reader as a monitored boundary: wire the reader's tamper switch back to the panel and actually watch it, mount readers so the back and the wiring aren't reachable without triggering that tamper, and run the reader-to-panel cable inside conduit or the secure side of the wall so there's no exposed Wiegand or OSDP pair to splice in the first place. When you walk a site, the tell is simple \u2014 a reader you can pop off the wall with a spudger, exposing two data wires and no tamper alarm, is the same finding whether the plastic in people's pockets is a 1985 prox card or a 2025 Seos card. Fix the wire and the credential together, or you've only fixed half the boat.",sources:["https://tagbase.ksec.co.uk/tutorials/clone-hid-prox2/","https://blog.kchung.co/rfid-hacking-with-the-proxmark-3/","https://github.com/RfidResearchGroup/proxmark3/blob/master/doc/T5577_Guide.md","https://docs.flipper.net/zero/rfid","https://docs.flipper.net/zero/rfid/read"],tags:["Reader","Credential","Cloning","iCLASS","SEOS","MIFARE","Secure Channel","Wiegand"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","key tag","card won't work","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","OSDP SC","encrypted reader","Data 0","Data 1","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","site code","FC","card range","wrong facility code","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","cover switch","case open"]},{id:"destructive-forced-entry",title:"Destructive & Forced Entry (The Last Resort)",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"First thing, before a drill bit ever touches metal: authorization. Destructive entry is the one place in this trade where \"I was pretty sure it was okay\" will end your career and possibly land you in a cell. On a lockout you verify the customer has authority over that door \u2014 photo ID against the lease or deed, a name on the utility bill, a property manager who can vouch, a landlord's written okay for a tenant unit. On a sanctioned pentest you do not put an eighth-inch bit to a cylinder until the rules-of-engagement document naming that address, that door, and that date is signed and in your bag. Get it in writing, keep it on you, and photograph the door before and after. Cutting and drilling leaves permanent evidence; the paper trail is the only thing that makes it lawful work instead of a burglary you performed in daylight. That is the whole ethic of this guide: you are learning how these attacks work so you can perform them cleanly when justified and, just as importantly, so you can look at a door and tell a customer exactly why it failed and how to make the next attacker's job miserable.\n\n[[diagram:destructive-entry-ladder|Climb down only as far as you must, and the restoration is part of the quote]]\n\nNow the judgment call, because that is what separates a locksmith from a guy with a drill. Destructive entry is a last resort by definition \u2014 it is what you reach for when picking, bypassing, decoding, or shimming would cost more in time and gear than the damage costs to repair. A standard Kwikset or Schlage residential deadbolt on a genuine lockout, tenant standing there with proof, no spare key coming? You will very often pick or bump it faster than you can set up a drill, and you leave the lock working. But a seized cylinder, a broken key snapped deep in a worn wafer stack, a lock someone superglued, a padlock whose core is rusted solid \u2014 those defeat non-destructive methods, and drilling becomes the honest, fastest, cheapest path. The discipline is this: you always run the cost-benefit out loud in your head. What does the lock cost to replace? What does the door cost if I force it? Which method leaves the customer secure tonight? If the answer is \"drill the ten-dollar cylinder and hand them a new one,\" you drill. If the answer is \"I'm about to wreck a four-hundred-dollar door and frame to save a twelve-dollar lock,\" you stop and go back to your picks.\n\nThe bread-and-butter destructive technique is drilling a pin-tumbler at the shear line, and understanding it is understanding the lock itself. In a pin-tumbler cylinder, spring-loaded driver pins sit on top of key pins, and the boundary between the rotating plug and the fixed housing is the shear line. The right key lifts every stack so the gap between key pin and driver pin lands exactly on that line, and the plug turns. Drilling doesn't pick that lock \u2014 it destroys the pins so they can no longer block rotation. You locate the pin chambers, which on most cylinders run in a straight line just above the keyway, and you drill along the shear line so the bit shears the driver pins off flush. Center-punch first so the bit doesn't wander off the brass, start a pilot with a small bit (about 1/8\"), then open it up. As you feed in you feel each chamber let go \u2014 a soft grab, then release, five times on a typical residential lock, six on many commercial ones, sometimes seven. Once the drivers are cut, a flat pick or a thin screwdriver turns the plug and the bolt retracts. Use cobalt or titanium-coated bits; a plain hardware-store high-speed-steel bit dulls on the brass and pins almost immediately. The defensive read here is blunt: a plain builder-grade cylinder offers essentially zero drill resistance, which is exactly why it is on every tract home in the country and exactly why it should never be the only thing between a real asset and the street.\n\nWhen you step up to a genuine high-security cylinder, the drill stops being a shortcut and starts being a fight \u2014 and it is designed that way. UL 437-listed cylinders from Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, and ASSA build in specific anti-drill countermeasures you need to recognize before you commit, because they change the whole economics of the job. The core defense is hardened steel: anti-drill pins and inserts sunk into the brass at exactly the points you'd want to attack. When your bit's tip catches one of those hardened inserts, the tip arrests while the shank keeps spinning \u2014 and the bit shatters. ASSA fronts the cylinder with a hardened anti-drill plate over an interactive side-pin design; Medeco leans on solid-brass construction with strategically placed hardened-steel inserts and a rotating-pin design where the key both lifts and rotates the pins, so the stack doesn't line up the way a standard one does; Mul-T-Lock's pin-in-pin telescopic pins add a second, nested pin to every chamber and complicate the whole geometry. On a safe or a serious commercial container you are looking at hardplate \u2014 steel that's been heat-treated to a much harder surface, sometimes layered, sometimes ball-bearing hardplate that spins loose bearings under the bit so it skates and snaps rather than bites, and at the high end diamond- or tungsten-carbide-embedded plate that strips the edge off carbide drills. The point of all of it is not to make you fail, exactly \u2014 it is to make you slow, loud, and expensive, and to buy the time an alarm response needs.\n\nAnd then there are the relockers, which you respect or you regret. A relocker is a secondary lock that fires when the primary is attacked \u2014 a spring-loaded bolt or pin that drops into the boltwork and freezes the whole door, independent of the lock you just destroyed. The nastiest to trip is the glass relocker: a sheet of tempered glass mounted between the door's steel drill plate and the lock body, with cables or linkages running from it to spring-loaded relocker pins spread around the boltwork. Punch or drill through and the glass shatters, and shattering releases those spring-loaded pins into the boltwork. Now the container is locked harder than before you started, and it typically cannot be opened without individually dismantling every fired relocker \u2014 a job that often means going in through the back or the side and can leave the container a total loss. The lesson for the attacker is humility: on a rated safe you do not freehand a hole and hope, you work from the manufacturer's drill-point diagrams and you know where the relock glass sits so you can avoid or defeat it deliberately. The lesson for the defender is that layered defense works \u2014 hardplate to break bits, relockers to punish the breach, internal shields that keep protecting the lock case after the drill plate is gone. That combination is why a real burglary-rated safe defeats casual attack: not one wall, but a sequence of nasty surprises.\n\nForcing and prying deserve their own hard warning, because they're the techniques an impatient or undertrained person reaches for and they are almost always the wrong call. You can pry a deadbolt, spread a frame, jamb-shim a spring latch, or wreck a strike \u2014 and sometimes on a sanctioned physical test that's exactly the finding you're there to demonstrate, that the door's weak point was never the lock but the one-inch screws in a softwood jamb. But as a service technique, forcing a door is where cheap jobs become expensive ones. Force it and you don't just kill the lock; you split the jamb, tweak the hinges, bend the strike, rack the door in its frame. Now you owe the customer a lock and a door and a carpenter, when a drilled cylinder would have cost them a fifteen-dollar part. So you rarely should. The professional move is to attack the lock, the cheap replaceable component, and leave the expensive structure intact. The defensive flip side is the single best hardening advice you can give any homeowner: the lock is usually not the weak point \u2014 the frame is. Most residential break-ins are kick-ins, and the deadbolt is often catching in a strike held by two short screws in one-inch pine. A box strike with three-inch screws driven through the jamb into the wall stud, a reinforced strike plate, longer hinge screws into the stud, and a solid-core door in a sound frame turns \"one kick\" into a project the average burglar won't finish.\n\nCutting shackles is the padlock version of the same last-resort logic. When a padlock's core is dead \u2014 rusted, painted over, key long gone \u2014 you cut, and the tool that wins is an angle grinder with a thin abrasive cut-off wheel, not bolt cutters. Grinders don't try to shear the metal, they ablate it, so a boron-alloy or hardened-steel shackle that laughs off bolt cutters still parts under a grinder \u2014 a standard shackle in seconds, a boron shackle in only a little longer, up to a minute or so on a thick or shrouded lock. Two things matter for a clean job. First, cut the shackle in two places, not one \u2014 cut once and the remaining stub binds in the body and you fight it; two cuts drop the whole top section free. Second, know that on most padlocks the shackle is the hardened part and the body is often softer brass or zinc, so on a lock where the shackle is the toughened bit, sometimes the smarter attack is the body or the retaining mechanism rather than the shackle itself. Sparks fly and the metal runs hot, so it's eye and ear protection every time, gloves, and mind what's downrange and downwind of the spark stream. The defensive takeaway your customer needs: a padlock is only as good as what it hangs on, and shackle exposure is the whole game. A shrouded or shielded shackle, a hardened boron shackle, a closed-shackle or hockey-puck body that leaves nothing for a wheel to bite gives you far more than a brass builder's lock \u2014 and no padlock survives an unhurried grinder, so the real defense is reducing shackle access and making the attack slow, loud, and visible.\n\nHere's the part beginners skip and pros never do: the job isn't the entry, it's leaving the customer secure. The moment you drill a cylinder, that lock is compromised and non-functional \u2014 pins sheared, plug wallowed out \u2014 and it is not something you rekey and walk away from. A drilled cylinder gets replaced, full stop; if drilling damaged the housing you're into a new lockset, not a rekey. So you never open a destructive job you can't close. Before the bit spins, you know what you're putting back: the matching cylinder or deadbolt in your van, the right backset and bore, keyed to a fresh key, and ideally keyed alike to whatever else the customer carries so they're not walking a ring of one-off keys. On a pentest the equivalent is your remediation writeup and, where scoped, physically restoring the door so the client isn't left exposed by your own test. The measure of the work is not that you got in \u2014 anyone with a grinder gets in. The measure is that when you drive away, the door locks, the key turns, the customer is as secure as before or more so, and the paper says you were authorized to do every bit of it. That's the whole trade in one sentence: get in clean, leave them secure, and be able to prove you had the right.",sources:["https://nationallocksupply.com/blog/medeco-vs-multlock-vs-assa-highsecurity-cylinders/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/locks-high-security/article/21163930/high-security-locks-what-makes-them-more-secure","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/best-high-security-door-locks/","https://surelockkey.com/blog/how-to-drill-out-a-cylinder-lock-safe-pro-methods/","https://dailyhandtools.com/how-to-drill-out-a-lock/"],tags:["Relocker","Boltwork","Hardplate","Shear line","Glass relocker","Strike","Backset","Cylinder"],aliases:["re-locker","relock trigger","glass plate","punch relocker","bolts","bolt work","handle spins","bolts won't retract","drill resistant plate","carbide plate","hard plate","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","tempered glass relock","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","2-3/8","2-3/4","distance to center","wrong backset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","lock barrel","key cylinder","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"social-engineering-tailgating",title:"Social engineering & tailgating (authorized)",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"RULES OF ENGAGEMENT FIRST. This one matters more than any other guide in the collection, because the human layer is where you can do the most collateral damage. You run this ONLY as a sanctioned locksmith or a physical penetration tester holding a signed scope, a named point of contact, and a get-out-of-jail letter \u2014 an authorization document naming the client, the buildings, the dates, and a live phone number that a real person will answer at 3 a.m. Carry it on your body every second you are inside. You are testing the client's system, not humiliating the receptionist who let you in. The person you pretext is a victim of your test the same way the lock is; treat them accordingly. Everything below is taught the way it is in the published physical red-team literature \u2014 Deviant Ollam's Keys to the Kingdom, Jeremiah Talamantes' Physical Red Team Operations (the REDTEAMOPSEC methodology), Kevin Mitnick's The Art of Deception on the pretexting and elicitation side, and the mainstream security-awareness and pentest bodies of work \u2014 at the conceptual, defensive level. The whole point of understanding the attack is to build the defense.\n\n[[diagram:rules-of-engagement-packet|The five things that must exist before you start, and the debrief that decides whether it helped anyone]]\n\nHere is the thing you have to internalize before you touch a badge reader or a lock: people are the softest access control in the building, and it is not close. You can spend a hundred grand on a mag-lock, a card system, and a mantrap, and every dollar of it gets voided the instant a friendly employee holds the door for a guy carrying two coffees. Hardware fails closed. Humans fail open \u2014 they are wired by a long stretch of social evolution to be helpful, to avoid awkwardness, to defer to apparent authority, and to not make a scene. Social engineering is just the disciplined exploitation of those defaults. Understand it as a system and you can defend it as a system.\n\nStart with pretexting, because it is the frame everything else hangs on. A pretext is a fabricated but plausible identity and reason-for-being-there that gives you a right to exist in a space. It is not a costume \u2014 it is a story with a job to do. The strong pretexts share three properties. First, they are low-status-to-verify and high-status-to-challenge: a delivery driver, an HVAC tech, a fire-inspection contractor, a temp from the staffing agency. Nobody wants to be the person who interrogates the exterminator. Second, they come with a prop that does the talking \u2014 a clipboard, a hi-vis vest, a toolbag, a badge on a lanyard, a box that needs two hands so someone has to get the door. Third, they carry their own reason to be somewhere they shouldn't: \"building management sent me to check the risers on every floor\" explains why you're wandering. The pretext answers the question before it's asked. Notice the discipline here \u2014 a professional builds a pretext they can hold under a follow-up question, not just for the first five seconds.\n\nUnderneath pretexting sits the psychology, and the cleanest map of it is Robert Cialdini's six principles of influence \u2014 authority, reciprocity, commitment/consistency, social proof (consensus), liking, and scarcity/urgency. Authority is the heavy hitter physically: a uniform, a title dropped casually, a clipboard, the confident body language of someone who belongs. Reciprocity is doing a small favor first \u2014 holding a door, handing someone their dropped keys \u2014 so they feel a pull to help you back. Social proof is \"everyone else badges through here and chats, so I will too.\" Liking is rapport built in the thirty seconds before the ask \u2014 a shared complaint about the parking, a compliment, a laugh. Scarcity and urgency compress the target's decision window: \"I've got a truck double-parked, can you just let me grab this?\" The tradecraft detail worth teaching is that attackers stack principles \u2014 authority plus urgency plus a prop \u2014 precisely to push the decision below the threshold where a person stops and verifies. The defense, which we'll come back to, is to reintroduce that pause on purpose.\n\nEliciting information is the reconnaissance arm of all this, and it usually happens long before anyone walks a door. Elicitation is extracting useful facts through what feels to the target like ordinary, pleasant conversation \u2014 never an interrogation. You don't ask \"what door system do you use?\" You say, at the coffee shop next to the building, \"man, my old job we had those awful badge readers that never worked in the rain,\" and let the friendly stranger correct you and tell you exactly what they run. People love to correct you, complete your sentence, show they're in the know, and fill an awkward silence. Feigned ignorance, a deliberate wrong guess, a shared grievance, flattery of expertise \u2014 these open people up. What a pro is really harvesting is the vocabulary and the org chart: the name of the facilities manager, the fact that the badge office is on 2, that Tuesdays are the cleaning contractor's day, that \"everybody just calls it the annex.\" Those specifics are what make the later pretext bulletproof, because nothing sells belonging like using the internal name for something.\n\nNow the door itself. Tailgating and piggybacking are cousins and the distinction is load-bearing for defense, so learn it precisely. Piggybacking is cooperative \u2014 the authorized person knows you're there and chooses to let you in, holds the door, badges you through, believes your story. Tailgating is non-cooperative \u2014 you slip through behind them in the closing gap, or in the crowd at shift change, without them consenting or even clearly registering you. (Heads-up on vocabulary: some references, including NIST, use the two terms loosely or interchangeably for the same threat \u2014 unauthorized entry behind an authorized person \u2014 so when you write a report, define which you mean rather than assuming your reader draws the line where you do.) The reason the split matters: awareness training and challenge culture attack piggybacking (they change what the human does), while physical barriers and sensors attack tailgating (they don't depend on the human at all). A program that only trains people has a stealth-tailgating hole; a program that only buys turnstiles has a friendly-employee hole. You need both, which is exactly what a good pentest report should surface.\n\nThe classic door plays, taught so you can recognize and defend them: the hands-full approach (a box, a tray of coffees, a stack of files \u2014 social pressure to get the door is nearly irresistible), the phone play (walking up mid-fake-call, distracted, gesturing at the reader, and getting waved in because interrupting feels rude), the pack-in at peak flow (morning rush or a smoke break, where the reader beeps once and six bodies move), and the \"my badge isn't working\" pretext at a door where a real employee will helpfully badge you through rather than watch you struggle. The badge/uniform/delivery pretexts all converge here: a plausible reason to be at the door plus a reason your own access should work but doesn't. The professional's tell is confidence and pace \u2014 belonging is performed, not stated. Someone who walks in like they own the place triggers nobody's alarm; someone who hesitates and looks around does.\n\nHere is where the guide turns, because understanding all of that is only worth having if it makes you a better defender. Every attack above has a specific counter, and the good news is the human layer that fails open can be re-engineered to fail closed. The single highest-leverage control is a genuine challenge culture \u2014 an organizational norm where any employee will politely, routinely stop an unbadged or unfamiliar person and say \"hi, can I help you find someone?\" This is the counter to piggybacking that no hardware provides, and the literature is blunt that it's an organizational change effort, not a purchase. It only works if leadership models it (an executive who cheerfully badges in and gets challenged, and thanks the challenger), if the policy explicitly removes the social cost \u2014 nobody gets in trouble for challenging a VP by mistake \u2014 and if \"help you find someone?\" is scripted as friendly, not confrontational, so the awkwardness the attacker exploits gets neutralized. You are teaching people to reintroduce the pause that the attacker's urgency was designed to erase.\n\nThe other controls stack behind it. Visitor control that actually works: every visitor signs in, gets a distinctly-colored temporary badge with a photo and an expiry, is escorted, and \u2014 critically \u2014 the absence of that badge becomes a visible anomaly anyone can spot. The value isn't the sign-in sheet; it's that a legitimate process makes the illegitimate one stick out. On the hardware side, access control vestibules \u2014 mantraps \u2014 are the direct physical answer to tailgating, and they're a recognized standard: NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 control PE-3(8) names access control vestibules and describes their purpose as preventing unauthorized individuals from following authorized individuals into a facility, calling out piggybacking and tailgating by name as the threat. The mechanism is two sets of interlocking doors with a containment space between; the interlock limits how many people enter at once and holds them in the containment area while authorization is verified, and the outer door must close before the inner opens. Occupancy sensing can hold both doors if it detects more than one body inside. Optical turnstiles do the lighter version. Anti-passback is the complementary logical control: a credential can't be used to enter again without a matching exit, which kills badge-sharing and pass-back-the-card tricks \u2014 but understand its limit, taught explicitly in the sources: anti-passback governs the logical use of a badge, and a tailgater never touches a credential at all, so it does not observe a body following a legitimate badge-in and is not a tailgating fix by itself. The newest layer is badge-to-body reconciliation \u2014 computer vision on the existing entry camera counting bodies through the threshold and flagging when the body count exceeds the credentials presented, no facial recognition required \u2014 which finally puts a sensor on the stealth-tailgating gap that training can't reach.\n\nPut numbers on why leadership should care, since a pentest report lands better with them. IBM's 2025 Cost of a Data Breach report found that physical security compromises have factored into roughly 9% of successful attacks since 2020; a Boon Edam survey of end users found 71% of security professionals rate a tailgating breach likely-to-very-likely at their own facility; and IBM put the 2025 mean time to identify and contain a breach at 241 days \u2014 its lowest in nine years, but still meaning an unaccounted-for entry can hide inside the system the better part of a year. (Treat the survey figure as a vendor-sponsored data point rather than peer-reviewed research, and cite it that way.) The defensive takeaway, said plainly: the human layer is the softest control, but it is also the only one that can think, notice, and challenge in real time. Hardware buys you the pause; culture is what makes a person use it. A mature program does both \u2014 trains and empowers people to challenge (kills piggybacking), and installs vestibules, anti-passback, and body-count sensing (kills tailgating) \u2014 and then, once a year, hires someone like you, on paper and under scope, to prove it actually holds.",sources:["https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/final","https://csf.tools/reference/nist-sp-800-53/r5/pe/pe-3/pe-3-8/","https://grcacademy.io/nist-800-53/controls/pe-3-8/","https://intellisee.com/intelligence/tailgating-piggybacking-2026-threat-intelligence-access-control-blind-spot-nist-vestibule-badge-to-body/","https://www.ibm.com/reports/data-breach"],tags:["Tailgating","Anti-passback","Mantrap","Reader","Handing","Credential"],aliases:["piggyback","following through the door","door held open","APB","no re-entry","buddy punching","airlock","double door interlock","sally port","card reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"key-bumping",title:"Key bumping & advanced mechanical attacks",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"Rules of engagement first, and I mean it. Everything in this guide is for locksmiths working their own trade and for physical penetration testers operating under a signed, scoped authorization \u2014 a rules-of-engagement document that names the doors, the hours, and the client contact who can vouch for you when someone calls the cops. No written scope, no touching the lock. Bumping in particular leaves a subtle forensic trail but almost no obvious damage, which is exactly why an unauthorized entry with a bump key reads as burglary in every jurisdiction that matters. We teach this the way Deviant Ollam teaches it in \"Practical Lock Picking\" and the way the declassified CIA manual teaches picking \u2014 the physics and the defense, not a playbook against your neighbor's front door. If that framing bothers you, close the guide.\n\n[[img:diagram-bump-keys|A bump-key set \u2014 each cut to the deepest (999) position for its keyway. A sharp tap snaps the driver pins above the shear line for a split second, and light tension catches the plug. Photo: Wikimedia, CC0]]\n\nStart with what's actually happening inside a pin-tumbler lock, because bumping is just an exploit of the same shear line every other attack lives on. A standard cylinder has a row of chambers, usually five or six. Each chamber holds a spring, a driver pin (top pin) and a key pin (bottom pin). With no key in, the springs shove the driver pins down so they straddle the gap between the plug (the part that turns) and the shell (the fixed housing). That gap is the shear line. As long as a driver pin bridges it, the plug is locked to the shell and nothing rotates. The correct key lifts each key pin exactly enough that the split between key pin and driver pin lands right at the shear line \u2014 every chamber \"gapped\" at once \u2014 and the plug spins free. That's the whole game: get all the pin stacks to break at the shear line simultaneously.\n\nNow the bump. A bump key \u2014 often called a \"999 key\" because every cut is filed to the deepest bitting the code allows, the 9 position, hence 99999 or 999999 \u2014 is a blank for that keyway with all cuts maxed out. You insert it one notch shy of fully seated (pull it back a click, the \"minimal movement\" or \"pull-back\" method) so the ramped teeth sit just before each key pin. Then you strike the bow of the key sharply with a bump hammer or a capped screwdriver while applying a whisper of turning tension. Here's the physics, and it's genuinely Newton's-cradle behavior: the impact drives each key pin upward hard for an instant. The key pin slams into the driver pin above it and transfers nearly all its momentum \u2014 like the cradle where one ball hits a row and the far ball flies off while the middle stays put. The driver pins jump up above the shear line, the key pins (having given up their energy) stay low, and for a few milliseconds every chamber is momentarily gapped clean at the shear line. In that flicker of time, the light tension you're holding walks the plug around before the springs slap the driver pins back down. Done well it's one tap and you're in \u2014 no lifting each pin, no feedback reading, no skill curve like picking has.\n\nWhich locks are vulnerable? Essentially every conventional pin-tumbler cylinder with standard pins and a normal spring configuration \u2014 a huge share of residential deadbolts and knob locks, filing cabinets, padlocks, and a lot of commercial hardware from the big domestic manufacturers. If it takes a flat cut key and it's a plain pin-tumbler, assume it bumps unless proven otherwise. Wafer locks (many desks, cabinets, older cars) are even softer \u2014 they're trivially raked and often bumped. What resists bumping is not brand-name marketing but mechanism: anything that requires more than a simple vertical pin lift to open. That's the key insight you carry into the defense conversation.\n\nBefore we get to defense, a word on the \"advanced\" cousins, because a real physical red-teamer decodes and manipulates, not just bumps. Single-pin picking (the CIA manual's core teaching) reads the lock chamber by chamber: apply light rotational tension, and because no plug is machined perfectly, one pin binds first at the shear line. You push it, it sets with a tiny click and countertorque, you move to the next binding pin, and you've decoded the binding order by feel. Raking is the sloppy fast version \u2014 scrub a zig-zag pick while feathering tension and let pins set semi-randomly. Impressioning is decoding by marking: you seat a blank, torque it, and the binding pins scratch or mark the blade; you file the marks down, reinsert, repeat, and the key literally cuts itself to the lock with no picking at all. And then there's the master-key attack, which every facility manager should lose sleep over. Matt Blaze's paper \"Rights Amplification in Master-Keyed Mechanical Locks\" (published in IEEE Security & Privacy, 2003) laid it out cleanly: in a master-keyed system each chamber has TWO valid shear points \u2014 one for the change (individual) key and one for the master \u2014 created by adding an extra \"master pin,\" a floating spacer wafer that sits in the stack between the key pin and driver pin. If you hold a single legitimate change key to one lock, you can probe the master cut in each chamber one position at a time: cut a test blank deep at chamber one only, try it, and if it turns you've found the master's bitting for that chamber. A half-dozen or so test blanks and a file later you've reconstructed the whole facility's master key \u2014 no special skill, no special access, and, as Blaze stresses, no suspicious behavior at the lock and no evidence left behind. That's \"rights amplification\" \u2014 turning one user's credential into the admin credential \u2014 and the industry sat on the knowledge for the better part of a century before Blaze published it in the open.\n\nNow defend, because that's the job. Against bumping specifically, the cheapest real upgrade is security pins \u2014 spool, serrated, and mushroom driver pins. A spool pin has a waisted, cotton-reel shape; when the plug tries to rotate it catches on the false shear the spool creates, gives deceptive feedback, and disrupts the clean momentum transfer a bump relies on. Serrated pins do the same with a stack of little ledges. They don't make a cylinder bump-proof \u2014 a patient, practiced attacker still gets some open \u2014 but they raise the skill and repetition needed dramatically, and a re-pin with a high-security pin kit runs roughly $15\u2013$35 per cylinder in parts. That is some of the best dollar-for-dollar hardening a locksmith can sell. Bump-resistant designs go further with trap pins, sidebar interlocks, and sliders that a vertical impact simply can't actuate. Beyond that sit genuine high-security cylinders whose mechanisms defeat bumping by design, not just by making it harder: Medeco's angled, rotating pins with a sidebar that must be twisted into alignment (a straight upward bump can't rotate the pins to free the sidebar); ASSA's Twin 6000, which stacks a set of separate side pins and a sidebar on top of the six normal pin-tumblers so a vertical bump alone never satisfies the sidebar; Mul-T-Lock's telescoping pin-in-pin (an inner \"telescoping\" pin inside the main pin); BiLock's twin-sidebar, twelve-pin design (two rows of six pins, no driver pins to bounce, marketed as bump-proof); and disc-detainer locks (Abloy) that have no springs or driver pins at all for an impact to separate. Honest caveat \u2014 Marc Weber Tobias and Tobias Bluzmanis publicly broke several of these under lab conditions (their Medeco work at DEF CON 16 / HOPE in 2008 pushed Medeco to walk back its bump- and pick-immunity marketing), so \"high-security\" means \"raises cost, time, and required skill, and gives you real key control,\" not \"invulnerable.\" Pair the mechanism with tight manufacturing tolerances (loose tolerances give bumps and picks the slop they need), UL 437 / ANSI Grade 1 ratings, and \u2014 critically \u2014 patent-protected key control so blanks can't be walked into a hardware store and cut. For master systems, Blaze's own countermeasure guidance holds: the safest option is often to not master-key your most sensitive doors at all (use locked key-control cabinets instead), and where you must master-key, split functions into separate smaller master systems rather than one facility-wide one, use restricted keyways so test blanks are hard to get, and move your highest-value access to systems where the credential can't be reverse-engineered from one legitimate key. The defensive throughline for the whole guide is one sentence: conventional pin-tumbler locks are keyed-alike to the laws of physics, so if a door genuinely matters, spend on mechanism and key control, not on a heavier-looking deadbolt.",sources:["https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/what-is-key-bumping/","https://www.art-of-lockpicking.com/how-to-use-a-bump-key/","https://www.lockpickingforensics.com/bumping.php","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Bumping","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_bumping"],tags:["Shear line","Binding order","Shell","Security pin","Raking","Plug","Change key","Credential"],aliases:["picks open","won't set","line of separation","set order","which pin binds","lock body","cylinder body","anti-pick pin","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","operating key","user key","day key","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"master-key-attacks",title:"Master-key system attacks: privilege escalation",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"Rules of engagement first, because this one bites harder than most. Everything below is for locksmiths and for physical penetration testers working under a signed scope with the system owner's written authorization. The attack we're walking through \u2014 reverse-engineering a building's top master key from a single change key you were legitimately issued \u2014 is exactly the kind of thing that turns an \"authorized locksmith\" into a defendant if you run it on a system you don't own or have permission to test. Get it in writing. Know your state's key-duplication and burglary-tool statutes. This is a field guide so you understand the mechanism well enough to design against it \u2014 not a how-to for walking into someone's building. The whole reason it's worth teaching is that once you see how it works, you understand why over-mastered systems are structurally fragile and what to do about it. That defensive payoff is the point.\n\n[[img:diagram-pt-key|Each pin chamber holds a key pin (red) and driver pin (blue); a master system adds a third piece so the chamber opens at two heights \u2014 the change key and the master. Diagram: Wikimedia, CC BY-SA]]\n\nStart with the pin stack, because master keying lives there. A plain pin-tumbler lock has a plug that turns inside a shell, and a row of spring-loaded pin stacks that drop across the plug/shell boundary \u2014 the shear line \u2014 and jam it. Each stack is cut in exactly one place. Lift it with the right key and that single cut lines up at the shear line; every stack aligned, the plug turns. Wrong depth at even one position and you're blocked, either too low (cut still down in the plug) or too high (cut shoved up into the shell). The depths at each position are the key's bitting \u2014 that's the whole secret. Typical commercial locks run four to seven pins with four to ten possible depths each.\n\nNow master key one lock. The common way \u2014 the one Matt Blaze's paper \"Rights Amplification in Master-Keyed Mechanical Locks\" dissects \u2014 doesn't use special hardware at all. You just cut some or all of the pin stacks in TWO places instead of one. That introduces a second, extra cut in the stack, which means there are now two lift heights at that position that will put a cut at the shear line. One height corresponds to the change key (the individual key for that door), the other to the master. Do that across the stacks and the lock happily opens for two different keys: the change key and the master. Blaze's toy example: lock A cut so every stack accepts depth 1 or depth 4 opens to change key 11111 AND to master 44444; a second lock B cut for 2-or-4 opens to change key 22222 and to that same 44444 master. 44444 is the master that crosses both. In the trade this gets organized formally \u2014 Total Position Progression (TPP), where the master depth at each position never appears on any change key, and Rotating Constant (RC), where change keys share the master depth on a rotating subset of positions. Both build hierarchies: sub-masters, grand masters, and at the top the Top Master Key, the TMK, that opens everything.\n\nHere's the flaw, and it's not a manufacturing defect \u2014 it's baked into the concept. The pins don't talk to each other. There's no rule that says \"all change depths or all master depths.\" A stack that has two valid heights doesn't care what its neighbors are doing. So the lock will open for ANY mix \u2014 some stacks lifted to the change depth, others to the master depth. In Blaze's five-pin example, 11111 and 44444 both work, but so do 11114, 11141, 11411 and a whole family of hybrids. The lock is, in cryptographic terms, an oracle: you hand it a key, it gives you one bit back \u2014 turns or doesn't. And critically, you can test one pin position at a time, independently of the rest. Any cryptographer will tell you a system where you can verify one \"bit\" of the key in isolation is broken, because the work to crack it drops from exponential to linear. Blaze's own note is that this is the same weakness behind a famous character-by-character attack on the old Tenex password check, ported straight into brass.\n\nThe attack \u2014 the \"adaptive oracle\" procedure \u2014 falls right out of that. You hold one change key and its lock. You take that change key's bitting and, at position 1 only, you re-cut it to each possible depth in turn, leaving every other position at the known change cut. Try each in the lock. Every depth except the change depth is a hybrid probe: if the lock turns, the depth you just tried at position 1 is a valid shear point there, and since it isn't the change depth, it's the MASTER depth for that pin. You just read one cut of a key you have never seen. Move to position 2, repeat. Then 3, 4, 5, and so on. Position by position, the lock leaks the entire master bitting. If no alternate depth works at some position, that pin either isn't mastered or it's an RC system where the master shares the change depth there \u2014 so you just keep the change cut. Recover all positions, cut a fresh blank to that recovered bitting, and you've fabricated a working TMK without ever having touched the real one.\n\nThe cost is what makes this ugly. Naively it's on the order of the number of pins times the number of depths in blanks and probes. But the paper's optimization: cut between probes with a file and you only need ONE blank \u2014 cut the test position to the highest depth, probe, file it a step lower, probe again, walk it down. Total cost, Blaze's own figure, less than about two US dollars in the worst case. Blanks in his tests ran fourteen to thirty-five cents apiece (US$0.14 to US$0.35) from ordinary commercial sources found with a search engine, nothing restricted. And you're not doing anything suspicious \u2014 you're standing at a door you're allowed to open, opening it, which is exactly what your change key is for. No lock disassembly, no picking, no forced entry, essentially no forensic trace, and it can be spread across several interrupted visits. If the master cuts fall between the standard depths (older systems, or ones rekeyed off the standard), you just file in smaller increments \u2014 about five thousandths of an inch (.005\") at a time, same feel as impressioning. Blaze ran this against real Arrow and Best small-format interchangeable core, Corbin Russwin (system 70), Schlage, and Yale institutional systems, both factory- and field-keyed. It recovered the master every time. In his sample the Arrow and Best cores were fully mastered in TPP; the Corbin Russwin locks had three of six pins mastered in TPP; the Schlage and Yale systems were RC-based.\n\nThat's why \"over-mastered\" is a dirty word. Every extra layer of mastering, every additional pin you master, every additional door that shares the top master, widens the blast radius of one compromised change key. Sectional or multiplex mastering \u2014 where different key sections run different keyways but a single master blank crosses all of them \u2014 is the juiciest target of all, because one recovered TMK, cut onto the master-section blank, walks a huge population of locks that were supposedly separated; Blaze calls this out by name. And the recovery from a real compromise is brutal: you can't just change one lock. Every mastered cylinder in the affected scope has to be rekeyed and every holder reissued, which is expensive and slow, and every un-rekeyed lock stays wide open the whole time. Worse, you usually don't even know it happened \u2014 the attack leaves essentially no forensic evidence behind.\n\nNow defend. Blaze is blunt that the attack only works when master and change keys share ONE shear line, which is the case for the overwhelming majority of mastered locks. Designs that don't share a shear line resist it intrinsically: master-ring cylinders (two concentric plugs, two distinct shear lines, so aligning the change cuts tells you nothing about the master line) and bicentric / positional and dimple schemes where each lock uses a different subset of positions. Those aren't free \u2014 master-ring locks are actually easier to reverse-engineer by disassembly, and they've largely fallen out of fashion \u2014 but they break the oracle. The \"false cut\" countermeasure (adding extra decoy cuts to stacks so the attacker recovers many candidate masters, only one real) is the one simple mitigation Blaze says can frustrate the adaptive oracle, but it burns your available differs and, on a four-to-seven-pin lock, you usually can't spare them; treat it as a niche tool for limited applications, not a general fix.\n\nThe countermeasures that actually hold up in the field are policy and hardware, in this order. One: minimize mastering, up to eliminating it. Blaze's first-listed defense is simply not to master-key where you don't have to. This is the big one and it's cultural, not technical \u2014 most systems are mastered far deeper than the org actually needs because it's convenient. Every level you don't build is an attack you don't have to defend. Push clients hard on whether facilities really need a grand master or whether department-level sub-masters, or no mastering at all on the highest-value doors, would do. And separate functions into different, unrelated master systems so one compromise doesn't cross the whole site \u2014 Blaze names that too. Two: kill easy blank supply with restricted and patented high-security keyways \u2014 Medeco, Schlage Everest / Primus, Abloy, and similar. The point isn't that they're pick-proof; it's that the oracle attack still needs a blank to cut, and a patent-protected, factory-controlled blank that only an authorized dealer can order raises the bar from \"fourteen cents on the internet\" to \"commit fraud or fabricate by casting.\" Blaze is honest that a determined attacker who already holds a working change key can sometimes cast or mill a blank anyway, and that some \"restricted\" blanks leak through aftermarket vendors \u2014 so this is a serious speed bump, not a wall, and it only helps if you actually enforce the authorization chain and audit who can order. Three: serialized, tracked key control (the InstaKey-style model \u2014 every key serial-numbered, logged to a holder, with a documented what-does-it-open) so that issuance and return are auditable and a lost or over-privileged key is a known quantity rather than a mystery. Four: where key control genuinely matters \u2014 data centers, pharmacies, cash rooms, IDFs \u2014 seriously weigh electronic access control. An electronic credential doesn't have a physical bitting you can probe pin by pin; it can be revoked in seconds instead of a building-wide rekey; and it logs every use, which is exactly the forensic trail the mechanical oracle attack lacks. It's not a cure-all (mechanical override cylinders, tailgating, and credential cloning are their own chapters), but for the doors that hurt most if the master leaks, it removes this entire class of attack. The defensive takeaway in one line: the master key is a global secret hidden inside brass that every change-key holder is standing in front of every day \u2014 so hold as few masters as the mission allows, make the blanks genuinely hard to get and cut, and put your crown-jewel doors on something that doesn't have a pin stack to interrogate.",sources:["Matt Blaze, \"Cryptology and Physical Security: Rights Amplification in Master-Keyed Mechanical Locks,\" IEEE Security & Privacy 1(2):24-32 (March/April 2003); preprint (rev. 2 March 2003) https://www.mattblaze.org/papers/mk.pdf and IACR ePrint 2002/160 https://eprint.iacr.org/2002/160","Matt Blaze, \"Master-Keyed Lock Vulnerability\" overview and FAQ. https://www.mattblaze.org/masterkey.html","Deviant Ollam, \"Keys to the Kingdom: Impressioning, Privilege Escalation, Bumping, and Other Key-Based Attacks Against Physical Locks,\" Syngress, 2012 \u2014 chapter on Master Key Privilege Escalation. https://www.sciencedirect.com/book/9781597499835/keys-to-the-kingdom","Locksmith Ledger, \"High Security Through Key Control\" (patented/proprietary/restricted key blanks). https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10209382/high-security-through-key-control","Schlage, Everest 29 high-security keyways / key control overview. https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/everest-29-keyways.html"],tags:["Change key","Shear line","Shell","Bitting","Tailgating","Progression","Plug","Credential"],aliases:["operating key","user key","day key","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","housing","lock body","cylinder body","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","piggyback","following through the door","door held open","master key system","MK system","pin chart","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"team-based-physical-ops",title:"Team-based physical entry operations (authorized red team)",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"Rules of engagement first, always. Before anybody touches a door, you have a signed contract, a defined scope, and a get-out-of-jail letter that names the client's authorizing officer, the property, the window of operation, and a 24/7 phone number that answers on the first ring. This is authorized work only \u2014 sanctioned physical red-team and covert-entry engagements for a client who owns the building or has legal authority over it. If you can't tie the authorization back to someone who can actually consent on behalf of the property owner, you don't have a job, you have a burglary. Everything below is taught at the published, educational level you'll find in Deviant Ollam's work, the physical red-team literature, and the declassified lock manuals \u2014 the point is to understand the methodology well enough to run it cleanly and, more importantly, to defend against it.\n\n[[diagram:red-team-roles|Four roles, an abort word, and everyone carrying their own copy of the letter]]\n\nLet me tell you why the paperwork is the most important tool in the kit, and I'll do it with the story every pro in this trade knows. In September 2019, two Coalfire testers, Justin Wynn and Gary DeMercurio, entered the Dallas County courthouse in Adel, Iowa after midnight. They'd been hired by Iowa's judicial branch \u2014 the state court administration \u2014 to test the building's physical security. They carried a written authorization letter. And they still spent nearly 24 hours in jail on felony third-degree burglary and possession-of-burglary-tools charges, held on $100,000 bail. Here's the part that matters for you: the sheriff who showed up read that same letter and arrested them anyway, because he decided their entry method \u2014 shimming a door latch with a notched plastic cutting board after they'd found the door unlatched and closed it to test the lock \u2014 counted as \"force-opening\" a door, which he argued the contract didn't authorize. The contract allowed impersonation and access to restricted areas; the county's read was that forcing hardware and tripping the alarm went past it. On top of that ambiguity sat the deeper failure: the state court administration who hired them had authority to consent, but nobody had squared it with the *county* sheriff, and the county believed the building was theirs to defend. Charges got reduced to trespass that October and were dropped entirely at the end of January 2020, and years later the county settled a civil claim for $600,000. What failed wasn't their picking or their tradecraft \u2014 it was the chain of authorization and the scope language. The industry took two lessons. One: your letter is only as good as the people who've been told about it and the exact methods it names. Two: get authorization from an executive with real legal authority over the property \u2014 not a helpful IT manager who \"thinks it's fine\" \u2014 plus written notification to local law enforcement dispatch, plus a tested 24/7 contact you call before you ever step off, plus a scope that spells out permitted entry techniques in black and white. That's the difference between an assessment and an arrest.\n\nThe RoE document itself is where you kill ambiguity dead. It spells out what's in scope \u2014 which buildings, which floors, which entry methods are allowed (covert bypass, social pretext, or both) \u2014 and, learning straight from Adel, exactly which techniques are permitted, because \"physical access\" and \"you may shim a latch\" are not the same sentence to a responding deputy. It spells out just as loudly what's out of scope. You explicitly forbid the things that get people hurt or create real liability: no destructive entry unless specifically authorized in writing, no touching life-safety systems, no accessing genuine customer or medical data (you photograph the *exposure* and leave the data), no engaging or deceiving actual law enforcement, no physical confrontation with guards or staff \u2014 you break contact and produce the letter. You define deconfliction: how a nervous employee's 911 call gets resolved in ninety seconds by the client's contact vouching for you. You define hours, because midnight entry into a government building reads very differently to a responding deputy than a 2 p.m. lobby pretext. Sign-off on that document by the client, the property owner, and the team lead is what converts your crew from intruders into contractors.\n\nReconnaissance splits into two phases, passive then active. OSINT is the passive half and it's astonishing how much a building tells you before you're within a mile of it. LinkedIn gives you the org chart, job titles, who's new (new hires don't know faces yet and won't challenge a stranger), and enough vocabulary to build a credible pretext \u2014 a persona that \"belongs.\" Public filings, permit records, and vendor logos on the company site tell you who has legitimate reason to be inside: the elevator inspector, the HVAC contractor, the shredding-service driver, the fire-alarm testing company. Job postings leak the badge system and the security stack. Street-view imagery and real-estate listings hand you floor plans, door hardware, camera placement, and where the smokers' door is. You build a target package from all of it without ever exposing yourself.\n\nThen you case the site on foot, and this is where locksmith eyes earn their keep. You're reading hardware: is that a mortise cylinder you could bypass or a decent electric strike; does the crash-bar door have a latch you can shim or thumb through the gap; is there a request-to-exit motion sensor above the inside of the door that a puff of air or a coat-hanger under the frame will trip; are the hinges pinned on the outside; is the drop ceiling continuous over the demising wall so the wall doesn't actually reach the deck. You note guard rotations, badge-reader models, tailgating culture at the main doors, which entrance the caterers use, when the cleaning crew arrives. You do it in short, boring, forgettable visits \u2014 a coffee across the street, a walk-through of the lobby to \"wait for someone,\" a delivery that goes to the wrong suite. Casing is patience. The best entry is one you already understood completely from the sidewalk.\n\nPlanning turns the recon into a run of play, with a primary plan, a backup, and an abort. You pick the vector: covert (nobody ever knows you were there \u2014 you bypass, photograph the objective, and leave the environment as you found it) or overt-pretext (you walk in as the fire inspector and rely on social permission), and often a blend where a pretext gets you past the lobby and quiet bypass gets you through the locked doors beyond it. You set the objective concretely \u2014 reach the server room and leave a marker, plug a drop box into a network jack, photograph the CEO's unlocked workstation, lift a document from the \"confidential shred\" bin \u2014 because a vague objective produces a vague report. You define go/no-go triggers, rally points, and the exact wording of the story you'll tell if challenged, because a pretext that changes under questioning is a pretext that's already failed.\n\nTeam roles are what separate a professional crew from one talented individual gambling. The entry operator is the one with hands on the hardware and the nerve to do slow, quiet work while exposed \u2014 the technical skills, the picks, the shims, the under-the-door tools, the practiced pretext delivery. Overwatch is positioned to see the approaches and the responders, calling movement over comms: \"guard just left the desk, you've got four minutes,\" \"vehicle pulling into the lot, hold.\" Comms discipline is its own role \u2014 an earpiece, brevity codes, a check-in cadence, and a hard rule that a missed check-in means everyone moves to the rally point. The social operator works the human layer, holding a conversation with the receptionist so she isn't watching the hallway, or being the friendly \"new contractor\" who gets waved through. And there's a role people forget: the person holding the authorization letter and the client's phone number ready to dial, whose entire job in a bad moment is to de-escalate and produce proof \u2014 the exact role that, at Adel, still wasn't enough when the scope language was contested, which is why you fix the scope up front. On a two-person shop these roles double up, but the *functions* still all have to be covered.\n\nThe entry itself is anticlimactic when it's done right, and that's the goal \u2014 no drama, no damage, no trace. Tailgating is the single most reliable technique in the literature because it beats the best lock in the building by exploiting the person holding the door: you follow a badged employee through, arms full of boxes or coffee, and social courtesy does the rest. Piggybacking is the consenting version \u2014 the employee holds the door because you look like you belong. Past the lobby you fall back on covert bypass of the interior doors, and the whole time you obey the non-destructive rule the community holds to: bypass, don't break \u2014 and only using the methods your RoE actually named. You reach the objective, you *prove* it \u2014 photos, the drop box, the marker left in place, the document \u2014 and you document your path so the client can see exactly which control failed. Then the most disciplined part: you leave the environment exactly as you found it, relock what you bypassed, and exfil clean. Exfil is planned as carefully as entry \u2014 a rehearsed reason to be leaving, a route that avoids the cameras you can't avoid on the way in, and a clean handoff to the vehicle. A sloppy exfil burns an otherwise perfect operation.\n\nNow the part that actually pays the client: the report and debrief. The entry was the fun; the report is the *product*. You reconstruct the whole engagement as a narrative \u2014 here's the OSINT that leaked, here's the door that shimmed, here's the RTE sensor we tripped, here's the tailgate that worked at 8:05 a.m. because the badge line was backed up and nobody wanted to be the person who slammed the door. Every finding gets a photo, a root cause, a severity, and a concrete, prioritized remediation the client can actually fund: fix the strike, pin the hinges, install a vestibule at the main entry, run tailgating-awareness training, add badge-to-body reconciliation on the secure floor. You rank by risk, not by how clever the bypass was. And you do a live debrief with the client, walking the building with them, because watching your team demonstrate the shim on the door they swore was secure changes budgets in a way a PDF never will. The debrief is also where you re-cover the responders and staff who did their jobs, so the assessment strengthens the security culture instead of humiliating it.\n\nWhich brings it back to defense, because that's the whole point of learning any of this. The counters are known and they layer. Against the paperwork failure: multi-level authorization, notified law enforcement, tested 24/7 contacts, and unambiguous scope language \u2014 the whole Adel mess was scope and notification, not tradecraft. Against tailgating and piggybacking \u2014 the technique that beats your locks \u2014 the strong controls are physical: access-control vestibules and mantraps with interlocking doors where the outer door must close and lock before the inner one unlocks (NIST SP 800-53 rev. 5 names this as control PE-3(8), Access Control Vestibules, precisely to stop unauthorized people following authorized ones in), optical turnstiles and overhead stereo-vision sensors that detect two bodies on one badge scan, and security revolving doors at the highest-value entries. Understand that anti-passback does *not* stop tailgating \u2014 a follower behind a valid badge generates no violation, because the credential sequence still looks correct \u2014 so the modern layer is badge-to-body reconciliation, matching credential events to actual occupancy on video. Against covert bypass: better hardware, pinned hinges, RTE sensors that can't be tripped from outside, seismic and door-position sensors, and camera coverage of the doors that matter. And against all of it, the cheapest control there is \u2014 a security culture where a courteous employee feels empowered to say \"I don't recognize you, can I badge you in at the desk?\" That single sentence defeats more sophisticated entries than any turnstile. That's what a good red team is really selling: not the thrill of the break-in, but the evidence that makes the client fix the door \u2014 and train the person holding it \u2014 before someone who didn't sign an RoE walks through.",sources:["https://cyberscoop.com/coalfire-security-pros-arrested-for-breaking-into-iowa-courthouse-are-still-bitter/","https://krebsonsecurity.com/2020/01/iowa-prosecutors-drop-charges-against-men-hired-to-test-their-security/","https://www.theregister.com/2020/01/31/dumb_charges_dropped_in_iowa/","https://www.darkreading.com/cybersecurity-operations/county-pays-600k-wrongfully-jailed-pen-testers","https://www.cnbc.com/2019/11/12/iowa-paid-coalfire-to-pen-test-courthouse-then-arrested-employees.html"],tags:["Tailgating","Bypass","Anti-passback","Mortise cylinder","Electric strike","Credential","Strike","Reader"],aliases:["piggyback","following through the door","door held open","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","APB","no re-entry","buddy punching","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"ca-licensing-bsis-cslb",title:"Getting Legal in California: BSIS, the CSLB, and the Paper That Lets You Work",module:"Licensing & Compliance (California)",body:"Before you cut a single key for money in California, understand this: there are two different state agencies that care what you're doing, and they don't talk to each other. One is BSIS, the Bureau of Security and Investigative Services. The other is the CSLB, the Contractors State License Board. You will deal with both, and confusing them is the fastest way to sound green in an interview or catch a stop-work notice on a job.\n\n[[diagram:two-agencies|Two agencies, two questions, and one job can cross the line in an afternoon]]\n\nStart with BSIS, because that's the agency that says you're allowed to be a locksmith at all. California is one of the few states that licenses locksmiths directly, and BSIS runs it. There are two pieces to it. The first is the Locksmith Company license \u2014 that's the business itself, and the application has to name the individual who will manage that business day to day. The second is the Locksmith Employee registration \u2014 that's you, the individual working under that company. Every locksmith who touches a customer's locks needs to be registered, not just the owner. If Metro hires you, you're getting registered as an employee whether or not you ever own a shop.\n\nRegistration is a background check, plain and simple. You submit the application and the fee, get Live Scan fingerprinted, and BSIS runs you through both the California DOJ and the FBI. One detail trips people up: submit the application before you go do the Live Scan, not after \u2014 do it backwards and you'll be starting the clock over. New employees are issued a 120-day temporary registration so you can legally work while the full check clears, but that temporary card is off the table if you've got a criminal conviction in your record. File the whole thing through BreEZe, the state's online portal; it shaves a couple of weeks off the mail route.\n\nNow the CSLB, and here's where people blur the line. BSIS says you can be a locksmith. The CSLB says you can be a contractor. And the line between the two is money. Any single job \u2014 or a whole project at one site \u2014 where labor and materials together add up over the state threshold requires a contractor's license, not just your BSIS card. For years that threshold was five hundred dollars, and you'll still hear old-timers quote it that way. As of January 1, 2025, Assembly Bill 2622 raised it to one thousand dollars. Under a grand at one site, your locksmith registration carries the work. A thousand or more, you're doing contractor's work, and you'd better be licensed for it or working under someone who is. Know the current number, because quoting the old five hundred out loud dates you instantly.\n\nThere's more than one way to hold that contractor's license. The one built for our trade is the C-28, Lock and Security Equipment. It's the full classification, earned with a trade exam and four years of journeyman-level experience. If a shop doesn't want the whole C-28, there's a narrower door: the C-61 Limited Specialty, carried with a D-16 endorsement for Hardware, Locks, and Safes. The C-61 is the only classification that carries no trade exam, which is exactly why some outfits take that route \u2014 but it's limited to precisely what the endorsement spells out, and nothing more.\n\nWhichever license the shop holds, the CSLB wants security that the work stands behind the customer. Every California contractor license requires a $25,000 contractor's bond \u2014 and that figure jumped from fifteen thousand to twenty-five on January 1, 2023, so don't quote the old number any more than you'd quote the old threshold. On top of the bond comes insurance: general liability to cover the damage you might do to a customer's building or property, and workers' compensation the moment the shop has employees. Workers' comp isn't a nice-to-have in California \u2014 with employees on the payroll it's the law, full stop.\n\nThe last piece is the one people forget until a city inspector reminds them: local permits. A state license does not exempt you from your own town. Here in the Tracy and greater San Joaquin County area, you'll need a city or county business license just to operate, and depending on the work \u2014 alarms especially \u2014 a local police or alarm permit on top of it. In a lot of jurisdictions, installing a monitored alarm means the customer, and sometimes the installer, registers with the police department so the false-alarm ordinance has someone to bill when the thing trips at two in the morning.\n\nPut it together and the picture is simple, even when the paperwork isn't. BSIS makes you a locksmith. The CSLB makes you a contractor once the job gets big enough. A bond and insurance stand behind your work. And the city lets you hang the shingle. Know which piece answers which question, in that order, and you'll sound like someone who has actually run the trade \u2014 not just turned a wrench for it.",sources:["https://www.bsis.ca.gov/forms_pubs/locksmith_fact.shtml","https://www.bsis.ca.gov/industries/locksmith.shtml","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/contractors/maintain_license/bond_information/bond_requirements.aspx","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C28","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB2622"],tags:["CSLB","Contractor's bond","Workers' compensation"],aliases:["contractors license","California license","surety bond","license bond","$25000 bond","workers comp","comp insurance","employee injury"]},{id:"ca-scope-c28-c7",title:"C-28 vs C-7: What Each License Actually Lets You Touch",module:"Licensing & Compliance (California)",body:"Two contractor classifications matter to a security tech in California, and knowing exactly where the line falls between them is what separates a pro from a parts-changer. One is the C-28, Lock and Security Equipment. The other is the C-7, Low Voltage Systems. They overlap right in the middle of modern access control, which is precisely why you need to know what each one actually authorizes.\n\n[[diagram:c28-c7-boundary|Where the two classifications overlap, and the exclusion they both share]]\n\nStart with the C-28, because it's home turf. On paper, a Lock and Security Equipment contractor evaluates, installs, maintains, and repairs all doors and door assemblies, gates, locks and locking devices, panic and fire-rated exit devices, manual and automatic gate and door closers and releases, jail and prison locking devices, and permanently installed or built-in safes and vaults. That's the mechanical heart of the trade, and none of it should surprise you. But read the rest of the classification, because it reaches further than most people think. It also covers master key systems, metal window guards, security doors, and \u2014 here's the part that catches people off guard \u2014 card-activated and electronic access control systems, the control equipment behind them, motion and other types of detectors, and the computer systems that control and audit those systems. In plain terms: a C-28 can install an electric strike, a card reader, a maglock, and the panel that runs them, as long as it's security access control.\n\nThen comes the one hard limit, and you should be able to recite it cold: fire alarm systems are specifically not included in the C-28. That carve-out is written directly into the classification. The moment a job crosses into fire detection and alarm, you are outside your license \u2014 that's C-10 electrical or C-16 fire protection territory. Access control that releases a door on a fire signal is a coordinated gray zone: you do the locking hardware, the fire-alarm contractor does the alarm, and the two systems shake hands at the relay.\n\nNow the C-7. A Low Voltage Systems contractor \u2014 the older title was communication and low voltage \u2014 installs, services, and maintains communication and low-voltage systems that are energy-limited and do not exceed 91 volts. The classic list: telephone systems, sound systems, cable television, closed-circuit video \u2014 your CCTV cameras \u2014 satellite dish antennas, instrumentation and temperature controls, and low-voltage landscape lighting. The rule of thumb: if it's a signal running on skinny wire under ninety-one volts, it tends to live under the C-7.\n\nThe C-7 has its own fire-alarm carve-out, and it's worth memorizing right alongside the C-28's, because they mirror each other: low-voltage fire alarm systems are specifically not included in the C-7 either. Read that twice. Neither of your two natural classifications lets you do fire alarm. Burn it in, because the day an owner assumes you can and you can't, it's your name on the permit.\n\nSo where do the two meet? Electronic access control and video surveillance. A card-access system is security equipment, which is C-28, and it's also a low-voltage communication system, which is C-7. CCTV is squarely C-7, but it's sold on the very same job as the locks and readers. This is why a real security integrator usually carries both classifications \u2014 so nothing on the bid falls outside the license. When Metro sends you out to wire a reader, run camera cable, and set an electric strike, the honest breakdown is that the reader and the strike ride the C-28 and the camera run rides the C-7, and a shop doing the whole package wants both tickets in the drawer.\n\nWhy does any of this matter to you, turning a wrench? Two reasons. First, liability. Work outside your classification and the bond, the insurance, and the CSLB all have a problem with you \u2014 and so does the customer's insurer the day something goes wrong and a claims adjuster reads the license. Second, it's the language of the trade at the level Metro is hiring for. When an owner asks in an interview whether you can \"do access control,\" the sharp answer isn't just \"yes.\" It's: \"Yes \u2014 the reader, strike, maglock, and panel are C-28 access control, and if you want cameras or intercom on the same job that's C-7, and neither of us is touching the fire alarm.\" Say that, and he knows you understand not just the hardware, but the lines the state drew around it.\n\nBoth classifications live in the same rulebook, by the way \u2014 the California Code of Regulations, Title 16, Division 8, Article 3 \u2014 if you ever want to read the exact words the board will hold you to.",sources:["https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C28","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C-7","https://www2.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/GuidesAndPublications/DescriptionOfClassifications.pdf"],tags:["Maglock","Electric strike","Reader","CSLB","Strike","Relay"],aliases:["mag lock","magnetic lock","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","Securitron","door holds shut","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","contractors license","state license board","California license","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close"]},{id:"video-surveillance",title:"Video surveillance: IP vs analog, NVR/DVR, PoE & camera choice",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Two worlds still coexist on the truck, so learn both. The old world is analog-over-coax, and the modern world is IP-over-Ethernet. Don't let anybody tell you analog is dead. Traditional analog was 480 lines and looked like garbage, but the industry gave it a second life with HD-over-coax: HD-TVI (Hikvision's baby), HD-CVI (Dahua's), and AHD (an open-ish standard out of Korea). All three push 1080p and up \u2014 modern chipsets go to 4K/8MP over the same RG59 that's already in the wall. Frame rate is the honest tell between the two worlds, though, and it is the number to check before you promise a customer 4K on their existing coax: 4K over coax often tops out near 7 fps, while 4K over IP holds 30. That is why IP still wins anywhere motion matters, a register or a doorway, no matter how good the coax resolution spec looks on the box. The pitch is simple: a customer has coax runs from a 1990s system, they want HD, and you don't want to re-pull cable through a finished building. You swap the cameras and the recorder, keep the coax, done. The gotcha: TVI, CVI, and AHD are proprietary and generally don't talk to each other. A camera and recorder from mismatched formats will give you no picture or a black-and-white roll. Buy \"tribrid\" or \"penta-brid\" DVRs that auto-detect all formats plus legacy CVBS plus one IP channel, and you stop caring which flavor the camera is.\n\n[[diagram:video-ip-vs-analog|Coax against Ethernet, where the intelligence sits, and the 100 m wall]]\n\nIP is where new work lives. The camera is a little networked computer with its own IP address; it encodes video onboard and streams it over Cat5e/6. Upside: resolution has no ceiling (4K, 8MP, multisensor 180/360 units), you get one cable for both power and data, and you can run analytics \u2014 line-crossing, loitering, license plate, face \u2014 on the camera or the recorder. That's why Axis and Avigilon shops are pure IP. Downside: it's a network, so now you own IP conflicts, switch config, and firmware.\n\nThat splits your recorders. A DVR (Digital Video Recorder) takes coax/BNC analog-HD inputs and does the encoding inside the box \u2014 cameras are dumb, the DVR is the brain. An NVR (Network Video Recorder) takes finished IP streams over Ethernet; the cameras already encoded, the NVR just records and manages. The practical tell: DVR has a wall of BNC connectors on the back, NVR has RJ45s. Most NVRs sold for small jobs are \"PoE NVRs\" with a built-in PoE switch \u2014 4, 8, or 16 ports right on the back \u2014 so you patch each camera straight into the recorder and it powers them. Clean for a house or small shop. For anything bigger you separate the switch from the recorder.\n\nWhich brings us to power. Power over Ethernet sends DC down the same Cat cable as the data, so no separate power run and no electrician for low-voltage. Know the tiers cold: **802.3af (PoE)** delivers 15.4W at the port, ~12.95W at the camera after cable loss. **802.3at (PoE+)** delivers 30W at the port, ~25.5W at the device. **802.3bt Type 3/4** go to 60W/90W at the port (~51W/71W at the device) \u2014 that's for heated PTZ domes and the hungry stuff. A plain fixed dome or turret sips 4\u20136W. A PTZ with its motors and heater peaks 12\u201325W, and a heated dome in a cold climate can hit 25\u201360W during warm-up. Now the trap that burns installers: **per-port wattage is not the switch's total budget.** A 24-port PoE+ switch sounds like 24 \xd7 30 = 720W, but the box ships with maybe a 370W power budget \u2014 barely half. You physically cannot run every port hot. So size by adding real draws plus headroom: eight 6W turrets = 48W, no problem; but eight PTZs at 25W = 200W and you're eyeing that 370W budget carefully. Always leave 20\u201325% margin for cold-weather heater spikes and future cameras.\n\nCable rules follow from Ethernet, not from us. **100 meters (328 ft)** is the hard limit from switch/NVR port to camera \u2014 that's IEEE 802.3, and it's the same for Cat5e, Cat6, and Cat6A. Cat5e is the floor and fine for 1080p under ~70m; spec Cat6 on new work; Cat6A for 4K or runs near the limit. In the real world power quits before data does \u2014 voltage drop over a long thin run can rob 20\u201330% of the wattage at the far end, so a camera that boots on the bench browns out at 90m. Past 100m, a PoE extender regenerates data and power for another 100m per hop. On analog jobs it's different cable entirely: RG59 coax runs 300m+, and you'll usually pull **Siamese** \u2014 coax bonded to a two-conductor 18/2 power lead in one jacket \u2014 so video and 12V DC go in a single pull.\n\nCamera bodies, quick: **dome** is discreet and ceiling-flush but the bubble smears in IR and collects dust; **bullet** is obvious (a deterrent, and easy to aim) but a perch for wasps and a handle for vandals; **turret** (eyeball) is the installer's favorite \u2014 no bubble so no IR glare, easy to aim, weathers well; **PTZ** pans/tilts/zooms and is for one operator actively tracking, not for set-and-forget coverage, because it's only ever looking one way. Lens picks the picture. Focal length trades width for reach: **2.8mm \u2248 100\u2013110\xb0 wide**, good for a room or entry; **4mm \u2248 80\u201390\xb0**, the do-everything middle; **6mm \u2248 55\u201360\xb0**; longer for parking-lot reach. Wider sees more but shrinks faces; the money question is always pixels-on-target, not raw megapixels \u2014 a 4K camera zoomed wide across a lot still can't ID a face. Motorized varifocal lenses let you dial the exact frame without swapping the camera.\n\nNight vision is IR: built-in 850nm LEDs, rated in meters (20m, 30m, 50m). Real range is less than the sticker, domes lose it to bubble glare, and bugs/spiderwebs on a bullet turn IR into a white-out. Ratings you quote to customers: **IP** is ingress \u2014 first digit dust (6 = dust-tight), second water (**IP66** rated against jets, **IP67** survives brief submersion). **IK** is impact \u2014 **IK10** takes a 20-joule hit, everyday vandalism and a moderate hammer swing, but it is not bulletproof and a real bat swing (200\u2013400J) blows past it. Outdoor exposed cameras want IP66/67 *and* IK10; only one and you get a cracked lens or a fogged dome.\n\nStorage is math you should do in front of the customer. Rough rule: **GB/day \u2248 bitrate(Mbps) \xd7 10.8** (1 Mbps sustained is ~10.8 GB over 24 hours). A 1080p camera on H.264 runs 4\u20136 Mbps \u2248 43\u201365 GB/day; on H.265 that halves to 2\u20133 Mbps because HEVC hits the same quality at about half the bitrate, and H.265+ shaves another 30\u201340% on static scenes. 4K on H.264 is 12\u201316 Mbps \u2248 130\u2013170 GB/day. Multiply by cameras and retention days: sixteen 1080p H.265 cameras \xd7 30 days \u2248 16 \xd7 30GB \xd7 30 \u2248 14TB, so you're speccing an 8-bay NVR or a couple of surveillance-rated drives (WD Purple / Seagate SkyHawk \u2014 CMR, not shingled). Motion-only recording cuts it hard but risks missing pre-roll; leave headroom.\n\n**ONVIF** is what saves you when brands don't match. It's the open standard that lets a camera from one maker record on another maker's NVR. Learn the profiles: **Profile S** = live streaming/PTZ (the baseline), **Profile G** = recording and playback/search, **Profile T** = modern streaming with H.265, and analytics/tamper metadata. Caveat: ONVIF gets you basic streaming and record, but vendor-specific analytics, smart events, and full menus often only work fully within one brand's ecosystem, and \"ONVIF compatible\" is not a guarantee two boxes play nice \u2014 test before you promise.\n\nOn brands and one thing that'll bite you: **Hikvision and Dahua** make excellent, cheap hardware and are the OEM behind a huge slice of rebadged budget cameras \u2014 but they're named in the **NDAA** (Section 889 bars them from federal contracts and federally-funded buys), and the **FCC** already prohibits *new* equipment authorizations for their gear \u2014 the 2022 Covered List rule blocks importing or selling new Hikvision/Dahua models. What's still in motion: in late 2025 the FCC adopted rules giving itself the authority to *revoke* authorizations already granted to older, previously-approved models, but as of mid-2026 it has not enacted a blanket revocation \u2014 that piece is proposed, not done. Either way, for any government, school, critical-infrastructure, or federally-funded job you cannot legally install them, and many rebadged Amazon brands are the same silicon underneath. **Axis** (Swedish, invented the network camera, open and secure) and **Avigilon** (Motorola, end-to-end with strong AI/plate/appearance search) are your NDAA-compliant premium picks. In California's CSLB framework this is **C-7 Low Voltage Systems** work \u2014 closed-circuit video lives under C-7 \u2014 and know that's a distinct classification from burglar alarm, which needs a separate Alarm Company Operator (ACO) license through BSIS, even when you're pulling video, alarm, and access on the same job.",sources:["https://www.fs.com/blog/understanding-poe-standards-and-wattage-21.html","https://pvrblog.com/tech/poe/","https://www.szsanyi.com/en/blog/poe-power-budget-calculator-switch-sizing-guide","https://learncctv.com/hd-tvi-hd-cvi-ahd-comparison-best-explained/","https://www.globalmediapro.com/content/WhatAreTheDifferencesBetweenHDTVIAHDAndHDCVISystems","https://nellyssecurity.com/blogs/articles/cctv-ip-and-ik-ratings-explained","https://www.arcadian.ai/blogs/blogs/understanding-camera-standards-ip66-ik10-and-beyond-a-complete-2025-guide-for-surveillance-buyers","https://reolink.com/blog/cctv-storage-calculation-formula/","https://pvrblog.com/cameras/tech/storage-calculator/","https://www.onvif.org/profiles/","https://www.verkada.com/blog/what-is-onvif/","https://pvrblog.com/security-camera-lens-guide/","https://www.cctvsecuritypros.com/articles/security-camera-lens-viewing-angle-guide/","https://crimpshop.com/blog/ethernet-cable-security-cameras/","https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/04/2025-21928/protecting-against-national-security-threats-to-the-communications-supply-chain-through-the","https://www.fcc.gov/laboratory-division/equipment-authorization-approval-guide/equipment-authorization-system","https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/LSB10895","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C-7","https://www.pelco.com/blog/ndaa-compliant-cameras"],relatedGuides:["commercial-security-survey","camera-defense","networking-basics-for-security-techs","surveillance-storage-raid-redundancy"],tags:["ONVIF","AHD","Bitrate","Digital video recorder","HD-TVI","HD-CVI","Network video recorder","Power over Ethernet"],aliases:["contractors license","C-28","state license board","California license","wire gauge","long run","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door"]},{id:"gate-operators",title:"Vehicle gate operators, UL 325 & electrified fence",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"A vehicle gate operator is a motor, a gearbox, and a control board that move a big chunk of steel across a driveway. That steel can weigh a thousand pounds and it does not care about your fingers, a kid on a bike, or the dog. Everything in this trade \u2014 the whole UL 325 rulebook \u2014 exists because gates have crushed and killed people, and the lawsuits followed. So learn the safety side cold. On a gate call, the operator is the easy part. Making it stop when it hits something is the part that keeps you out of court.\n\nStart with the three body styles. A slide operator moves the gate sideways on a track or on cantilever rollers; you'll see chain-drive and rack-and-pinion (gear rack bolted to the gate, pinion on the motor). Slides are the workhorse for commercial and industrial driveways where you don't have room to swing. A swing operator pushes or pulls a hinged leaf like a door \u2014 linear arm actuators (ram style) or below-grade/underground operators for the pretty residential jobs where you hide the hardware. And a barrier-arm operator is the parking-garage boom: a counterweighted arm that lifts, fast cycle, high traffic. Barrier arms control traffic \u2014 they do NOT secure a perimeter. Anybody can walk or duck under a boom. If security matters, the barrier arm is the wrong tool; you want a slide or swing.\n\nNow the classes, because UL 325 sorts every operator into one of four based on where it lives, and it changes what safety you're required to install. Class I is residential \u2014 one to four single-family homes. Class II is commercial with general public access: apartment entrances (five or more units), hotels, retail, a public parking lot. Class III is industrial/limited access \u2014 and note that the operator is only half of what gets inspected: ASTM F2200 governs the GATE itself, meaning guarded rollers, screened openings from the bottom up to 72 inches on a slide gate, and no exposed pinch points. Verifying the gate meets it is the installer's responsibility, not the gate fabricator's alone, and an operator listed correctly on a non-compliant gate still fails \u2014 a factory yard or loading dock where only authorized people go through, not the general public. Class IV is restricted access \u2014 a guarded facility with security personnel controlling entry, like an airport security area or a substation. The manufacturer builds and labels the operator for specific classes; you don't get to promote a residential opener to guard a commercial complex just because it fit the budget. Match the class to the site.\n\nHere's the heart of it \u2014 the UL 325 revision that took effect January 12, 2016, and is still the standard you install to. Every entrapment point (that's any spot where the gate can trap a body \u2014 the leading edge, the back, the pinch points) must be protected by at least two independent means of entrapment protection, in each direction of travel. UL sorts protection into types. Type A is inherent entrapment protection \u2014 the operator's own sensing: it detects an obstruction by force or current draw and reverses or stops on its own. That's your inherent reversing, and it's built into the board. Type B is external sensors you add \u2014 B1 non-contact (photo eyes, an infrared beam across the opening) and B2 contact (a sensing edge, a rubber pressure strip on the leading edge of the gate that triggers when it squishes against something). Type C is inherent force limiting (an adjustable clutch or pressure-relief \u2014 swing gates and barrier arms only, never a slide, vertical lift, or vertical pivot). Type D is constant-pressure hold-to-run, where the gate only moves while somebody holds the button and can see the whole gate, and the second they let go it stops. A typical Class I or II slide is inherent reversing plus a monitored photo eye plus a monitored edge \u2014 that's your two means covered.\n\nBefore any of that, though, settle what the radio actually is, because the single most common parts-ordering mistake on a gate is matching the frequency and ignoring the coding. A rolling-code transmitter will not operate a fixed-code receiver, and the reverse holds too, even when both are printed 390 MHz. Identify the generation first and the frequency second.\n\n[[diagram:gate-radio-generations|Coding generations: rolling code against fixed DIP, the frequencies each uses, and where you meet them]]\n\nThe word that changed everything in 2016 is \"monitored.\" Placement is policed too: controls meant for people have to sit far enough from the gate that somebody cannot touch the moving gate while pressing the button, which in practice means at least 6 ft back. Emergency access devices are the exception. A monitored device gets checked by the operator every cycle \u2014 for correct connection and correct operation \u2014 so the board knows the sensor is there and working. If a photo eye or edge is missing, cut, unplugged, or faulted, a monitored operator will NOT run in automatic mode \u2014 it flat refuses, or drops to constant-pressure. That killed the old dodge of \"customer's photo eye is acting up, I'll just unplug it to stop the nuisance reversals.\" Do that on a modern board and you'll fault the whole system. So when a gate \"won't close\" and the owner swears nothing's wrong, your first move is check the safety devices \u2014 a spider web across the photo eye or a car parked over a loop reads as an obstruction. On mine property, note that MSHA has jurisdiction instead of OSHA, so gate guarding on those sites gets inspected under mine-safety rules \u2014 same physics, different inspector.\n\nLong-range UHF vehicle tags are the other way onto a gate, and there the install IS the aiming. Range set too wide opens for the next lane or opens far too early; set too short and every resident stops and waits. The rule that settles it is two-sided... wide enough that a tagged car rolls through without stopping, tight enough that an untagged car cannot fit between the far edge of the zone and the gate.\n\n[[diagram:uhf-read-zone|Aiming a long-range UHF read zone, and the symptom-to-cause list when it is wrong]]\n\nLoop detectors are the buried piece. A loop is a continuous run of wire \u2014 no splices \u2014 saw-cut into the pavement in a rectangle, wound a few turns, and fed to a detector card. It works on inductance: a car's metal mass detunes the loop's field and trips the relay. You'll wire three jobs. A free-exit (egress) loop opens the gate automatically for a car leaving from the inside. A safety/reversing loop sits under the gate's path and holds it open or reverses it so it can't close on a vehicle. And a shadow loop is a swing-gate trick \u2014 buried in the arc the gate swings through, active only when the gate's open, so it won't swing into a car sitting in the pocket. Keep loops the required distance apart or they'll cross-talk and phantom-trip.\n\nAccess control is how the gate knows to open for the right people. Keypads (PIN codes), card and fob readers, and telephone-entry systems that dial a resident so they can buzz a visitor in \u2014 the DoorKing 1834/1835 is the box you'll see on half the apartment complexes in the country. Learn the big brands: LiftMaster/Chamberlain (the most-installed, huge dealer network, MyQ app), DoorKing/DKS (telephone entry and access control kings \u2014 the 1601 barrier operator, plus slide and swing operators), FAAC (Italian-built, heavy-duty commercial), Nice Apollo (Titan series, solar/DC-friendly swing and slide for residential and light commercial), All-O-Matic (straightforward commercial slide/swing), and Viking Access (heavy-duty, high-cycle). Different boards, different programming, same UL 325 rules underneath.\n\nElectrified security fence is a separate animal and people panic about it, so know the truth. A proper security energizer is non-lethal by design \u2014 it pushes a very short high-voltage pulse (roughly 0.3 milliseconds) at a low, current-limited energy, typically five joules or less per pulse under IEC 60335-2-76 (adopted in the U.S. as ANSI/CPLSO 60335-2-76). It's a hard, memorable jolt meant to deter and to trip an alarm on cut or climb \u2014 not a lethal continuous line. It is NOT the same as putting mains voltage on a wire, which is illegal and will kill. Signage is mandatory: the standardized yellow \"Caution \u2014 Electric Fence\" warning signs, visible on both faces of the fence, at every gate, access point, and corner, and spaced along the accessible perimeter. Exact spacing is set by local code, not the energizer maker \u2014 California, for instance, requires a sign at least every 30 feet, Michigan every 60 feet \u2014 so check your state's rule and the NEC before you energize anything, because the permitted energy and the sign spacing both vary by jurisdiction, and a mis-installed fence is a liability and a code violation waiting to bite you.\n\nA lot of what actually opens these gates isn't a card or a keypad but a plain RF remote, and the receiver is a part you'll add, pair, and troubleshoot far more than the operator itself. The workhorse is a small [[model:transmitter-solutions-sel2641r433-nn|433 MHz nano receiver]] wired to the operator's open input and paired to a [[model:transmitter-solutions-setmc1c9v|433 MHz transmitter]] on the customer's visor. Pairing is learn mode: press the learn button on the receiver \u2014 the LED changes to tell you it's listening \u2014 press the transmitter once, and the receiver stores that code and the LED drops back out. Add every remote the same way; delete-all is usually a long hold on the same button. The wiring is where people go wrong: the receiver's relay is a dry contact \u2014 it doesn't send power to the operator, it just closes a pair of terminals the way the button on the wall would. Land the receiver's COM and NO across the operator's OPEN or single-button-control input, power the receiver from its own 12/24 V (or the operator's accessory terminals if they're rated for it), and never feed the operator's input voltage back through the relay.\n\n[[diagram:rf-receiver-wiring|433 MHz receiver wiring: learn the remote to the receiver, then its dry-contact relay closes across the operator's OPEN input like a button. Power the receiver separately; keep the antenna vertical and out of the metal.]]\n\nAntenna and range are the other half. The whip wants to be vertical, out of the metal enclosure, and up \u2014 a receiver buried in a steel operator box with the antenna coiled inside will read a fob at ten feet and nothing at forty. Diagnose range loss in order: a fresh battery in the transmitter first (a weak cell is the number-one the-remote-stopped-working), then antenna position and cable, then look for a new RF source nearby \u2014 an LED sign, a cell booster, another 433 device \u2014 stepping on the band. If one remote works up close and another doesn't at all, the dead one was never learned or got wiped on a delete-all; re-learn it before you condemn the receiver.",sources:["https://www.liftmaster.com/builder-knowledge-center/UL-guidelines","https://www.doorking.com/safety/ul-325/","https://www.dasma.com/wp-content/uploads/pubs/TechDataSheets/OperatorElectronics/TDS353.pdf","https://www.dasma.com/wp-content/uploads/pubs/TechDataSheets/OperatorElectronics/TDS356.pdf","https://www.gateoperator.net/ul-325","https://www.ul.com/resources/ul-325-external-entrapment-protection-devices","https://www.engineeringexpress.com/wiki/the-ul-325/","https://apollogateopeners.com/store/pdf/ul325-faq.pdf","https://www.gateoperator.net/doorking-1835","https://www.doorking.com/traffic-control/1601-barrier-gate/","https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1835-pc-programmable-80-series/","https://gatedepot.com/blog/making-sense-of-loop-detectors-and-loops","https://bdloops.com/explaining-loop-systems-to-your-customers/","https://cplso.org/ansi-cplso-60335-2-76-electric-fence/","https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/60232","https://www.advancedperimetersystems.com/how-or-why-electric-pulse-fencing-is-safe/","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB1622","Michigan Compiled Laws, MCL 125.1528a (gate operator provisions) \u2014 original link retired.","https://transmittersolutions.com/"],tags:["UL 325","Photo eye","Relay","Rolling code","Current draw","Dry contact","Fob","Opening"],aliases:["relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","hopping code","code grabbing","remote won't pair","volt free","form C","clean contact","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"door-reinforcement",title:"Hardening a door: strikes, jambs, hinges, glass & bars",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here's the thing you need to understand before you touch a single screw: almost nobody picks a lock to get in. They kick. And when a door gets kicked, it isn't the deadbolt that fails and it isn't the door slab that fails \u2014 it's the jamb. The bolt stays right where it is, welded into a little tin strike plate, and that whole plate rips out of the soft pine trim, splitting the jamb along the grain. Go look at any forced-entry job and you'll see the same thing: a clean strike plate lying on the floor with the wood still stuck to it, and two stripped short screws. That's the whole failure mode. Everything we do to harden a door is about moving the load off that flimsy trim board and into the wall.\n\n[[diagram:reinforced-jamb-anatomy|Section through a properly anchored latch side, plus the three ways in that never touch the door]]\n\nWhy does it fail? The strike plate the factory ships is held by two 3/4-inch screws that bite into nothing but the 3/4-inch jamb and maybe the 1.5-inch stop behind it. There's no bite into the stud. So when a size-eleven boot lands next to the knob, all the force lands on those two little screws in soft wood, and it lets go on the first or second kick. That's your enemy. Fix that and you've fixed most of the door.\n\nThe first and cheapest fix is the highest-value thing you'll ever do: pull the deadbolt strike and the latch strike, and put in a box strike backed by 3-inch screws. A box strike fully encases the bolt instead of leaving it hanging in an open mortise, so a pry bar can't peel it. But the magic is the screws \u2014 3-inch hardened screws reach past the 3/4-inch trim and jamb and dig into the framing stud behind it. Now the kick loads the wall, not the trim. One tip from doing a lot of these: stagger the long screws slightly so they don't all drive into the same grain line \u2014 one screw per grain, and you won't split the jamb you're trying to save. Do the same on the deadbolt strike and make sure that deadbolt has a full 1-inch throw. A short-throw or spring-latch-only door is a five-second entry no matter what plate you hang.\n\nWhen the jamb's already soft, chewed up, or you want belt-and-suspenders, you go to a full-length reinforcement wrap. Two names you'll hear: Door Armor from Armor Concepts, and the StrikeMaster II Pro from Safe Homes International. Both do the same job \u2014 spread the impact over a long steel channel instead of two screw holes \u2014 but they wrap differently. Door Armor runs a bit cheaper, turns 90 degrees across the face of the jamb and tucks slightly under the casing. The StrikeMaster II is a true U-channel that wraps the jamb, installs without pulling casing or planing the door, and the company's been backing it hard for years on kick-in claims. Either one, installed with the long screws into the stud, turns a builder-grade door into something a person just bounces off of.\n\nNow don't stop at the lock side. Once the strike side is solid, the hinge side becomes the weakest link \u2014 and it ships with the same junk 3/4-inch screws. Swap at least one screw per hinge for a 3-incher into the stud; bonus, it pulls a sagging door back into square. Two more hinge-side moves matter, mostly on outswing doors where the pins are exposed to the outside: a set screw tapped into the hinge barrel that seats into a groove on the pin so nobody backs the pin out with a screwdriver, and a hinge bolt \u2014 also called a dog bolt or security stud \u2014 a fixed stud on the door leaf that drops into a matching hole in the frame leaf. That stud holds the door in the frame even if somebody knocks the pins clean out. Non-removable-pin (NRP) hinges do the same thing built in. On an inswing door the pins are inside and this matters less, but on any outswing exterior door it's not optional.\n\nLayer on top of the slab with a proper security screen door and you get a second locked barrier you can still see and breathe through. Look for marine-grade 316 stainless steel mesh \u2014 Meshtec and the Titan doors are the common ones \u2014 not the aluminum \"security-ish\" decorative junk. Real screens carry a 3-point lock and have been run through AAMA pry, pull, knife-shear, and impact testing. Stainless mesh basically can't be cut without a powered saw, and that noise and time is what sends someone looking for an easier house.\n\nGlass is its own conversation, and here you've got two tiers. Laminated glass \u2014 two panes bonded to a PVB or stiffer SGP interlayer \u2014 is the real answer: smash it and the glass stays stuck to the interlayer and stays in the frame, so the burglar has to keep pounding, loudly, for a long time. That's why security laminates rate up in the EN 356 P6B\u2013P8B \"burglar-resistant\" band, the classes measured by how many axe and hammer blows it takes to chop a 400mm opening. Security film is the budget play \u2014 you're bonding a thick film to glass you already own. It stops the smash-and-grab and the glass-hazard injury, but by itself film only earns the lower impact-only ratings, and here's the gotcha every installer gets burned on: film that isn't anchored to the frame just lets the whole filmed pane pop out of the stop in one piece. If you're selling film for security, you sell it with a wet-glaze or mechanical attachment system to the frame, or you're selling a false sense of safety.\n\nLast, and this is the part that gets people killed if you get it wrong: every one of these hardening moves cuts both ways, because a door that won't let a burglar in also won't let a family out. Window bars are the classic trap. On any sleeping room \u2014 and every basement bedroom \u2014 the emergency escape opening bars, grilles, or grates have to release from the inside in a single action, without a key, a tool, special knowledge, or any more force than opening the window normally takes (IRC R310, the bars-and-grilles provision at R310.4 in the 2015 book). So spec quick-release bars a scared kid or a grandmother can pop in the dark, and never bolt a fixed grille over a bedroom egress window, ever. Same logic on that security screen: don't leave it locked from the inside with a key, and don't put a double-cylinder deadbolt behind it with the key hidden \u2014 in a fire, people die fumbling for keys behind a barred door. The whole game is: hard to get in from the outside, dead simple to get out from the inside. When those two ever conflict, egress wins. Every time.",sources:["https://driscolllockandkey.com/how-to-reinforce-your-door-frame-against-break-ins-a-diy-guide/","https://www.readylocksa.com/strike-plates-kick-in-protection","https://titanfloridadoors.com/blog/door-strike-plate-guide/","https://trueprepper.com/best-door-reinforcement-kit/","https://asafehome.net/PAGES/Safe-Homes-International-StrikeMaster-II-Pro.html","https://engineerfix.com/how-non-removable-hinge-pins-improve-door-security/","https://www.hingeoutlet.com/products/security-hinge-pins-make-any-hinge-a-security-hinge","https://www.danddhardware.com/exterior-door-hinges-security.html","https://titansecurity.com/","https://www.meshtec.com/security-doors-and-windows/","https://www.atlasarmor.com/security-screen-door/","https://tecdur.com/en-356-p6b-to-p8b-glass-axe-attack-resistance-explained/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IRC2015/chapter-3-building-planning/IRC2015-Pt03-Ch03-SecR310","https://www.rockwellinc.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/07/Egress-Code-IRC-2015-R310-and-R303.pdf","https://www.windowfilmdepot.com/blog/the-security-window-film-specification-mistakes-that-create-real-risk/","https://www.riotglass.com/solutions-window-film-vs-security-glass/","https://agnora.com/secureglaze-the-difference-between-film-and-lamination/","https://blog.ansi.org/ansi/ansi-grade-levels-bhma-locks-hardware-tests/"],tags:["Strike","Opening","Cylinder"],aliases:["won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"alarm-systems-service",title:"Burglar alarm systems: panels, zones, sensors & monitoring",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"A burglar alarm is just a control panel deciding whether a bunch of switches and sensors add up to \"someone's inside who shouldn't be,\" then telling somebody about it. Everything else \u2014 keypads, motions, the app on the customer's phone \u2014 hangs off that panel. Learn the panel and the rest falls into place. And keep one thing straight from day one: this is intrusion, low-voltage burglar work. Low-voltage FIRE alarm is a different animal \u2014 different code (NFPA 72), different inspections, different licensing (fire marshal / C-16 territory, not the C-7 low-voltage or lock/security classes you live in for intrusion). Don't cross that line without the paper to back it.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-panel-anatomy|Sensors report, the panel judges, and only the comms path is worth paying for]]\n\nFour panel families cover most of what you'll touch. Resideo/Honeywell VISTA is the old workhorse \u2014 the VISTA-20P gives you 8 hardwired zones on the board, doublable up to 15, expandable with 4219 modules to 48 hardwired, or 56 wireless with a 5881 receiver, and up to two partitions. It's everywhere, it's cranky, and it's programmed by punching numeric fields at a keypad. DSC PowerSeries Neo (HS2016/2032/2064/2128) is the cleaner hardwired-plus-wireless option, scaling to 128 zones, and its wireless is PowerG at 915 MHz \u2014 encrypted, two-way, near 2 km line-of-sight and battery life pushing 8 years because the sensor throttles its own transmit power. DMP (XT50, XR550) is the American-made dealer panel \u2014 proprietary, meaning DMP-authorized dealers program it and it phones home to DMP's own infrastructure; the XR550 scales into the hundreds of zones and crosses into access control and commercial fire. Qolsys IQ Panel 4 is the all-in-one touchscreen: 128 zones, up to 4 partitions, two wireless cards (PowerG 915 MHz plus a 319.5 MHz card that reads legacy Interlogix/GE and Qolsys S-line sensors), and it lives on Alarm.com for transport and the customer app.\n\nZones are the heart of it. A zone is one loop the panel watches. Hardwired zones run a physical pair back to the board, usually with an end-of-line resistor so the panel can tell \"open\" from \"shorted\" from \"cut wire\" \u2014 that supervision is why you don't skip the EOL. Wireless zones are a sensor talking RF to a receiver, each one enrolled by serial. Hardwired is bulletproof and cheap to monitor but you're pulling wire; wireless is fast to deploy and every device now carries a battery you'll be replacing. Partitions let one panel act like several independent systems \u2014 arm the shop floor while the office stays disarmed \u2014 which is how you cover a duplex or a business with separate tenants off one board.\n\nSensors are the vocabulary. Door/window contacts are just a reed switch and a magnet \u2014 the cheapest, most reliable point you can install, and the backbone of any job. PIR motions read moving heat against a background; pet-immune models ignore animals under a rated weight (commonly ~40 lb, some up to ~88 lb) by shaping the lens and logic. Dual-tech motions pair a PIR with a microwave (K-band) sensor and only alarm when BOTH trip \u2014 that AND-logic is the standard fix for a motion that keeps false-tripping on HVAC, sunlight, or drafts. Glass-break sensors listen for the thud-plus-shatter acoustic signature and cover a room's worth of windows from one spot. Shock/vibration sensors catch the hit before the glass is even broken \u2014 good on safes, display cases, and doors people pry. Match the sensor to the failure mode, not to the catalog.\n\nKeypads are the user's steering wheel \u2014 arm, disarm, read trouble conditions. Communication is where the whole trade turned over. POTS is dead: the FCC lifted price caps and cleared carriers to retire copper (the 2022 milestone, loosened further in 2025), so a panel dialing out over a phone line is a liability, not a plan. Everything now rides cellular (LTE-M / 5G on a dedicated SIM, no internet needed) and/or IP over the customer's broadband. Best practice is dual-path: IP primary for speed, cellular as automatic failover, each path supervised so a cut line or dead modem is caught in minutes instead of discovered during a burglary. That supervision is exactly why POTS had to go \u2014 nobody knew the line was dead until it mattered.\n\nMonitoring is the panel handing a signal to a central station that decides whether to call the customer and then dispatch police. For jobs that need certified protection, the letters matter: UL 827 governs the central station itself (building, redundancy, staffing, records), and UL 681 covers how the alarm is installed and classified at the premises. A UL-listed panel plus UL-listed communicator plus UL 827 station gives you an end-to-end certified chain \u2014 banks, pharmacies, and anything an insurer or AHJ demands it for.\n\nArming modes are simple once you say them out loud. AWAY arms everything, including interior motions \u2014 nobody's home. STAY (home) arms the perimeter but bypasses interior motions so the customer can walk to the kitchen at night without tripping it. INSTANT kills the entry delay so any perimeter trip alarms immediately. Entry/exit delays are the countdown windows: exit delay lets you arm and leave without setting it off, entry delay gives you time to reach the keypad and disarm on the way in. Set them too long and you've handed an intruder a free window; too short and you'll generate false alarms \u2014 most jurisdictions run 30\u201360 seconds.\n\nWhich brings you to the part that actually costs customers money: false alarms. Most cities require an alarm PERMIT and fine repeat offenders \u2014 many run an escalating schedule, e.g. free early hits then a stiff per-alarm fine once you cross a threshold (Albuquerque assesses $500 for each false alarm past ten in a permit year), and some have moved to verified response, where police won't roll without a confirmed event. Central stations are typically required to call the responsible party before requesting dispatch, and a cancel before police arrive doesn't count against you. The install decisions above \u2014 dual-tech where a plain PIR would nuisance-trip, sane entry delays, good contact placement \u2014 are how you keep your customer off the fine list.",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-many-hardwired-zones-are-available-on-a-vista-20p","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/honeywell-vista-system-how-many-zones-can-it-handle","https://www.dsc.com/index.php?n=products&o=view&id=2444","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS2032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2128-E-Alarm-Control-Installation-Guide-TIS/1.35/Specifications","https://www.dmp.com/products/control-panels/xr-series/xr550","https://www.dmp.com/products/control-panels/xt-series/xt50","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/products/qolsys-iq-panel-4-powerg-319-5-touchscreen-wireless-alarm-system","https://suretyhome.com/qolsys-iq-panel-explained/","https://www.dmp.com/resources/news-updates/replacing-pots-lines","https://usmadesupply.com/resources/guides/fire-alarm-communicators-guide","https://www.ul.com/services/central-station-service-certification","https://tec-tel.com/glossary/ul-827","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-pg9984p","https://www.dsc.com/?n=products&o=view&id=1015","https://www.cabq.gov/dfa/treasury/alarm-fees/false-alarm-ordinance","Fremont PD alarm permit requirements, cited as a municipal example \u2014 original link retired; check your own AHJ, these pages move often."],relatedGuides:["commercial-security-survey","alarm-zone-wiring","alarm-comms-monitoring","alarm-troubleshooting","alarm-install-steps"],tags:["PIR","Entry delay","Central station","NFPA 72","Handing","Schedule","AHJ"],aliases:["motion detector","motion sensor","passive infrared","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off"]},{id:"adams-rite-sagging-doors",title:"Adams Rite storefront failures: loose cylinders and sagging pivot doors",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Here are the two Adams Rite calls you'll run more than any other on aluminum storefront, and both look like a broken lock when they're really a loose screw and a tired door. Learn to tell them apart from the customer's first sentence and you'll fix most of them before you set your bag down.\n\nStart with the single most common one, because it's the dumbest and the fastest: the **cylinder set screw backs out.** An Adams Rite MS deadlock is driven by a standard mortise cylinder threaded into the lock case, and the only thing keeping that cylinder from spinning is one small **set screw** \u2014 the retainer screw \u2014 that seats into the groove around the cylinder body from the edge of the door. Vibration and years of slamming walk that screw loose, or it was never properly snugged at install. Once it backs out, the cylinder is free to **turn.** The customer's tell is almost always the same: \"my key just spins and won't catch.\" That's the whole diagnosis, right there. And it's not just an annoyance \u2014 a thief who knows the trick grips the cylinder, often with nothing more than a wrench or bare fingers, and **unthreads it right out of the door,** exposing the cam. From there the lock throws with a screwdriver in about two seconds. Sometimes the cylinder just falls out on the mat and the door's standing open.\n\nThe fix looks like magic to the customer and takes two minutes. Pull the faceplate or find the set screw in the door edge, thread the cylinder back to the correct depth so the cam clocks to the right position, and **snug the set screw down into the cylinder's groove \u2014 with a dab of blue threadlocker** so it can't walk out again. Two habits kill the callback: use the *blue* removable threadlocker, never red, because you'll want to service this again someday; and after you tighten, run the key and confirm the cam still drives the bolt through its **full throw** \u2014 crank a set screw into the wrong spot and you'll bind the cylinder or short the throw. While you're there, make sure it's the **right length cylinder** for the door thickness: one sticking proud of the face is easier to grab and spin, so a cylinder and a cam that sit flush are security, not just neatness. This one failure \u2014 the loose set screw \u2014 is the bread-and-butter Adams Rite call.\n\nThe second call is the one that scares customers, because it usually shows up as \"someone popped my door with a screwdriver.\" A pair of narrow-stile pivot doors, and you can shim or knock the latch right back to retracted. The instinct is to blame the strike or the lock. Don't. The strike is the symptom; the door is the disease.\n\nHere's why the screwdriver trick works. An Adams Rite deadlatch \u2014 the 4710/4711 family \u2014 has two bolts sticking out of the faceplate, not one: the main spring latchbolt, and beside it a smaller **auxiliary plunger.** That little plunger is the entire security story. When the door hangs square and closed, the plunger rides **against the face of the strike** and stays pushed in, and being pushed in is what mechanically **deadlocks** the main latch so it physically cannot be shoved back. That is the anti-loiding feature. It is the whole reason a deadlatch is called a deadlatch instead of just a latch.\n\n[[img:ar-4710-deadlatch|The deadlatch: the main latchbolt plus the small auxiliary deadlocking plunger beside it. The plunger must rest on the strike face to keep the latch deadlocked.]]\n\nNow sag the door. When the lock stile drops even an eighth of an inch, that auxiliary plunger stops landing on the strike face and instead **falls into the strike cavity** right alongside the latchbolt. The instant it drops in, it's no longer depressed, and the main latch is no longer deadlocked \u2014 it's just a spring bolt with nothing holding it out. A putty knife, a shim, an \"assault card,\" a flathead \u2014 anything flat walks it back. That same drop is why the bolt \"won't throw all the way\": the stile is low, so the latch hits the strike lip instead of dropping into the pocket. The strike not catching and the loiding are not two problems. They're one \u2014 **the lock stile has dropped and racked out of square** \u2014 showing up in two places.\n\nWhy do these doors sag? Short list, in order. Number one, cheapest to fix: the **door has racked.** A narrow-stile aluminum door is four extrusions held into a rectangle by **tension tie-rods** running through the top and bottom rails into the corner blocks. As those loosen, or the corner keys strip, the door deforms from a rectangle into a parallelogram, and the corner that drops is the lock stile \u2014 the one farthest from the pivots. Number two: **worn pivots.** On an offset-pivot door the whole weight rides the bottom pivot bearing; worn, it lets the door settle, and a loose top pivot or closer spindle does the same from above. Number three, easy to miss: **the glass** \u2014 failed setting blocks let that heavy lite shift in the stops and pull the door out of square.\n\nFix it in order, and fix the door before you touch the strike. **Re-square it first** \u2014 snug the corner tie-rod tension nuts to pull the lock stile back up; re-key the corner blocks if they're stripped. **Then the pivots** \u2014 adjust the bottom pivot (many have a vertical set-screw to raise the door) or replace it if the bearing's shot, and tighten the top pivot and closer arm. **Then the glass,** if it's shifted. Only now, with the door hanging square, do you go to the hardware: confirm the auxiliary plunger lands fully on the strike face and stays depressed, and shim or adjust the strike vertically until the latchbolt gets full throw and the plunger is solidly deadlocking. Then test it like the burglar will \u2014 closed and locked, you should not be able to shim or knock that latch back a hair.\n\nAnd the upgrade that earns the repeat account: if it's a pair that keeps sagging and keeps getting loided, stop asking a spring deadlatch to be the security. Move the customer to a keyed **MS deadlock with a hookbolt** \u2014 a hookbolt has no spring and physically **hooks** into the strike, so there's nothing to knock back, and the hook resists the two leaves being spread and pried apart, which is the other way pairs get beaten. Then lock the inactive leaf like you mean it, with a proper astragal and top-and-bottom flush bolts. Tell the customer straight: the lock was never the problem \u2014 a screw backed out, or the door dropped out of square. That's a service call and an upsell, not a teardown, and it's the difference between a parts-changer and a locksmith who reads the opening.",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://us.allegion.com/en/home/products/brands/adams-rite.html"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","storefront-loiding","storefront-cylinder-attacks"],tags:["Cylinder","Stile","Deadlatch","Loiding","Hookbolt","Cam","Strike","Astragal"],aliases:["Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","latch guard","anti-shim","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"rekey-pin-tumbler-steps",title:"Rekey a pin-tumbler cylinder, step by step",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Rekeying is the single most common thing you'll do for money, so let's walk it start to finish on a standard pin-tumbler cylinder \u2014 the everyday Kwikset/Schlage-style deadbolt, not a SmartKey. The whole job is one idea: swap the bottom pins so a different key lines the stack up at the shear line. Do it in order, keep the springs from launching, and you'll turn a rekey in a few minutes on the tailgate. Follow along, one step at a time.\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Get the lock off the door. Pull the deadbolt and back the mounting screws out of the interior (thumb-turn) side with a Phillips so the cylinder assembly comes free. Work over a tray or a towel \u2014 everything from here on is small and spring-loaded.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-1|Back out the mounting screws on the thumb-turn side to free the cylinder.]]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Separate the cylinder assembly. Slide the cylinder out of the lock body so you've got just the barrel in your hand and room to work the plug and pins.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-2|Pull the cylinder assembly away from the rest of the lock body.]]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Pop the retaining clip. On the back of the cylinder is a thin C-clip holding the stack together. Ease it off with tweezers or pliers \u2014 it's delicate, and it likes to fly, so keep a thumb over it.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-3|Remove the C-clip on the back of the cylinder with tweezers.]]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Slide off the tail piece. Take the tail piece off the back, and note which way its slit faces \u2014 it goes back the same orientation or the lock won't drive right on reassembly.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-4|Remove the tail piece, noting the slit orientation for reassembly.]]\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Lift the spring cover. Hold the cylinder upright and pry the spring cover off with a flathead. Those springs underneath are compressed and will launch if you let the cover snap \u2014 go slow and keep it contained.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-5|Pry off the spring cover while containing the compressed springs.]]\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Remove the springs. Lift each spring out with tweezers and set them aside undamaged. You're reusing these, so don't stretch or kink them.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-6|Lift out each spring and set it aside for reassembly.]]\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Pull the top driver pins. Take out the top pins \u2014 the ones flat on both ends \u2014 and keep them separate from the bottoms. On a standard rekey the drivers and springs go back exactly as they were; only the bottoms change.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-7|Remove the flat-ended top driver pins and keep them separate.]]\n\nStep 8 \u2014 Slide the plug out of the shell. Push the plug out while keeping it upright so the bottom key pins stay seated in their chambers. This is where a plug follower earns its keep on a full lock \u2014 here on a deadbolt cylinder you're just keeping it level.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-8|Push the plug out of the shell, keeping it upright so the bottom pins stay put.]]\n\nStep 9 \u2014 Dump the old bottom pins. Turn the plug over and tip the old cone-tipped bottom pins out. Empty every chamber \u2014 leftover pins are the number-one cause of a rekey that binds.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-9|Tip the old bottom pins out of the plug, emptying every chamber.]]\n\nStep 10 \u2014 Insert the new key and read it. Slide the new key all the way into the plug. The cut depths now tell you which bottom pin each chamber needs \u2014 a deep cut wants a longer pin, a shallow cut a shorter one. Decode it with a key gauge if you want to be sure.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-10|Insert the new key; its cut depths tell you which bottom pin each chamber needs.]]\n\nStep 11 \u2014 Fit the new bottoms to the shear line, then rebuild. Drop the matching bottom pins in until each one sits dead flush with the top of the plug at the shear line \u2014 that flush line is the whole game. Then reverse everything: plug back in the shell, top pins, springs, spring cover, tail piece, clip. Run the new key before you hang it, and confirm the old key no longer turns it.\n\n[[img:rekey-step-11|Fit each new bottom pin flush with the plug at the shear line, then reassemble in reverse.]]\n\nThat's a rekey. The judgment that comes with reps: read the cuts before you pin, keep your drivers and springs organized so reassembly is boring, and always test both keys \u2014 new one works, old one doesn't \u2014 before it goes back on the door. Charge for the cylinder and the labor, and you've just done the job that pays the truck.",sources:["https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/How+to+Rekey+a+Kwikset+Deadbolt+Lock/51672","Step photos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA)"],relatedGuides:["smartkey-rekey-steps"],tags:["Cylinder","Shear line","Shell","Key gauge","Plug","Plug follower"],aliases:["Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","housing","cylinder body","key decoder gauge","depth gauge","read the key","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","rekey tool","pins fell out"]},{id:"commercial-security-survey",title:"The commercial security survey: assessing and hardening a business",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",body:"When a business owner calls you out after a break-in \u2014 or better, before one \u2014 you are not just a guy who sells locks. You are the security consultant standing in their doorway, and the walk-around you do in the first ten minutes is what separates a parts-changer from someone who gets handed the keys to every door in the building. This is how you run that survey, think about it the way a burglar does, and prescribe in layers instead of selling one lonely deadbolt.\n\n[[diagram:security-survey-walk|One pass round the envelope, then the findings ranked in three tiers the owner can choose between]]\n\nStart with the mindset, because it organizes everything else: real security is layered, and the layers each do a different job. Deter (make them pick someone else), deny (keep them out), detect (know the moment they try), delay (buy time so the response beats the getaway), and respond (someone actually comes). A camera deters and detects but denies nothing. A steel door denies and delays but detects nothing. Sell one layer and you have sold a false sense of safety. The whole trade of hardening a commercial property is stacking those five so a failure in one is caught by the next.\n\nNow walk it like the offender does, because burglars are not criminal masterminds \u2014 they are opportunists picking targets by convenience and by what is worth grabbing. Interviews with commercial burglars say it plainly: they want cash and small, high-value, easily-fenced goods \u2014 jewelry, electronics, luxury brands, liquor \u2014 and they choose the store that makes it easy. So your job on the walk is to find the easy, and close it.\n\nFirst, the entry points. ADT's number gets quoted for a reason: roughly two out of three burglars come in the front door. So start there and be honest about the glass \u2014 a frameless glass storefront is a five-second entry with a rock or a hammer, the classic smash-and-grab. Then work around the building to the places owners forget: the recessed back door in the alley where nobody can see a man working, the roll-up delivery door, ground-floor and basement windows, the roof (more roofs get cut than people think), and any door with a glass lite next to the lock that turns a locked door into a reach-through. Note every one, and note WHY it is weak \u2014 hollow door, soft jamb, short-throw bolt, single-pane glass, exposed hinge, no contact on the alarm.\n\nSecond, the blind spots \u2014 because a burglar's favorite thing is somewhere to work unseen. Overgrown landscaping and tall hedges give concealed approaches; a big dumpster shoved against the back wall is both cover and a ladder to the roof; dark alleys, unlit parking lots, and camera dead zones let someone stage the whole job in shadow. Walk the perimeter at the angle a prowler would and mark every place you could stand for two minutes without being seen or lit. Those are the spots that get worked.\n\nThird, the routines, because predictability is a vulnerability you cannot bolt shut. A till left full overnight, identical open-and-close times to the minute, the same person alone at closing, premium merchandise sitting in the front window after dark \u2014 all of it tells a watcher when and where. A burglar scouts; part of your job is to make the scouting come up empty.\n\nThen you prescribe, in layers, matched to what you found and what they can spend. Deny and delay is your bread and butter as a locksmith: reinforced steel or solid-core doors in place of hollow ones, commercial Grade 1 deadbolts and \u2014 on aluminum storefront pairs \u2014 a keyed hookbolt instead of a spring latch, jamb and strike reinforcement so the frame does not split on a kick, and longer bolt throw. On the glass, which is usually the real weakness, the move is shatter-resistant security film or laminated glass so the pane holds together and a smash-and-grab turns into a smash-and-still-locked-out; steel security grates or roll-down shutters on rear and basement windows where code and appearance allow. And the safe: a cash or valuables safe is worthless sitting loose, so bolt it to the concrete floor, out of sight of the front glass, so it cannot be walked out on a hand truck.\n\nDetect and deter is the electronics layer, and here you are coordinating the alarm and camera work. A monitored alarm with door contacts, motion, and glass-break sensors so a broken pane trips it before a foot is inside; high-definition cameras mounted openly at every entrance and over the high-value displays, with overlapping angles and tamper detection turned on so blinding or covering one camera raises a flag instead of a clean path; and motion-activated lighting washing the alleys, the parking lot, and every blind spot you marked, because light both deters the prowler and makes your cameras actually usable at night. Visible cameras and a good alarm sign do real deterrent work on their own \u2014 a lot of burglars simply move to the softer target next door.\n\nRespond and operate is the part the owner controls, and you coach them on it because it is free and it works. Empty the till at closing and leave the drawer standing open so a night prowler can see from the window there is nothing to take \u2014 police reports on register-smash sprees literally recommend it. Move the premium, recognizable stock away from the front windows overnight. Vary the closing routine and the deposit run so there is no pattern to scout. Keep the stockroom locked as its own layer. And where the goods justify it, a smoke-cloak device that fills the space with dense fog in seconds ends most grab-and-runs before they start, without damaging stock.\n\nClose the survey the way a consultant does, not a salesman: ask the three questions that let you tailor it \u2014 what kind of business is it and what is actually worth stealing here, is there rear or alley access, and what is already in place \u2014 then hand them a prioritized plan, worst risk first, in the order their budget can absorb. Fix the front-door glass and the alley door before you upsell a fourth camera. Do that, and you are not quoting a lock; you are the person who secured their building, and that is the customer who calls you for the next ten years and refers you to every business owner they know.",relatedGuides:["door-hardening","door-reinforcement","adams-rite-sagging-doors","video-surveillance","alarm-systems-service","storefront-anatomy-threats"],sources:["https://popcenter.asu.edu/content/burglary-retail-establishments","https://www.urban.org/research/publication/preventing-retail-burglary","Small business physical security (uschamber.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published."],tags:["Hookbolt","Strike"],aliases:["hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{body:"Walk any strip mall, any downtown block, any office lobby, and you're looking at the same door over and over \u2014 a skinny aluminum frame wrapped around a big sheet of glass, swinging on pivots, with an Adams Rite lock buried in the stile. That's the aluminum storefront entrance, and it is the single most-attacked opening in all of commercial hardware. Not because locksmiths built it wrong \u2014 because of what it has to be. It has to be mostly glass so the retailer can show product. It has to be light so a closer can swing it ten thousand times a day. And by code, from the inside, it has to open with one motion so nobody dies in a fire \u2014 one releasing operation, no key, no twisting, no second latch to fumble. Glass, aluminum, and a legally-required easy way out \u2014 three things a burglar loves \u2014 all in one door. Your job isn't to make it a vault. You can't. Your job is to know every way it gets beaten so you can raise the time-and-noise cost past what a two-in-the-morning smash-and-grab is willing to pay. This guide is the map of the whole series. Learn the anatomy first, then we'll walk each attack in its own guide.\n\nStart with how the door is even held together, because half the failures live here. A narrow-stile aluminum door is four extruded tubes \u2014 two vertical **stiles** and two horizontal **rails** \u2014 squeezed into a rectangle and held there by **tie-rods**: 3/8\" steel rods running through the rails into the corner blocks, torqued down with nuts. There's no solid slab anywhere in it. The rod tension is the only thing keeping it square. The \"lock stile\" is the vertical on the swing side \u2014 narrow-stile means that stile might be barely two inches wide, which is exactly why the lock inside it is a special skinny Adams Rite case with a 1-1/8\" backset and not a normal mortise lock. The door hangs on **offset pivots**, top and bottom, not butt hinges \u2014 the whole weight of the leaf rides the bottom pivot bearing, and when that bearing wears the door drops. The glass sits in the frame behind removable **glazing stops** on setting blocks, and on a storefront those stops face *outward*, which matters more than it sounds. Inside, egress is a **thumbturn** on a deadlock or a **paddle** on a deadlatch \u2014 one motion, retract, walk out.\n\n[[img:ar-4710-deadlatch|Narrow-stile deadlatch: main spring latchbolt plus the small auxiliary deadlocking plunger. The plunger rides the strike face to keep the latch from being shoved back \u2014 that's the whole anti-pry story.]]\n\nThe lock is one of two Adams Rite families and you have to know which you're looking at. A **deadlatch** (the 4710, or the 4711 with the radius armored faceplate) is spring-loaded \u2014 it latches on its own when the door closes, and a little auxiliary plunger deadlocks it *if* the door hangs square. An **MS deadlock** (the 1850S) is a keyed, spring-less bolt you have to throw \u2014 a straight bolt or, better, a **hookbolt**. Don't underrate that bolt on its own: it's an eight-ply laminated stainless steel bolt with an alumina-ceramic center ply built specifically to kill a hacksaw, including the rod-type \"super\" saws \u2014 so once it's thrown, cutting *through* it isn't the play, and the attack shifts to the stile, the strike, or the glass. On a *pair* of doors there's a fourth thing to look at: the **mid-gap** where the two leaves meet, usually with no mullion, just an astragal and flush bolts on the inactive leaf. That gap is a doorway all its own.\n\n[[img:photo-adams-rite-ms1850|Adams Rite MS1850S deadlock. Keyed, no spring \u2014 the laminated bolt (straight or hookbolt) has to be thrown. A hookbolt resists a pair being spread, which a spring latch cannot.]]\n\nNow the map. Every attack in this series exploits one of the things I just named, and here's the whole menu so you can see the shape of it:\n\n**Loiding** \u2014 shimming the spring latch back with a flat tool. Only works on a deadlatch, and only when the door has sagged out of square so the anti-loid plunger drops into the strike cavity instead of riding its face. This is the classic \"somebody popped it with a screwdriver,\" and nine times out of ten the real fault is a dropped door, not a bad lock. Covered in depth in the **Adams Rite storefront failures** guide \u2014 read that one for the sag diagnosis.\n\n**Cutting** \u2014 the aluminum stile and the glazing stops are soft. A saw, a cutoff wheel, even aggressive prying can open the stile or pop the outward-facing stops and drop the glass right out of the frame without breaking it. Loud, but fast.\n\n**Break-and-reach** \u2014 smash the lite, or the small vision panel, reach in, and simply *use the code-required egress hardware from the outside*. The thumbturn or paddle that the fire code demands is the burglar's best friend \u2014 one motion, and he's using it through a hole in your glass. This is the attack the whole opening is built to allow.\n\n**Cylinder attacks** \u2014 the mortise cylinder in the stile gets wrenched, plugged, pulled, or spun out when the retainer screw has walked loose. A proud, unguarded cylinder in a thin stile is a grab handle.\n\n**Prying and spreading** \u2014 on a pair, jack the two leaves apart at the mid-gap until a straight latchbolt clears its keeper, or pry the lock stile off the strike jamb. A spring latch and a short bolt throw lose this fight; a hookbolt reaching into the strike \u2014 and its over-center MS action that resists a pull up, down, or sideways \u2014 plus a real astragal, wins it.\n\nHere's the mindset that ties it together, and it's the thing that separates a parts-changer from someone worth calling back. You are never going to make a glass door burglar-proof \u2014 anybody willing to make enough noise gets through glass. So you don't sell \"unbreakable.\" You sell **time and noise**, layered, because a smash-and-grab is a stopwatch decision: he takes what he can grab in the seconds before the alarm brings a car. Every layer you add \u2014 a hookbolt so there's nothing to loid or spread, a laminated ceramic-core bolt he can't saw, a latch guard over the gap, a longer bolt throw, security glazing or laminated glass so the lite holds together on its PVB interlayer and the reach-through turns into loud minutes instead of quiet seconds, a cylinder that sits flush with a set screw locked in \u2014 each one buys seconds and forces noise. You're not building a wall. You're making his own stopwatch tell him to move on to an easier door. That's the whole game, and every guide that follows is one more way to run his clock out.",id:"storefront-anatomy-threats",title:"Aluminum storefront: the opening and every way it fails",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",sources:["https://idighardware.com/2011/02/single-operation-egress/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IBC2021P2/chapter-10-means-of-egress","https://up.codes/s/door-operations","https://buildershardware.com/Portals/0/Files/codes-in-context/Clarifying%20One%20Releasing%20Motion%20for%20Doors%20in%20the%20Means%20of%20Egress%20(1).pdf","https://www.kawneer.com/products/doors-and-entrances/190-350-500-standard-entrances/","https://www.kawneer.com/kawneer_files/shared%20files/97911-Arch_Manual/ADMA010EN.pdf","https://www.crossaluminum.com/products/narrow-stile-aluminum-door/","https://crossaluminum.com/narrowstile.html","https://installixwnd.ca/blog/commercial-aluminum-door-sagging/","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.riotglass.com/who-we-serve/retail-storefronts/","https://www.defenselite.com/blog/looting-prevention-storefront-glass-how-to-protect-your-store-in-a-crisis/","https://www.scgma.com/laminated-glass-pvb-and-sgp-interlayers/"],relatedGuides:["storefront-loiding","storefront-cutting","storefront-glass-reach","storefront-cylinder-attacks","storefront-prying-spreading","adams-rite-sagging-doors","commercial-security-survey","door-hardening"],tags:["Stile","Hookbolt","Deadlatch","Opening","Astragal","Loiding","Strike","Mortise lock"],aliases:["narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","anti-shim","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","lockout","locked out","get in","emergency opening","credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","2-3/8","2-3/4","distance to center","wrong backset","strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"storefront-loiding",title:"Loiding & latch shimming on storefront deadlatches",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",body:"Loiding is the oldest trick there is, and it works on exactly one thing: a spring latch. Understand that and you understand the whole attack. A spring latchbolt has a bevel \u2014 an angled face \u2014 so the door can slam shut without you turning a key; the bevel hits the strike lip, the bolt rides back, then the spring shoves it into the pocket. That convenience is the vulnerability. Anything flat and stiff that can reach the bevel from outside can do to that bolt exactly what the strike lip does \u2014 push on the angle and walk it back \u2014 except now the door swings open instead of closed. Slipping that flat thing into the gap and riding the bevel is loiding. Carding is the same move with a plastic card. The guys who do it for a living carry a purpose-made \"assault card\" or a shove-knife \u2014 a thin spring-steel blade, sometimes notched \u2014 but a putty knife, a paint scraper, a strip of mica, or a cut-down political yard sign all do the job. It is quiet, it leaves no marks, and on a tired storefront door it takes about four seconds.\n\n[[diagram:loiding-latch-geometry|The bevel is the attack surface... and a misaligned deadlatch is just a spring latch again]]\n\nNow, the Adams Rite deadlatch \u2014 the 4710/4510 family \u2014 was built specifically to kill this attack, and it does, when it's set up right. The trick is that little auxiliary plunger riding next to the main latchbolt: when the door hangs square, the plunger sits depressed against the face of the strike, and being depressed is what mechanically deadlocks the main bolt so it physically cannot be shimmed back. Adams Rite's own catalog language is blunt about it \u2014 the auxiliary bolt \"deadlocks the latch bolt to prevent loiding or case-knife entry.\" That is the entire security story of a deadlatch. It is a spring latch that stops being shimmable the instant it's closed on a good strike. So when you get the call \"somebody carded my door,\" you are not looking at a defeated lock. You are looking at a deadlatch whose plunger was not doing its job \u2014 and there are only a handful of reasons for that.\n\nBefore you chase any of them, there is a five-second test that tells you which world you are in. With the door closed, push it inward in its frame and watch the latch. If the latch gives and slides back, the plunger is not deadlocking and that door is shimmable right now. If the latch stays put, the plunger is riding the strike face and doing its job. Do that test on every deadlatch you touch, because the failure is invisible otherwise... the door latches, feels normal, and looks perfect on a walk-by.\n\nReason one, and the big one: the door sagged. (A weak or gummed-up plunger spring belongs on the same list: if the plunger does not spring back out to meet the strike face, it cannot be depressed by it, and the latch stops being deadlocked. That one is a latch replacement, not an alignment fix.) The lock stile drops an eighth of an inch, the plunger stops landing on the strike face and falls into the strike cavity right beside the latchbolt, and the moment it's no longer depressed the main bolt is just a naked spring latch again. Card it and it's open. I'm not going to re-teach the sag here \u2014 read the companion guide, \"Adams Rite storefront failures: loose cylinders and sagging pivot doors,\" for why narrow-stile doors rack into parallelograms and how to pull them back square with the tie-rods and pivots. Just carry this in your head: on a deadlatch, sag and loiding are the same failure showing up in two places. Reason two: the door gap is too wide. The 4710 is rated for a maximum operational door gap around 5 mm \u2014 worn weatherstrip, a bent stile, or a strike shimmed out too far opens that gap, and a wide gap both lets the shim in easy and can let the plunger miss the strike edge. Reason three: the plunger itself is worn, gummed, or broken \u2014 they do wear a flat and stick in. Reason four, which I see more than I'd like: some hack drilled the strike prep too big or used a full-lip open strike, so the plunger drops into the same hole as the bolt. On a deadlatch the strike has to give the latchbolt its pocket while keeping the plunger up on solid metal.\n\nHere's how you inspect for it, and it's the same test the burglar runs. Close and lock the door. Get down and look at the two bolts through the gap: the fat latchbolt should be in its pocket and the skinny plunger should be pinned flat against the strike face, not hanging in air. Now push on the latchbolt with a screwdriver or a stiff shim \u2014 if a properly deadlocked bolt is set right, it will not move a hair. If it walks back, that plunger isn't landing, full stop. Don't fix the strike first. Fix the door.\n\nDefense, in the order that actually holds. First, square the door \u2014 tie-rod tension, pivots, glass, per the failures guide \u2014 because a plunger that lands on the strike is free security you already own. Second, set the gap and the strike so the plunger stays depressed on solid metal through the full swing; shim the strike vertically until the latchbolt gets full throw and the plunger is solidly up. Third, if the opening lives on a rough street or the gap can't be perfected, add a latch guard \u2014 Don-Jo and Keedex make them. On a storefront this matters: most storefront egress doors swing out \u2014 an egress door serving 50-plus occupants has to swing in the direction of travel \u2014 so the latch and the gap face the street where the attacker stands, and you want the outswing angle/wrap style latch protector (Don-Jo LP/ALP series) that armors over the edge and the bolt gap so no card or knife can reach the bevel. Confirm door material and stile width before you order \u2014 aluminum narrow-stile guards are a different geometry than the hollow-metal ones. Fourth, and this is the real upgrade for a door that keeps getting loided: stop asking a spring bolt to be the security and move the customer to a keyed Adams Rite MS deadlock \u2014 the MS1850S. A deadbolt has no bevel and no spring, so there's nothing to ride back at all, and the MS \"maximum security\" over-center action means prying the door up, down, or sideways only cams the bolt in harder. The bolt is eight-ply laminated stainless with an alumina-ceramic center core that eats hacksaw blades, even the rod-type \"super\" saws. On a single swinging leaf that's the straight bolt; on a pair or a sliding leaf you spec the hookbolt version, because the hook throws over-center into the frame and also resists the two leaves being spread apart \u2014 which is the other way pairs get beaten.\n\nOne code gotcha before you spec that deadlock: a storefront door is a means of egress, and the IBC is blunt about it \u2014 the egress side has to be readily openable without a key, special knowledge, or effort (\xa71010.1.9), and unlatching everything can't take more than one operation (\xa71010.1.9.5, \"Unlatching,\" in the 2018 code; the 2021 edition tightened \"one operation\" to one releasing motion in a single linear or rotational direction and renumbered it 1010.2.1). You cannot bolt a keyed-both-sides deadbolt onto an egress door and walk away. If you add or convert to an MS deadlock, the egress side gets a thumbturn \u2014 or the lock is tied into the exit device \u2014 so leaving is still one motion. Get that wrong and you've traded a loiding problem for a life-safety violation and your own liability. Fix the sag, keep the plunger on the strike, guard the latch, and upgrade the ones that keep failing to a hookbolt or straight MS bolt \u2014 that's the whole playbook.\n\nOne piece of vocabulary, because it is the kind of thing that gets you corrected. Many commercial deadlatches, Adams Rite included, let you hold the latch retracted so the door swings freely both ways during business hours. That is the **hold-back**, or night-latch, function. It is not dogging. Dogging is specifically an exit-device term... holding a panic bar's latch retracted. Same idea, different hardware, and using dogging for a deadlatch marks you as not fluent to anyone who lives in exit devices.",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches","https://www.allmar.com/products/data/uploads/Adams_Rite_4710_catalogue.pdf","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlatches/4510-standard-duty-deadlatch","https://www.don-jo.com/wp-content/uploads/LatchProtectors.pdf","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://idighardware.com/2011/02/single-operation-egress/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IBC2018/chapter-10-means-of-egress/IBC2018-Ch10-Sec1010.1.9.5","https://up.codes/s/door-operations"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","adams-rite-sagging-doors","door-hardening"],tags:["Loiding","Deadlatch","Dogging","Stile","Hookbolt","Hold-back","Strike","Exit device"],aliases:["credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","anti-shim","deadlatching plunger","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","crash bar","push bar","Von Duprin","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"storefront-cutting",title:"Cutting the bolt: saws, grinders & the door gap",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",body:"Every pair of aluminum storefront doors has one honest weakness, and it is not the lock \u2014 it is the seam down the middle where the two leaves meet. That mid-gap, and the jamb gap on a single door, is where a blade goes in. You spend all day worrying about picks and pry bars, but the crews that hit strip malls and standalone shops after close are not finesse artists. They bring a saw. And a saw does not care how good your cylinder is, because it never touches the cylinder \u2014 it reaches through the gap and cuts the bolt in half. Understand that, and you stop selling locks and start selling gap control, which is the thing that actually holds.\n\n[[diagram:storefront-gap-attack|Where the blade goes, why crews prefer it to picking, and what actually closes the seam]]\n\nWalk the three tools with me, slowest to nastiest, because you need to know what each one beats. The hand hacksaw is the quiet one \u2014 a guy standing at the seam of a pair, blade dropped into that gap, working the throw of an Adams Rite MS deadbolt. It is patient and it is silent, and that silence is exactly what the MS bolt was built to defeat. Then the reciprocating saw \u2014 a Sawzall \u2014 with a bi-metal or, worse, a carbide-tipped metal blade. Bi-metal is the economy blade, high-speed-steel teeth welded to a spring-steel body; carbide-tipped is the one that ruins your day, because the carbide teeth grind through hardened steel and last many times longer doing it. A cordless recip in that gap is maybe fifteen, twenty seconds of noise and the bolt is gone. And the angle grinder with a thin cut-off wheel \u2014 that is the loud, sparking, few-seconds answer to anything metal. It throws a light show and screams, but if nobody is listening, it wins. Grinder or carbide recip: those two beat everything on the market eventually. Your job is to make \"eventually\" long enough and loud enough that they walk.\n\nHere is the piece most apprentices get wrong, so get it right. The Adams Rite MS deadlock \u2014 the MS1850S you'll install more than any other \u2014 has a real anti-saw defense built into the bolt, but it is specifically an *anti-hacksaw* defense, and that distinction is the whole lesson. Adams Rite builds the bolt as eight-ply laminated stainless steel, and the center ply carries an alumina-ceramic core \u2014 their own literature says it defeats any hacksaw attack, including rod-type \"super\" hacksaws. When a saw tooth bites into that ceramic, it skates. Alumina is harder than the steel teeth, so the blade polishes it and goes nowhere. That is genuine, and it defeats the quiet patient guy at the seam every single time. What it does *not* do is stop a grinder or a carbide blade, because those cut by abrasion and don't care that the core is ceramic \u2014 they turn steel and ceramic alike into dust. So when a customer or a rep tells you the MS bolt is \"saw-proof,\" you correct them cleanly: it is hacksaw-proof, and Adams Rite claims exactly that much and no more. It is worth real money against a silent attack, but it is not grinder-proof, and nothing that fits inside a bolt is. The defense against the power tools is not a harder bolt. It is closing the gap the blade needs and moving the cut somewhere the geometry fights back.\n\nThat is where the hardware conversation actually lives. Start with the bolt shape. A straight MS deadbolt throws about 1-3/8\" of laminated steel across the gap \u2014 strong, but it sits square in that mid-gap of a pair, both faces exposed to a blade dropped straight down the seam. Swap to the hookbolt version (the MS1850S-X5X family, the one Adams Rite lists for sliding and narrow-stile pair conditions) and two things change. The hook wraps the strike, so a pry bar can't spread the leaves apart or lift the door off its track \u2014 and that hooked geometry means a saw in the gap has to make an awkward, deeper cut against a curved section instead of a clean square nick. Hookbolts are the default answer on sliding and pair conditions for exactly this reason.\n\nThen close the gap so the blade can't get to the bolt at all. On a pair, that is an astragal. An overlapping astragal mounts to one leaf and laps over the edge of the other, covering the seam from outside \u2014 now there is metal, not air, where the saw wants to go, and the latch or bolt is shielded on the secure side. For a hardened job, a security astragal (an interlocking two-piece, one leg on each mating leaf, running full height) both stops prying and denies the saw a straight shot at the throw. On the deadlatch or the strike side of a single door, a latch guard \u2014 a steel plate spanning the gap and running six-ish inches above and below the latch \u2014 covers the same vulnerability at the jamb. And buy engagement while you're at it: full throw properly adjusted so the bolt is deep in the strike, plus second- and third-point locking bolts on tall pairs, so cutting one point doesn't open the door.\n\nOne caveat you never skip: whatever you add to close that gap has to respect egress. Under the life-safety code \u2014 IBC 1010.2 \u2014 the door on the exit side has to unlatch with not more than one releasing motion, and it has to be readily openable from the egress side without a key, special knowledge, or effort. An astragal or guard that adds a second operation, or that jams up free egress, fails the fire marshal and gets somebody killed. So keep the hardening on the security side and keep the push-to-exit clean and single-motion. If this door is also throwing \"my key just spins\" or sitting crooked in the frame, that is a different failure \u2014 see the \"Adams Rite storefront failures\" guide for the loose set screw and the sagging pivot, because a bolt that isn't seating fully is a cutting target that's already halfway open.\n\nSo what do you sell to stop the saw? The MS deadlock for the silent hacksaw guy \u2014 real defense, name it honestly and name its limit. Then a hookbolt over a straight bolt on every pair, an overlapping or interlocking security astragal to erase the gap the blade needs, latch guards on the deadlatch and jamb, full bolt throw, and multi-point locking on tall leaves. You are not out-hardening the grinder. You are taking away its reach and making the cut ugly, slow, and loud \u2014 which is how you win this one in the real world.",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://nationallocksupply.com/adams-rite-ms1850s-ms-deadlock-w-flat-faceplate/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IBC2021P1/chapter-10-means-of-egress/IBC2021P1-Ch10-Sec1010.2","https://www.pemko.com/en/products/astragals-meeting-stiles/t-overlapping-and-security-astragals","https://www.silvaconsultants.com/using-latch-guards-and-astragals","https://www.mkmorse.com/products/reciprocating-saw-blades/","https://www.allglassparts.com/door-latch-protectors-guards-astragals/"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","adams-rite-sagging-doors","door-hardening"],tags:["MS deadbolt","Astragal","Hookbolt","Deadlatch","Free egress","Stile","Strike","Cylinder"],aliases:["Adams Rite MS1850","storefront deadbolt","narrow stile deadbolt","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","anti-shim","plunger","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"storefront-glass-reach",title:"Break-and-reach: glass and the thumbturn/paddle",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",body:"Here's the attack that beats more storefronts than every pick and every bypass tool combined, and it needs no skill: pick up a rock, smash the glass next to the lock, reach through, flip the thumbturn or push the paddle, walk in. Break-and-reach. It works because on a code-legal storefront exit, the thing that lets a trapped person get *out* in a hurry is the same thing that lets a hand reaching through a hole get *in*. You can't design that tension away \u2014 the code won't let you, and it shouldn't \u2014 so your whole game is making the reach fail before it starts.\n\n[[diagram:break-and-reach|The vulnerability and the egress requirement are the same piece of hardware, so you attack the reach instead]]\n\nFirst, get the life-safety code right, because this is where hacks get people killed and get you sued. On the egress side of an occupied building, the door has to open **without a key, without special knowledge, and without special effort.** The 2021 IBC nails it down further: releasing all the latching and locking has to take **not more than one releasing motion in a single direction** \u2014 one push, one turn. A thumbturn passes. A push-paddle passes. A Von Duprin 99 or a Detex panic device passes \u2014 that's the whole point of a paddle. What does *not* pass is a **double-cylinder deadbolt** \u2014 key both sides \u2014 on a door people exit through. That's the \"solution\" every scared shop owner asks you for after a break-in: \"just put a lock that needs a key on the inside too, then reaching through does nothing.\" And they're right that it stops the reach. It also means the last person in a smoke-filled room is fumbling for a key on a ring in the dark. It's an egress violation on an occupied exit, full stop.\n\nThere is one narrow, much-abused exception, and you need to know it cold so you can say no intelligently. IBC lets the **main exterior door** of a Group B, F, M, or S occupancy (offices, retail, storage), a Group A assembly of 300 or fewer, or a place of religious worship carry a **key-operated lock on the egress side** \u2014 but only if the lock is readily distinguishable as locked, and there's a **durable sign, 1-inch letters on a contrasting background,** on or adjacent to it reading **\"THIS DOOR TO REMAIN UNLOCKED WHEN THIS SPACE IS OCCUPIED.\"** It applies to the *main* door only, not side or rear exits, and the building official can revoke it the day they decide it's being abused. Translation: it exists so a shop can key-lock the front when *closed and empty*, not so you can leave a double-cylinder engaged during business hours. If a customer wants a double cylinder, that thumbturn comes off only after close and goes back to a thumbturn \u2014 or a paddle \u2014 the moment they unlock in the morning. Don't spec a defeat of egress as a security feature. Verify the occupancy and the AHJ's stance before you touch it.\n\nSo if you can't lock the reach out with a key, you lock the *hand* out of the glass. That's where the real defenses live, and they're what you actually sell.\n\n**Laminated glass** is the gold standard. A PVB interlayer bonds two lites together, so a hammer cracks the surface but the pane **holds as a membrane** \u2014 no hole, no shards, no reach. It's true security glazing, and with a 0.030-inch-plus interlayer it also passes CPSC 16 CFR 1201 / ANSI Z97.1 safety glazing, so it does double duty. It's what you spec on a full re-glaze or new build. The catch is cost and lead time: you're replacing the lite, and on a big storefront that runs into real money and weeks of waiting.\n\nFor the far more common retrofit \u2014 existing annealed or tempered glass, customer doesn't want to re-glaze \u2014 you sell **security window film, 8-mil minimum**, and you sell the **attachment system with it or you've sold nothing.** Bare film stuck to the glass just holds the shards together in a sheet that a burglar shoves out of the frame in one piece. The film has to be anchored to the frame with a **wet-glaze structural bead (a silicone like Dow 995) or a mechanical batten** so the cracked pane stays *captured in the opening.* Done right, 8-mil-plus with a proper wet-glaze attachment turns a three-second smash into a noisy, sustained fight against a rubbery, cracked membrane \u2014 seconds stretch toward a minute, and 30 to 90 seconds is all you usually need for the alarm to trip, the video to roll, and the guy to quit. Thicker film \u2014 12-to-14-mil, the tier that's tested for sustained forced entry under ASTM F1233/F588 \u2014 buys minutes. Set the expectation honestly: film delays and deters, it does not make glass unbreakable.\n\nThen layer the cheap stuff that makes the reach pointless even if the glass does open. **Move the operable hardware out of arm's reach** of any pane a hand can breach \u2014 relocate the thumbturn, or put a **thumbturn guard** (a shrouded cover) over it so a groping hand can't get purchase. **Monitored acoustic glass-break sensors** wired to the alarm catch the smash the instant it happens, not when the door finally opens \u2014 pair it with the film delay and you've turned dead time into response time. And **keep a good hookbolt** underneath all of it: a hookbolt MS deadlock \u2014 Adams Rite MS1850S family \u2014 has no spring to knock back, it physically hooks the strike so a pried or lifted door can't spread off the jamb, and it takes a full 360\xb0 turn to throw. It also answers the next threat over: that bolt is eight-ply laminated stainless with an **alumina-ceramic center ply that eats a hacksaw** \u2014 including a rod-type \"super\" saw \u2014 so a hand or a blade coming through the glass can't just cut the bolt, it has to find and work the turn. That ties straight back to the \"Adams Rite storefront failures\" guide \u2014 a sagged, loided deadlatch is *already* a hand-alone entry with no glass broken at all, so fix the sag and the loose cylinder first or the fancy glazing is protecting a door that opens itself.\n\nWhat you're really selling is a **stack**, and you pitch it as one: laminated glass or 8-mil film-plus-attachment so the pane holds, a thumbturn guard or relocation so the reach finds nothing, a monitored glass-break sensor so the smash calls for help, and a sound saw-resistant hookbolt so a hand alone isn't enough \u2014 all while the egress hardware stays legal single-motion no-key. Never the double cylinder on an occupied exit. That's the difference between a locksmith who hardens an opening and one who just sold the customer a lawsuit.",sources:["https://idighardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/2021-IBC-Key-Operated-Locks.pdf","https://idighardware.com/2025/07/decoded-key-operated-locking-devices/","https://up.codes/s/door-operations","https://buildershardware.com/Portals/0/Files/codes-in-context/2023/Clarifying%20Not%20More%20Than%20One%20Releasing%20Motion%20for%20Doors%20in%20the%20Means%20of%20Egress.pdf","https://coderedconsultants.com/insights/means-of-egress-door-locking/","https://blog.americanwindowfilm.com/best-security-film-thickness-for-storefronts","https://installixwnd.ca/blog/security-film-for-storefronts-4mil-vs-8mil-vs-12mil-a-smash-and-grab-guide/","https://www.windowfilmdepot.com/blog/the-security-window-film-specification-mistakes-that-create-real-risk/","https://www.riotglass.com/solutions-window-film-vs-security-glass/","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://syracuseglass.com/E-DOCS/Architectural%20Glass/EDOCS/CPSC%2016CFR%201201%20Category%20I%20Laminated%20Glass.pdf"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","adams-rite-sagging-doors","door-hardening"],tags:["Hookbolt","Deadlatch","AHJ","Cylinder","Opening","Bypass","Strike"],aliases:["hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","latch guard","anti-shim","plunger","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","get past the lock","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{body:"Every storefront lock in this trade lives or dies at one part \u2014 the mortise cylinder threaded into the edge of an Adams Rite MS deadlock. That little brass plug is the whole ballgame. Throw the bolt, retract the bolt, all of it runs off the cam on the back of that cylinder. And here's the ugly truth: the cylinder is the softest, most exposed, most attackable thing on the entire opening. A burglar who understands storefront doesn't pick your MS1850 \u2014 nobody picks anything at 2am \u2014 he goes after the cylinder three different ways. Learn all three cold, because the customer who just got hit is going to ask you \"how'd they get in,\" and your answer sells the fix.\n\n[[diagram:mortise-cylinder-attacks|Three ways at one brass plug, all closed by the same five-minute habit]]\n\nAttack one is the loose set screw, and we cover the mechanics of that failure in depth in the **Adams Rite storefront failures** guide \u2014 go read it. Short version for the attacker's side: an MS deadlock holds its 1-inch mortise cylinder with exactly one retainer set screw \u2014 a little #8-32 \u2014 seating into the groove around the cylinder body, driven from the door edge under the faceplate. When that screw walks loose from vibration and years of slamming \u2014 or was never snugged at install \u2014 the cylinder is free to spin. A thief who knows the trick grabs the cylinder face with a pipe wrench or channel-locks and simply **unthreads it right out of the door.** Cam's now exposed. He throws the bolt with a screwdriver in two seconds. No noise, no glass, no alarm. The customer's door was standing open and the lock looks untouched. This is the number-one way aluminum storefront gets beaten, and it's a fifty-cent screw's fault.\n\nAttack two is **cylinder pulling** \u2014 and this is where the body-shop tools come out. A dent puller or a slide-hammer is built to grab sheet metal and yank it toward you with inertia. Turn one on a lock cylinder and it does the exact same thing to the plug. The attacker screws a self-tapping stud into the keyway or bites the cylinder face with a gripper attachment, then hammers the slide back. That inertia either rips the whole cylinder out of the case \u2014 set screw be damned, it shears or strips \u2014 or pulls the plug clean out of the shell, again exposing the cam and the cavity behind it. It's fast and it's quick. On a proud cylinder \u2014 one sitting out past the door face because somebody grabbed the wrong length off the truck \u2014 the puller has a beautiful shoulder to grab. Flush cylinders give it much less to bite. The exposed lip is the enemy.\n\nAttack three is **snapping.** Now \u2014 I want you to file this one correctly. Cylinder snapping as an epidemic is a **euro-profile / European and residential** problem, not your daily storefront call in the States. The euro cylinder is that dogbone-shaped body held by a single fixing screw through the middle, and its weak neck at the cam means an attacker grips the exposed end, torques it, and snaps the cylinder in half at that screw \u2014 the outer half comes off, the cam's exposed, done. UK burglars did this so much it spawned a whole standard. But understand the **principle**, because it applies to any cylinder left proud: an exposed cylinder body is a lever, and a lever is leverage. A mortise cylinder standing a quarter inch out of an aluminum door isn't snapping like a euro, but it is a handle for a wrench and a shoulder for a puller. Same root cause \u2014 exposed metal to grab.\n\nSo now the defense, and this is what you actually put on the truck and sell.\n\n**Snug the set screw with blue threadlocker.** Every MS cylinder you touch, thread it to the correct depth so the cam clocks right (again \u2014 see the Adams Rite guide for depth), then a drop of blue Loctite 242 on the retainer screw before you seat it. Blue, not red \u2014 red (271) is high-strength permanent and you'll cook the case trying to service it later. Blue holds against vibration and still backs out with a driver. This one habit kills attack one and stiffens attack two for free.\n\n**Correct cylinder length \u2014 flush, never proud.** Measure the door and cam throw and spec the cylinder so the face sits flush with the ring or barely below. A flush cylinder gives the wrench nothing to grip and the puller a poor shoulder. This is spec discipline: proud cylinders are an install sin, not a mystery.\n\n**Cylinder guard collars and guard plates.** This is the money add-on. Adams Rite's own **MS4043 cylinder guard** is a hardened-steel ring, fully beveled so a tool can't get purchase and **free-swiveling** so a wrench or puller that does catch it just spins \u2014 no torque reaches the cylinder body. It also covers the exposed lip so there's nothing to bite, and drive a chisel behind it and the attacker has to drag a hardened plate through a round hole in the stile. GMS and Don-Jo make hardened spin collars in the same spirit. Tapered, hardened, free-spinning \u2014 that's the recipe. Cheap insurance, and it visibly upgrades the door so the customer sees what they're paying for.\n\n**Security-rated cylinders.** Step the customer up to a **UL 437** listed high-security cylinder \u2014 Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, ASSA, Abloy. And here's the part that sells attack two: UL 437 doesn't just test picking and drilling, it runs an actual **pull test** \u2014 a slam-hammer (three-pound head) and a screw-type puller \u2014 so a listed cylinder has hardened anti-drill pins, tighter tolerances, and a plug built to resist the exact dent-puller yank we just talked about. For euro applications where snapping is the real threat, you spec to that standard: **TS007 3-star** or **Sold Secure SS312 Diamond**, which build in a sacrificial break point so the outer section shears off but the interlocking cam stays protected and the lock still throws.\n\nOne life-safety line before you leave \u2014 cross-referenced from the Adams Rite guide and worth repeating: whatever you add on the outside, egress from the inside must stay **single-motion.** That's not a preference, it's IBC 1010.2 \u2014 unlatching for egress takes not more than one motion in a single linear or rotational direction, and that one motion has to release every latch and lock at once. A guard collar, a longer cylinder, a high-security core \u2014 none of it touches the inside thumbturn or exit device. One motion out, always, no key, no special knowledge. Harden the attack side; never trap a person on the egress side.",id:"storefront-cylinder-attacks",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",title:"Cylinder attacks: the loose set screw, pulling & snapping",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/accessories/ms4043-cylinder-guard","https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/accessories/4036-mortise-cylinder-4066-thumbturn","https://www.amazon.com/GMS-COL626D-Hardened-Cylinder-Security/dp/B07GRFGGZ9","https://securitysnobs.com/Abloy-Hardened-Rim-Mortise-Lock-Protective-Spin-Collar.html","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/UL_437","https://patcosta.com/ul-437-requirements/","https://idighardware.com/2011/02/single-operation-egress/","https://up.codes/s/door-operations","https://www.homesecureshop.co.uk/blogs/news/why-you-should-install-a-ts007-3-star-anti-snap-euro-cylinder-today","https://yalehome.co.uk/platinum-3-star-cylinder/"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","adams-rite-sagging-doors","door-hardening"],tags:["Cylinder","Cam","Mortise cylinder","Exit device","Plug","Stile","Shell","Opening"],aliases:["Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","panic bar","crash bar","push bar","Von Duprin","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","housing","lock body","lockout","locked out","emergency opening","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{body:"A pair of aluminum storefront doors looks locked. Both leaves are pulled to, the center feels solid, the customer hears the deadbolt throw. But a pair has a weakness a single doesn't: there's a seam right down the middle, at eye level, exactly where the lock is. And a straight deadbolt on that seam only holds if the two leaves stay where you left them. Spread them, or rack them out of square, and the bolt walks right out of its keeper. This is one of the most common ways a storefront pair gets beaten without breaking any glass, and once you see why, you'll spec against it on every quote.\n\nStart with the physics, because that's the whole thing. On most pairs, one leaf is the \"active\" leaf carrying the lock, and its deadbolt throws sideways into the meeting stile of the other leaf, or into a keeper mounted in the astragal between them. A standard Adams Rite MS straight bolt throws 1-3/8\". Sounds like plenty \u2014 until you remember part of that is eaten up crossing the gap between the two stiles. Real engagement into the keeper is often more like a half to five-eighths of an inch. Now picture a burglar sinking a flat bar or a wonder bar into that center seam, right at the lock, and levering the two leaves apart. Aluminum is soft and the leaves are only pinned top and bottom at the pivots. The gap opens. The moment it opens wider than the bolt is buried \u2014 half an inch, less \u2014 the bolt clears the keeper and the door swings. No cut, no pick, no noise but a groan of metal.\n\nRacking is the same defeat by a different hand. Instead of prying the seam straight apart, you shove the top of the leaf one way and lean on the bottom the other, and the door goes from a rectangle to a parallelogram. The meeting stiles slide out of line with each other, the bolt and its keeper no longer point at each other, and again the bolt is out. A door that's already tired \u2014 worn pivots, a dropped lock stile \u2014 is *pre-racked*. It's halfway defeated before anybody touches it, which is exactly why sag and security are the same conversation (see the companion guide, \"Adams Rite storefront failures: loose cylinders and sagging pivot doors\").\n\nThe single-door version is jamb spreading. There's no center seam, so the attack moves to the lock jamb: pry between the door edge and the frame at the deadbolt, and flex the aluminum frame away from the door. A shallow strike pocket and a short-thrown bolt make it trivial \u2014 pop the frame out three-eighths of an inch and the bolt is standing in open air.\n\nSo the short-throw straight deadbolt on a racked or floppy pair is the setup. Here's what you sell and install to take it away.\n\n[[img:photo-adams-rite-ms1850|The Adams Rite MS deadlock. The straight bolt (left) clears its keeper the instant the leaves spread; the hookbolt version throws a hook that catches behind the keeper, grabbing harder the more you pry.]]\n\n**Hookbolt first.** This is the single best answer to spreading, and it's a factory option, not a custom part \u2014 the MS1850S takes either a straight bolt or a laminated stainless *hookbolt*, and Adams Rite's own line is that the hook \"repels pry bar attempts to spread the door from its jamb or lift it off its track.\" The hook rotates out and catches *behind* the edge of the keeper, so when a bar tries to spread the leaves apart, the pulling motion loads the hook and drags it tighter into the strike instead of sliding it out. Don't get hung up on throw numbers here \u2014 the hook's linear throw is actually shorter than the straight bolt's, about 13/16\" against 1-3/8\" \u2014 because with a hook it's the grab that holds, not the reach. Either bolt, straight or hook, is eight-ply laminated stainless with an alumina-ceramic center ply built specifically to kill a hacksaw blade, including the rod-type \"super\" saws, so you're buying anti-saw and anti-spread in one part. On a pair, spec the hookbolt into the active leaf throwing into a keeper in the opposite stile. That one line-item change defeats the most common attack in this guide.\n\n**Tie the two leaves together with an astragal.** An overlapping or interlocking astragal down the meeting stiles closes the center seam so a bar can't get in to spread it, and it stops the leaves from moving independently. Just respect egress: on an occupied space the doors still have to release with not more than one motion in a single direction, no key, no special knowledge or sequence from the inside (2021 IBC 1010.2; NFPA 101 carries the same one-releasing-operation rule), so coordinate any astragal with the panic hardware \u2014 don't build yourself a code violation to stop a pry bar.\n\n**Make sure the bolt is actually throwing full.** A bolt that only engages a quarter inch because the cam's clocked wrong or the strike's too shallow is half-beaten from the factory. Confirm the full 1-3/8\" throw and a deep box strike or keeper that swallows all of it. (That cam-and-cylinder setup is covered in the companion guide \u2014 don't re-cut it here, just verify it.)\n\n**Keep the door square \u2014 pivots and tie-rods.** A door that can't rack can't be racked open. Service the top and bottom offset pivots so the leaf hangs true, and if the stile flexes, a tie-rod through the stile keeps the leaf rigid instead of folding into a parallelogram. Square doors and tight pivots are security hardware, not just fit-and-finish.\n\n**And where the opening earns it, a mullion.** For a high-loss pair, the cleanest fix is to stop treating it like a pair at all. Drop in a steel mullion between the leaves \u2014 a Von Duprin KR9954 keyed-removable mullion with rim exit devices gives you \"single-door performance in a double-door opening.\" Each leaf's latch throws into a rigid steel post you cannot spread, the center seam is gone, and it's still legal egress because each leaf has its own single-motion panic. It's keyed-removable, so the customer can pull it to move a couch through, then drop it back.\n\nPut it together on the quote: for most storefront pairs, upgrade the straight bolt to a hookbolt, add an astragal, set a deep keeper for full throw, and put the pivots and tie-rod right so the door stays square. For the openings that keep getting hit \u2014 jewelry, pharmacy, electronics \u2014 quote the removable mullion and rim panics. Every one of those defeats the spread, and every one is something you carry and install.",id:"storefront-prying-spreading",title:"Prying and spreading a pair of doors",module:"Storefront: Attacks & Defense",sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/en/products/deadlocks/ms1850S_series","https://www.cookandboardman.com/adams-rite-ms1850s-deadlock-hook-bolt/","https://www.vonduprin.com/en/products/options-accessories/mullions.html","https://www.americanlocksets.com/von-duprin-kr9954-5in-steel-mullion-prepped-for-two-499f-or-268-strikes-keyed-removable","https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IBC2021P1/chapter-10-means-of-egress/IBC2021P1-Ch10-Sec1010.2","https://idighardware.com/2022/08/2021-ibc-commentary-on-egress-doors-with-access-control/"],relatedGuides:["storefront-anatomy-threats","adams-rite-sagging-doors","door-hardening"],tags:["Keeper","Hookbolt","Astragal","Stile","Cam","Strike","NFPA 101","Opening"],aliases:["strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","hook bolt","sliding door lock","storefront hook","narrow stile","medium stile","wide stile","aluminum door rail","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{body:"Here's the thing about a camera - it's the easiest device on the job to defeat, because a thief doesn't have to hack it. He just has to stop it from seeing. Half the \"camera failures\" you'll get called out on aren't failures at all - somebody blinded, blocked, or cut the thing. Learn the attacks so you spec the layout that survives them.\n\n[[diagram:camera-attack-and-defence|Six ways a camera is defeated, and the mounting decision that answers each]]\n\n**Laser blinding.** This is the one that goes viral. Somebody points a laser at the dome and the footage whites out. Two very different outcomes here, and you need to keep them straight. Temporary whiteout is the common one - a $10 green pointer throws intense glare and internal reflections off the front glass, and the whole frame blows out for as long as the beam's on it. Move the beam, the picture comes back. That's flare, not damage. Green (around 520-532nm) shows up worse than red because the sensor and your eye both peak green, and it's ugly at night when the camera's on the IR-sensitive night mode. Permanent damage is a different animal - dead pixels, or a burn streak that shows up as a line across every recording from then on. But that takes real power, close range, steady aim, and time. Testing shows you basically don't get permanent burns past ~50 feet with common pointers - and even up close you need the beam held near dead-on into the lens, inside roughly 15 feet, to actually cook the chip. So a laser off the sidewalk is a nuisance; a determined guy on a ladder with a high-power unit locked on the lens can kill a sensor. Spec accordingly.\n\n**IR flooding.** Quieter attack, and it works on the exact cameras people trust for night coverage. At night a true day/night camera swings its IR-cut filter out and goes wide open to near-infrared. Point a strong 850nm illuminator - or these covert 940nm floods - straight at it and you wash the whole scene to milk. To your eye the attacker's standing in the dark; to the camera he's a walking supernova. 850nm carries a faint red telltale glow at the source; 940nm shows nothing at all, which is exactly why it's the covert choice even though it throws maybe 30-50% less range for the same power. Nasty because it's invisible and it targets your best money-shot camera when it's most vulnerable.\n\n**The dumb attacks that work anyway.** Spray paint, a strip of tape, a plastic bag, a sticker over the dome. A gob of grease. These are the most common by a mile because they need no skill and no gear. Same bucket: cutting the cable. On a PoE camera one snip kills power and video in one move - and if that run is exposed on the outside of the building, you've handed them the off switch. Analog and PoE both die the same way to a knife.\n\n**Now the defenses - this is where you earn the invoice.**\n\nOverlapping coverage from different angles. This is the whole game. One camera watching a door is one laser, one bag, one snip from blind. Two cameras covering the same zone from different directions means blinding one still leaves you a witness - and the second camera catches the guy reaching up to blind the first. Tie this back to the video-surveillance guide: you're already speccing a redundant overview camera separate from the money-shot. That overview isn't just for context, it's your anti-tamper insurance. Money-shot gets sprayed, the wide angle still shows who did it and when.\n\nCamera-tamper / blinding analytics. Nearly every real camera - Axis, Hanwha, Bosch, Avigilon, even mid-tier Hikvision/Dahua - ships video tampering detection that alarms on whiteout, blackout, defocus, and lens-covered or camera-moved events. It watches edge and scene content and trips when the picture suddenly goes flat. Here's the gotcha that gets integrators burned: on most cameras it's not enabled out of the box, and nobody ever turns it on. So the bag goes over the dome at 2am and nobody knows till someone pulls footage next week and finds a gray rectangle. Turn. It. On. Then tune the sensitivity so headlights and cloud shadows don't spam the client into disabling it - a shootout of these detectors will show you they false-trip wildly out of the box, and a client drowning in nuisance alerts just switches the whole thing off.\n\nPlacement. High and recessed. Get the lens up out of arm's and ladder's reach - a dome tucked into a soffit or behind vandal glass is a lot harder to spray, tape, or aim a steady laser at than one at 8 feet on a pole. Recessed also cuts the angle a ground-level laser can hit the sensor dead-on, and dead-on is what it takes to do real damage.\n\nCable protection. Run PoE in conduit, keep the whole path inside the secure perimeter, and put the switch/NVR behind a locked door. An exposed exterior cable run is a defeat waiting to happen.\n\nKnow your WDR limits. Wide dynamic range helps against a backlit doorway or headlights - real, everyday problems. It does not save you from a laser or an IR flood. WDR balances a scene with a bright region against a dark one; it can't recover a frame that's fully saturated to white. Don't let a salesman sell WDR as blinding protection. The fix for blinding is angles and analytics, not a menu setting.\n\nSpec it all together: overlapping fields of view, tamper analytics switched on and tuned, lenses up high and out of reach, cable in conduit, and always a redundant overview backing the money-shot. Defeat one layer, the next one's still watching.",id:"camera-defense",title:"Blinding, tampering & defending cameras",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",tags:["Camera","CCTV","Laser blinding","IR flooding","Tamper detection","Camera placement","PoE","WDR"],sources:["https://www.ilda.com/camera-sensor-damage.htm","https://www.laserpointersafety.com/ilda-camera-info.html","https://buysecuritycameras.com.au/can-a-laser-pointer-damage-a-cctv-camera-myth-or-reality/","https://www.schneier.com/blog/archives/2019/08/disabling_secur.html","https://ipvm.com/reports/tamper-detection-shootout","How camera tampering detection enhances surveillance security systems (trueye.io) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.mdpi.com/1424-8220/15/5/10315","https://tech-led.com/850nm-vs-940nm-infrared-leds-night-vision-covert-illumination-comparison/","https://ellipsesecurity.com/2018/05/850nm-vs-940nm-ir-illuminator/","https://www.a2zsecuritycameras.com/lighting-for-security-cameras-wdr-ir-color-at-night-guide/"],relatedGuides:["video-surveillance","commercial-security-survey"],aliases:["CCTV","IP camera","bullet cam","camera system","DVR","camera blinding","laser pointer attack","infrared blinding","IR washout","night vision defeat","cover removed","field of view","mounting height","blind spot","power over ethernet","802.3af","802.3at","injector","switch port power","backlight","blown out image"]},{id:"maglocks",title:"Electromagnetic locks (maglocks): holding force, wiring, fail-safe & code",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A maglock is the dumbest device in access control and that's exactly why people screw it up. There's no bolt, no latch, no moving parts. It's an electromagnet \u2014 a coil of wire around a steel core \u2014 bolted to the door frame, and a flat steel plate called the armature bolted to the door. Energize the coil, it grabs the armature, door won't open. Kill the power, the magnetism dies, door opens. That's the whole machine. What makes it a real job is not the hardware, it's the code that governs when it's allowed to let go.\n\n[[diagram:maglock-holding-and-release|How it holds, why there is no fail-secure version, and the series chain that drops it]]\n\nHow it holds: current through the coil makes a magnetic field, the field pulls the armature flat against the magnet face. The rating you see \u2014 600 lb or 1200 lb \u2014 is the pull force in a straight line, magnet face to armature face, clean and flush. A 1200 lb lock is the standard workhorse for a single perimeter or interior door. The 600 lb is a lighter unit for interior doors, cabinets, gates \u2014 anywhere you don't need to stop a shoulder. Understand the rating is theoretical: it assumes perfect contact. A bent armature, a shim of paint, a warped door, dirt on the face \u2014 any air gap \u2014 and holding force drops off a cliff. Same reason the armature is mounted on a rubber-shouldered sex bolt: it has to be able to pivot a hair and self-align to the magnet, or it never makes full contact.\n\nSingle vs double is just count \u2014 one magnet for one leaf, a double is one long housing with two magnet faces for a pair of doors. Holding force also climbs by model: Securitron's surface line runs M32, M62, M82 at rising strength, with the M62 at 1200 lb and the M82 at 1800 lb for a heavier or higher-abuse single opening. Then there's the shear lock, a different animal. Instead of pulling face-to-face, a shear lock's magnet and armature interlock with alloy locking tabs and resist a shearing (sideways) force \u2014 SDC's HiShear 1560 comes in 2000 and 2700 lb versions, and Dynalock's concealed units are in the same class. You mortise it into the top of the frame and door so it's invisible, and it works on doors that swing both directions. The catch: shear locks need tight alignment tolerances and usually a short energize/de-energize delay to release cleanly. More to go wrong. Use them when concealment or a double-swing demands it, not by default.\n\nNow the thing that dictates everything: a maglock is fail-safe by nature. No power, no lock. There is no fail-secure maglock \u2014 physically impossible, no current means no field. That single fact drives every decision downstream. It means a maglock can never be the only thing holding a door people escape through unless the code-required releases are wired in, because in a fire when the power or the panel dies, that door has to be openable. It also means a maglock is not a security lock the way a deadbolt is \u2014 cut power, cut the wire, drop the breaker, and it's open. That's why on the M62-style units the coil is fully potted and the whole thing mounts on the secure side with concealed wiring \u2014 to make tampering harder, since the lock itself gives you nothing once de-energized.\n\nMounting: the magnet lives on the frame header, armature on the door, both as close to the latch edge (strike side) as you can get for maximum leverage against a pry. Doors are never square to the header, so you fill the gap with brackets \u2014 an L-bracket drops the armature down for inswing doors, a Z-bracket (L plus a Z) reaches around for outswing, and filler/spacer plates take up depth. The goal every time: magnet face and armature face parallel and flush at rest. Sloppy bracket, you never get rated hold.\n\nPower: maglocks run 12 or 24 VDC, field-selectable on most. Draw varies by make \u2014 a Securitron M62 is on the efficient end at 250 mA at 12V and 150 mA at 24V, while plenty of generic 1200 lb units pull closer to half an amp at 12V and a quarter amp at 24V. Bumping to 24V roughly halves the current, and depending on the design the wattage stays about the same or a touch higher, so the wire and the supply see less current the higher you go. Size the supply for the sum of every lock's inrush plus margin, and remember the code-required release cuts power, so your PSU and any battery backup are life-safety wiring, not convenience. Size the copper too. A maglock is a steady current draw at the end of a long run, so 18 AWG is the floor on a short pull and 16 or 14 AWG is what a long one needs \u2014 a lock that under-holds on a door with clean, flush contact is almost always starving, not weak. Prove it with the meter at the lock while it's energized, not at the supply: a run that reads a clean 12 V open-circuit can sag well under that once the coil is pulling, and that sag is the diagnosis. Low-voltage wiring & power has the drop math and the gauge-versus-distance rules. What that math gets judged against is the lock's own stated minimum, not the nominal 12 V on the box... a 12 V unit typically stops holding somewhere around ten and a half, and the number that matters is the one on that model's datasheet. Deliver less than it at the end of the run and the lock does not fail cleanly, it holds weakly and lets go when somebody leans on the door, which is the intermittent nobody enjoys chasing. Across the coil you'll see suppression \u2014 the collapsing magnetic field kicks back a voltage spike every release that, over thousands of cycles, chews up power supplies and controller relays. Good maglocks have internal MOV suppression already. Watch two traps: adding an external MOV backwards, or adding a reverse diode across the coil, both cause a \"sticky\" lock \u2014 the diode recirculates the coil current on release and drags out the drop time so the door won't open promptly. Sticky maglock, check what somebody put across the coil first.\n\nMost units include a bond sensor and a door-position switch. The bond sensor tells you the armature is actually magnetized and seated (real hold, not just powered). The DPS tells you the door is physically closed. Both feed the panel so you know secured-vs-not, and so you can alarm a forced or propped door.\n\nCode is the whole point, and it's non-negotiable. Under NFPA 101 and the IBC, a maglock on a means of egress must release on fire alarm, on sprinkler activation, and on loss of power, and stay released until the fire system is manually reset. Those live in NFPA 101's access-controlled egress door provisions and the IBC's special locking sections \u2014 and the IBC section numbers move between editions, so quote the edition your AHJ has actually adopted rather than a number you memorized. Low-voltage wiring & power breaks the renumbering down edition by edition. There are two legal ways to do the everyday egress release. Scheme one, sensor release (\"access-controlled egress\" / special locking arrangement): a REX motion sensor over the door drops the lock when someone approaches, plus a manual push-to-exit button mounted 40 to 48 inches above the floor and within 5 feet of the door, labeled PUSH TO EXIT, that directly interrupts power to the lock independent of all other electronics and holds it off at least 30 seconds. Scheme two, delayed egress: push the door, an alarm sounds, and after applying force (up to 15 lbf) for no more than 3 seconds an irreversible timer starts and the lock releases in 15 seconds (30 where the AHJ approves). Note the 3-second figure \u2014 NFPA and the 2015-and-later IBC both allow up to 3 seconds of applied force to start the process; the older 1-second number came from pre-2015 IBC editions, so if a spec or an inspector cites 1 second, they're on an old code. Delayed egress is still barred from Assembly, Educational, and High-Hazard (Group H) occupancies, with only narrow exceptions added in the 2018 IBC \u2014 classroom doors where the room's occupant load is under 50, and secondary courtroom egress doors in fully sprinklered buildings. Either way, a panic bar or RTE integrated into the door's own hardware is the belt-and-suspenders that many AHJs want, because it's mechanical and never depends on the electronics.\n\nTwo last field realities. Residual magnetism: even de-energized, a little magnetism can linger and stick the door \u2014 quality locks manage it with an instant-release circuit (the M62 is rated no residual magnetism), cheap ones need that release delay to shake loose. And weather: outdoor and gate maglocks must be the sealed/potted, weatherproof-rated variety or moisture kills the coil. Brand-wise you'll see Securitron (M62/M82, ASSA ABLOY), HES/ASSA ABLOY, Dynalock, and SDC \u2014 all reputable; match the model to the door, the occupancy, and the code, not to the price tag.",sources:["https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m62","https://www.securitron.com/adobe/assets/urn:aaid:aem:023934f5-c7c3-4703-b8d5-76e253d86e16/original/as/AADSS1204562.pdf","https://www.securitron.com/en/products/electromagnetic-locks/m82","https://www.securitron.com/en/about/news-articles/where-to-use-a-maglock","https://www.hooverfence.com/mas_assets/theme/hooverfence/pdfs/maglock-install.pdf","https://idighardware.com/2017/06/code-requirements-for-electromagnetic-locks/","https://idighardware.com/2017/07/decoded-access-controlled-egress-doors-august-2017/","https://accesshardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/08/IBC-IFC-Access-Controlled-Egress-Doors.pdf","https://idighardware.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/Decoded-Quick-Tips-Delayed-Egress.pdf","https://idighardware.com/2016/04/controlled-egress-vs-delayed-egress/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/s/IBC2021P1/chapter-10-means-of-egress/IBC2021P1-Ch10-Sec1010.2.13","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/article/12441255/electromagnetic-locks-7-applications-and-the-codes-that-apply","https://sdcsecurity.com/products/1560-series-concealed-electromagnetic-shear-locks","https://www.dynalock.com/surface-mount-single-1200-holding-force-maglock-detail.html","https://ipvm.com/discussions/solve-the-problem-sticky-maglock"],relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","fail-safe-fail-secure","fire-alarm-interface","low-voltage-wiring","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","stairwell-reentry"],tags:["Maglock","Holding force","Fail-safe","Delayed egress","Bond sensor","Suppression","Access-controlled egress door","Fail-secure"],aliases:["mag lock","magnetic lock","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","door holds shut","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","transorb","TVS diode","surge suppressor","1N4004","flyback diode","spike suppression","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","request to exit","PIR REX","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"keypad-reader-servicing",title:"Servicing keypads & card readers",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",sources:["https://tech.napcosecurity.com/techlibrary/downloadfile/id/565","https://tech.napcosecurity.com/index.php/faq/details/id/240/?embed=iframe","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/449589/Alarm-Lock-Trilogy-Dl2700.html?page=2","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/709763/Kaba-E-Plex-5000.html?page=7","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/709763/Kaba-E-Plex-5000.html?page=29","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/39507534193179-How-to-do-the-Hard-reset-when-User-does-not-know-the-Master-code-in-E-Plex-2000","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/43910243311003-Simplex-1000-L1000-Changing-the-Code","https://www.sopl.us/uploads/1/3/0/1/1301029/1194549950_simplexunicanlocks.pdf","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10448915/servicing-kaba-simplex-pushbutton-locks","https://www.securityindustry.org/2021/11/09/there-is-a-hole-in-the-boat-why-access-control-professionals-need-to-move-from-wiegand-to-osdp/","https://getsafeandsound.com/blog/26-bit-wiegand-format/","https://whitepapers.axis.com/en-us/osdp-protocol-in-access-control","https://help.verkada.com/access-control/users-and-credentials/commission-access-control-cards/osdp-and-wiegand"],body:"Two very different animals wear the \"keypad\" name, and the first thing you do on any call is figure out which one is in front of you. A standalone lock \u2014 Alarm Lock Trilogy, dormakaba E-Plex, mechanical Simplex \u2014 has no wires running back to anything. All the brains, the code list, and usually the motor live in the lock itself. A networked keypad or card reader is dumb by comparison: it reads a credential and shunts the data down a cable to a panel in a closet, and the panel decides whether the door opens. Standalone is a lock problem. Networked is a wiring-and-voltage problem. Don't mix up which fight you're in.\n\nStart with the standalone units, because that's where most of your service calls live. And the number one call \u2014 by a mile \u2014 is dead batteries. A Trilogy DL2700 runs on five AA alkalines; an E-Plex 5000 runs on four AA. Both warn you before they quit: the Trilogy flashes a red LED with a high/low warble on any keypress, the E-Plex flashes red and green on a valid code. Customers ignore that warning for weeks, then call you when it's dead. Carry fresh AAs. Use alkaline, not the cheap carbon-zinc and not lithium unless the manual blesses it \u2014 lithium's voltage curve fools the low-battery detection. And here's the field trick: when a Trilogy is stone dead outside and there's no external 9V jump terminal, you're pulling the lock to get at the battery pack, so bring a screwdriver, not just batteries.\n\nLost or forgotten master code is call number two, and every line resets differently, so know your three big families cold. Trilogy factory master is 1-2-3-4-5; if a customer has changed it and lost it, you do a hardware reset \u2014 pull the battery pack, and with the batteries disconnected press and hold any number key about ten seconds to bleed the caps, then reconnect the batteries and within about three seconds press and hold the A key until you hear the run of acknowledgment beeps (six of them), then release. No beeps means you missed the timing \u2014 start over. That wipes all programming back to factory. E-Plex is different: factory master is 1-2-3-4-5-6-7-8, and it has tiered resets \u2014 code 099# resets settings to factory but keeps the master, manager, and user codes and the time/date, while 299# dumps all user codes. When the master itself is lost on an E-Plex you're into the documented hard-reset with the reset pin and a specific power-cycle dance; follow dormakaba's exact sequence for that model, because the 2000, 5000, and 5900 don't all reset the same way. Reprogramming users afterward is straightforward once you're in: on the Trilogy the master-code length sets the length for every user code, so if you program a 5-digit master, every user is 5 digits \u2014 pick that on purpose.\n\nMechanical Simplex has no batteries and no master code \u2014 it's all springs and cams \u2014 so its failures are mechanical and so is its combination change. Two ways to change a 1000-series: newer units change from the front with the existing combo, older ones need the DF-59 control key in the back. Whichever it is, the door stays OPEN the entire time \u2014 change it closed and get the sequence wrong and you've locked yourself out of your own work. Each button is used once, you can press two at once as a single step, and you test the new combo at least three times before the door ever swings shut. When a Simplex gets mushy or won't retract, it's usually a worn cam or a tired spring inside the chassis, not the combination.\n\nOn the electronic standalones, clutch and motor failure is the mechanical-sounding one. You enter a valid code, you hear the motor whir or the solenoid click, but the lever spins free and nothing throws the latch \u2014 that's a stripped clutch or a failed drive, and it's a lock swap or a factory rebuild, not a reprogram. Outdoor units add water. Weatherproofing means the gasket behind the escutcheon is intact, the keypad membrane isn't cracked, and you've got a drip loop and sealant at the cable entry \u2014 most \"random lockouts\" on an exterior E-Plex are corrosion on the battery contacts or water wicking into the harness. Dielectric grease on the contacts is cheap insurance.\n\nNow the networked side. A card reader is only as good as its cable and its voltage, so you carry a meter and you use it. Two protocols run the world here. Wiegand is the old standard: three signal wires \u2014 a common ground, DATA0 (D0), and DATA1 (D1) \u2014 where both data lines idle high and the reader briefly pulls D0 low to send a zero-bit or D1 low to send a one-bit, plus power. The classic 26-bit format is 8 bits facility code, 16 bits card number, and 2 parity bits bracketing the data (a leading even-parity bit and a trailing odd-parity bit). Wiegand is one-way, unencrypted, and limited to roughly 500 feet of cable \u2014 and because the bits ride in the clear, it's cloneable, which is exactly why the industry is moving off it. OSDP (Open Supervised Device Protocol) is the modern replacement: it runs over two-wire RS-485, is bidirectional, supports AES-128 encryption on Secure Channel, and stretches to about 4000 feet. OSDP also supervises the reader, so a cut wire or a yanked reader actually reports instead of going silently dead.\n\n[[diagram:standalone-vs-panel-keypad|Standalone versus dumb reader: what sits outside the door in each, and why it matters when the keypad comes off the wall]] The name is older than any of this: Wiegand is called that after John Wiegand, whose 1975 discovery of the magnetic effect the original cards used gave the format its name.\n\nTwo things about that swap are worth knowing before you quote one. First, OSDP readers are not encrypted just because they are OSDP... to talk at all a reader needs its polling address and baud rate set, and to actually encrypt, a Secure Channel base key has to be installed. A reader left with no key set is only marginally better than Wiegand, so setting the key is part of the install, not an option. Second, on a single-vendor job the vendor's own encrypted RS-485 may beat the open standard: ICT's proprietary RS-485 uses AES-256 against OSDP's AES-128, so on an all-ICT system their native readers are the stronger pick and OSDP is what you reach for to mix vendors or to satisfy a spec that names the open standard. Either way, run RS-485 on new work rather than Wiegand.\n\nAnd check the cable before you promise a reader-only retrofit. Swapping heads and flipping the controller port to 485 is often all it takes, but an old Wiegand run may not be the shielded twisted pair OSDP wants, and a run pulled against Wiegand's 500-foot assumption will not stretch to OSDP distances just because the readers changed.\n\nDiagnose by isolating three things: reader, panel, and wiring. First, put the meter on the reader's power pins \u2014 you want the reader's rated voltage, usually 12V nominal, actually present AT the reader, not at the panel. Long runs and thin gauge cause voltage sag; a reader spec'd for 12V that's seeing 9 will read intermittently or not power its RF. Second, read the reader's own LED and beep, because that's its status language: most readers idle on a steady or slow-flashing state, chirp and flash green on a good read, and give a distinct pattern when they can't talk to the panel. A reader that beeps and flashes on a card but the door doesn't open means the reader and cable are fine \u2014 the credential got to the panel and the panel said no. That's a format or database problem: wrong bit format (37-bit card into a 26-bit reader), facility-code mismatch, or the card simply isn't enrolled. Third, if the reader is dark and silent, it's power or the data pair \u2014 check ground, check DATA0/DATA1 or the RS-485 A/B, and confirm they're not swapped. Don't forget the EOL resistors and tamper: a supervised input wants its end-of-line resistor at the device, and a tamper switch that trips when the reader's pulled off the wall will hold the point in alarm until you reseat it. Meter first, LED second, panel last \u2014 the reader almost always tells you where the fault is if you slow down and read it.",relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","t-smart-locks","wire-tracing-shorts"],relatedModels:["ict-tsec-reader","ict-prt-rdm2-din"],tags:["Reader","Wiegand","OSDP","Bit format","RS-485","Secure Channel","Supervised input","Baud rate"],aliases:["badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","Data 0","Data 1","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","OSDP SC","encrypted reader","SCBK","supervision","cut wire detection","shorted loop","core removal key","pull key","IC control","cover switch","tamper alarm","case open","site code","FC","card range","wrong facility code","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet"]},{id:"conduit-bending",title:"Bending conduit: 90s, offsets, saddles, back-to-back & the math",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"The CCTV guide showed you why we run conduit and how to strap it. This one is about making the pipe do what you want \u2014 clean bends, right lengths, no wasted stick. On the low-voltage side you're mostly in 1/2\" and 3/4\" EMT feeding card readers, REX motions, mag locks, and camera drops. Master the [[tool:conduit-bender|hand bender]] on those two sizes and you'll look like you've been doing it twenty years. The math is simple arithmetic \u2014 the skill is reading the bender and not fighting the pipe.\n\nLearn the bender itself first, because every mark you make references a symbol on the shoe. There are three that matter. The ARROW on the side is your take-up/stub reference and your offset mark. The STAR or TEARDROP sits at the back of the shoe and is used for back-to-back bends. The RIM NOTCH \u2014 a little notch or dimple on the bender's rim, forward of the arrow \u2014 is your center-of-bend reference, and it's what you line up for the middle bend of a saddle. Klein and Ideal both stamp bend-angle degree marks on the shoe too (10, 22, 30, 45, 60) and most have a built-in level vial or the newer \"angle setter\" so you can hit your degrees without eyeballing the floor. Know which three marks are which before you ever pull a handle.\n\nThe 90 / stub-up is the foundation, and it runs on TAKE-UP. When the bender wraps the pipe through 90 degrees, it \"uses up\" a fixed amount of pipe measured from the back of the bend to the end of the stub. That amount is the deduct, and it's stamped by size: 1/2\" EMT = 5\", 3/4\" = 6\", 1\" = 8\", 1-1/4\" = 11\". So to make a stub that stands 8\" tall in 1/2\": 8 minus 5 = 3. Measure 3\" from the end of the pipe, mark it, put the ARROW on that mark, foot on the pedal, and pull to 90. Your stub reads 8\" from the floor to the back of the bend. Foot pressure the whole pull is what keeps the bend from kinking and keeps the radius tight \u2014 lean on it. (Take-up values from the Elliott Electric and conduitbending.com bender charts.)\n\n[[diagram:conduit-90|90\xb0 stub-up: measure the stub height you want, subtract the take-up deduct for the pipe size, mark, put the bender ARROW on the mark and pull to 90\xb0.]]\n\nBack-to-back is two 90s facing each other \u2014 think a pipe that comes up a wall, runs across, and drops down, or a \"U\" between two boxes. First 90 you make normal with the arrow. For the second one you flip the reference to the STAR/TEARDROP. Mark your finished back-to-back length on the pipe, line the star up with that mark, and bend. The star already accounts for the radius of the first bend so the two 90s land exactly the distance apart you marked, back-of-bend to back-of-bend. Trying to do this with the arrow and guessing the deduct twice is how you end up with a pipe that's an inch short.\n\n[[diagram:conduit-back-to-back|Back-to-back 90s: make the first bend on the ARROW, then flip to the STAR/teardrop on your length mark so the two backs of bend land exactly that far apart.]]\n\nOffsets get you around obstacles \u2014 a junction box, a beam, stepping the pipe off the wall into a knockout. Two equal bends in opposite directions. Two numbers do all the work: the MULTIPLIER (how far apart to space the two bends) and the SHRINK (how much the pipe shortens because the diagonal steals length). By angle: 22.5\xb0 \u2192 multiplier 2.6, shrink 3/16\" per inch of offset. 30\xb0 \u2192 multiplier 2.0, shrink 1/4\" per inch. 45\xb0 \u2192 multiplier 1.4, shrink 3/8\" per inch. 60\xb0 \u2192 multiplier 1.2, shrink 1/2\" per inch (the true math is 1/sin 60\xb0 = 1.15, but every chart rounds it to 1.2 \u2014 use 1.2). To offset 4\" using 30s: 4 \xd7 2.0 = 8\" between your two marks. Mark, put the arrow on the first mark and bend up 30, slide to the second mark, rotate the pipe 180\xb0 so the second bend goes the opposite way, bend 30 again \u2014 keep the handle in the same plane so the offset doesn't spiral. 30\xb0 is the shop default because the multiplier is a clean 2 and the pipe stays easy to fish. Go 22.5 for shallow offsets in a crowded rack where you don't want the pipe kicking way out; go 45 for a tight step into a box; save 60 for deep offsets where nothing shallower clears. (Multipliers and shrink per shieldenstrut and utilitypipesupply charts.)\n\n[[diagram:conduit-offset|Offset: two equal bends. Distance between them = offset depth \xd7 the angle multiplier; add the shrink back to your measurement so the connector still lands in the box.]]\n\nShrink is the number people forget and then their connector lands short of the box. If you offset 4\" at 30\xb0, the pipe pulls up 4 \xd7 1/4\" = 1\" shorter overall. So when your end point matters \u2014 landing a connector in a fixed knockout \u2014 add that 1\" back to your measurement before you make the first bend.\n\nSaddles go OVER something in the middle of a run \u2014 a water line, another conduit crossing yours. The three-point saddle is a 45\xb0 center bend with two 22.5\xb0 bends flanking it. Find the center of the obstruction, mark it on the pipe, and that center mark goes on the RIM NOTCH (not the arrow). Bend 45 there. Then measure out from the center mark by 2.5 \xd7 the saddle depth to each side \u2014 a 3\" obstacle: 3 \xd7 2.5 = 7.5\" each way \u2014 put the arrow on those marks and bend each to 22.5, rotated so they close the saddle back down. Note the saddle multiplier is 2.5, not the 2.6 you use for a straight 22.5\xb0 offset \u2014 the center bend here is a 45, so the geometry's different; mix them up and your saddle lands crooked. Shrink is 3/16\" per inch of depth (a 3\" obstacle pulls up about 9/16\"), so hook your center mark forward a hair to compensate. The 4-bend saddle (four 45s, two offsets back to back) is for going over something wide or square where a 3-point's single peak won't clear flat \u2014 you make an offset up, run level across the top, then offset back down. More marks, more shrink, but it rides flat over a wide obstruction. (Saddle math per elecalculator, empoweredkc and circuitiq.)\n\n[[diagram:conduit-saddle|Three-point saddle: a 45\xb0 center bend on the RIM NOTCH over the obstacle, flanked by two 22.5\xb0 shoulders marked 2.5 \xd7 the depth out each side.]]\n\n[[diagram:conduit-4bend-saddle|Four-bend saddle: two offsets back to back \u2014 offset up with two 45s, a level run across the top, then offset down \u2014 so it rides flat over a wide or square obstruction a single peak won't clear.]]\n\nA kick is just half an offset \u2014 one bend to nudge the pipe's direction, usually a stub-up that needs to angle over to hit a box that isn't straight above the penetration. Bend your 90, then add a small kick (10-30\xb0) at the base to walk the top of the stub over to the box. Handy coming out of a slab into a wall of\n\n[[diagram:conduit-kick|Kick: half an offset. Bend the 90, then a small 10\u201330\xb0 kick at the base walks the top of the stub over to a box that isn't straight above the penetration.]] readers.\n\nKnow your material, because EMT is the easy one. EMT (thinwall) bends cold in a hand bender and takes setscrew or compression connectors \u2014 no threads. IMC and RIGID (RMC) are thick-wall and threaded; you don't hand-bend rigid past small sizes, you use a HICKEY or a hydraulic/mechanical bender (Greenlee 555, \"Chicago\"), and the take-up numbers are completely different \u2014 a rigid bender is not an EMT bender. For low voltage you're almost always in EMT; you'll only see rigid where it goes underground or somewhere it can get hit.\n\nLast few truck-earned things. SPRING-BACK: EMT relaxes 2 to 3 degrees after you release, so pull a couple degrees past 90 \u2014 to about 92 \u2014 to land a true 90; overbend a touch more on rigid. GAIN: a 90 travels a shorter diagonal path than two straight legs measured to the corner, so a pipe with bends finishes shorter than the sum of its legs \u2014 that's why we cut long and trim, or work off take-up instead of adding leg lengths. ROLLING OFFSET: when the box is offset both sideways and up, the true offset is the diagonal (\u221a(horizontal\xb2 + vertical\xb2)); run that number through your multiplier and it's just a normal offset tilted in space.\n\n[[diagram:conduit-rolling-offset|Rolling offset: when the box is offset sideways AND up, the true offset is the diagonal \u221a(H\xb2 + V\xb2); run that number through the multiplier like a normal offset.]] CODE: NEC 358.26 caps you at 360\xb0 of total bend \u2014 the equivalent of four 90s \u2014 between pull points, so a run with a couple of offsets and a saddle can eat your budget before you think; add a box if you're over. Dry-fit before you cut, mark with a fine Sharpie not a fat crayon, and keep a wrap of tape on the pipe as your rotation reference so your second bend doesn't spiral out of plane. Real tools you'll actually hold: Klein and Ideal hand benders for EMT (both have the angle vial now), Greenlee for the bigger mechanical and hydraulic stuff.",sources:["https://www.elliottelectric.com/StaticPages/ElectricalReferences/Guides/electrical-conduit-bending-chart-emt-bender-guide.aspx","https://conduitbending.com/insights/90-degree-bend/","https://conduitbending.com/insights/offset-bend/","https://shieldenstrut.com/blogs/news/emt-conduit-bending-multipliers/","https://utilitypipesupply.com/blogs/news/conduit-bending-basics-offsets-multipliers-and-field-techniques","https://elecalculator.com/calculator/conduit-bending/saddle-bend-calculator/","https://circuitiq.ai/blogs/blog/emt-bending-how-to-make-a-3-point-saddle-bend-without-charts-or-apps","https://www.empoweredkc.com/blog-posts/how-do-i-bend-a-3-point-saddle"],relatedGuides:["low-voltage-wiring","conduit-cctv"],tags:["REX"],aliases:["request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX"]},{id:"alarm-zone-wiring",title:"Alarm zones & EOL resistors: wiring the loops",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"A hardwired burglar zone is just a loop \u2014 two wires leaving the panel, running out to a device, and the panel watching the electrical state of that loop. That's it. Door contact, motion PIR, glassbreak, tamper switch \u2014 the panel doesn't know or care what's on the end. It knows one thing: is this loop the way I expect it, or not. Everything else in this guide is about making \"the way I expect it\" mean something you can trust.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-zone-eol-states|Three states, not two, and why the resistor has to live at the far device]]\n\nStart with the device contact. Almost every burglar sensor you'll wire is normally-closed \u2014 NC. A recessed door contact, a motion's relay output, a roller-ball plunger \u2014 at rest, sitting normal, the circuit is closed and current flows. Open the door, the magnet leaves the reed, the contact breaks, the loop goes open. NO \u2014 normally-open \u2014 is the exception: some pressure mats, some panic buttons, certain relay outputs. The rule of thumb: burglar loops are NC and break on alarm, because a cut wire then reads the same as an intrusion \u2014 fail-safe. A cut NO loop reads normal, which is why you rarely see NO on a security perimeter.\n\nMultiple devices on one zone follow the contact logic. NC devices go in series \u2014 daisy-chain them, one contact to the next, so opening any single door breaks the whole loop. NO devices go in parallel \u2014 any one closing makes the loop. Don't mix them on a raw zone. And know that a plain series loop tells you a door is open, but it tells you nothing about someone cutting or shorting the wire. That's the hole EOL closes.\n\nAn end-of-line resistor is a fixed resistor the panel expects to see through the loop at rest. Normal isn't \"closed\" anymore \u2014 normal is \"I read exactly 2,000 ohms\" (or 5,600, whatever the panel wants). Now three faults become distinct. Loop reads the resistor value: normal. Loop reads open \u2014 infinite, no path: someone opened a door or cut a wire. Loop reads a dead short \u2014 zero ohms: someone jumped the contact trying to defeat it, and the panel calls that a trouble, not a normal. Without EOL a defeater just staples the two wires together and your zone never trips. With EOL, that short is a fault the panel screams about.\n\nHere's the part techs get wrong: the resistor goes at the far DEVICE, not in the panel. Physically inline at the last contact on the loop. Put it at the panel and you're only supervising the six inches of pigtail inside the can \u2014 the entire home run out to the door is unsupervised, and a cut or short anywhere on that run reads normal. EOL at the device means the whole length of wire, all the way out, is under supervision. If you ever open a panel and find the resistors bundled on the terminal strip, somebody took a shortcut and the system's protection is a fiction.\n\nValues are panel-specific, so read the manual, don't guess. Honeywell/Ademco VISTA hardwire zones use 2,000 ohm \u2014 2k. DSC PowerSeries uses 5,600 ohm \u2014 5.6k. You'll also see 1k, 2.2k, and 4.7k across other brands (Interlogix/GE and some Napco gear run 4.7k, Paradox 1k). Wrong value and the panel either won't see normal at all, or sits in a permanent trouble. Keep an assortment; they're color-banded and cost pennies. On the VISTA, know the tolerance is tight too \u2014 zone 1 wants that 2k within about 100 ohms, so a corroded splice adding resistance can throw it before it ever bothers a looser zone.\n\nSingle EOL gives you open/normal/short. Dual EOL \u2014 double end-of-line, common on DSC and European panels \u2014 adds tamper discrimination by wiring a second resistor across the contact. On DSC double-EOL: one 5.6k sits inline as the EOL, a second 5.6k goes in parallel across the alarm contact. Contact closed shorts out that parallel resistor, so you read just the 5.6k \u2014 normal. Contact open puts the two 5.6k in series \u2014 11.2k \u2014 for alarm. A dead short reads zero for tamper, and a full open reads a wire-cut fault. Four states off two wires. Great for cabinet tamper and cut-line detection without burning a second zone. VISTA typically keeps tamper on its own zone instead \u2014 again, panel-dependent.\n\nReading a zone with a meter is your fastest diagnosis. Kill panel power or pull the loop from the terminals \u2014 you're reading the loop itself, not fighting the panel's pull-up. Ohms across the two wires: the resistor value (2k, 5.6k) is a healthy normal loop. Infinite/OL is open \u2014 door open, broken wire, or a resistor that fell off. Near zero is a short \u2014 pinched wire, staple through the jacket, wet run, or a defeat jumper. Way off the expected value means the wrong resistor or a corroded splice adding resistance.\n\nPowered vs unpowered matters. A dry contact \u2014 door reed, plunger \u2014 needs no power; it's just a switch on a loop. A motion, glassbreak, or shock sensor is powered: it draws current from the panel's AUX 12V and typically has a separate relay output that goes on the zone loop. Two circuits: power in, dry contact out. Watch your AUX budget \u2014 every powered device pulls milliamps, and the panel's AUX rail is finite. A residential VISTA-20P gives you 600mA on AUX, and under UL the AUX and bell outputs combined can't exceed 600mA with AUX capped at 500mA; DSC PowerSeries panels sit in the same ballpark, roughly 500\u2013550mA. Bigger commercial boards and outboard supervised supplies go higher, but on a typical house panel you're working inside half an amp, not a full one. On long runs, voltage drop bites: 22ga over a couple hundred feet can sag a PIR below its spec voltage and make it flaky or dead. Do the math, drop to 18ga, or power the far devices from a local supervised supply.\n\nFire is its own animal \u2014 keep it separate. Two-wire smokes share power and signal on one supervised loop with a special zone and an EOL; four-wire smokes take power on one pair and alarm contacts on another, with a relay module so a power loss trips trouble. Don't freelance fire; it's life-safety, code-driven, and often its own listed panel.\n\nZone doubling stretches an 8-zone panel to 16 by putting two contacts in parallel, each in series with a different resistor value, so the panel reads which combination is present and infers which zone opened. It works, but it's fussy, it usually kills tamper supervision, and one dies as a duplicate if both trip together. Use an expander module before you double \u2014 cleaner, fully supervised, less to explain to the next tech.\n\nTroubleshooting a trouble zone: trouble means the loop is outside its expected window \u2014 not clean open, not clean normal. Walk it in order. Meter the loop at the panel first: short or open tells you which way it failed. If it's open, the resistor fell off, a splice let go, or a contact's stuck open \u2014 check the far device last, since the EOL lives there. If it's shorted, hunt for a pinched or stapled wire, water in a box, or a defeat jumper. Confirm the resistor value matches the panel. Halve the run \u2014 disconnect at the midpoint, meter each leg \u2014 to isolate which half holds the fault. And check the obvious: a wandered-open window, a battery-dead wireless device reporting as loop trouble, or a corroded outdoor contact after rain.",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-configure-a-wired-zone-on-a-vista-panel","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/all-about-resistors","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS2032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2128-E-Alarm-Control-Installation-Guide-International/1.35/Double-End-of-Line-DEOL-Resistors","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS2032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2128-E-Alarm-Control-Installation-Guide-International/1.35/Single-End-of-Line-SEOL-Resistor","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/honeywell-vista-system-how-much-power-does-it-provide","http://myalarmguy.net.s3-website.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/dw6.html","https://www.home-security-systems-answers.com/ademco-vista-20p.html"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["Dry contact","PIR","Daisy-chain","Home run","Relay","Tamper switch","Voltage drop","Fail-safe"],aliases:["volt free","NO NC COM","form C","clean contact","motion detector","motion sensor","passive infrared","false alarms","relay clicks","contact won't close","cover switch","tamper alarm","case open","wire gauge","long run","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{body:"Here's the thing nobody tells the apprentice: an alarm panel that only trips a siren is a noisemaker. The value is the panel telling somebody, reliably, that something happened \u2014 and that \"telling somebody\" path is where most of your service calls and most of your liability live. For thirty years that path was a phone line. The panel would seize the line, dial the central station, and squawk tones. That world is gone. Copper POTS is being retired, VoIP mangles alarm tones (the DACT handshake doesn't survive the codec), and any panel still dialing out over a phone line is a panel you should be quoting a communicator for.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-dual-path-monitoring|Two roads off the property, and why one of them can be cut from inside]]\n\nSo how does a panel talk to the world now. Two roads: cellular and IP.\n\nCellular means a communicator with its own dedicated SIM on an LTE-M (or NB-IoT) data plan \u2014 not your phone's network in the consumer sense, a low-bandwidth machine band built for exactly this: tiny messages, deep building penetration, low power. The dealer doesn't buy the SIM retail; it rides on the alarm platform (Alarm.com, Telguard, the manufacturer's network) and the monthly monitoring fee covers the data. IP means the panel reaches the central station over the customer's broadband \u2014 cheap, fast, but it dies the instant the internet does, or the instant somebody clips the drop or pulls the router to defeat the system.\n\nThat's why the standard of care is dual-path. IP primary \u2014 free, fast, real-time \u2014 with cellular failover. The communicator watches the network; the second IP drops or a signal can't get through, it rolls to LTE automatically and the central station never misses the event. Cut the cable, kill the Wi-Fi, the cell path still reports. For fire and for anything commercial, dual-path isn't a nicety, it's often code (NFPA 72) and it's what a UL certificate requires.\n\nBoth paths are supervised. The communicator \"checks in\" with the platform on an interval \u2014 an IP heartbeat can run every 60\u201390 seconds, cellular test signals anywhere from every few minutes to daily depending on how it's programmed and whether it's fire or burg. A sole-path cellular fire communicator under NFPA 72 typically pings often enough that a lost path gets annunciated inside a set window (think a check-in every few minutes, failure flagged within the hour). Miss enough check-ins and the platform flags the account as a comm failure before anyone's even broken in. That supervision is the whole point: a dead panel that can't call for help is worse than no panel, and supervision is how the station knows the panel is alive.\n\nThe box that does this is the communicator, and you'll install a handful of brands. Alula (BAT-Connect / BAT-LTE) is the universal darling \u2014 it'll bolt onto almost any legacy Honeywell Vista or DSC PowerSeries panel and add IP, LTE-M, even Wi-Fi as a triple path, plus an app, and it auto-programs off the panel's audio/keypad bus. Telguard (TG-7 series) is the other big universal \u2014 dual-path LAN plus LTE-M off a single DACT connection, and the fire multi-carrier models (TG-7FEM) sniff AT&T, T-Mobile and Verizon and lock onto the strongest. Napco StarLink is everywhere in commercial fire \u2014 the StarLink Fire MAX line runs dual-SIM Verizon and AT&T, sniffs both carriers at the site and locks onto the stronger one, and it's panel-powered so you're not fishing a separate transformer and conduit. Then there's built-in: DMP panels take a CellCom/DualCom card, DSC PowerSeries Neo/Pro takes a TL880-style plug-in module (dual-path LTE + Ethernet on Alarm.com, ties in through the PCLink bus). Built-in is cleaner; universal is how you save a rip-and-replace on somebody's ten-year-old system.\n\nNow \u2014 what actually happens when a signal fires. The panel formats the event and ships it. The formats you'll see: Contact-ID \u2014 the old Ademco standard, formalized by SIA as DC-05, a compact DTMF numeric code that hands you the account, a zone and an event and not much else. And SIA-DCS, the richer SIA format that can carry more granular and text data. Those are the payload. DC-09 is the modern piece \u2014 the SIA IP transport standard that wraps either a SIA-DCS or a Contact-ID message and carries it over the network to the receiver, supervised and optionally AES-encrypted. The station's receiver decodes it, pops it on an operator's screen with the account, the zone, and the action plan.\n\nThat station is a UL 827 central station \u2014 and that listing means something physical: a hardened, fire-rated building, redundant alarm receivers, backup power (generator plus battery), redundant telco/internet, minimum trained staffing, and documented signal-handling times. A UL certificate on the account is often what the insurance discount and the AHJ are actually paying for.\n\nOperator gets a burglary signal and does not just dispatch. Enhanced Call Verification kicks in \u2014 a minimum of two calls, primary contact then a second number, before police roll. That two-call rule kills better than 30% of false dispatches (some jurisdictions report 30\u201350%), which matters because false alarms get you fined and get accounts put on no-response lists. Fire and holdup/panic are different \u2014 those go straight to dispatch, no verification.\n\nOpening and closing reports are the other half. On a supervised O/C schedule, the panel reports every arm and disarm; if the store opens at an odd hour, or never closes, the station sees the exception and acts. That's how you catch the employee who disarmed at 2am.\n\nTwo service tiers. Professional monitoring is the real central station on Alarm.com or Total Connect 2.0 (Resideo/Honeywell's platform) \u2014 humans watching, dispatch happens whether or not the customer sees their phone. Self-monitoring is app-only: the panel pings the customer's phone, and it's on them to call 911. Cheaper, no dispatch, no UL certificate, no insurance credit. Know which one you sold.\n\nLast thing, and it's the one that bites: 3G is dead \u2014 AT&T pulled the plug February 22, 2022, T-Mobile finished off its 3G (and Sprint's old CDMA) through mid-2022, Verizon shut down December 31, 2022 \u2014 and LTE-M is the only game now. Keep one thing straight so you don't get fooled by a spec sheet: LTE-M (Cat-M1) is a 4G low-power machine band. When you see \"5G LTE-M\" stamped on a Napco or Telguard box, that's the same LTE-M being carried forward onto the carriers' 5G-era networks \u2014 it is not 5G NR. The point is it's the long-life band the carriers have committed to keeping up, so a communicator you install today isn't staring down another sunset any time soon. So you verify signal at the install, every time. Pull up the communicator's RSSI, walk the mounting spot, and if it's marginal at the panel location get an antenna up high or relocate before you close the ticket \u2014 because a communicator that can't reach the tower is a silent alarm nobody's monitoring, and you won't find out until the night of the break-in.",id:"alarm-comms-monitoring",title:"Alarm communication & monitoring: dual-path, cell/IP & the central station",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",sources:["https://www.fcc.gov/consumers/guides/plan-ahead-phase-out-3g-cellular-networks-and-service","https://www.phonearena.com/news/t-mobile-verizon-att-sprint-2g-3g-4g-lte-network-shut-down-date_id134159","https://napcosecurity.com/starlink/","https://napcosecurity.com/news/starlinkfiremax2","https://www.geoarm.com/sle-max2-fire-napco-starlink-commercial-fire-dual-path-sim-cellular-att-verizon-alarm-communicator.html","https://alula.com/communicators/bat-connect/","https://alula.com/communicators/","https://www.telguard.com/product/tg-7/","https://www.telguard.com/product/tg-7e","https://www.dmp.com/products/cell-network-communicators/universal-communicators/cellcom","https://www.dmp.com/products/cell-network-communicators","https://www.dsc.com/alarm-security-products/TL880LTVZ%20-%20PowerSeries%20Neo%20LTE/Internet%20Dual-Path%20Alarm%20Communicator/2630","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-tl880ltvz-n","https://www.ul.com/services/central-station-service-certification","https://www.intertek.com/life-safety-security/standards/ul-827/","https://webstore.ansi.org/standards/SIA/siadc05199909","https://www.securityindustry.org/industry-standards/dc-05-2016/","https://fortsense.net/blog/glossary/sia-dc09","https://www.roombanker.com/blog/sia-alarm-protocol/","https://www.geoarm.com/ecv-enhanced-call-verification.html","https://www.kirschenbaumesq.com/article/whats-ecv","https://www.sptnews.ca/3777-understanding-enhanced-call-verification/","https://safehomecentral.com/alarm-monitoring/comparing-alarm-interactive-services-apps-alarm-com-total-connect-2-securenet-alula-telguard/"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["Central station","NFPA 72","Codec","Schedule","AHJ","Plug","Opening"],aliases:["hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"alarm-troubleshooting",title:"Troubleshooting a dead or faulting alarm system",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Here's the rule that'll save you an hour on nearly every \"dead panel\" or \"beeping keypad\" call: work the system in the order the power flows \u2014 transformer, battery, bus, keypad, zones. Don't start at the sensor that's tripping. Start at the can. Nine times out of ten a panel that went totally dead on a brief power blip, or a keypad chirping \"trouble\" in the middle of the night, comes down to one dead 12V 7Ah sealed lead-acid battery. Learn to suspect that battery first and you'll close calls fast.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-troubleshooting-order|Work it in the order the power flows, and what each symptom already tells you]]\n\nPower first. Open the metal can and find two power sources. The transformer feeds AC in \u2014 DSC PowerSeries runs a 16.5VAC 40VA plug-pack, Honeywell Vista uses a 16.5VAC transformer too, usually screwed to a receptacle down low where a vacuum cleaner knocks the plug half out. Put your meter on AC volts across the panel's AC terminals \u2014 you want to see roughly 16 to 18VAC. Zero means chase it back: tripped breaker, GFI the transformer shares, a switched outlet somebody flipped, or a transformer that finally cooked. The second source is that 12V 7Ah battery. This is your number-one failure. A lead-acid battery is done in three to five years, and when it dies two things happen: the keypad chirps a low-battery trouble, and the whole panel drops dead the instant utility power blips, because there's no reserve to ride through. A resting battery reading 12.6V looks fine and lies to you \u2014 it's a surface charge. Test it under load.\n\nUsing the multimeter. Three measurements carry most alarm work. One: aux/bell voltage. On a healthy DSC the AUX and battery terminals should sit at float, right around 13.8VDC, because the panel is charging. Read much below 13V with AC present and either the charging circuit is hurt or the battery is a dead short pulling it down. Two: battery under load. The real test \u2014 pull AC (unplug the transformer), let the panel run on battery, and watch that battery voltage while the system draws. A good 7Ah holds up near 12.5\u201313V; a bad one sags to 11, 10, or collapses and the panel resets. That sag under load is the tell a resting reading hides. Honeywell Vista panels automate this \u2014 every four hours they run a two-minute load test (and check every few minutes that a battery is even connected), which is why the low-battery trouble often shows up hours after the battery actually went weak. Three: continuity. Meter on ohms or the beep setting, panel powered down, to check a zone loop end to end, verify your end-of-line resistor (DSC standard is 5.6k, Vista's 2k), or hunt a short.\n\nBus and keypad comms. If a keypad reads \"no response,\" is blank, or the whole partition of keypads locks up, you've got a bus problem, not a zone problem. DSC keypads and modules ride a four-wire Keybus \u2014 red, black, yellow, green \u2014 and it's fussy about voltage drop and wiring. A \"busy bus\" or dead keypad is usually a loose green/yellow data pair, a nicked wire, a keypad addressed to a slot that isn't enabled, or too many devices on undersized wire over a long run. Meter red-to-black at the keypad: you want that same ~13V. Low there means voltage drop or an overloaded aux \u2014 and that whole budget is shared: about 500mA total on the classic PC1616/1832/1864 (700mA on a UL/ULC install or a NEO), split across AUX, the PGMs, and the Keybus. Blow past it and everything downstream browns out. One shorted keypad or a pinched Keybus can take the whole bus down, so pull devices off one at a time until the bus comes back.\n\nZones and the common faults. Get to the codes before you guess. On DSC press [*][2] and the keypad shows numbers 1\u20138, each a trouble type: 1 service required (press again for low battery, bell circuit, etc.), 2 AC failure, 3 telephone line, 4 FTC \u2014 failure to communicate to the central station, 5 zone fault, 6 zone tamper, 7 wireless zone low battery, 8 loss of clock/time. Honeywell Vista throws numbered fault codes and text like \"COMM FAILURE\" or \"CHECK 21.\" Zone trouble/tamper on a wired loop means an open, a short, or a wrong EOL resistor \u2014 a double-EOL setup reports tamper separately from alarm. For a comm/monitoring failure, remember most FTCs today are a dead landline or a failed IP/cellular communicator, not the panel.\n\nGround fault. A ground-fault trouble means a conductor is leaking to earth \u2014 DSC flags it when it sees a low-resistance leak, under about 40k ohm, between a field conductor and the EGND terminal. On the classic PowerSeries that ground detection rides on the fire capability/module; NEO and Pro do it on-board. Chase it like the fire guys do: power down, lift every field wire off the panel, and see if the trouble clears. If it clears, the fault's out in the field, not internal. Then meter each conductor to a known earth ground on ohms \u2014 a good circuit reads open (infinite); the faulted one reads low resistance to ground. Split long runs in half and re-test to zero in on it. Cause is almost always damaged insulation: wire dragged over a sharp box knockout, pinched under a device screw, or water in a junction.\n\nPanel vs. device vs. wiring. The judgment that comes with time: if every zone and keypad is troubled at once, suspect power or the panel. If one zone or one keypad is out, it's that device or its wiring \u2014 swap the device to a known-good input to split the two. Wireless devices report their own low-battery and RF supervision loss (the panel hasn't heard a check-in), so those are battery-and-mounting problems at the sensor, not panel faults. Panel's genuinely dead only after you've proven good AC and a good battery under load.",sources:["https://cms.dsc.com/download2.php?t=1&id=14079","https://cms.dsc.com/download.php?t=1&id=12609","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/api/khub/documents/50LbGJiH1KmomhfE42xxCQ/content","https://blog.tremtech.com/blog/understanding-dsc-alarm-trouble-codes/","https://support.reedsecurity.com/portal/en/kb/articles/dsc-understanding-alarm-trouble-codes","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/all-about-resistors","https://www.geoarm.com/ptd1640u-plug-in-ac-power-transformer-for-dsc-powerseries-neo-and-honeywell-vista-panels.html","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-clear-a-low-battery-in-my-honeywell-vista-p-series-pane","https://www.homesecurityonline.com.au/blogs/home-security/alarm-battery-replacement-guide","https://www.firealarmsonline.com/2015/09/quickest-way-to-locate-ground-faults-on.html","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016-4-Alarm-Panel-User-Manual-South-Africa/1.2/User-Functions/Trouble-conditions","https://emcsecurity.com/troubleshoot-beeps-chirps-alerts-and-more/"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["Voltage drop","EOL resistor","Central station","Plug"],aliases:["wire gauge","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","end of line resistor","120 ohm","termination resistor","supervision resistor","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"alarm-false-alarms",title:"False alarms, permits & verified response",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Here's the part nobody at trade school teaches you: the alarm you install is a promise to the police department, and the city keeps score. Somewhere north of 90 percent of alarm dispatches nationwide are false \u2014 a lot of jurisdictions put it at 94 to 98. Cops know it, they hate it, and they've built ordinances and fine schedules to push the cost back onto the alarm owner \u2014 which means onto the install that caused it. Your job isn't just to make the siren work. It's to keep your customer off the fine list. Do that and you keep the customer.\n\n[[diagram:alarm-false-alarm-causes|What actually trips them, what prevents them, and what the city does about it]]\n\nStart with what actually trips a false alarm, because it's almost always the install, not the burglar. Bad motion placement is number one \u2014 a PIR aimed at a west-facing window bakes in the afternoon sun, or points straight at an HVAC register so every time the furnace kicks on, that rush of warm air across the lens reads like a body walking through. Same with a PIR over a heat source, near a bathroom exhaust, or looking at a Mylar balloon left over from a birthday party drifting on the draft. Glassbreak detectors are their own circus: a spider crawling across the mic, a dropped pan, a dog's bark at the right pitch. Pets are constant \u2014 a 40-pound dog reads as a person to a standard PIR mounted at knee height. Then the dumb stuff that's most of your callbacks: low sensor batteries throwing tamper or supervisory faults, a door contact with too big a gap so wind flexes the door and breaks the reed, and unsupervised users \u2014 the cleaning crew, the kid, the new employee who never got the code and just walks in.\n\nNow the business side, and this is where your local knowledge pays. Most California cities require an alarm PERMIT, registered to the monitored address, renewed annually. In Tracy the permit runs about $25 to $26 through the police department's false-alarm program, and their year runs on the fiscal calendar \u2014 July 1 through June 30 \u2014 not a rolling twelve months from the day you signed up. It's separate from anything the monitoring company charges. One useful wrinkle to tell your customer: alarms in the first fifteen days of a brand-new system don't count against them, so that shakedown period where you're still tuning sensitivity won't cost them a citation. The fines escalate on a schedule, and here the sources don't fully agree, so pull the current code before you quote anybody. The city's own false-alarm program lists the first three false alarms as warnings at no charge, then a flat fee per occurrence after that, with the permit subject to revocation once an address runs past roughly ten false alarms in a single fiscal year. Local press has reported a tiered version \u2014 warnings, then $100, then $200, then $500, then non-response \u2014 which is the kind of schedule that gets amended by City Council resolution. Either way the pattern is universal: it starts as a nuisance and ends as real money, the counting resets at the fiscal year, and after enough hits the address can lose response entirely until the owner shows the police a certified repair. It's the customer who pays, remembering who installed the system. Pull the current Tracy Municipal Code Chapter 3.40 before you put a number in front of anyone.\n\nHere's the shift that changes everything \u2014 verified response and ECV. Enhanced Call Verification means the monitoring center must make at least two calls to two different numbers (premises first, then the account owner's cell) before police will roll. That one step cuts needless dispatches by double digits and is written right into many city ordinances now. Verified response goes further: cities like Salt Lake City \u2014 first major city to do it, back in December 2000 \u2014 won't dispatch to an unverified burglar alarm at all. Somebody has to confirm it's real: an eyewitness, a guard, or increasingly video. That's why video verification and audio monitoring are worth selling \u2014 in a verified-response city, an alarm with no verification is a siren nobody comes to. Know which bucket your city is in before you promise a customer the cops will show.\n\nThe fix for nuisance trips is design, and it's mostly free. Lean on the SIA CP-01 standard \u2014 it's ANSI-recognized (ANSI/SIA CP-01) and baked into every reputable panel (DSC PowerSeries Neo, Honeywell Vista, Qolsys IQ). CP-01 mandates entry and exit delays with progress annunciation and an exit-delay restart, and gives you the tools: swinger shutdown, where a chattering zone alarms the programmed number of times \u2014 default is one or two trips depending on the panel \u2014 then locks out for the rest of the arming period, and cross-zoning, where two separate sensors must trip inside a short window before the panel calls it a burglary. Know two things about cross-zone: it ships DISABLED on most panels, so you have to turn it on, and the window is measured in SECONDS, not minutes \u2014 the default is around 60 seconds on DSC and HAI, adjustable from roughly 15 seconds up to a few minutes. Set the entry delay long enough that the legit user actually reaches the keypad \u2014 CP-01's floor is 30 seconds, and 30 to 45 is the sweet spot \u2014 and the exit delay long enough to clear the door, which is why the CP-01 default sits at 60 seconds with an automatic restart if someone ducks back in. On hardware, spec dual-technology motions (PIR plus microwave) that only alarm when both agree; that kills the sun-and-HVAC false trips cold, and it's the single best money you'll spend in a pet home. Mount PIRs at spec height, away from vents and glass, pet-immune models in animal homes, and glassbreaks off the kitchen.\n\nEvery one of those decisions is the difference between a customer who's off the fine list and one who calls you angry after the third citation. Good install choices are the product.",sources:["https://library.municode.com/ca/tracy/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=TIT3PUSA_CH3.40ALSY_3.40.150FAALOSRE","https://library.municode.com/ca/tracy/codes/code_of_ordinances?nodeId=TIT3PUSA_CH3.40ALSY_3.40.160RIAP","https://cityalarmpermit.com/FAMS/City/Tracy/OrdinanceAtGlance.html","https://tracypd.com/document/alarm-permit-application","https://www.ttownmedia.com/tracy_press/false-alarm-fines/article_6d143d1a-e2fb-5929-b437-9f7f6df5e429.html","https://www.securityindustry.org/industry-standards/cp-01-2019/","https://okbfaa.org/wp-content/uploads/2014/06/CP01_2010.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/797952/Hai-Omni-Pro-Ii.html?page=61","https://cms.dsc.com/download2.php?t=1&id=12565","https://www.guardiansecurity.com/what-is-enhanced-call-verification/","https://police.slc.gov/ass3ts/uploads/verified_response_summary.pdf","https://www.kirschenbaumesq.com/article/more-on-ecv-enhanced-call-verification"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["PIR","Schedule","Entry delay"],aliases:["motion detector","motion sensor","passive infrared","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal"]},{id:"alarm-install-steps",title:"Installing & wiring an alarm panel, step by step",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Before you strip a single wire \u2014 this is a DSC PowerSeries or Honeywell Vista style hardwired panel. The metal box on the wall is the \"can.\" The green circuit board inside is the panel. Everything home-runs back to that board. You are the hub, not a daisy chain. Keep that picture in your head and the rest is just following wires.\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Plan and locate the can. Pick an interior spot: a closet, utility room, basement \u2014 near AC power, near where your phone/network line or cell antenna gets signal, and central-ish to your device runs. Not the garage (heat, cold, theft), not next to the breaker panel (noise). You want it hidden but reachable, because you'll service it. Rough out on paper where every contact, motion, keypad, and siren lives and how the wire gets home. [Diagram: floor plan with panel location and home-run wire routes fanning out.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-1|Step 1: Plan and locate the can]]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Mount the can. Hold it level, mark your holes, anchor into studs or use drywall anchors rated for the weight \u2014 the box plus a 7Ah battery is heavier than it looks. Knock out the wire-entry tabs you need before it's on the wall. Leave the door swing clear. [Picture: can screwed to wall, knockouts open, door hanging open.]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Run the transformer AC \u2014 but do NOT plug it in. The panel wants a 16.5VAC 40VA plug-in transformer (DSC PTD1640U, Honeywell 1361-GT). Run its two-conductor wire from a NON-switched receptacle \u2014 one nobody can flip off with a light switch (UL requires this) \u2014 back to the panel's AC terminals (terminals 1 and 2 on a Vista, the AC terminals on a DSC). Land it at the board. Keep it OFF the wall outlet until Step 10. Screw the transformer down with the retaining screw so it can't be yanked loose. [Picture: transformer screwed down at outlet, two wires landed on panel AC terminals, outlet unplugged.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-3|Step 3: Run the transformer AC \u2014 but do NOT plug it in]]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Set the backup battery aside. You've got a 12V 4\u20137Ah sealed lead-acid battery. Leave it OUT for now, red and black leads disconnected. Powering up on battery first can mask wiring faults and pop the battery fuse if there's a short. Battery is the LAST power source you connect. Park it in the bottom of the can. [Picture: SLA battery sitting in can floor, leads dangling, unconnected.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-4|Step 4: Set the backup battery aside]]\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Home-run the zone wires and the keypad bus. Every device gets its own wire back to the can \u2014 that's the home run. Zones (contacts, motions) run on 22/2 or 22/4. The keypad and any bus devices run on 22/4: red and black are power (+12V and ground), yellow and green are the data pair \u2014 the Keybus on DSC, ECP on Honeywell. Red/black/yellow/green, every time, so you never guess later. Pull all your runs, leave 12\u201318 inches of service loop at the can. Label every wire at the panel end with tape as you go. [Diagram: four-conductor bus cable color-coded R/B/Y/G, and separate 2-conductor zone runs, all converging on the can.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-5|Step 5: Home-run the zone wires and the keypad bus]]\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Land the zones with the EOL at the DEVICE. Supervised zones use an end-of-line resistor \u2014 5.6k\u03a9 on DSC PowerSeries, 2k\u03a9 (2000 ohm) on Honeywell Vista control-panel zones. The resistor goes at the FAR end, in series at the sensor, NOT in the panel. That's what lets the panel tell \"normal\" from \"cut wire\" from \"shorted.\" Land the zone pair on its zone terminal and the common. Normally-closed contact: resistor in series with the loop. [Diagram: sensor with EOL resistor wired in series at the device, wire running back to zone + common on the board.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-6|Step 6: Land the zones with the EOL at the DEVICE]]\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Mount the sensing devices. Door/window contacts: magnet on the moving part, switch on the frame, gap under a half-inch. Motions (PIR): 7\u20138 feet up, aimed across traffic not at glass or heat vents, wired to +12/ground plus the zone loop with its EOL. Sirens: land on the bell output, observe polarity, park the EOL if the manual calls for it. Keypad: hang it by the door you arm from, land red/black/yellow/green on its matching terminals. [Picture panel: contact on a door, PIR in a corner, keypad by the entry, siren under an eave.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-7|Step 7: Mount the sensing devices]]\n\nStep 8 \u2014 Connect the communicator. This is how the panel calls for help \u2014 a cellular or IP dialer, or a built-in Alarm.com/Telguard module. Seat it on the board's header or serial connector, run its antenna or network lead to where signal is good (back to Step 1), and land its bus wires. No signal, no monitoring \u2014 confirm placement before you close up. [Picture: comm module seated on panel header, antenna lead routed out the top knockout.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-8|Step 8: Connect the communicator]]\n\nStep 9 \u2014 Double-check every landing. Walk the board left to right: AC, zones, bus, bell, comm. Tug each wire. Look for stray copper whiskers bridging terminals. This is the free step that saves the callback.\n\n[[img:alarm-install-9|Step 9: Double-check every landing]]\n\nStep 10 \u2014 Power up: transformer FIRST, then battery. Now plug the transformer into the outlet. The panel and keypad should light and boot. Once it's alive on AC, connect the battery \u2014 red to positive, black to negative. Order matters: AC brings it up clean, battery just backs it up. A keypad trouble light on battery-first is a classic self-inflicted headache. [Picture: hand plugging transformer into outlet, then clipping battery leads red-to-red.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-10|Step 10: Power up: transformer FIRST, then battery]]\n\nStep 11 \u2014 Walk-test. Put the panel in installer/walk-test mode and physically trip every device \u2014 open each door, wave through each motion, confirm the zone reports and the keypad shows the right zone number. Trip the siren. Force a communicator test signal and confirm the monitoring center or app sees it. A zone that reads open at rest usually means a missing or wrong EOL \u2014 go back to Step 6. Test everything before you sign off. [Diagram: apprentice walking the building, each device lighting up on the keypad in sequence.]\n\n[[img:alarm-install-11|Step 11: Walk-test]]",sources:["https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Pro-Reference-Manual/1.3.1/Installation/Installing-modules/Zone-wiring/Single-end-of-line-SEOL-resistor","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/all-about-resistors","https://safehomecentral.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/Vista-20P-Installation-Instructions.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/documents/honeywell-vista-15p-15psia-20p-and-20psia-install-guide","https://24incontrol.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/DSC-PowerSeries-PC1616-PC1832-PC1864-Panel-Programming-Guide.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/dsc-ptd1640u","https://www.alarmgrid.com/products/honeywell-1361","https://shop.ssandsi.com/dsc-16-5vac-40va-plug-in-ac-transformer-for-dsc-powerseries-neo-honeywell-vista-security-panels-ptd1640u/"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["Daisy chain","PIR","Home run","EOL resistor","Plug"],aliases:["bus wiring","chained devices","T-tap","motion detector","motion sensor","passive infrared","false alarms","end of line resistor","120 ohm","termination resistor","supervision resistor","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"alarm-program-steps",title:"Programming zones & users on a panel, step by step",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Two panels dominate the field \u2014 Honeywell/Ademco VISTA (15P/20P) and DSC PowerSeries (PC1616/1832/1864 and the newer Neo). The logic is identical on both: get into installer programming, tell the panel what each zone IS, tell it who to call and who can arm it, set your timers, get out, then walk-test every point before you leave. What changes is the keystrokes. Learn both \u2014 you will meet both in the same week.\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Know your installer code before you touch anything. VISTA factory default is 4112; DSC factory default is 5555. Master (end-user) default on both is 1234. If a previous installer changed the code and did not leave it, here is the out: on VISTA, power the board down and back up, and within roughly the first 30\u201350 seconds the panel will accept the DEFAULT installer code (4112 + 800) even if the code was changed \u2014 get into programming that way, then run *97 to load factory defaults. That backdoor only dies if a prior tech exited with *98 (lockout), which permanently kills it. On DSC you do a hardware default \u2014 the jumper/PGM shorting procedure printed on the install sheet (Neo confirms it with a keypad prompt). No shortcut around a truly locked-out code. [PIC: keypad with the default codes written on a tag]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Enter installer programming. VISTA: type installer code + 8 0 0 (so 4 1 1 2 8 0 0). The keypad shows \"20\" or a field prompt \u2014 you are in. DSC: press *8 then the installer code (*8 5555). The keypad lights up and waits for a 3-digit section number. [PIC: keypad display showing programming mode entry]\n\n[[img:alarm-program-2|Step 2: Enter installer programming]]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Set your zone types / definitions. This is the single most important step \u2014 the type decides how the panel reacts. VISTA: enter *56 (zone programming menu) or *58 (expert mode). For each zone you pick a Zone Type \u2014 01 entry/exit #1, 02 entry/exit #2, 03 perimeter (instant), 04 interior follower, 06 24-hour silent, 07 24-hour audible, 08 24-hour auxiliary, 09 fire, 10 interior w/delay, 16 fire w/verification. Note 05 is NOT fire \u2014 05 is \"trouble by day / alarm by night.\" Fire is 09. DSC Neo (3-digit codes; classic PowerSeries uses the same numbers as 2-digit): enter section [001], scroll to the zone, then the definition \u2014 001 delay 1, 002 delay 2, 003 instant, 004 interior, 005 interior stay/away, 010 interior delay, 008 standard 24-hour fire, 007 delayed 24-hour fire. The full list lives under [001] Assign Zone Types in the Neo reference manual \u2014 keep it open. [PIC: zone-type worksheet/chart taped inside the can lid]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Define each zone: loop response and the physical connection. VISTA *56 walks you zone-by-zone: enter the zone number, its type, the partition, and the \"input type\" \u2014 1 hardwired (the panel's built-in loops), 2 aux-wired (off an expander), 3 RF (supervised 5800-series wireless), 4 unsupervised RF, 5 button RF (5804-style keyfobs). For a hardwired zone you also confirm loop response (the panel reads the resistor/EOL). For a wireless point you enroll the transmitter serial: choose input 3, then either key in the 7-digit serial off the transmitter label OR trip the sensor twice (open the door, tamper it) and the panel captures it \u2014 confirm the loop number (loop 1 = door/window reed, loop 2/3 for extra inputs). DSC hardwired: wire to the zone terminals with your EOL resistor (5.6k single EOL, or DEOL for a combined zone+tamper), enable the zone in section [201] (bank 001 = zones 1\u20138, 002 = 9\u201316, etc.), and assign it to a partition in [202] if it is not partition 1. DSC wireless (Neo): section [804] \u2014 enter the sub-section for the slot, key the transmitter serial or trip it to auto-enroll, then set its zone attributes. [PIC: 5800 transmitter serial label + the EOL resistor across a hardwire loop]\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Set the account number and comm path. This is what makes it a monitored system instead of a loud bell. VISTA: field *43 = primary subscriber/account number (*44 secondary); the reporting format and phone/IP/LTE path live across *41\u2013*49 (or the communicator's own menu on an LTE/dualcom module). DSC: section [310][000] = system account code; receiver phone numbers, reporting formats and codes live in the [300]-series sections. Wrong account number = central station gets alarms for someone else. Double-check it. [PIC: central-station account sheet next to the keypad]\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Set master and user codes. VISTA: leave programming first (Step 8), then at the keypad enter installer code + 8 + 0 2 + new master (4112 8 02 4321) \u2014 the master is user 02. The homeowner then adds users with master + 8 + user-slot (03 and up) + 4-digit code. DSC: normally you just hand the master code (default 1234) to the customer and they add users via *5 + master code. Never leave 1234 live on a finished job. [PIC: user-code slot list you fill in for the customer]\n\n[[img:alarm-program-6|Step 6: Set master and user codes]]\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Set entry and exit delays. VISTA: *34 = exit delay, *35 = entry delay 1, *36 = entry delay 2, each programmed per partition (entry delays run 0\u201399 sec; exit delay tops out at 120 sec on the 20P). DSC: section [005][001] = partition 1 timers \u2014 entry delay 1, entry delay 2, exit delay, all 3-digit up to 255 sec. Heads up on the floor: Neo ships CP-01 compliant, and SIA CP-01 (the false-alarm-reduction standard) forces the entry delays to 030 sec minimum and the exit delay to 045 sec minimum \u2014 you physically cannot go lower unless you're on non-CP-01 firmware. Match the delay to the walk path \u2014 a back door on a long hallway needs more entry time or you train the customer to false-alarm. [PIC: front door with a stopwatch showing the walk time]\n\nStep 8 \u2014 Exit programming cleanly. VISTA: press *99 to exit (*98 exits AND locks out the installer menu \u2014 do not use *98 unless you mean it, it kills the power-up default backdoor). DSC: press # repeatedly until the keypad returns to Ready/normal. If you power down instead of exiting properly you can lose the last edits. [PIC: keypad back at \"Ready to Arm\"]\n\nStep 9 \u2014 TEST every zone in test mode. Non-negotiable. VISTA: at the keypad enter security code + 5 (TEST) \u2014 the panel chimes each time a zone trips; open every door, every window, wave every motion, hit the pulls, test the smokes. Code + OFF (1) to exit. DSC: enter *8 + installer + section [901] to start walk test \u2014 the keypad and bell confirm each point as you trip it, and it flags any zone you missed. Trip fire zones too (per AHJ) and verify the central station receives the test signal before you sign off. A zone you programmed but never walked is a zone you will get called back for. [PIC: tech opening each door with the keypad chiming in the background]",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-get-into-programming-mode-on-my-vista-20p","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/honeywell-vista-series-15p-20p-and-21ip-delay-zone-programming","https://24incontrol.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/Ademco-Vista-Sensor-Group-Numbers.pdf","https://www.alarmgrid.com/videos/honeywell-vista-program-star-43-subscriber-account-number","https://www.alarmgrid.com/blog/honeywell-vista-15p-20p-21ip-cheatsheet","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/805098/Honeywell-Ademco-Vista-20p.html","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-security-powerseries-neo-how-do-i-program-a-hardwired-zone","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-program-a-hardwired-zone-to-a-dsc-powerseries-neo","https://cms.dsc.com/download2.php?t=1&id=25054","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS3032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2129-E-Alarm-Controller-Reference-Manual/1.2/Programming/Programming-Descriptions/Zone-Setup/001-AssignZone-Types","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-security-powerseries-neo-how-do-i-change-the-entry-and-exit-delays","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Neo-HS2016/HS2016-4/HS3032/HS2064/HS2064-E/HS2128/HS2129-E-Alarm-Controller-Reference-Manual/1.2/Programming/Programming-Descriptions/System-Communications/310-Account-Codes"],relatedGuides:["alarm-systems-service"],tags:["Entry delay","EOL resistor","Central station","AHJ","Opening"],aliases:["end of line resistor","120 ohm","termination resistor","supervision resistor","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"cheatsheet-vista",title:"Cheat-sheet: Honeywell/Resideo VISTA programming",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"FIELD-VERIFIED QUICK REFERENCE \u2014 Ademco/Honeywell/Resideo VISTA-15P & VISTA-20P (hardwired residential/light-commercial). Panel keypad programming from a fixed-word or alpha keypad. Same *-fields on 21iP unless noted.\n\n[[diagram:vista-programming-card|Get in, change a field, get out... and the one key that locks you out]]\n\nGET IN\nInstaller code + 8 0 0 \u2192 enters program mode (default installer = 4112, so 4112 8 0 0). Alpha keypad shows \"Installer Code 20\"; fixed-word shows \"20\".\nPower-up backdoor: if a prior installer's code is unknown, kill panel power (unplug transformer AND disconnect the backup battery), reapply power, then within ~50 sec press and HOLD [\u2731] and [#] together for about 3 seconds \u2014 that drops you into programming without the code. Catch: if it was locked out with *98 (or dealer/SIA-locked), you get a long error tone and the backdoor is dead. At that point only the dealer who locked it can get in, via remote downloader software.\n\nGET OUT\n*99 = exit AND allow re-entry with installer code next time. This is your normal exit.\n*98 = exit WITHOUT re-entry \u2014 installer code and the power-up backdoor are BOTH locked out of programming until a dealer defaults it remotely. Use it to lock out DIYers; never fire it by accident on your own job.\n\nCORE *-FIELDS\n*20 = Installer Code (4 digits). Installer code can arm, but can only disarm if it did the arming.\n*21 = Quick-Arm enable. ON = user arms with [#] + STAY/AWAY instead of a code.\n*22 = RF Jam / RF supervision option (0 = no RF jam report, 1 = send RF jam report). NOT the house ID \u2014 people mix these up.\n*24 = RF House ID (00-31). This is the one that matters for wireless keypads/bidirectional units (5827, 5827BD, 5804BD) \u2014 set panel and keypad to the SAME ID. 00 = disables all wireless-keypad use.\n*91 = Option Selection field (RIS / Remote Interactive Services enable, call-waiting-defeat, etc.) \u2014 bit-mapped, set per worksheet. This is NOT quick-arm; quick-arm is *21.\nData fields in general: enter *[field] then the value; two-digit fields auto-advance, longer ones need [\u2731] to accept.\n\nZONE PROGRAMMING\n*56 = Zone Programming (menu mode) \u2014 walks you prompt-by-prompt: zone #, zone type, partition, report code, hardwired/RF, then \"learn\" a 5800-series serial (trip the sensor twice, or type the printed serial). Use this one on the truck.\n*58 = Expert mode \u2014 same data as *56, fewer prompts, faster once you know the sequence. Easy to fat-finger; new guys stay on *56.\n\nZONE TYPES (enter at the \"ZT\" prompt)\n00 = zone not used / off\n01 = Entry/Exit #1 (front door, uses entry delay 1)\n02 = Entry/Exit #2 (longer delay \u2014 garage/back)\n03 = Perimeter (doors/windows, instant when armed)\n04 = Interior Follower (motion \u2014 delayed only while an entry zone is counting down)\n05 = Day trouble / Night alarm (trouble beep disarmed, alarm armed \u2014 foil, tamper loops)\n06 = 24-hr Silent\n07 = 24-hr Audible\n08 = 24-hr Auxiliary (panic/medical, always active, no siren by default)\n09 = Fire (no verification)\n10 = Interior w/ Delay (motion that also gets entry delay)\n12 = Monitor Zone\n14 = Carbon Monoxide\n16 = Fire w/ Verification\n20 = Arm-STAY  |  21 = Arm-AWAY  |  22 = Disarm  |  23 = No Alarm Response \u2014 these four are for 5800-series BUTTON transmitters (keyfobs like the 5804), not general sensors.\n24 = Silent Burglary\n77 = Keyswitch (hardwired keyswitch on zone 7).\n\nDEVICE / RF / SERVICE MENUS\n*93 = Device (menu) Programming \u2014 add/address keypads, RF receivers (6160/6150 keypads, 5881 receiver), zone expanders (4219/4229), relay modules (4204), VIP/comm devices, and output programming.\n*94 = Downloader Callback phone-number field \u2014 part of remote-access (Compass) setup along with the enable/phone fields.\n*96 = Initialize Download ID / subscriber account number \u2014 sets the account back to a known state for the downloader.\n*97 = Reset to factory defaults \u2014 sets ALL data fields to factory (Master \u2192 1234, Installer \u2192 4112, deletes other user codes, clears programmed zones). Your recovery when a panel's programming is unknown. To fully clean-slate a panel: *97 then *96, then *99 out.\n\nLOCKOUT LOGIC (don't get stuck)\nInstaller locked out = someone exited with *98, or the account is SIA/dealer-locked. If it's only \"code unknown\" (not *98-locked), the power-up [\u2731]+[#] backdoor gets you back in, then *97 to default. If *98 was used, the backdoor throws a long error tone and you're done locally \u2014 only the locking dealer's remote downloader recovers it. Losing installer access on a live account means a truck roll \u2014 always exit with *99 unless the customer paid for lockout.\n\nGOTCHAS\n- 4112 is the world's most-known default \u2014 change *20 on every install.\n- Don't confuse *22 (RF jam option) with *24 (RF house ID) \u2014 wrong field, and your wireless keypad never talks.\n- After *56 zone edits, walk-test before you leave: installer code + [5], then [0] at the prompt. Trip every device; keypad beeps roughly every 40 sec to remind you it's in test, and it self-exits after 4 hours (or installer code + OFF).\n- *98 vs *99 is the #1 self-inflicted callback on these panels \u2014 burn it in.\n- VISTA panels are legacy but everywhere; Resideo still ships 15P/20P. Spec details vary by revision \u2014 confirm against the panel's own programming guide sticker/PDF.",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-get-into-programming-mode-on-my-vista-20p","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/home-security-hack-backdoor-into-the-honeywell-vista","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-factory-default-a-honeywell-vista-p-series-system","GeoArm support article: Honeywell VISTA 15P/20P factory default and hard reset procedure \u2014 original link retired.","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-set-the-house-id-on-a-6160","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/blogs/diy/rf-house-code-on-honeywell-alarm-panels","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-enter-walk-test-mode-on-a-vista-panel","https://www.alarmgrid.com/blog/honeywell-vista-15p-20p-21ip-cheatsheet","https://24incontrol.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/Vista-15P-20P-Programming-Guide.pdf","https://site.aesecurity.com/Manuals/v15pand20pprogrammingguide.pdf"],tags:["Entry delay","Relay"],aliases:["dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close"]},{body:"FIELD-VERIFIED QUICK REFERENCE \u2014 DSC PowerSeries. Covers classic PC1616 / PC1832 / PC1864 (v4.x) and PowerSeries Neo (HS2016/2032/2064/2128). Section numbers below are the classic panel; Neo overlaps heavily but shifts some sections and adds sub-menus \u2014 always confirm against the sticker manual for the exact model and firmware in front of you. This gets you moving, not a substitute for the worksheet.\n\n[[diagram:dsc-programming-card|Star-eight, sections, and the toggle rows that invert a setting if you type at them]]\n\nENTER PROGRAMMING: [*][8][installer code]. Default installer code = 5555. Default master code = 1234. Keypad shows section prompt; type the 3-digit section, then the data. [#] backs out one level, hold [#] to exit.\n\nDEFAULT (software reset, when you HAVE the installer code): [*][8][installer], then at the section prompt enter [999][installer code][999] \u2014 you re-key the code and the section to confirm. Wipes the main panel back to factory. Note [999] only defaults the MAIN panel; keypads, wireless receiver, and add-on communicators have their own default options ([996] receiver, [993] alt-communicator, etc.). Neo: same idea but it may ask which module/partition to default \u2014 read the prompt.\n\nDEFAULT (NO installer code, classic PC16xx): hardware reset. Power down completely (unplug transformer AND disconnect battery). Remove any wires on Zone 1 (Z1) and PGM1, then short Z1 to PGM1 with a jumper. Power up on AC only for about 10 seconds (that's the DSC manual value \u2014 plenty of techs leave it up closer to a minute, either way is fine), then power down again, remove the jumper, re-land your wires, and power back up. Restores installer 5555 / master 1234. GOTCHA: if the last guy enabled Installer Lockout in [990], a hardware default WILL NOT work \u2014 the panel ignores the jumper. Locked-out board means DLS with the code or a swap. Neo differs \u2014 check the model.\n\n[001] ZONE DEFINITIONS. This is what each zone DOES. Classic: [001] covers zones 1-16 (goes to [004] on a full 64-zone build), keyed 2 digits per zone, in order starting at zone 1. The codes that bite people:  [00] null/unused, [01] delay 1 (entry/exit \u2014 front door, follows Entry Delay 1), [02] delay 2, [03] instant, [04] interior, [05] interior stay/away (auto-bypass when armed STAY \u2014 this is your motions), [06] delay stay/away, [11] 24-hr burglary (audible), [12] 24-hr hold-up (silent), [35] 24-hr bell/buzzer (bell when armed, keypad buzzer when disarmed). Fire and CO are their own codes and people fat-finger them constantly: [07] delayed 24-hr fire (hardwire, 30s comm delay), [08] STANDARD 24-hr fire (hardwire), [09] 24-hr supervision (alarms but does NOT ring the bell or buzzer), [41] 24-hr CO hardwired, [81] 24-hr CO wireless. Watch out \u2014 [24] is 'for future use', NOT fire; [87]/[88] are the WIRELESS/addressable fire versions of [07]/[08]. If a motion trips the second you arm stay, it got defined instant ([03]) instead of stay/away ([05]) \u2014 fix it here.\n\n[002] ZONE ATTRIBUTES (toggle options per zone). Bell audible, force arm, bypass enable, swinger shutdown (a chattering zone stops re-reporting after N trips in one armed period), transmission delay, cross-zone. Leave defaults unless a zone is misbehaving.\n\n[013] EOL / LOOP SUPERVISION (system-wide, all zones). Option [1] ON = normally-closed loops, no resistors. [1] OFF = EOL resistors in use, and then option [2] picks single/SEOL (OFF) or double/DEOL (ON). Classic DSC EOL = 5.6k single; double is 5.6k + 5.6k. On the panel that reads: secure loop \u2248 5600 ohm, shorted/faulted = 0 ohm, violated (contact open) \u2248 11,200 ohm, cut wire = infinite/tamper. Get [013] wrong and every zone reads open or tampered. Note per-zone terminal overrides live in [101]-[108] opt [14]/[15]/[16]. Neo uses different values and per-terminal config \u2014 check the model.\n\n[016] SYSTEM OPTIONS (a system-options toggle block). AC power supervision/trouble display, quick-arm, keypad backlight, tamper behavior, DLS toggles, and misc bits. If AC-loss trouble won't clear or quick-arm won't work, look in the system-options blocks here.\n\n[201]-[265] ZONE-TO-PARTITION ASSIGNMENT \u2014 and this is NOT one section per partition. [201] is the Partition Selection MASK: it turns partitions 1-8 on/off, it does not assign zones. The actual zone-to-partition assignment is eight sub-sections PER partition: [202]-[209] = Partition 1 zones, [210]-[217] = Partition 2, and so on up to [265] for Partition 8. All zones default into Partition 1. A zone assigned to more than one partition = global zone (arms only when ALL its partitions are armed). Neo lays this out differently \u2014 confirm on the model.\n\n[006] INSTALLER CODE and [007] MASTER CODE \u2014 these are two SEPARATE sections, not sub-menus of one. [006] holds the installer code, [007] the master, [008] the maintenance code (arms only, can't disarm). Day-to-day user codes are NOT here \u2014 those live under [*][5]. Change 5555 and 1234 on every install \u2014 leaving defaults is how a panel gets walked into.\n\n[301] PHONE / COMM. Telephone number(s) for the receiver / central station. [302] second number. That's for land-line dialer panels; on IP/cellular (TL280, LTE communicators) the reporting config moves into the communicator's own sections, not [301].\n\n[377] COMMUNICATION VARIABLES. Dialer attempts, swinger/comms retries, delays between attempts, receiver/T-link supervision timers. Where you tune how hard and how often the panel tries the CS. Don't touch without a reason \u2014 bad values = missed signals or nuisance comm-fail troubles.\n\n[005] SYSTEM & PARTITION TIMERS \u2014 the delay/siren block. Per-partition sub-sections hold Entry Delay 1, Entry Delay 2, and Exit Delay (in seconds, valid 001-255, or 045-255 on SIA CP-01 panels for egress). Sub-section [09] is the Bell Cut-Off Time (in minutes, 001-255) \u2014 factory default is 4 minutes. Typical factory delays: entry \u2248 30s, exit \u2248 120s. If a siren runs forever or times out too fast, or a door won't give you enough egress time, it's [005].\n\nWALK TEST: [*][8][installer][901] toggles installer walk test ON \u2014 trip each zone, the bell chirps ~2 seconds and logs to the buffer. Enter [901] AGAIN to turn it OFF (it does NOT end on exit, and [902] is Module Supervision Reset, not a walk-test stop). It also auto-terminates after 15 minutes of no zone activity. User-side system test is [*][6][master][4] \u2014 fires the bell, keypad buzzer, status lights for 2s, tests the battery, and sends a test report. Always walk-test after any zone or EOL change.\n\nACCESS CODES (day-to-day, no installer needed): [*][5][master code] \u2014 add / change / delete user codes, set partition and attribute flags per user. This is the master's menu, hand it to the customer; keep [*][8] to yourself.\n\nGOTCHAS: (1) Neo section numbers and menus differ from classic \u2014 a PC1864 worksheet won't map 1:1 to an HS2128. (2) EOL mismatch [013] is the #1 'all zones faulted' call. (3) The fire/24-hr zone codes in [001] are the ones people scramble \u2014 [07]/[08] are fire, [24] is nothing, [35] is bell/buzzer, [41]/[81] are CO. Verify against the printed table, not memory. (4) Change default codes every time. (5) After a [999] default you lose ALL programming including comms \u2014 dump the config to DLS or write the worksheet first. (6) Installer Lockout [990] blocks the hardware reset \u2014 check it's off before you promise a customer you can recover a lost-code panel. (7) Firmware 4.1 vs 4.6 shifted a few option bits \u2014 verify against the guide for the firmware in the box.",id:"cheatsheet-dsc",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",title:"Cheat-sheet: DSC PowerSeries programming",sources:["https://24incontrol.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/DSC-PowerSeries-Sensor-Group-Zone-Descriptions.pdf","https://24incontrol.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/DSC-PowerSeries-PC1616-PC1832-PC1864-Panel-Programming-Guide.pdf","https://cms.dsc.com/download.php?t=1&id=16913","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-power-series-pc1616-pc1832-pc1864-quickstart","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powerseries-1616-1832-1864-how-do-i-turn-off-end-of-line-eol-resistor-use","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powerseries-1616-1832-1864-how-do-i-default-panel-if-i-don-t-have-the-installer-code","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-pc1616-pc1832-pc1864-default-master-installer-code","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powerseries-1616-1832-1864-how-do-i-change-delay-times","https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/dsc/r/DSC/en-US/PowerSeries-Pro-Reference-Manual/1.3.1/Programming/Programming-descriptions/System-times/005-System-times/000-System-Area/Bell-Cutoff-Time","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powersseries-neo-hs2016-hs2032-hs2064-hs2128-quickstart"],tags:["Entry delay","Central station","Bypass"],aliases:["shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"cheatsheet-defaults",title:"Cheat-sheet: default codes & factory resets",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",tags:["Default codes","Factory reset","Installer code","Installer lockout","Takeover","Alarm panel","Sensor enrollment","Standalone lock"],body:"READ THIS FIRST. Everything below is a STARTING point \u2014 factory defaults and legacy codes, the numbers a panel ships with before anybody touches it. Half the residential jobs you roll up on still have them because nobody changed them. That's your way in on a takeover, and it's exactly why you change them the second the system is yours. On dealer-locked panels these defaults are dead \u2014 the dealer burned in a lockout so a hardware default won't wipe programming and the \"default\" installer code won't get you into programming. Assume any panel that came from a big national (or DMP, period) is locked until proven otherwise.\n\n[[diagram:factory-defaults-discipline|When a published default is a tool, when it is the wrong tool, and what you owe the customer]]\n\nVISTA (Honeywell / Resideo \u2014 15P, 20P, 21iP, P-series). Installer code 4112. Master code 1234. Enter programming: [Installer Code] + 8 0 0 \u2014 so 4112 then 800. Keypad shows \"20\" / \"Installer Code 20,\" you're in. Factory default from inside programming: *97, three beeps, done \u2014 wipes master back to 1234, installer back to 4112, dumps user codes and zones. LOCKED OUT of the installer code (previous installer changed it): pull the transformer AND the battery, dead the panel completely, reconnect transformer + battery, then within 50 seconds of power-up press [*] and [#] together \u2014 that drops you into programming (\"20\"), then key *97 to default. THE CATCH: this power-up trick only works if the previous installer exited normally (or with a plain *98 lockout). VISTA field *88 sets the lockout options \u2014 if it was set to kill [*]+[#] re-entry (or lock local programming entirely), the power-up trick does nothing and you're re-flashing or replacing. So always exit YOUR jobs with *99, never *98, or you'll lock your own self out.\n\nDSC (PowerSeries PC1616/1832/1864, and NEO). Installer code 5555. Master code 1234. Enter installer programming: *8 then installer code (*8 5555). Hardware factory default (no code, panel not locked): power down \u2014 unplug transformer AND lift both battery leads. On the board, strip the wires off terminals Zone 1 (Z1) and PGM1, then jumper Z1 to PGM1 with a short piece of wire. Power the transformer back up with the jumper in place and watch Zone 1 \u2014 the Zone 1 LED comes ON when the default finishes (about 30 seconds). Then pull power, remove the jumper, rewire. Master back to 1234, installer 5555. THE CATCH: programming section [990] Installer Lockout. If lockout was enabled, the hardware default is impossible \u2014 the jumper does nothing. Locked DSC = you need the old installer code or a new board.\n\nQOLSYS (IQ Panel 2 / 2+ / 4 / IQ Pro). Installer code 1111. Dealer code 2222. Master/default user 1234. Dealer code outranks installer \u2014 it's the top tier. Factory reset (Master Reset) lives in Settings \u2192 Advanced Settings \u2192 enter DEALER code 2222 \u2192 then the reset options. THERE IS NO BACKDOOR. If a dealer changed 2222 and 1111 and didn't give them to you, Qolsys has no hardware jumper, no power-up trick, no reset pinhole that clears codes \u2014 the panel is bricked to you. That's by design. Only path is the original dealer releasing it or a factory RMA. Don't waste an hour looking for a jumper that isn't there.\n\nDMP (XR150 / XR550, XT series \u2014 dealer-programmed, assume LOCKED). DMP doesn't ship a universal \"4112\" you'll find in the field \u2014 it's built for dealers. Out-of-box programmer defaults you may see referenced: PROG code 6653 (spells PROG on the keypad) to enter the programmer menu, INST 3577. User code default: prior to 2020 the default user was 99; on newer panels (2020+) it's generated at the factory and printed on the box label \u2014 it is NOT the last-4-of-serial, so read the box. Reset: momentarily short the RESET header/pins on the board, then enter 6653 within 30 minutes and the keypad shows \"PROGRAMMER\" \u2014 wait longer than that and it shows \"RESET PANEL\" and you start the reset over. BUT \u2014 DMP panels are tied to a dealer App Key and Remote Options; a monitored DMP is effectively account-locked, and shorting reset on a live monitored panel throws a tamper/trouble to central station. Treat every DMP as dealer property. Don't default one you don't own.\n\nINTERLOGIX / GE \u2014 WATCH THE MODEL, the code splits two ways. Simon / Simon XT / Simon XTi (self-contained wireless panels): installer 4321, dealer 4321. That's the 4321 everybody quotes. NetworX (NX-4/6/8/8E, the hardwired boards): the default programming/installer code is 9713, NOT 4321 \u2014 enter program mode with *8 then 9713. Some NetworX field defaults to try on a takeover: 9713, 6321, 4321. Test a code without committing: *8 [code] \u2014 Service LED flashes + function LEDs steady = valid. Long beep = wrong. And know your out: there is no reset for the installer code on a NetworX board \u2014 no code, you get it from the last dealer or you swap the board.\n\nWIRELESS SENSOR ENROLL \u2014 quick version, three big platforms. VISTA: in programming, go to zone programming *56 (interactive) \u2014 set up the wireless zone, then trip the sensor (open/close the contact or tamper) twice and the panel learns the serial automatically; confirm the ID, save. DSC PowerSeries: enter installer programming *8, then section [898] for auto-enroll \u2014 trip the sensor tamper (or open/close the contact) and the panel captures and displays the ESN, press [*] to accept, then key the two-digit zone (01\u201364). Section [804] is where you hand-key the printed ESN or set the wireless zone attributes; on NEO the PowerG enrollment lives under [804] and enrolls the same way by activating the device. Qolsys: Settings \u2192 Advanced \u2192 Installer/Dealer code \u2192 Installation \u2192 Devices \u2192 Security Sensors \u2192 Auto Learn Sensor, then trip the sensor \u2014 it grabs the S-Line/serial, you set group/zone type/name, Add New. Rule of thumb across all three: put the panel in learn mode, then MAKE THE SENSOR TALK (open the reed, pull the cover tamper, press the test button) \u2014 the panel learns by hearing it, not by you typing, on modern gear.\n\nSTANDALONE LOCKS \u2014 the mechanical/electronic defaults you'll meet constantly. Alarm Lock Trilogy DL2700 (and T2 family): factory Master Code 1 2 3 4 5. Open with 12345, then within 5 sec press A to enter program, enter new master. Full reset if it's scrambled: disconnect the battery pack, hold any number key ~10 sec to bleed it, reconnect batteries and within 3 sec press-and-hold A until 6 beeps \u2014 clears all codes back to factory. Kaba/dormakaba Simplex 1000 / L1000 (mechanical pushbutton, no battery): factory combo is 2 and 4 pressed TOGETHER (simultaneously, then release), then 3. Turns the knob/lever. Tons of these in the wild were never changed off 2+4/3 \u2014 that's a real-world gotcha, treat a default Simplex as unlocked. Kaba E-Plex 2000 (electronic pushbutton): default Master is the 8-DIGIT 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \u2014 not 12345. Enter program mode #12345678#, then 000# to change master, key the new 8-digit code twice each followed by #, then # to close. E-Plex master is ALWAYS 8 digits; the factory 12345678 stops working the moment you change it, and it won't let you program anything else until you do.\n\nBOTTOM LINE. These are the numbers the box ships with, not the numbers a real job runs on. On a takeover, defaults are your first three things to try before you assume you're locked out. On a fresh install or an ownership transfer, change the installer/master/dealer code before you leave the site \u2014 a system sitting on 4112/1234 or 5555/1234 is a system anybody with this same cheat-sheet can walk into. And when a panel is genuinely dealer-locked (Qolsys with no dealer code, DMP on account, DSC/VISTA with lockout set), stop hunting for a magic jumper \u2014 there isn't one, and the answer is the original dealer, an RMA, or a new board.",sources:["https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-factory-default-a-honeywell-vista-p-series-system","https://security.geoarm.com/support/solutions/articles/48000304593-how-to-reset-a-honeywell-vista-20p-system-","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/can-a-honeywell-vista-panel-be-locked-out-of-programming-mode","https://www.alarmliquidators.com/content/Vista%2020P-%20Programming%20Guide.pdf","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powerseries-1616-1832-1864-how-do-i-default-panel-if-i-don-t-have-the-installer-code","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/1039204/Dsc-Pc1616.html?page=30","https://help.aartech.ca/portal/en/kb/articles/factory-reset-dsc-pc1616-pc1832-pc1864","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/dsc-powerseries-1616-1832-1864-how-do-i-program-a-wireless-zone","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-program-a-wireless-zone-to-a-dsc-powerseries-neo","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-set-the-installer-code-back-to-default-on-a-qolsys-iq-p","https://qolsys.reamaze.com/kb/top-support-questions/can-you-default-the-system-without-entering-the-dealer-code","https://assets.dmp.com/assets/LT-1232.pdf","https://www.dmp.com/support/tech-faqs/control-panel-faqs","https://www.alarmsystemstore.com/pages/interlogix-networx-nx4-nx6-nx8-nx8e-quickstart","https://www.alarmgrid.com/faq/how-do-i-set-the-installer-code-back-to-default-on-an-interlogix","https://safehomecentral.com/alarm-monitoring/how-to-check-if-your-alarm-installer-code-is-valid/","https://tech.napcosecurity.com/index.php/faq/details/id/240/","https://www.lockmasters.com/site/pdfs/Trilogy%20DL2700%20Programming%20Cheat%20Sheet.pdf","https://kb.dormakaba.com/hc/en-us/articles/37592664018843-Simplex-1000-Resetting-the-Code","https://www.allfrom1supply.com/docs/simplex/simplex-1000-resetting-the-code-kaba.pdf","https://www.abklockandsafe.com/e-plex-2000-operations-manual.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/758925/Kaba-E-Plex-2000.html?page=9"],aliases:["factory code","default PIN","out of box code","wipe the panel","reset to defaults","programming code","tech code","locked out of programming","dealer lock","takeover blocked","panel swap","replace the panel","reuse existing sensors","Honeywell Vista","Ademco","burg panel","security panel","learn the sensor","pair the sensor","enroll zone","battery lock","no wires","offline lock"]},{id:"dropping-fishing-wire",title:"Dropping & fishing low-voltage wire: walls, ceilings & drops",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Half of access-control and alarm work is not the device \u2014 it is getting a clean wire from the panel to the door without tearing the building apart. Learn to drop and fish cable and you turn a two-hour \"we have to open the wall\" job into a twenty-minute drop. Here is how it actually goes.\n\nStart by reading the building, because the path picks itself if you look. A drop ceiling is a gift \u2014 pop a tile, and you have an open highway above the whole floor to lay cable in, no fishing at all. A wall below an accessible attic is nearly as easy: you drill DOWN through the top plate from above and drop to the opening. A wall below a basement or crawlspace, you drill UP. The hard case is a wall boxed in on both ends \u2014 finished floor above and below \u2014 and that is where the real fishing happens.\n\nThe wall drop is the bread-and-butter move. Above the wall, in the attic or the ceiling space, find the top plate \u2014 the horizontal 2x4 sitting on top of the wall studs \u2014 directly over where you want the wire to come out. Drill a hole through it with a long auger or a flex bit. Now you feed a fish down the wall cavity from that hole and catch it at the device cutout below. [[tool:glow-rods|Fiberglass fish rods (glow rods)]] pushed up from the bottom, or a fish tape fed down from the top, or the old reliable: a length of beaded chain or a nut tied to a string, dropped down the cavity \u2014 gravity does the work and you fish it out the low opening with a hook or a bent rod. Tie your cable to whatever made the connection and pull it through. Mind the fire blocks \u2014 a horizontal 2x4 nailed mid-cavity will stop your fish cold, and then you are drilling it or finding another bay.\n\nA few rules that keep you out of trouble. Keep low-voltage cable away from line-voltage romex \u2014 run it in a different stud bay, or cross it at 90 degrees, never lay it parallel and touching, or you will hum induced 60-cycle noise onto your data and buzz onto your audio. Do not share a hole with power. Leave a service loop \u2014 a few feet of slack coiled at both the panel and the device \u2014 so the next tech (probably you) has wire to work with. Any hole you drill through a fire-rated wall or floor gets firestopped with the proper putty or caulk; an inspector will fail an open penetration, and it is code for a reason. Use plenum-rated cable in any air-handling space above a drop ceiling. And label both ends the moment you land them \u2014 \"front door reader,\" \"stockroom REX\" \u2014 because a bundle of identical white cables with no labels is a day of your life you will never get back.\n\n[[img:photo-lv-labeled-runs|What that looks like when it is done right: every run tagged at the panel end. TO ELEVATOR EXP 2, to I/O CONTROL ROOF, TO FRONT DESK CLOSET EXP 1, DOLPHIN.]]\n\n[[img:photo-lv-punchdown|The same bundle mid-termination, before the tags go on. This is the moment to label, not later.]]\n\n[[img:photo-lv-labeled-12vdc|Voltage marked on the tape as well as the destination, so nobody has to meter a run to find out what it carries.]]\n\nOn a long conduit run, rods are not the only option and often not the best one. A [[tool:conduit-blower|conduit blower]] sends a foam carrier and a pull string down the pipe on air, which beats feeding rods through several bends and needs one person instead of two. The same unit run in reverse, vacuuming from the far end, is what you reach for when the run is wet, partly blocked or already has cable in it.\n\nThe tools that earn their spot: fiberglass glow rods (the segmented push kit), a steel or nylon fish tape, a good drill with a long flex/auger bit, a stud finder, a chain or fish weight, and a strong magnet-and-pull set for guiding a fish through insulation. On old work, a flexible drill bit that lets you drill the top or bottom plate right through the device cutout is worth its weight \u2014 you drill blind and fish the same hole.\n\nNow the difficult areas \u2014 the runs where a straight drop won't work and you're fishing blind through a wall packed with insulation, fire blocks, or a long horizontal reach across bays. This is where fish sticks (splined push/pull rods) earn their keep over a floppy fish tape. Screw the segments together into a rigid enough column that you can steer it \u2014 thread on a hook tip to grab and drag, a J-tip or bullnose to nose past a fire block, a flexible leader to work around a corner. Push from the accessible end, and when you hit an obstruction don't just jam harder \u2014 back off, rotate a quarter turn, and walk the tip past it. For a horizontal run you push one rod across the bay and, if it stalls, drive a second rod in from the far opening and marry the two tips in the middle. On a long or blind pull, tie a mule tape or pull string to the tip first so you never fish the same bay twice.\n\n[[diagram:fish-step-1|Rig the rod: glow-rod segments threaded end to end, with a hook, bullnose, or flex-leader tip.]][[diagram:fish-step-2|Push from the opening; at a fire block, back off, rotate, and walk the tip past.]][[diagram:fish-step-3|Long horizontal run: push a rod in from each end and marry the tips in the middle.]][[diagram:fish-step-4|Tie the pull string to the tip, draw it up, and leave a service loop.]]\n\nWhen you genuinely can't see what is stopping you, drill a small inspection hole and put eyes in the wall with a [[tool:borescope|borescope]] \u2014 the same safe scope you snake into a safe body works just as well down a stud bay. Feed the camera in and you learn in ten seconds what would otherwise cost you an hour of blind poking: where the fire block sits, whether the bay is stuffed with batt insulation, if there is a horizontal brace or a stray romex run in your path, and exactly where your fish tip actually is versus where you think it is. Use it to guide two rods together, to confirm a clear bay before you commit to a hole, and to spot a firestop you will have to drill or route around. A borescope hole is a 3/8-inch patch; opening the wall because you guessed wrong is drywall, tape, mud, and a paint match. Look before you cut.\n\n[[diagram:borescope-inspect|Feed the scope through a small inspection hole; the view cone finds the fire block and your fish tip before you commit to a cut.]]\n\nWhen the wall genuinely will not cooperate, stop fighting it. Surface raceway (a paintable plastic channel, Wiremold and the like) run neatly along the trim gets you there clean and legal when fishing is impossible, and on commercial block or brick you were running EMT anyway. The mark of a pro is not that you never surface-mount \u2014 it is that when you do, it looks intentional.",relatedGuides:["low-voltage-wiring","conduit-bending","wire-tracing-shorts","access-control-systems"],sources:["https://www.kleintools.com/catalog/wire-fish-and-glow-rods/splinter-guard-fish-and-glow-rod-kit-bag-33-foot","https://up.codes/s/firestopping","https://www.nfpa.org/"],tags:["Opening","REX","Reader"],aliases:["lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading"]},{id:"wire-tracing-shorts",title:"Tracing wire & killing shorts: the tone-and-probe (snooper)",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Two low-voltage skills separate a tech who finishes the call from one who stands there scratching his head: finding which wire is which in a bundle of identical cables, and running down a dead short that keeps tripping a device. Both come down to a tone-and-probe set and a meter, and a method.\n\n[[diagram:tone-probe-and-shorts|Tracing a pair, the live-versus-dead rule, and halving a run to corner a short]]\n\nStart with tracing, because you cannot fix what you cannot find. The tool is a two-piece kit every low-voltage tech carries \u2014 a tone generator and an inductive probe, together the \"toner\" or \"snooper\" (Fluke Pro3000, Triplett, Tempo). The generator clips onto one end of a conductor or pair and injects a warbling audio tone onto the copper. The probe is a wand with a speaker; you sweep its tip across a bundle of cables and it squeals loudest over the one carrying the tone. That is how you find your one cable in a hundred in a panel, an attic, or a punch-down block \u2014 clip the toner on at the door, go to the closet, and sweep the bundle until it screams. That is tracing a run to its source.\n\nKnow the live-versus-dead rule, because it bites people. A tone generator is happiest on a DEAD, disconnected pair \u2014 energized or terminated circuits leak the tone to ground and bleed it onto neighboring cables, so everything sounds \"kind of loud\" and you cannot pin it. Best practice: disconnect the far end and toner a bare pair. Many pro toners do have a mode that traces a live/terminated low-voltage pair, but the trace is always cleaner on a lifted conductor. And understand what the snooper is NOT \u2014 it is not a line-voltage detector. To confirm a 120V circuit is hot or dead, you use a non-contact voltage tester (the pen that lights near live AC), not the low-voltage toner. Different tool, different job, and mixing them up is how someone grabs a wire they thought was dead.\n\nNow the short \u2014 the call where a reader is dead, or an electric strike will not fire, or the power supply keeps tripping its PTC fuse and resetting. A short is simply two conductors touching that should not, or a conductor touching ground/the metal frame, giving current a free path home so it never reaches the device. Your tell is often the power supply: a resettable PTC that keeps clicking off, a fuse that blows the second you power up, or an aux output reading zero volts under load but fine unloaded.\n\nRun it down with a method, not a prayer. Kill the power first. Split the system in half \u2014 disconnect the suspect device or the run at a midpoint \u2014 and see if the short clears; that instantly tells you which half it is in. On the run itself, put your meter on continuity or ohms across the two conductors with BOTH ends disconnected: a good pair reads open (infinite); a dead short reads near zero ohms. Then chase where two wires can touch on an access-control device: the classic is the electric transfer hinge or door-loop where the cable flexes every open-and-close until the insulation wears through and a conductor grounds to the frame \u2014 wiggle-test it. A stripped-too-long conductor whiskering against the strike body or a grounded backbox. Water ingress in an exterior reader or gate bridging the terminals. A REX or contact wire pinched under a screw. Reader wiring miswired D0 to D1 or a data line shorted to ground. Isolate by disconnecting one conductor at a time and watching the meter drop from zero back to open \u2014 when it opens, the last thing you lifted is your short. Fix the pinch, re-strip and re-land clean, reseal the outdoor gear, and only then power back up and confirm the device pulls its rated current.\n\nThe discipline is the whole thing: power off, meter across a disconnected pair, halve the run to localize, then walk the flex points and terminations where copper meets metal. Tone to find it, meter to prove it, and you will clear a short in fifteen minutes that has a parts-changer swapping a good panel.",relatedGuides:["dropping-fishing-wire","low-voltage-wiring","access-control-systems","keypad-reader-servicing","alarm-troubleshooting"],sources:["https://www.fluke.com/en-us/product/networks/cable-testing/tone-generators-probes/fluke-networks-pro3000","https://www.jimdunlop.com/","https://www.tempo.textron.com/"],tags:["Reader","Electric strike","REX","Strike"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"aloa-ladder",title:"The ALOA certification ladder: RL, CRL, CPL & CML",module:"Certifications & Credentials",body:"A state license \u2014 your BSIS registration, your C-28 \u2014 says you are legally allowed to work. An ALOA certification says you are actually good at it. They are two different things, and the customers who pay the best, the property managers and institutions and insurance-driven accounts, read the ALOA letters after your name like a menu. Here is the ladder and how you climb it.\n\n[[diagram:cert-ladder|Permission and proof are two different documents, bought by two different customers]]\n\nALOA is the Associated Locksmiths of America, the trade's largest professional body, and its certification track is the Proven Professional Registration Program \u2014 the PRP. It is not a class you sit and pass; it is a stack of category exams you challenge, each scored, most at a 70% bar. You test up the ladder one rung at a time.\n\nThe bottom rung is RL, Registered Locksmith. It is largely a legacy designation now \u2014 grandfathered in at the program's start \u2014 so treat it as the entry marker, not the goal. The real first credential you earn is CRL, Certified Registered Locksmith. Per ALOA, CRL means passing the program's mandatory categories \u2014 on the order of ten core subject exams \u2014 plus two electives on top. Those mandatory categories are exactly the fundamentals of the trade: codes and code equipment, key blank and keyway identification, key duplication, key impressioning, cylinder servicing and pinning, master keying, and professional lock opening. If that list sounds familiar, it should \u2014 it is most of the Keys, Cylinders & Keying shelf of this app. CRL is the credential that gets you past a building manager's insurance requirement and turns \"some guy with a drill\" into \"a certified locksmith.\"\n\nFrom there the ladder keeps going by electives. CPL, Certified Professional Locksmith, is a CRL who has passed roughly twelve additional elective category exams. CML, Certified Master Locksmith \u2014 the top of the tree \u2014 is a CPL who clears about nine more, the advanced ground including electronic and specialty categories. The elective pool itself is deep, north of thirty categories, so you tailor the climb to your specialty: an access-control tech loads up on the electronic electives, a storefront guy on the commercial hardware ones.\n\nTwo things to keep straight. First, these numbers and category lists are set by ALOA and they revise them \u2014 always pull the current PRP requirements from ALOA before you plan your testing, because the exact counts and mandatory list shift over the years. Second, certification is not licensure. CML after your name is proof of skill and it opens doors and bids; it is not the thing the state of California requires to contract \u2014 that is still your C-28 and your BSIS card. Carry both. The license lets you work; the certification lets you charge what you're worth.\n\nHow to study it here: this whole app is, in effect, a CRL study path. Walk the master keying, keyway and blank identification, cutting-by-code, impressioning, and lock-picking guides for the mandatory categories, and the high-security and business guides round out the rest. Get CRL as fast as you honestly can \u2014 it is the single credential with the best return on your time in this trade.",relatedGuides:["t-master-keying","keyway-blank-id","cutting-by-code","impressioning","pick-types-technique","high-security-keyways","business-of-locksmithing","specialized-certs","institutional-ila"],sources:["https://www.aloa.org/","https://www.aloa.org/certifications"],tags:["Impressioning","Key blank","Credential","Keyway","Opening","Cylinder"],aliases:["file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","KW1","SC1","SC4","Ilco","JMA","Silca","blank won't fit","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"specialized-certs",title:"Specialized certifications: automotive (CAL), safe & vault (CPS/CMST), and electronic access control",module:"Certifications & Credentials",body:"Once you have your footing, the money is in a specialty, and the specialties have their own letters. Three are worth knowing cold because they are where the high-dollar, low-competition work lives: automotive, safe-and-vault, and electronic access control.\n\n[[diagram:specialty-comparison|Three specialties compared on what they actually cost and the life they buy]]\n\nAutomotive: the CAL, Certified Automotive Locksmith, offered through ALOA. It is the credential for the guy who does car keys \u2014 and car keys have quietly become one of the most profitable corners of the trade, because a dealer wants a fortune and a tow to make a lost smart key, and you can do it in the customer's driveway. CAL is about vehicle key origination (making a key when all keys are lost), transponders and immobilizer systems, key and remote programming, and advanced automotive opening. It is a moving target \u2014 every model year brings new chips, new security, new tooling \u2014 so it rides on a subscription to information and equipment as much as on the cert. Study it here in the automotive locksmithing guide, and lean on the Lishi guide for the decode-and-open side.\n\nSafe and vault: this world runs through SAVTA, the Safe & Vault Technicians Association, an ALOA-affiliated body, and its ladder mirrors the main one. CPS, Certified Professional Safe Technician, is the working credential; CMST, Certified Master Safe Technician, is the top tier for advanced safe and vault service \u2014 manipulation, drilling and penetration, lock changes, and vault work. A lot of the hands-on training for both the automotive and the safe side comes through the Lockmasters Security Institute (LSI), which is the big independent training school the trade uses to prep for these designations and to get manufacturer-specific instruction.\n\nElectronic access control: this is the third specialty, it is the one growing fastest, and its credentials do *not* come from ALOA \u2014 they come from the alarm and security-integration world, which is why locksmiths who only read locksmith literature never hear about them. The ladder worth chasing, roughly in order: ESA's National Training School (the Electronic Security Association) runs the certified alarm and access technician tracks and is the standard entry credential on the integration side. SIA, the Security Industry Association, runs access control courses that are strong on the systems-design and standards end. BICSI is the one to chase for the cabling craft specifically \u2014 installer training, structured cabling practice, the discipline of terminations and pathways done right \u2014 and it is the cert that separates someone who *pulls* wire from someone who *installs* a cable plant. At the top, ASIS International's PSP, Physical Security Professional, is the design-and-mastery credential; it is a security-management certification rather than a hands-on one, so treat it as where you go once you are specifying systems rather than terminating them. The standard book on the subject is Thomas Norman's *Electronic Access Control* \u2014 buy it and work through it.\n\nAnd before any of that, the free education that most people skip: manufacturer install manuals. Read them like textbooks, not like references you flip open when you are stuck. Whatever panel line the shop you work for has standardized on \u2014 Keri's PXL and Borealis docs, Mercury, Software House, whatever is in the racks \u2014 those manuals contain the wiring diagrams, the current draws, the jumper settings, and the failure modes that no third-party course will teach you, and they are sitting on the manufacturer's website for nothing. The tech who has actually read the manual cover to cover is the one who knows why the board has that jumper, and that shows on a job site within a week. Study the systems side here in the access control systems guide and the wiring side in the low-voltage wiring and power guide.\n\nThe pattern to see: the main ALOA ladder (CRL \u2192 CPL \u2192 CML) proves you are a broad, competent locksmith, and these specialty designations \u2014 ALOA's own on the mechanical side, ESA/SIA/BICSI/ASIS on the electronic side \u2014 prove you are a specialist an insurer or a fleet or a bank will trust with the hard, expensive jobs. Pick the specialty that matches the work in your market \u2014 around here, automotive and commercial safe service both have real demand \u2014 and stack the specialty cert on top of your CRL.\n\nAs always: verify current requirements straight from ALOA, SAVTA, and LSI before you plan, and remember the certification is the proof of skill that sits on top of your state license, not a replacement for it. Study the safe side here in the safe-basics and safe-anchoring-and-opening guides.",relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","lishi","t-safe-basics","safe-anchoring-opening","aloa-ladder","institutional-ila","auto-nastf-vsp","auto-transponders-immo","access-control-systems","low-voltage-wiring"],sources:["https://www.aloa.org/","https://savta.org/","https://www.lockmasters.com/","https://www.esaweb.org/nts/","https://www.securityindustry.org/professional-development/","https://www.bicsi.org/","https://www.asisonline.org/certification/physical-security-professional/"],tags:["Anchoring","Credential","Manipulation","Opening","Lishi","Immobilizer"],aliases:["anchor bolts","lag bolts","sleeve anchor","bolt it down","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","contact points","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","2-in-1","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies"]},{id:"institutional-ila",title:"Institutional locksmithing & the ILA exam",module:"Certifications & Credentials",body:"There is a whole branch of this trade that never turns a lockout on the side of the road: the institutional locksmith. That is the person on staff at a university, a hospital, a school district, a prison, a big corporate campus \u2014 one facility, thousands of doors, one enormous master key system, and a life-safety code hanging over every opening. It is steady, salaried, benefited work, and it has its own professional body and its own credential.\n\n[[diagram:institutional-role|Almost everything inverts, and the whole job comes down to keeping a key system true]]\n\nThe body is the ILA, the Institutional Locksmiths' Association, and its certification is earned through a mandatory exam of about 250 questions. What that exam weighs heaviest tells you what the job actually is: life-safety and building codes, and broad general locksmith knowledge. An institutional locksmith lives and dies by egress and fire code \u2014 every door they touch has to let people OUT in a fire in a single motion, has to keep fire doors closing and latching, has to satisfy an inspector and the AHJ \u2014 because when it is a hospital or a dorm, a locked egress door is how people die and how the institution gets sued. So the exam leans hard on codes, on fire-door and exit-hardware knowledge, on master key system design and management, and on the mechanical fundamentals.\n\nIf you look at what that exam covers, you already have most of it in this app. The master keying guide is the core skill \u2014 an institution's whole security model is one giant keying hierarchy, and running it without creating a key-interchange nightmare is the institutional locksmith's daily art. The fire-code and closing-speed material, the exit-device and door-closer guides, and the C-28-versus-C-7 and licensing material cover the code and life-safety weight of the exam. The commercial security survey guide is the mindset. What the ILA adds on top is the scale and the setting \u2014 managing a system of that size, keeping records, and living inside an institution's procedures and its AHJ relationship.\n\nWhy it matters even if you never take the exam: institutional work is where a locksmith with a family goes for a steady paycheck and a pension, and the credential is how you get hired into it. If a big campus or hospital in the area is what you're aiming at, the ILA cert plus a mastered understanding of egress code and master keying is the resume that gets you the interview.\n\nVerify the current ILA exam content and requirements from the association before you sit it \u2014 associations revise their exams \u2014 but the study path is right here: master keying, the fire-code and egress guides, exit devices and closers, and the licensing and survey material.",relatedGuides:["t-master-keying","closing-fire-code","ca-scope-c28-c7","commercial-security-survey","door-closers-adjustment","business-of-locksmithing","aloa-ladder"],sources:["https://www.institutionallocksmiths.org/","https://www.aloa.org/"],tags:["AHJ","Credential","Opening"],aliases:["fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{body:"Impressioning is how you make a working key for a lock you can't decode and won't take apart. No key gauge, no bitting list, no pulling the cylinder. You read the marks the lock's own pins beat into a blank, file those spots down a hair at a time, and let the lock tell you the cuts. Done right, the lock hands you its own key. It's real trade knowledge \u2014 impressioning is one of the ten mandatory categories on the ALOA Certified Registered Locksmith (CRL) exam, so you have to know the tools and technique cold to get certified. (Fair warning: the CRL itself is a written test \u2014 the proctored, cut-a-key-on-the-clock version of impressioning lives in the contests, like the ones Jos Weyers has won at the hacker cons. Either way, learn it clean and by feel.)\n\n[[diagram:impressioning-marks|A mark means keep filing; a clean position means stop. Filing a clean position is how the blank dies]]\n\nThe physics first, because the whole technique hangs on it. When you put turning tension on a plug, the pins don't all bind at once. One pin \u2014 the tightest in its chamber relative to shear \u2014 binds first and stops the plug. That binding pin is pressing its tip against your blank. Every other pin is loose and marks nothing yet. So each round of work reads exactly one cut. Bind, mark, read, file, repeat. You're not carving a key; you're answering one question at a time.\n\nSetup. Pick a blank that fits the keyway snug \u2014 right section, seated all the way to the bottom of the keyway, no slop side to side. A loose or tilted blank walks in the keyway, and a tilted one already has some pins pushed up, so you get smeared, lying marks. Prep the top of the blade so it'll take a clean impression: draw-file it flat, then knock the shine off with fine paper lengthwise. That fine tooth is what you want \u2014 a slightly dull, faintly corrugated surface a pin tip will burnish with almost no pressure. A mirror-polished blade won't show a thing.\n\nMaterial. The trade answer, and what the top competition impressioners run, is soft brass \u2014 it marks readily and files predictably. Aluminum and other soft blanks take a mark with even less pressure, which is why some practice and impressioning-specific blanks are soft alloy: the marks jump out. The catch is aluminum is weak and cracks under hard turning tension, so you baby the torque or you snap the blade in the plug. Steel blanks are the opposite \u2014 durable, but so hard the pins barely mark them and you file forever. For learning and for most openings, soft brass is the sweet spot: soft enough to read, tough enough to torque. One quirk to know \u2014 soft brass tends to load up the file teeth, which dulls the fine surface you're trying to keep, so clean the file often.\n\nMaking the marks. Seat the blank, put turning tension on it, and work it. There are three named methods \u2014 wiggling, tapping, and pulling \u2014 but they all do the same job: with the plug torqued so one pin binds, you flex the blade with small up-and-down movements so the binding pin's tip rubs and polishes its position onto the surface. Tension is the whole ballgame. Too little and no pin marks. Too much and the pins jam solid \u2014 they have to be able to move a hair to burnish the blade, and if you crank the torque they lock up and mark nothing, or you crack an aluminum blank. There's no magic angle; the plug only rotates a few degrees before the binder stops it anyway. You want firm, deliberate, repeatable tension and light flex so exactly one pin \u2014 the binder \u2014 leaves a clean witness mark this round.\n\nRead the marks. Pull the blank and get it under a 10x jeweler's loupe with hard raked light. A flashlight low across the blade throws the marks into relief; some impressioners use fluorescent marking fluid and UV to make faint marks pop. On a sanded bare-brass blade the binding pin shows as a tiny bright, burnished spot where the pin polished the dull surface \u2014 a shiny dot in the matte. That's your position. To make marks pop harder, the old smoke method: hold the blade in a candle or match flame until it carbons black, then the pin wipes a clean spot through the soot. Soot on a bright blank, or bright burnish on a sanded blank \u2014 either way you're hunting the one spot the metal moved.\n\nFile. At the marked position, take down metal with a 6-inch #4 Swiss-cut round or pippin file. The pippin's teardrop section \u2014 rounded belly on one side, two flats meeting at a knife edge on the other \u2014 cuts a proper pin-shaped valley and reaches into the keyway grooves; the round file does general cutting. The #4 Swiss cut is chosen on purpose: it removes metal in fine bites and leaves that slightly dull, corrugated finish that re-marks on the next round without re-sanding. File a little \u2014 a few light strokes, a few thousandths, cutting on the forward push only and lifting on the return. Match the file's round profile to the round pin tip so the cut seats the pin. Then re-blank, re-tension, re-work, re-read. The mark at that position gets fainter as the pin approaches its shear cut, and when the pin sets it stops binding and stops marking. Now the next-tightest pin binds and marks somewhere else. Chase them one at a time down the blade. When the last pin sets, the plug turns. That's your working key \u2014 cut by the lock, no code ever known.\n\nWhat it's good for. Lost-key openings where you need a working key, not just an open door \u2014 you walk away with a key that runs the lock. It's non-destructive and covert: nothing drilled, cylinder never pulled, often no visible sign. Good on standard pin-tumbler and wafer locks. On wafers, sharpen both edges of the blade so the wafers cut their own grooves, and expect marks at the bit edges.\n\nThe limits. It's slow and it's a feel skill \u2014 a fresh apprentice may take many rounds and a few ruined blanks per lock. High-security cylinders are built to beat it: tight tolerances, hardened anti-drill pins, serrated and spool pins that throw false marks, sidebars and secondary systems (Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, ASSA) that don't read off a single top pin. Worn or gummy locks give mushy marks. And every wrong file stroke is permanent \u2014 over-file a position and that blank is scrap.\n\nCousins worth knowing. Foil impressioning: fold soft aluminum foil over a blank or carrier, work it into the lock under tension with small up-and-down moves, and the binding pins push the foil down until each hits shear \u2014 the foil becomes the bitting directly, no filing. Clay and silicone methods take a negative impression of an existing key rather than reading the lock, so they need the key in hand \u2014 a duplication trick, not a no-code opening. Same family, different tool: let the mechanism do the measuring.",id:"impressioning",title:"Key impressioning: cutting a working key with no code",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",sources:["https://unitedlocksmith.net/blog/a-beginners-guide-to-lock-impressioning","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Impressioning","https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Locksmithing/Key_Impressioning","https://s3.amazonaws.com/s3-absupply-net/pdf/abs/impressioning-manual.pdf","https://www.lockpickersmall.com/pippin-file-fine-cut/","https://www.lockpickworld.com/blogs/news/world-champ-shows-us-how","https://www.aloa.org/certifications","https://www.howtobecomealocksmith.org/how-to-become-a-certified-registered-locksmith/","https://lock401.com/blogs/academy/impressioning-101","https://www.lockpickworld.com/blogs/news/impressioning-lock-picking-with-a-working-key","https://boingboing.net/2010/02/18/foil-impressioning-l.html","https://blackbag.toool.nl/?p=1122"],relatedGuides:["cutting-by-code","keyway-blank-id","pick-types-technique","aloa-ladder"],tags:["Impressioning","Key gauge","Plug","Bitting","Keyway","Cylinder","Opening"],aliases:["file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","key decoder gauge","depth gauge","read the key","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","lockout","locked out","get in","emergency opening"]},{id:"automotive-locksmithing",title:"Automotive locksmithing: transponders, key origination & opening",module:"Automotive",body:"Automotive is its own trade inside the trade. ALOA carves it out as the CAL \u2014 Certified Automotive Locksmith \u2014 the entry credential built on three legs: vehicle opening, key/fob duplication, and key origination. Pass it at 70% or better and you can climb to CMAL, the master ticket. What makes it its own animal is that since roughly the late 1990s the key stopped being just a piece of cut metal. Cut a perfect key today and turn the ignition, and a lot of cars still won't start. That's the immobilizer talking, and understanding it is the whole game.\n\n[[diagram:auto-key-anatomy|Three jobs in one key, and which failure signature points at which]]\n\nTHE IMMOBILIZER (IMMO)\n\nModern keys carry a transponder \u2014 a tiny glass or chip antenna in the plastic head, no battery. Turn the ignition and the coil ring (exciter coil) around the lock cylinder energizes that chip by induction. The chip answers back with an ID. The immobilizer function (often living in the BCM, or a dedicated IMMO/PATS module) checks that ID against what it has learned; only then does it authorize the ECU to enable fuel and spark. Wrong chip, no chip, unlearned chip \u2014 engine cranks, catches, and dies, or never fires. The mechanical cut turns the lock; the chip starts the car. Two separate jobs, and you have to solve both.\n\nChips split into two families. Fixed-code transponders (Texas Instruments 4C, Philips/Temic ID33) send the same static number every time. Simple, and that's exactly why they clone easily. Crypto chips do challenge-response: the car sends a random challenge, the chip encrypts it with a secret key and sends back a computed answer that's never the same twice. Key length depends on the cipher \u2014 TI DST40 (the 4D/\"Texas Crypto\" chips) runs 40 bits, Hitag2 (Philips Crypto/ID46, PCF7936) runs 48, and Megamos Crypto (ID48) runs 96. Hitag2 sends a fresh initialization vector every transmission so the answer never repeats \u2014 specifically to kill replay attacks. Here's the catch that matters at the counter: \"crypto\" doesn't mean \"uncloneable.\" DST40 and Megamos were both cracked by researchers years ago, which is why the older 4D and many ID46 chips clone in practice even though they're technically challenge-response. Newer DST80 and Hitag3 chips hold up. Know your chip before you quote \u2014 that's what a transponder catalog is for.\n\nKEY ORIGINATION \u2014 ALL KEYS LOST (AKL)\n\nThis is the money job and the one that separates pros. Customer has zero working keys. You need to produce a cut key AND get a valid transponder recognized.\n\nGet the cut. Two legit paths. One, pull the key code: through NASTF's Secure Data Release Model (SDRM) you request the factory bitting and, on many cars, the immobilizer PIN. NASTF vets and credentials you as a Vehicle Security Professional (VSP) \u2014 application, business documentation, background check, roughly $435 for a two-year subscription (a $100 application fee plus a $335 account fee) \u2014 and every transaction is logged. That registry is the difference between a locksmith and a car thief in the eyes of a manufacturer and a court. Two, decode the lock yourself with a Lishi 2-in-1. There's a tool per keyway (TOY48 for late Toyota/Lexus, HU100 for GM 8-cut, HU101 for Ford, and roughly a hundred more); it picks the wafers and reads the cut depths off the same tool, so you decode the working bitting directly from the door or ignition. Feed those codes to a code-cutting machine (Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus, Dolphin, Triton) and cut a fresh blade.\n\nGet the chip learned. Cutting is half. Now the transponder has to be added to the IMMO. Usually OBD-II: plug an Autel IM508/IM608, Xhorse VVDI, or Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro into the diagnostic port, read or enter the PIN, and add the key. Some ECUs won't give it up over OBD \u2014 then you go on the bench: pull the module, read the EEPROM (or MCU) with a programmer, extract the PIN/secret or write the key data directly, reinstall. Expect security wait timers. AKL on many GM cars runs about a 12-minute immobilizer relearn where you cycle the ignition (some procedures have you work the door) and the tool counts down; older Passkey systems can hold you 30 minutes; Chrysler/FCA and others have their own cycles. Budget for it \u2014 AKL on a push-to-start car is commonly 90\u2013150 minutes on site, $600\u2013$900, because of these timers and bench work. On some dealer/OEM procedures AKL specifically requires a NASTF PIN or dealer-tool token that no aftermarket shortcut replaces.\n\nCLONING vs PROGRAMMING\n\nTwo different operations, and picking wrong wastes a blank.\n\nCloning copies an existing working key's chip onto a blank. No car connection, two minutes, no PIN, no timer \u2014 but you need a working key to copy from. Works cleanly on fixed-code (4C) and on the broken-crypto chips (DST40 4D, many ID46, though ID46 is genuinely hit-or-miss). Tools: Ilco/Advanced Diagnostics AD900, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool, 2M2 Magic Tag, Zed-Bull, JMA. Great for a spare when the customer has one key.\n\nProgramming a virgin key adds a brand-new, unlearned chip to the car's immobilizer via the procedures above. Required whenever you can't clone \u2014 a chip whose crypto still holds, or AKL where there's no source key. This is where PIN, OBD/bench, and wait timers live.\n\nRule of thumb: got a working key and want a spare on a cloneable chip? Clone. All keys lost, or a chip that won't clone? Originate and program.\n\nPUSH-TO-START / PROXIMITY SMART KEYS\n\nThe fob IS the key \u2014 no blade in the ignition, an LF/RF proximity handshake unlocks and enables start (there's usually a hidden emergency blade and a backup start method \u2014 hold the fob to the button or a marked spot). AKL on prox systems is the hardest tier: you often program on the bench or with high-end tools, some require a gateway bypass cable, and the timers are unforgiving. These systems also change fastest \u2014 a procedure that worked last quarter can need a software update this quarter.\n\nAUTO OPENING (VEHICLE ENTRY)\n\nLockouts pay the bills between key jobs, and the standard is non-destructive. The workhorse combo: an air wedge (inflatable bladder \u2014 slip it into the door/frame gap, pump gently for a few millimeters of space, stiff enough to enter but soft enough not to crease the door) plus a long-reach tool (Access Tools, Jiffy-Jak) fed through that gap to hit the unlock button, inner handle, or lock stalk. Under-window try-out tools work the linkage on older cars. Wedge only as much as you need; overinflate and you bend sheet metal or pop a window. These tools sell to trade only \u2014 locksmiths, repo, dealers, tow.\n\nTWO THINGS TO CARRY OUT THE DOOR: legitimacy and currency. Run everything through NASTF/VSP and keep records \u2014 it's your license and your legal cover. And accept that tools, chips, and procedures change constantly; the pro is the one who keeps software updated and never assumes last year's method still works.",sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/11360683/programming-and-cloning-automotive-transponder-equipped-keys-remotes-and-fobs","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2026/05/26/id46-vs-pcf7936-vs-hitag2-transponder-chip/","https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~garciaf/publications/Dismantling_Megamos_Crypto.pdf","https://keyless-city.com/blogs/locksmith-101/the-truth-about-transponder-super-chips-a-guide-for-automotive-locksmiths","https://sdrm.nastfsecurityregistry.org/","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755447-what-does-the-vsp-subscription-cost-","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755440-what-is-nastf-credentials-for-mechanics-technicians-locksmiths-","https://wp.nastf.org/?page_id=367","https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-hu100-b116-2-in-1-pick-ignition-door-trunk-8-cut-ag","https://www.obdii365.com/service/lishi-key-cutting-machine-read-toy48-key-bitting-code-when-akl.html","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/key-machine-key-cutters/article/21151947/the-condor-xc-mini-plus-a-high-precision-automatic-key-cutting-machine","https://iamcarhacker.com/obd2-scanner/service-functions/how-to-program-car-keys-complete-beginner-to-pro-guide/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/11/11/program-gm-2021-2024-all-keys-lost-with-autel-im508-im608/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/accesstools-large-super-air-wedge-long-reach-car-opening-stick-tool-bundle","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230077/understanding-wedging","https://www.aloa.org/certifications","https://autokey.ca/certified-automotive-locksmith/"],relatedGuides:["lishi","cutting-by-code","specialized-certs","auto-transponders-immo","auto-key-origination","auto-smart-fobs","auto-nastf-vsp"],tags:["Transponder","Opening","Immobilizer","Air wedge","Push-to-start","Cloning","Fob","PATS"],aliases:["chip key","immo chip","Hitag","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","wedge bag","pump wedge","car lockout tool","gap the door","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","card","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"auto-transponders-immo",title:"Transponders & immobilizers: how the car checks the key",module:"Automotive",body:"Here is the thing that trips up every apprentice who came from house work: on a car, cutting the key right is only half the job. You can cut a blade that turns the ignition cylinder like butter, and the engine will still sit there dead. That is not a bad cut. That is the immobilizer doing exactly what it was built to do. Get this system in your head before you ever quote a \"lost all keys\" job, because it is the difference between a 20-minute origination and a tow to the dealer.\n\n[[diagram:transponder-handshake|The challenge and answer that happen in the second before the engine keeps running]]\n\nTHE HANDSHAKE\n\nBuried in the head of the key... not the blade, the plastic bow... is a glass or ceramic transponder, a tiny coil of wire and a chip. It has no battery. Around the ignition lock cylinder sits an antenna ring, sometimes called the exciter coil or halo. When you turn the key to ON, the car pushes a burst of low-frequency energy (125 kHz territory) through that ring. That field powers up the chip by induction... the same way a wireless charger works... and the chip wakes up and transmits its ID back through the ring.\n\nThat signal goes to the immobilizer control unit. Depending on the platform that is the BCM (body control module), the ECU/ECM, a dedicated IMMO box, or on BMW a CAS/FEM unit. The module checks the chip ID against the list of keys it was taught. Match... it releases the immobilizer and authorizes the ECU to deliver fuel, spark, and injector pulse, and lets the starter engage. No match... it kills one or more of those. That is why an unprogrammed key behaves the way it does: the mechanical cuts turn the tumblers, the starter may even crank, but the ECU refuses to fuel it and it stalls or never lights. Some platforms cut the crank entirely, some let it crank and starve it. Either way, no valid chip, no run. A dash light shaped like a car with a key, or the word SECURITY, flashes to tell you it locked you out.\n\nThat is the whole game: a mechanically correct key that is not programmed turns but will not start.\n\nFIXED-CODE VS CRYPTO... THE TWO ERAS\n\nEarly chips, roughly mid-90s into the early 2000s, were fixed-code. The chip just shouts one static number every time, like a name badge. Texas 4C, the early Philips and Megamos fixed types (ID13, ID33). Because the number never changes, you can read it off a working key and write it to a blank cloneable chip on the bench... a CN5, a TPX, a Super Chip... and the car cannot tell the copy from the original. Cheap, fast, and the reason those cars are easy money and easy theft. No online anything.\n\nOne thing to keep straight from this era: GM's early VATS / PASS-Key was NOT a transponder at all. It was a resistor pellet embedded in the key blade, and the column read the resistance value... analog, 15 possible values, no radio handshake. Ford's PATS (SecuriLock), out in 1996, was the real transponder version on the domestic side. Do not let an old GM \"chip in the blade\" fool you into thinking you need a programmer when all it wants is the right resistance.\n\nThen the manufacturers got smart and went to crypto, also called rolling or challenge-response. Now the module does not just listen for a name... it sends the chip a random challenge, a number that changes every cycle. The chip runs that challenge through a secret key baked into it and sends back an answer. The module runs the same math and compares. An eavesdropper hears a different exchange every time, so recording it buys you nothing, and you cannot clone it by copying a static ID. The families you will actually see:\n\nTexas Crypto (DST) \u2014 the 4D chip, in its ID60/62/63 variants. This is where 40-bit vs 80-bit comes from. The original DST40 used a 40-bit secret key. In 2005 a team out of Johns Hopkins and RSA reverse-engineered the cipher and brute-forced that 40-bit key with an array of FPGAs... a single one took about 11 hours, a bank of them ran it down in under an hour. TI answered with DST80, an 80-bit key: on Toyota that is the \"G\" chip (4D-72 / ID72), on Ford/Mazda the ID63 \"6F.\" Bigger keyspace, much harder to brute force... though KU Leuven later showed some carmakers configured DST80 so sloppily it was barely better than DST40. Toyota then jumped again to DST-AES, 128-bit, the \"H\" chip (4D-74/75)... that one lives in the AES tier below. Toyota/Lexus, older Ford, GM leaned on Texas.\n\nPhilips/NXP Hitag2 \u2014 the ID46 chip (PCF7936/7946/7947 family), a 48-bit crypto. This one is everywhere: Renault, Fiat, Opel/GM, Nissan, Honda, Chrysler, and BMW CAS2/CAS3. Workhorse of the 2000s. Also since broken by researchers, which is why the tools can generate keys for a lot of it.\n\nMegamos Crypto \u2014 ID48, a 96-bit key, the VAG staple: VW, Audi, Seat, Skoda, plus Porsche, Volvo, some Fiat and Honda. Long keyspace, held up longer, until the \"Dismantling Megamos Crypto\" paper (Verdult and Garcia, Radboud/Birmingham) tore it open... an attack that recovers the 96-bit key from two eavesdropped handshakes, or from a weak-key setup in minutes on a laptop. VW got a UK court injunction that held the paper back two years; it finally published at USENIX Security 2015.\n\nAES tier / ID4A and friends \u2014 the current generation moved to AES-128, and there is no single chip that owns it. NXP's Hitag-AES (ID4A, the PCF7939 family) is the workhorse here: newer Hyundai/Kia, Nissan, Renault, and FCA/Jeep. VAG did the same thing to Megamos... the MQB cars run Megamos AES (ID88). Toyota's H chip is DST-AES. Different silicon, same idea: a 128-bit AES challenge-response. This is the \"you are not cloning it, and you are not brute-forcing it\" tier. These jobs go through proper programming with dealer-level access, and increasingly through NASTF... you need VSP credentials (obtained through the Secure Data Release Model, the SDRM registry) and often a Secure Gateway (SGW... FCA/Stellantis since 2018, plus GM and others) unlock to even talk to the module. FCA specifically also wants a NASTF PIN for 2014-and-up immobilizer work. Those requirements tighten every year, so verify current before you promise a customer.\n\nHOW TO IDENTIFY WHAT A CAR USES\n\nDo not guess. Start with year/make/model and pull it from a transponder catalog (Transpondery, the Ilco/Advanced Keys cross-reference) which lists chip family and blank. Cross-check the FCC ID printed on any existing remote. Best confirmation is electronic: drop a working key on a chip reader or your programmer (Autel, Zed-Full, AD, Tango, Smart Pro) and let it report the chip ID... 4C, 4D-60, ID46, ID48, ID4A. That also tells you cloneable vs crypto, which decides whether you clone on the bench or program to the module through the OBD port.\n\nWHY THIS IS AUTOMOTIVE-ONLY\n\nThere is no equivalent in house or commercial work, and here is why. A residential deadbolt or a commercial mortise lock is pure mechanics... the right cuts lift the pins, the plug turns, done. Even electronic access control on a building checks a credential to unlock a door; it does not disable a running machine. The immobilizer exists because a car is a valuable object sitting outside that historically you could hot-wire or force the column and drive off. The chip is a second, electronic lock that defeats the mechanical bypass: even with the cylinder turned, the car will not power its own drivetrain without the handshake. A door has nothing to immobilize. That is the whole reason transponders live in your automotive kit and nowhere else.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_signature_transponder","https://www.ise.io/casestudies/exploiting-rfids/","https://www.esat.kuleuven.be/cosic/blog/dismantling-dst80-based-immobiliser-systems/","https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~garciaf/publications/Dismantling_Megamos_Crypto.pdf","https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity15/technical-sessions/presentation/verdult","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/toyota_transponder_catalog.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2026/05/26/id46-vs-pcf7936-vs-hitag2-transponder-chip/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/megamos-aes-transponder-chip-mqb-id88","https://keytectools.com/product/kia-hitag-128-bits-aes-id4a-transponder-chip/","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755446-what-is-a-nastf-vehicle-security-professional-","https://www.fcarusa.com/SGW","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SecuriLock"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-key-origination","auto-smart-fobs","auto-nastf-vsp","specialized-certs"],tags:["Immobilizer","Transponder","FCC ID","PATS","Mortise lock","Cloning","Cylinder","Bypass"],aliases:["anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"auto-key-origination",title:"Making Car Keys: Origination, OBD Programming & All-Keys-Lost",module:"Automotive",body:"This is the money skill. Anybody can duplicate a key that already exists. Originating one from nothing \u2014 getting into a locked car, decoding a lock you have no key for, cutting metal to a code, and marrying an encrypted chip to the immobilizer so the engine actually starts \u2014 that is what the customer pays real money for and what the CAL exam wants you to know cold.\n\n[[diagram:key-origination-decision|One question splits the job: is there a working key, or is this all-keys-lost]]\n\nFIRST QUESTION ON EVERY JOB: is there a working key present, or is this all-keys-lost? That one answer decides everything downstream. \"Adding a spare\" means the customer hands you a key that starts the car. You already have a working credential the immobilizer trusts, so most systems let you add another key through the OBD port in a couple of minutes \u2014 no security wait, no PIN drama on a lot of makes. \"All-keys-lost\" (AKL) means nothing runs the car. Now you have to prove to the immobilizer, from cold, that you're authorized. That's the hard, slow, expensive job. Quote accordingly.\n\nTHE WORKFLOW, START TO FINISH: (1) get in, (2) decode the cuts, (3) cut a mechanical key that turns the lock, (4) program the transponder to the car. On a spare-key job you can sometimes skip straight to programming because the customer's key already cuts and turns \u2014 but on AKL you walk every rung.\n\nGET IN + DECODE. Your primary tool is the Original Lishi 2-in-1: a single-pin pick and a decoder in one stainless blade, one tool per keyway. HU100 covers most GM/Opel/Vauxhall (there's an 8-cut and a 10-cut version, so match the car); HU101 covers modern Ford/Lincoln, Volvo, Jaguar, Land Rover; HU66 is the older VAG family (VW/Audi/Porsche/Seat/Skoda, pre-2015 \u2014 2015-2021 VAG moved to the HU162T \"three-track\"); HU64 is Mercedes-Benz (and a few Sprinter/Crossfire Chryslers built off Mercedes locks \u2014 don't confuse it with VAG); TOY-series for Toyota/Lexus. You single-pin-pick each wafer to open the lock, and with the plug held open the tool's internal scale reads each wafer's depth \u2014 you write the cuts straight off the decoder. A clean HU100 door in a couple minutes with practice. Pick the DOOR when you can, not the ignition \u2014 door locks usually carry the same code, have fewer wafers, and don't risk the ignition's steering-lock complications. Two other roads to the cuts: pull the code from the lock cylinder if you remove it (stamped or read on a scope), or get the cut code from the manufacturer via your NASTF login and cut \"to code.\" Decoding on the car is faster than pulling cylinders on most jobs.\n\nCUT THE KEY. Two ways: cut TO THE DECODE (the depths you just read off the Lishi) or cut BY CODE (the code you pulled, run through software to the bitting). Modern automotive is high-security/internal-cut (sidewinder/laser) far more than edge-cut now, so you want a machine that handles it \u2014 Xhorse Condor/Dolphin, Triton, or a code machine with the right jaw and cutter. The blade has to physically turn the lock before any chip talk matters. Get the mechanical right first.\n\nPROGRAM THE TRANSPONDER. Cutting metal only defeats the mechanical lock. Two quick history notes the exam likes: GM's VATS (1985 Corvette on) was NOT a transponder \u2014 it was a resistor pellet in the blade, and the car read its resistance value; and Ford's PATS (1996+) was the first mainstream true RF transponder immobilizer. Since the mid-to-late '90s essentially every car has a transponder immobilizer: a chip in the key head that has to answer the immobilizer's challenge or the ECU kills fuel/spark. Know the chip families for the exam: 4C (Texas Instruments fixed-code, old Toyota/Ford, easy clone); the 4D family (TI DST40, IDs roughly 4D60 through 4D72 \u2014 Toyota/Nissan/Ford \u2014 the plain ones like 4D60/63 clone, the encrypted ones must be generated); ID46 (NXP/Philips Hitag2 crypto, PCF7936/7941 \u2014 GM/Chrysler/Honda era); and the modern encrypted AES generation you CANNOT clone. In that last group, keep them straight: Toyota's 8A chip IS the \"H-chip\" \u2014 that's TI DST-AES (128-bit) (Lexus/Toyota smart ~2010+, non-smart H 2014+); HITAG-AES/Hitag3 is the separate NXP AES family (newer VAG, BMW, others); Megamos AES (ID88) is VAG; Ford DST-AES (128-bit) is newer Ford/Lincoln. Rolling-code and AES chips change their answer every use, so cloning is out \u2014 you GENERATE a fresh key and program it in.\n\nThe everyday tool is an OBD-II programmer plugged into the DLC (the 16-pin under the dash): Autel MaxiIM (IM508S/IM608 Pro), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Max / Key Tool Plus, or Advanced Diagnostics SmartPro. It talks to the immobilizer/BCM, and you tell it to ADD a key, ERASE lost keys, or read key count and PIN. On a spare-key job with the customer's working key, that's frequently the whole programming step. Two big real-world gotchas at the port: FCA (Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep/RAM, 2018+) has a Secure Gateway Module that blocks aftermarket write access \u2014 you either subscribe to FCA AutoAuth (about $50/yr) or splice in a 12+8 star-connector bypass cable at the SGW (note: on 2021+ vehicles where the SGW is folded into the BCM, the bypass cable won't work \u2014 AutoAuth only). And many makes demand a security PIN / secret code before they'll let you write a key.\n\nWHERE IT GETS HARD \u2014 PIN/SECRET KEY AND AKL. That PIN (called incode/outcode, secret key, CS, or PIN depending on make) is the gate. Three ways to get it: your programmer calculates it on-tool for older systems; you pull it legitimately from the manufacturer through NASTF (below); or on locked-down systems you go on-bench \u2014 remove the BCM or immobilizer box and read the EEPROM directly to extract the PIN/secret data, then program. AKL routinely forces the bench: Toyota 8A/H-chip AKL, for instance, means reading immo data (Xhorse's 8A AKL adapter can do it via OBD on many without opening the box) or generating an all-keys-lost key against the extracted data.\n\nTHE SECURITY WAIT TIMER. On AKL, many immobilizers won't accept a new key until a security/anti-scan timer expires \u2014 key on, tool connected, and you sit and wait. It varies by make: GM is classically ~10 minutes (sometimes cycled several times), and some systems run 16 minutes or longer. It's an anti-theft delay, not a fault. Build it into your quote and your patience.\n\nNASTF. The legitimate channel. The National Automotive Service Task Force runs the Secure Data Release Model (SDRM) and vets locksmiths as Vehicle Security Professionals (VSP). A VSP account (your credential is the VSC/LSID) lets you pull key codes, PINs, and immobilizer data straight from the automaker. Getting in requires business registration, a valid driver's license, locksmith license where applicable, commercial general liability insurance (currently $1M aggregate / $500K per-event for primary account holders), and a background check; the 2FA is Twilio Authy. Approval historically runs on the order of days to a couple weeks \u2014 verify current timing. Some late Toyota and GM AKL jobs now require manufacturer-secured data you can ONLY get this way. Get registered \u2014 it's the difference between \"sorry, can't do it\" and a completed AKL.\n\nRequirements, chip coverage, insurance minimums, and gateway rules change constantly \u2014 verify the specific year/make/model against your tool's current coverage and NASTF before you quote.",sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-hu100-10","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-hu66-twin-lifter","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/mercedes-hu64-v-3-2-in-1-tool-by-original-lishi","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21255220/lishi-the-right-pick-for-automotive-locksmiths","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/which-transponder-keys-are-critical-for-locksmiths-in-the-usa","https://keyless-city.com/blogs/locksmith-101/the-truth-about-transponder-super-chips-a-guide-for-automotive-locksmiths","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SecuriLock","https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/understanding-the-ford-pats-passive-anti-theft-system/","https://autel.us/product-category/key-and-immobilizer-programming/key-programming/","https://www.mykeysupply.com/product/advanced-diagnostics-smart-pro-vehicle-key-programmer-with-6-months-utp/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2023/05/30/where-to-get-toyota-8a-4a-all-keys-lost-12-digit-security-code/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/xhorse-toyota-8a-non-smart-key-all-keys-lost-adapter-via-obd","https://www.youcanic.com/fca-security-gateway-module-explained-obd2-sgm-sgw/","https://abkeys.com/products/lonsdor-fca-sgw-128-special-adapter-bypass-jeep-security-gateway-5674","https://sdrm.nastfsecurityregistry.org/","https://wp.nastf.org/?page_id=367","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000562902-nastf-application-process-new-vsp-primary-account-set-up","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755446-what-is-a-nastf-vehicle-security-professional-"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-transponders-immo","auto-smart-fobs","auto-nastf-vsp","specialized-certs"],tags:["OBD programming","Immobilizer","Transponder","Decoder","Security pin","PATS","Lishi","Bypass"],aliases:["OBD2","onboard diagnostics","plug in the tool","Xtool","MVP Pro","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","spool","serrated","mushroom","spool pin","anti-pick pin","Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","lockout","locked out","emergency opening","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"auto-smart-fobs",title:"Smart keys, proximity & push-to-start",module:"Automotive",body:"Let's kill the confusion first, because customers and half the counter guys mix these words up. A chip key is a blade you turn \u2014 it just happens to have a transponder glass inside the head so the immobilizer will let the engine run. A smart key, a \"prox,\" a \"fob\" \u2014 that's a different animal. You don't turn anything. The car finds the fob for you and lets you unlock by grabbing the handle and start by pushing a button. Under the plastic it's really two radios doing two different jobs, and if you understand that split you'll diagnose these things in your sleep.\n\n[[diagram:smart-fob-and-relay|Why proximity works, why relaying defeats it, and what actually helps]]\n\nJob one is the transponder side \u2014 same idea as a chip key, just wireless and hands-free. There are Low Frequency (LF) antennas buried in the car: in the door handles, one or two in the cabin, one in the trunk, and a ring right around the START button or the ignition slot. Those antennas whisper at roughly 125 kHz (you'll see 125\u2013134 kHz). That's a short-range, near-field field \u2014 it barely reaches past the sheet metal, and that's the whole point. When you pull or touch a door handle, the car fires an LF challenge out that handle antenna. If a valid fob is standing in that little bubble, it wakes up, does its crypto handshake, and answers. Door pops. Same thing at the button: the cabin ring has to see the fob inside the car, brake pressed, button pushed, engine cranks. No fob in the bubble, no start. That in-cabin LF detection is why you can leave the fob in your pocket and why the car yells at you if you try to walk away with it running.\n\nJob two is the remote \u2014 the UHF side. That's the button-press lock/unlock/panic/trunk, and the fob's answer back to the car's challenge. In North America that rides on 315 MHz. Europe and most of Asia run 433.92 MHz (you'll also hear 868 in Europe). Get the frequency right when you order \u2014 a 315 fob is deaf to a car looking for 433. So a smart key is an LF transponder that listens at 125 kHz and a UHF transmitter that talks at 315 or 433. Two radios, one shell.\n\nNow the security, because this is where the money and the theft live. That UHF remote uses a rolling code \u2014 a hopping code, KeeLoq being the classic. Every press the fob and car both advance a synchronized counter and encrypt it, so a code that flew once is dead the next second. That kills the dumb replay attack, recording your press and playing it back. But rolling code doesn't save you from two attacks you need to know cold, both for defending customers and for sounding like you've been around. First, the relay attack \u2014 this hits passive entry. Two thieves, two boxes: one stands by your front door where the fob sits on the hall table, catches the car's faint LF and amplifies it out to the fob; the fob answers over UHF, the boxes relay it back, and the car thinks the key is at the handle. They open and drive off. The fob never moved. Second, RollJam \u2014 Samy Kamkar's DEF CON 2015 trick, a roughly $30 SDR rig \u2014 the attacker jams the car so it can't hear your press, quietly records your rolling code, you press again thinking the first didn't take, they record that too and let the second through. Now they're holding one unused valid code. Defensive gospel for customers: a Faraday pouch or even a steel tin for the fob, park in the garage, keep the fob away from the front door and windows, and know that newer fobs with motion sensors go to sleep when they sit still \u2014 that alone beats most relay rigs.\n\nThe emergency blade. Every real smart key hides a mechanical key \u2014 pop the release, the blade slides out of the fob body, and there's a physical keyway on the driver's door (often under a plastic cap on the handle). That's your way in when the 12V battery is flat and the handle's dead, or when the fob battery died in the parking lot. And there's usually a backup start: the fob has a spot \u2014 a slot in the console, a pocket, or you hold it against the START button \u2014 where the car's LF ring couples to it directly at 125 kHz even with a dead fob battery, same near-field trick as a chip key against a coil. Teach customers both. Half your \"car won't open\" calls end with \"there's a real key in that thing, sir.\"\n\nBatteries and re-sync. Most fobs run a CR2032 coin cell \u2014 a two-dollar part that generates a hundred-dollar panic. Symptoms: shorter remote range, having to be right on the door, intermittent no-start that the backup-start slot cures. Swap the coin, mind the polarity, snap it shut. Some platforms want a quick re-sync after a battery swap or a dead cell \u2014 cycle the ignition, press a remote button, or follow the OEM onboard steps; many just re-sync on the next valid handshake.\n\nWhy these cost real money to cut and why you can't always do it in the driveway. Adding a spare when the customer still has a working key is usually an OBD job \u2014 plug in, enter the security PIN, add the fob, done. All-keys-lost is the beast. No working key means you're reaching into the immobilizer, and on modern cars that PIN or incode comes through NASTF's Vehicle Security Professional program \u2014 a background-checked credential (currently about $425 for a two-year registration, and you have to carry commercial liability insurance) that unlocks the OEM's security data through the SDRM registry. That credential gets you the code; getting the tool to talk to the car is a separate hurdle. FCA/Stellantis 2018-and-up cars sit behind a Secure Gateway Module (SGW) that blocks the OBD port until your scan tool authenticates through AutoAuth. GM's newer Global B cars and Ford's FDRS platform each make you run an online session against the OEM's own servers. Add the fob itself running $200\u2013$600-plus and dealer-level programming, and you see why a lost prox is a $400\u2013$900 ticket, not a $90 chip key. That's not gouging \u2014 that's the credential, the tool subscription, the online authorization, and the part.",sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_key","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_keyless_system","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_code","https://www.analog.com/en/resources/app-notes/remote-keyless-entry-systems-overview.html","https://www.americanradioarchives.com/what-radio-frequency-does-car-key-fobs-run-on/","https://github.com/CR11CS/RollJam-315MHz-433MHz","https://slate.com/technology/2015/08/rolljam-hacker-device-defeats-rolling-codes-security-for-cars-garages.html","https://dl.acm.org/doi/full/10.1145/3627827","https://wp.nastf.org/?page_id=3628","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755446-what-is-a-nastf-vehicle-security-professional-","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21215988/opening-the-fca-security-gateway","https://www.youcanic.com/fca-security-gateway-module-explained-obd2-sgm-sgw/","https://toca.site/en/blogs/news/keyless-go-relay-attack-protection"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-transponders-immo","auto-key-origination","auto-nastf-vsp","specialized-certs"],tags:["Rolling code","Push-to-start","Fob","Relay","Security pin","Transponder","Credential","Immobilizer"],aliases:["code grabbing","remote won't pair","proximity key","keyless go","prox fob","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","spool","serrated","mushroom","spool pin","anti-pick pin","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","card","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","housing","lock body","cylinder body","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"auto-nastf-vsp",title:"NASTF, the VSP & legitimate code access",module:"Automotive",body:"Here is the thing nobody teaches you at the key machine: cutting the key is the easy half. The hard half is being allowed to. Every code you pull, every PIN, every immobilizer packet \u2014 the OEM hands that to you because you are on a list of people the industry has decided to trust. That list is NASTF. Lose your spot on it and you are just a person with a key machine and no data. Understand this backbone or you will forever be running back to the dealer.\n\n[[diagram:nastf-authorisation-chain|Identify, record, authorise, obtain... and when to walk away]]\n\nWHAT NASTF ACTUALLY IS\n\nNASTF is the National Automotive Service Task Force \u2014 a not-for-profit sit-down between the automakers and the independent aftermarket. It exists because when cars went to immobilizers in the late '90s, the security data (key codes, PINs, immo resets) got locked behind the OEMs, and independent shops and locksmiths were shut out. NASTF is the treaty that reopened that door legitimately. It is not a government agency and it is not a licensing board \u2014 think of it as the referee that lets the OEMs release security-sensitive data to vetted outsiders without handing the keys to every car thief with a laptop.\n\nTHE SDRM AND THE VSP\n\nThe mechanism is the Secure Data Release Model \u2014 the SDRM \u2014 and its front door is the VSP Registry. VSP stands for Vehicle Security Professional. You register, you get vetted, and when you are approved you are issued a Vehicle Security Credential (VSC). Your login username under that credential is your VSPID \u2014 that's the current term; the older paperwork and a lot of old-timers still call it the LSID, or Locksmith ID. With that credential you go to the individual OEM websites \u2014 the manufacturer portals, what the trade loosely calls D2D access \u2014 and you pull three kinds of data straight from the source:\n\n- KEY CODES \u2014 the bitting so you can cut a mechanical key by code on your Triton or Condor instead of needing an existing key or picking the lock. This is how you originate a key from a VIN.\n- PINs / SECURITY CODES \u2014 the numbers that let you into a component: radio anti-theft, and on many makes the incode/outcode or seed you need before a programmer will talk to the immobilizer.\n- IMMOBILIZER / SECURITY PACKETS \u2014 reset, relearn, and activation data so you can add a key, do an all-keys-lost, or introduce a replacement module (BCM, IMMO) to the vehicle network.\n\nThat is the whole point. The immobilizer means a cut key won't start the car \u2014 you have to marry a transponder to the vehicle, and on a lot of modern makes you cannot do that at all without a valid NASTF pull. VSP access is what turns you from a guy who opens doors into a locksmith who makes working keys without the dealer.\n\nGETTING VETTED\n\nThis is not a checkbox. The application wants a legible color copy of a current U.S. or Canadian driver's license, plus proof you are who you say \u2014 proof you own the business or are a full-time (not 1099/contract) employee of it. That's a current W2 with the SSN redacted, a recent pay stub, or a dated government business license naming you. W4s and W9s are not accepted. You carry commercial general liability insurance \u2014 NASTF's floor is $1 million aggregate / $500,000 per event \u2014 and the certificate has to come to them straight from your agent, not from you. If your business runs sub-accounts for employees, expect an employee-dishonesty/surety bond requirement on top. There is a background check. You sign the terms and conditions of the Registry. You set up two-factor with the Twilio Authy app, because that login is worth money to the wrong people \u2014 when you hit an OEM site you enter your VSPID as the username and the 7-digit Authy token as the passcode. Cost lands around $425 for a two-year credential, and approval typically runs a couple of weeks. Treat every one of these numbers as a moving target \u2014 insurance minimums, fees, and accepted documents all change, so verify at nastf.org before you quote yourself anything.\n\nTHE D1 FORM \u2014 WHY YOU DON'T SKIP IT\n\nHere is the discipline that separates a pro from a thief, and it is one piece of paper: the D1 form. Every security transaction is supposed to be tied to a real owner and a real vehicle. Positive ID policy means before you pull that code you verify the customer with a valid state-issued photo ID and you record it: name, address, phone, driver's license, year/make/model, plate, VIN, odometer, and their signature. You compare the ID to the form. If the story doesn't match \u2014 the \"owner\" has no ID, the plate doesn't match the VIN, they're nervous about signing \u2014 you walk. That instinct is the job.\n\nAnd you keep the paper. D1 forms get retained two years in a secured spot, because NASTF randomly audits them. A completed D1 is due within five days of the transaction \u2014 leave one unresolved on your dashboard past those five days and the system locks you out of new transactions until you clear it. Let it slip past ten days and you're looking at account suspension. Records are not busywork \u2014 they are the thing that proves, when a stolen car turns up and the trail runs through a code pull, that the pull was yours and it was clean.\n\nTHE LINE YOU DON'T CROSS\n\nYour VSPID is yours. Sharing passcodes or your credential with anyone but the OEM website is grounds for suspension, full stop \u2014 NASTF actively hunts code-sharing, because a shared VSP login is exactly how a chop operation gets legitimate-looking data. The moment you hand your login to a buddy, or pull a code for a car you never verified an owner on, you have stopped being a locksmith and started being the supply chain for a car thief. The whole SDRM only survives because the OEMs believe the people holding credentials police themselves. Every clean D1, every verified ID, every code you refuse to pull on a sketchy job is you paying into that trust so the door stays open for all of us.\n\nFOR THE EXAM AND THE TRUCK\n\nKnow the alphabet cold: NASTF (the task force), SDRM (the release model), VSP (you, the professional), VSC (your credential) with the VSPID/LSID as your login, D1 (the ownership/ID record). Know that it exists to release security data to vetted independents, that it requires vetting plus a background check plus insurance, and that positive ID and record-keeping are mandatory, not optional. On the truck, know that no VSP credential means no legitimate all-keys-lost on most modern makes \u2014 you'd be handing that revenue back to the dealer. Requirements shift constantly; the only current source of truth is NASTF itself.\n\nOne distinction worth keeping straight, because customers and job posts blur it: automotive \"security\" is really two different careers. What you do \u2014 physical vehicle access and key/immobilizer programming \u2014 is the NASTF VSP path above, and for a locksmith the VSP is THE credential, full stop. The other path is automotive CYBERSECURITY: designing, testing, and hardening the ECU software, the CAN bus, and connected-car networks. That world certifies against ISO/SAE 21434, the road-vehicle cybersecurity standard (T\xdcV Rheinland runs the courses and the CySec Specialist exam), with senior credentials like the CACSE \u2014 Certified Automotive Cyber Security Expert (SGS-T\xdcV Saar) \u2014 for engineers with years of experience who pass a live expert audit. That is an engineering/IT track, not a locksmith one. Know it exists so you can speak to it, but for making keys and beating immobilizers legally, your credential is the NASTF VSP.",sources:["https://wp.nastf.org/?page_id=367","https://sdrm.nastfsecurityregistry.org/","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755440-what-is-nastf-credentials-for-mechanics-technicians-locksmiths-","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000562902-nastf-application-process-new-vsp-primary-account-set-up","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000562437-certificate-of-insurance","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755451-what-documentation-do-i-need-to-provide-","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755643-nastf-application-checklist","https://wp.nastf.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/VSP-TIP-SHEET-NASTF-V15.pdf","https://curtslock.com/images/1-SDRMRegistryPositiveIDPolicyandD1Form.pdf","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755977-choosing-a-d1-form","https://support.nastf.org/support/solutions/articles/43000755979-d1-form-issues","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10229579/where-are-we-now-nastf-key-codes-and-the-locksmith","https://www.vehicleservicepros.com/distribution/parts-distributor/aftermarket-technology/article/21196101/nastf-working-to-stop-vsp-code-sharing","https://www.aloa.org/certifications"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-transponders-immo","auto-key-origination","auto-smart-fobs","specialized-certs"],tags:["Handing","Credential","Immobilizer","Bitting","Transponder"],aliases:["LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start"]},{id:"auto-opening",title:"Opening a car without damage: wedges, long-reach & tools",module:"Automotive",body:"You get more lockout calls than any other kind of automotive work, so learn to open a car clean \u2014 no scratched paint, no bent frame, no cracked glass, no set-off airbag. Do it right and the customer never knows you were in there. Do it wrong and you're buying a door skin or, worse, a curtain airbag.\n\n[[diagram:car-opening-hazards|What is behind the glass, and the method that avoids all of it]]\n\nFIRST THING, EVERY TIME: verify the car is theirs. Before a wedge touches the glass, confirm the person is authorized. Ask for a driver's license and current registration with matching names. It is not legally mandated in most states, but every pro shop requires it, and the ALOA / CAL expect it. Metro's policy will be the same. If names don't match or the \"owner\" can't produce paper, you walk. This protects you from a theft or liability charge and keeps you off the hook for helping a car thief or a domestic-dispute repo. No verification, no entry \u2014 no exceptions. Log it.\n\nTHE BASIC PLAY: WEDGE, GAP, REACH. Non-destructive entry on a modern car is three moves. Gap the top of the door with a wedge, slide a long-reach tool through the gap, and manipulate whatever the car gives you inside \u2014 the unlock button on the door panel, the lock knob on the sill, or the inside door handle. That's \"wedge and probe.\" You are not prying the door; you're making a controlled inch of space and threading a rod in.\n\nTHE WEDGE. Start with a plastic corner wedge or a trim tool to break the seal at the top rear corner of the door, where the frame flexes most. Slip weatherstrip aside gently \u2014 don't tear it. Then seat an air wedge (pump wedge / air bladder) into that corner and pump SLOWLY. You want about an inch, maybe an inch and a half, of clearance \u2014 just enough to pass a rod. Watch for resistance. If the door stops lifting under moderate pressure, STOP. Forcing it bends the door skin, warps the frame, breaks the weather seal, and the customer gets a water leak next rain. Over-inflation is the number-one rookie mistake and it can crack glass. The air wedge spreads load better than a hard wedge, but it can still deform sheet metal if you keep pumping. Moderate is the word.\n\nKEEP THE WEDGE LOW AND FORWARD OF THE AIRBAG. This is the caution that matters most on today's cars. Curtain (side) airbags run along the roofline and down the A-pillar, tucked behind the headliner. Wedging high at the top-front corner of the door, right at the A-pillar, is exactly where you do NOT want to jam a rigid tool or spread hard. Keep your wedge point low and toward the rear of the window, work below the airbag line, and never drive a probe up into the headliner. A curtain bag is an expensive mistake and a safety hazard if you disturb the module or wiring. Seat airbags and door-panel bags are another reason to stay off the lower interior panel with a slim jim \u2014 one snag can catch airbag wiring, the lock module, or the window regulator.\n\nGLASS. Most cars still run tempered side glass, but laminated side glass is showing up more and more \u2014 premium models and a lot of newer SUVs use it for a quieter cabin and theft deterrence, and heavy aftermarket tint changes how any glass and film behave. So don't assume: laminated flexes differently and doesn't forgive a hard wedge the way tempered does \u2014 spread pressure is what cracks it, and laminated fights you instead of shattering clean. Go easy, spread slow, and if the car has thick tint, remember you can't always see your rod's contact point through it, so go by feel.\n\nTHE LONG-REACH TOOL. Once you've got your gap, feed the long-reach rod (the long steel or coated rod, 40\u201355 inches typical in a kit) down through the opening at an angle toward the interior control. Coated/wrapped tips protect the trim. Your target depends on the car: the electric unlock button on the armrest, the manual lock knob on the door top or sill, or the inside door handle pull. Go slow and gentle \u2014 patience, not muscle. Too much force damages the linkage, the trim, or the button. Know your target before you start: sit on your side, look through the glass, figure out whether it's a button, a knob, or a handle pull, and plan the path. A fiber-optic or inspection camera through the gap helps you see the target on unfamiliar interiors.\n\nUNDER-WINDOW AND LEVER TOOLS. Besides the long rod, keep under-window tools and lever tools in the kit. Under-window tools slip between the glass and the outer weatherstrip to reach linkage just inside the door top on some designs; lever tools give you a purchase point to work a stubborn knob or press a button that the straight rod can't angle onto. Match the tool to the interior \u2014 a straight rod for a clean shot at a button, a hooked or lever tool when you have to go around trim.\n\nMIND THE PINCH POINTS. When you deflate the wedge, the door snaps back to the frame. Keep fingers, rods, and your camera cable out of the closing gap. A frameless-window door or a hardtop with no B-pillar frame can pinch hard. Ease the air out; don't let it slam.\n\nLISHI \u2014 ACTUALLY PICK THE DOOR LOCK. When the interior gives you nothing to grab \u2014 flush buttons, guarded knobs, deep-set handles \u2014 or you just want the cleanest possible entry, pick the door lock itself with a 2-in-1 Lishi. The Lishi picks AND decodes the wafers in one tool, matched to the keyway. You pick to the right keyway or the tool won't seat, so know these: HU101 for Ford / Land Rover / Jaguar / Volvo (the high-security Ford keyway, roughly 2011 on, plus Volvo and JLR of that era); HU100 for GM (Chevy/GMC ~2014\u20132018 trucks and SUVs, some Cadillac later); TOY2 (TOY2Track) for Toyota/Lexus; and for VW/Audi it depends on the year. Older VW/Audi (~1996\u20132017) is HU66, but newer ones (~2015 up) switched to the HU162T keyway \u2014 and that comes in 8-cut, 9-cut, and 10-cut variants that do NOT interchange, so you need to identify which lock you're on before you reach for a tool. Pick the exposed lock \u2014 usually the driver's door or trunk \u2014 and it opens the door without ever touching the airbag or the glass. Some HU101 tools, like the V3 CutOut, are cut so the tool clears a concealed door-handle lock and won't scratch the paint. Bonus: because it decodes as it picks, you read the cuts and can cut a working key from the same job. On many newer cars the door lock is hidden under a cap or the fob is the only \"key,\" so know which models even have a mechanical door cylinder before you commit to Lishi.\n\nWHAT I'D REACH FOR. Buttons or a knob you can see and reach: wedge-and-probe, fastest clean open. Nothing to grab inside, or a car where you don't want to risk the interior: Lishi the door lock. Either way \u2014 verify ownership first, keep the wedge low and off the airbag, go slow, and leave no marks.",sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230077/understanding-wedging","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/how-to-open-a-locked-car-door-without-a-key-lockout-kit-guide-for-beginners-pros","https://parts.ectts.com/air-wedge-car-door-lockout-kit/","https://www.atc-locks.com/blog/emergency-car-lockout-tips-you-need-to-know/","https://www.originallishi.com/hu101-v3-cutout-2-in-1-pick/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-hu101-cutout-v3-ford-10-cut-2-in-1-pick-decoder-ag","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-hu100-2-in-1-pick-ignition-door-trunk-10-cut-ag","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-hu162t-2015-2021-audi-vw-9-cut-2-in-1-pick-decoder-ignition-ag","https://blog.xhorsevvdi.com/how-to-choose-lishi-vw-hu162t-tools/","https://www.keyfirst.eu/en/audi/89-lishi-hu66-original-tool-audi-vw-seat-skoda-ford-porsche.html","https://www.socal-autoglasskings.com/post/tempered-vs-laminated-glass","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/locksmiths-verifying-car-ownership-before-making-keys-or-unlocking","https://expresslocksmithhouston.com/how-do-locksmiths-verify-ownership/"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-transponders-immo","auto-key-origination","auto-smart-fobs","specialized-certs"],tags:["Air wedge","Opening","Lishi","Keyway","Fob","Cylinder"],aliases:["wedge bag","car lockout tool","gap the door","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"auto-key-cutting",title:"Automotive keys & cutting: edge-cut, laser/sidewinder, VATS/PATS",module:"Automotive",body:"Let's talk about the metal, because every car job still starts and ends with a blade that has to physically turn a lock \u2014 even on a push-to-start car, that emergency blade in the fob has to work when the battery dies. Get the mechanical side right and the electronics are just the second half of the job.\n\nTwo families of automotive cuts, and you sort every key into one of them the second it hits your hand. First is the edge cut, the traditional key \u2014 cuts filed into the top and/or bottom EDGE of the blade, the same shape of key you've cut a thousand times residential. Ford's old H75, GM's B-series, most everything domestic through the 2000s. You cut these on a standard machine with a wheel, guided off the original or off a code card. Second is the internal cut \u2014 call it laser, sidewinder, or high-security. The cut is a winding TRACK milled into the FLAT FACE of the blade, running down the center, not the edge. Introduced in the '90s as premium anti-theft; you'll hear it called \"worm cut.\" It's harder to pick, harder to duplicate, and it does NOT go on your old wheel machine. You need a milling machine with an end-mill cutter and a tracer or decoder probe \u2014 a Triton (Lock Labs), a Miracle, or an Xhorse Condor XC-MINI. Those machines run edge AND laser, decode by tracing an existing key, and cut from a code. If a shop tells you they can't do a Euro or a late-model Toyota key, this machine is why \u2014 they never bought one.\n\n[[diagram:auto-keyways|Automotive keyways by make: edge vs laser/sidewinder]]\n\nThen there's the oddball you'll meet on older Ford and Jaguar: the Tibbe. It's neither edge nor a normal sidewinder \u2014 it's a rotary DISC lock, roughly '97 into the 2010s. The key is round-ish with cuts that rotate discs until a sidebar drops and the lock turns. Ford Scorpio/older Mondeo ran 6 cuts, 4 depths; Jaguar ran 8 cuts, 3 depths. Here's the mentor tip the ledger will back up: no electronic machine decodes a Tibbe. Depth 1 is a no-cut and there are only 4 depths, so you decode it by EYE, or with an Original Lishi Tibbe pick/decoder (6-cut Ford, 8-cut Jaguar), read the cuts, and cut the blank on a dedicated Tibbe fixture. Slow, manual, but dead simple once you've done one.\n\nNow the blank itself, because modern automotive is two products fused into one: the BLADE and the TRANSPONDER. The blade is the mechanical part I just described. The transponder is a glass or carbon chip in the head of the key that the car's immobilizer has to see or the engine won't run \u2014 you'll cut a key that turns the lock perfectly and the car still won't start, because you cut the blade but never programmed the chip. That's the number-one apprentice call I get. So when you order a blank you're specifying BOTH: the mechanical (H75 edge, HU101 laser, FO21 Tibbe) and the chip. On Ford that's PATS.\n\nPATS \u2014 Passive Anti-Theft System \u2014 is Ford's transponder immobilizer, on basically everything from the late '90s. The chip talks to a transceiver ring around the ignition; no valid code, no fuel/spark. First thing to get straight: don't confuse the chip TYPE with the bit length \u2014 two different things people mush together. The EARLIEST PATS, roughly '96 to the mid-2000s, ran a Texas Instruments 4C chip. That 4C is a FIXED-CODE chip \u2014 no rolling encryption \u2014 which is exactly why those early keys got cloned so easily. Then Ford moved to the ENCRYPTED 4D63 chip, and that's where the bit length matters: 40-bit (roughly 2001 into 2010) and 80-bit (2011 and up; you'll see the 80-bit called 4D63 80-bit or 4D83). Ford jumped to 80-bit around 2011 specifically to kill the sniffer/cloning attacks the older chips were falling to. The gotcha to memorize is between those two encrypted flavors: an 80-bit blank is BACKWARD compatible \u2014 it'll work on a 40-bit car \u2014 but a 40-bit key will NOT work on an 80-bit car. Strattec originals stamp \"S\" (40) or \"SA\" (80); Huf stamp \"H\" or \"HA.\" When in doubt, buy the 80-bit. PATS keys usually need on-board programming or a tool (and two working keys, or a timed relearn), and a lot of post-2011 work needs a capable aftermarket tool or, on the locked-down stuff, dealer-side codes pulled through Ford's channel \u2014 that's where NASTF and a Vehicle Security Professional (VSP) credential come in. Flag it plainly: those access requirements change, so verify current before you quote.\n\nGM's legacy system is VATS \u2014 the resistor key. This one's beautiful because it's pure ohms, no computer. From '86 into the mid-2000s, GM put a little black pellet on the blade with a resistor in it, 1 of 15 values. The ignition reads the resistance; wrong value, the car locks you out for a few minutes. Pre-'95 keys are 6-cut single-sided (available in all 15 values, codes 1-15); '95-up went 10-cut double-sided (14 values, codes 2-15 \u2014 value 1 got dropped because the system couldn't reliably read that lowest resistance). The whole job is measuring that pellet. Set your multimeter to the 2K ohm scale, touch the two pads on the pellet, read it, match it to the chart: Code 1 is 402\u03a9, then 523, 681, 887, 1130, 1470, 1870, 2370, 3010, 3740, 4750, 6040, 7500, 9530, and Code 15 is 11800\u03a9. Cut the blade, buy the matching-value blank, done \u2014 no programming machine needed. If you can't reach the pellet on a broken key, you can read the resistance across the car's own harness at the VATS module. Old system, still rolls into the shop.\n\nTwo mindsets before you cut, and knowing which one you're in is half the skill. DUPLICATING means you have a good key \u2014 trace it, copy the cuts, clone or program the chip off the original. Fast, cheap. ORIGINATING means there's no key, or a broken one \u2014 a lockout, a lost-all-keys. Now you're decoding: pull the code off the lock (Lishi 2-in-1 to pick AND read the wafers on many cars), or look up the key code from the VIN through a NASTF LSID / dealer channel, and cut BY CODE. Cutting by code means you punch the depth-and-space numbers straight into the Triton/Condor and it mills the blade from the numbers \u2014 no original needed at all. That's the difference between a real auto locksmith and a guy with a copy machine: originate a working key from nothing but the car and a code. Lost-all-keys also means you then have to program the immobilizer with no existing key to lean on \u2014 which is where all-keys-lost is a bigger job (and bigger ticket) than a simple spare.\n\nBottom line for the van: carry edge blanks, the common laser blanks (HU101, Toy44), Tibbe blanks for the Euro-Ford holdouts, VATS blanks in all values, both 40- and 80-bit Ford, a multimeter, a Lishi set for your common keyways, and a milling machine that does laser or you're turning down half the modern calls.",sources:["https://mcguirelocksmith.com/service-articles/automotive/vats-pellet-number/","https://remotesandkeys.com/blogs/blog/how-to-determine-which-vats-resistor-value","https://www.lockmasters.com/site/pdfs/TT4002%20Vats%20Overview%20Article.pdf","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/intro-to-vats-keys","https://lockcity.co.uk/learning-centre/ford-pats-immobiliser-guide/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10250626/technology-on-the-move-ford-80-bit-key-blanks","https://www.securityinfowatch.com/access-identity/access-control/key-control/product/10225724/strattec-security-corp-ford-40-and-80-bit-key-blanks","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/transponder-keys-explained-h94-ford-80-bit","https://locksmithcharley.com/transponder/ford.shtml","https://treasurecoastlocksmith.com/blog/ford-pats-key-system-explained/","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/a-guide-to-the-tibbe-key","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10279128/tibbe-locks-live-on","https://www.genuinelishi.com/products/genuine-lishi-tibbe-decoder/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21219615/shifting-to-laser-cut-keys","https://www.lockpicks.com/products/triton-plus-ultimate-edition-tpue","https://transponderisland.com/shop/tit-xh-cmp-ii-xhorse-condor-xc-mini-plus-ii-automatic-key-cutting-machine-35071"],relatedGuides:["automotive-locksmithing","auto-transponders-immo","auto-key-origination","auto-smart-fobs","specialized-certs"],tags:["PATS","Decoder","Lishi","Immobilizer","Transponder","Push-to-start","Cloning","Key code"],aliases:["Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","decode the lock","reading tool","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","immo","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"auto-key-history",title:"A century of car keys: from crank locks to digital keys",module:"Automotive",body:"Understanding where the car key came from tells you why your automotive kit looks the way it does \u2014 every era left a tool in your bag.\n\n[[diagram:car-key-generations|Five generations, none of them retired, all still on the road]]\n\nIt starts in 1910. The very first \"car key\" did not start anything \u2014 early cars were hand-cranked to life, so the key only locked the electrical system to keep someone from running your battery down or tampering with the ignition circuit. A lock, not a starter.\n\n1949 is the year that changed the job. Chrysler introduced the key-operated ignition that let a driver turn the cylinder to crank and start the engine \u2014 no more separate starter button and hand crank. That is the ancestor of every ignition lock you service today, and the reason \"turn the key to start\" feels like it was always there. It wasn't; it's a Chrysler idea from just after the war.\n\n1965, Ford gives you the double-sided key \u2014 cut on both edges so it drops into the lock either way, no fumbling for the right orientation in the dark. Before that era, drivers commonly carried two keys, one for the doors and one for the ignition; the industry slowly moved to a single key that did both. When you cut an old edge-cut car key today, you're cutting Ford's 1965 convenience.\n\nThe 1990s bring the key fob and the beginning of electronics in the key. Automakers like Mercedes-Benz and Chevrolet built remote transmitters into the key so an owner could lock and unlock \u2014 and eventually start \u2014 the car wirelessly. This is also the decade the transponder and the immobilizer arrive, and the moment your job stopped being purely mechanical: now the key had to be *programmed*, not just cut. Everything in the transponders-and-immobilizers guide starts here.\n\nPresent day, the physical blade is disappearing. Push-button start with a proximity fob is standard, and manufacturers like Tesla, BMW, and Volkswagen now ship smartphone-based digital keys \u2014 your phone (or a card) is the credential, over Bluetooth/UWB/NFC. The mechanical cylinder is becoming a hidden emergency backup, and the \"key\" is software. That is exactly why automotive work moved from a key machine to a diagnostic tablet, and why staying current \u2014 and legally credentialed through NASTF \u2014 is the whole game now.\n\nRead that timeline as your toolbox: the pre-'90s cars want a key machine and a decoder; the transponder era wants a programmer; the smart-key era wants a tablet, online access, and a VSP credential. One trade, a hundred years of locks.",relatedGuides:["auto-transponders-immo","auto-smart-fobs","automotive-locksmithing","auto-nastf-vsp","auto-key-origination","specialized-certs"],sources:["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Car_key","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_key"],tags:["Fob","Transponder","Credential","Decoder","Immobilizer","Cylinder"],aliases:["keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"auto-cybersecurity",title:"Automotive cybersecurity: the car-hacking landscape (awareness)",module:"Automotive",body:"You are not going to hack an ECU on a lockout call, but you should know this world exists \u2014 because customers ask about it, thieves use pieces of it, and it is the other half of \"automotive security\" you keep hearing about. This is the DEF CON Car Hacking Village territory: the digital attack surface of a modern car. Here is the map, and at the end, the parts that actually touch your trade.\n\n[[diagram:auto-attack-surface|Where the ways in actually are, and what it means for your van]]\n\nCAN bus and hardware. The CAN bus is the car's internal nervous system \u2014 a shared network every module talks on, and by design it trusts any message on the wire. That trust is the whole problem. CAN INJECTION is now a real-world car theft method: a thief reaches a wire behind a headlight or wing mirror, splices a small device onto the CAN, and injects spoofed messages that tell the car \"a valid key is present \u2014 unlock and start.\" No key, no code \u2014 just a lie on a trusting network. Related attacks abuse the CAN spec to throw a DoS and crash vehicle systems, or pull firmware straight off an ECU through a physical debug port (JTAG). And man-in-the-middle rigs on the sensor signals can commit ODOMETER FRAUD \u2014 altering the recorded mileage \u2014 which is why a scan-tool mileage check is part of a smart buyer's due diligence.\n\nWireless and charging. On the radio side, older remote keyless entry that never encrypted its signal is beaten by a simple REPLAY attack \u2014 capture the unlock code once, play it back later (Honda's CVE-2022-27254 is the textbook case). The relay attack is its cousin: two thieves relay a proximity fob's signal from inside the house out to the car so it thinks the key is at the door. Newer research pokes at EV charging \u2014 the SLAC routine (Signal Level Attenuation Characterization) that pairs a car to a charger can be abused to hop onto charging networks and intercept the session's keys \u2014 and at legacy automotive Bluetooth profiles.\n\nADAS and V2X. At the top of the stack, PHANTOM attacks fool driver-assistance systems by projecting fake road signs or a split-second image of a pedestrian, tricking the car into braking or throwing false alerts. And V2X \u2014 the systems letting cars talk to each other and to infrastructure \u2014 has its own research showing how the misbehavior-detection meant to catch liars can itself be gamed.\n\nNow the part that matters to you. Most of this is engineering and IT work \u2014 the ISO/SAE 21434 and CACSE world, not a locksmith's. But three pieces land squarely in your customer conversations, and knowing them is how you sound like a pro instead of a key-cutter. First, keyless RELAY and REPLAY theft is the number-one thing owners of push-to-start cars ask about \u2014 and you have real answers: a Faraday pouch or box for the fob at home, enabling the car's motion-sensor fob sleep or a PIN-to-drive if it has one, and parking defensively. Second, CAN-injection theft is why aftermarket steering locks, OBD-port locks, and ghost immobilizers sell \u2014 legitimate hardware you can advise on and install. Third, when a customer or a job posting says \"automotive security,\" you can now separate the two worlds cleanly: the physical key-and-immobilizer trade you work in (credentialed by the NASTF VSP), and the vehicle-cybersecurity engineering discipline (ISO 21434 / CACSE). Know the landscape, own your half of it.\n\nAuthorized framing: this is threat awareness for defending customers and advising on countermeasures \u2014 the same reason you study covert entry. Actual vehicle-network research belongs on a bench you own or a Car Hacking Village CTF, never on a car that isn't yours.",relatedGuides:["auto-smart-fobs","auto-nastf-vsp","auto-transponders-immo"],sources:["https://www.carhackingvillage.com/","https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2022-27254","https://kentindell.github.io/2023/04/03/can-injection/"],tags:["Relay","Fob","Push-to-start","Immobilizer"],aliases:["dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies"]},{id:"law-licensing",title:"CSLB licensing law: classifications, bonds & discipline",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",body:"THE LICENSE ITSELF. In California a contractor's license is issued by the Contractors State License Board (CSLB) under the Business and Professions Code (BPC), Division 3, Chapter 9 (the Contractors State License Law, BPC 7000 et seq.). The license is held by the business entity \u2014 sole owner, partnership, corporation, or LLC \u2014 and every license must have a qualifying individual who proves the trade experience. No entity may contract, bid, submit a proposal, or even advertise as a contractor without a current, active license. The Law & Business exam tests this framework hard: know that the license is a privilege regulated by the state, not a right, and that CSLB both licenses AND disciplines.\n\n[[diagram:license-scope-and-qualifier|The entity holds the licence, a person supplies the experience, and the $1,000 threshold needs all three conditions]]\n\nTHE $1,000 WORK THRESHOLD. This is the single most likely updated fact on your exam. As of January 1, 2025, AB 2622 (Carrillo, Chapter 240, Statutes of 2024) raised the license-required threshold from $500 to $1,000. A person may perform work WITHOUT a license only if the total contract price \u2014 labor, materials, and all other costs combined \u2014 is under $1,000, AND the job requires no building permit of any kind, AND the person hires no employees or workers to help. All three conditions must hold. The $1,000 is an aggregate: you cannot split one $2,000 job into two $1,000 bids to dodge the requirement (that is the \"splitting\" trap tested repeatedly). An unlicensed person doing this \"handyperson\" work who advertises MUST state in the ad that they are not licensed. Cross it and CSLB can charge a misdemeanor. Remember the old number was $500 \u2014 if a question uses $500 it is testing whether you know the current $1,000 figure.\n\nCLASSIFICATIONS RELEVANT TO A LOCKSMITH. CSLB issues three license types: A \u2013 General Engineering (fixed works: roads, bridges, utilities); B \u2013 General Building (structures needing at least two unrelated trades); and the C specialties. For lock and security work:\n- C-28 Lock and Security Equipment \u2014 the full classification. Evaluates, installs, maintains and repairs doors and door assemblies, gates, locks and locking devices, panic/fire-rated exit devices, gate and door closers and releases, jail and prison locking devices, master key systems, metal window guards, security doors, card-activated and electronic access-control systems, motion and other detectors, and permanently installed (built-in) safes and vaults. Note the tested exclusion: fire alarm systems are specifically NOT included in C-28.\n- C-7 Low Voltage Systems \u2014 communication, data, sound, and low-voltage signal systems; relevant when access control ties into low-voltage wiring, but it is a distinct trade from C-28.\n- C-61 Limited Specialty / D-16 Hardware, Locks and Safes \u2014 C-61 is the catch-all specialty; D-16 is its subcategory covering a locksmith who \"installs, modifies or repairs power and/or manually activated door and window locks with related hardware, built-in safes and vaults.\" D-16 is NARROWER than C-28 \u2014 it does not reach the full electronic access-control and security-system scope C-28 covers. Pick C-28 for a full security-equipment business; D-16 for a hardware-and-locks-focused shop.\n(Separate from CSLB, California locksmiths who service others' locks must also hold a Bureau of Security and Investigative Services (BSIS) locksmith license \u2014 a different agency, not part of the CSLB exam, but do not confuse the two.)\n\nWHO QUALIFIES \u2014 RMO / RME. Every license needs a qualifying individual (QI) who supplies the experience. A QI is one of: the sole owner; a Responsible Managing Officer (RMO) \u2014 an officer of a corporation or a member/manager of an LLC; or a Responsible Managing Employee (RME) \u2014 a bona fide W-2 employee. The experience requirement: at least four (4) years of journey-level, foreman, supervising, or contractor-level experience in the classification, gained within the ten (10) years immediately before filing (years need not be consecutive). Journey-level means fully qualified/skilled, not an apprentice. You may receive credit for up to three (3) of the four years from approved technical training, apprenticeship, or education, but at least one year must be practical experience. An RME must be actively, directly supervising and permanently employed \u2014 CSLB's standard is at least 32 hours per week OR 80% of the total hours the business operates, whichever is less. An RMO must exercise direct supervision and control. The QI cannot be a figurehead; renting out a qualifier is a serious violation.\n\nTHE BONDS. Effective January 1, 2023, SB 607 raised the bond amounts, and these are heavily tested current figures:\n- Contractor's Bond (license bond): $25,000 (raised from $15,000). Required of every active license.\n- Bond of Qualifying Individual: $25,000 (raised from $12,500). Required when the QI is an RME, or an RMO who owns less than 10% of the voting stock/equity. Exemption: an RMO who owns 10% or more is NOT required to file this bond.\n- Disciplinary Bond: $25,000 when CSLB requires one after discipline.\n- LLC Employee/Worker Bond: $100,000 \u2014 additional and unique to LLC licensees.\nBonds must be maintained continuously; a lapse suspends the license.\n\nDISCIPLINARY ACTIONS AND GROUNDS. CSLB can investigate, cite, suspend, or revoke. Statutory grounds tested most often: abandonment of a job without legal excuse (BPC 7107); diversion of funds or failure to pay for materials/labor (7108); willful departure from or disregard of plans and specifications (7109); fraud, misrepresentation, or false statements; and contracting without a license or beyond your classification. Enforcement escalates: a Letter of Admonishment or Notice to Correct for minor matters; a Citation with an order to correct plus a civil penalty; and an Accusation leading to suspension or revocation before an administrative law judge. Get the citation numbers right \u2014 they are commonly tested and commonly misremembered. Under BPC 7099.2, the MAXIMUM administrative civil penalty against a LICENSED contractor is $8,000 for most violations, rising to $30,000 for the most serious ones (violations tied to unlicensed practice and to workers' compensation coverage). Separately, effective July 1, 2026, SB 779 (Archuleta) set MINIMUM civil penalties: the minimum for acting as a contractor without a valid license jumped from $200 to $1,500, with minimums of $500 or $1,500 for other specified violations, and CSLB may adjust these for inflation every five years. Do not confuse the $8,000/$30,000 maximums with the new $1,500 unlicensed minimum. A licensee may appeal a citation, and citations must issue within the statutory limitations period.\n\nADVERTISING RULES. Under BPC 7030.5, a licensee must place the license number in ALL advertising \u2014 cards, trucks, websites, print, flyers, contracts, and proposals. Home improvement solicitations carry additional required disclosures (BPC 7030). An unlicensed person doing lawful sub-$1,000 work must state in the ad that they are not a licensed contractor. Advertising for work requiring a license without holding one is itself a violation.\n\nLICENSE MAINTENANCE AND RENEWAL. Licenses renew every two (2) years on active or inactive status. Keep the bond current and, if you have employees, workers' compensation coverage current the entire time \u2014 a lapse in either automatically suspends the license. Miss the renewal date and the license goes delinquent; you may still renew (with a delinquent fee) within a limited window before it expires and forces a re-application. Report any change in personnel (loss of the QI, change of officers) to CSLB promptly \u2014 losing the qualifier can suspend the license if not replaced in time.",sources:["https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/IndustryBulletins/2024/AB%202622%20Implementation.FINAL.pdf","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/PressReleases/2024/AB2622.FINAL.pdf","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/contractors/maintain_license/bond_information/bond_requirements.aspx","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C28","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/StudyGuides/C28StudyGuide.pdf","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/library/licensing_classifications/c-61_limited_speciality/default.aspx","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Contractors/Applicants/Contractors_License/Exam_Application/Experience_For_Exam.aspx","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Contractors/Maintain_License/Change_In_Personnel.aspx","https://www.cal-smacna.org/clsb-highlights-new-contracting-laws-going-into-effect-on-january-1-2022/","CSLB industry bulletin, 2025 new laws summary (cslb.ca.gov) \u2014 original link retired."],relatedGuides:["law-contracts","law-mechanics-liens","law-employment-payroll","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["CSLB","Workers' compensation","Business entity","Contractor's bond"],aliases:["contractors license","California license","workers comp","comp insurance","employee injury","sole proprietor","S corp","form a business","surety bond","$25000 bond"]},{id:"law-contracts",title:"Contracts, Home-Improvement Rules & Change Orders",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",body:"CALIFORNIA CONTRACT LAW FOR CONTRACTORS \u2014 the single most heavily tested topic on the Law & Business exam. Master the dollar thresholds and the mandatory contract elements. The Board writes questions to catch you on the exact numbers, so memorize them cold.\n\n[[diagram:down-payment-cap|Two worked examples of \"whichever is less\", and the other numbers on the same page]]\n\nWHEN A WRITTEN CONTRACT IS REQUIRED. Any home improvement contract (and any change order) where the combined labor-and-materials price exceeds $500 MUST be in writing and signed by both parties before work begins (BPC 7159). Under $500, no formal written HIC is mandated \u2014 but on the exam, if the number is over $500, the written-contract answer is correct. A \"home improvement contract\" means work on existing residential property \u2014 a dwelling of four units or fewer, or the buyer's residence. New-home construction is governed by BPC 7164, which carries the same down-payment cap and lien-warning requirements. Commercial contracts are NOT covered by Article 10 at all \u2014 no down-payment cap, no 3-day cancel; they run on ordinary negotiated contract law. That residential-vs-commercial line is a favorite trap: the consumer protections attach to homeowners, not to business owners contracting for commercial property.\n\nREQUIRED ELEMENTS OF A HOME IMPROVEMENT CONTRACT (BPC 7159). The contract must contain, at minimum: the title \"Home Improvement\" in at least 10-point boldface; the contractor's name, business address, and license number; the date the buyer signed; the approximate start date and approximate completion date; a description of the project and the significant materials to be used; the total contract price in dollars and cents; the down-payment amount; and a schedule of progress payments stated in dollars and cents tied to specific phases of work. It must also carry the down-payment cap statement (12-point boldface), the 3-day right-to-cancel notice (text in at least 12-point boldface) with a detachable Notice of Cancellation form, the Mechanics Lien Warning, and the required insurance and CSLB consumer-information notices. Missing elements make the contract unenforceable by the contractor and are grounds for discipline.\n\nTHE DOWN-PAYMENT CAP \u2014 MEMORIZE THIS. The down payment may not exceed $1,000 OR 10% of the contract price, WHICHEVER IS LESS. The contract must state this in at least 12-point boldface: \"THE DOWN PAYMENT MAY NOT EXCEED $1,000 OR 10 PERCENT OF THE CONTRACT PRICE, WHICHEVER IS LESS.\" Work the examples the way the exam does. A $25,000 kitchen: 10% is $2,500, but $1,000 is less \u2014 cap is $1,000. A $6,000 job: 10% is $600, which is less than $1,000 \u2014 cap is $600. The word \"less\" is the whole question. On large jobs the cap is almost always $1,000; on small jobs it is the 10% figure. Note: this cap is for home improvement and new-home construction only \u2014 it does NOT apply to commercial work.\n\nPROGRESS PAYMENTS. Except for the down payment, a contractor may neither request nor accept a payment that exceeds the value of work performed or materials delivered (BPC 7159.5). You cannot front-load the schedule or collect ahead of the work. The payment schedule must reference specific phases in dollars and cents. Taking money ahead of value delivered is a disciplinable act and, like an excessive down payment, a misdemeanor \u2014 punishable by a fine of not less than $100 and not more than $5,000, or imprisonment in county jail not exceeding one year, or both, plus license discipline.\n\nTHE 3-DAY RIGHT TO CANCEL. For contracts solicited or signed somewhere other than the contractor's fixed place of business \u2014 typically the buyer's home \u2014 the buyer may cancel until midnight of the third BUSINESS day after signing (Civil Code 1689.5\u20131689.7). Count business days, not calendar days. The notice must appear in the contract and the contractor must give the buyer TWO copies of a detachable Notice of Cancellation form. Until you deliver that form properly, the cancellation clock never starts \u2014 the buyer can cancel indefinitely. If cancelled, the contractor returns all payments within 10 days.\n\nTHE 5-DAY AND 7-DAY EXTENSIONS. Seniors 65 and older get FIVE business days to cancel (AB 2471, effective January 1, 2021). Contracts to repair or restore a residence damaged in a disaster get SEVEN business days (BPC 7151.2 / Civil Code 1689.6). The exam wants these kept separate: 3 days standard, 5 days senior, 7 days disaster. If a question mentions a wildfire or flood restoration job, the answer is 7. If it mentions a buyer who is 65 or older on an ordinary home-solicitation job, the answer is 5.\n\nCHANGE ORDERS. Extra work and change orders must be in writing and signed by both parties BEFORE the work in the change order begins. The change order must describe the scope of the extra or changed work, the added or subtracted cost, and the effect on the schedule of progress payments. A verbal \"go ahead and add it\" is unenforceable and a compliance violation. Once signed, the change order becomes part of the contract. Remember the same $500 threshold logic and the same writing requirement apply.\n\nTHE MECHANICS LIEN WARNING. Every home improvement and new-construction contract must include the statutory Mechanics Lien Warning telling the owner that subcontractors and material suppliers who are not paid can record a lien against the home even if the owner already paid the prime contractor \u2014 and that the owner can protect themselves by using joint checks, obtaining lien waivers, and requiring a Preliminary Notice. This warning is the required lien disclosure and is mandatory contract content; omitting it is a violation. Do not confuse the contract's Mechanics Lien Warning with the separate Preliminary Notice that subs and suppliers must serve within 20 days of first furnishing labor or materials to preserve their own lien rights \u2014 the exam tests both, and they are different documents serving different parties. (The old separate \"Notice to Owner\" from earlier law is now folded into the Mechanics Lien Warning; if an exam item still uses that label, it means this warning \u2014 not the sub's Preliminary Notice.)\n\nEXAM QUICK-HITS. Written HIC required over $500. Down payment: lesser of $1,000 or 10%. Progress payments never exceed value delivered. Cancel: 3 business days (5 senior, 7 disaster). Change orders: written and signed before work. Contract must carry the down-payment statement (12-pt bold), cancel notice, and Mechanics Lien Warning. Preliminary Notice (subs/suppliers) is a separate 20-day document, not the contract's lien warning. Residential protections do not extend to commercial jobs. Nail those and you own this module.",sources:["https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/business-and-professions-code/bpc-sect-7159/","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=7159.","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/business-and-professions-code/bpc-sect-7159-5/","https://law.justia.com/codes/california/code-bpc/division-3/chapter-9/article-10/section-7159-5/","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/IndustryBulletins/2022/Industry_Bulletin_Progress_Payment_Restrictions.pdf","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=201920200AB2471","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-1689-7/","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-1689-6/","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/business-and-professions-code/bpc-sect-7164/","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/Resources/GuidesAndPublications/HomeownersGuideToPreventingMechanicsLiens.pdf"],relatedGuides:["law-licensing","law-mechanics-liens","law-employment-payroll","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["Mechanics lien","Home Improvement Contract","Preliminary notice","Schedule","CSLB"],aliases:["lien claim","get paid","unpaid invoice","residential contract","3 day right to cancel","prelim","20-day notice","lien notice","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","contractors license","C-28","state license board","California license"]},{body:"The mechanics lien is your ultimate collection tool in California: it puts a cloud on the owner's title so the property itself secures your money. But the lien is the END of a chain, and every link has a hard deadline. Miss one and you are just another unsecured creditor. The exam tests the chain and the numbers. Memorize them cold. Authority is the California Civil Code, sections 8000-8848 (the \"works of improvement\" statutes that replaced the old lien law effective July 1, 2012).\n\n[[diagram:lien-deadline-chain|20 days, then 90 or 60/30, then 90 more... and what a late preliminary notice actually costs you]]\n\nTHE 20-DAY PRELIMINARY NOTICE (California Preliminary Notice). This is link one, and it is where most people lose their rights before they ever start. Under Civil Code 8200, anyone who wants to preserve a mechanics lien, a stop payment notice, OR a payment bond claim must serve a preliminary notice \u2014 UNLESS they contracted directly with the owner. So the rule the exam wants: subcontractors, sub-subs, material suppliers, and equipment lessors MUST serve it. The direct (prime) contractor does NOT have to serve the owner, but the direct contractor STILL must serve the construction lender if there is one. Wage laborers (employees earning wages) are exempt entirely.\n\nServe it on three parties: the owner (or reputed owner), the direct contractor, and the construction lender (if any). Deadline: no later than 20 days after you FIRST furnish labor or materials to the job. That 20 days runs from your first delivery, not from signing and not from completion.\n\nThe late-notice trap the exam loves: serving late does not kill your lien entirely \u2014 it limits it. A late preliminary notice only preserves lien rights for work furnished in the 20 days BEFORE you served it, plus everything after. So serve on day 40 and you lose the first roughly 20 days of work. Serve on time and the lien reaches back to day one. Service is by certified/registered mail or overnight delivery (or personal service); it is effective when DEPOSITED in the mail, not when received.\n\nTHE MECHANICS LIEN \u2014 RECORDING DEADLINE. Link two. You record a verified mechanics lien claim in the county recorder's office where the property sits. The deadline depends on who you are and whether a Notice of Completion or Notice of Cessation was recorded:\n\n- No notice recorded: everyone gets 90 days after actual COMPLETION of the whole work of improvement.\n- Notice of Completion or Cessation recorded: the DIRECT contractor gets 60 days after that recording; EVERYONE ELSE (subs, suppliers) gets only 30 days.\n\nThat 90 / 60 / 30 split is a favorite exam question. Notice the completion notice SHORTENS your window and shortens it MORE for subs than for the prime. The owner has 15 days after actual completion to record a valid Notice of Completion (Civil Code 8182); a Notice of Cessation can be recorded after labor has ceased continuously for at least 30 days. You must serve a copy of the recorded lien on the owner \u2014 failure to serve makes the lien unenforceable.\n\nTHE FORECLOSURE LAWSUIT. Link three, and the one people forget because they think recording is the finish line. It is not. A recorded lien is only good for 90 days. Under Civil Code 8460 you must file suit to FORECLOSE the lien within 90 days after RECORDING it. Miss that and the lien expires and is unenforceable \u2014 you cannot even record a new one because the original recording deadline is long gone. (A recorded agreement to extend credit can push the suit deadline to 90 days after the credit period expires \u2014 but in no case later than one year after completion of the work.) Best practice and exam-safe answer: sue within 90 days of recording.\n\nQuick chain to memorize: 20 days to serve preliminary notice \u2192 90 days (or 60/30 after a completion notice) to record the lien \u2192 90 days after recording to file the foreclosure suit.\n\nSTOP PAYMENT NOTICE. A parallel remedy, tested alongside the lien. Instead of reaching the property, a stop payment notice (Civil Code 8500 et seq.) reaches the MONEY \u2014 it freezes undisbursed construction funds held by the owner or, better, the construction lender. Same preliminary-notice prerequisite applies. On private work, serving a \"bonded\" stop payment notice (backed by a bond equal to 125 percent of the claim) on the construction LENDER actually forces the lender to withhold funds; an unbonded notice to the lender is optional for the lender to honor (a private OWNER, by contrast, must withhold on either a bonded or unbonded notice). The service deadline mirrors the lien: give it within the same period you would have to record a lien (90 days from completion, or 30/60 after a completion notice). Then enforce by lawsuit filed no sooner than 10 days after service and within 90 days after that period ends. The advantage: it works even when there is no equity in the property, because it grabs cash the lender still holds.\n\nRELEASE / WAIVER FORMS. Getting paid means signing waivers, and California voids any waiver that does not \"substantially\" follow the statute. There are exactly FOUR statutory forms (Civil Code 8132-8138), and the exam wants you to pick the right one on two axes \u2014 timing (progress vs. final) and condition (conditional vs. unconditional):\n\n- 8132 Conditional Waiver and Release on PROGRESS payment\n- 8134 Unconditional Waiver and Release on PROGRESS payment\n- 8136 Conditional Waiver and Release on FINAL payment\n- 8138 Unconditional Waiver and Release on FINAL payment\n\nCONDITIONAL means it only takes effect once you are actually paid (the check clears / payment is verified) \u2014 sign this BEFORE you have the money. UNCONDITIONAL takes effect the instant you sign, whether or not you get paid \u2014 only sign after cash is in hand, because it wipes out your rights immediately. PROGRESS covers work through a specific date and preserves retention and later work; FINAL waives everything. The trap: never sign an UNCONDITIONAL waiver in exchange for a promise. A waiver that does not follow these forms is null, void, and unenforceable, and notarization is neither required nor helpful.\n\nEXAM TAKEAWAYS. The numbers: 20 (preliminary notice), 15 (owner's window to record a completion notice), 90 (lien from completion / foreclosure suit after recording), 60 (direct contractor after a completion notice), 30 (subs/suppliers after a completion notice), 30 (continuous stoppage before a cessation notice), 125% / 1.25x (bonded stop notice and lien release bond). Know WHO must serve a preliminary notice (everyone but the prime-to-owner and wage laborers \u2014 and the prime still notices the lender), and know the four waiver forms by their two axes.",id:"law-mechanics-liens",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",sources:["https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=8460.&lawCode=CIV","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-8200/","https://law.justia.com/codes/california/2010/civ/8200-8216.html","https://law.justia.com/codes/california/2010/civ/8180-8190.html","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=4.&title=2.&part=6.&chapter=5.&article=3.","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/consumers/legal_issues_for_consumers/mechanics_lien/conditional_and_unconditional_waiver_release_form.aspx","https://codes.findlaw.com/ca/civil-code/civ-sect-8138/","https://schorr-law.com/mechanics-liens-90-days-to-foreclose-on-a-mechanics-lien/","https://mechanicslien.com/mechanics-lien-california-its-all-about-timing/"],title:"Mechanics liens, preliminary notice & getting paid",relatedGuides:["law-licensing","law-contracts","law-employment-payroll","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["Preliminary notice","Mechanics lien"],aliases:["prelim","20-day notice","lien notice","unpaid invoice"]},{id:"law-employment-payroll",title:"Employees, payroll taxes & workers' comp",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",body:"The day you put one tech on payroll, you become an employer, and California treats that as a serious legal status with hard deadlines. Miss them and you are not looking at a warning; you are looking at fines, a stop order, and a hit on your contractor license. Know these cold.\n\n[[diagram:abc-test-and-comp|What one employee triggers, and why prong B decides it for a locksmith]]\n\nWORKERS' COMPENSATION \u2014 MANDATORY WITH ONE EMPLOYEE. Labor Code 3700 requires every employer with even ONE employee (part-time counts) to carry workers' comp. There is no small-shop exemption. A locksmith with a single tech must have a policy in force before that tech turns a key. A true sole owner with no employees is exempt, and corporate officers or LLC managing members can waive out by filing an exclusion, but the moment you hire, coverage is not optional. Going without is one of the most serious violations in the code:\n- Criminal: misdemeanor, fine of NOT LESS THAN $10,000, or up to one year in county jail, or both (LC 3700.5). Note the fine is a floor of $10,000, not a cap \u2014 a common exam trap.\n- Civil/WCAB: if an uninsured worker is hurt, the WCAB assesses $10,000 per employee on payroll at the time of injury if the claim is compensable, or $2,000 per employee if non-compensable, capped at $100,000 \u2014 on top of paying the full claim yourself.\n- Stop Order: the DLSE can issue a stop order halting ALL use of employee labor until you get coverage. Violating it is a misdemeanor (up to 60 days county jail and/or a $10,000 fine).\n- License: CSLB will SUSPEND your contractor license for operating without required workers' comp. Every licensee must file a Certificate of Workers' Comp (or an exemption) with CSLB.\n\nEMPLOYEE vs INDEPENDENT CONTRACTOR \u2014 THE ABC TEST (AB5). You do not get to call a worker \"1099\" to dodge these obligations. Since AB5 (2019), codified at Labor Code 2775 and rooted in the Dynamex (2018) decision, every worker is PRESUMED an employee. To classify someone as an independent contractor, YOU (the hiring party) must prove ALL THREE prongs:\n- A \u2014 the worker is free from your control and direction, in contract and in fact;\n- B \u2014 the work is OUTSIDE the usual course of your business;\n- C \u2014 the worker is customarily engaged in an independently established trade of the same nature.\nFail even one prong and the worker is an employee. Prong B is what traps locksmiths: if your business is locksmithing and the person is doing locksmith work, that work is inside your usual course of business \u2014 they are an employee, full stop, no matter what the contract says. Misclassification is expensive: Labor Code 226.8 penalizes willful misclassification $5,000 to $15,000 per violation, rising to $10,000 to $25,000 per violation for a pattern or practice, plus back taxes, unpaid workers' comp, and wage claims.\n\nPAYROLL WITHHOLDING (2026 numbers \u2014 these are tested). For a W-2 employee you withhold and remit both federal and California amounts.\nFederal (FICA and unemployment):\n- Social Security: 6.2% employee + 6.2% employer, on wages up to the $184,500 wage base (2026, up from $176,100 in 2025).\n- Medicare: 1.45% employee + 1.45% employer, NO wage cap. Additional 0.9% Medicare on employee wages over $200,000 (employee only).\n- FICA combined employee share = 7.65%.\n- FUTA (federal unemployment): 6.0% on the first $7,000 of each employee's wages, normally reduced to 0.6% with the state credit. California is a credit-reduction state because it still owes its federal UI loan, so the effective FUTA rate is higher \u2014 1.8% for 2025 (a 1.2% credit reduction, about $126 per employee), and it climbs another 0.3% each year the loan is unpaid.\nCalifornia (paid to EDD):\n- State income tax (PIT) withholding, based on the employee's DE 4 / W-4.\n- SDI (State Disability Insurance, includes Paid Family Leave): 1.3% for 2026, up from 1.2%, with NO wage cap since 2024 (SB 951) \u2014 the 1.3% applies to ALL wages. Withheld from the EMPLOYEE.\n- UI (state unemployment / SUTA): employer-paid, on the first $7,000 wage base; NEW employers pay 3.4% for the first 2\u20133 years.\n- ETT (Employment Training Tax): employer-paid 0.1% on the first $7,000.\nEmployer taxes (UI, ETT) come out of your pocket; PIT and SDI are withheld from the worker. A W-2 goes to employees. A federal 1099-NEC goes to a properly classified independent contractor \u2014 but the threshold CHANGED: the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (signed July 2025) raised the 1099-NEC/1099-MISC filing floor from $600 to $2,000 for payments made in 2026, indexed to inflation from 2027. So for 2026 you issue a federal 1099-NEC only at $2,000 or more. Do NOT confuse that federal form with California's DE 542 state reporting, which still triggers at $600 (see below).\n\nEDD REPORTING AND NEW-HIRE REPORTING.\n- Register with EDD within 15 days of paying more than $100 in wages in a calendar quarter.\n- New-Hire Reporting (form DE 34): report every new or rehired employee within 20 DAYS of their start-of-work date. Rehire after a 60-day separation must be reported again. Penalty $24 per failure, $490 if a willful conspiracy not to report.\n- Independent Contractor Reporting (form DE 542): report within 20 days of paying OR contracting for $600 or more, whichever is earlier. This $600 state threshold is unchanged by the federal 1099 change. Same $24 / $490 penalties.\n- File quarterly DE 9 (contribution return) and DE 9C (wage report); deposit withholdings on the EDD schedule.\n\nPREVAILING WAGE / CERTIFIED PAYROLL (PUBLIC WORKS). On any public works project over $1,000 (Labor Code 1771), you must pay the DIR-determined prevailing wage for the craft and locality \u2014 you cannot split a job to duck the $1,000 threshold. You must register as a public works contractor with the DIR (DAS), and maintain and submit CERTIFIED PAYROLL records (Labor Code 1776) at least monthly through the DIR's electronic eCPR system. Public works also carries apprenticeship obligations. This is separate from your ordinary payroll \u2014 prevailing wage is usually far above market.\n\nPOSTING REQUIREMENTS. California requires physical postings where employees can see them: the IWC Wage Order for your industry, current minimum wage, the payday notice, the workers' comp coverage notice naming your carrier and telling injured workers their rights, the Cal-OSHA safety poster (\"Safety and Health Protection on the Job\"), EDD notices (UI/SDI/PFL \u2014 DE 1857A / DE 2515), harassment/discrimination and family-leave notices, and the emergency/injury procedures. On public works, also post the applicable prevailing wage rates at the jobsite.",sources:["https://www.dir.ca.gov/dwc/faqs.html","https://law.justia.com/codes/california/code-lab/division-4/part-1/chapter-4/article-1/section-3700-5/","https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/faq_independentcontractor.htm","https://www.ftb.ca.gov/file/business/industries/worker-classification-and-ab-5-faq.html","https://edd.ca.gov/en/payroll_taxes/rates_and_withholding/","https://edd.ca.gov/siteassets/files/pdf_pub_ctr/de44.pdf","https://www.ssa.gov/oact/cola/cbb.html","https://hrwatchdog.calchamber.com/2025/12/2026-social-security-taxable-wage-base-california-sdi-withholding-rate-increase/","https://edd.ca.gov/en/payroll_taxes/federal-unemployment-tax-act/","https://payroll.org/news-resources/news/news-detail/2025/11/11/california-virgin-islands-face-futa-credit-reduction-for-2025","https://edd.ca.gov/en/payroll_taxes/new_hire_reporting/","https://edd.ca.gov/en/payroll_taxes/independent_contractor_reporting/","https://edd.ca.gov/siteassets/files/pdf_pub_ctr/de542.pdf","https://www.littler.com/news-analysis/asap/tax-bill-changes-1099-reporting-thresholds","https://www.dir.ca.gov/public-works/prevailing-wage.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/Public-Works/FAQ-certified-payroll-reporting.html","http://www.dir.ca.gov/public-works/PublicworksSB854FAQ.html"],relatedGuides:["law-licensing","law-contracts","law-mechanics-liens","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["CSLB","Cal-OSHA","Workers' compensation","Schedule"],aliases:["contractors license","C-28","state license board","California license","workplace safety","safety compliance","workers comp","comp insurance","employee injury","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal"]},{id:"law-safety-osha",title:"Cal-OSHA & Jobsite Safety",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",body:"CAL-OSHA & JOBSITE SAFETY\n\n[[diagram:iipp-eight-elements|The eight elements of the standard they cite most, and where the written copy has to live]]\n\nCalifornia runs its own OSHA. The Division of Occupational Safety and Health (DOSH), branded \"Cal/OSHA,\" sits inside the Department of Industrial Relations (DIR). It's a state-plan program, which means Cal/OSHA is at least as strict as federal OSHA and usually stricter \u2014 the silica rule, the heat-illness rule, and the mandatory IIPP are all California peculiarities. The rules live in Title 8 of the California Code of Regulations (T8 CCR). For the exam: Cal/OSHA enforces, the Occupational Safety and Health Standards Board writes the standards, and the Appeals Board hears citation appeals. As a licensed contractor with employees, you ARE an employer under this scheme.\n\nTHE IIPP \u2014 THE ONE THEY LOVE TO TEST\n\nEvery California employer, regardless of size or industry \u2014 even one employee \u2014 must establish, implement, and maintain a WRITTEN Injury and Illness Prevention Program (IIPP). Authority: T8 CCR \xa73203. This is the single most-cited Cal/OSHA standard and a near-guaranteed exam question. \"Written\" is the tested word \u2014 a verbal safety policy does not satisfy \xa73203.\n\nMemorize the eight required elements:\n1. Responsibility \u2014 name the person(s) with authority for the program.\n2. Compliance \u2014 how you ensure employees follow safe practices.\n3. Communication \u2014 a two-way system so workers can report hazards without fear of reprisal.\n4. Hazard assessment \u2014 scheduled inspections to identify hazards.\n5. Accident/exposure investigation \u2014 a procedure to investigate injuries and illnesses.\n6. Hazard correction \u2014 fix unsafe conditions in a timely manner, worst hazards first.\n7. Training \u2014 new hires, new assignments, new equipment/substances, newly discovered hazards, and supervisors.\n8. Recordkeeping \u2014 document inspections and training.\n\nA copy must be kept at each worksite, or at a central location for employers with non-fixed worksites (that's you \u2014 keep it in the truck/office and make it available). Records of inspections and training generally kept at least one year.\n\nHAZCOM & SAFETY DATA SHEETS\n\nThe Hazard Communication Standard (T8 CCR \xa75194) \u2014 \"right to know.\" If employees are exposed to hazardous chemicals (adhesives, solvents, lubricants, aerosols), the employer must:\n- Maintain a written HazCom program.\n- Keep a Safety Data Sheet (SDS) for every hazardous chemical, readily accessible to employees on every shift.\n- Label containers.\n- Train employees on the hazards.\n\nTerminology: it's SDS now, not the old \"MSDS\" \u2014 GHS standardized them into 16 sections. Exam may still say \"MSDS\"; know they mean the same document. The SDS comes from the manufacturer/supplier; the employer's job is to keep it on hand and accessible.\n\nREPORTING A SERIOUS INJURY OR FATALITY \u2014 8 HOURS\n\nThis is the deadline they test. Under Labor Code \xa76409.1 and T8 CCR \xa7342, an employer must report IMMEDIATELY to the nearest Cal/OSHA district office any serious injury, illness, or death occurring in the workplace. \"Immediately\" is defined as as soon as practically possible but no longer than 8 HOURS after the employer knows or should have known. (Old rule was 24 hours; it was tightened.) Report by telephone or through Cal/OSHA's online reporting portal.\n\nA \"serious injury or illness\" now means any inpatient hospitalization (regardless of length, and NOT merely for observation or diagnostic testing), an amputation, or loss of an eye. The old \"24-hour hospitalization\" and dollar thresholds are gone. Do NOT disturb the accident scene (beyond aiding the injured or making it safe) until Cal/OSHA authorizes it.\n\nRECORDKEEPING \u2014 THE LOG 300\n\nSeparate from the 8-hour report. Employers with MORE THAN 10 employees (and not in a designated low-hazard exempt industry) must keep injury/illness records:\n- Form 300 \u2014 the Log of Work-Related Injuries and Illnesses (running log).\n- Form 301 \u2014 the individual Incident Report.\n- Form 300A \u2014 the Annual Summary.\n\nPost the 300A summary from FEBRUARY 1 through APRIL 30 each year, in a visible spot, covering the prior year \u2014 even if there were zero injuries. Electronic submission of 300A data is due by MARCH 2. Record an injury if it involves medical treatment beyond first aid, days away from work, restricted duty/job transfer, loss of consciousness, or death. Retain records five years. Construction is NOT on the exempt low-hazard list \u2014 assume you must keep the Log 300 once you pass 10 employees.\n\nCOMMON HAZARDS THEY ASK ABOUT\n\nLadders (T8 \xa73276) \u2014 inspect before use; portable/extension ladder side rails must extend at least 36 inches (3 feet) above the upper landing, or be secured with a grab-rail; maintain three points of contact; don't stand on the top rungs.\nPPE \u2014 employer provides required personal protective equipment (hard hats, eye/face, hearing, gloves) at no cost to the employee, and trains on its use.\nFall protection \u2014 CAUTION, don't parrot the federal 6-foot number. In Cal/OSHA general industry, guarding of open sides/platforms is triggered at 30 inches above the level below (T8 \xa73210). In CONSTRUCTION, the general trigger for unprotected sides, edges, and perimeters is 7.5 feet (T8 \xa71670). The big change: effective July 1, 2025, Cal/OSHA LOWERED the fall-protection trigger for RESIDENTIAL construction (framing and roofing) from 15 feet down to 6 feet. Safety nets are required at 25 feet or more. So: general construction 7.5 ft, residential framing/roofing now 6 ft \u2014 federal OSHA's flat 6 ft is not the Cal/OSHA general-construction answer.\nLockout/Tagout (LOTO) \u2014 T8 \xa73314: de-energize and lock out energy sources before servicing machinery so it can't unexpectedly start. Each worker applies their own lock.\nRespirable crystalline silica \u2014 T8 \xa75204. Cutting/grinding concrete, brick, stone, tile releases silica dust (silicosis, a lung disease). PEL = 50 \xb5g/m\xb3 (8-hour TWA); Action Level = 25 \xb5g/m\xb3, at/above which you must assess exposure and provide medical surveillance. Controls: wet-cutting, vacuum dust collection (engineering controls first), then respirators. California added tightened emergency silica protections (2023\u20132024), aimed heavily at engineered-stone fabrication.\nHeat illness \u2014 T8 \xa73395: for outdoor work, provide potable water, shade at 80\xb0F, rest breaks, and acclimatization. A California-specific rule.\n\nCITATIONS & PENALTIES\n\nCal/OSHA classifies violations and the maximums are adjusted for inflation annually. These are the current (2025) figures, which carry into 2026 (no inflation bump this cycle) \u2014 know the categories and order of magnitude, not exact cents:\n- Regulatory / General (includes posting & recordkeeping) \u2014 up to $16,285.\n- Serious \u2014 up to $25,000.\n- Willful or Repeat \u2014 up to $162,851 (minimum for willful $11,632).\n\nA \"serious\" violation means a substantial probability of death or serious harm. \"Willful\" means intentional/knowing disregard. \"Repeat\" means a substantially similar violation within the look-back period. Failure to abate carries a per-day penalty. You have 15 working days from receipt of a citation to appeal to the Occupational Safety and Health Appeals Board. Exam takeaway: the written IIPP, the SDS on site, the 8-hour serious-injury report, and posting the 300A are the cheap, testable ways to stay off the citation list.\n\nSources listed below.",sources:["https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3203.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/342.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/Serious-injury-FAQ.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/5194.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/respiratory-silica-FAQ.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/calosha-updates/log300-reporting.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3276.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/etools/08-010/TE_FallProtection.htm","https://calchamberalert.com/2025/05/09/residential-construction-fall-protection-trigger-height-drops-to-6-feet/","https://www.dir.ca.gov/DIRNews/2025/2025-10.html","https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/336.html"],relatedGuides:["law-licensing","law-contracts","law-mechanics-liens","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["Cal-OSHA"],aliases:["workplace safety","safety compliance"]},{body:"RUNNING THE BUSINESS. The CSLB Law & Business exam devotes roughly 15% to business finance and organization. You are not asked to be a CPA \u2014 you are asked to know entity liability, how to price a job so you do not go broke, and which records and insurance the state makes you carry. Learn the numbers cold; they are tested as fill-in-the-blank math and definitions.\n\n[[diagram:bid-formula-and-entities|The four-term bid, and the California numbers asked as fill-in-the-blank]]\n\nENTITY TYPES \u2014 LIABILITY AND TAX. Four structures show up. A SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP is you: no legal separation between owner and business, so ALL business debts are personal debts \u2014 your house and bank accounts are exposed. Simplest and cheapest to form; income is reported on your personal return (pass-through, taxed once). A PARTNERSHIP is two or more owners; general partners share UNLIMITED personal liability, and each partner can bind the others. Also pass-through. A CORPORATION is a separate legal person: it gives owners LIMITED liability (shareholders risk only what they invested), but a C-corp has DOUBLE TAXATION \u2014 the corporation pays tax on profit, then shareholders pay again on dividends. An S-corp election keeps limited liability but makes income pass-through, avoiding the double hit. An LLC (limited liability company) is the popular hybrid: limited liability like a corporation, pass-through taxation like a partnership by default (single-member LLC is taxed as a sole prop, multi-member as a partnership), and it can elect corporate/S-corp treatment. Exam trap: in California every LLC and every corporation pays the $800 minimum annual franchise tax to the Franchise Tax Board regardless of profit. CSLB point: each entity type files its own license \u2014 a license belongs to the entity, not portable between a sole owner and their new corporation. Construction-specific numbers you must know: a licensed contractor LLC must carry a separate $100,000 LLC EMPLOYEE/WORKER BOND (BPC 7071.6.5) IN ADDITION to the standard $25,000 contractor bond \u2014 so an LLC contractor posts $125,000 total \u2014 and an LLC with five or fewer members/personnel must also carry at least $1 million in general liability.\n\nESTIMATING A JOB. The bid formula is the backbone: SELLING PRICE = LABOR + MATERIAL + OVERHEAD + PROFIT. Labor and material are DIRECT (job) costs \u2014 traceable to the specific contract, including the burden on labor (payroll taxes, workers comp, benefits). OVERHEAD is INDIRECT cost \u2014 rent, office salary, the truck, insurance, licensing \u2014 expenses you pay whether or not you win the bid; you recover them by spreading (allocating) them across all jobs, usually as a percentage of direct cost. PROFIT is what is left for the owner after all costs, and it is NOT overhead \u2014 a common exam trick is calling profit \"the cost of staying in business.\" Overhead is a cost; profit is the reward for risk.\n\nMARKUP VS MARGIN \u2014 the single most-missed calculation. MARKUP is figured on your COST; MARGIN is figured on the SELLING PRICE. They are not the same percentage. If a part costs $100 and you add 50% markup, price = $150, but your margin is only 50/150 = 33%. Formulas: Selling price = Cost x (1 + markup%). Margin% = (Price \u2212 Cost) / Price. To hit a target margin, DIVIDE, do not multiply: for a 33% margin on a $100 cost, price = 100 / (1 \u2212 0.33) = $150. Contractors who \"add\" their desired margin as a markup consistently underprice. Worked exam example: direct cost $37,000, overhead 12%, profit 5%. Because both are typically taken on the same base, price = 37,000 / (1 \u2212 0.12 \u2212 0.05) = 37,000 / 0.83 = about $44,578. If instead overhead and profit are simple markups on cost: 37,000 x 1.17 = $43,290. Read the question to see whether the percentage is on cost (markup) or on price (margin).\n\nOVERHEAD & BURDEN RATE. The BURDEN RATE (labor burden) is the true cost of an employee above the base wage \u2014 employer payroll taxes (Social Security, Medicare, FUTA/SUTA), workers comp premium, and benefits. A $30/hr worker can cost $40+/hr loaded. You must bid the burdened rate, not the wage, or every hour loses money. Overhead RATE = total annual overhead / total annual direct cost (or direct labor hours); apply it to each bid so every job carries its share of the office.\n\nCASH FLOW & FINANCIAL STATEMENTS. CASH FLOW is timing of money in vs out \u2014 a profitable contractor still fails if receivables lag payroll. WORKING CAPITAL = current assets \u2212 current liabilities; positive working capital means you can pay near-term bills. Two statements you must distinguish: the BALANCE SHEET is a snapshot at a point in time and follows ASSETS = LIABILITIES + OWNER'S EQUITY (net worth). ASSETS are what you own (cash, receivables, tools, trucks); LIABILITIES are what you owe (payables, loans); equity is the difference. The P&L / INCOME STATEMENT covers a PERIOD and shows Revenue \u2212 Expenses = Net Profit. Exam shorthand: balance sheet = what you're WORTH now; income statement = how you PERFORMED over time. Current vs long-term: current = within one year.\n\nINSURANCE & BONDS. GENERAL LIABILITY covers third-party bodily injury and property damage you cause. COMMERCIAL AUTO covers company vehicles. WORKERS COMPENSATION covers YOUR employees' job injuries and is mandatory in California the moment you have even ONE employee, part-time included; you file a Certificate of Workers' Comp or a self-insurance certification, or a signed exemption if you truly have no employees. Exception to memorize: C-8 (concrete), C-20 (HVAC), C-22 (asbestos), C-39 (roofing), and C-61/D-49 (tree service) must carry workers comp EVEN WITH NO EMPLOYEES. Current-law note: SB 216's mandate extending workers comp to essentially ALL licensed contractors (with or without employees) was delayed by SB 1455 (2024) from January 1, 2026 to January 1, 2028, with CSLB standing up an exemption-verification process (sworn statements, audits) by January 1, 2027 \u2014 so as of 2026 the no-employee exemption still exists outside those five trades. BONDS are not insurance: the required CONTRACTOR'S LICENSE BOND is $25,000 (raised from $15,000 by SB 607, effective 1/1/2023); the BOND OF QUALIFYING INDIVIDUAL is $25,000, and the DISCIPLINARY BOND is set by the Registrar at not less than $25,000 (up to ten times the contractor bond). A bond protects the CONSUMER and unpaid workers, and the surety can seek repayment FROM YOU \u2014 that is the key difference from insurance, which protects you.\n\nRECORD RETENTION. Payroll records: California Labor Code 1174 requires at least 3 years; accountants advise keeping tax and payroll records 4 years to cover the wage-claim/PAGA statute of limitations, and up to 7 years for IRS/audit defense. Personnel files: 4 years after creation or the employment action (SB 807). Keep contracts, change orders, and job records as well \u2014 CSLB and the statute of limitations for construction defect (up to 10 years for latent defects) make longer retention prudent.",id:"law-business-finance",module:"Law & Business (Contractor Exam)",title:"Business entities, estimating & finance",sources:["https://www.cslb.ca.gov/contractors/maintain_license/bond_information/bond_requirements.aspx","https://cslb.ca.gov/Resources/StudyGuides/LawStudyGuide.pdf","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/contractors/maintain_license/workers_compensation.aspx","https://www.cslb.ca.gov/about_us/llc.aspx","https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220SB216","https://gaslampinsurance.com/2026-california-workers-comp-changes/","https://www.shouselaw.com/ca/labor/labor-code-1174/","https://www.ftb.ca.gov/forms/misc/1123.html","https://www.sos.ca.gov/business-programs/business-entities/starting-business/types","https://properdefenselaw.com/sb-807-new-california-law-expands-records-retention-requirements-for-employers/"],relatedGuides:["law-licensing","law-contracts","law-mechanics-liens","ca-licensing-bsis-cslb","ca-scope-c28-c7","business-of-locksmithing"],tags:["CSLB"],aliases:["contractors license","C-28","state license board","California license"]},{id:"padlock-shimming-bypass",title:"Padlock attacks: shimming, shackle bypass & which locks resist",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Everybody has seen the video: someone wraps a strip cut from a soda can into a little U, slips it down the shackle of a padlock, twists, and it pops open in two seconds. That is shackle shimming, and understanding exactly why it works \u2014 and which padlocks laugh at it \u2014 is the difference between a locksmith and a guy who watched a video.\n\n[[img:photo-padlock-american|American commercial padlocks... the shackle, the heel and toe, and what a shim is actually reaching for.]]\n\nHere is the mechanism. A cheap padlock is single-locking: the shackle has a notch cut in one leg, and a spring-loaded pawl (a latch or a single ball bearing) drops into that notch to hold the shackle down. That is the ONLY thing holding it. A shim is a thin piece of springy metal \u2014 shim stock, a feeler gauge, or the classic beer/soda-can strip \u2014 bent into a U that wraps the shackle. You work it down into the hairline gap between the shackle and the lock body, right at the pawl, and it wedges the pawl back out of the notch. Shackle springs up, lock open. No picking, no cutting, no marks. On a single-locking padlock it is genuinely a few-second job, and that is why the cheap brass Master locks on a school locker are a joke.\n\nNow the part TOOOL and every serious locksmith harps on: which padlocks CANNOT be shimmed. The answer is double-locking (dual ball-bearing) shackles. A double-locking lock traps the shackle on BOTH legs \u2014 two ball bearings, one in each side \u2014 so a single shim only frees one side and the shackle still will not lift. To shim it you would need two shims working both sides at once, which in a tight, hardened lock is effectively impractical. This is the single most important spec on a commercial padlock. American Lock (the 1100 and 700 series), the better Abus and Mul-T-Lock units, and quality Master commercial locks are double-locking; the ninety-nine-cent luggage lock is not. When a customer asks what padlock to buy, \"double-locking shackle\" is the first thing out of your mouth.\n\nShimming is not the only padlock bypass, so know the family. Wafer-style padlocks (and a lot of cabinet and cam padlocks) fall to a comb pick \u2014 a stepped pick that pushes all the wafers above the shear line at once. Cheap combination dial padlocks decode: the old Master exploit reads the sticking points to cut the possible combinations from 64,000 down to about a hundred, then to eight, in a few minutes (covered in the combination-decoding material). And when finesse is not worth it, padlocks lose to destructive entry \u2014 bolt cutters on a thin shackle, an angle grinder, or shimming the WEAK point, which is usually the shackle, not the body. The pros know the shackle diameter and metal (hardened boron vs plain steel) decides how fast a cutter wins.\n\nSo when you spec or defend a padlock, you are really answering three questions: is the shackle double-locking (shim-proof), is the shackle hardened boron and short/shrouded (cutter- and pry-resistant), and is the cylinder decent (pick- and bump-resistant). A shrouded or \"hockey puck\" padlock that hides the shackle in the body beats bolt cutters; a double-locking ball-bearing shackle beats the shim; a good cylinder beats the pick. Cheap out on any one and that is the attack you just invited.",relatedGuides:["bypass-lockout","pick-types-technique","key-bumping"],sources:["https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/Shimming","https://toool.us/education/","https://www.americanlock.com/"],relatedModels:["padlock-american"],tags:["Bypass","Cam","Shear line","Cylinder"],aliases:["no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"auto-decode-read",title:"Reading & decoding auto locks: easy readers, Lishi & code stamps",module:"Automotive",body:"You're on a car you were hired to service, no working key, and you need the cuts before you can cut a blade. There are four ways to get them: read the code off the vehicle, pull the code from the VIN, decode a lock directly with a tool, or decode part of it and progress the rest. You pick the one that wins for THAT car sitting in front of you. Here's how each one works and when I reach for it.\n\n[[diagram:auto-decode-paths|By code, read in place, or pull it... and the worn-lock trap]]\n\nFIRST, A WORD ON WHAT THIS IS AND ISN'T. Reading and decoding a lock is mechanical work in the door or ignition cylinder. You're nowhere near the SIR/airbag circuit, so don't let anybody talk you into \"disconnecting the battery\" or \"pulling the airbag fuse\" just to decode a lock \u2014 that caution belongs to clockspring, steering-column, or airbag R&R, not to reading wafers. And there's no magic \"jumper\" that hands you key cuts on a Toyota or anything else. The TC/CG jumper you'll read about on Toyota is a DIAGNOSTIC trick (flash SRS/OBD codes), not a way to make a key. Getting cuts is decode or code, full stop.\n\nGET THE CODE OFF THE CAR FIRST. Before you touch a lock, look for the number. Older GM stamped a key code right on the lock cylinder face or the side of the housing \u2014 pull the door panel or the ignition lock and you may find a short alphanumeric code (modern GM references run like \"1A02,\" older stampings vary) that a code book or software turns straight into cuts. A lot of cars came with a metal or plastic KEY CODE CARD in the owner's packet, the glovebox, or a knockout tag. If the customer still has that card, you're done in thirty seconds \u2014 no picking, no decoding. This is the laziest win and it's the right one. Always ask and always look before you get fancy.\n\nPULL THE CODE FROM THE VIN. No card, no stamp you can reach? The factory still knows the cuts. As a registered NASTF Vehicle Security Professional (VSP) under the Secure Data Release Model, you log into the OEM's site with your credentials, enter the VIN, and it hands back the factory key code \u2014 and for a lot of makes the blade profile and transponder info too. There are also code services (locksmith code houses like the ones behind InstaCode/Codes On Line) that will look it up. The catch: you have to BE a VSP \u2014 identity verification and background check, a business registration, roughly $1M general-liability insurance, and for shops with employees a surety/employee-dishonesty (fidelity) bond around $100K. That's the whole point of the system: it keeps code access to legit, insured pros working on cars they're hired to service. VIN-to-code wins when the lock is high-security, worn, or a pain to pull, and when you're already set up in the registry. It's clean and it's provable you were authorized.\n\nEASY/EZ READERS AND TRY-OUT KEYS. When you want to decode a lock in the door without a fancy 2-in-1, an EEZ Reader (H.E. Mitchell, Portland OR) is about the simplest wafer decoder there is \u2014 a modified key tool plus a slope-tipped slide, in versions for GM, Ford, Chrysler, Saturn and more, including the GM \"Z\" 10-wafer non-warded locks. You feel each wafer, read the depth off the tool, write it down. Note that word: FEEL. You learn to feel the slide bottom on the wafer, not eyeball it. Try-out (jiggler) keys are a different animal \u2014 sets like AeroLock or SouthOrd that you rock and jiggle to walk an OLD or worn lock open. They exploit mechanical slop, so they shine on vintage stuff, beat fleet trucks, and damaged cylinders where a tight modern lock would just laugh at them. Try-outs get you IN; they don't give you cuts to cut a key from. Different job.\n\nTHE LISHI 2-IN-1 \u2014 MY BREAD AND BUTTER. The Original Lishi 2-in-1 is a pick and a decoder in one stainless body: single lifter to work each wafer, and a scale on the tool face that reads the wafer position as you pick it. Workflow is the same across keyways: insert, single-pin pick each wafer until the plug turns and the lock opens, then hold light tension and read each wafer's depth number off the printed scale, position by position. You write down the bitting, walk to the machine, and cut to code. Three minutes on a door you've done a hundred times. The tool tells you which spaces it covers \u2014 that matters, because a door lock rarely carries every cut.\n\nTOY43 (Toyota, 8-cut). The TOY43 2-in-1 reads positions 1 through 8, and positions 4 and 7 are SPLIT wafers \u2014 watch those, they'll bite you if you rush the read. Door/trunk versions exist plus a separate ignition tool; cross-references to TR47 / TOYO-15 blanks. Covers a huge run of Camry, Corolla, Avalon, Celica, Prizm, RAV4 and Tacoma from the early '90s into the 2000s (varies by model).\n\nHON66 (Honda/Acura high-security). This is the laser/sidewinder high-security key, roughly 2003-up (Accord, Civic, CR-V, Odyssey, Pilot and the Acura line). The Lishi reads the wafer positions right off the scale, no disassembly, then you cut by the bitting on any automatic machine. Gotcha: the DOOR lock on a lot of Hondas doesn't hold all the cuts \u2014 you'll read what's there and progress the missing ones (next method).\n\nGM HU100 vs. B111. Two different beasts, and don't let the \"Z\" label confuse you. HU100 is the GM high-security 10-cut (Malibu, Impala, Cruze, Camaro and friends, roughly 2010-up; there's also an 8-cut HU100/B116). Here's the real-world trick: the IGNITION carries positions 1-7 and the DOOR/tailgate carries 5-10. Neither lock alone gives you all ten. So you decode the door for 5-10, decode the ignition for 1-7, and the overlap in the middle (5,6,7) confirms you read both right. Two reads, one complete key, zero progression. B111 is the older GM \"Z\" keyway edge-cut WARDED lock (that \"Z keyway\" name belongs to the B106/B107/B111 family, not to HU100). Warded cylinders carry wafers in positions 3-10, non-warded 4-10; the B111 tool works spaces 3-10. Quick test: if an MZ31/X249 blank slides in, it's non-warded \u2014 know which one you're on before you read.\n\nDECODE BY FEEL FOR THE FIRST CUTS, THEN PROGRESS THE REST. Sometimes you can only get SOME of the cuts \u2014 a worn door lock, a cylinder that only holds part of the bitting, a read you only trust halfway. That's normal and there's a clean answer. You decode what you can read solid, then PROGRESSION-cut the rest: cut keys stepping the unknown positions through each possible depth until one throws the wafers and turns the lock. This is the classic GM edge-cut move \u2014 the door often holds only 8 of the 10 cuts, so you decode those and FILL/progress the last couple positions. Software (InstaCode's Fill/progression add-on, code-house fill programs) hands you the shortest list of blanks to try instead of brute-forcing all of them. Decode-and-progress wins when you've got a partial read and no code access \u2014 slower than a clean decode but it always converges.\n\nWHEN EACH ONE WINS, short version. Code card or stamp in hand \u2192 use it, fastest and cleanest. VIN-to-code as a VSP \u2192 best on high-security or hard-to-pull locks when you're registered. Lishi 2-in-1 \u2192 your default on the door for anything it covers; pick, read, cut. EEZ/easy reader \u2192 simple wafer decode by feel, older domestics. Try-out keys \u2192 getting a worn/old lock OPEN, not making a key. Decode-then-progress \u2192 partial read, no code, fill the gaps. Most days it's a hierarchy: look for the code, then Lishi the door, then progress whatever the door wouldn't give up.",sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12042138/auto-lock-decoding","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230702/lock-decoding-made-easier-with-the-eez-reader","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-toyota-8-cut-toy43-dr-2-in-1-pick-decoder-ag","https://www.mk3.com/original-lishi-toy43-for-old-toyota-8-cut-split-wafertoyota-camry-door-onlyanti-glare","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-honda-hon66","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/lishi-hon66-why-it-s-a-must-have-tool-for-automotive-locksmiths","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-gm-hu100-10-v-3","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-b111-z-keyway-2-in-1-pick-decoder-warded-ag","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/solving-the-gm-z-keyway-mystery-a-lishi-guide","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10229579/where-are-we-now-nastf-key-codes-and-the-locksmith","https://wp.nastf.org/?page_id=367","https://sdrm.nastfsecurityregistry.org/","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/nastf-oem-quick-reference-for-locksmiths","https://www.americankeysupply.com/category/aerolock-try-out-keys-90","https://www.southord.com/products/auto-jigglers-car-opening-tool-sjg-10","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/instacode-live-locksmith-software-add-on-fill-progressioning-find-bitting-6746","https://www.lockcodes.com/"],relatedGuides:["gm-sidebar-picking","gm-steering-column","toyota-ecu-jumper","auto-lock-removal","lishi","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Lishi","Reader","Progression","Key code","Decoder","Bitting","Cylinder","Keyway"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","cut depths","depth code","bitting list","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"gm-sidebar-picking",title:"Picking GM Sidebar Ignitions with Rocker Picks",module:"Automotive",body:"This is the GM 10-cut Strattec sidebar ignition \u2014 the workhorse cylinder across the GM lineup from the mid/late 1990s through roughly 2009 and lingering later on some platforms (Chevy, Pontiac, Buick, Olds, Cadillac, GMC, Saturn, Hummer, plus badge-shared Suzuki and Isuzu). Early ones ride the B102 keyway (B99 is the related sister blank). The 2005-up cars moved to the \"Z\" keyway family \u2014 B106, B107, B111 \u2014 in both non-warded and warded flavors, and that family runs on out toward 2021 on some trucks and SUVs. You'll only be in one of these on a vehicle you're hired to service. Know which keyway you've got before you pick a tool, because warded vs. non-warded and B102 vs. Z decide everything downstream. Quick field check: try an MZ31/X249 test key in a Z-keyway lock \u2014 if it enters, it's non-warded; if it stops at the mouth, it's warded.\n\n[[diagram:gm-sidebar-anatomy|Why a sidebar gives no feedback, and what the rocker is really doing]]\n\nHOW THE LOCK ACTUALLY WORKS\n\nForget top pins. This is a sidebar lock. As many as ten wafers sit in the plug (not every position is always populated \u2014 non-warded ignitions typically use positions 4-10, warded 3-10), but the wafers are NOT what blocks rotation \u2014 a spring-loaded sidebar does. The sidebar is a long bar down the side of the plug with fingers that, at rest, poke out into a groove in the lock housing and jam the plug solid. Each wafer has a V-notch machined into its side. When every wafer is moved to exactly the right height \u2014 some up, some down, because these are double-sided wafers \u2014 all the notches line up into one continuous channel, the sidebar's fingers fall inward out of the housing groove, and the plug is free to turn.\n\nHere's where I have to correct a myth apprentices repeat: the GM 10-cut Z-keyway is an EDGE-cut key, not a side-milled/laser key. The working cuts are on the top edge of the blade, four depths per position, and those cuts lift and drop the wafers to height \u2014 same basic idea as an old edge-cut Chevy, just decoded off four depths and a sidebar instead of a shear line. The word \"Z\" refers to the warded milling profile of the blade \u2014 the Z-shaped groove that lets the blade even enter that keyway \u2014 NOT to where the cuts live. Don't confuse this with GM's true high-security laser side-milled keys (HU100-style stuff on 2010-up Cruze/Malibu and the like); that's a different lock and a different tool.\n\nWHY YOUR PICK SET IS USELESS HERE\n\nOn a normal pin or wafer lock you set one element at a time. It binds, climbs to the shear line, catches on a ledge, stays set, you move on. That method depends on a shear line at the plug and on elements holding once set.\n\nA sidebar has neither. There is no per-wafer shear line to catch on \u2014 the wafers are spring-loaded straight back to rest, nothing holds them where you put them. And rotation is blocked by the bar, not the wafers, so binding a wafer does nothing useful. You can set nine perfect wafers and the tenth spits the other nine back before the sidebar ever sees a clean channel. Single-pin picking has nothing to grab. That's the whole reason these got a reputation for being \"unpickable\" with a standard kit.\n\nTHE ROCKER PICK AND THE \"PUSH THE SIDEBAR\" METHOD\n\nA rocker (rock) pick is a fat, wavy blade that touches all the wafers at once. You're not setting wafers one by one \u2014 you can't. You're rocking and raking the whole stack up and down through their depth positions simultaneously while the sidebar is loaded against them, waiting for a moment where all the notches flash into line at once.\n\nThe key is tension. You put light rotational tension on the plug. That tension pushes the sidebar's fingers hard against the flat sides of the wafers \u2014 the bar is now leaning on the notches, hunting for the channel. This is the \"push the sidebar\" method: the loaded sidebar itself becomes your feedback and your set-holding mechanism. As you rock the pick, wafers that momentarily present their notch let the fingers bite in a hair. You'll feel the plug take up a tiny bit of rotation \u2014 a few degrees of \"give.\" Chase that give. Keep rocking, keep the tension whisper-light, and the bar walks into the full channel. Then the plug rolls.\n\nTENSION BY FEEL\n\nThis is a by-feel job, not a by-force job. Too much tension and the sidebar clamps the wafers so hard they can't move through their depths \u2014 the lock goes dead and you're just polishing wafers. Too little and the bar never loads against the notches, so you get no feedback and nothing sets. You want just enough to feel the sidebar breathing on the wafers. I run a very light oscillating tension \u2014 barely there, easing on and off \u2014 while the rocker works. On an ignition remember the cylinder actually has to rotate to Run, so once it cracks, keep gentle rotation going and let it walk around; don't stop at the first click.\n\nPROGRESSING TO A DECODE OR IMPRESSION\n\nGetting it to turn gets the customer mobile, but you do NOT have a key yet. The second it turns, switch jobs: read the wafer depths and originate a real key. Two ways.\n\nDecode: the pro move. A 2-in-1 tool traps each wafer one at a time and indexes its depth against marks on the tool head \u2014 Original Lishi GM39 (B102) for the older 10-cut; for the Z-keyway, Original/Classic Lishi B111 is the warded 2-in-1 pick/decoder, GM37 is the non-warded 2-in-1, and the B106/B107 is a non-warded direct reader/decoder. Dedicated readers do the same job \u2014 the EEZ Reader GM-Z, the Lockmasters Kobra, the OctoKeys/Determinator system. All of them read the four depths by feel, position 1 through 10, and hand you the bitting. Read it twice and confirm.\n\nImpression: if you're reading by hand, lightly impression a blank of the correct keyway. Marks tell you which wafers are riding too high; file those cuts down a step, re-seat, repeat until every wafer clears and the blank turns freely. On a four-depth system your marks land on clean steps, so it comes together fast once you commit to reading them honestly.\n\nORIGINATE THE KEY\n\nCut a proper key on the correct blank \u2014 B102 for the earlier stuff, B106/B111 for the Z-keyway. These are edge cuts, but the Z blank is warded, so cut it on a machine set up for that keyway (or with the GM Z-keyway adapter) \u2014 the milled Z profile has to sit in the right lane or the blade won't even enter a sister lock, and an edge key clamped off-center miscuts easy. Test in the ignition AND the door.\n\nNow the immobilizer reality, and this is where the old version of this guide was flat wrong: it depends on the system. Passlock (Passlock I/II) has NO transponder \u2014 the security is a magnet and Hall-effect sensor built into the lock cylinder, so a correctly cut mechanical key alone WILL start the car; you only relearn if the BCM or cylinder was disturbed. The transponder cars are the PassKey III / PK3 and PK3+ systems \u2014 including the B111 \"circle-plus\" key, which carries a Philips ID46 (PCF7936) crypto transponder. On those, a mechanically perfect key cranks and dies until you program or clone the chip. So: know your system before you promise the customer a start.\n\nCAUTIONS \u2014 AND WHEN TO JUST DECODE\n\nIgnition wafers are worn and gummy from a decade of the customer's keychain hanging off them, and a rocking pick under tension can jam a tired wafer or roll it past where you want it. On a lot of these it's faster, cleaner, and lower-risk to skip picking: throw a Lishi 2-in-1 in the DOOR lock \u2014 the door decodes gentler, often gives the full code or enough of a progression to cut a key, and the door frequently shares the ignition's cuts. Decode the door before you fight the ignition. Pick the ignition when the door is damaged, keyed different, or you genuinely need it turning right now.\n\nOne safety word on the column: picking itself stays clear of the airbag circuit, so you don't need to disarm anything just to pick or decode. But if the job escalates to pulling the lock cylinder or drilling, remember the GM ignition lock lives in the steering column right next to the SIR coil/clockspring \u2014 kill the battery and give it a minute before you go poking around column wiring. And always confirm the keyway and warded/non-warded variant first \u2014 the wrong Lishi or reader in a Z-keyway lock just wastes your afternoon.",sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-gm39-b102-10-cut-2-in-1-pick-ag","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/original-lishi-gm-v-5-gm39-w-sidebar-2-in-1-reader-decoder-ag-15384","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/solving-the-gm-z-keyway-mystery-a-lishi-guide","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-b106-b107-gm-no-warded-key-reader","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-b111","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/original-lishi-gm-z-keyway-non-warded-b106-b111-gm37-2-in-1-ag-9382","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/eez-reader-2005-2021-gm-z-keyway-10-cut-non-warded-b106-b107-b111","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/locksmithing-101-everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-b111-transponder-key","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/gm-chip-id-46-for-b111","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/gm-z-keyway-adapter-for-cutting-b106-b111-pt","https://ricksfreeautorepairadvice.com/passkey-versus-passlock/","https://www.cardone.com/blog/gm-theft-deterrent-systems-vats-passlock-passkey-passkey-ii-and-passkey-iii/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10238356/general-motors-vehicles-equipped-with-passlock","https://www.camaros.org/keysandlocks.shtml","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/aable-side-kick-for-all-gm-sidebar-cylinders-6-wafer","https://www.lockpicking101.com/viewtopic.php?t=8169","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12042138/auto-lock-decoding"],relatedGuides:["auto-decode-read","gm-steering-column","toyota-ecu-jumper","auto-lock-removal","lishi","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Edge-cut key","Shear line","Crypto transponder","Lishi","Keyway","Decoder","Raking","Plug"],aliases:["double sided key","standard cut","non-laser key","picks open","won't set","line of separation","rolling chip","encrypted chip","80-bit","ID48","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list"]},{id:"gm-steering-column",title:"Pulling steering columns to service GM ignition locks",module:"Automotive",body:"Alright, listen up. Servicing an ignition lock on an older GM Saginaw column \u2014 think GM cars and trucks from roughly the mid-'70s clear through the mid-2000s on the non-column-shift stuff \u2014 is bread-and-butter work, but there's a right order and a couple of ways to hurt yourself or the car. I'll walk you through it.\n\n[[diagram:steering-column-order|The order, the airbag wait, and the clockspring that must not rotate]]\n\n**When you pull the column versus work in place**\n\nMost of the time you do NOT drop the whole column. On these Saginaw columns the ignition LOCK CYLINDER (the part your key goes into) lives up top by the steering wheel, and you can get to it right there on the car by pulling the wheel and the lock plate. You pull the entire column \u2014 unbolt it from the dash and the coupler at the box \u2014 only when you're chasing the actual IGNITION SWITCH (the electrical switch, which is way down the column near the shift bowl on the outside of the mast jacket), a bent mast, a broken housing, or a job where you need the column on the bench to fight the tilt guts. For a worn or failed lock cylinder, or a rekey, work in place nine times out of ten.\n\n**Tilt versus non-tilt**\n\nNon-tilt column: simplest. Wheel, lock plate, turn signal switch, buzzer, then the lock cylinder \u2014 done. Tilt column adds a tilt HOUSING with a spring, and a pivot-pin and lock-shoe setup up in the head. On a tilt you may have to tip the column to the full up or full down position to line up an access hole to get a tool on the cylinder retaining screw. If you have to go deeper than the cylinder on a tilt, you're into the pivot pins, and those want the pin remover \u2014 J-21854 (the -01 or -A) or equivalent. That same tool works the Saginaw pivot pins on GM, Ford, and Chrysler columns back to '69, so it earns its spot in the box. For a straight cylinder swap you usually don't have to disturb the pivot pins. Know which column you've got before you start.\n\n**Airbag / SIR FIRST \u2014 this is the safety-critical part**\n\nIf the vehicle is SIR (air-bag) equipped \u2014 and by the mid-'90s most are \u2014 you disable it BEFORE you touch anything, because the wheel comes off and the clockspring/coil and the yellow SIR harness are right there. Kill it wrong and you can fire a driver bag into your face.\n\nOrder: turn the key OFF. Disconnect the NEGATIVE battery cable and tape the terminal so it can't flop back on. Then WAIT. GM's spec on most of these SIR platforms is wait at least TWO MINUTES \u2014 some manuals say one, a few of the older systems call for longer \u2014 so the honest floor is two minutes and there's no harm giving it five. That wait lets the reserve energy in the SDM (the sensing and diagnostic module) bleed down, because that capacitor can still fire a bag after the battery's off. Don't skip it, don't rush it. Then find the YELLOW connectors \u2014 a driver bag will have a yellow connector at the base of the column under the wheel. Pop the CPA (the little Connector Position Assurance clip) and unplug the yellow connector to the inflator. Yellow means live pyro. Carry a removed bag face-UP, set it face-up, never lean it against the bench. That's the whole ballgame on safety. Battery off, wait two-plus, yellow unplugged \u2014 in that order, every time.\n\n**Pulling the wheel and the lock plate**\n\nPull the horn pad/center, pop the retainer, and remove the wheel with a proper steering-wheel puller (bolts INTO the threaded holes in the wheel hub \u2014 never hammer or pry a GM wheel, you'll crack the casting or bend the shaft). Mark the wheel-to-shaft relationship so it goes back straight.\n\nNow the LOCK PLATE. It sits under the wheel and is held by a snap ring, and that plate rides on a stout spring \u2014 under real load. Do NOT pry that snap ring off dry; it'll launch. You use a LOCK PLATE COMPRESSOR \u2014 the Kent-Moore J-23653 family. The -D is the universal one, the -SIR is the GM/Isuzu version, and on '92-and-later columns you add the J-23653-91A cancel-cam adapter to press the cam plate for the snap ring. It's a bridge with a forcing screw: thread it onto the shaft nut or bridge over the plate, screw it down to compress the plate past the snap-ring groove, pick the snap ring out, then back the screw off SLOWLY and lift the plate, the cancelling cam, and the upper bearing spring off.\n\n**Turn signal switch and buzzer**\n\nWith the plate off, remove the turn-signal (multifunction) switch screws \u2014 on the later columns those are a T20 Torx \u2014 unplug or free it enough to swing it out of the way. You usually don't have to fully unplug the connector, just let it hang. Under it is the KEY WARNING BUZZER switch \u2014 that little contact that beeps when the key's in and the door's open. On most of these it's a tiny clip you tease out with a 90-degree pick or a bent paperclip with the key in RUN; work it gently back and forth. Don't yank straight out \u2014 that clip bends and breaks and they're a pain to source.\n\n**Removing the ignition lock cylinder**\n\nTurn the key to RUN. Now find the lock-cylinder retaining screw. Older columns use a long thin screw down the housing bore \u2014 a stubby screwdriver or the right-length driver reaches it. On the later \"Dorman-era\" columns it's a TORX, a T20, holding a retaining pin/tab. Back that screw/pin out \u2014 DON'T drop it down the column, magnet it or bag it, because if it falls into the mast you'll be pulling the column to fish it out. With the retainer out and the key at RUN, the cylinder pulls straight out. On some of the newest ones there's a spring-loaded release button you press with a small screwdriver at the CRANK position instead of a screw \u2014 feel for which one you've got. If it fights you, you're not fully at RUN or the retainer isn't all the way clear.\n\n**Replacing a worn or failed cylinder**\n\nWorn GM cylinders wobble, the key won't return crisp from START, or the wafers are shot and it turns on a butter knife. You can rekey the new cylinder to the existing key with a GM wafer/tumbler kit and the code, or on a \"coded\" later cylinder match it to the door/glovebox. Coat the new cylinder and the housing bore with a dry lube or a light dielectric \u2014 not heavy grease, it collects grit. Install with the key at RUN, push it home so it seats and the retaining tab lines up, then reinstall the retaining screw/pin. Cycle the key through LOCK-OFF-RUN-START several times BEFORE you button anything up \u2014 it must turn smooth and spring back from START on its own.\n\n**Reassembly**\n\nReverse it: buzzer switch and clip back in as an assembly, turn-signal switch back with its screws (route the harness clear of moving parts), then the lock plate. Set the spring and cancelling cam, use the compressor again to press the plate down, seat a NEW snap ring in the groove \u2014 don't reuse a stretched one \u2014 and back the tool off slow. Wheel back on to your alignment mark, torque the nut. THEN reconnect the SIR: plug the yellow connector back, seat the CPA, reinstall the bag face-out. Reconnect the battery LAST, key off, stand clear, and turn the key on \u2014 the airbag light should flash a few times and go out. If it stays lit, you've got a stored code or a connector not seated. Test the buzzer, the start, and every key position before you hand it back.\n\nBattery off, wait two-plus minutes, yellow off \u2014 then work. Every single time.",sources:["https://www.gm-trucks.com/forums/applications/core/interface/file/attachment.php?id=130911","https://info.myairbags.com/kb/how-long-should-i-wait-after-disconnecting-the-battery-before-starting-work-on-the-acm-airbag-control-module-or-airbags","https://www.techconnectcanada.com/blog/2020/07/tech-tip-avoid-unintended-air-bag-deployment","https://www.jegs.com/InstallationInstructions/400/470/470-61203.pdf","https://tillmantools.com/products/kent-moore-j-23653-sir-lock-plate-compressor","https://etoolcart.com/gm-kent-moore-steering-column-lock-plate-compressor-j-23653-sir.aspx","https://www.toolsource.com/steering-wheel-pullers-c-433_549/lock-plate-compressor-p-148286.html","https://www.freedomracing.com/pivot-pin-remover-j-21854-a.html","https://tillmantools.com/products/ford-gm-j-21854-a-pivot-pin-remover-alt","https://www.autozone.com/diy/videos/how-to-install-dormans-924-894-924-896-gm-ignition-lock-cylinder-219719805","https://www.smpknowledgecenter.com/en/emd/ignition-and-doors-locks/ignition-lock-cylinder-replacements","https://gbodyforum.com/threads/how-to-fix-loose-tilt-steering-column-pic-heavy-now-with-video.75149/"],relatedGuides:["auto-decode-read","gm-sidebar-picking","toyota-ecu-jumper","auto-lock-removal","lishi","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Cam","Ring out","Cylinder","Plug"],aliases:["tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"toyota-ecu-jumper",title:"The Toyota/Lexus jumper reset: emergency all-keys-lost",module:"Automotive",body:"Alright, watch how this one works, because half the guys who \"know\" it are actually describing three different jobs mashed into one. There's the ECU/immobilizer *reset* (making the box forget every key it knew), and there's *registration* (teaching it a fresh master). On older Toyota and Lexus you can sometimes do both at the car with nothing but a jumper wire, a key blank cut to the lock, and patience. Emphasis on older, and emphasis on sometimes. This is for cars you're hired to service \u2014 you're standing there with proof of ownership, not \"learning\" in a parking lot.\n\n[[diagram:toyota-jumper-reset|What the reset erases, when it is the right call, and why no sequence is printed]]\n\nWHAT ERA THIS LIVES IN\nThis is a roughly 1998\u20132004 trick, give or take by model. You're looking at the 4C (Texas fixed-code) and early 4D (Texas crypto, the 4D67/4D68 DST40 family) transponder cars \u2014 Camry, Corolla, 4Runner, Tacoma, ES300/RX300/GS, that generation. The tell is the physical key with a chip in the head and a SECURITY light on the cluster, no proximity fob, no push-button start. Two families of connector matter here: the round under-hood DLC1 box (labeled DIAGNOSIS) on the pre-'98-ish stuff, and the 16-pin OBD2 under the dash (Toyota calls it DLC3) on the later ones. Once you're past ~2005 and into the H-chip / 8A and Smart Key cars, the jumper is dead \u2014 I'll get to why at the bottom.\n\nTHE HONEST PART ABOUT \"ALL KEYS LOST\"\nThe ignition-cycle registration sequence only *adds* a key when the box already trusts a working master. True AKL means the box trusts nothing, so before any of that timing dance means anything, the immobilizer has to be brought back to virgin. On these older cars there are three ways that happens, and you need to know which you're actually doing:\n\n1. Immobilizer/ECU already reset for you \u2014 a reflash or bench service (Tanin, Key Innovations, etc.) hands you back a virgin box. Then you just register.\n2. EEPROM reset in-house \u2014 pull the immo ECU, read the 93C56 (or similar) chip, virginize the dump, write it back. This is the *reliable* AKL path on 4C/4D. It's a solder-and-read job, not a jumper.\n3. The jumper \"super reset\" \u2014 shorting diagnostic terminals with the key on and letting it sit ~30 minutes. On *some* early ECM-based immobilizers this genuinely clears the key memory; on many it does nothing but flash the cluster. Treat it as a maybe, not a method. Try it before you tear a dash apart, but don't promise the customer it'll take.\n\nSo the flow is: get the box to virgin (one of those three) \u2192 then run the registration sequence. The jumper appears in two places \u2014 as the optional reset, and as a *resync* step after programming on some cars.\n\nSAFETY FIRST \u2014 AND NO, THIS ISN'T AIRBAG WORK\nGet this straight so you don't over-scare a customer or under-respect a wiring harness: key and immobilizer registration does not touch the SRS/SIR (airbag) system at all. There is no airbag to disable for the jumper trick or the ignition-cycle dance \u2014 you're on the diagnostic and ignition circuits, nowhere near a squib. Anybody telling you to pull the airbag fuse to program a key is confused. The *one* place SRS enters the picture is the EEPROM path (option 2): to pull the immo ECU on some of these you disconnect the battery, and on an airbag-equipped car that means you respect the SRS capacitor drain \u2014 Toyota's number is give it about 90 seconds (call it a couple minutes) after the battery's off before you go poking around the dash, and you keep your hands off the yellow SRS connectors and clockspring wiring. That's the whole safety story: not \"disable the airbag,\" just \"don't wake it up by accident while you're behind the dash with the battery down.\"\n\nTHE JUMPER, TERMINAL BY TERMINAL\nTwo pinouts, don't cross them up. What you're doing in both cases is the exact same short you'd use to blink diagnostic codes \u2014 grounding the TC line (TC is the modern name; on the older boxes it's stamped TE1, same terminal):\n\n- Round DLC1 under the hood: bridge TE1 to E1. Back-probe, don't force.\n- 16-pin OBD2 / DLC3 under the dash: the correct pair is TC to CG \u2014 that's pin 13 (TC) to pin 4 (CG, chassis ground). Verify against the actual DLC3 pinout for that VIN, but 13-to-4 is the right short. You'll see \"3 and 14\" in some writeups \u2014 treat that as a miscount, not an alternative. Pin 14 is CAN-Low and pin 3 is a manufacturer/unused terminal; grounding those does nothing useful and CAN-Low is the last thing you want a paperclip on. Straightened paperclip bent into a U works fine, just make sure you're in 13 and 4.\n\nFor the reset/resync attempt: master-cut key in, ignition to ON (engine off, all accessories live, do NOT crank), jumper in, and walk away ~30 minutes (some say up to 45). You'll see the cluster light up like a Christmas tree \u2014 lamps flashing, SECURITY doing its thing. That's normal. Pull the jumper, key off, then try to start / register. If it takes, great. If it throws the same \"not registered,\" it didn't, and you're going to the EEPROM. Note the difference: with a *working* master key inserted this is a resync and it's reliable; as a true no-key AKL \"super reset\" it's the coin-flip from option 3.\n\nTEACHING THE FIRST MASTER (THE REGISTRATION DANCE)\nOnce the box is virgin, the automatic sequence is simple and it's all about the SECURITY light and short waits. On a virgin box the light sits blinking, waiting:\n\n1. Insert master key #1, leave it, do NOT turn it. On a master the SECURITY light stops blinking / goes solid to acknowledge. Wait a few seconds (the DIYs say ~5; some models want up to 15). Pull it.\n2. Insert master key #2 the same way, few-second wait, pull it.\n3. Insert the third key and wait \u2014 on a valet key the SECURITY light stays solid ~2 seconds and then goes OUT. That closes the cycle.\n4. Give it ~30 seconds for the mode to fully close before you crank.\n\nOn these cars the first two keys registered become MASTER (black head) keys \u2014 the ones that can authorize future registrations \u2014 and the third becomes the VALET (grey) key that can't. If you're only cutting one master for the customer, you still often have to feed the sequence enough insertions to close it out, or close it by cycling the ignition ON/OFF about five times or pumping the brake five times, depending on model. And the gotcha nobody mentions: the car's key buzzer/dinger has to work. The registration logic keys off the door-courtesy and key-reminder switch signals \u2014 if that \"key's in the ignition, door's open\" chime is dead, the box won't see the insertions and the sequence won't take. Check that dinger first if a clean sequence keeps failing.\n\nRanges and \"varies\" apply to every number above. A '99 Camry, an '01 4Runner, and an '03 ES300 will each want slightly different waits and close-out. Pull the actual service-manual registration page (Procedure A/B for that model) before you commit \u2014 the manual gives you master-vs-sub order and the exact second-count.\n\nWHERE THE JUMPER DIES \u2014 H-CHIP AND SMART KEY\nRoughly 2005+ Toyota went to the 8A / \"H\" chip and an encrypted immobilizer that talks over CAN, and the free lunch ends. There's no terminal you can short to virginize it. AKL on H-chip and Smart Key cars is a *reset* operation done with a tool: Techstream (or a clone Mini-VCI / VXDIAG) pulls a Seed Number off the immo, you send that seed to Toyota's TIS to get a one-time 12-digit Pass-Code for that VIN \u2014 new seed and code every attempt. In North America that seed-to-passcode lookup is gated behind NASTF (the Secure Data Release program, ~$65 for a short LSID subscription) \u2014 no credential, no code, that's by design. You enter the code, the box resets, and *then* you program keys through the wizard: SECURITY flashes through a countdown \u2014 figure ~90 seconds on the plain H-chip cars, and a full 16-minute \"smart code reset\" wait on the Smart Key cars. Aftermarket boxes \u2014 Autel IM508/IM608, OBDSTAR X300, Lonsdor K518 \u2014 do the same seed/passcode over OBD, and some (the Lonsdor especially) grab the challenge and cloud-calculate to skip the 16-minute wait entirely. Two catches to know: the newer Autel path can do 8A smart AKL straight off OBD without the G-box on many cars, and on roughly 2017+ Toyota changed the algorithm again, so the old passcode calculators quit working on that newer stuff \u2014 it's tool-and-server or nothing.\n\nRule of thumb: physical chip key + SECURITY lamp + no fob = jumper era, verify pins per model (13-to-4 on the OBD2). Fob or push-start = tool and seed code, and in the States that means NASTF. When in doubt, pull the manual for the VIN before you touch a terminal.",sources:["https://taninautoelectronix.com/product/repair-service-1998-2005-lexus-toyota-ecu-immobilizer-key-programming-reset/","https://www.clublexus.com/forums/es-1st-to-6th-gen-1990-2018/815703-diy-immobilizer-hacking-for-lost-keys-or-swapped-ecu.html","https://keyinnovations.com/blog/how-to-program-keys-to-a-toyota-or-lexus-vehicle-when-a-traditional-reflash-service-has-been-performed-on-an-ecu-or-immobilizer-module/","https://www.toyotanation.com/threads/diy-immobilizer-hacking-for-lost-keys-or-swapped-ecu.1306497/","http://www.sopl.us/uploads/1/3/0/1/1301029/toyotapostflash.pdf","https://forum.ih8mud.com/threads/programming-a-master-or-valet-key.935935/","https://fatboysoffroad.com/how-to-program-toyota-ecu-manually/","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2018/MC-10143927-9999.pdf","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2016/MC-10149214-9999.pdf","http://blog.obdii365.com/2023/05/30/where-to-get-toyota-8a-4a-all-keys-lost-12-digit-security-code/","https://vxdiag.com/blogs/blog/how-to-solve-vxdiag-toyota-smart-key-programming-asking-for-pass-code","https://www.weboctopus.nl/lexus-scion-toyota-pass-code-by-seed-code-calculator.php"],relatedGuides:["auto-decode-read","gm-sidebar-picking","gm-steering-column","auto-lock-removal","lishi","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Seed code","Immobilizer","Key blank","Fob","Transponder","Credential"],aliases:["PIN code","outcode incode","dealer code","security access code","anti-theft","car won't start","cranks but dies","KW1","SC1","SC4","Ilco","JMA","Silca","blank won't fit","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","card","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"auto-lock-removal",title:"Pulling door lock cylinders \u2014 handles, clips & door panels",module:"Automotive",body:"Alright, first thing: before you pull anything, ask whether you even have to. On most cars you're servicing you can decode the lock in place with an Original Lishi 2-in-1 for that keyway (TOY43R for Toyota/Lexus \u2014 also Subaru and some GMC/Chevy, HU66 for VW/Audi, HU100 for late GM, B111/B106 older GM, HON66 for Honda/Acura). Insert it, single-pin/wafer pick until it turns, then read the bitting straight off the decoding grid. On the TOY43R the door lock only gives you eight of the ten positions (it reads spaces 3 through 10) \u2014 you decode those, cut a door key, and progress or read the last two cuts off the ignition. A good hand does a door in about three minutes and never touches a screwdriver. Pulling the cylinder is what you do when the Lishi won't read clean, the keyway's chewed up, you're replacing the cylinder anyway, or there's no tool for that keyway. So: try to decode in place first. Everything below is for when that's off the table.\n\n[[diagram:lock-cylinder-removal|Fasteners, clips, lift not pull... and the rods that look symmetrical]]\n\nSAFETY \u2014 SIR/AIRBAG, AND THE MYTH. Get this straight before you open a door. The old bulletin that a slim jim sets off a side-impact airbag is a debunked myth \u2014 pulling trim and popping clips does nothing to the bag. Most side airbags live in the seat, not the door; what's usually IN the door is the side-impact crash sensor and its wiring. The real risk isn't an explosion, it's this: unplug or short an SRS/SIR connector with the system live and you set a permanent airbag light \u2014 a comeback and a scan-tool clear you may not have. So on any car where you'll be unplugging harnesses inside the door, or that has a door-mounted side bag (some BMW, Mercedes, a few Toyota/Lexus), do it right: key off, disconnect the battery NEGATIVE, and WAIT about two minutes (GM SIR spec; some call for longer) for the capacitor to bleed down before you touch any yellow SRS connector. Yellow connectors and yellow harness tape mean airbag circuit \u2014 leave those alone when you can. And no, you don't \"jumper\" or bypass the SRS to kill a light \u2014 there's no legit Toyota jumper trick for this, that's shop-lore, not a procedure. Fix the fault, don't mask it.\n\nWORKING THROUGH THE HANDLE (pull from outside). Lots of cars let you get the cylinder without dropping the panel. On GM full-size trucks and countless '80s\u20132000s domestics the cylinder is held by a horseshoe/C-clip or a U-spring clip on the back side of the cylinder body. On a GM you often reach it up behind the handle: pull the clip and the cylinder slides out the front. On many late imports (Toyota, Honda, VW) there's a single screw or Torx bolt hidden on the door's rear shut face \u2014 open the door, pop the little rubber cap, and you'll find a T25/T30 (Toyota) behind it. Back it out a few turns; on most you DON'T remove it fully \u2014 the handle pops to its \"out\" position and you pull the cylinder cap and cylinder out the front. If you can pull it from outside, do it. Less to break, and you never go near the SRS wiring.\n\nTHE RETAINING CLIP AND LOCK ROD. Once you're behind the cylinder you'll see how it's held and how it drives the latch. Three common setups:\n- Horseshoe/C-clip or E-clip around the cylinder body \u2014 pop it with a pick or small flat driver. They fling, so cup your hand.\n- Pawl/cam (also called the lever, cam, or tailpiece) clipped to the back of the plug that hooks the lock rod \u2014 GM style. The pawl itself is retained by a little clip; the rod snaps into a plastic clip on the pawl.\n- A tailpiece screw or roll pin (some Ford/Chrysler).\nThe lock rod is the stiff wire running from the cylinder down to the latch. Where it meets the pawl or latch there's almost always a snap-fit plastic clip \u2014 flip the little green/white plastic keeper up 90\xb0 with a screwdriver and the rod pops off. Barely any force. If you're cranking on it, you've got the wrong clip. Note which hole in the pawl the rod sits in before you unhook it, or the lock will feel wrong on reassembly.\n\nWHEN YOU MUST DROP THE DOOR PANEL. No external screw, no reachable clip \u2014 panel comes off. Order of operations:\n1. Find the hidden fasteners. Behind the inner door pull, behind the armrest, behind the little triangle trim by the mirror, and inside the door-release bezel there are almost always screws under plastic caps or plugs \u2014 Phillips or Torx T15/T20/T25. Pop the caps with a trim pick.\n2. Pull the panel with a wide plastic trim wedge, working the perimeter. The Christmas-tree clips grab hard. Pull straight out at each clip; don't pry from the middle or you crack the panel. Expect to break a couple \u2014 carry a generic clip kit.\n3. Lift the panel UP and off the window sill (it hooks over the top), swing it out, and now disconnect the switch harnesses, lock knob rod, and interior handle cable. This is where the battery-neg/two-minute wait pays off if there's an SRS connector back there.\n4. Behind it: the vapor barrier \u2014 a plastic sheet glued with butyl (black gooey rope) to the door shell. Peel it back gently from one corner, don't tear it. Keep the butyl clean so it re-sticks. That barrier keeps water out of the cabin; rip it or skip the reseal and you'll get a wet carpet and a comeback. Reseal every edge with the original or fresh butyl rope, not duct tape.\n\nGOTCHAS INSIDE THE DOOR:\n- Window regulator: if the glass is down it blocks the cylinder and eats knuckles. Run the window UP before you kill power, or prop the glass so it can't drop on your hand.\n- Wiring: switch, actuator, tweeter, mirror, and that side-impact sensor all run right where you're reaching. Don't yank the cylinder out through a loop of wire, and don't pinch the sensor lead.\n- Plastic clips break \u2014 rod keepers, pawl clip, panel clips. Have replacements. A broken lock-rod keeper means the door won't lock right when you're done.\n- Actuator rod vs. cylinder rod: don't confuse the electric actuator's rod with the manual cylinder rod. Unhook the cylinder's.\n\nDECODING ON THE BENCH. Cylinder's out \u2014 now get your cuts:\n- Read the wafers directly. Hold the plug to the light and look down each chamber, or use a direct-read tool: the EEZ Reader or the AccuReader \u2014 a milled blank plus a slide/feeler that measures each wafer against scribe marks for that depth system (most GM/Ford/import door locks are 4-depth; some more). Write the cuts space by space.\n- Disassemble the plug: pop the cap clip, slide the plug out of the shell, lay the wafers out in order \u2014 space 1 at the tip. Match each wafer's height to the depth chart for that platform (GM B-series/Strattec, Toyota, etc.). Exact cuts, not a progression.\n- If it's ugly or you're missing chambers (door locks often omit a couple of the ignition's cuts), impression or progress the rest. A Determinator traps the deep wafers (depth 3 and greater, passes 1 and 2) so it gets you close enough to finish by impression.\nCut the key, verify it turns the cylinder freely both directions on the bench before you reinstall \u2014 way easier to fix a cut now than after the panel's back on.\n\nTRUNK AND TAILGATE. Same logic, usually easier \u2014 open the lid/gate and work from inside the sheet metal. Cylinder's held by a clip or a couple of small screws/nuts on a bracket. Many trunk locks you can read the six wafer depths straight through the weep hole with the lock still mounted \u2014 Lishi it or read it, no removal. If you do pull it, watch the actuator rod and the little return spring; they launch. Squeeze-tab cylinders: pull the clip, spread the two tabs on the back, plug comes out the front. Trunk and door often share the ignition keyway but not all the cuts, so decode the actual lock in front of you, not what you think the key should be.\n\nBottom line: decode in place with a Lishi when you can; pull through the handle when you can't; drop the panel only when the cylinder hides behind it \u2014 kill the battery and wait before you unplug near the SRS, and respect the vapor barrier, the glass, and the little plastic clips, because those are what make a clean job versus a comeback.",sources:["https://www.pro-lok.com/side-impact-airbag-myths-and-misconceptions/","https://auto.howstuffworks.com/car-driving-safety/accidents-hazardous-conditions/slim-jims-side-impact-airbags.htm","https://www.vehicleservicepros.com/industry-news/article/53089802/airbag-deactivation-tips-how-to-avoid-accidental-deployment-and-prevent-expense-and-injury-2017-10-27","https://www.tacomaworld.com/threads/how-to-replacing-and-rekeying-door-lock-cylinders.786676/","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/lishi-toy43r-2in1-decoder-and-pick","https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.lockpickworld.com/products/lishi-2-in-1-pick-decoder","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10911373/lock-decoding-made-easy-with-newest-lishi-tools","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/locksmith-tip-remove-stubborn-clips-on-the-back-of-lock-cylinders","https://gbodyforum.com/threads/become-the-key-master-everything-you-didnt-want-to-know-about-re-keying-your-locks.88703/","https://www.gmt400.com/threads/door-lock-pawl-retainer-clip-anywhere-other-then-lmc.8099/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/12042138/auto-lock-decoding","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230702/lock-decoding-made-easier-with-the-eez-reader","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10227713/accureader-a-new-reader-design","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10230696/determining-the-proper-cuts","https://dardoor.com/how-to-remove-a-door-panel-complete-repair-guide/","https://www.elementownersclub.com/threads/replacing-door-lock-cylinder.52802/","https://www.yourmechanic.com/article/how-to-replace-a-tailgate-lock-cylinder-by-jessica-howe"],relatedGuides:["auto-decode-read","gm-sidebar-picking","gm-steering-column","toyota-ecu-jumper","lishi","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Cylinder","Tailpiece","Keeper","Lishi","Shell","Cam","Plug","Progression"],aliases:["Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","stem","tail","spindle","deadbolt tail","strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","housing","lock body","tailpiece cam","cam won't turn","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading"]},{body:"GM is the make you cut your teeth on. High volume, forgiving systems, and for two decades you could originate keys with a meter and a code \u2014 no scan tool. But the blade and the chip changed hard around 2010, so you have to know which era you're standing in before you reach for a blank. Everything on GM is EDGE-CUT until the HU100 laser keyway shows up \u2014 that's the single biggest mistake I see new guys make: grabbing an HU100 laser blank for a 2008 Impala that wants a plain B111 edge cut.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blade/keyway \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path):\n- 1986\u2013~2004 VATS / PASS-Key I & II \u2192 edge-cut GM keyway \u2192 Ilco B82/B84/B86 (VATS PELLET blanks, the B82-P family) \u2192 RESISTOR PELLET, no transponder (15 values, 402\u03a9\u201311,800\u03a9) \u2192 meter it, match the pellet, or wire in a bypass module.\n- ~1998\u20132007 PASS-Key III (PK3) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B99-PT (also B97-PT) \u2192 Megamos ID13 (PK3), Megamos Crypto ID48 (PK3+) \u2192 onboard 30-min relearn, no tool needed.\n- ~2006\u20132018 Circle+ / Hitag2 flip & remote-head \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 Philips Crypto ID46 / Hitag2 \"Circle Plus\" (PCF7936/7937) \u2192 OBD add or AKL.\n- 2010\u2013~2018 high-security laser cars (Cruze, Camaro, Malibu, LaCrosse, Regal, Verano) \u2192 LASER/sidewinder HU100 keyway \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E (PCF7937E) \u2192 OBD.\n- ~2015\u2013present smart/proximity (push-button) \u2192 prox fob w/ HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46 \u2192 Hitag-2/AES \u2192 OBD, later cars CAN FD + server-calculated code.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 VATS / PASS-Key (mid-'80s to mid-2000s). This is not a transponder at all and the exam loves to trip you here. VATS (Vehicle Anti-Theft System, later marketed PASS-Key I/II) puts a black RESISTOR PELLET in the blade. The column reads its resistance; wrong value or an open/short and it timers you out for ~4 minutes. There are 15 values: 402, 523, 681, 887, 1130, 1471, 1871, 2370, 3010, 3740, 4750, 6040, 7500, 9530, 11,800 ohms. To originate: cut the mechanical key first (by code or by decoding the lock), then either read the customer's pellet with an ohmmeter and match a pellet blank (B82/B84/B86 \u2014 the pellet ones), or \u2014 the field standard on a dead car \u2014 install a VATS bypass module (a single resistor of the right value wired into the two harness wires under the column). With the bypass inline, any correctly-cut GM blank turns the car \u2014 you don't even need a pellet blank at that point. There's nothing to \"program.\" Cut it right, feed the right resistance, done.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 PASS-Key III (PK3), roughly 1998\u20132007. First true transponder era on GM. The chip is a Megamos \u2014 ID13 fixed-code on PK3, Megamos Crypto ID48 on PK3+. Blade is still EDGE-CUT, the 10-cut GM keyway, Ilco B99-PT (B97-PT is the same job). The beautiful part: GM's onboard relearn. ADD a key with one working key present = insert working key, cycle, then the new one \u2014 a couple minutes. ALL KEYS LOST with no tool = the 30-minute security relearn: cut a mechanical key that turns the lock (Lishi-decode the ignition or pull the code), insert it, turn to ON, wait ~10 minutes for the SECURITY telltale to go out, off ~5 seconds; repeat two more times (10+10+10 \u2248 30 min). The BCM learns the chip on the OFF-to-ON transitions. No IM608 required \u2014 a properly cut B99 and patience originates a PK3 from scratch.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 Circle Plus / Hitag2, roughly 2006\u20132018. Chip moves to Philips Crypto ID46 (Hitag2), the \"Circle Plus\" transponder, PCF7936/7937. Two physical blade families run side by side here, so DECODE THE LOCK, don't guess:\n- Edge-cut cars (Impala, older Silverado/Sierra 1500, Traverse, Enclave, Malibu classic): Ilco B111-PT, still the 10-cut GM edge keyway.\n- High-security LASER cars (2010+ Cruze, Camaro, Malibu, LaCrosse, Regal, Verano): Ilco B119-PT, HU100 sidewinder/milled keyway, chip ID46E (PCF7937E).\nThese need an OBD programmer. Add-key and AKL both go through the tool; PIN/security access is usually auto-calculated or read on the bench, no dealer trip on most.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 Modern smart / proximity, ~2015 to now. Push-button-start fobs, ID46/Hitag-AES, emergency insert blade is HU100-profile. Program by OBD on GM's older (Global A) architecture. On 2021+ Global B cars GM locked third parties out of the security server \u2014 you now run a CAN FD adapter and pull the ECM/BCM security codes through a third-party calculation service (Autel and others send the challenge out and get back the 24-digit ECM code and a 24-digit BCM code). The AKL routine is the door-cycle timer: with the tool driving it, open/close the driver door every 3\u20134 seconds until the ~12-minute timer expires, then drop the new fob in the programming pocket/cup holder and learn it. Always pull the exact FCC ID and GM part number off the old fob and confirm frequency before you order.\n\nTOOLS (the real menu, GM-specific):\n- Lishi 2-in-1: HU100 (comes in 8-cut and 10-cut versions) for the laser cars and newer high-security ignitions; B111 for the Circle+ edge 10-cut (GM \"Z\" warded keyway); B106 (GM37) for older non-warded GM 8-cut. On GM the Lishi picks the ignition and reads the cuts in one shot \u2014 that's your primary path to a code.\n- Meter/ohmmeter + VATS pellet chart, or a VATS decoder box \u2014 Era 1 only.\n- OBD programmer: Autel IM508/IM608 (II), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus / VVDI2, Lonsdor K518 \u2014 any of the three do GM add-key and AKL across the ~'06\u20132020 span; 2021+ Global B needs the CAN FD adapter plus the security-code service.\n- Code retrieval: GM VIN-to-key-code through the dealer or an ACDelco/NASTF key-code channel; GM codes are readily available and cut on a code machine (or hand-cut to a depth chart).\n\nFROM SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST, step by step:\n1. ID the era off the VIN and the door-lock face \u2014 laser keyway or edge cut? That picks the blank.\n2. Get IN and get the CUTS. Best GM path: Lishi the ignition (HU100 or B111/B106) and decode the bitting directly \u2014 GM ignitions read clean. Alternatives when the ignition is worn or jammed: pull the driver door lock and bench-decode it with the Lishi (GM door and ignition bitting often run close), read a code stamp on an older cylinder, pull the key code by VIN through the dealer/NASTF and cut to code, or impression the door.\n3. Cut the mechanical blank (B82/B84/B86 for VATS \u2014 or any GM blank once a bypass is inline; B99/B97 for PK3; B111 for Circle+ edge; B119/HU100 for laser cars).\n4. Originate the electronic side per era: VATS \u2014 match or bypass the resistor; PK3 \u2014 onboard 30-min relearn (10 min \xd73); Circle+/laser/smart \u2014 OBD program the ID46 (add-key or AKL), pulling the security code via server/calc where the car demands it.\n5. Verify crank and remote, hand over two keys.\n\nExact blank and chip vary by VIN \u2014 an '08 Silverado can be B111 edge OR a different profile by trim, and Circle+ ID46 vs ID46E matters. Before you cut, confirm the exact blank + chip on the specific VIN with a transponder lookup \u2014 Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or the Ilco/JMA app.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-gm|GM 5-button remote-head flip key (OHT01060512, HU100 blade) \u2014 the flip key most GM jobs on 2010-2020 Chevy, GMC, and Buick land on.]]\nTwo non-transponder GM security systems live inside this section. VATS/PASS-Key (Era 1, resistor pellet) is a car system (Corvette, Camaro/Firebird, Cadillac cars, Grand Am/Prix, Riviera) \u2014 it was never fitted to GM full-size trucks or SUVs. Passlock (PASS-Lock I/II/III) is the economy-line successor: the key is a plain, chip-less mechanical blank \u2014 the Hall-effect sensor and magnet live in the ignition lock cylinder, not the key. Cut it like any mechanical key; if the BCM has lost its code, do the Passlock relearn: three 10-minute KOA cycles (~30 min total). No tool, no chip. Don't sell a transponder key for a Passlock car, and don't meter for a pellet on a truck/SUV.\n\nChevrolet Silverado 1500/2500/3500 / GMC Sierra (full-size pickup twins)\n\u2022  1988\u20131998 (GMT400 \"OBS\") \u2192 edge-cut GM A/B keyway \u2192 Ilco B44 / B45 (plain) \u2192 no immobilizer (full-size GM trucks did not use the VATS resistor pellet on any trim); Passlock arrived with the GMT800 \u2192 cut by code (GM key code) or decode the lock.\n\u2022  1999\u20132006 (GMT800) \u2192 edge-cut double-sided 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B106 / B102 (chip-less) \u2192 Passlock, NO transponder \u2192 cut by code or decode (Lishi GM37/GM39); dead BCM = 30-min Passlock relearn. Don't chip these.\n\u2022  2007\u20132013 (GMT900) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 Philips Crypto ID46 \"Circle Plus\" / Hitag2 (PCF7936/7937) \u2192 add-a-key onboard with a working key, AKL via OBD tool (immo PIN) or 30-min relearn; Lishi GM37/GM39 to decode.\n\u2022  2014\u20132018 (K2XX) \u2192 LASER / sidewinder HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E (PCF7937E) \u2192 OBD add/AKL; base = bladed HU100, upper trims = push-button prox (HU100 emergency blade); Lishi HU100.\n\u2022  2019+ (T1XX) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 bladed flip key on work trims (B119 blade) + prox smart fob on the rest \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD; later builds are CAN FD, server-calculated code \u2014 confirm tool coverage on lookup.\n\nChevrolet Tahoe/Suburban / GMC Yukon / Cadillac Escalade (full-size SUV twins)\n\u2022  1992\u20131999 (GMT400) \u2192 edge-cut GM keyway \u2192 Ilco B44 / B45 (plain) \u2192 no immobilizer early; Passlock (Hall sensor in cylinder, chip-less) on the latest builds where fitted \u2014 GM full-size SUVs did not use the VATS resistor pellet \u2192 cut by code or decode the lock; Passlock 30-min relearn if the BCM lost its code.\n\u2022  2000\u20132006 (GMT800) \u2192 edge-cut double-sided 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B106 / B102 \u2192 Passlock, NO chip \u2192 cut by code/decode; 30-min relearn on dead BCM.\n\u2022  2007\u20132014 (GMT900) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus / Hitag2 \u2192 onboard add with working key, AKL by OBD tool or 30-min relearn. (These are bladed remote-head keys; push-button prox did not arrive until the 2015 K2XX \u2014 verify by dash before you cut.)\n\u2022  2015\u20132020 (K2XX) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD add/AKL; base blade keys use B119-PT laser.\n\nCHEVROLET IMPALA\n\u2022  2000\u20132005 (8th gen, W-body) \u2192 edge-cut double-sided \u2192 Ilco B106 / B102 \u2192 Passlock, NO transponder \u2192 cut by code/decode; 30-min relearn.\n\u2022  2006\u20132013 (9th gen, W-body) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus (PCF7936) \u2192 onboard add with working key, AKL via OBD tool or 30-min relearn.\n\u2022  2014\u20132020 (10th gen, Epsilon II) \u2192 LASER HU100 flip/remote-head (B119 blade) + prox on premium \u2192 ID46E \u2192 OBD add/AKL. Split-year gotcha: the fleet-only \"Impala Limited\" 2014\u20132016 is the OLD W-body \u2014 it stays B111-PT edge-cut Circle Plus, not the laser key. Confirm which Impala you're standing at.\n\nChevrolet Malibu / Saturn Aura / Pontiac G6 (Epsilon/Epsilon-II midsize)\n\u2022  2004\u20132012 Malibu / 2007\u20132009 Aura / 2005\u20132010 G6 \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus (remote-head KOBGT04A on many) \u2192 onboard add with working key; AKL OBD tool or 30-min relearn. (Very earliest 2004 Malibu \"Classic\" is the old Passlock chip-less car. Early 2004\u20132007 Malibu / 2005\u20132006 G6 may run PK3 Megamos ID48 / B99-PT rather than ID46 Circle Plus \u2014 meter/confirm on lookup.)\n\u2022  2013\u20132015 Malibu (8th gen, Epsilon II) \u2192 LASER HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E \u2192 OBD add/AKL; Lishi HU100.\n\u2022  2016+ Malibu (9th gen) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob HYQ4EA, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD.\n\nChevrolet Cobalt / Pontiac G5 (Pursuit) / Chevrolet HHR / Saturn Ion (Delta compacts)\n\u2022  2003\u20132005 Ion / 2005 Cobalt \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 chip-less blank \u2192 Passlock, NO transponder \u2192 cut by code/decode; 30-min relearn. (2005 Cobalt takes no chip \u2014 do not program.)\n\u2022  2006\u20132010 Cobalt / 2006\u20132007 Ion / 2007\u20132010 G5 / 2008\u20132011 HHR \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus \u2192 onboard add with working key, AKL OBD tool or 30-min relearn. (Earliest 2006 builds may still be PK3 ID48 / B99-PT \u2014 meter/confirm on lookup.)\n\nCHEVROLET CRUZE\n\u2022  2011\u20132015 (1st gen) \u2192 LASER HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E (PCF7937E) \u2192 OBD add/AKL; Lishi HU100.\n\u2022  2016\u20132019 (2nd gen) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 bladed B119 on base, prox smart fob HYQ4EA on RS/Premier \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD.\n\nChevrolet Equinox / GMC Terrain / Pontiac Torrent / Saturn Vue (compact SUV)\n\u2022  2005\u20132009 Equinox / 2006\u20132009 Torrent / 2008\u20132010 Vue \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus \u2192 onboard add, AKL OBD tool or 30-min relearn. (Earliest units may be PK3 ID48 / B99-PT \u2014 meter/confirm on lookup.)\n\u2022  2010\u20132017 Equinox (2nd gen) / 2010\u20132017 Terrain (1st gen) \u2192 LASER HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E \u2192 OBD add/AKL; Lishi HU100.\n\u2022  2018+ Equinox / 2018+ Terrain (3rd gen / 2nd gen Terrain) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD.\n\nCHEVROLET CAMARO\n\u2022  1993\u20132002 (4th gen) \u2192 edge-cut GM keyway \u2192 VATS pellet B82/B84 \u2192 resistor pellet, no transponder \u2192 cut mechanical, meter & match pellet, or VATS bypass module.\n\u2022  2010\u20132015 (5th gen) \u2192 LASER HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E \u2192 OBD add/AKL; Lishi HU100.\n\u2022  2016+ (6th gen) \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob HYQ4EA, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD.\n\nCADILLAC CTS / SRX / DTS (MID-2000S LUXURY)\n\u2022  2003\u20132007 CTS / 2004\u20132009 SRX \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus (PCF7936) \u2192 onboard add with working key, AKL OBD tool or 30-min relearn. (Early 2003\u20132005 CTS / 2004\u20132005 SRX may be PK3 Megamos ID48 / B99-PT \u2014 meter/confirm on lookup. DTS/DeVille of this window = B111 Circle Plus as well, though 2000\u20132005 DeVille can be PK3 B99-PT.)\n\u2022  2008\u20132013 CTS \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob (M3N5WY7777A) on most, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46 \u2192 OBD add/AKL.\n\u2022  2014\u20132019 CTS / 2015\u20132016 SRX / 2015\u20132020 Escalade \u2192 HU100 \u2192 prox smart fob, HU100 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/AES \u2192 OBD.\n\nBUICK LACROSSE / REGAL / VERANO / ENCORE (HIGH-SECURITY LASER ERA)\n\u2022  2010\u20132016 LaCrosse / 2011\u20132017 Regal / 2012\u20132017 Verano / 2013\u20132016 Encore \u2192 LASER HU100 \u2192 Ilco B119-PT \u2192 ID46E (PCF7937E) \u2192 OBD add/AKL; upper trims run prox (HU100 emergency blade) \u2014 check for push-button start; Lishi HU100. (Earlier LaCrosse/Lucerne 2005\u20132009 = edge-cut B111-PT Circle Plus, early builds possibly PK3 B99-PT \u2014 confirm on lookup.)\n\nPONTIAC GRAND PRIX / GRAND AM (W-BODY / N-BODY)\n\u2022  1997\u20132003 Grand Prix / Grand Am \u2192 edge-cut GM keyway \u2192 VATS pellet B84 on early, then Passlock chip-less on later builds \u2192 no transponder either way \u2192 meter/match pellet or bypass (VATS); cut & 30-min relearn (Passlock). Verify which system by year/VIN.\n\u2022  2004\u20132008 Grand Prix (7th gen) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut \u2192 Ilco B111-PT \u2192 ID46 Circle Plus \u2192 onboard add with working key, AKL OBD tool or 30-min relearn.",id:"autokeys-gm",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/vehicle-security-systems/vats-pass-key-description.html","https://remotesandkeys.com/blogs/blog/how-to-determine-which-vats-resistor-value","https://www.lockmasters.com/site/pdfs/TT4002%20Vats%20Overview%20Article.pdf","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/chevrolet_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/1997-2009-gm-transponder-key-id13-chip-pk3-b99-pt","https://my.cardone.com/techdocs/pt%2077-0011.pdf","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/gm-chip-id-46-for-b111","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/gm-circle-plus-transponder-key-b111-pt-5903089","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-hu100-2-in-1-pick-ignition-door-trunk-10-cut-ag","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-gm-hu100-b116-2-in-1-pick-ignition-door-trunk-8-cut-ag","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/11/11/program-gm-2021-2024-all-keys-lost-with-autel-im508-im608/","https://blog.obdii365.com/2022/03/13/autel-im608-gm-security-code-calculation-by-autel/"],title:"Making Keys: GM (Chevy, GMC, Buick, Cadillac, Pontiac, Saturn)",relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Fob","Transponder","Bypass","Key code","Keyway","FCC ID","Add-a-key"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","extra key","spare key","add key to system","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","read the cuts","reading tool","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-ford",title:"Making keys: Ford, Lincoln & Mercury (PATS)",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Ford's the make you'll cut more than any other, and once you understand PATS (Passive Anti-Theft System, marketed as SecuriLock) the whole Blue Oval family \u2014 Ford, Lincoln, Mercury \u2014 reads the same way. One transponder family carries you across two decades, and the two-key onboard trick means a big slice of \"add a key\" jobs need no tool at all. Learn where the eras break and you'll rarely be stuck.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blade \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path)\n- Pre-'96 mechanical: edge-cut, Ilco H50/H51/H54 (Ford 8-cut) & H60/H67 (10-cut), no chip \u2192 cut by code/impression.\n- Jaguar-era & Transit Connect Tibbe: rotary-disc, FO21 / JMA FO-6.P (6-cut), 4D60 chip \u2192 Lishi FO21 Tibbe pick (PFTPD), cut by decode.\n- PATS Gen1 '96\u2013'00: edge-cut FO38/H72-style, Ilco H84 / Strattec 599114, chip 4D63 40-bit \u2192 2-key onboard or OBD.\n- PATS Gen2 '01\u2013'10: edge-cut FO38, Ilco H92 (Strattec 5913441 \"SA\") / H84, chip 4D63 80-bit \u2192 2-key onboard or OBD add/AKL.\n- Laser/flip '11\u2013'14: 10-cut sidewinder HU101, Ilco H128/HU101 blade, 4D63 80-bit (fixed or flip) \u2192 OBD add/AKL, timed.\n- Flip '15\u2013'17+: 10-cut HU101 flip N5F-A08TAA (315MHz), chip ID49/Hitag-Pro (NCF2951-class), blade HU101 \u2192 OBD/FDRS add/AKL, timed.\n- Smart/PEPS '13+ push-button: prox fob M3N-A2C93142300 / P/N 164-R8163 (315MHz), Hitag-Pro, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL.\nFitment varies by VIN \u2014 confirm on Transpondery / UHS / Ilco before you cut.\n\nBLADE TYPES \u2014 CALL THEM OUT\nThree blade families live under the Ford tent and you cannot mix them. First, the classic EDGE-CUT (tip-stop, 8-cut) that runs from the '80s through the mid-2010s on most cars and trucks \u2014 that's your FO38 keyway, Ilco H84/H92. Second, the TIBBE ROTARY-DISC key \u2014 a round-shank, 6-cut key that turns discs, not tumblers \u2014 found on Ford-era Jaguar (S-Type, X-Type, XJ) and the first-gen Transit Connect (roughly 2010\u201313 US); that's FO21, and it needs a dedicated Tibbe pick, not a standard Lishi. Third, the LASER/SIDEWINDER (internal-milled) 10-cut that arrived around 2011 on Fiesta/Focus/Escape/Fusion and the newer trucks \u2014 HU101 keyway. Smart/PEPS fobs carry an HU101 emergency blade to open the door and to start via the backup slot/console. Grab the wrong blank and nothing you do afterward matters.\n\nCHIPS \u2014 THE 4D63 STORY (AND WHERE IT ENDS)\nNearly all transpondered Ford/Lincoln/Mercury from 1996 through the early-mid 2010s use the Texas Instruments 4D63. The only thing that changes early on is the crypto length: PATS Gen1 ('96\u2013'00) is 4D63 40-bit; from about 2001 it's 4D63 80-bit. The gotcha to memorize: an 80-bit blank (Ilco H92, Strattec's \"SA\"-stamped 5913441) is backward-compatible and starts a 40-bit car, but a 40-bit key will NOT start an 80-bit car. So when in doubt, cut 80-bit. Ilco's H92 is often sold as an upgradeable dual chip \u2014 it presents 40-bit until the vehicle programs it, then locks to 80-bit. HERE'S THE CORRECTION people get wrong: not every flip key is a 4D63. The 2011\u20132014 flip and fixed-head keys are still 4D63 80-bit, but from about 2015 the common Ford flip (FCC N5F-A08TAA, 315MHz) and all the push-to-start prox fobs moved to NXP Hitag-Pro (ID49, NCF2951-class), a rolling-code proximity chip \u2014 NOT a 4D63. Don't order an 80-bit flip for a 2016 F-150 and expect it to program; check the FCC and chip family first.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS \u2014 the real menu\nFor an edge-cut Ford, your first reach is the Original Lishi FO38 (also sold as H75/FO38, 8-cut) \u2014 it picks the door and decodes the cuts in one pass, usually inside three minutes, and you cut straight to code off the reading. On the newer laser cars, that's the Lishi HU101 (10-cut, V3). On a Ford-era Jaguar or old Transit Connect, you need the Lishi FO21 Tibbe pick (PFTPD) \u2014 a regular reader won't touch a disc lock. If you'd rather not pick: pull the door or trunk lock and bench-decode it with the same Lishi or by disassembly; on trucks the tailgate/door plug comes out cleanly. You can also pull the cut code from Ford \u2014 the key code is available through Ford's parts system / NASTF locksmith channels tied to the VIN, then cut by code on your machine (InstaCode/Ilco). Impression is a valid last resort on the edge-cut keyways. Don't default to \"Lishi every door\" \u2014 on a Ford the door and ignition are keyed alike far more often than not, so one good FO38 decode usually gives you the ignition cut too.\n\nPROGRAMMING BY ERA \u2014 including all-keys-lost\n\nPATS Gen1/Gen2 edge-cut ('96\u2013'10): ADD A KEY with the famous two-key onboard trick \u2014 if the customer already has TWO working programmed keys, no tool is needed. Insert programmed key #1, turn to RUN, then OFF and remove. Within ~5 seconds insert programmed key #2, RUN, OFF, remove. Within ~10 seconds insert the NEW cut key and turn to RUN; the theft light gives a solid confirmation flash and the key is programmed. Two keys in, blank programmed \u2014 that's the whole trick, and it's why you always leave a customer with at least two keys. If they only have ONE key, onboard won't self-teach a second, so you go to OBD.\n\nALL-KEYS-LOST / from scratch (edge-cut): you must cut a working blade first (Lishi FO38 decode, lock pull, or code from Ford), then program by OBD. Plug an aftermarket programmer into the DLC \u2014 Autel IM508/IM608, Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro, XTool, or the budget route FORScan with an ELM327 (MS-CAN/HS-CAN switch) on a laptop. In the tool's PATS/Service menu choose \"Erase all keys / program keys,\" follow the on-screen timing (many years drop a ~10-minute security wait \u2014 let it run, don't yank the tool), then program each cut key. On older PATS modules you can also read the incode/PIN by EEPROM off the PATS/instrument-cluster module on the bench, but OBD has made that rare.\n\nLaser/flip era ('11\u2013'17): same OBD flow \u2014 cut the HU101 blade (Lishi HU101 or code), then Autel/Smart Pro/FDRS to add or do AKL. The 2011\u201314 flips are 4D63 80-bit and program identically to a fixed-head cut key; the 2015+ N5F-A08TAA is a Hitag-Pro flip and programs through the same tools but as a Hitag-Pro/ID49 key \u2014 pick the right key type in the menu. Most of these enforce the timed security delay on AKL; budget the 10 minutes.\n\nSmart/PEPS push-to-start ('13+): these are proximity fobs (M3N-A2C93142300 / 164-R8163, Hitag-Pro). Add-a-fob and AKL run through Ford's dealer online \u2014 IDS/FDRS with an internet subscription and the vehicle's security access \u2014 or through aftermarket (Autel IM608, Smart Pro) that emulate the same handshake. AKL on PEPS typically forces the 10-minute-per-cycle timed access; some late models need FDRS specifically. Cut/insert the HU101 emergency blade so you can open the door and use the backup start slot while you work.\n\nBOTTOM LINE\nFord is friendly to originate: one dominant chip family for two decades (4D63, 40- then 80-bit), a forgiving two-key onboard for adds, and a solid Lishi (FO38 edge / HU101 laser / FO21 Tibbe) that reads cuts fast. But know where 4D63 stops \u2014 2015+ flips and every prox fob are Hitag-Pro/ID49, not 4D63. Carry H84 and H92 blanks, HU101 stock (blade and flip), and either FORScan or a proper programmer, and you'll clear most Blue Oval work on the driveway. Exact blade, blank and chip still shift by VIN and even by build month \u2014 before you cut, confirm the precise blank + chip on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or the Ilco/Strattec cross-reference) for that specific VIN.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-ford|Ford H92/H84 PATS transponder key (H75 keyway, 4D63 80-bit chip) \u2014 the key a locksmith cuts and programs on nearly every 2000-2017 Ford, Lincoln, and Mercury.]]\n\nRanges below are already collapsed: consecutive generations that key the same way are wrapped into one row. A new row only starts where the blade, blank, chip, or programming path actually changed. On any 2001\u20132011 edge-cut Ford the 4D63 chip is cloneable (40-bit clones with basic tools; 80-bit needs a modern cloner \u2014 Xhorse/VVDI super chip, KD, etc.), so cloning a working key is often the fastest add-a-key. The moment a model goes HU101 128-bit / ID49 / Hitag-Pro, cloning is dead \u2014 you program on OBD/FDRS only. Note the HU101 laser era itself splits on chip: '11\u2013'14 laser keys are still 4D63 80-bit (cloneable); '15+ laser keys are ID49/Hitag-Pro (not cloneable). \"Confirm on lookup\" = verify blank/chip on Transpondery, UHS, or Ilco by exact VIN before you cut.\n\nFORD F-150 & F-SERIES SUPER DUTY\n\u2022  F-150 (1997\u20132014) / Super Duty (1999\u20132016) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H84 (Gen1) then H92 (Gen2, Strattec 5913441 \"SA\"); later years are a CWTWB1U793 remote-head key \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '97\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 cut by code or Lishi FO38 decode; add: 2-key onboard or OBD; clone a working key (fast, no relearn); AKL: OBD (Autel/Smart Pro/XTOOL) or dealer.\n\u2022  F-150 (2015\u20132020) / Super Duty (2017\u20132022) \u2192 HU101 laser/sidewinder \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 blade (OEM 164-R8130) \u2192 ID49 128-bit (remote-head key) *or* PEPS prox (M3N-A2C31243300, push-button trims) \u2192 cut by code or Lishi HU101 decode; add/AKL: OBD or FDRS only \u2014 not cloneable; AKL is a ~10-min timed relearn.\n\u2022  F-150 (2021+ / 14th gen) \u2192 PEPS standard \u2192 prox fob (confirm FCC on lookup), HU101 emergency blade \u2192 FDRS or aftermarket (Autel/Smart Pro), 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD MUSTANG\n\u2022  1996\u20132004 (SN95/New Edge) & 2005\u20132014 (S197) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H84 then H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '96\u2013'00, 80-bit '01\u2013'14) \u2192 Lishi FO38 or by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, or clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2015\u20132017 (S550, turn-key trims) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101, 128-bit ID49 \u2192 OBD/FDRS add & AKL; not cloneable; Lishi HU101 to decode/cut.\n\u2022  2018\u20132023 (S550 facelift) \u2192 PEPS push-button \u2192 prox M3N-A2C93142300 (315MHz, Hitag-Pro), HU101 emergency blade \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD EXPLORER\n\u2022  1996\u20132010 \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H72/H84 then H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '96\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2011\u20132019 (5th gen) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 CWTWB1U793 remote-head key on turn-key trims (Intelligent-Access smart key on Limited/Platinum) \u2192 Ilco H92/H75 blade, 4D63 80-bit \u2192 onboard/OBD/clone; smart trims: OBD/FDRS.\n\u2022  2020+ (6th gen) \u2192 PEPS standard \u2192 prox (M3N-A2C931426 / confirm on lookup), HU101 emergency blade, ID49 \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD ESCAPE (+ MERCURY MARINER TWIN '05\u2013'11)\n\u2022  2001\u20132012 (1st/2nd gen) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92 \u2192 4D63 80-bit ('01+, Gen2) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2013\u20132014 (3rd gen, early) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 4D63 80-bit (cloneable) \u2192 Lishi HU101 decode; add: OBD or clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2015\u20132019 (3rd gen) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 ID49 128-bit (PEPS smart on Titanium) \u2192 Lishi HU101 decode; OBD/FDRS add & AKL \u2014 not cloneable, ~10-min timed.\n\u2022  2020+ (4th gen) \u2192 PEPS standard, prox + HU101 emergency blade \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD FOCUS\n\u2022  2000\u20132011 (1st/2nd gen NA) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; onboard/OBD/clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2012\u20132014 (3rd gen, early) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 4D63 80-bit (cloneable) \u2192 Lishi HU101; add: OBD or clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2015\u20132018 (3rd gen, to NA end) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 ID49 128-bit (non-prox key or PEPS flip) \u2192 Lishi HU101; OBD/FDRS add & AKL \u2014 not cloneable, ~10-min timed.\n\nFORD FUSION (+ MERCURY MILAN TWIN '06\u2013'11)\n\u2022  2006\u20132012 (1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92 \u2192 4D63 80-bit \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2013\u20132014 (2nd gen, early) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 4D63 80-bit (cloneable) \u2192 Lishi HU101; add: OBD or clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2015\u20132020 (2nd gen) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 ID49 128-bit, non-prox HU101 key *or* PEPS smart M3N-A2C31243300 \u2192 Lishi HU101 decode; OBD/FDRS only \u2014 not cloneable, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD TAURUS (+ MERCURY SABLE TWIN)\n\u2022  1996\u20132007 \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H72/H84 then H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '96\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; onboard/OBD/clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2008\u20132019 (D3/D4) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 CWTWB1U793 remote-head key (IA smart on SHO/Limited) \u2192 Ilco H92, 4D63 80-bit \u2192 onboard/OBD/clone; smart trims OBD/FDRS.\n\nFORD EDGE\n\u2022  2007\u20132014 (1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 CWTWB1U793 remote-head key \u2192 Ilco H92/H75 \u2192 4D63 80-bit \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2015\u20132024 (2nd gen) \u2192 PEPS smart standard \u2192 prox M3N-A2C93142300, HU101 emergency blade, ID49/Hitag-Pro; base trims may use non-prox HU101 128-bit key \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL; not cloneable.\n\nFORD EXPEDITION\n\u2022  1997\u20132017 \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H84 then H92 (later a CWTWB1U793 remote-head key) \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '97\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2018+ (4th gen) \u2192 PEPS standard \u2192 prox (confirm FCC on lookup), HU101 emergency blade, ID49 \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFORD RANGER\n\u2022  1998\u20132011 (1st NA run) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '98\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  2019\u20132023 (T6 return) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 ID49 128-bit non-prox key *or* PEPS smart with HU101 emergency blade \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket OBD \u2014 not cloneable, 10-min timed AKL.\n\nFord Transit Connect  *(the Tibbe oddball \u2014 do not grab a Lishi FO38 or HU101)*\n\u2022  2010\u20132013 (1st gen US) \u2192 TIBBE rotary-disc (round shank, 6-cut, turns discs not tumblers) \u2192 FO21 / JMA FO-6.P \u2192 4D60 chip \u2192 dedicated Lishi FO21 Tibbe pick (PFTPD), cut by decode; add/AKL via OBD \u2014 standard Lishi will not fit.\n\u2022  2014 (2nd gen, early) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 4D63 80-bit (cloneable) \u2192 Lishi HU101; OBD add / clone.\n\u2022  2015+ (2nd gen, C-Max platform) \u2192 HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco H128/HU101 \u2192 ID49 128-bit (or PEPS smart) \u2192 Lishi HU101; OBD/FDRS, 10-min timed AKL \u2014 not cloneable.\n\nLINCOLN \u2014 TOWN CAR, NAVIGATOR & MKZ/MKX\n\u2022  Town Car (1998\u20132011) & Navigator (1998\u20132017) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 (Panther/truck) \u2192 Ilco H84 then H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '98\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  MKZ (2007\u20132012) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92 \u2192 4D63 80-bit \u2192 onboard/OBD/clone.\n\u2022  MKX (2007\u20132015, 1st gen \u2014 Ford Edge twin) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H92/H75 \u2192 4D63 80-bit \u2192 onboard/OBD/clone.\n\u2022  MKZ (2013+) / MKX\u2192Nautilus (2016+) / Navigator (2018+) \u2192 PEPS smart standard \u2192 prox M3N-A2C93142300 (Hitag-Pro), HU101 emergency blade; base MKZ may use non-prox HU101 128-bit \u2192 FDRS/aftermarket, 10-min timed AKL \u2014 not cloneable.\n\nMercury \u2014 Grand Marquis, Sable, Milan & Mariner  *(all edge-cut, all discontinued by 2011 \u2014 no laser/smart Mercurys exist)*\n\u2022  Grand Marquis (1998\u20132011, Panther twin of Crown Vic/Town Car) \u2192 edge-cut FO38 \u2192 Ilco H84 then H92 \u2192 4D63 (40-bit '98\u2013'00, 80-bit '01+) \u2192 Lishi FO38 / by code; add: 2-key onboard, OBD, clone; AKL: OBD.\n\u2022  Sable, Milan, Mariner \u2192 key exactly like their Ford twins (Taurus / Fusion / Escape) for the edge-cut/4D63 years \u2014 same blank, same chip, same paths.\nFitment varies by VIN and trim (turn-key vs push-button splits a single model-year). Confirm blade, blank, and chip on Transpondery / UHS Hardware / Ilco before you cut.",sources:["https://locksmithcharley.com/transponder/ford.shtml","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2000-2017-ford-h92-h84-transponder-key-4d63-80-bit-chip-k-h92","https://www.intelligentkeysolutions.com/product/ford-80-bit-h84-tp-4d63/","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/ford-h92-h84-h85-80-bit-sa-transponder-key-ilco-6357","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/lishi-fo38-2in1-decoder-and-pick","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-hu101-cutout-v3-ford-10-cut-2-in-1-pick-decoder-ag","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/lishi-2-in-1-pick-decoder-for-ford-8-cut-h75-fo38-9378","https://abkeys.com/products/original-lishi-ford-tibbe-pick-tool-fo21-lishi-pftpd-original-lishi-3519","https://abkeys.com/products/ford-mondeo-focus-transponder-key-4d60-fo21-tibbe-key-2317","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/ford-transit-connect-3-button-remote-head-tibbe-key-3b4-by-ilco","https://www.honrow.com/For-Ford-2-1-button-smart-remote-key-315MHz-ASK-NCF2951F-HITAG-PRO-49-CHIP-FCC-ID-M3N-A2C93142300-P-N-164-R8163-5929508-p2074851.html","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2015-2019-ford-flip-key-3b-fcc-n5f-a08taa-5923667","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/ford-fusion-4-button-flip-key-oem-w-ford-logo-tex-4d-63-80-bit-315mhz-by-strattec","https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/pats-programming-with-forscan/","https://ohptools.com/blogs/news/how-to-program-your-transponder-keys-using-forscan","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2017-2024-ford-3-button-smart-key-peps-164-r8163-m3n-a2c93142300-oem-refurb","https://www.key4.com/smart-remote-key-ford-m3na2c93142300-164r8163"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["PATS","Lishi","Fob","Push-to-start","Cloning","Transponder","Add-a-key","Key code"],aliases:["Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","Ford key programming","2-in-1","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","proximity key","keyless go","start button","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading"]},{id:"autokeys-chrysler",title:"Making keys: Chrysler, Dodge, Jeep, Ram & Fiat (Stellantis)",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Stellantis (Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep/Ram, plus Fiat since 2012) is one of the friendlier makes to originate keys on IF you respect two facts: nearly everything is immobilizer-protected by a PIN (the \"SKIM code\"), and from 2018 on there's a Secure Gateway (SGW) firewall you must get past before your tool even talks to the car. Blades stayed edge-cut for decades \u2014 no laser/sidewinder here except Fiat's SIP22 \u2014 so getting the cuts is easy; the security is the work.\n\nBLADE TYPE, all eras: standard EDGE-CUT (double-sided) on the Chrysler family. No milled/sidewinder, no Tibbe. The workhorse keyway is CY24. Ilco Y157/Y159/Y160/Y164/Y170, Silca CY24, JMA CHR-15/CHR-16 all ride this same blade \u2014 the different Ilco numbers are mostly chip/head/plastic variations, not different cuts (Y159 is the common test key; 7 cuts). Lishi LI-CY24 (2-in-1 pick/decoder) is THE tool for the door or ignition on the whole edge-cut line. Fiat 500 / Ram ProMaster break from this with the SIP22 laser/internal-cut blade (older Fiat used GT15R) \u2014 Lishi SIP22 for those.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blade \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path):\n- 1998\u20132004 (SKIM, Sentry Key): Ilco Y157/Y159/Y160, Silca CY24. Chip 4D-64 (Texas \"T8\"). PIN via OBD on most; first-gen SKIMs often need an EEPROM read of the module. Add/AKL by OBD once you have the PIN.\n- 2005\u20132007 (SKREEM): Y159/Y160, CY24. Chip 4D-64. Modern tools pull the PIN over OBD; a few stubborn SKREEMs still want a module read.\n- 2008\u20132015 (Fobik / WIN): \"fobik\" remote-head that plugs into the WIN (Wireless Ignition Node) slot. Emergency blade Y160/Y164, CY24. Chip ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7941/7961). OBD PIN + OBD program.\n- 2013+ (Proximity / RFH): true push-button smart fob, RFH (Radio Frequency Hub) runs the immobilizer. Chip PCF7953/7953A Hitag2, newest = Hitag-AES/ID4A. OBD.\n- 2018+ (Secure Gateway): everything above PLUS you must bypass SGW (12+8 cable / Star connector / AutoAuth) before programming. PIN still required.\n- Fiat 500 / 500L / Ram ProMaster 2012\u20132020: SIP22 laser blade (older Fiat GT15R), chip ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7946; some earlier Fiat PCF7936), Marelli/Delphi BSI. Programs on OBD like the CDJ line but a different menu.\n\nExact blank+chip flexes by VIN and trim \u2014 always confirm on a transponder lookup before you cut.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 SKIM, 1998\u20132004 (300M, Concorde, PT Cruiser, Sebring, Town & Country, Caravan, Durango, Intrepid, Neon, Ram, Grand Cherokee, Liberty, Wrangler). System is SKIM (Sentry Key Immobilizer Module / SKIS). The transponder is 4D-64 (Texas T8) in a Y159/Y160 blade (Silca CY24) \u2014 that chip is the whole line, don't chase a \"4C\" story here. You need the 4-digit PIN to add a key, and the SKIM always holds a secret. On first-gen SKIMs the PIN isn't reliably broadcast over OBD, so the dependable AKL path is an EEPROM read of the SKIM module (Autel IM608, Zed-Full, or Tango reading the 93-series eeprom; clip or unsolder). Get IN and get CUTS: Lishi CY24 on the door or the ignition, decode, cut a blade by hand. AKL flow: pick/decode with Lishi \u2192 cut mechanical blade \u2192 read PIN (OBD if the tool supports that year, else EEPROM) \u2192 program transponder by OBD.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 SKREEM, 2005\u20132007. SKREEM = Sentry Key Remote Entry Module (immobilizer + RKE combined). Still 4D-64, still Y159/Y160 / CY24 edge-cut. Good news: a modern tool (Autel IM508/IM608, Smart Pro) pulls the PIN over OBD on most SKREEMs \u2014 some techs even use a cheap Chrysler PIN-puller app on an ELM327. Bad news: a handful of SKREEMs (and any first-gen SKIM carryover) still refuse OBD and want a direct module/EEPROM read, so keep a clip and an XP400 handy. AKL: Lishi CY24 to make a mechanical key that turns the lock, then OBD PIN read (or module read) + OBD key add.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 FOBIK / WIN, roughly 2008\u20132015 (300, Charger, Challenger, Durango, Grand Cherokee, Wrangler, Journey, Caravan, Ram). This is where the \"fobik\" appears: an integrated remote-head key that PLUGS into a slot in the WIN (Wireless Ignition Node) instead of a turned ignition. There's still an emergency edge-cut blade (Y160/Y164, CY24) hidden in the fobik for the door \u2014 and you still cut it with a Lishi CY24 on the door lock. Chip is now ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7941/7961 family). PIN reads over OBD on most of these. AKL flow: Lishi CY24 the driver door \u2192 decode \u2192 cut the emergency blade \u2192 OBD PIN read \u2192 program the fobik(s) by OBD. FCC IDs you'll see: IYZ-C01C (fobik), OHT692427AA / OHT692713AA (remote-head keys on the sedans).\n\nERA 4 \u2014 PROXIMITY / RFH, 2013+ overlapping into current. True push-to-start smart fobs, immobilizer handled by the RFH (Radio Frequency Hub), which can be hidden almost anywhere. Chip is PCF7953/7953A Hitag2, newest platforms move to Hitag-AES/ID4A. Emergency blade still edge-cut CY24 in most fobs (Lishi CY24 still your way into the door). Common prox FCC IDs: M3N-40821302, GQ4-53T, GQ4-54T, GQ4-76T. AKL here can require putting the RFH into a learn state and, on some, an EEPROM/renew of the RFH if all fobs are truly gone \u2014 Autel IM608 Pro or Smart Pro handle most; a few need the module on the bench.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 SECURE GATEWAY (SGW), 2018+. FCA dropped a Secure Gateway module (the \"star connector\") in the network that blocks aftermarket tools from writing to modules. Nothing about the blade or chip changed \u2014 it's a firewall. Three real options: (1) a 12+8 bypass cable (Autel/Lonsdor) that plumbs you in behind the gateway, (2) unplug the star connector and jump it, or (3) AutoAuth \u2014 register at autoauth.com ($50/yr per shop) so your registered tool authenticates through FCA's server. IMPORTANT: AutoAuth only authorizes DIAGNOSTICS (bidirectional/clear-codes); it does NOT hand you the immobilizer PIN. So SGW cars = bypass FIRST, then read the PIN with your key tool (OBD or module), then program.\n\nTHE ALTERNATIVES MENU (getting IN and getting CUTS, Chrysler-specific):\n- Lishi LI-CY24: picks and decodes the door or the ignition on the entire edge-cut line \u2014 your first reach on almost every CDJ/Ram job. SIP22 Lishi for Fiat/ProMaster.\n- Pull the passenger door or boot lock and bench-decode it if the driver lock is worn/paint-packed \u2014 CY24 wafers read clean on the bench.\n- Code service / NASTF: Stellantis \"locked-vehicle\" key CUT codes come through NASTF SDRS so you can cut to code instead of picking (needs LSID credentials). Note this gives you the mechanical bitting \u2014 FCA restricted the immobilizer PIN itself to dealers on newer platforms, so don't expect a PIN from the code service; pull the PIN off the car.\n- PIN read: OBD on most 2005+ (SKREEM/WIN/RFH); EEPROM/module read on stubborn pre-2005 SKIMs and the odd SKREEM.\n- Impression works on CY24 but it's slow \u2014 Lishi or code service beats it every time here.\n\nTOOLS THAT ACTUALLY DO IT: Autel IM508/IM608 Pro (best all-round for PIN read + program + EEPROM), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus / Smart Pro (AD), Zed-Full, Autopropad, Lonsdor. For SGW: Autel 12+8 cable or AutoAuth account. For EEPROM AKL: Autel XP400, Tango, or the module clip. Lishi CY24 and SIP22, a manual key machine for edge cuts (or laser machine for SIP22), and blank stock in Y159/Y160/Y164/Y170 and SIP22.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST, generic Chrysler flow: 1) ID the exact system by VIN (SKIM vs SKREEM vs WIN vs RFH, and SGW yes/no). 2) Get in: Lishi CY24 (SIP22 on Fiat) on the door. 3) Get cuts: decode the lock or pull a cut code via NASTF; cut the mechanical/emergency blade. 4) On SGW cars, install the 12+8 bypass or authenticate via AutoAuth. 5) Read the PIN: OBD for most 2005+, EEPROM/module read for stubborn pre-2005. 6) Program the transponder/fob(s) to the immobilizer by OBD. 7) Test start and lock/unlock, then program a second key so the customer isn't AKL again.\n\nBlank and chip fitment varies by exact VIN and build date \u2014 always confirm the precise Ilco/Silca blank and chip family on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or Ilco/Silca reference) for the specific VIN before you cut and program.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-chrysler|Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep/Ram (Stellantis) 5-button \"tombstone\" smart proximity fob (FCC M3N-40821302) \u2014 the fob locksmiths see most across Grand Cherokee, Durango, Charger, 300 and Ram.]]\n\nFind your nameplate, then your year band. Bands are already merged across generations that key the same way \u2014 a new row means the blade, blank, chip, or programming path actually changed. All edge-cut CDJ keys ride the CY24 keyway \u2192 Lishi CY24 to decode/cut; code cuts come off the VIN through NASTF/your code service. 4D-64 (era 1\u20132) can be crypto-cloned for a spare with a working key (Keyline 884 / Ilco / TRS-5000 sniff clone); ID46 Hitag2 and all prox are not clone-friendly \u2014 treat them as OBD-only. \"Confirm on lookup\" means verify on Transpondery/UHS before you cut.\n\nJEEP WRANGLER\n\u2022  TJ (1997\u20132006) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 (Silca CY24) \u2192 4D-64 (Texas T8) *SKIM was optional \u2014 grey head = Sentry, black head = no chip; non-chip TJs just cut a mechanical Y159* \u2192 cut by code or Lishi CY24; add-a-key clone the 4D-64 off a working key; AKL = OBD PIN, first-gen SKIM often wants an EEPROM read of the module.\n\u2022  JK (2007\u20132018) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y160 / Y164 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (conventional bladed transponder + separate/integrated remote \u2014 *not* a WIN fobik) \u2192 OBD PIN + OBD program; Lishi CY24 to decode/cut; no clone.\n\u2022  JL (2018\u2013present) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head, CY24 \u2192 Y164 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 std, passive-entry prox optional on upper trims \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW bypass (12+8 / AutoAuth).\n\nJEEP GRAND CHEROKEE\n\u2022  WJ (1999\u20132004) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 \u2192 4D-64 \u2192 cut by code/Lishi CY24; clone for spare; AKL = OBD PIN or SKIM EEPROM read.\n\u2022  WK1 (2005\u20132010) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head, CY24 \u2192 Y160 \u2192 4D-64 (05\u201307) \u2192 transitions to ID46 Hitag2 (08\u201310) \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 OBD PIN + program; kept a bladed ignition the whole gen (no fobik here).\n\u2022  WK2 (2011\u20132021) \u2192 fobik/WIN emergency blade Y160, CY24; upper trims push-button prox/RFH \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (fobik) / PCF7953 Hitag2 (prox) \u2192 OBD PIN + program; 2018+ requires SGW bypass.\n\u2022  WL (2021\u2013present) \u2192 smart prox only \u2192 Hitag-AES (ID4A) \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 OBD through SGW.\n\nJEEP CHEROKEE & LIBERTY\n\u2022  Cherokee XJ (1997\u20132001) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 \u2192 4D-64 \u2192 code/Lishi CY24; clone for spare; AKL OBD PIN or SKIM EEPROM.\n\u2022  Liberty KJ / KK (2002\u20132012) \u2192 edge-cut bladed transponder, CY24 \u2192 Y160 / Y164 \u2192 4D-64 (02\u201307) \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (08\u201312) \u2192 OBD PIN + program; Lishi CY24; no clone on ID46.\n\u2022  Cherokee KL (2014\u20132023) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head (base) / push-button prox (upper), CY24 \u2192 Y164 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 / PCF7953 prox \u2192 OBD PIN + program; 2018+ SGW bypass required.\n\nJEEP COMPASS & PATRIOT\n\u2022  Compass / Patriot MK (2007\u20132017) \u2192 edge-cut bladed transponder, CY24 \u2192 Y160 / Y164 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (conventional key, not fobik) \u2192 OBD PIN + program; Lishi CY24 to decode/cut; no clone.\n\u2022  Compass MP (2017\u2013present) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head / push-button prox, CY24 \u2192 Y164 \u2192 ID46 / PCF7953 prox \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW (2018+).\n\nRAM / DODGE RAM 1500\u20133500 (PICKUP)\n\u2022  BR/BE + DR/DH (1998\u20132008) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 \u2192 4D-64 *(note: 2nd-gen BR/BE trucks were largely non-immobilizer; Sentry Key/4D-64 came in with the 3rd-gen DR in 2002+ \u2014 confirm on lookup, early trucks may just cut a mechanical Y159)* \u2192 cut by code/Lishi CY24; clone for spare; AKL = OBD PIN, early SKIM may need module EEPROM.\n\u2022  DS / DJ (2009\u20132018) *(same key on the 2019+ Ram Classic body)* \u2192 conventional edge-cut bladed remote-head transponder \u2014 turn-key ignition, NOT a WIN fobik, CY24 \u2192 Y160 / Y164 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7941/7961) \u2192 OBD PIN + OBD program; Lishi CY24 to decode/cut; no clone. *(Upper trims 2013+ may add push-button prox \u2014 confirm on lookup.)*\n\u2022  DT (2019\u2013present) \u2192 smart prox/RFH, push-button \u2192 PCF7953 Hitag2 / newest Hitag-AES \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW bypass.\n\nDODGE GRAND CARAVAN & CHRYSLER TOWN & COUNTRY (+ PACIFICA)\n\u2022  (1996\u20132000) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y157/Y159 \u2192 transponder from ~1998 only: 4D-64; 1996\u20131997 are non-chip (mechanical Y157) \u2192 code/Lishi CY24; clone spare (chipped yrs); AKL OBD PIN / SKIM EEPROM.\n\u2022  (2001\u20132007) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 \u2192 4D-64 \u2192 OBD PIN + program.\n\u2022  Grand Caravan / T&C RT (2008\u20132020) \u2192 fobik/WIN, emergency blade Y160, CY24 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD PIN + program; no clone. *(2018+ Grand Caravan may need SGW \u2014 confirm on lookup.)*\n\u2022  Chrysler Pacifica (2017\u2013present) \u2192 smart prox/RFH \u2192 PCF7953 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW bypass.\n\nCHRYSLER 300 / DODGE CHARGER / DODGE MAGNUM (LX/LD)\n\u2022  LX (2005\u20132007) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head key (FCC KOBDT04A/OHT692427AA \u2014 confirm the exact FCC on lookup), CY24 \u2192 Y160 \u2192 4D-64 (SKREEM) \u2192 OBD PIN + program; clone 4D-64 for a spare; Lishi CY24.\n\u2022  LX (2008\u20132010) \u2192 fobik/WIN, emergency blade Y160, CY24 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD PIN + program; no clone. *(Magnum ends 2008.)*\n\u2022  300 LD / Charger (2011\u20132014) \u2192 fobik/WIN or push-button prox \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 / PCF7953 prox \u2192 OBD PIN + program.\n\u2022  (2015\u2013present) \u2192 smart prox/RFH standard \u2192 PCF7953 \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW (2018+).\n\nDODGE CHALLENGER\n\u2022  (2008\u20132010) \u2192 fobik/WIN, emergency blade Y160, CY24 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD PIN + program; no clone.\n\u2022  (2011\u20132014) \u2192 fobik or push-button prox \u2192 ID46 / PCF7953 \u2192 OBD PIN + program.\n\u2022  (2015\u20132023) \u2192 smart prox/RFH \u2192 PCF7953 \u2192 OBD PIN + program through SGW (2018+).\n\nDODGE DURANGO\n\u2022  1st gen (1998\u20132003) \u2192 edge-cut, CY24 \u2192 Y159/Y160 \u2192 4D-64 \u2192 code/Lishi CY24; clone spare; AKL OBD PIN / SKIM EEPROM.\n\u2022  2nd gen (2004\u20132009) \u2192 edge-cut remote-head, CY24 \u2192 Y160 \u2192 4D-64 (SKREEM) \u2192 OBD PIN + program; clone for spare.\n\u2022  3rd gen WD (2011\u2013present) \u2192 fobik/WIN Y160 (11\u201313) \u2192 push-button prox/RFH \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 / PCF7953 \u2192 OBD PIN + program; 2018+ SGW bypass required.\n\nDODGE JOURNEY / AVENGER & CHRYSLER SEBRING / 200\n\u2022  Sebring (2007\u20132010), Avenger (2008\u20132014), Chrysler 200 1st gen (2011\u20132014), Journey (2009\u20132020) \u2192 fobik/WIN, emergency blade Y159/Y160, CY24 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 *(the fobik emergency insert is a standard CY24 Y159/Y160 \u2014 verify the exact blade on lookup)* \u2192 OBD PIN + OBD program; no clone.\n\u2022  Chrysler 200 2nd gen (2015\u20132017) \u2192 smart push-button prox/RFH \u2192 PCF7953 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD PIN + program (ends 2017, pre-SGW).\n\nDODGE DART\n\u2022  (2013\u20132016) \u2192 conventional edge-cut bladed remote-head transponder \u2014 twist-key ignition, NOT a WIN fobik, emergency/blade Y159, CY24; upper trims push-button prox \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7941A) / PCF7953 prox \u2192 OBD PIN + OBD program; cut blade by code/Lishi CY24; no clone.\n\nFIAT 500 / 500L / 500X (+ RAM PROMASTER)\n\u2022  500 / 500L (2012\u20132019, US) \u2192 laser/sidewinder, SIP22 (older Fiat = GT15R) \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7946; some early Fiat PCF7936) \u2192 laser-cut by code or decode with Lishi SIP22; OBD program via the Fiat/Marelli-Delphi BSI menu (not the CDJ SKIM menu); no clone.\n\u2022  500X / ProMaster (2015\u20132020) \u2192 SIP22 laser blade \u2192 ID46 Hitag2, some push-button prox \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 Lishi SIP22 / code cut; OBD program; 2018+ may gate through SGW.",sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/k-y160-transponder-key-chrysler-dodge-jeep-y160","https://www.bestkeysolution.com/products/chrysler-dodge-jeep-y160-pt-transponder-key-blank-chip-id-4d-64","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/chrysler_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/4d64-transponder-chip","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2004-2019-chrysler-dodge-jeep-transponder-key-y164-chip-46-k-y164","https://www.bestkeysolution.com/products/chrysler-dodge-jeep-y164-pt-key-blank-chip-id46","https://www.car-keys-online.com/aftermarket-transponder-key-for-jeep-chrysler-fobik-systems-y160-id46.html","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10229399/chrysler-skim-pin-code-retrieval-tool","https://www.key-programmer.com/2024/07/04/autel-im608-read-2005-chrysler-crossfire-pin-from-skreem/","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GXGMF99y4aY","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21215988/opening-the-fca-security-gateway","http://blog.obdii365.com/2020/01/09/sgm-solution-for-chrysler-using-autel-fca-128-cable/","https://autel.us/secure-gateway-access/","https://www.alldata.com/us/en/support/diagnostics/article/fca-secure-gateway","https://maxtec1.com/key-fob-programming/","https://www.mk3.com/chrysler-jeep-dodge-smart-remote-key-5buttons-433mhz-pcf7953a-m3n-40821302","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/smart-key-unlocking-service-for-dodge-chrysler-jeep-fccid-m3n-40821302-m3m-40821302-gq4-54t","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2012-2018-fiat-500-ram-promaster-sip22-transponder-key-46-chip-k-sip22","https://abkeys.com/products/fiat-transponder-key-pcf7936-chip-id46-sip22-71776117-71749671-1304","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2012-2018-fiat-500-ram-promaster-transponder-key-id46-sip22"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Immobilizer","Bypass","Transponder","Fob","Add-a-key","Push-to-start","Decoder"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","read the cuts","reading tool","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list"]},{id:"autokeys-toyota",title:"Making keys: Toyota, Lexus & Scion",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"TOYOTA, LEXUS & SCION \u2014 MAKING KEYS FROM SCRATCH\n\nToyota is one of the friendlier makes to originate once you know which era you're standing in front of. Every bladed Toyota is EDGE-CUT (mechanical wafer blade) \u2014 no laser/sidewinder, no Tibbe, no high-security milling. The blade profile barely changes for 25 years; what changes is the CHIP and how you program it. Nail the era, pick the right blank and chip, pick the right programming path. Read the VIN, but let the year and whether it's a bladed key or a smart (proximity) fob tell you the story. Note 2010 and 2013 are split years \u2014 same model can be one chip early in the run and the next chip late, so decode the head stamp or run the VIN.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 NO CHIP (mid-1990s and older). Straight mechanical, no immobilizer. Blank Ilco TOY43 / TOY40 family. Cut it and it starts. AKL: pull the door or ignition, decode with Lishi TOY43, or read the wafers on the bench, cut a key, done. No electronics.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 4C (~1997\u20132004, early \"chip\" Toyota). Fixed-code Texas 4C transponder. Blank TOY43AT4 (Ilco), 4C glass or aftermarket cloneable chip. Corolla, Camry, Tacoma, 4Runner, Avalon, early Highlander/Sequoia, Sienna. 4C is cloneable \u2014 clone an existing key onto a JMA TPX or Super Chip and skip onboard programming entirely. AKL/add-key onboard: Toyota's 2-master-key self-programming works if the customer has two masters; with one or none you cut a key, drop in a 4C, and onboard-register or clone. Easiest Toyota era.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 4D-67 \"Dot\" chip (~2003\u20132013 depending on model). Crypto DST40, Texas 4D-67. Blank TOY44D / TOY44D-PT, keyway TR47. Camry, Corolla, Tacoma, Tundra, Sienna, RAV4, Matrix, Yaris, Venza, plus Scion tC/xB/xA. Not cloneable the old way \u2014 it's crypto, so it needs the immobilizer's onboard registration or a diagnostic tool. Add key: two-master onboard programming still works on many. AKL: you need an onboard-reset-capable programmer or Techstream to reset the immobilizer and register the first key. Techstream (MINI-VCI cable) + the immobilizer reset path is the clean route. OBDSTAR F101 and similar also do 4D67 immo reset AKL.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 G chip / 4D-72 (~2010\u20132019, DST80, 80-bit, \"G\" stamp on the head). Blank TOY44G / TOY44G-PT, keyway TR47. This is where the 16-MINUTE lockout appears. G keys can NOT self-program with two masters the old way and can NOT be cloned to a bench chip \u2014 the immobilizer time-delays registration. Add-key with a working master: a G-and-H OBD dongle or Techstream writes the new key with a short handshake. AKL on G: the immobilizer requires a RESET; after you authorize the reset (Techstream / seed-pass-code) the box enforces a ~16-minute security wait with ignition ON and all doors closed before it accepts the first new key. Cut the blade FIRST (Lishi or code) \u2014 you can't burn the 16 minutes hunting for a cut key. Some aftermarket boxes (e.g. Lonsdor K518, CN900 with Toyo adapter) can bypass or shortcut the 16-minute wait; verify by exact model.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 H chip / DST-AES 128-bit, 4D-74 (ID 8A) (~2013\u20132020, \"H\" stamp). Bladed keys on trucks/vans/cars that kept a mechanical ignition (Tacoma, Tundra, Sienna, some Corolla/RAV4/Yaris, 2020 4Runner) into the late 2010s \u2014 i.e. models WITHOUT push-button start. Blank TOY44H; chip TEX 4D-74, 128-bit DST-AES. Same story as G but stronger crypto: no cloning, tool + reset only. Same ~16-minute AKL reset delay. Techstream or a capable aftermarket (Autel IM508/IM608, Smart Pro, AutoProPad, XTOOL, OBDSTAR F101) handle both G and H add-key and AKL reset.\n\nERA 6 \u2014 SMART KEY / PROXIMITY (push-button start, ~2010+ and now dominant). No blade in the ignition \u2014 the fob talks to the Smart (Certification ECU / \"Smart Box\"). There's still an emergency mechanical blade inside the fob and a hidden door lock, and that emergency blade is your way IN and your Lishi decode target for AKL. Programming runs through the Smart Box: add-key with a working smart key is quick. ALL SMART KEYS LOST requires the Smart Code RESET. On Techstream/GTS this is the \"Smart Code Reset\": it needs current Techstream software, erases ALL currently registered smart keys PERMANENTLY (they can never be reused on any car), generates a seed \u2192 you get a pass-code, enter it, then the system runs its security wait before registering the new fobs. On 2020+ Toyota/Lexus this tightened hard: the reset/seed now requires Toyota's TIS \"Security Professional\" subscription AND a Toyota employee login to authorize \u2014 effectively dealer/online-only on the newest models. Aftermarket AKL smart programming (Autel IM608 + APB112 + G-Box, Smart Pro) works on a wide swath of older smart systems but check coverage by exact model/year before you quote.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS \u2014 the real menu for this make:\n- LISHI is king here. TOY43 (8-cut, ignition) for older bladed Toyota. TOY48 is the workhorse 2-in-1 pick/decoder for most Toyota/Lexus/Scion (10-cut, cross-refs TOY50/TOY51) \u2014 covers ES/GS/IS/LS/RX Lexus and countless Toyota door, trunk, and ignition. TOY51 and TOY40 on other profiles. Pick and DECODE the lock, read the cuts off the Lishi gauge, cut a blade to code. That single decode gives you the mechanical key without touching the immobilizer.\n- On smart-key cars, Lishi the DRIVER DOOR (emergency lock) or bench-decode the pulled door lock to cut the emergency blade \u2014 you need that blade to get in and as a mechanical backup even though the ignition is push-button.\n- CODE SERVICE / DEALER: Toyota supports key codes. Pull the code by VIN from a locksmith code service or the dealer and cut directly \u2014 often faster than picking. (Toyota is not a plain NASTF SDRS programming source the way some makes are; immobilizer/smart auth runs through Toyota's own TIS.) Some cylinders carry a code stamp.\n- IMPRESSIONING and try-out/jiggler sets work on the older 4C/4D bladed wafers if you can't pick.\n- EEPROM / on-bench: for stubborn AKL you can pull the immobilizer/Smart Box and read EEPROM to extract data or reset, but for Toyota the Techstream reset path is usually cleaner than soldering.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY: Lishi TOY43 / TOY48 / TOY51 / TOY40; a code cutter or duplicator (Framon/Ilco); Techstream software + MINI-VCI (or genuine GTS+VIM) for the factory reset path; a capable aftermarket programmer (Autel IM508/IM608 + APB112 + G-Box for smart AKL, Xhorse Key Tool Plus, Smart Pro, AutoProPad); G-and-H OBD dongle for quick G/H add-key; blank stock TOY43AT4, TOY44D, TOY44G, TOY44H; 4C and cloneable chips for the old stuff; blank smart fobs / emergency blades per model.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / AKL QUICK STEPS: (1) ID era by year + bladed-vs-smart (watch 2010/2013 split years). (2) Get in: Lishi door or pull-and-decode. (3) Get the cut: Lishi decode, code service, or impression \u2192 cut blade/emergency blade. (4) Bladed 4C/4D: onboard or tool register; G/H: tool add-key with master, or reset + ~16-min wait for AKL. (5) Smart: add with working fob, or Smart Code Reset (erases all) + security wait, Toyota-login/online auth on 2020+ models.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path):\n- No-chip (\u22641996): TOY43/TOY40 \u2192 none \u2192 cut & go.\n- 4C (~1997\u20132004): TOY43AT4 \u2192 4C fixed \u2192 clone or onboard 2-master.\n- 4D-67 Dot (~2003\u20132013): TOY44D (TR47) \u2192 4D-67 DST40 \u2192 onboard 2-master or Techstream/tool.\n- G (~2010\u20132019): TOY44G (TR47, \"G\") \u2192 4D-72 DST80 \u2192 G/H dongle add-key; AKL = reset + 16-min.\n- H (~2013\u20132020): TOY44H (\"H\") \u2192 4D-74 DST-AES 128 (ID 8A) \u2192 tool add-key; AKL = reset + 16-min.\n- Smart (~2010+): emergency blade + smart fob \u2192 Smart Box \u2192 add w/ master, or Smart Code Reset (erases all) + wait; 2020+ needs Toyota TIS Security Professional + employee login.\n\nFitment varies by exact VIN \u2014 confirm the precise blank + chip/FCC and programming coverage on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, Ilco/JMA cross-reference) before you cut or quote.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-toyota|Toyota 4-button HYQ14FBA smart fob with its TOY44 emergency insert blade \u2014 the push-to-start key most modern Toyota, Lexus & Scion jobs land on.]]\n\nFind your nameplate, drop to the year band, read across: blade/keyway \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 how you originate it. Ranges are wrapped so consecutive generations that key the same way sit in one row; a new row means the blade, blank, chip, or path actually changed. Cross-check the exact VIN before you cut \u2014 trim and build month move these lines by a year.\n\nTOYOTA CAMRY (SOLARA TRACKS IT; AVALON SPLITS \u2014 SEE NOTE)\n\u2022  1997\u20132003 (XV20 / early XV30) \u2192 edge-cut, TOY43 keyway (Lishi TOY43, 8-cut) \u2192 TOY43AT4 \u2192 4C fixed \u2192 clone the 4C (glass/carbon transponder) or onboard 2-master add; AKL = decode/impression with Lishi TOY43, cut to code, then onboard reset.\n\u2022  2004\u20132011 (late XV30 / XV40) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D (Ilco TR47 / X217) \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master add; AKL = Techstream or aftermarket tool reset (external immobox on some \u2014 confirm). *2004 is the 4C\u2192dot split.* Smart key becomes a factory option on higher trims from ~2007 \u2014 see Smart note.\n\u2022  2012\u20132017 (XV50) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44G \u2192 4D-72 DST80 (G) \u2192 G-dongle / tool add-key over OBD; AKL = reset + 16-min. Smart optional (H) on many.\n\u2022  2018\u20132024 (XV70) \u2192 base edge-cut TOY44H \u2192 4D-74 DST-AES, ID 8A; most trims smart \u2192 tool add-key over OBD; AKL = reset + 16-min.\n\u2022  *Avalon note:* Avalon keys like a Camry, not a Lexus. Through the XX30 car (2005\u20132012) it's edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D dot (4D-67), same as Camry \u2014 not a laser TOY48. Smart becomes standard on the 2013+ (XX40) car, but with an edge-cut emergency blade, still not a laser TOY48. Don't grab a TOY48 Lishi for an Avalon.\n\nTOYOTA COROLLA (MATRIX TRACKS IT)\n\u2022  1998\u20132004 (E110 / early E120) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 no chip on US base \u2192 cut & go (Lishi TOY43 or code). Some late/optioned cars add 4C \u2014 confirm on lookup.\n\u2022  2005\u20132013 (late E120/E130 / E140) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D (TR47) \u2192 4D-67 dot *(US Corolla ran non-transponder well into the late 2000s \u2014 dot didn't go universal until ~2009 E140; confirm chip vs. no-chip on early cars)* \u2192 onboard 2-master add; AKL = reset, or external immobox reset on immobox-equipped cars (two US variants exist: external immobox vs. ECU-integrated \u2014 confirm). *G-chip creeps in on the late end (~2011\u20132013) \u2014 confirm G vs. dot before you cut.*\n\u2022  2014\u20132019 (E170) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 (H on late build \u2014 confirm) \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min. Smart optional.\n\u2022  2020\u20132024 (E210) \u2192 TOY44H / smart standard on most trims \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\nTOYOTA RAV4\n\u2022  1996\u20132000 (XA10) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 early no chip / 4C \u2014 confirm \u2192 cut & go or clone/onboard.\n\u2022  2001\u20132005 (XA20) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY43AT4 (4C) early, TOY44D dot (4D-67) from ~2004 \u2192 clone/onboard, or 2-master add on dot cars.\n\u2022  2006\u20132012 (XA30) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master; AKL reset/immobox.\n\u2022  2013\u20132018 (XA40) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min. Smart optional (H).\n\u2022  2019\u20132024 (XA50) \u2192 TOY44H / smart standard most trims \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\nTOYOTA TACOMA\n\u2022  1995\u20132004 (1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 early no chip, 4C mid-run, 4D-67 dot 2003\u20132004 \u2192 cut & go / clone / onboard per year \u2014 confirm the split on lookup.\n\u2022  2005\u20132009 (2nd gen, early) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master.\n\u2022  2010\u20132015 (2nd gen, late) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min. Smart optional on top trims.\n\u2022  2016\u20132023 (3rd gen) \u2192 base edge-cut TOY44H, smart standard on many \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\nTOYOTA TUNDRA\n\u2022  2000\u20132006 (1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 4C then 4D-67 dot (2004+) \u2192 clone/onboard, or 2-master add on dot.\n\u2022  2007\u20132009 (2nd gen, early) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master.\n\u2022  2010\u20132021 (2nd gen, late/facelift) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 (H on later build \u2014 confirm) \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min. Smart optional.\n\u2022  2022\u20132024 (3rd gen) \u2192 smart standard \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min (2020+ smart security applies \u2014 see Smart note).\n\nTOYOTA HIGHLANDER\n\u2022  2001\u20132007 (XU20) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 4C then 4D-67 dot (2004+) \u2192 clone/onboard or 2-master.\n\u2022  2008\u20132013 (XU40) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master. Smart optional on Limited from ~2008 (Smart note).\n\u2022  2014\u20132019 (XU50) \u2192 smart standard on most; base edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 \u2192 tool add-key; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\u2022  2020\u20132024 (XU70) \u2192 smart standard \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\nTOYOTA 4RUNNER\n\u2022  1996\u20132002 (3rd gen, N180) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 no chip (1996\u201397), 4C from ~1998 \u2192 cut & go on the no-chip early cars, clone or onboard 2-master on the 4C cars.\n\u2022  2003\u20132009 (4th gen, N210) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master.\n\u2022  2010\u20132024 (5th gen, N280) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80, TOY44H / 8A on late build \u2192 tool add-key OBD; AKL reset + 16-min. *4Runner kept a bladed key unusually long;* smart appears only on late trims \u2014 confirm smart vs. blade per VIN.\n\nTOYOTA SIENNA\n\u2022  1998\u20132003 (1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 4C \u2192 clone/onboard.\n\u2022  2004\u20132010 (2nd gen, XL20) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44D \u2192 4D-67 dot \u2192 onboard 2-master. Smart optional late.\n\u2022  2011\u20132020 (3rd gen, XL30) \u2192 edge-cut TOY44G \u2192 G / DST80 (H on late) \u2192 tool add-key; AKL reset + 16-min. Smart on higher trims.\n\u2022  2021\u20132024 (4th gen, XL40) \u2192 smart standard \u2192 8A \u2192 tool add-key; AKL reset + 16-min.\n\nTOYOTA PRIUS (PRIUS C IS BLADED G/H \u2014 SEE NOTE)\n\u2022  2001\u20132003 (1st gen, NHW11) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 4C \u2192 clone or onboard 2-master. *(Only bladed-ignition Prius.)*\n\u2022  2004\u20132015 (2nd/3rd gen, XW20/XW30) \u2192 smart standard (Prius pioneered it): emergency blade insert (edge-cut \u2014 Lishi TOY43/TOY44 on the door cylinder, NOT the laser TOY48) + smart fob \u2192 Smart Box \u2192 add with a working master, or Smart Code Reset (erases all fobs) then re-add and wait.\n\u2022  2016\u20132022 (4th gen, XW50) \u2192 smart, 8A \u2192 add with master or reset-all; 2020+ requires Toyota TIS Security Professional + employee login to originate AKL.\n\u2022  *Prius c (2012\u20132019):* bladed edge-cut TOY44G/H, smart optional \u2014 key it like a Corolla, not like a Prius liftback.\n\nLEXUS RX\n\u2022  1999\u20132003 (XU10, RX300) \u2192 laser/sidewinder TOY48 (Lishi TOY48, 2-track) \u2192 4C \u2192 clone or onboard 2-master.\n\u2022  2004\u20132009 (XU30, RX330/350) \u2192 laser TOY48 \u2192 4D-68 dot *(Lexus dot is 4D-68, not the Toyota -67 \u2014 confirm)* \u2192 onboard 2-master / tool; smart offered on some late trims (Smart note).\n\u2022  2010\u20132015 (AL10, RX350/450h) \u2192 smart standard: emergency blade TOY48 + smart fob \u2192 Smart Box \u2192 add with master or Smart Code Reset + wait.\n\u2022  2016\u20132022 (AL20) \u2192 smart, 8A \u2192 add with master or reset-all; 2020+ TIS Security Professional required.\n\nLexus ES (rear-drive IS/GS/LS run the same laser-TOY48 \u2192 smart progression \u2014 confirm year)\n\u2022  1997\u20132001 (XV20, ES300) \u2192 laser TOY48 \u2192 4C \u2192 clone/onboard 2-master.\n\u2022  2002\u20132006 (XV30, ES300/330) \u2192 laser TOY48 \u2192 4D-68 dot *(confirm -68)* \u2192 onboard 2-master / tool.\n\u2022  2007\u20132012 (XV40) \u2192 smart standard: emergency blade TOY48 + smart fob \u2192 Smart Box \u2192 add with master or Smart Code Reset + wait.\n\u2022  2013\u20132024 (XV60 / XZ10) \u2192 smart, H / 8A \u2192 add with master or reset-all; 2019+/2020+ TIS Security Professional + employee login required.\n\nSCION \u2014 MOSTLY REBADGED TOYOTAS, BUT TWO OF THEM ARE NOT TOYOTA-KEYED\n\u2022  xA / xB (2004\u20132006, 1st gen) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 no chip on many, 4C on some \u2014 confirm per VIN \u2192 cut & go or clone/onboard.\n\u2022  tC (2005\u20132016), xB (2008\u20132015), xD (2008\u20132014), iQ (2012\u20132015) \u2192 edge-cut TOY43 \u2192 TOY44D dot (4D-67) early, TOY44G (G/DST80) later \u2192 onboard 2-master on dot; tool add-key + 16-min reset on G.\n\u2022  iM (2016) \u2192 Corolla-based: TOY44G/H, treat as 2016 Corolla.\n\u2022  FR-S (2013\u20132016) \u2192 NOT a Toyota key. Subaru BRZ/86 platform \u2014 Subaru laser keyway + Subaru transponder; originate as a Subaru/86, not off any TOY blank.\n\u2022  iA (2016) \u2192 NOT a Toyota key. Mazda2 platform \u2014 Mazda blank + Mazda chip; originate as a Mazda2.\n\n*Recurring split years to watch: 2004 (4C\u21924D-67 dot across the line), ~2010 (dot\u2192G/DST80 on trucks & mid-size), ~2015\u20132018 (G\u2192H / DST-AES 8A), and 2020 (smart AKL locks behind Toyota TIS Security Professional + employee login). Lexus laser TOY48 cars never take a TOY43 edge blank \u2014 but the Avalon is NOT one of them; it's edge-cut like a Camry. Don't grab the wrong Lishi.*",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/toyota_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2003-2013-toyota-toy44d-transponder-key-texas-id-4d-67-chip","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2010-2019-toyota-scion-toy44g-transponder-key-g-chip-k-toy44g","https://www.lockpicks.com/toy44gpt-transponder-key.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2013-2020-toyota-toy44h-transponder-key-h-chip-aftermarket","https://www.zippylocks.com/products/h-chip-for-toyota-vehicles","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/toyota-toy43-transponder-key-4c-chip-aftermarket","https://tomskey.com/products/dot-chip-transponder-key-for-select-toyota-and-scion-vehicles-toy4-dot","https://www.obd2tool.com/blog/toyota-h-chip-all-keys-lost-solution/","Obdstar f101 toyota pin code reading and key programming tool (uobdii.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-toyota-lexus-toy48-2-in-1-pick-decoder-quad-ag","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-toy43-ign","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2024/MC-10253225-9999.pdf","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2016/MC-10149214-9999.pdf","https://www.car-auto-repair.com/toyota-tis-techstream-all-keys-programming-for-toyota/","https://cartipsdaily.com/toyota-immobilizer-reset"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Onboard programming","Lishi","Fob","Immobilizer","Transponder","Duplicator","Progression","Keyway"],aliases:["OBP","no tool needed","key dance","onboard procedure","decoder pick","Lishi tool","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","proximity key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","reading tool","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-honda",title:"Making keys: Honda & Acura",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"MAKING KEYS: HONDA & ACURA\n\nHonda is one of the friendlier Japanese makes to originate for, but you have to know two things up front: which BLADE the VIN takes (edge-cut vs high-security), and which CHIP era you're in. Honda ran both blade styles side-by-side for years, and the same model year can go either way by trim. Look at the blade, then verify on a lookup. Never assume.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era -> blank -> chip/fob -> path). Exact fitment varies by VIN \u2014 confirm on Transpondery/UHS.\n- ~1991-1998 mechanical (no immo): Ilco HD106 (X172 keyway), 8-cut edge. No chip. Cut to code / decode, done.\n- ~1998-2006 edge-cut + immo: Ilco HD106-PT / HD111-PT. Chip: ID13 Megamos fixed (Honda \"F\" chip) essentially the whole run \u2014 do NOT expect ID48 here. Remote-head FCC G8D-380H-A, OUCG8D-380H-A. Program OBD.\n- ~2003+ Accord / 2006+ Civic / 2007+ CR-V / most Acura: HIGH-SECURITY. Ilco HO01 / HO03 (later HO05) 4-track blade. Chip: ID13 \"F\" on the earliest ('02-'06 HO01), then ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7936) ~2006-2013, then ID47 Hitag3 \u2014 the \"G\" chip (Ilco GTHT49C / HO05-PT(G)) \u2014 ~2013-2019. Remote-head FCC MLBHLIK-1T, N5F-A05TAA. Program OBD.\n- ~2013+ smart / push-to-start (widespread 2016+): prox fob, HON66 emergency insert blade. Chip ID47 Hitag3 / ID49 Hitag-Pro / AES. FCC ACJ932HK1210A, KR5V2X, KR5V1X, KR5T41, KR5TP-4. OBD add; AKL often needs i-HDS + online immobilizer registration or a bench read.\n\nBLADE \u2014 GET THIS RIGHT\nTwo families. The OLD one is the Ilco HD106 (X172 keyway), an 8-cut EDGE key \u2014 cuts on the two edges, standard duplicator or 8-cut code card. Honda ran this from the early '90s and it lived on economy trims well into the mid-2000s. The NEW one is the HON66 / Ilco HO01 (laser variants HO03, later HO05) \u2014 Honda/Acura HIGH-SECURITY, a 4-track \"worm-cut\" blade with cuts milled into the flats, positions 1-6. It is NOT edge-cut and will not go on a standard duplicator; you need a high-security machine to originate it. Accord jumped to it around 2003, Civic around 2006, CR-V around 2007, and essentially all Acura from 2007. Because both blades overlap the same years, the tell is the physical key: edges cut = HD106; smooth edges with dimpled/milled tracks = HON66. Honda never went TIBBE and never used a separate door vs ignition keyway \u2014 one key runs door, trunk/boot, and ignition, and door + ignition usually share the same bitting, which is why pulling and decoding a door lock originates the whole car.\n\nCHIP ERAS \u2014 the part the draft got wrong\nHonda was late to transponders and simple once they got there, but the trade nicknames trip people up. Immobilizer starts about 1998. The earliest immo keys through 2006 used the ID13 Megamos FIXED-code \u2014 that's the Honda \"F\" chip \u2014 on BOTH the HD106 edge-cut keys and the earliest HO01 high-security keys ('02-'06). There is no ID48 Honda immobilizer key; if a lookup or an old note says ID48, it's wrong for this make. From roughly 2006 the high-security cars moved to ID46 Hitag2 (Philips/NXP PCF7936) \u2014 call it the \"46\" chip, NOT the \"G\" chip. The \"G\" chip is ID47 Hitag3, which came in around 2013 on the newer remote-head high-security keys (Ilco GTHT49C \"Honda G\", blank HO05-PT(G)) and runs through about 2019. Push-to-start smart keys use ID47 Hitag3 or ID49 Hitag-Pro / AES. So the clean bladed timeline is: ID13 \"F\" (~1998-2006) -> ID46 Hitag2 (~2006-2013) -> ID47 \"G\" Hitag3 (~2013-2019). Loose chips or Xhorse/OEM-style keys cover the bladed side; for smart you buy the fob to FCC + part number (35118-Txx / 72147-Txx). Any specific VIN can surprise you \u2014 verify the chip on Transpondery or UHS before you cut and clip in a transponder.\n\nGETTING IN + GETTING THE CUTS \u2014 the real menu for Honda\n1) Original Lishi HON66 (2-in-1 / 3-in-1). This is the money tool for anything HO01/HO03/HO05 high-security \u2014 '02-'18 CR-V, '03-'19 Accord/Civic/Odyssey/Pilot, and the Acura line. It picks AND decodes the door, trunk, or ignition in one keyway; read positions 1-6, write the cuts down, cut a blank, walk to programming. On Honda the ignition picks clean, so you can decode straight off the ignition on an AKL.\n2) Old edge-cut (HD106) cars \u2014 there's no elegant single Lishi that everyone loves here, so the bread-and-butter is PULL THE DRIVER DOOR LOCK and bench-decode the 8 wafers, or single-lifter pick/impression. Because door and ignition share the code, the door decode gives you the ignition cuts. Impressioning an HD106 blank in the door is also very doable on these \u2014 Honda wafers give good marks.\n3) Code service / cut-to-code. Honda key codes can be pulled from the dealer (Honda iN) or a code service by VIN; then cut HD106 on an 8-cut code machine, or HO01 on a high-security machine from the code series. Good when you'd rather not disassemble.\n4) HON58R \u2014 the older 2-track door/trunk decoder (older Preludes, some JDM/motorcycle applications). Largely superseded and discontinued; you'll rarely reach for it, but know the name.\nSkip the \"Lishi every door\" nonsense \u2014 on Honda you decode ONE lock (usually the ignition on high-security, the door on edge-cut) and you have the whole car.\n\nCUTTING\n- HD106 edge: standard 8-cut. Duplicate off a decoded pattern or cut to code on a Framon/ITL/Blitz. Clip in the era-correct chip.\n- HON66 high-security: cut HO01/HO03/HO05 on a Triton, Ilco Futura Pro, Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus / Dolphin XP-005, or Keyline Ninja from the Lishi-decoded cuts. This is a milled blade \u2014 no manual duplicator.\n\nPROGRAMMING \u2014 ADD-A-KEY (you have a working key)\nHonda is one of the easiest OBD adds in the business. With one good key, an Autel IM508/IM608, Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro (Honda ADS module), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus/Max, XTool, or dealer i-HDS/HDS will add a bladed key or fob through the DLC in a couple minutes. Some model years want two keys present or a short relearn cycle. No PIN needed for most bladed adds.\n\nFROM SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 the procedure\nEdge-cut era (~1998-2006, HD106):\n1) Get in and decode: pull the driver door lock cylinder and bench-decode the 8 wafers (or Lishi/impression). Door code = ignition code.\n2) Cut an HD106-PT to the decoded bitting; the mechanical key now unlocks and turns.\n3) Chip it: clip in the ID13 Megamos \"F\" chip.\n4) Program the immobilizer: most of these do AKL by OBD with Autel/Smart Pro/MVP/i-HDS. If the box won't let you in, read the immobilizer/ECU EEPROM (93C46/56/66) on the bench with VVDI Prog / XProg / Autel XP400 to pull the code and make a dealer-level key.\n\nHigh-security / smart era (~2003+ and push-to-start):\n1) Get in and decode: Original Lishi HON66 on the ignition (or door) \u2014 pick and read cuts 1-6 in one shot. On smart cars, decode the door with the HON66 to cut the emergency insert, then get in.\n2) Cut HO01/HO03/HO05 on a high-security machine to the decoded cuts.\n3) Chip/fob: bladed ID46 (2006-2013) or ID47 \"G\" (2013-2019), or the correct smart fob by FCC (ACJ932HK1210A, KR5V2X, KR5V1X, KR5TP-4) + part number, HON66 insert fitted.\n4) Program AKL: bladed high-security cars do AKL by OBD with Autel IM508/608, Smart Pro, or Xhorse. Newer SMART Honda/Acura AKL is the tricky one \u2014 many need dealer i-HDS with a valid subscription plus Honda's ONLINE immobilizer registration (NASTF LSID / Honda iN), and some want an immobilizer-control-unit or PCM/MICU EEPROM read to recover the code. Budget a security-relearn wait on some units. When a smart AKL fights you, that's your cue it wants the online registration or a bench read \u2014 not more OBD attempts.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY\nOriginal Lishi HON66 (2-in-1/3-in-1); pick set + door-lock removal tools for edge-cut decode; high-security cutter (Triton / Futura Pro / Condor / Dolphin); Autel IM508 or IM608 Pro (G-Box/APB112 for some smart), Advanced Diagnostics Smart Pro with Honda module, Xhorse Key Tool Plus, or dealer i-HDS; EEPROM/bench kit (VVDI Prog, XProg, Autel XP400) for immobox/PCM reads; blanks Ilco HD106-PT/HD111-PT and HO01/HO03/HO05; loose chips ID13/ID46/ID47 or OEM-style keys and smart fobs by FCC/PN.\n\nLAST WORD\nBlade and chip both drift by VIN and trim on Honda more than most makes, and the trade nicknames lie \u2014 \"F\" is ID13, \"G\" is ID47 Hitag3, and ID46 is just ID46. Before you cut or clip anything, run the VIN on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS, or Ilco) to confirm the exact blank + chip + FCC for that specific car.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-honda|Honda 5-button smart fob (KR5TP-4, 2022+ Civic/CR-V/Accord) with its HON66 emergency blade \u2014 the key most modern Honda/Acura jobs land on.]]\n\nStanding in front of the car? Confirm the blade in your hand first (edges cut = HD106 8-cut; smooth edges with milled worm-track = HO01/HON66 high-security; no metal blade, just a fob with a pull-out insert = smart/prox). Then find the row. Exact fitment varies by VIN and trim \u2014 verify on Transpondery/UHS before you cut or order a chip.\n\nHONDA CIVIC\n\u2022  Civic (1996\u20132000, 6th gen) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 keyway \u2192 Ilco HD106 (early trims no immo; immo appears late in the run) \u2192 no chip on non-immo cars, ID13 \"F\" fixed where immo is fitted \u2192 cut to code/decode; if chipped, program OBD or clone the ID13.\n\u2022  Civic (2001\u20132005, 7th gen) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT \u2192 ID13 \"F\" Megamos fixed; remote-head FCC G8D-380H-A \u2192 OBD onboard add (two working keys does it), or code-cut + clone the ID13 (fixed chip clones clean), or dealer/NASTF code service for the blade.\n\u2022  Civic (2006\u20132011, 8th gen) \u2192 jumps to high-security milled 4-track, HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01 (HO03 laser variant) \u2192 split: earliest '06 cars ID13 \"F\", then ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7936) for the bulk of the run; remote-head MLBHLIK-1T \u2192 Lishi HON66/HO01 to decode/originate the blade, then OBD add; ID46 is Hitag2 \u2014 clone only with a Hitag2-capable cloner, otherwise program OBD.\n\u2022  Civic (2012\u20132015, 9th gen) \u2192 high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 early, ID47 Hitag3 \"G\" chip (Ilco GTHT49C) from ~2013\u20132014; MLBHLIK-1T / N5F-A05TAA \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD; ID47 does not clone to a fixed transponder \u2014 OBD or reset route only.\n\u2022  Civic (2016\u20132021, 10th gen) \u2192 smart / push-to-start prox fob, HON66 emergency insert blade (base LX trims may still carry a bladed high-security remote-head \u2014 check for a physical ignition) \u2192 ID47 Hitag3 / ID49; FCC KR5V2X, KR5TP-4 \u2192 OBD add with a working key; AKL typically needs i-HDS + online immobilizer registration.\n\nHONDA ACCORD\n\u2022  Accord (1998\u20132002, 6th gen) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT \u2192 ID13 \"F\" fixed; G8D-380H-A \u2192 OBD onboard add, or code-cut + clone the ID13.\n\u2022  Accord (2003\u20132007, 7th gen) \u2192 jumps to high-security HON66 (Accord led the switch) \u2192 Ilco HO01 \u2192 split: '03\u2013'05 ID13 \"F\", then ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 Lishi HON66/HO01, OBD add; ID13 years clone, ID46 needs Hitag2 cloner or OBD.\n\u2022  Accord (2008\u20132012, 8th gen) \u2192 high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2; MLBHLIK-1T \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD; Hitag2 clone possible.\n\u2022  Accord (2013\u20132017, 9th gen) \u2192 trim split: base = high-security bladed HON66 (Ilco HO01/HO05), Touring/EX-L = smart prox \u2192 ID47 Hitag3 \"G\" (bladed) / ID47 smart \u2192 Lishi HON66 for bladed cars + OBD; smart via OBD add; no clone on ID47.\n\u2022  Accord (2018\u20132022, 10th gen) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert blade \u2192 ID49 / AES; FCC KR5V2X, CWTWB1G0090 \u2192 OBD add; AKL needs i-HDS + online registration or bench read.\n\nHONDA CR-V\n\u2022  CR-V (1997\u20132006, gen 1\u20132) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106 / HD106-PT (later remote-head OUCG8D-380H-A) \u2192 no chip early, ID13 \"F\" once immo fitted (~2002+) \u2192 cut to code; chipped cars OBD or clone ID13.\n\u2022  CR-V (2007\u20132011, gen 3) \u2192 jumps to high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2; MLBHLIK-1T \u2192 Lishi HON66/HO01 + OBD; Hitag2 clone possible.\n\u2022  CR-V (2012\u20132016, gen 4) \u2192 high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03/HO05 \u2192 ID46 early, ID47 \"G\" (GTHT49C) from ~2013\u20132014 \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD.\n\u2022  CR-V (2017\u20132022, gen 5) \u2192 trim split: LX = bladed high-security HON66, EX+ = smart prox \u2192 ID47 / ID49; FCC KR5V2X \u2192 OBD add; smart AKL via i-HDS + online reg.\n\nHONDA PILOT\n\u2022  Pilot (2003\u20132008, gen 1) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT (remote-head OUCG8D-380H-A) \u2192 ID13 \"F\" fixed (glass bulb '03\u2013'04, plastic chip '05\u2013'08; later builds ID46 \u2014 confirm by VIN) \u2192 standard duplicator / cut to code; OBD onboard add (two working keys) or clone the chip. NOT high-security \u2014 no Lishi HON66 needed. (High-security laser HO03-PT arrives with the 2009 redesign, gen 2.)\n\u2022  Pilot (2009\u20132015, gen 2) \u2192 high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03 (HO03-PT) \u2192 ID46 Hitag2, ID47 \"G\" from ~2013 on later cars; MLBHLIK-1T \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD.\n\u2022  Pilot (2016\u20132022, gen 3) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert blade \u2192 ID47 / ID49; FCC KR5V2X, KR5V1X \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online registration.\n\nHONDA ODYSSEY\n\u2022  Odyssey (1999\u20132004, gen 2) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT \u2192 ID13 \"F\" fixed \u2192 OBD add or clone ID13.\n\u2022  Odyssey (2005\u20132010, gen 3) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT (remote-head OUCG8D-380H-A on LX / OUCG8D-399H-A on power-door trims) \u2192 ID13 \"F\" early, ID46 (Philips 46) later (confirm by VIN) \u2192 cut to code / standard duplicator; OBD add or clone the chip. NOT high-security \u2014 standard machine, no Lishi HON66. (High-security/smart begins with the gen 4 redesign, 2011.)\n\u2022  Odyssey (2011\u20132017, gen 4) \u2192 high-security HON66 \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03/HO05 \u2192 ID46 early, ID47 \"G\" from ~2013; remote-head N5F-A04TAA \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD; higher trims (EX-L/Touring) add smart prox (KR5V1X) \u2014 smart via OBD add.\n\u2022  Odyssey (2018\u20132022, gen 5) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID49 / AES; FCC KR5V2X \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online reg.\n\nHONDA FIT\n\u2022  Fit (2007\u20132008, gen 1 US) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT (remote-head OUCG8D-380H-A) \u2192 chip confirm on lookup \u2014 ID13 \"F\" or Megamos 8E depending on build (blade may be stamped \"H\") \u2192 OBD add or clone once chip confirmed.\n\u2022  Fit (2009\u20132013, gen 2) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 (confirm \u2014 some late cars carry high-security) \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT \u2192 ID46 (Philips 46) (confirm on lookup) \u2192 OBD add; clone only with a compatible cloner if edge blade confirmed.\n\u2022  Fit (2015\u20132020, gen 3) \u2192 trim split: base = high-security bladed HON66 (Ilco HO01), higher = smart prox \u2192 ID47 \u2192 Lishi HON66 for bladed + OBD; smart OBD add.\n\nHONDA RIDGELINE\n\u2022  Ridgeline (2006\u20132014, gen 1) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT (remote-head OUCG8D-380H-A, HD106 profile the whole run) \u2192 ID13 \"F\" early, ID46 (Philips 46) later \u2192 cut to code / standard duplicator; OBD add or clone the chip. Edge-cut the entire run \u2014 NOT high-security, no Lishi HON66, no ID47/\"G\" on gen 1. (Smart/prox arrives with gen 2, 2017+.)\n\u2022  Ridgeline (2017\u20132024, gen 2) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID47 / ID49; FCC KR5V2X, KR5T41 \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online reg.\n\nHONDA HR-V\n\u2022  HR-V (2016\u20132022, gen 1) \u2192 trim split: LX/base = high-security bladed HON66 (Ilco HO01/HO03), EX+ = smart prox \u2192 ID47 Hitag3 \u2192 Lishi HON66 for bladed + OBD; smart OBD add, AKL i-HDS + online reg.\n\nACURA MDX\n\u2022  MDX (2001\u20132006, gen 1) \u2192 edge-cut, X172 \u2192 Ilco HD106-PT \u2192 ID13 \"F\" fixed \u2192 OBD add or clone ID13.\n\u2022  MDX (2007\u20132013, gen 2) \u2192 high-security / early smart \u2014 bladed HON66 remote-head on base, smart-key push-start on Tech/Advance trims \u2192 Ilco HO01 (bladed) \u2192 ID46 Hitag2; MLBHLIK-1T (bladed), smart fob on top trims \u2192 Lishi HON66 for bladed + OBD; smart via OBD/HDS.\n\u2022  MDX (2014\u20132020, gen 3) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID47 Hitag3; FCC KR5V1X (P/N A2C32523200) \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online registration.\n\nACURA TL / TLX\n\u2022  TL (2004\u20132008, gen 3) \u2192 high-security HON66 (Acura ran high-sec ahead of Honda) \u2192 Ilco HO01 \u2192 ID13 \"F\" early, ID46 Hitag2 later \u2192 Lishi HON66/HO01 + OBD.\n\u2022  TL (2009\u20132014, gen 4) \u2192 high-security bladed flip / early smart \u2192 Ilco HO01/HO03 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2; flip-key FCC MLBHLIK-1T \u2192 Lishi HON66 + OBD; Hitag2 clone possible on bladed cars.\n\u2022  TLX (2015\u20132020, gen 1) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID47 Hitag3; FCC KR5V1X, A2C\u2026 \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online reg.\n\nACURA RDX\n\u2022  RDX (2007\u20132012, gen 1) \u2192 high-security / smart-key push-start \u2192 Ilco HO01 (bladed) \u2192 ID46 Hitag2; MLBHLIK-1T on bladed \u2192 Lishi HON66 for bladed + OBD; smart via OBD/HDS.\n\u2022  RDX (2013\u20132018, gen 2) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID47 Hitag3; FCC KR5V1X (A2C32523200) \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online reg.\n\u2022  RDX (2019\u20132024, gen 3) \u2192 smart / push-to-start, HON66 insert \u2192 ID49 Hitag-Pro / AES; FCC KR5T41, KR5TP-4 \u2192 OBD add; AKL i-HDS + online immobilizer registration or bench read.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/honda_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/hon66-2-in-1/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/ho01-svc-honda-acura-key-blank-ilco","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1996-2006-honda-acura-hd106-transponder-key-megamos-id-13-chip","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2002-2006-honda-transponder-key-id13-chip-f-ho01-pt","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2013-2019-honda-transponder-key-id47-chip-g-ho05-ptg","https://keydirect.ca/product/ilco-gtht49c-cloneable-nxp-hitag-3-honda-g-chip/","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2016-2018-honda-pilot-smart-key-w-hatch-4b-fcc-kr5v2x","https://www.tlkeys.com/products/OEM-4-Button-Smart-Key-433-MHz-HITAG-3-ID47-chip-34418","https://www.go-parts.com/garage/keyless-entry-transmitter-honda-hr-v-honda-cr-v-honda-civic-2014-2026","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/philips-46-crypto-2-tag-blank-transponder-chip-for-honda-nissan-hyundai-kia-gm","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/ilco-ho01-hon66-honda-acura-metal-key-blank"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Push-to-start","Duplicator","Lishi","Immobilizer","Fob","Transponder","Add-a-key","Keyway"],aliases:["proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","HPC 1200","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-nissan",title:"Making keys: Nissan & Infiniti",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Nissan and Infiniti share one anti-theft platform: NATS (Nissan Anti-Theft System). Learn NATS and you can originate for both marques. The whole make stays EDGE-CUT (single-sided, NSN11 then NSN14 keyways) right up through the newest proximity smart keys \u2014 there is no laser/sidewinder era on mainstream Nissan/Infiniti, which is a common exam trap. The complication is never the blade; it is the immobilizer PIN, called the BCM code. Get the PIN and every job becomes routine OBD registration.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path). Confirm the exact blank+chip by VIN on a transponder catalog; fitment overlaps across model years.\n\nPre-NATS / early NATS, ~1995\u20132002: Ilco/JMA blank DA31 (Silca NSN11), older 8-cut single-sided keyway. Chip: Texas Crypto 4D60 glass (aftermarket key NI02T); very early cars a Philips 33/T5. Path: onboard/PIN-based registration, or clone the 4D60. NSN11 also appears cut-only (no chip) on base models.\n\nMainstream NATS, ~2003\u20132019 (Altima, Sentra, Maxima, Rogue, Murano, Versa, Juke, Frontier, Pathfinder, Titan; Infiniti G/M/EX/FX/QX): Ilco NSN14 / JMA DAT-17 / Silca NSN14 (10-cut, aftermarket transponder key NI04T). Chip: Philips PCF7936 = ID46 Hitag2 (crypto). High-line cars migrated to the PCF7952 package (still ID46/Hitag2, e.g. 2008+ Maxima), and the 2013\u20132015 Maxima jumped to PCF7952X = ID47/Hitag3. Path: OBD add-a-key or AKL with BCM PIN.\n\nIntelligent Key / smart-prox, ~2007\u2013present: proximity fob, ID46 Hitag2 early, ID47 / Hitag-AES on newer. Emergency insert blade is still NSN14. Fobs carry an FCC ID (e.g. KR55WK48903, CWTWB1U787, KR5TXN7 on recent cars) and a Nissan/Infiniti part number (285E3-xxxxx). Path: OBD smart-key registration with BCM PIN; fob must be in the antenna zone (start button / steering column) during enroll.\n\nTHE BCM PIN \u2014 THIS IS THE JOB. Nissan does not program without the immobilizer PIN, and the make splits into two PIN worlds. The split is roughly 2013, not late-2010s \u2014 that is the correction techs get burned on.\n- Older BCM (roughly pre-2013): a STATIC code you can decode. It's a 5-character alphanumeric out-code, sometimes printed on the BCM/ICU label as a SEC or ICU code, otherwise read over OBD. You run it through a Nissan calculator (weboctopus NATS \"by SEC/ICU label,\" cardumps out-code converter, Nissan PIN Wizard, or the calculator inside Autel/Xhorse) to get the 4-digit PIN. On these the label read still works if OBD won't talk.\n- Newer BCM (roughly 2013+, starting with the Gen-5 2013 Altima, then Rogue 2014+, Sentra, Kicks, recent Infiniti QX): 20-DIGIT ROLLING/encrypted code. It is NOT printed and it CHANGES every ignition cycle \u2014 read it, cycle the key, and the old value is dead. You read the live 20-digit code over OBD, then it converts server-side/token-based (Autel, Xhorse token, NSPC001 pin reader with dongle, or NASTF SDRS). Budget tokens for these; a static calculator will not touch them.\n\nFinding/reading the code: pull it via OBD in the immobilizer menu (Autel IM508/IM608, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus / MVP Pro, or Nissan CONSULT-III+/IV). If OBD read fails on an AKL, drop the BCM (behind the kick panel / under the dash, varies by model) and read the label directly on older cars, or read the out-code from the BCM EEPROM on the bench.\n\nFROM SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 the real menu, in the order I actually work it:\n\n1) Get IN and get the cut. Nissan/Infiniti is one keyway. Reach for the Original Lishi NSN14 2-in-1 pick & decoder (cross-references DA34 / DAT-15 / SUB1 / X237). Best practice: work the DOOR lock, not the ignition \u2014 the door houses only 8 of the 10 wafers, so the Lishi reads door positions 3\u201310, and Nissan wafers are forgiving. Decode, then fill the two missing shoulder cuts (positions 1\u20132) by progression or by reading them off the ignition once you're in. If you prefer the ignition, the same NSN14 Lishi reads all 10 cuts directly, but the ignition is stiffer and higher-risk. Cut the mechanical blade on your Ilco NSN14 (or clip a smart emergency blade) from the decode.\n   - Alternatives to Lishi decode: pull the door or ignition cylinder and BENCH-DECODE the wafers directly; pull the KEY CODE via NASTF (Nissan runs the SDRS/SDRM code service \u2014 use your LSID) or a code service, then cut to code on the NSN14/DA34 spacing; impression the door lock as a last resort. No Tibbe, no rotary-disc, no HU-style milling on this make \u2014 do not bring VAG/Mercedes tooling to a Nissan.\n\n2) Solve the transponder. Cutting the blade gets the doors and turns the lock; the car still will not crank until the chip is registered. That is the PIN step above. For older 4D60 cars you can sometimes CLONE a working chip \u2014 but AKL means no donor, so you go the PIN route regardless.\n\n3) AKL registration. With the PIN in hand: plug in the tool (IM608 / Key Tool Plus / CONSULT-III+), enter the immobilizer/NATS menu, select the model, enter the BCM PIN (4-digit or the derived 20-digit), and register all keys. Nissan AKL usually wants ALL keys present in the session re-registered because adding wipes and re-writes the key table. For a bladed transponder car, program the ID46/ID47 key. For an Intelligent Key car it's SMART-KEY registration: PIN in, then hold each fob at the start button / prox antenna as the tool enrolls it, then confirm push-to-start and remote functions.\n\n4) Add-a-key (spare, key still in hand). Same tool, immobilizer menu, \"add key,\" enter PIN, enroll the new blade or fob. Faster; no door picking needed if the customer has a working key to unlock with.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY for this make: Original Lishi NSN14 (pick + decode, door and ignition); Ilco NSN14 blanks and NI04T transponder keys, DA31/NSN11 and NI02T for older cars; Nissan Intelligent Key fobs by FCC/part number; a programmer with Nissan PIN capability and tokens \u2014 Autel IM508/IM608 (or IM608 Pro), Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus or MVP Pro, or dealer-level CONSULT-III+/IV; a PIN path for BOTH worlds \u2014 a static out-code calculator (weboctopus/cardumps/PIN Wizard) for pre-2013, and token/server conversion or NSPC001/NASTF SDRS for the 20-digit 2013+ cars; and for stubborn AKL where OBD won't read, EEPROM/bench read of the BCM. A cutter that supports NSN14/DA34 10-cut spacing rounds it out.\n\nEXAM ANCHORS: NATS is the immobilizer name; the immobilizer secret is the BCM PIN, not an ignition code. Blade is edge-cut, NSN11/DA31 \u2192 NSN14/DA34, never laser. Chips run T5/4D60 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7936, later PCF7952) \u2192 ID47 Hitag3/AES (PCF7952X). Older PIN = static out-code (printed SEC/ICU or OBD-read) \u2192 4-digit via calculator; newer PIN, ~2013+ from the Gen-5 Altima on = 20-digit ROLLING code, OBD-read live + token/NASTF conversion, never printed. Lishi reads door positions 3\u201310 (8 cuts) and the full ignition 10-cut.\n\nExact blank and chip vary by VIN and even by build month \u2014 always confirm the precise Ilco/JMA blank, the transponder family (ID46 vs ID47), and the fob FCC/part number on a transponder lookup (Transpondery Nissan catalog, UHS Hardware, or Ilco) before you cut or order.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-nissan|Nissan & Infiniti 5-button intelligent smart fob (KR5S180144014 family) \u2014 the proximity key most Nissan/Infiniti jobs land on.]]\n\nCutting note first (applies to every row below): every mainstream Nissan/Infiniti here is edge-cut \u2014 no sidewinder/laser on these lines, so don't reach for a Miracle/Condor track jaw. Older cars = NSN11 / Ilco DA31, 8-cut (JMA cross varies \u2014 DAT-15/-16 circulate, confirm on catalog). NATS-era keys and *every* Intelligent-Key emergency blade = NSN14 / Ilco EZ (DA34 keyway) / Silca NSN14, 10-cut (JMA DAT-15/-17 both cross this keyway \u2014 confirm on a catalog; don't trust one hard reference). AKL tip: the door wafers only give 8 of 10 cuts (Lishi NSN14 reads positions 3\u201310 on the door) \u2014 always decode the IGNITION for the full 10-cut code, or you'll cut a key that works the door but not the ignition. Clone route exists *only* on the pre-2003 4D60 glass chip; every ID46/ID47/AES chip below is crypto \u2014 no clone, must program.\n\nNISSAN ALTIMA\n\u2022  2002\u20132012 (L31/L32): NSN14 edge \u2192 Ilco NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 transponder; Intelligent-Key prox optional from '07 (ID46, confirm FCC on fob). Path: OBD add-a-key, or AKL = Lishi NSN14 to originate + decode ignition + read static 5-char BCM SEC/ICU PIN (weboctopus/cardump) \u2192 register on Smart Pro / AutoProPad / Autel. *(Pre-2002 L30: NSN11/DA31 8-cut, 4D60 \u2014 clone or onboard.)*\n\u2022  2013\u20132018 (L33): NSN14 (NI04T base key / emergency blade on prox) \u2192 base transponder ID46; prox ID46 (2013\u20132015) \u2192 ID47 (2016+). FCC KR5S180144014 applies to 2016\u20132018; 2013\u20132015 prox fob differs \u2014 confirm FCC. Path: OBD register w/ BCM PIN \u2014 PIN now newer-world, read via tool over OBD, not the old static decode.\n\u2022  2019\u20132025 (L34): prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 fob PN and FCC in the KR5TXN family \u2192 ID47/Hitag-AES (confirm exact FCC/PN on the fob). Path: OBD smart-key register w/ BCM PIN, fob in start-button zone; AKL needs AES-capable tool + PIN, no forced immobilizer wait.\n\nNISSAN SENTRA\n\u2022  2000\u20132019 (B15/B16/B17): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 (prox optional on late B17). Earliest B15 (2000\u20132001) \u2014 verify chip/keyway by VIN, transition-era. Path: OBD add-a-key, or AKL Lishi NSN14 + BCM PIN (static pre-2013, tool-read after). *(True pre-NATS base: NSN11/4D60.)*\n\u2022  2020\u20132025 (B18): prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade; base S trim = NI07T metal key \u2192 ID4A Hitag-AES (NATS7); prox = KR5TXN family, ID47/AES (confirm FCC). Path: OBD register + PIN, AES tool required.\n\nNISSAN MAXIMA\n\u2022  2004\u20132008 (A34): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936, migrating to the PCF7952 package in 2008 (still ID46). Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + static BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2009\u20132023 (A35/A36): Intelligent-Key prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade. Chip walks: ID46 (PCF7952) \u2192 A35 SPLIT 2013\u20132014 = PCF7952X / ID47 Hitag3 (no 2015 Maxima existed \u2014 A35 ended MY2014, A36 began MY2016) \u2192 2016+ (A36) ID47/AES (KR5S180144014 \u2192 later, confirm FCC). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN.\n\nNISSAN VERSA\n\u2022  2007\u20132019 (C11/N17): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 (very base trims shipped cut-only / no chip \u2014 verify chip presence). Path: OBD add-a-key, or AKL Lishi NSN14 + BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2020\u20132025 (N18): base S = NI07T metal key, ID4A Hitag-AES; SV/SR = prox (KR5TXN family, NSN14 emergency blade), ID47/AES (confirm FCC). Path: OBD register + PIN, AES tool.\n\nNISSAN ROGUE\n\u2022  2008\u20132013 (S35): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936; prox optional. Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + static BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2014\u20132020 (T32): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 (metal transponder base; prox ID46, stepping toward ID47 on late trims \u2014 confirm by VIN). This generation is NOT AES \u2014 the NI07T / ID4A Hitag-AES metal key does not appear on the Rogue until the T33 (2021+). Path: OBD add-a-key, or AKL Lishi NSN14 + decode ignition + BCM PIN (static pre-2013 / tool-read after). No AES tool required for the ID46 cars.\n\u2022  2021\u20132025 (T33): prox only, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID47/AES (KR5TXN family, confirm FCC). Path: OBD smart register + PIN, AES tool.\n\nNISSAN MURANO\n\u2022  2003\u20132007 (Z50): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936. Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + static BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2009\u20132014 (Z51): Intelligent-Key prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID46 (CWTWB1U787-era fob, confirm FCC). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN (static pre-2013 / tool after).\n\u2022  2015\u20132024 (Z52): prox \u2192 ID47/AES (KR5S180144014 \u2192 later, confirm FCC). Path: OBD register + PIN, AES tool.\n\nNISSAN PATHFINDER\n\u2022  2005\u20132012 (R51): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 (Titan/Frontier body-on-frame platform). Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + static BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2013\u20132021 (R52): Intelligent-Key prox, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID46\u2192ID47, FCC KR5S180144014 (confirm on fob). MY2021 is the last R52 carryover before the all-new MY2022. Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN (tool-read post-2013).\n\u2022  2022\u20132025 (R53): prox \u2192 ID47/AES (KR5TXPZ-family, confirm FCC). Path: OBD register + PIN, AES tool.\n\nNissan Frontier \u2014 the textbook *don't-split* case: one platform ran ~17 years unchanged.\n\u2022  2005\u20132021 (D40): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 the ENTIRE run \u2014 do not split by year. Path: OBD add-a-key, or AKL Lishi NSN14 (decode ignition for the 10-cut) + static BCM PIN \u2014 this platform kept the older SEC/ICU-decode PIN world late, so the static calc still works on many D40s past 2013.\n\u2022  2022\u20132025 (D41): all-new; Intelligent-Key prox, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID47/AES (KR5TXPZ-family, confirm FCC). Path: OBD register + PIN, AES tool.\n\nNISSAN TITAN\n\u2022  2004\u20132015 (A60): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 (prox optional on late trims). Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2016\u20132024 (A61): Intelligent-Key prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID47/AES (confirm FCC). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN, AES tool.\n\nInfiniti G35 \u2192 G25/G37/Q40/Q60 (G-platform, all brands = Infiniti here)\n\u2022  2003\u20132007 (V35 G35 sedan/coupe): NSN14 edge \u2192 NI04T \u2192 ID46 PCF7936 transponder key; Intelligent-Key prox appears ~2005. Path: OBD/AKL Lishi NSN14 + static BCM PIN.\n\u2022  2008\u20132015 (V36 G25/G37/Q40 sedan, G37/Q60 coupe): Intelligent-Key prox standard, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID46, FCC KR55WK48903 (PN 285E3-JK65A \u2014 confirm on fob). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN.\n\nINFINITI JX35 / QX60\n\u2022  2013\u20132020 (JX35 '13 \u2192 QX60): Intelligent-Key prox, NSN14 emergency blade \u2192 ID47, FCC KR5S180144014 (PN 285E3-xxxxx, confirm on fob). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN (tool-read), AES-class tool.\n\u2022  2022\u20132025 (QX60 gen2): prox \u2192 ID47/AES, newer FCC (confirm on fob). Path: OBD register + PIN.\n\nINFINITI Q50 / Q60 COUPE (V37)\n\u2022  2014\u20132025: don't be fooled \u2014 edge-cut NSN14 emergency blade, not a sidewinder, prox standard \u2192 ID47; FCC KR5S180144014 (2014\u20132019) \u2192 later KR5TXPZ-family (confirm FCC). Path: OBD smart register + BCM PIN, AES-class tool, fob in column/start zone. Single method across the whole run \u2014 don't split unless the chip/FCC on the actual fob differs.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/nissan_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-nsn14-nissan-infiniti-10-cut-2-in-1-pick-decoder-dr-bt-ag","https://www.genuinelishi.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/09/NSN14-Genuine-Lishi-Picking-and-Decoding.pdf","https://www.carandtruckremotes.com/products/original-lishi-nissan-decoder-reader-tool-nsn14-da34-sub1-x237-ignition-door-trunk-direct-read","https://www.weboctopus.nl/nissan-infiniti-NATS.php","https://nissan-techinfo.locksmithsdrm.com/sdrm/ni_02_4218/Overview%20of%20the%20SDRM%20process.pdf","https://carinterior.alibaba.com/question/nissan-bcm-code-location-pin-conversion-guide","https://blog.obdii365.com/2026/05/26/id46-vs-pcf7936-vs-hitag2-transponder-chip/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2002-2019-nissan-infiniti-ni04t-transponder-key-chip-46-k-ni04","https://www.locksco.com/buy/product/Nissan-DA31-Key-Blank-NSN11/CLENCc517b6b46561b185","https://www.key4.com/nissan-transponder-key-ni02t","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/nissan-pin-code-wizard"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Add-a-key","Lishi","Fob","Transponder","Immobilizer","Rolling code","FCC ID","Progression"],aliases:["extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","immo","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","hopping code","code grabbing","remote won't pair","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","smart key","keyless go","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-hyundai",title:"Making keys: Hyundai, Kia & Genesis",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Hyundai, Kia and Genesis (Genesis is Hyundai's luxury brand, spun off 2015-16) share one immobilizer architecture, so learn one and you can work all three. The through-line for a locksmith: on this make you almost always need a PIN (a 4- or 6-digit teaching code) before the module accepts a new key, and on modern cars you also need internet/server access to get that PIN. Get IN and get the CUTS first, then PIN, then teach. Everything below is edge-cut mechanical \u2014 Hyundai/Kia never went milled/sidewinder or Tibbe; the blade just got skinnier and higher-security over the years, moving from a wide external cut to a narrow internal two-track wafer lock (still Lishi-pickable, NOT a true sidewinder).\n\nERA 1 \u2014 Early transponder, wide external blade (roughly 1998-2010). Families: Elantra, Sonata, Santa Fe, Tucson, Accent; Kia Sportage, Optima, Sorento, Sedona. Keyway is the wide HY15 (Ilco HY15) with KIA7/HY17-style blades on some Kia. Blank: Ilco HY15 / JMA HY-14; older Kia KIA7 / HY17. Chip: early cars ran Texas 4C (glass), moving to Texas 4D60 (DST40, 80-bit) and Philips ID46 (PCF7936/Hitag2) as the decade closed. Lishi: the wide-blade decoders for these external wafers. These are cheap clone-or-teach jobs \u2014 4C and 4D60 clone onto a compatible chip with a Xhorse/JMA cloner; ID46 you teach with the PIN. Note: 4D60 80-bit did not die in 2010 \u2014 budget transponder (non-smart) keys still ship 4D60 on some models into the late 2010s (2015-2023 aftermarket 4D60 keys exist), so confirm chip by VIN, not year.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 ID46 mainstream + high-security internal blade (roughly 2006-2018). Bread-and-butter era. Flip keys and prox both appear, and TWO keyways run in parallel: the older wide HY15 AND the newer high-security internal-cut keyway. The high-security mechanical is the HY18/HY22 family. Lishi is where you MUST be precise, because there are three distinct tools here:\n- HY20 \u2014 standard high-security 8-cut, 2-track internal keyway (test key HY18-P). Accent, Veloster, Elantra GT, Veracruz, etc.\n- HY20R (a.k.a. HY18R) \u2014 the REVERSED keyway (test key HY18R-P). This is a SEPARATE tool, NOT an alias for HY20. It fits newer/other cars: Santa Fe ~2013-2018, Kia Sorento ~2015-2018 and similar. Carry BOTH HY20 and HY20R \u2014 guessing wrong wastes a trip.\n- HY22 \u2014 the Lishi for the HY22 emergency-blade profile (2009-2014 HS: Azera, Equus, Genesis sedan, Borrego).\nBlade for the exam: HY18/HY22 is an internal/laser-track edge cut on a wafer lock, still Lishi-decodable, just narrow and two-track. Blank: Ilco HY18 / HY22 (emergency inserts for prox keys are these same profiles). Chip: predominantly Philips ID46 (Hitag2, PCF7936/7952). Fobs: 3-4 button flips and early smart/prox (e.g. 2015-16 Genesis 4-button smart). Programming: PIN required, taught by OBD.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 ID47 and ID4A / Hitag-AES smart keys (roughly 2016-present). Blade feel is unchanged (emergency insert is still the HY18/HY22 internal laser edge cut), but the electronics jumped. Chip: NXP ID47 (PCF7938X, Hitag3) on push-to-start and later transponder keys, then ID4A (Hitag-AES, 128-bit AES, PCF7939-family) on 2018+ smart systems. Nearly everything is push-to-start proximity now: Santa Fe, Palisade, Telluride, Sorento, Sportage, Tucson, Elantra, K5, Seltos, and Genesis GV70/GV80/G70/G80. Fobs are model-specific by FCC ID and part number \u2014 always match the FCC ID and part number on a donor, don't eyeball button count. Programming: PIN required AND the PIN increasingly must be pulled from a server (dealer or online PIN service), not calculated. 2024+ new-style smart keys added anti-scan/anti-theft handshakes that break older tool versions \u2014 route through the diagnostic/SMK menu (not the generic IMMO path) and keep firmware current. Note: the module locks up on repeated rapid PIN-read attempts, so don't hammer it.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 programming path):\n- 1998-2006 wide blade: Ilco HY15 / KIA7 \u2192 4C then 4D60 \u2192 clone or OBD teach, PIN if immobilized.\n- 2006-2015 wide + HS: HY15 / HY18 / HY22 \u2192 ID46 (Hitag2); budget keys sometimes 4D60 \u2192 OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n- 2015-2020 smart: HY18/HY22 emergency blade \u2192 ID46/ID47 \u2192 OBD smart-key teach, PIN from server.\n- 2018-present AES: HY18/HY22 blade \u2192 ID4A Hitag-AES \u2192 OBD teach, PIN from online/dealer server, current firmware, diagnostic/SMK path on 2024+.\nExact fitment varies by VIN \u2014 confirm the blade profile (HY15 standard vs HY18/HY20 standard-cut vs HY18R reversed) and chip before you cut or order.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS (the real menu, in the order I use it on THIS make):\n1. Lishi the door \u2014 pick the RIGHT one: wide-blade decoder for HY15 cars; HY20 for standard high-security; HY20R (HY18R) for the reversed keyway; HY22 for the HY22-profile cars. Hyundai/Kia doors read clean and are usually not high-side-restricted, so a full door decode typically gives you the complete cut. Cut a blade on your Triton/Miracle/Xhorse Dolphin.\n2. If the door won't decode fully, pull the driver door lock or the trunk/tailgate cylinder, bench-decode the wafers, reassemble. On prox cars the ignition is a slot or gone \u2014 the DOOR or trunk is your mechanical source, don't fight the ignition.\n3. Pull the PIN, in order: your tool's server (Autel, Xhorse Key Tool Max Pro, SmartPro ADS, OBDSTAR H105, Abrites AVDI HK011) reads it via OBD on many ID46 cars; on newer AES cars the tool uploads data to a server that returns the PIN; dealer with proof of ownership is the fallback. Abrites HK011 and Autel can also pull the PIN/data from a WORKING key when you still have one \u2014 that's the add-key shortcut. Xhorse tends to be the most reliable PIN reader on late Korean; Autel has struggled on the newest ones.\n4. Code service / by-code cutting: Hyundai/Kia key codes are available through code services and the dealer by VIN with ID \u2014 a good AKL fallback when a lock won't decode cleanly.\n5. Impression is a last resort here and rarely needed given how well the doors decode.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST PROCEDURE:\n1. Identify exactly: VIN, year, model, and whether it's transponder-in-blade or push-to-start smart. Pull the FCC ID/part number if a donor's around.\n2. Confirm blank + chip on a transponder lookup for THAT VIN (blade HY15 vs HY18/HY20 vs HY18R reversed; chip 4D60 vs ID46 vs ID47 vs ID4A). Don't assume from year \u2014 overlap years exist.\n3. Get IN: Lishi the correct door keyway (wide-blade, HY20, HY20R, or HY22), decode, cut the blade \u2014 or emergency blade for a prox key.\n4. Get the PIN: OBD tool/server, online PIN service, or dealer. AKL specifically needs the PIN before any key is accepted.\n5. Teach: OBD is the standard path on virtually all of these \u2014 Autel IM508/IM608, Xhorse VVDI/Key Tool, OBDSTAR, SmartPro, or Abrites. True AKL erases all keys and teaches your new one; add-key appends. On 2024+ AES smart keys use the diagnostic/SMK menu and current firmware to clear anti-scan errors.\n6. Test: crank/start, lock-unlock, panic, and confirm the blade turns the door and trunk.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY: Original Lishi wide-blade (HY15-class), HY20, HY20R (HY18R), and HY22 2-in-1s; a code-capable cutter (Triton/Miracle/Dolphin); Autel IM608 or Xhorse VVDI2/Key Tool Max Pro or SmartPro with Hyundai/Kia software; Abrites AVDI + HK011 for the PIN-stubborn and AES cars; a chip/fob kit (ID46, ID47, ID4A) plus HY18/HY22 emergency blades; and an online PIN-service account.\n\nFinal check: this data shifts by exact VIN \u2014 confirm the precise blank + chip (and FCC/part number for the fob) on a transponder lookup such as Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or the Ilco/Silca catalog before you cut or order.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-hyundai|Hyundai/Kia SY5HMFNA04 4-button smart fob with pull-out emergency blade \u2014 the proximity key most Hyundai/Kia/Genesis jobs of this era land on.]]\n\nFind your nameplate, match the year range, read across: blade/keyway \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 how to originate. Rows are wrapped so consecutive generations that key the SAME way share one line \u2014 a split only appears at the year the blade, chip, or path actually changed. Blade profile still varies by VIN; verify HY15 (wide edge) vs HY18/HY20 (high-security laser) vs HY18R (reversed) before you cut.\n\n---\n\nHYUNDAI ELANTRA\n\u2022  2001\u20132006 (XD) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 4C early / 4D60 later (base trims non-chip) \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the 4C/4D60 to skip programming; or OBD add-key, PIN only if immobilized. AKL: OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2007\u20132010 (HD) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (some 4D60) \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the ID46/4D60 where cloneable; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2011\u20132016 (MD) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; code service off the door wafers; clone the ID46 where cloneable; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2017\u20132020 (AD) \u2192 HY18 laser blade; smart-key prox on higher trims \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID47 bladed or smart prox \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the emergency/blade cut; OBD smart-key teach with PIN from server; AKL OBD, PIN required. ID47 is not clone-friendly \u2014 OBD only.\n\u2022  2021\u2013present (CN7) \u2192 HY18/HY22 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A Hitag-AES smart \u2192 OBD teach only, PIN from online/dealer server, current firmware; AKL via OBD/diagnostic path, server round-trip. No cloning.\n\nHYUNDAI SONATA\n\u2022  2001\u20132010 (EF \u2192 NF) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 4D60 early / ID46 later \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2011\u20132014 (YF) \u2192 high-security laser blade; smart prox on Limited \u2192 Ilco HY20 (some internal-cut, confirm on lookup; ID46) \u2192 ID46, smart on Limited \u2192 Lishi HY20 for the cut; code service off wafers; clone the ID46 bladed cars; OBD add/AKL with PIN.\n\u2022  2015\u20132019 (LF) \u2192 HY18 laser blade / smart prox \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2020\u20132023 (DN8) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nHYUNDAI SANTA FE\n\u2022  2001\u20132012 (SM \u2192 CM) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 4D60 early / ID46 later \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2013\u20132018 (DM/NC) \u2192 HY18 laser blade; smart prox optional \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID46/ID47 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; OBD add/smart teach with PIN; AKL OBD. Clone only the ID46 bladed cars.\n\u2022  2019\u20132023 (TM) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nHYUNDAI TUCSON\n\u2022  2005\u20132009 (JM) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 4D60 / ID46 \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2010\u20132015 (LM) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; code service off door wafers; clone the ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2016\u20132021 (TL) \u2192 HY18 laser blade / smart prox \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2022\u2013present (NX4) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nHYUNDAI ACCENT\n\u2022  2000\u20132011 (LC \u2192 MC) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 \u2192 4C/4D60, many base trims non-chip \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode; mechanical cut may be all you need on non-immobilized cars; clone the chip if present; OBD add-key with PIN where immobilized.\n\u2022  2012\u20132017 (RB) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; clone the ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2018\u20132022 (HC) \u2192 HY18 blade \u2192 HY18 blade \u2192 ID47 bladed transponder (Accent stayed bladed, no prox) \u2192 Lishi HY18; OBD add/AKL with PIN from server. No clone.\n\nKIA OPTIMA / K5\n\u2022  2001\u20132010 (Magentis \u2192 MG) \u2192 wide edge-cut \u2192 HY15 / KIA7 \u2192 4D60 / ID46 \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2011\u20132015 (TF) \u2192 high-security laser blade; smart prox optional \u2192 Ilco HY18/HY20 \u2192 ID46, smart on top trims \u2192 Lishi HY18/HY20 for the cut; code service off wafers; clone the ID46 bladed cars; OBD add/AKL with PIN.\n\u2022  2016\u20132020 (JF) \u2192 HY18 laser blade / smart prox \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2021\u2013present (K5, DL3) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nKIA SORENTO\n\u2022  2003\u20132009 (BL) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 HY15 / KIA7 \u2192 4D60 / ID46 \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door/tailgate; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2011\u20132015 (XM) \u2192 high-security laser blade; smart prox optional \u2192 Ilco HY18 (some internal-cut, confirm on lookup; ID46) \u2192 ID46, smart on top trims \u2192 Lishi HY18/HY20; code service off wafers; clone the ID46 bladed cars; OBD add/AKL with PIN.\n\u2022  2016\u20132020 (UM) \u2192 HY18 laser blade / smart prox \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2021\u2013present (MQ4) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nKIA SPORTAGE\n\u2022  2005\u20132010 (KM) \u2192 wide-blade edge-cut \u2192 Ilco HY15 / KIA7 \u2192 4D60 / ID46 \u2192 Lishi wide-blade decode the door for the cut; clone the 4D/ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2011\u20132016 (SL) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 Lishi HY18/HY20 for the cut; code service off door wafers; clone the ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2017\u20132022 (QL) \u2192 HY18 laser blade / smart prox \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2023\u2013present (NQ5) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nKIA SOUL\n\u2022  2010\u20132013 (AM) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; clone the ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2014\u20132019 (PS) \u2192 HY18 blade / smart prox on top trims \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID46/ID47, smart optional \u2192 Lishi HY18; OBD add/smart teach with PIN; AKL OBD. Clone only the ID46 bladed cars.\n\u2022  2020\u2013present (SK3) \u2192 HY18 blade or emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox / bladed (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID47 bladed or ID4A AES smart by trim \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; OBD teach, server PIN, current firmware; AKL OBD/diagnostic. No clone.\n\nKIA FORTE\n\u2022  2010\u20132018 (TD \u2192 YD) \u2192 high-security laser blade \u2192 Ilco HY18 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the cut; code service off wafers; clone the ID46; OBD add-key with PIN; AKL OBD once PIN pulled.\n\u2022  2019\u20132024 (BD) \u2192 HY18 blade / smart prox on top trims \u2192 HY18 blade / OEM prox \u2192 ID47 / smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD teach with PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\nGENESIS (SEDAN) \u2192 GENESIS G80\n\u2022  2009\u20132014 (BH sedan) \u2192 high-security laser blade; smart prox on later cars \u2192 Ilco HY20 (internal-cut laser, confirm exact blank on lookup; ID46) \u2192 ID46, smart on later trims \u2192 Lishi HY20 for the cut; code service off door/trunk wafers; clone the ID46 bladed cars; OBD add/AKL with PIN.\n\u2022  2015\u20132020 (DH / G80 RG) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID47 smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 emergency cut; OBD smart teach, PIN from server; AKL OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  2021\u2013present (G80 RG3) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade, OEM insert confirm part on lookup \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID4A AES smart \u2192 OBD/diagnostic (SMK) teach only, server PIN, current firmware; AKL diagnostic path, server round-trip. No clone.\n\nGENESIS G70 / GV70 / GV80\n\u2022  2019\u2013present (G70 IK; GV80 JX1 2021+; GV70 JK1 2022+) \u2192 HY18 emergency blade, OEM insert confirm part on lookup \u2192 OEM prox (confirm on lookup) \u2192 ID47 (early G70) moving to ID4A Hitag-AES smart \u2192 Lishi HY18 for the emergency cut; OBD/diagnostic (SMK) smart teach, PIN from online/dealer server, current firmware required; AKL diagnostic path, server round-trip. No clone \u2014 confirm ID47 vs AES on lookup by VIN.\n\n---\n\nReading the rows: anything ID46/4D60 is your clone-or-OBD era \u2014 fastest add-key is a clone off a working key. ID47 kills cloning; you're OBD with a PIN. ID4A AES is OBD/diagnostic only, PIN off the server, and you must be on current tool firmware. Where a row says smart \"optional/on top trims,\" decode the door with the listed Lishi for the mechanical, then confirm bladed-transponder vs prox at the ignition slot before ordering.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/hyundai_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2006-2018-hyundai-kia-hy20-transponder-key-46-chip-k-hy20","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2015-2023-hyundai-kia-transponder-key-4d60-80-bits-chip-kk12-25-pack","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2012-2017-hyundai-accent-transponder-key-id46-chip-hy18","https://yourcarkeyguys.com/products/transponder-chip-for-hyundai-kia-pcf7938x","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/id4a-transponder-hitag-aes-type-for-hyundai-nissan-jeep-renault-aes-pcf7939ma","https://keytectools.com/product/kia-hitag-128-bits-aes-id4a-transponder-chip/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-hyundai-kia-hy20","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-hyundai-kia-hy20r","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/original-lishi-hyundai-hy20r-hy18r-2-in-1-pick-decoder-9428","https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/hy22-2-in-1/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/hyundai-kia-hy22-2009-2014-hs-emergency-key-blade","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10444013/kia-hyundai-lock-facts","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/abrites-avdi-hk011-pin-and-key-manager","https://www.obd2sales.com/product/obdstar-h105-hyundai-key-programmer/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2025/03/25/autel-im508-im608-newer-hyundai-kia-smart-key-error-solution/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2025/11/24/autel-im508s-program-kia-soul-2024-smart-key-using-at100-super-chip/","Key coding information (diagwiki.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/this-will-change-how-you-program-kia-and-hyundai-keys-new-smartpro-program-for-pin-codes"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","FCC ID","Push-to-start","Transponder","Code cutting","Fob","Cloning","Keyway"],aliases:["2-in-1","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","cut to code","cut by code","originate a key","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-mazda",title:"Making keys: Mazda",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"MAKING KEYS: MAZDA\n\nMazda is the easy make in your bag most days, because it never went to laser. From the '90s wafer locks straight through today's push-to-start cars, the metal blade is EDGE-CUT (external cut, wafer lock). Even a current smart fob hides an edge-cut MAZ24R emergency blade \u2014 there is no HU66-style milled/sidewinder key anywhere in the Mazda line. Keep a MAZ24 Lishi and a code/decode plan and you cover almost everything. What changes across the eras is the electronics: which transponder, and whether you can do it on the OBD port or have to go to the bench.\n\nKeep one thing straight before you start: for roughly 1998-2010 Mazda lived under Ford control, so the immobilizer is PATS (Passive Anti-Theft System) with the Ford outcode/incode PIN dance. That heritage carries into how you program even later cars, and a couple of Mazdas are literally rebadged Fords.\n\nBLANK NAMING \u2014 get this right when you order: the bare mechanical blade is MAZ24R (Silca MAZ24, JMA MAZ-15/16), keyway H76. The transponder version \u2014 plastic head with the chip cavity \u2014 is Ilco MAZ24R-PT (the -PT, not -P; -PT is the standard cloneable/onboard transponder blank; Jet MZ34-PHT, Silca MAZ24RT17, JMA TP20MAZ). Ordering a \"-P\" or a chipless blade for a chipped car is the classic time-waster.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (confirm exact fitment by VIN)\n- Pre-2000, no chip: MAZ24R / older MAZ21 blade, mechanical only. Cut and go.\n- ~1999-2010 PATS, 4D63 (40-bit): blade MAZ24R-PT; chip Texas 4D63 40-bit. OBD add or onboard AKL, PIN via outcode/incode.\n- ~2011-2016, 4D63 80-bit: same MAZ24R-PT blade; chip 4D63 80-bit (backward-compatible \u2014 an 80-bit key covers older 40-bit cars, not the reverse). Remote-head keys F1Y1-76-2GX family. OBD.\n- Ford-platform Mazdas: Tribute (2001-2011, a Ford Escape) uses Ford blade FO40R, 4D63 chip, programs like a Ford (IDS / ForScan / PATS tool). Mazda2 (DE, 2011-2014) is Ford-platform and programs via ForScan/PATS \u2014 but confirm its blade per VIN (NA cars often still take MAZ24R, not FO40R).\n- Some ID46 (Hitag2/PCF7936) turn up on select trims/markets \u2014 verify per VIN, don't assume 4D63.\n- 2014+ Smart/prox (Mazda3 BM, Mazda6 GJ, CX-5 KE, CX-3, CX-9 TC, MX-5 ND): fob FCC WAZSKE13D01/D02, chip NXP PCF7953P = Hitag-Pro ID49 128-bit, 315 MHz (434 some markets). PNs KDY3-67-5DY (3-btn), GJY9-67-5DY (4-btn Mazda3/6/MX-5), TKY2-67-5DY (4-btn CX-5/CX-9). Emergency blade MAZ24R edge cut. OBD add/AKL.\n- 2019+ SkyActiv (Mazda3 BP, CX-30, CX-50, CX-90, refreshed CX-5): still ID49 smart. Aftermarket OBD AKL still works on current tools (Autel IM608 II did a 2023 CX-5 AKL by OBD; Lonsdor K518 pulls a free PIN on 2019-2020+), but a dealer-level PCM/module reflash needs an online MDARS subscription + Mazda VCM-II. Use OEM fobs here (see below).\n\nERA 1 \u2014 PRE-CHIP AND EARLY PATS (up to ~2005)\nBlade MAZ24R. Locks are 8-10 cut wafer, external. No chip on the oldest cars; from the late '90s you get 4D63 40-bit. Getting in and getting cuts is Lishi bread-and-butter: run the Original Lishi MAZ24 2-in-1 on the door, or better, pull the door/boot cylinder and bench-decode it clean \u2014 you read the bitting in a couple minutes and cut a code-correct key on a Triton/MVP/Ilco. Alternatives: Mazda key codes are recoverable (stamped on the lock plug, and available through the dealer or NASTF SDRS by VIN) so you can originate purely by code if the locks are worn; try-out/jiggler sets also pop these tired wafer ignitions fast. On 4D63 cars you still must program the chip \u2014 cutting alone will crank but not start.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 4D63 PATS PEAK (~2004-2016: Mazda3 BK/BL, Mazda6 GG/GH, MX-5 NC, CX-7, CX-9, MPV, Protege)\nThis is the volume work. Blade MAZ24R-PT, chip 4D63 \u2014 40-bit early, 80-bit from about 2011. Add-a-key with one working key is a two-minute OBD job on any decent tool: Autel IM508/IM608, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Max/Plus + MINI OBD, SmartPro, MVP Pro. All-keys-lost is the real skill: many use the Ford-style PIN (outcode/incode). Your tool reads the outcode off OBD, converts to an incode (tool calculates, or NASTF code service / Ford PATS incode calculator), then runs onboard AKL to write the first keys. Where the OBD PIN pull fails or the module is stubborn, go to the bench: read the immobilizer/instrument-cluster EEPROM (93C66-class) with an Autel XP400 or Xhorse programmer, pull the PIN, program back. Don't forget the mechanical side \u2014 the transponder is dead metal until the blade is cut, so Lishi the door or pull-and-decode as in Era 1 to get a blade that turns the ignition, then do the electronics.\n\nFORD-PLATFORM ODDBALLS: The Tribute (2001-2011) is a Ford Escape \u2014 blade FO40R (H72/H92 family), chip 4D63, programs like a Ford (IDS, ForScan, or any PATS-capable aftermarket tool). The Mazda2 (DE, 2011-2014) shares Ford's B-platform and its add-key/AKL is a documented ForScan/PATS job \u2014 treat the electronics as Ford, but confirm the blade on the actual car before you cut.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 ID46 STRAGGLERS\nA handful of mid-cycle/market Mazdas show up with a Philips Crypto ID46 (PCF7936/Hitag2) instead of 4D63, on the same MAZ24R blade. It's trim/market variance, not a whole generation \u2014 which is exactly why you look it up per VIN. Programming is still OBD on Autel/Xhorse; the only thing that bites you is grabbing a 4D63 blank for an ID46 car and wondering why it won't onboard.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 SMART / PROXIMITY, ID49 (2014-2018)\nPush-to-start Mazda3 (BM), Mazda6 (GJ), CX-5 (KE), CX-3, CX-9 (TC), MX-5 (ND). Fob FCC WAZSKE13D01/D02, NXP PCF7953P Hitag-Pro ID49 128-bit, 315 MHz North America. Inside is a MAZ24R edge-cut emergency blade \u2014 so for AKL you still get IN and cut a mechanical blade exactly like the metal-key cars (Lishi the door, or decode the door cylinder), which drops the doors and lets you seat the fob for programming. Add-a-fob and AKL are OBD on Autel IM508/608 and Xhorse \u2014 AKL writes new smart keys after PIN, sometimes needing all old fobs erased. Keep dead ID49 fobs; you can renew/refresh them rather than always buying new.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 2019+ AND THE FIRMWARE WALL (mostly a myth if your tool is current)\nCurrent SkyActiv cars (Mazda3 BP, CX-30, CX-50, CX-90, refreshed CX-5) stayed on ID49 smart keys. Mazda tightened security, but aftermarket AKL by OBD is still landing: Autel IM608 II has done 2023 CX-5 AKL, Lonsdor K518 pulls a free PIN on 2019-2020 cars, Xhorse covers much of it \u2014 so this is rarely a tow-to-dealer. Two real cautions: (1) a genuine PCM/module reflash needs an online MDARS subscription and a Mazda VCM-II (dealer-level), and (2) some AFTERMARKET (non-OEM) fobs will program and start but throw a \"keyless entry system malfunction\" fault that won't clear \u2014 spend the money on OEM WAZSKE13D01 fobs on these late cars and run the tool's \"AKL on car\" routine to clear warnings. Before you quote a late AKL, check your tool's current coverage on that exact year.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST PROCEDURE (general Mazda)\n1. VIN it and look up the exact blade + chip/fob before you touch the car.\n2. Get IN and get CUTS: Original Lishi MAZ24 on the door, or pull the door/boot cylinder and bench-decode; fall back to NASTF/dealer key code by VIN, or try-out keys on old wafer ignitions. Cut a code-correct MAZ24R blade (FO40R on Tribute).\n3. Get the PIN: OBD outcode-to-incode on your tool (4D63 PATS), or bench-read the immobilizer/cluster EEPROM (XP400) and pull it. Smart cars: tool handles PIN over OBD (often free on Lonsdor).\n4. Program: onboard/OBD AKL to write the first transponder or smart key, then add the second. Verify crank-and-start, remote lock/unlock, and prox unlock before you pack up.\n\nTOOLS: Original Lishi MAZ24 (2-in-1 pick/decoder), plug follower + cylinder removal picks, Triton/MVP/Ilco code cutter, Autel IM608 II + XP400 Pro + APB112, Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Max/Plus + MINI OBD, SmartPro, Lonsdor K518 (late free-PIN AKL), ForScan + J2534 cable (Ford-platform Mazdas), MAZ24R-PT and FO40R blanks, 4D63 80-bit chips and OEM WAZSKE13D01 fobs on the shelf.\n\nFitment shifts by VIN, year, trim and market \u2014 confirm the exact blade and chip/fob on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or Ilco/Silca cross-reference) before you cut or order.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-mazda|Mazda WAZSKE13D03 4-button smart proximity fob \u2014 the modern Mazda smart key most locksmiths see on 2020-2025 CX-5/CX-9/Mazda3.]]\n\n*All Mazda keyways here are the edge-cut MAZ24R (Ilco MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT) \u2014 external wafer, Lishi MAZ24 fits the whole line. Mazda never went to a sidewinder/laser or Tibbe key. The ONLY exception is Ford-platform cars (Tribute, some Mazda2), which take the Ford edge-cut FO40R. Smart-key cars still carry an MAZ24R edge-cut emergency blade inside the fob. Confirm chip generation (40- vs 80-bit) and smart-vs-bladed by VIN before you cut. (Cross-reference the exact key profile/head style on lookup \u2014 Ilco MAZ24R \u2014 rather than trusting any single Ilco/Silca card number.)*\n\nMAZDA3 (2004-2018 HATCH/SEDAN; BK/BL THEN BM/BN)\n\u2022  2004-2013 (BK, BL) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut (confirm exact key profile on lookup) \u2192 blank Ilco MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT (RHK is F1Y1-76-2GX family) \u2192 Texas 4D63, 40-bit pre-~2011 / 80-bit after (same blank either way) \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24 on door or ignition; cut by code (VIN\u2192key code, dealer/online outcode-incode); 40-bit era is cloneable with a Texas cloner off a working key; onboard 2-key add if two working keys present; else OBD add/AKL (PIN via outcode/incode). *Split: 40-bit\u219280-bit around 2011 \u2014 an 80-bit key covers the older car, not the reverse.*\n\u2022  2014-2018 (BM/BN) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut emergency blade \u2192 smart fob FCC WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN GJY9-67-5DY (4-btn) \u2192 NXP PCF7953 = Hitag-Pro ID49 128-bit, 315 MHz \u2192 Path: OBD add or OBD AKL (Autel/Lonsdor/etc.); NOT cloneable. *Base trims shipped a bladed 4D63 80-bit key instead of prox \u2014 confirm per VIN; those follow the 2004-2013 row.*\n\nMAZDA3 (2019+ BP)\n\u2022  2019+ \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart ID49 (WAZSKE13D02 family) \u2192 Path: aftermarket OBD add/AKL still works (Lonsdor K518 pulls a free PIN on many 2019-2020+); NOT cloneable. Dealer-level PCM/module reflash needs online MDARS + Mazda VCM-II. Prefer OEM fobs.\n\nMAZDA6 (2003-2021; GG/GH THEN GJ/GL)\n\u2022  2003-2013 (GG, GH) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT (RHK F1Y1-76-2GX) \u2192 4D63, 40-bit early / 80-bit after ~2011 \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24; cut by code; clone (40-bit only, sniff off a working key); onboard 2-key add or OBD add/AKL.\n\u2022  2014-2021 (GJ/GL) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart fob WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN GJY9-67-5DY (4-btn) \u2192 ID49 128-bit \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL; not cloneable.\n\nCX-5 (2013-2024; KE THEN KF)\n\u2022  2013-2024 (KE, KF) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN TKY2-67-5DY (4-btn) \u2192 ID49 128-bit \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL (Autel IM608 II did a 2023 KF AKL by OBD); not cloneable. *KE and KF share the key method \u2014 no split. Post-2017 refresh only matters if a dealer module reflash is needed (MDARS + VCM-II); routine add/AKL is aftermarket OBD.* Use OEM fobs on current cars.\n\nCX-9 (2007-PRESENT; TB THEN TC)\n\u2022  2007-2015 (TB) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut (confirm exact key profile on lookup) \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT (remote-head key) \u2192 4D63, 40-bit early / 80-bit later \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24; cut by code; clone (40-bit only); onboard 2-key add or OBD add/AKL. *Note: this TB generation is a bladed transponder car, NOT smart \u2014 don't assume prox on an early CX-9.*\n\u2022  2016+ (TC) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN TKY2-67-5DY (4-btn) \u2192 ID49 128-bit \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL; not cloneable; dealer reflash = MDARS.\n\nCX-30 / CX-3 (2016-PRESENT)\n\u2022  CX-3 (DK, 2016-2021) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN confirm on lookup (KDY3-67-5DY 3-btn family) \u2192 ID49 128-bit \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL; not cloneable.\n\u2022  CX-30 (2020+) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart ID49 (WAZSKE13D02) \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL (Lonsdor free-PIN on many); not cloneable; dealer reflash = MDARS. OEM fobs preferred.\n\nMX-5 MIATA (1990-PRESENT; NA/NB/NC/ND)\n\u2022  1990-~2000 (NA, early NB) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut (earliest cars older MAZ21 blade) \u2192 mechanical, no chip \u2192 Path: cut and go \u2014 Lishi MAZ24 or cut by code; no programming.\n\u2022  ~2001-2015 (late NB, NC) \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT \u2192 4D63, 40-bit then 80-bit \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24; cut by code; clone (40-bit only); onboard 2-key add or OBD add/AKL. *Split: PATS/4D63 appears on late NB \u2014 pre-chip cars need no transponder, confirm by VIN.*\n\u2022  2016+ (ND) \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart fob WAZSKE13D01/D02, PN GJY9-67-5DY (4-btn, shared with Mazda3/6; confirm button count per VIN \u2014 some ND fobs are 3-btn) \u2192 ID49 128-bit \u2192 Path: OBD add/AKL; not cloneable.\n\nCX-7 (2007-2012, ER)\n\u2022  2007-2012 \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut (confirm exact key profile on lookup) \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT (remote-head key) \u2192 4D63, 40-bit early / 80-bit later \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24; cut by code; clone (40-bit only); onboard 2-key add or OBD add/AKL. Bladed transponder car, not smart.\n\nTRIBUTE (2001-2011) \u2014 FORD ESCAPE PLATFORM, PROGRAM LIKE A FORD\n\u2022  2001-2011 \u2192 Ford FO40R edge-cut (8-cut), NOT MAZ24R \u2192 blank Ilco FO40R \u2192 4D63 (Ford PATS) \u2192 Path: Lishi FO38/FO40 or cut by code; onboard 2-key add works (Ford PATS timed sequence); AKL needs a PATS tool (IDS / ForScan / equivalent) \u2014 ForScan AKL has the Ford security wait (~10 min) on many years. Do NOT reach for the Mazda MAZ24 blade or Mazda OBD flow on this car. *Earliest 2001-2004 blade \u2014 confirm FO40R vs earlier Ford profile on lookup.*\n\nMAZDA2 (DE, 2011-2014) \u2014 FORD-PLATFORM, PROGRAMS VIA FORSCAN/PATS\n\u2022  2011-2014 \u2192 blade per VIN: NA cars often still take MAZ24R, not FO40R \u2014 confirm before cutting \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT (or FO40R if Ford-blade VIN) \u2192 4D63 \u2192 Path: cut by code or Lishi (profile to match confirmed blade); program via ForScan/PATS flow, onboard 2-key add or OBD AKL. Ford-platform behavior despite the Mazda badge.\n\nProtege / Protege5 (1990-2003; BG/BH/BJ \u2014 BJ code covers only ~1999-2003)\n\u2022  1990-~2000 \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut (earliest early-'90s cars may take the older MAZ21/2-track blade \u2014 confirm on lookup) \u2192 mechanical, no chip \u2192 cut and go (Lishi MAZ24 or by code).\n\u2022  ~2001-2003 \u2192 MAZ24R edge-cut \u2192 blank MAZ24R / MAZ24R-PT \u2192 4D63 40-bit \u2192 Path: Lishi MAZ24; cut by code; clone (40-bit, sniff off a working key); onboard 2-key add or OBD add/AKL. *Split: transponder appears ~2001 \u2014 verify chip presence by VIN, some late Proteges are still no-chip.*\n\nCX-90 / CX-70 / CX-50 (2023+) \u2014 CURRENT LARGE SKYACTIV PLATFORM\n\u2022  2023+ \u2192 MAZ24R emergency blade \u2192 smart ID49 (WAZSKE13D02 family, PN confirm on lookup) \u2192 Path: aftermarket OBD add/AKL works on current tools (Autel IM608 II, Lonsdor free-PIN on many); not cloneable. Dealer-level module/PCM reflash needs online MDARS subscription + Mazda VCM-II. Use OEM fobs here.\n\n*Cross-check every blank and chip against a live VIN lookup (Transpondery / UHS Hardware / Ilco-JMA) before cutting \u2014 trims and markets vary, and some Mazdas carry ID46 (Hitag2/PCF7936) rather than 4D63 on select trims/markets; where a PN is marked \"confirm on lookup\" I did not have it verified.*",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/mazda_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2003-2016-mazda-transponder-key-4d63-80-bits-chip-maz24r-pt","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2014-2019-mazda-smart-prox-key-4b-fcc-wazske13d01-wazske13d02","https://www.honrow.com/For-Mazda-2-button-keyless-Smart-remote-key-with-315MHz-FSK-PCF7953P-HITAG-PRO-49-CHIP-FCC-ID-WAZSKE13D01-Model-SKE13D-01-p2075304.html","https://abkeys.com/products/mazda-cx-5-cx-9-smart-key-4buttons-315mhz-wazske13d01-tky2-67-5dy-2336","https://abkeys.com/products/mazda-tribute-transponder-key-4d63-chip-fo40r-2005up-690212-698","http://blog.autelshop.de/autel-im608-ii-program-2023-mazda-cx-5-all-keys-lost-by-obd/","https://medium.com/@obd2eshopcom/lonsdor-k518ise-program-2020-mazda-3-all-key-lost-free-pin-98546bd99444","http://blog.obdii365.com/2022/05/06/solve-mazda-smart-key-works-but-keyless-system-error/","https://mazdas247.com/forum/threads/mazda2-additional-key-programming-you-can-with-forscan.123883812/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-mazda-maz24","http://locksmithcharley.com/transponder/mazda.shtml"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["PATS","Lishi","Fob","Tibbe key","Transponder","Push-to-start","Key code","Immobilizer"],aliases:["Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","Ford Tibbe","Jaguar key","6-disc key","Tibbe pick","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","proximity key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","immo","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","rekey tool","pins fell out","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"autokeys-subaru",title:"Making keys: Subaru",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Subaru is one of the friendlier Japanese makes to originate, right up until the newest push-to-start cars where a few years fight you on all-keys-lost. The blade only comes in two flavors across the whole history \u2014 a plain edge-cut and one high-security laser blade \u2014 so once you know the year you know the tool. What changes is the chip and, on later cars, whether you can touch it over OBD or have to lean on a key simulator or pull a module. Confirm the exact blade/chip on the VIN before you cut; Subaru shared its edge-cut keyway with Nissan and there are overlaps.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path)\n- ~1990\u20131998 non-immobilizer: Ilco X251 / SUB1 edge-cut (NSN14 keyway), NO chip \u2192 cut and go.\n- ~1998\u20132007 edge-cut transponder: Ilco SUB1-PT / SUB4-PT (NSN14), chip Texas 4D-62 (40-bit) \u2192 clone or OBD add.\n- 2008\u20132014 high-security: DAT17T13 laser blade (Ilco/Silca DAT17), chip 4D-62 40-bit early, 80-bit DST80 \"G\" chip on later cars \u2192 OBD add / AKL with aftermarket tool.\n- ~2012\u20132019 edge-cut \"G\": Ilco B110/SUB1 transponder, Subaru \"G chip\" = DST80 (labeled 4D-82/4D-60, ID82, \"6E-17\"), 80-bit \u2192 OBD add / AKL.\n- 2014\u20132020 smart (push start): fob HYQ14AHC, 315 MHz, board 281451/231451-5801, G-chip DST80 \u2192 OBD add, most AKL doable.\n- 2018\u20132025 smart (push start): fob HYQ14AHK, 434 MHz, board 231451-7000, chip 8A / H (DST-AES 128-bit), PN 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add; 2020+ AKL needs a key simulator, 2023+ can fight.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 Pre-immobilizer, ~1990\u20131998 (Legacy, Impreza, Loyale, SVX)\nPlain edge-cut metal key, Ilco X251 / SUB1, keyway NSN14 (Subaru rode on the Nissan/Datsun keyway family \u2014 the tool world calls it NSN14/DA34, not \"DAT13\"). No electronics. Getting in and getting the cuts is old-school: wedge and long-reach through the door, or better, pull the door lock and bench-decode, or read a code stamped on a tag or the lock. To originate AKL, pick the door with a Lishi NSN14 (2-in-1 for that keyway), decode the wafers, cut and go. Impressioning works fine on these soft wafer locks if you don't have a decoder. No chip, so once it's cut, it starts.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 Edge-cut transponder, ~1998\u20132007 (Legacy, Outback, Forester, Impreza, Baja)\nSame NSN14 edge-cut blade, but now there's a Texas 4D-62 transponder (40-bit, Subaru's encrypted 4C-derived chip) in the head. Blank is Ilco SUB1-PT / SUB4-PT (transponder version). Getting the cuts is the same menu as Era 1: run a Lishi NSN14 on the door or ignition \u2014 it reads 8 cuts (positions 3\u201310) off the door and all 10 off the ignition \u2014 or pull and bench-decode the cylinder, or pull the code from Subaru by VIN through NASTF/dealer. For the chip: with one working key, cloning the 4D-62 onto a blank is the fast play (RW4-class cloner handles the 40-bit Texas 4D). AKL, cut the mechanical key then program the transponder over OBD with an aftermarket immobilizer tool \u2014 Subaru add on this generation does not require a PIN.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 High-security laser, 2008\u20132014 (Impreza/WRX/STI, Forester, Legacy, Outback, Tribeca)\nThis is the big shift: high-security laser (sidewinder) blade, DAT17T13, Ilco/Silca DAT17, 10-cut 4-track. Chip is Texas 4D \u2014 early 2008\u20132011 cars are 40-bit 4D-62, later cars moved to the 80-bit DST80 \"G\" chip (catalogs list it 4D-60/4D-82) \u2014 so verify which is on the VIN before you plan to clone, because the 80-bit DST80 does NOT clone the easy way. This is where the Lishi DAT17 (2-in-1 pick & decoder, HS) earns its keep. Getting in and getting the cuts: run the Lishi DAT17 on the DOOR or TRUNK lock, pick it, decode the bitting straight off the tool \u2014 that's your whole origination for the blade. The WRX ignition has a known tight-insertion issue; use the DAT17+IGN version for the ignition, or just decode the door/trunk and cut from that. If the Lishi fights you, pull the door lock and bench-decode. Cut on a laser/HS machine (Condor XC-Mini/Dolphin, Triton HS, Futura). Program: on a 40-bit car with a working key you can clone; on 80-bit and AKL either way, cut the DAT17 blade then add the transponder over OBD with SmartPro, Autel IM508/IM608, Lonsdor, or Xhorse.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 \"G chip\" edge-cut + first smart keys, ~2012\u20132019\nSubaru went two directions at once. Base/mid trims went BACK to an edge-cut B110/SUB1 blade but with the newer Subaru \"G chip\" \u2014 that's a DST80 (80-bit) transponder, cataloged as 4D-82/4D-60, ID82, \"6E-17\" (NOT ID83/88, and it's a different animal from the older 40-bit 4D-62). Push-to-start trims got a proximity smart fob, HYQ14AHC (315 MHz, board 281451/231451-5801), running the same G/DST80 transponder. Blade origination on the edge-cut cars is easy \u2014 Lishi NSN14 on the door, decode, cut. The smart cars have a mechanical emergency blade in the fob and a hidden door keyway under a cap; you can still Lishi the door for a bench/entry key if you need in, but the fob does the starting. DST80 is program-only, not clone-and-go, so program over OBD: SmartPro, Autel IM608, Lonsdor K518. Add is straightforward; AKL on this window is generally doable with aftermarket tools.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 Modern smart keys, 2018\u20132025 (Legacy, Outback, Forester, Ascent, Crosstrek, Impreza, WRX)\nProximity push-to-start, fob HYQ14AHK, 434 MHz, board 231451-7000, part numbers 88835-FL0xx / 88835-AN00C. Note the chip changed: this fob runs an 8A / H chip (DST-AES, 128-bit), not the older G/DST80. Emergency blade is an edge-cut tucked in the fob; a hidden door keyway exists for mechanical entry. Add-key with a working fob is fine over OBD (IM608/IM508, Lonsdor, SmartPro). AKL is where it gets real: 2020\u20132022 is doable but usually needs a key simulator \u2014 Autel IM608/IM508 with the APB112 simulator generates an analog key and pulls the immo data, and Lonsdor (LT20 smart + K518/KM100) is the other proven path. It is NOT flatly dealer-only. The catch is the newest cars: 2023+ Outback/others have burned techs where Autel/OBDSTAR failed and needed updated software or a bench fallback. Always check current tool coverage for the exact year before you commit.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 the working procedure\n1. Identify: VIN \u2192 confirm blade (edge-cut vs DAT17 HS), chip (40-bit 4D-62 vs 80-bit DST80 \"G\" vs 8A/H smart), and transponder vs smart fob on a lookup before touching the car.\n2. Get in / get the cuts (pick your entry off the make, don't just \"Lishi every door\"):\n   - HS 2008\u20132014: Lishi DAT17 on the DOOR or TRUNK, pick and decode \u2014 that IS your cut. Use DAT17+IGN for stubborn WRX ignitions.\n   - Edge-cut eras: Lishi NSN14 on the door (8 cuts, positions 3\u201310) or ignition (all 10); or pull and bench-decode the cylinder.\n   - Any era: pull the key code from Subaru by VIN via NASTF SDRS / dealer, or read a stamped code if present; impression the soft wafer locks as a fallback.\n3. Cut: HS blade on a laser/HS machine (Condor, Triton, Futura); edge-cut on any code/duplicator.\n4. Program: OBD add/AKL with Autel IM508/IM608, SmartPro, Lonsdor K518, or Xhorse \u2014 no PIN on older immobilizer add. Clone only the 40-bit 4D-62 (1998\u20132011) with a working key; DST80 and 8A are program-only. On 2020+ smart AKL expect to need the APB112 or Lonsdor LT20 simulator.\n5. Wall on newest cars (some 2023+ smart AKL): if OBD/security access is blocked or the tool errors, fall back to updated software or bench work \u2014 read the immobilizer/BIU EEPROM to pull data. Confirm the module and tool support first; don't open a car on a guess.\n\nTOOLS: Lishi DAT17 (HS door/ign/trunk) and Lishi NSN14/DA34 (edge-cut door/ign/trunk); Autel IM508/IM608 with APB112 simulator, SmartPro, Lonsdor K518/KM100 with LT20, Xhorse VVDI; HS cutter (Condor XC-Mini/Dolphin, Triton, Futura) plus a standard code cutter; RW4-class cloner for 40-bit 4D-62; wedge/long-reach for mechanical entry.\n\nChip families, blanks, Lishi keyways and programming paths shift by exact VIN \u2014 before you cut or program, verify the blade + chip/fob for that specific VIN on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or Ilco/Silca cross-reference).\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-subaru|Subaru 4-button smart fob (HYQ14AHC / 88835-AL04A) \u2014 the push-to-start proximity key on most 2014-2020 Subaru jobs.]]\n\nRead your build first: key turns in a barrel = edge-cut metal key (Subaru stayed on the NSN14 edge-cut blade far longer than most makes); push-button start = smart/prox fob, no ignition barrel. Subaru almost never went to a high-security laser blade on mainstream cars \u2014 if you see a milled/sidewinder blade, confirm it (Lishi DAT17 / DAT17T13) because it's the exception, not the rule. Blank + chip below map straight onto the era table above.\n\nOne lineup-wide gotcha: Subaru swapped the smart-fob platform mid-stream at model year 2018 \u2014 from HYQ14AHC (315 MHz, DST80 \"G\") to HYQ14AHK (434 MHz, 8A/H DST-AES). That switch lands *inside* the Legacy, Outback and Impreza generations, so read the model year, not just the generation.\n\nLEGACY (1990\u20132024)\n\u2022  1990\u20131999 (BC/BF, BD/BG) \u2192 edge-cut, keyway NSN14 \u2192 Ilco X251 / SUB1 \u2192 no chip (pre-immobilizer) \u2192 Lishi NSN14 to pick/decode the door, impression the wafer lock, or pull code from lock/tag; cut and go.\n\u2022  2000\u20132009 (BE/BH, BL/BP) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 Ilco SUB1-PT / SUB4-PT \u2192 Texas 4D-62 40-bit \u2192 clone a working key (4D-62 sniffs/clones to TPX-style) is fastest; else OBD add key (Autel IM508/608, SmartPro, XTool); AKL = Lishi NSN14 to originate blade + OBD onboard; cuts from code via NASTF/dealer.\n\u2022  2010\u20132014 (BM/BR) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 on keyed trims, Ilco B110/SUB1 \u2192 Subaru G chip DST80 (80-bit, \"4D-82/ID82\") on most cars (early 2010\u20132011 builds may still be Texas 4D-62 \u2014 confirm by VIN) \u2192 G-chip not cloneable \u2192 OBD add / AKL via Lishi NSN14 + capable tool. Push-start trims from ~2013 \u2192 smart HYQ14ACX / HYQ14AHC, 315 MHz, G/DST80 \u2192 OBD add, AKL doable.\n\u2022  2015\u20132019 (6th gen) \u2192 smart standard, split mid-generation: 2015\u20132017 = HYQ14AHC, 315 MHz, board 281451 / 231451-5801, G DST80 \u2192 OBD add, AKL doable via OBD; 2018\u20132019 switched to HYQ14AHK, 434 MHz, board 231451-7000, chip 8A / H (DST-AES), PN 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add, AKL needs a key simulator. Base keyed trims = edge-cut G-chip B110.\n\u2022  2020\u20132024 (7th gen) \u2192 smart \u2192 HYQ14AHK, 434 MHz, board 231451-7000, chip 8A / H (DST-AES 128-bit), PN 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add; AKL needs a key simulator/emulator, 2023+ can fight the gateway.\n\nOutback (1995\u20132024) \u2014 tracks Legacy (Legacy-derived through 2019, own platform 2020+).\n\u2022  1995\u20131999 \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 Ilco X251/SUB1 \u2192 no chip \u2192 pick/impression/code, cut and go.\n\u2022  2000\u20132009 \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT / SUB4-PT \u2192 4D-62 \u2192 clone, or OBD add; AKL Lishi NSN14 + tool.\n\u2022  2010\u20132014 \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 (B110, G DST80; early 2010\u20132011 may still be 4D-62, confirm by VIN) OR early smart HYQ14ACX/AHC 315 \u2192 OBD add / AKL.\n\u2022  2015\u20132019 \u2192 smart, split mid-generation: 2015\u20132017 = HYQ14AHC 315, 5801 G board, DST80 \u2192 OBD add; 2018\u20132019 = HYQ14AHK 434, 8A/H, PN 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add, AKL needs simulator.\n\u2022  2020\u20132024 \u2192 smart HYQ14AHK 434, 8A/H, 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add; AKL needs simulator.\n\nFORESTER (1998\u20132024)\n\u2022  1998\u20132002 (SF) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 X251/SUB1 early (no chip) \u2192 SUB1-PT + 4D-62 once immobilizer arrives (~2000, confirm on lookup by VIN) \u2192 pick/impression/code early; clone or OBD once chipped.\n\u2022  2003\u20132013 (SG, SH) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT (4D-62) early, transitions to G DST80 (B110) on later SH (~2011+) \u2192 4D-62 years clone or OBD; G-chip years OBD add / AKL Lishi NSN14, no clone.\n\u2022  2014\u20132018 (SJ) \u2192 keyed base = edge-cut NSN14 G-chip B110; push-start = smart HYQ14AHC 315, DST80 \u2192 OBD add / AKL.\n\u2022  2019\u20132024 (SK) \u2192 smart HYQ14AHK 434, 8A/H \u2192 OBD add; AKL simulator.\n\nImpreza (1993\u20132024) (incl. RS/Sport; WRX split out below)\n\u2022  1993\u20132001 (GC/GM/GF) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 X251/SUB1 \u2192 no chip (pre-immob) \u2192 pick/impression/code, cut and go.\n\u2022  2002\u20132011 (GD/GG, GE/GH/GR/GV) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT \u2192 4D-62 \u2192 clone or OBD add; AKL Lishi NSN14 + tool.\n\u2022  2012\u20132016 (GJ/GP) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 Ilco B110 \u2192 G DST80 \u2192 not cloneable \u2192 OBD add / AKL Lishi NSN14.\n\u2022  2017\u20132023 (GK/GT) \u2192 smart, split mid-generation: 2017\u20132018 = HYQ14AHC 315 MHz, G DST80 \u2192 OBD add / AKL via OBD; 2019\u20132023 = HYQ14AHK 434 MHz, 8A/H \u2192 OBD add, AKL needs simulator. Base keyed trims edge-cut G-chip B110 (confirm trim).\n\nCROSSTREK / XV CROSSTREK (2013\u20132024)\n\u2022  2013\u20132017 (GP) \u2192 keyed base = edge-cut NSN14 G-chip B110 (DST80); push-start = smart HYQ14AHC 315, 5801 board \u2192 OBD add / AKL, no clone on G.\n\u2022  2018\u20132023 (GT) \u2192 smart HYQ14AHK 434, 8A/H, 88835-FL0xx \u2192 OBD add; AKL simulator.\n\u2022  2024 (new gen) \u2192 smart HYQ14AHK-class 434, 8A/H \u2192 OBD add; confirm PN/AKL on lookup.\n\nWRX / WRX STI (2002\u20132021) (Impreza-based, keyed longer than the economy cars)\n\u2022  2002\u20132007 (GD) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT \u2192 4D-62 \u2192 clone or OBD add.\n\u2022  2008\u20132014 (GE/GV, GR/GV STI) \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT (4D-62) \u2192 G DST80 (B110) on later cars \u2192 clone only on 4D-62 years; G-chip years OBD add / AKL Lishi NSN14.\n\u2022  2015\u20132021 (VA) \u2192 keyed base edge-cut G-chip B110 (STI held the metal key longest); smart trims = HYQ14AHC 315 (2016\u20132018) then HYQ14AHK 434 8A (2019\u20132021) \u2192 OBD add; AKL Lishi NSN14 on keyed, simulator on 434 smart.\n\nAscent (2019\u20132024) \u2014 smart only, no barrel.\n\u2022  2019\u20132024 \u2192 smart HYQ14AHK (some listed HYQ14AKB \u2014 confirm on lookup) 434 MHz, board 231451-7000, chip 8A / H, PN 88835-XC0xx (confirm) \u2192 OBD add key; AKL needs key simulator, later years fight the gateway.\n\nTribeca / B9 Tribeca (2006\u20132014) \u2014 edge-cut throughout.\n\u2022  2006\u20132014 \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT (4D-62) early \u2192 later cars G DST80 (B110) (confirm split year by VIN) \u2192 4D-62 clone or OBD; G-chip OBD add / AKL Lishi NSN14.\n\nBaja (2003\u20132006) \u2014 edge-cut only.\n\u2022  2003\u20132006 \u2192 edge-cut NSN14 \u2192 SUB1-PT / SUB4-PT \u2192 4D-62 40-bit \u2192 clone a working key, or OBD add; AKL Lishi NSN14 + aftermarket tool; cuts from code via NASTF/dealer.\n\nBRZ (2013\u20132024) \u2014 Toyota co-developed (86/BRZ), immobilizer differs from mainstream Subaru; verify chip on the tool.\n\u2022  2013\u20132020 (ZC6) \u2192 keyed remote-head = HYQ12BBY 4-btn G-chip remote head key, edge-cut blade (Toyota-derived DST80 \"G\" \u2014 confirm keyway/blank on lookup, not the standard NSN14/B110) \u2192 OBD add; smart trims = HYQ14ACX (2013\u20132014) / HYQ14AHC (2014\u20132017) 315 MHz, DST80 \u2192 OBD add / AKL.\n\u2022  2022\u20132024 (ZD8, 2nd gen) \u2192 smart push-start, 434 MHz 8A/H-class (confirm FCC/PN \u2014 Toyota-side platform) \u2192 OBD add; AKL simulator, confirm on lookup.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/subaru_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2008-2011-subaru-dat17t13-high-security-transponder-key-4d62-chip-k-sub3","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/subaru-transponder-key-dat17t13-everything-you-need-to-know","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/gm-subaru-b110-transponder-key-subaru-g-chip-k-b110","https://www.reidsremotes.com.au/home/1764-subaru-4d82-dst80-tp34-subaru-g-chip-transponder-chip-idsub-g-9351307002824.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/subaru-g-chip-texas-id-4d60-80-bit-oem","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-nissan-ford-subaru-nsn14-2-in-1-pick-decoder-door-trunk","https://transponderisland.com/shop/tik-ilc-sub1-ilco-x251-sub1-subaru-key-blank-2099","https://covertinstruments.com/products/automotive-lishi-subaru-dat17","https://yourcarkeyguys.com/products/hyq14ahc-subaru-2014-2020-4b-4-button-smart-key-fob-remote","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2017-2022-subaru-smart-key-4b-fcc-hyq14ahk","https://northcoastkeyless.com/product/subaru-outback-smart-key-fob-remote-fcc-hyq14ahk-p-n-88835fl03a-g-board-231451-7000/","https://help.autosvs.com/kb/tips-to-program-2020-2021-subaru-all-smart-keys-lost-with-autel-im608-im508/","https://www.key-programmer.org/2024/06/05/autel-km100-program-2020-subaru-crosstek-all-keys-lost/","https://blog.obdii365.com/2026/02/08/autel-obdstar-failed-to-program-subaru-outback-2023-all-key-lost-solution/","https://subaru.oemdtc.com/238/replacement-key-and-immobilizer-information-2000-subaru-to-current-all-models"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Fob","Push-to-start","Transponder","Immobilizer","Decoder","Keyway","Duplicator"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","smart key","keyless go","start button","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","HPC","key copier","key cutting machine","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{body:"Mitsubishi is one of the friendlier Japanese makes to originate on. The blades stay edge-cut the whole way through the mainstream line \u2014 no laser/sidewinder, no Tibbe, ever \u2014 so you're working an 8-cut or 10-cut wafer lock. The mechanical cut is never the hard part; the chip and the 4-digit PIN are. Watch your era, because three things move: the keyway (MIT8 8-cut vs. MIT11 10-cut), the transponder (4D60/4D61 fixed-code \u2192 ID46/Hitag2 crypto \u2192 ID47/Hitag3 crypto \u2192 Hitag-AES/ID4A smart), and the platform. The oddball is the 4th-gen 2022+ Outlander: it rides the Renault-Nissan CMF platform, uses a Nissan Rogue BCM and a repackaged Nissan-style AES smart key \u2014 treat it as a Nissan job, not a Mitsubishi one.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 keyway \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 programming path):\n\n- ~1990s\u20132000: no chip, or early fixed 4D. Blank Ilco MIT1/MIT3 (MIT8 8-cut) or MIT4 (10-cut). Cut and go.\n- ~2000\u20132008: 4D60/4D61 (Texas fixed-code) transponder, chip letter \"N.\" Blank Ilco MIT8 (also seen MIT12/MIT13 blade variants). Cloneable; onboard or OBD add.\n- ~2003\u20132019: ID46 (Hitag2, PCF7936/7961), chip letter \"A\"/\"46.\" Blank MIT3/MIT16A/MIT17A on the MIT11 10-cut keyway. OBD add or AKL with PIN.\n- ~2007\u20132021: ID46 remote-head keys (RHK), 3-button, MIT11 keyway. OBD, PIN for AKL.\n- ~2008\u20132021: FAST-Key proximity smart, ID46/Hitag2 (PCF7952A), emergency blade MIT11R. PN 8637A662 (433MHz), 8637C803 (315MHz). OBD add/AKL with PIN or bypass cable.\n- ~2016+ (L200/Triton, Eclipse Cross, later Attrage/Space Star, some ASX): ID47/Hitag3 (NCF2951X/NCF29A1X, \"ID52\" in diag-speak), MIT11R blade. Eclipse Cross FCC OUCJ166N, PN 6370C135/8637C153/8637C984. OBD/BCM with PIN.\n- 2022+ Outlander (4th gen): Nissan platform, Hitag-AES ID4A, FCC KR5MTXN1, PN 8637C251/252/254, 433MHz. Nissan-style key. OBD + BCM40/40-pin bypass cable.\n\nVerify the exact blank+chip on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS, Ilco) against the VIN before you cut or buy chips \u2014 keyway and chip family shift mid-model-run.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 Pre-transponder / early fixed (pre-2000 into early 2000s). Diamante, Galant, Mirage, Montero, early Eclipse/Lancer. Plain wafer locks, 8-cut. Blank Ilco MIT1/MIT3 on the MIT8 keyway. AKL is pure mechanical: Lishi the door, decode, cut, done. Later cars in this window carry a fixed-code 4D that onboard procedures accept.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 4D60/4D61 fixed-code (~2000\u20132008). Eclipse, Galant, Endeavor, Lancer, Montero/Montero Sport, Diamante. Still edge-cut 8-cut on MIT8; blank Ilco MIT8, chip letter \"N.\" The 4D61 is a Texas fixed-code chip \u2014 no rolling crypto \u2014 so cloning works: read a working key onto a Texas 4D transponder with a TinyTag/Xhorse/JMA cloner and it starts. For origination without a working key, add via OBD or the documented turn-key onboard learn.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 ID46 / Hitag2 crypto, turn-key and remote-head (~2003\u20132019). The bread-and-butter Mitsubishi: Outlander, Lancer, Eclipse, Galant, Endeavor, Mirage, Outlander Sport/RVR/ASX, i-MiEV. Keyway moves to the MIT11 10-cut; blank Ilco MIT3/MIT16A/MIT17A (PCF7936/7961). ID46 is crypto \u2014 you cannot clone it; every key is programmed to the immobilizer. Add-a-key and AKL both go through OBD with a 4-digit PIN. Mitsubishi PINs are not free-read off the BCM the way a Nissan BCM code is \u2014 you pull it with a capable tool that calculates/reads it, or request it from the dealer against the VIN (they won't release a code if the transponder ID already shows assigned to another VIN).\n\nERA 4 \u2014 ID46/Hitag2 FAST-Key smart proximity (~2008\u20132021). Push-button-start Outlander, ASX, Lancer, Mirage, Outlander PHEV on the older platforms. Proximity fob, ID46/Hitag2 (PCF7952A), with an emergency MIT11R blade inside for the door and backup slot. PN 8637A662 (433MHz) or 8637C803 (315MHz). Origination is OBD only \u2014 no onboard self-learn for smart keys. AKL means all fobs synced fresh, PIN required.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 ID47/Hitag3 crypto (~2016+). L200/Triton and Shogun/Pajero Sport (2016+), Eclipse Cross (2018+), later Attrage/Space Star, and some late ASX. Chip jumps to Hitag3 (ID47, NCF2951X/NCF29A1X \u2014 also called \"ID52\" in some diag menus). Blade still MIT11R, still edge-cut. Eclipse Cross keys read FCC OUCJ166N with PNs 6370C135 / 8637C153 / 8637C984 across years. This is a common miss: do not order an ID46 for an Eclipse Cross \u2014 it's ID47 and won't learn. OBD/BCM programming, PIN required.\n\nERA 6 \u2014 4th-gen 2022+ Outlander, the Nissan oddball. Built on Renault-Nissan CMF, Nissan Rogue BCM, repackaged Nissan smart key: Hitag-AES ID4A, FCC KR5MTXN1, PN 8637C251/252/254, 433MHz, 2/3/4-button. Emergency blade is a Nissan-style edge-cut blade, mechanical door only. Origination is OBD with a Nissan-style flow, and the PIN is bypassed with the right tool plus a BCM40/40-pin cable rather than a dealer code. Program with Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus / Max Pro (+ Xhorse BCM40 cable) or OBDSTAR X300 DP Plus/G3 (+ OBDSTAR 40-pin cable). Think Rogue, not Mitsubishi.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST PROCEDURE:\n\n1) Identify precisely. VIN \u2192 year/model/trim, confirm turn-key vs. push-button, then run the VIN on a transponder lookup for the exact blank + chip before you cut. Keyway (MIT8 vs MIT11) and chip letter change mid-model.\n\n2) Get in and get the cut. In the order I actually reach for it:\n   - Lishi first. MIT8 2-in-1 for the older 8-cut ignition/door; MIT11 2-in-1 for the 10-cut \u2014 note there are separate MIT11 door/boot and MIT11 ignition tools. Pick it, read the wafers off the tool, bitting straight from the lock. On smart cars, pull the door lock or the emergency-blade slot \u2014 you only need the mechanical blade cut; the fob starts it.\n   - Pull and bench-decode. Worn or shrouded ignition? Yank the door cylinder and decode on the bench for a cleaner read.\n   - Code service / dealer. Mitsubishi cylinders carry no usable street code stamp, so cuts come from Lishi/decode; the PIN comes from NASTF/dealer against the VIN.\n   - Impression is a fine backup on these simple 8/10-cut wafer locks if you're toolless, but Lishi is faster.\n\n3) Cut the blade. MIT8 for the 8-cut era; MIT3/MIT16A/MIT17A (MIT11 keyway) for the 10-cut era; MIT11R emergency blade for smart. Duplicator or code-cut to the decoded bitting.\n\n4) Handle the chip/PIN. Get the 4-digit PIN \u2014 calculate/read with your programmer if it supports it, or request from Mitsubishi. Then OBD: add key or full AKL sync. On the 2022+ Outlander, run the Nissan-style AES flow with the BCM40/40-pin bypass. WARNING: repeated wrong PIN attempts lock you out; on AKL cars, cycling an unprogrammed key in the ignition can trip theft/immobilizer lockout \u2014 leave ignition on and wait the relearn timeout before retrying. Don't rush the count.\n\n5) Verify all keys, prox/remote functions, lock/unlock/panic, and start before you leave.\n\nTOOLS: Original Lishi MIT8 (ign) and MIT11 (door/trunk + ign) 2-in-1 pick/decoders; a code machine or duplicator for the edge cuts; MIT8 and MIT3/MIT16A/MIT17A blanks plus loose ID46/ID47 chips or cut-to-code transponder keys; a Texas 4D cloner (only useful on 4D60/4D61 fixed-code cars \u2014 useless on ID46/ID47/AES). Programmer/PIN: Mitsubishi MUT-III with CAN pass-through and a key-disbursement subscription is the dealer path; aftermarket, an Autel IM508/IM608, OBDSTAR X300 DP Plus / Pro4 / G3 (with the 40-pin cable for late smart keys), or Xhorse VVDI Key Tool Plus (+ BCM40 cable) covers most add/AKL and PIN work.\n\nBlade stays edge-cut across every mainstream Mitsubishi era \u2014 no laser, no Tibbe. What shifts is keyway (MIT8\u2192MIT11) and chip (4D60/61 \u2192 ID46 \u2192 ID47 \u2192 AES/ID4A), plus the platform swap on the 2022+ Outlander. Confirm the specific blank + chip on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS, Ilco) for the VIN in front of you before you cut and buy chips.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-mitsubishi|Mitsubishi 3-button smart fob (OUC644M) with emergency insert blade \u2014 the smart key most Mitsubishi jobs land on.]]\n\nRows are grouped so consecutive generations that key identically sit on one line; a new line only appears where the blade, blank, chip, or programming path actually changes. Split years are called out. Always confirm blank+chip against the VIN on a transponder lookup before cutting \u2014 Mitsubishi shifts keyway and chip family mid-run, and trim level (base vs prox) changes the answer on the same model-year.\n\nLANCER / LANCER SPORTBACK / LANCER EVOLUTION\n\u2022  Lancer (2002\u20132007, 9th-gen CS/CH) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT8 \u2192 early cars 4D60/4D61 fixed \"N,\" later cars ID46 \"A\" (split ~2003\u20132004, VIN-verify) \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD add with a working key; 4D60 clones to TPX2/CN1/EH2, ID46 clones with a crypto-capable device; AKL by OBD + PIN.\n\u2022  Lancer / Sportback / Evo X (2008\u20132017, CJ/CY/CZ) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut, MIT11 keyway \u2192 Ilco MIT3 / MIT16A / MIT17A \u2192 ID46 (Hitag2, PCF7936/7961) chip \"A\"; 3-button remote-head key on same blade also OEM \u2192 Lishi MIT11; OBD add is the fast path; ID46 clones with a Hitag2-capable cloner; AKL by OBD + PIN (PIN from VIN/dealer or pulled from immo EEPROM).\n\nOUTLANDER (SUV \u2014 NOT OUTLANDER SPORT)\n\u2022  Outlander (2003\u20132006, gen1 CU) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut, MIT8 keyway (shares the CS Lancer platform) \u2192 Ilco MIT8 (also MIT1/MIT3) \u2192 early 4D60/4D61 \"N,\" later ID46 \"A\" (split ~2005, VIN-verify) \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD add with a working key; 4D60 clones to TPX2/CN, ID46 clones with a crypto cloner; AKL by OBD + PIN.\n\u2022  Outlander (2007\u20132013, gen2 CW/GF) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut, MIT11 keyway \u2192 Ilco MIT17A (also MIT3) \u2192 ID46 \"A\"; base = transponder/RHK, and from ~2007 the FAST-Key prox smart appears on higher trims (PN 8637A662 433MHz / 8637C803 315MHz, emergency blade MIT11R) \u2192 Lishi MIT11 for the mechanical; OBD add with working key; ID46 clone for transponder blades only (prox is not clone); AKL by OBD + PIN or bypass cable.\n\u2022  Outlander (2014\u20132021, gen3 GF/GG, incl. PHEV) \u2192 laser/emergency blade MIT11R (prox is standard; base transponder still MIT11 blade) \u2192 blank MIT11R prox / MIT17A transponder \u2192 FAST-Key ID46/Hitag2 (PCF7952A); watch for late-run ID47/Hitag3 \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 OBD/BCM add; AKL OBD + PIN or bypass cable; no clone on prox.\n\u2022  Outlander (2022+, gen4 GN \u2014 Nissan CMF platform) \u2192 laser emergency blade, Nissan-style key \u2192 OEM PN 8637C251/252/254 (blade sold separately; confirm exact PN on lookup) \u2192 Hitag-AES ID4A (NCF29A1M), FCC KR5MTXN1, 433MHz \u2192 OBD + BCM/40-pin bypass cable, PIN required; not cloneable, no aftermarket transponder \u2014 buy OEM/OEM-grade fob.\n\nOutlander Sport / RVR / ASX (GA0 compact CUV \u2014 one platform, three market names)\n\u2022  2011\u20132024 \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut, MIT11 keyway; prox trims use MIT11R emergency blade \u2192 Ilco MIT17A (transponder) / MIT11R (prox) \u2192 ID46 \"A\" transponder or FAST-Key prox (prox PN varies \u2014 confirm on lookup); late-run cars may run ID47 \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 Lishi MIT11; OBD add; ID46 clone for transponder blades; AKL by OBD + PIN or bypass cable.\n\nMIRAGE / MIRAGE G4 / ATTRAGE / SPACE STAR\n\u2022  Mirage (1990s\u20132002, older gens) \u2192 edge-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT1/MIT3 (MIT8 keyway) \u2192 no chip or early fixed 4D \u2192 Lishi MIT8, cut-and-go / mechanical AKL; onboard for the fixed-4D cars.\n\u2022  Mirage / G4 / Attrage / Space Star (2012\u20132024, current gen) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut, MIT11 keyway \u2192 Ilco MIT17A / MIT3 \u2192 ID46 \"A,\" typically remote-head key (no prox on most trims); watch for late-run Attrage/Space Star ID47/Hitag3 \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 Lishi MIT11; OBD add; ID46 clone; AKL OBD + PIN.\n\nECLIPSE (COUPE/SPYDER \u2014 THE SPORT CAR, NOT ECLIPSE CROSS)\n\u2022  Eclipse (1990\u20131999, 1G/2G) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT1/MIT3 (MIT8) \u2192 no chip \u2192 Lishi MIT8, cut-and-go, mechanical AKL.\n\u2022  Eclipse (2000\u20132012, 3G/4G) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT8 \u2192 4D60/4D61 \"N\" on early cars, ID46 \"A\" on later 4G \u2014 VIN-verify the split (~2006) \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD add; 4D60 clones to TPX2/CN, ID46 clones with crypto cloner; AKL OBD + PIN.\n\nECLIPSE CROSS (CUV \u2014 2018+)\n\u2022  2018\u20132024 \u2192 laser emergency blade MIT11R \u2192 prox smart (blade sold separately) \u2192 ID47/Hitag3 (NCF2951X/NCF29A1X), FAST-Key prox, FCC OUCJ166N, PN 6370C135 / 8637C153 / 8637C984 \u2192 OBD/BCM with PIN; not cloneable \u2014 OBD add or AKL only.\n\nGALANT\n\u2022  Galant (1990s\u20132003) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT1/MIT3 (MIT8) \u2192 no chip / early fixed 4D \u2192 Lishi MIT8, cut-and-go or onboard.\n\u2022  Galant (2004\u20132012, 9th-gen US) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT8 \u2192 4D60/4D61 \"N\" \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD add; 4D60 clones to TPX2/CN1/EH2 (fastest spare-key route); AKL OBD + PIN.\n\nENDEAVOR (2004\u20132011)\n\u2022  2004\u20132011 \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT8 \u2192 4D60/4D61 \"N\" \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD add; 4D60 clone for spares; AKL OBD + PIN.\n\nMONTERO / MONTERO SPORT / PAJERO / PAJERO SPORT\n\u2022  1990s\u20132000 \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT1/MIT3 (MIT8) \u2192 no chip / early fixed 4D \u2192 Lishi MIT8, mechanical AKL / cut-and-go.\n\u2022  2001\u20132006 (US Montero/Montero Sport end) \u2192 edge-cut 8-cut \u2192 Ilco MIT8 \u2192 4D60/4D61 \"N\" \u2192 Lishi MIT8; onboard/OBD; 4D60 clone; AKL OBD + PIN.\n\u2022  Pajero/Pajero Sport (global, ~2006\u20132021) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut MIT11, later prox \u2192 MIT17A / MIT11R \u2192 ID46 \"A\" transponder or FAST-Key prox; newest Pajero Sport ID47 \u2014 confirm on lookup \u2192 Lishi MIT11; OBD add; AKL OBD + PIN or bypass cable.\n\nL200 / TRITON (GLOBAL MID-SIZE PICKUP \u2014 VERY HIGH VOLUME OUTSIDE US)\n\u2022  L200/Triton (2006\u20132015, gen4 KA/KB) \u2192 edge-cut 10-cut, MIT11 keyway \u2192 Ilco MIT17A \u2192 ID46 \"A,\" transponder or FAST-Key prox on top trims \u2192 Lishi MIT11; OBD add; ID46 clone (transponder only); AKL OBD + PIN.\n\u2022  L200/Triton (2015\u20132024, gen5 KJ/KK + facelift) \u2192 laser emergency blade MIT11R \u2192 prox smart (blade separate) \u2192 ID47/Hitag3 (NCF2951X/NCF29A1X) \u2192 OBD/BCM with PIN; not cloneable; OBD add or AKL only.\n\nRAIDER (2006\u20132009 \u2014 REBADGED DODGE DAKOTA, CHRYSLER INTERNALS)\n\u2022  2006\u20132009 \u2192 this is a Chrysler key, not a Mitsubishi one \u2192 edge-cut Y159/Y160-family blade \u2192 4D64/PCF-family Chrysler chip \u2192 confirm on Chrysler lookup, not the Mitsubishi charts; program by Chrysler SKIM OBD procedure, PIN from VIN.",id:"autokeys-mitsubishi",title:"Making keys: Mitsubishi",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/mitsubishi_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-mitsubishi-mit8-2-in-1-igntition-pick-decoder","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-mitsubishi-mit11-2-in1-decoder-pick-door-trunk-ag","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-mitsubishi-mit11-2-in1-decoder-pick-ignition-ag","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-mit8-ign","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2001-2006-mitsubishi-transponder-key-4d61-chip-letter-n-mit6-1","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2007-2019-mitsubishi-transponder-key-id46-chip-letter-a-mit3-mit17a-pta","https://keyshop-online.com/products/oem-mitsubishi-outlander-asx-2008-2015-smart-key-2b-433mhz-mit11r-id46-8637a662","https://keyshop-online.com/products/oem-mitsubishi-asx-2006-2021-smart-key-3b-315mhz-mit11r-id46-8637c803","https://www.amazon.com/Mitsubishi-2018-2022-YIKEBALOG-Replacement-OUCJ166N/dp/B0DXCZ9ZZR","https://keyshop-online.com/products/oem-mitsubishi-eclipse-cross-2019-2022-smart-key-2b-434mhz-mit11r-id47-8637c153","https://keyshop-online.com/products/oem-mitsubishi-eclipse-cross-2022-smart-key-2b-434mhz-mit11r-id47-8637c984","https://keyshop-online.com/products/aft-mitsubishi-l200-smart-key-2b-434mhz-mit11r-id47-8637b107","https://abkeys.com/products/mitsubishi-outlander-smart-key-2021up-4buttons-8637c254-kr5mtxn1-5125","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/mitsubishi-new-oem-2021-2022-outlander-smart-key-4b-hatch-fccid-kr5mtxn1-pn-8637c254","https://www.vvdi.com/vd3322-mitsubishi-outlander-smart-key-4-button-8637c254","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/10/04/top-tools-to-program-mitsubishi-outlander-2023-smart-key-to-bypass-pin-code/","http://www.key-programmer.org/2024/07/22/mitsubishi-4-digit-pin-code-list/","https://mirageforum.com/forum/showthread.php/3957-FAST-Key-Programming","https://lost-car-keys-replacement.com/mitsubishi/program/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Transponder","Bypass","Duplicator","Fob","Keyway","Add-a-key","Immobilizer"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-vag",title:"Making keys: Volkswagen & Audi (VAG)",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Blade type first, because VAG really only has two mechanical families and techs still grab the wrong Lishi. From roughly 1995 on, everything is a LASER/MILLED (sidewinder) blade with an internal 2-track milling \u2014 NOT edge-cut, NOT Tibbe (that's old Jaguar/Ford, never VW/Audi). The old profile is HU66 (Ilco HU66 family), used ~1996\u20132017 across VW, Audi, Seat, Skoda and some Porsche. The MQB-era cars (2013/15+) moved to a tighter, flip-resistant profile the trade calls HU162T (keyway VW39). Cut both on a laser machine (Triton, Miracle A9, Xhorse Condor XC-Mini Plus / Dolphin) \u2014 you cannot hand-cut a decent HU66 on a code machine.\n\nGet IN and get the CUTS with a Lishi: the HU66 2-in-1 (twin-lifter) covers ~1996\u20132017 doors, trunks and ignitions on one tool. HU162T is split by LOCK, not by year \u2014 HU162T(8-cut) is the DOOR/TRUNK tool, HU162T(9-cut) is the IGNITION tool, and there's now a HU162T(10) for the newest locks. Buy the 8 and the 9 at minimum; the HU66 Lishi will NOT decode an MQB lock. All are true pick+decode, so on AKL you pick the lock, read the cuts off the tool, and cut a working mechanical blade \u2014 the immo fight is a completely separate problem.\n\nQuick reference (era \u2192 blade \u2192 blank \u2192 chip/fob \u2192 path). Confirm exact fitment by VIN on a transponder lookup before cutting or ordering.\n- Immo 2 ~1995\u201398: HU66 / Ilco HU66 / Megamos fixed (13/\"48\" fixed) \u2192 clone/match.\n- Immo 3 ~1998\u20132005: HU66 / Ilco HU66-T6, JMA TP08HU-HAA.P1 / Megamos Crypto ID48 \u2192 7-digit SKC (PIN) + login; bench-read immo/cluster 24Cxx.\n- Immo 4 VW ~2005\u20132016: HU66 / HU66-T6 / Megamos Crypto ID48 (CAN) \u2192 CS/PIN from NEC+24C cluster.\n- BCM2 Audi ~2008\u20132016: HU66 / Hitag2 ID46 (PCF7945AC) \u2192 CS across modules; locked after ~2013.\n- MQB Gen1 (48) 2013\u2013~2020: HU162T / \"MQB 48-AES\", Megamos AES ID88 \u2192 immo data + CS, aftermarket AKL OK.\n- MQB Gen2 / 49 / 5C / 5D ~2020+: HU162T / Hitag-Pro ID5C / RH850 \u2192 add-key only aftermarket, AKL dealer.\n- MLB / MLB-Evo 2016+ (big Audi, Q7/Q5 g2, Porsche): HU162T / dealer-level \u2192 AKL largely dealer/limited.\n\nEra 1 \u2014 Immo 2, ~1995\u20131998 (early Golf 3/Passat B4/A4 B5 start). Blade HU66. Chip is Megamos fixed-code (early \"48\" fixed / Megamos 13). Add-a-key by cloning on a Megamos-capable cloner or match by VAG-COM. Rare now; if you see one, treat it like early Immo 3.\n\nEra 2 \u2014 Immo 3, ~1998\u20132005 (A4 B6, A6 C5, Golf 4, Passat B5/B5.5, Beetle, TT 8N). Blade HU66; blank Ilco HU66 / HU66-T6 / JMA TP08HU-HAA.P1; chip Megamos Crypto ID48. On many the immo box is separate from the cluster, with a 24C-series EEPROM. You need the 7-digit SKC (PIN). Either bench-read the immo/cluster EEPROM (24C32/24C64) to pull the PIN and login, or read PIN via a Micronas/immo dump. Tools: VVDI2 (VAG auth), Autel IM508/608, AVDI/Abrites, or an old VAG Tacho for cluster dumps. AKL: Lishi HU66 the door \u2192 decode \u2192 cut mechanical blade; then read immo EEPROM (or online SKC) to add transponders. Ross-Tech VCDS does Immo 3 key matching once you have the SKC.\n\nEra 3 \u2014 Immo 4 VW-side, ~2005\u20132016 (Golf 5/6, Jetta A5/A6, Passat B6/B7, Tiguan 1, Audi A3 8P). Immo 4 lives in the CLUSTER on an NEC+24C32 or NEC+24C64 (the classic \"NEC\" cluster). Blade HU66, HU66/HU66-T6 blank, chip Megamos Crypto ID48 (CAN), or a flip-remote. Programming: pull CS + PIN. VVDI2/Autel/Launch read the NEC IMMO data over OBD on most Golf 5/6 to calculate the CS/PIN, then learn keys. AKL: Lishi HU66 \u2192 cut key; read cluster IMMO data (OBD if supported, otherwise pull cluster and read NEC+24C on bench with VVDI Prog / XProg / Autel XP400), generate a dealer/precoded ID48 key, learn.\n\nDo NOT lump Audi into this. The 2008\u20132016 Audi A4 B8, A5, Q5, A6 C7, A8 D4 run BCM2, and BCM2 is a different animal: the transponder is Hitag2 / ID46 (PCF7945AC), NOT Megamos ID48, and Component Security is spread across the BCM, each key, the TCU and the ECU. Pre-2013 BCM2 is often OBD-friendly (Abrites/AVDI, some Autel) and AKL is doable on-site. Post-2013 BCM2 is LOCKED from the factory and won't talk over OBD \u2014 you either read the BCM2 on the bench (BDM/boot method, module out) or use Abrites AVDI with VN020 online authentication. Plan for BCM/cluster removal on the locked ones.\n\nEra 4a \u2014 MQB (Immo 5), 2013\u2013present transverse cars (Golf 7/8, Passat B8, Tiguan 2, Atlas, Jetta 2019+, Arteon, Audi A3 8V/8Y, Q2, Q3, TT 8S, plus Seat/Skoda). This is the wall. Blade moves to HU162T (keyway VW39) \u2014 HU162T Lishi only. Blank: \"MQB 48-AES\" HU162T; chip is Megamos AES / ID88 (Gen1), NOT ID48. The immo data / component protection is split \u2014 roughly 16 bytes in the dash immo module plus ~32 bytes of sync data \u2014 held on NEC35xx or similar. For MQB48/Gen1 (V850, common Golf 7 ~2013\u20132020) aftermarket AKL is real: read the MQB IMMO data on the bench (VVDI Prog + VVDI2, Autel IM608 with XP400/G-Box, OBDSTAR G3 with RH850/RandD kit, CG/Abrites), pull the CS, calculate, generate and learn a dealer key. Some tools also request PIN/CS online. Watch for the SFD/SFD2 gateway on later cars \u2014 2020+ (and some 2017+) need an online GeKo/SFD unlock before you can write. MQB Gen2 / MQB49 / 5C / 5D (RH850, ~2020+, chip trending to Hitag-Pro ID5C) is add-key-only for aftermarket right now (Xhorse Key Tool Plus/Multi Prog, Keydiy KD-X4, Autel, Yanhua ACDP \u2014 all needing a working key + sync data); true AKL is still dealer/ODIS online. There's early Immo6/SHE-ECU AKL work surfacing in 2026, but don't promise MQB49/5C AKL on the phone \u2014 verify the exact platform first.\n\nEra 4b \u2014 MLB / MLB-Evo, 2016\u2013present longitudinal cars (Audi A4 B9, A5 F5, A6 C8, A7, A8 D5, Q5 gen2, Q7 gen2, plus Porsche Panamera/Cayenne 9YA and Touareg gen3). Do NOT call these MQB \u2014 different platform, different (harder) security. Blade HU162T. AKL is NOT widely available outside dealer-level tools; expect spare-key-with-working-original at best on many, and dealer/online for all-keys-lost. This is where jobs go sideways if you quoted them as MQB.\n\nKESSY / smart (proximity). Loaded A-range, Q-range, Touareg, Passat CC and higher Golf/Tiguan run KESSY with a slot or an emergency blade (still HU66/HU162T inside the fob). Older KESSY (Touareg/Phaeton/Cayenne, separate immo box) is Megamos ID48; MQB KESSY is AES. AKL on smart KESSY = bench-read the immo data + a fresh OEM/aftermarket smart fob, learn via VVDI2/Autel/Abrites; cut the emergency blade off the Lishi-decoded door so you can physically get in and reach the slot.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST flow: (1) Identify the PLATFORM by VIN/year, not the badge \u2014 Immo3 vs VW-Immo4 vs Audi-BCM2 vs MQB48 vs MQB49/5C vs MLB decides everything. (2) Get in and get cuts: Lishi the driver door (HU66 pre-2015, HU162T 8-cut for MQB doors), decode, cut a mechanical blade; if the door won't read, pull the door or boot lock and bench-decode. (3) Get the code path: bench-read the immo EEPROM/immo data (24Cxx for Immo3/4, NEC35xx/MQB data for Immo5), OR pull CS/PIN online (NASTF SDRS / tool online service / dealer ODIS), OR read a code from the cylinder. (4) Make and learn: generate the correct chip (ID48 pre-2015, AES for MQB48), learn via VVDI2/Autel/Abrites; for BCM2-locked, MQB49/5C and MLB, send it to the dealer or use online authentication. Confirm ELV/steering-lock and component protection are happy afterward. Impression and try-out/jiggler keys get you a mechanical HU66 but do nothing for the immo \u2014 you still need the code path.\n\nTool kit for VAG AKL: Lishi HU66 (twin-lifter) + HU162T 8-cut and 9-cut (10-cut for newest); a laser cutter (Condor/Triton/Miracle A9); VVDI2 with VAG auth + VVDI Prog; Autel IM608 Pro with XP400 Pro + G-Box; OBDSTAR G3 with RH850 kit for MQB bench; Abrites/AVDI (VN020) for BCM2 and stubborn MQB/MLB; XProg/Orange5 for raw EEPROM; ID48/AES chips and OEM fobs on hand. Confirm the exact blank + chip against the VIN before you cut or order \u2014 VAG fitment splits mid-model-year. Verify on a transponder lookup: Transpondery's VW/Audi catalog, UHS Hardware, or the Ilco/JMA cross-reference, using the specific VIN.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-vag|Volkswagen/VAG MQB flip remote key (HU66 blade, 5G0-959-752) \u2014 the flip key most VW & Audi jobs land on, with the VW roundel on the reverse.]]\n\n*Blade shorthand:* HU66 = 2-track internal-cut laser track (Lishi HU66) \u2014 the dominant VAG blade ~1997\u20132016. HU162T = narrower internal-cut laser track (Lishi HU162T), the MQB/MLB-era blade from ~2017. HU49 = old double-sided edge-cut (pre-'97 VW). *Chip shorthand:* ID48 = Megamos Crypto (Immo3 = PIN-in-cluster; Immo4 = CAN, PIN via OBD); AES = Megamos AES (MQB, not cloneable); BCM2 = Audi body-control immo; MLB/MLBevo = Audi smart platform.\n\nVW JETTA\n\u2022  Jetta Mk4 (1999\u20132005) \u2192 HU66 internal-cut laser track \u2192 blank Ilco HU66 / HU66-P (confirm exact transponder blank on lookup) \u2192 ID48 Immo3 (separate remote or non-remote key) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66 to decode/cut, or cut by key code; read PIN from cluster EEPROM then OBD-program (VVDI2/AVDI/Autel); ID48 is cloneable off a working key (Zed-Full/Tango) for a fast spare. AKL: OBD once PIN is read.\n\u2022  Jetta Mk5 (2005\u20132010) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo4 (CAN) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD add-key with PIN read via OBD; ID48 clone still works for a spare; AKL OBD (Immo4 stays OBD-doable).\n\u2022  Jetta Mk6 (2011\u20132018, PQ35) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 chip splits mid-cycle: ID48 Immo4 early \u2192 Megamos AES ~2015+ \u2192 Path: OBD with capable tool; ID48 years clone, AES years do NOT clone (read immo data via OBD). *Confirm chip on lookup at the 2015 boundary.*\n\u2022  Jetta Mk7 (2019\u20132024, MQB) \u2192 HU162T (flip on base, Kessy prox emergency blade on higher trim) \u2192 HU162T \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 Path: Lishi HU162T; OBD immo-data programming (VVDI2 + MQB / Autel IM508-608 + token / AVDI); AKL OBD, no PIN needed. Not cloneable.\n\nVW GOLF / GTI\n\u2022  Golf Mk4 (1999\u20132005) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo3 \u2192 same as Jetta Mk4: Lishi/code cut, EEPROM PIN + OBD, ID48 clone for spare.\n\u2022  Golf/GTI Mk5\u2013Mk6 (2006\u20132014) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo4 \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD add-key with PIN-via-OBD; clone off working key; AKL OBD. *(Two generations wrapped \u2014 same method throughout.)*\n\u2022  Golf/GTI Mk7 (2015\u20132021, MQB) \u2192 kept HU66 flip/Kessy-prox \u2192 HU66 \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD MQB immo-data (VVDI2/Autel+token/AVDI); AKL OBD. Not cloneable. *(Note: this gen is HU66, NOT HU162T.)*\n\u2022  Golf/GTI Mk8 (2022+) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 AES MQB-Evo \u2192 Path: Lishi HU162T; OBD/dealer immo-data; gateway may need unlock.\n\nVW PASSAT\n\u2022  Passat B5/B5.5 (1998\u20132005) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo3 (shares Audi A4 platform) \u2192 Path: Lishi/code; EEPROM PIN + OBD; ID48 clone.\n\u2022  Passat B6/B7 & US NMS (2006\u20132022) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo4 \u2192 AES on later builds \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD with PIN (ID48) or immo-data (AES); AKL OBD. *US NMS kept the HU66 flip late; confirm chip at ~2016.*\n\u2022  Euro Passat B8 (2015+, MQB) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 OBD immo-data / dealer. *(B8 and US NMS diverge \u2014 verify body/market before cutting.)*\n\nVW TIGUAN\n\u2022  Tiguan Gen1 (2009\u20132017, PQ35) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo4 \u2192 AES late \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD add-key/AKL with PIN or immo-data.\n\u2022  Tiguan Gen2 (2018\u20132024, MQB) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 Path: Lishi HU162T; OBD immo-data (VVDI2/Autel+token) / dealer.\n\nVW ATLAS (2018+, MQB)\n\u2022  Atlas (2018\u20132024) \u2192 HU162T prox smart key \u2192 HU162T (emergency blade) \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 Path: Lishi HU162T for the blade; OBD immo-data programming (VVDI2/Autel+token/AVDI) or dealer; AKL OBD. Not cloneable.\n\nVW BEETLE\n\u2022  New Beetle (1998\u20132010) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo3 \u2192 Immo4 \u2192 Path: Lishi/code; EEPROM PIN (early) or OBD PIN (later) + OBD; ID48 clone.\n\u2022  Beetle A5 (2012\u20132019, PQ35) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 \u2192 AES late \u2192 OBD add-key/AKL with PIN or immo-data.\n\nVW Touareg *(shares Q7/Cayenne PL71 hardware \u2014 harder than mainstream VW)*\n\u2022  Touareg 7L (2004\u20132010) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 (Kessy prox optional) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD where supported, otherwise cluster/immo bench work; treat as advanced.\n\u2022  Touareg 7P (2011\u20132018) \u2192 HU66 Kessy smart prox \u2192 HU66 emergency blade \u2192 advanced immo \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66 for blade; programming is specialist/dealer (bench or online), not routine OBD.\n\nAUDI A3\n\u2022  A3 8P (2006\u20132013) \u2192 HU66 flip \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo4/Immo5 (cluster-based, CS in instrument cluster \u2014 NOT BCM2) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD add-key with PIN read from cluster; ID48 clone for spare; AKL straightforward OBD (no BCM2 lock on this platform).\n\u2022  A3 8V (2015\u20132020, MQB) \u2192 HU66 flip / prox \u2192 HU66 \u2192 AES MQB \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; OBD immo-data (VVDI2/Autel+token/AVDI); AKL OBD. Not cloneable.\n\u2022  A3 8Y (2022+) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 AES MQB-Evo \u2192 dealer/advanced.\n\nAUDI A4 / S4 / A5 *(Q5 TRACKS THE SAME PLATFORMS)*\n\u2022  A4 B6/B7 (2002\u20132008) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo3\u2192Immo4 \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; EEPROM PIN (B6) or OBD PIN (B7) + OBD; ID48 clone for spare; AKL OBD.\n\u2022  A4 B8 / A5 / Q5 8R (2009\u20132016, BCM2) \u2192 HU66 slot key \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48/ID49 \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; add-key OBD (VVDI2/AVDI). Big AKL split at ~2013: pre-2013 BCM2 is unlocked \u2192 OBD AKL; 2013+ BCM2 is locked \u2192 BCM2 must be unlocked first (Abrites AVDI VN020 online, or bench-read BCM2/cluster) or tow to dealer. Not cloneable.\n\u2022  A4 B9 / A5 / Q5 FY (2017+, MLBevo) \u2192 smart prox, HU162T emergency blade \u2192 HU162T \u2192 MLBevo immo \u2192 Path: Lishi HU162T or code for the blade; programming is advanced \u2014 dealer, or specialist with MLB immo extraction (bench/online). AKL is largely a dealer/specialist job; budget time.\n\nAUDI A6\n\u2022  A6 C5/C6 (2001\u20132011) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48 Immo3\u2192Immo4/Immo5 (cluster) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; EEPROM/OBD PIN + OBD; ID48 clone (early); AKL OBD on unlocked immo.\n\u2022  A6 C7 (2012\u20132018, BCM2) \u2192 HU66 slot/prox \u2192 HU66 \u2192 ID48/ID49 \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66; add-key OBD. Same 2013+ locked-BCM2 rule: AKL needs BCM2 unlock (Abrites VN020/bench) or dealer.\n\u2022  A6 C8 (2019+, MLBevo) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 MLBevo \u2192 dealer/advanced.\n\nAUDI Q5 *(LISTED FOR LOOKUP; MIRRORS A4/A5 ABOVE)*\n\u2022  Q5 8R (2009\u20132017) \u2192 HU66 \u2192 HU66 \u2192 BCM2 ID48/ID49 \u2192 OBD add-key; AKL per pre/post-2013 BCM2-lock rule (VN020/bench or dealer).\n\u2022  Q5 FY (2018+) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 MLBevo \u2192 dealer/advanced.\n\nAUDI Q7\n\u2022  Q7 4L (2007\u20132015, PL71) \u2192 HU66 Kessy prox \u2192 HU66 emergency blade \u2192 advanced immo (Touareg/Cayenne-family) \u2192 Path: Lishi HU66 for blade; programming specialist/dealer, not routine OBD.\n\u2022  Q7 4M (2017+, MLBevo) \u2192 HU162T prox \u2192 HU162T \u2192 MLBevo \u2192 dealer/advanced.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/volkswagen_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/audi_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.capitollocksmithdc.com/blog/vw-amp-audi-key-programming-guide-immo-4-bcm2-mqb-amp-mlb-systems-explained","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/megamos-aes-transponder-chip-mqb-id88","https://transponderisland.com/shop/tik-vw-39-2015-2020-volkswagen-transponder-key-mqb-hu66-keyway-3515","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-hu162t-vag2015-2014-2015-audi-vw-8-cut-2-in-1-pick-decoder-door-trunk-ignition-ag","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-hu162t9-ign","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/original-lishi-vw-audi-ford-hu66-2-in-1-pick-decoder-sl-ag","http://blog.obdii365.com/2022/03/10/decode-hu162t9-hu162t10-lock-all-keys-lost-with-lishi/","https://www.premierlocks.com/automotive/tp08hu-haa-p1-jma-transporter-car-key-hu66pt","https://www.ross-tech.com/vag-com/cars/Immo3-key-matching.html","https://www.xhorsevvdi.com/service/how-vvdi-prog-vvdi2-read-mqb48-immo-data.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/07/11/solution-for-vag-mqb49-5c-make-dealer-key/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2026/01/19/difference-between-vag-mqb48-mqb49-5c-and-5d/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2025/04/27/obdstar-g3-program-vag-mqb-5a-all-keys-lost-with-rh850-kit/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2026/05/31/program-vag-mqb-all-keys-lost-with-immo6-she-ecu/","https://blog.obdii365.com/2026/01/15/how-to-add-vag-mqb49-5c-5d-key-with-keydiy-kd-x4/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2024/08/26/program-vag-immo4-nec24c64-all-keys-lost-by-launch-x431/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Transponder","Fob","Add-a-key","Code machine","Cloning","Key code","Keyway"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","chip key","immo chip","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-bmw",title:"Making keys: BMW & Mini",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"BMW is an immobilizer-system make, not a year make. You diagnose by the module that holds the keys \u2014 EWS, CAS, or FEM/BDC \u2014 because that module dictates the blade, the chip, and whether you work OBD or on the bench. Mini rides on the same platforms (R-series = E-platform electronics, F-series = F/G electronics), so everything here applies to Mini with the same tools. Get the module right first; the blank and chip fall out of it.\n\nBLADE TYPES YOU WILL SEE. Two only, and the cutover is the E-to-F chassis change around 2011-2013. HU92 is a two-track INTERNAL-CUT (milled-track) blade \u2014 the cuts are milled into the flats of the blade, not the edge. It covers everything EWS through CAS3+ (E-chassis BMW and all R-chassis Mini, roughly 1995-2014). HU100R is the high-security internal-track blade for F- and G-chassis (CAS4/CAS4+ and FEM/BDC, ~2011-present, plus F-series Mini). No edge-cut, no Tibbe on modern BMW \u2014 that is Ford/Jaguar territory. Emergency blades inside smart fobs follow the same rule: slot-key CAS3+ fobs carry an HU92 blade, proximity CAS4/FEM fobs carry an HU100R blade.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 EWS (EWS2/EWS3/EWS4, ~1995-2005). Blade: HU92. Chip: PCF7935, called ID44 \u2014 a fixed/crypto Philips chip, NOT ID46. The immobilizer secret (ISN) lives in the EWS module EEPROM. AKL and add-a-key are a bench job on the EWS box: pull the EWS module, read its EEPROM (935D0WT / D80 chip) with AK90/AK90+ (covers EWS2.1/2.2/3/3.3/4/4.4), VVDI Prog, or XPROG. You get the ISN plus the used/free key slots, then write a virgin PCF7935 to a free slot and cut the HU92 to the door code. There is no clean OBD path for EWS AKL \u2014 plan on removing the module. Cut the blade by decoding a pulled lock (see tools) or by the key code.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 CAS1/CAS2/CAS3/CAS3+ (Hitag2 ID46, ~2004-2013). Blade: HU92. Chip: ID46 Hitag2 (PCF7936/PCF7945). This is the classic BMW slot key. CAS1/CAS2 show on early E65/E66 7-series and E60/E90 launch cars; CAS3 is the volume system (E90, E60, E70 X5, E82, E84 X1, E89) and R56 Mini. Programming: CAS1 through early CAS3 read the ISN and program keys over OBD with VVDI2, CGDI BMW, or Autel IM608 \u2014 fast, no soldering. CAS3+ is the gotcha: after a mid-2011 firmware/ISTA update BMW encrypted the EEPROM, so OBD key-learn is blocked. On CAS3+ you read the CAS EEPROM on the bench (VVDI Prog / CGDI / IM608 + XP400 read the Motorola-MCU-guarded EEPROM), let the tool decrypt and calculate key data, write the ID46, then program over OBD. AKL here always ends in a bench read and a decrypt of the CAS dump \u2014 budget for it.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 CAS4/CAS4+ (Hitag Pro ID49, ~2009-2017). Blade: HU100R high security. Chip: ID49 Hitag Pro (fob commonly marked PCF7945P, transponder is Hitag Pro; FCC IDs like KR55WK49863). Found on F01/F02 7-series, F10 5-series, F25 X3, F15 X5. Key data and ISN handling live in a 95128 (CAS4) or 95256 (CAS4+) EEPROM inside the module. Standard AKL flow: open the CAS4, read the EEPROM \u2014 Yanhua Mini ACDP does this solder-free with the CAS4 interface board, or use VVDI Prog / CGDI with the bench harness. The tool calculates key data; you also need the engine ISN. You write the ISN into the new ID49 key, program it, and restore the EEPROM. Read the EEPROM two or three times and compare \u2014 a loose clip gives a bad dump and a dead key.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 FEM/BDC (Hitag Pro ID49, ~2011-present). Blade: HU100R. Chip: ID49 Hitag Pro. FEM (Front Electronic Module) and its successor BDC (Body Domain Controller) swallow the old CAS/FRM/gateway functions. This is F20/F30/F32/F15/F16 and the G-chassis, plus F-series Mini (F54/F55/F56/F60). AKL is a defined unlock procedure: pull the FEM/BDC, read its 95128 (FEM) or 95256 (BDC) flash on the bench, the tool patches/unlocks it (Autohex II unlocks solder-free; VVDI2 + VVDI Prog, or Yanhua ACDP Module 2 also do it), reinstall, then program the key over OBD and restore the flash. Note the newest G-chassis cars (roughly 2019+) use BDC2/BDC3 with tighter firmware \u2014 confirm your tool supports that exact version before you start, some need a firmware bump or a dealer-key restore path. Critical detail on FEM/BDC and CAS4: you cannot make a key that STARTS the car without the engine ISN. Read the ISN from the DME/DDE \u2014 E-chassis MSV/MSD read over CAN/OBD, F-chassis N20/N55/B38/B48 typically read in bench mode; ACDP needs its ISN sub-module for AKL. Match that ISN into the module and key or the car cranks but will not run.\n\nERA 5 \u2014 Comfort Access / smart proximity. Not a separate module \u2014 a fob variant across CAS3+, CAS4, and FEM/BDC. Same programming path as the underlying module; you fit a proximity fob at the correct frequency (315 US, 433/868 elsewhere) with the right emergency blade (HU92 on CAS3+, HU100R on CAS4/FEM). Confirm FCC ID and part number against the VIN before ordering.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blade \u2192 chip \u2192 path):\n- EWS2/3/4 (~95-05): HU92 \u2192 PCF7935/ID44 \u2192 bench-read EWS module (AK90/VVDI Prog), write slot.\n- CAS1/2/3 early (~04-11): HU92 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 OBD read ISN + program (VVDI2/CGDI/IM608).\n- CAS3+ (~11-13): HU92 \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 \u2192 bench-read + decrypt CAS EEPROM, then OBD.\n- CAS4/CAS4+ (~09-17): HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro \u2192 bench 95128/95256 read (ACDP/VVDI Prog) + ISN from DME.\n- FEM/BDC (~11-present): HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash (Autohex/ACDP) + ISN from DME/DDE, program OBD.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS (the real menu, BMW-specific):\n- Pick the ignition or door with a Lishi. HU92 2-in-1 for all E/R cars \u2014 it reads and decodes the two internal tracks; the E-chassis door lock decodes cleanest, ignition wafers can be worn. HU100R Lishi (or Turbo Decoder HU100R) for F/G cars \u2014 high-security, slower to master; practice on a bench lock. Decode, cut the blade, and you have a mechanical key that turns the lock \u2014 but it will NOT start the car until the chip is programmed.\n- Pull a lock and bench-decode when the in-situ pick is fighting you: the door cylinder or trunk/tailgate lock comes out and reads clean on the bench with the same Lishi.\n- Key code: BMW key codes are dealer/NASTF-restricted; a locksmith with a NASTF LSID can request cut codes, but for AKL you will usually just decode the lock \u2014 faster than the code path.\n- Do NOT rely on impressioning internal-track HU92/HU100R blades; it is impractical. The Lishi or a pulled-lock decode is the answer on this make.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST, START TO FINISH: 1) ID the module by chassis/year (EWS vs CAS vs FEM/BDC). 2) Get a mechanical cut \u2014 Lishi HU92 or HU100R on door/ignition, or pull and bench-decode. 3) Cut a virgin blade to that code. 4) Handle the electronics for that era: EWS/CAS3+/CAS4/FEM all mean a bench read of the module EEPROM/flash (CAS1-3 early is OBD). 5) For CAS4 and FEM/BDC, additionally read the ISN from the DME/DDE and match it. 6) Program the correct chip (ID44 / ID46 Hitag2 / ID49 Hitag Pro) into the virgin key, restore any dump, verify start and remote. Core toolkit: VVDI2 + VVDI Prog, Autohex II, Yanhua Mini ACDP (solder-free CAS4/FEM + ISN module), CGDI BMW or Autel IM608 for OBD work, AK90+ for EWS, plus Lishi HU92 and HU100R and a key machine that mills internal-track blades.\n\nExact blade cut series, chip sub-variant, and fob FCC/part number vary by VIN and build date. Before you cut or order, confirm the precise blank+chip on a transponder lookup \u2014 Transpondery (BMW catalog), UHS Hardware, or Ilco \u2014 against the specific VIN.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-bmw|BMW F-series CAS4+/FEM smart proximity fob (FCC YGOHUF5662, 434 MHz) \u2014 the key most BMW jobs land on across 2009-2018 3/5/7 Series.]]\n\n*Find your chassis + build year, read across. \"Same method\" generations are wrapped into one row \u2014 a split only appears where the blade, blank, chip, or programming path actually changes. BMW/Mini are NOT a clone make: the ID44 EWS chip and the ID46/ID49 CAS/FEM chips are all written to the car's module, not cloned to a transponder \u2014 so no clone route is listed (it does not exist here). Code route is the same for every row: keys are dealer/NASTF-restricted, so a NASTF LSID holder can request cut codes, but for AKL you will almost always decode the lock faster than the code comes back.*\n\nBMW 3 Series (incl. 4 Series F32/G22 \u2014 same key method as its 3er sibling)\n\u2022  E46 (1999\u20132006) \u2192 2-track internal-cut, HU92 keyway (very early '99 builds can be HU58 4-track \u2014 confirm on lookup) \u2192 Ilco HU92RP / Silca HU92 / JMA BM-6.P \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 (EWS) \u2192 AKL/spare: pull the EWS module, bench-read/write a free key slot with AK90 or VVDI Prog, cut a virgin HU92 to a Lishi HU92 2-in-1 door decode (or pulled door/trunk lock). No OBD add on EWS.\n\u2022  E90/E91/E92/E93 (2005\u20132010) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS2/CAS3) \u2192 OBD read ISN + program with VVDI2 / CGDI BMW / IM608; cut via HU92 Lishi door decode. Add-key and most AKL are OBD in this window.\n\u2022  E90/E92/E93 CAS3+ (2010\u20132013 builds) \u2192 HU92 / HU92RP / ID46 \u2192 *OBD blocked (CAS3+ ISTAP)*: bench-read the CAS EEPROM, decrypt the ISN, then program OBD or bench (VVDI Prog + VVDI2 / CGDI). Same key, harder path \u2014 split is the CAS3\u2192CAS3+ lockout, not a hardware change.\n\u2022  F30/F31/F34 (2012\u20132019) \u2192 high-security single-track, HU100R keyway \u2192 Silca HU100R blade / Ilco HU100R insert (confirm exact Ilco no. on lookup) \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (PCF7953P) \u2192 FEM/BDC bench route \u2014 the F30 launched on FEM and has no CAS4 phase: unlock the FEM/BDC flash (ACDP Module 3 / Autohex / Abrites) + ISN from DME/DDE, then program OBD. Early builds are FEM, ~2016 LCI builds move to BDC \u2014 same blade/blank/chip, only the flash target/tooling moves. Cut via HU100R Lishi or Turbo Decoder.\n\u2022  G20/G21 (2019\u20132025) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox/Comfort Access \u2192 BDC bench or dealer-online; AKL coverage is tool-dependent (Autohex/Abrites/ACDP) \u2014 confirm your tool covers the build before quoting AKL.\n\nBMW 5 SERIES\n\u2022  E39 (1996\u20132003) \u2192 early = HU58 4-track; post-facelift (~2000) = HU92 2-track \u2014 *confirm by build date* \u2192 Ilco HU92RP (HU58 blank confirm on lookup) \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 (EWS) \u2192 bench EWS read/write (AK90/VVDI Prog), Lishi cut.\n\u2022  E60/E61 (2003\u20132010) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS2/CAS3) \u2192 OBD read ISN + program (VVDI2/CGDI/IM608); Lishi HU92 door decode. Latest builds may hit CAS3+ lockout \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS then OBD.\n\u2022  F10/F11/F07 (2010\u20132017) \u2192 HU100R high-security \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (CAS4/CAS4+, F10 stayed CAS4 its whole life) \u2192 bench-read 95128/95256 EEPROM + ISN from DME (ACDP/VVDI Prog), program OBD; HU100R Lishi/Turbo Decoder for cuts.\n\u2022  G30/G31 (2017\u20132023) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox \u2192 BDC bench/online (Autohex/Abrites/ACDP) \u2014 confirm AKL coverage.\n\nBMW 7 SERIES\n\u2022  E38 (1994\u20132001) \u2192 HU58 4-track internal-cut \u2192 HU58 blank (confirm Ilco no. on lookup) \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 (EWS) \u2192 bench EWS read/write (AK90), HU58 cut by pulled-lock decode.\n\u2022  E65/E66 (2001\u20132008) \u2192 HU92 2-track (first Comfort Access BMW) \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS/CAS2) \u2192 OBD read ISN + program (VVDI2/CGDI); Lishi HU92.\n\u2022  F01/F02 (2008\u20132015) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (CAS4/CAS4+) \u2192 bench 95-series EEPROM + ISN from DME, program OBD.\n\u2022  G11/G12 (2015\u20132022) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox \u2192 BDC bench/online \u2014 confirm tool coverage for AKL.\n\nBMW X5\n\u2022  E53 (2000\u20132006) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 (EWS3/4) \u2192 bench EWS read/write, Lishi HU92 cut.\n\u2022  E70 (2007\u20132013) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3/CAS3+) \u2192 early builds OBD (VVDI2/CGDI); later CAS3+ builds \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS EEPROM then OBD. Lishi HU92 door decode.\n\u2022  F15 (2014\u20132018) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (FEM early, BDC on later/LCI builds \u2014 no CAS4, the F15 launched after the CAS4 era) \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash + ISN from DME/DDE, program OBD.\n\u2022  G05 (2019\u20132024) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox \u2192 BDC bench/online \u2014 confirm AKL coverage.\n\nBMW X3\n\u2022  E83 (2004\u20132010) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 \u2014 *note: the E83 kept the EWS-style immobilizer through its whole run* (2007+ builds still read HU92/PCF7935) \u2192 bench module read/write (AK90/VVDI Prog), Lishi HU92 cut. Confirm module on lookup if a build reads as CAS.\n\u2022  F25 (2011\u20132017) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (CAS4/CAS4+ its whole run \u2014 like the F10 it never moved to FEM) \u2192 bench-read 95128/95256 EEPROM + ISN from DME, program OBD.\n\u2022  G01 (2018\u20132024) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox \u2192 BDC bench/online \u2014 confirm coverage.\n\nBMW 1 SERIES\n\u2022  E81/E82/E87/E88 (2004\u20132013) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3/CAS3+) \u2192 early = OBD (VVDI2/CGDI/IM608); post-~2010 CAS3+ builds \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS then OBD. Lishi HU92 door decode.\n\u2022  F20/F21 (2012\u20132019) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (FEM early, BDC later \u2014 no CAS4; even the earliest 2011/12 builds are FEM) \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash + ISN from DME/DDE, program OBD.\n\nBMW X1\n\u2022  E84 (2010\u20132015) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3+) \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS EEPROM then OBD program (VVDI Prog + VVDI2/CGDI); Lishi HU92. (OBD-only on very early builds \u2014 confirm.)\n\u2022  F48 (2016\u20132022) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (BDC; FEM on earliest builds \u2014 confirm) \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash + ISN from DME, program OBD.\n\nBMW Z4\n\u2022  E85/E86 (2003\u20132008) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 PCF7935/ID44 (EWS) on early builds \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS) on later \u2014 *confirm module by build date* \u2192 EWS: bench read/write (AK90); CAS: OBD read ISN + program (VVDI2/CGDI). Lishi HU92.\n\u2022  E89 (2009\u20132016) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3+) \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS then OBD, or OBD on earliest builds. Lishi HU92.\n\u2022  G29 (2019\u20132026) \u2192 HU100R \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro, prox \u2192 BDC bench/online \u2014 confirm AKL coverage (shares Toyota Supra platform but BMW BDC key system).\n\nMINI Hardtop / Hatch / Cooper (Convertible & Clubman track the same generations)\n\u2022  R50/R52/R53 (2002\u20132008) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 PCF7935 / ID44 (EWS3) \u2192 AKL/spare: bench EWS read/write (AK90/VVDI Prog), cut virgin HU92 to Lishi HU92 door decode. Remote head keys 315/433 MHz.\n\u2022  R55/R56/R57/R58/R59 (2007\u20132015) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3/CAS3+); fobs PCF7945A/PCF7953A \u2192 early = OBD (VVDI2/CGDI/IM608); later CAS3+ builds \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS EEPROM then OBD. Lishi HU92 door decode. Semi-smart/Comfort Access variants exist \u2014 treat prox fobs as CAS3+ bench.\n\u2022  F55/F56/F57 (2014\u2013present) \u2192 HU100R high-security \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (FEM early, BDC later), prox \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash (ACDP/Autohex/Abrites) + ISN from DME, program OBD. Cut via HU100R Lishi/Turbo Decoder.\n\nMINI COUNTRYMAN / PACEMAN\n\u2022  R60/R61 (2011\u20132016) \u2192 HU92 2-track \u2192 Ilco HU92RP \u2192 ID46 Hitag2 (CAS3+) \u2192 bench-read/decrypt CAS EEPROM then OBD program (VVDI Prog + VVDI2/CGDI); Lishi HU92 door decode.\n\u2022  F60 (2017\u20132023) \u2192 HU100R high-security \u2192 Silca HU100R \u2192 ID49 Hitag Pro (BDC; FEM on earliest builds), prox \u2192 bench unlock FEM/BDC flash + ISN from DME, program OBD.\n\n*Two hardware facts do all the sorting on this make: (1) blade \u2014 HU92 2-track on every E-chassis and R-Mini through ~2013, HU100R high-security on every F/G BMW and F-Mini from ~2013; no BMW/Mini uses edge-cut, sidewinder, or Tibbe. (2) chip \u2014 ID44 = EWS (bench the module), ID46 = CAS (OBD early, bench-decrypt once CAS3+ locks OBD out), ID49 = CAS4/FEM/BDC (always bench the immo module + pull ISN from the DME/DDE). Nail those two and the path follows. One more sort inside the ID49 group: the CAS4/CAS4+ holdouts are only F01/F02, F10/F11/F07, and F25 X3 (read the 95128/95256 EEPROM); every other F-car \u2014 F20, F30, F15, F48, F5x Mini \u2014 is FEM or BDC (unlock the flash).*",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/bmw_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2000-2009-bmw-2-track-hu92-transponder-key-pcf7935-chip-k-hu92","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/pm-ak90","https://www.classiclishi.com/product/classic-lishi-hu92-key-reader","https://www.npshops.com/products/bmw-cas4-4-button-smart-remote-key-315mhz-pcf7945p-hitag-pro-id49-chip-fcc-id-kr55wk49863","http://www.autelscanner.org/autel-im608-ii-im508s-read-bmw-cas3-eeprom-on-bench/","https://www.vvdishop.com/service/vvdi-key-tool-plus-program-bmw-cas3-all-keys-lost.html","https://www.yanhuaacdp.com/wholesale/yanhua-mini-acdp-key-programmer-for-bmw-cas4-fem-edc-and-isn.html","https://www.yanhuaacdp.com/wholesale/yanhua-mini-acdp-bmw-fem-edc-module.html","https://www.microtronik.com/technical-info/autohex/fem-and-bdc-unlocking","https://www.vvdishop.com/service/how-to-use-vvdi2-vvdi-prog-to-program-bmw-bdc-all-keys-lost-5036.html","http://blog.obdii365.com/2025/08/22/how-to-restore-bmw-bdc2-immo-module-with-yanhua-acdp/","https://gosolockpicks.com/product/turbo-decoder-hu100r-v-2-for-bmw-f-series","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/bmw-mini-cooper-keys-complete-starter-pack-all-years-for-vvdi2-im608-acdp","https://mhhauto.com/Thread-BMW-Car-List-for-CAS1-CAS2-CAS3-CAS4-FEM"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Decoder","Fob","Key code","Immobilizer","FCC ID","Transponder","Add-a-key"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","read the cuts","reading tool","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","panel","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-mercedes",title:"Making Keys: Mercedes-Benz",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Mercedes is its own animal. Forget standard 4D/ID46 thinking \u2014 from the mid-'90s on, Mercedes talks to the ignition switch over INFRARED, not RF, and the immobilizer brain is the EIS/EZS (Electronic Ignition Switch). The mechanical blade is almost an afterthought; the real work is the crypto handshake between key and EIS. Know your era before you touch anything, because a DAS3/FBS3 car is a bench-and-token job you can do, and an FBS4 car is largely an online-dealer job you often cannot.\n\nFIELD TELL BEFORE ANYTHING: look at the number printed on the key. A 7-digit key number = FBS3. An 8-digit key number = FBS4. That one glance saves you from quoting a 40-minute add on a car you can't finish.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blade/blank \u2192 chip/system \u2192 programming path). Exact fitment varies by VIN \u2014 confirm on a transponder lookup.\n\n\u2022 1994\u20131998 DAS1 \u2014 W124 facelift, W140, R129. Blade: HU39 4-track (Ilco/Silca HU39) IR flip key. System: the earliest DAS is IR-based immobilization; the crypto transponder proper comes in with DAS2, so don't assume an ID44 on the oldest cars. Path: legacy IR keymaker, mostly dealer/specialist now.\n\u2022 1998\u20132004 DAS2 \u2014 W202, W210, R170, early W203/W211. Blade: this is where the HU64 keyway shows up \u2014 W202/W210/R170 IR flip keys are HU64 (Ilco/Silca HU64), while the carryover big-body W140/R129 stayed HU39. Chip: ID44 (Philips Crypto, PCF7935). Path: MB IR keymaker/legacy tools, some Abrites/aftermarket coverage. HU64 with a 44 chip is a very common older-Mercedes key.\n\u2022 2004\u20132015 DAS3/FBS3 \u2014 W211, W219, W164, W221, W204, W207, W212, W169, W245, W166, W246, W197, W117. Blade: HU64 emergency blade in a chrome plastic IR \"smart\" key. System: EIS + ESL/ELV, proprietary crypto, no separate external transponder chip \u2014 the secret lives in the key/EIS pairing (BGA-type key). Path: VVDI MB (BGA), CGDI MB, Autel IM608+G-Box/APB, Abrites \u2014 password/SSID calc + EIS read, then generate a BGA key. THIS is your money era.\n\u2022 2015\u2013present FBS4 \u2014 W205, W213, W222, W217, W447, W167, later 172/205/217/218/222/231. Blade: HU64 emergency blade in an FBS4 smart/BGA key. System: AES, online-anchored. Path: Xentry + authorized online account (VeDoc/XSF case); aftermarket AKL is limited/server-dependent, not reliable calc.\n\nNote the terminology trap: some catalogs lump everything pre-2015 as \"FBS3.\" Use the generational breakdown above \u2014 the EIS/ESL BGA-crypto FBS3 you actually calc-and-write is the ~2004+ W211-onward era, not the older IR flip cars.\n\nWHY IR MATTERS. On FBS3 the key doesn't just sit in a coil field \u2014 it exchanges IR with the EIS. Your programmer needs an IR adapter/coil (\"IR key head\") to read the EIS and to write keys. VVDI MB's IR mode and CGDI's IR head both auto-recognize the EIS and read SSID + EIS number over OBD or IR.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS \u2014 the real menu for Mercedes, not \"Lishi every door.\"\n1) Lishi HU64 (2-in-1, and the V3/V4 3-in-1) \u2014 THE tool for this make from ~1997 on. Pick and decode the DOOR lock or, better, the IGNITION/EIS lock directly, because Mercedes frequently keys the doors and ignition alike to the same HU64 bitting. HU64 is a tight, track-heavy keyway that fights you \u2014 practice on a bench lock. Decoding the EIS lock gives you the emergency-blade cuts in one shot.\n2) Pull the door lock and bench-decode \u2014 cleaner reads than in-situ picking on worn cars. Pull it, decode the wafers, cut the HU64 blade (e.g., Cut-by-Bitting HU64 on a Dolphin XP005).\n3) Code service / NASTF \u2014 Mercedes mechanical bitting can be pulled by key code through a legitimate code service/dealer with proof of ownership. Good when the EIS lock is chewed up.\n4) The blade is NOT the immobilizer. A working HU64 gets you mechanical turn only. On FBS3/FBS4 the car still won't crank until the EIS accepts an electronically valid key. Mechanical origination and electronic origination are two separate jobs \u2014 don't confuse a cut blade with a made key.\n\nFBS3 ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 the core procedure. Two ways: OBD/online and full bench.\nPassword/SSID concept: every EIS has an SSID (serial/status ID) and a key PASSWORD. To make a new key you must recover that password. With no working key you \"collect data\" \u2014 the tool logs many EIS crypto responses (the several-minutes \"data collection\" step, roughly 3.5\u20138 min depending on EIS type), uploads them, and an online token/server calculates the password (VVDI MB, CGDI MB \u2014 about one token, ~1 minute compute, high success on FBS3). Real-world OBD calc often runs closer to 15\u201320 minutes end to end.\nOBD route: plug in, read EIS via OBD/IR, run collect-data, spend a token to calc the password, then read the EIS key slots. FBS3 EIS holds 8 key positions/tracks; find a used/empty slot, generate a key file, and WRITE it to a blank BGA key (Xhorse or CGDI BGA key, HU64 emergency blade inside). Some jobs need you to \"renew\" a used key first to reset it to writable.\nBench route (when OBD is locked, EIS damaged, or ESL faulted): pull the EIS, read it on the bench via the IR adapter (or read the EIS EEPROM directly \u2014 Motorola/NEC/Infineon depending on generation). Bench-collect data, calc password, restore/write the EIS data file, then generate the key. Bench is also how you clone to a replacement EIS or personalize a virgin/used EIS to the car.\nESL/ELV \u2014 the trap. W204/W207/W212 and friends love to kill the electronic steering lock. A dead ESL means no crank even with a perfect key. The ESL/ELV emulator answers the EIS's hash-validation request in place of a failed lock. Keep one on the truck; on AKL you often deactivate/emulate the ESL and re-sync it to the EIS as part of the job. Abrites/VVDI walk this.\nTOOLS for FBS3: VVDI MB BGA Tool (IR head + BGA blanks + token subscription) or CGDI MB (IR head, free daily token is the draw), Autel IM608 Pro II + G-Box2/APB, Abrites AVDI with MB special functions, EIS test cables/bench sockets, ESL emulator, and a Lishi HU64. Blanks: Xhorse/CGDI BGA smart keys with Ilco/Silca HU64 emergency blades; loose blades Ilco HU64.\n\nFBS4 ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 manage expectations. FBS4 (~2015+) moved to stronger AES and anchored key generation to Mercedes' back-end. Aftermarket boxes can READ an FBS4 key's status (working/damaged) but generally CANNOT calculate FBS4 passwords the way they do FBS3 \u2014 CGDI states outright it reads FBS4 but won't calc it; only genuine Xentry and the Daimler FBS server program FBS4. The dependable path is Xentry (genuine) with a high-authorization ONLINE account: confirm via VeDoc that the car is truly FBS4 \u2014 if VeDoc's theft-relevant section shows FBS3, you don't need an XSF case, just order a TRP key as a normal part \u2014 otherwise open the XSF/online case and let Xentry authorize and program a blank/ordered key through the factory handshake. Most independents route FBS4 to the dealer or a specialist with an account. Some server-based aftermarket solutions exist for specific FBS4 years \u2014 treat them case-by-case and verify before you quote.\n\nFIRST-KEY-ADDED (owner has one working key): far easier both eras. FBS3 \u2014 read EIS with the working key present, no data-collection token needed, add the new BGA key to an open slot. FBS4 \u2014 still needs Xentry/online authorization to add.\n\nPRE-JOB DISCIPLINE. Pull the VIN, read the digit count on any existing key, identify EIS generation, and confirm FBS3 vs FBS4 before you commit. Check battery voltage \u2014 Mercedes crypto reads and ESL operations are voltage-sensitive, so put a stable supply on it. Log the EIS number and SSID.\n\nEXACT BLANK + CHIP VARIES BY VIN. HU39 vs HU64, ID44 vs FBS3/FBS4 key type, and the right BGA blank and IR head all shift by model/year/EIS. Before you cut or order, confirm blade profile, key type, and system generation for the specific VIN on a transponder lookup \u2014 Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or Ilco/Silca cross-reference \u2014 and verify FBS3-vs-FBS4 in VeDoc where you have access.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-mercedes|Mercedes-Benz chrome oval \"fish\" smart key with red PANIC button and emergency insert blade \u2014 the keyless-go fob a locksmith sees most on Mercedes jobs.]]\n\n*Blade note: HU64 is a high-security 4-track internal (laser/milled) blade \u2014 cut by code on an HSS machine or decode with a Lishi HU64 2-in-1; it is NOT edge-cut. HU39 is the older 4-track milled blade (different keyway/profile). Mercedes has essentially no chip-clone route on any era \u2014 DAS2 is Philips crypto, FBS3 is BGA (no external chip), FBS4 is AES/online \u2014 so \"clone it\" is not on the menu below; the real alternatives are OBD add-key, bench EIS read + password calc, or dealer/online.*\n\nC-CLASS\n\u2022  W202 (1994\u20132000) \u2192 HU64 high-security, 4-track \u2192 Ilco HU64 \u2192 ID44 Philips crypto (PCF7935), IR flip key \u2192 DAS2: legacy MB IR keymaker / Abrites / specialist. Add-key with a working key via IR; AKL is dealer/specialist. *Earliest 1994\u201397 cars may pre-date full DAS2 \u2014 confirm on lookup.*\n\u2022  W203 pre-facelift (2001\u20132004) \u2192 HU64 \u2192 Ilco HU64 \u2192 ID44 (PCF7935) IR flip \u2192 DAS2 IR keymaker; VVDI MB / CGDI IR coverage.\n\u2022  W203 facelift (2004\u20132007) + W204 (2008\u20132014) \u2192 HU64 emergency blade in chrome BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA (no external chip) \u2192 FBS3 EIS/ESL \u2192 money era: VVDI MB BGA / CGDI MB / Autel IM608+G-Box+APB / Abrites. OBD add-key if a working key exists; AKL = read EIS (bench/EIS emulator) \u2192 password/SSID calc \u2192 generate BGA key.\n\u2022  W205 (2015\u20132021) + W206 (2022+) \u2192 HU64 blade in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES, online-anchored \u2192 Xentry + authorized online (VeDoc/XSF). Aftermarket AKL limited/server-dependent. *Very early W205 (mid-2014) \u2014 verify FBS3 vs FBS4 on lookup.*\n\nE-CLASS\n\u2022  W210 (1996\u20132002) \u2192 HU64 \u2192 Ilco HU64 \u2192 ID44 (PCF7935) IR flip \u2192 DAS2 IR keymaker / Abrites / specialist. *Earliest 1996\u201397 confirm on lookup.*\n\u2022  W211 (2003\u20132009) + W212 pre-FBS4 (2010\u2013~2013) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel G-Box: OBD add-key, or bench EIS read + calc for AKL.\n\u2022  W212 FBS4 (~2013\u20132016) + W213 (2017\u20132023) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online. *Split is NOT a clean year: FBS4 appears on W212 from mid-2013 on SA-804 cars \u2014 verify per VIN on transponder lookup.*\n\nS-CLASS\n\u2022  W140 (1994\u20131999) \u2192 HU39 4-track \u2192 Ilco HU39 \u2192 earliest DAS1 IR immobilization (crypto chip comes later in run) \u2192 legacy IR keymaker, dealer/specialist. *Carryover big-body stays HU39, not HU64.*\n\u2022  W220 (2000\u20132006) \u2192 HU64 IR \"smart\" key \u2192 OEM BGA-era \u2192 FBS3-era EIS \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI (IR) + EIS read/calc; OBD add-key with working key. *Earliest W220 may be DAS2/DAS3 IR with external chip \u2014 confirm on lookup.*\n\u2022  W221 (2007\u20132013) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS read + calc. *Latest 2013 W221 may be FBS4 \u2014 verify.*\n\u2022  W222 (2014\u20132020) + W223 (2021+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online only; aftermarket AKL not reliable.\n\nM-CLASS / GLE\n\u2022  W163 (1998\u20132005) \u2192 HU64 flip \u2192 Ilco HU64 \u2192 ID44 (PCF7935) IR flip \u2192 DAS2 IR keymaker / legacy / Abrites.\n\u2022  W164 (2006\u20132011) + W166 ML (2012\u20132015) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS read + calc.\n\u2022  W166 GLE (2016\u20132019) + W167 GLE (2020+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online.\n\nGL / GLS\n\u2022  X164 GL (2007\u20132012) + X166 GL (2013\u2013~2015) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS + calc.\n\u2022  X166 GLS (~2016\u20132019) + X167 GLS (2020+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online. *X166 FBS3\u2192FBS4 changeover ~2015\u201316 \u2014 verify per VIN.*\n\nGLK / GLC\n\u2022  X204 GLK (2010\u20132015) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS + calc.\n\u2022  X253/C253 GLC (2016\u20132022) + X254 GLC (2023+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online.\n\nA-CLASS / B-CLASS / CLA / GLA (COMPACT NGCC)\n\u2022  W169 A (2005\u20132012) + W245 B (2006\u20132011) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS + calc.\n\u2022  W176 A / W246 B / C117 CLA / X156 GLA (2013\u20132015) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 same VVDI/CGDI/Autel calc path.\n\u2022  W177 A / W247 B / C118 CLA / H247 GLA (2016/2019+) + facelift compacts (2016+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online. *2016 is the typical FBS3\u2192FBS4 line on the 176/246/117/156 platform \u2014 confirm on lookup.*\n\nSLK / SLC (ROADSTER)\n\u2022  R170 (1998\u20132004) \u2192 HU64 \u2192 Ilco HU64 \u2192 ID44 (PCF7935) IR flip \u2192 DAS2 IR keymaker / legacy / Abrites.\n\u2022  R171 (2005\u20132011) + R172 SLK (2012\u20132015) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS + calc.\n\u2022  R172 SLK/SLC (2016\u20132020) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online.\n\nCLS\n\u2022  W219 (2005\u20132010) + W218 pre-FBS4 (2011\u2013~2013) \u2192 HU64 in BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI / Autel: OBD add-key or bench EIS + calc.\n\u2022  W218 FBS4 (~2014\u20132017) + C257 (2018+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online. *W218 FBS3\u2192FBS4 changeover ~2013\u201314 \u2014 verify per VIN.*\n\nSPRINTER (VAN)\n\u2022  T1N US Dodge/Freightliner Sprinter (2003\u20132006) \u2192 NOT a Mercedes DAS car \u2192 Chrysler SKREEM immobilizer, Chrysler-style transponder \u2192 program with Chrysler tooling, not MB. *Confirm system on lookup before quoting \u2014 this one breaks the Mercedes pattern.*\n\u2022  NCV3 Sprinter (2007\u20132018) \u2192 HU64 IR/BGA smart key \u2192 OEM BGA \u2192 FBS3 \u2192 VVDI MB / CGDI: OBD add-key or bench EIS read + calc. (Shares blade/system with VW Crafter of the era.)\n\u2022  VS30 Sprinter (2019+) \u2192 HU64 in FBS4 smart key \u2192 OEM FBS4 \u2192 FBS4 AES \u2192 Xentry online.",sources:["https://www.obdii365.com/service/vvdi-mb-bga-tool-car-list-mercedes-benz-lost-all-keys.html","http://blog.uobdii.com/vvdi-mb-4-7-0-download-with-full-list-of-eis-fbs3-all-keys-lost/","https://abkeys.com/products/cgdi-mb-benz-key-programmer-support-mercedes-fbs3-1free-token-2250","https://www.xhorsevvdi.com/wholesale/xhorse-vvdi-benz-vvdi-mb-bga-tool.html","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1998-2005-mercedes-hu64-2-track-transponder-key-44-chip-k-hu64","https://bma-autohaus.com/products/mercedes-single-button-flip-hu64","https://bma-autohaus.com/products/mercedes-single-button-flip-hu39","https://auto-keys.eu/emergency-smart-key-for-mercedes-fbs3-fbs4-blade-signature-hu64-model-04","http://blog.obdii365.com/2020/11/25/mercedes-fbs3-fbs4-difference-fbs4-key-programmer-reviews/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2018/12/23/cgdi-mb-and-vvdi-mb-can-program-fbs4-keys/","https://blog.uobdii.com/benz-fbs4be-and-51-key-information-identification-by-cgdi-mb/","https://www.montyslocksmith.ca/blog/fbs4-mercedes-key-programming-what-you-need-to-know-and-what-we-can-do/","https://blog.obd2.ltd/how-to-do-mercedes-fbs4-key-programming/","http://blog.obdii365.com/2022/05/10/program-mercedes-w164-eis-2009-all-keys-lost-with-autel-im608/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/mercedes-benz-esl-elv-steering-lock-emulator-for-benz-204-207-212-for-use-with-vvdi-mb","https://abkeys.com/products/original-lishi-hu64-v3-pick-decoder-for-mercedes-lishi-tool-3931","https://abrites.com/blog/das-manager-deep-dive","https://www.catautokeys.co.uk/faq/what-is-the-mercedes-das/","https://bma-autohaus.com/products/mercedes-single-button-flip-hu39"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Crypto transponder","Transponder","Lishi","Immobilizer","Bitting","Key code","Keyway","Fob"],aliases:["rolling chip","encrypted chip","80-bit","ID48","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","decoder pick","Lishi tool","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","code series","blind code","direct code","code stamped on lock","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"autokeys-volvo",title:"Making Keys: Volvo",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Volvo is a make where the platform tells you almost everything. Learn the platform names \u2014 P80, P2, P1, P3, SPA/CMA \u2014 and the key, the blank, the chip, and the origination path fall right out. Volvo never went Tibbe or high-security milled; the whole edge-cut era rides one keyway family (HU56, the 4-track also called NE66), and then it jumps straight to proximity smart. The hard part on Volvo isn't the blade, it's the electronics: the immobilizer data lives in the CEM (Central Electronic Module), and on the tough ones you either go online with VIDA or you pull the CEM and read it on the bench.\n\nQUICK REFERENCE\n- 1993\u20131998 P80 (850, early S70/V70/C70, 960/S90/V90): edge-cut 4-track, HU56/NE66 keyway. Early cars pre-chip; transponder phased in ~1998 = Philips Crypto ID44 (PCF7935), the \"blue dot\" key (OE 9187694). Blank Ilco/Silca NE66; clone the ID44 to a Silca T15 / JMA TP14. Onboard/dealer add.\n- 1999\u20132013 P2 (S60, S80, V70, XC70, XC90): edge-cut 4-track NE66, HU56 keyway. Chip = Megamos Crypto ID48. OE key PN 9203132. Program via VIDA online or Abrites; AKL needs VIDA-online (long timeout) or a CEM read.\n- 2004\u20132013 P1 (S40, V50, C30, C70): edge-cut NE66/HU56, ID48; later cars semi-smart. Single \"Master Key\" download in VIDA.\n- 2007\u20132016 P3 (S60 II, S80 II, V60, V70 III, XC60, XC70 II): mix of edge-cut ID48 and semi-smart fobs; VIDA online, CEM read for AKL.\n- 2015\u2013present SPA/CMA (XC90 II, XC60 II, S60/S90, V60/V90, XC40): true proximity smart. Chip = Texas Crypto DST80 (ID8A); newer/orange keys use the AES variant. FCC YG0HUF8423 (orange AES = YG0HUF8423MS), ~433.92 MHz, IC 4008C-HUF8423. Emergency blade is an HU101-profile insert in the fob. Origination = VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM.\n\nConfirm the exact blank+chip on a real VIN \u2014 see the closing note.\n\nBLADE TYPE \u2014 say it out loud\nEvery Volvo you'll cut in the shop is EDGE-CUT. The keyway is HU56 (Silca HU56R \u2014 the Lishi you reach for), and the 4-track blade carries the reference NE66; on Volvo those are the same key system, not two tools. NE66 is the Ilco/Silca number you order most, and the 4-track shell (S66NN-P style) is the common aftermarket transponder key. No sidewinder, no milled/laser, no Tibbe on Volvo. On SPA/CMA smart cars the \"blade\" is just an HU101-profile emergency insert for the door \u2014 the car reads the fob, not the blade.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 P80, 1993\u20131998 (850, early S70/V70/C70, 960/S90/V90)\nEarly P80 (esp. the 1993\u201397 850) is often no-chip: cut an NE66 by code or by decoding the door lock and you're driving. From about 1998 Volvo added the Philips Crypto ID44 (PCF7935) transponder \u2014 the so-called blue-dot key, most associated with the S70/V70/C70. Blank Ilco/Silca NE66; clone the ID44 onto a Silca T15 / JMA TP14 with a TPX-type cloner if you have a working key. AKL here is rare and usually handled through the dealer immobilizer; most P80 work is cut-to-code plus onboard remote sync.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 P2, 1999\u20132013 (S60, S80, V70, XC70, XC90) \u2014 the bread and butter\nThis is the Volvo you'll see most. Edge-cut 4-track NE66, HU56 keyway, Megamos Crypto ID48 chip, OE key part 9203132. On the early P2 sedans the transponder key and the remote were separate items; the later flip keys integrate them, but the transponder registration and the remote sync are still two separate operations logically \u2014 cutting a key only gets you turning the ignition.\n\nProgramming path: VIDA (Volvo's dealer software) with a DiCE or J2534 pass-thru, ONLINE. Volvo stores key data on their servers \u2014 that's why a VIDA-added key survives a later CEM swap, and why true dealer-online is the clean route. Abrites AVDI with the Volvo Commander (AV002) replicates the CEM protocol on-site and is the locksmith's workhorse for P2. On P2 the blade must be cut and the ignition turned to enter programming, so decode/cut FIRST, then add the chip. Note the remote data on some P2 cars lives in the rear-view-mirror module EEPROM, not the CEM \u2014 worth knowing when a remote won't take.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 P1, 2004\u20132013 (S40, V50, C30, C70) and P3, 2007\u20132016\nP1 uses the same ID48 NE66 platform but VIDA handles it with a single \"Master Key\" application \u2014 download it and add keys. Later P1 and much of P3 went semi-smart: a keyless fob that still slots into a dash reader to start. Same idea for origination \u2014 VIDA online, or CEM read for AKL. Don't assume blade type by year alone on P3; some are still edge-cut ID48, some are semi-smart. Check the VIN.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 SPA / CMA, 2015\u2013present (XC90 II, XC60 II, S60/S90, V60/V90, XC40)\nTrue proximity smart keys. The chip is Texas Crypto DST80 (ID8A); newer and the orange keyless keys run the AES variant (YG0HUF8423MS). FCC YG0HUF8423, ~433.92 MHz, IC 4008C-HUF8423. The fob carries an HU101-profile emergency blade for the door only. Origination is all electronic: VIDA online through the BECM/CEM, or Abrites for OBD BECM pairing and, when you're locked out or AKL, an on-bench CEM read. A spare fob with a working key is ~25\u201335 min on Abrites; AKL is far longer \u2014 see the timeout note below.\n\nGETTING IN AND GETTING THE CUTS \u2014 the real menu (not \"Lishi every door\")\nVolvo-specific, in the order a pro actually reaches for them:\n1. Lishi HU56R (2-in-1 pick/decode) on the DOOR lock \u2014 this is your primary. It reads the 4-track wafers directly and hands you the cuts. On many Volvos the door and ignition don't fully match, so also...\n2. Pull the door lock or ignition and BENCH-DECODE it if the in-situ pick fights you \u2014 cheap insurance on worn P2 locks.\n3. Cut to code: Volvo key codes can be pulled through Volvo's code service / dealer with proof of ownership; feed the code to your code machine for a clean NE66.\n4. Impression the NE66 as a fallback on a stubborn lock \u2014 the edge-cut blade impressions fine.\nOn SPA/CMA, \"getting in\" is the HU101 emergency blade for the door, then all-electronic pairing; there's no ignition cylinder to pick.\n\nFROM SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 step by step\n1. Identify by VIN \u2192 platform (P80/P2/P1/P3/SPA-CMA). That sets blank (NE66/HU56 or smart fob), chip (ID44 / ID48 / Texas DST80-ID8A / AES), and path.\n2. Get in: Lishi HU56R on the door, or emergency blade on smart cars.\n3. Get the cuts: decode the door/ignition with the Lishi, or pull-and-bench-decode, or cut to code. Cut a fresh NE66 (edge-cut era).\n4. Provide the transponder: fit the correct ID44/ID48 chip or a fresh smart fob (match DST80 vs AES).\n5. Register the key \u2014 the Volvo-specific part:\n   - Best: VIDA ONLINE with DiCE/J2534 \u2014 writes the key and syncs to Volvo's servers.\n   - Field: Abrites AVDI Volvo Commander for OBD add on P1/P2/P3 and BECM pairing on SPA/CMA.\n   - Hard AKL / dead or corrupted CEM: PULL THE CEM, read/repair the dump on the bench (EEPROM), recover the security data, or run \"Reload CEM\" in VIDA \u2014 then keys re-add cleanly. A corrupted CEM dump is the classic Volvo AKL trap; keep a known-good backup before you write.\n6. THE TIMEOUT \u2014 the Volvo AKL gotcha: on VIDA-online AKL the CEM sits through a long security delay before it will accept the first new key \u2014 roughly 4\u20135 hours on non-keyless cars and around 10 hours on keyless cars, LED going solid/fast-green when it's ready. Plan the job (and the customer) around that; it is not a quick add.\n7. Program the remote separately, then verify lock/unlock and crank.\n\nTOOLS: Lishi HU56R; NE66/HU56 blanks (Ilco/Silca) and 4-track ID48 shells (9203132-style); TPX/TP-class cloner for ID44/ID48 where cloning applies; VIDA + DiCE or a J2534 pass-thru with online Volvo access; Abrites AVDI with AV002 Volvo Commander; EEPROM/bench gear for CEM (and P2 mirror-module) reads; SPA/CMA smart-fob stock in FCC YG0HUF8423 (and the MS/AES orange variant).\n\nVolvo fitment shifts by exact VIN and build date \u2014 one model-year can span two chip families (DST80 vs AES on SPA is the live example). Before you cut or order, confirm the precise blank + chip on the specific VIN against a transponder lookup: Transpondery's Volvo catalog, UHS Hardware, or Ilco/Silca cross-reference.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-volvo|Volvo YGOHUF8423 4-button smart key fob \u2014 the proximity fob on nearly every 2016+ Volvo a locksmith touches.]]\n\nFind your nameplate, drop to the year range that brackets your VIN. Every non-smart Volvo here is edge-cut, HU56 keyway (Silca HU56R), Lishi HU56 (Volvo) \u2014 the 4-track blade also cross-refs as NE66 (same key system, not a second tool). Smart cars carry an HU101-profile emergency insert in the fob. Confirm blank + chip on the VIN before you cut.\n\n850 (1993\u20131997) \u2014 P80\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 blank Ilco/Silca NE66 \u2192 no transponder (mechanical only; 850 ended before the chip phase-in) \u2192 cut to code or impression through the lock; no programming step. Lishi HU56 the door/ignition to read cuts.\n\nS70 / V70 (1998\u20132000), C70 1ST-GEN (1998\u20132004) \u2014 P80\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 (OE 9187694, the \"blue dot\") \u2192 Philips Crypto ID44 / PCF7935 \u2192 clone route works here: copy the ID44 to a Silca T15 / JMA TP14 and cut, no car needed; or dealer/onboard add. Early 1997\u201398 cars may be pre-chip \u2014 confirm on lookup. (S70/V70 1st-gen end 2000; the P80 C70 carries on to ~2004 on the same key system.)\n\nV70 / XC70 (2001\u20132007) \u2014 P2\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 (OE 9203132) \u2192 Megamos Crypto ID48 \u2192 add-a-key or AKL via VIDA online or Abrites. ID48 here is not a simple clone \u2014 program it. Cut path: Lishi HU56, or code service (Volvo key code). AKL: VIDA-online (long timeout, budget the wait) or CEM read with Abrites.\n\nV70 / XC70 (2008\u20132016) \u2014 P3\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 (some flip/semi-smart fobs) \u2192 NE66 / OE semi-smart blade \u2192 ID48 edge-cut on base cars, semi-smart fob on higher trim \u2192 VIDA online to add; CEM read for AKL. Not clone-friendly. Confirm edge-cut vs semi-smart on the VIN.\n\nS60 (2001\u20132009) \u2014 P2\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 (OE 9203132) \u2192 Megamos ID48 \u2192 same as P2 V70: VIDA online / Abrites to program, code service or Lishi HU56 to cut. AKL: VIDA-online long timeout or CEM read.\n\nS60 II / V60 (2011\u20132018) \u2014 P3\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66, most trims semi-smart fob \u2192 ID48 / semi-smart \u2192 VIDA online add; CEM read (Abrites) for AKL. No clone.\n\nS60 III / V60 II (2019\u2013PRESENT) \u2014 SPA\n\u2022  True proximity smart \u2014 blade is an HU101 emergency insert only \u2192 Texas Crypto DST80 (ID8A), newer/orange keys are AES (FCC YG0HUF8423, orange = YG0HUF8423MS, ~433.92 MHz) \u2192 origination via VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM. Car reads the fob, not the blade.\n\nS80 (1999\u20132006) \u2014 P2\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 (OE 9203132) \u2192 Megamos ID48 \u2192 VIDA online / Abrites to program; Lishi HU56 or code service to cut. AKL: VIDA-online long timeout or CEM read.\n\nS80 II (2007\u20132016) \u2014 P3\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66, largely semi-smart fob \u2192 ID48 / semi-smart \u2192 VIDA online add, CEM read for AKL.\n\nXC90 (2003\u20132014) \u2014 P2\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 (OE 9203132) \u2192 Megamos ID48 \u2192 VIDA online / Abrites; Lishi HU56 or code service to cut. AKL: VIDA-online long timeout or CEM read. (Highest-volume P2 you'll see \u2014 treat like S80/S60 P2.)\n\nXC90 II (2016\u2013PRESENT) \u2014 SPA\n\u2022  Proximity smart, HU101 emergency insert only \u2192 DST80 (ID8A); 2019+ orange keys AES (FCC YG0HUF8423MS) \u2192 VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM to originate.\n\nXC60 (2010\u20132017) \u2014 P3\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66, most trims semi-smart fob \u2192 ID48 / semi-smart \u2192 VIDA online add; CEM read (Abrites) for AKL. No clone. Confirm edge-cut vs semi-smart on VIN.\n\nXC60 II (2018\u2013PRESENT) \u2014 SPA\n\u2022  Proximity smart, HU101 insert \u2192 DST80 (ID8A) / AES orange (FCC YG0HUF8423 / \u2026MS) \u2192 VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM.\n\nS40 / V50 (2004\u20132012) \u2014 P1\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 (later trims semi-smart) \u2192 NE66 \u2192 Megamos ID48 \u2192 single \"Master Key\" download in VIDA is the P1 path; add/AKL both go through VIDA. Lishi HU56 or code service to cut. No practical clone.\n\nC30 (2007\u20132013) \u2014 P1\n\u2022  Same as S40/V50: edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 \u2192 ID48 \u2192 VIDA Master Key download; Lishi HU56 / code service to cut.\n\nC70 (2006\u20132013) \u2014 P1 (2ND-GEN RETRACTABLE-HARDTOP)\n\u2022  Edge-cut HU56/NE66 \u2192 NE66 \u2192 Megamos ID48 \u2192 VIDA Master Key download (P1 path); Lishi HU56 or code service to cut. (1st-gen C70 1998\u20132004 is covered under the P80 S70/V70/C70 row above \u2014 ID44 blue-dot, cloneable.)\n\nS90 / V90 (2017\u2013PRESENT) \u2014 SPA\n\u2022  Proximity smart, HU101 emergency insert \u2192 DST80 (ID8A); orange keys AES (FCC YG0HUF8423MS) \u2192 VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM to originate.\n\nXC40 (2018\u2013PRESENT) \u2014 CMA\n\u2022  Proximity smart, HU101 insert \u2192 DST80 (ID8A) / AES orange (FCC YG0HUF8423 / \u2026MS, IC 4008C-HUF8423) \u2192 VIDA online or Abrites CEM/BECM. CMA platform but the key system matches SPA \u2014 treat the same.\nSplit-year flags: transponder phase-in lands ~1998 (P80 pre-chip vs blue-dot ID44). P3 cars (2007\u20132016) straddle edge-cut ID48 vs semi-smart fob by trim \u2014 read the key in hand, don't assume. SPA/CMA smart keys switch DST80 \u2192 AES (orange) around 2019 \u2014 the AES FCC suffix is \u2026MS. Anything you can't confirm on the VIN: confirm on lookup, don't guess the blank or chip.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/volvo_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/1999-2013-volvo-transponder-key-id48-chip-ne66","https://quickeysupply.com/product/pn-9203132-transponder-key-ne66-megamos-id-48-chip-for-1999-2013-volvo-s60-s80-v70-xc70-xc90/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/1999-2013-volvo-s60-s80-v70-xc70-xc90-s66nn-p-4-track-transponder-key-48-chip-aftermarket","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/1997-2005-volvo-s70-v70-c70-transponder-key-id44-chip-blue-dot","Vol133 volvo car key including id44 pcf7935 transponder chip key blade ne66 after market product (eurocarkeyshop.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2016-2022-volvo-smart-key-4b-fcc-yg0huf8423","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/2016-2024-volvo-smart-key-orange-4b-fob-fcc-yg0huf8423ms","https://yourcarkeyguys.com/products/insert-2016-2024-volvo-smart-emergency-key-blade-hu101","https://www.swedespeed.com/threads/all-keys-lost-vida-master-key-programming.678704/","https://www.swedespeed.com/threads/p2-xc90-key-programming-with-vida.647765/","https://svdns.info/2024/01/20/key-coding-p1p2p3-spa-cma/","https://abrites.com/media/user_manuals/html/abrites-diagnostics-for-volvo-user-manual/index.html","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b1kzvAp0wc0","https://blog.uobdii.com/volvo-xc90-2005-all-keys-lost-programming-with-lonsdor-vvdi-prog/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Fob","Transponder","Add-a-key","Keyway","Immobilizer","Code machine","Shell"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Hitag","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","extra key","spare key","add key to system","all keys lost","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","housing","lock body","cylinder body","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"autokeys-jlr",title:"Making keys: Jaguar & Land Rover",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Jaguar and Land Rover live under one roof (JLR), but the key work splits into four eras, and the blade type changes under you as you move through them. Get the era right first \u2014 Tibbe rotary-disc, then Ford HU101 laser, then a BMW-era outlier, then proximity smart with a KVM/RFA module \u2014 because the tool you grab and whether you can even do it on the driveway all hang on that. And know this going in: a big share of modern JLR all-keys-lost is bench-EEPROM or online-only. This is not a make where you walk up and OBD everything. One more constant across every era: North American cars run 315 MHz remotes/fobs, rest-of-world runs 433 MHz \u2014 match the board frequency or the remote is dead even after you cut and chip it.\n\nERA 1 \u2014 TIBBE ROTARY-DISC JAGUAR (roughly mid-1990s to ~2009). This is the classic Jaguar key: an FO21 Tibbe blade, a flat 6-cut rotary-disc key, NOT edge-cut and NOT laser. The blade rotates discs inside the lock instead of lifting pins. X-Type (2001-2009), S-Type (2000-2008) and XJ X350 (2004-2009) run this blade. Blank: Ilco FO21 / FO21T7 (JMA TP00FO-21, Silca card 2240). Chip: the earliest carbon/non-immobilizer keys had NO chip; the transponder cars carry a Texas Instruments 4D-60 (ID60) glass crypto chip \u2014 that's the one, not a 4C fixed-code. Getting in and getting cuts: use the Lishi FO21 Tibbe 2-in-1 \u2014 it picks AND decodes the rotary discs, and it is the single best tool for this era; you read the disc positions right off the tool. Alternatives: pull the door or boot lock and bench-decode the discs, or pull the key code via a Jaguar/NASTF code path. Cut on a Tibbe/rotary-disc machine (or an aftermarket FO21 cutter); a standard flat-key duplicator will NOT cut this correctly. Programming: onboard PIN/relearn or IDS-era diagnostic depending on year and whether it's chipped; the X-Type especially is fussy \u2014 verify the exact relearn per VIN.\n\nERA 2 \u2014 BMW-ERA LAND ROVER OUTLIERS. Don't get blindsided: the L322 Range Rover (2002-2005) is a BMW platform. It runs an HU92 (BMW two-track) blade with BMW EWS immobilizer, ID44/PCF7935 chip \u2014 not a JLR system at all. You treat it like a contemporary BMW: read/write the EWS module on the bench (AK90+, or EWS EEPROM read), cut the HU92 with a Lishi HU92. From the 2006 refresh on, the L322 dropped BMW electronics and moved to HU92-with-ID46 and a Jaguar-style setup \u2014 different job, so pin the exact build year. Older Freelander 1, Discovery 2 and Defender of this period use Rover-heritage blades with early Lucas/transponder immobilizers. The EKA (Emergency Key Access) number is worth knowing but don't overstate it: it's a 4-digit code you dial in at the door/ignition to disarm the immobilizer for ENTRY on the pre-2000, non-transponder cars \u2014 it is NOT a programming code and won't order or make a key, and Freelander 1 from ~2000 on (once it went transponder) has no EKA at all. Chip and blank vary a lot across these \u2014 look it up rather than assume. Flag the L322 EWS point on the exam; it's a common trip-up.\n\nERA 3 \u2014 FORD HU101 LASER, HITAG2 (roughly 2006-2011). When JLR went to the Ford high-security blade, the blade became HU101 \u2014 an internal/laser side-milled (sidewinder) blade, cut on a laser/milling machine, not a flat cutter. Jaguar XF (X250, 2008+), XK, later XJ; Land Rover LR2/Freelander 2, LR3/LR4 (Discovery 3/4), Range Rover Sport. Blank: Ilco HU101 (JMA TP00HU-101, Silca HU101). Chip: PCF7941 / Hitag2 / ID46 (remote-head keys, and early proximity). Getting in / cuts: Lishi HU101 2-in-1 pick-and-decode on the door \u2014 this is your bread and butter for the sidewinder era; decode, then cut HU101 on a Triton/Miracle/laser machine. Alternative is pulling the lock and bench-decoding. Programming: dealer IDS/SDD, or aftermarket OBD \u2014 Autel IM608, Lonsdor K518, Zed-Full handle a lot of the add-key and some AKL. Verify per VIN which side (OBD vs bench) the specific car wants.\n\nERA 4 \u2014 PROXIMITY SMART, KVM then RFA (roughly 2011/2015-2018 and up). These are keyless proximity fobs (push-to-start). There's still an emergency blade inside the fob \u2014 HU101 \u2014 for the driver's door and to mechanically decode if you need cuts. The brains is the KVM (Keyless Vehicle Module) on 2015-2018 cars, and the RFA (Remote Function Actuator) module \u2014 the KVM's successor \u2014 from ~2018 on. This is where JLR gets hard. FCC IDs and fob part numbers vary widely by year/model \u2014 always pull the exact number off the customer's dead fob or the catalog. AKL paths that actually work: (1) BENCH EEPROM \u2014 pull the KVM (2015-2018) and read it with Yanhua Mini ACDP/ACDP-2 Module 9; the chip is a Freescale MC9S12XEQ384, and the tool reads EEPROM/D-Flash/P-Flash with NO soldering and NO crystal/cap removal, generate a working key from the dump, then relearn. This is the go-to for 2015-2018 AKL. Note the hard limit: ACDP Module 9 does NOT do all-keys-lost on JLR after 2018. (2) ONLINE \u2014 JLR Pathfinder (or older SDD) over a genuine/compatible DoIP VCI, using JLR's Topology network read; needs a live subscription and account. (3) OBD \u2014 Lonsdor and Autel cover a range of 2010-2020 duplicate/AKL where a working key or the right module access exists (2018-2022 often needs a KVM/RFA change to add keys). Big warning for 2018+ RFA cars: if the alarm is active with no working key, it locks the BCM, and adding a key needs a working key to disarm \u2014 no working key can mean renewing/virginizing the RFA processor or replacing the RFA/KVM (sometimes the BCM too). Price the job and set expectations before you commit.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST PROCEDURE (general JLR flow):\n1) Identify the era by VIN and by looking at the key/ignition \u2014 Tibbe flat blade? HU101 sidewinder? Proximity fob with push-start? That decides everything below.\n2) Tibbe era: Lishi FO21 to pick the door and decode discs \u2192 cut FO21 on a rotary-disc/Tibbe machine \u2192 onboard/diagnostic relearn for chipped cars.\n3) HU101 mechanical era: Lishi HU101 on the door to pick and decode \u2192 cut HU101 on a laser machine \u2192 add/AKL via IDS/SDD or Autel/Lonsdor OBD.\n4) Proximity KVM (2015-2018): get in with the HU101 emergency blade (Lishi HU101 to decode if you also need a cut) \u2192 locate and PULL the KVM \u2192 read EEPROM with ACDP Module 9 (MC9S12XEQ384) \u2192 generate key \u2192 relearn. Or do it online via Pathfinder if you won't open the module.\n5) Proximity RFA (2018+): usually online via Pathfinder/Topology, or a module renew/virginize service; expect possible module replacement if the alarm has locked the BCM. Confirm feasibility before quoting.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY: Lishi FO21 Tibbe 2-in-1, Lishi HU101 2-in-1, and Lishi HU92 for the L322; a rotary-disc/Tibbe cutter for FO21 and a laser/milling machine (Triton, Miracle, or dealer-grade) for HU101; Yanhua Mini ACDP/ACDP-2 with Module 9 for JLR KVM bench work; a DoIP-capable VCI plus JLR Pathfinder/SDD (Topology) subscription for online AKL; Autel IM608 II, Lonsdor K518, or Zed-Full for OBD add/AKL; AK90+ or an EWS reader for the L322; FO21 and HU101 blanks (Ilco/JMA/Silca), plus 4D-60, ID44 and Hitag2-ID46 chips or the correct proximity fobs on the shelf.\n\nLAST WORD: exact blade, chip family, fob FCC/part number and the AKL route all shift by exact VIN and build date. Before you order a blank or a fob, confirm the precise blank + chip/fob against a transponder lookup \u2014 Transpondery's Jaguar/Land Rover catalog, UHS Hardware, or Ilco \u2014 for that specific VIN.\n\nMODELS & GENERATIONS \u2014 look up your exact vehicle\n\n[[img:photo-autokey-jlr|Jaguar/Land Rover 5-button smart fob (FCC KOBJTF10A, 2011-2020) \u2014 the shared JLR proximity key most jobs on these cars land on.]]\nBlade shorthand: Tibbe = 4-track rotary (Ford/Jaguar FO21) \xb7 HU92 = BMW 2-track (dual-sided milled) laser \xb7 HU101 = Ford/JLR/Volvo 2-track internal laser (also the emergency blade inside every JLR smart fob) \xb7 Edge = standard shoulder-cut tumbler key. \"OBD\" below means onboard/aftermarket programming through the diagnostic port (Smart Pro ADS2207, Autel IM508/608, Abrites AVDI, JLR Doctor) or dealer (SDD/Pathfinder/Topix). \"Clone\" is only listed where the chip is genuinely cloneable.\n\n---\n\nJaguar S-Type (1999\u20132008) & X-Type (2001\u20132009) \u2014 one method the whole run:\n\u2022  Both, all years \u2192 Tibbe rotary, keyway FO21 \u2192 blank Ilco FO21 / FO21T7 (transponder version) \u2192 chip Texas 4D60 glass, encrypted \u2014 *not cloneable* (confirm exact transponder ref on lookup) \u2192 Origination: cut-to-code from the 6-digit Jaguar key code (111111\u2013444444, 4 depths) is the fast route; otherwise a Tibbe pick/decoder (FO21) reads the lock. Transponder is added by OBD (SDD or Smart Pro/Autel). AKL = OBD onboard, read PIN then add \u2014 no clone, no reset shortcut.\n\nJAGUAR XK / XK8 / XKR \u2014 SPLITS AT THE X100\u2192X150 CHANGE:\n\u2022  XK8/XKR X100 (1997\u20132006) \u2192 Tibbe FO21 \u2192 Ilco FO21/FO21T7 \u2192 4D60 glass encrypted (early '97\u201399 keys are a simpler read-only variant \u2014 *confirm on lookup*) \u2192 same paths as S-Type above (cut-to-code, Tibbe decoder, OBD add).\n\u2022  XK X150 (2007\u20132014) \u2192 smart/insert key, emergency blade HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco HU101 blade \u2192 NXP Hitag2 (ID46), 5-button KR55WK49244 (*early X150 ~2007\u201309 used an insert-style bladed key before full push-button proximity \u2014 confirm insert-vs-proximity and FCC on lookup*) \u2192 Origination: Lishi HU101 decodes the door for the emergency blade (or cut-to-code); add key by OBD; AKL by OBD (may need PIN/online). No clone.\n\nJAGUAR XF \u2014 ONE METHOD ACROSS BOTH BODIES:\n\u2022  X250 (2008\u20132015) and X260 (2016\u20132020) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 X250 = Hitag2 (ID46) (*late-X250 builds may move to Hitag Pro \u2014 confirm on lookup*); X260 = Hitag Pro, KVM/RFA security \u2192 Origination: Lishi HU101 or cut-to-code for the blade; add key by OBD (X260 often needs KVM/RFA security data or online); AKL on X260 typically needs module bench-read (Yanhua ACDP Module 9) or dealer online. No clone.\n\nJAGUAR XJ \u2014 SPLITS AT THE X358\u2192X351 CHANGE:\n\u2022  X308 / X350 / X358 (1997\u20132009) \u2192 Tibbe FO21 \u2192 Ilco FO21/FO21T7 \u2192 4D60 glass encrypted \u2192 cut-to-code / Tibbe decoder, OBD add. Same as S-Type.\n\u2022  X351 (2010\u20132019) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 early = Hitag2 (ID46), later builds move to Hitag Pro / KVM \u2014 *confirm split (~2013) on lookup* \u2192 Lishi HU101 / cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD; AKL OBD or KVM bench-read. No clone.\n\nJaguar XE, F-Type, F-Pace, E-Pace, I-Pace (2014/2015\u2013present) \u2014 all one modern method:\n\u2022  All, all years \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 blade \u2192 Hitag Pro / AES, KVM (approx. 2014\u20132018) then RFA module (approx. 2018+) \u2192 Origination: Lishi HU101 or cut-to-code for the emergency blade; add key by OBD where security data is available (Autel/AVDI/Pathfinder); AKL needs KVM/RFA bench-read (Yanhua ACDP Module 9) or dealer online token. No clone, no reset.\n\n---\n\nLAND ROVER DISCOVERY \u2014 THREE DISTINCT METHODS, SPLIT HARD:\n\u2022  Discovery 1 & 2 (1989\u20132004) \u2192 Edge-cut blade \u2192 blank cut to code \u2192 NO transponder (Disco 2 immobiliser has no chip) \u2192 Origination: cut blade to code; security is the 4-digit EKA code (Disco 2 range 1\u201315, no \"0\") entered at the door + remote self-learn. No programmer needed.\n\u2022  Discovery 3 / LR3 (2004\u20132009) & Discovery 4 / LR4 (2009\u20132016) \u2192 flip remote, blade HU101 laser \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag2 (ID46), 315/433MHz by region \u2192 Origination: Lishi HU101 or cut-to-code; add key + remote by OBD (SDD or Smart Pro/Autel). AKL by OBD. No clone.\n\u2022  Discovery Sport (2015+) & Discovery 5 / L462 (2017+) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag Pro, KVM\u2192RFA \u2192 Lishi/cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD w/ security data; AKL = KVM/RFA bench-read or dealer online.\n\nLAND ROVER FREELANDER \u2014 ONE FL1 METHOD, THEN LR2:\n\u2022  Freelander 1 (1998\u20132006) \u2192 Edge-cut blade \u2192 blank cut to code \u2192 Land Rover/Rover immobiliser (NOT BMW EWS) + EKA code \u2192 chip Philips-type transponder \u2014 *confirm exact chip and whether cloneable on lookup* (facelift builds ~2004+ may differ) \u2192 cut to code + EKA; 2nd key by clone if the chip proves cloneable, otherwise OBD.\n\u2022  Freelander 2 / LR2 (2006\u20132014) \u2192 flip remote, blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag2 (ID46) \u2192 Lishi HU101 / cut-to-code; add key + remote by OBD; AKL OBD.\n\nRange Rover (full-size) \u2014 three eras, split at drivetrain/electronics changes:\n\u2022  L322 BMW era (2002\u20132005) \u2192 HU92 BMW laser blade \u2192 blank HU92 \u2192 BMW EWS3 chip \u2192 Origination: cut/decode HU92; transponder written on the bench via AK90 / dealer DIS (BMW-style); AKL = EWS bench read. Aftermarket OBD is limited here.\n\u2022  L322 Ford/Jaguar era (2006\u20132012) \u2192 HU92 bladed remote (same L322 locks, Ford/Jaguar electronics) \u2192 HU92 blank \u2192 Hitag2 (ID46) \u2192 cut/decode HU92; add key by OBD (SDD or Smart Pro/Autel). *Late 2010\u201312 builds \u2014 confirm bladed-vs-smart on lookup.*\n\u2022  L405 (2013\u20132021) & L460 (2022+) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag Pro / AES, KVM\u2192RFA \u2192 Lishi/cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD w/ data; AKL = KVM/RFA bench-read (ACDP Module 9) or dealer online.\n\nRANGE ROVER SPORT \u2014 SPLITS AT L320\u2192L494:\n\u2022  L320 (2005\u20132013) \u2192 flip remote, blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag2 (ID46) \u2192 Lishi HU101 / cut-to-code; add key by OBD; AKL OBD. (Shares method with LR3/LR4.)\n\u2022  L494 (2014\u20132022) & L461 (2023+) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag Pro / AES, KVM\u2192RFA \u2192 Lishi/cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD w/ data; AKL = bench-read or dealer online.\n\nRANGE ROVER EVOQUE \u2014 EARLY FLIP VS LATER SMART:\n\u2022  L538 early (2012\u20132015) \u2192 flip/remote, blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag2 (ID46) \u2192 Lishi HU101 / cut-to-code; add key by OBD; AKL OBD.\n\u2022  L538 later (2016\u20132018) & L551 (2019+) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag Pro / AES, KVM\u2192RFA \u2192 Lishi/cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD w/ data; AKL = KVM/RFA bench-read or dealer online. *Confirm exact flip\u2192smart split (~2016) on lookup.*\n\nLAND ROVER DEFENDER \u2014 CLASSIC VS NEW IS A TOTAL BREAK:\n\u2022  Classic Td5 / Puma (1998\u20132016) \u2192 Edge-cut blade \u2192 blank cut to code \u2192 early Td5 immobiliser uses EKA code (10AS/5AS), no chip; Puma (2007\u201316) uses a Ford-derived edge key \u2014 *confirm blank/chip on lookup* \u2192 cut to code + EKA / OBD for Puma remotes.\n\u2022  L663 new Defender (2020+) \u2192 smart proximity, emergency blade HU101 \u2192 Ilco HU101 \u2192 Hitag Pro / AES, RFA module \u2192 Lishi/cut-to-code for blade; add key OBD w/ security data or dealer online; AKL = RFA bench-read or dealer Pathfinder online. No clone.\n\n---\n\nRule of thumb by era: Tibbe FO21 = every Ford-era Jaguar sedan/coupe through ~2009 (cut-to-code + OBD, never clone). HU101 flip on ID46 (2004\u20132014 Land Rovers) = the OBD-friendly middle years (Lishi + OBD add). HU101 smart on Hitag Pro (2013+ everything, KVM then RFA) = blade is easy, but AKL means pulling the KVM/RFA to bench-read or going dealer-online \u2014 budget for it.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/jaguar_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/land_rover_transponder_catalog.html","https://abkeys.com/products/jaguar-transponder-key-4d60-id60-chip-1999up-fo21-tibbe-key-321","https://royalkeysupply.com/products/jaguar-aftermarket-2000-2009-x-type-xj-transponder-key-fo21t7","https://autolox.co.uk/range-rover-l322-2002-2006-lost-key-replacement/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2010-2019-land-rover-jaguar-emergency-key-hu101-pn-ch22-15k601-ab-ekb-lrj-001","https://www.remotekey.co.uk/help/land-rover-freelander-key-fob-and-immobiliser-problems/","https://www.myfreelander.co.uk/Electrical/elecprobs1.htm","https://www.yanhuaacdp.com/wholesale/yanhua-mini-acdp-key-programmer-for-jlr-kvm-immo.html","https://www.yanhuaacdp.com/wholesale/yanhua-mini-acdp-land-rover-module-for-jlr-immo.html","https://blog.uobdii.com/top-3-jaguar-land-rover-2015-2018-akl-programmers/","https://abrites.com/blog/the-ultimate-guide-to-modern-jaguar-land-rover-key-programming-from-an-abrites-perspective","https://www.ecusaver.com/product/land-rover-jaguar-2018-rfa-kvm-module-renew-virginize-service-jpla-k8d2-j9c3/65","http://blog.obdii365.com/2020/11/10/jaguar-land-rover-kvm-position-for-yanhua-acdp-key-programming/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Lishi","Transponder","Fob","Decoder","Key code","Immobilizer","Duplicator","Push-to-start"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","HPC","key copier","key cutting machine","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"autokeys-harley",title:"Making keys: Harley-Davidson & Buell (motorcycle)",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Harley-Davidson (and its Buell cousins) is the make where you throw out most of your car-key instincts. The ignition/fork-lock key on a Harley is a plain mechanical WAFER key with NO transponder chip in it. There is no 4C, no ID46, no Megamos hiding in the head. The immobilizer function lives in a SEPARATE radio fob (Smart Security System), and the mechanical key is cut by code or by decode just like a house lock. Keep those two problems apart in your head: cutting the metal blade is one job, mating a security fob is a completely different job. Confusing the two is the number-one mistake on this make.\n\n[[diagram:harley-two-systems|A plain wafer blade and a separate radio fob... two jobs on one invoice, and only one of them is cutting]]\n\nQUICK REFERENCE (era \u2192 blank \u2192 security \u2192 path)\n- 1970s\u20131985 (Shovelhead/early Evo, FL/FX): older barrel & wafer locks, Ilco X101/X126/X127/X142 depending on lock. No electronics. Decode/code-cut.\n- 1984\u20131995 Softail/Wide Glide, round flip-cap ignition: Ilco HYD12 (Taylor/Silca X226). No chip. Code stamped under the lock.\n- ~1994\u20132011 Touring/Softail/Dyna/Sportster wafer ignition: Ilco HYD13 (X234, JMA HD-4D); some accessory/fork locks HYD17 (X284, Sportster \"D\" series). No chip. Code on key/lock.\n- ~2006\u20132013 (security-equipped): same mechanical blank as the model + TSSM (Turn-Signal Security Module) push-button fob. Pair via dealer DT II OR aftermarket TechnoResearch Centurion.\n- 2012+ Sportster: two code series \u2014 Ilco HYD16 (\"A\" series, X283) and HYD15 (\"B\" series, X287). No chip. Cut by code/decode.\n- ~2011/2014+ Touring/Softail (Rushmore on): mechanical blank per model + HFSM (Hands-Free Security Module), buttonless proximity fob, 5-digit PIN. Pair via dealer DT II OR Centurion.\nFitment varies by VIN and by which lock (ignition vs fork vs saddlebag/Tour-Pak vs gas cap) \u2014 always confirm on a transponder/blank lookup before you cut.\n\nTHE MECHANICAL KEY \u2014 WHAT IT FITS\nOn most Harleys the ignition switch and the separate FORK LOCK (steering-neck lock) are keyed alike from the factory, and on baggers the saddlebag/Tour-Pak/hard-bag latches and locking gas cap are usually keyed to match too. So one correctly cut blade opens the whole bike. When a customer says \"I only lost the ignition key,\" you can still originate the whole set from any one lock on the bike.\n\nOlder round flip-cap ignitions (mid-'80s to mid-'90s Softail/Wide Glide, HYD12): the KEY CODE is stamped on a metal tab on the UNDERSIDE of the lock \u2014 you have to pull the lock to read it, and the code is the last three (numeric) characters of the stamped string. Cut that on a code machine and it works first try.\n\nModern bikes: the KEY NUMBER is stamped on the TOP of the factory key under the black plastic head \u2014 pop the cover and you have the code. A dealer will also pull the key number from the VIN with proof of ownership and either cut a key or sell you a keyed-to-match ignition switch kit. That VIN-to-code service is the fastest legitimate mechanical AKL path on this make.\n\nTHE SECURITY FOB \u2014 TSSM vs HFSM\nTwo generations. TSSM (roughly 2006\u20132013 on many models) uses a fob WITH a button; you can arm/disarm and, importantly, you can self-enter a PIN via the turn-signal switches to disarm if the fob battery dies. HFSM (later, \"hands-free,\" ~2011+) uses a BUTTONLESS proximity fob with an antenna in the module; the bike disarms automatically when the fob is in range.\n\nPIN entry / limp-home (fob dead): the PIN is FIVE digits, each digit 1\u20139, NO zeros; the factory default is 3-1-3-1-3 and a lot of bikes were never changed off it \u2014 try it first. Sequence (HFSM): run/stop switch to RUN, ignition ON-OFF-ON-OFF-ON, then LEFT turn twice + RIGHT turn once \u2014 the current PIN shows on the odometer; LEFT signal scrolls a digit, RIGHT signal advances the slot; turn ignition off to store. TSSM button-fob version is similar but you press the FOB BUTTON to advance slots. It must be done briskly or it times out and you restart. This lets a rider move a bike with a dead fob \u2014 memorize it; the dead-fob call is common.\n\nPairing a NEW fob / recovering an UNKNOWN PIN: this is NOT dealer-only anymore \u2014 that's the correction to make. Yes, Harley DIGITAL TECHNICIAN II on the data-link connector pairs fobs and resets PINs at the dealer (you feed it the fob's 24-digit serial). But the real locksmith tool for this make is the TechnoResearch CENTURION / Super Pro. Plugged into the bike, Centurion will \"Recover Unknown PIN\" and report the 5-digit PIN to disarm via turn signals, program hands-free fobs (roughly 2007\u20132023), program push-button TSSM fobs, and unlock/handle the security module. One gotcha it forces you to identify: whether the bike is a 4-PIN J1850 (older) or a 6-PIN CAN-bus machine \u2014 pick the right cable/mode. So on Harley you can own BOTH the metal AND the RF with Centurion; the dealer DT II is the fallback, not the only door.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST\nMechanical side, the MENU in the order you actually reach for it:\n1. Read the code. Modern: pull the key-head cover if any key survives; none survive \u2192 dealer VIN-to-key-code with proof of ownership. Old round locks: pull the ignition or fork lock and read the stamped code on the underside tab. Cleanest mechanical AKL.\n2. Bench-decode a pulled lock. The fork lock and saddlebag locks come out easily; disassemble and read the wafer depths, or decode with the lock in hand, then originate the blade. Everything's keyed alike, so decode the EASIEST lock (a saddlebag latch) and you've got the ignition too.\n3. Impression. Harley wafer locks impression well \u2014 a HYD12/13/15/16 blank, a file, and the ignition or fork lock gives you a working key if you can't get the code and don't want to tear the switch down.\n4. Pick / decode in place. Low-security wafer locks; pick to turn or use a wafer decoder, then code the depths.\n5. Keyed-to-match switch kit (dealer/HD part) as a last resort if a lock is trashed.\nSecurity/electronics side (bikes with TSSM/HFSM): after you've solved the metal, use Centurion to Recover Unknown PIN (so the rider can limp-home via turn signals) and to program a fresh fob to the module \u2014 or send the fob job to a dealer for DT II. No mainstream 2-in-1 Lishi is listed for Harley wafer ignitions, so decode/impression/code is the real mechanical workflow, not \"Lishi every door.\"\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY\nCode machine (Ilco/HPC/Framon) with Harley card/spacing-depth data; blanks HYD12, HYD13, HYD15, HYD16, HYD17 and older X101/X126/X127/X142; wafer picks and a wafer decoder; impressioning file + handle and blackening; small drivers and snap-ring pliers to pull fork/saddlebag locks; the PIN-entry limp-home sequence and the 3-1-3-1-3 default; and for fob/PIN work a TechnoResearch Centurion (Super Pro) with the right 4-PIN J1850 vs 6-PIN CAN cable \u2014 dealer Digital Technician II is the backup. Buell uses the same H-D blanks and the same TSSM-era security, so the same kit covers it.\n\nEXAM POINTS TO NAIL\nThe Harley ignition key is mechanical, no transponder \u2014 the immobilizer is the fob, not the key. Ignition, fork lock, bags, and gas cap are keyed alike. Old round locks stamp the code under the lock; modern keys stamp it under the head. PIN is five digits, 1\u20139, no zero, default 3-1-3-1-3. Fob pairing and unknown-PIN recovery can be done by dealer DT II OR by aftermarket TechnoResearch Centurion (identify 4-PIN J1850 vs 6-PIN CAN).\n\nVERIFY THE EXACT BLANK + CODE SERIES ON A SPECIFIC VIN before you cut \u2014 Sportster A vs B series (HYD16 vs HYD15) and touring vs older locks differ. Confirm on a transponder/blank lookup (Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or the Ilco/Kaba search) for the precise blank and, on security-equipped bikes, the fob part number/FCC ID for that VIN.",sources:["https://mrlock.com/brand-index/ilco-kaba-ilco/key-blanks-by-kaba-ilco/motorcycle-key-blanks-by-kaba-ilco/harley-davidson-key-blanks-by-ilco/","https://carlocksandkeys.com/blog/how-to-find-the-key-code-for-many-harley-davidson-softail-and-wide-glide-models-from-1984-to-1994/","https://onlinelocksmithstore.com/shop/hyd12-uncut-motorcycle-key-for-various-models-of-harley-davidson-1986-1995/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/x234-hyd13-harley-davidson","https://mrlock.com/key-blank-jet-hyd17-np-for-harley-davidson/","https://www.ebay.com/itm/265334504511","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2017/MC-10109612-9999.pdf","https://manual.technoresearch.info/manuals/common-jobs/customer-lost-key-fobs-and-doesnt-know-5-digit-pin/","https://manual.technoresearch.info/manuals/centurion-manual/8-centurion-specific-functions-for-harley-davidson-motorcycles/8-3-centurion-super-pro-keyfob-programming/","https://manual.technoresearch.info/manuals/centurion-manual/8-centurion-specific-functions-for-harley-davidson-motorcycles/8-23-centurion-super-pro-security-push-button-fob-manual-programming/","https://carscanners.net/harley-davidson-key-programmig/","https://www.redlineharley.com/blog/how-do-you-reset-a-harley-davidson-key-fob--66991","https://www.safetywish.com/how-to-program-harley-key-fob/","https://www.harley-davidson.com/us/en/shop/smart-security-system/p/68207-07A"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Fob","Code machine","Transponder","Decoder","FCC ID","Lishi","Immobilizer","Impressioning"],aliases:["key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","chip key","immo chip","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","remote FCC","fob FCC ID","identify the remote","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","blind code","direct code","code stamped on lock"]},{id:"autokeys-japanmoto",title:"Making keys: Honda, Yamaha, Suzuki & Kawasaki motorcycles",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Japanese bikes are almost all EDGE-CUT (single- or double-sided wafer) blades \u2014 no laser/sidewinder, no Tibbe. That never really changes across the eras; what changes is the transponder layered on top of the blade from the early-to-mid 2000s. So the blade is the easy part on a bike, and unlike a car you don't have to \"get in\" \u2014 there are no doors. Your whole job is (1) make a blade that turns the ignition and (2) satisfy the immobilizer IF the bike has one. Big real-world truth first: on Suzuki and Kawasaki the immobilizer was largely a EUROPE/AUSTRALIA/UK-market fitment \u2014 US-spec Suzukis and US-spec Kawasakis very often have NO immobilizer at all, so on a US bike you're frequently just cutting to code. Don't assume a chip; look for the immo warning light on the cluster and a chip in the key head first.\n\n[[diagram:moto-key-era-decision|Cut the blade, then satisfy the immobiliser... and there is no door to open at all]]\n\nTHE UNIVERSAL \"GET THE CUTS\" MENU (all four makes): every one of these bikes keys the ignition, seat/tail lock, fuel cap, and helmet lock ALIKE to one bitting. The seat lock and steering/fork lock are usually the same code. So: (a) pull the SEAT LOCK or FUEL-CAP LOCK \u2014 two screws, far easier than the ignition \u2014 bench-decode the wafers, and you have the ignition cut. (b) Read the CODE STAMP: on the lock cylinder body, on the plastic key clip/knock-out tag that came with the bike, or on the original key. (c) CODE FROM DEALER: Honda/Yamaha/Suzuki/Kawasaki dealers can pull a key code from the VIN/frame number on most pre-smart-key bikes (newer models increasingly restricted). (d) PICK/DECODE the ignition with a motorcycle wafer pick + tension or a moto Lishi if the seat lock is gone. (e) IMPRESSION or try-out/jiggler keys on the older 5\u20136 wafer locks \u2014 they impression easily. (f) PULL the ignition cylinder and decode on the bench for a true AKL. Cut on a Silca/JMA code machine or hand-cut to the wafer depths.\n\nHONDA \u2014 mechanical era (pre-2000): edge-cut wafer blade; the exact Ilco/Silca moto reference is model-specific (older twins/singles on small motorcycle keyways, Gold Wing families on their own blanks) \u2014 confirm the number on the Ilco motorcycle key-blank reference for that model, don't guess. Note the widely-quoted \"HD106\" is actually the Honda CAR keyway (X181/HD90, X214/HD103 are car blanks too); it matters because the modern aftermarket Honda HISS key is commonly built on an HD106/HD108-STYLE transponder shell, which is where that number comes from. No chip on pre-2000 mechanical \u2014 cut to code and done. HISS era (Honda Ignition Security System, ~2000 on): first on CBR929RR/CBR954, then CBR600RR, VFR800, ST1300, Gold Wing GL1800 (2001+), later CBRs, VFR1200, Africa Twin. Edge-cut blade with the transponder in the head. Chip is Philips/NXP CRYPTO \u2014 Hitag2 / ID46 / PCF7936 family (cross-refs JMA TP12, Silca T14, XT27) \u2014 confirmed for HISS; verify per VIN. Because it's a crypto Hitag2, straight cloning is NOT guaranteed (only password-mode ID46 clones cleanly); plan on programming, not cloning, for most HISS. HISS registers a fixed number of keys to the HISS control unit; the red immobilizer light must go out or it won't fire. ADD-KEY with a working key: cut the new blade, then a working master + the new key are learned through the HISS unit (dealer HDS or aftermarket moto tool). AKL HONDA: cut the blade from the seat lock/decode, then feed the immobilizer \u2014 read the HISS/ECU EEPROM on the bench to extract stored codes/PIN with a motorcycle programmer, or dealer online. Newest Gold Wing (2018+) is keyless smart-key \u2014 proximity FOB, not a blade.\n\nYAMAHA \u2014 mechanical era: blade is Ilco X248 / YM63 (Silca YH35; older keyways YM45); classic code series like A7001\u2013A8500 stamped on lock or tag. Cut to code. IMMOBILIZER era (mid-2000s+): YZF-R1 (2004+), R6, FJR1300, T-Max, FZ1/FZ6, Super T\xe9n\xe9r\xe9, MT line. Still an edge-cut blade (X248/YM63 family) with the chip in the head; chip is Texas 4D-69 (confirmed). Yamaha uses a MASTER/USER-KEY design: the RED key is the registration/master, the BLACK keys are working user keys. With the red master you self-register new black keys (turn-on/turn-off learn sequence per model, or via tool). LOSE THE BLACK KEYS ONLY, still have red = trivial add. AKL YAMAHA (red master also lost) is far worse \u2014 the immo lives in the transponder aerial ring around the ignition AND the ECU, both must agree; you're into ECU/immo EEPROM reading, dealer, or a matched immo-box swap. Newest T-Max/scooters use a smart-key FOB.\n\nSUZUKI \u2014 mechanical era: blade is Ilco X241 / SUZ18 (Silca SUZ; older SUZ14 keyways); code series like A6001\u2013A7000. Pre-'04 SV650/1000 and older GSX-R are plain cut-to-code. SAIS era (Suzuki Advanced Immobiliser System, ~2005\u20132008+, mainly Euro/AUS-spec): GSX-R (2006+), Hayabusa Gen2 (2008+), B-King, Bandit, V-Strom, some GSX-S. Edge-cut transponder blade \u2014 the SAIS blade is commonly the SZ17R shell; chip is Texas 4D6B (some 4D62) \u2014 confirmed. Because it's a Texas 4D, the very handy Suzuki trick works: cut a fresh blade to code and CLONE the 4D transponder from a working key onto it (Keyline/JMA/Xhorse/Texas cloner); the bike accepts it with no ECU programming because the chip ID is identical. That's your first move when the customer has one working key. ADD-KEY/AKL true programming uses dealer SDS or aftermarket (OBDSTAR, Autel IM, EasyKey, Carmo bench service). AKL SUZUKI: decode the seat/cap lock for the blade, then read the ECU/immo to pull the PIN and register a virgin chip, or swap a matched immo/ECU set. Remember: a US-spec Suzuki usually has NO SAIS \u2014 confirm before you buy a chip.\n\nKAWASAKI \u2014 mechanical era: edge-cut blade, Ilco KW14 / X259 (also KA-14 / X103 on some models; Silca KW14). Cut to code from the lock/tag/VIN. IMMOBILIZER era (\"KI\", EUROPE/UK-market bikes ~2004+, American bikes generally never got it): chip in the key head, edge-cut blade; antenna at the top of the keyswitch. Chip family varies (Hitag2/ID46 or Texas on different models) \u2014 verify on the Kawasaki transponder catalog per VIN, don't assume. KIPASS (Kawasaki Intelligent Proximity Activation Start System): keyless proximity FOB on 1400GTR/Concours 14, ZX-14R (some markets), Ninja H2/H2 SX, Versys 1000, Ninja 1000SX. KIPASS keeps a hidden mechanical emergency blade for the steering lock, but starting is FOB-only. ADD-KEY/AKL KAWASAKI: immobilizer bikes register to the ECU \u2014 dealer or aftermarket (OBDSTAR, EasyKey); KIPASS is FOB registration, and AKL usually means dealer or ECU/EEPROM work plus cutting the hidden emergency blade by decoding the steering lock.\n\nFROM-SCRATCH / ALL-KEYS-LOST \u2014 the repeatable procedure: (1) Identify immo or not (immo light on the cluster, chip visible in a cutaway key head, model/year/MARKET \u2014 US Suzuki/Kawasaki often none). (2) Get the blade: pull the SEAT LOCK or FUEL-CAP lock, bench-decode the wafers \u2192 that IS the ignition cut; or pull the ignition cylinder and decode; or code from VIN via dealer. Cut on a Silca/JMA machine. (3) No immo \u2192 done. (4) Immo present \u2192 the cut blade cranks but dies/no-fire until the chip is accepted: prefer CLONE from a working key (Suzuki 4D6B especially, any clonable 4D/Hitag2) onto the new blade; else read the ECU/immo EEPROM to extract the PIN and register a virgin chip; else dealer online. (5) Master-key systems (Yamaha red) \u2192 use it to self-learn; if lost, ECU/immo work.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY: motorcycle wafer picks + tension / moto Lishi, seat/cap lock removal kit, a code machine (Silca/JMA), calipers/decoder gauges, jigglers for older wafers; a transponder cloner (Keyline 884, JMA TRS-5000, Xhorse VVDI) for 4D/clonable chips; a moto immobilizer programmer (OBDSTAR, Autel IM508/IM608 moto, EasyKey, OBDEMOTO 900Pro, Carmo bench service) for register/EEPROM work. Chips to stock: Hitag2/ID46 (PCF7936, JMA TP12), Texas 4D-69 (Yamaha), Texas 4D6B/4D62 (Suzuki); blade blanks HD106/HD108-style HISS shell (Honda), X248/YM63 (Yamaha), X241/SUZ18 and SZ17R (Suzuki), KW14/X259 (Kawasaki). Exact blade + chip + FCC on any specific bike VARIES by VIN and market \u2014 before you buy, confirm on a transponder lookup (Transpondery, UHS, Ilco/Silca catalog) for that frame number.",sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/honda_transponder_catalog.html","https://transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/yamaha_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/suzuki_transponder_catalog.html","https://www.transpondery.com/transponder_catalog/kawasaki_transponder_catalog.html","https://images.carid.com/advanced-diagnostics/items/pdf/motorcycle-key-blank-reference.pdf","https://www.keycraze.com/ilco/motorcycle-key-blank-reference.pdf","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/philips-46-crypto-2-tag-blank-transponder-chip-for-honda-nissan-hyundai-kia-gm","https://www.americankeysupply.com/product/honda-transponder-key-shell-hd106-hd108-style-jma-hond-21-p-303","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/ym63-x248-yamaha-motorcycle-key-jma","https://www.ebay.com/itm/154891600481","https://bikedecoders.com/suzuki-motorcycle-key-programming/","http://www.autoelecs.com/index.php?main_page=product_info&products_id=2823","https://abkeys.com/products/suzuki-motorcycle-suzuki-gsx-r-hayabusa-chip-less-key-sz17-black-2817","https://www.carmo-electronics.com/repair/key-service/cark-su-chip-key-program-1","https://keymateinc.com/shop-online/key-blanks/automotive-keys/motorcycle/ilco-x259-kw14-key-blank-kawasaki-suzuki-triumph-motorcycles-detail.html","https://www.motorcycleproject.com/text/kawasaki_kipass.html","https://bikedecoders.com/kawasaki-motorcycle-key-programming/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Immobilizer","Transponder","Shell","Code machine","Fob","Cloning","Lishi","Key code"],aliases:["anti-theft","security light","car won't start","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","housing","lock body","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","clone the chip","copy the key","clonable chip","4C clone","2-in-1","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"autokeys-eurmoto",title:"Making keys: BMW, Ducati, Triumph, KTM & Indian/Polaris motorcycles",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Bikes are not cars. The keyway is usually simple edge-cut or a two-track external blade, not a Mercedes-grade sidewinder, so the CUTTING is the easy part. The immobilizer and the fact that most of these makes keep NO key records by VIN is what will eat your afternoon. Read the era, know where the secret lives (key chip, dashboard, ECU, or a paper code card), and have an EEPROM plan for all-keys-lost, because on bikes AKL is often bench work, not a plug-in.\n\n[[diagram:euromoto-secret-location|Four places the secret can live, and which one it is decides the tooling and the price]]\n\nBMW MOTORRAD. Three eras. (1) Airheads and the oilhead R/K bikes through roughly the late 1990s-early 2000s (R1100/R1150 GS/RS/RT, R1200C, K1200) = pure mechanical, NO transponder. Two-track external key; aftermarket blanks vary \u2014 verify on a lookup. No electronics: cut by duplication, impression the ignition, or pull the ignition/steering-lock and decode on the bench. BMW does keep some mechanical key codes at the dealer by VIN for this era, worth a call. (2) EWS (\"Elektronische Wegfahr Sperre\") immobilizer era on the CAN-bus bikes from about 2004 (K1200S/R/GT, R1200GS/RT hexhead, later F- and S-bikes). Transponder-chipped ignition key on an HU92 two-track blade; chip is the PCF7935 (ID44) family on the common 2000s EWS keys, with ID46/PCF7936 appearing on some later units \u2014 verify per VIN. The immo talks to the ring antenna at the ignition and the EWS/immo electronics. (3) Keyless Ride from about 2014 on the big tourers and adventure bikes (K1600, R1200/1250 RT and GS, S1000 variants) \u2014 proximity fob, no blade turned in a lock; a passive plastic emergency key rides in/near the fob and, on a dead-battery start, gets held against a marked RFID spot (on the RT-family, under the rear-fender liner ahead of the rear tire) to authenticate. QUICK REF: pre-'04 = mechanical two-track, no chip; ~'04-'13 = HU92 + PCF7935/ID44 (or ID46); '14+ = Keyless Ride prox fob. AKL/from-scratch: get IN by pulling the steering-lock/ignition and decoding, or impression. IMPORTANT TOOL CORRECTION: the GS-911 (HexCode) is a DIAGNOSTIC tool \u2014 fault codes and service functions \u2014 it does NOT program keys or do EWS immobilizer adaptation. For key add / EWS matching use Abrites (the BMW-bikes BN017 EWS/HUF/X_SLZ key & immo package), Autel IM508/IM608 with the moto/bike license, or OBDSTAR; true AKL usually means reading the EWS/immo or cluster EEPROM on the bench and writing a virgin transponder, or the dealer via ISTA. Keyless Ride AKL is generally a dealer/fob-sync job.\n\nDUCATI. Magneti Marelli immobilizer, and the whole system revolves around the RED master key, the black working keys, and the paper CODE CARD. The immo secret lives in the dashboard (instrument cluster) and is mirrored to the ECU; the red key + code card is what authorizes programming new black keys. The code card actually carries two numbers \u2014 an electronic immo code and a mechanical (blade) code, the mechanical one also stamped on a dog-tag. CHIP CORRECTION: don't quote one universal chip. Ducati spans platforms/years and the transponder family varies \u2014 many black keys are fixed-code and CLONE cleanly onto a T5/TPX (that's why \"clone the working key\" works), while other years use a crypto Megamos (ID48) type that will NOT simply clone. Verify the exact chip per model-year on a transponder lookup before you promise a clone. Blade is a simple double-sided edge-cut motorcycle blank (refs vary by year \u2014 verify). From-scratch / AKL is the classic Ducati trap: WITH the red key AND code card, a dealer or SOS Diagnostics programs new blacks in minutes. WITHOUT the red key or card, Ducati has nothing on file by VIN \u2014 the \"official\" path is replace dash + ECU + lockset as a matched set. The locksmith path: read the dashboard EEPROM on the bench to recover the immo code \u2014 on the 848/1098/1198 I2K-type dashes that's a 24Cxx serial EEPROM; other clusters use 93Cxx \u2014 then reissue keys and the code card. SOS Diagnostics, TMPro2 and Zed-Full handle Ducati, and Aprilia/Moto Guzzi share the Marelli platform. Bottom line: never let a Ducati owner leave without knowing where their red key and code card are.\n\nTRIUMPH (Hinckley). Pre-2003ish carb/early Triumphs = mechanical, no chip, simple edge-cut \u2014 duplicate or decode the lock. Immobilizer arrives roughly 2003-2004 and becomes standard across Sprint ST, Tiger, Speed Triple, Daytona, Rocket III, Trophy, Scrambler, Bonneville EFI, etc. Transponder is typically ID48 (Megamos Crypto) on the ignition key; some years use Texas-based chips \u2014 verify. Self-programming: on many Triumphs, with TWO working keys you can add a new key by the ignition-cycle procedure; with only ONE key you generally need a tool. QUICK REF: ~pre-'03 no chip; ~'03+ ID48 immo, ignition-key transponder, edge-cut blade. From-scratch / AKL: OBDSTAR iScan Triumph is the go-to dedicated tool (covers most models into the 2020s) and can program keys / read immo; alternatives are pulling the immobilizer or ECU and reading the EEPROM on the bench, or a dealer visit. Triumph, like the Italians, does not hand out key data by VIN \u2014 plan on the bike, not the parts counter.\n\nKTM. Most small/older KTMs have NO immobilizer \u2014 mechanical key, done. Immobilizer/transponder shows up on the bigger street and adventure bikes (RC8/RC8R, 690, 990/1190/1290 Adventure and Super Duke, SMT, later Duke street bikes) from around 2008 onward, and KTM Keyless Ride (proximity) on the 1290 Super Adventure and similar flagships. KTM uses an ORANGE master key to program BLACK slave keys. Chip families vary \u2014 Hitag2/ID46 and Megamos both appear depending on model/year \u2014 VERIFY on a lookup, KTM is inconsistent. QUICK REF: small/dirt = usually no chip; big street/ADV ~'08+ = transponder immo, orange master; 1290 flagships = Keyless Ride fob. From-scratch / AKL: SOS Diagnostics covers KTM; OBDSTAR iScan has KTM coverage; TMPro2 does KTM EEPROM; otherwise read the ECU/immo EEPROM on the bench and write a virgin chip, or dealer via KTM diagnostics. Blade is a standard edge-cut motorcycle blank (verify ref). Because so many KTMs skip the immo entirely, always confirm the specific bike actually HAS one before you quote an immo job.\n\nINDIAN / POLARIS (also Victory, Slingshot). Modern Polaris-built Indian (2014+, Thunder Stroke / Scout / Chief / FTR). Two ignition worlds: Scout and base Chief trims use a conventional MECHANICAL keyed ignition (cut a blade \u2014 Indian blank #5258212 is the common OEM reference; duplicate from an existing key or decode the lock). Roadmaster, Challenger, Pursuit and other touring trims use Indian's KEYLESS fob (passive start), with a PIN backdoor: if the fob's dead or gone, enter the 4-digit PIN via the turn-signal switches or the RIDE COMMAND display to unlock the security system and start. QUICK REF: Scout/base Chief = mechanical key blade; touring trims = keyless fob + PIN. From-scratch / AKL: this is a DEALER-LOCKED ecosystem \u2014 only an authorized dealer can program/sync a fob or read the master PIN, done through Polaris Digital Wrench with the VIN (the master PIN lives on the Digital Wrench Key Fob Maintenance screen). There is no aftermarket bench path today the way there is on Ducati/Triumph, so for a lost-all-fobs touring bike your honest answer is dealer + VIN + proof of ownership. For the mechanical Scout/Chief, standard locksmith work applies: cut by duplication, or pull and decode the ignition/fork lock.\n\nTOOLS TO CARRY for this group: GS-911 (BMW DIAGNOSTICS only \u2014 not key programming); Abrites BN017 for BMW-bike EWS/immo key work; OBDSTAR iScan (Triumph/KTM/Ducati and broad moto); Autel IM508/IM608 with the moto/bike license and adapters; Zed-Full and TMPro2 for chip/EEPROM work; a soldering setup and EEPROM programmer (XProg/Orange5/CH341) for 24Cxx/93Cxx dashboard reads on Ducati and immo/ECU reads elsewhere; plus a transponder cloner and virgin ID48/ID46/PCF7935 and T5/TPX cloneable chips. Cutting is mundane \u2014 a standard duplicator or code machine handles these edge-cut and two-track blades; the money is in the electronics and the EEPROM.\n\nALWAYS verify the exact blank and chip for the specific VIN on a transponder lookup \u2014 Transpondery, UHS Hardware, or Ilco \u2014 because moto chip families and blades shift by model-year and market and none of these makers reliably cut keys from records by VIN.",sources:["https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2000-2009-bmw-2-track-hu92-transponder-key-pcf7935-chip-k-hu92","https://www.hexgs911.com/tech-info/ews/","https://abrites.com/products/bn017","https://motorcycleinfo.co.uk/bmw-ews-electronic-immobilizer-on-can-bus-bmw-motorcycles-index0d47/","https://forums.bmwmoa.org/threads/keyless-ride-new-fob-initial-sync-and-low-fob-battery-engine-start-procedures.98131/","https://www.ducati.ms/threads/guide-how-to-retrieve-immo-code-code-card-code.734349/","https://www.ducati.community/threads/recovering-bypass-key-codes-848-1098-1198-dash.32327/","Ducati DUC-101 key blank listing (ducatipartsunlimited.com) \u2014 original link retired.","https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2020/MC-10173096-0001.pdf","http://sosdiagnostics.com/ducati.html","http://sosdiagnostics.com/triumph.html","http://sosdiagnostics.com/ktm.html","https://www.triumphrat.net/threads/programming-keys-with-immobilizer.972604/","https://www.obdstarstore.com/wholesale/obdstar-iscan-motorcycle-diagnostic-scanner.html","https://bikedecoders.com/ktm-motorcycle-key-programming/","https://www.indianmotorcycle.com/en-us/self-help/article/KA-01094/","https://www.indianmotorcycle.com/en-us/self-help/article/KA-02962/","https://www.amazon.com/Indian-Motorcycle-Blank-Key-5258212/dp/B01FV0PT22","http://tmpro2.com/software-modules-description-and-prices/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination"],tags:["Fob","Transponder","Immobilizer","Duplicator","Code machine","Plug","Keyway"],aliases:["keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","chip key","immo chip","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"vehicle-key-index",title:"Vehicle key index: find your make",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"This is the directory for making keys \u2014 pick the manufacturer family and its guide walks the key eras by year, the blank and chip/fob for each, the alternatives for getting in and getting the cuts, and the all-keys-lost procedure.\n\n[[diagram:vehicle-key-triage|The four questions that price any vehicle key job before you turn a wheel]]\n\nHow to use it: know the make and the year, open that family's guide, find the matching era in the quick-reference block, and confirm the exact blank + transponder on a lookup (Transpondery, UHS, or the Ilco/JMA app) for the specific VIN \u2014 trim splits happen. Start with the \"Reading & decoding auto locks\" guide for HOW to read cuts, then come here for WHAT the car takes.\n\nCARS are grouped by corporate family because they share key systems: GM covers Chevy / GMC / Buick / Cadillac / Pontiac / Saturn; Ford covers Lincoln / Mercury; Chrysler covers Dodge / Jeep / Ram / Fiat; Toyota covers Lexus / Scion; Honda covers Acura; Nissan covers Infiniti; Hyundai covers Kia / Genesis; VW covers Audi. Pick the parent make and the rest of the badge-family is in there.\n\nMOTORCYCLES are grouped by region \u2014 Harley-Davidson / Buell, the Japanese four (Honda / Yamaha / Suzuki / Kawasaki), and the European / other bikes (BMW / Ducati / Triumph / KTM / Indian).",relatedGuides:["autokeys-gm","autokeys-ford","autokeys-chrysler","autokeys-toyota","autokeys-honda","autokeys-nissan","autokeys-hyundai","autokeys-mazda","autokeys-subaru","autokeys-mitsubishi","autokeys-vag","autokeys-bmw","autokeys-mercedes","autokeys-volvo","autokeys-jlr","autokeys-harley","autokeys-japanmoto","autokeys-eurmoto","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination","auto-transponders-immo","autokeys-fleet","autokeys-powersports","autokeys-ev-digital","auto-universal-remotes"],sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/"],tags:["Fob","Transponder"],aliases:["key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start"]},{id:"autokeys-fleet",title:"Fleet & commercial vehicle keys: semis, box trucks, RVs & buses",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"Fleet and commercial work is where the locksmith trade goes backward in time. Half of what rolls into the yard has less anti-theft tech than a 1990 Camry, and the other half \u2014 the newer vans \u2014 is harder than any passenger car you'll touch. Know which is which before you quote, because the money and the tooling are on opposite ends.\n\n[[diagram:fleet-two-extremes|The same yard holds the easiest and the hardest jobs you will take all week]]\n\nCLASS-8 SEMIS (Freightliner, Peterbilt, Kenworth, International, Volvo, Mack)\n\nStart with the good news: most Class-8 tractors have no immobilizer at all. The ignition is a plain wafer-tumbler lock. There is no chip, no transponder, no programming. You cut a metal blank to code or by impression and it starts. Fleets order this way on purpose \u2014 a truck that needs a laptop to start is a truck stranded at 2am in Nebraska.\n\nBlanks by make. Freightliner and International share the Ilco 1629-P (cross-refs JMA FRE-1P, Strattec 690356, OEM 2506694C1) \u2014 one blank covers a huge swath of both fleets, non-transponder. Peterbilt and Kenworth use a double-sided key. The old universal was the Kenworth/Ilco K1994, and for years it opened an embarrassing number of trucks. Newer trucks moved to coded, higher-security locks: Peterbilt runs stamped code ranges you'll see as C250\u2013C483, G101\u2013G598, and P0001\u2013P1500. Here's the trap \u2014 the K1994 is a six-wafer blank, and the newer \"P-code\" style locks are seven-wafer, groove-86 keyway. A K1994 will not cut a seven-wafer lock, so don't grab it off the board and assume it fits; read the lock and match the wafer count. Volvo and Mack run their own blanks (Volvo 20719248 non-laser and similar) \u2014 still overwhelmingly wafer, no chip on the tractor ignition.\n\nFleet keying is the whole game here. When a carrier orders 25-plus trucks, they're commonly keyed alike, or the builder splits them across a handful of patterns so the shop only stocks a few keys. A classic trick: two keys per year/make, one cutting the even-VIN trucks and one the odd, so a driver's key won't wander into the wrong cab but the shop only carries two. That means your fastest path is often not the VIN \u2014 it's asking the fleet manager for the key code they already own. With the code, you cut on a code machine and you're done. No truck required.\n\nAKL / lost-key path: door and ignition are usually the same low-security wafer lock. Pick or impression the ignition, read the wafers, cut to code, done \u2014 no module, no security dance. Locksmith Ledger publishes heavy-truck depth/space and code series for exactly this. Where a code series exists (the Peterbilt/Kenworth ranges), progression cutting off the series is faster than impressioning.\n\nBOX TRUCKS & CUTAWAYS (Ford E/F, GM, Isuzu NPR, Hino)\n\nHere it splits. The cutaway chassis under a box body carries the same security as its pickup/van cousin.\n\nFord E-Series and F-Series cutaways run PATS. Blank is the \"blue oval\" H92 (supersedes the older H84/H85), Texas Instruments 4D63 chip. The 40-bit older keys and the 80-bit later keys share the H92 blank \u2014 it's backward compatible, so you stock H92 and stop worrying about H84. Two working keys = onboard programming in the dash. One key or AKL = you're on a tool (Autel/Smart Pro/Ford IDS) reading via OBD, and later 80-bit trucks may need a security-access wait timer.\n\nIsuzu NPR / N-Series: the mechanical blade is the X275/B113 (cross B1113), fitting 2008-up N and F trucks, cut in the 80,000\u201380,999 code series. Older N-Series are straight non-transponder \u2014 cut to code and go. But heads up: many 2008-plus N-Series carry a transponder/immobilizer, and that box wants a 4-digit PIN no scan tool pulls, forcing EEPROM/bench work on the immo unit. Second caution that bites people: N/F-Series run a 24-volt system \u2014 never hang a 12V programmer on a 24V port, use a converter or go to the bench. Hino uses the HN3/HIN-1 blank, similar wafer-and-code approach.\n\nGM cutaways (Express/Savana chassis) follow GM security below.\n\nCOMMERCIAL VANS (Transit, Sprinter, ProMaster, Express/Savana)\n\nThis is the hard end. Ford Transit is PATS like the E-Series it replaced \u2014 H-series/side-cut keys and fobs depending on year, OBD programming, later trucks with security-access delay.\n\nMercedes/Freightliner Sprinter is the wall. FBS3 immobilizer on older, FBS4 (roughly 2015-up) on newer \u2014 Mercedes' encrypted, server-side system. FBS3 AKL a good locksmith can do on-site: pull the EIS, generate a key file, cut to VIN, 30\u201360 minutes, sometimes fighting the ESL (steering lock). FBS4 AKL is currently dealer-only \u2014 Mercedes hasn't released aftermarket immobilizer access \u2014 so many of us sublet or refer it. Quote it accordingly; don't promise a Sprinter AKL you can't finish.\n\nRam ProMaster is a Fiat Ducato underneath \u2014 SIP22 blade, ID46/Hitag2 immobilizer. From 2014 up, Stellantis went to a secured immo needing a rolling PIN pulled through NASTF, plus the right tooling (Autel/Abrites/wiTECH). Chevy Express / GMC Savana: older are Passlock/PK3 or plain; newer transponder \u2014 standard GM AKL via security access. GM commercial is the friendliest of the van group.\n\nRVs & MOTORHOMES \u2014 two locks, two worlds\n\nEvery motorhome is really two vehicles. The chassis key runs the drivetrain and matches the chassis maker: Ford (E/F PATS as above), Freightliner/Spartan/Workhorse for diesel pushers, GM for smaller Class C. Treat the chassis key exactly like that maker's van.\n\nThe coach is where the CH751 lives \u2014 and it's the single most-quoted fact in RV security. The large majority of RVs use the CH751 on the exterior baggage/bay compartments. One key, stamped \"CH751\" right on the bow, opens the storage bays on most brands. It's a convenience code, not security. The entry door itself is usually a Bargman or Global/RV Designer lock on a small set of common codes. Owners who care get the bays and door re-keyed keyed-alike to a real code \u2014 that's a solid upsell. Bay locks are cam-lock cores you swap in minutes.\n\nBUSES\n\nSchool and shuttle buses are back to easy. Bodies from Blue Bird, Thomas, IC, Collins, Girardin, Midbus largely share a small set of standardized ignition keys \u2014 the Bluebird D601 and D701 being the famous ones (part refs 83451-601 and 17A12683/83451-701), and these cross the major body brands. The generic \"601\" key is so common it also fits mowers, cranes, snowmobiles and mini-excavators. Most are non-transponder switches you buy as a keyed unit or cut to the stamped code. The chassis underneath (often International/IC or Freightliner) may or may not add its own security, but the driver-facing ignition is typically the simple bus key.\n\nTHE ONE RULE\n\nBefore you tool up, ask the immobilizer question. Class-8 tractors, most medium-duty cabovers, and buses: no immo, cut to code, biggest job is finding the code \u2014 and the fleet usually already has it. Newer vans, Ford/GM cutaway chassis, and transponder-equipped Isuzu N-Series: full programming/AKL work. Sprinter FBS4 and secured Stellantis: know your limits. Match the tool to the truck and half these \"impossible\" fleet calls are ten-minute cuts.",sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/codes-key/article/21083480/heavy-truck-codes","https://vehiclesecurityinnovators.com/product/kenworth-peterbilt-ignition-lock-set-p-key-code/","https://www.raneystruckparts.com/peterbilt-replacement-truck-key-c250-c483-g101-g598-p0001-p1500/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/k1994-new-2013-kenworth-and-peterbilt-key","https://keymateinc.com/shop-online/key-blanks/automotive-keys/metal-head/ilco-k1994-key-blank-kenworth-peterbilt-detail.html","https://lockpicks.com/international-690356-non-transponder-key-by-strattec.html","https://ekeyblanks.com/Freightliner-1629-P-Key-Blanks/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/uncut-plastic-head-key-blank-freightliner-1629-p","https://class8truckparts.com/volvo-truck-20719248-key-blank-non-laser-cut.html","https://www.lockmasters.com/isuzu-npr-box-truck-x275-key-blank-x275","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/10229465/new-automotive-key-blanks-bring-versatility","https://www.saxajdmparts.com/store/p1156/Isuzu_Truck_N_Series_Genuine_Replacement_Immobilizer_Key.html","https://www.locksmithkeyless.com/products/hino-truck-key-blank-hin-1-hn3","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/2000-2017-ford-h92-h84-transponder-key-4d63-80-bit-chip-k-h92","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/locksmithing-101-everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-h92-transponder-key","https://www.montyslocksmith.ca/blog/fbs4-mercedes-key-programming-what-you-need-to-know-and-what-we-can-do/","https://blog.obdii365.com/2026/03/03/identify-benz-fbs3-vs-fbs4-with-autel/","https://knowledgebase.aetools.us/stellantis-vehicles-requiring-rolling-pin-codes-for-immobilizer-access","https://yourcarkeyguys.com/products/transponder-key-sip22-for-ram-promaster-fiat-id46-chip","https://ch751.com/","https://www.rvwithtito.com/articles/rv-security-ch751-lock-replacement/","https://midwestbusparts.com/product/d701-keys-17a12683/","https://midwestbusparts.com/product/d601-keys-576-83451-601/","https://www.amazon.com/Ignition-Excavator-Broderson-Bluebird-Snowmobile/dp/B0CCKCGHFJ"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["PATS","Immobilizer","Transponder","Onboard programming","OBD programming","Progression","Code machine","Impressioning"],aliases:["Ford PATS","Ford immobilizer","theft light","Ford key programming","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","OBP","no tool needed","key dance","onboard procedure","OBD2","onboard diagnostics","plug in the tool","Xtool","SmartPro","MVP Pro","master key system","MK system","grand master","pin chart","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","key machine","cutter","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{body:"Powersports is the easy money in this trade, but only if you know which machines are dumb wafer locks and which ones hide a real immobilizer. The rule of thumb: land vehicles (ATV/UTV) and golf carts are mostly cut-by-code wafer or even keyed-alike; water and newer high-end UTVs are where digital security lives. Sort the job by that line first.\n\n[[diagram:powersports-water-line|One question sorts the call: a metal blade, or a fob and a lanyard?]]\n\nATV & UTV \u2014 wafer ignitions, cut by code\n\nAlmost every ATV and older side-by-side runs a simple 4-to-7 wafer ignition switch with no electronics. There's no chip, no immobilizer, no programming \u2014 a correctly cut blank turns the switch and the machine starts. If you have a code or an existing key you can originate without programming, and both-keys-lost is a lock-read/impression or switch-swap job, not a module reflash.\n\nPolaris (Sportsman, RANGER, RZR, ACE, General): the newer machines use 4000-series blanks \u2014 blank 4080125 (cover 5458344) is the common one \u2014 and you identify the series by the 4 digits stamped on the blade. Reality check on AKL, though: Polaris does not keep a retrievable bitting database for the 4000-series keys the way a car maker does, so with both keys gone their own instruction is to replace the ignition switch (it ships with new matched keys \u2014 a cheap part). Cut-to-code/duplicate is your play when the customer still has one key or a documented code; true all-keys-lost is a switch swap or a lock read. Older Polaris also used a 4-digit Key Code series and youth/older units run 0453143 (right-hand groove) or 0453372 (left-hand groove); Ilco X120/YH46 crosses many of these.\n\nCan-Am/BRP off-road: two worlds. Older Outlander/Renegade and base Defender/Commander use plain wafer keys (Ilco X120/YH46 crosses). But the Maverick X3 and higher-trim Defender/Commander use BRP's D.E.S.S. \u2014 the same digital security family as their skis (below). These use the green RFID \"ball\" key (P/N 710005232, fits 2017+ X3/Outlander/Renegade) that a dealer VIN-programs with BUDS/BUDS2 (Maptuner and aftermarket DESS services also exist). D.E.S.S. also gates power via \"learning keys\": the key is assigned a mode in BUDS \u2014 Normal, Performance, or Work (speed/torque limited) \u2014 it's a software setting, not a preset in the key. One catch to know cold: Work mode is NOT available on the Maverick X3 \u2014 X3 keys are Normal or Performance only. The Work-mode limiter lives on machines like the Defender, where it caps top speed and torque (figures vary by model/year). Bottom line: if it's a D.E.S.S. machine, it's a dealer/tool program job, not a cut job.\n\nHonda, Yamaha, Kawasaki, Suzuki, Arctic Cat ATVs: all plain wafer. Honda TRX/Foreman/Rancher/Rubicon \u2014 cut to code, blades often keyed to the fuel cap and seat. Yamaha (Grizzly, Kodiak, Raptor, Wolverine) \u2014 Ilco X69/YH37 and X248/KW14R blanks; ignition, tank, and seat frequently share one cut. Kawasaki (Brute Force, Prairie, Bayou, Mule) \u2014 Ilco X255 (176K-250K code series), X254/KA20, X257/KA23 blanks. Arctic Cat \u2014 number code on the key; the aftermarket blank is Ilco X72/YH36, which is actually a Yamaha blank (beats dealer price). Yamaha's X120/YH46 is the true crossover blank, spanning Kawasaki, Polaris, Arctic Cat, Bombardier/Can-Am, Suzuki, and Yamaha for many years \u2014 one blank stocks a lot of shelf.\n\nFrom-scratch/AKL on any of these: read the wafer lock with a pick/tension or impression, or pull the switch and decode. No electronics means once you have the bitting, you're done \u2014 no relearn.\n\nSnowmobiles\n\nSame split as UTVs. Polaris, Arctic Cat, and Yamaha sleds use \"regular\" mechanical keys. Polaris uses either a 4-digit Key Code or a \"Dot Code\" (square key identified by the number of dots). Arctic Cat uses a numeric code, blank Ilco X72/YH36 (Yamaha blank). Yamaha sleds cut to code on Yamaha blanks. All cut-by-code, no chip.\n\nSki-Doo (BRP) is the exception: it carries the D.E.S.S. tether key \u2014 the same digital tether the skis use. The tether holds a fixed code; the dealer programs the sled's MPEM to accept it (a sled can learn up to ~9 keys, and one key can be learned into many sleds). Traditionally you brought the whole sled in so it could be run to program, but for 1997-and-up sleds third-party services can program off the MPEM alone \u2014 you don't always need the whole machine. Both lost = program a new tether to the MPEM. Treat any BRP D.E.S.S. sled like a PWC job.\n\nBoats & PWC\n\nConventional boats (outboard/sterndrive \u2014 Mercury, Johnson/Evinrude/OMC, Yamaha outboard) use a keyed 3-position (Off-Ign-Start) or 4-position ignition switch from Cole Hersee, BEP, Sierra, or SeaChoice. These are famously keyed-alike \u2014 a huge number of switches ship on the same small set of key codes, so one common blank often starts thousands of boats. Replacement is a bolt-in switch with two keys; no security beyond the mechanical wafer. Great for \"lost all keys\" \u2014 fit a new keyed-alike switch or cut the common key.\n\nPWC is where it gets digital. Sea-Doo D.E.S.S. (Digitally Encoded Security System) is not a metal key \u2014 it's a tether lanyard carrying a unique code, seated on the DESS post. Two eras: the older style (mid-90s through ~2015) reads the code through metal contacts on a magnetic post/reed switch \u2014 corrosion causes intermittent no-starts and false codes, so clean the contacts first on any complaint. 2015/2016-up models use RF D.E.S.S. \u2014 a 13.56 MHz RFID key with no contacts to corrode (it still carries a magnet for the reed-switch kill function). Note the base Sea-Doo Spark ships with a NON-DESS key: magnet-only, activates the kill switch but has no RFID chip and no security. DESS is an option/upgrade on the Spark (different harness + keys). All DESS keys must be programmed to that hull's MPEM/ECM; only the matched code unlocks and starts. Sea-Doo also sells Normal vs Learning/Rental keys that cap RPM (older throttle-cable skis) or GPS speed (newer iTC skis). Program with BUDS/BUDS2 or third-party (Maptuner, aftermarket DESS services). Yamaha WaveRunner four-strokes with the Security/Learning system use a programmable key paired to the craft; older two-stroke WaveRunners are plain mechanical keys. Kawasaki Jet Ski and older skis: mechanical key plus the tether kill-switch clip.\n\nGolf carts \u2014 the near-universal keys\n\nThis is the keyed-alike king. Within a brand, one fleet key runs the whole yard:\n\nClub Car: a single common key fits essentially every Club Car (DS and Precedent) built 1982 and up.\n\nE-Z-GO: fleet key 17063G1 covers most gas and electric from 1976 to present \u2014 the big exception is the RXV (and some newer connected/LSV models), which use a different switch/key assembly.\n\nYamaha: J17-82511 runs G1/G2/G8/G9/G11 (roughly 1985-1995; last two digits are the key's year of manufacture); G14/G16/G19/G22/G29 use a different cut (JU-/1012505 family).\n\nA Club Car key won't start an E-Z-GO or Yamaha and vice-versa, but within each brand it's one key for the fleet. Aftermarket combo blanks (10L0L and similar) and dealer 4-key master sets carry all three cuts on a ring \u2014 that's your golf-cart service kit. Lost all keys on a cart is trivial: cut or buy the brand's common key, no code needed. Newer lithium/connected models (some E-Z-GO ELiTE, Club Car with a dash controller, FleetQi-style keypad systems) may add a PIN pad or Bluetooth authorization, but the mechanical key itself stays universal.\n\nQuick job-sort at the machine\n\nMetal key, turns a switch, no dash \"insert key\" prompt \u2192 wafer; cut by code, duplicate, or read the lock. Tether lanyard on a post (PWC, Ski-Doo, Can-Am X3/high Defender) \u2192 D.E.S.S., needs BUDS/Maptuner programming to the VIN/hull/MPEM. Golf cart \u2192 brand fleet key, keyed-alike. When in doubt, check the blade for a 4-digit code (Polaris/Arctic Cat/Kawasaki) versus a chip-in-lanyard (BRP digital). That one look tells you cut versus program, and that's the whole quote.",id:"autokeys-powersports",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",title:"Powersports keys: ATV, UTV/side-by-side, PWC, snowmobile & golf cart",sources:["https://www.polaris.com/en-us/off-road/owner-resources/help-center/article/KA-01001/","https://www.polaris.com/en-us/off-road/owner-resources/help-center/article/KA-02918/","https://apps.polarisportal.com/files/PGA/English/ReplacementKeyInfo.pdf","https://ekeyblanks.com/key-blanks/motorcycle-atv-and-moped-keys/polaris-key-blanks/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/yh46-x120-yamaha-motorcycle-atv-key-blank-ilco","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/x255-kawasaki-atv-key-blank-ilco","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/products/x69-yh37-yamaha-motorcycle-atv-key-blank-ilco","https://can-am.brp.com/off-road/us/en/owner-zone/getting-started/vehicle-information/dess-learning-key.html","https://can-am-shop.brp.com/off-road/us/en/710005232-rfid-key-green-dess.html","https://canampartsguy.com/product/can-am-maverick-x3-outlander-renegade-green-rfid-ball-ignition-key-710005232/","Seadoo dess keys saftey security jetski (projetski.com.au) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://rivaracing.com/sea-doo-rf-dess-key-lanyard-all-2015-models-green-278003093.html","https://marinediagnostictools.com/blogs/news/programming-a-sea-doo-dess-key","https://www.jetdrift.com/lost-sea-doo-key-replacement/","https://seadoopartsdepot.com/ski-doo-snowmobile-key-program/","https://www.dootalk.com/forums/topic/530391-can-dealer-program-any-skidoo-dess-key/","https://powersportsguide.com/lost-snowmobile-key/","https://golfcartsearch.com/blog/are-golf-cart-keys-universal-brand-guide-2026","https://jsgolfcarts.com/blog/golf-cart-keys-universal/","https://heavyequipmentkeys.com/e-z-go-golf-cart-ignition-key-17063g1/","https://10l0l.com/products/2pcs-golf-cart-replacement-key-for-yamaha-g14-g16-g19-g22-g29-ju2-h2511-00-p4309373","https://www.westmarine.com/ignition-switches/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Key code","Immobilizer","Bitting","Controller","Fob"],aliases:["blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","panel","access panel","head end","ACU","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge"]},{id:"autokeys-ev-digital",title:"EV & digital keys: Tesla, Rivian, phone-as-key & NFC cards",module:"Vehicle Keys \u2014 by Make",body:"There is no cylinder, no wafer stack, no cut to read, and no code database to pull. On an EV like this, the \"lock\" is a radio and a secure element (SE) inside the body computer. A credential is a cryptographic key pair \u2014 the car holds a public key and trusts anything that can prove it owns the matching private key. \"Cutting a key\" becomes \"enrolling a credential,\" and enrollment is gated by proof-of-possession: you must already be inside/authenticated to authorize a new one. That single fact rewrites the whole job. VIN-to-code is dead here \u2014 a VIN maps to bitting or a PIN-code on a mechanical or transponder car, but there is no bitting and no algorithm that derives the car's private trust from the VIN. Nobody, including the manufacturer's back office, can hand you a \"key code\" that opens the SE. AKL (all-keys-lost) is not a bench operation; it is an account-recovery and, often, a module-provisioning operation done through the maker's service tooling.\n\n[[diagram:ev-credential-trust|Nothing copyable crosses the air... which is why this is an account problem, not a key problem]]\n\nTESLA. Three credential types, one enrollment path. (1) Key CARD \u2014 a passive NFC card (13.56 MHz, ISO 14443/DESFire-style SE), the primary \"always works\" credential. (2) PHONE KEY \u2014 the Tesla app over Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE); once the phone authenticates to the car it works offline, no cell signal needed. (3) Optional FOB \u2014 BLE/passive-entry, sold separately, not included on 3/Y by default. Enrollment for all of them is owner-present at the car: Controls > Locks > Keys, touch + to add, then tap the NEW card on the key reader, then authenticate by tapping an EXISTING already-paired card. The reader on current Model 3/Y is on the center console behind the cupholders (on some earlier builds the card antenna sits below the driver's side of the center-console display \u2014 placing a card on the wireless phone pad is NOT the enrollment reader). Chime = enrolled. Phone key is added inside the Tesla app while physically at the car, confirmed on the touchscreen. There is NO locksmith code, no OBD programming for a normal add. The car stores up to 19 keys total, of which up to three can be connected phone/fob/watch keys at once (add a fourth phone and you must move an existing paired phone out of range or kill its Bluetooth to authenticate). As a locksmith you CAN pair a customer's spare card or new fob in under a minute IF the owner has a working key to authorize it \u2014 that is the entire legitimate service. What you CANNOT do: enroll a credential with no existing key present. Tesla AKL (\"Replacement Keys \u2014 Program, No Keys Present\") is a service-tool procedure tied to the Tesla account and, on many builds, requires body-controller re-provisioning through Tesla's Toolbox \u2014 not something on a standard locksmith's programmer. So the honest answer to a no-keys Tesla call is: verify ownership, then it routes through Tesla service, not VIN-to-code.\n\nRIVIAN / LUCID. Same architecture, tighter gating. RIVIAN R1T/R1S ships a KeyCard (NFC) plus the Rivian app; a physical fob is a separate accessory. Phone-as-key rides BLE/UWB and now, on MY2025+ with software 2025.34+ and app 3.5.0+, a CCC digital key you provision into your wallet from inside the vehicle; the owner can share access with up to seven other drivers through the wallet. Phone keys the owner adds themselves; new KEYCARDS historically get paired by a Rivian service tech (owners report an ~$80 labor charge), so card enrollment is not reliably an owner-DIY step the way Tesla's is. LUCID AIR gives fob (default, pre-paired), NFC key card (reader just above the camera by the driver's door), and Mobile Key over BLE. Lucid's twist: when you access with the card \u2014 or with a dead-battery fob held to the driver B-pillar below the camera \u2014 the car demands a PIN before it will drive, a knowledge factor on top of the token, so a found/cloned card alone is not enough. Across all three, adding a credential is owner-present + existing-authority; losing all credentials is an account/service event.\n\nTHE BROADER DIGITAL KEY (CCC). The standard is the Car Connectivity Consortium (CCC) Digital Key, and it is what Apple Wallet and Google/Samsung Wallet implement. Three radios, three jobs: NFC (13.56 MHz) for tap-to-enter and the guaranteed low-power fallback; BLE for approach detection and remote lock/unlock/climate/trunk/horn; UWB (ultra-wideband) for secure ranging \u2014 precise, relay-attack-resistant hands-free entry that BLE alone can be spoofed on. Release history for exams: DK 2.0 (2020) standardized NFC; DK 3.0 (2021) added BLE+UWB hands-free; DK 4.0 (2025) pushed cross-device interoperability and sharing, still built on NFC/BLE/UWB (a device must support at least one). The private key lives in the phone's SE/secure enclave, not in an app, so it survives app deletion and works when the phone battery is low (\"power reserve\" NFC). Owner-to-friend SHARING is done in-wallet over the maker's cloud (e.g., iMessage/account invite) with optional restricted profiles \u2014 the owner does this, not a locksmith. UWB is still a small share of new cars (low single-digit percent in 2024, projected to grow toward roughly 40% by 2030 per industry forecasts \u2014 treat as an estimate that varies by source), so for years yet NFC+BLE is the working combination you will actually touch.\n\nWHAT THE LOCKSMITH CAN AND CANNOT DO. CAN: with the owner and a working credential present, enroll a spare NFC card or an accessory fob through the car's own menu (Tesla especially); coach an owner through provisioning a phone/wallet key; diagnose and fix the real reasons a \"no key\" call comes in \u2014 a dead 12V. CANNOT: derive access from a VIN; read or clone the car's private trust; add any credential when zero valid keys exist; bypass an account or a wallet's SE. Everything past \"owner-present pairing\" is account/app-gated and routes to the maker's service channel. Set that expectation on the phone before you roll.\n\nEMERGENCY MECHANICAL ACCESS & THE 12V-DEAD LOCKOUT. This is the call that actually pays. These cars have electronic door releases; a flat 12V (low-voltage) battery kills the handles and radios, and the classic symptom is \"my key/phone won't open it and nothing lights up.\" There is no key cylinder to pick. TESLA 3/Y: no internal 12V access \u2014 you wake the car from OUTSIDE. Pop the tow-eye cover on the front bumper (press the top corner to release it), pull the two leads out, and apply external 12V \u2014 red (+) to the red positive terminal, black (\u2013) to the black negative. This ONLY releases the hood/frunk latches \u2014 it does not charge the battery \u2014 and Tesla says do not leave it connected more than ~30 seconds; disconnect black first. Open the frunk, service the 12V, then the doors work. Front doors from inside use the manual release lever ahead of the window switches; rear doors have hidden cable releases (Model S: fold the carpet edge under the rear seat; Model X falcon-wing: pop the speaker grille and pull the cable). Later Model S/X front doors use the same forward-of-switches manual release; there is no owner-facing external mechanical lock. Lucid/Rivian follow the same logic: interior manual releases plus an external 12V wake point per their manuals; Lucid still wants the PIN once powered. The takeaway for every one of these: the emergency path is power + a mechanical release lever, never a cut key \u2014 because there is no cylinder, and the VIN was never going to give you one.",sources:["https://www.tesla.com/support/tesla-vehicle-keys","https://service.tesla.com/docs/Public/diy/model3/en_us/GUID-67E192C9-4308-44B7-899F-65344F487F1B.html","https://service.tesla.com/docs/ModelY/ServiceManual/en-us/GUID-EF8DFFF9-1CC6-454A-8CA1-4C2D9DB0E6E6.html","https://service.tesla.com/docs/ModelY/ServiceManual/en-us/GUID-55917424-BE25-40DF-98B2-B3BBF1ED9646.html","https://www.tesla.com/ownersmanual/model3/en_us/GUID-E004FAB7-1C71-448F-9492-CACF301304D2.html","https://www.tesla.com/ownersmanual/model3/en_us/GUID-34181E3A-B4A7-4658-906A-38C6647B5664.html","https://www.tesla.com/ownersmanual/modelx/en_us/GUID-AAD769C7-88A3-4695-987E-0E00025F64E0.html","https://soyacincau.com/2024/06/25/guide-unlock-tesla-model-3-y-lv-battery-dies/","https://rivian.com/support/article/what-is-rivian-digital-key","https://rivian.com/support/article/rivian-digital-key-tutorials","https://gearshop.rivian.com/products/key-fob","https://www.edmunds.com/car-news/new-2025-rivian-r1s-r1t-wont-have-physical-key-fob-for-now.html","https://lucidmotors.com/knowledge/vehicles/air/lucid-air-in-depth/access-and-keys","https://publications.infotainment.prod.atieva.com/d4e90c3b-0f98-4f8e-b027-64c5c6c8a47c/content_en-US/GUID-7D9E7DAD-E492-48C3-A87D-3D71AFF777A9.html","https://carconnectivity.org/car-connectivity-consortium-delivers-digital-key-release-3-0-specification-businesswire/","https://carconnectivity.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/CCC_Digital_Key_Whitepaper_Approved.pdf","https://vicone.com/blog/from-fob-to-phone-how-ccc-digital-key-40-shapes-automotive-cybersecurity/","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_digital_keys_in_mobile_wallets","https://www.kbb.com/car-advice/vehicles-using-digital-keys/"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Enrollment","Credential","Fob","DESFire","OBD programming","Reader","Bitting","Key code"],aliases:["badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","OBD2","onboard diagnostics","plug in the tool","Autel","Xtool","SmartPro","MVP Pro","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","code series","blind code","direct code","key number","code stamped on lock","panel","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start"]},{id:"auto-universal-remotes",title:"Aftermarket & universal remotes: generate, clone & flip",module:"Automotive",body:"Aftermarket remotes are how you make money on volume. OEM keys are expensive and slow to source; a drawer of universal blanks lets you build the right remote for almost any car on the van, on the spot. The two ecosystems you'll live in are Xhorse (VVDI) and Keydiy (KD). Learn one deeply, keep a few of the other for the cars it covers better.\n\n[[diagram:remote-clone-vs-generate|Clone, generate or flip, decided by whether a remote is missing or just broken]]\n\nTHE TWO WORDS THAT MATTER: GENERATE vs CLONE\n\nThese get confused constantly on exams and on the phone. They are different jobs.\n\nGenerate = you take a blank universal remote and tell your tool to turn it INTO a specific car's remote. The tool writes that make/model's remote data and remote code onto the blank so it looks and acts like, say, a Toyota H-chip flip or a VW MQB blade key. A generated remote is a fresh, empty remote \u2014 it still has to be ONBOARDED/PROGRAMMED to the car afterward (OBD or the car's own procedure) so the immobilizer learns it. Generate makes the blank into the right kind of remote; it does not marry it to the car by itself.\n\nClone = you COPY an existing working key's transponder into a chip, so the car's immobilizer already recognizes it \u2014 no programming to the car needed. Clone is for the transponder (the immobilizer chip in the head), not the RF remote buttons. On a clone job the remote-lock/unlock side may still need its own pairing, but the engine-start chip is a straight copy.\n\nRule of thumb: All-Keys-Lost or adding a key from scratch = GENERATE and program to the car. Customer has one good key and wants a spare fast = CLONE the chip (and cut/pair the remote). Cloning shines when you can't get online with the car, or the car is precoded-only for programming but the chip type is clonable.\n\nXHORSE (VVDI) UNIVERSAL REMOTES\n\nXhorse color-codes the boxes, and knowing the colors is the whole game. The boxes also tell you the points each remote earns toward your VVDI account (points buy the online calculations below):\n\n- XK series (red box) \u2014 WIRED universal remotes, no chip inside, just a socket. You plug the PCB into the tool with the data cable to generate. Cheapest, reusable essentially unlimited times, and they earn ~25 points each. XKxx part numbers (e.g., XKXH01EN 4-button). Wired is a hair slower to generate but bulletproof and the value pick.\n- XN series (blue box) \u2014 WIRELESS universal remotes, generate over the air, NXP chip inside, ~40 points each. XZ series are special/wireless PCBs (Honda-type and similar) generated the same way. Faster to program in volume because there's no cable.\n- XE super remotes (purple box) \u2014 wireless universal remote with the Super Chip (XT27) already built in, ~40 points each. One remote SKU covers a huge spread of makes because the embedded chip is the multi-clone Super Chip.\n- XS smart remotes (orange box) \u2014 proximity/keyless-go universal smart keys for push-to-start cars, ~60 points each. XS supports 46/47/48/49/4A simulation and generates prox keys for a wide model list.\n\nAll of these generate on the VVDI family: VVDI Key Tool, VVDI Mini Key Tool, Key Tool Max / Max Pro, Key Tool Plus, VVDI2. A blank can be generated to 600+ remote types across Ford, Mazda, Hyundai/Kia, VW/Audi, Toyota/Lexus, Honda, and more. Wired XK remotes regenerate unlimited times \u2014 miscut or wrong model, just regenerate to something else. That reusability is the case for stocking wired.\n\nTHE SUPER CHIP (XT27) AND MULTI-CLONE CHIPS\n\nThe Xhorse Super Chip (XT27A01 / XT27A66) is the single most useful part in the drawer. One physical chip replaces a wall of transponder types: it clones/generates as ID46, 4D, 4C, 8A, 8C, 8E, 40/43, T3, 47, 49, 4E, 48 (Megamos) and more \u2014 2000+ car models. You stock ONE chip instead of a dozen glass transponders. It's rewritable and regenerable \u2014 a wrong write doesn't brick it, you just redo it. That's why it lives inside the XE super remotes too. Keydiy's equivalents are the KD multi-clone chips and the NB-Multi remote, which carries a built-in 46-type chip. Buy Super Chips in bundles (50/100/250/500) \u2014 they're a consumable and the per-unit price drops hard.\n\nNote the 48 caveat, because this is where people over-promise: the Super Chip can SIMULATE/generate ID48 (Megamos, a lot of VW/Audi), but COPYING a customer's existing ID48 key is not free and not instant. You have two routes. One, collect the immobilizer data through the car over OBD with a VVDI2 plus the 48 data collector adapter. Two, run the 96-bit online calculation from the original key \u2014 and that calc costs points/tokens (roughly 100 points or 1 token per shot; you earn points generating remotes, which is why the point values above matter). Two more heads-ups: the 96-bit-from-key route does NOT cover the MQB platform \u2014 newer VW/Audi MQB48 is a separate MQB tool/adapter job \u2014 and older 96-bit collection generally covers roughly 2015\u20132017-era Megamos. Know all that before you promise an Audi customer a same-day chip.\n\nKEYDIY (KD) UNIVERSAL REMOTES\n\nSame concept, different flavor of parts:\n- B series \u2014 universal flip/remote PCBs, all sharing the same function set; body styles differ. Only the B01 Luxury and B10 carry a crystal chip for certain special cars. Generated on KD-X2, KD Max, KD900/KD900+, Mini KD.\n- NB series \u2014 \"multi\" style, body-shaped to a make (NB12 Ford, NB15 VW MQB, NB29 BMW, NB11 PSA/Peugeot, NB22 GM, etc.). The NB-Multi supports all NB remotes and includes a built-in 46-type chip.\n\nMany locksmiths run both brands: Xhorse for the deep smart-key and Toyota/VW work, KD as a strong, cheaper remote generator with great app support. Pick by which one covers the car in front of you better.\n\nBLADE SWAP, FREQUENCY, AND PAIRING\n\nUniversal remotes ship as a HEAD/PCB with NO blade. You supply the blade:\n- Blade: get the right universal flip blade for the lock (e.g., HU101, HU100, TOY43, HU66, VA2). Cut it to the customer's code/decode, then seat it in the flip head with a ROLL PIN (sold separately \u2014 keep an assortment). Wrong blade profile or a loose roll pin is the #1 callback on flip jobs.\n- Frequency: the car cares about RF frequency \u2014 mostly 315 MHz (North America/Asia) or 433/434 MHz (Europe and many newer cars), with some at 434.42. When you generate to a specific make/model, the tool sets the correct frequency for you. Super/wireless remotes can carry it; always verify with the tool's frequency tester if a remote is dead \u2014 a wrong-frequency remote will program the chip fine but the buttons won't range. Test the original if you have it.\n- Pairing: after generating, the remote is blank to the car. Onboard it \u2014 OBD programming with your tool, or the vehicle's own key-learn dance where supported. The transponder gets learned to the immobilizer; the RF gets learned to the BCM/RF receiver. On a clone job the chip is already recognized, so you may only need to sync the remote buttons.\n\nWHEN AFTERMARKET SAVES MONEY vs WHEN ONLY OEM/PRECODED WORKS\n\nAftermarket wins on: standard transponder keys, most flip remotes, tons of pre-2016 push-to-start, and any clone job where the chip type is clonable. This is the bread and butter \u2014 cheap parts, reusable, fast.\n\nAftermarket loses / OEM or precoded required when:\n- Some smart keys are precoded-only. Certain systems (a lot of newer Toyota/Lexus smart, some Hyundai/Kia proximity, some Honda smart) won't accept a freely-generated universal smart key on an All-Keys-Lost \u2014 you need an OEM or a locksmith-precoded smart key matched to that vehicle before it'll program, and often a PIN/seed calc on top. Support keeps expanding (Xhorse XS smart keys now cover a lot of Toyota via Key Tool Plus), but the newest AKL cases still gate you. Generating a generic universal won't take.\n- SGW / Secure Gateway cars. All 2018+ FCA/Stellantis (Chrysler, Dodge, Jeep, RAM, Fiat, Alfa) have a Security Gateway Module that blocks the OBD2 port to anything but authorized/dealer tools. The aftermarket key itself may be fine \u2014 the problem is PROGRAMMING it. You get past it with a 12+8 SGW bypass cable (unplug the two connectors at the module, splice in the cable, then your tool talks CAN normally) or an SGW bypass block that replaces the module. No bypass = no aftermarket programming, and you're sending them to the dealer. Other makes are adding their own gateways (GM, some VAG), so this list grows.\n- Newest rolling-code smart systems where aftermarket support simply hasn't landed yet in the tool's software.\n\nPractical read for the exam and the van: reach for generate-and-program with a universal + Super Chip first (cheapest, reusable). Fall back to clone when you can't get online or the chip's clonable and the customer has a working key. Reach for OEM/precoded only when the system refuses aftermarket smart keys or when SGW/gateway blocks you and you can't bypass. Always confirm frequency and stock the roll pins and blades \u2014 the fancy tool is useless if the flip won't close.",sources:["https://www.keyfirst.eu/en/blog/xhorse-universal-car-remote-keys-differences-and-ranges-n15","http://blog.xhorse.co.uk/how-to-choose-xhorse-universal-remotes-with-different-colors/","http://blog.vvdishop.com/xhorse-wired-vs-wireless-vs-smart-vs-super-remote/","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/vvdi-remote/xk-wire-remote/","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/vvdi-remote/xe-super-remote/","https://www.vvdishop.com/service/xhorse-universal-remote-list.html","https://abkeys.com/products/xhorse-vvdi-super-chip-transponder-super-chip-xt27a01-xt27a66-4696","https://www.mk3.com/xhorse-super-transponder","https://www.amazon.com/Xhorse-Super-Transponder-XT27A01-XT27A66/dp/B0B41YKVFP","https://www.vvdishop.com/wholesale/vvdi2-copy-id48-transponder-authorization.html","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/vvdi2-copy-48-transponder-authorization.html","https://www.xhorsetool.com/wholesale/vvdi-key-tool-max.html","https://abkeys.com/products/keydiy-kd-universal-remote-key-3buttons-vw-mqb-type-nb15","https://abkeys.com/products/keydiy-kd-universal-remote-key-4buttons-ford-type-nb12-3-1-nb12-4","https://abkeys.com/collections/keydiy-remotes-kd-smart-keys","https://www.obdgenie.com/products/2018-fca-security-gateway-module-bypass-device","https://www.americankeysupply.com/pages/everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-chrysler-security-gateway","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/automotive-tools/article/21091606/everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-chrysler-security-gateway","https://www.zautomotive.com/products/z_sgw"],relatedGuides:["vehicle-key-index","auto-decode-read","auto-key-origination","automotive-locksmithing"],tags:["Immobilizer","Transponder","Push-to-start","Bypass","OBD programming","Cloning","Plug"],aliases:["immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","proximity key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","onboard diagnostics","plug in the tool","Autel","Xtool","SmartPro","MVP Pro","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"auto-key-blade-types",title:"Automotive key blade types: edge-cut, sidewinder, Tibbe, RHK, flip & smart",module:"Automotive",body:"Before you can cut, program, or even quote a car key, you have to name the blade in your hand. Get the type wrong and you grab the wrong blank, the wrong machine, and the wrong tool. Here is the whole family, how each one is cut, and how to spot it on sight.\n\nEDGE-CUT (the traditional car key). Cuts on the top edge (or both edges on double-sided), made on a standard duplicator or code machine. Shallow, stepped teeth. This is most domestic and Asian stuff into the 2000s \u2014 GM B111, Ford H-series, the bread and butter. If you can see the cuts along the edge and a normal key machine will trace it, it is edge-cut.\n\n[[img:photo-transponder-chip|The transponder chip lives in the head of most modern keys \u2014 the blade gets you in and turns the ignition; the chip is what lets the car start.]]\n\nSIDEWINDER / LASER / INTERNAL-CUT (high security). The cut is a milled TRACK down the center of the blade, not teeth on the edge. It needs a laser/dimple machine (Xhorse Condor, Triton, Miracle) to cut and a keyway-specific Lishi to decode. Thicker blade, a wavy channel you can feel with a fingernail. Toyota TOY48, GM HU100, VW HU66, Mercedes HU64 \u2014 2010-ish and up on a lot of makes. Do not try to trace one on an edge-cut duplicator; it can't reach the track.\n\n[[img:photo-sidewinder-laser-key|Sidewinder / laser key: the cut is a milled central track, not edge teeth \u2014 cut on a laser machine, decoded with a keyway-specific Lishi.]]\n\nTIBBE (rotary disc). The oddball. A round-ish blade with flats milled at four levels around it; it turns a 6-disc rotary lock instead of lifting wafers. Older Ford (Mondeo, Transit, Ka) and Jaguar, FO21 keyway. You decode and cut it with a dedicated Tibbe tool, not a normal machine. If it looks like a little barrel with steps, it's Tibbe.\n\n[[img:photo-tibbe-key-lock|Tibbe (rotary) key: a barrel blade milled at four depths around six positions \u2014 Ford/Jaguar FO21, cut with a dedicated Tibbe tool.]]\n\nREMOTE HEAD KEY (RHK). One piece: mechanical blade (edge or laser) plus the transponder plus the RKE remote buttons molded into the head. Common on 2000s\u20132010s domestics. If the whole thing breaks you replace the unit and re-cut the blade; the remote and chip program to the car.\n\n[[img:photo-remote-head-key|Remote head key (RHK): blade, transponder, and remote buttons in one molded head.]]\n\nFLIP / SWITCHBLADE. The blade folds into the fob body and springs out with a button. Remote + transponder in the fob, a replaceable blade. Factory on a lot of VW/GM, and the whole aftermarket universal-remote world (Xhorse, Keydiy) is built on flip housings you generate to the car and cut a blade for.\n\n[[img:photo-flip-key|Flip / switchblade key: blade folds into the fob; the remote and chip pair to the car, the blade cuts to the lock.]]\n\nSMART / PROXIMITY FOB (push-to-start). No blade in daily use \u2014 the car detects the fob in the cabin and you start with a button. There's an emergency mechanical blade hidden in the fob for a dead battery, and it's usually laser/sidewinder. Programming is by OBD or, on the newest cars, online. This is where \"cutting a key\" becomes \"programming a fob.\"\n\n[[img:photo-smart-key-fob|Smart / proximity fob: push-to-start, detected in-cabin; the hidden emergency blade (usually laser) is your dead-battery backup.]]\n\nREAD IT IN FIVE SECONDS: edge teeth \u2192 edge-cut, trace it. Milled center channel \u2192 sidewinder, laser machine + Lishi. Barrel with steps \u2192 Tibbe, special tool. Buttons in the head with a fixed blade \u2192 remote head key. Blade folds out \u2192 flip. No blade, push-button car \u2192 smart fob, program don't cut. Name it first; everything else follows.",relatedGuides:["auto-key-cutting","auto-decode-read","vehicle-key-index"],sources:["https://www.transpondery.com/","https://www.uhs-hardware.com/"],relatedModels:["sidewinder-laser-key","tibbe-key-lock","remote-head-key","flip-key","smart-key-fob"],tags:["Fob","Duplicator","Transponder","Push-to-start","Lishi","Code machine","Keyway"],aliases:["key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","Ilco","HPC","Silca","key copier","key cutting machine","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","2-in-1","decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","Framon","HPC 1200","Blitz","Ilco Ultracode","cutter","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction"]},{id:"smartkey-rekey-steps",title:"Rekey a Kwikset SmartKey \u2014 the no-disassembly tool method",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Kwikset SmartKey rekeys in under a minute with no disassembly \u2014 no pinning kit, no follower, no plug out. It's not a pin-tumbler at all; it's a sidebar-and-slider system that mechanically re-learns the current key's cuts when you run the reset sequence with the SmartKey Learn Tool (SKT). You need the CURRENT working key, the NEW key already cut, and the little SmartKey tool (the tiny flat rod \u2014 a bent paperclip works in a pinch). Follow the sequence exactly; the order is the whole trick.\n\n[[img:diagram-smartkey-rekey|Kwikset's official SmartKey re-key sequence: turn the working key a quarter turn (A), insert and remove the Learn Tool (B), swap in the new key and turn it 90\xb0 (C), then OK.]]\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Confirm it's a SmartKey and gather your three things. A SmartKey cylinder has a small round TOOL HOLE just beside the keyway \u2014 that hole is the tell. Have the current working key, the freshly-cut new key, and the SmartKey Learn Tool in hand before you start.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Insert the working key and turn it 90\xb0 clockwise. Put the key that operates the lock NOW into the plug and rotate it a quarter turn, to horizontal. Leave it there.\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Push the Learn Tool into the hole until it clicks. With the key still at 90\xb0, push the SmartKey tool firmly and fully into the round hole next to the keyway until you feel it seat/click. That arms the reset.\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Pull the Learn Tool back out. Remove the tool straight out. The lock is now in learn mode, waiting for a new key.\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Remove the current (old) key. Keeping the plug at 90\xb0, withdraw the old working key. Do not turn the plug back yet.\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Insert the new key all the way. Push the new, correctly-cut key fully into the plug until it seats.\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Turn the new key 90\xb0 back to vertical. Rotate the new key the quarter turn back to the upright position. That motion is what teaches the lock the new key's cuts \u2014 the sidebar re-registers to the new bitting.\n\nStep 8 \u2014 Remove the new key. Pull it out. The lock is rekeyed; the old key is dead.\n\nStep 9 \u2014 Test both keys. New key operates the lock smoothly; old key no longer enters/turns. Cycle it a few times to confirm it caught.\n\nWhat trips people up: if the new key won't turn or the reset won't take, the usual causes are a key cut out of spec, forcing the plug instead of letting it turn freely, or a SmartKey cylinder that's been over-cycled and worn (they get notchy after many rekeys). Don't force it \u2014 re-cut a clean new key and run the sequence again. And know the security note so you can advise the customer: older SmartKey (Gen 1/2) had documented bypass and bump/force vulnerabilities; the current SmartKey Security generation hardened them, but for a high-target door you still spec a Grade-1 deadbolt over any consumer SmartKey. The rekey convenience is real; it isn't a high-security lock.",relatedGuides:["rekey-pin-tumbler-steps","keyway-blank-id","padlock-shimming-bypass"],sources:["https://www.kwikset.com/smartkey/default","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/2612848/Kwikset-Smartkey.html","Diagram: Kwikset SmartKey Re-Key Technology instructions"],relatedModels:["kwikset-smartkey"],tags:["Pinning kit","Plug","Keyway","Bypass","Bitting","Cylinder"],aliases:["pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{module:"The Business of Locksmithing",id:"fire-door-inspection",title:"Fire-door inspection: the NFPA 80 walk & the recurring-revenue play",sources:["https://www.nfpa.org/news-blogs-and-articles/blogs/2025/04/11/fire-doors-faqs","https://steeldoor.org/fire-door-assembly-inspections-101/","https://www.firetestingsolutions.com/nfpa-80-fire-door-inspection-13-point-checklist","https://www.dhi.org/Education-Credentialing/Certified-Fire-Egress-Door-Assembly-Inspector-CFDAI","https://iqp.intertek.com/firedoor","https://www.intertek.com/iqp/fire-door-inspector/","https://www.intertek.com/product-certification-marks/wh/","https://www.safenetix.com/2020/11/02/nfpa-80-qualified-person-fire-door-inspections/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/door-hardware/article/53071072/become-a-fire-door-inspector","https://www.locksmithledger.com/door-hardware/fire-door-fixtures/article/21152007/how-to-capitalize-on-fire-door-inspections","https://www.hfmmagazine.com/articles/3577-understanding-recent-door-inspection-and-maintenance-codes","https://www.cdfdistributors.com/blog/post/nfpa-80-fire-door-requirements","https://www.cms.gov/medicare/health-safety-standards/conditions-coverage-participation/life-safety-code","https://usmadesupply.com/resources/building-codes-standards/emergency-life-safety/nfpa-80","https://idighardware.com/2021/04/fire-door-clearance-follow-up/","https://www.safenetix.com/2021/05/10/swinging-fire-door-inspections/","https://www.formsonfire.com/blog/fire-door-inspection-checklist-report"],body:"Here is a business most locksmiths walk past because it does not look like locksmithing: you are not picking anything, not cutting a key, not selling a lock. You are walking a building with a clipboard, a feeler gauge, and a credential, and you are getting paid to write down what is wrong. Then \u2014 and this is the whole trick \u2014 you come back with a truck and fix everything you just flagged. Same doors, same building, every single year, forever. That is the fire-door inspection play, and it is the closest thing to an annuity in this trade.\n\n[[diagram:fire-door-walk|What you check on every door, and why an annual legal requirement is a better business than it looks]]\n\n**Why the building has no choice.** NFPA 80, \"Standard for Fire Doors and Other Opening Protectives,\" requires that every fire door assembly be inspected and tested after installation, after any maintenance work, and then at a minimum annually after that. On its own NFPA 80 is just a standard. What gives it teeth is NFPA 101, the Life Safety Code, which cross-references NFPA 80 and pulls it into the code that actually governs occupied buildings. For a hospital the chain is real money and worth knowing precisely: CMS still enforces the 2012 edition of NFPA 101 on every Medicare/Medicaid-certified facility \u2014 as of 2026 it has not adopted the 2018, 2021, or 2024 editions \u2014 and the 2012 Life Safety Code in turn references the 2010 edition of NFPA 80, which is where the annual-inspection mandate lives. Joint Commission surveyors show up expecting that paperwork. Add nursing homes, schools, hotels, high-rise residential, assisted living \u2014 anywhere a lot of people sleep or can't self-evacuate \u2014 and you have a large pool of building owners legally required to buy this from someone every twelve months. If they can't produce a signed inspection, that is a survey deficiency, and a survey deficiency can threaten their funding. They are not price-shopping. They need it done and documented.\n\n**Who is allowed to do it.** NFPA 80 says the inspection must be performed by a \"qualified person,\" and it deliberately does not hand you a license definition \u2014 the AHJ decides. In practice one training path feeds the two credentials facility managers ask for by name, and it helps to understand they are layered, not either/or. You start by taking DHI's DAI600 course (Fire and Egress Door Assembly Inspection) and passing the exam; that earns you the CFDAI \u2014 Certified Fire and Egress Door Assembly Inspector \u2014 from the Door and Hardware Institute. Then, if you want the Intertek IQP (Intertek Qualified Personnel) fire-door designation, run in partnership with DHI under the Warnock Hersey mark, you register on top of that same DAI600 training. IQP adds third-party auditing of your inspection work by Intertek, requalification every three years, and the right to apply an IQP inspection label to the assembly. Either way, get the credential on the wall behind you and you are no longer \"a locksmith who looked at the door\" \u2014 you are the qualified person the standard names. That credential is the moat. It is why you can charge for the walk.\n\n**The walk itself \u2014 NFPA 80 5.2.4.2.** The standard lists the minimum items you must verify, inspecting from both sides of the opening. Vendors market it as an \"11-point\" or \"13-point\" checklist depending on how they split the line items; the substance is the same. On every swinging fire door assembly you confirm:\n\n1. **Labels present and legible.** The fire rating label is permanently affixed to both the door leaf and the frame, readable, not painted over or obscured. No label, no rating \u2014 automatic fail.\n2. **No open holes or breaks** in the surfaces of the door or the frame. That old cylinder prep someone left open when they swapped hardware? Fail.\n3. **Glazing, vision panels, and glazing beads** are intact and securely fastened.\n4. **Door, frame, hinges, hardware, and the noncombustible threshold** are secured, aligned, and in working order with no visible damage.\n5. **No parts missing or broken.**\n6. **Self-closing device operational** \u2014 the door closes completely and latches from the full-open position. You open it all the way, let go, and watch. If it stalls, drifts, or stops short of latch, it fails.\n7. **Coordinator sequences the pair.** On a pair, the inactive leaf must close before the active leaf so the astragal seats. Watch the sequence.\n8. **Latching hardware catches and secures** the door in the closed position. A latch that doesn't throw fully is a door that blows open in a fire.\n9. **No auxiliary hardware** installed that interferes with operation \u2014 no field-added kickstands, no wedges, no unlisted deadbolts.\n10. **No field modifications** that void the label \u2014 no oversized cut-outs, no welded-on brackets, nothing done in the field that the listing doesn't allow.\n11. **Gasketing and edge seals**, where required, present and intact.\n\nThen you pull the feeler gauge, because clearances are where most doors quietly fail. Per NFPA 80 4.8.4 and 6.3.1.7, and measured on the pull side: clearance at the door edge to the frame, top and sides, is nominally 1/8 in with a tolerance of plus or minus 1/16 in \u2014 so anything over about 3/16 in is a finding. At the bottom the maximum is 3/4 in above the finished floor. Watch the exceptions: if the door's own label specifies a tighter undercut (often 1/2 in) or carries a temperature-rise rating, the label wins and overrides the 3/4 in ceiling. Gauge every gap. A settled building throws these off constantly.\n\n**Document every door.** This is not optional and it is the product you are actually selling. Every assembly gets its own record \u2014 location, tag/number, rating, pass or fail, the specific deficiencies. The signed report is what the facility hands the surveyor. Barcode or number every door and you own that building's data set for next year.\n\n**Now the play \u2014 the real revenue math.** Third-party fire-door inspections run roughly $50 to $150 per door for smaller jobs; on large healthcare contracts the per-opening rate drops with volume, often landing around $35 to $75 a door. Call a 300-door hospital $50/door and that is about $15,000 for a couple of days of walking with a clipboard. Fine money. But the walk is not where the business lives.\n\nHere is the mechanism. You inspect 300 doors and, in a building that hasn't been maintained, 30 to 50 percent fail something \u2014 a dragging closer, a latch that won't throw, an over-wide bottom gap, a missing gasket, a cut-out that needs a listed filler plate. Say 120 doors flunk. You hand the facility manager a deficiency report that legally obligates them to correct those items. And you \u2014 the qualified person who already knows exactly what's wrong with each one \u2014 are standing right there to bid the repairs. Closer rebuild, hinge shim, latch adjustment, gasket kit, label-legal filler plate: call it $150 to $600 a door in parts and labor. 120 doors at $350 average is roughly $42,000 in repair work that the inspection created and that no one is better positioned to win than the person who wrote the report.\n\nThat is the flywheel: a five-figure walk generates a $40,000-plus repair backlog, and twelve months later NFPA 80 sends you back to do it again \u2014 re-inspect the ones you fixed, catch the new failures a year of use produced, write a new report, bid a new round. One credential, one building, one recurring six-figure relationship. Land three hospitals and a school district and you have a route.\n\nGet the CFDAI or layer up to the IQP. Walk the doors. Hand them the report. Then bid every door you flunked. Every year.",relatedGuides:["closing-fire-code","door-closers-adjustment","business-of-locksmithing","commercial-security-survey"],tags:["Life safety","NFPA 101","Credential","Astragal","AHJ","Opening","Cylinder"],aliases:["IBC","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{body:"Detention hardware is its own trade inside the trade. A commercial lock keeps honest people honest and slows a burglar down for a few minutes. A detention lock has to defeat a resident who lives against it 24 hours a day, has unlimited time, improvised tools, and one job: get through it. That single fact reorders every priority. Everything here lives under the C-28 (Lock & Security Equipment) contractor scope, and the manufacturers, the metallurgy, the keying, and the codes are all different from the commercial world you already know.\n\n[[diagram:detention-priorities|The same words mean different things once the adversary lives against the door]]\n\nTHE MANUFACTURERS. This is a small, closed field. The dominant name is Southern Folger, the result of the 2004 merger of the two historic giants: Southern Steel of San Antonio (founded 1897) and Folger Adam of Illinois (in the high-security business since around 1905; the line was later built in Lemont, then consolidated into San Antonio after the merger). Southern Folger is the reference-standard maker of certified security and detention locking systems. Folger Adam's mechanical line, the 10 Series (the \"10-lock\") mortise deadlock, is the archetype most people picture. Other real brands you will meet: Airteq (the Norment / Norment Security Group locking line), R.R. Brink (Illinois; strong in mechanical mortise locks), Willo, Adtec, Stewart-Decatur, DDS/Universal, and Roanoke. Sargent makes a detention line, and ASSA supplies the high-security mogul cylinders that go into most of these locks. Aftermarket parts (CPC / Corrections Products Co., Craftmaster) exist because facilities run 40- and 50-year-old hardware and OEMs discontinue lines. Know the brand before you touch the door: a Folger Adam key will not turn a Southern Steel lock even in the same corridor.\n\nTHE LOCKS THEMSELVES. Detention locks are heavy mortise cases, typically for 1-3/4\" hollow-metal swinging doors, with a deadbolt (not a spring latchbolt) and a deadlocking latch that resists shimming and loiding. The governing performance spec is ASTM F1577, Standard Test Methods for Detention Locks for Swinging Doors, which rates a lock against battering, prying, and fire; sliding devices are tested under ASTM F1643. Three actuation types coexist in one building: mechanical (key only), electromechanical (a solenoid or gear-motor throws the bolt on command from a control point, with mechanical key override), and pneumatic (a compressed-air cylinder throws the bolt: old but bulletproof, nothing to burn out, common in older max-security; Airteq pioneered pneumatic locking). Every powered lock carries lock-position and door-position switches feeding the control room, plus a deadlocking indicator. The other half of the world is sliding: cell and dayroom sliders driven by an electric motor through a rack-and-pinion, locked top and bottom by a concealed vertical lock bar, with a remote mechanical gang release so an officer can dump a whole tier at once.\n\nMOGUL CYLINDERS & PARACENTRIC KEYWAYS. This is the heart of detention keying and where commercial habits get you in trouble. A mogul cylinder is a physically oversized cylinder, roughly 2\" in diameter against a standard mortise cylinder's 1-5/32\", using thick, long pins and a massive key that runs about twice the size of a standard mortise key. That extra size is not only about pick resistance; it is brute-force and destruction resistance (you cannot drill or snap it the way you would a commercial cylinder), it gives more torque to throw a heavy bolt, and the larger keyway sheds the debris inmates jam into the slot. Paracentric keyways are the other signature: a keyway with wards that cross the vertical centerline, forcing a crooked, deeply convoluted key path. It exists to defeat picking and impressioning by a resident who has one bent piece of metal and years to practice. Keys are big brass paracentric blanks in dozens of keyways (Southern Folger \"A\" keyway blanks and 40-plus variants are stocked). Some systems use mogul and paracentric together. You will handle mogul and paracentric keys and blanks that look nothing like anything on your commercial key board.\n\nFRAMES, GLAZING, AND PASSES. The lock is only as good as what surrounds it. Detention doors and frames are security hollow metal: heavy-gauge (commonly 12- to 14-gauge faces, versus 16- to 18-gauge commercial), often internally reinforced or grouted, with continuous (piano/geared) hinges or heavy detention hinges so there is no removable pin and no pry gap. Vision panels use security glazing, not window glass: laminated glass-clad polycarbonate assemblies tested to ASTM F1233 (the security-glazing forced-entry standard, five classes running from Class 1's basic blunt-tool resistance up to Class 5's combined ballistic-plus-forced-entry) and often to UL 752 for ballistics in courthouses. F1233 rates by attack sequences of hammer and tool blows, not by a single clock, so a \"Class\" is a measured count of sledge, pipe, axe, thermal and chemical assaults survived, not a marketing claim. Doors carry engineered ports: the food pass (bean slot) and the cuff pass (hand/cuff port), each its own self-closing, lockable, ligature-conscious little door with its own hardware.\n\nBEHAVIORAL HEALTH & ANTI-LIGATURE. Behavioral-health and suicide-watch areas flip the threat model: the danger is self-harm, so the enemy is any ligature point. Hardware here is ligature-resistant/anti-ligature, sloped and closed, with no lever or knob a cord can be tied to. Sargent's 8200 Series with BHW behavioral-health trim is a good example: a sloped, tapered lever and escutcheon with no gaps to loop a cord, over a 3/4\" stainless deadlatch that still deadlocks against shimming, and the lockbody itself exceeds ANSI/BHMA A156.13 Grade 1. Vendors: Sargent, Schlage, Marks, TownSteel, plus ASSA ABLOY DSS behavioral lines. Everything (hinges, closers, pulls, even the food-pass) gets specified for zero ligature attachment while still meeting egress.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT DIFFERENT FROM COMMERCIAL. Three things. (1) Attack resistance: designed against unlimited-time forced entry from the secure side, not a five-minute burglary. (2) Life safety under lock: residents are locked in, so NFPA 101 Chapters 22 (new) and 23 (existing) govern egress by \"use condition,\" Condition I (free movement to the exterior) through Condition V (movement restricted, staff-controlled manual release at each door). Where remote release governs (Conditions III and IV), staff must be able to release the locks within 2 minutes with minimum available staff; a limited manual scheme can substitute where no more than 10 locks need unlocking, with no more than two separate keys, to move occupants to an area of refuge as promptly as remote release would. Every power-operated lock or slider needs a manual mechanical release and emergency power at the door. Get this wrong and you have a mass-casualty fire trap, not a paperwork violation. (3) Maintenance regime: these are inspected, tested, and logged like fire-life-safety systems (NFPA 80 governs the fire-rated door assemblies), on scheduled PM, with control-room switch verification every cycle.\n\nCONTROL SYSTEMS & KEY CONTROL. Behind the doors sits the electronics: control panels and PLC-driven logic run the sliders and electric locks, with graphic touchscreens at officer stations, interlock logic on sallyport vestibules (both doors can never be open at once), and hard-wired mechanical gang releases as the failsafe. You read lock labels, wiring diagrams, and ladder logic as fluently as you read a lock. Key control is at a different level entirely: paracentric mogul keys are serialized, blind-coded, never duplicated on-site, stored in audited high-security cabinets, and a single lost key can force a re-core of an entire wing.\n\nCAREER NOTE. The institutional/detention locksmith is a specialist and paid like one. State and county DOCs hire \"Locksmith I, Correctional Facility\" (California posts roughly $5,790 to $6,936/month; Illinois DOC runs about $5,893 to $8,368/month), typically requiring two years' locksmith experience plus corrections training, and the work is peace-officer-adjacent (you supervise inmates while you work). Certification exists: Maximum Detention's Certified Detention Locksmith, accredited by the American Correctional Association (and to Florida Jail Standards), covering Folger Adam, Southern Steel, Adtec, R.R. Brink and others; the Institutional Locksmiths' Association (ILA) is the trade body. It is steady, pensioned, technically deep, and there are never enough people who can do it. For a working locksmith, detention is one of the few remaining places the trade still commands real specialist wages.",id:"detention-hardware",title:"Detention & institutional locking hardware",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",sources:["https://www.southernfolger.com/about-us_our-history","https://correctionalnews.com/2005/12/09/southern-steel-acquires-folger-adam/","https://www.southernfolger.com/southern_steel","https://www.southernfolger.com/folger_adam","https://airteq.com/","http://www.correctionsproductsco.com/history.html","http://www.correctionsproductsco.com/prod_mech_10.html","http://correctionsproductsco.com/prod_keys.html","https://www.assalock.com/en/products/cylinders-keys-keying/assa-mogul","https://www.assalock.com/en/detention","https://static1.squarespace.com/static/58fe002c37c5811d5b89a0ce/t/590a0966579fb34e49c2b5fd/1493830002719/keycylndrs.pdf","https://www.craftmasterhardware.com/shop-by-category/detention/prison-keys/ssparablank-a-southern-folger-96960050-paracentric-key-blank-a-keyway","https://store.astm.org/f1577-05r19.html","https://www.tssbulletproof.com/astm-f1233","https://www.intertek.com/building/standards/astm-f1233/","https://www.tssbulletproof.com/product/custom/detention-center-glazing","https://idighardware.com/2012/02/prison-egress/","https://up.codes/viewer/florida/nfpa-101-2018/chapter/22/new-detention-and-correctional-occupancies","https://up.codes/viewer/florida/nfpa-101-2018/chapter/23/existing-detention-and-correctional-occupancies","https://www.sargentlock.com/en/products/healthcare-hardware/8200-series-mortise-lock-with-bhw-trim1/","https://www.doorwaysplus.com/shop/sargent-8237-bhw-ligature-resistant-classroom-mortise-lockset-31550","https://www.locksmithledger.com/door-hardware/article/11135514/the-where-why-and-how-of-ligature-resistant-locks","https://calcareers.ca.gov/JOBSGEN/7CEAB.PDF","https://www.maximumdetention.org/locksmith-course","https://ilanational.org/cer/"],relatedGuides:["door-hardening","commercial-security-survey","ca-scope-c28-c7","institutional-ila"],tags:["Fire-rated door","Life safety","Loiding","Mortise cylinder","Deadlatch","NFPA 101","Cylinder","Impressioning"],aliases:["IBC","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","credit card","shove knife","carding the latch","slip the latch","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","latch guard","anti-shim","plunger","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{body:"You install a Kwikset SmartKey or a builder's Schlage on a new-construction door and you've done nothing an experienced burglar respects \u2014 and here's the trap that fools most homeowners and half the salesmen: both of those locks can carry an ANSI Grade 1 label. The Kwikset 980 SmartKey deadbolt and the Schlage B60 are BOTH certified ANSI/BHMA Grade 1, the top residential durability rating. That number tells you the bolt is strong and the lock will cycle for decades; it tells you NOTHING about whether the cylinder resists a skilled attacker. And these cylinders don't. Both run open, unrestricted keyways \u2014 anyone can cut a copy at a hardware kiosk. SmartKey's wafer-and-sidebar plug, marketed as bump-proof \"BumpGuard,\" famously falls to a screwdriver-and-wire torque attack (the Tobias/Bluzmanis method, publicly demonstrated, 15\u201330 seconds), and cheap SmartKey decoder tools exist. The factory strike on either is held by 3/4-inch screws into pine trim. That's the lesson before we start: ANSI grade measures brawn, not attack resistance. Upgrading a home \"past builder grade\" means three separate moves that people wrongly collapse into one: a high-security CYLINDER (pick/bump/drill resistance plus key control), a Grade-1 DEADBOLT BODY (the mechanical strength around that cylinder), and a REINFORCED STRIKE (so the frame doesn't blow out first). Buy only the cylinder and you have a pickproof lock on a door a boot opens in one kick. Understand the three as a stack.\n\nWHAT THE RATINGS ACTUALLY MEAN. Two independent standards, and people confuse them constantly. UL 437 is Underwriters Laboratories' attack-resistance listing for key locks and cylinders. To earn it a cylinder must survive 10 minutes of expert picking and impressioning and 5 minutes of drilling, plus defined attacks by driving, pulling, prying, sawing, and forcing \u2014 and it must contain hardened-steel inserts (drill pins, ball bearings) placed to defeat a drill bit. That's the \"can't be defeated by manipulation or a drill\" credential. ANSI/BHMA is a completely different axis: durability and physical strength, graded 1/2/3. For deadbolts the governing standard is ANSI/BHMA A156.36 (auxiliary locks; A156.5 covers cylinders and input devices, A156.2 the bored locksets). Grade 1 deadbolt means 250,000 cycles, a full 1-inch bolt projection, survival of two impact blows at each of 150, 120, 90, and 60 foot-pounds, plus the bolt-strength (static-load) and torque tests on the projected bolt. So: UL 437 = resists the skilled attacker and the drill; ANSI Grade 1 = resists the brute and lasts decades. You want BOTH on the label. A lock can be Grade 1 and still have a $2 pickable cylinder \u2014 that's exactly what your builder's SmartKey and B60 are; a UL 437 cylinder can sit in a flimsy Grade 3 body. The real product (\"UL 437 Listed, BHMA A156.5/A156.36 Grade 1\") is what the Medeco Maxum and Mul-T-Lock Hercular and Abloy Protec2 deadbolts all carry.\n\nTHE REAL VALUE FOR A HOME IS KEY CONTROL. Homeowners fixate on picking, but statistically your break-in risk isn't a lockpicker \u2014 it's a kicked door or an unauthorized key. High-security lines are built on RESTRICTED, PATENTED keyways. The patent is the teeth: while a utility patent is live (typically ~20 years from filing), it is illegal for anyone to manufacture the key blank, so no hardware store, no kiosk, no locksmith outside the factory's authorized dealer network can cut you a copy. Duplicates require a signed authorization card on file and are cut only by the dealer or factory. THAT is what you're actually buying \u2014 a contractor, cleaner, ex-tenant, or nanny cannot walk out and quietly make three copies. Note the difference between \"restricted\" (dealer-network controlled distribution) and true patent protection: Medeco's original Biaxial patents expired (which is why M3 alone no longer carries live patent cover), so they roll versions \u2014 Medeco3/M3, now Medeco4/M4 \u2014 to keep a live patent and add a moving element in the key. M4's factory-installed stainless shuttle pin defeats casting and 3D-printed copies and is patent-pending. Mul-T-Lock's MT5+ and Abloy's Protec2 are current-patent systems. If key control is the goal, ask the dealer point-blank: \"Is this keyway under an active patent, and are you the geographically exclusive holder?\"\n\nTHE CYLINDER TECHNOLOGIES, PLAINLY. Medeco (M3/M4): standard-looking pin tumbler, but each bottom pin must be both LIFTED to height AND ROTATED to the correct angle by chisel-cut key bittings, and only then does a SIDEBAR drop into alignment. Rotating pins + sidebar + false slots + hardened inserts = UL 437. Familiar to service because it retrofits like a normal cylinder. Mul-T-Lock (MT5+): TELESCOPING pin-in-pin \u2014 a pin inside a pin, so the double-bitted dimple key reads two independent pin stacks per chamber, plus the MT5+ adds the \"Alpha\" interactive spring element and an internal sidebar: three active locking mechanisms. Very high theoretical pick difficulty, huge master-key capacity. Abloy Protec2: not pins at all \u2014 rotating DISC DETAINERS. The key rotates a stack of discs to align a sidebar channel; there are no springs to bind against, which is precisely why it is effectively UNPICKABLE by conventional means. The DBS disc-blocking system locks the discs as one package against manipulation. Abloy is the connoisseur's choice where picking is the threat model. ASSA (Twin/Twin Exclusive): dual-sidebar pin tumbler, geographically exclusive keyways \u2014 the commercial-grade European workhorse, ASSA ABLOY's own high-security line. BiLock: twin blades, two parallel rows of pins engaging a moving sidebar, no traditional keyway to rake. Bowley: a boutique lock designed to physically SHIELD the keyway from tools \u2014 the key is inserted deep before it engages pins, denying pick access geometrically. BiLock and Bowley are enthusiast/high-end niche; Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, and Abloy are what you'll actually stock and service.\n\nRETROFIT INTO A RESIDENTIAL DEADBOLT. Most of this is a cylinder swap, not a new door. Know your three cylinder formats: RIM (screws to the back of a rim/surface deadbolt via a tailpiece), MORTISE (threaded body, screws into a mortise lock case, uses a cam), and KIK \u2014 \"key-in-knob,\" the round plug that drops into most modern bored deadbolts and knobsets. For a homeowner already running a Schlage B60 or similar, in many cases you can pull the existing cylinder and drop a UL 437 replacement cylinder keyed to a restricted keyway, keeping the door hardware. Cleaner and what I recommend: buy the purpose-built Grade-1 high-security DEADBOLT complete (Medeco Maxum, Mul-T-Lock Hercular, Abloy Protec2 ME153T) so the bolt, hardened free-spinning collar, anti-drill plate, and cylinder are engineered together.\n\nTHE PRICE CONVERSATION. Be honest with the customer. Complete high-security deadbolts run roughly: Mul-T-Lock MT5+ Hercular ~$200\u2013260; Abloy Protec2 single-cylinder ~$250, ~$330 with lockable thumbturn; Medeco Maxum residential roughly $150\u2013250 depending on keyway/finish/single-vs-double. Then labor, plus every duplicate key costs $8\u201320 through the dealer (that's the key-control tax, and it's the point). Against a $35 builder deadbolt that feels steep \u2014 but you're buying a 250,000-cycle Grade-1 body, a drill-proof UL 437 cylinder, and a patent that stops silent copying. Add the reinforced strike regardless of lock: a box strike with 3-inch screws driven past the trim into the stud is a $10\u201315 part that does more against the most common attack (the kick) than any cylinder upgrade. Never let a customer buy the $250 cylinder and keep the 3/4-inch factory strike screws.\n\nWHEN A HOMEOWNER ACTUALLY NEEDS IT. Not everyone does. Push high security when: RENTALS/turnover \u2014 you rekey without recutting the world and no tenant retains a copyable key; HOME OFFICES / home-based businesses \u2014 insurance, client data, sometimes controlled inventory; HIGH-VALUE contents \u2014 collections, firearms, a home safe room; anyone who has handed keys to contractors, cleaners, exes, or nannies; and short-term-rental hosts. For a low-risk owner-occupied house, one Grade-1 Abloy or Medeco on the primary entry plus reinforced strikes on all exterior doors is the sane package \u2014 don't oversell six high-security cylinders where the back slider and the garage-to-house door are the real holes. Sell the stack, size it to the threat, and always finish with the strike and 3-inch screws.\n\nHOW THE KEYWAYS WORK\n\nMedeco and Mul-T-Lock earn their keep with the key itself, not just the keyway warding \u2014 two very different mechanisms.\n\n[[diagram:medeco-keyways|Medeco keyways & generations]]\n\nMedeco rotates its bottom pins: each cut is angled, so the right key both lifts the pin to height AND twists it so a gate lines up a sidebar at three o'clock. The line runs Original \u2192 Biaxial (a second, fore/aft axis) \u2192 M3 (a spring slider in the keyway for patent-protected key control). Blanks are factory-restricted, and Biaxial angled cuts need a special cutting machine.\n\n[[diagram:mul-t-lock-keyways|Mul-T-Lock keyways & platforms]]\n\nMul-T-Lock stacks a pin inside a pin \u2014 telescoping pin-in-pin \u2014 so every chamber has two shear points, cut on a dimple key. The platforms climb Interactive \u2192 MT5 (an Alpha Spring at the tip adds a rear shear line) \u2192 MT5+ (a milled locking bar makes a third, independent mechanism on a double-sided key). Also restricted, key-control blanks.\n\n[[diagram:abloy-keyways|Abloy disc-detainer generations]]\n\nAbloy throws out pins and springs entirely \u2014 a rotating disc detainer. Angled cuts on the key turn each disc to a set angle, and when every disc's gate lines up, a sidebar drops in and the plug turns. With no springs there is nothing to bump or impression. The line runs Classic \u2192 Protec (a locking bar and false-gate discs) \u2192 Protec2 (the current UL437 platform; Protec2 CLIQ layers on an electronic audit trail). Restricted, key-control keyways.\n\nFor the everyday Schlage and Kwikset keyway families, see the Keyways & key blanks guide.",id:"high-security-residential",title:"High-security residential: Medeco, Mul-T-Lock, Abloy & restricted keys",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",sources:["https://patcosta.com/ul-437-requirements/","https://www.lockwiki.com/index.php/UL_437","https://blog.ansi.org/ansi/ansi-grade-levels-bhma-locks-hardware-tests/","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A15636-2020-Auxiliary-Hardware","https://buildershardware.com/ANSI-BHMA-Standards/Hardware-Highlights/A1565-2020-Cylinders-and-Input-Devices-for-Locks","https://hardwarehut.com/products/schlage-b-series-maximum-security-single-cylinder-grade-1-deadbolt-b60n-single-cyl-deadbolt","https://www.amazon.com/Kwikset-Cylinder-Deadbolt-featuring-SmartKey/dp/B001009QP4","Kwikset smartkey alert (locknerds.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.medeco.com/en/key-systems-and-key-management/mechanical-solutions/product-details.aehpdp-medeco3-aeh_medeco_219721","https://www.fraserlock.com/post/medeco-4-m4-the-next-generation-in-high-security-mechanical-locks","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/cylinders-high-security/article/21247704/inside-the-medeco-4","https://www.medeco.com/en/products/deadbolts/product-details.aehpdp-medeco4-maxum-aeh_medeco_227524","https://zensupply.com/medeco-maxum-residential-double-cylinder-deadbolt-dl-keyway-captive-key/","https://www.abloy.com/au/en/products/keying-platforms/abloy-protec2","https://securitysnobs.com/Abloy-Protec2-Single-Cylinder-Deadbolt.html","Abloy protec2 me153t 26d high security single cylinder deadbolt (edlocks.com) \u2014 original link retired, page no longer published.","https://www.mul-t-lock.com/us/en/products/locksets/hercular-deadbolt","https://www.edlocks.com/mul-t-lock-mt5-hercular-double-cylinder-captive-key-deadbolt/","https://www.assalock.com/en/technology/product-details.aehpdp-assa-twin-maximum-aeh_assa_lock_482527","https://securitysnobs.com/BiLock/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/21288490/a-high-security-primer"],relatedGuides:["high-security-keyways","door-hardening","t-master-keying","t-keyways-schlage-kwikset"],relatedModels:["kwikset-smartkey"],tags:["Manipulation","Tailpiece","Rake","Strike","Cylinder","Key blank","Keyway","Mortise lock"],aliases:["dial manipulation","safe opening","no drill","contact points","stem","tail","spindle","deadbolt tail","raking","city rake","bogota","snake pick","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","SC4","Ilco","JMA","Silca","blank won't fit","KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"doorknob-replace-steps",title:"Replace a door knob or lever set, step by step",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",tags:["Knob","Lever set","Latch","Latch faceplate","Install","Handing"],body:"Swapping a knob or lever is the most common install a locksmith does, and it's a five-minute job once you've done a few. The whole thing is two long screws through the knob and two short screws in the latch faceplate \u2014 pull the old set, drop the new latch and knobs in, screw it down. Here it is, step by step.\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Parts: (in order from left to right). Short Screws. Latch mechanism with latch face. Long screws. Door knob with peg and screw holes (inside of the room). Door knob with screw hole\n\n[[img:dknob-step-1|Step 1]]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Face the inside of the knob, you should see two Phillips head screws on either side of the central knob.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-2|Step 2]]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Unscrew both long screws.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-3|Step 3]]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Remove the door knob on both sides of the door.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-4|Step 4]]\n\nStep 5 \u2014 You will see the latch face plate (the rectangular piece of metal) where the latch of the door knob is located.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-5|Step 5]]\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Unscrew the two short screws on the latch face plate.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-6|Step 6]]\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Remove the latch face plate and latch mechanism.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-7|Step 7]]\n\nStep 8 \u2014 Push the new latch mechanism with latch face plate into the door.. The slanted side of the latch mechanism will be facing the direction that the door closes.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-8|Step 8]]\n\nStep 9 \u2014 Screw in both short screws of the latch face plate.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-9|Step 9]]\n\nStep 10 \u2014 Insert the door knob that goes on the inside of the room, aligning the peg and screw holes with the threaded cylinders of the latch mechanism.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-10|Step 10]]\n\nStep 11 \u2014 Align the door knob that goes on the outside of the room, aligning the peg and screw holes with the threaded cylinders of the latch mechanism.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-11|Step 11]]\n\nStep 12 \u2014 Screw in both long screws on both sides of the door knob.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-12|Step 12]]\n\nStep 13 \u2014 Turn the door knob to test your work.\n\n[[img:dknob-step-13|Step 13]]\n\nPhotos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA).",relatedGuides:["sagging-doors","hinges","door-prep-jigs","rekey-pin-tumbler-steps"],sources:["iFixit repair guide #39987, source of the step photos used here (CC BY-NC-SA) \u2014 original link retired.","Step photos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA)"],aliases:["knobset","Grade 2 knob","round knob","lever handle","ADA lever","cylindrical lever","latch bolt","won't catch","door pops open","latch plate","latch trim","installation","hang the door","mount the hardware","prep the door","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing"]},{id:"mortise-install-steps",title:"Change a mortise lock & cylinder, step by step",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"A mortise lock lives in a pocket cut into the door edge \u2014 the commercial workhorse. Servicing one means pulling the trim, the spindle, and the cylinder, then sliding the lock body out of the mortise. This is the body/cylinder swap into an existing mortise pocket; cutting a fresh mortise is its own job. Step by step:\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Determine the position of the screws on both door handles and the front plate.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-1|Step 1]]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Use a flathead screwdriver to remove the eight 6 mm screws on both handles.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-2|Step 2]]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Pull out the door handles on both sides of the door.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-3|Step 3]]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Pull out the spindle (the rectangular piece of metal) from the door.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-4|Step 4]]\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Loosen the two 6 mm flathead screws at the top and bottom of the lock using a flathead screwdriver.. Remove the third 6 mm Phillips screw in the middle using a Phillips #1 screwdri\n\n[[img:mortise-step-5|Step 5]]\n\nStep 6 \u2014 Pull the lock cylinder out to either side of the door.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-6|Step 6]]\n\nStep 7 \u2014 Grasp the mortise lock on either side and pull it out from the edge of the door.\n\n[[img:mortise-step-7|Step 7]]\n\nPhotos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA).",relatedGuides:["sagging-doors","hinges","door-prep-jigs","rekey-pin-tumbler-steps"],sources:["iFixit repair guide #139990, source of the step photos used here (CC BY-NC-SA) \u2014 original link retired.","Step photos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA)"],tags:["Mortise lock","Cylinder"],aliases:["Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"hinge-realign-steps",title:"Realign a sagging door at the hinge, step by step",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",tags:["Hinge","Hinge pin","Sagging door","Door alignment","Knuckle","Won't latch"],body:"A door that drags or won't latch is usually sagging at the hinge, not a bad lock. The quick fix works the hinge pin to bring the leaf back into position. (For a badly racked or stripped-hinge door, see the sag and hinge guides.) The fast realign, step by step:\n\nStep 1 \u2014 Grab an unaligned door.. Either you or a person who is helping hold the door up to the desired position.\n\n[[img:hinge-step-1|Step 1]]\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Grab a hammer and a screwdriver.. Hold the screwdriver up to the pin.. Hold the hammer below the screwdriver.. Knock the pin out of the hinge by banging the screwdriver using the h\n\n[[img:hinge-step-2|Step 2]]\n\nStep 3 \u2014 Drop the pin back into the hinge using the hammer.. If the pin gets stuck this means that the knuckles need to be bent back into place.. Using pliers bend whichever knuckles need a\n\n[[img:hinge-step-3|Step 3]]\n\nStep 4 \u2014 The door should now be in your desired position.. To test if the door is back in place, just open and close the door to make sure that the door is in the position you want and func\n\n[[img:hinge-step-4|Step 4]]\n\nPhotos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA).",relatedGuides:["sagging-doors","hinges","door-prep-jigs","rekey-pin-tumbler-steps"],sources:["iFixit repair guide #167340, source of the step photos used here (CC BY-NC-SA) \u2014 original link retired.","Step photos: iFixit (CC BY-NC-SA)"],aliases:["butt hinge","continuous hinge","door sags","screws stripped","pin and barrel","NRP","non-removable pin","door drags","door rubs","hits the frame","won't shut","out of square","racked frame","hinge knuckle","barrel","hinge leaf","door won't close","door pops open","latch misses strike","strike alignment"]},{id:"t-drilling-metal",title:"Drilling metal: speeds, feeds, bits & lubrication",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Drilling metal well is one of those shop skills nobody teaches you and everybody judges you on. Get it right and a hole takes twenty seconds and the bit lasts a year. Get it wrong and you burn up a bit, work-harden the metal into glass, snap the tip off in the hole, or let the drill grab and spin the part out of your hand. The whole craft comes down to one idea, so start there.\n\n[[img:photo-tool-drill-bits|The bits that survive metal, and the ones that do not.]]\n\nTHE ONE PRINCIPLE: MAKE CHIPS, NOT DUST. A [[tool:drill-bits|drill bit]] is a cutting tool. It's supposed to peel a continuous curl of metal off the bottom of the hole \u2014 you should see little spirals or ribbons coming up the flutes. If you're making powder, smoke, or a screaming squeal, the bit is RUBBING, not cutting, and rubbing makes heat. Heat is the enemy of everything: it draws the temper out of the bit (once the tip turns blue it's dead), and on some metals it work-hardens the surface into a skin harder than the bit. So every decision below \u2014 speed, pressure, lube, bit choice \u2014 exists to keep the bit cutting a clean chip and to carry the heat away.\n\nSPEED: BIGGER AND HARDER = SLOWER. This is the rule people get backwards. The cutting edge at the rim of a big bit travels much farther per turn than a small one, so a big bit must spin SLOWER to keep that edge cutting at a sane speed. And harder metal must be drilled slower than soft. So: a 1/16\" bit in aluminum can spin flat-out; a 1/2\" bit in steel wants to crawl. If your drill has a clutch/speed setting or you're on a press, gear down for big holes and hard metal. On a cordless, that means easing the trigger, not burying it.\n\nPRESSURE (FEED): FIRM AND STEADY, ENOUGH TO KEEP IT CUTTING. Push hard enough that the bit is always biting into fresh metal and throwing a chip. Too little pressure is the classic rookie mistake \u2014 the bit spins on the surface, glazes it, heats it, and (on stainless especially) hardens it so now you CAN'T drill it. Too much pressure and you snap a small bit or the drill grabs. The feel you want: continuous chip, steady progress, bit staying cool enough to touch after. Let the tool do the work, but keep it working.\n\nNOW, METAL BY METAL:\n\nMILD (CARBON) STEEL. Moderate-to-slow speed, firm steady pressure, and a few drops of cutting oil or tapping fluid (WD-40 works in a pinch for light steel). You'll get nice blue-silver curls. If the chips come off blue and brittle, you're running too hot \u2014 slow down or add lube. Standard HSS bits are fine. Center-punch first so the bit doesn't walk.\n\nSTAINLESS STEEL and HARDENED STEEL. This is where technique matters most. Stainless WORK-HARDENS the instant a bit rubs instead of cuts \u2014 glaze it once and you've created a skin harder than your bit that laughs at you. So: go SLOW, use HEAVY steady pressure to keep it biting, never let it spin without cutting, and use real cutting fluid, not just WD-40. Reach for cobalt bits (M35/M42) \u2014 they hold an edge at heat far better than plain HSS. If you do glaze a spot, you have to get the bit's edge underneath the hardened layer (heavier feed, or spot it with a fresh sharp bit) or step up to carbide. Patience beats speed every time here.\n\nALUMINUM. Faster speed, LIGHT pressure \u2014 and don't skip lube, even though it's soft. Aluminum is gummy: the chips want to weld to the cutting edge (built-up edge) and pack the flutes, which then jams and grabs the bit. So use WD-40 or kerosene or cutting fluid, take a lighter feed, and clear the chips often \u2014 back the bit out to fling them off. Sharp bit, light touch, keep it clean and it drills like butter.\n\nBRASS and BRONZE (lock bodies, cylinders, padlock shells). Moderate speed, but here's the trap: brass GRABS. A normal sharp bit self-feeds \u2014 it screws itself into the brass, grabs, and either snaps or spins the part, and it's worst right at breakthrough on thin brass. The fix pros use is to DUB the bit: grind a tiny flat on the leading edge of each flute to kill the rake so the bit scrapes instead of digging. Even without dubbing, ease WAY off the pressure as you feel breakthrough coming. Light lube or dry. This is the bit you keep separate and blunted just for brass.\n\nCAST IRON. Run it DRY \u2014 no oil (oil turns the graphite dust into a grinding paste). Moderate speed, steady feed. It makes fine abrasive dust rather than chips, so wear a mask and expect a mess. Comes up on some old lock bodies, mortise boxes, and hardware.\n\nHARDENED PLATE / ANTI-DRILL (safe hardplate, drill-resistant cylinders). Different animal. This is carbide or diamond territory \u2014 plain HSS and even cobalt just polish it. Slow speed, heavy CONSTANT pressure (never let a carbide bit chatter or rub \u2014 carbide is hard but brittle and hates vibration), keep it cool, and be patient; it eats bits and time. Expect anti-drill defenses to fight back: a hard ball bearing set into the plate will spin in place instead of drilling, and a glass or spring relocker may fire if you punch through the wrong spot. Safe drilling is its own discipline \u2014 see the safe guides for drill points and what's behind them; this section is only about the metallurgy of the cut.\n\nBITS, QUICK VERSION. HSS for mild steel and aluminum. Cobalt (M35/M42) for stainless and hardened steel \u2014 worth owning. Carbide or carbide-tipped for hardplate and case-hardened surfaces. A masonry bit is NOT a metal bit (its carbide tip is ground to smash, not cut) \u2014 don't confuse them. And above all: a SHARP bit fixes ninety percent of drilling problems. A dull bit rubs, heats, hardens, wanders, and grabs. If it stopped cutting, it's usually dull, not stubborn \u2014 resharpen or replace.\n\nSETUP AND TECHNIQUE THAT SAVE BITS AND KNUCKLES.\n- Center-punch every hole. A punched dimple gives the bit tip somewhere to bite so it can't skate across the surface and score your work.\n- Pilot then step up for big holes. Drill a small pilot the width of the big bit's dead center (the chisel point), then open it up \u2014 the big bit then cuts cleanly instead of pushing.\n- Peck-drill deep or hard holes: drill a bit, back all the way out to clear the chips and re-lube, go again. Packed chips are what snaps bits and burns holes.\n- CLAMP THE WORK. Drilling grabs \u2014 it will snatch a loose lock body or bracket and spin it into a propeller. Vise it or clamp it. A spinning part off a grabbing bit is the most common way to get cut in the shop.\n- Ease off at breakthrough. The instant the tip breaks out the far side, the bit tries to grab and pull through \u2014 lighten up, especially in brass and thin sheet, or it'll catch, snap, or leave a torn burr.\n\nREADING WHAT THE METAL TELLS YOU. Long clean curls and a bit you can touch = you've got it right. Fine powder, a blued tip, or a rising squeal = too fast, dull, or dry: slow down, sharpen, add lube. The bit screwing itself in and grabbing = too much rake for the material (brass), ease off or dub the edge. It's a conversation \u2014 watch the chip and listen to the cut, and adjust before you cook a bit, not after.",relatedGuides:["t-safe-basics","safe-anchoring-opening","t-fasteners","door-prep-jigs","storefront-cutting","t-drill-bits-sharpening","t-drilling-out-cores"],sources:["http://www.norsemandrill.com/feeds-speeds-drill.php","https://www.imperialsupplies.com/pdf/3660-5_F_Drill_Bit_Sheet_Update.pdf","Manufacturer feed-and-speed references. Figures vary by bit grade (HSS vs cobalt vs carbide) \u2014 match the chart to the bit you are actually holding."],tags:["Hardplate","Rake","Relocker"],aliases:["drill resistant plate","carbide plate","hard plate","raking","city rake","bogota","snake pick","re-locker","relock trigger","glass plate","punch relocker"]},{id:"t-instacode",title:"InstaCode: the key-code database and how to use it right",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"There's a moment on every code job where you've got a number \u2014 stamped on a lock, pulled off a cylinder, written on a customer's paperwork \u2014 and you need it to become cut depths you can put on a machine. That translation, code to bitting, is what a code database does, and InstaCode is the one the trade runs on.\n\n[[diagram:code-lookup-workflow|What goes in, what comes out, and the part the subscription does not cover]]\n\nWHAT IT IS. InstaCode (delivered today as InstaCode Live, by WH Software) is a subscription key-code database and lookup tool. It's been built and updated for around 30 years and has grown into the biggest code reference in the business: over 8,500 key code series, more than 180 key-blank manufacturers, and \u2014 because a single series can hold thousands of codes \u2014 north of three billion individual key codes. Residential, commercial, automotive, safe, and padlock; direct codes and blind codes; the code books, the depth-and-space cards, and the cross-references all live in one place.\n\nWHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES FOR YOU. You hand it a manufacturer and a code, and it hands you the bitting \u2014 the depth numbers \u2014 plus the space-and-depth for that keyway, so you can cut to code without ever seeing the original key. That's the whole point of code cutting: a customer reads you the code off their lock, or you pull it from the cylinder, and you originate a key from the number instead of picking, impressioning, or tearing the lock down. InstaCode also drives many code machines directly \u2014 pick the code, send it to the machine, cut it \u2014 and carries automotive notes (chip type, tools, programming procedure) right alongside the mechanical cuts.\n\nBLIND CODES ARE THE REAL VALUE. Keep the two kinds of code straight. A direct code IS the bitting: 35426 means cut 3-5-4-2-6, no lookup needed. A blind code is a manufacturer's reference number that means nothing until you run it through that maker's secret series \u2014 Kwikset's, Schlage's, a carmaker's \u2014 to get the actual bitting. Those blind series are exactly what InstaCode licenses and keeps current, and they're what your subscription really buys. This is the tool the app's Key Code Calculator sends you to when it refuses a blind code: pull the bitting from InstaCode (or a comparable service), then the calculator turns it into depths, spacing, and a MACS check.\n\nIT'S GATED, ON PURPOSE. You can't buy this like a phone app. To hold an InstaCode Live subscription you have to be in the locksmithing and security industry with a verifiable business, because the data is \u2014 functionally \u2014 the ability to make a key to someone else's lock from a number. It's sold in tiers: Just General (residential, commercial, and master-key data), Just Auto (vehicle codes, programming guides, and tools), and The Works (all of it). And the gating runs deeper on the sensitive stuff: certain high-security and automotive series are further restricted, and for locked-down modern cars the secure VIN-to-code path still goes through NASTF's Secure Data Release Model or the dealer. InstaCode is where a code you're entitled to becomes cuts \u2014 it is NOT a back door around a manufacturer's secure data. Same line the trade draws everywhere: the database is legitimate, and access is earned.\n\nPLATFORM. InstaCode Live is web-based \u2014 it runs in a browser on Windows, Mac, Android, or iOS, so it's on your shop PC and on your phone at the truck \u2014 and it needs an internet connection because the database is live and constantly updated. (The older desktop InstaCode was a Windows install with periodic data updates; Live moved that to an always-current cloud model.)\n\nWHERE IT SITS IN YOUR WORKFLOW. Get the code legitimately \u2014 off the lock, from the customer's records, from the cylinder, or through NASTF for a car. Look it up in InstaCode to turn it into a bitting. Confirm the space-and-depth for the keyway (InstaCode gives it; the app's Key Code Calculator will validate your cuts and flag MACS). Cut to code, test, done. It doesn't replace knowing how to decode, impression, or read a lock \u2014 it replaces guessing, and on a code job it turns a fifteen-minute origination into a two-minute one. Competitors exist \u2014 Genericode (Framon), Blitzcode, HPC's CodeSource \u2014 but InstaCode is the one most shops standardize on.",relatedGuides:["cutting-by-code","auto-key-cutting","auto-nastf-vsp","auto-key-origination","t-master-keying"],sources:["https://www.instacodelive.com/","https://www.whsoftware.com/IC/","https://www.americankeysupply.com/category/instacode-1007"],tags:["MACS","Key code","Code cutting","Bitting","Impressioning","Cylinder","Keyway"],aliases:["maximum adjacent cut","cut-to-cut","key won't cut","steep cut","key number","code stamped on lock","cut by code","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"lishi-commercial",title:"Lishi for commercial locks: 6-pin, IC cores & what won't decode",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Residential Lishi work is forgiving \u2014 five pins, standard pinning, a KW1 or SC1 and you're reading a code in thirty seconds. Commercial is where the tool earns real money and also where it hits walls, and knowing the difference between the two is what separates a tech who quotes confidently from one who gets surprised on site. Commercial throws four things at you the house never did: a sixth pin, a lock full of security pins, interchangeable cores with a second shear line, and high-security keyways that a 2-in-1 simply cannot read.\n\n[[diagram:lishi-commercial-limits|What decodes, what decodes ambiguously, and what will not decode at all]]\n\nMATCH THE PIN COUNT \u2014 5 VS 6. The single most common commercial mistake is bringing a 5-chamber reader to a 6-pin lock. A standard SC1 Lishi reads five positions; a huge amount of commercial Schlage is 6-pin (SC4 keyway and friends), and Sargent, Corbin Russwin, Yale and the rest run 6-pin cylinders all day. If you pick and decode only five chambers on a six-pin lock, you cut a key that binds on the sixth wafer you never read. There are dedicated 6-pin tools for exactly this \u2014 use them, and count the chambers before you trust a number.\n\nSECURITY PINS ARE THE NORM HERE, NOT THE EXCEPTION. Where a house Kwikset might have none, a commercial cylinder is often loaded top to bottom with spools and serrated drivers. That matters for DECODING, not just picking, and this is the subtle part: a driver hung on a spool's false groove holds the plug at a false set, and if you read that chamber while it's sitting on the false shear you can read the wrong depth. The rule is absolute \u2014 every pin must be set to the TRUE shear line before the number on the scale means anything. Feel each false set, rock the pin through it, confirm the plug is genuinely at the operating shear, THEN decode. A commercial lock that reads clean on the first pass and then cuts a key that won't turn was almost always a spool you decoded at its false position.\n\nINTERCHANGEABLE CORES \u2014 THE SECOND SHEAR LINE. SFIC/Best-style cores are the commercial Lishi's signature job and its signature trap, because an IC core has TWO shear lines: the operating shear (where the change and master keys work) and the control shear (where the control key pulls the core). A 2-in-1 SFIC Lishi is built to pick and decode to the OPERATING shear \u2014 that gives you the change/master bitting, the key that runs the lock. It does NOT hand you the control key: the control bitting is a separate keying you generally cannot derive from the operating decode (that's exactly what the SFIC Master-Key Calculator in this app exists to work out). Two more IC specifics: Best A2 is a 7-pin system, so you want the 7-pin SFIC tool for it, and the tool's scale is calibrated to BEST'S depth system (.0125\" A2), not to Kwikset or Schlage numbers \u2014 read a \"3\" on an SFIC Lishi and it's a Best 3, not a Schlage 3. Get the wrong tool's scale in your head and every number is off.\n\nTHE MASTER-KEY AMBIGUITY (this is the one nobody warns apprentices about). When you decode a master-keyed cylinder, every master-pinned chamber has TWO valid positions \u2014 the change-key cut and the master cut \u2014 because the master pin created a second shear line there on purpose. As you pick, each of those chambers can set at EITHER position, so the combination your Lishi reads can be a mix that is neither the pure change key nor the pure master: it can be a GHOST key (see the ghost-keys section in the master-keying guide). It will often still turn the lock \u2014 that's what makes it dangerous \u2014 but it may not be the actual change key the customer's system chart says belongs to that door. On a master-keyed lock: decode, cut, and test, but understand that a working read is not proof you found the intended change key. When you specifically need the true change bitting (to add it to a system chart or master intelligently), the honest move is to pull the cylinder and read the pin stack directly rather than trust one ambiguous decode.\n\nWHAT THE LISHI COVERS ON THE COMMERCIAL SIDE. Standard pin-tumbler is its home turf: Schlage (SC1 5-pin, SC4/6-pin, and Everest \u2014 the Everest's undercut check pin means an Everest-specific tool), Sargent (LA/LB), Corbin Russwin, Yale (Y1/Y11), Weslock, and the small wafer/cam tools that read the common desk, cabinet, and cam locks. If it's pins riding straight up and down on a bitted key, a Lishi in the right keyway will read it.\n\nWHAT IT CAN'T DECODE \u2014 KNOW THE WALL BEFORE YOU QUOTE. High-security keyways defeat a 2-in-1 by design, and you need to recognize them on sight so you don't promise a curbside decode you can't deliver:\n- MEDECO \u2014 the key rotates each pin to line up an internal sidebar (elevation AND rotation). A standard Lishi reads elevation only; it cannot read the rotation, so it cannot non-destructively decode a true Medeco.\n- MUL-T-LOCK \u2014 telescoping pin-in-pin dimple system, cut on the flats of the blade, not the edge. That needs a DIMPLE Lishi (a different tool family) at best, and the interactive/alpha elements fight even that.\n- ABLOY and other DISC-DETAINER locks \u2014 no pins at all, just rotating discs. The Lishi concept doesn't apply; disc detainers need their own dedicated tools.\n- ASSA, high-security Schlage Primus (the finger-pin sidebar), and similar sidebar/telescoping systems \u2014 generally beyond a standard 2-in-1.\n\nSo the commercial judgment call is: standard pin-tumbler, 5- or 6-pin, even with security pins and even in an IC core \u2014 Lishi is your fast, non-destructive, code-in-hand first move. True high-security with a sidebar, dimple, or disc system \u2014 recognize it, and reach for the right specialty tool or plan a different (often destructive) entry. And on every commercial cylinder: exercise and silicone-lube it first (they're filthy, painted, and stiff), match the pin count, set every security pin to true shear, and read it twice before you cut.",relatedGuides:["lishi","t-master-keying","t-sfic-pinning","high-security-keyways","pick-types-technique"],sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-lishi-lock-pick-and-how-does-it-work","Original Lishi is the manufacturer of record; its site refuses automated fetches, so it reports as blocked rather than dead in a link check."],tags:["Lishi","Second shear line","SFIC","Change key","False set","Control key","Shear line","Ghost key"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","master pin ghost","MK shear line","small format interchangeable core","Falcon","A4","figure-8 core","operating key","user key","day key","hangs up","feels open but won't turn","counter-rotation","core removal key","pull key","IC control","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","phantom key","unintended key","cross key","MK collision","tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","mushroom","spool pin","anti-pick pin","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading"]},{id:"lishi-automotive",title:"Lishi for car keys: profiles by make, twin-lifter technique & the door-first rule",module:"Automotive",body:"On a modern car the Lishi isn't a convenience, it's the origination tool \u2014 it's how you get a mechanical bitting out of a high-security lock with no key present so you can cut a blade before you ever touch the programmer. The mental shift from residential is this: you pick the tool by KEYWAY, not by make. One keyway spans a dozen badges, and the same make can change keyways across a model year, so learn the profile map, not a make list.\n\n[[diagram:lishi-twin-lifter|Why the profile is the tool, and working two tracks without disturbing either]]\n\nTHE PROFILE MAP (pick the tool to the keyway, confirm the exact version on the listing):\n- HU100 \u2014 GM: Chevrolet, Buick, GMC, Cadillac, plus Opel/Vauxhall. Comes in an 8-cut (older) AND a 10-cut (2010+ laser) \u2014 these are different tools, so match the cut count to the car.\n- HU101 \u2014 Ford and Lincoln laser (roughly 2011+), Volvo, Jaguar, Land Rover. (Don't confuse this with the old Ford/Jaguar TIBBE rotary key \u2014 Tibbe is a round disc key, a completely different tool.)\n- HU66 \u2014 the VAG family: VW, Audi, Seat, Skoda, Porsche, up to about 2015. HU162T (the 9-cut \"three-track\") took over on 2015+ VAG \u2014 a separate tool. Confirm HU66 vs HU162T by year or you'll bring the wrong one.\n- HU64 \u2014 Mercedes-Benz, a tight 4-track milled key; steady hands only, and many techs prefer pulling the door lock to bench-decode it.\n- TOY48 \u2014 Toyota/Lexus 2-track laser high-security; TOY43 \u2014 the older edge-cut Toyota; different tools. On a smart-key Toyota you Lishi the emergency-blade DOOR lock, since there's no ignition cylinder.\n- HON66 \u2014 Honda/Acura 2-track.\n- NSN14 \u2014 Nissan/Infiniti; the Hyundai/Kia profiles (HY, KIA families) and Mazda (MAZ24), Mitsubishi (MIT) each have their own. Verify the exact profile and cut count against the tool's listing before you buy \u2014 this is where clones and mislabels bite.\n\n2-IN-1 vs READER-ONLY. The automotive tool you want is the 2-IN-1: it picks the lock open AND decodes, which is the whole game on an all-keys-lost job \u2014 no key, no problem, you pick the door and read the code cold. A reader-only tool just decodes a lock you've already opened or pulled; useful on the bench, useless at the curb on AKL. Buy the 2-in-1.\n\nTWIN LIFTERS AND DOUBLE-SIDED KEYS. Most automotive keyways are 2-track \u2014 wafers on both sides \u2014 so the tool carries two lifter arms and you pick and read BOTH tracks. The body has a printed arrow showing the read direction (on HU100/HU101 you decode in the same direction you pick). The touch is everything: feather-light fingertip pressure, feel the first contact with the wafer, ease past the spring until it stops, read the number. Force it and you bend a lifter, and a bent lifter misreads a two-sided key \u2014 which on a car means a wasted blank and, worse, a wasted programming appointment.\n\nTHE DOOR-FIRST RULE. Always Lishi the DOOR, never the ignition, and here's the why so you never forget it: the door lock is almost always keyed to the same code as the ignition from the factory, but it has FEWER wafers than the ignition, so it's faster and easier to pick and read. The ignition is buried behind the steering column, carries more wafers, and picking it risks fouling the steering lock or the ignition mechanism itself. Decode the door, cut the blade to that code, and that blade turns the ignition. On a push-to-start car the door's emergency lock (or the door lock pulled and read on the bench) is your only mechanical decode target anyway.\n\nMAKE-SPECIFIC GOTCHAS.\n- GM sidebar ignitions: the HU100 reads the wafers, but older GM 10-cut and VATS-era ignitions add a SIDEBAR that the standard pick/decode doesn't defeat \u2014 that's a rocker-pick or pull-the-column job. The DOOR is still your clean decode.\n- Toyota/Lexus smart keys: no ignition cylinder \u2014 Lishi the emergency-blade door lock; TOY48 for the laser era, TOY43 for the old edge keys.\n- VAG: the 2015 HU66-to-HU162T switch is the classic wrong-tool call \u2014 check the year.\n- Mercedes HU64: tight and fragile; if the in-door read fights you, pull the lock and decode on the bench rather than bend a lifter.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE THAT PAYS. Decode the door \u2192 cut the correct blade to that code \u2192 THEN program the transponder. Never walk up and start guessing the mechanical cut, because on an immobilizer with a security/lockout timer a wrong blade can burn your one programming window and cost you the job. Solve the mechanical side cleanly and completely with the Lishi first, and your programming time goes entirely to programming.\n\nTHE LIMITS. The mechanical decode is only ever HALF the key on a transponder or proximity car \u2014 the chip still has to be married to the immobilizer, and that's a separate booked procedure. Some pure-proximity cars hide the emergency cylinder behind a handle or trim you must remove to reach. And a rusted, sand-blasted, or damaged lock may simply not give a trustworthy read. As always: verify you have the right to access the vehicle before the tool comes out, keep the lifter tips clean and dead straight, and re-read any lock you're about to cut a code from \u2014 on a car, a lying read doesn't just waste a blank, it can waste your shot at the immobilizer.",relatedGuides:["lishi","auto-decode-read","auto-key-cutting","auto-key-origination","gm-sidebar-picking"],sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.lockpicks.com/blogs/blogs/what-is-a-lishi-lock-pick-and-how-does-it-work","Original Lishi is the manufacturer of record; its site refuses automated fetches, so it reports as blocked rather than dead in a link check."],tags:["Lishi","Immobilizer","Transponder","Reader","Push-to-start","Keyway","Cylinder","Bitting"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","immo","anti-theft","security light","car won't start","cranks but dies","chip key","immo chip","Megamos","Hitag","Texas Crypto","4D chip","46 chip","key turns but won't start","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","proximity key","smart key","keyless go","start button","prox fob","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list"]},{id:"t-drill-bits-sharpening",title:"Drill bits, sharpening & making your tools last",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"A [[tool:drill-bits|drill bit]] is the cheapest thing on your truck that fails you at the worst time... standing at a deadlatch on a commercial door at 4:45 on a Friday, bit gone blue and skating. Everything in this guide is about not being that guy. We covered speeds, feeds, lube, and the make-chips-not-dust rule in the companion guide, \"Drilling metal: speeds, feeds & lubrication\" (t-drilling-metal)... that stuff is assumed here. This one is about the hardware itself: what the bit is made of, what shape it is, the geometry at the tip, how you put an edge back on it, and how you keep the whole kit alive.\n\n[[img:photo-tool-drill-bits|Cobalt, carbide and titanium... the assortment this guide is about keeping sharp.]]\n\nBIT MATERIALS... WHAT YOU'RE ACTUALLY PAYING FOR\n\nPlain HSS (high-speed steel, usually M2) is the baseline. It cuts mild steel, aluminum, brass, wood... everything a locksmith meets on a residential door. A sharp HSS bit is a good bit. Its weakness is heat: let it get hot and the temper walks out of the edge.\n\nBlack-oxide HSS is the same steel with a surface treatment. The black oxide is not a hard coating... it resists rust and holds a little lube, that's it. It buys you a slightly tougher-feeling bit and better shelf life in a damp truck box for a couple dollars. Worth it as your everyday set. Do not expect it to cut anything HSS won't.\n\nCobalt is where it gets real for us, because we hit stainless. Cobalt bits are HSS with cobalt alloyed into the steel itself, not a coating... so when you sharpen them the cobalt is still there. Two grades you'll see: M35 is about 5% cobalt, roughly 65 to 67 HRC. M42 is about 8% cobalt, roughly 68 to 70 HRC. The extra cobalt buys heat resistance and red-hardness... the bit keeps its edge hotter, which is exactly what you need in stainless because stainless work-hardens and throws heat. The trade is that harder means more brittle... M42 chips easier if you let the bit chatter or grab. For a locksmith: keep a dedicated M35 or M42 cobalt set for stainless hardware, security strikes, and hardened components. Don't buy cobalt for wood doors... you're paying for heat resistance you'll never use.\n\nSolid carbide is a whole different animal... the entire bit is tungsten carbide. Brutally hard, holds an edge at temperatures that would cook HSS, and it will shatter like glass if you flex it or drop it. It wants a rigid setup and a steady feed, which a handheld drill on a wobbly door is not. Carbide-tipped bits (carbide braze onto a steel body) are the practical middle... that's what your masonry bits and most hole saws for abrasive material use. For handheld door work, solid carbide is usually the wrong tool. Where carbide earns its keep for a locksmith is drilling hardened lock components... a hardened plate, a spool, an anti-drill pin... where HSS just polishes and quits. Then you want a carbide-tipped bit specifically sold for hardened security material.\n\nCOATINGS... AND WHICH ONE IS MARKETING\n\nTiN (titanium nitride, the gold-colored coat) is a genuine hard skin, around 85 Rc, good to roughly 600 C. It reduces friction and adds edge life on HSS. Real benefit... but only a few microns thick, so the first time you sharpen the bit you grind the coating off the cutting edge and you're back to bare HSS at the lips. TiN is worth it on an unsharpened production set, less so once you're a sharpener.\n\nTiAlN / AlTiN (titanium aluminum nitride, the dark grey/black-purple coat) is harder and takes more heat, good to around 800 C, because it forms an aluminum-oxide skin as it heats that shields the tool. This is the coating for hard ferrous work and stainless. One honest warning worth knowing: do not use TiAlN on aluminum... the aluminum in the coating has a chemical affinity for aluminum stock and builds up on the edge. Use bare or TiN on aluminum.\n\nBottom line on coatings for a truck: they're a bonus on a factory edge, not a substitute for the right base material, and they don't survive resharpening at the lip. Buy the right steel first.\n\nBIT TYPES... WHAT YOU REACH FOR ON A DOOR\n\nTwist bits are 95% of your drilling. The length matters more than people think. Jobber length is the standard long bit... flexible, walks and wanders if you lean on it. Screw-machine (stub) length is short and fat by comparison, and short means stiff means straight. When you're drilling a precise hole for a deadbolt cross-bore fixture, a strike, or through a mortise faceplate, a stub-length bit gives you a rounder, on-location hole with less walking and less breakage. Keep both lengths.\n\nSplit-point twist bits are the ones you want to live on... more on the point below. Brad-point bits (the sharp center spur and two outer spurs) are wood bits... clean-edged holes in a wood door or jamb, do not use them in metal, the spur just breaks. [[tool:spade-bits|Spade/paddle bits]] are cheap fast wood bits for rough holes, fine for running cable or a big rough bore in a wood jamb, ugly and grabby. Forstner bits bore flat-bottomed, clean, controlled holes... this is your bit for boring a mortise pocket or a clean counterbore in wood.\n\nHole saws with an arbor are how you cut the 2-1/8\" cross bore for a bore-kit or a big strike. Bi-metal hole saws (HSS teeth welded to a spring-steel body) from Lenox, Milwaukee, Starrett, Morse cut wood and thin metal. For thicker or abrasive material, carbide-tipped hole saws. Always run the arbor's pilot bit... it's what keeps the saw from wandering off the mark. Step bits (unibits) are for sheet metal and thin panels only... they deburr as they go and give clean round holes in a strike box or a thin door skin, but they have no business in thick steel. Masonry bits have a carbide tip and are ground for percussion and grinding brick/block/concrete... they are NOT metal bits. They don't have real cutting lips, they crush and grind. Never chuck a masonry bit into a lock or a steel door expecting it to cut... it'll just polish, heat up, and go nowhere.\n\nPOINT GEOMETRY... THE PART THAT ACTUALLY CUTS\n\nThe point angle is the total included angle at the tip. 118 degrees is the classic general-purpose grind... it self-feeds nicely in softer, more ductile material (mild steel, aluminum, wood). 135 degrees is flatter... it spreads the load, resists walking, and is the grind for hard and abrasive metal and stainless. Flatter point plus stainless is why nearly every quality cobalt set (Norseman/Viking out of Minnesota, for one) ships 135.\n\nThe chisel edge (the web) is the flat span across the center where the two flutes meet... it does not cut, it plows. That's what makes a plain chisel-point bit walk before it bites, and why you center-punch. A split point grinds two small relief slots into that chisel edge, turning the dead center into cutting edges that come almost to a point. Result: the bit self-centers and bites right where you set it, so you can skip the punch on a hardware install. That's the single most useful feature on a locksmith's bit... 135 split point, buy them that way.\n\nTwo more geometry terms you must own to sharpen. The cutting lips are the two main edges running from the point out to the flute... they do the cutting, and they MUST be equal in length and angle. The lip relief (clearance) angle is the way the surface behind each lip falls away so the edge, not the heel, contacts the metal. No relief and the bit rubs and burns instead of cutting. On bigger bits the web gets thicker toward the shank, so you web-thin... grind a little material off the center to narrow that chisel edge so it isn't just pushing.\n\nSHARPENING ON A DRILL DOCTOR\n\nThe [[tool:drill-doctor|Drill Doctor]] 750X (drilldoctor.com) is the one you'll see on locksmith benches. It handles 3/32\" up to 3/4\", HSS, cobalt, TiN-coated, carbide, and masonry. You set the point angle on a shuttle... adjustable 115 to 140 degrees with the common 118 and 135 marked. You clamp the bit in the chuck against a set of fingers that index the flutes so both lips come out even, then rotate it against the diamond wheel. It has a separate split-point port with a \"push to stop\" so you can add or restore a split point without over-splitting, and a material-take-off adjustment so you're not grinding away more bit than needed. For a shop that goes through bits, it makes even, repeatable edges faster than freehand and it does the split point... which is the hard part by hand.\n\nSHARPENING FREEHAND ON A BENCH GRINDER\n\nThis is the real skill, and it's worth learning because a Drill Doctor isn't always in the van. Set your bit next to a drill point gauge so your eye learns 118 and 135. Present one lip to the wheel horizontal, cutting edge parallel to the wheel face, at the point half-angle. Then, in one smooth motion, roll the bit slightly AND drop the shank hand down... that roll-and-drop is what carves the lip relief behind the edge. Lift, rotate 180, do the other lip identically. The whole game is symmetry: both lips the same length and the same angle. Uneven lips is the classic failure... one lip does all the cutting, the bit drills oversize, walks, and screams. Take light passes, dip in water often to keep it cool (a bit small enough to hold barehanded is cool enough), and check on the gauge constantly. Once the two lips are clean and equal, you can touch a split point with the corner of a dressed wheel, but that's advanced... get symmetric first.\n\nWHEN TO SHARPEN AND WHEN TO TOSS\n\nSharpen anything 1/4\" and up that's just dulled or slightly chipped... that's where your time pays back. Below about 1/8\" it's fiddly, cheap, and easier to replace... toss it. A badly blued bit (see below) has lost its temper down into the flute... you can grind past the blue to good steel if there's length left, otherwise it's scrap. Carbide you do not touch on a normal grinder... it needs a diamond wheel, and if it's chipped, replace it. Masonry bits you can dress the carbide tip but it's rarely worth it.\n\nTOOL LONGEVITY... HEAT KILLS BITS, EVERYTHING ELSE IS DETAIL\n\nBluing is death. When the tip turns straw, then purple, then blue, that color is oxide from the steel getting hot enough to draw the temper right out of the cutting edge. A blued HSS edge is soft... it will never hold again until you grind back to bright steel. You prevent it with the fundamentals from the companion guide: correct (slower) speed, real cutting lube, and pecking to clear chips so heat leaves with the chip instead of soaking into the tool.\n\nDo NOT water-quench a red or blued HSS bit. HSS is air-hardening... plunge it hot into water and the thermal shock micro-cracks the cutting edge, and those cracks are where it chips next time. If a bit got that hot, let it cool in air and then sharpen past the damage. When you're sharpening on the grinder, that's the opposite case... there you dip often to keep it from ever getting hot in the first place.\n\nA sharp bit lasts longer than a dull one... that sounds backwards but it's the core truth. A sharp edge slices and the chip carries the heat away. A dull edge rubs, and rubbing is pure heat with no chip to remove it, so a dull bit cooks itself faster the longer you push it. The moment a bit stops making chips and starts making dust and smoke, stop and sharpen. Pushing harder on a dull bit is how you snap it and how you blue it.\n\nStore bits in an index or a case, tips separated. A drawer full of loose bits clashing tip-to-tip chips the cutting edges before they ever touch metal... a chipped lip is a walking, oversize hole. A cheap steel or plastic index earns itself back the first week.\n\nCare for the grinder too. Dress and true the wheel with a dressing stick or diamond dresser... a glazed, out-of-round wheel burns bits instead of cutting them and makes symmetric grinding impossible. And care for the drill: let a cordless cool between big holes, don't stall the motor in a bind (that's what cooks brushes and gearboxes), keep batteries off a dead-flat discharge, and keep the chuck clean so bits run true. A bit that runs out because of a gummed-up chuck drills oversize no matter how well you sharpened it.\n\nBuy good base material, keep it sharp, keep it cool, keep the tips apart. Do that and a $12 cobalt bit outlasts a drawer of cheap ones and never leaves you stranded at a door.",relatedGuides:["t-drilling-metal","door-prep-jigs","t-drilling-out-cores","impressioning"],sources:["https://www.drilldoctor.com/products/750x","https://d347awuzx0kdse.cloudfront.net/industrialtools/product-download/dd750x_u.pdf","https://www.hk-tools.com/news/m35-and-m42-cobalt-drill-bits-differences-and-usage/","http://norsemandrill.com/","https://www.jacotools.com/blog/drill-bit-coatings-tin-tialn-black-oxide-guide","https://www.locksmithledger.com/"],tags:["Drill point","Deadlatch","Strike"],aliases:["drill spot","drill template","drill chart","where to drill","latch guard","anti-shim","plunger","latch bolt","deadlatching plunger","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"t-drilling-out-cores",title:"Drilling out a lock: pin-tumbler, SFIC & LFIC cores",module:"Covert & Emergency Entry (Authorized)",body:"DRILLING OUT A LOCK: PIN-TUMBLER, SFIC & LFIC CORES\n\n[[diagram:drill-point-geometry|Where the shear line sits, and why an IC core gives you two of them to choose from]]\n\nRead this as what it is... a last-resort chapter. Drilling is the tool you reach for when the non-destructive toolbox is empty and you have the authority to open the opening anyway. Everything here assumes you are a licensed hand on a job you can prove is yours to be on... a lost control key, a seized core, a lockout with a legitimate customer and a paper trail. Drilling destroys the lock. On an interchangeable core that is a feature, not a tragedy, because the thing you destroy is a cheap, swappable core and the housing lives to see another day. On a conventional cylinder you are buying the customer a new cylinder. Either way, you cut, you extract, you replace, you repin, you document. This guide does not re-teach speeds, feeds, or bit selection for metal... that lives in t-drilling-metal. Go read it first. Here we only talk about where to put the hole and why.\n\nWHEN YOU DRILL... AND NOT A MINUTE BEFORE\n\nDrilling is the bottom of the ladder. You climb down to it, you do not start there. Pick it. If it will not pick, bypass it. On an IC core there is a third rung most people skip... try to pick to the control shear and pull the whole core intact, because a core you pull clean is a core you can decode and reuse. Only when picking the operating line fails, picking to control fails, bypass fails, and the core is physically seized or the plug is spun and jammed... only then do you spin up the drill. The honest test is time and certainty. Locksmith Ledger says it plainly... when the control key will not remove the core and time is the constraint, drilling offers a predictable finish time. That predictability is the whole argument for it. You are trading a cheap core for a guaranteed open.\n\nPIN-TUMBLER: DRILL THE SHEAR LINE, NOT THE LOCK\n\nA pin-tumbler plug is trapped by pin stacks straddling the gap between plug and shell. That gap is the shear line. Every stack has a bottom pin in the plug and a top driver crossing the line into the shell, and as long as one driver bridges the gap the plug cannot turn. Your job is not to demolish the cylinder. It is to sever every stack exactly at the shear line so nothing bridges anymore and the plug spins free.\n\nRead the keyway first. Sight down it and find the pin line... the row of pin tips sitting at the top of the keyway. That row sits at the shear line, and that height, just above the top of the plug, is your drill line. Level, dead on the pin row, straight in on the plug axis.\n\nStart small. A 1/8\" bit gives you a clean pilot that tracks the pin line without wandering, and small means you are cutting pins, not eating the whole face. Walk it across the stacks one at a time, front to back. As each driver is cut at the line it stops bridging... you will often feel the plug want to give partway through. When the pilot has crossed every chamber, put a pick or a cut-down key blank in and turn. If it turns, you are done and you did the least damage possible. If a stack or two still holds, open the pilot up a hair with the next size and clear them. Go slow. Slow is what keeps the hole on the line instead of chewing the shell and welding brass to your bit. The goal is a row of clean cuts at the shear line, not a crater.\n\nSFIC / BEST: TWO SHEAR LINES, AND CONTROL IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS\n\nThe figure-8 Best-format core is a different animal because it has two shear lines, not one. The operating shear line sits between the plug and the sleeve... defeat it and the lock turns. The control shear line sits between the sleeve and the housing... defeat it and the core comes out. A control lug at the rear of the core, part of that sleeve, is what locks the core into the housing. The control key rotates roughly 15 degrees clockwise, that retracts the lug into the housing, and the core slides out. That is the entire trick of an IC... the control function is a second, deeper shear line you must defeat to extract.\n\nThe two lines are set apart by pin height, and control cuts differ from the operating cuts by a fixed step in the depth series... enough that a stack lifted to the operating line is nowhere near the control line and vice versa. That is exactly why picking is hard... you feel the operating line first because it takes less lift and less resistance, and most hands set that line by reflex, turn the plug, and never reach control. If your goal is extraction, the operating line is the wrong line.\n\nSo you have two drilling answers, and you pick based on what you need. If you just need the door open right now, drill the operating shear line like a normal pin-tumbler... cut the stacks between plug and sleeve, turn the plug, throw the bolt. The core is scrap but the door is open. If you need the core out with the housing intact, you defeat the control function instead. The published, verifiable field technique for that is not blowing out the whole pin column... it is reaching the control lug directly. Locksmith Ledger describes drilling a small pilot, on the order of 3/32\", on a slight angle, just deep enough to barely touch the lug that protrudes on the control shell, then applying pressure to that lug with a pin-extractor tool and rotating the sleeve clockwise to the removal position so the core pulls. Check your depth constantly on that one... the whole point is to kiss the lug, not bury the bit past it. If you would rather not eyeball it, jig it. Peterson's I-Core drill jig and the HPC/CLK cylinder drill jigs exist precisely to index the control-lug hole so the core survives to be decoded and reused.\n\nOne honest note on the deeper pin-column approach. Yes, you can drill the pin column to release the sleeve so the lug can rotate. But treat exact drill-point measurements you cannot verify against a jig or a manufacturer print as principle, not gospel. The principle is fixed... the control line lives above the operating line, higher and farther out in the stack, and you are trying to clear metal off the sleeve so it can turn to control. Do not invent a number. Verify it or work the lug. And mind 6-pin versus 7-pin... a 7-pin core is longer, the chambers sit differently, and it is the tougher pick, so know which one is in your hand before you commit a hole.\n\nLFIC / FULL-SIZE: BIGGER, TOUGHER, DIFFERENT CONTROL\n\nFull-size interchangeable cores, the Schlage 23-030 family and the Corbin Russwin and Sargent housings, are the same figure-8 idea scaled up, but the control mechanism is genuinely different from brand to brand, and that changes where you drill. This matters... an SFIC habit applied to an LFIC housing wastes holes.\n\nSchlage FSIC does not use an SFIC-style sleeve. The control key carries a longer blade and a tip cut that works a retaining mechanism at the rear of the plug, and the rotation to remove is counter-clockwise on Schlage where SFIC and the Corbin/Sargent cores go clockwise. Corbin Russwin and Sargent build control into extra pin chambers instead... Corbin runs a four-chamber control section, Sargent locates its control cuts in specific spaces. So the mechanism you must defeat to free the core is not in the same place across these three, and there is no single measurement that spans them. Get the housing identified first, then get to that specific control mechanism to free the core. And respect the size... more brass, longer plug, more chambers, tougher going than a little Best core. Do not bring an SFIC plan to an LFIC door. When in doubt, pull the Schlage LFIC service manual and the Allegion cylinder compatibility sheet and confirm what you are looking at before the bit touches metal.\n\nANTI-DRILL: KNOW WHEN THE DRILL IS THE WRONG CALL\n\nNot every lock wants to be drilled, and the good ones will tell you no. Learn the tells. Hardened steel or carbide anti-drill pins seated ahead of the plug will shatter or skate a standard bit. Spinning ball bearings and hardened inserts turn under the bit instead of letting it cut. Hardened control lugs on better IC do the same at the extraction point. And high-security interchangeable core exists now... Medeco and KeyMark SFIC and Mul-T-Lock IC drop UL 437 hardware, hardened inserts, and rotating or telescopic pin designs into a Best-compatible housing specifically to defeat both picking and drilling. When you see a UL 437 core or a name like Medeco or Mul-T-Lock, understand that a casual drill is not the play... you are into carbide, patience, and manufacturer-specific attack points, or you stop and call the factory. Recognizing that you are beat before you dull three bits is a professional skill, not a failure.\n\nAFTER: EXTRACT, REPLACE, DOCUMENT... THEN UPSELL THE LESSON\n\nOnce you are through, finish clean. On an IC job the payoff arrives now... pull the drilled core, drop in a new or freshly repinned core, and the customer is back in business for the price of a core and a few minutes. That cheap swap is the entire reason interchangeable core exists, and it is why you fought to keep the housing alive. On a conventional cylinder, replace the whole cylinder. Either way, vacuum and blow every brass chip out of the housing, the bolt works, and the door edge... chips left behind jam the next core and come back as a callback. Then document. What you drilled, why non-destructive failed, whose authority you were under, what you installed.\n\nAnd carry the lesson forward, because it is the sale. Every lock you just drilled in ten minutes is a lock that should have been drill-resistant. That is the honest pitch to the customer... this opened fast because it was never built to resist a bit, and here is what a UL 437 cylinder or a high-security IC would have cost the guy trying to do this without your paperwork. Anti-drill cylinders and high-security interchangeable core are not upsells you invented. They are the exact answer to the afternoon you just had.",relatedGuides:["t-drilling-metal","t-drill-bits-sharpening","destructive-forced-entry","t-sfic-pinning","t-master-keying","high-security-keyways"],sources:["https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10229658/interchangeable-core-basics","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/10230765/working-with-interchangeable-cores-when-keys-are-not-available","https://www.locksmithledger.com/keys-tools/article/21228355/servicing-interchangeable-cores","https://commercial.schlage.com/content/dam/allegion-us-2/web-files/schlage/technical-documents/Schlage_LFIC_Service_Manual_015806.pdf","https://www.thinkpeterson.com/i-core-drill-jig-for-sfic-cores/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/locks/article/12438295/can-interchangeable-cores-be-high-security-","https://www.medeco.com/en/products/cylinders-keys-keying/small-format-interchangeable-core-sfic","https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/fsic-full-size-interchangeable-cores.html"],tags:["LFIC","SFIC","Control key","Shear line","Shell","Plug","Key blank","Bypass"],aliases:["large format interchangeable core","Schlage Everest","removable core","small format interchangeable core","Falcon","A2","A4","figure-8 core","core removal key","pull key","IC control","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","lock body","cylinder body","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","SC4","Ilco","JMA","Silca","blank won't fit","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"t-lockset-grades",title:"Lockset grades: ANSI/BHMA A156.2 (Grade 1, 2, 3)",module:"Doors, Frames & Installation",body:"Grade is the fastest way to size up a lockset: how many times it will operate before it wears out, and how much force it shrugs off. The rating comes from ANSI/BHMA A156.2, the standard for bored (cylindrical) locks and latches \u2014 the everyday knob and lever sets you install. Three grades; Grade 1 is highest.\n\n[[diagram:grade-table|ANSI/BHMA A156.2 grade comparison]]\n\nGrade 1 is heavy commercial. A million operating cycles and ten ram-impacts at the cylinder face \u2014 the lever on a school corridor, a hospital, an apartment entry, anywhere the door swings all day. Schlage's ND line and Sargent's 10-line are the Grade 1 levers you will see most.\n\nGrade 2 is light commercial and heavy residential: 400,000 cycles, five impacts. A small office, a multifamily unit door, a back room. Schlage AL and Falcon T sit here.\n\nGrade 3 is residential \u2014 200,000 cycles, two impacts. Builder-grade knobs and levers on a house. Fine for a bedroom or a closet; underbuilt for a business.\n\nTwo cautions. First, the number is only as good as the category: A156.2 covers bored locks, but mortise locks run under A156.13 and deadbolts under A156.5/36, so a 'Grade 1' deadbolt is graded on a different test than a Grade 1 lever \u2014 do not cross-compare across standards. Second, grade measures durability and strike resistance, not key security; a Grade 1 body can still wear a low-security keyway. Match the grade to the traffic, then match the cylinder to the security need.\n\nGrade is only half of what A156.2 pins down. The other half is **function**... what the lock actually does, expressed as a number you will see on every schedule and every order. 10 is passage, latch only, no lock at all. 40 and 44 are the privacy pair, bathroom and hospital. 50 and 53 are entrance and office. 70 is classroom, keyed outside with no inside button. 80 is storeroom, always locked outside, always free out. Those numbers are the same across manufacturers, which is what makes a door schedule readable no matter whose hardware is specified.\n\nWhat the function actually decides, before you get anywhere near a lever design or a finish, is what the OUTSIDE of the door can be. A passage function has nothing to key, so its outside is a closed rose. A storeroom or classroom function must be keyed, so it takes a cylinder or a core. Get the function wrong on the order and no amount of trim selection rescues it.\n\n[[diagram:schlage-al-function-trim|Schlage AL/ALX: which outside trim each function can take, and the four-step build order]]\n\nOne detail on that chart is worth committing to memory because it bites during a retrofit: the button prep and the key-in-lever prep are not the same hole. The button hole is slightly smaller, so a door prepped for a privacy button will not simply accept a keyed lever without rework.",sources:["https://www.buildershardware.com/bhma-standards/ansibhma-a1562-bored-preassembled-locks-and-latches","https://blog.ansi.org/ansi/ansi-grade-levels-bhma-locks-hardware-tests/"],tags:["Door schedule","Schedule","Cylinder","Strike","Keyway"],aliases:["hardware schedule","spec book","submittal","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"t-keyways-schlage-kwikset",title:"Keyways & key blanks: Schlage & Kwikset",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Two keyway families cover most of what you cut: Schlage and Kwikset. Get the keyway right before you pick a blank \u2014 the wrong section will not enter, and a few brand quirks trip up new hands.\n\nSCHLAGE\n\nSchlage's Classic Obverse family runs seven single-section keyways \u2014 C, CE, E, EF, F, FG, G \u2014 used to build masterkey systems where a section key will not cross into another section's cores. C is the everyday keyway.\n\n[[diagram:schlage-keyways|Schlage keyways, blanks & security tiers]]\n\nThe standard C blank is SC1 for five-pin and SC4 for six-pin (Schlage part 35-101). Above the open Classic tier sit two restricted, patented tiers: Everest, which adds an undercut 'check' pin and controlled blanks, and Primus, which layers a sidebar of finger pins over the keyway for UL437 high security. A P keyway passes all seven sections and an L blank enters all seven \u2014 the master and service keys.\n\nKWIKSET\n\nKwikset is simpler: KW1 is the five-pin residential keyway you meet everywhere; KW10 is the six-pin (Titan and older six-pin knobs), same side millings but a longer key.\n\n[[diagram:kwikset-keyways|Kwikset keyways & the SmartKey gotcha]]\n\nThe gotcha that catches everyone: Kwikset SmartKey \u2014 the rekeyable line \u2014 does NOT use KW1. It takes the Schlage SC1 keyway, so you cut a SmartKey on a Schlage-C blank. A KW1 key will physically enter a KW10 lock but stops on the wrong shoulder, so it will not turn.\n\nFor BEST/SFIC keyways (A\u2013R, the 1A1 blank series and multiplex masters), see the SFIC control-system guide.",relatedGuides:["t-sfic-pinning"],relatedModels:["schlage-nd","kwikset-smartkey"],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/classic-obverse-keyways.html","https://www.clksupplies.com/blogs/news/how-to-turn-a-kwikset-kw1-into-a-kw10-when-needed"],tags:["Keyway","SFIC"],aliases:["KW11","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","small format interchangeable core","Falcon","A2","A4","figure-8 core","IC core"]},{id:"mortise-to-cylindrical-conversion",title:"Mortise-to-Cylindrical Conversion (Shelter & Utility Doors)",module:"Conversion",body:"WHEN TO USE THIS PLAY. You get called to a metal or fiberglass door \u2014 a pump shed, an equipment shelter, a prefab utility building \u2014 with a dead lock in it, and when you pull the trim you find an OEM or import mortise case: a euro-pattern body, a non-standard trim footprint, internals that no local supplier stocks. The client needs the door secure and functional today, and there is no OEM parts source that gets you there in time. That is the conversion play. You rip the orphaned mortise, prep the door for a standard [[model:schlage-nd|Grade 1 cylindrical lever]], and cover the scars with [[model:donjo-conversion-plates|Don-Jo conversion and wrap plates]] so the finished door reads factory instead of field-butchered. It is a same-day, parts-on-the-truck fix for the doors nobody makes keys for anymore. Before you swing a single tool, be sure the work order actually calls for a lockset and not a deadbolt \u2014 converting a door the client needed to hold a separate deadbolt function is the expensive way to learn to read the WO.\n\n[[img:photo-schlage-nd|A Schlage ND cylindrical lever, the usual destination of one of these conversions.]]\n\nSTEP 0 \u2014 BEFORE YOU ORDER. This is where the job is won or lost, at the supply counter, not on the ladder. Two things decide what goes on the truck. First, keying: read the work order and confirm site key versus client-supplied interchangeable core \u2014 that single answer picks [[model:schlage-nd|the PD versus BD lever]]. A site-keyed door takes the ND80PD (conventional C keyway, keyed to the site); a national-account door where the client hands you their own core takes the ND80BD (SFIC-prep, less core). If you can't confirm the core status, stock [[model:best-sfic|an uncombinated 7-pin core]] so you can pin on site instead of making a second trip. Second, door thickness: the [[model:donjo-conversion-plates|wrap plates]] are thickness- and backset-specific, so measure the door (1-3/8\" vs 1-3/4\") and confirm the 2-3/4\" backset before you order. Then photograph both faces, the edge, and the frame strike \u2014 those photos are your proof of scope and your template reference.\n\n1. Strip the old lock. Remove the trim, spindle, mortise cylinder, and case \u2014 on most of these it's two faceplate screws and the body slides out. Bag every piece of removed hardware for client disposition; on institutional and government sites you do not throw away or keep their hardware, you hand it back.\n\n2. Mark your geometry. Measure and mark the lever-height centerline off the old hub, and check the backset \u2014 you're planning the cross bore at 2-3/4\". Get this line right and every plate and strike downstream lines up for free.\n\n3. Dry-fit the plates FIRST, then mark. Set [[model:donjo-conversion-plates|the CV-86 edge plate]] into the mortise edge cutout and mind the 3/8\" off-center latch prep \u2014 the standard CV-86 does not center the latch, so mark the centerline the plate actually gives you, not the middle of the plate. Then set the wrap plate on the face and mark the 2-1/8\" cross bore THROUGH the wrap, using it as your template, so the finished bore lands where the wrap fully covers the racetrack cutout and the old cylinder hole. Marking off the wrap before you bore is the whole difference between a clean cover and a crescent of old hole peeking out.\n\n4. Bore it. Cut the 2-1/8\" cross bore with a carbide hole saw \u2014 this is a metal skin, so run slow speed, use cutting fluid, and deburr both sides. Bore the 1\" edge hole through the CV-86 prep for the latch.\n\n5. Hang the lever. Run the latch through the CV-86, then mount [[model:schlage-nd|the cylindrical chassis]] with a wrap plate sandwiched on each face. Through-bolt it, keep the lever square, and verify the latch retracts smooth and the lever returns before you take the fasteners to final torque.\n\n6. Do the frame. Install the ASA strike (mortise or box as the frame prep allows), and close the old euro strike mortise with [[model:donjo-conversion-plates|the flat filler / blank strike plate]] so the jamb isn't left gaping. Shut the door and confirm the latch throws fully into the new strike with no rub.\n\n7. Key it and function it. Rekey the C-keyway cylinder to the site key, or seat [[model:best-sfic|the client's interchangeable core]]. Then prove the function like a storeroom lock should behave: key retracts the latch from outside, inside lever always free, and the door locks behind you every time it closes.\n\n8. Finish clean. If the plates need to match the door, mask and rattle-can them \u2014 that's why they ship prime-coat. Then sweep every steel shaving off the floor and the threshold with a magnet; shavings left on a metal door rust into orange freckles by next week and that's a callback for looks.\n\n9. Close out. Function-test ten full cycles, photograph the completed work, get the manager's name, and check out per your dispatch SOP. On someone else's building, the paperwork is part of the repair, not an afterthought.\n\nGOTCHAS \u2014 THE FOUR THAT CAUSE THE CALLBACK. One, wraps ordered for the wrong door thickness \u2014 they won't sit flat or won't cover the cutout, and you find out standing at the door; measure and confirm before you order. Two, boring before you dry-fit the wrap \u2014 the old cutout peeks past the plate edge and now you need a bigger plate to hide your own hole. Three, forgetting the frame side \u2014 a perfect door with the old strike mortise gaping in the jamb still looks like a hack job; fill it. Four, converting when the client actually required a deadbolt function \u2014 read the work order before you pull anything, because a storeroom lever is not a deadbolt and swapping the wrong function means doing the whole job twice.\n\nFIELD LOAD-OUT \u2014 WHAT TO PACK. Before you roll, this is the mortise-to-cylindrical kit for shelter and utility doors:\n\u2022 Drill/driver + carbide 2-1/8\" hole saw + 1\" hole saw or step bit\n\u2022 Center punch, tape measure, combination square, marker/scribe\n\u2022 Cutting fluid, files (flat + round), deburring tool\n\u2022 Screwdrivers (Phillips #2/#3), Torx/security bit set\n\u2022 Self-tapping screws assortment, sex bolts\n\u2022 SC1 pinning kit, plug follower, key gauge, cut keys/blanks\n\u2022 Clamps, masking tape (template + paint masking)\n\u2022 Rattle-can primer/paint (common utility tans/grays)\n\u2022 Magnet sweep, shop vac or brush\n\u2022 Camera/phone for closeout photos",relatedModels:["schlage-nd","donjo-conversion-plates","best-sfic","kaba-simplex","alarm-lock-trilogy"],sources:["https://www.don-jo.com/product/cv-86/","https://www.don-jo.com/wp-content/uploads/WraparoundPlates.pdf","https://www.trudoor.com/products/don-jo-14-s-cw-wrap-around-plate-for-converting-from-simplex-1000-or-alarm-lock-trilogy-to-cylindrical-lock","https://us.allegion.com/en/home/products/categories/locks/schlage-nd-series.html"],tags:["Hole saw","SFIC","Backset","Key gauge","Strike","Plug follower","Pinning kit","Mortise cylinder"],aliases:["door prep bit","2-1/8 hole","bore the door","small format interchangeable core","Falcon","A2","A4","figure-8 core","IC core","2-3/8","distance to center","wrong backset","key decoder gauge","depth gauge","read the key","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","rekey tool","pins fell out","pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","threaded cylinder","cam cylinder","mortise cyl","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","barrel","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"dw-spectrum-lts-cameras",title:"Digital Watchdog servers & LTS Platinum cameras: install, config, troubleshoot",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"First thing to get straight: Digital Watchdog and LTS are two different companies, and this job is marrying them. DW is the recorder and the software \u2014 a Blackjack server (or a smaller DW NVR) running DW Spectrum, their video management system. LTS Platinum is the camera line, and under the hood those are Dahua OEM units wearing an LTS label. So you are not installing a matched kit \u2014 you are onboarding Dahua-based cameras onto a DW Spectrum system over ONVIF, and ninety percent of the pain on these jobs lives in that handshake. Learn the DW side, learn the Dahua quirks the LTS cameras inherit, and you will never be the tech standing in a server closet at 6pm wondering why a camera pings but will not add.\n\n[[img:dw-blackjack-schematic|The Blackjack server and where the cameras, the VMS and the network meet it.]]\n\nDW Spectrum is three pieces and it helps to keep them separate in your head. The Media Server runs on the Blackjack and does the actual work \u2014 it discovers cameras, pulls their streams, writes the archive to disk, and serves it back out. The Desktop client is the app you run on your laptop or the head-end monitor to configure and view; it is not the recorder, it just talks to the server. And DW Cloud (the dwspectrum.com side, Nx Cloud underneath \u2014 DW Spectrum is Network Optix's Nx Witness rebranded) is the optional layer that lets a client reach the server from anywhere without you port-forwarding a thing. First boot: open the Desktop client, it finds the new server on the LAN, and you name the System and set the admin password right then. Set a real password and write it in the job file \u2014 a DW server you cannot log into is a truck roll.\n\nRack or shelf the Blackjack and put it on a UPS, full stop. The recorder is a computer writing a database; yank its power mid-write and you can corrupt the archive or the OS, and now you are reimaging instead of billing. Battery the server and the network switch both, so a flicker does not blind the whole system. Give it airflow and do not bury it in a sealed cabinet \u2014 these run warm.\n\nPower the cameras with PoE and know where it comes from. The rack-mount Blackjack servers usually have NO built-in PoE \u2014 you feed them from a separate PoE switch, which is the right way to build anyway because it scales and you can swap a switch without touching the recorder. The smaller DW NVR appliances do have built-in PoE ports on an isolated camera subnet, convenient for a small job but limited in count and total watts. Either way, respect the power budget: a fixed dome or bullet is 802.3af, about 15 watts; a PTZ, or anything with an IR illuminator ring or a heater and blower for outdoor, wants 802.3at PoE+, about 30 watts. Add up the camera draw and stay under the switch's total budget \u2014 do not load every port to the ceiling. Cable is Cat5e or Cat6, and PoE dies past 100 meters (328 feet) camera-to-switch; beyond that you need a PoE extender, a midspan injector, or fiber with a media converter. Run every camera home to the switch and label both ends.\n\nSet the shot before you leave. Power the camera, pull it up on a phone or the client, and frame it \u2014 cover the choke points, not the sky, and mind backlight from a glass storefront that will silhouette everyone who walks in. Fixed-lens cameras are what they are; varifocal and motorized-zoom LTS units you zoom to the framing you want and then focus, and on the motorized ones you do both from the camera web page or the DW client. Lock it and confirm the night image \u2014 IR wash-out and hot spots only show up in the dark.\n\nNow the LTS cameras, and here is where the ONVIF handshake bites. Because they are Dahua OEM, a new camera out of the box is usually unactivated \u2014 it will not just answer to admin/admin like old gear. The first connection, from the LTS or Dahua ConfigTool or the camera's own web page, forces you to activate it by setting a strong admin password. Until you do that, DW Spectrum cannot authenticate to it and the camera shows up unauthorized or not at all. So the order is: activate the camera first, set its IP (DHCP or a static on the same subnet as the server's camera NIC), enable ONVIF, and confirm the ONVIF user \u2014 on some Dahua firmware the ONVIF login is separate from the web admin login, and DW authenticates over ONVIF, so a camera you can log into in a browser can still refuse DW if the ONVIF user is disabled or has a different password. That one detail is the single most common reason a healthy camera will not onboard.\n\nSet the streams deliberately. These cameras dual-stream: a main stream at full resolution for recording and fullscreen, and a sub stream at low resolution and bitrate that DW pulls for multi-camera layouts so it is not decoding twenty 4K feeds at once. Configure both on the camera \u2014 main stream H.265 if you want to roughly halve your storage, sub stream H.264 at D1 or CIF. DW Spectrum decodes H.265 fine on a current client; on an old client a black tile is your clue to update it or drop that camera to H.264. And point the camera's NTP at the DW server or the same time source the server uses \u2014 clock drift between camera and recorder is what puts gaps and wrong timestamps in the archive, and it is invisible until someone needs footage from a specific minute.\n\nAdding the cameras in DW Spectrum is easy once they are activated and on-subnet: the server auto-discovers ONVIF devices on its own network and they populate the resource tree \u2014 you enter the credentials once and they come alive. If a camera does not auto-appear it is almost always on a different subnet or VLAN than the scanning NIC; add it by IP manually, or as a last resort add it as a generic RTSP stream with the Dahua URL the LTS cameras use \u2014 rtsp://user:pass@camera-ip:554/cam/realmonitor?channel=1&subtype=0 for the main stream, subtype=1 for the sub. RTSP-only loses you some camera-side controls in DW, so prefer ONVIF and keep RTSP in your pocket for the stubborn ones.\n\nConfigure recording per camera, not globally, because every camera earns its own schedule. In Camera Settings, Recording, you paint a weekly grid: continuous during business hours, motion-only overnight is the classic mix that saves disk without missing the break-in. Set resolution, framerate, and bitrate per cell. Storage is just math \u2014 bitrate times cameras times seconds per day is your data per day, and DW records to the server data disks and auto-purges the oldest once you set a max retention (and a legally-required minimum on some accounts). Watch disk health; DW flags a failing drive on SMART, and a dead archive disk is a silent failure until you need it. For users, make operator and viewer roles and do not hand out the admin login \u2014 the guard watching the wall does not need to reconfigure cameras.\n\nFor remote viewing, connect the system to DW Cloud and you get phone and browser access with no port forwarding, no DDNS, no fighting the customer's ISP. The old way \u2014 forward the server port and ride a DDNS name \u2014 still works but breaks the day the ISP puts the customer behind CGNAT, and more of them do every year. Cloud sidesteps all of it. Set it up at install while you are already logged in.\n\nWhen something is down, work it in order. Camera pings but will not add: it is unactivated, or the ONVIF user is disabled or has the wrong password, or it is on the wrong subnet \u2014 log into its web page directly to prove it is alive, then fix the ONVIF side. Camera adds but shows black: codec mismatch on an old client, a broken sub or main stream config, or PoE undervolt from too long a run. A camera that works all day and drops at dusk is a power problem every time \u2014 the IR ring kicks on at night and pushes the draw past what a long cable run or an overloaded switch can deliver; measure the voltage at the camera, shorten the run, or move it to a PoE+ port or a midspan injector. Recording gaps: disk full or failing, clock drift, or a motion schedule with the detection zone set wrong so nothing triggers. Whole system choppy in the client: you are almost certainly pulling main streams into a multi-view instead of sub streams, or the server CPU is pegged decoding too many H.265 mainstreams \u2014 turn on hardware decode and view substreams. Cannot reach it from home: Cloud is not connected, or the client and server versions do not match.\n\nOne thing that is not a wiring problem but will end a job: NDAA. LTS Platinum cameras are Dahua OEM, and Dahua is on the federal Section 889 covered list, so LTS Platinum generally is not NDAA-compliant. On a government building, a school or hospital taking federal money, or any job with a compliance clause, that matters \u2014 they cannot legally run those cameras, and Digital Watchdog sells its own NDAA-compliant camera line for exactly this reason. Ask about funding and compliance before you spec LTS on anything institutional, or you will be pulling cameras you already hung.",relatedGuides:["video-surveillance","conduit-cctv","camera-defense","low-voltage-wiring","dropping-fishing-wire"],sources:["https://www.digital-watchdog.com/","https://dwspectrum.com/","https://www.networkoptix.com/nx-witness/","https://www.ltsecurityinc.com/","https://dahuawiki.com/Remote_Access/RTSP_via_VLC","https://www.onvif.org/profiles/","https://www.fs.com/blog/understanding-poe-standards-and-wattage-21.html","https://www.pelco.com/blog/ndaa-compliant-cameras"],relatedModels:["dw-blackjack-spectrum","lts-platinum-ip-camera"],tags:["ONVIF","RTSP","Bitrate","Cam","Video management system","Schedule","Head-end","Codec"],aliases:["tailpiece cam","actuator","cam won't turn","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal"]},{id:"dks-1834-telephone-entry",title:"DoorKing 1834 80-Series telephone entry: power, wiring & programming",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"The DoorKing 1834 is the PC-programmable telephone entry panel you meet on apartment and commercial gates \u2014 a visitor at the callbox dials a resident, the resident presses a key on their phone, and the board fires a relay to open the gate or door. The 80-Series is the flavor with no built-in card reader, programmed from a PC over its modem rather than at the keypad, and one board stores up to 3000 numbers (the 6705 Memory Module 3000) and drives two independent entry points. Get the power and the comm path right and these run for years; get either wrong and you get intermittent faults that have you chasing ghosts.\n\nStart with power, because this is the part that bites people: the two transformers power the PANEL and nothing else. Each is a 16.5 VAC, 20 VA UL-listed unit, both required \u2014 T1 lands on the main 16AC / 16AC terminals (19 and 20), T2 on the Aux terminal, polarity does not matter on AC, and you clip the supplied ferrite around both pairs to keep hum off the phone line \u2014 that ferrite is a soft-ferrite choke, not a magnet: it passes the 60 Hz power untouched but soaks up the radio-frequency hash that would otherwise ride onto the voice line, and for a stubborn hum you loop the leads through it a second time to roughly quadruple the suppression. What you do NOT do is run a strike or a mag lock off them. Twenty VA will not carry a strike's inrush, and the moment you try you brown out the board and inherit random resets. The locks get their own supply.\n\n[[diagram:dks-1834-power-wiring|Power, ground and standby: two 16.5 VAC transformers to terminals 19/20 and Aux (ferrite on both pairs), CGND to earth, optional 12 VDC standby to 18 and 6.]]\n\nGive the strikes a separate, UL-listed power supply sized to both strikes' inrush plus holding current, because the 1834's relays do not power anything \u2014 they are dry contacts, 30 volts and 3 amps maximum, that just close the circuit. Relay 1 is a full form C: for a fail-secure strike (energize to unlock) you land the supply positive on 1C, run 1NO to the strike, and return the strike to the supply negative \u2014 for a fail-safe strike or a mag lock, move that leg from 1NO to 1NC so it holds unlocked and drops on command. Relay 2 gives you a single switched contact on 2C and 2RY, and you choose normally-open or normally-closed with the Relay 2 jumper on the board (section 4.6) \u2014 a fail-secure strike wants the jumper on NO. Before you land any of it, confirm the existing strike's voltage, AC versus DC, and fail-safe versus fail-secure, because it sets the supply: an AC strike buzzes on hold and needs a continuous-duty rating, and a DC strike wants a diode or MOV across the coil or the inductive kick pits your relay contacts over time.\n\n[[diagram:dks-1834-relay-strike|Relay to strike wiring off a separate supply: Relay 1 form-C (1C/1NO/1NC, 1NC for fail-safe or mag), Relay 2 single contact (2C/2RY, NO/NC by jumper). Dry contacts, 30 V / 3 A max.]]\n\nMatch the power wire to the run: 18 AWG up to 100 feet, 16 AWG up to 200 feet, 600-volt insulated, and keep it as short as you can, because these low-voltage leads pick up noise that ends up on the voice line. And do not plug the transformers into a switched receptacle \u2014 one flipped switch and the whole entry system is dead.\n\nThis board lives outside on a metal enclosure at the end of a long wire run, which makes it a lightning target, so ground and surge-protect it like you mean it. Run CGND with 12 AWG minimum to a real ground within 10 feet \u2014 the electrical panel, a metallic cold-water pipe, or a proper 10-foot ground rod. A gate post set in concrete is not a ground no matter how solid it feels, so do not fool yourself. DKS also calls for two of their 1878-010 low-voltage surge suppressors within 10 feet of the unit; install them. A surge board is a few dollars next to a cooked main board and the truck roll to swap it.\n\nSort the comm path before you are standing at the gate. Classic installs ride a POTS landline, but plenty of sites no longer have one \u2014 which is why these ship with the 1830-010 AG cellular and modem PCB, giving the board DKS cellular voice and data. The catch: a cellular board will not dial out until the DKS account is provisioned, so start that ahead of time or you will hang a beautiful system that cannot call a single resident. Know which path your box is built for \u2014 check for the AG board and the 1891-010 LCD display \u2014 and have the line or the account ready.\n\nProgramming comes in two layers. The phone directory, up to 3000 numbers, is the PC job you do with DoorKing's Remote Account Manager (RAM) software, version 6.3.b or higher, over the modem and data link \u2014 you do not type 3000 numbers into a keypad. The behavior you set at the unit: relay (strike) times with * 03, then 1* or 2* for the relay, then two digits; talk time with * 08. The touch-tone open codes default to 9876 for Relay 1 and 5432 for Relay 2 (* 05 to change them), and the postal-lock switch fires Relay 1 by default (* 50 to reassign it). Know which relay drives which gate before you set open codes, or a resident buzzes open the wrong door.\n\nA word on the master code before you touch administration, because people assume wrong here: the 1834 has NO default master password. It ships with none. You set a unique four-digit code in the field by opening the cabinet, flipping the master-code switch ON, entering your four digits, pressing *, then switching it back OFF (section 3.2.1). Write it in the job file, because it cannot be recovered \u2014 forget it and your only move is to switch in a brand-new master code, and while your directory survives, the old code is gone for good. Every keypad command then works the same way: press the two-digit command number, enter the four-digit master code, wait for the beep; you close most sequences by pressing * and 0 together, and a mistake mid-entry clears with #.\n\nTurning over a unit \u2014 erase the old tenant and every name tied to it, then drop in the new resident \u2014 is a directory-code job, because each resident hangs off the directory code a visitor dials for them. Delete the old phone number first: * 01, master code, enter that unit's directory code, clear the number (section 3.5.6). Deleting the number does NOT remove the name, so delete the name too: * 65, master code, use the A and Z buttons to scroll to the old resident on the LCD, press * to erase it (section 3.5.7). The directory code is now empty. Program the new tenant onto the SAME code: * 01, master code, the directory code, then the seven-digit phone number (section 3.5.2 \u2014 run * 24 then * 41 first if the site needs a 10-digit area code), and add the name with * 66, master code, the directory code, then the resident's name one character at a time off the lettered keypad, * after each character (section 3.5.5). Same directory code, new resident: the callbox now rings the new tenant and shows the new name.\n\nTwo cautions sit right next to those. Do not reach for * 60 or * 95 to fix one unit. Neither is an erase command, which is exactly why they catch people: * 60 sets how many alternate area codes the panel allows (3.2.2) and * 95 re-programs the memory size (3.2.11). Both END on an ERASE MEM? 1=Y 0=N prompt, and answering 1 clears the whole directory \u2014 as does taking an existing panel from 255 area codes down to 10. And never change the directory-code length (* 20) on a live system to make room; reprogramming the length erases every phone number and name in the box and you are re-entering the whole building by hand. For anything past a single move-in, edit the directory in RAM on the PC and push it, rather than thumbing it in at the keypad.\n\nFor ride-through on a power blip, add a 12 VDC 0.7 Ah sealed lead-acid battery: positive to terminal 18 (BAT), negative to terminal 6 (COM). If you are swapping an older unit whose strip had BAT already landed, the site ran standby \u2014 carry it forward, and if you are backing up both the system and the aux / RS-232 side that is two batteries, and DKS does not supply them. When you need the details, the manual is P/N 1834-065 (Rev W, 11/24); the full wiring diagram is on page 21, and the terminal quick-guide is inside the front cover.\n\nThe 1834 grammar carries across the whole 1800 family. Walk up to a [[model:doorking-1800-series|smaller 1800-series board]] \u2014 an 1802, an 1808, a 1812 \u2014 and you'll find the same three things you just learned here: the master code that gets you into programming, the step that assigns an entry code or a resident's relay press to relay 1, 2, 3 or 4, and the postal/vendor and hold codes. What changes model to model is only capacity and relay count \u2014 the compact boards hold a few dozen codes and one relay, the big PC-programmable 1834/1837 hold thousands and talk to DKS's account manager over the cell or IP path. So the no-dial-tone and no-relay faults triage the same on all of them: a board that won't take programming is almost always the master-code switch left ON or a programming lockout, not a dead board, and a relay that won't fire is a strike-power or relay-contact problem before it's a board problem. Reach for the DoorKing manual for the exact keystroke order \u2014 it differs in the details per model and DoorKing publishes one per board; this is the family map, not a transcription of any one sequence.",relatedGuides:["gate-operators","access-control-systems","low-voltage-wiring","cable-noise-hum-rfi","dks-1834-vs-epd"],relatedModels:["dks-1834-80-series"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1834-pc-programmable-80-series/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1834-065-W-11-24_80_Series.pdf","https://www.doorking.com/","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/"],tags:["Ferrite","Relay","Standby","Fail-secure","Fail-safe","Holding current","Strike","RS-232"],aliases:["ferrite bead","noise suppression","EMI choke","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","battery backup","UPS","standby battery","how long on battery","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","transorb","TVS diode","surge suppressor","1N4004","flyback diode","spike suppression","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"fail-safe-fail-secure",title:"Fail-safe vs fail-secure: how power & fault defeat access control, and how to harden it",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Two words decide what an electrified door does the instant the power dies, and every access-control decision hangs on getting them right. Fail-safe (also called fail-open) means the lock UNLOCKS when it loses power. Fail-secure (fail-locked) means it stays LOCKED when it loses power. That is the whole vocabulary, and people mix them up constantly because 'fail-safe' sounds like the secure choice when it is the opposite \u2014 safe refers to people getting out, not to the door staying shut. A magnetic lock is fail-safe by its nature: it holds by electromagnet, so no power means no magnetism means the door is open, period. Electric strikes and electrified mortise and cylindrical locksets you can order either way, and that choice is the job.\n\nEgress code, not preference, drives which one you hang. On any door in the path people use to get out, they must ALWAYS be able to get out \u2014 free egress, no key, no thought, even in the dark with the power off. That is why a mag lock, which is fail-safe, is legal on an egress door only when it is wired to drop on the fire alarm, drop on loss of power, and release from a code-approved request-to-exit \u2014 a push bar, a motion sensor, a button that cuts the mag directly. A fail-secure electric strike on a leverset sidesteps most of that, because the electric side only controls the OUTSIDE trim; the inside lever always retracts the latch mechanically, so you walk out by turning the handle whether the strike has power or not. So the rule of thumb writes itself: fail-secure wherever mechanical egress already exists, fail-safe only where code forces it and only with the egress interlocks that make it legal.\n\nHere is where the choice stops being vocabulary and lands on a screw terminal. Most access controllers switch the lock through a Form C dry relay \u2014 a common, a normally-open contact, and a normally-closed contact \u2014 and which one you land on is decided entirely by the lock's failure mode. A fail-secure strike goes on normally-open: the relay energizes to pass power and unlock, so a dead panel leaves the door locked. A fail-safe mag lock goes on normally-closed: the relay energizes to hold power on the lock, and de-energizes to release, so a dead panel drops the door open. Wire one of them onto the other's contact and the door does the precise opposite of what you specified on every single cycle\n\n[[diagram:relay-fail-safe-secure|Fail-secure lands on NO, fail-safe on NC: the same Form C relay, opposite terminals]]\n\n \u2014 the strike sits unlocked all day, or the mag never locks at all. On an [[model:ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din|ICT Protege controller]] both contacts are right there on the same output, which is exactly why this gets crossed. The full terminal-by-terminal treatment, including the series fire and manual release chain a mag lock needs, is in \"Dry contacts, NO/NC, and wiring a lock to a relay\". And whichever contact you use, keep the code-required releases in the power path itself, not behind the relay logic, so they drop the lock regardless of what the panel is doing.\n\nSuppress the coil while you are in there. Every DC lock is an inductance, and cutting it kicks a spike back that pits and eventually welds relay contacts. The suppressor goes at the lock, not at the panel. For a strike, a reverse-biased diode across the coil is the standard answer and controllers commonly ship one in the box \u2014 ICT supplies a 1N4007 for exactly this. For a mag lock, use the MOV the manufacturer built in and do not add a diode: as the maglock guide covers, a diode recirculates the coil current on release and gives you a sticky lock that will not let go promptly. Diode on the strike, MOV on the mag.\n\nNow the security consequence, because it is the real version of every 'make it panic and the door opens' story. A fail-safe lock opens on loss of power. That is not a bug, it is the fire code \u2014 but it means anything that interrupts the power or faults the controller releases the door. Kill the supply, brown it out, pop the breaker it is on, or cut the feed if you can reach it, and a fail-safe mag lock has exactly zero holding force the instant it goes dark. A mag lock has no latch, no bolt, nothing mechanical \u2014 it is pure magnetism, so power is the only thing holding that door, and whoever controls the power controls the door.\n\nThe wiring is the rest of the attack surface. Request-to-exit and door-release run on dry contacts \u2014 bridge those two terminals and the strike or mag releases exactly as if someone pushed the button. If the REX wiring, the reader wiring, or the mag's own leads are reachable from the UNSECURED side \u2014 an exterior reader with its cable in the wall behind it, a mag mounted so the bond wiring is exposed outside, a controller sitting in an accessible drop ceiling on the public side \u2014 then someone bridges or cuts what they can reach and the door lets go. And the oldest one in the book: a REX motion sensor mounted over the inside of the door, tripped from outside by fishing a tool or a puff of warm air through the gap above the door, so the building politely unlocks itself for the intruder. None of this needs vinegar or a corroded intercom \u2014 the intercom is just the voice station and cannot command the lock anyway; it needs power interruption and reachable wiring, which is exactly what you defend.\n\nSo harden it in that order. First, hang the right lock for the door: use fail-secure strikes and electrified locksets on perimeter and non-egress openings, so a power cut locks them harder, not softer, and save fail-safe mag locks for the doors where egress code demands them. Second, protect the power and the wiring, because that is what the attack actually touches \u2014 run every lock, reader, REX, and controller conductor on the SECURE side of the door in conduit, put the power supply and the access controller inside the protected space rather than in the ceiling on the public side, and give a fail-safe lock a battery backup so a lazy power cut does not drop it (within the egress rules that still have to release it on a real alarm).\n\nThird, supervise and alarm it so a defeated door tells on itself: tamper switches on the enclosures and the reader, a monitored door-position switch, and a forced-door or door-held-open alarm, so a released or propped door reports instead of sitting open all night. Fourth, do REX right on the doors that matter \u2014 aim the motion sensor down and inward and seal the gap over the door so it cannot be tripped from outside, or better, use a real exit button or the panic bar's own switch instead of a motion sensor a coat hanger defeats. And fifth, treat the reader on the outside as untrusted: run modern encrypted credentials and OSDP so a cloned card or a shorted reader line buys the attacker nothing. The reader lives in hostile territory; it should hold no secret that opening it up reveals.\n\nLast, kill the myth so a student does not waste a service call on it: salt water and vinegar corroding an intercom to 'make it panic and open the door' is not a technique. Corrosion is slow, uncontrolled, and works over days, and the intercom cannot command the lock in the first place \u2014 it is the call box, not the controller. What that story is fumbling toward is real, and you now know its actual name: fail-safe locks drop on loss of power, and exposed power and REX wiring is what lets someone cause it. Defend the power feed and the wiring, put the right lock on each door, and the 'panic' never comes.",relatedGuides:["maglocks","access-control-bypass","access-control-systems","keypad-reader-servicing","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring"],relatedModels:["securitron-maglock","hes-1006","von-duprin-6211","ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din"],sources:["https://www.nfpa.org/","https://codes.iccsafe.org/","https://www.securityindustry.org/industry-standards/open-supervised-device-protocol/","https://www.securitron.com/"],tags:["Fail-safe","Fail-secure","REX","Form C","Controller","Relay","Reader","Magnetic lock"],aliases:["fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","request to exit","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","always exits","one motion egress","can't trap people","egress requirement","electromagnetic lock","600 lb magnet","Securitron","door holds shut","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","lockout","locked out","get in","emergency opening"]},{id:"cable-noise-hum-rfi",title:"Killing hum & RFI on low-voltage runs: ferrites, separation & grounding",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Half the callbacks on a telephone entry or an alarm are not the device \u2014 they are noise: a hum on the callbox voice, a buzz on an audio line, a data line that drops packets, all because a low-voltage run picked up interference it never should have. Noise gets onto a run three ways: it is induced from a line-voltage cable running parallel to yours, it rides in as radio-frequency hash from the environment, or it circulates as a ground loop between two pieces of gear grounded at different points. Learn to kill all three and the mystery buzz stops being a mystery.\n\n[[diagram:noise-hum-rfi-cures|Ferrite, separation and the ground loop... three noises, three different cures]]\n\nThe ferrite is your first reach for high-frequency hash. It is a soft-ferrite core \u2014 a bead, a snap-on clip, or a toroid you thread the wire through \u2014 and despite the name it is not a magnet; it works as a frequency-dependent resistor, letting your DC power and audio pass untouched while absorbing the radio-frequency noise and shedding it as a trace of heat. Clip it on the run close to the device you are protecting, right at the terminals. If one pass is not enough, loop the wire back through the core a second or third time \u2014 doubling the turns roughly quadruples the impedance at a given frequency, so a second pass is a big jump. For an inline PCB bead you size its current rating at least 20 percent over the circuit's draw, because the bead has its own resistance and a heavy load through an undersized bead just drops voltage; for the clip-on chokes you meet in the field on a power or comm cable, current is not the worry \u2014 placement and turns are.\n\nFerrites treat the symptom; separation prevents it. Keep low-voltage cable away from line voltage: run it in a different stud bay or a separate conduit, never share a hole or a raceway with 115V, and where a low-voltage run has to cross a power run, cross it at ninety degrees instead of laying the two parallel. Parallel-and-touching is what induces the 60-cycle hum in the first place; a foot of separation or a perpendicular crossing starves it. Code backs this up \u2014 most jurisdictions want low voltage kept a set distance, commonly 18 inches, off unshielded line voltage anyway.\n\nGround loops are the sneaky one. When two connected devices tie to ground at different points with a voltage difference between them, current flows on the shield or the common and shows up as a hum you cannot chase with a ferrite. The fix is single-point grounding: ground the cable shield at ONE end only, usually the head end, so there is no loop for current to circulate in. And ground the system itself to a real, low-impedance earth \u2014 the same discipline the DKS 1834 wants on its CGND \u2014 because a floating or high-resistance ground both invites noise and leaves the gear unprotected against a surge.\n\nFor runs that live in a noisy environment, start with the right cable: shielded twisted-pair, the twist rejecting induced noise and the shield draining RF, with the drain wire landed at one end. But before you chase noise at all, rule out power, because a lot of what looks like interference is really a sagging supply \u2014 a browning-out transformer or a long undersized run drops voltage and the gear misbehaves in ways that mimic noise. Measure the voltage at the device under load first. If the power is solid and the run is separated and grounded, then the ferrite is the finishing touch, not the whole fix.",relatedGuides:["low-voltage-wiring","dks-1834-telephone-entry","fail-safe-fail-secure","conduit-cctv"],relatedModels:["dks-1834-80-series"],sources:["https://www.analog.com/en/resources/app-notes/an-1368.html","https://passive-components.eu/how-to-use-a-ferrite-bead-in-your-design-to-reduce-emi/","https://www.flywing-tech.com/blog/ferrite-bead-guide/"],tags:["Ferrite"],aliases:["ferrite bead","noise suppression","EMI choke"]},{id:"intercom-triage",title:"Triaging a dead or misbehaving intercom / telephone entry",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A dead or flaky intercom is a chain, not a mystery: power feeds the board, the board talks over a phone or cellular path, the audio path carries the voice, a relay closes to release the lock, and the programming ties a resident to a directory code and a gate. Knowing which architecture you are on narrows the audio half before you start: a 2-wire analog system is half-duplex, so audio only travels one way at a time and a caller and resident talking over each other is normal rather than a fault, while 4-wire and IP systems are full-duplex and a one-way audio complaint there is a real defect. Triage means you walk that chain in order from the supply outward and prove each stage before you touch the next, and if the fault is a release relay that has died once already, fit the manufacturer's isolation relay between the intercom and the lock while you are in there... a lock coil kicks back every time it drops, and an intercom's small onboard relay is exactly what that spike destroys, which is why the same board comes back a second time, instead of swapping parts and hoping. Do it in sequence and you find the break in ten minutes; guess, and you replace a good board and still have the problem.\n\n[[img:photo-dks-1834|A DKS 1834 callbox on a gate... the unit most of these triage calls are standing in front of.]]\n\nPower first, always, because a starved board fakes every other symptom. Is it alive \u2014 LCD lit, keypad beeps, dial tone when you go off-hook? Dead means power: check the transformer output at the terminals under load (on a DKS 1834, both 16.5 VAC supplies present at 19/20 and Aux), a tripped breaker, a switched receptacle someone flipped, a blown fuse, or a dead standby battery. If it is alive but resets or faults at random, that is almost always undersized or single-transformer power, or a strike stealing the entry supply \u2014 the transformers feed the panel only, never the locks. Fix power before you believe anything else the box is telling you.\n\nComm path next: the board is alive but will not call a resident. Put a test set on the line and listen for dial tone \u2014 a dead POTS line, a wet or corroded pair, or a PBX that eats the number is your culprit, and if the unit runs on a cellular board instead of a landline, confirm the DKS account is actually provisioned, because an unprovisioned cellular board looks alive and simply never dials out. If it dials the wrong person or nobody, suspect the directory entry \u2014 wrong or stale number under that directory code \u2014 before you blame the hardware.\n\nAudio path: it dials, it connects, but the voice is wrong. No audio at all points at the mic and speaker wiring or the volume pots; one-way audio isolates to a single leg \u2014 caller cannot be heard is the mic side, cannot hear the resident is the speaker side. Hum, buzz, or a whine on the voice is noise, not a dead part: a missing ferrite, a run laid parallel to line voltage, or a ground loop \u2014 chase it as cable noise, not with a new board.\n\nRelay and lock: the resident presses the open key and now you listen. If you hear the relay click but the gate or door does not move, the fault is downstream of the intercom entirely \u2014 the relay is only a dry contact, so check the strike or mag's own separate supply, the wiring from the relay to the lock, and whether the lock is fail-safe or fail-secure and wired to match. If you hear NO click on the open key, it is the tone-open code or the programming \u2014 the wrong code, or a code mapped to the other relay. Prove the relay with a meter across its contacts before you condemn a lock, and prove the lock on its own supply before you condemn the relay.\n\nProgramming is the last bucket, and its tells are specific: the callbox reaches the wrong resident, opens the wrong gate, or cannot find a tenant who moved in last week. That is directory data and tone-code assignment \u2014 the phone number, the directory code, the name, and which relay each open code fires \u2014 not a wiring fault. On a PC-programmed unit you fix it in the management software and push it; at the keypad you edit the directory entry directly. The rule that saves the callback: verify each stage before you move downstream, and remember that no amount of abusing the callbox from the outside opens the door \u2014 it is power, comm, audio, relay, and programming, in that order, every time.\n\nThe chain above assumes copper to the callbox, and more and more there isn't any \u2014 the board hands its dial-out to a cellular gateway that carries the call over LTE/VoLTE, and the triage grows one link: board to gateway to cell network. When you meet a [[model:doorking-volte-gateway|DKS VoLTE gateway]], a black modem sitting in the DKS enclosure, a dead panel with a healthy board is more often the gateway than anything else. Check it in order. Signal first \u2014 the gateway has status and signal LEDs, and one parked at the back of a metal gatehouse on a single bar drops calls that read at the callbox like the board won't dial. The SIM next: open the enclosure door, read the SIM ID printed there, and confirm with the carrier or the DKS account that the line is active and paid, because a suspended SIM looks exactly like a dead board. Then the admin page \u2014 the gateway carries its own network name, password, and a 192.168.0.1 label; join its network from a phone or laptop, open 192.168.0.1, and read signal, registration, and dial settings straight off the gateway instead of guessing. The trap is swapping the board or the whole panel because the box is silent when the fix is an antenna moved out of the metal, a re-seated SIM, or a carrier re-provision. Prove which side is dead: run a test entry code and watch the relay \u2014 if it clicks and the strike releases but the call never connects, the fault is downstream in the gateway or the cell path, not the board.\n\nWhen the path is still copper, stop guessing whether it's the board or the line and listen. Clip a [[tool:linemans-butt-set|lineman's butt set]] onto the pair at the board's phone terminals and read what the handset tells you. On-hook (monitor), the polarity LED tells you the line is live and which conductor is tip and which is ring \u2014 a steady lit LED means voltage on the pair, no LED means the line is dead before you've touched the board. Go off-hook to seize the line: a healthy line answers with a steady dial tone. From there the failures have distinct sounds. LED lit but no dial tone means voltage without dial tone \u2014 a central-office or gateway problem, not the board. A fast busy or a your-call-cannot-be-completed recording the instant you seize points at provisioning or a dead account. A dial tone that breaks up when the board dials points at a marginal connection or a shared line the board is fighting for. And if the butt set gives you a clean dial tone and you can dial out by hand but the board can't, the board or its programming is the fault, not the line \u2014 you've cut the problem in half without a truck roll to the phone company. If you would rather read a number than judge a sound, meter the pair instead. Tip to ring on a live idle line sits at roughly 48 to 52 VDC on-hook, and collapses to about 6 to 12 VDC the instant anything goes off-hook and seizes it. A reading near zero on-hook means the line is dead upstream of the callbox and the intercom is not at fault... that one measurement ends the argument about whose problem it is before anybody opens a panel. On a Linear telephone-entry board the terminals are labelled the way the phone company thinks: line positive lands on TIP, line negative on RING, and there is a separate EARTH GROUND lug beside them that is a surge path, not a return. Land that lug on a real earth point... leaving it floating is how a nearby strike or a summer storm takes the board out. On a cellular install there's no dial tone to read; that silence is your cue the line is a gateway, and you move to the signal-and-SIM check above.",relatedGuides:["dks-1834-telephone-entry","fail-safe-fail-secure","cable-noise-hum-rfi","access-control-systems","low-voltage-wiring"],relatedModels:["dks-1834-80-series","linear-access-telephone-entry-panel","sentex-infinity-telephone-entry"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1834-pc-programmable-80-series/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1834-065-W-11-24_80_Series.pdf","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/"],tags:["Relay","Half-duplex","Ferrite","Standby","Dry contact","Strike","Fail-secure","Fail-safe"],aliases:["relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","ferrite bead","noise suppression","EMI choke","battery backup","UPS","how long on battery","volt free","form C","clean contact","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior"]},{id:"dks-1834-vs-epd",title:"DKS 1834 vs EPD-series: don't cross the code sets",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"DoorKing runs two telephone-entry families you will meet in the field, and their programming codes are NOT the same set. Grab the wrong manual and you send * 17 to a 1834 that wants * 03 for a strike time and nothing happens \u2014 or worse, you reach for a code that means one harmless thing on one family and wipes the whole directory on the other. Know which box you are standing at before you press a single star.\n\nThe 1834 is the newer, PC-first line \u2014 programmed mainly from a computer with Remote Account Manager, keypad commands as the backup \u2014 and its manual (P/N 1834-065) covers the 1834, 1814, 1816, 1835, 1837, and 1838. The EPD series \u2014 the 1802, 1808, 1810, and 1812 \u2014 is the older keypad-programmed family, with the scrolling message-and-name directory that lives on the 1802EPD. Read the part number on the board and on the manual cover; do not assume the codes carry across.\n\n[[diagram:dks-1834-vs-epd|1834 vs EPD-series: the red cells are codes that DIFFER between the two families \u2014 do not cross them. The '=' rows are the same on both.]]\n\nTwo things are the same on both, which is exactly what makes the trap easy to fall into: * 01 adds or deletes a phone number under a directory code, and * 20 sets the directory-code length (and wipes the whole directory the moment you touch it). Everything else in the common workflow moves. Relay strike time is * 03 on the 1834 and * 17 on the EPD. Talk time is * 08 versus * 05. The tone-open numbers live under * 05 on the 1834 and * 23 on the EPD \u2014 both default to 9876 for relay one and 5432 for relay two. And the EPD puts the tone-open sound toggle on * 08, the very code the 1834 uses for talk time. That overlap is how a 'quick fix' turns into an afternoon.\n\nSo the rule writes itself: never program a DoorKing from memory across families. Pull the manual for the exact part number on the board in front of you, and treat the wipe codes as live grenades \u2014 * 20 on either family, plus * 44 and * 45 (the 7- and 16-digit mode switches) on the EPD, erase the entire directory and leave you re-entering every resident by hand. When you only want to CHECK a setting, read it back rather than pressing a program code to 'see' what is in there \u2014 but the read command is not the same on both: the 1834 has * 06, which displays the phone number under a directory code you already know and cannot delete anything (3.5.9). * 25 is an EPD code and does not exist on an 1834 at all.",relatedGuides:["dks-1834-telephone-entry","intercom-triage","access-control-systems"],relatedModels:["dks-1834-80-series","dks-1802-epd"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1834-pc-programmable-80-series/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1834-065-W-11-24_80_Series.pdf"],tags:["Relay","Strike"],aliases:["dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"electric-strike-power-diagnosis",title:"Electric strike power diagnosis: the sound tells you the power",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A ten-month-old Grade 1 strike in your hand, dead \u2014 not defective, not worn out. A strike like the Adams Rite 7170 is rated 250,000 cycles and this one did not get near that. Somebody fed it the wrong KIND of power, and if you replace it with the same part number you will be back. The spine of the whole diagnosis is one line off the manufacturer sheet: DC continuous strikes are silent, AC intermittent strikes buzz on operation. So a strike that is supposed to be silent but buzzes is a DC coil being fed AC \u2014 and it is dying while you listen to it. AC reverses sixty times a second, a DC solenoid's field slams back and forth with it, the armature chatters instead of holding, and the coil cooks fighting itself. The chatter is the buzz; the heat is the failure.\n\n[[img:photo-field-electric-strike|A field electric strike in a buzz-in gate \u2014 ivory faceplate, ramped keeper. Brand unconfirmed: read the case label for voltage, duty, and make before you spec a replacement.]]\n\nStand at the opening and have someone press the button. A buzz means AC is present at the strike. A clack then silence means DC. A buzz on a strike spec'd DC is your fault condition \u2014 wrong power type. Nothing at all is no power, a dead coil, or an open circuit. Then read the case label for three fields: the model and dash number (which decodes to voltage and duty \u2014 see the Adams Rite 7160/7170 entry in the parts catalog), the duty checkbox (INTR or CONT), and the date code, because a young strike that is dead is a design problem, not a wear problem.\n\nThe diagnosis that matters is that three things must agree, not two: the transformer (AC or DC, what voltage, what VA), the strike solenoid (AC or DC, what voltage, what current), and the application (momentary buzz-in or hold-open, and how many strikes). Any disagreement is a callback with a delay fuse on it. The classic failure is the old multifamily buzz-in system, which is AC by design \u2014 doorbell transformer, tenant presses the button, the strike buzzes, the visitor pushes in, and the buzz is the system working. A strike dies, a tech drops in whatever is in the van, the replacement is DC on an AC transformer, it buzzes \u2014 so it seems normal, because this building has always buzzed \u2014 and it cooks itself in under a year. In a building that is supposed to buzz, a wrong strike is indistinguishable by ear from correct operation. You cannot diagnose it by sound in a buzz-in system; you must read the label.\n\n[[diagram:strike-three-agree|Transformer, strike solenoid, and application must all agree \u2014 AC/DC, voltage, and current/VA across all three.]]\n\nMeasure at the strike leads, under load, with the button held \u2014 at-rest voltage lies, reading fine and then collapsing the instant the coil pulls. Put the meter on the strike leads, not the transformer; set it for the type you expect and then check the other, because that is the whole question; hold the button; record voltage under load and at rest; and repeat at every strike on the system and compare. Voltage that collapses under load is an undersized transformer or too many coils on one supply. Fine near, low far, is voltage drop on the run \u2014 gauge or distance. AC present on a strike spec'd DC is a wrong solenoid or supply. Correct voltage but the strike will not pull is a mechanical bind, preload, or a dead coil. And a bench caution: a 9-volt battery will not actuate a 24-volt solenoid, so a strike that does not fire on a battery has proven nothing \u2014 test at rated voltage and type.\n\nThe load is real math, not an assumption. On the 7170, AC intermittent draws roughly four times DC continuous at the same voltage \u2014 24 volts is 0.74 amps AC against 0.17 amps DC. Two 24-volt AC strikes run about 1.5 amps, near 35 VA before you count anything else, and Adams Rite's own 4605 transformer is 40 VA \u2014 the manufacturer's implicit answer to what is enough. A doorbell transformer sized for a chime is not sized for two strike coils, and a transformer that carried one strike for fifteen years can sag the moment it has to carry two. Adding a second strike to an existing supply is a load calculation, not an assumption.\n\n[[diagram:strike-current-draw|7170 current draw: AC intermittent pulls roughly 4x DC continuous at the same voltage.]]\n\nThree fixes, picked deliberately. One, match the strike to the power: order it in the voltage and duty the building already has (on a 7170 that is digit two, a 4 for AC intermittent) \u2014 no new supply, but AC intermittent must never be used continuously, so buzz-in only, no hold-open ever. Two, match the power to the strike with a rectifier and a solenoid change: the Adams Rite 4603 rectifier converts AC to DC, installs in the low-voltage line between transformer and strike, rated 2 amps \u2014 silent, no duty-cycle limit, and one DC bus can standardize several mismatched openings. Three, rebuild the supply with a proper regulated DC power supply, sized and outdoor-rated where needed \u2014 most expensive, most headroom. The rectifier trap: rectified AC is not the same number as the AC you started with. Unfiltered it averages below the nominal AC; filtered with a capacitor it climbs toward the peak, roughly 1.4 times the RMS, so an 18 VAC source can land anywhere from about 16 to 25 volts DC. That spans won't-pull to overvoltage \u2014 measure the actual bus under load and confirm every coil sits inside its window.\n\nAnd the failure mode nobody documents: if you change a solenoid, the case label is now a lie. The plate still says 7170-510 \u2014 decoding to 24-volt DC \u2014 after you have put a different coil behind it, and it keeps saying that after you are gone. The next tech reads the plate, orders a -510, drops it in, and reintroduces the exact mismatch you just fixed. Your repair lasts as long as anyone remembers you made it. So tag it every time: Sharpie the actual voltage, duty, date, and initials on the case; note what changed and why on the work order; and photograph the junction and its conductor labels before anything moves. On the reference call for this manual the only documentation on the whole system was the last tech's masking tape \u2014 hand-lettered A, 5, 2, M, DOOR, 18V \u2014 on unboxed splices in a hole knocked through stucco. That tape was the single source of truth for the building.",relatedGuides:["fail-safe-fail-secure","maglocks","low-voltage-wiring","cable-noise-hum-rfi","intercom-triage"],relatedModels:["adams-rite-7160-7170"],sources:["https://www.adamsrite.com/","https://www.assaabloyopeningsolutions.us/en/brands/adams-rite","Field photo: own job photo (electric strike, brand unconfirmed), 2026"],tags:["Strike","Electric strike","Rectifier","Preload","Current draw","Keeper","Voltage drop","Opening"],aliases:["strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","HES","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","pressure on the latch","door under tension","strike binding","strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","wire gauge","long run","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"dks-1802-telephone-entry",title:"DoorKing 1802 / 1802EPD telephone entry: board, wiring & programming",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Half the \"broken\" 1802s are not broken \u2014 a jumper got left wrong during a feedback adjustment, or the master-code switch never went back OFF, and the unit has been long-toning every thirty seconds since. So open the lid and triage the board before you touch the phone line. The MASTER CODE switch must be OFF (left ON gives a long tone every ~30 seconds and abnormal behavior \u2014 and the LCD staying lit ~30 seconds after you flip it back is normal). The TONE jumper must be on TONE OFF (a constant tone in the speaker is this jumper left on TONE ON after a feedback adjust \u2014 the number-one callback on this unit). The RING jumper must be installed or the unit never answers inbound calls and you get no remote programming. The HF/HS jumper sits in one position only, never both \u2014 HF unless a handset kit (1807-012, surface 1802 only). And CLCK SENSE goes full counter-clockwise unless the site has rotary phones, because too high and loud noises trip the relay.\n\nThe 1802 comes two ways. The plain 1802 uses a printed directory and holds 600 numbers by default (1000 in the legacy 7-digit mode). The 1802EPD adds an electronic 16-character LCD directory with A/Z scroll and a CALL button, and holds 100. Both mount surface or flush and program from the front keypad or remotely by touch-tone. The circuit board is the 1862-010, Rev P or higher.\n\nPower is 16.5 VAC, 20 VA minimum from the supplied transformer or a UL-listed equal, landed on the 16AC terminals (13 and 14), with the ferrite clipped on those 16 VAC leads. Do not power any strike, mag, or light off it, and do not put it on a switched receptacle \u2014 polarity does not matter. CGND is earth ground only, not a low-voltage common, and grounding is a requirement, not a suggestion: 12 AWG minimum to a ground point within 10 feet \u2014 the panel, a buried metallic cold-water pipe, or a 10-foot rod driven into soil \u2014 never a post set in concrete, because a bad ground gives you hum, dropped audio, and flaky dialing. Standby is a 12 VDC 0.7 Ah SLA (DKS 1801-008) on BAT (positive) and COM (negative).\n\n[[diagram:dks-1802-terminals|DKS 1802 terminal map \u2014 Relay 1 order (10/11/12) is [VERIFY] per the board silkscreen; SW2 and Relay 2 are separate terminals upper-left.]]\n\nMatch wire to run. Power is 18 AWG to 100 feet, 16 AWG to 200 feet, 600-volt insulated. The phone pair must be twisted and completely isolated from ground \u2014 24 AWG to 800 feet, 22 AWG to 1600. Keep all entry wiring at least 18 inches off high voltage and never in the same conduit. DKS wants surge suppressors within 10 feet: 1877-010 on the phone line, 1878-010 on the low voltage, with their grounds within 3 feet of the ground source.\n\nConnection type sets what works. A POTS copper line does voice and remote programming both. DKS cellular (voice only, kit 1801-080, AT&T 4G LTE), DKS VoIP (kit 1815-568, Cat-5, 325 feet), or a third-party ATA carry voice \u2014 but cellular and VoIP may not pass clean DTMF, so off-site programming from a phone can fail and you program from the keypad. Ordering a POTS line: touch-tone / loop-start, ringer equivalence 0.0, jack RJ11C, and order it about two weeks ahead.\n\nThe relays are dry contacts, 30 volts and 3 amps max, and they provide no lock power \u2014 the lock gets its own UL-listed supply, wired in CL2-type cable. Land an electric strike on NO, a magnetic lock on NC, a gate operator on NO. And the gate-safety line is non-negotiable: on a vehicular gate the entry system mounts at least 6 feet from the gate and operator, or so a person cannot touch the gate and operate the callbox at once \u2014 if it is closer, stop and move it before you wire it. Never mount to a moving door or gate; vibration from slamming kills the board.\n\nAudio is three interacting adjustments \u2014 speaker, mic, and feedback \u2014 done on a live call (section 2.3.1): set SPK VOL while the resident talks, set MIC VOL until they say you are clear, then null feedback by moving the jumper to TONE ON, rotating the FEEDBACK pot for minimum tone, and \u2014 this is the callback \u2014 moving the jumper back to TONE OFF. Programming then follows one pattern: star, the command code, the four-digit master code, do the thing, and press 0 and star together to end (the same combo bails a botched entry). A short beep means a step took; a long beep ends the sequence. The master code itself is set with * 04 and the master-code switch ON, there is no factory default and no recovery \u2014 program a new one (all other data survives) and log it.\n\nThe command set is the EPD family's, NOT the 1834's \u2014 do not cross them. Relay strike time is * 17, talk time * 05, tone-open sound * 08, and tone-open numbers are * 23 (relay 1 defaults 9876, relay 2 5432, where the four digits are momentary / hold / release / hold-one-hour, and residents get only the momentary open). Phone numbers program and delete individually on * 01 under a directory code; names on the EPD are * 66 to program, * 65 to delete one, * 67 to delete all, keypad only. And three codes wipe the entire directory and cannot be undone \u2014 * 20 (directory length), * 44 (16-digit mode), * 45 (7-digit mode) \u2014 so never press them to check a setting; read a setting back with * 06 or * 25 instead. To add and then remove a test number: confirm the code length with * 06 (never * 20), then * 01 and master code, an unused directory code and star, the number as the line must dial it and star, then 0-and-star to end; clean up with * 01, master code, the directory code, star, and star again.\n\nTwo more that bite. The board is static-sensitive \u2014 discharge on a real ground before you pull it, and the memory chip installs only with power OFF, dimple to the top; upside-down is permanent damage and the pins bend easily. And the UL 294 tamper switch \u2014 a gravity NC contact under the microphone board, two white wires \u2014 must be connected to an alarm or the UL 294 certification is void, and depending on how the site is armed you may need to notify the authorities before you open the faceplate.",relatedGuides:["dks-1834-vs-epd","dks-1834-telephone-entry","intercom-triage","cable-noise-hum-rfi","fail-safe-fail-secure"],relatedModels:["dks-1802-epd"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/","https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1802-entry-system/"],tags:["UL 294","Relay","Magnetic lock","Ferrite","Tamper switch","Standby","Electric strike","Strike"],aliases:["dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","ferrite bead","noise suppression","EMI choke","cover switch","tamper alarm","case open","battery backup","UPS","standby battery","how long on battery","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"schlage-primus",title:"Schlage Primus & Primus XP: the sidebar, rekeying, servicing",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"A Schlage Primus is two locks in one plug. The top of the key runs a conventional Schlage pin-tumbler stack \u2014 the standard bitting you cut and rekey like any Schlage \u2014 and that alone is not enough to turn it. Underneath sits a second, independent system: a sidebar that blocks the plug until a row of finger pins, reading a set of angled cuts milled into the SIDE of the key blade (the side bitting), lift and rotate to line their gates up with the sidebar. Both must be satisfied at once \u2014 the top pins reach the shear line AND the finger pins position the sidebar. That is the whole security and key-control story: the standard bitting is the combination you manage, and the side bitting is the level, assigned and controlled by Schlage.\n\n[[diagram:primus-two-systems|The stack you control and the sidebar you do not, both of which must be satisfied at once]]\n\nTwo families. Primus (Classic) rides the older Schlage Classic keyway; Primus XP rides the Everest 29 keyway and inherits its check pin, so an XP key also has to lift the Everest undercut (see the Schlage Everest entry). The side bitting is issued in security levels \u2014 dealer, geographic, or single-end-user exclusivity \u2014 and the finger pins in the cylinder are set to that level. It is UL 437 listed, patented, and restricted: you cannot walk into a big-box store and get a Primus blank, and cutting the side bitting takes a Primus-authorized machine and the level's authorization.\n\nRekeying a Primus in the field is more familiar than it looks, because you only touch the top. The standard pin stack rekeys exactly like a normal Schlage \u2014 pull the plug, change the bottom pins to the new bitting with a Schlage pinning kit, reassemble \u2014 and you do NOT disturb the sidebar or the finger pins, because the side bitting is the level and stays put. Practically that means your new keys must be cut to the new standard bitting but the SAME side bitting and level as the system, so you order or cut Primus keys on that level. Change the level itself \u2014 the finger pins and side bitting \u2014 only through the Primus-authorized process; that is not a bench rekey.\n\nServicing splits along the same line. Top-side trouble \u2014 a key that will not reach the shear line, a bound pin, a rekey gone wrong \u2014 is ordinary pin-tumbler work. Sidebar trouble is Primus-specific: a key with correct standard cuts that still will not turn is almost always the side \u2014 a worn or wrong-level side bitting, a gummy or bound sidebar, or a stuck finger pin. Clean and lightly lube the sidebar channel and the finger-pin chamber, confirm the key is the right level for that cylinder, and check the side cuts are not worn round, because a Primus key dragged on a ring for years loses its side bitting before its top cuts show wear. Never force it: a Primus that binds on the sidebar is telling you level or wear, not that it wants more torque.\n\nOne discipline on numbers. The standard pin stack uses Schlage's normal depth-and-space \u2014 Classic on Primus, Everest on XP \u2014 but treat exact depth increments, root depths, and especially the finger-pin heights, side-cut angles, and level codes as data to confirm against a Schlage/Primus technical source before you cut. The side-bitting specs in particular are restricted and are not reproduced here. Rekey the top from the standard bitting; source the side from Schlage.",relatedGuides:["schlage-everest","high-security-keyways","t-master-keying","keyway-blank-id"],relatedModels:["schlage-primus","schlage-everest"],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/primus-security-upgrade.html","https://us.allegion.com/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Schlage_Primus"],tags:["Bitting","Shear line","Pinning kit","Plug","Keyway","Cylinder"],aliases:["cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","pin tray","LAB kit","pin wedge","universal pin kit","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"schlage-everest",title:"Schlage Everest: the check pin, rekeying, servicing",module:"Keys, Cylinders & Keying",body:"Everest is Schlage's patented keyway family, and its trick is a check pin \u2014 a small spring-loaded pin sitting in the bottom of the keyway that blocks the plug from turning until it is lifted. An Everest key carries an undercut, a ramped groove milled along the bottom of the blade, that lifts the check pin as the key seats. A non-Everest blank, even one cut to the correct bitting, cannot raise the check pin and will not turn \u2014 and usually will not fully enter. The point to hold onto: the check pin is a KEYWAY and patent feature, not part of the combination. It is there for key control, not to add a second combination the way a Primus sidebar does.\n\n[[img:schlage-everest-checkpin|The Everest check pin, and why a standard key blank will not enter the keyway.]]\n\nEverest comes in generations. Everest B was the original patented family; Everest 29 is the current generation, with a fresh patent term and a revised keyway and undercut (the number references the keyway section). Everest also underpins Primus XP, which stacks the Primus sidebar on top of the Everest check pin. All of it is restricted \u2014 Everest blanks are controlled, so like Primus you are not buying them at a big-box counter.\n\nRekeying Everest is ordinary Schlage pinning, because the check pin is not a combination element: you change the bottom pins to the new bitting with a standard Schlage kit and leave the check pin alone. The one thing that bites is the blank \u2014 you must use an Everest blank with the correct undercut and keyway section, or the new key will not lift the check pin and will not turn no matter how perfectly the bitting is cut. Get the right Everest blank, and the right generation (B versus 29), before you cut.\n\nService is mostly keyway and check pin. A key that is cut right but will not enter or turn is a keyway or undercut mismatch \u2014 a wrong-generation blank, a worn undercut, or a check pin stuck up or down. The check pin can gum or corrode, so clean and lightly lube the keyway and confirm the pin springs freely. And a worn Everest key loses its undercut ramp over years on a ring, so it stops lifting the check pin reliably \u2014 cut a fresh one on the right blank before you chase a cylinder problem that is really a tired key.\n\nOn numbers: Everest 29 uses a depth-and-space shifted from Classic, so do not assume a Classic chart will cut an Everest key. Confirm the Everest depth-and-space and the correct undercut and keyway section against a Schlage source before cutting. Exact depths, the undercut dimension, and the keyway sections are restricted, patented data and are not reproduced here.",relatedGuides:["schlage-primus","high-security-keyways","keyway-blank-id","t-master-keying"],relatedModels:["schlage-everest","schlage-primus"],sources:["https://commercial.schlage.com/en/products/key-systems/everest-29-keyways.html","https://us.allegion.com/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Schlage_Everest"],tags:["Keyway","Key blank","Bitting","Plug","Cylinder"],aliases:["KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Ilco","JMA","Silca","blank won't fit","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","Kwikset","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder"]},{id:"medeco-picking",title:"Picking Medeco: why the pick set loses, and what you do instead",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Let me set the expectation before you waste an afternoon: Medeco is the lock that was designed, on purpose, to beat the tool in your hand. Every other cylinder in this course has one gate... lift the pins to the shear line and the plug turns. Medeco has two gates that have nothing to do with each other, and you have to satisfy both at the same instant. That is the whole story of why single-pin picking, the thing you are good at, stops working here. This guide is honest about it: you will learn why it resists, what a real picking attempt actually involves, and, more important on a paid call, what you do instead.\n\nTHE TWO-GATE PROBLEM\n\n[[img:medeco-three-coordinate|Depth sets the shear line; angle and fore/aft set the sidebar]]\n\nA Medeco bitting carries three coordinates at every pin position, not one. Depth is how far the pin lifts... that is the ordinary shear line, the gate you already know. Angle is how far the pin rotates, left, center, or right. Fore/aft is a tiny offset of the cut from nominal spacing. Angle and fore/aft together drive a second, independent locking element: the sidebar, a bar down the side of the plug whose fingers sit in a groove in the housing and jam the plug solid until every pin is rotated so its gate lines up. So the plug turns only when the shear line is clean AND the sidebar has dropped. Lift all six pins perfectly to height and the plug still will not move a hair, because the sidebar never saw its channel. That is not a worn-out lock... that is the design working.\n\nWHY YOUR PICK CAN'T DO IT\n\nA standard [[tool:pick-set|pick]] lifts. That is all it does... it pushes a pin up to a height and the [[tool:tension-wrench|tension wrench]] catches it on a ledge at the shear line. Medeco's pins have a chisel-pointed tip, and they have to be rotated to a specific angle by that tip riding the angled cut of the key. Your pick has no way to rotate a pin. You can set every pin to the correct height and you have solved exactly half of each pin... the plug is still locked by the sidebar because not one pin is at its angle. To pick a Medeco you must lift each pin to depth AND twist it to angle AND hold both while you hunt for the next one. A flat pick blade cannot twist a pin.\n\nAND IT FIGHTS BACK WHILE YOU TRY\n\nMedeco doesn't just fail to help you... it actively feeds you lies. The sidebar is spring-loaded against the pins, so the moment you put tension on, the bar leans on them hunting for gates and hands you false feedback: you feel 'sets' that are the sidebar catching a false gate, not the real one. The pins themselves carry the usual security profiles... mushroom and serrated drivers that false-set your shear line... layered on top of the angle problem. And the plug and housing are loaded with hardened steel anti-drill inserts, so the drill isn't an easy out either. In the genuine sport-picking attempts you will see online, a skilled picker uses custom rotation picks... tools with a tip that can both lift and turn a pin... and still spends serious time, on a bench, on a lock that isn't fighting a decade of pocket grime. That is a demonstration, not a service method.\n\nM3 AND M4 CLOSE THE DOOR FURTHER\n\n[[img:medeco-m4-exploded|M4 stacks side pins, a lift pin, and a factory shuttle pin on top of the sidebar]]\n\nEverything above is the classic Biaxial. Modern Medeco stacks more gates on. Later ARX-style pins rotate freely... they spin under your tool, so there is no fixed reference to hold an angle against. M3 adds a keyway slider driven by a lug on the blade; if the slider isn't pushed you never even reach the pins. M4 replaces the slider with a set of side pins... three finger pins that read factory-cut secondary bitting on the blade, and a lift pin driven by a movable shuttle pin permanently retained in the key. Those are whole extra locking elements with no interface a pick can reach: the finger pins read cuts that only exist on a factory blade, and the shuttle pin is a moving steel part you cannot fake with a tool. This is why the '3D-printed Medeco key' videos work in theory and open nothing in practice... print the geometry, you still have no shuttle pin, and the sidebar never drops.\n\nWHAT YOU ACTUALLY DO ON THE CALL\n\nSo you are standing at a door with a Medeco and a customer who wants in. You are not going to pick it open. Here is the real decision tree.\n\nFirst, and before anything mechanical: key control. Medeco is a restricted, patented system. The lock exists to stop unauthorized entry, so your job is to prove authorization first. Are you on the signature card for that system? If this is a lockout, is this person actually entitled to the space? Get that right or the mechanical question is moot... this is a be-on-the-card-first trade, covered in the restricted-keyway guide.\n\nIf you are authorized and you hold any legitimate key, or the code and the card, the clean answer is to originate a key, not to defeat the lock. The parts-catalog entry for Medeco walks the three-coordinate cutting reality... angle comes from the machine, fore/aft is a pin-selection fact... and that is the professional path back into the door.\n\nIf you genuinely need it open now and picking is off the table, this becomes a destructive-entry decision like any other high-security cylinder: you are drilling the sidebar or the plug, then replacing or repinning the cylinder afterward. Respect the anti-drill inserts... this is a slow, carbide, know-where-the-sidebar-lives job, not a quick pilot hole, and on many calls the honest quote is 'we replace the cylinder,' because the customer is buying a new Medeco either way once you drill it. A [[tool:borescope|borescope]] to read the plug and a plan to re-key or swap the cylinder afterward beats an hour of pretending your [[tool:pick-set|picks]] will suddenly rotate a pin.\n\nIMPRESSIONING? BARELY.\n\nPeople ask about impressioning a Medeco. In principle you impression the depths like any lock, but you cannot impression an angle or a fore/aft with a file and a blank... those are the sidebar's whole job, and they are set by pin selection at the bench, not read off a mark. So impressioning gets you, at best, a key that satisfies the shear line and dies on the sidebar. It is not a Medeco entry technique. File it next to picking: understand it, don't sell it.\n\nBOTTOM LINE\n\nMedeco is worth understanding precisely because it is the counterexample to everything else you pick. Two independent gates, a pin you must rotate as well as lift, hardened everything, and on M3/M4 a stack of extra elements with no interface for a tool. The professional skill here is not a secret picking technique... it is recognizing the cylinder on sight, confirming key control, and choosing decode, originate, or documented destructive entry over burning an hour on a lock built to make that hour fail.",relatedGuides:["high-security-keyways","high-security-residential","pick-types-technique","keyway-blank-id"],relatedModels:["medeco-high-security","schlage-primus"],sources:["https://www.medeco.com/","https://lockwiki.com/index.php/Medeco","https://www.locksmithledger.com/","https://lsamichigan.org/Tech/KeyBittingSpecifications-TylerJThomas.pdf"],tags:["Shear line","Impressioning","Tension wrench","Plug","Bitting","Cylinder","Keyway"],aliases:["binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","turning tool","tension tool","TOK","BOK","pick tension","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","cut depths","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank"]},{id:"medeco-lishi-decode",title:"Can you Lishi a Medeco? No... and how you actually decode one",module:"Opening Locks: Picking, Bypass & Lockouts",body:"Someone will tell you to 'just throw a Lishi on it,' so let me kill that on a Medeco before you buy the wrong tool. There is no Original Lishi... no Lishi of any kind... for a Medeco Biaxial, M3, or M4 high-security cylinder. Search it and you will find a 'Lishi M4,' get excited, and discover it is the Master Lock M4 padlock tool... a completely different lock that happens to share the letter-number. Original Lishi's residential and commercial range is keyway tools for cars and standard pin-tumbler; Medeco is simply not in it, and not by accident.\n\n[[img:medeco-m4-exploded|The M4 exploded... the sidebar and elevated pins that make a Medeco a decode job rather than a pick.]]\n\nWHY NO LISHI CAN READ A MEDECO\n\nA Lishi 2-in-1 does one clever thing: it traps a single pin or wafer at the keyway mouth and indexes its DEPTH against marks on the tool head, position by position, and hands you the bitting. That is a one-coordinate read... height. It works because a normal lock hides one secret per position: how far the pin lifts to the shear line.\n\nMedeco hides three secrets per position, and only one of them is depth. Angle is which way the chisel-tipped pin is rotated, left, center, or right. Fore/aft is a tiny offset of the cut from nominal spacing. Angle and fore/aft are read by nothing at the keyway mouth... they are the sidebar's business, down the side of the plug, and a Lishi has no finger that reaches them and no scale that measures a rotation. Worse, the sidebar jams the plug regardless of depth, so the self-impressioning feedback a Lishi leans on... bind, set, catch... never happens. You could index all six depths perfectly and you would still hold one-third of the bitting and none of the sidebar. It is the same two-gate wall that stops a pick, covered in the Medeco picking manual: satisfy the shear line AND the sidebar, or the plug does not move.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY DECODES A MEDECO\n\nYou decode a Medeco three ways, and the fast one is paperwork.\n\nFrom the code, if you are on the card. Medeco is a registered, restricted system... the distributor and end-user hold the bitting on record. If you are authorized on the signature card, you pull the code and originate the key from it. No decoding, no disassembly, and it is the cleanest path back into the door. This is a be-on-the-card-first trade... prove authority, then cut.\n\nFrom the cylinder, on the bench... the honest hands-on decode. Pull the cylinder, cap it, and push the plug out on a [[tool:plug-follower|plug follower]] so the top pins and springs stay captured. Now read all three coordinates off each bottom pin with [[tool:pinning-tweezers|pinning tweezers]]: its depth (measure it), its angle (which way the chisel tip points, L/C/R), and its fore/aft (the pin's offset, a pin-selection fact, not a filed cut). Combine depth-plus-angle-plus-fore/aft per position and you have the true bitting. This needs the cylinder in your hand and the authorization to have it there.\n\nReading depth, mind the caliper trap. Medeco bottom pins measure about .012 inch SHORTER than their listed reference length, and cut depths read about .008 inch SHALLOWER on standard calipers. So a chart's 'reference depth' and a measured 'root depth' differ by roughly .008 inch... know which convention your number is in, use a [[tool:key-gauge|pin gauge]] you trust, and confirm against a pin-kit lid before you commit a critical cut. The Medeco entry in the parts catalog carries the depth-and-space and the pin lengths.\n\nImpression? Barely, and not really. You can impression the depths like any lock, but you cannot impression an angle or a fore/aft with a file... those are set by pin selection at the bench, not read off a mark. Impressioning gets you a key that clears the shear line and dies on the sidebar. Understand it, do not sell it.\n\nBOTTOM LINE\n\nIf a supplier offers you 'a Lishi for Medeco,' they mean the Master Lock M4 padlock tool, or they are wrong. There is no keyway-mouth shortcut into a Medeco, because the security lives in the angle and the fore/aft, and no depth-reading decoder can see them. You get a Medeco open and keyed the professional way: from the code if you are on the card, or by pulling the cylinder and reading all three coordinates on the bench. The skill is knowing that, and not burning an hour proving a tool wrong.",relatedGuides:["medeco-picking","lishi","high-security-keyways","impressioning"],relatedModels:["medeco-high-security"],sources:["https://www.originallishi.com/2-in-1tools/","https://www.clksupplies.com/products/original-lishi-2-1-pick-decoder-for-master-lock-m4-1092v","https://www.medeco.com/","https://lsamichigan.org/Tech/KeyBittingSpecifications-TylerJThomas.pdf"],tags:["Lishi","Shear line","Bitting","Impressioning","Plug follower","Plug","Keyway","Cylinder"],aliases:["decoder pick","Lishi tool","pick and decode","binding","picks open","won't set","line of separation","key cuts","depth code","bitting list","file and fit","make a key by hand","blank marks","no code key","rekey tool","pins fell out","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","KW1","SC1","KW11","SC4","WR5","keyway restriction","wrong blank","Kwikset","Schlage","Weiser","Baldwin","rekey","re-key","keyed alike","lock barrel","key cylinder","read the cuts","decode the lock","reading tool"]},{id:"ses-tec1-triage",title:"SES TEC1 field triage: fobs, programming & the site-code trap",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"The TEC1 is SES's basic telephone entry, and this is the triage order for one that has stopped behaving. It covers TEC101, TEC1, TEC1C, TEC1CA, TEC1M and TEC1MCA at firmware 1.XX. Two manuals sit behind it: the TEC1 user/installer guide (600TEC1, Rev 10/08) and the TEC Series v3.2X manual (600TECMANUAL, Mar 2015). They do not agree on everything, so every fact below carries a provenance tag. [T1 pN] means the TEC1 guide documents it at that page and you can trust it on a 1.XX unit. [T32 pN] means only the 3.2X manual documents it \u2014 the hardware and menu structure match, but verify on the panel before you rely on it. [INF] means it is an inference, not stated in any manual: treat it as a lead, not a fact. SES tech support is 1-800-342-5737.\n\n[[img:photo-ses-telephone-entry-panel|An SES TEC1 panel in the field... the unit behind the fob and site-code traps below.]]\n\nQuick access, the things you need before you open anything. Enter programming by pressing star and 0 together. At the password prompt, enter the password then star. The factory default password is 777777. Star and 0 together also exits from anywhere. Backspace during entry is 0 and pound together. You have 6 seconds to start entering the password after the prompt appears, and 60 seconds of no keypress during programming drops you back to normal operation. [T32 p27]\n\nThe main menu is nine items: 1 Codes/Names, 2 Entrance Control, 3 System Parameters, 4 Comm Setup, 5 Clock Setup, 6 Access Control, 7 Buffer Info, 8 Clear Data, 9 Display Data.\n\nTRIAGE: THE FOB SYSTEM ISN'T THERE\n\nThe symptom is a panel behaving as if it has no card capability at all \u2014 no CARD field when you edit a resident, and fobs that do nothing. This is a solved case, so work the most likely cause first.\n\nSomebody ran Clear Data then Set Parameters (8 then 1). That restores Comm Setup, System Parameters and Entrance Control to factory, which sets the site code back to 000. A site code of 000 disables card reading and hides the CARD field from the programming menu, so the panel looks like it never had the feature. [T32 p37, p46]\n\nThe tell is that directory names and codes are still intact, because Set Parameters does not touch the directory. Directory alive plus fob system gone equals this fault. [INF] The card numbers on the resident records survive the reset too, so restoring the correct site code typically brings the system back with no re-enrollment. [INF]\n\nDiagnostic order, in sequence:\n\n- Check the site code first. Star and 0, password, then 4 for Comm Setup, then 5. If it reads 000, that is your fault. [T32 p37] On a 1.XX unit the TEC1 guide does not document a site code item, so if 5 lands somewhere else, back out with star and 0 and read what Comm Setup actually lists.\n- Confirm the model from the label inside the door. A TEC1 or TEC1C with no \"CA\" has no factory card capability at all \u2014 somebody may have hung a reader on a panel that cannot use it.\n- Card diagnostics. Present a fob with card diag enabled and the site code data prints to the LCD. Be careful here: on the 3.2X board this is DIP switch 5, but the TEC1 manual lists switches 4, 5 and 6 as reserved and warns the unit will not work properly if S1 is set wrong. [T1 p6] Do not flip switch 5 on a 1.XX board without confirming with SES first.\n- Check the Wiegand module is present and seated. Open it up, confirm the module is on its header and the reader is landed: black common, green D0, white D1, red power, brown LED. [T1 p8] A loose or absent module presents exactly as \"no fob system\".\n- Measure power at the reader, not at the board. Red to black at the reader itself, 5 V or 12 V per the voltage-select jumper on the module. Prox and fob readers are usually 12 V, Wiegand swipe and insert usually 5 V. The wrong jumper gives you a reader that lights up but never decodes. [T32 p11]\n- Rule out the modem. An active 56K modem session cuts power to the card reader module, so cards go dead until it disconnects. Toggle the panel power switch and retest. [T1 p21, T32 p21]\n\nRecovering a site code nobody wrote down: start with the fob packaging or the original card order paperwork and ask the manager. Then the property's Selcom database, if the original installer left one. Then SES tech support on 1-800-342-5737 \u2014 give them the serial off the inside-door label and they can often pull the original order, which is the fastest real path. Card diagnostics is the last resort, with the DIP switch warning above.\n\nThere is a stopgap. Site code 255 makes the panel ignore site code entirely, so fobs work \u2014 but so does any fob with a coincidentally matching card number. Use it to prove the reader is alive, never as a leave-behind, and flag it to the manager if you use it. [T32 p13, p37]\n\nTRIAGE: THE OTHER COMMON CALLS\n\n- Panel appears dead or erratic. Check the S1 DIP bank: 1 is program enable, 2 is speaker enable, 3 is always OFF, 7 is always ON, 8 is reset. A wrong 3 or 7 is the classic. [T1 p6]\n- Power LED flashing means call for service \u2014 that is a board fault. [T1 p6]\n- Display unreadable. Contrast pot is on the display board, inside the door above the keypad. [T1 p6]\n- Audio poor or feeding back. Mic sensitivity and speaker volume are separate pots on the main board and they interact with each other, so adjust them together rather than one at a time. [T1 p6, T32 p8]\n- Fob reads but the door will not open. If Access Groups are enabled (1 then 8), every code must have a group assigned or it fails. Group 0 is unlimited 24/7. [T32 p42]\n- A name is missing from the directory. A blank phone number hides the name, and a leading pause (pound) in the phone number is an intentional Hide Name. [T1 p6, T32 p31]\n- Gate opens intermittently or sticks open. Solid-state gate controllers react to the SES surge protection \u2014 add an isolating external relay, the OPTKFRMC. [T32 p4]\n- Directory codes corrupted. Run 8 then 6, Clean Codes. It does not destroy good records. [T1 p13]\n- Recurring surge damage. Verify the earth ground: #16 stranded or larger, under 50 ft, to a cold water pipe or ground rod. Pedestal mounts need their own rod, #14, under 25 ft. [T1 p3, T32 p4]\n\nPROGRAMMING REFERENCE\n\nTo add or edit a resident: 1, then 1, then code and star, name and star, phone and star, PIN and star, CARD and star. It loops back to CODE= for the next entry. [T1 p7] The record structure is CODE#, NAME, PHONE, PIN, CARD, ACCESS GROUP. [T32 p30] Code is 1 to 6 digits. Name is up to 14 characters, all caps, last name first. Phone is up to 14 digits with no spaces or dashes. PIN is 1 to 6 digits, default length 4. Card is 5 digits, one per code.\n\nName entry is multi-tap: 1 gives comma, period, ampersand and dash; 2 is ABC; 3 is DEF; 4 is GHI; 5 is JKL; 6 is MNO; 7 is PQRS; 8 is TUV; 9 is WXYZ. Star enters, pound is space or next character, 0 followed by a number gives that digit, and 0 with pound is backspace. Press pound to move past the last letter before pressing star, or the last letter is not stored. [T32 p31]\n\nTo delete a whole code, enter the code, pound, star \u2014 that erases name, phone, PIN and card. [T1 p8] To delete one field, star to that field then 0 then star. [T32 p30] To change only the phone number, 1, 1, code, two stars to skip the name, the new number, star, star. [T1 p9]\n\nPasswords are one credential in three tiers, and this is the part that bites. Changing the program password changes all three, and whoever gets the first three digits can edit the directory. [T32 p37] The program tier is all 6 digits and gets everything. The manager tier is the first 3 digits and gets Codes/Names only. The control tier is the first 2 digits and gets remote touch-tone relay control. Change it at 4 for Comm Setup, then 3, then the new password, then star star. [T1 p14]\n\nTwo warnings on passwords. The front keypad supports digits only, but over a terminal the password can be alphanumeric \u2014 so if somebody sets one with letters in it, keypad programming is locked out entirely and recovery is a terminal or another factory reset. [T32 p37] And write the new password on the work order and hand it to the manager before you leave, because the only recovery is Set Parameters, which zeroes the site code and lands you back in the fob triage above.\n\nEntrance control is 2, then the entrance, then the function: 1 unlock time (1-99 seconds, default 10), 2 unlock tone (0-9, default 6), 3 auto group (0-7, default 0), 4 latch tone (default 0). [T32 p28] Two stars to store. The rule of thumb for unlock time is 3 seconds plus 2 seconds for every step from the panel to the door. [T32 p32] If the unlock tone and the latch tone are the same number, the relay will neither latch nor time out \u2014 it blips and drops. [T32 p33] And Auto Group overrides all other latch control. [T32 p33]\n\nSystem Parameters is 3: 1 talk time (1-99 minutes, default 1), 2 alarm time, 3 code length (1-6, default 3), 4 PIN length (0-6, default 4, where 0 disables PINs), 5 back beep, 6 tone dial (1 is touch-tone), 7 ring count (default 3, where 0 means it never answers), 8 strike out (0 is unlimited PIN guesses), 9 title page. [T32 p28]\n\nComm Setup is 4: 1 terminal type, 2 RS-232 baud (0 is 300 through 5 is 9600 default and 6 is 19200), 3 program password, 4 PIN on entrance, 5 site code, 6 directory on/off, 7 keypad on/off. [T32 p29]\n\nClock is 5: 1 set clock, 2 time zones (0-7), 3 holidays (0-8). The clock entry order is minute, hour, day of week, date, month, year \u2014 and day of week is 1 for Sunday through 7 for Saturday, not the day of the month. [T1 p15] Once clock programming starts, every field must be completed or nothing takes effect. [T32 p38]\n\nAccess Control is 6: 1 set groups, 2 set levels. Plan it in reverse of the menu order \u2014 time zones first, then access levels (a time zone plus entrances), then access groups (combinations of levels). Group 0 is predefined as unlimited, 24/7/365. One time zone per access level, and at least one entrance must be selected or the level does nothing. [T32 p42-43]\n\nClear Data is 8, and it is destructive. 1 is Set Parameters, which resets Entrance Control, System Parameters and Comm Setup to factory \u2014 it zeroes the site code and leaves the directory intact. [T32 p46] 2 clears time zones, 3 clears holidays, 4 clears access levels, 5 clears access groups, and 6 is clean codes, which is safe and repairs corrupted records.\n\nHARDWARE\n\nThe TEC1 terminal strip runs 1 and 2 for the phone line, 3 and 4 for the SES transformer (16.5 VAC 20 VA), 5 common for battery and REX, 6 battery positive, 7 and 8 for N/O request to exit on doors 1 and 2, 9 and 10 for the door 1 relay, and 11 and 12 for the door 2 relay. [T1 p4] The relays are rated 24 V AC or DC at 3 A maximum and ship N/O from the factory; both jumpers per relay must be moved together to select N/C. [T1 p5] TEC series boards use TBS-13/14 for entrance 1 and TBS-15/16 for entrance 2 with jumpers J201 and J202 \u2014 different numbering, same idea. [T32 p7]\n\nPower and ground: the supplied transformer is 16.5 VAC 20 VA, 16 ga twisted, 50 ft maximum, or 14 ga up to 100 ft. Never run the AC transformer and a DC supply at the same time. Earth ground is mandatory \u2014 #16 stranded or larger, under 50 ft \u2014 and the surge protection degrades without it. [T1 p3, T32 p4]\n\nCard reader wiring is black common, brown LED, green D0, white D1, red power. [T1 p8] For distance: Wiegand swipe or insert on Belden 9941 gives 500 ft; prox, RF or infrared on Belden 9941 gives 100 ft; prox or RF on Belden 83656 gives 500 ft. [T32 p11] The voltage select jumper on the module picks 5 V (most Wiegand swipe and insert) or 12 V (most prox, RF and infrared). The on-board supply gives 125 mA per reader \u2014 above that you need an external PSU. And reader-to-relay mapping is fixed: reader 1 always drives relay 1, reader 2 always drives relay 2, and it is not remappable. [T32 p11]\n\nShield grounding has to be done exactly right or you void the warranty and defeat the surge protection. [T1 p9] Strip 3 inches of outer jacket, poke through the braid, spread it and peel the conductors out without nicking the insulation. Wire-nut an insulated #18 stranded to the shield, long enough to reach the ground lug in the back box. Tape the shield and the nut so it cannot short to the PC board. The shield lands on the panel ground stud only \u2014 at the reader end, cut the shield off and tape it. Many reader makers tie shield to power common, and that defeats the SES on-board surge protection.\n\nOn Wiegand format, SES ships 30-bit. Enter 300 as a site code for 30-bit or 260 for 26-bit; neither changes the actual stored site code. [T32 p13]\n\nMounting is 52 inches AFF to centre as standard, 42 to 48 for drive-up, and 40 to 46 for handset units to meet ADA. The flush cutout is 8 1/2 inches wide by 11 3/4 high, and you leave 1/2 inch behind the door so it can open. Seal all penetrations with RTV. [T1 p3] The unit is 8 1/2 W by 11 3/4 H by 3 1/2 D, about 11 lbs, rated 0 to 158 F and 5 to 95% RH non-condensing, with non-volatile memory, and it detects a 50 ms burst tone. [T1 p16]\n\nAccessories are the OPTEC1W2-1 card reader module, OPTKSERIAL serial port (9600 8-N-1, 100 ft max), OPTKMODEM56K modem, OPTKNIC Ethernet, and OPTKMUI phone line sharing for up to four TEC1s. [T1 p7-8] Turn the power switch off before installing or removing any accessory. [T1 p7]\n\nREMOTE CONTROL FROM A PHONE\n\nCall the panel and it answers after the programmed ring count. Wait for the tone, then enter the control password \u2014 the first two digits of the program password \u2014 digit by digit, waiting for the acknowledgment beep after each, then star. Two beeps confirms. From there the entrance open tone, 6 by default, fires relay 1, and 0 hangs up. The panel does not hang up on its own. [T32 p26] A long error tone means a bad password and the panel hangs up.\n\nTERMINAL AND PC PROGRAMMING\n\n9600 baud, 8-N-1, VT-100B emulation, or set terminal type to dumb. Press Esc once and you have 10 seconds to start the password; 60 seconds idle disconnects. [T32 p18-19] The serial cable is 100 ft maximum. The software is Selcom Basic or Selcom 7+. Run a loop-back test by jumpering TX to RX to prove the PC side before you blame the panel. [T32 p19]\n\nKNOWN MANUAL ERRORS\n\nThe TEC1 guide contradicts itself in four places worth knowing. Page 7, \"Add a New Name\", gives the default password as 777 \u2014 every other page says 777777, and 777777 is correct. Page 13, \"Keypad Enable\", says press 3 for Comm Setup, but Comm Setup is 4. Page 14's password example says 87654 then shows keying 987654. And the TEC1 PDF binds the installer guide after the user guide with the pages running backward, so scroll rather than trusting the page counter.\n\nCLOSE-OUT CHECKLIST\n\n- New program password written on the work order and handed to the manager\n- Site code recorded on the work order and in your own records\n- Any 255 site code stopgap explicitly flagged to the manager as temporary\n- DIP switches returned to normal, card diag off\n- Panel penetrations resealed with RTV\n- Tested: directory scroll, one call-out, one PIN, one fob at each reader\n- Photo of the completed work\n- Manager name captured\n- Check out",tags:["SES","TEC1","Telephone entry","Site code","Wiegand","Fob reader","Factory reset","Field triage"],relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"],relatedModels:["ses-tec1-series","ses-tec2-series"],sources:["https://www.selectses.com/clientuploads/TEC%201%20PDF/man_tec1_ca.pdf","https://www.selectses.com/clientuploads/TEC%202%20PDF/man_t2_32x.pdf","Provenance tags in the body: [T1 pN] = TEC1 user/installer guide (600TEC1, Rev 10/08). [T32 pN] = TEC Series v3.2X (600TECMANUAL, Mar 2015), not documented in the TEC1 guide \u2014 verify on the panel. [INF] = inference, not stated in any manual."],aliases:["Security Engineered Systems","TEC2","SES panel","telephone entry TEC","TEC10","callbox","call box","entry panel","DoorKing","DKS","Linear","ButterflyMX","buzz-in system","gate phone","facility code","FC","card batch","Data 0","Data 1","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","key fob","keyfob","tag reader","prox reader","wipe the panel","reset to defaults","master reset","troubleshooting","service call","diagnose","no trouble found","prox tag","key tag","badge","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"siedle-in-home-bus-audio",title:"Siedle In-Home Bus (audio): identify, program & troubleshoot",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Siedle's In-Home Bus is a two-wire audio intercom system, and it is European in every way that matters on a US job: the part numbers, the programming ritual, the terminal labels and the supply voltage all follow conventions American techs rarely meet. This covers the BNG 650-0 and BNG 650-1 (USA) bus line rectifiers, the BTLM 650-xx bus door loudspeaker, BTM 650-0x call button modules, BTS/BTC/BFC 850-xx bus telephones and the AIB 150-xx Siedle Basic.\n\n[[img:photo-siedle-door-station|A Siedle multi-tenant door station, the outside end of the In-Home Bus.]]\n\nSource of record is the Siedle System Manual In-Home bus: Audio (2021, doc 210011022-00) and the BNG 650-0 / BNG 650-1 USA Product Information (doc 200035144). Where this guide and Siedle disagree, Siedle wins. Verify against the current system manual at siedle.com before relying on any figure for a quote or a life-safety application.\n\n**Identify the system before you touch anything.** Siedle also sells Classic 1+n, and the two are not interchangeable. Telling them apart:\n\n- Handsets: In-Home Bus uses BTS, BTC or BFC 850-xx. Classic 1+n uses HTS 811 or the BTS/BTC 750 series.\n- Handset terminals: bus handsets land on just Ta and Tb, plus an optional ERT. Classic has multiple numbered terminals.\n- Door station: bus uses a BTLM 650-xx behind the grille with a ribbon cable to the button modules. Classic uses a TLM 612 with TLE electronics.\n- Controller: bus uses a BNG 650-x on DIN rail. Classic uses an NG transformer plus TLE.\n\nTwo wires at the handset means bus. A ribbon cable from the speaker module to the button modules confirms it.\n\n**The BNG 650-x is the power supply AND the brains.** There is no separate controller, which catches out anybody expecting the US pattern of transformer plus control board. The BNG holds the door release relay, the light relay, the addressing and the programming interface. The hardware set:\n\n- BNG 650-0 \u2014 bus line rectifier, 230 V, EU.\n- BNG 650-1 \u2014 bus line rectifier, 125 V, the USA variant. Otherwise identical to the -0.\n- ZBVG 650-x \u2014 a plug-in card inside the BNG, required for multi-line systems or for PC programming. Only one is permitted in the entire system.\n- PRI 602-x USB \u2014 the PC programming interface, plugging into the RJ socket on the ZBVG.\n- BPS 650-x \u2014 the Windows programming software, shipped with the PRI 602 USB.\n- BTLM 650-04 \u2014 bus door loudspeaker module, carrying the light/programming button and its own door release contact.\n- BTM 650-01 through -04 \u2014 call button modules with one to four buttons, ribbon-cabled to the BTLM, LED lighting fed from b/c.\n- TR 603-0 \u2014 supplementary 12 V AC transformer, 1.3 A, needed past 20 illuminated BTM modules.\n\nCapacity is counted in users, and devices consume different numbers of them. A line takes 31 users and a system takes up to 15 lines, for a theoretical maximum of 465 users. The BTLM 650 and BTLE 050 each occupy 2 users; BTS, BTC, BFC and AIB each occupy 1; the BNG, NG, TR and PRI occupy none. One BNG supplies a single door release and a maximum of 20 illuminated BTM modules \u2014 past that you add a TR 603. Button count caps at 80 for Siedle Classic and 130 for Siedle Steel.\n\n**BNG 650-1 (USA) specifications.** Operating voltage 125 V AC plus or minus 10 percent at 50/60 Hz, drawing 360 mA. Output is 27.5 V DC for the bus and 12 V AC auxiliary, at 0.5 A DC and 1.0 A AC. The primary fuse is Si 1, T 500 mA L \u2014 note the 230 V -0 variant uses T 250 mA L instead, so do not swap fuses between them. Secondary is short-circuit proof. Protection IP 30, ambient 0 to +40 degrees C, 9 modules at 162 by 89 by 60 mm.\n\nTerminal assignment on the BNG:\n\n- L1, N \u2014 mains.\n- Ta, Tb \u2014 the In-Home bus, supplying all bus users.\n- Sa, Sb \u2014 system bus audio, linking lines in a multi-line system.\n- b, c \u2014 12 V AC supply out.\n- To, To \u2014 door release relay contact, max 24 V / 2 A, fixed at 3 seconds.\n- Li, Li \u2014 light relay contact, max 24 V / 2 A, 0.4 seconds by default.\n\n**The USA variant carries compliance conditions that are easy to violate.** The BNG 650-1 must be installed in a closed switch cabinet \u2014 a ZAP 9-0 cover alone is not sufficient. It may only be used with Information Technology Equipment. There is no surface-mount option, because ZAP 9-0 surface mounting is a 230 V arrangement only. And stranded conductors must have wire end ferrules, with no exceptions. On an inspection, an open-mounted 650-1 is a deficiency to flag, not a style preference.\n\nIf the unit has gone into overload, the case label gives the recovery: check the fuse, then disconnect the unit from power for a full minute. Do not shortcut the minute.\n\n**Read the two LEDs before anything else. LED 1 is operation, LED 2 is fault.** LED 1 patterns:\n\n- A 20 ms blip with a 1 second pause \u2014 normal, system functional.\n- Even 0.3 s on, 0.3 s off \u2014 system ramp-up. If it never settles, you have a short or an overload on Ta/Tb.\n- 0.3 s on with a 2 second pause \u2014 programming mode is active.\n- Solid and continuous \u2014 Plug+Play programming is active.\n\nLED 2 is the fault indicator and dark is good:\n\n- Off \u2014 no fault.\n- 2 s on, 0.3 s off \u2014 own address incorrect.\n- 2 s on, 0.3 s off, 0.3 s on, 0.3 s off, 2 s on \u2014 more than 31 users on the line.\n- Solid \u2014 address error at another BNG or BVNG in the system.\n- Even 0.3 s flash \u2014 more than one ZBVG 650 is plugged in on a multi-line system.\n- Irregular 0.3 / 0.3 / 0.2 / 2 s \u2014 an unsuitable device logged in during Plug+Play.\n- Even 2 s on, 2 s off \u2014 no BTLM or BTLE connected while in Plug+Play mode.\n\n**Front-panel controls live on the ZBVG card.** The Betriebsart slide selects 1, Norm or 2: position 1 is backward compatible with the BSG 650 and first-series BTS/BTC 750 handsets, Norm is the new system, and 2 is identical to Norm. The Prog. Mode button toggles programming mode on a brief press, and a five-second hold enters Plug+Play. The Adr. rotary sets address 1 to 15, shown as 1 to F \u2014 a single line stays at 1, multi-line systems use consecutive addresses all different from each other, and address 0 is invalid. The PRI 602 USB jack is only present if the ZBVG card is fitted.\n\nPhotograph the Adr. and Betriebsart positions before you touch anything. They are the two settings most likely to be wrong after somebody else has been in the box, and the only record of what they were is the one you make.\n\n**Programming a door call to a bus telephone, manually.** This is the standard procedure for BTS/BTC 850 handsets with receivers. Before you start: installation complete, buttons inscribed and the assignment documented, Betriebsart on Norm (or on 1 if 750-series handsets are on the line). Two people are worth having for anything above a couple of units.\n\nThe sequence, in order:\n\n- At the BNG, briefly press Prog. Mode. LED 1 changes to a 0.3 second flash with a 2 second pause.\n- At the door station, hold the light/programming button for 4 seconds. You get a sustained tone, then a repeat every 5 seconds.\n- At the handset, lift the receiver.\n- At the handset, hold the light button for 4 seconds. A sustained tone comes through the receiver, the muting button flashes and the speech path opens. Leave the receiver off-hook.\n- At the door station, hold the desired call button for 4 seconds. A sustained tone from the door speaker means it is assigned.\n- Repeat for the next unit, or exit programming mode at the BNG.\n\nYou do not need to exit and re-enter programming mode between units.\n\nIf you have no access to the apartment, skip the two handset steps and instead hold that unit's storey call button (ERT) for 4 seconds, then hold the call button at the door. This requires an ERT to be wired to that handset, and it means no ringing anywhere in the system during that window.\n\nFor a parallel call \u2014 one button ringing several handsets \u2014 run the receiver-lift and light-button steps at the first handset and leave it off-hook, do the same at the second handset and leave that off-hook too, and only then hold the call button at the door. Replace all receivers afterwards. Manual programming handles 2 handsets in parallel without a ZPS or ZPSF 850 board; PC programming handles 4 without one.\n\nHandsfree devices enter programming mode on a different button, which is a common five-minute time waster. BTS and BTC 850 use the light button with the receiver lifted first. The BFC 850 uses the light button. The AIB 150 Siedle Basic uses the speech button. Jung SI 4 A and SI AI use the light button.\n\n**The deletion trap. Holding an already-programmed call button at an activated door loudspeaker will erase it.** You get about one second of warning tone \u2014 multiple short descending beeps \u2014 and at about three seconds a confirmation tone. What happens then depends entirely on state: with no indoor device active, the button assignment is DELETED; with an indoor device active, the button is overwritten with the new address.\n\nThe rule that keeps you out of trouble: never hold a call button in programming mode unless a handset is currently active and off-hook. If you hear warning beeps at one second, you are either on the wrong button or you skipped the handset steps. Let go immediately.\n\n**Constraints that cause silent failure.** These do not announce themselves and each one wastes a visit:\n\n- Ten-minute timeout. No programming activity for 10 minutes and the BNG reverts to standard mode. LED 1 tells you.\n- Only one door loudspeaker can be in programming mode at a time.\n- No ringing during programming. A resident hitting a buzzer aborts the operation. On occupied buildings, tape a notice over the panel.\n- All BNG and BVNG units must be energised during programming.\n- On multi-line systems, pressing Prog. Mode at any one BNG puts every connected BNG into programming mode.\n- The BTS 850 light button is freely configurable and may have been reassigned by a previous installer. If holding it does nothing, fall back to the ERT method.\n\n**Plug+Play, and when not to use it.** Hold Prog. Mode for 5 seconds and LED 1 goes solid. Use it only for virgin commissioning, because of one disqualifier that matters enormously on service work: bus telephones already assigned to a door loudspeaker in the same line are not reprogrammed. On an occupied building every handset is already assigned, so Plug+Play will appear to do nothing at all.\n\nIts other conditions: new-generation hardware only (BTLM 650-04 or BTLE 050-03, BTM 650-0x, BRMA 050-01); handset housings open with base plates wired and the phones not yet clipped on; buttons numbered in the order the BTM modules are chained IN to OUT; handsets clipped to base plates in call-button order, with the storey call chirping after about 7 seconds as the acknowledgement. The BTLM and BTLE call buttons are disabled while Plug+Play is running.\n\nIf the mapping comes out wrong, the Plug+Play reset is a specific dance: kill supply to the BNG, disconnect Ta and Tb, change the Adr. switch (1 to 2, for instance), hold Prog. Mode about 3 seconds while restoring power and release after about 3 seconds, wait for LED 1 to show normal mode, reconnect Ta/Tb and wait for full ramp-up, unclip all handsets from their base plates, return Adr. to its original position, wait for ramp-up again and confirm all handsets have released. Then begin Plug+Play afresh.\n\n**PC programming is the preferred path.** It needs a ZBVG 650 card in the BNG, a PRI 602-x USB, BPS 650 software at V2.50 or later, and a Windows laptop. What it buys you is the full function set, a saved configuration file, and no guessing at beeps in a stairwell. On any building over four units, or any site you will return to, it is worth the setup time. The PRI 602 can be left permanently installed or plugged in through an 8-pin Western jack.\n\nSeveral functions are available only through the PC: door call forwarding, doormatic, light contact timing, the second button level, BSE groups, status LED feedback, and intercom or paging.\n\n**Troubleshooting starts with a meter.** The values worth knowing: Ta/Tb at the BNG should read 26 to 29 V DC; Ta/Tb at the most distant user must be at least 16 V DC; b/c reads 12 V AC; and To is a dry normally-open contact, open at rest, closing for 3 seconds on a door release press.\n\nConductor limits on J-Y(St)Y or YR at 0.8 mm: BNG to the most distant door station 300 m, BNG to the most distant handset 300 m, total cable within one line 1500 m, BNG to BNG on Sa/Sb 300 m, and handset to ERT button 50 m. At 0.6 mm, halve the 300 m figures. On CAT cable, AWG26 gives 80 m, AWG24 gives 120 m and AWG22 gives 200 m.\n\nWork it in this order. If LED 2 is lit or flashing, read the LED 2 patterns above \u2014 you have an address or capacity fault, and it gets fixed before anything else. If LED 2 is dark, go to LED 1. A 20 ms blip with a 1 second pause means the BNG is healthy and the fault is downstream: meter Ta/Tb at the BNG for 26 to 29 V, and if it is low, pull the bus pair \u2014 if it recovers, you have a short in the riser. If the voltage at the BNG is good, meter at the farthest handset for at least 16 V; low there is a distance, gauge or junction problem, and good there points at the BTLM, the handset or the programming. A fast even strobe that never settles is a short or overload on Ta/Tb. A 0.3 second flash with a 2 second pause means somebody left it in programming mode \u2014 tap Prog. Mode. Solid means it was left in Plug+Play \u2014 tap Prog. Mode.\n\n**Door release wiring has three topologies, and one of them is not tamper-proof.** There are two door release contacts in the system: one in the BNG, which fires on every door-release press, and one in the BTLM, which fires only at the door that last placed a call.\n\n- BNG contact only \u2014 4 cores to the door, tamper-proof, but single door station only.\n- BNG plus BTLM contacts \u2014 5 cores, tamper-proof, supports multiple doors.\n- BTLM contact only \u2014 4 cores, supports multiple doors, but NOT tamper-proof, because the contact is accessible from outside the building.\n\nStrike specification is 12 V AC with a minimum 20 ohm impedance, the TO 615 being the reference example, drawing roughly 600 mA. Use high-resistance releases for range and reliability. If the strike exceeds the 2 A contact rating, drive an interposing relay off the To contact rather than the strike directly.\n\n**Site inspection checklist:**\n\n- BNG variant matches the supply voltage \u2014 the 650-1 is the 125 V part.\n- The 650-1 is in a closed cabinet. Flag it as a deficiency if it is open-mounted.\n- All stranded conductors are ferruled at the terminal block.\n- No splayed strands bridging b to c or across the To pair.\n- Ventilation slots clear.\n- Adr. and Betriebsart positions photographed.\n- The existing button-to-unit map documented before any programming happens.\n- Li/Li landed, or confirmed unused.\n- Any interposing relays traced and documented.\n- Ta/Tb metered at the BNG and at the farthest handset.\n\n**Quick card, programming one unit on a BTS 850.** At the BNG, tap Prog. Mode and watch for the 0.3 second flash with 2 second pause. At the door, hold the light button 4 seconds for a tone that repeats every 5 seconds. At the handset, lift the receiver, then hold the light button 4 seconds for a tone and leave it off-hook. At the door, hold the call button 4 seconds \u2014 a sustained tone means done. Then repeat, or exit at the BNG.\n\nNever hold a call button unless a handset is active. One second of beeps is the warning; three seconds and it is deleted. Ten minutes idle and the BNG exits programming. No ringing anywhere in the system while you work.\n\nA few things about the release side and the platform generations that decide whether a job goes smoothly or not at all. Siedle calls the door release TO, for Turoffner, and it is a potential-free dry contact... on the older 1+n stations it is rated to about 15 VAC or 30 VDC at 2 A with the release time fixed at three seconds. Note the European lean: the release is commonly 12 VAC and many European strikes are AC buzz-type, so match the strike to the release rather than assuming the DC strike in your van will do.\n\nIdentify the generation before you touch anything, because components do not cross between them. A site may be 1+n (the older multi-wire scheme), In-Home Bus (the 2-wire bus), IQ 2wire, or Access IP, with Vixinity Cloud as the current hosted offering. Wiring, parts and programming all differ, and a swap that looks like a like-for-like replacement will simply not work across a generation boundary.\n\nCable and distance are planning-manual matters rather than judgement calls. The bus runs on telephone-grade J-Y(St)Y 0.8 mm, with roughly 100 m per segment and video branches out to about 150 m. A long run or the wrong cable presents as poor audio, poor video, or a dead branch rather than as an obvious wiring fault, so check the run against the planning distances before you start swapping stations. And if the customer wants to answer the door on a phone, that needs a gateway... an SG 150 or an Axiom. Without one it is a fixed-station system, full stop.",tags:["Siedle","In-Home Bus","BNG 650","BTLM 650","Bus telephone","Door release","Programming","European systems"],relatedGuides:["intercom-triage"],relatedModels:["siedle-multi-tenant-door-station"],sources:["Siedle System Manual, In-Home bus: Audio (2021), document 210011022-00.","Siedle BNG 650-0 / BNG 650-1 USA Product Information, document 200035144.","Figures here are transcribed from the above. Verify against the current system manual at siedle.com before relying on any value for a quote or a life-safety application \u2014 where this guide and Siedle disagree, Siedle wins."],aliases:["Siedle Vario","German intercom","IHB","Siedle bus","two-wire bus","house phone","intercom station","door opener","buzz the door","unlock button","release button","reprogram","program the panel","keypad commands","star codes","command codes","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","volt free","form C","clean contact"]},{id:"dks-takeover-reset",title:"Taking over a DoorKing panel: clearing a lost master code & reprogramming from scratch",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",tags:["Telephone entry","Master code","Takeover","Factory reset","Default codes","Field triage","Programming"],body:"The call comes in some version of this: new management took the building, nobody knows the code to the callbox, half the directory rings people who moved out three years ago, and can you be there Thursday. That is a takeover, and it is a different job from a service call. On a service call you fix one thing. On a takeover you inherit somebody else's undocumented system and hand back one you can document, which means the order of operations matters more than any single keystroke.\n\nFirst rule, before you press a single star: **identify which DoorKing family you are standing at.** Read the part number off the board and match it to the manual. The 1834 and its 80-Series siblings (1814, 1816, 1835, 1837, 1838, manual P/N 1834-065) are the PC-first line. The EPD series (1802, 1808, 1810, 1812) is the older keypad-programmed family. Their command sets are NOT the same, and the overlap is what gets people \u2014 * 01 adds a phone number on both, * 20 wipes the directory on both, and everything else moves. Send an EPD's * 17 to an 1834 and nothing happens; reach for the wrong code in the other direction and you can erase the entire building before you have written any of it down. The side-by-side lives in the DKS 1834 vs EPD guide linked below; the short version is that you never program a DoorKing from memory across families.\n\n[[diagram:dks-1834-vs-epd|Red cells are codes that DIFFER between the 1834/80-Series and the EPD family. Confirm which board you are at before pressing a program code.]]\n\nNow the part everybody gets wrong about the lost master code: **you do not need the old one, and there is nothing to \"reset\" to.** DoorKing panels ship with no default master password \u2014 not 0000, not 1234, nothing. The old code cannot be recovered by DKS, by a service tool, or by you. What you do instead is re-authorize yourself physically. Open the cabinet, flip the MASTER CODE switch ON, program a new four-digit code (* 04 on the EPD family; the same switch-and-enter sequence per \xa73.2.1 on the 1834), then flip the switch back OFF. Every other bit of data in the box survives \u2014 directory, timers, entry codes, all of it. The old code is simply gone and yours replaces it.\n\nTwo things follow from that, and the second one is the one to say out loud to the customer. One: the LCD staying lit for about thirty seconds after you flip the switch back is normal, not a fault. Two: on a DoorKing, physical access to the enclosure IS administrative access to the system. Anybody who can open that cabinet can take ownership of the panel in under a minute without knowing a single existing code. So a takeover is not finished when the programming is right \u2014 it is finished when the enclosure is locked, the key is controlled, and the old management company's key no longer opens it.\n\n**Capture the existing configuration before you erase any of it.** This is the step people skip and regret, because on these boards there is no undo and no backup you did not make yourself. Read settings back with the read commands \u2014 * 06 or * 25 \u2014 and never press a program code just to peek at what is in there; on the EPD family * 20, * 44 and * 45 all erase the whole directory the instant you touch them, and they are exactly the codes a curious tech reaches for. On an 1834, do the whole capture in software instead: pull the directory down into Remote Account Manager over the modem or data link first, and you have a restorable file plus a working copy you can edit on a laptop rather than thumbing 3000 entries into a keypad. Photograph the board before you change anything \u2014 jumper positions, every landed terminal, the transformer leads \u2014 and write down the tone-open numbers, which relay drives which opening, and where the postal switch is pointed. Fifteen minutes of documentation here is the difference between a clean cutover and a Saturday callback.\n\nThen ask whether you need to wipe at all, because usually you do not. Ordinary tenant turnover is a per-directory-code job, not a reset: on the 1834 you delete the phone number with * 01 and the name with * 65, then program the new resident onto the SAME directory code with * 01 and * 66. Nobody else in the building is disturbed. A full wipe earns its place only when the inherited directory is genuinely unusable \u2014 wrong code length for the new numbering scheme, hundreds of dead entries, or a building that has been re-unitized.\n\nWhen a wipe is the right call, know exactly which grenade you are pulling. On the 1834/80-Series the three to fear are * 20, * 60 and * 95, and none of them is called \"erase\" \u2014 * 20 sets the directory-code length, * 60 the number of area codes allowed, * 95 the memory size. Each one ends on the same ERASE MEM? 1=Y 0=N prompt, and 1 clears the entire directory. That prompt is the last thing standing between you and re-entering the building by hand, so read it rather than tapping through it. On the EPD family the list is longer: * 20 (directory length), * 44 (16-digit mode) and * 45 (7-digit mode) each erase the directory; * 22 deletes all phone numbers; * 00 and * 11 delete all entry codes; * 67 deletes all names. None of it is recoverable. And on an 1834 there is a better path than any of these \u2014 build the directory you want in RAM on the PC and push it, which overwrites cleanly and leaves you holding a file you can restore.\n\n**Rebuild in the right order, because one of the settings destroys the others.** Set the directory-code length FIRST, before a single resident goes in, since changing it later wipes everything you just entered. Then area codes, if the site needs 10-digit or long-distance dialling \u2014 on the 1834 that is * 24 to program the area code behind a pointer, then * 41 to enter numbers that reference it, and get this right before data entry, not after. Touch-tone versus rotary is a different setting again, * 07. Then the behavior: relay/strike time (* 03 on the 1834, * 17 on the EPD), talk time (* 08 versus * 05), tone-open numbers (* 05 versus * 23), and the postal-lock relay assignment (* 50 on the 1834). Confirm which relay actually drives which gate or door before you set open codes, or residents will buzz open the wrong opening. The directory goes in last.\n\nTreat the takeover as a **security handover, not just a data-entry job**, and price it that way. The tone-open numbers default to 9876 for relay one and 5432 for relay two, they are printed in a manual anybody can download, and on a building that has changed hands twice they are effectively public \u2014 change them. Every entry code issued by the previous management is a credential you cannot account for, so on a real takeover you reissue rather than inherit. Set a new master code even if somehow you were handed the old one, log it in the job file, and confirm who holds Remote Account Manager access and who owns the DKS cellular or VoIP account, because on a management change that account frequently still sits with the outgoing company. A panel you have perfectly programmed is still theirs if they hold the account and the cabinet key.\n\nSort the comm path early, since it decides how you can program at all. A POTS copper line does voice and remote programming both. DKS cellular and VoIP kits carry voice but may not pass clean DTMF, which means off-site touch-tone programming can silently fail and you will be doing it at the keypad in the weather \u2014 plan for that. A cellular board will not dial out until the DKS account is provisioned, so start that days ahead of the site visit rather than discovering it on the ladder.\n\nLast, before you blame programming for anything, open the lid and triage the board, because on these units a \"dead\" panel usually is not. The MASTER CODE switch left ON gives you a long tone every thirty seconds and abnormal behavior. On a 1802 the TONE jumper left on TONE ON after a feedback adjustment is the single most common callback on the model, and a missing RING jumper means the unit never answers an inbound call, which kills remote programming outright. A board that will not accept programming is almost always one of those or a programming lockout, not a failed board. And on any UL 294 install, the tamper switch under the microphone board must stay connected to the alarm or the listing is void \u2014 depending on how the site is armed, notify whoever monitors it before you open the faceplate, because a takeover is exactly the situation where nobody has told the alarm company you are coming.\n\nClose the job the way you wish the last tech had. Master code and any new entry codes in the job file, the directory exported and stored, board photos attached, cabinet key accounted for, and the customer told in writing which codes were changed and which accounts they need to take ownership of. That record is what makes the next takeover somebody else's easy day instead of another Thursday like this one.",relatedGuides:["dks-1834-vs-epd","dks-1834-telephone-entry","dks-1802-telephone-entry","intercom-triage","access-control-systems"],relatedModels:["dks-1834-80-series","dks-1802-epd","doorking-1835","doorking-1800-series","dks-telephone-entry-board"],sources:["https://www.doorking.com/","https://www.doorking.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/09/1834-065-W-11-24_80_Series.pdf","https://www.doorking.com/entry-system/1802-entry-system/","https://www.doorking.com/tech-support/product-documents-and-download-owners-manuals/"],aliases:["call box","entry panel","Linear","ButterflyMX","buzz-in system","gate phone","lost the code","no default password","unrecoverable","locked out of the panel","panel swap","replace the panel","reuse existing sensors","wipe the panel","reset to defaults","master reset","factory code","default PIN","out of box code","troubleshooting","diagnose","no trouble found","reprogram","program the panel","keypad commands","star codes","command codes","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","cover switch","tamper alarm","case open","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"request-to-exit-rex-wiring",title:"Request to exit: devices and wiring",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Request to exit lets someone leave without presenting a credential. It does two jobs at once: release the lock for exit, and tell the access control system the exit was legitimate so the door contact opening does not read as a forced entry or a held-open alarm.\n\n[[img:photo-ict-protege-cabinet|The controller end of a REX run, where the device you fit at the door has to land.]]\n\nThe common REX devices: a push button or plate (mushroom button, or a large 'Push to Exit' plate), a REX motion sensor (a PIR mounted over the door on the egress side that fires when someone approaches), electrified hardware with a built-in request-to-exit switch (a mortise lock or exit device with an RX switch that trips when the lever or bar is operated), and touch-sense bars.\n\nThere are two wiring philosophies, and mixing them up is where people get in trouble.\n\nFirst philosophy: REX into a controller input. The button or PIR drives a dry contact into a controller input. The controller fires the lock relay and shunts the door contact for the unlock time. This is clean for electric strikes and any controller-managed lock. There is a small processing delay. On the [[model:ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din|Protege controller]] the REX input can be programmed to operate regardless of the door contact state and to recycle the door alarm time so a held door does not nuisance-alarm.\n\nSecond philosophy: REX that directly breaks lock power. On a mag lock egress door, the code-required manual release has to interrupt power directly, independent of the panel, and release immediately. So the 'Push to Exit' button's normally-closed contact goes in series with the lock power, not just into a controller input. Break the circuit, the magnet drops, done. If the only path is button to controller to relay, any panel delay means the door does not release immediately and it fails inspection.\n\nKnow your NO versus NC on the device. Exit buttons are usually SPDT with both a normally-open and a normally-closed contact. For a controller input, use the contact that matches how the input is configured (often a normally-open that closes on press, or a monitored normally-closed). For a direct power interrupt, use the normally-closed contact that opens on press to break the circuit. A button landed on the wrong contact either does nothing or holds the door open forever.\n\nIntercoms and relays release the same door. A building intercom or an ICT relay is just another dry contact. Wire the intercom's door-release relay output in parallel with the REX or button path... into the controller input, or across the power-interrupt point, depending on the lock. Tenant presses release, the relay closes, the door drops. Same logic as a button, different trigger.\n\nCommon failures: a PIR aimed at through-traffic or set too sensitive, so the door unlocks when people just walk past. A button on a mag lock wired only to the panel, which is an immediate-release inspection fail. A door contact that never gets shunted, so every legitimate exit throws a forced-door alarm. And a REX watching the wrong side of the door... it should see the egress side only.",sources:["https://www.constructionspecifier.com/code-requirements-for-doors-with-access-control/","https://idighardware.com/2017/06/code-requirements-for-electromagnetic-locks/","https://ict.co/media/y5ylq4dx/prt-ctrl-din-1d_protege_gx_din_rail_single_door_controller_installation_manual.pdf"],relatedGuides:["maglocks","fail-safe-fail-secure","low-voltage-wiring"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din"],tags:["Request to exit","REX","Controller","PIR","Dry contact","Access control system","Relay","Mortise lock"],aliases:["exit button","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","motion detector","passive infrared","false alarms","volt free","NO NC COM","form C","clean contact","relay clicks","contact won't close","Best 45H","Schlage L9000","Corbin Russwin ML2000","Sargent 8200","mortise body","pocket lock","panic bar","crash bar","push bar","Von Duprin","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","lockout","locked out","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"access-control-grammar-and-the-lead-role",title:"The grammar of an access control job, and what makes a lead",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Every access control job you will ever touch is one sentence, and once you can hear it, the parts stop being a pile of devices and become grammar.\n\n[[img:photo-ict-protege-cabinet|An access-control cabinet as installed... the controller, the expander and the supply that every job on this page ends up in front of.]]\n\nHere is the sentence. A person presents a CREDENTIAL. The READER turns it into data. The CONTROLLER checks that data against its database... is this card valid, at this door, right now? If yes, it throws a RELAY, and that relay powers or unpowers the LOCK. A REQUEST-TO-EXIT device lets them back out. A DOOR CONTACT tells the controller whether the door is actually open or closed. And the FIRE ALARM sits above all of it with the authority to override everything and release egress.\n\nCredential to reader to controller to relay to lock, with the fire alarm and the network wired into the margins. That is it. Every job is a variation on that one sentence, and every fault you will ever chase is a break somewhere along it. When a door misbehaves, do not start guessing at parts... walk the sentence. Did the reader see the card? Did the controller get the data? Did the relay throw? Did the lock get power? Four questions in order, and the answer is always at the first 'no'.\n\nA tech memorizes parts. A lead reads the sentence those parts form. That difference is worth naming precisely, because it is the whole gap between the two roles, and it is crossable.\n\n**The lead's document is the sequence of operations.** The SOO is the written spec of how every door behaves in every mode: normal hours, after hours, lockdown, fire alarm, power loss. Not what hardware is on it... how it BEHAVES. A tech installs what the schedule says. A lead writes the schedule, and in writing it discovers the contradictions before they become a failed inspection: this door is on the egress path AND the client wants it card-only from the outside AND the fire marshal needs it to drop on alarm. Those three requirements have exactly one correct hardware answer, and you find it at the desk, not on the ladder. Start by reading every SOO that crosses your bench, then draft one for a door you have already installed and see what you cannot answer. The door-mode vocabulary it is written in, and a per-opening checklist for producing one, are in \"Door modes and writing a sequence of operations\".\n\n**The lead owns the submittal.** That is the approval package that goes out before anyone pulls wire: cut sheets for every device, the riser diagram showing how the system stacks through the building, the point-to-point showing terminal by terminal what lands where, and the SOO. The riser is a single-line drawing... what connects to what, not to scale, floor by floor. The point-to-point is what you actually wire from. Get the submittal approved and rough-in is bookkeeping. Skip it and you are improvising inside a wall.\n\n**Two scopes ambush techs and never ambush leads: the fire alarm interface and the network.** The relay that drops mag lock power on alarm frequently belongs to the fire alarm contractor, not to you. If nobody confirms whose scope it is, the lock gets mounted, the walk happens, and the interface was never wired. Same story on the IT side: the controller needs an address, a subnet, and usually its own VLAN, and if that conversation happens the week of trim-out instead of at submittal, you will be standing at a panel waiting on somebody's network admin. Both of these are coordination, not craft, and coordination is the lead's job.\n\n**The lead owns the code conversation.** Know the egress requirements cold, because they gate everything else. NFPA 101 and the IBC both govern electrified locking on egress doors, and the IBC section numbers move between editions... so never quote a memorized number. Quote the edition your AHJ has adopted, and when there is genuine doubt, ask the AHJ. Not the customer, not the GC. The authority having jurisdiction is the one whose interpretation is final, and a five-minute phone call before rough-in is cheaper than a failed final.\n\n**The lead speaks IT and telecom as well as access.** Subnets and VLANs, PoE budgets, RS-485 versus OSDP, dial tone on a telephone entry line. This crossover is where the edge is, because it is the common blind spot: plenty of good door techs go their whole career without being able to read a subnet mask, and it caps them. The trade has been converging on the network for twenty years and is not going back.\n\nThe rest of the vocabulary lives in the Glossary... contact logic and Form C relays, credential formats and bit counts, the protocol stack, the code standards, the recorder and camera terms, and the field-craft words the office uses (submittal, BOM, riser, punch list, as-built, T&M, scope creep). Tap any of them where they appear in a guide and the definition comes up.\n\nIf you want a study order that pays fastest, take it in this sequence. Learn the sentence at the top of this page until it is reflex. Then door and lock behavior... fail-safe versus fail-secure, and how each one lands on a relay contact. Then the code section, because it gates what you are allowed to build. Then the IT half, because it is the gap most techs never close, and closing it is what gets you handed the submittal instead of the drill.",sources:["https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-101-standard-development/101","https://codes.iccsafe.org/","https://www.securityindustry.org/industry-standards/open-supervised-device-protocol/","Field practice, A&D access control crew 2026 - submittal, SOO and scope-coordination workflow from a telecom-engineer lead."],relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","fail-safe-fail-secure","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","low-voltage-wiring","maglocks"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din"],tags:["Submittal","Rough-in","Sequence of operations","Controller","Riser diagram","Subnet mask","Scope creep","Punch list"],aliases:["access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","building inspector","who signs off","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","panic bar","crash bar","push bar","Von Duprin","Precision","Detex","Falcon 25","Yale 7100","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior"]},{id:"dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring",title:"Dry contacts, NO/NC, and wiring a lock to a relay",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Everything in this guide hangs on one idea, and if you take nothing else, take this: **the access controller does not power the lock. It only switches it.** The relay inside the panel is a dry contact... a plain switch with no voltage of its own. Your power supply provides the current, and the relay only decides whether that current reaches the lock. If it helps, think of a tap. A dry contact is the valve on its own, sitting on the bench with no pipe behind it... turn the handle all day and nothing comes out, because you have not plumbed it to anything yet. A wet contact is the tap already mounted on the wall with the main behind it, so opening it delivers water immediately. Once you see it that way, every NO/NC decision falls out of it instead of having to be memorized.\n\nA dry contact is a switch with no voltage of its own... it makes or breaks a circuit that you power. The controller's lock relay, a request-to-exit button, a door contact... all dry. A wet contact already carries voltage, and landing one where a dry contact was expected back-feeds the circuit. If you feed voltage into an input that wanted a dry contact, or expect a dry contact to source power on its own, you get nothing at best and damage at worst.\n\nThat is worth slowing down on, because wet contacts are not a mistake somebody makes... they are a deliberate kind of output and you will be handed them on purpose. A dry contact is for SWITCHING something you power. A wet output is for DRIVING something directly: an alarm panel's bell and strobe terminals, a lot of PGM and auxiliary outputs, the LED and beeper drives going out to a reader head. The panel has already decided the voltage, and your job is to hang the right load on it and nothing else. Plenty of boards make it worse by being both... a jumper or a programming option flips a single output between wet and dry, so the terminal you metered on the last job is not necessarily the same terminal on this one.\n\nSo meter it before you land anything, and it takes twenty seconds. Field wiring off, meter on DC volts, black probe on the supply negative, red probe on each output leg in turn... then trigger the output and read it again. A dry contact reads zero volts in both states, because it has nothing to give. A wet output reads zero at rest and then its supply voltage when it fires. That single reading tells you which half of this page you are on.\n\nThe two ways to get it wrong fail very differently, which is why only one of them is famous. Landing a wet output into an input expecting a dry contact back-feeds voltage into a board that wanted a bare switch, and that is the loud one... a blown input, a zone that behaves strangely, sometimes a dead panel. The quiet one is the reverse, and it is the one that actually eats your afternoon: hang a siren or a strobe on a dry contact and absolutely nothing happens, because nobody ever gave it power. The wiring looks perfect, the relay clicks, the meter says the contact is closing, and the device sits there silent. If a load does nothing and the switching side checks out, stop testing the switch and ask where the current was supposed to come from.\n\n[[diagram:dry-vs-wet-contact|Who brings the power, how to tell in twenty seconds, and the two ways it fails]]\n\n**Dry means you bring the power. Wet means it brings the power.** Never wire it so both do.\n\nA dry relay contact has up to three terminals. COM, the common, is the pole... everything switches relative to it. NO, normally open, has no path to COM at rest and closes when the relay energizes. NC, normally closed, has a path to COM at rest and opens when the relay energizes. The word doing all the work there is **normally**, and it always means de-energized, idle, at rest. Relays come as Form A (NO only), Form B (NC only), and Form C, which gives you COM plus both contacts on one output. Form C is the standard on access panels, including the [[model:ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din|ICT Protege]], and it is precisely what lets you wire either failure mode off the same relay.\n\nPicture the circuit as a loop. The power supply's positive leg runs to the relay, the relay's switched terminal runs to the lock, and the lock's negative returns to the supply. The relay opens or closes that top leg, your supply pushes the current, and the controller's only job is to energize or de-energize the relay coil at the right moment. There are two states and only two. De-energized, at rest, COM is tied to NC... that is the door's normal resting condition. Energized, on a valid grant, the coil pulls COM over to NO. In nearly every access system the lock relay sits de-energized in the secure idle state and energizes briefly to grant access. Hold that constant, and the only variable left is which terminal the lock lands on.\n\nWhich terminal depends entirely on the lock's failure mode, and the reason it flips between a strike and a mag lock is that **\"powered\" means opposite things on the two devices**. A powered fail-secure strike is unlocked. A powered mag lock is locked. So to get the same secure-at-rest, release-on-grant behavior out of both, they have to land on opposite terminals.\n\nA fail-secure electric strike is powered to unlock. At rest you want it off and locked, and on a valid card you energize the relay to send it power. So it lands on **NO**. Supply positive to COM, NO out to the strike, strike negative back to the supply. At rest NO is open, the strike is unpowered, the door is locked. On a grant the coil closes COM to NO, the strike energizes, the latch releases. Two things bite here in the field. First, duty cycle: some strikes are rated intermittent only, and energizing one continuously... say, to hold a door unlocked on a schedule... will cook the coil, so check the rating before you set up a scheduled unlock. That rating also decides whether the strike may be fail-safe at all: fail-safe means energized to stay locked, which is continuous by definition, so an AC intermittent-duty solenoid must never be set fail-safe. Fail-safe requires a continuous-duty unit, full stop. If the job wants fail-safe, or wants the strike held unlocked on a schedule, you need continuous duty and you check that before you order, not at the door. Second, AC versus DC: an AC strike buzzes on release, which is the classic sound, while a DC strike is silent and needs a suppression diode. Many are dual-voltage. AC also costs you real current: a 12V Adams Rite pulls roughly 1.4A on AC against about 0.33A on DC, which is the difference between a comfortable supply and a browning-out one, and it drives your wire gauge too. And if a strike is ordered or set fail-safe, it wires like a mag lock instead... land it on NC.\n\nA magnetic lock is powered to lock, always, with no fail-secure variant possible. At rest you want it energized and holding, and on a grant you cut power. So it lands on **NC**. Supply positive to COM, NC out to the mag, mag negative back to the supply. At rest COM is tied to NC, the mag is powered and holding. On a grant the coil pulls COM away from NC, power drops, the door releases.\n\nThat gets the access control behavior right, but a mag lock on an egress door is not finished there, because code demands two more release paths that have to work independently of the panel. The way you satisfy them is to run the mag's power leg through a **series chain of normally-closed hold contacts**, so that any one of them opening drops the lock. Supply positive goes through the fire alarm relay's NC contact, then through the push-to-exit button's NC contact, then to the mag. The mag's return goes through the access control relay back to the supply. Now the lock releases if any link opens: the fire alarm activates and its relay opens, the push-to-exit is pressed and its contact opens, power is lost and the whole loop dies, or a valid credential opens the controller relay for a normal grant.\n\n[[diagram:maglock-fire-release|Mag lock power through the fire relay and push-to-exit in series, returning via the access relay]]\n\n\n\nThe layout matters, not just the parts. The push-to-exit button has to break the mag's power directly, in series, rather than signalling the controller to drop its relay... code wants that manual release immediate and independent of the panel electronics, and a button wired only as a panel input adds delay and fails inspection. The fire alarm interface, whether that is a dry relay in the fire panel or a fire input on the lock power supply, drops the same power leg... and confirming whose scope that wire belongs to is the single most common thing missing at a mag lock inspection. That signal usually comes from an addressable relay module on the fire panel's signaling line, which lets the panel release specific doors off specific fire zones by its own programming rather than dropping everything at once... use a UL 864 listed module, and expect the interface itself to be supervised so a broken wire annunciates as a trouble instead of failing silently.\n\nOne design question will come up on every job with more than a couple of mag locks, and it is an AHJ call rather than a settled rule: one fire relay releasing every lock, or an individual relay in the power run at each lock? A single input at the power supply is cheaper and simpler, but a failure of that one input or supply leaves every door on it locked. Plenty of AHJs want the per-door relay so a single failure drops a single opening. Ask before you design it, not after the rough-in. The exact code sections that require these move between code editions, so cite the edition your AHJ has actually adopted rather than a number you memorized... low-voltage wiring & power breaks the renumbering down.\n\nSo the decision reduces to one line: **power-to-unlock lands on NO, power-to-lock lands on NC.** Fail-secure is NO. Fail-safe is NC. A fail-secure strike or electrified lever sits unpowered and locked at rest and energizes to release. A fail-safe strike, lever, or any mag lock sits powered and locked at rest and drops on the grant. ICT documents it in exactly those words, and every other panel worth using does the same.\n\nKill one myth before it costs you a relay: a dry contact is not an unlimited one. It frees you from the panel's VOLTAGE, not from its current rating, and the contacts have a published limit you can absolutely exceed... a [[model:keri-systems-pxl-nxt|legacy Keri PXL]] switches 1.0 A at 24 VDC, where ICT's Form C relays manage 7 A. Push a mag lock or a latch-retraction device with real inrush through the small one and you pit the contacts, then weld them, and a welded relay is a door that never locks again.\n\nThere are two genuinely different ways to use that dry contact, and picking the right one matters more as the lock gets bigger. In the first pattern you power the lock through the relay, so the lock's current physically crosses the relay contacts. That is fine for a strike, but watch the rating... ICT's Form C relays switch up to 7A, and a large mag lock or an electric latch retraction device with real inrush can exceed a smaller relay. In the second pattern the relay is only a trigger. Access power supplies commonly have their own fused lock outputs with a fire alarm disconnect and a trigger input, so the panel's dry relay carries no lock current at all... it just tells the supply's board to switch its own output. That keeps the switching load off the panel relay, centralizes the fire drop, and is usually the right build on any real job involving mag locks or a fire interface.\n\nWhichever pattern you use, suppress the coil, and do not talk yourself out of it. The comforting story is that a dry contact isolates the panel so the spike lands somewhere cheap... it does not. The kick comes from the LOCK's own inductance, and it appears across whatever opened the circuit, which is your relay contacts, dry or wet, panel or power supply. Isolation buys you a lot; it does not buy you this. Any DC lock is an inductance, and cutting it kicks back a spike that pits and eventually welds the relay contacts. A DC strike gets a 1N4007 diode across the coil with the band toward positive, mounted at the strike and not at the panel. A mag lock gets an MOV, usually already built in... and do not add a diode to a mag, because it drags out the release and gives you a sticky lock. An AC strike gets an MOV too, never a diode, since diodes are DC only. ICT ships a 1N4007 with its strike outputs precisely because this gets skipped, and a door that works for months and then goes intermittent is very often pitted contacts from a missing suppressor.\n\nThe failure signatures are worth knowing cold. A door that does exactly the opposite of what you intended... locking on a grant, or sitting unlocked at rest... is a lock landed on the wrong terminal for its failure mode, and the fix is to re-land it. Works for months then goes intermittent is missing suppression. A mag that feels weak and under-holds on a door with clean flush contact is voltage drop, so measure at the lock under load and go up in wire gauge. Passing a function test but failing inspection is almost always a push-to-exit wired to the panel instead of breaking mag power directly, or a fire alarm interface that never got landed. Every legitimate exit throwing a forced-door alarm means the door contact is not being shunted by the request-to-exit. The reader granting while the lock never moves usually means nobody ran the lock power leg to COM at all, or the lock is sitting on the terminal that is open in that state. And a controller that resets or a relay that chatters when the lock fires is a lock drawing more than the relay or the shared supply can give... move it to the power supply's own lock output.\n\nBefore you energize a door, run the same short list every time. Identify the failure mode: a mag is fail-safe always, a strike you read off the FS/FSE tab, a lever you check against the order. Pick the terminal from it... fail-secure to NO, fail-safe to NC. Confirm the relay really is a dry contact and that your supply feeds the COM leg, because the panel is not sourcing lock power. Match voltage and current, and account for inrush on latch retraction and large mags. Land the suppressor at the lock. On a mag egress door, put the fire drop and the push-to-exit in series in the power leg, independent of the panel, and confirm who owns the fire interface. Then meter it: verify the secure state at rest, verify release on a grant, and [[tool:multimeter|drop-test under load]] at the lock rather than open-circuit. Test the overrides last... kill power and confirm it fails to the right state, trip the fire relay and confirm the mag drops, press the manual button and confirm it drops immediately. Test the fire drop specifically, even on a door that releases perfectly every day. A mag released by a motion sensor a hundred times a day proves the sensor and the relay, and proves nothing whatsoever about the fire interface... a door can be miswired for the fire drop and behave flawlessly until the alarm actually sounds. That gap is invisible until you test it, or until the fire. And this is a recurring obligation rather than a commissioning task, so it belongs on the periodic test list.\n\nThe reflex worth building is short. Someone says fail-safe, and your hand is already going to NC, your brain to the fire and manual releases having to break power in series, and your kit to the suppressor at the lock. Someone says fail-secure, and it is NO, powered to unlock, with mechanical egress carrying the life-safety side. Failure mode to terminal to override... that sequence is the wiring fluency that separates a tech who follows a diagram from a lead who draws one.",sources:["https://www.ict.co/media/jwahkfsl/prt-ctrl-din_protege_gx_din_rail_system_controller_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.altronix.com/products/power-supplies-chargers","https://idighardware.com/2017/06/code-requirements-for-electromagnetic-locks/","https://www.locksmithledger.com/home/article/12441255/electromagnetic-locks-7-applications-and-the-codes-that-apply","Field practice, A&D access control crew 2026 - pre-energize checklist and failure signatures."],relatedGuides:["electric-strike-power-diagnosis","fail-safe-fail-secure","fire-alarm-interface","low-voltage-wiring","maglocks","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"],relatedModels:["access-control-plugin-power-supplies","ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-prt-px8-din","ict-prt-rdm2-din","keri-systems-pxl-nxt"],tags:["Dry contact","Relay","Fail-safe","Latch retraction","Wet contact","Form C","Fail-secure","Suppression"],aliases:["volt free","NO NC COM","clean contact","relay output","contact won't close","fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","transorb","TVS diode","surge suppressor","1N4004","flyback diode","spike suppression","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal"]},{id:"elevator-access-control",title:"Elevator access control and floor control",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Elevator access control restricts which floors a person can select. A reader goes in the cab, or on higher-end systems at a lobby kiosk, and presenting a valid credential enables only the floors that credential is allowed to call. It is floor control layered onto the elevator, not a lock on a door... nobody is ever locked in. They simply cannot register a floor they are not permitted to.\n\n[[diagram:elevator-interface-options|Low-level against high-level, the arithmetic between them, and where the trades meet]]\n\nThere are two ways to interface with the elevator, and which one you are on changes the entire job.\n\nThe traditional one is **low-level, relay-per-floor**, and it is what \"wiring an elevator reader\" usually means. The access controller drives a bank of relays, one per floor or per floor group. The cab reader sends the read to the controller, and on a valid credential the controller closes the relays for that person's allowed floors for a few seconds, enabling those buttons in the elevator's car-call circuit. No valid credential, the buttons do nothing. You need a relay board with as many outputs as there are floors... an elevator expander module or a dedicated relay board in the machine room.\n\nThe other is **high-level, a serial or network interface** to the elevator controller itself, sometimes called an HLI. The access system passes the credential and its floor permissions across to the elevator manufacturer's controller, and the elevator does the button logic. This is what destination dispatch requires, where a passenger badges at a lobby terminal and the system assigns them a car before they board. More capable and more expensive, and it needs the elevator manufacturer's interface module and their active cooperation.\n\nThe choice between the two is usually settled by arithmetic rather than preference, so do the sum early. Low-level scales as **floors times cars**. Twelve secured floors across three cars is thirty-six relay outputs, thirty-six enable pairs to terminate at the elevator company's end, and a reader run up every traveler. Add a car and it multiplies again. Somewhere on that curve the relay boards, the labour and the traveler conductors cost more than the elevator company's fee for a high-level interface, and past that point arguing for low-level is arguing to do more work for less money. Get the HLI quote before you assume it is the expensive option... and remember it is the ONLY option on destination dispatch, where there are no in-car buttons to enable in the first place.\n\n**The wiring reality is the traveler cable.** The cab reader has to move up and down with the car, so its conductors ride the traveler... the thick festooned flexible cable that follows the cab down the hoistway. Spare conductors in that traveler are the elevator company's resource, not yours to help yourself to. Coordinate early: tell them how many you need for reader data, reader power, and any monitoring, and they will run or assign them. Size for the drop, too, because that is a long run and a reader browning out at the top of the shaft is a miserable thing to chase.\n\n**Know who owns what, because this is a multi-trade job and the seams are where it goes wrong.** The elevator company owns the elevator controller and the car-call circuit... they land your enable relays into their button matrix and they tell you which terminals. You own the reader, the access controller, the relay board, and the credential database. Do not wire into the elevator controller's internals yourself. You hand over clean dry-contact enables and their technician integrates them. Get that interface scope in writing before anyone mobilizes, because \"who lands the relays\" discovered on site is a day lost.\n\nNot every floor is secured. The lobby is normally free access, and floors commonly flip between secured and free on a schedule... open through business hours, credentialed after. Program free floors to need no credential and secured floors to need an allowed one. Garage and amenity levels are the usual secured targets, which is exactly the mixed-use case where an elevator reaches resident parking from a public lobby.\n\n**Fire recall overrides all of it, and this is the non-negotiable line.** Under ASME A17.1 and NFPA 72 the fire alarm drives elevator recall, and recall must override access control completely. In Phase I the alarm recalls every car nonstop to the designated level, or to an alternate level when the fire is at the designated one, doors open, cars park. In Phase II a firefighter takes manual control with a key switch in the car operating panel, and Phase II overrides building security entirely so they reach every floor with no credential. Your floor-control relays must never be able to block recall or firefighter operation. Note what actually does the work: the fire alarm panel does not run the elevator... it signals the elevator controller, typically through recall relays for the designated level, the alternate level, and the firefighter's hat indicator for a fire in the hoistway or machine room, and the elevator controller performs the recall. Wire your enables so recall bypasses them. The elevator company and the AHJ own this sequence, and your only job is to make certain access control gets out of the way.\n\nThe reader normally mounts in or beside the car operating panel inside the cab. Some jobs add readers at each landing as well. On destination dispatch there is no in-cab floor selection at all, so the permissions ride the high-level interface and the cab may have no reader.\n\nMind where the reader lands, because in a cab it is an operable part and the panel beside it is governed. The ADA reach range puts the lowest operable part at 15 inches minimum and the highest at 48 inches for a front approach, or 54 inches on a side reach, and the elevator's emergency controls are grouped at the bottom of the panel with centrelines no lower than 35 inches. So put the reader inside that band, and do not let it crowd or obscure the emergency controls to get there. If the tidy answer is to cut into the car operating panel, that is the elevator company's panel and their work... ask, do not improvise, and price their involvement into the job rather than discovering it on installation day.\n\nFailures cluster predictably. No buttons work for anyone: the relay board is not enabling, the controller is offline, or the elevator company never landed the enables... confirm the controller actually sees the read, meter the enable relays, then verify the elevator-side terminations. A credential works but lights the wrong floors: the floor-to-relay mapping is wrong, or the relays are on the wrong car-call terminals... re-check your floor map against the elevator company's terminal list. Reader dead in the cab: a broken traveler conductor or voltage drop over that run... meter at the cab, with the elevator technician, not alone in the shaft. Buttons stay live long after the grant: the enable time is set too long, or a relay contact has welded. And if the elevator will not recall, or access control blocks firefighter operation, stop... that is a life-safety defect, not a programming preference. Recall and Phase II must bypass access control, and the elevator company and the AHJ need to be involved immediately.",sources:["Field practice, elevator floor-control installs - reader and relay coordination with the elevator company, no single public doc.","https://coderedconsultants.com/insights/fire-alarm-interface-with-elevator-recall/","https://elevatorworld.com/article/fire-alarm-systems-and-elevator-interaction/","https://up.codes/s/elevator-car-controls","https://www.access-board.gov/ada/guides/chapter-4-elevators-and-platform-lifts/","https://www.corada.com/documents/2010ADAStandards/407","https://www.lowvoltageinstaller.com/learning-center/fire-alarm/fire_alarm_wiring_for_elevator_recall_and_flash_hat_part-1"],relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","ada-operators","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","fail-safe-fail-secure","fire-alarm-interface","keypad-reader-servicing","low-voltage-wiring","sequence-of-operations"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-prt-rdm2-din"],tags:["Floor control","Car operating panel","Controller","Relay","Reader","Credential","AHJ","NFPA 72"],aliases:["access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","badge","card","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","wire gauge","sagging voltage","18 AWG","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"credential-technology-and-card-formats",title:"Credential formats: reading, matching and ordering cards",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Every credential has two independent things going on, and confusing them causes half of all \"why will this card not work\" calls. The physical layer... the frequency and chip, 125 kHz proximity or a 13.56 MHz smart card... is the carrier. The data layer... the bit format, 26-bit Wiegand and its relatives... is the number written onto it. You choose both, separately, and a card can be modern on one axis and obsolete on the other.\n\nThe near-universal open data format is 26-bit Wiegand, ordered from HID as H10301. Twenty-six bits: a leading even parity bit, then an 8-bit facility code, then a 16-bit card number, then a trailing odd parity bit. That is 256 possible facility codes with 65,535 card numbers each, so about 16.7 million unique cards, and the two parity bits are only a misread check. The security consequences of that format... how few facility codes there are, how guessable the number is, and how fast it copies off a 125 kHz chip... are covered in the credential cloning guide.\n\n[[diagram:wiegand-26bit|26-bit Wiegand (H10301): parity, 8-bit facility, 16-bit card, parity]]\n\n This guide is about the other half: living with formats on a service call.\n\n**Facility code and card number are both gates.** The facility code identifies the site or system; the card number identifies the person. The panel has to be programmed to accept both. A card carrying a perfectly valid number under a facility code the panel does not recognise will read cleanly and then deny... and that facility-code mismatch is the single most common cause of \"it scans but says no\" on a fresh batch of cards. When a new box of credentials fails site-wide, check the facility code before you suspect anything else.\n\n**Bit count is not the format, and this one catches people who should know better.** \"34-bit\" or \"37-bit\" does not name a single layout. Many different formats share a bit length while putting the facility and card fields in different places and at different widths. H10304 is a 37-bit format with a 16-bit facility code and a 19-bit card number, but a different 37-bit format can be laid out nothing like it. The panel has to be set for the exact format, not merely the right bit count, or it will parse the same bits into the wrong numbers. Longer formats also carry much larger facility and card ranges, which is part of why sites that care move past 26-bit.\n\n**Reading an unknown credential is a real field skill**, because you will inherit systems with no documentation at all. Start with the card itself: a printed site code, a serial, an iCLASS logo, or part numbers like 0009P or 0006L all narrow the technology down. Then read it... a reader that reports raw output, or a [[tool:flipper-zero|Flipper]], will give you the bit length, the facility code and the card number. Failing that, send a known-working card to a credential vendor, who will identify the format, the site code and the range. You need all three of those facts before you can order anything that will work.\n\n**Ordering more cards for an existing system is where all of this pays off.** To add credentials you have to match the exact format, use the site's own facility code, and choose a card-number range that does not overlap the numbers already issued. Overlap is worse than failure: two people badging as the same identity, with the event log unable to tell them apart. Pull those three facts from the customer's records or by reading a known-good card. Order blind and you get a batch where half the cards do not work and the other half collide with people who already have one.\n\n**The upgrade conversation is a lead's job.** A customer running 125 kHz 26-bit prox has convenience and very little security, and the fastest way to make that concrete is to show them rather than tell them... those cards copy in seconds in the hallway with a tool you already carry. The path from there is a 13.56 MHz encrypted credential, DESFire EV3 or SEOS, on an OSDP or vendor-encrypted RS-485 reader, which closes the hole at the card and at the wire in one move. Frame it as risk and liability rather than as features, and be honest that the reader and the credential both have to change for it to mean anything.",sources:["https://www.getkisi.com/blog/how-to-calculate-facility-code-using-card-bit-calculators","https://www.idesco.com/files/articles/HID%20-%20Understanding%20card%20formats.pdf","https://www.proxwriter.com/prox-formats/","https://accessgrid.com/tools/bit-formats/standard-26bit-wiegand-h10301"],relatedGuides:["rfid-credential-cloning","keypad-reader-servicing","access-control-systems","low-voltage-wiring"],relatedModels:["securakey-et-wxm","ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-tsec-reader"],tags:["Credential","Facility code","Bit format","Card number","Wiegand","Smart card","Event log","DESFire"],aliases:["badge","prox card","key tag","MIFARE","card won't work","FC","card range","wrong facility code","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","card reader","badge reader","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone"]},{id:"sequence-of-operations",title:"Door modes and writing a sequence of operations",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A sequence of operations is the written spec of how every door behaves, in every mode, under every condition. It turns \"install a reader\" into \"here is exactly what this opening does at nine in the morning, at midnight, on a holiday, during a lockdown, and on a fire alarm.\" A tech wires what the diagram shows. A lead writes the SOO that the diagram serves. Producing a clear one for a building is the clearest single signal that you are thinking like an engineer rather than installing hardware.\n\n[[diagram:soo-worked-example|One row per opening, and the two columns people leave blank]]\n\nBefore you can write one you need the vocabulary, because every access platform has the same handful of door modes under different marketing names.\n\n**Card Only** is secure and is the resting state of most doors: locked, opens on a valid credential. **Unlocked**, also called passage, hold-open or free access, means the door stays open to anyone with no credential... a public entrance through business hours. **PIN Only** and **Card plus PIN** cover keypad codes and two-factor, and Card plus PIN commonly appears as a scheduled elevation during secure hours or as something a lockdown forces on. **First Person In**, sometimes first card unlock, is a scheduled unlock that will not engage until an authorised person actually badges in. **Lockdown** forces doors locked and ignores unlock schedules on command. And a **double-tap toggle**, which some platforms call DoorStat, lets an authorised user present a credential twice to latch a door locked or unlocked regardless of schedule... the front desk holding the lobby secure until someone is there to cover it.\n\nEvery mode change is driven by one of five triggers, and a good SOO names the trigger for each behavior rather than only the behavior. A schedule fires on time of day and week. A manual command comes from an operator in software. A credential drives first-person-in and the double-tap toggle. A physical input covers a lockdown button or a request to exit. And an emergency, meaning the fire interface, overrides the rest.\n\n**First Person In is the one people botch, and the failure is a security hole rather than an inconvenience.** A plain unlock schedule opens the door at the scheduled time whether or not anybody has arrived, so someone who beats the manager in walks into an unlocked store at opening. First Person In fixes exactly that: the schedule arms but the opening stays secure until the first authorised credential, and only then does it follow the unlock schedule. The field trap is the mirror image... if the site is unexpectedly closed and an authorised person turns up off-schedule, they can trip the unlock and then, on some platforms, find they cannot re-lock it from the reader. Spell out the re-lock method in the SOO rather than discovering it on a holiday. It is also the first thing to reach for on the call that comes in as \"the door will not unlock on schedule, but our cards still work.\" A working card proves power, reader, relay and comms are all healthy, which leaves the schedule as the only thing left standing... First Person In waiting on a credential that never arrived, or a time zone, holiday list or door mode pointed at the wrong thing.\n\n**Lockdown has two hard rules.** Egress is never defeated: lockdown stops entry, and people must always be able to get out. Detention, mantraps and sally ports are the narrow exceptions, and if you are not working on one of those, egress stays free. Second, plan the exit from lockdown before you deploy it... who clears it, by what method, and what happens if the person who set it is unreachable. It also has to hold without a live server connection, which means the door controller enforces it locally. Worth speccing as extras: elevating readers to Card plus PIN for the duration, suspending normal credentials, and issuing a first-responder credential set that works only while lockdown is active.\n\n**Fire always wins, and the SOO has to say so explicitly.** A door can sit in any access mode you like, but on alarm the egress locks release per the fire interface. Access modes govern entry and convenience; life safety governs egress and takes priority over all of it. Never write an SOO in which an access mode can block egress or defeat a fire release, and never let a reviewer assume it... put the sentence in.\n\nWriting one is a per-opening exercise, because a front entrance, an IT room and a stairwell each behave differently. For every door, answer: the hardware and its failure mode, the normal secure state, the scheduled behavior including days and holidays, how it grants access, any first-person-in requirement, the request-to-exit method, the door-held and door-forced alarm behavior, the lockdown behavior, and the fire-alarm release. That set of answers is the document.\n\nThere is a sixth question the modes above do not answer, and it is the one most of your service calls will happen inside: **what does this door do when the head end is gone?** Access controllers are not terminals... they keep deciding on their own. A [[model:keri-systems-pxl-nxt|legacy Keri PXL]] holds its own cardholder list and grants locally with the PC switched off, and an [[model:ict-prt-rdm2-din|ICT reader expander]] has an explicit offline access mode you configure in advance. So the opening HAS a degraded behaviour whether or not anyone wrote one down. The only question is whether you chose it or inherited it. Give every row an offline cell: grants locally from the last download, fails secure and needs a key, or falls open. Then say how long the controller keeps deciding and what happens to the events it buffered. A sequence of operations that describes only the healthy system is silent about the state the building is actually in when somebody phones you.\n\nSign it, and commission against it rather than against a feeling. Walk the site row by row with the customer or their rep and prove every cell: present a credential in each mode, run the schedule forward, press the REX, drop the comms and try the door again. The fire column is the one you do not test alone... put the panel into alarm with the [[guide:fire-alarm-interface|fire contractor]] present, confirm every affected door releases and stays released until reset, then reset and confirm each re-secures. When it all passes, both parties sign the document. That signature is what turns an unsigned wish into acceptance criteria, and on the day a customer insists a door was supposed to behave differently, the signed sequence is the only thing in the room that remembers what was agreed.\n\nIts value outlives the install twice over. It is your acceptance criteria, so you commission against it door by door instead of against a feeling that the system seems fine. And it is what protects you when a customer says the door was supposed to do something else... if the behavior is in the signed SOO it is scope, and if it is not, it is a change order. Equal parts engineering spec and commercial protection, which is exactly why it belongs at the lead's desk.",sources:["https://help.getgenea.com/en/articles/1058817-automatic-door-schedules-how-to-change-the-access-mode-of-a-door-on-a-regular-schedule","https://blog.dga.com/what-are-the-first-person-in-and-doorstat-features-in-access-control-systems","https://www.dsxinc.com/pdfs/emergencylockdownconfig.pdf","https://ipvm.com/discussions/lockdown-buttons-in-a-ip-based-access-control-system"],relatedGuides:["access-control-grammar-and-the-lead-role","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","elevator-access-control","fail-safe-fail-secure","fire-alarm-interface","keri-pxl-takeover","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-prt-px8-din","ict-prt-rdm2-din","keri-systems-pxl-nxt"],tags:["Sequence of operations","Lockdown","First person in","Schedule","Credential","Request to exit","Cardholder","Controller"],aliases:["hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","access panel","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","NFPA 101","IBC","fire marshal","egress code","code compliance","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close"]},{id:"wiring-and-testing-a-reader-kt400",title:"Wiring and testing a card reader on a Kantech KT-400",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"The Kantech KT-400 is a 4-door Ethernet controller, managed by EntraPass. Its reader, input and output terminals sit on removable plug-in terminal blocks... the green Phoenix-style connectors that lift straight off the board. That detail is the whole trick to a clean reader add: you never work the board live, and you never disturb the readers already landed. Pull only the plug for the port you are adding, land the new reader on it in your hand, and snap it back. Every existing wire stays terminated on its own plug, untouched.\n\nCheck three things before you strip a wire. Reader voltage first... the KT-400's reader terminal is +12 VDC, and a 5 VDC reader's red wire on that terminal will damage the reader, so read the head's spec and power a 5 V unit properly. Then technology and format: each of the four reader ports is programmable for Wiegand or ABA clock-and-data, and Kantech's own ioProx heads run 26-bit Wiegand or Kantech XSF, field-selectable. You cannot mix Wiegand, ABA and OSDP readers on the same KT-400, so establish what the site runs before you order anything. Then the power budget... the board feeds reader power, and another head is another load to add up before you hang it on there.\n\nThe standard Kantech Wiegand colour map lands as red to +12 VDC, black to ground, green to Data 0 and white to Data 1. The feedback leads go to that door's auxiliary outputs, brown for the LED and the buzzer lead alongside it, which is what lights the reader and beeps it on a grant. A tamper pair lands on the matching host tamper input. Ground the shield or drain at the controller end only, as on any shielded reader run.\n\n[[diagram:kt400-reader-wiring|KT-400 Wiegand reader colours, and the two mistakes that cost the most time]]\n\n\n\nThe mod-plug procedure, in order. Identify the reader port you are adding, door 1 through 4, and its removable terminal block. Pull only that plug off its header... if you are replacing a head, the other three readers' plugs stay seated and terminated, and if you are filling a spare port, that port's plug is the only one that moves. With the plug off the board and in your hand, strip and land the new reader under the correct screws: red to +12, black to ground, green to D0, white to D1, then the LED and buzzer leads to the aux outputs. Tug-test every conductor. Leave the other plugs exactly as they are. Then re-seat the loaded plug firmly onto its header, and that plug is the only contact you have made with the board at all.\n\n[[diagram:kt400-board-layout|What is on the board, and the two current budgets that catch people out]]\n\nAn ioSmart RS-485 head is wired differently and the colour map does not carry over. Red still goes to +12 and black to ground, but the data goes RS-485-A to COM2 485+ and RS-485-B to COM2 485-, and the COM2 bus speed has to be set in both the reader and EntraPass. Land an RS-485 reader on the Wiegand data terminals and you get nothing at all. Note which way Kantech labels that pair... A on 485+ and B on 485-, the reverse of the A-is-inverting convention you will read elsewhere. Follow the board's legend, not the convention, and if the head is silent on a bus you believe is correct, swap the two data conductors before you suspect the reader. If you would rather prove the head before it ever goes near the board, a [[tool:rs485-to-usb-converter|485-to-USB converter]] powers it on the bench and puts its raw output on a laptop.\n\nThen set the reader type in software, because the controller has to be told what it is looking at. In EntraPass, or the standalone web interface, set the door's reader type and card format to match the head... Wiegand 26-bit, XSF, whatever it actually is. A perfectly wired reader configured as the wrong type reads nothing or returns garbage, and this is the step people skip and then chase for an hour with a meter in their hand.\n\nTesting runs start to finish. Power up and watch the head: most beep and flash at power-up, so a dead reader at that moment is almost always missing V+ or missing ground... put the [[tool:multimeter|meter]] on +12, or 5, and ground right at the reader head, under load. Then present a valid card and watch the EntraPass live events for a read on that door. The reader's own LED and beep only confirm local power and a read attempt; the event log is what confirms the data actually reached the KT-400. No read at all means checking that green and white are not swapped, that V+ and ground are present at the head, and that the reader type and format are right in software... swapped data lines are the classic reader that beeps and registers nothing. A read that denies means the card's format or facility code does not match what is configured, or the card was never enrolled. Last, confirm a valid card actually throws the door relay... if it grants in software and the door does not move, you are on the lock and relay side now, not the reader.\n\nThe reason to leave the other plugs alone is risk control rather than tidiness. Working a live board, or re-terminating readers that were working fine, risks shorting a reader port, dropping the other three doors, or miswiring an opening nobody asked you to touch. Pulling one plug isolates the job to the port you are adding... the rest of the system keeps running, and if you fumble a conductor it happens on the plug in your hand rather than on an energised board.",sources:["https://docs.johnsoncontrols.com/kantech/v/u/Kantech/en-US/KT-400-Ethernet-Four-Door-Controller-Datasheet","https://www.turnstiles.us/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/KT-400-Installation-Manual-www-TURNSTILES-us-w.pdf","https://www.anixter.com/content/dam/Suppliers/Kantech/Literature/KT-400%20Four%20Door%20Controller%20-%20LITERATURE.pdf","https://www.manualslib.com/manual/3288318/Johnson-Controls-Tyco-Kantech-Kt-400.html"],relatedGuides:["keypad-reader-servicing","credential-technology-and-card-formats","low-voltage-wiring","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring"],relatedModels:["kantech-kt-400","securakey-et-wxm","ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-tsec-reader","ict-prt-rdm2-din"],tags:["Reader","Wiegand","RS-485","Card format","Terminal block","Controller","Event log","Plug"],aliases:["badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","panel","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","board in the closet","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins","site code","FC","card range","wrong facility code","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"networking-basics-for-security-techs",title:"Networking basics for security techs",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"Modern access control and cameras ride the same IP network as everything else on site. You do not need to be a network engineer, but you need enough to plug a controller or an NVR in, get it talking, and troubleshoot it when it will not. This is the working vocabulary, and it is the half of the trade most door techs never close.\n\n[[diagram:network-basics-for-techs|Switch, router, VLAN and DHCP... the four words that have to mean the right thing]]\n\nA **switch** connects devices inside one local network. It forwards traffic by MAC address, learning which device sits on which port and sending each frame only where it needs to go. That is layer 2... it moves frames within a subnet. Your cameras, NVR and access controllers usually all land on a switch.\n\nA **router** connects different networks and moves traffic between them by IP address, which is layer 3. The box that gives a site its internet is a router, usually with a switch and Wi-Fi built into the same chassis. Anything that has to leave the local subnet goes through it. A camera talking to an NVR on the same subnet never touches the router; a camera you view from off-site does.\n\nA **layer 3 switch** is a switch that can also route between subnets in hardware, at switch speed. On a larger job you put cameras on their own VLAN and access control on another, isolated from the customer's data network, and the L3 switch routes between those VLANs quickly rather than hauling the traffic back to a slower router. It is not a full WAN router... no internet features, just fast internal routing.\n\n**NAT**, network address translation, lets many devices on private addresses share one public IP to reach the internet, with the router rewriting addresses on the way out and back. Two consequences matter on site. Devices on the LAN talk to each other freely, but reaching the system from outside means getting through NAT... a port forward, a VPN, or the manufacturer's cloud relay. And double NAT, a second router sitting behind the first, is a classic reason remote viewing quietly stops working with nothing obviously broken.\n\n**PoE** carries power and data on one Cat5e or Cat6 run, so an IP camera or reader needs no separate supply at the device. Know the tiers by what the device actually draws. 802.3af delivers up to 15.4 W at the switch and about 12.95 W at the device, which covers fixed indoor cameras and VoIP phones. 802.3at, PoE+, delivers up to 30 W at the switch and about 25.5 W at the device... cameras with IR or motorised lenses, PTZ heads, modern access points. 802.3bt, PoE++, delivers 60 W on Type 3 (about 51 W at the device) or 90 W on Type 4 (about 71 W), for heated PTZ domes and high-power APs. A higher-tier switch powers a lower-tier device without complaint, but a hungry device on an underpowered port simply will not come up. Spec the switch to the hungriest device and keep a power budget for the whole switch, not just per port... a PTZ with a built-in heater can pull 45 W and will never run on plain PoE.\n\n**Bandwidth and throughput are not the same thing**, and the gap between them is where camera jobs go wrong. Bandwidth is the theoretical maximum a link can carry, the size of the pipe... a gigabit port is 1000 Mbps of bandwidth. Throughput is what you actually get after protocol overhead, cheap cable, distance and contention with everything else on the wire. They are never equal. It bites on camera systems because a stack of 4K cameras can generate more real traffic than the link, or the recorder's write path, can absorb. If aggregate camera bitrate outruns throughput to the recorder, you drop frames, and you drop them during motion, which is exactly when the footage mattered. Size the network to the real stream load rather than to the number printed on the port.",sources:["https://www.tp-link.com/us/blog/2595/poe-vs-poe+-vs-poe++-power-over-ethernet-standards/","https://www.fs.com/blog/understanding-poe-standards-and-wattage-21.html","Field practice, IP access control and camera installs 2026 - networking fundamentals, no single public doc."],relatedGuides:["video-surveillance","surveillance-storage-raid-redundancy","low-voltage-wiring","access-control-grammar-and-the-lead-role","dw-spectrum-lts-cameras"],tags:["NAT","VLAN","Layer 3 switch","MAC address","IP camera","VPN","IP address","DHCP"],aliases:["panel","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","barrel","core","key plug","plug won't turn","key spins"]},{id:"surveillance-storage-raid-redundancy",title:"Surveillance storage: RAID, redundancy and drive integrity",module:"Alarms, Cameras & Perimeter",body:"A recorder writes many camera streams around the clock and has to hold them for a retention window... thirty days is the usual insurance and police ask... while surviving a drive failure without losing footage. Two separate problems live in that sentence: how much capacity you need, and how not to lose it when a disk dies.\n\n[[diagram:raid-and-retention|Sizing from retention, and what each RAID level actually survives]]\n\n**Sizing follows retention.** Storage needed is total camera bitrate times recording hours times retention days. Per camera, a 4MP camera at 20 fps on H.265 averages roughly 3 to 4 Mbps in a normal scene and spikes to 8 to 12 Mbps under heavy motion, which is about 1.1 to 1.5 TB over thirty days of continuous recording. The quick arithmetic is cameras times bitrate in Mbps times 3600 times 24 times retention days, divided by 8, for bytes. Sixteen cameras at 4 Mbps on H.265 for thirty days lands near 20 TB before any RAID overhead... which is just that 1.3 TB per camera multiplied by sixteen, and it is worth doing the multiplication out loud, because a single camera's figure quoted for a whole system is the classic way a job gets sold a recorder a tenth the size it needed. Add 30 to 50 percent headroom on top for motion spikes, smart-codec swing and growth. Most recorders auto-overwrite the oldest footage once full, so the real sizing question to ask the customer is not how many terabytes they want but how many days they need to keep.\n\n**RAID is not backup, and you should say that out loud.** RAID protects against a disk dying. It does nothing about deletion, a stolen recorder, fire, or corruption. What it buys is that the array keeps running through a failure so you can swap a drive without losing footage. A customer who believes RAID means they are backed up is a customer who will lose everything the day the recorder walks out of the closet. A second copy, ideally off-site, is backup; RAID is uptime.\n\nThe levels worth knowing. **RAID 1**, a mirror, is two drives holding identical copies at 50 percent usable capacity... simple, survives one drive, and the right answer on a small two-bay recorder. **RAID 5**, single parity across three or more drives, survives any one drive and costs one drive's worth of capacity, which is 25 to 33 percent on a four-drive set... the common commercial pick. Its weakness is that a rebuild reads every surviving drive, which is a stressful few hours on a large array. **RAID 6**, double parity across four or more drives, survives two simultaneous failures for about 20 percent more overhead than RAID 5, and it is the right call on big arrays and large drives where a second failure during a long rebuild is a genuine risk rather than a theoretical one. **RAID 10**, mirroring plus striping across four or more drives in even numbers, is fast to run and fast to rebuild with good redundancy, at the cost of half your capacity. **RAID 0** stripes with no redundancy at all, so one bad drive loses everything... never for surveillance. If the recorder supports a hot spare bay, use it, so the array starts rebuilding the moment a drive drops rather than when somebody notices.\n\n**Drive choice is not a detail.** Use surveillance-rated drives... WD Purple, Seagate SkyHawk, Toshiba S300. They are firmware-tuned for constant multi-stream writes, using ATA streaming commands that prioritise not dropping frames over retrying a marginal sector, and they are rated for 24/7 duty at around 180 TB a year with vibration tolerance for multi-drive bays. Desktop drives like WD Blue or Barracuda are rated for eight to ten hours a day and typically fail with bad sectors inside six to eighteen months under continuous CCTV write load. Match every drive in an array on model and capacity, or you waste the extra space on the larger drive and invite sync problems. Avoid SMR, shingled, drives in an array entirely... they stall under rebuild load and can be evicted as failed while perfectly healthy. Watch SMART for reallocated and pending sectors as your early warning, and put the recorder on a UPS good for at least fifteen minutes, because a power cut mid-write can corrupt RAID metadata and take out considerably more than the footage.",sources:["https://pvrblog.com/tech/raid/","https://www.ipsecuritydepot.com/guides/video-storage-hard-drive-buying-guide/","https://cctvinfo.com/best/nvr-hard-drives","https://raidsize.com/blog/en/raid-guide-complete"],relatedGuides:["networking-basics-for-security-techs","video-surveillance","dw-spectrum-lts-cameras"],tags:["RAID","Bitrate","Codec"]},{id:"stairwell-reentry",title:"Stairwell re-entry: getting back OUT of the stair",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Everybody thinks about the stair as the way out of the building. The code also has to think about it as somewhere you can get trapped. If the stair fills with smoke three floors down, or the discharge is blocked, an occupant needs to leave the stair and cross a tenant floor to reach a different one. That is stairwell re-entry, and it is the reason a stair door is not just another locked opening. It is also the single most common place a well-meaning access-control install quietly creates a code violation, because locking a stair door from the stair side is exactly what a security-minded customer will ask you to do.\n\nStart from the default and work outwards. IBC 2021 Section 1010.2.7 says interior stairway means-of-egress doors have to be openable from BOTH sides without a key, special knowledge or effort. Read plainly, that means full re-entry: passage hardware, or fire exit hardware with passage trim, and you can walk back onto any floor. Nothing electrified, nothing to fail. If the customer has no security requirement on the stair, this is the answer and you are done.\n\nThe exceptions are where the work is. In a high-rise, IBC Section 403.5.3 lets the doors be locked from the stairway side provided they unlock SIMULTANEOUSLY and WITHOUT UNLATCHING on a signal from the fire command center, and provided the stair carries a two-way communication system to a constantly attended station at not less than every fifth floor. Since the 2018 edition there is also a remote-release path for buildings that are not high-rise, where the doors release without unlatching from a switch at the fire command center or at a single location just inside the main entrance. NFPA 101 approaches the same problem with more flexibility: doors unlock automatically on initiation of the fire alarm, stairs serving four storeys or fewer may stay mechanically locked with no remote release, and it explicitly permits SELECTED re-entry... some doors locked, some not, with a minimum of two re-entry levels, no more than four consecutive locked levels, and signage telling you where the next open floor is. Confirm which code and which edition your AHJ has adopted before you quote any of this, because the answer genuinely changes the hardware.\n\nTwo words in there do the heavy lifting and people skim straight past them. **Simultaneously** means one signal releases every stair door in the building at once... not door by door from software, not as an operator task. **Without unlatching** means the release drops the lock without retracting the latch, so a fire door stays positively latched in its frame and keeps doing its fire-rating job while still being openable by hand. A device that unlatches the door on the fire signal has just propped open every stair door in a fire, which is the opposite of what you want.\n\nThat pair of words is what decides your hardware, and it is where most people get it wrong. **You cannot use a fail-safe electric strike on a labelled fire door.** A fail-safe strike releases its keeper when power drops, which means the door is no longer positively latched, which breaks the fire label... and every stair door worth talking about is a labelled fire door. So the strike side is out. What you use instead is a fail-safe electrified lockset or fail-safe electrified trim: the latch stays thrown and doing its job, and what the power controls is whether the outside lever is connected to it. Cut power, the lever turns and lets you in, and the door was latched the entire time. That is how you get released-but-still-latched, and it is the whole reason [[model:von-duprin-6211|fail-secure is the rule on strikes at rated openings]] while stair doors run fail-safe trim instead.\n\nFail-safe here also has to mean fail-safe for real, not just on the fire relay. Power loss releases it. Fire alarm releases it. The fire command centre switch releases it. Those are three independent paths to the same result and an inspector may test all three. Wire the release so it interrupts power to the trim directly, the same discipline as a mag lock's manual release, rather than asking the access panel to please issue an unlock command... a controller that has crashed, lost its network, or is waiting on a schedule is not a life-safety device.\n\n[[diagram:stairwell-reentry-options|The four arrangements, and which hardware each one actually allows]]\n\nSelective re-entry deserves its own paragraph because it is the arrangement customers actually want and the one you can most easily get wrong. The idea is that the stair stays secure on most floors but you can always get out of it within a couple of levels. NFPA's shape for that is at least two re-entry levels and never more than four locked levels in a row, plus signage inside the stair at each landing saying which way to the nearest floor you can leave on. Miss the signage and you have built something that is technically compliant and useless in smoke, because nobody knows whether to go up or down. When you commission it, walk the stair from the top landing to the bottom and physically try every door, then do it again with the fire alarm active. The as-built and the [[guide:sequence-of-operations|sequence of operations]] should name every floor and its state.\n\nThe last trap is the one that turns up on service rather than install. Somebody adds a card reader to a stair door years after handover, sets it to card-only, and never touches the fire interface because the door already had one. Now the opening is secure from the stair side with no release path that anybody tested. If you are called to a stair door for any reason, test the release before you leave, whatever you were actually there for. It is thirty seconds and it is the difference between a door and a trap.",sources:["https://up.codes/s/stairway-doors","https://up.codes/s/stairway-door-operation","https://idighardware.com/2016/10/decoded-stairwell-reentry-december-2016/","https://idighardware.com/2023/08/qq-locking-interior-stairway-doors/","https://buildershardware.com/Portals/0/Stairway%20Reentry%20Release%20Methods.pdf"],relatedGuides:["closing-fire-code","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","fail-safe-fail-secure","fire-alarm-interface","fire-door-inspection","maglocks","sequence-of-operations"],relatedModels:["von-duprin-6211","schlage-ad-series","hes-1006","sargent-8200"],tags:["Fail-safe","As-built","Sequence of operations","Keeper","Electric strike","Fail-secure","Strike","Schedule"],aliases:["fail unlocked","fail open","unlocks on power loss","mag lock behavior","strike keeper","gate keeper","receiver","HES","Adams Rite","Von Duprin 6211","Trine","buzz-in","door buzzer","buzzer strike","won't buzz open","electric door opener","fail locked","stays locked on power loss","strike behavior","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","fire marshal","building inspector","authority having jurisdiction","who signs off","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading"]},{id:"biometric-readers",title:"Biometric readers: matching, failure modes and the legal exposure",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A biometric reader is the only credential you cannot re-issue. A card that gets cloned is a five-dollar problem and a new card number. A fingerprint that leaks is permanent, and that single asymmetry drives everything else about how you sell, install and document these... including the part where the customer can be sued over a reader you fitted.\n\n[[diagram:biometric-matching-and-far-frr|Where the template lives, and the single dial behind FAR and FRR]]\n\nThe modalities you will actually meet on a door are fingerprint, face, and increasingly vein. Fingerprint splits into optical, which photographs the ridge pattern and is cheap and easily fooled, and capacitive, which senses the ridges electrically and is the better choice on anything that matters. Face is the one growing fastest because it is contactless and works through the flu season, but it is the most affected by lighting, and cheap units are demonstrably beatable with a photograph. Vascular or vein readers image the pattern of blood vessels under the skin, which is much harder to lift off a coffee cup than a fingerprint, at a higher price. Iris exists and is superb and you will almost never see it outside a data centre or a lab.\n\nThe number every vendor quotes is FAR, false accept rate, because it is the one that sounds like security... how often it lets in someone it should not. The number that decides whether the customer will still be speaking to you in a month is FRR, false REJECT rate: how often it refuses a legitimate, enrolled person. The two trade against each other on the same dial, and a reader tuned to a spectacular FAR will reject your customer's warehouse crew every wet Monday morning. Ask for both, ask at what threshold, and ask what the EER is where they cross. Then tune it for the population and the door: a server-room door can afford to be fussy, a shift entrance at 6 a.m. with four hundred people cannot.\n\nWhere the matching happens matters more than the modality, and it is the question almost nobody asks. Match-on-device keeps the templates in the reader on the wall, which means the wall now holds a biometric database and a stolen reader is a data breach. Match-on-server sends the sample to a controller or a cloud service, which centralises the exposure and makes the network a life-or-death dependency for the door. Match-on-card stores the template on the user's own smart card and compares locally, so the system as a whole holds no biometric database at all... the user carries their own. From a liability point of view match-on-card is the one to reach for first, and it is the one to raise when a customer asks you what the risk is.\n\nBe honest with people about templates, too. Vendors will tell you a template is not an image and cannot be reversed. That is broadly true and it is also not the point... a template is still biometric data in the eyes of every statute that matters, and treating it as anonymous because it is a hash is how organisations end up in court.\n\nWhich brings up the part that is genuinely yours to raise. **Illinois BIPA** requires informed WRITTEN consent before collecting a biometric identifier, plus a published retention and destruction schedule, and it carries a private right of action at 1,000 to 5,000 dollars per violation... which is why the eye-watering settlements you have read about are almost all Illinois. Texas CUBI and Washington have their own versions enforced by the state rather than by private suit. **California has no BIPA equivalent**, and anyone telling you otherwise is guessing; what California does is fold biometric information into the CCPA as amended by the CPRA, where it is SENSITIVE personal information with a right to limit its use, and the old employee-data exemption expired on 1 January 2023, so a company's own staff now have the same rights as its customers. Practically: get written consent, publish a retention schedule, delete templates when someone leaves, and put all three in the handover documents. You are not the customer's lawyer and should not pretend to be, but a locksmith who raises this before the install is worth keeping and one who does not is a liability.\n\nDesign the door as though the reader will fail, because sometimes it will. Trades with worn or chemically-damaged fingerprints, dry hands in winter, gloves, cuts, a bandage, and a percentage of any population simply do not enrol well on fingerprint at all. There must be an alternate path... a card, a PIN, or an attended entrance... and that is an accessibility and ADA question as much as a convenience one. Never build an opening where the only way in is a body part. Throughput matters too: a card is instant, a biometric read is a second or two per person, and one reader on a shift door at start of shift builds a queue that ends with somebody propping it.\n\nOn the install itself the fundamentals do not change. It is a reader, so it lands on Wiegand or, preferably, [[guide:credential-technology-and-card-formats|OSDP]] and everything in the reader wiring material still applies. What does change is enrolment: budget real time for it, enrol in the conditions people will actually use it in rather than at a clean desk, take multiple samples per person, and enrol a second finger for everyone because the first one will eventually be bandaged. Site the unit out of direct sun and driving rain, both of which wreck face and optical fingerprint units. And put the sensor-cleaning interval in the maintenance schedule... an unwiped optical sensor climbs the reject rate steadily until the customer decides biometrics do not work.",sources:["https://www.aclu-il.org/campaigns-initiatives/biometric-information-privacy-act-bipa/","https://www.termsfeed.com/blog/ccpa-biometrics/","https://www.californiaemploymentlawreport.com/2020/02/employee-biometric-data-issues-under-california-law/","https://www.nist.gov/programs-projects/face-recognition-vendor-test-frvt","https://pages.nist.gov/frvt/reports/11/frvt_11_report.pdf"],relatedGuides:["keypad-reader-servicing","credential-technology-and-card-formats","access-control-systems","rfid-credential-cloning","pacs-hardening"],relatedModels:["ict-tsec-reader","schlage-ad-series","hid-reader"],tags:["Reader","Schedule","Smart card","Card number","Wiegand","OSDP","Credential","Controller"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","hardware schedule","door schedule","spec book","submittal","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","26-bit","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","panel","access panel","head end","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"anti-passback",title:"Anti-passback: what it enforces, and how it turns into callbacks",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Anti-passback stops one credential being used twice in the same direction. Badge in, and your card will not badge you in again until it has been used to badge OUT. It exists to kill exactly one behaviour: handing your card back through the door, or over the fence, so your mate can follow you in on it. Every other benefit people claim for it... accurate occupancy, muster lists, tailgate prevention... falls out of that one rule or does not exist at all.\n\nThe first thing to understand is what it costs you architecturally, because this is where jobs go wrong before anybody writes a line of configuration. Anti-passback needs to know which WAY you went, and a door only knows that if there is a reader on BOTH sides. A reader in and a push-to-exit button out cannot support it, because the button is anonymous... the system never learns you left. So enabling anti-passback on an opening that was wired reader-in / REX-out gives you a system that lets everybody in exactly once and then locks them out permanently. If a customer asks for anti-passback, your first answer is a question about exit readers and a number for the extra hardware, not a configuration change.\n\nThe flavours are worth naming because vendors use different words for the same three things. **Hard or true** anti-passback denies the second entry outright. **Soft** anti-passback grants it but logs a violation, which is what you want when the goal is really an audit trail rather than enforcement, and it is dramatically less likely to generate angry phone calls. **Timed** anti-passback ignores direction entirely and just refuses the same credential at the same reader within a set window, thirty seconds or five minutes; it is the poor-man's version that works with no exit reader at all and it stops pass-back-through-the-window without stopping much else. Then there is scope: **area or regional** anti-passback tracks which zone a credential is in and enforces sensible movement between zones, and **global** does it across an entire site with multiple controllers, which is where you start depending on the head end being alive.\n\nWhich brings up the failure that will define your relationship with this feature. **Anti-passback state is a memory, and memories get lost.** When the panel reboots, when a module drops off the network and makes its own decisions, when someone is let through by a guard, when a door is propped, or when a person tailgates in behind a colleague without badging... the system's idea of where that credential is diverges from reality. The credential is now recorded inside when the person is outside, and it will be refused at the next reader. The user did nothing wrong and the system is working exactly as designed, which is a hard conversation. This is worth thinking about alongside a [[model:ict-prt-rdm2-din|reader expander's offline access mode]]: a module granting access on its own while the network is down is by definition not updating anybody's passback state.\n\n[[diagram:anti-passback-states|Why the state drifts, and the reset you configure on day one]]\n\nSo before you enable it, find the reset. Every platform has some combination of a forgive command that clears one credential, an amnesty or reset-all that clears the site, and a scheduled automatic forgive... commonly nightly, at a time when the building is genuinely empty. Configure the scheduled one from the start. A site with hard anti-passback and no nightly forgive will generate calls forever, and you will spend your evenings clearing individuals by hand for faults that were never their doing. Give the customer's own staff the forgive command too, and write it into the handover, because at 6 a.m. it is their problem to unstick and yours only if you kept the knowledge.\n\nJudgment on when it earns its keep. It is genuinely right for parking structures, where one card feeding a queue of cars is the whole fraud and the gates already have readers both ways. It is right for a data centre, a cash room, a controlled-substance store... small populations, high stakes, disciplined users. It is a reasonable soft-mode audit tool anywhere you want to see pass-back happening without punishing it. It is usually wrong on a busy staff entrance with a large casual population, on any door where a REX button covers the exit, and on anything where a propped door is routine, because you are choosing to convert an existing social problem into a stream of lockouts.\n\nAnd be straight with the customer about what it does not do. **Anti-passback does not stop tailgating.** It stops one credential doing two entries; it does nothing at all about two bodies through one grant, which is by far the more common way people get into buildings they should not. If tailgating is the actual worry the answer is a turnstile, a mantrap, or a person, not a checkbox. Selling anti-passback as a tailgating fix is the fastest way to be blamed for the next incident.",sources:["https://www.getkisi.com/blog/anti-passback","https://www.ict.co/media/jwahkfsl/prt-ctrl-din_protege_gx_din_rail_system_controller_installation_manual.pdf","https://www.affordableopeners.com/manuals/telephone_entry/sentex/man.pdf"],relatedGuides:["access-control-systems","sequence-of-operations","elevator-access-control","social-engineering-tailgating","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"],relatedModels:["ict-protege-prt-ctrl-din","ict-prt-rdm2-din","kantech-kt-400","sentex-infinity-telephone-entry"],tags:["Anti-passback","Tailgating","Muster","Mantrap","Credential","Reader","REX","Handing"],aliases:["APB","no re-entry","buddy punching","piggyback","following through the door","door held open","airlock","interlock","double door interlock","sally port","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","request to exit","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","LH","RH","LHR","RHR","left hand","right hand","which way does the door swing","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]},{id:"pacs-hardening",title:"Hardening what you install: defaults, segmentation and the reader on the wrong side",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Every access control and camera system you install is a computer network with a door on the end of it, and you are the one who decides whether it ships secure. Nobody else is going to. The customer does not know to ask, the manufacturer ships convenient defaults on purpose, and the GC wants it working by Friday. This is the short list that separates a professional install from a liability, and none of it takes long if you do it as you go rather than as a revisit.\n\n[[img:photo-ict-tsec-extra-reader|The reader is where the protocol argument stops being theoretical... this one speaks Wiegand, ICT RS-485 or OSDP.]]\n\nStart with credentials, because this is the one that actually gets buildings opened. Every platform in this catalogue ships with a published default and they are all one search away: an [[model:ses-tec1-series|SES panel on 777777]], a [[model:linear-access-telephone-entry-panel|Linear on 123456]], a Sentex on 000000, DoorKing panels on their factory master. Video is no better, and door stations are worse... an [[model:akuvox-video-door-station|Akuvox will open its door over a plain HTTP request]] with the username and password in the URL, so a default-credentialed unit on a reachable network is a door anybody can open from a browser. Change every default before the system goes live, record the new values in the customer's documentation rather than in your phone, and treat any panel you inherit on a factory default as a finding worth writing down. On the newer video gear, note that some manufacturers now ship un-activated with no default password precisely because of this... do not undo that by setting something obvious.\n\nThen put the system somewhere sensible on the network. Access control and cameras belong on their own VLAN or their own physical switch, not on the tenant's office LAN with the printers. Nothing in a normal PACS needs to be reachable from the internet, and a door controller with a port forward on it is the single worst thing you will find on a takeover. If the customer genuinely needs remote access, that is a VPN conversation with whoever runs their IT, not a NAT rule you add yourself. Give the head end a static address or a DHCP reservation and write it down, because the most common reason nobody can reach a panel a year later is that it moved.\n\nOn the reader side, the protocol IS the security control. Wiegand sends the card number in clear on two wires and has no authentication in either direction, which is why a reader on the unsecured side of a door is a gift... clip the pair behind it and you can capture credentials and replay them, and the door has no way to notice. That is the entire premise of the attack material in [[guide:rfid-credential-cloning|the credential cloning guide]], and the defence is not a better card, it is [[guide:credential-technology-and-card-formats|OSDP with Secure Channel]] so the link is authenticated and encrypted end to end. Specify it on every new install where the hardware supports it, and remember that turning OSDP on is not the same as turning secure channel on... a reader can speak OSDP in the clear, which buys you the wiring advantages and none of the security. Where you are stuck with legacy Wiegand on an exterior reader, at least get the controller behind the secure side of the door, use the reader's tamper output, and tell the customer plainly what the limitation is.\n\nThe physical layer deserves the same suspicion. Everything that can unlock the door should live on the secure side: the controller, the power supply, the REX device and its wiring, and the lock's own supply. A [[guide:request-to-exit-rex-wiring|REX loop]] or a lock feed accessible from outside is a bypass with no log entry, and it is the sort of thing that gets found during a penetration test rather than an inspection. Fit and terminate the tamper switches on enclosures and readers instead of leaving them dangling, because an unmonitored tamper is decoration.\n\nFirmware is the boring one that matters. Controllers, readers, NVRs and door stations all ship with updatable firmware and a security history, and the update path is usually easiest at install time when nothing is in service. Do it then, note the versions in the handover, and agree with the customer who is responsible for the next one... because if nobody owns it, nobody does it. While you are there, turn off the services the site is not using: unused HTTP interfaces, UPnP, discovery protocols and default sample accounts.\n\nFinally, hand over what you know. The documentation is a security control in its own right: changed credentials, IP addresses and VLAN, firmware versions, who has admin accounts, what the offline behaviour of each door is, and what the release paths are. A system nobody can administer is a system that gets left on defaults by the next person, and the fastest way to guarantee your work is undone is to be the only one who understands it.",sources:["https://www.cisa.gov/resources-tools/resources/securing-physical-access-control-systems","https://www.ict.co/media/2podvv4n/ict_card_reader_configuration_guide.pdf","https://www.securityindustry.org/report/osdp-the-standard-for-access-control/","https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-116r1.pdf"],relatedGuides:["rfid-credential-cloning","credential-technology-and-card-formats","networking-basics-for-security-techs","access-control-systems","request-to-exit-rex-wiring","biometric-readers"],relatedModels:["ict-tsec-reader","akuvox-video-door-station","ses-tec1-series","linear-access-telephone-entry-panel","hikvision-turret-ip"],tags:["Reader","OSDP","Secure Channel","VLAN","Wiegand","REX","NAT","VPN"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","OSDP SC","AES-128","encrypted reader","SCBK","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","26-bit","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","request to exit","exit button","push to exit","motion sensor over the door","PIR REX","access panel","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","clone the chip","copy the key","cloner","clonable chip","4C clone","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","shim","no-pick entry","shove knife","bypass tool","get past the lock"]},{id:"keri-pxl-takeover",title:"Taking over a legacy Keri PXL site: identify before you touch",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"A Keri PXL-500 panel is one of the most common things you will inherit on an older commercial or multi-dwelling property, and it is one of the easiest to get wrong on the first visit, because almost every decision you will be asked to make depends on facts that are not visible from the cabinet. The panel does not tell you which readers are on the doors. It does not tell you which credentials the site can buy. The enclosure is usually dense, field-labelled by three different people over twenty years, and entirely convincing about things that are no longer true. So the first visit is an identification job, and the discipline is to write down what you confirmed and what you only assumed.\n\nStart with what the platform is, because the architecture explains most of the behaviour you will be called about. The PXL-500 Tiger (and the Tiger II, and the PXL-510 which is the same board with an extra serial port for a NetworX NX-8E alarm panel) is a distributed-intelligence controller. One door onboard, two with an SB-593 satellite board. Controllers chain together on RS-485 from TB1, up to 128 of them, 256 doors, and only the controller at address 1 talks to the PC. Every controller holds its own cardholders and its own schedules and decides locally, so the doors keep working when the head end is off. That last property is the platform's great strength and the source of its strangest faults... a controller that has been off the network for a year is still happily granting access to the cardholder list it had a year ago.\n\nThen identify the reader, because this is the fact everything else hangs off. The controller was ordered as a **PXL-500P for Keri MS readers** or a **PXL-500W for Wiegand readers**, and the two are different products with the same silhouette. You cannot tell from the cabinet. You tell at the door, at the reader pigtail, and the tell is one wire: **blue on pin 1 is the reader antenna, and blue means Keri MS.** No blue, with green on pin 1 and white on pin 7, is Wiegand Data0 and Data1. The full Keri pinout on TB5 (the A reader) and TB6 (the B reader) runs 1 blue antenna, 2 green beeper, 3 red power, 4 black ground, 5 brown green-LED, 6 white red-LED, 7 silver shield.\n\n[[diagram:keri-reader-pinout|Both pinouts side by side, and the two colours that change meaning between them]]\n\nNotice what that means before you start probing. Green and white appear in both pinouts and do completely different jobs: green is the beeper on a Keri reader and Data0 on a Wiegand one, white is the red LED on a Keri reader and Data1 on a Wiegand one, and they sit on different pins in each. Match the wire's purpose to the pin, never the colour to the pin. Keri are explicit that the Wiegand colours are an industry convention and that plenty of manufacturers ignore it, so on a Wiegand head the reader's own manual outranks the colour every time.\n\nThe reason all of this matters commercially is the credential. Keri MS is **K-format**, Keri's own proprietary 125 kHz encoding, and generic prox stock will not read on it at any price. A PXL-500P site buys genuine KC-10X or MT-10X cards or PKT-10X key tags, matched to the site's facility code and card-number range... or compatible blanks encoded to that structure. A PXL-500W site buys ordinary Wiegand credentials in whatever format the head was set up for. Quote the wrong one and you eat the order. The newer [[model:keri-systems-pxl-nxt|NXT line]] is a third world again at 13.56 MHz, and an MS fob will not read on an NXT head or vice versa. Identify the technology first and the format second, and never assume a large site is homogeneous... a property that has been added to over two decades very often is not.\n\nWhile you are at the panel there are four more things worth recording, and all of them take a minute. The **address** of each controller, read by clicking S1 and watching the two-digit display... they must be unique and the master must be 1. Whether each controller carries an **SB-593**, because that decides whether it runs one door or two, and therefore what your door count actually is. The **head end**: Doors32 is the legacy software and Doors.NET is current, and which one is running decides whether a feature request is a configuration change or a migration project. And the **3 V lithium coin cell** on each board (CR2325 or BR2325), which holds RAM and the clock, because a flat one is what turns the next power cut into a site-wide outage.\n\nOne warning about addresses, since it is the classic way a good first visit becomes a bad one. **Changing a controller's address automatically resets its RAM**, which erases everything in that controller, and it then needs a Total Update from Doors to be usable again. The same is true of a deliberate RAM reset via the JP10 jumper. Neither is a casual diagnostic step on a live site... know what the head end will push back down, and know who can push it, before you touch S1.\n\nFinally, be honest in your notes about the difference between confirmed and inferred. On a takeover you will be tempted to write down the reader technology from the shape of the head, the door count from the controller count, and the standby time from the battery size. All three are guesses. Mark them as guesses, list them as open items for the next visit, and confirm them at the door... because the first person to write an assumption down as a fact is the person the next three years of service calls will quietly blame.",sources:["https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500_Installation_Guide.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500-Installation.pdf","https://help.kerisys.com/portal/en/kb/articles/pxl-500-installation-guide","https://cdn.adiglobaldistribution.us/pim/Original/10808/KF-MS5000BX_Product-Data-Sheet.pdf","https://kerisys.com/products/pxl-500/"],relatedGuides:["keri-pxl-reader-fault-checklist","access-control-systems","credential-technology-and-card-formats","low-voltage-wiring","pacs-hardening","sequence-of-operations"],relatedModels:["keri-ms-series-readers","keri-systems-pxl-nxt","hid-reader"],tags:["Wiegand","Controller","Reader","Cardholder","Standby","Facility code","RS-485","Fob"],aliases:["D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","26-bit","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","access panel","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","battery backup","UPS","standby battery","how long on battery","site code","FC","card range","wrong facility code","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","key tag","badge","prox card","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work"]},{id:"keri-pxl-reader-fault-checklist",title:"Keri PXL reader faults: the door-side checklist",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Work this one in order, because the order is what saves you the ladder. Most Keri reader calls get solved at the door in five minutes by somebody who watched the LED, and lost to an afternoon by somebody who opened the panel first.\n\nBefore anything else, get the symptom scope straight, because it names the layer. **A whole panel dead** after a power event is power and battery, not readers. **A group of doors down together** is the RS-485 chain or the head end. **One door misbehaving while its neighbours are fine** is that controller and that door. And **some credentials working at a door while others fail** is neither a dead reader nor a wiring fault... a broken reader, a bad relay or a cut pair fails every credential equally. A per-credential failure is range, technology, or the database, and that is a completely different afternoon.\n\n**Step 1 is free: present a credential and watch the reader.** The LED and beeper are driven by the CONTROLLER over the reader cable, not generated locally, so what the door does tells you how far the transaction actually got. Steady amber is idle and waiting. Green LED with one long beep is access granted. Flashing red with one short beep is access DENIED. A pulsing beep with flashing red for the duration is a door alarm. Learn those four and you have split the fault before you have taken a tool out.\n\n[[diagram:keri-fob-fault-tree|The three outcomes, and which half of the system each one eliminates]]\n\nRead the result properly, because the split is sharper than people expect.\n\n\u2022  **Nothing at all... no LED change, no beep.** The reader never saw the credential. Everything downstream is unproven but untouched: this is range, credential technology, or the reader's own power. Go to step 2.\n\u2022  **Flashing red and one short beep.** The read SUCCEEDED and the controller made a decision. The reader works, its cable works, its power works, and the controller is alive and listening. Nothing physical is wrong. Go to step 4, the database.\n\u2022  **Green and one long beep, but the door or gate does not open.** The credential is fine and the controller granted it. The fault is downstream of the decision: the lock relay on TB3, the lock power, or the gate operator's input. Go to step 5.\n\nOne exception to hold in mind: the **MS-4000 has no LED and no beeper at all** (it is the vandal-resistant model, and it takes a 4-conductor cable for exactly that reason). On an MS-4000 this whole split is unavailable and you go straight to the event log in Doors to see whether the read arrived.\n\n**Step 2, when the reader gave you nothing: measure at the reader, not at the panel.** Put the meter across pins 3 and 4 at the head, and take the reading with the credential presented so the reader is actually drawing. You are looking for 12-14 VDC. This is where the [[model:keri-ms-series-readers|MS-7000]] catches people: it needs 11 VDC minimum where an MS-3000 or MS-5000 will run down to 5, it draws 200 mA doing it, and 24 AWG only carries that 250 feet before you need 20 AWG. A parking or gate reader is by definition at the end of the longest run on the property. When its supply sags the reader does not die, it goes deaf... range drops from fourteen inches to two or three, and the failure you get told about is 'it works for some fobs, not others', because you have raised the bar to the strongest credentials in the building. A [[tool:multimeter|meter]] at the head under load settles it in thirty seconds.\n\n**Step 3, still nothing: work the rest of the range list.** Confirm the credential technology matches the reader... a K-format Keri fob will not read on a Wiegand or NXT head and no amount of wiring will change that. Try a known-good card as well as a fob, because every published range figure is quoted with a CARD held flat and parallel in clean RF, and a fob on a keyring at an angle reads materially less. Check the earth ground: Keri call out TB2 pin 3 specifically, and a missing or poor earth shows up as short read range across the whole controller. Check the cable shield is terminated at the controller end, on a corner screw, the green J6 lug, TB10 pin 3, or the enclosure lug. Then look for EMI... Keri want at least four feet of separation from the power supply, no reader cable run alongside power cable, and they specify a LINEAR supply rather than a switcher for these heads. Finally, look at the mounting: 125 kHz heads detune against metal and interfere with each other, so a reader flat on a metal frame, or two readers back to back on the same post, will underperform their spec for reasons no meter will show you.\n\n**Step 4, when the reader read it and the controller said no: go to the database, and start with the controller's own copy.** Remember the distributed intelligence... every controller holds its own cardholder list and grants locally. So the classic version of 'some fobs work, some do not' at ONE door on a multi-controller site is simply that this controller never received the newer cardholders. Confirm it is actually online in the software, then run a network update; if the panel has been reset, replaced or re-addressed, run an Auto-Configuration first so the master re-polls the network, then update. After that check the ordinary things: the credential is enrolled at all, it is in an access group that includes THIS door, and the group has not been edited without a network update pushed afterwards, which is exactly how Keri's own troubleshooting describes cardholders getting the wrong access. If every card at that controller has stopped, suspect RAM rather than the database... a power event can corrupt it, and the 3 V lithium cell (CR2325 / BR2325) that holds it should measure about 3 V. Replace, RAM reset with JP10 and S1, then a Total Update, and know before you start that the reset erases the controller completely.\n\n**Step 5, granted but nothing moved.** You are downstream of the decision now, which is ordinary lock work. The PXL's lock relay is a 1.0 A, 24 VDC dry Form C on TB3, and it is a TRIGGER, not a supply... a mag lock or a hungry strike gets switched through a separate power-supply relay rather than pulled through the controller. Check the relay actually transfers, check the lock's own supply and its fuse on the distribution board, and on a vehicle gate check what the operator wants to see on its input, because a gate operator taking a dry closure will sit there silently if it is being handed a voltage instead. If the lock releases but the door does not report, that is the door contact on TB4 pins 1-2, which is also the thing that makes a reader beep continuously at power-up when it is left open.\n\n**A few faults that look like reader faults and are not.** Continuous beeping from power-up is the door contact input, not the reader. A red power LED with a flickering address display is supply voltage outside 11.1 to 14.4 VDC, which is a power supply or a lock dragging the rail down, and it will make readers misbehave long before it makes anything obvious. A reader that behaves on the bench and misbehaves on the door is environmental every time. And on a PXL-500W only, a Wiegand head that does absolutely nothing is often the JP5 supply jumper: it defaults to 5 VDC on pins 2-3, needs pins 1-2 for a 12 V reader, and the warning LED lights when 12 V is being supplied. That jumper does not apply to Keri prox readers, which are always 12 V.\n\nClose the call properly. Write down what the LED did, what you measured at the head and under what load, and which of the five steps ended it... because on a site with a dozen of these controllers, the next call is going to sound exactly the same, and the note you leave is the difference between five minutes and an afternoon.",sources:["https://www.keri-kb.com/Content/DoorsNET/Troubleshooting/Troubleshooting_PXL.htm","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500-Installation.pdf","https://jmacfiles.s3.amazonaws.com/PXL-500_Installation_Guide.pdf","https://cdn.adiglobaldistribution.us/pim/Original/10808/KF-MS5000BX_Product-Data-Sheet.pdf","https://help.kerisys.com/portal/en/kb/articles/pxl-500-troubleshooting-and-diagnostics-p-n-01917-001-rev-a"],relatedGuides:["keri-pxl-takeover","keypad-reader-servicing","low-voltage-wiring","wire-tracing-shorts","dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"],relatedModels:["keri-ms-series-readers","keri-systems-pxl-nxt"],tags:["Reader","Controller","Distributed intelligence","Read range","Credential","Relay","Wiegand","Cardholder"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","access panel","ACU","Mercury","LenelS2","Software House","Kantech","board in the closet","badge","prox card","key tag","iCLASS","MIFARE","Seos","card won't work","dry contact","relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","D0","D1","Data 0","Data 1","26-bit","H10301","green and white wire","SIA Wiegand","key fob","keyfob","prox tag","strike plate","won't latch","door won't close","misaligned strike"]},{id:"wire-index",title:"Wire index: what to pull for every access-control device",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"This one is a lookup page, not a read. The physics of why a run sags... voltage drop, supply sizing, suppression, the door loop... is worked through properly in [[guide:low-voltage-wiring|the low-voltage wiring guide]], and there is no point saying it twice. What follows is the part you actually want at seven in the morning with a parts list to write: which cable goes to which device, what the number on the jacket means, and where you are allowed to run it.\n\n[[diagram:wire-index|Every cable type, what it feeds, and the jacket ratings that decide where it may run]]\n\nRead the shorthand first, because the whole trade writes it the same way and nobody explains it. **22/4 means 22 AWG, four conductors.** Not four pairs... four individual wires. Network cable is the exception that counts in PAIRS, so Cat5e is 24 AWG four-PAIR, eight conductors total. When a spec sheet says six-conductor shielded it wants 22/6 or 24/6 with a foil and a drain, and when a supplier offers you \"22/6 unshielded, same price\" the answer on a reader run is no.\n\nThe families are worth holding in your head as four groups rather than thirteen products. **Network** is Cat5e or Cat6, and it carries anything with an IP address plus PoE to power it. **Reader cable** is multi-conductor shielded, 22 or 24 AWG, sized by how many things the head needs... four conductors for a bare keypad, six once you want an LED and a buzzer, eight when tamper and hold join in. **Lock power** is fat and unshielded, 18 AWG as the default and heavier when the run is long or the lock is hungry, because it carries current rather than signal. **Bus** is shielded twisted pair with a defined impedance, and it is what RS-485 and OSDP want.\n\nThe distances in the table split into two completely different kinds of number and mixing them up is the classic error. Ethernet's 100 metres and RS-485's 1200 are HARD limits set by the protocol... exceed them and the link fails outright, no matter how good your copper. But every entry marked drop-limited has no fixed ceiling at all: a lock feed is limited by how much voltage you can afford to lose on the way, which depends on the current the lock draws and the gauge you pulled. There is no maximum length for 18/2. There is only a maximum length for THIS lock at THIS current on 18/2, and it is arithmetic.\n\nHere is that arithmetic once, on a real device, because it explains a published spec that otherwise looks arbitrary. Copper resistance is about 25.7 ohms per thousand feet at 24 AWG, 16.1 at 22, 10.2 at 20 and 6.4 at 18. Current goes out and comes back, so a 250-foot run is 500 feet of copper. Take a [[model:keri-ms-series-readers|Keri MS-7000]], which draws 200 mA and needs at least 11 V at the head. At 250 feet on 24 AWG: 500 feet of copper is 12.8 ohms, times 0.2 A is a **2.6 V drop**, so a 13.8 V supply arrives as 11.2 V. It works, barely. Double the run to 500 feet and the drop doubles to 5.1 V, the reader sees 8.7 V, and it goes deaf. Now pull the same 500 feet in 20 AWG: 10.2 ohms per thousand feet gives a 2.0 V drop and the head sees 11.8 V, which is fine. That is precisely why Keri publish 250 feet on 24 AWG and 500 feet on 20 AWG for that reader, and once you have done the sum once you can do it for any device on any run without asking anybody.\n\nTwo habits fall straight out of that. Measure at the LOAD rather than at the supply, because the supply is always fine and the far end is where the fault lives. And when a device has a stated minimum voltage, treat it as the number to design against rather than the nominal 12 V on the box... the difference between a reader that wants 11 V minimum and one that runs down to 5 V is the difference between a re-pull and a shrug.\n\nThe letters on the jacket are a code question, not a preference, and they are the part most likely to fail an inspection. **CM, CL2 and CL3 are general purpose. The R suffix is riser**, meaning a vertical shaft passing between floors. **The P suffix is plenum**, meaning an air-handling space, which in most commercial buildings is the void above the suspended ceiling that the return air travels through. Plenum jacket exists because ordinary PVC in a fire fills that void with dense toxic smoke and the air handling then distributes it through the building. The substitution rule runs one way only: you may always use a higher rating in place of a lower one, so CMP goes anywhere CMR goes and CMR goes anywhere CM goes, and you may never go the other way. CL2 is rated to 150 V and CL3 to 300 V, both power-limited under NEC article 725, while communications cable sits under article 800 and fire alarm cable under 760. If you are not certain whether a ceiling void is a plenum, ask the building, because you cannot tell by looking and the person who gets asked at inspection is you.\n\nShielding earns a paragraph because half of it gets thrown away at the termination. A foil shield with a drain wire works by giving induced noise somewhere to go, and it only has somewhere to go if the drain is landed at ONE end... the panel end. Land it at both and you have built a loop between two grounds that are never at quite the same potential, and current flows in the shield, and your reader run has become an antenna that performs worse than the unshielded cable you were trying to improve on. Shield the reader run and the bus. Do not bother shielding lock power, which is a current path and does not care.\n\nOn stocking: carry Cat6, 22/6 shielded, 18/2 and 18/4 and you can complete most access-control jobs. [[guide:keri-pxl-takeover|Legacy sites]] will hand you exceptions... a proprietary reader with a published cable spec you must honour, or a run already pulled in something you would not have chosen. Composite cable, which bundles a reader cable, a lock pair and a contact pair in one jacket, is a genuine time saver on a fresh multi-door install and a genuine nuisance on a retrofit where the three runs do not go to the same places. And pull a spare pair on every run you make, every time. Copper is cheap, the ceiling is open right now, and the second visit costs more than the whole spool.",sources:["https://www.cablinginstall.com/cable/article/14035166/tia-606-c-standard-requirements-for-cable-plant-administration","https://ramcorpwire.com/resources/plenum-vs-riser-cable/","https://bigacables.com/blogs/knowledge-center/cl2-cl3-cm-cmr-cmp-cable-ratings","https://us.misumi-ec.com/blog/copper-wire-resistance-awg-chart/","https://cdn.adiglobaldistribution.us/pim/Original/10808/KF-MS5000BX_Product-Data-Sheet.pdf","https://www.ict.co/media/uvtddsut/prt-rdm2-din_protege_din_rail_2_reader_expander_installation_manual.pdf"],relatedGuides:["low-voltage-wiring","dropping-fishing-wire","wire-tracing-shorts","credential-technology-and-card-formats","keri-pxl-reader-fault-checklist","request-to-exit-rex-wiring"],relatedModels:["keri-ms-series-readers","ict-tsec-reader","access-control-plugin-power-supplies"],tags:["RS-485","Reader","IP address","Suppression","Voltage drop","OSDP"],aliases:["card reader","badge reader","HID","Signo","ProxPoint","SecuraKey","reader won't beep","card not reading","transorb","MOV","TVS diode","surge suppressor","1N4004","flyback diode","spike suppression","wire gauge","long run","sagging voltage","reader browns out","not enough voltage at the door"]},{id:"fire-alarm-interface",title:"The fire alarm interface: where the release signal comes from",module:"Access Control & Low Voltage",body:"Everything about what to DO with the fire signal is settled elsewhere. [[guide:dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring|The dry-contact guide]] runs the mag's power leg through a series chain of hold contacts so any one of them drops the lock, and [[guide:low-voltage-wiring|the wiring guide]] sorts out which arrangements have to release automatically at all. This one is about the other end of that wire: what the fire alarm system actually hands you, who is allowed to touch it, and how you find out whether it works.\n\nStart with the thing that decides your whole posture on this job. **Fire alarm is not your licence.** In California a C-28 lock and security equipment classification and a C-7 low-voltage classification BOTH carve fire alarm out explicitly... it is C-10 electrical or C-16 fire protection territory, and that is covered properly in [[guide:ca-scope-c28-c7|the scope guide]]. So on a normal job you are not programming the panel, not adding modules to the loop, and not deciding which initiating devices drive the release. Somebody else does that. Your job is to specify what you need, land on what they give you, and prove the whole path works.\n\nWhat they give you is almost always a **dry contact**, and it usually comes from an addressable relay module hanging on the panel's signalling line circuit... a Notifier FRM-1 or its equivalent from whichever manufacturer the building runs. The module is powered from the loop itself, addressed at the panel, and presents Form-C dry contacts to you. It is worth knowing that shape even though you will not install it, because it tells you what to ASK for: a dry Form-C contact, dedicated to the locks, that changes state on a general alarm. If somebody offers you a live voltage instead you have a [[guide:wire-index|different wiring problem]] and a conversation to have.\n\n[[diagram:fire-alarm-interface|The two sides, the contact between them, and the arrangement that fails safe]]\n\nNow the part that is genuinely counterintuitive, and the reason this guide exists. You want a contact that is **held closed while the building is normal and opens on alarm**, because that contact sits in the lock's power leg and opening it drops the lock. The instinct is to reach for the terminal marked NC. Do not. **Ask for the relay to be held ENERGIZED in normal condition, with your lock power through its NORMALLY-OPEN contact.** Energized, that NO contact is closed, and the lock holds. On alarm the module de-energizes, the contact returns to open, and the door releases. The reason to do it that way round is everything that comes for free: a cut wire between the module and your lock releases the door, a failed relay releases the door, and a fire panel that has lost power releases the door. A contact wired the intuitive way sits there closed through every one of those failures and holds the door locked in exactly the conditions where you most needed it open.\n\nThat is not a contradiction of the series-chain material in the dry-contact guide, and it is worth being precise about why, because the two use the same word for different things. \"Normally closed\" there describes the STATE the contact is in during normal building operation... held closed, in series, ready to break. \"Normally open\" here describes the terminal LABEL on the relay, which means its de-energized resting state. A normally-open contact on a normally-energized relay IS a contact that is closed in normal operation. Same behaviour, opposite word, and mixing them up is how a door ends up wired to lock on alarm.\n\n**Supervision is what separates a professional interface from a wire.** NFPA 72 wants the interconnection monitored for integrity, so that a break or a short in the run between the fire alarm system and the thing it controls annunciates as a trouble rather than sitting silent until the day of the fire. An unsupervised pair looks identical to a supervised one right up until somebody's drywall screw finds it. Ask explicitly whether your interface is supervised and how, get the answer in the submittals, and understand that if the fire contractor is supervising it then the module and its wiring are theirs to fault-find and not yours to \"fix\" with a jumper. There is also a placement rule worth carrying: NFPA 72 puts the emergency control function interface device within about three feet of the component it controls, so a relay module is expected to live at the equipment it drops, not in a cupboard down the corridor.\n\nThe behaviour the code wants out the far end is simple to state and worth reciting on site. Every exit unlocks on ANY fire alarm signal, and stays released until the system is manually reset. Not a timed release, not a release that re-secures itself when the alarm clears, and not a release that depends on somebody at a workstation. It is also worth knowing that this reaches beyond your doors: fire recall drives the elevators under the same signal and [[guide:elevator-access-control|overrides access control entirely]], and on a stair door the release has to satisfy [[guide:stairwell-reentry|the re-entry rules]] as well, which are stricter again.\n\nTesting is where most of the real failures surface, and there is a right way and a comfortable way. The comfortable way is to jumper the contact at the lock and watch the door drop. That proves your wiring and absolutely nothing else... not that the module is addressed correctly, not that the panel is programmed to operate it, not that the right initiating devices are mapped to it. **Test it by putting the panel into alarm.** Walk every door on the interface, confirm each releases, then reset the panel and confirm each re-secures. While you are there, pull the interface conductor and confirm the panel goes to trouble, which is the only way to know supervision is real. Do this with the fire contractor present, because half of it is their equipment and all of it is a shared acceptance.\n\nOne last failure mode, and it is the one that survives a clean commissioning. If your release only works while your own power supply is up, you have not built a release... you have built a feature of your power supply. Loss of AC has to drop the lock as surely as the alarm does, which means the standby battery must not quietly hold a mag lock energized through an evacuation. Test it on the day with the breaker, not by reasoning about it, and write what you saw into the [[guide:sequence-of-operations|sequence of operations]] so the next tech inherits a tested claim rather than an assumption.",sources:["https://idighardware.com/2010/12/nfpa-72-on-access-control-2/","https://up.codes/s/control-units-annunciators-and-access-keys","https://www.securityinfowatch.com/access-identity/access-control/article/10713290/access-control-and-nfpa-101-compliance","https://firealarm.com/product/notifier-frm-1/","https://nationaltrainingcenter.com/nfpa-72-circuits-and-pathways/"],relatedGuides:["dry-contacts-no-nc-lock-wiring","low-voltage-wiring","maglocks","stairwell-reentry","elevator-access-control","ca-scope-c28-c7","sequence-of-operations"],relatedModels:["securitron-maglock","access-control-plugin-power-supplies","hes-1006"],tags:["NFPA 72","Relay","Normally closed","Normally open","Sequence of operations","Standby","Dry contact","Opening"],aliases:["relay clicks","NO NC COM","relay output","contact won't close","battery backup","UPS","how long on battery","volt free","form C","clean contact","lockout","locked out","get in","no key","emergency opening"]}]},772,[]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,_m,e,_d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"articleFor",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return n}}),Object.defineProperty(e,"ago",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});var t=r(_d[0]);const n=n=>t.WIKI[n.id]||(n.cards?.join("\n\n")??""),o=t=>{const n=Math.max(0,Date.now()-t),o=Math.floor(n/6e4),c=Math.floor(o/60),u=Math.floor(c/24);return u>0?`${u}d`:c>0?`${c}h`:o>0?`${o}m`:"now"}},773,[774]);
__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"WIKI",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}});const t={w1l1:"Locksmiths describe work by the opening \u2014 the door, the frame, and the hardware together. Before you touch anything, you read the opening: what kind of door and frame, what family of hardware is on it, and how the door is handed.\n\nHanding is the door's address. It's how you tell a supplier exactly which unit you need, and it drives which side trim and cylinders go on. The rule is fixed: you always determine handing from the OUTSIDE of the door \u2014 the secure side, the side a key goes.\n\nStanding outside, look at the hinges. Hinges on the left is a left-hand door; hinges on the right is right-hand. Then look at the swing: if the door pushes away from you it's a standard hand (LH or RH); if it pulls toward you it's a reverse (LHR or RHR), also called a reverse bevel.\n\nMost commercial exterior doors swing outward for egress, so from the outside they pull toward you. That's why so much commercial hardware is specified LHR or RHR. Get in the habit of naming the hand out loud on every opening \u2014 it removes guesswork from ordering and from setting the lock.\n\nFinally, learn the five families that cover most commercial doors: cylindrical lever locks, mortise locks, exit devices, deadbolts (including storefront), and the cylinders/cores that key them all. Everything in this module is one of these.",w1l2:"The cylindrical lever lock is the most common commercial interior lock. It's a 'bored' lock: the chassis passes through a 2-1/8 inch cross bore drilled in the door face, and the latch sits in a 1 inch bore in the door edge. That round, drilled look is how you recognize it at a glance.\n\nBecause it installs fast and takes standard door prep, it's everywhere: offices, back rooms, restrooms, stockrooms. Levers are now standard over knobs for accessibility.\n\nTwo numbers matter to a supplier. First, the ANSI/BHMA grade: Grade 1 is heavy commercial duty and what most commercial jobs call for; Grade 2 is lighter. Second, the function, which sets how the levers lock \u2014 storeroom, classroom, entry, passage, privacy. Storeroom is the commercial default: the outside lever is always locked and retracts by key only, while the inside lever always allows free egress.\n\nGo-to models to know by name: Schlage ND (Grade 1) and AL (Grade 2), and Falcon T. When someone says 'a storeroom ND lever in satin chrome,' you should picture the exact lock.",w1l3:"A mortise lock is a complete lock case housed in a rectangular pocket \u2014 the mortise \u2014 cut into the edge of the door. You recognize it by the tall faceplate along the door edge and the deep pocket prep, as opposed to the round bores of a cylindrical lock.\n\nIt costs more and takes more preparation, but it earns it: a mortise case is tougher and far more versatile. A single case can carry a latch, a deadbolt, and lever trim together, and offers a wide range of functions from one body.\n\nThe lock is keyed by a mortise cylinder \u2014 a threaded round cylinder that screws into the case and turns a cam. The cam is the arm that actually drives the lockwork, so cam type and length matter when you swap cylinders.\n\nCommercial mortise locks to know: Schlage L9000, Sargent 8200, and Corbin Russwin ML2000. They follow the ANSI mortise standard, so prep is consistent across brands even when trim differs.",w1l4:"The exit device \u2014 the panic bar \u2014 exists for one reason: free egress. Push the bar or pad and the latch retracts and the door opens, no key or thought required. That life-safety promise is why they're required on many commercial exits and why they must always let people out.\n\nDevices are grouped by chassis type, meaning how they latch. Rim devices latch on the surface of the door into a rim strike. Mortise devices drive a mortise lock case in the door edge. Surface Vertical Rod (SVR) devices run exposed rods up and down the door face to latch at the top and bottom. Concealed Vertical Rod (CVR) devices do the same but with the rods hidden inside the door.\n\nWhat the outside can do is set by the trim and function. EO means Exit Only \u2014 no outside trim at all, free egress inside and nothing outside. NL, night latch, adds an outside key cylinder that retracts the latch for entry. Other trims add levers or pulls with various locking behavior.\n\nDogging holds the pushpad down mechanically so the latch stays retracted, turning the door into push/pull for free entry during business hours. Important safety rule: fire-rated exit devices never dog, because the latch must always be free to relatch and hold the fire door closed.\n\nThe series to know cold is Von Duprin 98/99 \u2014 the heavy-duty benchmark, made in rim, mortise, SVR, and CVR versions. You will see it constantly.",w1l5:"Aluminum storefront doors \u2014 the glass entry doors on shops and offices \u2014 have a narrow vertical frame member called the stile. Because the stile is thin, storefront hardware is built narrow to fit it, which is why it looks different from wood-door hardware.\n\nThe everyday lock is the Adams Rite deadlatch, model 4710. It's a spring latchbolt with a small auxiliary bolt beside it. That auxiliary bolt is the deadlocking plunger: when the door closes, it rides on the strike and holds the latchbolt so the latch can't be shimmed or 'loided' open with a card.\n\nWhen a door needs real holding power, you step up to the MS deadbolt \u2014 Adams Rite MS1850 \u2014 the maximum-security storefront deadbolt. By default it throws a straight deadbolt; a hookbolt variant swings a hook into the strike, which resists the door being spread or pried, exactly what's needed on narrow-stile and paired doors.\n\nA handy fact: the 4710 deadlatch and the MS1850 deadbolt share the same stile prep and faceplate, so one can be swapped for the other without modifying the door. Both are operated by a mortise cylinder fitted with an MS cam.",w1l6:"The cylinder is the part the key actually turns, and its body style has to match the lock. Grabbing the right cylinder type is half of most keyed jobs, so learn to read them by shape.\n\nA rim cylinder mounts from behind the door and drives its lock through a flat blade called a tailpiece. It's what feeds a rim exit device; you'll recognize it by that tailpiece and the two mounting screws that come through from the back.\n\nA mortise cylinder is a threaded round body that screws into a mortise lock or a storefront lock and turns a cam. Thread it in, set the cam, and it's driving the lockwork.\n\nA KIK, or key-in-knob, cylinder is the small cylinder that lives inside a cylindrical knob or lever.\n\nFinally, the SFIC \u2014 small format interchangeable core \u2014 is the figure-8 shaped core you can pull out and swap using a control key, with no disassembly. It's built for fast rekeying: change the core and you've changed the key, in seconds. That figure-8 profile and control-key removal are its signature.",w2l1:"Rekeying is the most common billable task in the trade, and it runs out of the pinning kit. The kit holds top (driver) pins, bottom (key) pins, springs, and the small tools that load and hold them.\n\nThe plug follower is the tool that makes rekeying sane. As you push the plug out of the shell, the follower slides in right behind it, keeping the top pins and their springs trapped up in the shell. Without it, the springs launch the top pins across the room the moment the plug clears.\n\nAround the follower you use a plug holder to grip the plug while you change its bottom pins, tweezers to place each pin, and a pinning mat or tray to keep loose pins from rolling away. A key gauge (also called a decoder) reads the depth of each cut on the new key so you know which bottom pin height to drop into each chamber.\n\nA best-practice truck kit: a full follower set sized to the cylinders you service, fine tweezers, a magnetic tray, a key gauge, and a labeled pin tray organized by the keying system (for example, a specific pin-kit depth series) you work in most.",w2l2:"Lock picking is a two-handed skill: one hand keeps a light turning force on the plug with a tension wrench, the other lifts pins with a pick. Neither works without the other.\n\nThe tension wrench, also called a turning tool, is the foundation. It puts a gentle rotational load on the plug so that when a pin is lifted to the shear line, the plug rotates a hair and traps that pin set. Too much tension and pins bind before they reach the line; too little and they fall back. Developing that feel is most of picking.\n\nThere are two core techniques. Single Pin Picking (SPP) uses a hook pick to find and set one pin at a time, which is precise and works on higher-quality locks with security pins. Raking is faster and less surgical: a rake pick is scrubbed in and out across all the pins while tension is applied, bouncing them toward the shear line together. Raking shines on inexpensive, loosely toleranced locks.\n\nA sensible starter set is a couple of hook picks, two or three rakes of different profiles, and both top-of-keyway and bottom-of-keyway tension wrenches so you can work whichever end of the keyway a lock gives you.",w2l3:"On a lockout call, bypass is often faster and cleaner than picking. Bypass means opening the door by working the latch or the mechanism directly, never touching the pins.\n\nThe under-door tool (UDT) is a commercial staple: a long, angled tool that slides under the door, rises on the inside, and hooks the interior lever or the panic-bar push pad to release it. On many commercial exits with free egress hardware, it's a few seconds of work.\n\nA shove knife, or 'loid', slips a plain spring latch by camming the latchbolt back. It only works when there's no deadlocking bolt. When the door has a deadlatch, the auxiliary bolt holds the latch and loiding fails, which is exactly what the aux bolt exists to do.\n\nFor aluminum storefront doors, purpose-bent tools reach in to work a thumbturn or pull the latch, and air wedges plus long-reach tools create and hold a gap to work through. Jiggler or try-out keys can rock open worn wafer locks and simple warded locks. The skill is matching the right bypass to the lock in front of you before you start prying.",w2l4:"Cutting keys comes down to two machines. A duplicator copies a key you already have; a code machine originates a key from its numeric code with no original needed.\n\nA duplicator clamps the original key beside a blank. A tracer (guide) rides along the original's cuts while a cutting wheel mirrors that path onto the blank. It's quick and ideal for spares, but a duplicate is only ever as accurate as the key it copies, so worn originals make worn copies.\n\nA code machine cuts to a bitting, the set of depth numbers that define a key. Feed it the code, or a bitting you decoded from the lock, and it originates a fresh, in-spec key even when there is no key to copy. That's what you need after a total lockout or on new construction.\n\nEvery cut key starts as a blank, and the blank's keyway must match the lock or the finished key won't even enter. Blank identification is a real skill. Names you'll hear constantly: Ilco and Silca for blanks, and HPC, Framon, and Ilco for machines. Keep a rugged manual machine on the truck and a precise electronic one in the shop.",w2l5:"Hanging hardware on a new or replacement door is a boring-and-chiseling job, and the right jig turns a slow, error-prone task into a fast, straight one.\n\nAn installation jig (template) clamps onto the door edge and face and guides the tools: a hole saw cuts the 2-1/8 inch cross bore for the lock chassis, and a spade or auger bit bores the latch hole in the edge. Because the jig fixes the centers, the two bores line up the first time.\n\nA self-centering bit (Vix bit) is a small but vital tool: it centers pilot holes in hinge and strike screw holes so the screws pull the plate tight instead of wandering and pulling it crooked. A wood chisel mortises the shallow pockets for latch faceplates and strikes, and a file dresses metal edges or eases a strike that's binding.\n\nDrilling a lock to open it is a last resort, not a first move. When it's warranted, a sharp bit, a drill guide to hold your angle, and knowing exactly where the pins (at the shear line) or the bolt sit let you make one clean, targeted cut instead of destroying the whole door.\n\nFastening best practice on hollow metal (steel) doors: mount hardware with through-bolts (sex bolts / Chicago bolts) that clamp all the way through the door, not self-tapping screws. Self-tappers only bite into thin sheet metal and strip or pull out under the constant pull of a lever or the repeated slam of a closer; a through-bolt sandwiches the door skin and holds for the life of the hardware. On the frame side, machine-screw into a tapped reinforcement plate rather than sheet metal, and use sex bolts through a closer or exit device wherever the door is hollow. On wood doors, wood screws are fine as long as they reach solid backing, not just the veneer.",w2l6:"When there's no original key and no code, you can still make a key by reading the lock itself. Two skills do this: decoding and impressioning.\n\nA decoder reads a lock's cuts or a key's depths directly and converts them into a bitting you can cut on a machine. Some decoders work on the key, some probe the lock; either way they turn the physical lock into numbers.\n\nImpressioning makes a working key at the lock with nothing but a blank and hand tools. You fit a snug blank into the keyway, apply turning tension, and rock it. The binding pins press against the blank and leave tiny marks exactly where each cut needs to go. You file down to each mark with a round or pippin file, reinsert, and repeat. As each cut reaches the right depth, that pin stops marking; when every pin sets, the blank turns the lock.\n\nThe impressioning kit is simple: a fine round/pippin file, a way to grip the blank firmly (a vise or a dedicated handle), and a loupe to read the faint marks. The real tool is patience, but the payoff is a key made from nothing on the spot."}},774,[]);
